ystyle=\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\mathbb{E}\\left[{n_{k}(T)}\\right]\\Delta_{k}, (9) where Δ k = μ k ∗ − μ k \\Delta_{k}=\\mu_{k^{*}}-\\mu_{k} is the sub-optimality gap of arm k k with respect to the optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} , and n k ​ ( T ) n_{k}(T) is the number of times arm k k is pulled in T T slots. For the regret analysis, we assume without loss of generality that the rewards are between 0 and 1 for all k ∈ { 1 , 2 , … ​ K } k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots K\\} . Note that the C-Bandit algorithms do not require this condition, and the regret analysis can also be generalized to any bounded rewards. 4.1 Competitive and Non-competitive arms with respect to Arm k For the purpose of regret analysis in Section 4 , we need to understand which arms are empirically competitive as t → ∞ t\\rightarrow\\infty . We do so by defining the notions of Competitive and Non-Competitive arms. Definition 5 (Non-Competitive and Competitive arms) . An arm ℓ \\ell is said to be non-competitive if the expected reward of optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} is larger than the expected pseudo-reward of arm ℓ \\ell with respect to the optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} , i.e, if, Δ ~ ℓ , k ∗ ≜ μ k ∗ − ϕ ℓ , k ∗ &gt; 0 \\tilde{\\Delta}_{\\ell,k^{*}}\\triangleq\\mu_{k^{*}}-\\phi_{\\ell,k^{*}}&gt;0 . Similarly, an arm ℓ \\ell is said to be competitive if Δ ~ ℓ , k ∗ = μ k ∗ − ϕ ℓ , k ∗ &lt;= 0 \\tilde{\\Delta}_{\\ell,k^{*}}=\\mu_{k^{*}}-\\phi_{\\ell,k^{*}}&lt;=0 . The unique best arm k ∗ k^{*} has Δ ~ k ∗ , k ∗ = μ k ∗ − ϕ k ∗ , k ∗ = 0 \\tilde{\\Delta}_{k^{*},k^{*}}=\\mu_{k^{*}}-\\phi_{k^{*},k^{*}}=0 and is counted in the set of competitive arms. 3 3 3 As t → ∞ t\\rightarrow\\infty , only the optimal arm will remain in 𝒮 t \\mathcal{S}_{t} , and hence the definition of competitive arms only compares the expected mean of arm k ∗ k^{*} and expected pseudo-reward of arm k k with respect to arm k ∗ k^{*} We refer to Δ ~ ℓ , k ∗ \\tilde{\\Delta}_{\\ell,k^{*}} as the pseudo-gap of arm ℓ \\ell in the rest of the paper. These notions of competitiveness are used in the regret analysis in Section 4 . The central idea behind our correlated C-BANDIT approach is that after pulling the optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} sufficiently large number of times, the non-competitive (and thus sub-optimal) arms can be classified as empirically non-competitive with increasing confidence, and thus need not be explored. As a result, the non-competitive arms will be pulled only O ⁡ ( 1 ) \\mathrm{O}(1) times. However, the competitive arms cannot be discerned as sub-optimal by just using the rewards observed from the optimal arm, and have to be explored O ⁡ ( log ⁡ T ) \\mathrm{O}(\\log T) times each. Thus, we are able to reduce a K K -armed bandit to a C C -armed bandit problem, where C C is the number of competitive arms. 4 4 4 Observe that k ∗ k^{*} and subsequently C C are both unknown to the algorithm. Before the start of the algorithm, it is not known which arm is optimal/competitive/non-competitive. Algorithm works in an online manner by evaluating the noisy notions of competitiveness, i.e., empirically competitive arms, and ensures that only C − 1 C-1 of the arms are pulled O ⁡ ( log ⁡ T ) \\mathrm{O}(\\log T) times. We show this by bounding the regret of C-BANDIT approach. 4.2 Regret Bounds In order to bound 𝔼 ⁡ [ R ​ e ​ g ​ ( T ) ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[{Reg(T)}\\right] in ( 9 ), we can analyze the expected number of times sub-optimal arms are pulled, that is, 𝔼 ​ [ n k ​ ( T ) ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[{n_{k}(T)}\\right] , for all k ≠ k ∗ k\\neq k^{*} . 1 and 2 below show that 𝔼 ​ [ n k ​ ( T ) ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[{n_{k}(T)}\\right] scales as O ⁡ ( 1 ) O(1) and O ⁡ ( log ⁡ T ) O(\\log T) for non-competitive and competitive arms respectively. Recall that a sub-optimal arm is said to be non-competitive if its pseudo-gap Δ ~ k , k ∗ &gt; 0 \\tilde{\\Delta}_{k,k^{*}}&gt;0 , and competitive otherwise. Theorem 1 (Expected Pulls of a Non-competitive Arm) . The expected number of times a non-competitive arm with pseudo-gap Δ ~ k , k ∗ \\tilde{\\Delt