r(E_{1}(t))+\\Pr(E^{c}_{1}(t),k_{t}=k,I_{k,t-1}&gt;I_{k^{*},t-1}). (16) The first term sums to a constant, while the second term is upper bounded by the number of times UCB pulls the sub-optimal arm k k . Due to this the upper bound on the number of pulls of competitive arm by C-UCB / C-TS is only an additive constant more than the upper bound on the number of pulls for an arm by UCB / TS algorithms and hence we have same pre-log constants for the upper bound on the pulls of competitive arms. ii) Probability of identifying a non-competitive arm as empirically competitive jointly with optimal arm being pulled more than t K \\frac{t}{K} times is small. Notice that the first two steps of our algorithm involve identifying the set of arms 𝒮 t \\mathcal{S}_{t} that have been pulled at least t K \\frac{t}{K} times, and eliminating arms which are empirically non-competitive with respect to the set 𝒮 t \\mathcal{S}_{t} for round t t . We show that the joint event that arm k ∗ ∈ 𝒮 t k^{*}\\in\\mathcal{S}_{t} and a non-competitive arm k k is identified as empirically non-competitive is small. Formally, Pr ⁡ ( k t + 1 = k , n k ∗ ​ ( t ) ≥ t K ) ≤ t ​ exp ⁡ ( − t ​ Δ ~ k , k ∗ 2 ​ K ) . \\Pr\\left(k_{t+1}=k,n_{k^{*}}(t)\\geq\\frac{t}{K}\\right)\\leq t\\exp\\left(-\\frac{t\\tilde{\\Delta}_{k,k^{*}}}{2K}\\right). (17) This occurs because upon obtaining a large number of samples of arm k ∗ k^{*} , expected reward of arm k ∗ k^{*} (i.e., μ k ∗ \\mu_{k^{*}} ) and expected pseudo-reward of arm k k with respect to arm k ∗ k^{*} (i.e., ϕ k , k ∗ \\phi_{k,k^{*}} ) can be estimated fairly accurately . Since the pseudo-gap of arm k k is positive (i.e., μ k ∗ &gt; ϕ k , k ∗ \\mu_{k^{*}}&gt;\\phi_{k,k^{*}} ), the probability that arm k k is identified as empirically competitive is small. An implication of ( 17 ) is that the expected number of times a non-competitive arm is identified as empirically competitive jointly with the optimal arm having at least t K \\frac{t}{K} pulls at round t t is bounded above by a constant. iii) Probability that a sub-optimal arm is pulled more than t / K t/K times at round t t is small. Formally, we show that for C-UCB, we have Pr ⁡ ( n k ​ ( t ) ≥ t K ) ≤ ( 2 ​ K + 2 ) ​ ( t K ) − 2 ∀ t &gt; K ​ t 0 , k ≠ k ∗ \\Pr\\left(n_{k}(t)\\geq\\frac{t}{K}\\right)\\leq(2K+2)\\left(\\frac{t}{K}\\right)^{-2}\\quad\\forall t&gt;Kt_{0},k\\neq k^{*} (18) This component of our analysis is specific to the classical bandit algorithm used in C-BANDIT . Intuitively, a result of this kind should hold for any good performing classical multi-armed bandit algorithm. We reach the result of ( 18 ) in C-UCB by showing that Pr ⁡ ( k t + 1 = k , n k ​ ( t ) &gt; t 2 ​ K ) ≤ t − 3 ∀ t &gt; t 0 , k ≠ k ∗ \\Pr\\left(k_{t+1}=k,n_{k}(t)&gt;\\frac{t}{2K}\\right)\\leq t^{-3}\\quad\\forall t&gt;t_{0},k\\neq k^{*} (19) The probability of selecting a sub-optimal arm k k after it has been pulled significantly many times is small as with more number of pulls, the exploration component in UCB index of arm k k becomes small, and consequently it is likely to be smaller than the UCB index of optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} (as it has larger empirical mean reward or has been pulled fewer number of times). Our analysis in 9 shows how the result in ( 19 ) can be translated to obtain ( 18 ) (this translation is again not dependent on which bandit algorithm is used in C-BANDIT ). We show that the expected number of pulls of a non-competitive arm k k can be bounded as 𝔼 ⁡ [ n k ​ ( T ) ] ≤ ∑ t = 1 T Pr ⁡ ( k t + 1 = k , k ∗ = arg ​ max k ⁡ n k ​ ( t ) ) + Pr ⁡ ( k ∗ ≠ arg ​ max k ⁡ n k ​ ( t ) ) \\mathbb{E}\\left[{n_{k}(T)}\\right]\\leq\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\Pr\\left(k_{t+1}=k,k^{*}=\\argmax_{k}n_{k}(t)\\right)+\\Pr\\left(k^{*}\\neq\\argmax_{k}n_{k}(t)\\right) (20) The first term in ( 20 ) is O ⁡ ( 1 ) \\mathrm{O}(1) due to ( 17 ) and the second term is O ⁡ ( 1 ) \\mathrm{O}(1) due to ( 18 ). Refer to Appendix D for rigorous regret analysis of C-UCB. 4.4 Discussion on Regret Bounds Competitive Arms. Recall than an arm is said to be competitive if μ k ∗ \\mu_{k^{*}} (i.e., expected reward from arm k ∗ k^{*} ) &gt; 𝔼 ⁡ [ ϕ k , k ∗ ] = 𝔼 ⁡ [ 𝔼 ~ ​ [ R k ′ | R k ] ] &gt;\\mathbb{E}\\left[{\\phi_{k,k^{*}}}\\right]=\\mathbb{E}\\left[{\\tilde{\\mathbb{E}}[R_{k^{\\prime}}|R_{k}]}\\right] . Since the distribution of reward of each arm is unknown, initially the Algorithm does not know which arm is competitive and which arm is non-competitive . Reduction in effective number of arms. Interestingly, our result from 1 shows that the C-UCB algorithm, that operates in a sequential fashion, makes sure that non-competitive arms are pulled only O ⁡ ( 1 ) \\mathrm{O}(1) times. Due to this, only the competitive arms are pulled O ⁡ ( log ⁡ T ) \\mathrm{O}(\\log T) times. Moreover, the pre-log terms in the upper bound of UCB and C-UCB for these arms is the same. In this sense, our C-BANDIT approach reduces a K K -armed bandit problem to a C C -armed bandit problem. Effectively only C − 1 ≤ K − 1 C-1\\leq K-1 arms are pulled O ⁡ ( log ⁡ T ) \\mathrm{O}(\\log T) times, while other arms are stopped being pulled after a finite time. p 1 ​ ( r ) p_{1}(r) r s 2 , 1 ​ ( r ) s_{2,1}(r) s 3 , 1 ​ ( r ) s_{3,1}(r) 0.2 0 0.7 2 0.2 1 0.8 1.2 0.6 2 2 1 Table 3 : Suppose Arm 1 is optimal and its unknown probability distribution is ( 0.2 , 0.2 , 0.6 ) (0.2,0.2,0.6) , then μ 1 = 1.4 \\mu_{1}=1.4 , while ϕ 2 , 1 = 1.5 \\phi_{2,1}=1.5 and ϕ 3 , 1 = 1.2 \\phi_{3,1}=1.2 . Due to this Arm 2 is Competitive while Arm 3 is non-competitive Depending on the joint probability distribution, different arms can be optimal, competitive or non-competitive. Table 3 shows a case where arm 1 is optimal and the reward distribution of arm 1 1 is ( 0.2 , 0.2 , 0.6 ) (0.2,0.2,0.6) , which leads to μ 1 = 1.4 &gt; ϕ 3 , 1 = 1.2 \\mu_{1}=1.4&gt;\\phi_{3,1}=1.2 and μ 1 = 1.4 &lt; ϕ 2 , 1 = 1.5 \\mu_{1}=1.4&lt;\\phi_{2,1}=1.5 . Due to this Arm 2 is competitive while Arm 3 is non-competitive. Achieving Bounded Regret. If the set of competitive arms 𝒞 \\mathcal{C} is a singleton set containing only the optimal arm (i.e., the number of competitive arms C = 1 C=1 ), then our algorithm will lead to (see ( 15 )) an expected regret of O ⁡ ( 1 ) \\mathrm{O}(1) , instead of the typical O ⁡ ( log ⁡ T ) \\mathrm{O}(\\log T) regret scaling in classic multi-armed bandits. One such scenarion in which this can happen is if pseudo-rewards s k , k ∗ s_{k,k^{*}} of all arms with respect to optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} match the conditional expectation of arm k k . Formally, if s k , k ∗ = 𝔼 ⁡ [ R k | R k ∗ ] ​ ∀ k s_{k,k^{*}}=\\mathbb{E}\\left[{R_{k}|R_{k^{*}}}\\right]\\forall{k} , then 𝔼 ⁡ [ s k , k ∗ ] = 𝔼 ⁡ [ R k ] = μ k &lt; μ k ∗ \\mathbb{E}\\left[{s_{k,k^{*}}}\\right]=\\mathbb{E}\\left[{R_{k}}\\right]=\\mu_{k}&lt;\\mu_{k^{*}} . Due to this, all sub-optimal arms are non-competitive and our algorithms achieve only O ⁡ ( 1 ) \\mathrm{O}(1) regret. We now evaluate a lower bound result for a special case of our model, where rewards are correlated through a latent random variable X X as described in Section 2.3 . We present a lower bound on the expected regret for the model described in Section 2.3 . Intuitively, if an arm ℓ \\ell is competitive , it can not be deemed sub-optimal by only pulling the optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} infinitely many times. This indicates that exploration is necessary for competitive sub-optimal arms. The proof of this bound closely follows that of the 2-armed classical bandit problem lai1985asymptotically ; i.e., we construct a new bandit instance under which a previously sub-optimal arm becomes optimal without affecting reward distribution of any other arm. Theorem 3 (Lower Bound for Correlated MAB with latent random source) . For any algorithm that achieves a sub-polynomial regret, the expected cumulative regret for the model described in Section 2.3 is lower bounded as lim T → ∞ inf 𝔼 ⁡ [ R ​ e ​ g ​ ( T ) ] log ⁡ ( T ) ≥ { max k ∈ 𝒞 ⁡ Δ k D ( f R k | | f R ~ k ) if ​ C &gt