ables bounded between [ a , b ] : a ≤ Z i ≤ b [a,b]:a\\leq Z_{i}\\leq b , then for any δ &gt; 0 \\delta&gt;0 , we have Pr ⁡ ( | ∑ i = 1 n Z i n − 𝔼 ⁡ [ Z i ] | ≥ δ ) ≤ exp ⁡ ( − 2 ​ n ​ δ 2 ( b − a ) 2 ) . \\Pr\\left(\\left|\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}Z_{i}}{n}-\\mathbb{E}\\left[{Z_{i}}\\right]\\right|\\geq\\delta\\right)\\leq\\exp\\left(\\frac{-2n\\delta^{2}}{(b-a)^{2}}\\right). Lemma 1 (Standard result used in bandit literature) . If μ ^ k , n k ​ ( t ) \\hat{\\mu}_{k,n_{k}(t)} denotes the empirical mean of arm k k by pulling arm k k n k ​ ( t ) n_{k}(t) times through any algorithm and μ k \\mu_{k} denotes the mean reward of arm k k , then we have Pr ( μ ^ k , n k ​ ( t ) − μ k ≥ ϵ , τ 2 ≥ n k ( t ) ≥ τ 1 ) ≤ ∑ s = τ 1 τ 2 exp ( − 2 s ϵ 2 ) . \\Pr\\left(\\hat{\\mu}_{k,n_{k}(t)}-\\mu_{k}\\geq\\epsilon,\\tau_{2}\\geq n_{k}(t)\\geq\\tau_{1}\\right)\\leq\\sum_{s=\\tau_{1}}^{\\tau_{2}}\\exp\\left(-2s\\epsilon^{2}\\right). Proof. Let Z 1 , Z 2 , … ​ Z t Z_{1},Z_{2},...Z_{t} be the reward samples of arm k k drawn separately. If the algorithm chooses to play arm k k for m t ​ h m^{th} time, then it observes reward Z m Z_{m} . Then the probability of observing the event μ ^ k , n k ​ ( t ) − μ k ≥ ϵ , τ 2 ≥ n k ​ ( t ) ≥ τ 1 \\hat{\\mu}_{k,n_{k}(t)}-\\mu_{k}\\geq\\epsilon,\\tau_{2}\\geq n_{k}(t)\\geq\\tau_{1} can be upper bounded as follows, Pr ( μ ^ k , n k ​ ( t ) − μ k ≥ ϵ , τ 2 ≥ n k ( t ) ≥ τ 1 ) \\displaystyle\\Pr\\left(\\hat{\\mu}_{k,n_{k}(t)}-\\mu_{k}\\geq\\epsilon,\\tau_{2}\\geq n_{k}(t)\\geq\\tau_{1}\\right) = Pr ( ( ∑ i = 1 n k ​ ( t ) Z i n k ​ ( t ) − μ k ≥ ϵ ) , τ 2 ≥ n k ( t ) ≥ τ 1 ) \\displaystyle=\\Pr\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n_{k}(t)}Z_{i}}{n_{k}(t)}-\\mu_{k}\\geq\\epsilon\\right),\\tau_{2}\\geq n_{k}(t)\\geq\\tau_{1}\\right) (22) ≤ Pr ( ( ⋃ m = τ 1 τ 2 ∑ i = 1 m Z i m − μ k ≥ ϵ ) , τ 2 ≥ n k ( t ) ≥ τ 1 ) \\displaystyle\\leq\\Pr\\left(\\left(\\bigcup_{m=\\tau_{1}}^{\\tau_{2}}\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{m}Z_{i}}{m}-\\mu_{k}\\geq\\epsilon\\right),\\tau_{2}\\geq