be its CDF, i.e. F Γ ​ ( γ ) = P ​ ( γ ^ ​ ( 𝐱 , y ) &lt; γ | ( 𝐱 , y ) ∼ 𝒟 ) F_{\\Gamma}(\\gamma)=\\textbf{P}(\\hat{\\gamma}(\\mathbf{x},y)&lt;\\gamma\\;|\\;(\\mathbf{x},y)\\sim\\mathcal{D}) . Then we can state the following result about the natural and adversarial error of the derived classifier f baseline ​ ( ⋅ ) f_{\\text{baseline}}(\\cdot) . Theorem 1 . Let g : ℝ d → 𝒴 g:\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{Y} be a classifier and let F Γ F_{\\Gamma} be the CDF of the distribution of signed margins for g ⁡ ( ⋅ ) g(\\cdot) . Define f baseline : ℝ d → 𝒴 ∪ { a } f_{\\text{baseline}}:\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{Y}\\cup\\{a\\} as in ( 3 ) with threshold parameter γ ∗ {\\gamma^{*}} . Then for an attack radius ϵ \\epsilon , ℛ nat ​ ( f baseline ) \\displaystyle\\mathcal{R}_{\\text{nat}}(f_{\\text{baseline}}) = F Γ ​ ( γ ∗ ) \\displaystyle=F_{\\Gamma}({\\gamma^{*}}) ℛ adv ​ ( f baseline ) \\displaystyle\\mathcal{R}_{\\text{adv}}(f_{\\text{baseline}}) ≤ F Γ ​ ( ϵ − γ ∗ ) \\displaystyle\\leq F_{\\Gamma}(\\epsilon-{\\gamma^{*}}) The full proof can be found in Appendix A . While calculating F Γ F_{\\Gamma} exactly is intractable for deep networks, it can be approximated using an adversarial attack as in ( 4 ); see also Appendix F . 5 Jointly Learning a Robust Classifier and Abstain Region The margin-based baseline for abstaining on adversarial examples described in Section 4 has two drawbacks. First, it has a fixed tradeoff between natural and adversarial error. Second, it is computationally expensive to calculate, since an adversarial attack must be performed against every input at inference time. We propose a method to jointly learn a classifier and the region over which that classifier should abstain. This is in contrast to the baseline, in which a classifier is trained first and then an abstain region is calculated afterwards. We propose to train a classifier f : ℝ d → 𝒴 ∪ { a } f:\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{Y}\\cup\\{a\\} which learns to either output one of the classes y ∈ 𝒴 y\\in\\mathcal{Y} in the training data or an additional “abstain\" class a a . We would like f ⁡ ( ⋅ ) f(\\cdot) to minimize a combination ℛ ⁡ ( f ) = ℛ nat ​ ( f ) + λ ​ ℛ adv ​ ( f ) \\mathcal{R}(f)=\\mathcal{R}_{\\text{nat}}(f)+\\lambda\\,\\mathcal{R}_{\\text{adv}}(f) of robust and adversarial error as defined in Section 3.2 . However, directly minimizing the 0-1 losses in these formulations is intractable, so instead we experiment with surrogate loss functions. As a surrogate for natural error ℛ nat \\mathcal{R}_{\\text{nat}} , we use the standard cross-entropy loss. Let p i ​ ( 𝐱 ) p_{i}(\\mathbf{x}) be the probability assigned by the classifier f ⁡ ( ⋅ ) f(\\cdot) to class i i for input 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} . Then the cross-entropy loss, also known as negative log likelihood loss, is given by ℒ nat ( f ) = 𝔼 ( 𝐱 , y ) ∼ 𝒟 − log p y ( 𝐱 ) \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{nat}}(f)=\\mathop{\\mathbb{E}}_{(\\mathbf{x},y)\\sim\\mathcal{D}}-\\log p_{y}(\\mathbf{x}) Often, in adversarial training, the adversarial loss is given by the maximum of some function ℓ ⁡ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\ell(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) over an ϵ \\epsilon -ball around 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} : ℒ adv ​ ( f ) = 𝔼 ( 𝐱 , y ) ∼ 𝒟 max 𝐱 ~ ∈ ℬ ϵ ​ ( 𝐱 ) ⁡ ℓ ⁡ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{adv}}(f)=\\mathop{\\mathbb{E}}_{(\\mathbf{x},y)\\sim\\mathcal{D}}\\max_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}\\in\\mathcal{B}_{\\epsilon}(\\mathbf{x})}\\ell(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) (5) For instance, madry_towards_2018 use ℓ ⁡ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) = − log ⁡ p y ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) \\ell(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y)=-\\log p_{y}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}) . In the formulation of robustness given in Section 3.2 , the classifier may output either the true class y y or the abstain class a a within the ϵ \\epsilon -ball. Thus, we would like an alternate formulation of ℓ ⁡ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\ell(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) which is low when either the correct class or the adversarial class is likely to be predicted. In particular, here