sses have high probability for an adversarial example. 6 Attacks on Abstaining Classifiers We develop multiple adaptations of the standard pgd ( madry_towards_2018 ) and DeepFool ( moosavi-dezfooli_deepfool:_2016 ) adversarial attacks to the setting where an abstaining classifier f ⁡ ( ⋅ ) f(\\cdot) must be fooled to both misclassify and not abstain. The attacks are useful for both solving the inner maximization in the adversarial training approach described in Section 5 as well as evaluating trained abstaining classifiers. 6.1 Attacks Based on PGD x x ~ \\displaystyle\\widetilde{\\textbf{x}} Problem: gradient masking x x ~ \\displaystyle\\widetilde{\\textbf{x}} Solution: surrogate loss for PGD x x ~ \\displaystyle\\widetilde{\\textbf{x}} Problem: PGD jumps over max x x ~ \\displaystyle\\widetilde{\\textbf{x}} Solution: decay step size x x ~ \\displaystyle\\widetilde{\\textbf{x}} Problem: stuck in local max x x ~ \\displaystyle\\widetilde{\\textbf{x}} Solution: interpolate ℓ cw \\displaystyle\\ell_{\\text{cw}} and ℓ abstain \\displaystyle\\ell_{\\text{abstain}} ℓ ( 1 ) \\displaystyle\\ell^{(1)} ℓ abstain \\displaystyle\\ell_{\\text{abstain}} ℓ interp \\displaystyle\\ell_{\\text{interp}} Figure 5 : Various problems we solved when trying to find an adversarial example 𝐱 ~ \\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}} during CARL training using PGD. Top: directly maximizing the adversarial loss ℓ ( 1 ) \\ell^{(1)} or ℓ ( 2 ) \\ell^{(2)} is prone to gradient masking, so instead we use ℓ abstain \\ell_{\\text{abstain}} or ℓ interp \\ell_{\\text{interp}} . Middle: PGD can sometimes “jump\" over sharp maxima, so we use an exponentially decaying step size. Bottom: PGD can get stuck in local maxima, so we vary the loss function from the beginning of PGD to the end. pgd is an iterative method for attacking a classifier f ⁡ ( ⋅ ) f(\\cdot) given a loss function ℓ ⁡ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\ell(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) which is high when misclassification occurs. At each iteration t t , the current input 𝐱 ~ ( t ) \\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}^{(t)} is perturbed by step size δ \\delta in the direction ∇ 𝐱 ~ ℓ ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ ( t ) , y ) \\nabla_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}}\\ell(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}^{(t)},y) . Then, the resulting input is projected into the ball of allowable perturbations ℬ ϵ ​ ( 𝐱 ) \\mathcal{B}_{\\epsilon}(\\mathbf{x}) . This process repeats for a set number of iterations or until misclassification is achieved. For the standard adversarial robustness setting, a common choice of ℓ ⁡ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\ell(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) is given by Carlini and Wagner ( carlini_towards_2017 ) . Let z i ​ ( 𝐱 ) z_{i}(\\mathbf{x}) be the i i th logit (pre-softmax activation) of the classifier f ⁡ ( ⋅ ) f(\\cdot) for input 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} . Carlini and Wagner’s loss is ℓ cw ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) = max i ≠ y ⁡ ( z i ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) ) − z y ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) \\ell_{\\text{cw}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y)=\\max_{i\\neq y}\\big(z_{i}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}})\\big)-z_{y}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}) Maximizing this loss function reduces the probability the classifier outputs the correct class y y while increasing the probability the classifier outputs a different class. We can directly adapt this loss function to the case where our attack must both fool the classifier and prevent it from abstaining: ℓ abstain ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) = max i ≠ y , i ≠ a ⁡ ( z i ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) ) − max ⁡ ( z y ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) , z a ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) ) \\ell_{\\text{abstain}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y)=\\max_{i\\neq y,i\\neq a}\\big(z_{i}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}})\\big)-\\max\\big(z_{y}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}),z_{a}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}})\\big) We also experiment with three additional variations for ℓ abstain ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\ell_{\\text{abstain}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) . Some of these variations depend on the current iteration t t out of T T total iterations of pgd . They are ℓ sum ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\displaystyle\\ell_{\\text{sum}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) = max i ≠ y , i ≠ a ⁡ ( z i ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) ) − ( z y ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) + z a ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) ) \\displaystyle=\\max_{i\\neq y,i\\neq a}\\big(z_{i}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}})\\big)-\\big(z_{y}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}})+z_{a}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}})\\big) ℓ interp ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\displaystyle\\ell_{\\text{interp}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) = ( 1 − t / T ) ​ ℓ cw ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) + ( t / T ) ​ ℓ abstain ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\displaystyle=(1-\\nicefrac{{t}}{{T}})\\ell_{\\text{cw}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y)+(\\nicefrac{{t}}{{T}})\\ell_{\\text{abstain}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) ℓ switch ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) \\displaystyle\\ell_{\\text{switch}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y) = { ℓ cw ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) t &lt; T / 2 ℓ abstain ​ ( f , 𝐱 ~ , y ) otherwise \\displaystyle=\\begin{cases}\\ell_{\\text{cw}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y)\\quad&amp;t&lt;\\nicefrac{{T}}{{2}}\\\\ \\ell_{\\text{abstain}}(f,\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}},y)&amp;\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases} ℓ sum \\ell_{\\text{sum}} is similar to ℓ abstain \\ell_{\\text{abstain}} except that it sums z y ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) z_{y}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}) and z a ​ ( 𝐱 ~ ) z_{a}(\\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}) instead of taking their maximum. ℓ interp \\ell_{\\text{interp}} interpolates between ℓ cw \\ell_{\\text{cw}} at the beginning of the attack and ℓ abstain \\ell_{\\text{abstain}} at the end. ℓ switch \\ell_{\\text{switch}} uses ℓ cw \\ell_{\\text{cw}} for half the iterations and then switches to ℓ abstain \\ell_{\\text{abstain}} for the remaining iterations. Both ℓ interp \\ell_{\\text{interp}} and ℓ switch \\ell_{\\text{switch}} first attack the true label y y and only later also attack the abstain class a a . 6.2 Attacks Based on DeepFool (a) DeepFool (b) DeepFool-Abstain correct class incorrect class abstain region original input perturbed input Figure 6 : (a) Because DeepFool does not explicitly consider the abstain region, it may cross the linear approximation of the incorrect class’s decision boundary but still remain unsuccessful. (b) DeepFool-Abstain (see Appendix D ) considers the decision boundaries with both the correct class and abstain class, allowing it to reach the incorrect class in a single step. DeepFool ( moosavi-dezfooli_deepfool:_2016 ) is a generalized Newton’s method ( atzmon_controlling_2019 ) for finding an adversarial example 𝐱 ~ \\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}} close to an input 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} . Instead of taking uniform step sizes as in pgd , at each iteration t t DeepFool constructs a first-order approximation of the logits z i ​ ( ⋅ ) z_{i}(\\cdot) of the classifier at 𝐱 ~ ( t ) \\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}^{(t)} and then calculates the smallest perturbation (in L p L_{p} norm) to apply to 𝐱 ~ ( t ) \\widetilde{\\mathbf{x}}^{(t)} such that it will be misclassified under the approximation. 0% 10% 20% 30% 40% 50% 60% Natural error 0% 10% 20% 30% 40% 50% 60% Adversarial error Ideal classifier CIFAR-10 0% 2% 4% 6% 8% 10% Natural error 0% 2% 4% 6% 8% 10% Adversarial error Ideal classifier MNIST Baseline: upper bound Baseline: experimental CARL using ℓ ( 1 ) \\displaystyle\\ell^{(1)} CARL using ℓ ( 2 ) \\displaystyle\\ell^{(2)} Figure 7 : Natural and adversarial error, as defined in Section 3.2 , for the margin-based baseline as well as CARL -trained classifiers on MNIST and CIFAR-10; CARL improves on the baseline. Also, the bound given by Theorem 1 (shown in blue) holds for the baseline. We experiment with two variations of DeepFool applied to the setting where the classifier f ⁡ ( ⋅ ) f(\\cdot) may abstain. In the first variation, we simply target all classes besides the correct and abstain class. In the second variation, which we call DeepFool-Abstain, a different approach is used to calculate the minimum perturbation for each incorrect class. Rather than only considering a linear approximation of the boundary between the incorrect class and the current class—which could be the correct class y y or the abstain class a a —it explicitly considers boundaries between the incorrect class i i and both y y and a a . For a conceptual diagram,