Ω ) ] &lt; ∞ {\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P}}[g_{0}(\\|X\\|_{\\Omega})]&lt;\\infty and (8) S g ker ​ ( ℙ , y ) ≥ 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] + g 0 ​ ( 0 ) − 2 ​ 𝖤 ℙ ​ ( g 0 ​ ( ‖ X ‖ Ω ) ) − 2 ​ g 0 ​ ( ‖ y ‖ Ω ) . S^{\\text{ker}}_{g}(\\mathbb{P},y)\\geq{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]+g_{0}(0)-2{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P}}(g_{0}(\\|X\\|_{\\Omega}))-2g_{0}(\\|y\\|_{\\Omega}). If we now instead choose c 0 = c 2 = 1 c_{0}=c_{2}=1 and c 1 = − 2 c_{1}=-2 , then ( 7 ) gives the lower bound g ⁡ ( x , y ) ≥ 1.5 ​ g 0 ​ ( 0 ) + g 0 ​ ( ‖ x ‖ Ω ) + 0.5 ​ g 0 ​ ( ‖ y ‖ Ω ) g(x,y)\\geq 1.5g_{0}(0)+g_{0}(\\|x\\|_{\\Omega})+0.5g_{0}(\\|y\\|_{\\Omega}) . Thus (9) S g ker ​ ( ℙ , y ) ≤ 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] + 1.5 ​ g 0 ​ ( 0 ) − 𝖤 ℙ ​ ( g 0 ​ ( ‖ X ‖ Ω ) ) − 0.5 ​ g 0 ​ ( ‖ y ‖ Ω ) . S^{\\text{ker}}_{g}(\\mathbb{P},y)\\leq{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]+1.5g_{0}(0)-{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P}}(g_{0}(\\|X\\|_{\\Omega}))-0.5g_{0}(\\|y\\|_{\\Omega}). Since g 0 ​ ( ‖ x ‖ Ω ) ≍ ‖ x ‖ Ω α g_{0}(\\|x\\|_{\\Omega})\\asymp\\|x\\|_{\\Omega}^{\\alpha} and 𝖤 ℙ ​ [ g 0 ​ ( ‖ X ‖ Ω ) ] &lt; ∞ {\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P}}[g_{0}(\\|X\\|_{\\Omega})]&lt;\\infty the claim follows from ( 8 ) and ( 9 ). □ \\Box The theorem shows that S α ker S^{\\text{ker}}_{\\alpha} is not a robust scoring rule. However, we may modify the kernel in order to make it robust. For example: Corollary 1 . Let Ω = ℝ \\Omega=\\mathbb{R} and for c &gt; 0 c&gt;0 define (10) g c ​ ( x , y ) = { | x − y | | x − y | &lt; c , c otherwise. g_{c}(x,y)=\\begin{cases}|x-y|&amp;|x-y|&lt;c,\\\\ c&amp;\\mbox{otherwise.}\\end{cases} Then the robust CRPS (rCRPS) defined as S 1 , c ker ​ ( ℙ , y ) := S g c ker ​ ( ℙ , y ) S^{\\text{ker}}_{1,c}(\\mathbb{P},y):=S^{\\text{ker}}_{g_{c}}(\\mathbb{P},y) is a proper scoring rule on the class of Borel probability measures on ℝ \\mathbb{R} . Proof. By the definition of a negative definite kernel, one has that g ⁡ ( x , y 