ability measures ℚ θ \\mathbb{Q}_{\\theta} which are location-scale transformations satisfying Assumption 1 for α = 1 \\alpha=1 . Then the scale function of S 1 , c k ​ e ​ r S^{ker}_{1,c} on 𝒬 \\mathcal{Q} is s ⁡ ( ℚ θ ) = σ − 1 ​ 𝖤 ℚ , ℚ ​ [ H ℚ ​ ( X , Y ) ​ 𝕀 ​ ( | X − Y | &lt; c σ ) ] , s(\\mathbb{Q}_{\\theta})=\\sigma^{-1}{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{Q},\\mathbb{Q}}\\left[H_{\\mathbb{Q}}(X,Y)\\mathbb{I}\\left(\\left|X-Y\\right|&lt;\\frac{c}{\\sigma}\\right)\\right], where H ℚ ​ ( X , Y ) H_{\\mathbb{Q}}(X,Y) is a 2 × 2 2\\times 2 matrix independent of θ \\theta . This result implies that when applying S 1 , c k ​ e ​ r S^{ker}_{1,c} in a situation with varying σ \\sigma , i.e., the true predictive distribution has varying scale, observations with large values of σ \\sigma will be less important since c c is fixed. Note that it is not possible to set c c to depend on θ \\theta since it is unknown. This implies that the robustness will protect against outliers that are large on an absolute scale of the predictive measure, but the robustness cannot protect against outliers for predictions where σ \\sigma is small, i.e., outliers on a relative scale. In order to make the scoring rule robust against outliers of ℚ θ \\mathbb{Q}_{\\theta} the bound would need to be scaled with σ \\sigma , which as mentioned above is unknown a priori. It is to the authors’ knowledge an open question how to create a proper scoring rule that protects against outliers in ℚ θ \\mathbb{Q}_{\\theta} . One option that could work in practice is to set c c dependent on some reference predictive distribution. Since c c should only protect against outliers it does not seems as invasive as scaling the actual scoring rule with a reference score, but is nevertheless still problematic. 5. Generalized proper kernel scoring rules In the previous section, we saw that one could make the kernel scores robust by adjusting the kernel, but that they in general are scale dependent. Because of this, we now want to construct a new family of scoring rules which can be made locally scale invariant. This is done in the following theorem, where the generalized proper kernel scores are introduced. Theorem 3 . Let ℙ \\mathbb{P} be a Borel probability measure on a Hausdorff space Ω \\Omega . Assume that g g is a non-negative, continuous negative definite kernel on Ω × Ω \\Omega\\times\\Omega and that h h is a monotonically decreasing convex differentiable function on ℝ + \\mathbb{R}^{+} . Then the scoring rule (11) S g h ​ ( ℙ , y ) := h ⁡ ( 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] ) + 2 ​ h ′ ​ ( 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] ) ​ ( 𝖤 ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , y ) ] − 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] ) \\begin{split}S^{h}_{g}(\\mathbb{P},y):=&amp;h\\left({\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]\\right)+2h^{\\prime}\\left({\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]\\right)\\left({\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,y)\\right]-{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]\\right)\\end{split} is proper on the class of Borel probability measures on Ω \\Omega which satisfy 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] &lt; ∞ {\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]&lt;\\infty . Proof. For two measures ℙ \\mathbb{P} and ℚ \\mathbb{Q} in the class of Borel probability measures on Ω \\Omega which satisfy 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] &lt; ∞ {\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]&lt;\\infty , we need to establish that S g h ​ ( ℚ , ℚ ) ≥ S g h ​ ( ℙ , ℚ ) S^{h}_{g}\\left(\\mathbb{Q},\\mathbb{Q}\\right)\\geq S^{h}_{g}\\left(\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{Q}\\right) . By ( Berg et al. 1984 , Theorem 2.1, p.235) , 𝖤 ℙ , ℚ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] ≥ 1 2 ​ ( 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] + 𝖤 ℚ , ℚ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] ) . \\displaystyle{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{Q}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]\\geq\\frac{1}{2}\\left({\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]+{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{Q},\\mathbb{Q}}\\left[g(X,Y)\