}(\\mathsf{N}(\\hat{\\mu},\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}),y)] = σ ^ ​ ( 1 π − 2 ​ 𝖤 ​ [ φ ⁡ ( Y ~ ) ] − 2 ​ 𝖤 ​ [ Y ~ ​ Φ ​ ( Y ~ ) ] + 𝖤 ⁡ [ Y ~ ] ) \\displaystyle=\\hat{\\sigma}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}-2\\mathsf{E}[\\varphi(\\tilde{Y})]-2\\mathsf{E}[\\tilde{Y}\\Phi(\\tilde{Y})]+\\mathsf{E}[\\tilde{Y}]\\right) = σ ^ ​ ( 1 π − 2 ​ ( 1 + σ ~ 2 ) ​ 𝖤 ​ ( φ ⁡ ( Y ~ ) ) − 2 ​ μ ~ ​ 𝖤 ​ [ Φ ⁡ ( Y ~ ) ] + μ ~ ) \\displaystyle=\\hat{\\sigma}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}-2(1+\\tilde{\\sigma}^{2})\\mathsf{E}(\\varphi(\\tilde{Y}))-2\\tilde{\\mu}\\mathsf{E}[\\Phi(\\tilde{Y})]+\\tilde{\\mu}\\right) = σ ^ π − 2 ​ σ d ​ φ ​ ( μ d σ d ) − 2 ​ μ d ​ Φ ​ ( μ d σ d ) + μ d . \\displaystyle=\\frac{\\hat{\\sigma}}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}-2\\sigma_{d}\\varphi\\left(\\frac{\\mu_{d}}{\\sigma_{d}}\\right)-2\\mu_{d}\\Phi\\left(\\frac{\\mu_{d}}{\\sigma_{d}}\\right)+\\mu_{d}. For the scaled CRPS, similar calculations give 𝖤 𝖭 ⁡ ( μ , σ 2 ) ​ [ S 1 sta ​ ( 𝖭 ⁡ ( μ ^ , σ ^ 2 ) , y ) ] \\displaystyle\\mathsf{E}_{\\mathsf{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^{2})}[S^{\\text{sta}}_{1}(\\mathsf{N}(\\hat{\\mu},\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}),y)] = − π ​ 𝖤 ​ [ φ ⁡ ( − Y ~ ) ] + π 2 ​ ( 2 ​ 𝖤 ​ [ Y ~ ​ Φ ​ ( − Y ~ ) ] + 𝖤 ⁡ [ Y ~ ] ) − 1 2 ​ log ⁡ ( 2 ​ σ ^ π ) \\displaystyle=-\\sqrt{\\pi}\\mathsf{E}[\\varphi(-\\tilde{Y})]+\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}(2\\mathsf{E}[\\tilde{Y}\\Phi(-\\tilde{Y})]+\\mathsf{E}[\\tilde{Y}])-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(\\frac{2\\hat{\\sigma}}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}\\right) = − π ​ 𝖤 ​ [ φ ⁡ ( Y ~ ) ] + π 2 ​ 𝖤 ​ ( Y ~ ) − π ​ 𝖤 ​ [ Y ~ ​ Φ ​ ( Y ~ ) ] − 1 2 ​ log ⁡ ( 2 ​ σ ^ π ) \\displaystyle=-\\sqrt{\\pi}\\mathsf{E}[\\varphi(\\tilde{Y})]+\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}\\mathsf{E}(\\tilde{Y})-\\sqrt{\\pi}\\mathsf{E}[\\tilde{Y}\\Phi(\\tilde{Y})]-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(\\frac{2\\hat{\\sigma}}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}\\right) = − π σ ^ ​ ( σ d ​ φ ​ ( μ d σ d ) − μ d 2 + μ d ​ Φ ​ ( μ d σ d ) ) − 1 2 ​ log ⁡ ( 2 ​ σ ^ π ) . \\displaystyle=-\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{\\hat{\\sigma}}\\left(\\sigma_{d}\\varphi\\left(\\frac{\\mu_{d}}{\\sigma_{d}}\\right)-\\frac{\\mu_{d}}{2}+\\mu_{d}\\Phi\\left(\\frac{\\mu_{d}}{\\sigma_{d}}\\right)\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(\\frac{2\\hat{\\sigma}}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}\\right). Finally, to obtain the desired expressions for the robust scores, we just have to verify that 𝖤 𝖭 ⁡ ( μ , σ 2 ) ​ [ E ⁡ ( μ ^ − y , σ ^ , c ) ] = E ⁡ ( μ d , σ d , c ) . \\mathsf{E}_{\\mathsf{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^{2})}[E(\\hat{\\mu}-y,\\hat{\\sigma},c)]=E(\\mu_{d},\\sigma_{d},c). To that end, we define Y ~ 1 ∼ 𝖭 ( − ( μ d − c ) / σ ^ , σ ~ 2 ) \\tilde{Y}_{1}\\sim\\mathsf{N}(-(\\mu_{d}-c)/\\hat{\\sigma},\\tilde{\\sigma}^{2}) , Y ~ 2 ∼ 𝖭 ⁡ ( μ d / σ ^ , σ ~ 2 ) \\tilde{Y}_{2}\\sim\\mathsf{N}(\\mu_{d}/\\hat{\\sigma},\\tilde{\\sigma}^{2}) , and Y ~ 3 ∼ 𝖭 ⁡ ( ( μ d − c ) / σ ^ , σ ~ 2 ) \\tilde{Y}_{3}\\sim\\mathsf{N}((\\mu_{d}-c)/\\hat{\\sigma},\\tilde{\\sigma}^{2}) , and obtain 𝖤 𝖭 ⁡ ( μ , σ 2 ) ​ [ E ⁡ ( μ ^ − y , σ ^ , c ) ] = \\displaystyle\\mathsf{E}_{\\mathsf{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^{2})}[E(\\hat{\\mu}-y,\\hat{\\sigma},c)]= − μ d + 2 σ ^ ( 𝖤 [ φ ( Y ~ 2 ] ) + 𝖤 [ Y ~ 2 Φ ( Y ~ 2 ) ] ) \\displaystyle-\\mu_{d}+2\\hat{\\sigma}(\\mathsf{E}[\\varphi(\\tilde{Y}_{2}])+\\mathsf{E}[\\tilde{Y}_{2}\\Phi(\\tilde{Y}_{2})]) − σ ^ ​ ( 𝖤 ⁡ [ φ ⁡ ( Y ~ 1 ) ] + 𝖤 ⁡ [ Y ~ 1 ​ Φ ​ ( Y ~ 1 ) ] ) . \\displaystyle\\quad-\\hat{\\sigma}(\\mathsf{E}[\\varphi(\\tilde{Y}_{1})]+\\mathsf{E}[\\tilde{Y}_{1}\\Phi(\\tilde{Y}_{1})]). Evaluating the expectations and simplifying gives the desired result. □ \\Box Appendix B Generalized entropy characterizations In this section, we want to highlight the importance of the function H ⁡ ( ℙ ) = S ⁡ ( ℙ , ℙ ) H(\\mathbb{P})=S(\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}) and how it can be used to understand the behavior of the scoring rule S S in scenarios with varying uncertainty. The function H ⁡ ( ℙ ) H(\\mathbb{P}) can be seen as a measure of the variability of the probability measure ℙ \\mathbb{P} , and is often referred to either as the uncertainty function or as the generalized entropy ( Dawid 1998 ; Gneiting and Raftery 2007 ) . It should be noted that H ⁡ ( ℙ ) H(\\mathbb{P}) only depends on the predictive model ℙ \\mathbb{P} and not on the observed data. Thus by choosing to use a certain scoring rule S S , an implicit ordering of the possible measures is made through H H prior to observing any data. Hence, it is important to be aware that a choice is made and to understand how this affects the model selection. For the kernel scoring rules we have OPEN H ⁡ ( ℙ ) = 0.5 ​ 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] ) H(\\mathbb{P})=0.5{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]) , whereas the generalized proper kernel scoring rules have H ⁡ ( ℙ ) = h ⁡ ( 𝖤 ℙ , ℙ ​ [ g ⁡ ( X , Y ) ] ) H(\\mathbb{P})=h({\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{P}}\\left[g(X,Y)\\right]) . An advantage with the generalized proper kernel scoring rules is therefore that they, for each kernel g g , provide a family of proper scoring rules with a wide range of generalized entropies determined by h h . Let us now examine the generalized entropy for a set of measures with variable scale. Example 4 . Consider a family of probability measures { ℙ σ } \\{\\mathbb{P}_{\\sigma}\\} that differ only through their scaling parameter σ \\sigma . For the kernel g ⁡ ( x , y ) = | x − y | α g(x,y)=|x-y|^{\\alpha} with α ∈ ( 0 , 2 ] \\alpha\\in(0,2] , the kernel scoring rule then has generalized entropy H k ​ e ​ r ​ ( ℙ σ ) = σ α ​ 𝖤 ℙ 1 , ℙ 1 ​ [ | X − Y | α ] . H^{ker}(\\mathbb{P}_{\\sigma})=\\sigma^{\\alpha}{\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P}_{1},\\mathbb{P}_{1}}\\left[|X-Y|^{\\alpha}\\right]. The corresponding standarized kernel scoring rule has generalized entropy H s ​ t ​ a ​ ( ℙ σ ) = α ​ log ⁡ ( σ ) + log ⁡ ( 𝖤 ℙ 1 , ℙ 1 ​ [ | X − Y | α ] ) . H^{sta}(\\mathbb{P}_{\\sigma})=\\alpha\\log(\\sigma)+\\log\\left({\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{P}_{1},\\mathbb{P}_{1}}\\left[|X-Y|^{\\alpha}\\right]\\right). In a spatial setting like the one studied in Section 6.2 , H k ​ e ​ r ​ ( ℙ σ ) H^{ker}(\\mathbb{P}_{\\sigma}) compared to H s ​ t ​ a ​ ( ℙ σ ) H^{sta}(\\mathbb{P}_{\\sigma}) will therefore be more sensitive how the variance is chosen for observations at locations far from other locations, which typically have large values of σ \\sigma , while being less sensitive for observations at locations close to other locations, which typically have small values of σ \\sigma . Whether this is a desirable feature is something that needs to be decided by the person evaluating the forecasts when choosing which entropy function to use. Recently, it has been suggested to study the distribution of scoring rules for data sets rather than only considering the mean score ( Naveau and Bessac 2018 ; Taillardat et al. 2019 , see, e.g.,) . That is, to consider the distribution of S ⁡ ( ℙ θ ^ i , Y i ) S(\\mathbb{P}_{\\hat{\\theta}_{i}},Y_{i}) when ℙ θ ^ i \\mathbb{P}_{\\hat{\\theta}_{i}} is the measure which predicts observation Y i Y_{i} . When exploring the distribution we argue that it often makes sense to study the distribution of H ⁡ ( ℙ θ ^ i ) H(\\mathbb{P}_{\\hat{\\theta}_{i}}) and S ⁡ ( ℙ θ ^ i , Y i ) − H ⁡ ( ℙ θ ^ i ) S(\\mathbb{P}_{\\hat{\\theta}_{i}},Y_{i})-H(\\mathbb{P}_{\\hat{\\theta}_{i}}) separately. The distribution of the first term, H ⁡ ( ℙ θ ^ i ) H(\\mathbb{P}_{\\hat{\\theta}_{i}}) , provides no information about the fit of the model to the data but instead provides information about how much variability one expects the data to have a priori. The second term, S ⁡ ( ℙ θ ^ i , Y i ) − H ⁡ ( ℙ θ ^ i ) S(\\mathbb{P}_{\\hat{\\theta}_{i}},Y_{i})-H(\\mathbb{P}_{\\hat{\\theta}_{i}}) , on the other hand gives an indication about how close the model fits the data, where a zero value indicates a perfect calibration in the sense of the generalized entropy. Thinking of the two terms in a regression-analogy, the first term explores the variability of the covariates given the model in the defined entropy sense and it is importantly independent of the data. The second term, that uses the data, explores the difference between the observed score and the expected score if the predictive model was the true distribution. It is important to keep these two terms in mind when exploring distributions of scores over predictions. Especially troublesome is to fail to notice that the variability of the first term, which often is substantial, is data independent. Ignoring this when exploring the distributions for evaluating a model fit to data may lead to incorrect conclusions. CRPS SCRPS log score Figure 9. Three scoring rules for a stochastic volatility model. The black dots are the values for each observation. The three rows show the scoring rule (top), the corresponding entropy with theoretical values as solid lines (middle), and score minus the entropy (bottom). The dashed red lines are estimated 2.5 % , 50 % 2.5\\%,50\\% and 97.52.5 % 97.52.5\\% quantiles. As an example of the different terms, Figure 9 shows the CRPS, the scaled CRPS, and the log-score for the observations in Figure 3 . The middle row shows the entropy of the scoring rules, where one clearly sees the linear cost of increased variance for the CRPS and the logarithmic cost of increased variance for the SCRPS and the log-score. The bottom row displays the difference between the score and the entropy. Here it is interesting to note that the variability of the term increases as a function of the volatility for CRPS, whereas the standardized score has the same distribution regardless of the variance. Of general interest is also that it is hard to see a difference between the SCRPS and the log-score. Appendix C Further proofs Proof of Proposition 1 . Let S ⁡ ( ℚ θ , y ) = log ⁡ ( q θ ​ ( Y ) ) S(\\mathbb{Q}_{\\theta},y)=\\log(q_{\\theta}(Y)) denote the log-score, where q θ q_{\\theta} is the density of ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , and recall that S ⁡ ( ℚ θ , ℚ ) = 𝖤 ℚ ​ [ log ⁡ ( q θ ​ ( Y ) ) ] S(\\mathbb{Q}_{\\theta},\\mathbb{Q})={\\bf\\mathsf{E}}_{\\mathbb{Q}}[\\log(q_{\\theta}(Y))] . Using that S S is a proper scoring rule, we have that ∇