the desired inequality will hold if the respective derivatives at these two points satisfy g ′ ​ ( 0 ) ≤ f ′ ​ ( 0 ) g^{\\prime}(0)\\leq f^{\\prime}(0) and f ′ ​ ( 1 ) ≤ g ′ ​ ( 1 ) f^{\\prime}(1)\\leq g^{\\prime}(1) . To verify this, first observe that f ′ ​ ( r ) = 1 − e − η η ⁡ ( 1 + r ⁡ ( e − η − 1 ) ) = { 1 − e − η η if r = 0 , e η − 1 η if r = 1 . f^{\\prime}(r)=\\frac{1-e^{-\\eta}}{\\eta\\left(1+r(e^{-\\eta}-1)\\right)}=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1-e^{-\\eta}}{\\eta}&amp;\\mbox{if $r=0$,}\\\\ \\frac{e^{\\eta}-1}{\\eta}&amp;\\mbox{if $r=1$.}\\end{cases} On the other hand, we have g ′ ​ ( r ) = { 2 ​ c if r = 0 , 2 ​ ( 1 − c ) if r = 1 . g^{\\prime}(r)=\\begin{cases}2c&amp;\\mbox{if $r=0$,}\\\\ 2(1-c)&amp;\\mbox{if $r=1$.}\\end{cases} Thus, the condition on the derivatives we seek is satisfied when 1 − e − η η ≥ 2 ​ c and e η − 1 η ≤ 2 ​ ( 1 − c ) . \\frac{1-e^{-\\eta}}{\\eta}\\geq 2c\\qquad\\mbox{and}\\qquad\\frac{e^{\\eta}-1}{\\eta}\\leq 2(1-c). Also observing that 1 − e − η η ≥ 1 − η 2 \\frac{1-e^{-\\eta}}{\\eta}\\geq 1-\\frac{\\eta}{2} and e η − 1 η ≤ 1 + η 2 \\frac{e^{\\eta}-1}{\\eta}\\leq 1+\\frac{\\eta}{2} both hold for η &gt; 0 \\eta&gt;0 , we can verify that the conditions above are satisfied whenever η ≤ 2 ​ ( 1 − 2 ​ c ) \\eta\\leq 2(1-2c) , as required in the statement of the theorem. This concludes the proof. ∎ Putting Lemmas 3 and 4 together proves Theorem 1 . Corollary 2 is proved by first observing that 𝔼 ⁡ [ ℓ ^ t ] = r t − ( 1 − 2 ​ c ) ​ ( r t ∧ ( 1 − r t ) ) ≤ r t = ∑ i q t , i ​ ℓ t , i , \\mathbb{E}\\bigl[\\widehat{\\ell}_{t}\\bigr]=r_{t}-(1-2c)(r_{t}\\wedge(1-r_{t}))\\leq r_{t}=\\sum_{i}q_{t,i}\\ell_{t,i}, holds as long as c ≤ 1 2 c\\leq\\frac{1}{2} , and then using Hoeffding’s lemma (Lemma 2.2 in Cesa-Bianchi and Lugosi 2006 ) that guarantees ∑ i q t , i ​ ℓ t , i ≤ ℓ ~ t + η 8 . \\sum_{i}q_{t,i}\\ell_{t,i}\\leq\\widetilde{\\ell}_{t}+\\frac{\\eta}{8}. Putting this inequality together with Lemma 3 , we retrieve the standard bound of the exponentially weighted forecaster: R T ≤ log ⁡ N η + η ​ T 8 R_{T}\\leq\\frac{\\log N}{\\eta}+\\frac{\\eta T}{8} . Combining this bound with the one of Theorem 1 and tuning η \\eta proves the corollary. 3 Changing abstention costs Let us now consider a slight variation on our problem where the abstention cost c c may depend on time: in round t t , the cost of abstention is c t ≤ 1 2 c_{t}\\leq\\frac{1}{2} . The main question we address in this section is whether it is possible to attain fast rates even in the case where c t c_{t} can get arbitrarily close to 1 2 \\frac{1}{2} in a small number of rounds. It is easy to see that our algorithm given in Section 2 satisfies the following regret bound with its proof presented in Appendix B . Proposition 5 . Suppose that c t ≤ 1 2 c_{t}\\leq\\frac{1}{2} for all t t and η &gt; 0 \\eta&gt;0 . Then, the regret of our algorithm satisfies R T ≤ log ⁡ N η + η 8 ∑ t = 1 T 𝕀 { 2 ( 1 − 2 c t ) &lt; η } . R_{T}\\leq\\frac{\\log N}{\\eta}+\\frac{\\eta}{8}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\mathbb{I}_{\\left\\{2(1-2c_{t})&lt;\\eta\\right\\}}. Notably, this result asserts that it is not necessary to assume that c t c_{t} is strictly bounded away from 1 2 \\frac{1}{2} in order to obtain an improvement over the worst-case regret bound. In fact, we show that a clear improvement is possible when making the following quantitative assumption about the behavior of the abstention costs around 1 2 \\frac{1}{2} : Definition 6 (Tsybakov’s condition for abstention costs) . Assume that there are constants β &gt; 0 , α ∈ [ 0 , 1 ) \\beta&gt;0,\\alpha\\in[0,1) such that for any x &gt; 0 x&gt;0 , 1 T ∑ t = 1 T 𝕀 { 1 / 2 − c t &lt; x } ≤ β x α 1 − α . \\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\mathbb{I}_{\\left\\{1/2-c_{t}&lt;x\\right\\}}\\leq\\beta x^{\\frac{\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}}. (3) As the name suggests, this condition is directly inspired by the renowned Tsybakov’s margin assumption introduced first by Mammen and Tsybakov 1999 in the context of binary classification and which was further analyzed by Tsybakov 2004 . In the context of classification where one has to predict a random label Y ∈ { 0 , 1 } Y\\in\\left\\{0,1\\right\\} based on a random instance X X , Tsybakov’s assumption is used to characterize the distribution of the regression function 𝔼 [ Y | X ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left.Y\\right|X\\right] around 1 2 \\frac{1}{2} , which is related to the hardness of the classification problem at hand. Indeed, instances such that the regression function is further away from 1 2 \\frac{1}{2} are “easier” to classify than ones closer to this value, so restricting the density of 𝔼 [ Y | X ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left.Y\\right|X\\right] around this problematic region can make the overall learning problem easier—for more details, and relations of Tsybakov’s assumption with the so-called Bernstein assumption we refer the interested reader to van Erven et al. 2015 . Similarly, our own online prediction problem becomes “harder” when the abstention costs are densely distributed around 1 2 \\frac{1}{2} . By analogy with the case of binary classification, we choose to quantify the density of the quantity of interest around 1 2 \\frac{1}{2} through Tsybakov’s assumption: larger values of α \\alpha correspond to less frequent values around the margin, whereas smaller values correspond to more problematic points. Similarly, the situation where the abstention cost is strictly bounded away from 1 / 2 1/2 can be seen as an analog of Massart’s margin assumption in the statistical learning setup ( Massart and Nédélec 2006 ) . Assuming that the costs satisfy the inequality ( 3 ), our algorithm can be easily seen to achieve the following result: Corollary 7 . Suppose that the sequence of abstention costs satisfies Tsybakov’s margin condition. Then, setting η = ( log ⁡ N T ) 1 − α 2 − α \\eta=\\left(\\frac{\\log N}{T}\\right)^{\\frac{1-\\alpha}{2-\\alpha}} , the regret of our algorithm satisfies R T = O ⁡ ( ( log ⁡ N ) 1 2 − α ​ T 1 − α 2 − α ) . R_{T}=O\\left(\\left(\\log N\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2-\\alpha}}T^{\\frac{1-\\alpha}{2-\\alpha}}\\right). (4) The proof is immediate given Proposition 5 and the definition of Tsybakov’s condition. We remark that the bound of the very same form as ( 4 ) has been recently shown in Koolen et al. 2016 under the assumption that the losses are i.i.d. and that the Bernstein assumption is satisfied. However, our assumption is different and does not imply the Bernstein assumption, even if it leads to similar regret bounds. One downside of Corollary 7 is that it relies on a choice of the learning rate η \\eta that requires prior knowledge of the sequence of abstention costs. More generally, the learning rate η \\eta that minimizes the bound of Proposition 5 is clearly a function of the abstention costs. One may wonder if it is possible to attain a regret guarantee comparable to R T ∗ = min η &gt; 0 ( log ⁡ N η + η 8 ∑ t = 1 T 𝕀 { 2 ( 1 − 2 c t ) &lt; η } ) , R_{T}^{*}=\\min_{\\eta&gt;0}\\left(\\frac{\\log N}{\\eta}+\\frac{\\eta}{8}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\mathbb{I}_{\\left\\{2(1-2c_{t})&lt;\\eta\\right\\}}\\right), without having prior knowledge of the sequence of abstention costs. We answer this question in the positive by considering a simple variation of our algorithm based on the exponentially weighted forecaster with adaptive learning rates. Precisely, in each round t = 1 , 2 , … , T t=1,2,\\dots,T , we will choose a positive learning-rate parameter η t \\eta_{t} , compute the weights w t , i = e − η t ∑ k = 1 t − 1 ℓ k , i w_{t,i}=e^{-\\eta_{t}\\sum_{k=1}^{t-1}\\ell_{k,i}} , and then use these weights in the same way as our basic algorithm did. The intuition driving our algorithm design is to keep the learning rate as large as possible, and only decrease it when observing high abstention costs. Specifically, we let d t = ∑ i = 1 t 𝕀 { η i ≥ 2 ( 1 − 2 c i ) } d_{t}=\\sum_{i=1}^{t}\\mathbb{I}_{\\left\\{\\eta_{i}\\geq 2(1-2c_{i})\\right\\}} be the number of times that the learning rate has exceeded 2 ​ ( 1 − 2 ​ c 