d result which can be found in, e.g., Audibert 2009 . Lemma 10 (Online to batch conversion) . Consider any randomized online learning algorithm 𝒜 \\mathcal{A} that produces the possibly randomized 1 1 1 To simplify the notation we do not write the parameters responsible for randomization explicitly in the functions. hypothesis h t : 𝒳 → 𝒴 h_{t}:\\mathcal{X}\\rightarrow\\mathcal{Y} at time t t when the environment uses an i.i.d. sequence Z 1 , … , Z T Z_{1},\\ldots,Z_{T} . We define the randomized batch algorithm ℒ \\mathcal{L} that, given the data Z 1 , … , Z T Z_{1},\\ldots,Z_{T} and any new observation X X , predicts its label according to the hypothesis chosen uniformly at random among { h 1 , h 2 , … , h T } \\left\\{h_{1},h_{2},\\dots,h_{T}\\right\\} . We denote this randomized hypothesis by g ^ T \\widehat{g}_{T} . Then, the risk of ℒ \\mathcal{L} satisfies 𝔼 ⁡ [ ℓ ⁡ ( g ^ T ​ ( X ) , Y ) ] = 1 T ​ 𝔼 ​ [ ∑ t = 1 T ℓ ⁡ ( h t ​ ( X t ) , Y t ) ] , \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\ell(\\widehat{g}_{T}(X),Y)\\right]=\\frac{1}{T}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum\\limits_{t=1}^{T}\\ell(h_{t}(X_{t}),Y_{t})\\right], where the expectation on the right-hand side integrates over the random hypothesis chosen by ℒ \\mathcal{L} , the instances Z 1 , … , Z T Z_{1},\\ldots,Z_{T} , and the randomization of the online algorithm. Our main technical tool is the following lower bound in the batch setting. Lemma 11 (Theorem 8.4 in Audibert 2009 ) . Let B &gt; 0 , N ≥ 2 B&gt;0,N\\geq 2 and q &gt; 1 + ⌊ log 2 ⁡ N ⌋ 4 ​ T ∧ 1 q&gt;1+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\lfloor\\log_{2}N\\rfloor}{4T}\\wedge 1} , if 𝒳 \\mathcal{X} contains at least ⌊ log 2 ⁡ ( 2 ​ N ) ⌋ \\lfloor\\log_{2}(2N)\\rfloor points, there exists a set G G of N N hypotheses satisfying for any (possibly randomized 2 2 2 We note that the original version of this result does not highlight that g ^ \\widehat{g} is potentially randomized. Nevertheless, the standard argument (see, e.g., the discussions on Yao’s minimax principle in Shamir 2015 or Remark 8.1 in Au