Predictions Note that calibration is alone not sufficient for predictions to be accurate; sharpness is needed too. Our method can seen as naturally achieving both desiderata. While the usual negative log-likelihood (NLL) makes sure that the prediction are sharp, the quantile regularizer makes sure that those predictions are calibrated too, with λ \\lambda controlling strength of the regularization. As our experiments show, the RMSE and NLL scores do not worse much for even values as large as λ = 20 \\lambda=20 . 4 Experiments We evaluate our approach on various regression datasets in terms of the calibration error as well as other standard metrics, sich as root-mean-squared-error (RMSE) and negative log-likelihood (NLL). We experiment with two base models - MC Dropout [ 29 ] and [ 30 ] - by augmenting their objective functions with our proposed quantile regularizer. 4.1 Metrics l 2 l_{2} Quantile Calibration Error Given any model F : 𝒳 → ( 𝒴 → [ 0 , 1 ] ) F:\\mathcal{X}\\rightarrow(\\mathcal{Y}\\rightarrow[0,1]) , we define the l 2 l_{2} calibration error as follows 𝒞 ℰ ( F ) = ∫ 0 1 ( ℙ [ [ F ( X ) ] ( Y ) ≤ p ] − p ) 2 d p \\displaystyle\\mathcal{CE}(F)=\\int_{0}^{1}\\big(\\mathbb{P}\\big[[F(X)](Y)\\leq p\\big]-p\\big)^{2}dp Dataset HeteroScedastic MC Dropout model Calib Error(%) RMSE NLL base QR base QR base QR Air Foil 25.92 ± \\pm 5.40 19.00 ± \\pm 4.80 3.26 ± \\pm 0.06 3.75 ± \\pm 0.10 2.58 ± \\pm 0.03 2.71 ± \\pm 0.02 Boston Housing 44.99 ± \\pm 4.41 42.59 ± \\pm 5.30 4.67 ± \\pm 0.06 4.65 ± \\pm 0.17 3.73 ± \\pm 0.32 3.32 ± \\pm 0.10 Concrete Strength 58.75 ± \\pm 8.31 35.42 ± \\pm 6.32 8.61 ± \\pm 0.16 8.98 ± \\pm 0.14 3.74 ± \\pm 0.04 3.64 ± \\pm 0.02 Fish Toxicity 4.22 ± \\pm 1.56 3.94 ± \\pm 0.78 0.92 ± \\pm 0.00 0.96 ± \\pm 0.01 1.25 ± \\pm 0.01 1.24 ± \\pm 0.01 Kin8nm 12.37 ± \\pm 0.61 11.60 ± \\pm 1.12 0.09 ± \\pm 0.00 0.10 ± \\pm 0.00 -0.87 ± \\pm 0.00 -0.80 ± \\pm 0.01 Protein Structure 5.49 ± \\pm 0.59 3.79 ± \\pm 0.82 4.41 ± \\pm 0.06 4.41 ± \\pm 