{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2004.06857v1.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 65052, "equations": [], "text": "A parsimonious family of multivariate Poisson-lognormal distributions for clustering multivariate count data\nSanjeena Subedi\nNote:\nDepartment of Mathematical Sciences, Binghamton University, State University of New York, 4400 Vestal Parkway East, Binghamton, NY, USA 13902. e: sdang@binghamton.edu\nRyan Browne\nNote:\nDepartment of Statistics and Actuarial Science, University of Waterloo, 200 University Avenue West, Waterloo, ON, Canada. e: ryan.browne@uwaterloo.ca\nAbstract\nMultivariate count data are commonly encountered through high-throughput sequencing technologies in bioinformatics, text mining, or in sports analytics. Although the Poisson distribution seems a natural fit to these count data, its multivariate extension is computationally expensive.In most cases mutual independence among the variables is assumed, however this fails to take into account the correlation among the variables usually observed in the data. Recently, mixtures of multivariate Poisson-lognormal (MPLN) models have been used to analyze such multivariate count measurements with a dependence structure. In the MPLN model, each count is modeled using an independent Poisson distribution conditional on a latent multivariate Gaussian variable. Due to this hierarchical structure, the MPLN model can account for over-dispersion as opposed to the traditional Poisson distribution and allows for correlation between the variables. Rather than relying on a Monte Carlo-based estimation framework which is computationally inefficient, a fast variational-EM based framework is used here for parameter estimation. Further, a parsimonious family of mixtures of Poisson-lognormal distributions are proposed by decomposing the covariance matrix and imposing constraints on these decompositions. Utility of such models is shown using simulated and benchmark datasets.\nKeywords\n: BIC, clustering, count data, MCLUST, mixture models, model-based clustering, MPLN, variational approximations, Variational EM algorithm.\n1\nIntroduction\nWith the emergence of next generation sequencing technologies that provide a fast and cost-effective data generation platform, multivariate count data are becoming ubiquitous in bioinformatics. Although there has been a big explosion in the approaches for data generation and a dramatic reduction in cost and time, efficiently analyzing these complex biological data sets still remains a challenge. Cluster analysis is widely used in bioinformatics for identification and analysis of population heterogeneity. Clustering allows us to summarize data into homogenous groups or clusters, each with its unique characteristics.\nde Souto et al. 2008\ndid a comparative analysis of several clustering techniques on 35 different cancer gene expression data sets and concluded that a mixture model-based clustering approach showed superior performance in terms of recovering the true structure of the data as opposed to the traditional distance-based approaches such as\nk\nk\n-means and hierarchical methods. Model-based clustering, which utilizes mixture models, has been increasingly used in the last two decades\n(\nGollini and\nMurphy 2014\n;\nSubedi and\nMcNicholas 2014\n;\nBouveyron and\nBrunet 2012\n;\nSubedi et al. 2015\n;\nBrowne and\nMcNicholas 2015\n;\nKosmidis and\nKarlis 2016\n;\nDang\net al. 2015\n;\nVrbik and\nMcNicholas 2014\n;\nTortora et al. 2019\n;\nSubedi and\nMcNicholas 2020\n)\n.\nA finite mixture model assumes that the population consists of a finite mixture of subpopulations or components, which can be represented by a parametric model. If\nd\nd\n-dimensional random vector\n𝐘\n\\mathbf{Y}\narises from a\nG\nG\ncomponent finite mixture model, its density can be written as\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n∣\nϑ\n)\n=\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\nπ\ng\n​\nf\n​\n(\n𝐲\n∣\n𝝋\ng\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle f(\\mathbf{y}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\vartheta})=\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\pi_{g}f(\\mathbf{y}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\varphi}_{g}),\nwhere\nπ\ng\n\\pi_{g}\nfor\ng\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nG\ng=1,\\ldots,G\nis the mixing proportion such that\nπ\ng\n>\n0\n\\pi_{g}>0\nand\n∑\ng\nπ\ng\n=\n1\n\\sum_{g}\\pi_{g}=1\n,\nf\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\nf(\\cdot)\nis the component specific probability density function or the probability mass function with parameters\n𝝋\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\varphi}_{g}\n, and\nϑ\n\\boldsymbol{\\vartheta}\ncontains all the unknown parameters for the finite mixture. Depending on the nature of the data, an appropriate\nf\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\nf(\\cdot)\nis used.\nA number of initial works on univariate analysis for a count variable from next generation sequencing data focused on a Poisson model\n(\nMarioni et al. 2008\n;\nBullard\net al. 2010\n;\nWitten\net al. 2010\n)\n. However, a Poisson distribution also imposes a mean-variance relationship such that mean and variance must be equal and most modern biological data typically exhibits over-dispersion (i.e., the observed variation is larger than what is predicted by the Poisson model, see\nAnders and\nHuber 2010\n). A commonly used approach to incorporate this additional variability in univariate case is to model the mean parameter of Poisson distribution as a random variable and impose a distribution. Several different distributions has been proposed: the inverse-Gaussian distribution yields a Poisson-inverse Gaussian distribution\n(\nHolla 1967\n)\n, the inverse-Gamma distribution yields a Poisson-inverse Gamma distribution\nWillmot 1993\n, the lognormal distribution yields a Poisson-lognormal distribution\n(\nBulmer 1974\n)\n, and the gamma distribution yields a gamma-Poisson distribution (i.e., the negative binomial distribution)\n(\nGreenwood and\nYule 1920\n;\nCollings and\nMargolin 1985\n)\n. See\nKarlis and\nXekalaki 2005\nfor a detailed list of other possible priors. Another approach is to scale the mean parameter by a random variable and impose a beta prior on that scaling random variable resulting in a Poisson-Beta distribution\n(\nGurland 1958\n)\n. Amongst these, the most popular approach for statistical methods pertaining next generation sequence data is the gamma-Poisson distribution also known as the negative binomial distribution\n(\nRobinson\net al. 2010\n;\nYu\net al. 2013\n;\nLove\net al. 2014\n;\nDong\net al. 2016\n)\n. In a gamma-Poisson distribution,\nλ\n\\lambda\nis modeled as a random variable from the Gamma distribution such that\nY\ni\n|\nλ\ni\n∼\nPoisson\n​\n(\nλ\n)\nand\nλ\ni\n∼\nGamma\n​\n(\nα\n,\nβ\n)\nY_{i}\\mid\\lambda_{i}\\sim\\text{Poisson}(\\lambda)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\lambda_{i}\\sim\\text{Gamma}(\\alpha,\\beta)\n.\nThe resulting marginal distribution of\nY\ni\nY_{i}\nwill then be a negative binomial distribution\n(\nGreenwood and\nYule 1920\n;\nCollings and\nMargolin 1985\n)\nwith mean\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n=\nα\n​\nβ\n\\Ev(Y)=\\alpha\\beta\n, dispersion parameter\nϕ\n=\n1\nα\n\\phi=\\frac{1}{\\alpha}\n, and variance\n𝕍\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n+\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n2\n​\nϕ\n>\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n\\Var(Y)=\\Ev(Y)+\\Ev(Y)^{2}\\phi>\\Ev(Y)\n. Note that this is a univariate distribution.\nWhile the multivariate extensions of these distributions seem a natural choice to modelling multivariate count data, these models are computationally expensive and/or they impose restrictions on the covariance structure. For example, in addition to the computational burden\n(\nBrijs et al. 2004\n)\n, the multivariate Poisson distribution by\n(\nTeicher 1954\n;\nCampbell 1934\n)\ncan only allow for positive correlation among the variables\nKarlis and\nMeligkotsidou 2007\n;\nInouye\net al. 2017\n. Several different formulation of multivariate version of the Poisson-beta distribution was studied by\nSarabia and\nGómez-Déniz 2011\n; these formulations are either computationally intensive especially for high dimensional data, have restrictions on the covariance structure or the parameters in the marginal distributions are not free (i.e., they share the hyperparameters). Hence, independence between variables is assumed for multivariate analysis in most cases\n(\nRau et al. 2015\n;\nSi\net al. 2014\n;\nPapastamoulis et al. 2016\n)\n, which fails to take into account the correlation between variables.\nSilva et al. 2019\ndeveloped a mixtures of multivariate Poisson-lognormal\n(\nAitchison and\nHo 1989\n, MPLN;)\ndistributions to cluster multivariate count measurements. In MPLN models, the counts are modeled using a hierarchical structure such that\nY\ni\n​\nj\n|\nθ\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nPoisson\n​\n(\ne\nθ\ni\n​\nj\n)\n​\nand\n​\n𝜽\ni\n=\n(\nθ\ni\n​\n1\n,\n…\n,\nθ\ni\n​\nd\n)\n=\n(\nlog\n⁡\nλ\ni\n​\n1\n,\n…\n,\nlog\n⁡\nλ\ni\n​\nd\n)\n∼\n𝒩\nd\n​\n(\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n)\n,\nY_{ij}\\mid{\\theta_{ij}}\\sim\\text{Poisson}(e^{\\theta_{ij}})~\\text{and}~\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}=(\\theta_{i1},\\ldots,\\theta_{id})=(\\log\\lambda_{i1},\\ldots,\\log\\lambda_{id})\\sim\\mathscr{N}_{d}(\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}),\nwhere\n𝒩\nd\n​\n(\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n)\n\\mathscr{N}_{d}(\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma})\ndenotes a\nd\nd\n-dimensional Gaussian distribution with mean\n𝝁\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}\nand covariance\n𝚺\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\n.\nDue to this hierarchical structure, the MPLN model can account for over-dispersion as opposed to the traditional Poisson distribution and allows for correlation. An attractive property of MPLN distribution is that it can support both positive and negative correlation. However, the posterior of the latent variables do not have a closed form and therefore, these models came with heavy computation cost as they relied on Bayesian MCMC-based approaches, discussed further in Section\n2.1\n. Bayesian MCMC-based approaches for models with latent variables can be computationally intensive\n(\nBlei\net al. 2017\n)\nand the computational time is further compounded in model-based clustering where the expected value of the latent variable needs to be computed several times (further detailed in Section\n2.2\n). In this paper, we propose a computationally efficient framework for clustering using mixtures of multivariate Poisson-lognormal distributions using variational Gaussian approximations. We also impose constraints on the covariance matrices of latent variable using an eigen-decomposition which results in a parsimonious family of models. Section\n2.3\nprovides the variational EM estimation algorithm, Section\n3\nprovides results on simulations with comparisons to a popular Poisson-based mixture approach and results on a real data set. Section  concludes with some conclusions and future directions.\n2\nMethodology\n2.1\nMultivariate Poisson-lognormal distribution\nIn multivariate Poisson-lognormal\n(\nAitchison and\nHo 1989\n, MPLN;)\ndistribution, the counts are modeled using a hierarchical structure such that\nY\ni\n​\nj\n|\nθ\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nPoisson\n​\n(\ne\nθ\ni\n​\nj\n)\nand\n𝜽\ni\n∼\n𝒩\nd\n​\n(\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle Y_{ij}\\mid{\\theta_{ij}}\\sim\\text{Poisson}(e^{\\theta_{ij}})\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}\\sim\\mathscr{N}_{d}(\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}),\nwhere\n𝜽\ni\n=\n(\nθ\ni\n​\n1\n,\n…\n,\nθ\ni\n​\nd\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}=(\\theta_{i1},\\ldots,\\theta_{id})\nand\n𝒩\nd\n​\n(\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n)\n\\mathscr{N}_{d}(\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma})\ndenotes a\nd\nd\n-dimensional Gaussian distribution with mean\n𝝁\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}\nand covariance\n𝚺\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\n. The mean and expected value of the\n𝐘\n\\mathbf{Y}\n(\nAitchison and\nHo 1989\n;\nGeorgescu et al. 2014\n)\ncan be found using the law of iterative expectation,\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\nj\n)\n=\n\\displaystyle\\Ev(Y_{j})=\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\nj\n∣\nθ\nj\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle\\Ev\\left[\\Ev(Y_{j}\\mid\\theta_{j})\\right]\n=\ne\nμ\nj\n+\n1\n/\n2\n×\nΣ\nj\n​\nj\n,\n\\displaystyle=e^{\\mu_{j}+1/2\\times\\Sigma_{jj}},\n𝕍\n⁡\n(\nY\nj\n)\n=\n\\displaystyle\\Var(Y_{j})=\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕍\n⁡\n(\nY\nj\n∣\nθ\nj\n)\n]\n+\n𝕍\n⁡\n[\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\nj\n∣\nθ\nj\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle\\Ev\\left[\\Var(Y_{j}\\mid\\theta_{j})\\right]+\\Var\\left[\\Ev(Y_{j}\\mid\\theta_{j})\\right]\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\nj\n)\n+\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\nj\n)\n2\n​\n(\ne\nΣ\njj\n−\n1\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\Ev(Y_{j})+\\Ev(Y_{j})^{2}(e^{\\Sigma_{jj}}-1),\ncov\n​\n(\nY\nj\n,\nY\nk\n)\n=\n\\displaystyle\\text{cov}(Y_{j},Y_{k})=\n𝔼\n[\ncov\n(\nY\nj\n,\nY\nk\n∣\nθ\nj\n,\nθ\nk\n)\n]\n+\ncov\n[\n𝔼\n(\nY\nj\n∣\nθ\nj\n)\n𝔼\n(\nY\nk\n∣\nθ\nk\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle\\Ev\\left[\\text{cov}(Y_{j},Y_{k}\\mid\\theta_{j},\\theta_{k})\\right]+\\text{cov}\\left[\\Ev(Y_{j}\\mid\\theta_{j})\\Ev(Y_{k}\\mid\\theta_{k})\\right]\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\nj\n)\n​\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\nk\n)\n​\n(\ne\nΣ\njk\n−\n1\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\Ev(Y_{j})\\Ev(Y_{k})(e^{\\Sigma_{jk}}-1),\nwhere\nY\nj\nY_{j}\nis the\nj\nt\n​\nh\nj^{th}\nentry of\n𝐘\n\\mathbf{Y}\n,\nμ\nj\n\\mu_{j}\nis the\nj\nt\n​\nh\nj^{th}\nentry of\n𝝁\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}\n,\nΣ\nj\n​\nk\n\\Sigma_{jk}\nis the entry in the\nj\nt\n​\nh\nj^{th}\nrow and\nk\nt\n​\nh\nk^{th}\ncolumn of the matrix\n𝚺\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\nand\nj\n≠\nk\nj\\neq k\n. Due to this hierarchical structure, the MPLN model can account for over-dispersion and also allows for correlation.\nThe marginal probability density of\n𝐘\n\\mathbf{Y}\ncan be written as:\nf\n𝐘\n​\n(\n𝐲\n)\n=\n∫\nIR\nd\n[\n∏\nj\n=\n1\nd\np\n⁡\n(\ny\nj\n∣\nθ\nj\n)\n]\n​\nϕ\nd\n​\n(\n𝜽\n∣\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n)\n​\n𝑑\n𝜽\n,\n\\displaystyle f_{\\mathbf{Y}}(\\mathbf{y})=\\int_{\\text{\\rm I\\hskip-1.70717ptR}^{d}}\\left[\\prod_{j=1}^{d}p(y_{j}\\mid\\theta_{j})\\right]~\\phi_{d}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma})~d\\boldsymbol{\\theta},\nwhere\nθ\nj\n\\theta_{j}\nand\ny\nj\ny_{j}\nare the\nj\nt\n​\nh\nj^{th}\nelement of\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\nand\n𝐲\n\\mathbf{y}\nrespectively,\np\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\np(\\cdot)\nis the probability mass function of the Poisson distribution with mean\nλ\nj\n=\ne\nθ\nj\n\\lambda_{j}=e^{\\theta_{j}}\nand\nϕ\nd\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\phi_{d}(\\cdot)\nis the probability density function of\nd\nd\n-dimensional Gaussian distribution with mean\n𝝁\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}\nand covariance\n𝚺\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\n. Note that the marginal distribution of\nY\nY\ninvolves multiple integrals and cannot be further simplified. Hence,\nAitchison and\nHo 1989\nsuggested that the maximum likelihood estimation of the parameters requires a mix of Newton-Raphson and steepest ascent methods. An alternate approach is to use an expectation-maximization (EM) algorithm\n(\nDempster\net al. 1977\n)\n, which is an iterative approach for maximizing the likelihood when the data are incomplete or are treated as incomplete. In an MPLN distribution, the observed variables are the counts\n𝐘\n\\mathbf{Y}\nand the missing data are the latent variables\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\n. The EM-algorithm comprises of two iterative steps: an E-step and an M-step. In E-step, the expected value of the complete data (i.e. observed and missing data) log-likelihood given the observed data and current parameter estimate (i.e., updates from previous iteration) is computed which is then maximized in the subsequent M-step. These step are repeated until convergence to obtain the maximum likelihood estimate of the parameters. In order to compute the expected value of the complete data log-likelihood,\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n∣\n𝐲\n)\n\\Ev(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\mathbf{y})\nand\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n​\n𝜽\n′\n∣\n𝐲\n)\n\\Ev(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{\\prime}\\mid\\mathbf{y})\nneed to be computed. The conditional distribution of\nf\n𝜽\n|\n𝐘\n​\n(\n𝜽\n∣\n𝐲\n,\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n)\nf_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\mathbf{Y}}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\mathbf{y},\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma})\nis given by\nf\n𝜽\n|\n𝐘\n​\n(\n𝜽\n∣\n𝐲\n,\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n)\n=\n[\n∏\nj\n=\n1\nd\np\n⁡\n(\ny\nj\n∣\nθ\nj\n)\n]\n​\nϕ\nd\n​\n(\n𝜽\n∣\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n)\nf\n𝐘\n​\n(\n𝐲\n∣\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle f_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\mathbf{Y}}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\mathbf{y},\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma})=\\frac{\\left[\\prod_{j=1}^{d}p(y_{j}\\mid\\theta_{j})\\right]~\\phi_{d}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma})}{f_{\\mathbf{Y}}(\\mathbf{y}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma})}.\nHowever, as mentioned above, the marginal distribution of\nY\nY\ninvolves multiple integrals and cannot be further simplified.\nGeorgescu et al. 2014\nproposed a maximum likelihood estimation of the parameters for MPLN distribution in the Monte Carlo EM (MCEM) framework\n(\nWei and\nTanner 1990\n)\n; they utilized a Monte Carlo EM algorithm using importance sampling to find the empirical expected value of the complete data log-likelihood.\nChagneau\net al. 2011\nutilized an MCMC-based approach for parameter estimation. However, it comes with a heavy computational overhead, further exacerbated as the dimensionality and sample size rise\n(\nChagneau\net al. 2011\n)\n.\n2.2\nMixtures of Multivariate Poisson-lognormal distributions\nSilva et al. 2019\nproposed a mixture of multivariate Poisson-lognormal distributions to cluster multivariate count measurements. A\nG\nG\n-component mixture of MPLN distributions can be written as\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n∣\nϑ\n)\n=\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\nπ\ng\n​\nf\n𝐘\n​\n(\n𝐲\n∣\n𝝁\ng\n,\n𝚺\ng\n)\n=\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\nπ\ng\n​\n∫\nIR\nd\n[\n∏\nj\n=\n1\nd\np\n⁡\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n∣\nθ\ni\n​\nj\n)\n]\n​\nϕ\nd\n​\n(\n𝜽\ni\n∣\n𝝁\ng\n,\n𝚺\ng\n)\n​\nd\n​\n𝜽\ni\n,\n\\displaystyle f(\\mathbf{y}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\vartheta})=\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\pi_{g}f_{\\mathbf{Y}}(\\mathbf{y}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g})=\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\pi_{g}\\int_{\\text{\\rm I\\hskip-1.70717ptR}^{d}}\\left[\\prod_{j=1}^{d}p(y_{ij}\\mid\\theta_{ij})\\right]~\\phi_{d}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g})~d\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i},\nwhere\nϑ\n\\boldsymbol{\\vartheta}\ndenotes all model parameters and\nf\n𝐘\n​\n(\n𝐲\n∣\n𝝁\ng\n,\n𝚺\ng\n)\nf_{\\mathbf{Y}}(\\mathbf{y}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g})\ndenotes the distribution of the\ng\nt\n​\nh\ng^{th}\ncomponent with parameters\n𝝁\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g}\nand\n𝚺\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}\n. In model-based clustering, an additional component membership indicator variable\n𝐙\n\\mathbf{Z}\nis introduced which is assumed to be unknown and\nZ\ni\n​\ng\n=\n1\nZ_{ig}=1\nif the observation\ni\nt\n​\nh\ni^{th}\nbelongs to group\ng\ng\nand\nZ\ni\n​\ng\n=\n0\nZ_{ig}=0\notherwise. Hence, the complete data now comprises of observed expression levels\n𝐲\n\\mathbf{y}\n, underlying latent variable\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\n, and unknown group membership\n𝐳\n\\mathbf{z}\n. Therefore, the complete-data log-likelihood is\nl\nc\n(\n𝝁\n,\n𝚺\n∣\n𝐲\n,\n𝜽\n,\n𝐳\n)\n=\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nz\ni\n​\ng\n[\nlog\nϕ\nd\n(\n𝜽\ni\n∣\n𝝁\ng\n,\n𝚺\ng\n)\n+\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\nlog\np\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n∣\nθ\ni\n​\nj\n)\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle l_{c}(\\boldsymbol{\\mu},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\\mid\\mathbf{y},\\boldsymbol{\\theta},\\mathbf{z})=\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{ig}\\left[\\log\\phi_{d}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g})+\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\log p(y_{ij}\\mid\\theta_{ij})\\right].\nIn order to utilize the EM framework for parameter estimation for the mixtures of MPLN distributions, finding the expected value of the complete-data log-likelihood requires the conditional expectations\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nZ\nig\n​\n𝜽\ni\n∣\n𝐲\ni\n,\n𝝁\ng\n,\n𝚺\ng\n)\n\\Ev(Z_{ig}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}\\mid\\mathbf{y}_{i},\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g})\nand\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nZ\nig\n​\n𝜽\ni\n​\n𝜽\ni\n′\n∣\n𝐲\ni\n,\n𝝁\ng\n,\n𝚺\ng\n)\n\\Ev(Z_{ig}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}^{\\prime}\\mid\\mathbf{y}_{i},\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g})\n.\nSilva et al. 2019\nutilized an MCMC-EM algorithm using\nStan\n(\nHoffman and\nGelman 2014\n;\nStan Development\nTeam 2015\n)\nto compute the required conditional expectations.\nStan\nuses the No-U-Turn Sampler\n(\nHoffman and\nGelman 2014\n, NUTS;)\n, an adaptive variant of the Hamiltonian Monte Carlo\n(\nNeal et al. 2011\n, HMC;)\nthat requires no tuning parameters and can efficiently sample from a posterior distribution when the parameters are correlated. However, in a clustering context, even such efficient sampling techniques like NUTS comes with heavy computational burden as MCMC-EM needs to be conducted at every iteration of MCMC-EM after updating the model parameters and the component indicator variable. Additionally, Bayesian techniques based on MCMC sampling algorithms also comes with increased computational overhead and possible difficulty in determining convergence as the complexity of the model increases. When dealing with such complex models, convergence can also be quite slow. This crucial aspect is partly why applications of such models have been limited to small dimensions\n(\nGeorgescu et al. 2014\n)\n. Additionally, when the true number of groups, i.e., the number of components of the mixture model, is unknown, the MCMC-EM algorithm must be employed for every possible number of components and the optimal number of components is chosen in conjunction with a model selection criterion which might entail extreme computational cost.\n2.3\nVariational approximation of mixtures of MPLN distributions\nVariational approximation\n(\nWainwright\net al. 2008\n)\nis an approximate inference technique which has been very popular in machine learning. It presents an alternative parameter estimation framework for MPLN distribution by using a computationally convenient approximating density in place of a more complex but ‘true’ posterior density. Using computationally convenient Gaussian densities, complex posterior distributions are approximated by minimizing the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between the true and the approximating densities and therefore, reducing the computational overhead. Several studies have shown that it delivers accurate approximations\n(\nArchambeau et al. 2007\n;\nChallis and\nBarber 2013\n;\nArridge\net al. 2018\n)\n.\nKhan\net al. 2013\ndeveloped variational inference framework for several non-conjugate latent Gaussian models.\nArridge\net al. 2018\nalso developed a variational Gaussian approximation (VGA) to the posterior distribution arising from the univariate Poisson model with a Gaussian prior and derived an explicit expression for the lower bound, and showed the existence and uniqueness of the optimal Gaussian approximation.\nHere, we propose a variational Gaussian approximation to the marginal distribution of the observed variable i.e.\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n)\nf(\\mathbf{y})\nin the mixtures of MPLN distributions. Suppose, we have an approximating density\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\nq(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\n, the marginal log-likelihood can be written as\nlog\n⁡\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n)\n\\displaystyle\\log f(\\mathbf{y})\n=\n∫\nIR\nd\nlog\n⁡\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n)\n​\nq\n​\n(\n𝜽\n)\n​\n𝑑\n𝜽\n\\displaystyle=\\int_{\\text{\\rm I\\hskip-1.70717ptR}^{d}}\\log f(\\mathbf{y})q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})d\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\n=\n∫\nIR\nd\nlog\n⁡\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n,\n𝜽\n)\n/\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n∣\n𝐲\n)\n/\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\n​\nq\n​\n(\n𝜽\n)\n​\n𝑑\n𝜽\n\\displaystyle=\\int_{\\text{\\rm I\\hskip-1.70717ptR}^{d}}\\log\\frac{f(\\mathbf{y},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})/q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})}{f(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\mathbf{y})/q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})}q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})d\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\n=\n∫\nIR\nd\n[\nlog\nf\n(\n𝐲\n,\n𝜽\n)\n−\nlog\nq\n(\n𝜽\n)\n]\nq\n(\n𝜽\n)\nd\n𝜽\n+\nD\nK\n​\nL\n(\nq\n∥\nf\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\int_{\\text{\\rm I\\hskip-1.70717ptR}^{d}}\\left[\\log f(\\mathbf{y},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})-\\log q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\right]q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})d\\boldsymbol{\\theta}+D_{KL}(q\\|f)\n=\nF\n(\nq\n,\n𝐲\n)\n+\nD\nK\n​\nL\n(\nq\n∥\nf\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=F(q,\\mathbf{y})+D_{KL}(q\\|f),\nwhere\nD\nK\n​\nL\n(\nq\n∥\nf\n)\n=\n∫\nIR\nd\nq\n(\n𝜽\n)\nlog\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n∣\n𝐲\n)\nd\n𝜽\nD_{KL}(q\\|f)=\\int_{\\text{\\rm I\\hskip-1.70717ptR}^{d}}q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\log\\frac{q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})}{f(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\mathbf{y})}d\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\nis the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n∣\n𝐲\n)\nf(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\mid\\mathbf{y})\nand approximating distribution\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\nq(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\n, and\nF\n⁡\n(\nq\n,\n𝐲\n)\n=\n∫\nIR\nd\n[\nlog\n⁡\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n,\n𝜽\n)\n−\nlog\n⁡\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\n]\n​\nq\n​\n(\n𝜽\n)\n​\n𝑑\n𝜽\nF(q,\\mathbf{y})=\\int_{\\text{\\rm I\\hskip-1.70717ptR}^{d}}\\left[\\log f(\\mathbf{y},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})-\\log q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\right]q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})d\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\nis our evidence lower bound (ELBO). To minimize the KL divergence, we maximize our ELBO.\nIn VGA,\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\nq(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\nis assumed to be a Gaussian distribution. Assuming\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\n=\n𝒩\n⁡\n(\n𝐦\n,\n𝐒\n)\nq(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})=\\mathscr{N}(\\mathbf{m},\\mathbf{S})\n, the lower bound for the log\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n)\nf(\\mathbf{y})\nbecomes\nF\n⁡\n(\nq\n,\n𝐲\n)\n\\displaystyle F(q,\\mathbf{y})\n=\n−\n1\n2\n​\n(\n𝐦\n−\n𝝁\n)\n′\n​\n𝚺\n−\n1\n​\n(\n𝐦\n−\n𝝁\n)\n−\n1\n2\n​\ntr\n​\n(\n𝚺\n−\n1\n​\n𝐒\n)\n+\n1\n2\n​\nlog\n⁡\n|\n𝐒\n|\n−\n1\n2\n​\nlog\n⁡\n|\n𝚺\n|\n−\nd\n2\n+\n𝐦\n′\n​\n𝐲\n\\displaystyle=-\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{m}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu})^{\\prime}\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}^{-1}(\\mathbf{m}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu})-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mbox{tr}(\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}^{-1}\\mathbf{S})+\\frac{1}{2}\\log|\\mathbf{S}|-\\frac{1}{2}\\log|\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}|-\\frac{d}{2}+\\mathbf{m}^{\\prime}\\mathbf{y}\n−\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n(\ne\nm\nj\n+\n1\n2\n​\nS\nj\n​\nj\n+\nlog\n(\ny\nj\n!\n)\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle-\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\left(e^{m_{j}+\\frac{1}{2}S_{jj}}+\\log(y_{j}!)\\right).\nThis lower bound is strictly jointly concave with respect to the mean (\n𝐦\n\\mathbf{m}\n) and variance (\n𝐒\n\\mathbf{S}\n) of the approximating distribution and hence, similar to\nArridge\net al. 2018\n, parameter estimation can be obtained via Newton’s method and fixed-point method. This alleviates the need of the use of MCMC-based approach to get samples from the posterior distribution of the latent variable\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\nand the computational burden that comes along with such approaches. Additionally, the EM framework using variational Gaussian approximation algorithm is monotonic as opposed to MCMC-based EM. A similar variational framework for MPLN distribution was recently proposed by\nChiquet\net al. 2019\nfor network models.\nThe complete data log-likelihood of the mixtures of MPLN distributions can be written as:\nl\nc\n​\n(\nϑ\n∣\n𝐲\n)\n\\displaystyle l_{c}(\\boldsymbol{\\vartheta}\\mid\\mathbf{y})\n=\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nz\ni\n​\ng\n​\nlog\n⁡\nπ\ng\n+\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nz\ni\n​\ng\n​\nlog\n⁡\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\ni\n∣\n𝝁\ng\n,\n𝚺\ng\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{ig}\\log\\pi_{g}+\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{ig}\\log f(\\mathbf{y}_{i}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g})\n=\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nz\ni\n​\ng\nlog\nπ\ng\n+\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nz\ni\n​\ng\n[\n∫\nIR\nd\n[\nlog\nf\n(\n𝐲\n,\n𝜽\ni\n​\ng\n)\n−\nlog\nq\n(\n𝜽\ni\n​\ng\n)\n]\nq\n(\n𝜽\ni\n​\ng\n)\nd\n𝜽\ni\n​\ng\n+\nD\nK\n​\nL\n(\nq\ni\n​\ng\n∥\nf\ni\n​\ng\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{ig}\\log\\pi_{g}+\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{ig}\\left[\\int_{\\text{\\rm I\\hskip-1.70717ptR}^{d}}\\left[\\log f(\\mathbf{y},\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{ig})-\\log q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{ig})\\right]q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{ig})d\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{ig}+D_{KL}(q_{ig}\\|f_{ig})\\right]\nwhere\nD\nK\n​\nL\n(\nq\ni\n​\ng\n∥\nf\ni\n​\ng\n)\n=\n∫\nIR\nd\nq\n(\n𝜽\ni\n​\ng\n)\nlog\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\ni\n​\ng\n)\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\ni\n∣\n𝐲\ni\n,\nZ\ni\n​\ng\n=\n1\n)\nd\n𝜽\ni\n​\ng\nD_{KL}(q_{ig}\\|f_{ig})=\\int_{\\text{\\rm I\\hskip-1.70717ptR}^{d}}q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{ig})\\log\\frac{q(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{ig})}{f(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}\\mid\\mathbf{y}_{i},Z_{ig}=1)}d\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{ig}\nis the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between\nf\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\ni\n∣\n𝐲\ni\n,\nZ\ni\n​\ng\n=\n1\n)\nf(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}\\mid\\mathbf{y}_{i},Z_{ig}=1)\nand approximating distribution\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\ni\n​\ng\n)\nq(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{ig})\n. Assuming\nq\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\ni\n​\ng\n)\n=\n𝒩\n⁡\n(\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n,\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n)\nq(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{ig})=\\mathscr{N}(\\mathbf{m}_{ig},\\mathbf{S}_{ig})\n, the log-likelihood becomes:\nl\nc\n​\n(\nϑ\n∣\n𝐲\n)\n\\displaystyle l_{c}(\\boldsymbol{\\vartheta}\\mid\\mathbf{y})\n=\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nz\ni\n​\ng\nlog\nπ\ng\n+\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nz\ni\n​\ng\n[\nF\n(\nq\ni\n​\ng\n,\n𝐲\ni\n)\n+\nD\nK\n​\nL\n(\nq\ni\n​\ng\n∥\nf\ni\n​\ng\n)\n]\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{ig}\\log\\pi_{g}+\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{ig}\\left[F(q_{ig},\\mathbf{y}_{i})+D_{KL}(q_{ig}\\|f_{ig})\\right],\nwhere the ELBO for each observation\n𝐲\ni\n\\mathbf{y}_{i}\nis\nF\n⁡\n(\nq\ni\n​\ng\n,\n𝐲\ni\n)\n\\displaystyle F(q_{ig},\\mathbf{y}_{i})\n=\n1\n2\n​\nlog\n⁡\n|\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n|\n−\n1\n2\n​\n(\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n−\n𝝁\ng\n)\n′\n​\n𝚺\ng\n−\n1\n​\n(\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n−\n𝝁\ng\n)\n−\ntr\n​\n(\n𝚺\ng\n−\n1\n​\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n)\n−\n1\n2\n​\nlog\n⁡\n|\n𝚺\ng\n|\n−\nd\n2\n+\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n′\n​\n𝐲\ni\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2}\\log|\\mathbf{S}_{ig}|-\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{m}_{ig}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g})^{\\prime}\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}^{-1}(\\mathbf{m}_{ig}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g})-\\,\\mbox{tr}(\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}^{-1}\\mathbf{S}_{ig})-\\frac{1}{2}\\log|\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}|-\\frac{d}{2}+\\mathbf{m}_{ig}^{\\prime}\\mathbf{y}_{i}\n−\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n(\ne\nm\ni\n​\ng\n​\nj\n+\n1\n2\n​\nS\ni\n​\ng\n,\nj\n​\nj\n+\nlog\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n!\n)\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle-\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\left(e^{m_{igj}+\\frac{1}{2}S_{ig,jj}}+\\log(y_{ij}!)\\right),\nwhere\nm\ni\n​\ng\n​\nj\nm_{igj}\nis the\nj\nt\n​\nh\nj^{th}\nelement of the\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{m}_{ig}\nand\nS\ni\n​\ng\n,\nj\n​\nj\nS_{ig,jj}\nis the\nj\nt\n​\nh\nj^{th}\ndiagonal element of the matrix\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{S}_{ig}\n.\nThe variational parameters that maximize the ELBO will minimize the KL divergence between the true posterior and the approximating density.\nParameter estimation can be done in an iterative EM-type approach such that the following steps are iterated.\n1.\nConditional on the variational parameters\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n,\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{m}_{ig},\\mathbf{S}_{ig}\nand on\n𝝁\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g}\nand\n𝚺\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}\n, the\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nZ\nig\n)\n\\Ev(Z_{ig})\nis computed. Given\n𝝁\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g}\nand\n𝚺\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}\n,\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nZ\nig\n∣\n𝐲\ni\n)\n=\nπ\ng\n​\nf\n​\n(\n𝐲\n∣\n𝝁\ng\n,\n𝚺\ng\n)\n∑\nh\n=\n1\nG\nπ\nh\n​\nf\n​\n(\n𝐲\n∣\n𝝁\nh\n,\n𝚺\nh\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\Ev(Z_{ig}\\mid\\mathbf{y}_{i})=\\frac{\\pi_{g}f(\\mathbf{y}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g})}{\\sum_{h=1}^{G}\\pi_{h}f(\\mathbf{y}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{h},\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{h})}.\nNote that this involves the marginal distribution of\nY\nY\nwhich is difficult to compute. Hence, we use an approximation of\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nZ\nig\n)\n\\Ev(Z_{ig})\nwhere we replace the marginal density of the exponent of ELBO such that\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n=\ndef\nπ\ng\n​\nexp\n⁡\n[\nF\n⁡\n(\nq\ni\n​\ng\n,\n𝐲\ni\n)\n]\n∑\nh\n=\n1\nG\nπ\nh\n​\nexp\n⁡\n[\nF\n⁡\n(\nq\ni\n​\nh\n,\n𝐲\ni\n)\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle\\widehat{Z}_{ig}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\text{def}}}{{=}}\\frac{\\pi_{g}\\exp\\left[F\\left(q_{ig},\\mathbf{y}_{i}\\right)\\right]}{\\sum_{h=1}^{G}\\pi_{h}\\exp\\left[F\\left(q_{ih},\\mathbf{y}_{i}\\right)\\right]}.\nThis approximation is computationally convenient and a similar framework was utilized by\nTang\net al. 2015\n;\nGollini and\nMurphy 2014\n. This approximation works well in simulation studies and real data analysis.\n2.\nGiven\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n\\hat{Z}_{ig}\n, variational parameters\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{m}_{ig}\nand\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{S}_{ig}\nis updated conditional on\n𝝁\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g}\nand\n𝚺\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}\n.\nThe lower bound is strictly jointly concave with respect to\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{m}_{ig}\nand\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{S}_{ig}\nof the approximating distribution. Therefore, we can update\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{m}_{ig}\nand\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{S}_{ig}\nas following:\n(a)\nfixed-point method for updating\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{S}_{ig}\nis\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n(\nt\n+\n1\n)\n=\n{\n𝚺\ng\n−\n1\n+\n𝐈\n⊙\n𝐞𝐱𝐩\n⁡\n[\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n(\nt\n)\n+\n1\n2\n​\ndiag\n​\n(\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n(\nt\n)\n)\n]\n​\n𝟏\nd\n′\n}\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\hskip-46.97505pt\\mathbf{S}_{ig}^{(t+1)}=\\left\\{\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}^{-1}+\\mathbf{I}\\odot\\mathbf{exp}\\left[\\mathbf{m}_{ig}^{(t)}+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mbox{diag}\\left(\\mathbf{S}_{ig}^{(t)}\\right)\\right]\\mathbf{1}_{d}^{\\prime}\\right\\}^{-1}\nwhere the vector function\n𝐞𝐱𝐩\n⁡\n[\n𝐚\n]\n=\n(\ne\na\n1\n,\n…\n,\ne\na\nd\n)\n′\n\\mathbf{exp}\\left[\\mathbf{a}\\right]=(e^{a_{1}},\\ldots,e^{a_{d}})^{\\prime}\nis a vector of exponential each element of the\nd\nd\n-dimensional vector\n𝐚\n\\mathbf{a}\n,\ndiag\n​\n(\n𝐒\n)\n=\n(\n𝐒\n11\n​\n…\n,\n𝐒\nd\n​\nd\n)\n\\mbox{diag}(\\mathbf{S})=(\\mathbf{S}_{11}\\ldots,\\mathbf{S}_{dd})\nputs the diagonal elements of the\nd\n×\nd\nd\\times d\nmatrix\n𝐒\n\\mathbf{S}\ninto a d-dimensional vector,\n⊙\n\\odot\nthe Hadmard product and\n𝟏\nd\n\\mathbf{1}_{d}\nis a d-dimensional vector of ones ;\n(b)\nNewton’s method to update\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{m}_{ig}\nis\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n(\nt\n+\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf{m}_{ig}^{(t+1)}\n=\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n(\nt\n)\n−\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n(\nt\n+\n1\n)\n​\n{\n𝐞𝐱𝐩\n⁡\n[\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n(\nt\n)\n+\n1\n2\n​\ndiag\n​\n(\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n(\nt\n+\n1\n)\n)\n]\n+\n𝚺\ng\n−\n1\n​\n(\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n(\nt\n)\n−\n𝝁\ng\n)\n−\n𝐲\ni\n}\n;\nand\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{m}_{ig}^{(t)}\\!-\\!\\mathbf{S}_{ig}^{(t+1)}\\!\\left\\{\\mathbf{exp}\\left[\\mathbf{m}_{ig}^{(t)}\\!+\\!\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mbox{diag}\\left(\\mathbf{S}_{ig}^{(t+1)}\\right)\\right]\\!+\\!\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}^{-1}\\left(\\mathbf{m}_{ig}^{(t)}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g}\\right)\\!-\\!\\mathbf{y}_{i}\\right\\}\\!;\\quad\\mbox{and}\n3.\ngiven\nz\n^\ni\n​\ng\n\\hat{z}_{ig}\nand the variational parameters\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{m}_{ig}\nand\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{S}_{ig}\n, the parameters\n𝝅\n\\boldsymbol{\\pi}\n,\n𝝁\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g}\nand\n𝚺\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}\n.\nπ\ng\n\\displaystyle\\pi_{g}\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\nn\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}}{n},\n𝝁\ng\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g}\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n​\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n,\nand\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}\\mathbf{m}_{ig}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}},\\quad\\text{and}\n𝚺\ng\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n​\n(\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n−\n𝝁\ng\n)\n​\n(\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n−\n𝝁\ng\n)\n′\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n−\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n​\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}(\\mathbf{m}_{ig}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g})(\\mathbf{m}_{ig}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g})^{\\prime}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}}-\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}\\mathbf{S}_{ig}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}}.\n2.4\nParsimonious family of models\nFor the mixtures of MPLN distribution, the number of free parameters in the covariance matrices of the latent variable is\nG\n​\nd\n​\n(\nd\n+\n1\n)\n/\n2\nGd(d+1)/2\n, i.e., the number of parameters increases quadratically with\nd\nd\n.\nBanfield and\nRaftery 1993\nproposed an eigen-decomposition of the covariance matrices of mixtures of Gaussian distributions such that\n𝚺\ng\n=\nλ\ng\n​\n𝐃\ng\n​\n𝐀\ng\n​\n𝐃\ng\n′\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}=\\lambda_{g}\\mathbf{D}_{g}\\mathbf{A}_{g}\\mathbf{D}_{g}^{\\prime}\nwhere\n𝐃\ng\n\\mathbf{D}_{g}\nis the matrix of eigenvectors and\n𝐀\ng\n\\mathbf{A}_{g}\nis a diagonal matrix proportional to the eigenvalues of\n𝚺\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}\n, such that\n|\n𝐀\ng\n|\n=\n1\n|\\mathbf{A}_{g}|=1\n, and\nλ\ng\n\\lambda_{g}\nis the associated constant of proportionality. For a Gaussian distribution, this decomposition results in a geometric interpretation of the constraints such that\nλ\ng\n\\lambda_{g}\ncontrols the cluster volume,\n𝐀\ng\n\\mathbf{A}_{g}\ncontrols the cluster shape, and\n𝐃\ng\n\\mathbf{D}_{g}\ncontrols the cluster orientation.\nCeleux and\nGovaert 1995\nimposed constraints on these parameters of the covariance matrix to be equal or different among groups resulting in a family of 14 models known as known as Gaussian parsimonious clustering models (GPCM). Parameter estimation in an EM framework for 12 of these 14 GPCM family and MM framework of\nBrowne and\nMcNicholas 2014\nfor the remaining two models is available in the\nR\npackage\n𝚖𝚌𝚕𝚞𝚜𝚝\n{\\tt mclust}\n(\nScrucca\net al. 2016\n)\n. Here, we propose an eigen-decomposition of the component-specific covariance matrices of the latent variable\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\nto introduce parsimony. The geometric interpretation does not hold for observed data and therefore, we only focused on the following subset of the GPCM family of model given in Table\n1\n.\nTable 1:\nGeometric interpretation of the subset of the models considered in our study obtained via eigen-decomposition of\n𝚺\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{g}\n.\nModel (Covariance)\nVolume\nShape\nOrientation\nParameters\nEII (\nλ\n​\nI\n\\lambda I\n)\nEqual\nSpherical\n-\n1\n1\nVII (\nλ\ng\n​\nI\n\\lambda_{g}I\n)\nVariable\nSpherical\n-\nG\nG\nEEI (\nλ\n​\n𝐀\n\\lambda\\mathbf{A}\n)\nEqual\nEqual\nAx-Alg\nd\nd\nVVI (\nλ\ng\n​\n𝐀\ng\n\\lambda_{g}\\mathbf{A}_{g}\n)\nVariable\nVariable\nAx-Alg\nd\n​\nG\ndG\nEEE (\nλ\n​\n𝐃𝐀𝐃\nT\n\\lambda\\mathbf{D}\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{D}^{T}\n)\nEqual\nEqual\nEqual\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n+\n1\n)\n/\n2\nd(d+1)/2\nVVE (\nλ\ng\n​\n𝐃𝐀\ng\n​\n𝐃\nT\n\\lambda_{g}\\mathbf{D}\\mathbf{A}_{g}\\mathbf{D}^{T}\n)\nVariable\nVariable\nEqual\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n+\n1\n)\n/\n2\n+\n(\nG\n−\n1\n)\n​\nd\nd(d+1)/2+(G-1)d\nEEV (\nλ\n​\n𝐃\ng\n​\n𝐀𝐃\ng\nT\n\\lambda\\mathbf{D}_{g}\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{D}_{g}^{T}\n)\nEqual\nEqual\nVariable\nG\n​\nd\n​\n(\nd\n+\n1\n)\n/\n2\n−\n(\nG\n−\n1\n)\n​\nd\nGd(d+1)/2-(G-1)d\nVVV (\nλ\ng\n​\n𝐃\ng\n​\n𝐀\ng\n​\n𝐃\ng\nT\n\\lambda_{g}\\mathbf{D}_{g}\\mathbf{A}_{g}\\mathbf{D}_{g}^{T}\n)\nVariable\nVariable\nVariable\nG\n​\nd\n​\n(\nd\n+\n1\n)\n/\n2\nGd(d+1)/2\nNote that within a component\ncov\n​\n(\nY\nj\n,\nY\nk\n)\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\nj\n)\n​\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\nk\n)\n​\n(\ne\nΣ\njk\n−\n1\n)\n,\n\\text{cov}(Y_{j},Y_{k})=\\Ev(Y_{j})\\Ev(Y_{k})(e^{\\Sigma_{jk}}-1),\ntherefore, independence among the latent variable\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\nwill result in independence among the observed variable\n𝐘\n\\mathbf{Y}\n. We focused on EII, VII, EEI, and VVI to recover diagonal covariance structure when variables are independent. Additionally, to introduce some parsimony compared to VVV model, we also focused some additional non-diagonal constriants such as EEE, VVE and EEV. Given\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{m}_{ig}\nand\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n\\mathbf{S}_{ig}\nand using the sample covariance matrix of\n𝐦\ng\n\\mathbf{m}_{g}\nas\n𝚺\n^\nsample\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n​\n(\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n−\n𝝁\ng\n)\n​\n(\n𝐦\ni\n​\ng\n−\n𝝁\ng\n)\n′\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n−\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n​\n𝐒\ni\n​\ng\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\nZ\n^\ni\n​\ng\n\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}}_{\\text{sample}}=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}(\\mathbf{m}_{ig}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g})(\\mathbf{m}_{ig}-\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g})^{\\prime}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}}-\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}\\mathbf{S}_{ig}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\widehat{Z}_{ig}}\nthe parameter estimates are analogous to their Gaussian counterpart. See\nCeleux and\nGovaert 1995\n;\nBrowne and\nMcNicholas 2014\nfor details.\n2.5\nInitialization, Model Selection, and Performance assessment\nEM algorithm relies heavily on the initial values\n(\nBiernacki\net al. 2003\n)\nand hence, good starting values are crucial. While initialization of the clusters using\nk\nk\n-means is one of the widely used approach for symmetric distributions, it may not be appropriate for our over-dispersed count datasets as\nk\nk\n-means initialization is equivalent to fitting a Gaussian mixture models with spherical clusters which will not be ideal for these over-dispersed multivariate counts. Therefore, we utilized small EM initialization for our analysis. For each\nG\nG\n, the initialization “‘EII” model of consist of using 20 different random partitions of datasets into\nG\nG\nclusters and running 20 iterations of our variational EM algorithm. The parameters associated with the largest log-likelihood among the 20 random partitions was then used to initialize all eight models which is then run until convergence. Convergence of the algorithm for these models is determined using a modified Aitken acceleration criterion.\nwhere at iteration\nm\nm\n. Convergence is based on Aitken acceleration\n(\nAitken 1926\n)\nwhich is considered to satisfied when\nl\n∞\n(\nm\n+\n1\n)\n−\nl\n∞\n(\nm\n)\nl_{\\infty}^{(m+1)}-l_{\\infty}^{(m)}\nis positive and smaller than some\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n,\nwhere\nl\n(\nm\n)\nl^{(m)}\nis the value of the log-likelihood and\nl\n∞\n(\nm\n+\n1\n)\nl_{\\infty}^{(m+1)}\nis an asymptotic estimate of the log-likelihood given by\nl\n∞\n(\nm\n+\n1\n)\n=\nl\n(\nm\n)\n+\nl\n(\nm\n+\n1\n)\n−\nl\n(\nm\n−\n1\n)\n1\n−\na\n(\nm\n)\nwhere\na\n(\nm\n)\n=\nl\n(\nm\n+\n1\n)\n−\nl\n(\nm\n)\nl\n(\nm\n)\n−\nl\n(\nm\n−\n1\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle l_{\\infty}^{(m+1)}=l^{(m)}+\\frac{l^{(m+1)}-l^{(m-1)}}{1-a^{(m)}}\\qquad\\mbox{where}\\qquad a^{(m)}=\\frac{l^{(m+1)}-l^{(m)}}{l^{(m)}-l^{(m-1)}},\n(\nBöhning et al. 1994\n)\n. Here, we used\nϵ\n=\n0.001\n.\n\\epsilon=0.001.\nTypically, the number of component is unknown in cluster analysis. In our case, the covariance structure that provides the best fit is also unknown. Hence, we fit the model with all eight covariance structure to a range of\nG\nG\nand the Bayesian information criteria (BIC)\n(\nSchwarz\net al. 1978\n)\nis used to assess the goodness of fit of each fitted model and select the number of\nG\nG\nand corresponding to the corresponding covariance structure that has the best fitness to the data. The BIC of a fitted model is computed by\nBIC\n⁡\n(\nϑ\n^\n)\n=\n−\n2\n​\nℓ\n​\n(\nϑ\n^\n)\n+\nψ\n​\nlog\n⁡\n(\nn\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{BIC(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\vartheta}})}=-2\\ell(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\vartheta}})+\\psi\\log(n),\nwhere\nℓ\n⁡\n(\nϑ\n)\n\\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\vartheta})\nis the observed data log-likelihood function here,\nn\nn\nis the sample size, and\nψ\n\\psi\nis the number of free parameters in the model. Note that here we compute approximation of BIC using\nE\n​\nL\n​\nB\n​\nO\nELBO\n(\nChen\net al. 2018\n)\n. We will refer to the approximation of BIC as BIC in the paper. Additionally, the variational parameters can be regarded as proxies for the latent variables and therefore, we do not penalize for variational parameters. The model that has the smallest BIC value is selected and the number of underlying groups is therefore estimated. Performance assessment clustering performance was done in using the Adjusted Rand Index\n(\nHubert and\nArabie 1985\n, ARI;)\n: 1 indicates perfect agreement between the true and predicted classification and the expected value of the ARI under random classification is zero. A value\n<\n<\n0 indicate a classification that is worse than would be expected under random\nassignment.\nFor simulation studies where we know the true class labels, we also compared the performance of our proposed model to a Poisson mixture model implemented in the\nR\npackage\nHTSCluster\n(\nRau et al. 2015\n)\n. Note that it is a clustering algorithm designed for clustering high-throughput transcriptome sequencing (HTS) data and therefore has a built-in normalization option which could not be turned off. Therefore, we set the normalization option to “TMM” for our analysis.\n3\nAnalyses\n3.1\nSimulation Study 1\nWe generated 100 three dimensional datasets of size\nN\n=\n2000\nN=2000\nwith three components with mixing proportions\n𝝅\n=\n(\n0.2\n,\n0.5\n,\n0.3\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\pi}=(0.2,0.5,0.3)\n, known mean and a completely unconstrained VVV covariance structure (see Table\n2\nfor the values used to generate the data). We ran our model for\nG\n=\n1\n:\n4\nG=1:4\nand all eight covariance structures. In 100 out of the 100 datasets, a three component VVV model was selected with an average ARI of 0.99 and standard deviation (sd) of 0.003. Summary of the parameter estimates is provided in Table\n2\n.\nTable 2:\nSummary of the average and standard errors (SE) of the estimated parameters from the 100 datasets where a\nG\n=\n3\nG=3\ncomponent VVV model was selected in Simulation Study 1.\nTrue Parameters\nAverage of Estimated Parameters\nStandard errors\n𝝁\n1\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{1}\n(6, 3, 3)\n(6.00, 3.00, 3.00)\n(0.03, 0.04, 0.04)\n𝝁\n2\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{2}\n(3, 5, 3)\n(3.00, 5.00, 3.00)\n(0.02, 0.02, 0.02)\n𝝁\n3\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{3}\n(5, 3, 5)\n(5.00, 3.00, 5.00)\n(0.02, 0.03, 0.02)\n𝚺\n1\n=\n[\n0.30\n0.15\n0.20\n0.15\n0.40\n0.30\n0.20\n0.30\n0.40\n]\n,\n𝚺\n^\n1\n=\n[\n0.30\n0.15\n0.20\n0.15\n0.40\n0.30\n0.20\n0.30\n0.40\n]\n,\nSE\n​\n(\n𝚺\n^\n1\n)\n=\n[\n0.02\n0.02\n0.02\n0.02\n0.03\n0.03\n0.02\n0.03\n0.03\n]\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.30&0.15&0.20\\\\\n0.15&0.40&0.30\\\\\n0.20&0.30&0.40\\\\\n\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.30&0.15&0.20\\\\\n0.15&0.40&0.30\\\\\n0.20&0.30&0.40\\\\\n\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\text{SE}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}0.02&0.02&0.02\\\\\n0.02&0.03&0.03\\\\\n0.02&0.03&0.03\\\\\n\\end{bmatrix}\n𝚺\n2\n=\n[\n0.30\n0.15\n0.20\n0.15\n0.40\n0.30\n0.20\n0.30\n0.40\n]\n,\n𝚺\n^\n2\n=\n[\n0.30\n0.15\n0.20\n0.15\n0.40\n0.30\n0.20\n0.30\n0.40\n]\n,\nSE\n​\n(\n𝚺\n^\n2\n)\n=\n[\n0.02\n0.01\n0.01\n0.01\n0.02\n0.02\n0.01\n0.02\n0.02\n]\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.30&0.15&0.20\\\\\n0.15&0.40&0.30\\\\\n0.20&0.30&0.40\\\\\n\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.30&0.15&0.20\\\\\n0.15&0.40&0.30\\\\\n0.20&0.30&0.40\\\\\n\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\text{SE}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}0.02&0.01&0.01\\\\\n0.01&0.02&0.02\\\\\n0.01&0.02&0.02\\\\\n\\end{bmatrix}\n𝚺\n3\n=\n[\n0.20\n−\n0.15\n−\n0.10\n−\n0.15\n0.40\n−\n0.10\n−\n0.10\n−\n0.10\n0.20\n]\n,\n𝚺\n^\n3\n=\n[\n0.20\n−\n0.15\n−\n0.10\n−\n0.15\n0.39\n−\n0.10\n−\n0.10\n−\n0.10\n0.20\n]\n,\nSE\n​\n(\n𝚺\n^\n3\n)\n=\n[\n0.01\n0.01\n0.01\n0.01\n0.02\n0.01\n0.01\n0.01\n0.01\n]\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.20&-0.15&-0.10\\\\\n-0.15&0.40&-0.10\\\\\n-0.10&-0.10&0.20\\\\\n\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.20&-0.15&-0.10\\\\\n-0.15&0.39&-0.10\\\\\n-0.10&-0.10&0.20\\\\\n\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\text{SE}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}}_{3})=\\begin{bmatrix}0.01&0.01&0.01\\\\\n0.01&0.02&0.01\\\\\n0.01&0.01&0.01\\\\\n\\end{bmatrix}\nSimilarly, we also ran the Poisson mixture model in the\nR\npackage\nHTSCluster\nfor\nG\n=\n1\n:\n4\nG=1:4\nand selected the best model with BIC. In 100 out of the 100 datasets, it overestimated the number of components resulting in a four component model was selected with an average ARI of 0.75 and a standard deviation of 0.11.\n3.2\nSimulation Study 2\nHere, we generated 100 six dimensional datasets of size\nN\n=\n500\nN=500\nwith two components with mixing proportions\n𝝅\n=\n(\n0.59\n,\n0.41\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\pi}=(0.59,0.41)\n, known mean and a completely constrained EII (\nλ\n\\lambda\nI) covariance structure (see Table\n3\nfor the values used to generate the data). We ran our model for\nG\n=\n1\n:\n3\nG=1:3\nand all eight covariance structures. In 99 out of 100 datasets, a two component EII model was selected with an average ARI of 1.00 and a standard deviation of 0.00 and in one of the 100 dataset, a two component VII model was selected. Note, that VII is still imposes an isotropic (spherical) structure on\n𝚺\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\nbut it allows\n𝚺\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\nto be vary among groups. In our case, with\nG\n=\n2\nG=2\n, it just added one additional parameter so the penalty for selecting a slightly complicated model was very small. Summary of the parameter estimates of the 99 out of the 100 datasets where the correct model is selected is provided in\n3\n.\nTable 3:\nSummary of the average and standard errors (SE) of the estimated parameters from the 99 out the 100 datasets where a\nG\n=\n2\nG=2\ncomponent EII model was selected in Simulation Study 2.\ng\n𝝁\ng\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{g}\n𝝁\n^\ng\n\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\mu}}_{g}\n1\n(5, 6, 5, 5, 5, 6)\nAverage\n(5.00, 6.00, 5.01, 5.00, 5.01, 6.01)\nSE\n(0.05, 0.06, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.06)\n2\n(2.5, 3, 2.5, 3, 3, 2.5)\nAverage\n(2.49, 3.01, 2.50, 3.00, 3.00, 2.49)\nSE\n(0.07, 0.07, 0.07, 0.07, 0.07, 0.07 )\ng\nλ\n\\lambda\nλ\n^\n\\hat{\\lambda}\n1, 2\n1\nAverage\n0.99\nSE\n0.03\nSimilarly, we also ran the Poisson mixture model in the\nR\npackage\nHTSCluster\nfor\nG\n=\n1\n:\n3\nG=1:3\nand selected the best model with BIC. In 100 out of the 100 datasets, it overestimated the number of components resulting in a three component model was selected with an average ARI of 0.04 and a standard deviation of 0.02.\n3.3\nSimulation studies on data generated from other models\n3.3.1\nSimulation Study 3: Datasets generated using mixtures of negative binomial distributions\nHere, we generated 100 six dimensional datasets of size\nN\n=\n2000\nN=2000\nfrom a two component mixtures of independent negative binomial distributions with mixing proportions\n𝝅\n=\n(\n0.79\n,\n0.21\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\pi}=(0.79,0.21)\n. We ran all our eight models for\nG\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\n4\nG=1,\\ldots,4\nand model selection was done using BIC. In 100 out of 100 datasets, a two EII component model was selected with perfect classification. Note that, it always selected a diagonal covariance structure where the covariance is 0.\nTable\n4\nshows that our model was able to recover the true mean and variance of the data generated from the mixtures of negative binomial distributions.\nTable 4:\nSummary of the average and standard errors (SE) of the estimated means for the 100 datasets in Simulation Study 3 where the data was generated from a mixture of independent negative binomial distributions.\ng=1\nTrue Mean\n(1000, 500, 1000, 500, 1000 , 500)\nAverage of Estimated Means\n(1000.28, 500.24, 999.84, 500.09, 999.75, 500.25)\nSE of Estimated Means\n(2.66, 1.50, 2.31, 1.43, 2.91, 1.36)\nTrue Variance\n(11000, 3000, 11000, 3000, 11000, 3000)\nAverage of Estimated Variances\n(11045.99, 3012.67, 11036.90, 30011.06, 11034.85, 3012.86)\nSE of Estimated Variances\n(147.40, 37.25, 148.18, 37.31, 152.14, 39.56)\ng=2\nTrue Mean\n(500, 1000, 500, 1000, 500, 500)\nAverage of Estimated Means\n(500.10, 999.81, 500.00, 1000.53, 499.91, 499.75)\nSE of Estimated Means\n(2.44, 5.38, 5.37, 2.71, 2.39, 2.68)\nTrue Variance\n(3000, 11000, 3000,11000, 3000, 3000)\nAverage of Estimated Variances\n(3011.23, 11036.40, 3010.06, 11051.85, 3009.10, 3007.34)\nSE of Estimated Variances\n(43.19, 182.23, 45.03, 193.22, 43.65, 43.70)\nSimilarly, we also ran the Poisson mixture model in the\nR\npackage\nHTSCluster\nfor\nG\n=\n1\n:\n4\nG=1:4\nand selected the best model with BIC. In 100 out of the 100 datasets, it overestimated the number of components resulting in a four component model was selected with an average ARI of 0.29 and a standard deviation of 0.01.\n3.3.2\nSimulation Study 4: Datasets generated using mixtures of independent Poisson distributions\nHere, we used 100 four dimensional datasets generated using a mixtures of independent Poisson distributions with mixing proportions\n𝝅\n=\n(\n0.59\n,\n0.41\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\pi}=(0.59,0.41)\n.We ran all our eight models for\nG\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\n4\nG=1,\\ldots,4\nand model selection was done using BIC. In 100 out of the 100 datasets, a two component EII model was selected with perfect classification for all datasets. Note that here, the diagonal structure for\n𝚺\n\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\nwas always selected as the datasets were generated from mixtures of independent Poisson distributions. Table\n5\nshows that our model was able to recover the true mean the data generated from the mixtures of Poisson distributions very well but it overestimated the variable slightly which would be expected as MPLN imposes a structure such that\n𝕍\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n>\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n\\Var(Y)>\\Ev(Y)\nbut for Poisson distribution\n𝕍\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n.\n\\Var(Y)=\\Ev(Y).\nTable 5:\nSummary of the average and standard errors (SE) of the estimated means for the 100 datasets in Simulation Study 4 where the data was generated using a mixtures of independent Poisson distributions.\ng=1\nTrue Mean\n(1000, 1500, 1500, 1000)\nAverage of Estimated Means\n(999.98, 1500.16, 1500.27, 1000.09)\nSE of Estimated Means\n(1.31, 1.84, 1.77, 1.40)\nTrue Variance\n(1000, 1500, 1500, 1000)\nAverage of Estimated Variances\n(1027.97, 1563.15, 1563.27,1028.09)\nSE of Estimated Variances\n(6.21, 14.37, 14.28, 6.52)\ng=2\nTrue Mean\n(1000, 1000, 1000, 1500)\nAverage of Estimated Means\n(999.94, 1000.04, 999.96, 1499.97)\nSE of Estimated Means\n(1.39, 1,32, 1.38, 1.79)\nTrue Variance\n(1000, 1000, 1000, 1500)\nAverage of Estimated Variances\n(1027.93, 1028.03, 1027.95, 1562.95 )\nSE of Estimated Variances\n(6.50, 6.13, 6.57, 14.51)\nSimilarly, we also ran the Poisson mixture model in the\nR\npackage\nHTSCluster\nfor\nG\n=\n1\n:\n4\nG=1:4\nand selected the best model with BIC. In 100 out of the 100 datasets, it selected the correct\nG\n=\n2\nG=2\nmodel with an ARI of 1.\n3.4\nReal data analysis\n3.5\nChronic Kidney Disease Data Set\nHere, we used the Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) Dataset available via UCI Machine Learning repository. The dataset comprises of 25 attributes regarding various blood measurements and various categorical measurements pertaining disease status on 400 individuals. Here, we focus on three discrete count measurements:\nbgr\n: blood glucose random;\nwbcc\n: white blood cell count; and\npcv\npacked cell volume with the aim of classifying the disease status of the patient for CKD. Information on the status of CKD is available in the variable\nclass\n. Before the analysis, observations with missing values on our 4 variables (\nbgr, wbcc, pcv\nand\nclass\n) were removed resulting in 261 observations. We ran our algorithm for\nG\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\n4\nG=1,\\ldots,4\nand BIC selected a two component ”VVI” model. As seen in Table\n6\n, the first cluster comprised of mostly CKD patients and the second cluster comprised mostly of non-CKD patients.\nTable 6:\nCross tabulation of the clusters obtained by mixtures of MPLN and HTSCluster against the CKD status.\nMix. MPLN\nHTSCluster\n1\n2\n1\n2\n3\n4\nCKD\n95\n29\n28\n18\n29\n49\nNot CKD\n0\n135\n24\n0\n16\n95\nVisualization of the true cluster structure and predicted cluster structure are shown in Figure\n1\n. We also ran HTSCluster on the dataset for\nG\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\n4\nG=1,\\ldots,4\nand it selected a four component model. However, the cluster structure appears to be random (see Figure\n1\nand Table\n6\n).\nFigure 1:\nScatter plot matrix of the chronic kidney dataset where the colors/symbols represents the CKD status on the left, colors/symbols representing the cluster structure recovered by MPLN in the middle, and colors/symbols representing the cluster structure recovered by HTSCluster on the right.\n4\nConclusions\nHere, we propose an efficient framework for parameter estimation for clustering using mixtures of multivariate Poisson-lognormal distributions that utilizes a variational Gaussian approximation. The variational Gaussian approximation provides a fast and deterministic framework for estimating the model parameters and alleviates the computational overhead due to MCMC-EM. Through eigen-decomposition of the component’s covariance matrices of the latent variable and imposition of constraints, a family of parsimonious models is proposed. Through simulation studies, we show that the proposed models provide competitive performance, the parameters were very close to the true parameters (when the correct model was chosen), and close to perfect classification was obtained using the model selected by BIC. Additionally, we also generate data from mixtures of univariate Poisson distributions and mixtures of negative binomial distributions and show that our proposed model can recover the true mean of the models fairly well. When datasets were generated from a Poisson model which assumes\n𝕍\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n\\Var(Y)=\\Ev(Y)\n, the variance was overestimated slightly as MPLN imposes the structure that\n𝕍\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n>\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n.\n\\Var(Y)>\\Ev(Y).\nIn the case where the datasets were generated from a mixture of univariate negative binomial distributions, the variance estimation was much closer as both MPLN and negative binomial impose the structure that\n𝕍\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n>\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n.\n\\Var(Y)>\\Ev(Y).\nAdditionally, when data are generated from mixtures of univariate distributions, our approach selects a model with a diagonal covariance structure where variables are independent.\nWhile our proposed work is motivated by modern biological data such as RNA-seq data, here we only focus on a general framework that is applicable to any multivariate count data. For biological data, extensions of these approaches may require some structure that needs to be imposed on the datasets to normalize for library specific factors such as library size and transcript specific factors such as gene length. Although the use of variational Gaussian approximations alleviates some of the challenges of MCMC-EM, the algorithm can still be prone to computational issues encountered by any traditional EM algorithm such as convergence to local maxima, singularities, etc. Furthermore, a systematic evaluation for the speed and accuracy of variational EM against the MCMC-EM would be very valuable and is ongoing. In our work, BIC seems to perform fairly well, however, some future work will focus on investigation of different model-selection criteria for MPLN mixture models and for discrete data in general.\nReferences\nAitchison and\nHo (1989)\nAitchison, J. and C. Ho (1989).\nThe multivariate Poisson-log normal distribution.\nBiometrika\n76\n(4), 643–653.\nAitken (1926)\nAitken, A. C. (1926).\nA series formula for the roots of algebraic and transcendental\nequations.\nProceedings of the Royal Society of Edinburgh\n45\n,\n14–22.\nAnders and\nHuber (2010)\nAnders, S. and W. Huber (2010).\nDifferential expression analysis for sequence count data.\nNature Precedings\n, 1–1.\nArchambeau et al. (2007)\nArchambeau, C., D. Cornford, M. Opper, and J. Shawe-Taylor (2007).\nGaussian process approximations of stochastic differential equations.\nJournal of Machine Learning Research\n1\n, 1–16.\nArridge\net al. (2018)\nArridge, S. R., K. Ito, B. Jin, and C. Zhang (2018).\nVariational gaussian approximation for poisson data.\nInverse Problems\n34\n(2), 025005.\nBanfield and\nRaftery (1993)\nBanfield, J. D. and A. E. Raftery (1993).\nModel-based Gaussian and non-Gaussian clustering.\nBiometrics\n49\n(3), 803–821.\nBiernacki\net al. (2003)\nBiernacki, C., G. Celeux, and G. Govaert (2003).\nChoosing starting values for the em algorithm for getting the highest\nlikelihood in multivariate gaussian mixture models.\nComputational Statistics & Data Analysis\n41\n(3-4),\n561–575.\nBlei\net al. (2017)\nBlei, D. M., A. Kucukelbir, and J. D. McAuliffe (2017).\nVariational inference: A review for statisticians.\nJournal of the American statistical Association\n112\n(518), 859–877.\nBöhning et al. (1994)\nBöhning, D., E. Dietz, R. Schaub, P. Schlattmann, and B. Lindsay (1994).\nThe distribution of the likelihood ratio for mixtures of densities\nfrom the one-parameter exponential family.\nAnnals of the Institute of Statistical