g\\frac{1+K^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}{1+{K^{*}}^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}&gt;\\frac{1}{n}\\log c_{n}\\right)\\leq\\frac{1}{c_{n}}. Take c n := n 2 c_{n}:=n^{2} and summing all n n , we have ∑ n = 1 ∞ P ⁡ ( 1 n ​ log ⁡ 1 + K T ​ 𝒳 n 1 + K ∗ T ​ 𝒳 n &gt; 2 ​ log ⁡ n n ) ≤ ∑ n = 1 ∞ 1 n 2 &lt; ∞ . \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}P\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\log\\frac{1+K^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}{1+{K^{*}}^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}&gt;\\frac{2\\log n}{n}\\right)\\leq\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^{2}}&lt;\\infty. Therefore, applying the Borel-Cantelli Lemma; e.g., see [ 20 ] , it leads to P ⁡ ( 1 n ​ log ⁡ 1 + K T ​ 𝒳 n 1 + K ∗ T ​ 𝒳 n &gt; 2 ​ log ⁡ n n ​ infinitely often ) = 0 . P\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\log\\frac{1+K^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}{1+{K^{*}}^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}&gt;\\frac{2\\log n}{n}\\,\\,\\text{infinitely often}\\right)=0. Thus, there exists N &gt; 0 N&gt;0 such that for all n ≥ N n\\geq N , we have 1 n ​ log ⁡ 1 + K T ​ 𝒳 n 1 + K ∗ T ​ 𝒳 n ≤ 2 ​ log ⁡ n n . \\frac{1}{n}\\log\\frac{1+K^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}{1+{K^{*}}^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}\\leq\\frac{2\\log n}{n}. It follows that lim sup n → ∞ 1 n ​ log ⁡ 1 + K T ​ 𝒳 n 1 + K ∗ T ​ 𝒳 n ≤ 0 \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{n}\\log\\frac{1+K^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}{1+{K^{*}}^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n}}\\leq 0 with probability one. ∎ Remark: Note that for n ≥ 1 n\\geq 1 , V ⁡ ( n ) = ( 1 + K T ​ 𝒳 n ) ​ V ​ ( 0 ) V(n)=(1+K^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n})V(0) , thus, Lemma 3.4 implies that lim sup n → ∞ 1 n ​ log ⁡ V ⁡ ( n ) V ∗ ​ ( n ) ≤ 0 \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{n}\\log\\frac{V(n)}{V^{*}(n)}\\leq 0 with probability one where V ∗ ​ ( n ) = ( 1 + K ∗ T ​ 𝒳 n ) ​ V ​ ( 0 ) V^{*}(n)=(1+{K^{*}}^{T}\\mathcal{X}_{n})V(0) . IV Dominant Ratio Trading Algorithm Besides the theoretical interests, as mentioned in Section III , we view that Theorem 3.1 and Extended Dominate Asset Theorem 3.2 may be useful to design an algorithm for practical stock trading. The main idea is to take advantage of the Dominant Asset Condition stated in Theorem 3.2 ; i.e., 𝔼 ⁡ [ 1 + X i ​ ( k ) 1 + X j ​ ( k ) ] ≤ 1 , \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\frac{1+X_{i}(k)}{1+X_{j}(k)}\\right]\\leq 1, if it holds, then we set K j ∗ = 1 K_{j}^{*}=1 ; otherwise, K j ∗ = 0 . K_{j}^{*}=0. IV-A Bridging Theory and Practice To implement the idea described above, we proceed as follows: Using s i ​ ( k ) s_{i}(k) to denote the k k th daily realized prices for the i i th stock, we calculate the associated realized return , call it x i ​ ( k ) x_{i}(k) , where x i ​ ( k ) := s i ​ ( k + 1 ) − s i ​ ( k ) s i ​ ( k ) x_{i}(k):=\\frac{s_{i}(k+1)-s_{i}(k)}{s_{i}(k)} for i = 1 , 2 , … , m i=1,2,\\dots,m . It should be noted that, in practice, the realized returns x i ​ ( k ) x_{i}(k) are often nonstationary. Hence, when testing the dominant asset condition, we work with a sliding window consisting of the most recent M M trading steps. 4 4 4 Again, we note here that the unit of “steps” here can be any time stamp such as milliseconds, minutes, days, months, etc. That is, we estimate the expected ratio in the Dominant Asset Condition by R i ​ j ​ ( k ) := 1 M ​ ∑ ℓ = 0 M − 1 1 + x i ​ ( k − ℓ ) 1 + x j ​ ( k − ℓ ) . R_{ij}(k):=\\frac{1}{M}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{M-1}\\frac{1+x_{i}(k-\\ell)}{1+x_{j}(k-\\ell)}. Then, if R i ​ j ≤ 1 R_{ij}\\leq 1 for all i ≠ j i\\neq j , we set K j ∗ ​ ( k ) = 1 K_{j}^{*}(k)=1 ; otherwise, we set K j ∗ ​ ( k ) = 0 . K_{j}^{*}(k)=0. We call the procedure above the Dominant Ratio Trading Algorithm. An illustrative example using historical prices data is provided in the next subsection to follow. IV-B Illustrative Example Via Back-Testing Consider a one-year long portfolio consisting of three assets with duration from February 14, 2019 to February 14, 2020: Vanguard Total World Stock Index Fund ETF Shares (Ticker: VT) , Vanguard Total Bond Market Index Fund ETF Shares (Ticker: BND), and Vanguard Total World Bond EFT (Ticker: BNDX) where the price trajectories are shown in Figure 1 . 5 5 5 The data are provided by Wharton Research Data Services. Figure 1: Daily Closing Stock Prices S i ​ ( k ) , i = 1 , 2 , 3 S_{i}(k),i=1,2,3 for VT, BND, and BNDX, respectively. Begin with initial account value V ⁡ ( 0 ) = $ 1 V(0)=\\$1 , we implement the algorithm described above using a window size M = 20 M=20 days. That is, the initial trade is triggered after receiving the first twenty daily prices data. We ran MATLAB script and plot a typical trading performance in terms of the trajectory of account value V ⁡ ( k ) V(k) , which is shown in Figure 2 . In Figure 2 , we find that the account value obtained by Dominant Ratio Trading Algorithm is increasing from V ⁡ ( 0 ) = 1 V(0)=1 to V ⁡ ( 252 ) ≈ 1.23 V(252)\\approx 1.23 , which yields a returns about 23 % 23\\% and is obviously higher than the account value obtained by standard buy and hold strategy. We also reported the corresponding trading signal K i ​ ( k ) K_{i}(k) for i = 1 , 2 , 3 i=1,2,3 in Figure 3 where a flavor of bang-bang control is seen. To close this section, we also tested various sliding window sizes using equally-spaced M = 1 , 5 , 15 , … , 60 M=1,5,15,\\dots,60 with increment 5 5 between elements and we seen that the algorithm produces similar trading performance to the one seen in Figure 2 . This example provides a potential for bridging the theory and practice in stock trading. Further developments along this line might be fruitful to pursue as a direction of future research. For example, an initial computational complexity analysis and trading with various stocks may be of the next interests to pursue. Figure 2: Trading Performance Comparison: Dominant Ratio Trading Algorithm with M = 20 M=20 versus Buy and Hold strategy. Figure 3: Feedback Gains K i ​ ( k ) K_{i}(k) with i = 1 , 2 , 3 i=1,2,3 for VT, BND, and BNDX, respectively. One sees a bang-bang flavored control signals. V Conclusion and Future Work In this paper, we studied necessary and sufficient conditions for the frequency-based optimal Kelly portfolio. With the aid of these conditions, we derived various different optimality characterizations such as expected ratio optimality, asymptotic relative optimality, and Extended Dominant Asset Theorem. Moreover, to bridge the theory and practice, we used the notion of dominant asset to construct a trading algorithm which indicates the trader when to invest all available funds into the dominant asset. Regarding further research, one obvious continuation would be to study the case when K i &lt; 0 K_{i}&lt;0 is allowed; i.e., short selling should be considered as a next level extension of the formulation. In this situation, we envision a similar results along the lines of those given here. In addition, it would be of interest to relax some of the assumptions in the formulation from i.i.d. return sequences to time-dependent sequences. Finally, for cases when the distribution model for returns X i ​ ( k ) X_{i}(k) is either partially known or completely unknown, it would be of interest to study the extent to which the theory in this paper can be extended. For example, the line along the data-driven algorithm described in Section IV might be helpful. VI ACKNOWLEDGMENTS The author thanks Professor B. Ross Barmish and Professor John A. Gubner for leading the author into this field and seeing the potentiality and applicability of control theory. Appendix A Survival Considerations In the context of stock trading, the very first goal for a trader is to assure that the bankruptcy would never occur for the entire trading period; i.e., one must assure V ⁡ ( k ) &gt; 0 V(k)&gt;0 for all k k . If this is the case, we say the trades are survival . 6 6 6 As stability is to the classical control system, so is survivability to the financial system. In fact, in our prior work [ 12 ] , the survivability problem is regarded as a state positivity problem. Below, we provide a result which indicates that the any feedback gain K K satisfying the constraint set 𝒦 \\mathcal{K} considered in Section II assures survival. Lemma: If K ∈ 𝒦 K\\in\\mathcal{K} , then V ⁡ (