thcal{G}}_{K}(N) = 𝔼 ⁡ [ V ⁡ ( N ) ] − V ⁡ ( 0 ) \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}[V(N)]-V(0) = 𝔼 ⁡ [ ∏ k = 0 N − 1 ( 1 + K ​ X ​ ( k ) ) ​ V ​ ( 0 ) ] − V ⁡ ( 0 ) \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[{\\prod\\limits_{k=0}^{N-1}{\\left({1+KX(k)}\\right)}V(0)}\\right]-V(0)\\hfill = ( ∏ k = 0 N − 1 ( 1 + K ​ 𝔼 ​ [ X ⁡ ( 0 ) ] ) − 1 ) ​ V ​ ( 0 ) \\displaystyle=\\left({\\prod\\limits_{k=0}^{N-1}{\\left({1+K\\mathbb{E}\\left[{X(0)}\\right]}\\right)}}-1\\right)V(0)\\hfill = ( ( 1 + K ​ μ ) N − 1 ) ​ V ​ ( 0 ) . \\displaystyle=\\left({{{\\left({1+K\\mu}\\right)}^{N}}-1}\\right)V(0). To complete the proof, we substitute K = K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ K=K_{approx}^{*} into 𝒢 ¯ K ​ ( N ) \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K}(N) and 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N) is immediately obtained. ∎ Remark: If one adopts the Merton’s formula; i.e., K = K ~ a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ , K=\\widetilde{K}_{approx}^{*}, then 𝒢 ¯ K ~ a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) = ( ( 1 + μ 2 σ 2 ) N − 1 ) ​ V ​ ( 0 ) . \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{\\widetilde{K}_{approx}^{*}}(N)=\\left({{{\\left({1+\\frac{{{\\mu^{2}}}}{\\sigma^{2}}}\\right)}^{N}}-1}\\right)V\\left(0\\right). As seen in the corollary below, we can deduce more regarding the positivity of expected value of the cumulative gain or loss function. Corollary 5 ( Positive Expectation Property ) . For K = K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ K=K_{approx}^{*} , the expected cumulative gain or loss function is nonnegative; i.e., 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) ≥ 0 \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N)\\geq 0 for all N ≥ 1 N\\geq 1 . Moreover, if μ ≠ 0 \\mu\\neq 0 , then 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) &gt; 0 . \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N)&gt;0. Proof. The first statement is a simple consequence by the Lemma 4 . That is, for N ≥ 1 N\\geq 1 , we have ( 1 + μ 2 μ 2 + σ 2 ) N ≥ 1 {{{\\left({1+\\frac{{{\\mu^{2}}}}{{{\\mu^{2}}+{\\sigma^{2}}}}}\\right)}^{N}}}\\geq 1 and note that the inequality above is strict if μ ≠ 0 \\mu\\neq 0 . Thus, it follows that 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) = ( ( 1 + μ 2 μ 2 + σ 2 ) N − 1 ) ​ V ​ ( 0 ) ≥ 0 \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N)=\\left({{{\\left({1+\\frac{{{\\mu^{2}}}}{{{\\mu^{2}}+{\\sigma^{2}}}}}\\right)}^{N}}-1}\\right)V\\left(0\\right)\\geq 0 and if μ ≠ 0 \\mu\\neq 0 , we see that 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) &gt; 0 \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N)&gt;0 follows immediately. ∎ Remark: The statement of the corollary above holds true for the Merton’s formula K = K ~ a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ K=\\widetilde{K}_{approx}^{*} . Moreover, with the aid of the corollary, a somewhat stronger result, recorded below, can be proven. Theorem 6 ( Expected Growth Property of Optimum ) . For K = K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ K=K_{approx}^{*} , the expected cumulative gain or loss function satisfies 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N + 1 ) ≥ 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) ≥ 0 \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N+1)\\geq\\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N)\\geq 0 for all N ≥ 1 N\\geq 1 . Proof. Observe that 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) = ( ( 1 + μ 2 μ 2 + σ 2 ) N − 1 ) ​ V ​ ( 0 ) . \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N)=\\left({{{\\left({1+\\frac{{{\\mu^{2}}}}{{{\\mu^{2}}+{\\sigma^{2}}}}}\\right)}^{N}}-1}\\right)V\\left(0\\right). Since the term ( 1 + μ 2 μ 2 + σ 2 ) N − 1 {{{\\left({1+\\frac{{{\\mu^{2}}}}{{{\\mu^{2}}+{\\sigma^{2}}}}}\\right)}^{N}}-1} is increasing in N N and V ⁡ ( 0 ) &gt; 0 V(0)&gt;0 , it immediately follows that 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N + 1 ) ≥ 𝒢 ¯ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) . ∎ \\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N+1)\\geq\\overline{\\mathcal{G}}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N).\\qed In finance, the variance is a widely used risk metric; e.g., see [ 24 ] . Thus, it is interesting to study the variance of the cumulative gain or loss function induced by the approximate solution, call it var ​ ( 𝒢 K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) ) {\\rm var}(\\mathcal{G}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N)) . The following result gives a closed-form expression on it. Lemma 7 ( Variance of Cumulative Gain or Loss ) . Given any linear feedback K ∈ 𝒦 K\\in\\mathcal{K} and integer N ≥ 1 N\\geq 1 , the variance of the cumulative gain or loss function satisfies var ( 𝒢 K ( N ) ) = ( ( μ K 2 + σ K 2 ) N − μ K 2 ​ N ) V 2 ( 0 ) . {\\rm var}(\\mathcal{G}_{K}(N))=\\left((\\mu_{{}_{K}}^{2}+\\sigma_{{}_{K}}^{2})^{N}-\\mu_{{}_{K}}^{2N}\\right)V^{2}(0). where μ K := 1 + K μ \\mu_{{}_{K}}:=1+K\\mu and σ K := K σ . \\sigma_{{}_{K}}:=K\\sigma. In addition, for K = K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ K=K_{approx}^{*} , then var ⁡ ( 𝒢 K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ ( N ) ) = ( ( 4 ​ μ 2 + σ 2 μ 2 + σ 2 ) N − ( 2 ​ μ 2 + σ 2 μ 2 + σ 2 ) 2 ​ N ) ​ V 2 ​ ( 0 ) . {\\rm var}(\\mathcal{G}_{K_{approx}^{*}}(N))=\\left(\\left(\\frac{4\\mu^{2}+\\sigma^{2}}{\\mu^{2}+\\sigma^{2}}\\right)^{N}-\\left(\\frac{2\\mu^{2}+\\sigma^{2}}{\\mu^{2}+\\sigma^{2}}\\right)^{2N}\\right)V^{2}(0). Proof. Fix N ≥ 1 N\\geq 1 , begin by noting that var ⁡ ( 𝒢 K ​ ( N ) ) = 𝔼 ⁡ [ V 2 ​ ( N ) ] − ( 𝔼 ⁡ [ V ⁡ ( N ) ] ) 2 . {\\rm var}(\\mathcal{G}_{K}(N))=\\mathbb{E}\\left[{{V^{2}}\\left(N\\right)}\\right]-{\\left({\\mathbb{E}\\left[{V\\left(N\\right)}\\right]}\\right)^{2}}. Now, using the fact that X ⁡ ( k ) X(k) are i.i.d., we observe that 𝔼 ⁡ [ V ⁡ ( N ) ] \\displaystyle{\\mathbb{E}\\left[{V\\left(N\\right)}\\right]} = ( 1 + K ​ μ ) N ​ V ​ ( 0 ) \\displaystyle={\\left({1+K\\mu}\\right)}^{N}{V(0)} and hence ( 𝔼 ⁡ [ V ⁡ ( N ) ] ) 2 = ( 1 + K ​ μ ) 2 ​ N ​ V 2 ​ ( 0 ) . {\\left({\\mathbb{E}\\left[{V\\left(N\\right)}\\right]}\\right)^{2}}={\\left({1+K\\mu}\\right)}^{2N}V^{2}(0). On the other hand, using the fact that X ⁡ ( k ) X(k) are i.i.d. again, we have 𝔼 ​ [ V 2 ​ ( N ) ] \\displaystyle{\\mathbb{E}\\left[{V^{2}\\left(N\\right)}\\right]} = ∏ k = 0 N − 1 𝔼 ⁡ [ ( 1 + K ​ X ​ ( k ) ) 2 ] ​ V 2 ​ ( 0 ) \\displaystyle=\\prod\\limits_{k=0}^{N-1}\\mathbb{E}\\left[{\\left({1+KX(k)}\\right)^{2}}\\right]V^{2}(0) = 