​ ( k ) ) Z_{k}:=\\log(1+KX(k)) . In addition, it is readily verified that for each k = 0 , 1 , … , N − 1 k=0,1,\\ldots,N-1 , var ⁡ ( Z k ) \\displaystyle{\\rm var}(Z_{k}) = 𝔼 ⁡ [ log 2 ⁡ ( 1 + K ​ X ​ ( k ) ) ] − g 2 ​ ( K ) . \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}[\\log^{2}(1+KX(k))]-g^{2}(K). Therefore, a straightforward calculation leads to var ⁡ ( log ⁡ V ⁡ ( N ) V ⁡ ( 0 ) ) \\displaystyle{\\rm var}\\bigg(\\log\\frac{V(N)}{V(0)}\\bigg) = ∑ k = 0 N − 1 𝔼 ⁡ [ log 2 ⁡ ( 1 + K ​ X ​ ( k ) ) ] − N ​ g 2 ​ ( K ) \\displaystyle=\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\mathbb{E}[\\log^{2}(1+KX(k))]-Ng^{2}(K) = N ⁡ ( 𝔼 ⁡ [ log 2 ⁡ ( 1 + K ​ X ​ ( 0 ) ) ] − g 2 ​ ( K ) ) \\displaystyle=N\\bigg(\\mathbb{E}[\\log^{2}(1+KX(0))]-g^{2}(K)\\bigg) which is desired. ∎ V-D Performance Via Approximation: A Revisit Henceforth, if K = K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ K=K_{approx}^{*} is used, then g ⁡ ( K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ) g(K_{approx}^{*}) represents the corresponding expected logarithmic growth rate in wealth using the approximate optimum. Similarly, if K = K ∗ K=K^{*} , then g ⁡ ( K ∗ ) g(K^{*}) represents the expected logarithmic growth rate using true optimum. The following proposition estimates an upper bound for the difference between g ⁡ ( K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ) g(K_{approx}^{*}) and g ⁡ ( K ∗ ) g(K^{*}) . Proposition 9 . If K ∗ ≠ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ K^{*}\\neq K_{approx}^{*} and both of them satisfies the condition stated in the Survival Lemma, then 0 ≤ g ⁡ ( K ∗ ) − g ⁡ ( K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ) ≤ log ⁡ 𝔼 ⁡ [ 1 + K ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) 1 + K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) ] . 0\\leq g(K^{*})-g(K_{approx}^{*})\\leq\\log\\mathbb{E}\\left[{\\frac{{1+{K^{*}}X\\left(0\\right)}}{{1+K_{approx}^{*}X\\left(0\\right)}}}\\right]. Otherwise, | g ⁡ ( K ∗ ) − g ⁡ ( K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ) | = 0 . |g(K^{*})-g(K_{approx}^{*})|=0. Proof. When K ∗ = K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ K^{*}=K_{approx}^{*} , by definition of g g , it is trivial to see that | g ⁡ ( K ∗ ) − g ⁡ ( K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ) | = 0 . |g(K^{*})-g(K_{approx}^{*})|=0. To complete the proof, it suffices to show that the desired upper bound holds when K ∗ ≠ K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x K^{*}\\neq K_{approx} . Note that g ⁡ ( K ∗ ) ≥ g ⁡ ( K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ) g(K^{*})\\geq g(K_{approx}^{*}) , hence, it is obvious that 0 ≤ g ⁡ ( K ∗ ) − g ⁡ ( K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ) 0\\leq g(K^{*})-g(K_{approx}^{*}) . On the other hand, using the fact that K ∗ K^{*} and K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ K_{approx}^{*} satisfies the condition stated in the Survival Lemma, it is readily seen that both 1 + K ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) &gt; 0 1+K^{*}X(0)&gt;0 and 1 + K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) &gt; 0 1+K_{approx}^{*}X(0)&gt;0 for all admissible X min ≤ X ⁡ ( 0 ) ≤ X max X_{\\min}\\leq X(0)\\leq X_{\\max} with probability one. Now, using Jensen’s inequality on the logarithmic function, we obtain g ⁡ ( K ∗ ) − g ⁡ ( K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ) \\displaystyle{g\\left(K^{*}\\right)-g\\left({K_{approx}^{*}}\\right)} = 𝔼 ⁡ [ log ⁡ 1 + K ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) 1 + K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) ] \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[{\\log\\frac{{1+{K^{*}}X\\left(0\\right)}}{{1+K_{approx}^{*}X\\left(0\\right)}}}\\right]\\hfill ≤ log ⁡ 𝔼 ⁡ [ 1 + K ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) 1 + K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) ] . ∎ \\displaystyle\\leq\\log\\mathbb{E}\\left[{\\frac{{1+{K^{*}}X\\left(0\\right)}}{{1+K_{approx}^{*}X\\left(0\\right)}}}\\right].\\qed Remarks: It is worth noting that inside the upper bound of the performance difference 𝔼 ⁡ [ 1 + K ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) 1 + K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ X ​ ( 0 ) ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[{\\frac{{1+{K^{*}}X\\left(0\\right)}}{{1+K_{approx}^{*}X\\left(0\\right)}}}\\right] is of the form of a linear-fractional function. Thus, if needed, one can carry out a next level estimate on the upper bound by invoking linear-fractional programming technique. That is, one can consider an optimization problem given by max z ⁡ 1 + K ∗ ​ z 1 + K a ​ p ​ p ​ r ​ o ​ x ∗ ​ z \\displaystyle\\max_{z}\\frac{1+K^{*}z}{1+K_{approx}^{*}z} subject to ​ [ 1 − 1 ] ​ z ≤ [ X max − X min ] . \\displaystyle\\text{subject to}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}z\\leq\\begin{bmatrix}X_{\\max}\\\\ -X_{\\min}\\end{bmatrix}. Then, using Charnes-Cooper transformation; see [ 18 ] and [ 25 ] , one can readily recast above into a linear programming problem then solve the unknown z z above in a very efficient way. VI Conclusion and Future Works In this paper, we examined several properties for the existing approximate solution using the celebrated maximization of expected logarithmic growth as performance metric. We see that the solution indeed provides a certain degree of insights on risk-return tradeoffs. Several technical results such as best achievable upper bound performance, positivity of expected cumulative gain or loss and results related to survivability are provided. Regarding further research, one possible continuation is to generalize the betting function to include time-varying feedback gain K K ; i.e., K = K ⁡ ( k ) K=K(k) . Another immediate direction for future research would be to extend our framework to the stock trading scenario, which involves multiple stocks into considerations. Finally, since the Kelly criterion requires the bettor to know the distribution of returns, it is natural to ask what if the underlying distributions are not trustworthy? What is the associated performance lead by approximate solution? One possible approach is to formulate a Kelly problem involving uncertain distributional considerations; e.g., see [ 26 ] . References [1] J. L. Kelly, Jr., “A New Interpretation of Information Rate,” Bell System Technical Journal , vol. 35, pp. 917–926, 1956. [2] E. O. Thorp, “The Kelly Criterion in Blackjack Sports Betting, and the Stock Market,” 2011. [3] L. Garlappi and G. Skoulakis, “Taylor Series Approximations to Expected Utility and Optimal Portfolio Choice,” Mathematics and Financial Economics , vol. 5, no. 2, pp. 121–156, 2011. [4] V. Nekrasov, “Kelly Criterion for Multivariate Portfoli