{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2005.04544.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 60061, "equations": [], "text": "[2005.04544] Unified Models of Human Behavioral Agents in Bandits, Contextual Bandits and RL Unified Models of Human Behavioral Agents in Bandits, Contextual Bandits and RL Baihan Lin Affiliation: Center for Theoretical Neuroscience, Columbia University, New York, USA Guillermo Cecchi Affiliation: IBM Thomas J. Watson Research Center, Yorktown Heights, NY, USA Djallel Bouneffouf Affiliation: IBM Thomas J. Watson Research Center, Yorktown Heights, NY, USA Jenna Reinen Affiliation: IBM Thomas J. Watson Research Center, Yorktown Heights, NY, USA Irina Rish Affiliation: Mila - Quebec AI Institute, University of Montreal, Montreal, Canada E-mail baihan.lin@columbia.edu, gcecchi@us.ibm.com, {djallel.bouneffouf,jenna.reinen}@ibm.com, irina.rish@mila.quebec Abstract Artificial behavioral agents are often evaluated based on their consistent behaviors and performance to take sequential actions in an environment to maximize some notion of cumulative reward. However, human decision making in real life usually involves different strategies and behavioral trajectories that lead to the same empirical outcome. Motivated by clinical literature of a wide range of neurological and psychiatric disorders, we propose here a more general and flexible parametric framework for sequential decision making that involves a two-stream reward processing mechanism. We demonstrated that this framework is flexible and unified enough to incorporate a family of problems spanning multi-armed bandits (MAB), contextual bandits (CB) and reinforcement learning (RL), which decompose the sequential decision making process in different levels. Inspired by the known reward processing abnormalities of many mental disorders, our clinically-inspired agents demonstrated interesting behavioral trajectories and comparable performance on simulated tasks with particular reward distributions, a real-world dataset capturing human decision-making in gambling tasks, and the PacMan game across different reward stationarities in a lifelong learning setting. 1 1 1 The codes to reproduce all the experimental results can be accessed at https://github.com/doerlbh/mentalRL . Keywords: Reinforcement Learning Contextual Bandit Neuroscience 1 Introduction In real-life decision making, from deciding where to have lunch to finding an apartment when moving to a new city, and so on, people often face different level of information dependency. In the simplest case, you are given N N possible actions (“arms”), each associated with a fixed, unknown and independent reward probability distribution, and the goal is to trade between following a good action chosen previously (exploitation) and obtaining more information about the environment which can possibly lead to better actions in the future (exploration). The multi-armed bandit (MAB) (or simply, bandit) typically models this level of exploration-exploitation trade-off [ 24 , 4 ] . In many scenarios, the best strategy may depend on a context from current environment, such that the goal is to learn the relationship between the context vectors and the rewards, in order to make better prediction which action to choose given the context, modeled as the contextual bandits (CB) [ 2 , 25 ] , where the context can be attentive [ 12 , 35 ] , budgeted [ 31 ] or clustered [ 29 , 38 , 37 ] . The learning can be purely online or pretrained in offline fashion [ 30 ] . In more complex environments, there is an additional dependency between contexts given the action an agent takes, and that is modeled as Markov decision process (MDP) in reinforcement learning (RL) problem [ 47 ] . To better model and understand human decision making behavior, scientists usually predict the trajectories [ 34 ] and investigate reward processing mechanisms in healthy subjects [ 41 ] . However, neurodegenerative and psychiatric disorders, often associated with reward processing disruptions, can provide an additional resource for deeper understanding of human decision making mechanisms. From the perspective of evolutionary psychiatry, various mental disorders, including depression, anxiety, ADHD, addiction and even schizophrenia can be considered as “extreme points” in a continuous spectrum of behaviors and traits developed for various purposes during evolution, and somewhat less extreme versions of those traits can be actually beneficial in specific environments. Thus, modeling decision-making biases and traits associated with various disorders may enrich the existing computational decision-making models, leading to potentially more flexible and better-performing algorithms. In this paper, we extended previous pursuits of human behavioral agents in MAB [ 11 ] and RL [ 32 , 36 , 33 ] into CB, built upon the Contextual Thompson Sampling (CTS) [ 2 ] , a state-of-art approach to CB problem, and unfied all three levels as a parametric family of models, where the reward information is split into two streams, positive and negative. 2 Problem Setting Multi-Armed Bandit (MAB). The multi-armed bandit (MAB) problem models a sequential decision-making process, where at each time point a player selects an action from a given finite set of possible actions, attempting to maximize the cumulative reward over time. Optimal solutions have been provided using a stochastic formulation [ 24 , 4 ] , or using an adversarial formulation [ 3 , 5 , 10 ] . Recently, there has been a surge of interest in a Bayesian formulation [ 13 ] , involving the algorithm known as Thompson sampling [ 48 ] . Theoretical analysis in [ 1 ] shows that Thompson sampling for Bernoulli bandits is asymptotically optimal. Contextual Bandit (CB). Following [ 26 ] , this problem is defined as follows. At each time point (iteration) t ∈ { 1 , … , T } t\\in\\{1,...,T\\} , an agent is presented with a context ( feature vector ) x t ∈ 𝐑 N \\textbf{x}_{t}\\in\\mathbf{R}^{N} before choosing an arm k ∈ A = { 1 , … , K } k\\in A=\\{1,...,K\\} . We will denote by X = { X 1 , … , X N } X=\\{X_{1},...,X_{N}\\} the set of features (variables) defining the context. Let r t = ( r t 1 , … CLOSE , {\\textbf{r}_{t}}=(r^{1}_{t},..., OPEN r t K ) r^{K}_{t}) denote a reward vector, where r t k ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] r^{k}_{t}\\in[0,1] is a reward at time t t associated with the arm k ∈ A k\\in A . Herein, we will primarily focus on the Bernoulli bandit with binary reward, i.e. r t k ∈ { 0 , 1 } r^{k}_{t}\\in\\{0,1\\} . Let π : X → A \\pi:X\\rightarrow A denote a policy. Also, D c , r D_{c,r} denotes a joint distribution over ( 𝐱 , 𝐫 ) ({\\bf x},{\\bf r}) . We will assume that the expected reward is a linear function of the context, i.e. E ⁡ [ r t k | x t ] E[r^{k}_{t}|\\textbf{x}_{t}] = μ k T ​ x t =\\mu_{k}^{T}\\textbf{x}_{t} , where μ k \\mu_{k} is an unknown weight vector associated with the arm k k . Reinforcement Learning (RL). Reinforcement learning defines a class of algorithms for solving problems modeled as Markov decision processes (MDP) [ 47 ] . An MDP is defined by the tuple ( 𝒮 , 𝒜 , 𝒯 , ℛ , γ ) (\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{A},\\mathcal{T},\\mathcal{R},\\gamma) , where 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} is a set of possible states, 𝒜 \\mathcal{A} is a set of actions, 𝒯 \\mathcal{T} is a transition function defined as 𝒯 ⁡ ( s , a , s ′ ) = Pr ⁡ ( s ′ | s , a ) \\mathcal{T}(s,a,s^{\\prime})=\\Pr(s^{\\prime}|s,a) , where s , s ′ ∈ 𝒮 s,s^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{S} and a ∈ 𝒜 a\\in\\mathcal{A} , and ℛ : 𝒮 × 𝒜 × 𝒮 ↦ ℝ \\mathcal{R}:\\mathcal{S}\\times\\mathcal{A}\\times\\mathcal{S}\\mapsto\\mathbb{R} is a reward function, γ \\gamma is a discount factor that decreases the impact of the past reward on current action choice. Typically, the objective is to maximize the discounted long-term reward, assuming an infinite-horizon decision process, i.e. to find a policy function π : 𝒮 ↦ 𝒜 \\pi:\\mathcal{S}\\mapsto\\mathcal{A} which specifies the action to take in a given state, so that the cumulative reward is maximized: max ⁡ ∑ t = 0 ∞ π ⁡ γ t ​ ℛ ​ ( s t , a t , s t + 1 ) . \\max_{\\pi}\\sum_{t=0}^{\\infty}\\gamma^{t}\\mathcal{R}(s_{t},a_{t},s_{t+1}). 3 Background: Contextual Thompson Sampling (CTS) As pointed out in the introduction, the main methodological contribution of this work is two-fold: (1) fill in the missing piece of split reward processing in the contextual bandit problem, and (2) unify the bandits, contextual bandits, and reinforcement learnings under the same framework of split reward processing mechanism. We first introduce the theoretical model we built upon for the contextual bandit problem: the Contextual Thompson Sampling, due to its known empirical benefits. In the general Thompson Sampling, the reward r t i r^{i}_{t} for choosing action i i at time t t follows a parametric likelihood function P ​ r ​ ( r t | μ ~ i ) Pr(r_{t}|\\tilde{\\mu}_{i}) . Following [ 2 ] , the posterior distribution at time t + 1 t+1 , P ​ r ​ ( μ ~ i | r t ) ∝ P ​ r ​ ( r t | μ ~ i ) ​ P ​ r ​ ( μ ~ i ) Pr(\\tilde{\\mu}_{i}|r_{t})\\propto Pr(r_{t}|\\tilde{\\mu}_{i})Pr(\\tilde{\\mu}_{i}) is given by a multivariate Gaussian distribution 𝒩 ​ ( μ ^ i ​ ( t + 1 ) CLOSE \\mathcal{N}(\\hat{\\mu}_{i}(t+1) , OPEN v 2 ​ B i ​ ( t + 1 ) − 1 ) v^{2}B_{i}(t+1)^{-1}) , where B i ​ ( t ) = I d + ∑ τ = 1 t − 1 x τ ​ x τ ⊤ B_{i}(t)=I_{d}+\\sum^{t-1}_{\\tau=1}x_{\\tau}x_{\\tau}^{\\top} , and where d d is the context size 𝐱 i {\\bf x}_{i} , v = R ​ 24 ϵ ​ d ​ l ​ n ​ ( 1 γ ) v=R\\sqrt{\\frac{24}{\\epsilon}dln(\\frac{1}{\\gamma})} with R &gt; 0 R&gt;0 , ϵ ∈ ] 0 , 1 ] \\epsilon\\in]0,1] , γ ∈ ] 0 , 1 ] \\gamma\\in]0,1] constants, and μ ^ i ​ ( t ) = B i ​ ( t ) − 1 ​ ( ∑ τ = 1 t − 1 x τ ​ r τ ) \\hat{\\mu}_{i}(t)=B_{i}(t)^{-1}(\\sum^{t-1}_{\\tau=1}x_{\\tau}r_{\\tau}) . At every step t t , the algorithm generates a d d -dimensional sample μ i ~ \\tilde{\\mu_{i}} from 𝒩 ​ ( μ i ^ ​ ( t ) CLOSE \\mathcal{N}(\\hat{\\mu_{i}}(t) , OPEN v 2 ​ B i ​ ( t ) − 1 ) v^{2}B_{i}(t)^{-1