{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2005.11698.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 102763, "equations": ["\\nabla^{2}\\tilde{J}_{m}", "\\partial_{\\lambda}{\\nabla}\\tilde{J}", "\\partial_{\\mu}{\\nabla}\\tilde{J}", "\\Big(\\partial_{\\lambda}{\\nabla}\\tilde{J}\\Big)^{t}", "\\partial_{\\lambda}^{2}\\tilde{J}_{m}", "\\partial^{2}_{\\lambda\\mu}\\tilde{J}_{m}", "\\Big(\\partial_{\\mu}{\\nabla}\\tilde{J}\\Big)^{t}", "\\partial_{\\lambda\\mu}^{2}\\tilde{J}_{m}", "\\partial^{2}_{\\mu}\\tilde{J}_{m}", "B^{t}", "A^{-1}\\Big(I-B(A^{-1}BC^{-1})^{t}\\Big)", "A^{-1}BC^{-1}", "\\Big((A^{-1})BC^{-1}\\Big)^{t}", "-C^{-1}", "{1\\over 2}(\\det^{2}_{a}(u;v)\\ {\\mathrm{diag}}(a^{-1})-Z)", "|v|^{2}_{a}\\,\\overrightarrow{a^{-1}u}-(u,v)_{a}\\overrightarrow{a^{-1}v}", "|u|^{2}_{a}\\ \\overrightarrow{a^{-1}v}-(u,v)_{a}\\ \\overrightarrow{a^{-1}u}", "|v|^{2}_{a}\\,\\big(a^{-1}u\\big)^{t}-(u,v)_{a}\\big(a^{-1}v)^{t}", "-2|v|^{2}_{a}", "2(u,v)_{a}", "|u|^{2}_{a}\\,\\big(a^{-1}v\\big)^{t}-(u,v)_{a}\\big(a^{-1}u)^{t}", "-2|u|^{2}_{a}"], "text": "[2005.11698] Phase transitions in optimal betting strategies Phase transitions in optimal betting strategies L. Dinis 1 , J. Unterberger 2 and D. Lacoste 3 Affiliation: 1 GISC - Grupo Interdisciplinar de Sistemas Complejos and Dpto. de Estructura de la Materia, Física Térmica y Electrónica, Universidad Complutense de Madrid, 28040 Spain 2 Institut Elie Cartan, UMR CNRS 7502, Université de Lorraine, BP 239 F-54506 Vandoeuvre-lès-Nancy Cedex, France 3 Gulliver Laboratory, UMR CNRS 7083, PSL Research University, ESPCI, 10 rue Vauquelin, F-75231 Paris Cedex 05, France August 9, 2026 Abstract Kelly’s criterion is a betting strategy that maximizes the long term growth rate, but which is known to be risky. Here, we find optimal betting strategies that gives the highest capital growth rate while keeping a certain low value of risky fluctuations. We then analyze the trade-off between the average and the fluctuations of the growth rate, in models of horse races, first for two horses then for an arbitrary number of horses, and for uncorrelated or correlated races. We find an analog of a phase transition with a coexistence between two optimal strategies, where one has risk and the other one does not. The above trade-off is also embodied in a general bound on the average growth rate, similar to thermodynamic uncertainty relations. We also prove mathematically the absence of other phase transitions between Kelly’s point and the risk free strategy. pacs 05.70.Ln, 05.40.-a 02.50.Le I Introduction Developed in 1956 by Bell Labs scientist John Kelly, Kelly’s criterion applied the newly created field of information theory to gambling and investment Kelly 1956 . Largely popularized in books Poundstone 2005 , this criterion allows a gambler (or investment fund) to fix what proportion of bankroll should be risked on a given bet. It essentially exploits side information to maximize the expected geometric growth rate of a capital. This work was precursor to the growth optimal portfolio theory, which applied these ideas to capital market MacLean et al. 2011 . The ensemble of optimal investment strategies forms an efficient border Markowitz 1952 , or equivalently a Pareto front Seoane and Solé 2015 ; Shoval et al. 2012 , which is a term used in engineering and economics to call the set of designs that represent best trade-offs between different conflicting requirements. Recently, there has been a surge of interest in applying insights from optimal gambling theory and economy to biology. Kelly’s work led to an essential clarification of the concept of information value in biology Bergstrom and Lachmann 2004 ; Rivoire and Leibler 2011 , which was very helpful to understand strategies used by biological systems in a fluctuating environment. In particular the bet-hedging strategy turned out to be precisely an optimal strategy of the Kelly type Kussell and Leibler 2005 ; Tal and Tran 2020 . Here, we focus on betting strategies of Kelly’s type and draw inspiration from the field of Stochastic Thermodynamics, a recent branch of Thermodynamics with deep links to information theory, and with already several works specifically applied to gambling or betting problems Neri et al. 2019 ; Ito 2016 ; Vinkler et al. 2016 ; Hirono and Hidaka 2015 . A recent and an active line of research concerns the thermodynamic uncertainty relations Horowitz and Gingrich 2019 ; Falasco et al. 2020 ; Vroylandt et al. 2018 ; K. Proesmans and Peliti 2018 , which capture important tradeoffs in Thermodynamics. In this letter, we explore novel implications of these ideas for gambling models. We emphasize at this point that a background on Stochastic Thermodynamics is not required to understand this letter, since we only rely on basic notions of probability and optimization theory. To gain insight into the tradeoff present in gambling, we study the efficient border of Kelly’s model, and we find that it extends to a region of negative growth, never discussed in the literature to our knowledge, corresponding to catastrophic betting strategies. Inspired by works on optimal protocols Aurell et al. 2011 ; Then and Engel 2008 ; Schmiedl and Seifert 2007 , and specifically on phase transitions among optimal protocols Solon and Horowitz 2018 , we identify similar phase transitions in optimal betting strategies. We first prove such a result for uncorrelated races, and involving only two horses, which we then generalize to an arbitrary number of horses and to correlated races. In addition, we also give a general proof of the convexity in the most useful part of the front (positive part of the tradeoff branch), which rules out the existence of further phase transitions on that branch. II Kelly’s horse races Let us recall here the main features of Kelly’s horse race Kelly 1956 . This race involves M M horses, which are numbered as 1 , 2 ​ … ​ M 1,2...M . The odds paid by the bookmaker when the horse x x wins is o x o_{x} , and the probability for this to happen is p x p_{x} . A gambler can distribute his bets on the different horses, let b x b_{x} be the fraction of the bet set on horse x x , so that ∑ x = 1 M b x = 1 \\sum_{x=1}^{M}b_{x}=1 . For all x x , b x &gt; 0 b_{x}&gt;0 , because the gambler bets on all horses but only makes money from the horse x x that wins. A key feature of the model is that this dynamics is repeated, since all the money gained in one race is reinvested in the next race. Thus, the capital C N + 1 C_{N+1} of the gambler after N + 1 N+1 races is related to his capital after N N races, C N C_{N} , by the expression C N + 1 = o x ​ b x ​ C N , with probability ​ p x . C_{N+1}=o_{x}b_{x}C_{N},\\text{ with probability }p_{x}. (1) The important quantity is the long term growth rate of the capital which has the form lim N → ∞ 1 N ​ ln ⁡ C N = ∑ x p x ​ ln ⁡ ( o x ​ b x ) , \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{N}\\ln C_{N}=\\sum_{x}p_{x}\\ln(o_{x}b_{x}), (2) where the equality follows from the law of large numbers. Let us introduce the random variable W x = ln ⁡ ( o x ​ b x ) W_{x}=\\ln(o_{x}b_{x}) which describes the contribution of horse x x to this growth rate. Its average with respect to the probability density p x p_{x} , is the long term growth rate denoted ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle . Kelly’s strategy is defined from the optimization of this average growth rate over the betting strategy defined by b x b_{x} . A simple calculation given the constraint ∑ x b x = 1 \\sum_{x}b_{x}=1 leads to the proportional betting strategy b x ∗ = p x b_{x}^{*}=p_{x} . This particular solution is independent of the odds o x o_{x} , but if there was a track take, the optimal solution would depend on both o x o_{x} and p x p_{x} Kelly 1956 . Games of this type can be easily simulated in a computer using a random number generator to choose a winning horse for each race according to probability distribution p x p_{x} and using Eq. ( 1 ) to compute gambler’s capital SM . The growth of the capital is exponential and Kelly’s strategy dominates on long times all non-optimal strategies as shown in Fig. 1 . Figure 1: Logarithm of the capital of the gambler versus the number of races, for the optimal strategy (Kelly’s) (thick red line) and for a selection of three non-optimal strategies (thin blue lines). A central result of Stochastic Thermodynamics, namely fluctuation relations, can be obtained in a few steps for this model Hirono and Hidaka 2015 . Using the definition of W W , and given that b = p b=p for Kelly’s strategy, we obtain : ⟨ e − W ⟩ = ∑ x p x ​ 1 o x ​ p x = ∑ x 1 o x = 1 , \\langle e^{-W}\\rangle=\\sum_{x}p_{x}\\frac{1}{o_{x}p_{x}}=\\sum_{x}\\frac{1}{o_{x}}=1, (3) where in the last equality, we have used the normalization of the distribution r x = 1 / o x r_{x}=1/o_{x} valid when there is no track take (fair odds). By Jensen’s inequality, Eq. ( 3 ) implies ⟨ W ⟩ ≥ 0 \\langle W\\rangle\\geq 0 , which also follows from ⟨ W ⟩ = D ⁡ ( p | r ) ≥ 0 \\langle W\\rangle=D(p|r)\\geq 0 where D ⁡ ( p | r ) D(p|r) denotes the Kullback-Leibler divergence between the distributions p p and r r . This fluctuation relation ( 3 ) can be generalized for an arbitrary strategy of the gambler, not necessarily that of Kelly, and when the odds are not necessarily fair, by introducing the decomposition W ~ x = W x + I x , \\tilde{W}_{x}=W_{x}+I_{x}, where W x = ln ⁡ ( o x ​ b x ) W_{x}=\\ln(o_{x}b_{x}) as above, W ~ x = ln ⁡ ( o x ​ p x ) \\tilde{W}_{x}=\\ln(o_{x}p_{x}) and I x = ln ⁡ ( p x / b x ) I_{x}=\\ln(p_{x}/b_{x}) . In this way, W ~ \\tilde{W} represents the growth rate of the gambler according to Kelly’s strategy and I I measures the difference between the gambler’s strategy and that of Kelly’s in a KL sense, since ⟨ I ⟩ = D ⁡ ( p | b ) \\langle I\\rangle=D(p|b) . We have then ⟨ e − W ~ ⟩ = ⟨ e − W − I ⟩ = Λ , \\langle e^{-\\tilde{W}}\\rangle=\\langle e^{-W-I}\\rangle=\\Lambda, (4) with Λ = ∑ x 1 / o x \\Lambda=\\sum_{x}1/o_{x} . In the same way that Eq. ( 3 ) is the analog of Jarzynski equality, Eq. ( 4 ) is similar to its generalization for absolutely irreversible processes Murashita et al. 2014 . By Jensen’s inequality, the second-law like inequality ⟨ W ⟩ ≥ − ⟨ I ⟩ − ln ⁡ Λ , \\langle W\\rangle\\geq-\\langle I\\rangle-\\ln\\Lambda, follows which reduces to ⟨ W ⟩ ≥ 0 \\langle W\\rangle\\geq 0 in the particular case of Kelly’s strategy with fair odds. Note that in the general case, ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle can a priori be of any sign. III Mean-variance tradeoff : Choice of utility function Kelly’s strategy focuses on the maximization of the growth rate at the price of overlooking risk. Although bankruptcy is absent in Kelly’s scenario because the growth of the capital is geometric instead of arithmetic, the fluctuations of the capital are large as shown in Fig. 1 and represents a major concern. This problem has been widely recognized in the gambling community. In practice gamblers and investors know that optimal Kelly can be “too risky”; and that “fractional Kelly” should be preferred, which deviates from the optimal solution but reduces the effective variance of the stochastic growth MacLean et al. 2011 . In the same spirit, we study here the optimal betting strategy that gives the highest capital growth rate while keeping a certain low value of risky fluctuations and analyze the corresponding trade-off between risk and gain. A similar idea is behind the mean-variance analysis introduced by Markowitz optimization Markowitz 1952 . In contrast with Markowitz optimization however, which considers the mean and variance of the capital return in one race, we consider here the mean and the variance of the (long-term) growth rate of the capital after many races. This important conceptual difference allows us to recover Kelly’s point as a special case of our analysis, whereas Kelly’s point could not appear as a limiting case of Markowitz’s optimization for this reason. Hence, our utility function is a linear combination of the mean and standard deviation of the growth rate, namely ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle and σ W \\sigma_{W} : J ~ = α ⁡ ⟨ W ⟩ − ( 1 − α ) ​ σ W , \\tilde{J}=\\alpha\\langle W\\rangle-(1-\\alpha)\\sigma_{W}, (5) with 0 ≤ α ≤ 1 0\\leq\\alpha\\leq 1 . In practice, we use the modified utility function J = α ⁡ ⟨ W ⟩ − ( 1 − α ) ​ σ W + λ ​ ∑ x b x , J=\\alpha\\langle W\\rangle-(1-\\alpha)\\sigma_{W}+\\lambda\\sum_{x}b_{x}, (6) where λ \\lambda is a Lagrange multiplier associated to the normalization of the bets. An optimization of J J with respect to b x b_{x} leads to λ = − α \\lambda=-\\alpha . By reporting this into Eq. ( 6 ), the optimal bets b x b_{x} are solutions of : p x − b x = γ σ W ​ p x ​ [ ln ⁡ ( o x ​ b x ) − ⟨ W ⟩ ] , p_{x}-b_{x}=\\frac{\\gamma}{\\sigma_{W}}p_{x}\\left[\\ln(o_{x}b_{x})-\\langle W\\rangle\\right], (7) where γ = ( 1 − α ) / α \\gamma=(1-\\alpha)/\\alpha . As expected, when α = 1 \\alpha=1 ( γ = 0 \\gamma=0 ), we recover the proportional betting of Kelly’s strategy, which maximizes ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle . Instead when α = 0 \\alpha=0 ( γ → ∞ \\gamma\\to\\infty ), we obtain the null strategy also called the risk free strategy, because in this case ⟨ W ⟩ = σ W = 0 \\langle W\\rangle=\\sigma_{W}=0 . Between these two values, the strategy of the gambler is described as mixed since it combines aspects associated to the optimization of ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle and σ W \\sigma_{W} . IV Exact solution for two horses Before embarking on the full problem with an arbitrary number of horses, it is instructive to analyze the fully solvable case of two horses. Let the probability that the first horse wins (resp. loses) be p p (resp. 1 − p 1-p ); the bet and the odd on the first (resp. second) horse are b b and 1 / r 1/r (resp. 1 − b 1-b and 1 / ( 1 − r ) 1/(1-r) ) and let us introduce the parameter σ = p ⁡ ( 1 − p ) \\sigma=\\sqrt{p(1-p)} . From the optimization of J J , we obtain the optimal strategy b ± b^{\\pm} : b ± = p ± γ ​ σ , b^{\\pm}=p\\pm\\gamma\\sigma, (8) where the + + (resp. − - ) sign corresponds to an overbetting (resp. underbetting) strategy with respect to Kelly’s strategy where b = p b=p . By reporting the optimal bet given by Eq. ( 33 ) into the expression of J J , one obtains the efficient border. As shown in Fig. 2 , this border has two branches which meet at Kelly’s point. When p &lt; r p&lt;r the lower blue solid line is the trade-off branch associated with b + b^{+} , while the upper red solid line is the non-trade-off branch, associated with b − b^{-} . The roles of b − b^{-} and b + b^{+} exchange when instead p &gt; r p&gt;r . Let us first focus on the region where ⟨ W ⟩ ≥ 0 \\langle W\\rangle\\geq 0 . Figure 2: Trade-off branch (lower blue solid line) and non-trade-off branch (upper red solid line) in the plane ( ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle , σ W \\sigma_{W} ) for two horses and for the parameters ( p = 0.2 , r = 0.4 ) (p=0.2,r=0.4) . The two branches meet at the red square (Kelly’s strategy), and the blue circle represents the null strategy. We find that the slope of the Pareto border is d ​ σ W d ​ ⟨ W ⟩ | γ = σ p − b , \\left.\\frac{d\\sigma_{W}}{d\\langle W\\rangle}\\right|_{\\gamma}=\\frac{\\sigma}{p-b}, (9) where b b is equal to b − b^{-} when r &lt; p r&lt;p SM . Therefore the slope of the Pareto border is infinite at Kelly’s point where b ± = p b^{\\pm}=p ; while it reaches a finite value near the null strategy, namely d ​ σ W d ​ ⟨ W ⟩ | γ c = 1 γ c = σ | p − r | . \\left.\\frac{d\\sigma_{W}}{d\\langle W\\rangle}\\right|_{\\gamma_{c}}=\\frac{1}{\\gamma_{c}}=\\frac{\\sigma}{|p-r|}. (10) This signals a phase transition at this critical value γ c \\gamma_{c} , where the optimal strategy changes from the null strategy to a mixed strategy. As a result, the optimal J J versus γ \\gamma changes from zero when γ ≥ γ c \\gamma\\geq\\gamma_{c} (null strategy) to a non-zero value when γ ≤ γ c \\gamma\\leq\\gamma_{c} (mixed strategy). For two horses, such a plot is similar to what is shown for three horses in the inset of Fig. 3 . To prove the existence of the phase transition, we have checked that the border is convex near the null strategy. It is indeed the case since d 2 ​ σ W d ​ ⟨ W ⟩ 2 | γ = γ c = r ⁡ ( 1 − r ) σ 2 ​ γ c 3 &gt; 0 . \\left.\\frac{d^{2}\\sigma_{W}}{d\\langle W\\rangle^{2}}\\right|_{\\gamma=\\gamma_{c}}=\\frac{r(1-r)}{\\sigma^{2}\\gamma_{c}^{3}}&gt;0. (11) In the rest of this paper, we now focus on the general case for an arbitrary number of horses. V Numerical results Let us now explain how to obtain the Pareto front from a numerical optimization of the utility function using a simulated annealing algorithm, as illustrated in Fig. 3 for the case of three horses. Similarly to the case of two horses case, the lower and upper branch correspond to different optimization problems. The lower branch is formed by bets that maximize the growth rate ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle for a given value of the fluctuations σ W \\sigma_{W} , whereas the upper branch corresponds to maximal fluctuations σ W \\sigma_{W} for a given value of the growth rate ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle . For the lower branch, there are two regions where ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle is either positive or negative. In the former case, the front is convex and can be recovered by the maximization of the utility function J = J 1 J=J_{1} defined in Eq. ( 5 ). In contrast, in the negative ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle region, the front is concave and a different strategy is needed. Following Solon and Horowitz 2018 , we use a quadratic objective function J 2 = − ( ⟨ W ⟩ − W 0 ) 2 − k ​ σ W . J_{2}=-(\\langle W\\rangle-W_{0})^{2}-k\\sigma_{W}. (12) We use a global minus sign in order to keep the same maximization procedure, although we wish in fact to minimize both the value of σ W \\sigma_{W} and the distance to a target value W 0 W_{0} for the growth rate. By varying the target value W 0 W_{0} from 0 to a sufficiently negative value we can draw the negative lower branch. Parameter k k weighs the importance between the constraint of ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle being close to W 0 W_{0} or minimizing the value of the fluctuations. We took k = 0.5 k=0.5 although other moderate values would do. Similarly, the upper branch with positive ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle is concave and corresponds to the maximization of the objective function J 3 = α ⁡ ⟨ W ⟩ + ( 1 − α ) ​ σ W , J_{3}=\\alpha\\langle W\\rangle+(1-\\alpha)\\sigma_{W}, (13) where the plus sign before σ W \\sigma_{W} now ensures the maximization of the fluctuations in contrast with the lower branch case. The upper branch with negative ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle appears almost straight for large negative values of ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle . Thus, although J 3 J_{3} could still be used there, further numerical precision can be achieved by using a modified objective function J 4 = − ( ⟨ W ⟩ − W 0 ) 2 + k ​ σ W , J_{4}=-(\\langle W\\rangle-W_{0})^{2}+k\\sigma_{W}, (14) where again the plus sign in front of σ W \\sigma_{W} corresponds to the maximization of fluctuations. Figure 3: Pareto borders for 3 horses obtained from numerical optimization of the utility functions J 1 , J 2 , J 3 J_{1},J_{2},J_{3} and J 4 J_{4} (colored solid lines), together with a cloud of points generated by randomly choosing bets satisfying all relevant constraints. Parameters are p 1 = 0.2 p_{1}=0.2 , p 2 = 0.6 p_{2}=0.6 , r 1 = 0.4 r_{1}=0.4 and r 2 = 0.2 r_{2}=0.2 for the first two horses. Inset: J 1 J_{1} versus γ \\gamma along the trade-off branch ( i.e. on the dark blue border). General conclusions can also be obtained for this model near special points. Near Kelly’s point, we find that the slope of the Pareto border is always vertical. This means that in practice if one is willing to pay a sacrifice a small amount of the average growth rate, one can lower the fluctuations significantly, thereby accessing \"safer” strategies such as the blue curves in Fig. 1 . Near the null strategy, we find a similar phase transition as in the two horses case, which we now analyze in more details. VI Mean-variance trade-off : bounds We recall that r x := 1 / o x r_{x}:=1/o_{x} and we assume a fair game for which ∑ x r x = 1 \\sum_{x}r_{x}=1 . Then let q x := r x / p x q_{x}:=r_{x}/p_{x} , so that the first two moments of q q are ⟨ q ⟩ = 1 \\langle q\\rangle=1 and σ q 2 := ⟨ q 2 ⟩ − ⟨ q ⟩ 2 = ⟨ q 2 ⟩ − 1 \\sigma_{q}^{2}:=\\langle q^{2}\\rangle-\\langle q\\rangle^{2}=\\langle q^{2}\\rangle-1 . Let us focus on the branch of positive ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle . In this case, we find the following inequality, σ W ≥ ⟨ W ⟩ σ q , \\sigma_{W}\\geq\\frac{\\langle W\\rangle}{\\sigma_{q}}, (15) which has a similar structure as thermodynamic uncertainty relations Horowitz and Gingrich 2019 ; Uffink and Van Lith 1999 , and which captures a general trade-off between the mean and the variance of the growth rate. The proof goes as follows : we consider the quantity σ q 2 ​ σ W 2 \\sigma_{q}^{2}\\sigma_{W}^{2} , since σ q 2 = ⟨ q 2 ⟩ − 1 \\sigma_{q}^{2}=\\langle q^{2}\\rangle-1 , we have using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality σ q 2 ​ σ W 2 \\displaystyle\\sigma_{q}^{2}\\sigma_{W}^{2} = \\displaystyle= ⟨ ( q − 1 ) 2 ⟩ ​ ⟨ ( W − ⟨ W ⟩ ) 2 ⟩ , \\displaystyle\\langle(q-1)^{2}\\rangle\\langle(W-\\langle W\\rangle)^{2}\\rangle, (16) ≥ \\displaystyle\\geq ⟨ ( q − 1 ) ​ ( W − ⟨ W ⟩ ) ⟩ 2 ; \\displaystyle\\langle(q-1)(W-\\langle W\\rangle)\\rangle^{2}; ≥ \\displaystyle\\geq ( ⟨ q ​ W ⟩ − ⟨ W ⟩ ) 2 , \\displaystyle\\left(\\langle qW\\rangle-\\langle W\\rangle\\right)^{2}, Now since ⟨ q ​ W ⟩ = ∑ x r x / b x ​ log ⁡ ( b x / r x ) = − D ⁡ ( r | b ) ≤ 0 \\langle qW\\rangle=\\sum_{x}r_{x}/b_{x}\\log(b_{x}/r_{x})=-D(r|b)\\leq 0 , then Eq.( 15 ) follows. This inequality is saturated when b x = r x b_{x}=r_{x} , which corresponds to the null strategy. Similar inequalities can be derived using instead other relevant Kullback-Leibler divergences, such as D ⁡ ( b | p ) D(b|p) or D ⁡ ( r | p ) D(r|p) . To exploit the first divergence, we introduce the ratio s x = b x / p x s_{x}=b_{x}/p_{x} which is also a normalized probability distribution similar to q q , with a second moment σ s 2 \\sigma_{s}^{2} . Then, following the same steps, we obtain an inequality for the quantity I I introduced in Eq (3) : σ I ≥ ⟨ I ⟩ σ s , \\sigma_{I}\\geq\\frac{\\langle I\\rangle}{\\sigma_{s}}, (17) which is saturated when b x = p x b_{x}=p_{x} , i.e. for Kelly’s strategy. To exploit the second divergence, we now use the quantity W ~ \\tilde{W} , and we obtain the inequality σ W ~ ≥ ⟨ W ~ ⟩ σ q , \\sigma_{\\tilde{W}}\\geq\\frac{\\langle\\tilde{W}\\rangle}{\\sigma_{q}}, (18) which is saturated when p x = r x p_{x}=r_{x} . Note that Eqs. 17 and 18 represent new bounds which complement the inequalities ⟨ I ⟩ ≥ 0 \\langle I\\rangle\\geq 0 and ⟨ W ~ ⟩ ≥ 0 \\langle\\tilde{W}\\rangle\\geq 0 obtained previously. VII Phase transition in optimal strategies In order to prove that there are no tighter bounds of this type, we carry out a perturbation calculation near the null strategy using the vector ϵ x \\epsilon_{x} o x ​ b x = b x r x = 1 + ε x . o_{x}b_{x}=\\frac{b_{x}}{r_{x}}=1+\\varepsilon_{x}. (19) To ensure that b → \\vec{b} is still a probability measure, we require that the column vector ε → = ( ε x ) x \\vec{\\varepsilon}=(\\varepsilon_{x})_{x} lies on the hyperplane ( r → , ε → ) = ∑ x r x ​ ε x = 0 (\\vec{r},\\vec{\\varepsilon})=\\sum_{x}r_{x}\\varepsilon_{x}=0 . By evaluating ⟨ W ⟩ \\langle W\\rangle and σ W \\sigma_{W} to first order in ε → \\vec{\\varepsilon} , we find that σ W ∼ ⟨ W ⟩ / γ c \\sigma_{W}\\sim\\langle W\\rangle/\\gamma_{c} , with γ c = σ q , \\gamma_{c}=\\sigma_{q}, (20) an expression which we can be checked by plotting a zoom of the Pareto border near the null strategy SM . The evaluation of the second order derivative at the null strategy on the Pareto border requires a calculation to second order in ε → \\vec{\\varepsilon} , which gives d 2 ​ σ W d ​ ⟨ W ⟩ 2 | γ = γ c = C γ c 5 , \\left.\\frac{d^{2}\\sigma_{W}}{d\\langle W\\r