{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2006.11909v2.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 165939, "equations": [], "text": "Two-Sample Testing on Ranked Preference Data\nand the Role of Modeling Assumptions\nCharvi Rastogi\nSivaraman Balakrishnan\nNihar Shah\nAarti Singh\nMachine Learning Department\nDepartment of Statistics\nComputer Science DepartmentCarnegie Mellon UniversityEmail:\n{crastogi@cs, siva@stat, nihars@cs, aarti@cs}.cmu.edu\nAbstract\nA number of applications require two-sample testing on ranked preference data. For instance, in crowdsourcing, there is a long-standing question of whether pairwise-comparison data provided by people is distributed identically to ratings-converted-to-comparisons. Other applications include sports data analysis and peer grading. In this paper, we design two-sample tests for pairwise-comparison data and ranking data. For our two-sample test for pairwise-comparison data, we establish an upper bound on the sample complexity required to correctly test whether the distributions of the two sets of samples are identical. Our test requires essentially no assumptions on the distributions. We then prove complementary lower bounds showing that our results are tight (in the minimax sense) up to constant factors. We investigate the role of modeling assumptions by proving lower bounds for a range of pairwise-comparison models (WST, MST, SST, parameter-based such as BTL and Thurstone). We also provide tests and associated sample complexity bounds for the problem of two-sample testing with partial (or total) ranking data. Furthermore, we empirically evaluate our results via extensive simulations as well as three real-world data sets consisting of pairwise-comparisons and rankings. By applying our two-sample test on real-world pairwise-comparison data, we conclude that ratings and rankings provided by people are indeed distributed differently.\n1\nIntroduction\nData in the form of pairwise-comparisons, or more generally partial or total rankings, arises in a wide variety of settings. For instance, when eliciting data from people (say, in crowdsourcing), there is a long-standing debate over the difference between two methods of data collection: asking people to compare pairs of items or asking people to provide numeric scores to the items. A natural question here is whether people implicitly generate pairwise-comparisons using a fundamentally different mechanism than first forming numeric scores and then converting them to a comparison. Thus, we are interested in testing if the data obtained from pairwise-comparisons is distributed identically to if the numeric scores were converted to pairwise-comparisons\n(\nRaman and Joachims, 2014\n;\nShah et al., 2016\n)\n. As another example consider sports and online games, where a match between two players or two teams is a pairwise-comparison between them\n(\nHerbrich et al., 2007\n;\nHvattum and Arntzen, 2010\n;\nVan Der Maas and Wagenmakers, 2005\n)\n. Again, a natural question that arises here is whether the relative performance of the teams has changed significantly across a certain period of time (e.g., to design an appropriate rating system\n(\nCattelan et al., 2013\n)\n). A third example is peer grading where students are asked to compare pairs of homeworks\n(\nShah et al., 2013\n)\nor rank a batch of homeworks\n(\nLamon et al., 2016\n;\nRaman and Joachims, 2014\n)\n. A question of interest here is whether a certain group of students (female/senior/…) grade very differently as compared to another group (male/junior/…)\n(\nShah et al., 2018\n)\n. Additionally, consumer preferences as pairwise-comparisons or partial (or total) rankings can be used to investigate whether a certain group (married/old/…) make significantly different choices about purchasing products as opposed to another group (single/young/…)\n(\nCavagnaro and Davis-Stober, 2014\n;\nRegenwetter et al., 2011\n)\n.\nEach of the aforementioned problems involves two-sample testing, that is, testing whether the distribution of the data from two populations is identical or not. With this motivation, in this paper we consider the problem of two-sample testing on preference data in the form of pairwise-comparisons and, more generally, partial and total rankings. First, we focus our efforts on preference data in the form of pairwise-comparisons. Specifically, consider a collection of items (e.g., teams in a sports league). The data we consider comprises comparisons between pairs of these items, where the outcome of a comparison involves one of the items beating the other. In the two-sample testing problem, we have access to two sets of such pairwise-comparisons, obtained from two different sources (e.g., the current season in a sports league forming one set of pairwise-comparisons and the previous season forming a second set). The goal is to test whether the underlying distributions (winning probabilities) in the two sets of data are identical or different. Similarly, when the data comprises of partial or total rankings over a collection of items from two different sources, our goal is to test whether the distributions over total rankings for the two sources are identical or not. Specifically, we consider the case where a partial ranking is defined as a total ranking over some subset of the collection of items.\nContributions.\nWe now outline the contributions of this paper; the theoretical contributions for the pairwise-comparison setting are also summarized in Table\n1\n.\n•\nFirst, we present a test for two-sample testing with pairwise-comparison data and associated upper bounds on its minimax sample complexity. Our test makes essentially no assumptions on the outcome probabilities of the pairwise-comparisons.\n•\nSecond, we prove information-theoretic lower bounds on the critical testing radius for this problem. Our bounds show that our test is minimax optimal for this problem.\n•\nAs a third contribution, we investigate the role of modeling assumptions: What if one could assume one of the popular models (e.g., BTL, Thurstone, parameter-based, SST, MST, WST) for pairwise-comparison outcomes? We show that our test is minimax optimal under WST and MST models. We also provide an information-theoretic lower bound under the SST and parameter-based models. Conditioned on the planted clique hardness conjecture, we prove a computational lower bound for the SST model with a single observation per pair of items, which matches the sample complexity upper bound attained by our test, up to logarithmic factors.\n•\nFourth, we conduct experiments on two real-world pairwise-comparison data sets. Our test detects a statistically significant difference between the distributions of directly-elicited pairwise-comparisons and converting numeric scores to comparison data. On the other hand, from the data available for four European football leagues over two seasons, our test does not detect any statistically significant difference between the relative performance of teams across two consecutive seasons.\n•\nFinally, we present algorithms for two-sample testing on partial (or total) ranking data for two partial ranking models—namely, the Plackett-Luce model and a more general marginal probability based model. We provide upper bounds on sample complexity for the test for the Plackett-Luce model controlling both the Type I and Type II error. Moreover, our test for the marginal probability based model controls the Type I error. We apply our test to a real-world data set on sushi preferences. Our test finds a statistically significant difference in sushi preferences across sections of different demographics based on age, gender and region of residence.\nRelated literature.\nThe problem of two-sample testing on ranked preference data is at the intersection of two rich areas of research—two-sample testing and analyzing ranked preference data. The problem of two-sample testing has a long history in statistics, and classical tests include the t-test and Pearson’s\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ntest (see for instance\nLehmann and Romano, 2005\nand references therein). More recently, non-parametric tests\n(\nGretton et al., 2012a\n;\nGretton et al., 2012b\n;\nRosenbaum, 2005\n;\nKim et al., 2020b\n;\nSzekely and Rizzo, 2004\n)\nhave gained popularity but these can perform poorly in structured, high-dimensional settings.\nThe minimax perspective on hypothesis testing which we adopt in this work originates in the work of\nIngster, 1994\n(and was developed further in\nIngster, 1997\n;\nIngster and Suslina, 2003\n;\nIngster, 1994\n).\nSeveral recent works have studied the minimax rate for two-sample testing for high-dimensional multinomials\n(\nBalakrishnan and Wasserman, 2018\n;\nBalakrishnan and Wasserman, 2019\n;\nChan et al., 2014\n;\nValiant and Valiant, 2017\n;\nValiant, 2011\n)\n, and testing for sparsity in regression\n(\nCarpentier et al., 2018\n;\nCollier et al., 2017\n)\n, we build on some of these ideas in our work. We also note the work of\nMania et al., 2018\nwho propose a kernel-based two-sample test for distributions over total rankings.\nModel\n(\nℳ\n)\n(\\mathcal{M})\nUpper Bound\nLower Bound\nComputational Lower Bound\nModel-free\nfor\nk\n>\n1\nk>1\n,\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n≤\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}\\leq c\\dfrac{1}{kd}\n(Thm.\n1\n)\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\n​\n𝕀\n⁡\n(\nk\n>\n1\n)\nk\n​\nd\n+\n𝕀\n⁡\n(\nk\n=\n1\n)\n4\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>c\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}(k>1)}{kd}+\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}(k=1)}{4}\n(Prop.\n4\n)\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\n​\n𝕀\n⁡\n(\nk\n>\n1\n)\nk\n​\nd\n+\n𝕀\n⁡\n(\nk\n=\n1\n)\n4\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>c\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}(k>1)}{kd}+\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}(k=1)}{4}\nWST and MST\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n≤\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}\\leq c\\dfrac{1}{kd}\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>c\\dfrac{1}{kd}\n(Thm.\n3\n)\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>c\\dfrac{1}{kd}\nSST\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n≤\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}\\leq c\\dfrac{1}{kd}\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n3\n/\n2\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>c\\dfrac{1}{kd^{3/2}}\nfor\nk\n=\n1\nk=1\n,\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\nk\n​\nd\n​\n(\nlog\n⁡\nlog\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\n)\n2\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>\\dfrac{c}{kd(\\log\\log(d))^{2}}\n(Thm.\n6\n)\nParameter-based\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n≤\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}\\leq c\\dfrac{1}{kd}\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n3\n/\n2\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>c\\dfrac{1}{kd^{3/2}}\n(Thm.\n5\n)\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n3\n/\n2\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>c\\dfrac{1}{kd^{3/2}}\nTable 1\n:\nThis table summarizes our results for two-sample testing of pairwise-comparison data (introduced formally in Equation\n1\n), for common pairwise-comparison models. Here,\nd\nd\ndenotes the number of items, and we obtain\nk\nk\nsamples (comparisons) per pair of items from each of the two populations. In this work, we provide upper and lower bounds on the critical testing radius\nϵ\nℳ\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}\n, defined in (\n3\n). The upper bound in Theorem\n1\nis due to the test in Algorithm\n1\nwhich is computationally efficient. We note that the constant\nc\nc\nvaries from result to result.\nThe analysis of pairwise-comparison data is a rich field of study, dating back at least 90 years to the seminal work of Louis\nThurstone, 1927\nand subsequently\nBradley and Terry, 1952\nand\nLuce, 1959\n. Along with this,\nPlackett, 1975\nand\nLuce, 1959\nworked on the now-well-known Plackett-Luce model for partial and total rankings. In the past two decades, motivated by crowdsourcing and other applications, there is significant interest in studying such data in a high-dimensional setting, that is, where the number of items\nd\nd\nis not a fixed constant. A number of papers\n(\nShah et al., 2016\n;\nChen and Suh, 2015\n;\nNegahban et al., 2012\n;\nRajkumar and Agarwal, 2014\n;\nSzörényi et al., 2015\n;\nGuiver and Snelson, 2009\n;\nMaystre and Grossglauser, 2015\n, and references therein)\nin this space analyze parameter-based models such as the BTL and the Thurstone models for pairwise-comparison data and the Plackett-Luce model for partial (or total) ranking data. Here the goal is usually to estimate the parameters of the model or the underlying ranking of the items. The papers\n(\nAilon, 2012\n;\nBraverman and Mossel, 2008\n;\nChatterjee and Mukherjee, 2019\n;\nChen et al., 2018\n;\nFalahatgar et al., 2017\n;\nRajkumar et al., 2015\n, and references therein)\nalso study ranking from pairwise-comparisons, under some different assumptions.\nOf particular interest is the paper by\nAldous, 2017\nwhich uses the BTL model to make match predictions in sports, and also poses the question of analyzing the average change in the performance of teams over time. While this paper suggests some simple statistics to test for change, designing principled tests is left as an open problem. To this end, we provide a two-sample test without any assumptions and with rigorous guarantees, and also use it subsequently to conduct such a test on real-world data.\nA recent line of work\n(\nHeckel et al., 2019\n;\nShah et al., 2017\n;\nShah and Wainwright, 2018\n)\nfocuses on the role of the modeling assumptions in estimation and ranking from pairwise-comparisons. We study the role of modeling assumptions from the perspective of two-sample testing and prove performance guarantees for some pairwise-comparison models.\nOrganization.\nThe remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Section\n2\n, we formally describe the problem setup and provide some background on the minimax perspective on hypothesis testing. We also provide a detailed description of the pairwise-comparison models studied in this work. In Section\n3\nwe present our minimax optimal test for pairwise-comparison data and present the body of our main technical results for the pairwise-comparison setting with brief proof sketches and defer technical aspects of the proofs to Section\n6\n. Then, in Section\n4\nwe extend our results for the two-sample testing problem on partial (or total) ranking data. We describe two partial ranking models and provide testing algorithms and associated sample complexity bounds. The corresponding proofs are in Section\n6\n. In Section\n5\n, we present our findings from implementing our testing algorithms on three real-world data sets. Furthermore, we present results of simulations on synthetic data which validate our theoretical findings. We conclude with a discussion in Section\n7\n.\n2\nBackground and problem formulation for pairwise-comparison setting\nIn this section, we provide a more formal statement of the problem of two-sample testing using pairwise-comparison data along with background on hypothesis testing and the associated definition of risk, and various types of ranking models.\n2.1\nProblem statement\nOur focus in this paper is on the two-sample testing problem where the two sets of samples come from two potentially different populations. Here, we describe the model of the data we consider in our work. Specifically, consider a collection of\nd\nd\nitems. The two sets of samples comprise outcomes of comparisons between various pairs of these items. In the first set of samples, the outcomes are governed by an unknown matrix\nP\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP\\in[0,1]^{d\\times d}\n. The\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\nth\n(i,j)^{\\text{th}}\nentry of matrix\nP\nP\nis denoted as\np\ni\n​\nj\np_{ij}\n, and any comparison between items\ni\ni\nand\nj\nj\nresults in\ni\ni\nbeating\nj\nj\nwith probability\np\ni\n​\nj\np_{ij}\n, independent of other outcomes. We assume there are no ties. Analogously, the second set of samples comprises outcomes of pairwise-comparisons between the\nd\nd\nitems governed by a (possibly different) unknown matrix\nQ\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nd\n×\nd\nQ\\in[0,1]^{d\\times d}\n, wherein item\ni\ni\nbeats item\nj\nj\nwith probability\nq\ni\n​\nj\nq_{ij}\n, the\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\nth\n(i,j)^{\\text{th}}\nentry of matrix\nQ\nQ\n. For any pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nof items, we let\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\nk^{p}_{ij}\nand\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{q}_{ij}\ndenote the number of times a pair of items\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nis compared in the first and second set of samples respectively. Let\nX\ni\n​\nj\nX_{ij}\ndenote the number of times item\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\ni\\in[d]\nbeats item\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nj\\in[d]\nin the first set of samples, and let\nY\ni\n​\nj\nY_{ij}\ndenote the analogous quantity in the second set of samples. It follows that\nX\ni\n​\nj\nX_{ij}\nand\nY\ni\n​\nj\nY_{ij}\nare Binomial random variables independently distributed as\nX\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\np\ni\n​\nj\n)\nX_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k^{p}_{ij},p_{ij})\nand\nY\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nY_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k^{q}_{ij},q_{ij})\n. We adopt the convention of setting\nX\ni\n​\nj\n=\n0\nX_{ij}=0\nwhen\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\n0\nk^{p}_{ij}=0\n, and\nY\ni\n​\nj\n=\n0\nY_{ij}=0\nwhen\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n=\n0\nk^{q}_{ij}=0\n, and\nk\ni\n​\ni\np\n=\nk\ni\n​\ni\nq\n=\n0\nk^{p}_{ii}=k^{q}_{ii}=0\n.\nOur results apply to both the symmetric and asymmetric settings of pairwise-comparisons:\nSymmetric setting:\nThe literature on the analysis of pairwise-comparison data frequently considers a symmetric setting where “\ni\ni\nvs.\nj\nj\n” and “\nj\nj\nvs.\ni\ni\n” have an identical meaning. Our results apply to this setting, for which we impose the additional constraints that\np\nj\n​\ni\n=\n1\n−\np\ni\n​\nj\np_{ji}=1-p_{ij}\nand\nq\nj\n​\ni\n=\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\nq_{ji}=1-q_{ij}\nfor all\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n2\n(i,j)\\in[d]^{2}\n. In addition, for every\n1\n≤\ni\n≤\nj\n≤\nd\n1\\leq i\\leq j\\leq d\n, we set\nk\nj\n​\ni\np\n=\nk\nj\n​\ni\nq\n=\n0\nk^{p}_{ji}=k^{q}_{ji}=0\n(and hence\nX\nj\n​\ni\n=\nY\nj\n​\ni\n=\n0\nX_{ji}=Y_{ji}=0\n), and let\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n,\nX\ni\n​\nj\nk^{p}_{ij},\\;k^{q}_{ij},\\;X_{ij}\nand\nY\ni\n​\nj\nY_{ij}\nrepresent the comparisons between the pair of items\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n.\nAsymmetric setting:\nOur results also apply to an asymmetric setting where “\ni\ni\nvs.\nj\nj\n” may have a different meaning as compared to “\nj\nj\nvs.\ni\ni\n”. For instance, in a setting of sports where “\ni\ni\nvs.\nj\nj\n” could indicate\ni\ni\nas the home team and\nj\nj\nas the visiting team. This setting does not impose the restrictions described in the symmetric setting above.\nHypothesis test.\nConsider any class\nℳ\n\\mathcal{M}\nof pairwise-comparison probability matrices, and any parameter\nϵ\n>\n0\n\\epsilon>0\n. Then,the goal is to test the hypotheses\nH\n0\n:\nP\n=\nQ\nH\n1\n:\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n≥\nϵ\n,\n\\displaystyle\\begin{split}H_{0}&:P=Q\\\\\nH_{1}&:\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}\\geq\\epsilon,\\end{split}\n(1)\nwhere\nP\n,\nQ\n∈\nℳ\nP,Q\\in\\mathcal{M}\n.\n2.2\nHypothesis testing and risk\nWe now provide a brief background on hypothesis tests and associated terminology. In hypothesis testing, the Type I error is defined as the probability of rejecting the null hypothesis\nH\n0\nH_{0}\nwhen the null hypothesis\nH\n0\nH_{0}\nis actually true, an upper bound on the Type I error is denoted by\nα\n\\alpha\n; the Type II error is defined as the probability of failing to reject the null when the alternate hypothesis\nH\n1\nH_{1}\nis actually true, an upper bound on Type II error is denoted by\nβ\n\\beta\n. The performance of the testing algorithm is evaluated by measuring its Type I error and its power, which is defined as one minus the Type II error.\nConsider the hypothesis testing problem defined in (\n1\n). We define a test\nϕ\n\\phi\nas\nϕ\n:\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n,\nX\ni\n​\nj\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\n}\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n2\n↦\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\n\\phi:\\{k^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij},X_{ij},Y_{ij}\\}_{(i,j)\\in[d]^{2}}\\mapsto\\{0,1\\}\n. Let\nℙ\n0\n\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nand\nℙ\n1\n\\mathbb{P}_{1}\ndenote the distribution of the input variables under the null and under the alternate respectively. Here, we assume that the variables\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\nk^{p}_{ij}{}\nand\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{q}_{ij}{}\nare fixed for all\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n2\n]\n(i,j)\\in[d^{2}]\n. Let\nℳ\n0\n\\mathcal{M}_{0}\nand\nℳ\n1\n​\n(\nϵ\n)\n\\mathcal{M}_{1}(\\epsilon)\ndenote the set of matrix pairs\n(\nP\n,\nQ\n)\n(P,Q)\nthat satisfy the null condition and the alternate condition in (\n1\n) respectively. Then, we define the minimax risk\n(\nIngster, 1994\n;\nIngster, 1997\n;\nIngster and Suslina, 2003\n;\nIngster, 1994\n)\nas\nℛ\nℳ\n=\ninf\nϕ\n{\nsup\n(\nP\n,\nQ\n)\n∈\nℳ\n0\nℙ\n0\n​\n(\nϕ\n=\n1\n)\n+\nsup\n(\nP\n,\nQ\n)\n∈\nℳ\n1\n​\n(\nϵ\n)\nℙ\n1\n​\n(\nϕ\n=\n0\n)\n}\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{R}_{\\mathcal{M}}=\\inf_{\\phi}\\{\\sup_{(P,Q)\\in\\mathcal{M}_{0}}\\mathbb{P}_{0}(\\phi=1)+\\sup_{(P,Q)\\in\\mathcal{M}_{1}(\\epsilon)}\\mathbb{P}_{1}(\\phi=0)\\},\n(2)\nwhere the infimum is over all\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\n\\{0,1\\}\n-valued tests\nϕ\n\\phi\n. It is common to study the minimax risk via a coarse lens by studying instead the critical radius or the minimax separation. The critical radius is the smallest value\nϵ\n\\epsilon\nfor which a hypothesis test has non-trivial power to distinguish the null from the alternate. Formally, we define the critical radius as\nϵ\nℳ\n=\ninf\n{\nϵ\n:\nℛ\nℳ\n≤\n1\n/\n3\n}\n.\n\\displaystyle\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}=\\inf\\{\\epsilon:\\mathcal{R}_{\\mathcal{M}}\\leq 1/3\\}.\n(3)\nThe constant\n1\n/\n3\n1/3\nis arbitrary; we could use any specified constant in\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n(0,1)\n. In this paper, we focus on providing tight bounds on the critical radius.\n2.3\nA range of pairwise-comparison models\nA model for the pairwise-comparison probabilities is a set of matrices in\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nd\n×\nd\n[0,1]^{d\\times d}\n. In the context of our problem setting, assuming a model means that the matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\nare guaranteed to be drawn from this set. In this paper, the proposed test and the associated guarantees\ndo not\nmake any assumptions on the pairwise-comparison probability matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\n. In other words, we allow\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\nto be any arbitrary matrices in\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nd\n×\nd\n[0,1]^{d\\times d}\n. However, there are a number of models which are popular in the literature on pairwise-comparisons, and we provide a brief overview of them here. We analyze the role of these modeling assumptions in our two-sample testing problem.\nIn what follows, we let\nM\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nd\n×\nd\nM\\in[0,1]^{d\\times d}\ndenote a generic pairwise-comparison probability matrix, with\nM\ni\n​\nj\nM_{ij}\nrepresenting the probability that item\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\ni\\in[d]\nbeats item\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nj\\in[d]\n. The models impose conditions on the matrix\nM\nM\n.\n•\nParameter-based models:\nA parameter-based model is associated with some known, non-decreasing function\nf\n:\nℝ\n→\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nf:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow[0,1]\nsuch that\nf\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n=\n1\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\n−\nθ\n)\n​\n∀\nθ\n∈\nℝ\nf(\\theta)=1-f(-\\theta)~~~\\forall\\ \\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}\n. We refer to any such function\nf\nf\nas being “valid”. The parameter-based model associated to a given valid function\nf\nf\nis given by\nM\ni\n​\nj\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nw\ni\n−\nw\nj\n)\nfor all pairs\n​\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle M_{ij}=f(w_{i}-w_{j})\\quad\\textnormal{for all pairs }\\;(i,j),\n(4)\nfor some unknown vector\nw\n∈\nℝ\nd\nw\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nthat represents the notional qualities of the\nd\nd\nitems. It is typically assumed that the vector\nw\nw\nsatisfies the conditions\n∑\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nw\ni\n=\n0\n\\sum_{i\\in[d]}w_{i}=0\nand that\n‖\nw\n‖\n∞\n\\|w\\|_{\\infty}\nis bounded above by a known constant.\n–\nBradley-Terry-Luce (BTL) model:\nThis is a specific parameter-based model with\nf\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n=\n1\n1\n+\ne\n−\nθ\nf(\\theta)=\\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-\\theta}}\n.\n–\nThurstone model:\nThis is a specific parameter-based model with\nf\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n=\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\nf(\\theta)=\\Phi(\\theta)\n, where\nΦ\n\\Phi\nis the standard Gaussian CDF.\n•\nStrong stochastic transitivity (SST):\nThe model assumes that the set of items\n[\nd\n]\n[d]\nis endowed with an unknown total ordering\nπ\n\\pi\n, where\nπ\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n<\nπ\n⁡\n(\nj\n)\n\\pi(i)<\\pi(j)\nimplies that item\ni\ni\nis preferred to item\nj\nj\n. A matrix\nM\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nd\n×\nd\nM\\in[0,1]^{d\\times d}\nis said to follow the SST model if it satisfies the shifted-skew-symmetry condition\nM\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n−\nM\nj\n​\ni\nM_{ij}=1-M_{ji}\nfor every pair\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\ni,j\\in[d]\nand the condition\nM\ni\n​\nℓ\n≥\nM\nj\n​\nℓ\n​\nfor every\n​\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n​\nsuch that\n​\nπ\n​\n(\ni\n)\n<\nπ\n⁡\n(\nj\n)\n​\nand for every\n​\nℓ\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle M_{i\\ell}\\geq M_{j\\ell}\\;\\textnormal{ for every }\\;i,j\\in[d]\\textnormal{ such that }\\pi(i)<\\pi(j)\\text{ and for every }\\ell\\in[d].\n(5)\n•\nModerate stochastic transitivity (MST):\nThe model assumes that the set of items\n[\nd\n]\n[d]\nis endowed with an unknown total ordering\nπ\n\\pi\n. A matrix\nM\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nd\n×\nd\nM\\in[0,1]^{d\\times d}\nis said to follow the MST model if it satisfies\nM\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n−\nM\nj\n​\ni\nM_{ij}=1-M_{ji}\nfor every pair\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\ni,j\\in[d]\nand the condition\nM\ni\n​\nℓ\n≥\nmin\n⁡\n{\nM\ni\n​\nj\n,\nM\nj\n​\nℓ\n}\n​\nfor every\n​\ni\n,\nj\n,\nℓ\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n​\nsuch that\n​\nπ\n​\n(\ni\n)\n<\nπ\n⁡\n(\nj\n)\n<\nπ\n⁡\n(\nℓ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle M_{i\\ell}\\geq\\min\\{M_{ij},M_{j\\ell}\\}\\;\\textnormal{ for every }\\;i,j,\\ell\\in[d]\\;\\textnormal{ such that }\\pi(i)<\\pi(j)<\\pi(\\ell).\n(6)\n•\nWeak stochastic transitivity (WST):\nThe model assumes that the set of items\n[\nd\n]\n[d]\nis endowed with an unknown total ordering\nπ\n\\pi\n. A matrix\nM\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nd\n×\nd\nM\\in[0,1]^{d\\times d}\nis said to follow the WST model if it satisfies\nM\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n−\nM\nj\n​\ni\nM_{ij}=1-M_{ji}\nfor every pair\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\ni,j\\in[d]\nand the condition\nM\ni\n​\nj\n≥\n1\n2\n​\nfor every\n​\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n​\nsuch that\n​\nπ\n​\n(\ni\n)\n<\nπ\n⁡\n(\nj\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle M_{ij}\\geq\\frac{1}{2}\\;\\textnormal{ for every }\\;i,j\\in[d]\\;\\textnormal{ such that }\\pi(i)<\\pi(j).\n(7)\nModel hierarchy:\nThere is a hierarchy between these models, that is, {BTL, Thurstone}\n⊂\n\\subset\nparameter-based\n⊂\n\\subset\nSST\n⊂\n\\subset\nMST\n⊂\n\\subset\nWST\n⊂\n\\subset\nmodel-free\n3\nMain results for pairwise-comparison setting\nWe now present our main theoretical results for pairwise-comparison data.\n3.1\nTest and guarantees\nOur first result provides an algorithm for two-sample testing in the problem (\n1\n), and associated upper bounds on its sample complexity. Importantly, we do not make any modeling assumptions on the probability matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\n. First we consider a per-pair fixed-design setup in Theorem\n1\nwhere for every pair of items\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n, the sample sizes\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij}{},k^{q}_{ij}\nare equal to\nk\nk\n. Following that, in Corollary\n2\n, we consider a random-design setup wherein for every pair of items\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n, the sample sizes\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\nare drawn i.i.d. from some distribution\n𝒟\n{\\cal D}\nsupported over non-negative integers.\nInput\n: Samples\nX\ni\n​\nj\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\nX_{ij},Y_{ij}\ndenoting the number of times item\ni\ni\nbeat item\nj\nj\nin the observed\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\npairwise-comparisons from populations denoted by probability matrices\nP\n,\nQ\nP,Q\nrespectively.\nTest Statistic\n:\nT\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nX\ni\n​\nj\n2\n−\nX\ni\n​\nj\n)\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nY\ni\n​\nj\n2\n−\nY\ni\n​\nj\n)\n−\n2\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\nX\ni\n​\nj\n​\nY\ni\n​\nj\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n\\displaystyle\\hskip-17.07182ptT=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\mathbb{I}_{ij}\\dfrac{k^{q}_{ij}(k^{q}_{ij}-1)(X_{ij}^{2}-X_{ij})+k^{p}_{ij}(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(Y_{ij}^{2}-Y_{ij})-2(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(k^{q}_{ij}-1)X_{ij}Y_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(k^{q}_{ij}-1)(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})}\n(8)\nwhere\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n=\n𝕀\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n>\n1\n)\n×\n𝕀\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n>\n1\n)\n\\mathbb{I}_{ij}=\\mathbb{I}(k^{p}_{ij}>1)\\times\\mathbb{I}(k^{q}_{ij}>1)\n.\nOutput\n: If\nT\n≥\n11\n​\nd\nT\\geq 11d\n, where\n11\n​\nd\n11d\nis the threshold, then reject the null.\nAlgorithm 1\nTwo-sample test with pairwise-comparisons for model-free setting\nOur test is presented in Algorithm\n1\n. The test statistic (\n8\n) is designed such that it has an expected value of zero under the null and a large expected value under the alternate. The following theorem characterizes the performance of this test, thereby establishing an upper bound on the sample complexity of this two-sample testing problem in a random-design setting.\nTheorem 1\n.\nConsider the testing problem in (\n1\n) with\nℳ\n\\mathcal{M}\nas the class of all pairwise probability matrices. Suppose the number of (per pair) comparisons between the two populations is fixed,\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n=\nk\nk^{p}_{ij}=k^{q}_{ij}=k\n(for all\ni\n≠\nj\ni\\neq j\nin the asymmetric setting and all\ni\n<\nj\ni<j\nin the symmetric setting).\nThere is a constant\nc\n>\n0\nc>0\nsuch that for any\nϵ\n>\n0\n\\epsilon>0\n, if\nk\n>\n1\nk>1\nand\nϵ\n2\n≥\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n\\epsilon^{2}\\geq c\\dfrac{1}{kd}\n, then the sum of Type I error and Type II error of Algorithm\n1\nis at most\n1\n3\n\\frac{1}{3}\n.\nThe proof is provided in Section\n6.2\n. Theorem\n1\nprovides a guarantee of correctly distinguishing between the null and the alternate with probability at least\n2\n3\n\\frac{2}{3}\n. The value\n2\n3\n\\frac{2}{3}\nis closely tied to the specific threshold used in the test above. More generally, for any specified constant\nν\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\nu\\in(0,1)\n, the test achieves a probability of error at most\nν\n\\nu\nby setting the threshold as\nd\n​\n24\n​\n(\n2\n−\nν\n)\nν\nd\\sqrt{\\dfrac{24(2-\\nu)}{\\nu}}\n, with the same order of sample complexity as in Theorem\n1\n. Moreover, if the sample complexity is increased by some factor\nR\nR\n, then running Algorithm\n1\non\nR\nR\nindependent instances of the data and taking the majority answer results in error probability that decreases exponentially with\nR\nR\nas\n(\nexp\n⁡\n(\n−\n2\n​\nR\n)\n)\n(\\exp(-2R))\n, while the sample complexity increases only linearly in\nR\nR\n. One can thus have a very small probability of error of, for instance,\nd\n−\n50\nd^{-50}\nwith\nk\n=\nO\n~\n​\n(\n1\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n)\nk=\\widetilde{O}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{d\\epsilon^{2}}\\right)\n. Later, in Proposition\n4\n, we show that under the fixed\nk\nk\ncondition,\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n=\nk\nk^{p}_{ij}{}=k^{q}_{ij}{}=k\n, we have that\nk\n>\n1\nk>1\nis necessary for our two-sample testing problem. It is also interesting to note that the estimation rate to test the hypotheses in (\n1\n) is\nk\n=\nO\n⁡\n(\nlog\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\nϵ\n2\n)\nk=O\\left(\\frac{\\log(d)}{\\epsilon^{2}}\\right)\nwhile the rate for our testing algorithm is\nk\n=\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n)\nk=O\\left(\\frac{1}{d\\epsilon^{2}}\\right)\n.\nNow, we consider the random-design setup wherein for every pair of items\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n, the sample sizes\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\nare drawn i.i.d. from some distribution\n𝒟\n{\\cal D}\nsupported over non-negative integers. Let\nμ\n\\mu\nand\nσ\n\\sigma\ndenote the mean and standard deviation of distribution\n𝒟\n{\\cal D}\nrespectively, and let\np\n1\n≔\nPr\nZ\n∼\n𝒟\n​\n(\nZ\n=\n1\n)\np_{1}\\coloneqq\\textnormal{Pr}_{Z\\sim{\\cal D}}(Z=1)\n. We assume that\n𝒟\n{\\cal D}\nhas a finite mean and that\nμ\n≥\nc\n1\n​\np\n1\n;\nμ\n≥\nc\n2\n​\nσ\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mu\\geq c_{1}p_{1};\\quad\\mu\\geq c_{2}\\sigma,\n(9)\nfor some constants\nc\n1\n>\n1\nc_{1}>1\nand\nc\n2\n>\n1\nc_{2}>1\n.\nMany commonly occurring distributions obey these properties, for instance, Binomial distribution, Poisson distribution, geometric distribution and discrete uniform distribution, with appropriately chosen parameters.\nCorollary 2\n.\nConsider the testing problem in (\n1\n) with\nℳ\n\\mathcal{M}\nas the class of all pairwise probability matrices. Suppose the number of comparisons in the two populations\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\nare drawn i.i.d. from some distribution\n𝒟\n{\\cal D}\nthat satisfies (\n9\n)  (for all\ni\n≠\nj\ni\\neq j\nin the asymmetric setting and all\ni\n<\nj\ni<j\nin the symmetric setting).\nThere is a constant\nc\n>\n0\nc>0\nsuch that if\nϵ\n2\n≥\nc\n​\nmax\n⁡\n{\n1\nμ\n​\nd\n,\n1\nd\n2\n}\n\\epsilon^{2}\\geq c\\max\\{\\dfrac{1}{\\mu d},\\dfrac{1}{d^{2}}\\}\n, then the sum of Type I error and Type II error of Algorithm\n1\nis at most\n1\n3\n\\frac{1}{3}\n.\nThe proof of Corollary\n2\nis in Section\n6.1\n. In Corollary\n2\n, we see that the even under the random-design setup, our test achieves the same testing rate as in the per-pair fixed-design setup considered in Theorem\n1\n, for\nμ\n≤\nd\n\\mu\\leq d\n.\nWe now evaluate the performance of Algorithm\n1\nwhen\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\nare drawn i.i.d. from one of the following commonly occurring distributions. Consider any arbitrary matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\n. We specialise Corollary\n2\nto these distributions by stating the sample complexity that guarantees that the probability of error is at most\n1\n3\n\\frac{1}{3}\nin the two-sample testing problem (\n1\n), wherein constant\nc\nc\nmay depend on\nc\n1\n,\nc\n2\nc_{1},c_{2}\nfor each distribution. Note that, as in Corollary\n2\n, we assume\nϵ\n2\n​\nd\n2\n≥\nc\n′\n\\epsilon^{2}d^{2}\\geq c^{\\prime}\nwhere\nc\n′\nc^{\\prime}\nis some positive constant\n•\nBinomial distribution (\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\nBin\n​\n(\nn\n,\na\n)\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}\\textnormal{Bin}(n,a)\n) : Sufficient condition\nn\n≥\nc\n​\nmax\n⁡\n{\n1\na\n​\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n,\n1\na\n}\nn\\geq c\\max\\{\\dfrac{1}{ad\\epsilon^{2}},\\dfrac{1}{a}\\}\n.\n•\nPoisson distribution (\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\nPoisson\n​\n(\nλ\n)\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}\\textnormal{Poisson}(\\lambda)\n) : Sufficient condition\nλ\n≥\nc\n​\nmax\n⁡\n{\n1\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n,\n1\n}\n\\lambda\\geq c\\max\\{\\dfrac{1}{d\\epsilon^{2}},1\\}\n.\n•\nGeometric Distribution (\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\nGeometric\n​\n(\na\n)\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}\\textnormal{Geometric}(a)\n): Sufficient condition\n1\na\n≥\nc\n​\nmax\n⁡\n{\n1\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n,\n1\n}\n\\dfrac{1}{a}\\geq c\\max\\{\\dfrac{1}{d\\epsilon^{2}},1\\}\n.\n•\nDiscrete Uniform Distribution (\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\nUnif\n​\n(\n0\n,\nn\n)\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}\\textnormal{Unif}(0,n)\n): Sufficient condition\nn\n≥\nc\n​\nmax\n⁡\n{\n1\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n,\n1\n}\nn\\geq c\\max\\{\\dfrac{1}{d\\epsilon^{2}},1\\}\n.\nNext, we note that a sharper but non-explicit threshold in Algorithm\n1\ncan be obtained using the permutation test method to control the Type I error. We detail this approach in Algorithm\n2\n.\nInput\n: Samples\nX\ni\n​\nj\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\nX_{ij},Y_{ij}\ndenoting the number of times item\ni\ni\nbeat item\nj\nj\nin the observed\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\npairwise-comparisons from populations denoted by probability matrices\nP\n,\nQ\nP,Q\nrespectively. Significance level\nα\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\alpha\\in(0,1)\n. Iteration count\nγ\n\\gamma\n.\n(1)\nCompute the test statistic\nT\nT\ndefined in (\n8\n).\n(2)\nFor\nℓ\n←\n1\n​\nto\n​\nγ\n\\ell\\leftarrow 1\\text{ to }\\gamma\n:\n(i)\nRepeat this step independently for all\ni\n≠\nj\ni\\neq j\nin the asymmetric setting and for\nall\ni\n<\nj\ni<j\nin the symmetric setting. Collect the\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})\nsamples together and\nreassign\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\nk^{p}_{ij}\nof the samples chosen uniformly at random to\nP\nP\nand the rest to\nQ\nQ\n.\nCompute the new values of\nX\ni\n​\nj\nX_{ij}\nand\nY\ni\n​\nj\nY_{ij}\nbased on this reassignment.\n(ii)\nUsing the new values of\nX\ni\n​\nj\nX_{ij}\nand\nY\ni\n​\nj\nY_{ij}\n, recompute the test statistic in (\n8\n).\nDenote the computed test statistic as\nT\nℓ\nT_{\\ell}\n.\nOutput\n: Reject the null if\np\n=\n∑\nℓ\n=\n1\nγ\n1\nγ\n​\n𝟙\n​\n(\nT\nℓ\n−\nT\n)\n<\nα\np=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{\\gamma}\\frac{1}{\\gamma}\\mathbbm{1}(T_{\\ell}-T)<\\alpha\n.\nAlgorithm 2\nPermutation test with pairwise-comparisons for model-free setting.\nMore generally, the results in Theorem\n1\nand Corollary\n2\n(and the following converse results in Theorem\n3\nand Proposition\n4\n) also apply to the two-sample testing problem of comparing two Bernoulli matrices (or vectors)\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\n, wherein each entry of the matrices (or vectors) is a Bernoulli parameter. In this problem, we want to test whether two Bernoulli matrices are identical or not, and we have access to some observations of some (or all) of the underlying Bernoulli random variables.\nWe conclude this section with a proof sketch for Theorem\n1\n; the complete proof is provided in Section\n6.1\nand\n6.2\n.\nProof Sketch for Theorem\n1\n.\nThe test statistic\nT\nT\nis designed to ensure that\n𝔼\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\n=\n0\n\\mathbb{E}_{H_{0}}[T]=0\nfor any arbitrary pairwise probability matrices\nP\n,\nQ\nP,Q\nsuch that\nP\n=\nQ\nP=Q\n, and for any values of\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n}\n1\n≤\ni\n,\nj\n≤\nd\n\\{k^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\}_{1\\leq i,j\\leq d}\n. We lower bound the expected value of\nT\nT\nunder the alternate hypothesis as\n𝔼\nH\n1\n​\n[\nT\n]\n≥\nc\n​\nk\n​\nd\n2\n​\nϵ\n2\n\\mathbb{E}_{H_{1}}[T]\\geq ckd^{2}\\epsilon^{2}\n(Lemma\n10\n). Next, we show that the variance of\nT\nT\nis upper bounded under the null by\n24\n​\nd\n2\n24d^{2}\nand under the alternate by\n24\n​\nd\n2\n+\n4\n​\nk\n​\nd\n2\n​\nϵ\n2\n24d^{2}+4kd^{2}\\epsilon^{2}\n(Lemma\n11\n). These lemmas allow us to choose a suitable threshold value of\n11\n​\nd\n11d\n. Finally, using Chebyshev’s inequality comparing the square of expectation with the variance, we obtain the desired upper bound on the sample complexity with guarantees on both Type I and Type II errors.\n3.2\nConverse results and the role of modeling assumptions\nIn this section we look at the role of modeling assumptions on the pairwise-comparison probability matrices in the two-sample testing problem in (\n1\n).\nLower bound for MST, WST, and model-free classes.\nHaving established an upper bound on the rate of two-sample testing without modeling assumptions on the pairwise-comparison probability matrices\nP\n,\nQ\nP,Q\n, we show matching lower bounds that hold under the MST class. The WST and model-free classes are both supersets of MST, and hence the following guarantees automatically apply to them as well.\nTheorem 3\n.\nConsider the testing problem in (\n1\n) with\nℳ\n\\mathcal{M}\nas the class of matrices described by the MST model. Suppose we have\nk\nk\ncomparisons for each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nfrom each population. There exists a constant\nc\n>\n0\nc>0\n, such that the critical radius\nϵ\nℳ\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}\nis lower bounded as\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\nk\n​\nd\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>\\dfrac{c}{kd}\n.\nThe lower bound on the rate matches the rate derived for Algorithm\n1\nin Theorem\n1\n, thereby establishing the minimax optimality of our algorithm (up to constant factors). The MST class is a subset of the WST model class. This proves that Algorithm\n1\nis simultaneously minimax optimal under the MST and WST modeling assumptions in addition to the model-free setting. We provide a proof sketch for Theorem\n3\nin Section\n3.2.1\n; the complete proof is in Section\n6.3.3\n.\nNecessity of\nμ\n>\np\n1\n\\mu>p_{1}\n.\nRecall that the upper bound derived in Theorem\n1\nunder the model-free setting holds under the assumption that\nk\n>\n1\nk>1\nand, similarly, Corollary\n2\nholds under the assumption that\nμ\n≥\nc\n1\n​\np\n1\n\\mu\\geq c_{1}p_{1}\nwith\nc\n1\n>\n1\nc_{1}>1\n, as stated in (\n9\n). We now state a negative result for the case\nμ\n≤\np\n1\n\\mu\\leq p_{1}\n, which implies that\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n≤\n1\n​\n∀\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\leq 1\\,\\forall\\,(i,j)\nunder the random-design setup and\nk\n≤\n1\nk\\leq 1\nunder the per-pair fixed-design setup.\nProposition 4\n.\nConsider the testing problem in (\n1\n) with\nℳ\n\\mathcal{M}\nas the class of all pairwise probability matrices. Suppose we have at most one comparison for each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nfrom each population  (for all\ni\n≠\nj\ni\\neq j\nin the asymmetric setting and all\ni\n<\nj\ni<j\nin the symmetric setting). Then, for any value of\nϵ\n≤\n1\n2\n\\epsilon\\leq\\frac{1}{2}\n, the minimax risk defined in (\n2\n) is at least\n1\n2\n\\frac{1}{2}\n, thus,\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n≥\n1\n4\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}\\geq\\frac{1}{4}\n.\nWe provide some intuition for this result here. If\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n≤\n1\n​\n∀\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\nk^{p}_{ij}=k^{q}_{ij}\\leq 1\\,\\forall\\,(i,j)\n, then at best one has access to first order information of each entry of\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\n, that is, one has access to only\nPr\n⁡\n(\nX\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n,\nPr\n⁡\n(\nY\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n,\nPr\n⁡\n(\nX\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n\\Pr(X_{ij}=1),\\Pr(Y_{ij}=1),\\Pr(X_{ij}=1,Y_{ij}=1)\nfor each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n. This observation allows us to construct a case wherein the null and the alternate cannot be distinguished from each other by any test, due to the inaccessibility of higher order information of the underlying Bernoulli random variables. The complete proof is provided in Section\n6.3.2\n.\nLower bound for parameter-based class.\nWe now prove an information-theoretic lower bound for our two-sample testing problem wherein the probability matrices follow the parameter-based model.\nTheorem 5\n.\nConsider the testing problem in (\n1\n). Consider any arbitrary non-decreasing function\nf\n:\nℝ\n→\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nf:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow[0,1]\nsuch that\nf\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n=\n1\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\n−\nθ\n)\n​\n∀\nθ\n∈\nℝ\nf(\\theta)=1-f(-\\theta)~~\\forall\\ \\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}\n, with\nℳ\n\\mathcal{M}\nas the parameter-based class of probability matrices associated to the given function. Suppose we have\nk\nk\ncomparisons for each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nfrom each population. There exists a constant\nc\n>\n0\nc>0\n, such that the critical radius\nϵ\nℳ\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}\nis lower bounded as\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\nk\n​\nd\n3\n/\n2\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>\\dfrac{c}{kd^{3/2}}\n.\nThis lower bound also applies to probability matrices in the SST class described in (\n5\n). We provide a brief proof sketch in Section\n3.2.1\n; the complete proof is in Section\n6.3.4\n.\nComputational lower bound for SST class.\nGiven the polynomial gap between Theorem\n1\nand Theorem\n5\n, it is natural to wonder whether there is another polynomial-time testing algorithm for testing under the SST and/or parameter-based modeling assumption. We answer this question in the negative, for the SST model and single observation model (\nk\n=\n1\nk=1\n), conditionally on the average-case hardness of the planted clique problem\n(\nJerrum, 1992\n;\nKučera, 1995\n)\n. In informal terms, the planted clique conjecture asserts that there is no polynomial-time algorithm that can detect the presence of a planted clique of size\nκ\n=\no\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\n\\kappa=o(\\sqrt{d})\nin an Erdős-Rényi random graph with\nd\nd\nnodes. We construct SST matrices that are similar to matrices in the planted clique problem and as a direct consequence of the planted clique conjecture, we have the following result.\nTheorem 6\n.\nConsider the testing problem in (\n1\n) with\nℳ\n\\mathcal{M}\nas the class of matrices described by the SST model.\nSuppose the planted clique conjecture holds. Suppose we have one comparison for each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nfrom each population. Then there exists a constant\nc\n>\n0\nc>0\nsuch that for polynomial-time testing algorithms the critical radius\nϵ\nℳ\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}\nis lower bounded as\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\nd\n​\n(\nlog\n⁡\nlog\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\n)\n2\n\\epsilon_{\\mathcal{M}}^{2}>\\dfrac{c}{d(\\log\\log(d))^{2}}\n.\nThus, for\nk\n=\n1\nk=1\n, the computational lower bound on the testing rate for the SST model matches the rate derived for Algorithm\n1\n(up to logarithmic factors). The proof of Theorem\n6\nis provided in Section\n6.3.5\n. We devote the rest of this section to a sketch of the proofs of Theorem\n3\nand Theorem\n5\n.\n3.2.1\nProof sketches for Theorem\n3\nand Theorem\n5\nTo prove the information-theoretic lower bound under the different modeling assumptions, we construct a null and alternate belonging to the corresponding class of probability matrices. The bulk of our technical effort is devoted to upper bounding the chi-square divergence between the probability measure under the null and the alternate. We then invoke Le Cam’s lower bound for testing to obtain a lower bound on the minimax risk which gives us the information-theoretic lower bound. We now look at the constructions for the two modeling assumptions.\nLower bound construction for MST class\n(Section\n6.3.3\n). We construct a null and alternate such that under the null\nP\n=\nQ\n=\n[\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP=Q=[\\frac{1}{2}]^{d\\times d}\nand under the alternate\nP\n=\n[\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP=[\\frac{1}{2}]^{d\\times d}\nand\nQ\n∈\nΘ\nQ\\in\\Theta\nwith\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n=\nϵ\n\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}=\\epsilon\n. For this, we define a parameter\nη\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n2\n]\n\\eta\\in[0,\\frac{1}{2}]\nand then define\nΘ\n\\Theta\nas a set of matrices in which the upper right quadrant has exactly one entry equal to\n1\n2\n+\nη\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\nin each row and each column and the remaining entries above the diagonal are\n1\n2\n\\frac{1}{2}\n. The entries below the diagonal follow from the shifted-skew-symmetry condition. We consider the alternate where\nQ\nQ\nis chosen uniformly at random from the set\nΘ\n\\Theta\nof probability matrices in MST class.\nLower bound construction for parameter-based class\n(Section\n6.3.4\n). The construction is same as the construction given above except we define a different set\nΘ\n\\Theta\nof probability matrices. According to the parameter-based model, the matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\ndepend on the vectors\nw\np\n∈\nℝ\nd\nw_{p}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nand\nw\nq\n∈\nℝ\nd\nw_{q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nrespectively. Now, for simplicity in this sketch, suppose that\nd\nd\nis even. We set\nw\np\n=\n[\n0\n,\n⋯\n,\n0\n]\nw_{p}=[0,\\cdots,0]\n, which fixes\np\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n2\n​\n∀\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\np_{ij}=\\frac{1}{2}\\;\\forall\\;(i,j)\n. Consider a collection of vectors each with half the entries as\nδ\n\\delta\nand the other half as\n−\nδ\n-\\delta\n, thereby ensuring that\n∑\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nw\ni\n=\n0\n\\sum_{i\\in[d]}w_{i}=0\n. We set\nδ\n\\delta\nto ensure that each of the probability matrices induced by this collection of vectors obey\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n=\nϵ\n\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}=\\epsilon\n. We then consider the setting where\nQ\nQ\nis chosen uniformly at random from the set of pairwise-comparison probability matrices induced by the collection of values of\nw\nQ\nw_{Q}\n.\n4\nTwo-sample testing with partial or total ranking data\nIn this section, we extend our work from the previous sections to two-sample testing for ranking data. We focus on the two-sample testing problem where the two sets of samples from two potentially different populations comprise of partial or total rankings over various subsets of\nd\nd\nitems. Specifically, we consider the case where a partial ranking is defined as a total ranking over some subset of\nd\nd\nitems. Let\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\nand\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\nbe two unknown probability distributions over the set of all\nd\nd\n-length rankings. We observe two sets of partial or total rankings, one set from each of two populations. The partial rankings in the first set are assumed to be drawn i.i.d. according to\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\n, and the partial rankings in second set are drawn i.i.d. according to\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\n. Each sample obtained is a ranking over a subset of items of size ranging from\n2\n2\nto\nd\nd\n. Henceforth, we use the term total ranking to specify a ranking over all\nd\nd\nitems. We assume there are no ties.\nHypothesis test\nOur goal is to test the hypothesis,\nH\n0\n:\nλ\nP\n=\nλ\nQ\nH\n1\n:\nλ\nP\n≠\nλ\nQ\n.\n\\displaystyle\\begin{split}H_{0}&:\\lambda_{P}=\\lambda_{Q}\\\\\nH_{1}&:\\lambda_{P}\\neq\\lambda_{Q}.\\end{split}\n(10)\nIn the sequel, we consider this hypothesis testing problem under certain modeling assumptions on\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\nand\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\n.\n4.1\nModels\nWe now describe two partial ranking models that we analyse subsequently.\nMarginal probability based model\nThis is a non-parametric partial ranking model that is entirely specified by the probability distribution over all total rankings, given by\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\nin the first population and\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\nin the second population. The distribution\nλ\n\\lambda\ndefines the partial ranking model for the corresponding population as follows. Let\nS\nd\nS_{d}\ndenote the set of all total rankings over the\nd\nd\nitems. Consider some subset of items\nΩ\n⊆\n[\nd\n]\n\\Omega\\subseteq[d]\nof size\nm\n∈\n{\n2\n,\n⋯\n,\nd\n}\nm\\in\\{2,\\cdots,d\\}\n, and let\nτ\nΩ\n\\tau_{\\Omega}\nbe a ranking of the items in this set. Then, we define a set of all total rankings that obey the partial ranking\nτ\nΩ\n\\tau_{\\Omega}\nas\nS\n⁡\n(\nτ\nΩ\n)\n=\n{\nτ\n∈\nS\nd\n:\nτ\n⁡\n(\nτ\nΩ\n−\n1\n​\n(\n1\n)\n)\n<\nτ\n⁡\n(\nτ\nΩ\n−\n1\n​\n(\n2\n)\n)\n<\n⋯\n<\nτ\n⁡\n(\nτ\nΩ\n−\n1\n​\n(\nm\n)\n)\n}\n.\n\\displaystyle S(\\tau_{\\Omega})=\\{\\tau\\in S_{d}\\;:\\;\\tau(\\tau_{\\Omega}^{-1}(1))<\\tau(\\tau_{\\Omega}^{-1}(2))<\\cdots<\\tau(\\tau_{\\Omega}^{-1}(m))\\}.\n(11)\nThe marginal probability based partial ranking model gives the probability of a partial ranking\nτ\nΩ\n\\tau_{\\Omega}\nas\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nτ\nΩ\n)\n=\n∑\nτ\n∈\nS\n⁡\n(\nτ\nΩ\n)\nλ\n⁡\n(\nτ\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(\\tau_{\\Omega})=\\sum_{\\tau\\in S(\\tau_{\\Omega})}\\lambda(\\tau),\n(12)\nwhere\nλ\n\\lambda\nrepresents\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\nor\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\nfor the corresponding population. This model defines the probability of a partial ranking similarly to the non-parametric choice model described in\nFarias et al., 2013\n. In fact, their choice model defined over sets of size 2 is the same as our model over partial rankings of size 2.\nOur model has the desired property that given a partial ranking over the set\nΩ\n\\Omega\ncontaining item\ni\ni\nand item\nj\nj\n, the marginal probability that item\ni\ni\nis ranked higher than item\nj\nj\n, denoted by\nℙ\n⁡\n(\ni\n≻\nj\n)\n\\mathbb{P}(i\\succ j)\n, does not depend on other items in set\nΩ\n\\Omega\n. Subsequently, the marginal probability is expressed as\nℙ\n⁡\n(\ni\n≻\nj\n|\nΩ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(i\\succ j|\\Omega)\n=\n∑\nτ\n∈\nS\n⁡\n(\nτ\nΩ\n)\n,\nτ\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n<\nτ\n⁡\n(\nj\n)\nλ\n⁡\n(\nτ\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{\\tau\\in S(\\tau_{\\Omega}),\\tau(i)<\\tau(j)}\\lambda(\\tau)\n=\n∑\nτ\n∈\nS\nd\n,\nτ\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n<\nτ\n⁡\n(\nj\n)\nλ\n⁡\n(\nτ\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{\\tau\\in S_{d},\\tau(i)<\\tau(j)}\\lambda(\\tau)\nNow, for the two populations we define the marginal probability of pairwise-comparisons over items\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nas\np\ni\n​\nj\np_{ij}\nand\nq\ni\n​\nj\nq_{ij}\nfor all pairs\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nwith\ni\n<\nj\ni<j\n. Note that this model has the property that\np\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n−\np\nj\n​\ni\np_{ij}=1-p_{ji}\nand\nq\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n−\nq\nj\n​\ni\nq_{ij}=1-q_{ji}\nfor all\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n. We also note that the Plackett-Luce model described next is a subset of this model.\nPlackett-Luce model\nThis model introduced by\nLuce, 1959\nand\nPlackett, 1975\nis a commonly used parametric model for ranking data. In this model, each item has a notional quality parameter\nw\ni\n∈\nℝ\n,\n∀\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nw_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R},\\;\\forall\\;i\\in[d]\n. Under the Plackett-Luce model, the partial rankings in each population are generated according to the corresponding underlying quality parameters, namely\nw\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nP\nw^{P}_{i\\in[d]}\nand\nw\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nQ\nw^{Q}_{i\\in[d]}\n. The weight parameters completely define the probability distribution\nλ\n\\lambda\nover the set of all total rankings. In this model, a partial (or total) ranking\nτ\n\\tau\nis generated in a sequential manner where each item in a ranking is viewed as chosen from the set of items ranked lower. The probability of choosing an item\ni\ni\nfrom any set\nS\n⊆\n[\nd\n]\nS\\subseteq[d]\nis given by\nexp\n⁡\n(\nw\ni\n)\n∑\n(\ni\n′\n∈\nS\n)\nexp\n⁡\n(\nw\ni\n′\n)\n\\frac{\\exp(w_{i})}{\\sum_{(i^{\\prime}\\in S)}\\exp(w_{i^{\\prime}})}\n. To explain the sequential generation procedure, we show an example here,\nℙ\n⁡\n(\ni\n1\n≻\ni\n2\n≻\n⋯\n≻\ni\nℓ\n)\n=\n∏\nj\n=\n1\nℓ\nexp\n⁡\n(\nw\ni\nj\n)\n∑\nj\n′\n=\nj\nℓ\nexp\n⁡\n(\nw\ni\nj\n′\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(i_{1}\\succ i_{2}\\succ\\cdots\\succ i_{\\ell})=\\prod_{j=1}^{\\ell}\\dfrac{\\exp(w_{i_{j}})}{\\sum_{j^{\\prime}=j}^{\\ell}\\exp(w_{i_{j^{\\prime}}})}.\nAn important property of the Plackett-Luce model is that the marginal probability that item\ni\ni\nis ranked higher than item\nj\nj\n,\nℙ\n⁡\n(\ni\n≻\nj\n)\n\\mathbb{P}(i\\succ j)\ndoes not depend on the other items in the ranking, in fact,\nℙ\n⁡\n(\ni\n≻\nj\n)\n=\nexp\n⁡\n(\nw\ni\n)\n(\nexp\n⁡\n(\nw\ni\n)\n+\nexp\n⁡\n(\nw\nj\n)\n)\n\\mathbb{P}(i\\succ j)=\\frac{\\exp(w_{i})}{(\\exp(w_{i})+\\exp(w_{j}))}\n. For each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n, we denote the marginal probability\nℙ\n⁡\n(\ni\n≻\nj\n)\n\\mathbb{P}(i\\succ j)\ncorresponding to the parameters\nw\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nP\nw^{P}_{i\\in[d]}\nas\np\ni\n​\nj\np_{ij}\n. Similarly, we denote the marginal probability\nℙ\n⁡\n(\ni\n≻\nj\n)\n\\mathbb{P}(i\\succ j)\ncorresponding to the parameters\nw\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nQ\nw^{Q}_{i\\in[d]}\nas\nq\ni\n​\nj\nq_{ij}\n. These pairwise marginal probabilities\np\ni\n​\nj\np_{ij}\nand\nq\ni\n​\nj\nq_{ij}\nare collected in pairwise-comparison probability matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\nrespectively.\nFinally, with this notation, we specialise the hypothesis testing problem in (\n10\n) for the two partial ranking models described above, in terms of the pairwise probability matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\n. For any given parameter\nϵ\n>\n0\n\\epsilon>0\n, we define the two-sample testing problem as\nH\n0\n:\nP\n=\nQ\nH\n1\n:\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n≥\nϵ\n.\n\\displaystyle\\begin{split}H_{0}&:P=Q\\\\\nH_{1}&:\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}\\geq\\epsilon.\\end{split}\n(13)\nWe note that under the Plackett-Luce model, the null condition in (\n10\n) is equivalent to the null condition in (\n13\n). Moreover, under the Plackett-Luce model, difference in two probability distributions\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\nand\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\nis captured by difference in the pairwise probability matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\n. Thus, we specialise the alternate condition in (\n10\n) in terms of scaled Frobenius distance between the pairwise probability matrices,\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\n, denoted by the parameter\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n, to get the alternate condition in (\n13\n). Furthermore, under the marginal probability based model, the null condition in (\n10\n) implies\nP\n=\nQ\nP=Q\nwhereas the converse is not true. That is, there exist pairs of probability distributions over the set of all\nd\nd\n-length rankings\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\nand\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\n, that follow the marginal probability based model with\nλ\nP\n≠\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{P}\\neq\\lambda_{Q}\n, such that their corresponding pairwise probability matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\nare equal. Thus, under the marginal probability based model, by conducting a test for the hypothesis testing problem in (\n13\n) that controls the Type I error at level\nα\n\\alpha\n, we get control over Type I error at level\nα\n\\alpha\nfor the hypothesis testing problem in (\n10\n).\nWe are now ready to describe our main results for two-sample testing with partial (or total) ranking data.\n4.2\nMain results\nOur testing algorithms for ranking data build upon the test statistic in Algorithm\n1\n. To test for difference in probability distributions\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\nand\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\n, we first use a rank breaking method to convert the data into pairwise-comparisons, on which we apply the test statistic in (\n8\n). Given a rank breaking method, denoted by\nR\nR\n, and rankings from the two populations,\nS\nP\ni\nS_{P_{i}}\nand\nS\nQ\ni\nS_{Q_{i}}\nfor\ni\n∈\n[\nN\n]\ni\\in[N]\n, then the rank breaking algorithm yields pairwise-comparison data as\nR\n⁡\n(\nS\nP\ni\n∈\n[\nN\n]\n)\n=\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nX\ni\n​\nj\n}\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n2\nR(S_{P_{i\\in[N]}})=\\{k^{p}_{ij},X_{ij}\\}_{(i,j)\\in[d]^{2}}\nand, similarly,\nR\n⁡\n(\nS\nQ\ni\n∈\n[\nN\n]\n)\n=\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\n}\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n2\nR(S_{Q_{i\\in[N]}})=\\{k^{q}_{ij},Y_{ij}\\}_{(i,j)\\in[d]^{2}}\n. Here,\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n,\nX\ni\n​\nj\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\nk^{p}_{ij}{},k^{q}_{ij}{},X_{ij},Y_{ij}\nrepresent the pairwise-comparison data as defined in Section\n2.1\n.\nNow, we describe three rank breaking methods that we subsequently use in our testing algorithms, Algorithm\n3\nand Algorithm\n4\n.\n1.\nRandom disjoint\n: In this method, denoted by\nR\nR\nR_{\\textnormal{R}}\n, given a set of\nN\nN\npartial (or total) rankings, we randomly break each ranking up into pairwise-comparisons such that no item is in more than one pair. In this method, each\nm\nm\n-length ranking yields\n⌊\nm\n2\n⌋\n\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\rfloor\npairwise-comparisons.\n2.\nDeterministic disjoint\n: We use this rank breaking method, denoted by\nR\nD\nR_{\\textnormal{D}}\n, when we have\nN\nN\ntotal rankings. In this method, we deterministically break each ranking into pairwise-comparisons so that no item is in more than one pair. So, we get\n⌊\nd\n2\n⌋\n\\lfloor\\frac{d}{2}\\rfloor\npairwise-comparisons from each total ranking. First, we want the number of samples to be divisible by\nd\nd\n, so we throw away\n(\nN\nmod\nd\n)\n(N{}\\bmod d)\nrankings chosen randomly. Then arbitrarily without looking at the data, partition the remaining rankings into\n⌊\nN\nd\n⌋\n\\lfloor\\frac{N}{d}\\rfloor\ndisjoint subsets each containing\nd\nd\nrankings. Within each subset, we convert the\nd\nd\nrankings into\nd\n​\n⌊\nd\n2\n⌋\nd\\lfloor\\frac{d}{2}\\rfloor\npairwise-comparisons deterministically such that we observe at least one comparison between each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n(i,j)\\in[d]\nwith\ni\n<\nj\ni<j\n. We keep exactly one pairwise-comparison for each pair in a subset. In this manner, we get to observe exactly\n⌊\nN\nd\n⌋\n\\lfloor\\frac{N}{d}\\rfloor\ncomparisons between each pair of items.\n3.\nComplete\n: In this method, denoted by\nR\nC\nR_{\\textnormal{C}}\n, given a set of\nN\nN\npartial (or total) rankings, we break each ranking into all possible pairwise-comparisons for that ranking. In this method, each\nm\nm\n-length ranking yields\n(\nm\n2\n)\n\\binom{m}{2}\npairwise-comparisons.\nNow, equipped with the rank breaking methods, we describe our first result which provides an algorithm (Algorithm\n3\n) for the two-sample testing problem in (\n13\n) for the Plackett-Luce model, and associated upper bounds on its sample complexity.\nInput\n: Two sets\nS\nP\nS_{P}\nand\nS\nQ\nS_{Q}\nof\nm\nm\n-length partial rankings, where\n2\n≤\nm\n≤\nd\n2\\leq m\\leq d\n. The two sets of partial rankings,\nS\nP\nS_{P}\nand\nS\nQ\nS_{Q}\ncorrespond to pairwise probability matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\nrespectively, according to the Plackett-Luce model. Rank breaking method,\nR\n∈\n{\nR\nR\n,\nR\nD\n,\nR\nC\n}\nR\\in\\{R_{\\textnormal{R}},R_{\\textnormal{D}},R_{\\textnormal{C}}\\}\n.\n(1)\nUsing the rank breaking method get\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nX\ni\n​\nj\n}\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n2\n,\ni\n<\nj\n←\nR\n⁡\n(\nS\nP\n)\n;\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\n}\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n2\n,\ni\n<\nj\n←\nR\n⁡\n(\nS\nQ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\{k^{p}_{ij}{},X_{ij}\\}_{(i,j)\\in[d]^{2},i<j}\\leftarrow R(S_{P});\\quad\\{k^{q}_{ij}{},Y_{ij}\\}_{(i,j)\\in[d]^{2},i<j}\\leftarrow R(S_{Q}).\n(2)\nExecute Algorithm\n1\n.\nAlgorithm 3\nTwo-sample testing with partial ranking data for Plackett-Luce model.\nWe note that both Algorithm\n3\nand Algorithm\n4\n, defined in this section, can be used with any of the three rank breaking methods described. The subsequent guarantees provided depend on the rank breaking method used, as we see in Theorem\n7\nand Theorem\n8\n.\nIn our results for two-sample testing under the Plackett-Luce modeling assumption, we consider two cases. In the first case, for some\nm\n∈\n{\n2\n,\n⋯\n,\nd\n−\n1\n}\nm\\in\\{2,\\cdots,d-1\\}\n, each sample is a ranking of some\nm\nm\nitems chosen uniformly at random from the set of\nd\nd\nitems. In the second case, the samples comprise of total rankings, that is,\nm\n=\nd\nm=d\n.\nThe following two theorems characterize the performance of Algorithm\n3\nthereby establishing an upper bound on the sample complexity of the two-sample testing problem defined in (\n13\n). In these theorems we use the disjoint rank breaking methods so that the pairwise-comparisons created from a ranking are independent.\nTheorem 7\n.\nConsider the testing problem in (\n13\n) where pairwise probability matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\nfollow the Plackett-Luce model. Suppose we have\nN\nN\nsamples, where for some\nm\n∈\n{\n2\n,\n⋯\n,\nd\n−\n1\n}\nm\\in\\{2,\\cdots,d-1\\}\n, each sample is a ranking of some\nm\nm\nitems chosen uniformly at random from the set of\nd\nd\nitems. Then, there are positive constants\nc\n,\nc\n0\n,\nc\n1\nc,c_{0},c_{1}\nand\nc\n2\nc_{2}\nsuch that if\nN\n≥\nc\n​\nd\n2\n​\nlog\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\nm\n​\n⌈\nc\n0\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n⌉\nN\\geq c\\dfrac{d^{2}\\log(d)}{m}\\lceil\\dfrac{c_{0}}{d\\epsilon^{2}}\\rceil\nand\nϵ\n≥\nc\n1\n​\nd\n−\nc\n2\n\\epsilon\\geq c_{1}d^{-c_{2}}\n, then Algorithm\n3\nwith the “Random disjoint” rank breaking method will correctly distinguish between\nP\n=\nQ\nP=Q\nand\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n≥\nϵ\n\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}\\geq\\epsilon\n, with probability at least\n2\n3\n\\frac{2}{3}\n.\nThe proof of Theorem\n7\nis provided in Section\n6.4\n. The lower bound assumption on\nϵ\n\\epsilon\nin Theorem\n7\nis to ensure that the sufficient number of pairwise comparisons needed after applying the random disjoint rank breaking algorithm, is not very large. Theorem\n7\nis a combined result of the random disjoint rank breaking algorithm and the result in Theorem\n1\n. When we have total rankings, Algorithm\n3\nwith the “Deterministic disjoint” rank breaking method yields an improvement in the sample complexity by a logarithmic factor. We state this formally in the following theorem.\nTheorem 8\n.\nConsider the testing problem in (\n13\n) where pairwise probability matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\nfollow the Plackett-Luce model. Suppose we have\nN\nN\nsamples of total rankings from each population. Then, there are positive constants\nc\n,\nc\n1\nc,c_{1}\nand\nc\n2\nc_{2}\nsuch that if\nN\n≥\n2\n​\nd\n​\n⌈\nc\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n⌉\nN\\geq 2d\\lceil\\dfrac{c}{d\\epsilon^{2}}\\rceil\nand\nϵ\n≥\nc\n1\n​\nd\n−\nc\n2\n\\epsilon\\geq c_{1}d^{-c_{2}}\n, then Algorithm\n3\nwith the “Deterministic disjoint” rank breaking method will correctly distinguish between\nP\n=\nQ\nP=Q\nand\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n≥\nϵ\n\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}\\geq\\epsilon\n, with probability at least\n2\n3\n\\frac{2}{3}\n.\nThe proof of Theorem\n8\nis provided in Section\n6.5\n. These two results provide an upper bound on the sample complexity when using partial (and total) rankings for the two-sample testing problem in (\n13\n) under the Plackett-Luce model. In Theorem\n7\nand Theorem\n8\nthe lower bound of\n2\n3\n\\frac{2}{3}\non probability of success is tied to the specific threshold used in Algorithm\n1\nin the same manner as described for Theorem\n1\n. Specifically, for any constant\nν\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\nu\\in(0,1)\n, Algorithm\n3\ncan achieve a probability of error at most\nν\n\\nu\nwith the same order of sample complexity as mentioned in Theorem\n7\nand Theorem\n8\n.\nAlgorithm\n3\naddresses the problem of two-sample testing under the Plackett-Luce model. Now, we provide a permutation test based algorithm for the more general, non-parametric model, namely, marginal probability based model. The permutation test method described in Algorithm\n4\ngives a sharper (implicit) threshold than that in Algorithm\n3\n. Note that Algorithm\n4\ndoesn’t require any assumptions on the length of the partial-ranking data, the partial-ranking data in each population can be of varying lengths. Moreover, as we will see in Theorem\n9\n, the Type I error guarantee of Algorithm\n4\nholds even if the pairwise-comparisons created from the rank breaking method are dependent, hence the guarantee does not depend on the choice of the rank breaking method.\nThe key difference between the permutation testing algorithm for pairwise-comparison data, described in Section\n3.1\n, and the permutation testing algorithm for partial ranking data, described in Algorithm\n4\n, is the shuffling step. In our partial ranking based setup, each ranking sample is obtained independent of all else while the pairwise-comparisons obtained from a rank are not necessarily independent of each other. Hence, in the partial ranking based permutation testing algorithm (Algorithm\n4\n), we re-distribute ranking samples between the two populations and not the pairwise-comparisons.\nInput\n: Two sets of partial rankings\nS\nP\nS_{P}\nand\nS\nQ\nS_{Q}\nfrom two populations corresponding to the probability distributions\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\nand\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\n. Significance level\nα\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\alpha\\in(0,1)\n. Rank breaking method,\nR\n∈\n{\nR\nR\n,\nR\nD\n,\nR\nC\n}\nR\\in\\{R_{\\textnormal{R}},R_{\\textnormal{D}},R_{\\textnormal{C}}\\}\n. Iteration count\nγ\n\\gamma\n.\n(1)\nUsing the rank breaking method get\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nX\ni\n​\nj\n}\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n2\n,\ni\n<\nj\n←\nR\n⁡\n(\nS\nP\n)\n;\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\n}\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n2\n,\ni\n<\nj\n←\nR\n⁡\n(\nS\nQ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\{k^{p}_{ij}{},X_{ij}\\}_{(i,j)\\in[d]^{2},i<j}\\leftarrow R(S_{P});\\quad\\{k^{q}_{ij}{},Y_{ij}\\}_{(i,j)\\in[d]^{2},i<j}\\leftarrow R(S_{Q}).\n(2)\nCompute the test statistic\nT\nT\ndefined in (\n8\n).\n(3)\n{\n\\{\nRepeat\nγ\n\\gamma\ntimes\n}\n\\}\nPut the samples in\nS\nP\nS_{P}\nand\nS\nQ\nS_{Q}\ntogether and reassign the samples at random such that the number of samples assigned to each population is the same as before. Repeat Step 1 and Step 2. Denote the computed test statistic as\nT\nℓ\nT_{\\ell}\nfor the\nℓ\nt\n​\nh\n\\ell^{th}\niteration.\nOutput\n: Reject the null if\np\n=\n∑\nℓ\n=\n1\nγ\n1\nγ\n​\n𝟙\n​\n(\nT\nℓ\n−\nT\n)\n<\nα\np=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{\\gamma}\\frac{1}{\\gamma}\\mathbbm{1}(T_{\\ell}-T)<\\alpha\n.\nAlgorithm 4\nTwo-sample testing algorithm with partial ranking data for marginal probability based model.\nWe now show that Algorithm\n4\ncontrols the Type I error of the two-sample testing problem in (\n10\n) under the more general, marginal probability based partial ranking model. This result relies on considerably weaker assumptions than Theorem\n7\nand Theorem\n8\n. In particular, we do not assume that each ranking is of the same length. We only assume that the (sub)set of items ranked in each sample from each population is sampled independently from the same distribution. Specifically, let there be any probability distribution over all non-empty subsets of\n[\nd\n]\n[d]\n. Then, the set of items ranked in each sample for each population is sampled i.i.d. from this distribution. Moreover, the number of samples from the two populations need not be equal.\nTheorem 9\n.\nConsider any probability distributions\nλ\nP\n\\lambda_{P}\nand\nλ\nQ\n\\lambda_{Q}\nand the two-sample testing problem in (\n10\n). Suppose we have partial ranking data from each population such that the sets of items ranked in each sample in each population is sampled i.i.d. from any probability distribution over all non-empty subsets of\n[\nd\n]\n[d]\n. Suppose the partial ranking data follows the marginal probability based model. Then, for any significance level\nα\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\alpha\\in(0,1)\nthe permutation testing method of Algorithm\n4\nhas Type I error at most\nα\n\\alpha\n.\nThe proof of Theorem\n9\nis provided in Section\n6.6\n. Recall that the Plackett-Luce model is a special case of the marginal probability based model, and hence as a direct corollary, the guarantees for Algorithm\n4\nestablished in Theorem\n9\nalso apply to the Plackett-Luce model.\n5\nExperiments\nIn this section, we present results from experiments on simulated and real-world data sets, to gain a further understanding of the problem of two-sample testing on pairwise-comparison data.\n5.1\nPairwise-comparison data\nWe now describe real-world experiments and synthetic simulations we conduct for two-sample testing on pairwise-comparison data. In these experiments, we use the test statistic we designed in Algorithm\n1\nalong with the permutation testing method as described in Algorithm\n2\nto obtain an implicit value of the threshold and control Type I error.\n5.1.1\nSynthetic simulations\nWe conduct two sets of experiments via synthetic simulations. The first set of experiments empirically evaluates the dependence of the power of our test (Algorithm\n1\n) with respect to individual problem parameters. In each of the simulations, we set the significance level to be 0.05. Specifically, given the problem parameters\nn\n,\na\n,\nd\nn,a,d\nand\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n, we consider the random-design setting with\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\nBin\n​\n(\nn\n,\na\n)\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}\\textnormal{Bin}(n,a)\n. We consider the asymmetric and model-free setting, fix\nP\n=\n[\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP=[\\frac{1}{2}]^{d\\times d}\nand set\nQ\n=\nP\n+\nΔ\nQ=P+\\Delta\nwhere\nΔ\n\\Delta\nis sampled uniformly at random from the set of all matrices in\n[\n−\n1\n2\n,\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\n[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}]^{d\\times d}\nwith\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nΔ\n|\n‖\nF\n=\nϵ\n\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|\\Delta|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}=\\epsilon\n. In Figure\n1\na, b and c, we vary the parameter\nd\nd\n,\nϵ\n\\epsilon\nand\na\na\nrespectively, keeping the other parameters fixed. Recall that our results in Theorem\n1\nand Corollary\n2\npredict the sample complexity as\nn\n=\nΘ\n⁡\n(\n1\na\n​\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n)\nn=\\Theta(\\frac{1}{ad\\epsilon^{2}})\n. To test this theoretical prediction, we set the sample size\nn\nn\n(on the x-axis) as\nn\n=\n1\na\n​\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\nn=\\frac{1}{ad\\epsilon^{2}}\n, and plot the power of the test on the y-axis. Each plot point in Figure\n1\nis obtained by averaging over\n400\n400\niterations of the experiment, and the threshold for the test is obtained by running the permutation test method over\n5000\n5000\niterations. Observe that, interestingly in each figure, the curves across all values of the varied parameters nearly coincide, thereby validating the sample complexity predicted by our theoretical results.\n(a)\nVarying\nd\nd\n(b)\nVarying\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n(c)\nVarying\na\na\nFigure 1\n:\nPower of the testing algorithm versus the scaling factor of the sample size parameter\nn\n=\n1\na\n​\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\nn=\\frac{1}{ad\\epsilon^{2}}\n. We use Algorithm\n2\nwhich uses the test statistic in (\n8\n) with the permutation testing method. The test is conducted at a significance level of\n0.05\n0.05\n(indicated by the horizontal line at\ny\n=\n0.05\ny=0.05\n). Unless specified otherwise, the parameters are fixed as\nd\n=\n20\n,\nϵ\n=\n0.05\n,\na\n=\n1\nd=20,\\epsilon=0.05,a=1\n.\nThe second set of experiments empirically investigates the role of the underlying pairwise-comparison models in two-sample testing with our test (Algorithm\n1\n). We consider the random-design setup in the symmetric setting with\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\nBin\n​\n(\nn\n,\na\n)\n​\n∀\ni\n<\nj\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}\\textnormal{Bin}(n,a)~\\forall\\;i<j\n. We generate the matrices\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\nin three ways: model-free, BTL and SST. In the model-free setting, we generate\nP\nP\nand\nQ\nQ\nin a manner similar to the first set of simulations above, with the additional constraints\nΔ\nj\n​\ni\n=\n1\n−\nΔ\ni\n​\nj\n​\n∀\ni\n≤\nj\n\\Delta_{ji}=1-\\Delta_{ij}~\\forall\\;\\;i\\leq j\n.\nFor the BTL and SST models, we fix\nP\n=\n[\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP=[\\frac{1}{2}]^{d\\times d}\n. For the BTL model, we choose\nw\np\nw_{p}\naccording to the construction in Section\n6.3.4\nto obtain\nQ\nQ\n. For the SST model, we set\nQ\n=\nP\n+\nΔ\nQ=P+\\Delta\n, where matrix\nΔ\n\\Delta\nis generated as follows. We generate\nΔ\n\\Delta\nby arranging\n(\nd\n2\n)\n\\binom{d}{2}\nrandom variables uniformly distributed over\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n[0,1]\n, in a row-wise decreasing and column-wise increasing order in the upper triangle matrix\n(\nΔ\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n−\nΔ\nj\n​\ni\n)\n(\\Delta_{ij}=1-\\Delta_{ji})\nand normalizing to make\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nΔ\n|\n‖\nF\n=\nϵ\n\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|\\Delta|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}=\\epsilon\n. This construction ensures that\nΔ\n\\Delta\nlies in the SST class, and since matrix\nP\nP\nis a constant matrix,\nQ\nQ\nis also guaranteed to lie in the SST class. The results of the simulations are shown in Figure\n2\n. The results show that in the settings simulated, the power of the testing algorithm is identical in all the models considered. This leaves an open question whether there exists a tighter information-theoretic lower bound for the SST and the parameter-based model that matches the upper bound derived for the test in Algorithm\n1\nor if there exists a test statistic for these models with a better rate.\nFigure 2\n:\nPower of our test (Algorithm\n2\n) under three different models for pairwise-comparisons: BTL, SST and the model-free setting. The parameters of the problem are set as\nd\n=\n20\n,\nϵ\n2\n=\n0.05\n,\na\n=\n1\nd=20,\\epsilon^{2}=0.05,a=1\nand the test is conducted at a significance level of\n0.05\n0.05\n(indicated by the horizontal line at\ny\n=\n0.05\ny=0.05\n).\n5.1.2\nReal-world data\nIn this section, we describe the results of our experiments on two real-world data sets. In these experiments, we use Algorithm\n2\nto obtain a\np\np\n-value for the experiment.\nOrdinal versus cardinal\nAn important question in the field of crowdsourcing and data-elicitation from people is whether pairwise-comparisons provided by people (ordinal responses) are distributed similarly to if they provide ratings (cardinal responses) which are then converted to pairwise-comparisons\n(\nShah et al., 2016\n;\nRaman and Joachims, 2014\n)\n. In this section, we use the permutation based two-sample test described in Algorithm\n2\nto address this question. We use the data set from\nShah et al., 2016\ncomprising six different experiments on the Amazon Mechanical Turk crowdsourcing platform. In each experiment, workers are asked to either provide ratings for the set of items in that experiment (age for photo given, number of spelling mistakes in a paragraph, distance between two cities, relevance of web-based search results, quality of taglines for a product, frequency of a piano sound clip) or provide pairwise-comparisons. The number of items in each experiment ranged from 10 to 25. For each of the six experiments, there were 100 workers, and each worker was assigned to either the ordinal or the cardinal version of the task uniformly at random. The first set of samples corresponds to the elicited ordinal responses and the second set of samples are obtained by converting the elicited ratings to ordinal data. We have a total of 2017 ordinal responses and 1671 cardinal-converted-to-ordinal responses. More details about the data set and experiment are provided in the appendix.\nUsing Algorithm\n2\n, we test for difference in the two resulting distributions for the entire data set (\nd\n=\n74\nd=74\n). We observe that\nthe test rejects the null with a\np\np\n-value of 0.003, thereby concluding a statistically significant difference between the ordinal and the cardinal-converted-to-ordinal data\n.\nEuropean football leagues\nIn the second data set, we investigate whether the relative performances of the teams (in four European football leagues: English Premier League, Bundesliga, Ligue 1, La Liga) changed significantly from the 2016-17 season to the 2017-18 season. The leagues are designed such that each pair of teams plays twice in a season (one home, one away game), so we have at most two pairwise-comparisons per pair within a league (we do not consider the games that end in a draw). Each league has 15-17 common teams across two consecutive seasons. This gives a total of 801 and 788 pairwise-comparisons in the 2016-17 and 2017-18 seasons respectively. More details about the experiment are provided in the appendix.\nUsing the test statistic of Algorithm\n1\nwith permutation testing, we test for a difference in the two resulting distributions for the entire data set (\nd\n=\n67\nd=67\n). We observe that\nthe test fails to reject the null with a\np\np\n-value of 0.971, that is, it does not recognize any significant difference between the relative performance of the European football teams in 2017-18 season and the 2016-17 season from the data available\n. Running the test for each league individually also fails to reject the null.\n5.2\nPartial and total ranking data\nWe now describe the experiments we conducted on real-world data for two-sample testing on partial (and total) ranking data. In these experiments, we use the test statistic (\n8\n) along with the permutation testing method, as explained in Algorithm\n4\n.\nFor our experiments, we use the “Sushi preference data set”\nKamishima, 2003\n, in which subjects rank different types of sushi according to their preferences. The data set contains two sets of ranking data. In the first set, the subjects are asked to provide a total ranking over 10 items (popular types of sushi). In this set, all subjects are asked to rank the same 10 objects. This set contains 5000 such total rankings.\nIn the second set of ranking data, a total of 100 types of sushi are considered. We first describe how the 100 types are chosen. The authors in\nKamishima, 2003\nsurveyed menu data from 25 sushi restaurants found on the internet. For each type of sushi sold at the restaurant, they counted the number of restaurants that offered the item. From these counts, they derived the probabilities that each item would be supplied. By eliminating unfamiliar or low frequency items, they came up with a list of 100 items. Each subject in this set is asked to rank a subset of 10 items randomly selected from the 100 items, according to the probability distribution described above. This set contains responses from 5000 subjects.\nIn addition, this data set contains demographic information about all the subjects, including their\n(a)\nGender {Male, Female}\n(b)\nAge {Above 30, Below 30}\n(c)\nCurrent region of residence {East, West}\n(d)\nPrimary region of residence until 15 years old {East, West}.\n(a)\nGrouping by gender\n(b)\nGrouping by age\n(c)\nGrouping by primary region of residence until 15 years old\n(d)\nGrouping by current region of residence\nFigure 3\n:\nEmpirical power of our test statistic\nT\nT\nwith the permutation testing method described in Algorithm\n4\nin testing for difference in sushi preference from the first set of responses with\nd\n=\n10\nd=10\n. The responses obtained comprise of total rankings from each subject. Test results are shown for differences between demographic division based on the information available. Two different rank breaking methods are used for our algorithm, namely, “Random Disjoint” and “Complete”. We also show the results for kernel-based two-sample testing with Kendall’s kernel and Mallows’ kernel as in\nMania et al., 2018\n. The x-axis shows the number of samples (total rankings) from each group used to conduct the test and the y-axis shows the empirical power of our test. The test is conducted at a significance level of\n0.05\n0.05\n(indicated by the horizontal line at\ny\n=\n0.05\ny=0.05\n). Empirical power is computed as an average over 100 trials.\nUsing our testing algorithm, we test for a difference in preferences across the two sections within each demographic mentioned above. In the first set of experiments, we implement the permutation testing method with our test statistic (\n8\n) on the first set of ranking data with\nd\n=\n10\nd=10\nfor each demographic division. We show the results in Figure\n3\nfor two rank-breaking methods, namely, “Random Disjoint” and “Complete”. In addition, we show the results of two kernel-based two-sample testing methods for total rankings designed in\nMania et al., 2018\n, namely, Kendall’s kernel and Mallows’ kernel. We randomly sub-sampled\nn\nn\nsamples from each sub-group of subjects and used\n200\n200\npermutations to determine the rejection threshold for the permutation test. In these experiments, we show the empirical power of our testing method, which is the fraction of times our test rejected the null in a total of\n100\n100\ntrials. We show all the results of using this method on the sushi data set in Figure\n3\n.\nAcross each demographic division, our test detects a statistically significant difference in distribution over sushi preferences for the 10 types of sushi included\n. Moreover, our testing algorithm with “Complete” rank breaking method performs competitively with the kernel-based two-sample testing methods introduced in\nMania et al., 2018\n.\n(a)\nGrouping by gender\n(b)\nGrouping by age\n(c)\nGrouping by primary region of residence until 15 years old\n(d)\nGrouping by current region of residence\nFigure 4\n:\nEmpirical power of our test statistic\nT\nT\nwith the permutation testing method described in Algorithm\n4\nin testing for difference in sushi preference from the first set of responses with\nd\n=\n100\nd=100\n. Test results are shown for differences between demographic division based on the information available. Two different rank breaking methods are used, namely, “Random Disjoint” and “Complete”. The x-axis shows the number of samples (total rankings) from each sub-group used to conduct the test and the y-axis shows the empirical power of our test. The test is conducted at a significance level of\n0.05\n0.05\n(indicated by the horizontal line at\ny\n=\n0.05\ny=0.05\n). Empirical power is computed as an average over 100 iterations.\nWe note that since our testing algorithms also work with partial ranking data, our testing algorithms are much more general than the testing algorithms in\nMania et al., 2018\n, as we demonstrate in our next set of experiments on the second sushi preference data set. We use Algorithm\n4\nto test if the preferences of the subjects in the second sushi data set also varies across the different demographics. Recall that this data set has\nd\n=\n100\nd=100\nitems in total but each ranking only ranks a subset of\n10\n10\nitems. The other details of the experiment are the same as the previous experiment. The results are shown in Figure\n4\n. Again, across each demographic, our test detects a statistically significant difference in distribution over sushi preferences for the 100 types of sushi included.\n6\nProofs\nThis section is devoted to the proofs of our main results. In Section\n6.1\nand Section\n6.2\n, we prove the positive results from Section\n3.1\n, and in Section\n6.3\nwe prove the converse results from Section\n3.2\n. Lastly, Sections\n6.4\n-\n6.6\nare devoted to proofs of results under the partial (or total) ranking setting mentioned in Section\n4.2\n.\nThroughout these and other proofs, we use the notation\nc\n,\nc\n′\n,\nc\n0\n,\nc\n1\n{c,c^{\\prime},c_{0},c_{1}}\nand so on to denote positive constants whose values may change from line to line.\n6.1\nProof of Corollary\n2\nIn this section we present the complete proof of Corollary\n2\n. We first present the proof for the random-design setup described in Corollary\n2\nand then specialise the proof in Section\n6.2\nto the per-pair fixed-design setup in Theorem\n1\n. To prove our result, we analyse the expected value and the variance of the test statistic\nT\nT\nin Algorithm\n1\nin the following two lemmas. Recall that under the random-design setup\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\nare distributed independently and identically according to some distribution\n𝒟\n{\\cal D}\nthat satisfies the conditions in (\n9\n).\nLemma 10\n.\nFor\nT\nT\nas defined in Algorithm\n1\n, with\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\n𝒟\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}{\\cal D}\n, under the null\n𝔼\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\n=\n0\n\\mathbb{E}_{H_{0}}[T]=0\nand under the alternate,\n𝔼\nH\n1\n​\n[\nT\n]\n≥\nc\n​\nμ\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{H_{1}}[T]\\geq c\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}.\nThe proof of Lemma\n10\nis provided in Section\n6.1.1\n. Now, with a view to applying Chebyshev’s concentration inequality, we bound the variance of\nT\nT\n.\nLemma 11\n.\nFor\nT\nT\nas defined in Algorithm\n1\n, with\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\n𝒟\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}{\\cal D}\n, where\n𝒟\n{\\cal D}\nobeys the conditions described in (\n9\n), under the null\nVar\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\n≤\n24\n​\nd\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{0}}{[T]}\\leq 24d^{2},\nand under the alternate,\nVar\nH\n1\n​\n[\nT\n]\n≤\n24\n​\nd\n2\n+\n8\n​\nμ\n​\n|\n‖\nP\n−\nQ\n‖\n|\nF\n2\n+\nc\n′\n​\nμ\n2\n|\n​\n‖\nP\n−\nQ\n‖\n|\nF\n2\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{1}}{[T]}\\leq 24d^{2}+8\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}+c^{\\prime}\\mu^{2}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}\nwhere\nc\n′\n>\n0\nc^{\\prime}>0\nis a constant.\nThe proof for Lemma\n11\nis provided in Section\n6.1.2\n. We now have to control Type I error and Type II error. Using one-sided Chebyshev’s inequality for the test statistic\nT\nT\n, which has\n𝔼\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\n=\n0\n\\mathbb{E}_{H_{0}}[T]=0\n, we derive an upper bound on Type I error as follows\nℙ\nH\n0\n​\n(\nT\n≥\nt\n)\n≤\nVar\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\nVar\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\n+\nt\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}_{H_{0}}(T\\geq t)\\leq\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{0}}[T]}{\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{0}}[T]+t^{2}}.\n(14)\nObserve that if\nt\n=\n11\n​\nd\nt=11d\nthen the Type I error is upper bounded by\n1\n6\n\\frac{1}{6}\n.\nIn addition, if Type I error is required to be at most\nν\n\\nu\n, then we set the threshold equal to\nd\n​\n24\n​\n(\n1\n−\nν\n)\nν\nd\\sqrt{\\dfrac{24(1-\\nu)}{\\nu}}\n. We now move to controlling the Type II error of the testing algorithm. We again invoke Chebyshev’s inequality as follows\nℙ\nH\n1\n​\n(\nT\n<\nt\n)\n≤\nVar\nH\n1\n​\n[\nT\n]\nVar\nH\n1\n​\n[\nT\n]\n+\n(\n𝔼\nH\n1\n​\n[\nT\n]\n−\nt\n)\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}_{H_{1}}(T<t)\\leq\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{1}}[T]}{\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{1}}[T]+(\\mathbb{E}_{H_{1}}[T]-t)^{2}}.\n(15)\nTo guarantee that Type II error is at most\n1\n6\n\\frac{1}{6}\n, we substitute the bounds on\n𝔼\nH\n1\n​\n[\nT\n]\n,\nVar\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\n,\nVar\nH\n1\n​\n[\nT\n]\n\\mathbb{E}_{H_{1}}[T],\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{0}}[T],\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{1}}[T]\nfrom Lemma\n10\nand Lemma\n11\nin (\n15\n) to get the sufficient condition\n5\n​\n(\n24\n​\nd\n2\n+\n8\n​\nμ\n​\n|\n‖\nP\n−\nQ\n‖\n|\nF\n2\n+\nc\n′\n​\nμ\n2\n|\n​\n‖\nP\n−\nQ\n‖\n|\nF\n2\n)\n\\displaystyle 5(24d^{2}+8\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}+c^{\\prime}\\mu^{2}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2})\n≤\n(\nc\n​\nμ\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n−\n11\n​\nd\n)\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leq(c\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}-11d)^{2}\n40\n​\nμ\n​\n|\n‖\nP\n−\nQ\n‖\n|\nF\n2\n+\n22\n​\nc\n​\nd\n​\nμ\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n+\n5\n​\nc\n′\n​\nμ\n2\n|\n​\n‖\nP\n−\nQ\n‖\n|\nF\n2\n\\displaystyle 40\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}+22cd\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}+5c^{\\prime}\\mu^{2}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}\n≤\nc\n2\n​\nμ\n2\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n4\n+\nd\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leq c^{2}\\mu^{2}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{4}+d^{2}.\nThis condition yields\n40\n+\n22\n​\nc\n​\nd\n+\n5\n​\nc\n′\n​\nμ\n\\displaystyle 40+22cd+5c^{\\prime}\\mu\n≤\nc\n2\n​\nμ\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leq c^{2}\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}.\n(16)\nRecall that under the alternate\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n≥\nϵ\n\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}\\geq\\epsilon\n.\nAccording to the final condition derived here (\n16\n), under the regime\nμ\n>\nd\n\\mu>d\n, we have control over total probability of error if\nϵ\n2\n​\nd\n2\n≥\nc\n′\n\\epsilon^{2}d^{2}\\geq c^{\\prime}\nfor some constant\nc\n′\n>\n0\nc^{\\prime}>0\n. Under the regime\nμ\n≤\nd\n\\mu\\leq d\n, the condition (\n16\n) simplifies as\nϵ\n2\n≥\nc\n′′\nμ\n​\nd\n,\n\\displaystyle\\epsilon^{2}\\geq\\dfrac{c^{\\prime\\prime}}{\\mu d},\n(17)\nwhere\nc\n′′\n>\n0\nc^{\\prime\\prime}>0\nis some constant.\nThis gives the sufficient condition to control total probability of error (sum of Type I error and Type II error) to be at most\n1\n3\n\\frac{1}{3}\nunder the setting where\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\n𝒟\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}{\\cal D}\n.\n6.1.1\nProof of Lemma\n10\nWe now prove the bounds on the expected value of the test statistic defined in Algorithm\n1\n. Recall that for each\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n, given\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\n, we have\nX\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\np\ni\n​\nj\n)\nX_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k^{p}_{ij},p_{ij})\nand\nY\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nY_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k^{q}_{ij},q_{ij})\n. Also,\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\niid\n𝒟\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\textnormal{iid}}}{{\\sim}}{\\cal D}\nwherein\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n]\n=\nμ\n,\nVar\n⁡\n[\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n]\n=\nσ\n2\n,\nPr\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\n1\n)\n=\np\n1\n\\mathbb{E}[k^{p}_{ij}]=\\mu,\\mathrm{Var}[{k^{p}_{ij}}]=\\sigma^{2},\\textnormal{Pr}(k^{p}_{ij}=1)=p_{1}\nand\n𝒟\n{\\cal D}\nobeys (\n9\n). We denote the vector of\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\nk^{p}_{ij}\nand\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{q}_{ij}\nfor all\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nby\nk\np\n\\textbf{k}^{p}\nand\nk\nq\n\\textbf{k}^{q}\nrespectively. Now, the conditional expectation of\nT\nT\nis expressed as\n𝔼\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\,[\\,T\\,|\\,\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}\\,]=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2}.\n(18)\nUsing the law of total expectation, we have\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nT\n]\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\,[\\,T\\,]\n=\n𝔼\n[\n𝔼\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\,[\\,\\mathbb{E}\\,[\\,T\\,|\\,\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}\\,]\\,]\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n​\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n]\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2}\\right]\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n]\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\right]|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}\nClearly,\n𝔼\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\n=\n0\n\\mathbb{E}_{H_{0}}[T]=0\n. To find a lower bound for\n𝔼\nH\n1\n​\n[\nT\n]\n\\mathbb{E}_{H_{1}}[T]\n, we first note that\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n]\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\right]\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n0\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n]\n−\n2\n​\n∑\nk\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\n1\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n=\nk\n)\n​\nk\nk\n+\n1\n+\n1\n2\n​\nℙ\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\n1\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n=\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}^{0}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\right]-2\\sum_{k\\in[d]}\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}=1,k^{q}_{ij}=k)\\dfrac{k}{k+1}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}=1,k^{q}_{ij}=1)\n≥\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n0\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n]\n−\n2\n​\np\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\geq\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}^{0}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\right]-2p_{1}.\n(19)\nwhere\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n0\n=\n𝕀\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n>\n0\n)\n×\n𝕀\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n>\n0\n)\n\\mathbb{I}_{ij}^{0}=\\mathbb{I}(k^{p}_{ij}>0)\\times\\mathbb{I}(k^{q}_{ij}>0)\n. Furthermore, we see that for any event\nE\nE\n,\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n0\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n]\n≥\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n0\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n|\nE\n]\n​\nPr\n​\n(\nE\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}^{0}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\right]\\geq\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}^{0}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\;|\\,E\\,\\right]\\textnormal{Pr}(E).\n(20)\nWe define the event\nE\nE\nas\nμ\n−\nc\n​\nσ\n\\displaystyle\\mu-c\\sigma\n≤\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n≤\nμ\n+\nc\n​\nσ\n,\nand\n\\displaystyle\\leq k^{p}_{ij}\\leq\\mu+c\\sigma,\\textnormal{and}\n(21)\nμ\n−\nc\n​\nσ\n\\displaystyle\\mu-c\\sigma\n≤\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n≤\nμ\n+\nc\n​\nσ\n\\displaystyle\\leq k^{q}_{ij}\\leq\\mu+c\\sigma\nwith some constant\nc\n>\n1\nc>1\nsuch that\nμ\n−\nc\n​\nσ\n>\n0\n\\mu-c\\sigma>0\n.\nUsing Chebyshev’s inequality, we get that\nPr\n​\n(\nE\n)\n≥\n(\n1\n−\n1\nc\n2\n)\n2\n\\textnormal{Pr}(E)\\geq(1-\\frac{1}{c^{2}})^{2}\n. Finally, we combine (\n19\n), (\n20\n) and (\n21\n), to get\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n]\n≥\n(\nμ\n−\nc\n​\nσ\n)\n2\n2\n​\n(\nμ\n+\nc\n​\nσ\n)\n​\n(\n1\n−\n1\nc\n2\n)\n2\n−\n2\n​\np\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\right]\\geq\\dfrac{(\\mu-c\\sigma)^{2}}{2(\\mu+c\\sigma)}\\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{c^{2}}\\right)^{2}-2p_{1}.\n(22)\nSince\n𝒟\n{\\cal D}\nobeys the conditions in (\n9\n), we have\nμ\n≥\nc\n1\n​\np\n1\n\\mu\\geq c_{1}p_{1}\nand\nμ\n≥\nc\n2\n​\nσ\n\\mu\\geq c_{2}\\sigma\n. Therefore, there is a constant\nc\n>\n0\nc>0\nthat depends on\nc\n1\n,\nc\n2\nc_{1},c_{2}\n, such that\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nT\n]\n≥\nc\n​\nμ\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n\\mathbb{E}[T]\\geq c\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}\n.\nThis proves Lemma\n10\n.\n6.1.2\nProof of Lemma\n11\nTo analyse the variance of the test statistic\nT\nT\n, we note that pairwise-comparisons for each pair are obtained independently. This allows us to compute the variance for each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nseparately, as variance of sum is equal to the sum of variances. The following analysis of the variance of the test statistic\nT\nT\napplies under both the null and the alternate. The law of total variance states that\nVar\n[\nT\n]\n=\n𝔼\n[\nVar\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n+\nVar\n[\n𝔼\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}[T]=\\mathbb{E}[\\,\\mathrm{Var}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\\,]+\\mathrm{Var}[\\,\\mathbb{E}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\\,].\n(23)\nWe evaluated the term\nVar\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n\\mathrm{Var}{[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]}\n, present in the expression above, in Wolfram Mathematica. We show the output here,\nVar\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n2\n​\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n2\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n2\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n2\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\np\ni\n​\nj\n4\n​\n(\n3\n−\n2\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n)\nCLOSE\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\dfrac{2\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}(k^{p}_{ij}-1)k^{q}_{ij}(k^{q}_{ij}-1)}{(k^{p}_{ij}-1)^{2}(k^{q}_{ij}-1)^{2}(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})^{2}}\\Big(k^{q}_{ij}(k^{q}_{ij}-1)p_{ij}^{4}(3-2k^{p}_{ij})\n+\n2\n​\np\ni\n​\nj\n3\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\n−\n2\n+\n2\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n2\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n)\n\\displaystyle+2p_{ij}^{3}k^{q}_{ij}(k^{q}_{ij}-1)(-2+2q_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}-2q_{ij}+k^{p}_{ij})\n+\n2\n​\np\ni\n​\nj\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\n2\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n2\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n−\n2\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n\\displaystyle+2p_{ij}q_{ij}(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(k^{q}_{ij}-1)(1+2q_{ij}^{2}k^{p}_{ij}-q_{ij}-2q_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}+q_{ij}k^{q}_{ij})\n+\np\ni\n​\nj\n2\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\n2\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n−\n2\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n2\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n2\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle+p_{ij}^{2}(k^{q}_{ij}-1)(2q_{ij}(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(k^{p}_{ij}-1-2k^{q}_{ij})+k^{p}_{ij}-2q_{ij}^{2}(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}-1))\nOPEN\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n2\n​\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n−\n1\n)\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\n1\n−\n3\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n+\n2\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle-q_{ij}^{2}(q_{ij}-1)k^{p}_{ij}(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(1-3q_{ij}+2q_{ij}k^{q}_{ij})\\Big)\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n8\n​\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n2\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\n2\n​\np\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n)\nCLOSE\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\dfrac{8\\mathbb{I}_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})^{2}}\\Big(k^{p}_{ij}(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(k^{q}_{ij}-1)(2p_{ij}(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2})\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\n2\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n)\n\\displaystyle+k^{q}_{ij}(k^{q}_{ij}-1)(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(2q_{ij}(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2})\n+\n2\n​\np\ni\n​\nj\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\n1\n−\np\ni\n​\nj\n)\n​\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n\\displaystyle+2p_{ij}q_{ij}(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(k^{q}_{ij}-1)(1-p_{ij})(1-q_{ij})\nOPEN\n+\np\ni\n​\nj\n2\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\n1\n−\np\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n+\nq\ni\n​\nj\n2\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle+p_{ij}^{2}k^{q}_{ij}(k^{q}_{ij}-1)(1-p_{ij})^{2}+q_{ij}^{2}k^{p}_{ij}(k^{p}_{ij}-1)(1-q_{ij})^{2}\\Big).\n(24)\nApplying the trivial upper bound\np\ni\n​\nj\n≤\n1\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n≤\n1\n​\n∀\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\np_{ij}\\leq 1,q_{ij}\\leq 1\\;\\forall\\;(i,j)\n, we get\nVar\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n8\n​\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n​\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n+\n3\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}8\\mathbb{I}_{ij}\\left(\\dfrac{k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})}(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2}+3\\right)\n(25)\nFollowing this, we evaluate the first term on the right hand side of (\n23\n) as\n𝔼\n[\nVar\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n≤\n24\nd\n2\n+\n8\n|\n|\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n|\n|\nF\n2\n𝔼\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[\\,\\mathrm{Var}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\\,]\\leq 24d^{2}+8|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\right].\n(26)\nTo further simplify the upper bound in (\n26\n), we observe that\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n]\n≤\n1\n2\n​\n𝔼\n​\n[\nmax\n⁡\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n}\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\right]\\leq\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbb{E}[\\max\\{k^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\}].\n(27)\nWe exploit the independence of\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\nto get the CDF of\nmax\n⁡\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n}\n\\max\\{k^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\}\nas\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nmax\n⁡\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n}\n≤\nx\n)\n=\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n≤\nx\n)\n​\nℙ\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n≤\nx\n)\n.\n\\mathbb{P}(\\max\\{k^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\}\\leq x)=\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}\\leq x)\\mathbb{P}(k^{q}_{ij}\\leq x).\nThrough the CDF, we derive the PDF as\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nmax\n⁡\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n}\n=\nx\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(\\max\\{k^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\}=x)\n=\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nmax\n⁡\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n}\n≤\nx\n)\n−\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nmax\n⁡\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n}\n≤\nx\n−\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{P}(\\max\\{k^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\}\\leq x)-\\mathbb{P}(\\max\\{k^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\}\\leq x-1)\n=\nℙ\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n≤\nx\n)\n2\n−\nℙ\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n≤\nx\n−\n1\n)\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}\\leq x)^{2}-\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}\\leq x-1)^{2}\n=\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\nx\n)\n​\n(\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n≤\nx\n)\n+\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n≤\nx\n−\n1\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}=x)(\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}\\leq x)+\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}\\leq x-1))\n≤\n2\n​\nℙ\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\nx\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leq 2\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}=x)\n(28)\nWe substitute this inequality in (\n27\n) to get\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n]\n≤\nμ\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}\\right]\\leq\\mu.\n(29)\nAs a result, following from (\n26\n), we have\n𝔼\n[\nVar\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n≤\n24\nd\n2\n+\n8\nμ\n|\n|\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n|\n|\nF\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[\\,\\mathrm{Var}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\\,]\\leq 24d^{2}+8\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}.\n(30)\nNow, the remaining (second) term on the right hand side of (\n23\n) is\nVar\n[\n𝔼\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}[\\,\\mathbb{E}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\\,]\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\nVar\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n]\n​\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n4\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\mathrm{Var}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})}\\right](p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{4}\n≤\nVar\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n]\n​\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\mathrm{Var}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})}\\right]\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2}\n(31)\n≤\nVar\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n]\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\mathrm{Var}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})}\\right]|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}.\n(32)\nTo bound the variance term in the previous equation, we see that\nVar\n⁡\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})}\\right]\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n(\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n)\n2\n]\n−\n𝔼\n​\n(\n[\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n]\n)\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})}\\right)^{2}\\right]-\\mathbb{E}\\left(\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})}\\right]\\right)^{2}\n≤\n(\na\n)\n1\n4\n​\n𝔼\n​\n[\n(\nmax\n⁡\n{\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n}\n)\n2\n]\n−\nc\n​\nμ\n2\n\\displaystyle\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle(a)}}{{\\leq}}\\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left(\\max\\{k^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\\}\\right)^{2}\\right]-c\\mu^{2}\n≤\n(\nb\n)\n1\n2\n​\n𝔼\n​\n[\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n)\n2\n]\n−\nc\n​\nμ\n2\n\\displaystyle\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle(b)}}{{\\leq}}\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbb{E}[(k^{p}_{ij})^{2}]-c\\mu^{2}\n=\n1\n2\n​\n(\nμ\n2\n+\nσ\n2\n)\n−\nc\n​\nμ\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2}(\\mu^{2}+\\sigma^{2})-c\\mu^{2}\n≤\n(\nc\n)\n(\n1\n2\n+\n1\n2\n​\nc\n2\n2\n−\nc\n)\n​\nμ\n2\n=\nc\n′\n​\nμ\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle(c)}}{{\\leq}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2c_{2}^{2}}-c\\right)\\mu^{2}=c^{\\prime}\\mu^{2},\n(33)\nwhere inequality (a) follows from (\n22\n), inequality (b) follows similarly to the result in (\n28\n), and inequality (c) is a result of (\n9\n). Thus, the upper bound in (\n32\n) becomes\nVar\n[\n𝔼\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n≤\nc\n′\nμ\n2\n|\n|\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n|\n|\nF\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}[\\,\\mathbb{E}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\\,]\\leq c^{\\prime}\\mu^{2}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}.\n(34)\nFinally, we put together the terms in (\n23\n) by combining (\n30\n) and (\n34\n) to get the desired upper bound on the variance of the test statistic under the alternate hypothesis, which is\nVar\n⁡\n[\nT\n]\n≤\n24\n​\nd\n2\n+\n8\n​\nμ\n​\n|\n‖\nP\n−\nQ\n‖\n|\nF\n2\n+\nc\n′\n​\nμ\n2\n|\n​\n‖\nP\n−\nQ\n‖\n|\nF\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}{[T]}\\leq 24d^{2}+8\\mu|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}+c^{\\prime}\\mu^{2}|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}.\n(35)\nAdditionally, to obtain the upper bound on the variance of the test statistic under the null, we substitute\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}\nas zero in (\n35\n). This completes the proof of Lemma\n11\n.\n6.2\nProof of Theorem\n1\nIn this proof, we first specialise the statements of Lemma\n10\nand Lemma\n11\nto the per-pair fixed-design setup where\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n=\nk\n​\n∀\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nk^{p}_{ij}=k^{q}_{ij}=k\\;\\forall\\;(i,j)\\in[d]\n, for some positive integer\nk\n>\n1\nk>1\n. Under this setting, following from (\n18\n), we have\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nT\n]\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n1\n2\n​\n𝕀\n​\n(\nk\n>\n1\n)\n​\nk\n​\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n=\n1\n2\n​\nk\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[T]=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbb{I}(k>1)k(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}k|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}.\n(36)\nSimilarly, we note that in (\n23\n) we have that\nVar\n[\n𝔼\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n=\n0\n\\mathrm{Var}[\\,\\mathbb{E}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\\,]=0\n, which in combination with (\n26\n) implies that\nVar\n⁡\n[\nT\n]\n≤\n24\n​\nd\n2\n+\n4\n​\nk\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}{[T]}\\leq 24d^{2}+4k|\\!|\\!|P-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}.\n(37)\nNow, invoking Chebyshev’s inequality as described in (\n14\n) and (\n15\n) to control Type I and Type II error at level\n1\n6\n\\frac{1}{6}\n, we set the threshold as\n11\n​\nd\n11d\nto get the sufficient condition as\nϵ\n2\n≥\nc\nk\n​\nd\n\\displaystyle\\epsilon^{2}\\geq\\frac{c}{kd}\n(38)\nfor some positive constant\nc\nc\n. This proves Theorem\n1\n.\n6.3\nProof of converse results\nIn this section we prove all the claims made in Section\n3.2\n. We begin with some background.\n6.3.1\nPreliminaries for proof of lower bound\nWe begin by briefly introducing the lower bound technique applied in Theorem\n3\nand Theorem\n5\n. The main objective of the proof is to construct a set of null and alternate such that the minimax risk of testing defined in (\n2\n) is lower bounded by a constant. To lower bound the minimax risk, we analyse the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndistance between the resulting distributions of the null and the alternate. We construct the null and alternate as follows. Let\nP\n0\n=\n[\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP_{0}=[\\frac{1}{2}]^{d\\times d}\n. Under the null, we fix\nP\n=\nQ\n=\nP\n0\nP=Q=P_{0}\nand under the alternate,\nP\n=\nP\n0\n,\nQ\n∈\nΘ\nP=P_{0},Q\\in\\Theta\nwhere\nΘ\n\\Theta\nis a set of matrices from the model class\nℳ\n\\mathcal{M}\nto be defined subsequently. We assume a uniform probability measure over\nΘ\n\\Theta\n. The set\nΘ\n\\Theta\nis chosen such that\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n0\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n=\nϵ\n\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|P_{0}-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}=\\epsilon\nfor all\nQ\n∈\nΘ\nQ\\in\\Theta\n.\nIn our problem setup, we observe matrices\nX\n,\nY\nX,Y\nwherein each element is the outcome of\nk\nk\nobservations of the corresponding Bernoulli random variable. For each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n, we have\nX\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\n,\np\ni\n​\nj\n)\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nX_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k,p_{ij}),Y_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k,q_{ij})\n. For simplicity of notation, we will denote the matrix distribution corresponding to the pairwise-comparison probability matrix\nP\n0\nP_{0}\nby\nℙ\n0\n\\mathbb{P}_{0}\n, that is,\nX\n∼\nℙ\n0\nX\\sim\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nwhen\nP\n=\nP\n0\nP=P_{0}\n, and\nY\n∼\nℙ\n0\nY\\sim\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nwhen\nQ\n=\nP\n0\nQ=P_{0}\n. For the case where\nY\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nY_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k,q_{ij})\nand\nQ\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\nQ\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)\n, we denote the resulting matrix distribution as\nY\n∼\nℙ\nΘ\nY\\sim\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta}\n. We now have all the parts required to derive the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence between the null and the alternate defined in this section.\nThe\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence between the distribution of\nX\n,\nY\nX,Y\nunder the null and the distirbution of\nX\n,\nY\nX,Y\nunder the alternate is given by\nχ\n2\n​\n(\n(\nX\n,\nY\n)\nH\n0\n,\n(\nX\n,\nY\n)\nH\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}((X,Y)_{H_{0}},(X,Y)_{H_{1}})\n=\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nX\nH\n0\n,\nX\nH\n1\n)\n+\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nY\nH\n0\n,\nY\nH\n1\n)\n+\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nX\nH\n0\n,\nX\nH\n1\n)\n​\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nY\nH\n0\n,\nY\nH\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\chi^{2}(X_{H_{0}},X_{H_{1}})+\\chi^{2}(Y_{H_{0}},Y_{H_{1}})+\\chi^{2}(X_{H_{0}},X_{H_{1}})\\chi^{2}(Y_{H_{0}},Y_{H_{1}})\n=\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\n0\n)\n+\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n+\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\n0\n)\n​\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{0})+\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})+\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{0})\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\n=\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta}).\n(39)\nThis reduces our two-sample testing problem into a goodness of fit testing problem for the given model class, where the null distribution is given by\nℙ\n0\n\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nand the alternate distribution is given by\nℙ\nΘ\n\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta}\n. This goodness of fit testing problem is written as\nH\n0\n:\nP\n=\nP\n0\nH\n1\n:\nP\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\begin{split}H_{0}&:P=P_{0}\\\\\nH_{1}&:P\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta),\\end{split}\n(40)\nwhere\nP\n0\n=\n[\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP_{0}=\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\right]^{d\\times d}\n.\nContinuing with the reduction in (\n39\n) and (\n40\n), Le Cam’s method for testing states that the minimax risk (\n2\n) for the hypothesis testing problem in (\n40\n), is lower bounded as (Lemma 3 in\nCollier et al., 2017\n)\nℛ\nℳ\n≥\n1\n2\n​\n(\n1\n−\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{R}_{\\mathcal{M}}\\geq\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\sqrt{\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})}\\right).\n(41)\nTherefore, if the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence is upper bounded by some constant\nc\n<\n1\nc<1\n, then no algorithm can correctly distinguish between the null and the alternate with probability of error less than\n1\n2\n​\n(\n1\n−\nc\n)\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\sqrt{c})\n. Consequently, by deriving the value of\nϵ\n\\epsilon\ncorresponding to\nc\n=\n1\n9\nc=\\frac{1}{9}\n, we will get the desired lower bound on the critical radius defined in (\n3\n) for the two-sample testing problem in (\n1\n).\nWe now delve into the technical part of the proof in which we derive the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence between\nℙ\n0\n\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nand\nℙ\nΘ\n\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta}\n.\nFor a probability distribution\nℙ\n0\n\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nand a mixture probability measure\nℙ\nΘ\n\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta}\n, we know (from Lemma 7 in\nCarpentier et al., 2018\n) that\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n=\n𝔼\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n​\n(\n∫\nd\n​\nℙ\nQ\n​\nd\n​\nℙ\nQ\n′\nd\n​\nℙ\n0\n)\n−\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})=\\mathbb{E}_{(Q,Q^{\\prime})\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)}\\left(\\int\\dfrac{d\\mathbb{P}_{Q}d\\mathbb{P}_{Q^{\\prime}}}{d\\mathbb{P}_{0}}\\right)-1.\n(42)\nHere\n𝔼\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n\\mathbb{E}_{(Q,Q^{\\prime})\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)}\ndenotes the expectation with respect to the distribution of the pair\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n(Q,Q^{\\prime})\nwhere\nQ\nQ\nand\nQ\n′\nQ^{\\prime}\nare sampled independently and uniformly at random from the set\nΘ\n\\Theta\n(with replacement). According to the null and alternate construction described in the beginning of this section, recall that\nX\n∼\nℙ\n0\nX\\sim\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nimplies that\nX\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\n,\n1\n2\n)\n​\n∀\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\nX_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k,\\frac{1}{2})\\;\\forall\\;(i,j)\n. Similarly\nX\n∼\nℙ\nQ\nX\\sim\\mathbb{P}_{Q}\nimplies that\nX\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n​\n∀\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\nX_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k,q_{ij})\\;\\forall\\;(i,j)\n.\nWith this information, we simplify the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence as\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n=\n𝔼\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n​\n∑\nv\n∈\nV\n(\n∏\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∏\nj\n=\n1\nd\n(\nk\nv\ni\n​\nj\n)\nq\ni\n​\nj\nv\ni\n​\nj\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nk\n−\nv\ni\n​\nj\n(\nk\nv\ni\n​\nj\n)\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nv\ni\n​\nj\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nk\n−\nv\ni\n​\nj\n(\nk\nv\ni\n​\nj\n)\n​\n(\n1\n2\n)\nk\n)\n−\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})=\\mathbb{E}_{(Q,Q^{\\prime})\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)}\\sum_{v\\in V}\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^{d}\\prod_{j=1}^{d}\\dfrac{\\binom{k}{v_{ij}}q_{ij}^{v_{ij}}(1-q_{ij})^{k-v_{ij}}\\binom{k}{v_{ij}}(q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{v_{ij}}(1-q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{k-v_{ij}}}{\\binom{k}{v_{ij}}(\\frac{1}{2})^{k}}\\right)-1.\n(43)\nwhere\nV\n∈\nℝ\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\nV\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d(d-1)}\nis the set of all possible vectors with each element belonging to the set\n{\n0\n,\n1\n,\n⋯\n,\nk\n}\n\\{0,1,\\cdots,k\\}\n. There are\n(\nk\n+\n1\n)\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\n(k+1)^{d(d-1)}\nsuch vectors. We further simplify the summation over\nV\nV\nas\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n=\n𝔼\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n​\n∏\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∏\nj\n=\n1\nd\n(\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\nk\nℓ\n)\nq\ni\n​\nj\nℓ\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nℓ\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n(\n1\n2\n)\nk\n)\n−\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})=\\mathbb{E}_{(Q,Q^{\\prime})\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)}\\prod_{i=1}^{d}\\prod_{j=1}^{d}\\left(\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\dfrac{\\binom{k}{\\ell}q_{ij}^{\\ell}(1-q_{ij})^{k-\\ell}(q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{\\ell}(1-q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{k-\\ell}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{k}}\\right)-1.\n(44)\nThis gives us the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence for the construction defined in terms of the elements of the matrices in the set\nΘ\n\\Theta\n. Later, we will see that the set\nΘ\n\\Theta\ndesigned for the different modeling assumptions considered (namely, MST and parameter-based) consist solely of matrices with entries from the set\n{\n1\n2\n−\nη\n,\n1\n2\n,\n1\n2\n+\nη\n}\n\\{\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\\}\n. This information enables us to further simplify the expression for\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\n.\nConsider a pair of matrices\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n(Q,Q^{\\prime})\nsampled uniformly at random from the set\nΘ\n\\Theta\n. Let an agreement be defined as the occurrence of\n1\n2\n+\nη\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\n(or\n1\n2\n−\nη\n\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta\n) in the same position in\nQ\nQ\nand\nQ\n′\nQ^{\\prime}\nand a disagreement is defined as the occurrence of\n1\n2\n+\nη\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\nin\nQ\nQ\nor\nQ\n′\nQ^{\\prime}\nin the same position as\n1\n2\n−\nη\n\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta\nin\nQ\n′\nQ^{\\prime}\nor\nQ\nQ\nrespectively. Next, we define two statistics\nb\n1\nb_{1}\nand\nb\n2\nb_{2}\nthat quantify the number of agreements and disagreements, respectively, in the matrix pair\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n(Q,Q^{\\prime})\nas shown here\nb\n1\n​\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n[\n𝟙\n{\nq\ni\n​\nj\n=\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n=\n1\n2\n+\nη\n}\n+\n𝟙\n{\nq\ni\n​\nj\n=\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n=\n1\n2\n−\nη\n}\n]\n,\nb\n2\n​\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n[\n𝟙\n{\nq\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n2\n+\nη\n}\n𝟙\n{\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n=\n1\n2\n−\nη\n}\n+\n𝟙\n{\nq\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n2\n−\nη\n}\n𝟙\n{\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n=\n1\n2\n+\nη\n}\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle\\begin{split}b_{1}(Q,Q^{\\prime})&=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\left[\\mathbbm{1}_{\\{q_{ij}=q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\\}}+\\mathbbm{1}_{\\{q_{ij}=q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta\\}}\\right],\\\\\nb_{2}(Q,Q^{\\prime})&=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\left[\\mathbbm{1}_{\\{q_{ij}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\\}}\\mathbbm{1}_{\\{q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta\\}}+\\mathbbm{1}_{\\{q_{ij}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta\\}}\\mathbbm{1}_{\\{q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\\}}\\right].\\end{split}\n(45)\nUsing these definitions, we state the following Lemma to analyse\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\nin (\n44\n).\nLemma 12\n.\nConsider two pairwise-comparison probability matrices\nQ\nQ\nand\nQ\n′\nQ^{\\prime}\nwith\nq\ni\n​\nj\n∈\n{\n1\n2\n−\nη\n,\n1\n2\n,\n1\n2\n+\nη\n}\nq_{ij}\\in\\{\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\\}\nand\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n∈\n{\n1\n2\n−\nη\n,\n1\n2\n,\n1\n2\n+\nη\n}\nq_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}}\\in\\{\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\\}\n. Suppose\nb\n1\n​\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n=\nb\n1\nb_{1}(Q,Q^{\\prime})=b_{1}\nand\nb\n2\n​\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n=\nb\n2\nb_{2}(Q,Q^{\\prime})=b_{2}\n. Then, we have\n∏\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∏\nj\n=\n1\nd\n(\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\nk\nℓ\n)\nq\ni\n​\nj\nℓ\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nℓ\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n(\n1\n2\n)\nk\n)\n≤\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n−\nb\n2\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^{d}\\prod_{j=1}^{d}\\left(\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\dfrac{\\binom{k}{\\ell}q_{ij}^{\\ell}(1-q_{ij})^{k-\\ell}(q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{\\ell}(1-q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{k-\\ell}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{k}}\\right)\\leq(1+4\\eta^{2})^{k(b_{1}-b_{2})}.\n(46)\nThe proof is provided at the end of this subsection. Using Lemma\n12\nand (\n44\n), we get an upper bound on the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence as\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n≤\n𝔼\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n​\n[\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n−\nb\n2\n)\n]\n−\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\\leq\\mathbb{E}_{(Q,Q^{\\prime})\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)}\\left[(1+4\\eta^{2})^{k(b_{1}-b_{2})}\\right]-1.\n(47)\nWe conclude the background section on the converse results here. We will use the equations discussed in this section to derive the lower bound for the different modeling assumptions in Theorem\n3\nand Theorem\n5\nin their respective proofs.\nProof of Lemma\n12\nLet\nG\n⁡\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n=\n∏\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∏\nj\n=\n1\nd\n(\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\nk\nℓ\n)\nq\ni\n​\nj\nℓ\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nℓ\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n(\n1\n2\n)\nk\n)\n−\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle G(Q,Q^{\\prime})=\\prod_{i=1}^{d}\\prod_{j=1}^{d}\\left(\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\dfrac{\\binom{k}{\\ell}q_{ij}^{\\ell}(1-q_{ij})^{k-\\ell}(q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{\\ell}(1-q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{k-\\ell}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{k}}\\right)-1.\n(48)\nLet\ng\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\ng(q_{ij},q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})\ndenote the summation in the equation above, that is\ng\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\n=\n2\nk\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\nk\nℓ\n)\nq\ni\n​\nj\nℓ\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nℓ\n(\n1\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n.\n\\displaystyle g(q_{ij},q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})=2^{k}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\binom{k}{\\ell}q_{ij}^{\\ell}(1-q_{ij})^{k-\\ell}(q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{\\ell}(1-q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})^{k-\\ell}.\n(49)\nNotice that if\nq\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n2\nq_{ij}=\\frac{1}{2}\nor\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n=\n1\n2\nq_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}}=\\frac{1}{2}\nthen\ng\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\n=\n1\ng(q_{ij},q_{ij}^{{}^{\\prime}})=1\n. Additionally,\ng\n⁡\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n,\n1\n2\n+\nη\n)\n=\ng\n⁡\n(\n1\n2\n−\nη\n,\n1\n2\n−\nη\n)\ng(\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta,\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta)=g(\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta,\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta)\nand\ng\n⁡\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n,\n1\n2\n+\nη\n)\n=\n\\displaystyle g\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta,\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\\right)=\n2\nk\n​\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n)\n2\n​\nℓ\n​\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\n1\n2\n−\nη\n)\n2\n​\nk\n−\n2\n​\nℓ\n\\displaystyle 2^{k}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\\right)^{2\\ell}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta\\right)^{2k-2\\ell}\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n2\nk\n​\n(\n1\n2\n+\n2\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n​\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\nk\nℓ\n)\n​\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n1\n2\n+\n2\n​\nη\n2\n)\nℓ\n​\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n1\n2\n−\n2\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n\\displaystyle 2^{k}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+2\\eta^{2}\\right)^{k}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\binom{k}{\\ell}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{\\eta}{\\frac{1}{2}+2\\eta^{2}}\\right)^{\\ell}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{\\eta}{\\frac{1}{2}-2\\eta^{2}}\\right)^{k-\\ell}\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n.\n\\displaystyle(1+4\\eta^{2})^{k}.\n(50)\nAlso, note that\ng\n⁡\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n,\n1\n2\n−\nη\n)\n=\ng\n⁡\n(\n1\n2\n−\nη\n,\n1\n2\n+\nη\n)\ng(\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta,\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta)=g(\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta,\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta)\nand\ng\n⁡\n(\n1\n2\n−\nη\n,\n1\n2\n+\nη\n)\n\\displaystyle g(\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta,\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta)\n=\n2\nk\n​\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\nk\nℓ\n)\n​\n(\n1\n2\n−\nη\n)\nℓ\n​\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n​\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n)\nℓ\n​\n(\n1\n2\n−\nη\n)\nk\n−\nℓ\n\\displaystyle=2^{k}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\binom{k}{\\ell}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta)^{\\ell}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta)^{k-\\ell}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta)^{\\ell}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta)^{k-\\ell}\n=\n(\n1\n2\n−\n2\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n​\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\nk\nℓ\n)\n\\displaystyle=(\\frac{1}{2}-2\\eta^{2})^{k}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\binom{k}{\\ell}\n=\n(\n1\n−\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n.\n\\displaystyle=(1-4\\eta^{2})^{k}.\n(51)\nTherefore, if the pair of matrices\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\nQ,Q^{\\prime}\nhave\nb\n1\nb_{1}\nagreements and\nb\n2\nb_{2}\ndisagreements, then using (\n50\n), (\n51\n) we get\nG\n⁡\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n=\n\\displaystyle G(Q,Q^{\\prime})=\ng\n​\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n,\n1\n2\n+\nη\n)\nb\n1\n​\ng\n​\n(\n1\n2\n+\nη\n,\n1\n2\n−\nη\n)\nb\n2\n\\displaystyle g\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta,\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\\right)^{b_{1}}g\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta,\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta\\right)^{b_{2}}\n=\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n​\nb\n1\n​\n(\n1\n−\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n​\nb\n2\n\\displaystyle=(1+4\\eta^{2})^{kb_{1}}(1-4\\eta^{2})^{kb_{2}}\n≤\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n−\nb\n2\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leq(1+4\\eta^{2})^{k(b_{1}-b_{2})}.\nThis proves Lemma\n12\n.\n6.3.2\nProof of Proposition\n4\nIn this section, we provide a construction of the null and the alternate in (\n1\n) under the model-free assumption that proves the statement of Proposition\n4\n. To this end, let\nP\n0\nP_{0}\nbe a pairwise probability matrix with the\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\nt\n​\nh\n(i,j)^{th}\nelement denoted by\np\ni\n​\nj\np_{ij}\nfor all\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\ni,j\\in[d]\n. We will provide more details about\nP\n0\nP_{0}\nsubsequently. Consider the case where under the null\nP\n=\nQ\n=\nP\n0\nP=Q=P_{0}\nand under the alternate\nP\n=\nP\n0\nP=P_{0}\nand\nQ\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nP\n0\n)\nQ\\sim\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(P_{0})\n. Under this notation, we have that under the alternate\nq\ni\n​\nj\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n)\nq_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(p_{ij})\n. We choose\nP\n0\nP_{0}\nsuch that for all\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\ni,j\\in[d]\nwe have\n0\n≤\np\ni\n​\nj\n≤\n1\n2\n0\\leq p_{ij}\\leq\\frac{1}{2}\n. With this, we argue that under the alternate construction, for any realization of\nQ\nQ\n, we have\n‖\n|\nP\n0\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n≥\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\np\ni\n​\nj\n2\n.\n|\\!|\\!|P_{0}-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}\\geq\\sum_{i=1}^{d}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}p_{ij}^{2}.\nIn this manner, by choosing an appropriate\nP\n0\nP_{0}\n, we construct the alternate for any given\nϵ\n\\epsilon\nwhich satisfy the conditions in the two-sample testing problem in (\n1\n). Note that the maximum value of\n‖\n|\nP\n0\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n|\\!|\\!|P_{0}-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}\nattainable in this construction is when\np\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n2\np_{ij}=\\frac{1}{2}\nfor all\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n∈\n[\nd\n]\n(i,j)\\in[d]\n. In this setting,\n‖\n|\nP\n0\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n=\nd\n2\n4\n|\\!|\\!|P_{0}-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}=\\frac{d^{2}}{4}\n, for all realizations of\nQ\nQ\n. Thus, in our construction, the parameter\nϵ\n\\epsilon\nis at most\n1\n2\n\\frac{1}{2}\n.\nNow, in Proposition\n4\n, we consider the case where we have one pairwise-comparison for each pair in each population, that is,\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n=\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n=\n1\n​\n∀\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nk^{p}_{ij}=k^{q}_{ij}=1\\;\\forall\\;i,j\\in[d]\n. Recall that the observed matrices corresponding to the two populations are denoted by\nX\nX\nand\nY\nY\nwhich are distributed as\nX\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nP\n)\nX\\sim\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(P)\nand\nY\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nQ\n)\nY\\sim\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(Q)\n. Now, for our construction, we see that\nX\nX\nis distributed identically under the null and the alternate as\nX\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nP\n0\n)\nX\\sim\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(P_{0})\n, and\nY\nY\nis distributed as\nY\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nP\n0\n)\nY\\sim\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(P_{0})\nunder the null and\nY\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nQ\n)\nY\\sim\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(Q)\nunder the alternate. Thus, to distinguish between the null and the alternate, we must be able to distinguish between the product distribution\nℙ\n0\n≔\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nP\n0\n)\n×\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nP\n0\n)\n\\mathbb{P}_{0}\\coloneqq\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(P_{0})\\times\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(P_{0})\nand the product distribution\nℙ\n1\n≔\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nP\n0\n)\n×\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nQ\n)\n\\mathbb{P}_{1}\\coloneqq\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(P_{0})\\times\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(Q)\nwhere\nQ\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nP\n0\n)\nQ\\sim\\textnormal{Bernoulli}(P_{0})\n.\nFor the setting with one comparison per pair, we have access to only first order statistics for matrices\nX\nX\nand\nY\nY\n. Since the Bernoulli parameters for all pairs\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nare independently chosen under the model-free setting, we look at the first order statistics of any pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n, which are given by\nPr\n​\n(\nX\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n,\nPr\n​\n(\nY\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n,\nPr\n​\n(\nX\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n\\textnormal{Pr}(X_{ij}=1),\\textnormal{Pr}(Y_{ij}=1),\\textnormal{Pr}(X_{ij}=1,Y_{ij}=1)\n. Now, observe that under both the distributions\nℙ\n0\n\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nand\nℙ\n1\n\\mathbb{P}_{1}\nwe have that\nPr\n​\n(\nX\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n=\np\ni\n​\nj\n;\n,\nPr\n​\n(\nY\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n=\np\ni\n​\nj\n;\nPr\n​\n(\nX\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n,\nY\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n=\np\ni\n​\nj\n2\n.\n\\textnormal{Pr}(X_{ij}=1)=p_{ij};\\;\\;{},\\textnormal{Pr}(Y_{ij}=1)=p_{ij};\\;\\;\\textnormal{Pr}(X_{ij}=1,Y_{ij}=1)=p_{ij}^{2}.\nSince the first order statistics under both distributions\nℙ\n0\n\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nand\nℙ\n1\n\\mathbb{P}_{1}\nare identical, we conclude that no algorithm can distinguish between these distributions with a probability of error less than half. In turn, the minimax risk defined in (\n2\n) is at least half. This proves Proposition\n4\n.\n6.3.3\nProof of Theorem\n3\nIn this section, we establish a lower bound on the critical radius (\n3\n) for the two-sample testing problem defined in (\n1\n) under the assumption of the MST class as stated in Theorem\n3\n. First, we provide a construction for the null and the alternate in Section\n6.3.1\n. In this construction, we set\nP\n=\nQ\n=\nP\n0\nP=Q=P_{0}\nunder the null and\nP\n=\nP\n0\n,\nQ\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\nP=P_{0},Q\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)\nunder the alternate where\nΘ\n\\Theta\nis a set of matrices belonging to the MST class. To complete the description of the construction, we now describe the set\nΘ\n\\Theta\nfor the MST class of pairwise-comparison probability matrices. The probability matrices in\nΘ\n\\Theta\ncorrespond to a fixed ranking of items. Each matrix in\nΘ\n\\Theta\nis such that the upper right quadrant has exactly one element in each row and each column equal to\n1\n2\n+\nη\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\nfor some\nη\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n2\n)\n\\eta\\in(0,\\frac{1}{2})\n. The rest of the elements above the diagonal are half. The elements below the diagonal follow from the shifted-skew-symmetry condition imposed on MST probability matrices. It can be verified that all matrices\nQ\n∈\nΘ\nQ\\in\\Theta\nlie in the MST class. Note that the set\nΘ\n\\Theta\nhas\n(\nd\n/\n2\n)\n!\n(d/2)!\nmatrices. Since each matrix has a total of\nd\nd\nelements equal to\n1\n2\n±\nη\n\\frac{1}{2}\\pm\\eta\n, we get that\n1\nd\n2\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n0\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n2\n=\nϵ\n2\n=\nη\n2\nd\n\\frac{1}{d^{2}}|\\!|\\!|P_{0}-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}^{2}=\\epsilon^{2}=\\frac{\\eta^{2}}{d}\n. This implies\nϵ\n2\n≤\n1\n4\n​\nd\n\\epsilon^{2}\\leq\\frac{1}{4d}\n.\nNow, to derive bounds on the minimax risk according to (\n41\n), we analyse the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence between\nℙ\n0\n\\mathbb{P}_{0}\nand\nℙ\nΘ\n\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta}\n. From the analysis of\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\nin Section\n6.3.1\n, specifically (\n47\n), we have that\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\n≤\n𝔼\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n​\n[\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n−\nb\n2\n)\n]\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\mathbb{E}_{(Q,Q^{\\prime})\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)}\\left[(1+4\\eta^{2})^{k(b_{1}-b_{2})}\\right]-1\nwhere\nb\n1\nb_{1}\nand\nb\n2\nb_{2}\nare the number of agreements and disagreements between the matrices\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\nQ,Q^{\\prime}\n, as defined in (\n45\n).\nNow, to compute the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence, we want to find the probability that two matrices picked uniformly at random from\nΘ\n\\Theta\nhave\ni\ni\nagreements in the upper right quadrant(the total number of agreements is\n2\n​\ni\n2i\ndue to shifted-skew-symmetry). Given a matrix\nQ\nQ\nfrom set\nΘ\n\\Theta\n, for\ni\ni\nagreements, we want to choose a matrix\nQ\n′\n∈\nΘ\nQ^{\\prime}\\in\\Theta\nsuch that exactly\ni\ni\nof the perturbed elements share the same position as in\nQ\nQ\n. There are\n(\nd\n/\n2\ni\n)\n\\binom{d/2}{i}\nways of choosing the\ni\ni\nelements. Now that the\ni\ni\nelements have their position fixed, we have to find the number of ways we can rearrange the remaining\nd\n2\n−\ni\n\\frac{d}{2}-i\nelements such that none of them share a position with the remaining perturbed elements in\nQ\nQ\n. This problem is the same as reshuffling and matching envelopes with letters such that no letter matches with originally intended envelope. The number of ways to rearrange a set of\ni\ni\nobjects in such a manner is given by\ni\n!\n​\n(\n1\n2\n!\n−\n1\n3\n!\n+\n⋯\n+\n(\n−\n1\n)\ni\n​\n1\ni\n!\n)\ni!(\\frac{1}{2!}-\\frac{1}{3!}+\\cdots+(-1)^{i}\\frac{1}{i!})\n. Thus the number of ways of rearrangement for\nd\n2\n−\ni\n\\frac{d}{2}-i\nitems is upper bounded by\n1\n2\n​\n(\nd\n/\n2\n−\ni\n)\n!\n\\frac{1}{2}(d/2-i)!\n. Thus, the probability of\n2\n​\ni\n2i\nagreements is upper bounded as\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n=\n2\n​\ni\n)\n≤\n(\nd\n/\n2\n)\n!\n(\nd\n/\n2\n−\ni\n)\n!\n​\ni\n!\n​\n(\nd\n/\n2\n−\ni\n)\n!\n2\n​\n(\nd\n/\n2\n)\n!\n≤\n1\n2\n​\n(\ni\n)\n!\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(b_{1}=2i)\\leq\\dfrac{(d/2)!}{(d/2-i)!i!}\\dfrac{(d/2-i)!}{2(d/2)!}\\leq\\dfrac{1}{2(i)!}.\n(52)\nThen, we further simplify (\n47\n) as\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\n≤\n𝔼\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n​\n[\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n−\nb\n2\n)\n]\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\mathbb{E}_{(Q,Q^{\\prime})\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)}\\left[(1+4\\eta^{2})^{k(b_{1}-b_{2})}\\right]-1\n≤\n𝔼\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n​\n[\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n​\nb\n1\n]\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\mathbb{E}_{(Q,Q^{\\prime})\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)}\\left[(1+4\\eta^{2})^{kb_{1}}\\right]-1\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nd\n/\n2\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n=\n2\n​\ni\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\n2\n​\nk\n​\ni\n−\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=0}^{d/2}\\mathbb{P}(b_{1}=2i)(1+4\\eta^{2})^{2ki}-1.\n(53)\nNotice that if we choose\nk\n=\nc\n4\n​\nη\n2\nk=\\dfrac{c}{4\\eta^{2}}\nwith some constant\nc\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\nc\\in(0,1)\nthen we have that\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n\\displaystyle(1+4\\eta^{2})^{k}\n=\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nk\n(\nk\nℓ\n)\n​\n(\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nℓ\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{k}\\binom{k}{\\ell}(4\\eta^{2})^{\\ell}\n≤\n1\n+\n∑\nℓ\n=\n1\nk\n(\n4\n​\nη\n2\n​\nk\n)\nℓ\n\\displaystyle\\leq 1+\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{k}(4\\eta^{2}k)^{\\ell}\n≤\n1\n+\n∑\nℓ\n=\n1\nk\nc\nℓ\n\\displaystyle\\leq 1+\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{k}c^{\\ell}\n≤\n1\n+\nc\n′\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leq 1+c^{\\prime},\nwhere\nc\n′\nc^{\\prime}\nis some positive constant. Using this, we show that the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence in (\n53\n) is upper bounded by a constant for\nk\n≤\nc\nη\n2\nk\\leq\\dfrac{c}{\\eta^{2}}\n, as follows\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nd\n/\n2\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n=\n2\n​\ni\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\n2\n​\nk\n​\ni\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=0}^{d/2}\\mathbb{P}(b_{1}=2i)(1+4\\eta^{2})^{2ki}-1\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nd\n/\n2\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n=\n2\n​\ni\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\nc\n′\n)\n2\n​\ni\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=0}^{d/2}\\mathbb{P}(b_{1}=2i)(1+c^{\\prime})^{2i}-1\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nd\n/\n2\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n=\n2\n​\ni\n)\n−\n1\n+\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nd\n/\n2\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n=\n2\n​\ni\n)\n​\n(\n(\n1\n+\nc\n′\n)\n2\n​\ni\n−\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=0}^{d/2}\\mathbb{P}(b_{1}=2i)-1+\\sum_{i=0}^{d/2}\\mathbb{P}(b_{1}=2i)((1+c^{\\prime})^{2i}-1)\n≤\n(\na\n)\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nd\n/\n2\n1\n2\n​\n(\ni\n!\n)\n​\n(\n(\n1\n+\nc\n′\n)\n2\n​\ni\n−\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle(a)}}{{\\leq}}\\sum_{i=0}^{d/2}\\dfrac{1}{2(i!)}((1+c^{\\prime})^{2i}-1)\n≤\n1\n2\n​\n(\nexp\n⁡\n(\n(\n1\n+\nc\n′\n)\n2\n)\n−\nexp\n⁡\n(\n1\n)\n+\n∑\ni\n=\nd\n/\n2\n∞\n1\ni\n!\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\exp((1+c^{\\prime})^{2})-\\exp(1)+\\sum_{i=d/2}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{1}{i!}\\right)\n≤\nc\n′′\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leq c^{\\prime\\prime},\nwhere\nc\n′′\nc^{\\prime\\prime}\nis some positive constant. The inequality (a) follows from (\n52\n).\nThis proves that there exists a constant\nc\nc\n, such that if\nk\n≤\nc\nη\n2\n=\nc\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\nk\\leq\\dfrac{c}{\\eta^{2}}=\\dfrac{c}{d\\epsilon^{2}}\n, then the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence is upper bounded by\n1\n9\n\\frac{1}{9}\n. According to (\n41\n), this implies that the minimax risk is at least\n1\n3\n\\frac{1}{3}\n.\nThis establishes the lower bound on the critical testing radius for two-sample testing under the MST modeling assumption as\nϵ\nℳ\n2\n>\nc\n​\n1\nk\n​\nd\n\\epsilon^{2}_{\\mathcal{M}}>c\\dfrac{1}{kd}\nand proves Theorem\n3\n.\n6.3.4\nProof of Theorem\n5\nConsider any arbitrary non-decreasing function\nf\n:\nℝ\n→\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nf:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow[0,1]\nsuch that\nf\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n=\n1\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\n−\nθ\n)\n​\n∀\nθ\n∈\nℝ\nf(\\theta)=1-f(-\\theta)~~~\\forall\\ \\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}\n. In order to prove the lower bound on testing radius stated in Theorem\n5\n, we construct a set of matrices\nΘ\n\\Theta\nbased on the parameter-based pairwise-comparison probability model described in (\n4\n) associated to the given function\nf\nf\n. Observe that\nf\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n=\n1\n2\nf(0)=\\frac{1}{2}\n. Recall that under the parameter-based model the sum of all weights is fixed as\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nd\nw\ni\n=\n0\n\\sum_{i=1}^{d}w_{i}=0\n. We use the weight parameter to define the construction for the lower bound.\nRecall the null and alternate construction described in Section\n6.3.1\nto prove the lower bound. Accordingly, we set\nP\n=\nQ\n=\nP\n0\nP=Q=P_{0}\nunder the null and\nP\n=\nP\n0\n,\nQ\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\nP=P_{0},Q\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)\nunder the alternate where\nΘ\n\\Theta\nis a set of matrices belonging to the parameter-based class. The weights\nw\nP\n0\n=\n[\n0\n,\n⋯\n,\n0\n]\n∈\nℝ\nd\nw_{P_{0}}=[0,\\cdots,0]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\ncorrespond to the pairwise probability matrix\nP\n0\n=\n[\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP_{0}=[\\frac{1}{2}]^{d\\times d}\n. Now for creating the set\nΘ\n\\Theta\n, consider a collection of weight vectors\nw\nΘ\nw_{\\Theta}\neach with half the entries as\nδ\n\\delta\nand the other half as\n−\nδ\n-\\delta\n, thereby ensuring that\n∑\ni\n∈\n[\nd\n]\nw\ni\n=\n0\n\\sum_{i\\in[d]}w_{i}=0\n. We set\nδ\n\\delta\nto ensure that each of the probability matrices induced by this collection of vectors obey\n1\nd\n​\n‖\n|\nP\n0\n−\nQ\n|\n‖\nF\n=\nϵ\n\\frac{1}{d}|\\!|\\!|P_{0}-Q|\\!|\\!|_{{\\mbox{\\tiny{F}}}}=\\epsilon\n. We define the set of matrices\nΘ\n\\Theta\nas the set of pairwise-comparison probability matrices induced by the collection of values of\nw\nΘ\nw_{\\Theta}\n. Clearly, there are\n(\nd\nd\n/\n2\n)\n\\binom{d}{d/2}\nmatrices in\nΘ\n\\Theta\n. A similar argument holds for odd\nd\nd\nwherein\nd\n−\n1\n2\n\\frac{d-1}{2}\nelements of the weight vector are\nδ\n\\delta\nand\nd\n−\n1\n2\n\\frac{d-1}{2}\nelements are\n−\nδ\n-\\delta\n.\nSince\nf\nf\nis monotonic,\nf\n⁡\n(\n−\n2\n​\nδ\n)\n≤\nf\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n≤\nf\n⁡\n(\n2\n​\nδ\n)\nf(-2\\delta)\\leq f(0)\\leq f(2\\delta)\nand we have that\nf\n⁡\n(\n2\n​\nδ\n)\n=\n1\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\n−\n2\n​\nδ\n)\nf(2\\delta)=1-f(-2\\delta)\n, we define\nf\n⁡\n(\n−\n2\n​\nδ\n)\n=\n1\n2\n−\nη\nf(-2\\delta)=\\frac{1}{2}-\\eta\nand\nf\n⁡\n(\n2\n​\nδ\n)\n=\n1\n2\n+\nη\nf(2\\delta)=\\frac{1}{2}+\\eta\nfor some\n0\n<\nη\n≤\n1\n2\n0<\\eta\\leq\\frac{1}{2}\n.\nSimilar to the proof of Theorem\n3\n, we use (\n47\n) to bound the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence between the null and the alternate constructed. We first note that if we sample two matrices\nQ\nQ\nand\nQ\n′\nQ^{\\prime}\nuniformly at random (with replacement) from\nΘ\n\\Theta\n, then if the number of agreements is\ni\n2\n2\n\\frac{i^{2}}{2}\nthen the number of disagreements is equal to\n(\nd\n−\ni\n)\n2\n2\n\\frac{(d-i)^{2}}{2}\n. The probability of\ni\n2\n2\n\\frac{i^{2}}{2}\nagreements is given by\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n=\ni\n2\n2\n,\nb\n2\n=\n(\nd\n−\ni\n)\n2\n2\n)\n=\n(\nd\n/\n2\ni\n/\n2\n)\n​\n(\nd\n/\n2\ni\n/\n2\n)\n(\nd\nd\n/\n2\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}\\left(b_{1}=\\frac{i^{2}}{2},b_{2}=\\frac{(d-i)^{2}}{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{\\binom{d/2}{i/2}\\binom{d/2}{i/2}}{\\binom{d}{d/2}}.\nFollowing from (\n44\n), the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence is\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\n=\n𝔼\n(\nQ\n,\nQ\n′\n)\n∼\nUnif\n​\n(\nΘ\n)\n​\n[\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n⁡\n(\nb\n1\n−\nb\n2\n)\n]\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{(Q,Q^{\\prime})\\sim\\textnormal{Unif}(\\Theta)}\\left[(1+4\\eta^{2})^{k(b_{1}-b_{2})}\\right]-1\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nd\n(\nd\n/\n2\ni\n/\n2\n)\n​\n(\nd\n/\n2\ni\n/\n2\n)\n(\nd\nd\n/\n2\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\nk\n⁡\n(\ni\n2\n−\n(\nd\n−\ni\n)\n2\n)\n/\n2\n−\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=0}^{d}\\dfrac{\\binom{d/2}{i/2}\\binom{d/2}{i/2}}{\\binom{d}{d/2}}(1+4\\eta^{2})^{k(i^{2}-(d-i)^{2})/2}-1.\nFor ease of presentation, we replace\nd\n2\n\\frac{d}{2}\nby\nz\nz\nand\ni\n2\n\\frac{i}{2}\nby\nℓ\n\\ell\n, to get\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\n≤\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nz\n(\nz\nℓ\n)\n​\n(\nz\nℓ\n)\n(\n2\n​\nz\nz\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\n2\n​\nk\n​\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n​\nz\n−\nz\n2\n)\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{z}\\dfrac{\\binom{z}{\\ell}\\binom{z}{\\ell}}{\\binom{2z}{z}}(1+4\\eta^{2})^{2k(2\\ell z-z^{2})}-1\n≤\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nz\n(\n1\n2\n)\nz\n​\n(\nz\nℓ\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\n4\n​\nη\n2\n)\n2\n​\nk\n​\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n​\nz\n−\nz\n2\n)\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{z}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{z}\\binom{z}{\\ell}(1+4\\eta^{2})^{2k(2\\ell z-z^{2})}-1\n≤\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nz\n(\n1\n2\n)\nz\n​\n(\nz\nℓ\n)\n​\nexp\n⁡\n(\n8\n​\nη\n2\n​\nk\n​\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n​\nz\n−\nz\n2\n)\n)\n−\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{z}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{z}\\binom{z}{\\ell}\\exp({8\\eta^{2}k(2\\ell z-z^{2})})-1.\nHere, we see that the summation in the final expression is equal to the expectation of\nexp\n⁡\n(\n8\n​\nη\n2\n​\nk\n​\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n​\nz\n−\nz\n2\n)\n)\n\\exp({8\\eta^{2}k(2\\ell z-z^{2})})\nover the random variable\nℓ\n\\ell\nwhere\nℓ\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nz\n,\n1\n2\n)\n\\ell\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(z,\\frac{1}{2})\n. So,\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\n≤\n𝔼\nℓ\n​\n[\nexp\n⁡\n(\n8\n​\nη\n2\n​\nk\n​\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n​\nz\n−\nz\n2\n)\n)\n]\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\mathbb{E}_{\\ell}\\left[\\exp({8\\eta^{2}k(2\\ell z-z^{2})})\\right]-1\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n0\n∞\n(\n8\n​\nη\n2\n​\nk\n​\nz\n)\ni\ni\n!\n​\n𝔼\nℓ\n​\n[\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n−\nz\n)\ni\n]\n−\n1\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{(8\\eta^{2}kz)^{i}}{i!}\\mathbb{E}_{\\ell}\\left[(2\\ell-z)^{i}\\right]-1,\n(54)\nwhere\n𝔼\nℓ\n​\n[\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n−\nz\n)\ni\n]\n\\mathbb{E}_{\\ell}[(2\\ell-z)^{i}]\nis the scaled centered\ni\nth\ni^{\\textnormal{th}}\nmoment of\nBin\n​\n(\nz\n,\n1\n2\n)\n\\textnormal{Bin}(z,\\frac{1}{2})\n. To get the expression for the centered moments, we first find the moment generating function of the random variable\nℓ\n′\n=\n2\n​\nℓ\n−\nz\n\\ell^{\\prime}=2\\ell-z\n, as\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\ne\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n−\nz\n)\n​\nt\n]\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[e^{(2\\ell-z)t}\\right]\n=\ne\n−\nz\n​\nt\n​\n𝔼\n​\n[\ne\n2\n​\nℓ\n​\nt\n]\n\\displaystyle=e^{-zt}\\mathbb{E}\\left[e^{2\\ell t}\\right]\n=\ne\n−\nz\n​\nt\n​\n∑\nℓ\n=\n0\nz\n(\nz\nℓ\n)\n​\n(\n1\n2\n​\ne\n2\n​\nt\n)\nℓ\n​\n(\n1\n2\n)\nz\n−\nℓ\n\\displaystyle=e^{-zt}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{z}\\binom{z}{\\ell}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}\\right)^{\\ell}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{z-\\ell}\n=\ne\n−\nz\n​\nt\n​\n(\n1\n2\n+\n1\n2\n​\ne\n2\n​\nt\n)\nz\n\\displaystyle=e^{-zt}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}\\right)^{z}\n=\n(\ne\n−\nt\n+\ne\nt\n2\n)\nz\n\\displaystyle=\\left(\\dfrac{e^{-t}+e^{t}}{2}\\right)^{z}\n=\n(\ncosh\n⁡\nt\n)\nz\n.\n\\displaystyle=(\\cosh{t})^{z}.\nThen, we have\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n−\nz\n)\ni\n]\n=\nd\ni\n​\n(\ncosh\n⁡\nt\n)\nz\nd\n​\nt\ni\n|\nt\n=\n0\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[(2\\ell-z)^{i}\\right]=\\dfrac{d^{i}(\\cosh{t})^{z}}{dt^{i}}\\bigg|_{t=0},\nwhich is the\ni\nth\ni^{\\textnormal{th}}\nderivative of\n(\ncosh\n⁡\nt\n)\nz\n(\\cosh{t})^{z}\nevaluated at\nt\n=\n0\nt=0\n.\nThis leads to the fact that for odd\ni\ni\n,\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n−\nz\n)\ni\n]\n=\n0\n\\mathbb{E}[(2\\ell-z)^{i}]=0\nand for even\ni\ni\n,\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\n(\n2\n​\nℓ\n−\nz\n)\ni\n]\n≤\ni\n!\n(\ni\n/\n2\n)\n!\n​\nz\ni\n/\n2\n\\mathbb{E}[(2\\ell-z)^{i}]\\leq\\frac{i!}{(i/2)!}z^{i/2}\n. Using this with (\n54\n), we get\nχ\n2\n​\n(\nℙ\n0\n,\nℙ\nΘ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\chi^{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{0},\\mathbb{P}_{\\Theta})\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n1\n∞\nc\ni\n​\n(\n64\n​\nη\n4\n​\nk\n2\n​\nz\n3\n)\ni\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty}c_{i}(64\\eta^{4}k^{2}z^{3})^{i}\n(55)\nwhere\nc\ni\n=\n1\n(\ni\n/\n2\n)\n!\nc_{i}=\\frac{1}{(i/2)!}\n, that is,\nc\ni\nc_{i}\nis decreasing as\ni\ni\nincreases.\nThus, we see that if\nk\n≤\nc\nη\n2\n​\nd\n3\n/\n2\nk\\leq\\dfrac{c}{\\eta^{2}d^{3/2}}\nthen there is a small enough\nc\nc\nsuch that the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence is upper bounded by\n1\n9\n\\frac{1}{9}\n. In this construction, we have\nϵ\n2\n=\nη\n2\n/\n2\n\\epsilon^{2}=\\eta^{2}/2\n, therefore, using (\n41\n), the lower bound for two-sample testing under the parameter-based modeling assumption is given as\nϵ\n2\n=\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\nk\n​\nd\n3\n/\n2\n)\n\\epsilon^{2}=\\Omega\\left(\\dfrac{1}{kd^{3/2}}\\right)\n. This proves Theorem\n5\n.\n6.3.5\nProof of Theorem\n6\nTo prove Theorem\n6\n, we use the conjectured average-case hardness of the planted clique problem. In informal terms, the planted clique conjecture asserts that it is hard to detect the presence of a planted clique in an Erdös-Rényi random graph. In order to state it more precisely, let\nG\n⁡\n(\nd\n,\nκ\n)\nG(d,\\kappa)\nbe a random graph on\nd\nd\nvertices constructed in one of the following two ways:\nH\n0\nH_{0}\n: Every edge is included in\nG\n⁡\n(\nd\n,\nκ\n)\nG(d,\\kappa)\nindependently with probability\n1\n2\n\\frac{1}{2}\nH\n1\nH_{1}\n: Every edge is included in\nG\n⁡\n(\nd\n,\nκ\n)\nG(d,\\kappa)\nindependently with probability\n1\n2\n\\frac{1}{2}\n. In addition, a set of\nκ\n\\kappa\nvertices is chosen uniformly at random and all edges with both endpoints in the chosen set are added to\nG\nG\n.\nThe planted clique conjecture then asserts that when\nκ\n=\no\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\n\\kappa=o(\\sqrt{d})\n, then there is no polynomial-time algorithm that can correctly distinguish between\nH\n0\nH_{0}\nand\nH\n1\nH_{1}\nwith an error probability that is strictly bounded below\n1\n2\n\\frac{1}{2}\n. We complete the proof by identifying a subclass of SST matrices and showing that any testing algorithm that can distinguish between the subclass of SST matrices and the all half matrix, can also be used to detect a planted clique in an Erdös-Rényi random graph.\nConsider the null with\nP\n=\nQ\n=\n[\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP=Q=[\\frac{1}{2}]^{d\\times d}\nand the alternate such that\nP\n=\n[\n1\n2\n]\nd\n×\nd\nP=[\\frac{1}{2}]^{d\\times d}\nand\nQ\nQ\nis chosen uniformly at random from set\nΘ\n\\Theta\n. The set of probability matrices\nΘ\n\\Theta\ncontains all\n(\nd\n×\nd\n)\n(d\\times d)\nmatrices with the upper left and lower right quadrant equal to all half, the upper right quadrant is all half except a\n(\nκ\n×\nκ\n)\n(\\kappa\\times\\kappa)\nplanted clique (i.e., a\n(\nκ\n,\nκ\n)\n(\\kappa,\\kappa)\nsubmatrix with all entries equal to one).\nThen we have\nϵ\n2\n=\nκ\n2\n/\n2\n​\nd\n2\n\\epsilon^{2}=\\kappa^{2}/2d^{2}\n. The bottom left quadrant follows from the skew symmetry property. Recall that we observe one sample per pair of items\n(\ni\n>\nj\n)\n(i>j)\n. This testing problem is reduced to a goodness-of-fit testing problem as shown in (\n39\n) and (\n40\n).\nConsider the set of\n(\nd\n2\n×\nd\n2\n)\n\\left(\\frac{d}{2}\\times\\frac{d}{2}\\right)\nmatrices comprising the top right\n(\nd\n2\n×\nd\n2\n)\n\\left(\\frac{d}{2}\\times\\frac{d}{2}\\right)\nsub-matrix of every matrix in\nΘ\n\\Theta\n. We claim that this set is identical to the set of all possible matrices in the planted clique problem with\nd\n2\n\\frac{d}{2}\nvertices and a planted clique of size\nκ\n\\kappa\n. Indeed, the null contains the all-half matrix corresponding to the absence of a planted clique, and the alternate contains all symmetric matrices that have all entries equal to half except for a\n(\nκ\n,\nκ\n)\n\\left(\\kappa,\\kappa\\right)\nall-ones sub-matrix corresponding to the planted clique. We choose the parameter\nκ\n=\nd\nlog\n⁡\nlog\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\n\\kappa=\\frac{\\sqrt{d}}{\\log\\log(d)}\nso that any constant multiple of it will be within the hardness regime of planted clique (for sufficiently large values of\nd\nd\n). Now, we leverage the planted clique hardness conjecture to state that the null in our construction cannot be distinguished from the alternate by any polynomial-time algorithm with probability of error less than\n1\n2\n\\frac{1}{2}\n. This implies that for polynomial-time testing it is necessary that\nϵ\n2\n≥\nc\nd\n​\n(\nlog\n⁡\nlog\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\n)\n2\n\\epsilon^{2}\\geq\\dfrac{c}{d(\\log\\log(d))^{2}}\n. This proves Theorem\n6\n.\n6.4\nProof of Theorem\n7\nBounding the Type I error.\nIn this proof, we first bound the Type I error and subsequently bound the Type II error. To bound the probability of error of Algorithm\n3\n, we study the distribution of the test statistic\nT\nT\nunder the null and the alternate. Algorithm\n3\nuses the test statistic defined in (\n8\n). To understand the distribution of the test statistic, we first look at the distribution of\nX\ni\n​\nj\nX_{ij}\nand\nY\ni\n​\nj\nY_{ij}\n.\nIn Algorithm\n3\n, we break the partial ranks into disjoint pairwise-comparisons. Under the Plackett-Luce model disjoint pairwise-comparisons obtained from the same partial ranking are mutually independent. Additionally, since the Plackett-Luce model obeys the property of independence of irrelevant alternatives, the probability of observing item\ni\ni\nbeing ranked ahead of item\nj\nj\nin a partial ranking is independent of the other items being ranked in that partial ranking. Thus, for any pair of items\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n, the probability of\ni\ni\nbeating\nj\nj\n, conditioned on the event that the pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nwas observed, is always equal to\np\ni\n​\nj\np_{ij}\nfor the population corresponding to pairwise probability matrix\nP\nP\nand\nq\ni\n​\nj\nq_{ij}\nfor the population corresponding to pairwise probability matrix\nQ\nQ\n. This holds true irrespective of which other items are involved in that partial (or total) ranking. With this in mind, we identify the distribution of\nX\ni\n​\nj\nX_{ij}\nconditioned on\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\nk^{p}_{ij}\nas\nX\ni\n​\nj\n|\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\np\ni\n​\nj\n)\nX_{ij}\\,|\\,k^{p}_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k^{p}_{ij},p_{ij})\n. Similarly, we have\nY\ni\n​\nj\n|\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\nY_{ij}\\,|\\,k^{q}_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(k^{q}_{ij},q_{ij})\n.\nLet\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}\ndenote the vector of\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n,\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{p}_{ij},k^{q}_{ij}\nfor all\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n,\ni\n<\nj\n(i,j),\\,i<j\n. The conditional expectation of\nT\nT\nis\n𝔼\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nj\n−\n1\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n.\n\\mathbb{E}[T\\,|\\,\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]=\\sum_{i=1}^{j-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\dfrac{\\mathbb{I}_{ij}k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij}}(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2}.\nUnder the null we have\np\ni\n​\nj\n=\nq\ni\n​\nj\np_{ij}=q_{ij}\n. Clearly, using the law of total expectation, we see that\n𝔼\nH\n0\n[\nT\n]\n=\n𝔼\n[\n𝔼\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n=\n0\n\\mathbb{E}_{H_{0}}[T]=\\mathbb{E}[\\;\\mathbb{E}[T\\,|\\,\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\\;]=0\n. We now upper bound the variance of\nT\nT\nunder the null. Recall from (\n23\n) and (\n24\n) that\nVar\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\n\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{0}}[T]\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nj\n−\n1\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nd\n8\n𝕀\ni\n​\nj\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n​\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n+\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n)\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n+\n3\n)\n+\nVar\n[\n𝔼\n[\nT\n|\nk\np\n,\nk\nq\n]\n]\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=1}^{j-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}8\\mathbb{I}_{ij}\\left(\\dfrac{k^{p}_{ij}k^{q}_{ij}}{(k^{p}_{ij}+k^{q}_{ij})}(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2}+3\\right)+\\mathrm{Var}[\\,\\mathbb{E}[T|\\textbf{k}^{p},\\textbf{k}^{q}]\\,]\n≤\n24\n​\nd\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leq 24d^{2}.\n(56)\nNow, we have the information to bound the Type I error. To get a bound on the Type I error with threshold\nt\nt\n, we use the one sided Chebyshev’s inequality,\nℙ\nH\n0\n​\n(\nT\n≥\nt\n)\n≤\nVar\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\nVar\nH\n0\n​\n[\nT\n]\n+\nt\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}_{H_{0}}(T\\geq t)\\leq\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{0}}[T]}{\\mathrm{Var}_{H_{0}}[T]+t^{2}}.\n(57)\nUsing the bound in (\n56\n) and (\n57\n), we observe that if\nt\n=\n11\n​\nd\nt=11d\nthen the Type I error is at most\n1\n6\n\\frac{1}{6}\n.\nThis concludes the proof that Algorithm\n3\ncontrols the Type I error of the test (\n13\n) at level\n1\n6\n\\frac{1}{6}\n.\nBounding the Type 2 error.\nWe now analyse the Type II error of Algorithm\n3\n, that is, the probability of our algorithm failing to reject the null, under the alternate. We consider two cases depending on whether the pairwise-comparison data created through the rank breaking method has at least\nk\nk\npairwise-comparisons per pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n,\ni\n<\nj\n(i,j),\\,i<j\n, or not, for some positive integer\nk\nk\n. We will define\nk\nk\nlater in the proof. Let the case where the pairwise-comparisons created in each population have at least\nk\nk\ncomparisons of each pair be denoted by\nC\n1\nC_{1}\nand let the associated Type II error be denoted by\nβ\n1\n\\beta_{1}\n. Let the Type II error associated with the remaining case be denoted by\nβ\n2\n\\beta_{2}\n. Our objective is to provide an upper bound on the total Type II error which is\nβ\n=\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nC\n1\n)\n​\nβ\n1\n+\n(\n1\n−\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nC\n1\n)\n)\n​\nβ\n2\n\\beta=\\mathbb{P}(C_{1})\\beta_{1}+(1-\\mathbb{P}(C_{1}))\\beta_{2}\n.\nFirst, we derive a bound on\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nC\n1\n)\n\\mathbb{P}(C_{1})\n. To start, we note that the probability of observing a specific pair from a total ranking is\n1\nd\n\\frac{1}{d}\nif\nd\nd\nis odd and\n1\nd\n−\n1\n\\frac{1}{d-1}\nif\nd\nd\nis even. Recall that for a given\nm\nm\n, each sample is a ranking of some\nm\nm\nitems chosen uniformly at random from the set of\nd\nd\nitems. Under this setting, we see that the probability that “Random disjoint” rank breaking yields a specific pairwise-comparison from a\nm\nm\n-length partial ranking is\nm\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\n\\frac{m}{d(d-1)}\nif\nm\nm\nis even and\nm\n−\n1\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\n\\frac{m-1}{d(d-1)}\nif\nm\nm\nis odd. Henceforth, in this proof, we assume that\nm\nm\nis even. The proof follows similarly for odd\nm\nm\n. Thus, the number of pairwise-comparisons observed of any pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nis a binomial random variable with Bernoulli parameter\nm\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\n\\frac{m}{d(d-1)}\n. Consequently, if we have\nN\nN\nsamples from each population, then for the population corresponding to the pairwise probability matrix\nP\nP\n, for all pairs\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nwe have\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nN\n,\nm\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\n)\nk^{p}_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(N,\\frac{m}{d(d-1)})\n. Similarly for the population corresponding to pairwise probability matrix\nQ\nQ\n, for all pairs\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nwe have\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\n∼\nBin\n​\n(\nN\n,\nm\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\n)\nk^{q}_{ij}\\sim\\textnormal{Bin}(N,\\frac{m}{d(d-1)})\n. Now, we are equipped to compute the probability of case\nC\n1\nC_{1}\n. We divide the samples available in each population into\nk\nk\nsections of equal sizes. Let the samples in each population be indexed from\n1\n1\nto\nN\nN\nthen we assign the first\n⌊\nN\nk\n⌋\n\\lfloor\\frac{N}{k}\\rfloor\ninto the first section and so on. Now, we know that the probability of observing a pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nat least once in one such section is given by\n1\n−\n(\n1\n−\nm\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\n)\n⌊\nN\nk\n⌋\n1-(1-\\frac{m}{d(d-1)})^{\\lfloor\\frac{N}{k}\\rfloor}\n. Using this, we get the following union bound,\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nk\ni\n​\nj\np\n≥\nk\n)\n≥\n1\n−\nk\n​\n(\n1\n−\nm\nd\n⁡\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\n)\nN\nk\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(k^{p}_{ij}\\geq k)\\geq 1-k\\left(1-\\frac{m}{d(d-1)}\\right)^{\\frac{N}{k}}.\nThe same inequality holds for\nk\ni\n​\nj\nq\nk^{q}_{ij}\nfor all\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\n. Then, the probability that all pairs of items had at least\nk\nk\npairwise-comparisons in both populations is lower bounded as\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nC\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(C_{1})\n≥\n1\n−\n2\n​\nk\n​\nd\n​\n(\nd\n−\n1\n)\n2\n​\n(\n1\n−\nm\nd\n2\n)\nN\nk\n\\displaystyle\\geq 1-\\dfrac{2kd(d-1)}{2}\\left(1-\\frac{m}{d^{2}}\\right)^{\\frac{N}{k}}\n≥\n1\n−\nk\n​\nd\n2\n​\nexp\n⁡\n(\n−\nN\n​\nm\nk\n​\nd\n2\n)\n\\displaystyle\\geq 1-kd^{2}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{Nm}{kd^{2}}\\right)\nWe see that, if\nN\n=\nc\n​\nk\n​\nd\n2\n​\nlog\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\n/\nm\nN=ckd^{2}\\log(d)/m\nfor some positive constant\nc\nc\n, then\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nC\n1\n)\n≥\n1\n−\nk\nd\nc\n−\n2\n\\mathbb{P}(C_{1})\\geq 1-\\frac{k}{d^{c-2}}\n.\nConditioned on the case\nC\n1\nC_{1}\n, we invoke Theorem\n1\nto control the Type II error. Recall that Theorem\n1\nasserts that there is a constant\nc\n0\n>\n0\nc_{0}>0\nsuch that if we have\nk\nk\npairwise-comparisons of each pair\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nfrom each population where\nk\n≥\nmax\n⁡\n{\nc\n0\n​\n1\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n,\n2\n}\nk\\geq\\max\\{c_{0}\\frac{1}{d\\epsilon^{2}},2\\}\nthen the Type II error of Algorithm\n1\nfor the testing problem (\n13\n) is upper bounded by\n1\n12\n\\frac{1}{12}\n. To apply this result to Algorithm\n3\nconditioned on case\nC\n1\nC_{1}\n, we keep\nk\nk\npairwise-comparisons for each pair where\nk\n=\n2\n​\n⌈\nc\n0\n​\n1\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n⌉\nk=2\\lceil c_{0}\\frac{1}{d\\epsilon^{2}}\\rceil\n. Observe that, since we assume\nϵ\n≥\nc\n1\n​\nd\n−\nc\n2\n\\epsilon\\geq c_{1}d^{-c_{2}}\nfor some positive constants\nc\n1\nc_{1}\nand\nc\n2\nc_{2}\n, we get the inequality\nk\n≤\nc\n1\n′\n​\nd\nc\n2\n′\nk\\leq c_{1}^{\\prime}d^{c_{2}^{\\prime}}\nfor some positive constants\nc\n1\n′\nc_{1}^{\\prime}\nand\nc\n2\n′\nc_{2}^{\\prime}\n. Under this inequality, we get that there exist positive constants\nc\n,\nc\n1\n′\nc,c_{1}^{\\prime}\nand\nc\n2\n′\nc_{2}^{\\prime}\nsuch that\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nC\n1\n)\n>\n11\n/\n12\n\\mathbb{P}(C_{1})>11/12\n.\nNext, we observe that the Type II error conditioned on the complement of\nC\n1\nC_{1}\nis at most 1. Therefore, the total probability of failing to reject the null under the alternate is given by\nβ\n\\displaystyle\\beta\n=\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nC\n1\n)\n​\nβ\n1\n+\n(\n1\n−\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nC\n1\n)\n)\n​\nβ\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{P}(C_{1})\\beta_{1}+(1-\\mathbb{P}(C_{1}))\\beta_{2}\n≤\n1\n12\n+\n1\n12\n=\n1\n6\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{1}{6}.\nThis concludes the proof that for some constant\nC\n>\n0\nC>0\n, if\nN\n≥\nC\n​\nd\n2\n​\nlog\n⁡\n(\nd\n)\nm\n​\n⌈\nc\n0\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n⌉\nN\\geq C\\dfrac{d^{2}\\log(d)}{m}\\lceil\\dfrac{c_{0}}{d\\epsilon^{2}}\\rceil\n, then the probability of error of Algorithm\n3\nis at most\n1\n3\n\\frac{1}{3}\n.\n6.5\nProof of Theorem\n8\nThe proof of Theorem\n8\nfollows similarly to the proof of Theorem\n7\n. Both theorems establish the performance of Algorithm\n3\nfor the two-sample testing problem stated in (\n13\n). The difference lies in the assumption on the partial ranking data available and consequently in the rank breaking algorithm used. In Theorem\n8\n, we assume we have total ranking data that is then deterministically converted to pairwise-comparisons in the following manner. We have a total of\nN\nN\ntotal rankings available from each population. We divide these into subsets each containing\nd\nd\nrankings as described in the “Deterministic disjoint” rank breaking method. Notice that we can break the ranking data available in a section into pairwise-comparisons such that we observe each unique pair of items at least one time. We prove this statement at the end of the section. We repeat this breaking technique for all subsets. Consequently, we get\nk\n=\n⌊\nN\nd\n⌋\n≥\n2\n​\n⌈\nc\n​\n1\nd\n​\nϵ\n2\n⌉\nk=\\lfloor\\frac{N}{d}\\rfloor\\geq 2\\lceil c\\dfrac{1}{d\\epsilon^{2}}\\rceil\npairwise-comparisons for all pairs\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,j)\nfrom each population. With this in mind, we apply Theorem\n1\nto obtain the desired result.\nFinally, to complete the proof we show that it is indeed possible to break\nd\nd\ntotal rankings such that we observe each of\n(\nd\n2\n)\n\\binom{d}{2}\nunique pairs at least once. We use a mathematical induction based argument. As a first step we observe that our hypothesis is true for\nd\n=\n2\nd=2\n. In the inductive step, we assume that the hypothesis is true for all natural numbers\nd\n∈\n{\n2\n,\n⋯\n,\nr\n}\nd\\in\\{2,\\cdots,r\\}\n. Now, we wish to prove the hypothesis is true for\nd\n=\nr\n+\n1\nd=r+1\n. First, consider the case where\nr\nr\nis even. We divide the set of\nr\nr\nitems into two groups with\nr\n/\n2\nr/2\nitems in each. From the inductive step we know that our hypothesis is true for\nd\n=\nr\n/\n2\nd=r/2\n. Consequently, we get\n2\n​\n(\nr\n/\n2\n2\n)\n2\\binom{r/2}{2}\nunique pairs from within the two groups which use\nr\n/\n2\nr/2\ntotal rankings. Next, we arrange the items in group one in a list against the items in group two and make pairs by choosing the items in\ni\nt\n​\nh\ni^{th}\nposition in both the lists. This gives the breaking for one total ranking. We do this\nr\n/\n2\nr/2\ntimes, each time cyclically shifting the first list by one item. This step gives\nr\n2\n/\n4\nr^{2}/4\nunique pairs that are different from the pairs obtained in the previous step and uses\nr\n/\n2\nr/2\ntotal rankings. This proves our hypothesis for\nd\n=\nr\nd=r\nfor even\nr\nr\n. To prove our hypothesis for odd\nr\nr\n, we prove our hypothesis for\nr\n+\n1\nr+1\nwhich is even using the same method described in the previous step. We complete our proof by noting that if the hypothesis is true for even\nr\nr\nthen it must be true for\nr\n−\n1\nr-1\n. This concludes our proof of Theorem\n8\n6.6\nProof of Theorem\n9\nThe idea of the proof is to show that under the null hypothesis, a ranking sample sourced from the first population is mutually independent of and identically distributed as a ranking sample sourced from the second population. If this statement is true, then shuffling the population labels of ranking data, does not alter the distribution of the test statistic (\n8\n). This in turn controls the Type I error of the permutation test method.\nUnder the null, for some distribution\nλ\n\\lambda\nover all total rankings, we have that\nλ\nP\n=\nλ\nQ\n=\nλ\n\\lambda_{P}=\\lambda_{Q}=\\lambda\n. This implies that under the marginal probability based model, the probability of any given partial ranking over a set of items is the same for both the populations. Specifically, conditioned on the set of items being ranked, each partial ranking in each population is sampled independently and identically, according to the distribution\nλ\n\\lambda\n. Recall that the set of items being ranked in each population is sampled independently and identically from some distribution over all non-empty subsets in\n[\nd\n]\n[d]\n. Consequently, each ranking sample is independent of all other ranking samples obtained from the two populations. Moreover, using the law of total probability over all the non-empty subsets in\n[\nd\n]\n[d]\n, we get that each ranking sample obtained in each population is identically distributed. With this, we conclude that shuffling the population labels of ranking data does not alter the distribution of the test statistic. Thus, for a permutation test with\nγ\n\\gamma\niterations, the\np\np\n-value of the test is distributed uniformly over\n{\n0\n,\n1\n/\nγ\n,\n2\n/\nγ\n,\n⋯\n,\n1\n}\n\\{0,1/\\gamma,2/\\gamma,\\cdots,1\\}\n. Hence, for any given significance level\nα\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\alpha\\in(0,1)\n, by applying a threshold of\nα\n\\alpha\non the\np\np\n-value of the test, we are guaranteed to have Type I error at most\nα\n\\alpha\n.\n7\nDiscussion and open problems\nWe conclude with a discussion focused on open problems in this area. We provide algorithms for two-sample testing on pairwise-comparison and ranking data to distinguish between two potentially different populations (in terms of their underlying distributions). Through our analysis, we see that our testing algorithm for pairwise-comparison data is simultaneously minimax optimal under the model-free setting as well as the MST and WST model. There is a gap between the testing rate of our algorithm and our information-theoretic lower bound for the SST and parameter-based models, and closing this gap is an open problem of interest. Second, in the future, our work may help in studying two-sample testing problems pertaining to more general aspects of data from people such as function evaluations\n(\nXu et al., 2020\n;\nNoothigattu et al., 2018\n)\n, issues of calibration\nWang and Shah, 2019\n, and strategic behavior\n(\nXu et al., 2019\n)\n.\nThirdly, in practice we use the permutation method to calibrate our tests, ensuring valid Type I error control even when the distribution of the test statistic is difficult to characterize analytically (for instance,\nin the setting with partial rank data). Understanding the power of tests calibrated via the permutation method is an active area of research\n(\nKim et al., 2020a\n)\nand it would be interesting to understand this in the context of the\ntests developed in our work.\nFinally, the literature on analyzing pairwise-comparison data builds heavily on probability models from social choice theory (some are described in this work). A natural related question (that has received some recent attention\n(\nSeshadri and Ugander, 2019\n)\n) is the design of goodness-of-fit hypothesis tests, that is, testing whether given pairwise-comparison data obeys certain modeling assumptions.\nAcknowledgments\nThis work was supported in part by NSF grants DMS 1713003, CCF 1763734, 1755656 and CAREER 1942124.\nAppendix A. Additional details of experiments\nWe now provide more details about the experiments described in Section\n5.1.2\n.\nOrdinal versus cardinal\nThe data set from\nShah et al., 2016\nused in the “Ordinal versus cardinal” experiment comprises of six different experiments on Amazon Mechanical Turk crowdsourcing platform. We describe each experiment briefly here.\n•\nPhoto age: There are 10 objects in this experiment wherein each object is a photograph of a different face. The worker is either shown pairs of photos together and asked to identify the older of the two or they provide the numeric age for each photo. There are a total of 225 ordinal responses and 275 cardinal-converted-to-ordinal responses.\n•\nSpelling mistakes: There are 8 objects in this experiment wherein each object is a paragraph of text in English possibly with some spelling mistakes. The worker is either shown pairs of paragraphs and asked to identify the paragraph with more spelling mistakes or they are asked to provide the count of spelling mistakes for all 8 paragraphs. There are a total of 184 ordinal responses and 204 cardinal-converted-to-ordinal responses.\n•\nDistances between cities: There are 16 objects in this experiment wherein each object is a pair of cities (no two objects share a common city). The worker is either shown two pairs of cities at a time and asked to identify the pair that is farther from each other, or they are asked to estimate the distances for the 16 pairs of cities. There are a total of 408 ordinal responses and 392 cardinal-converted-to-ordinal responses.\n•\nSearch results: There are 20 objects in this experiment wherein each object is the result of an internet based search query of the word \"internet\". The worker is either asked to compare pairs of results based on their relevance or they are shown all the results and asked to rate the relevance of each result on a scale of 0-100. There are a total of 630 ordinal responses and 370 cardinal-converted-to-ordinal responses.\n•\nTaglines: There are 10 objects in this experiment wherein each object is a tagline for a product described to the worker. The worker is either asked to compare the quality of pairs of taglines or they are asked to provide ratings for each tagline on a scale of 0-10. There are a total of 305 ordinal responses and 195 cardinal-converted-to-ordinal responses.\n•\nPiano : There are 10 objects in this experiment wherein each object is a sound clip of a piano key played at a certain frequency. The worker is either given pairs of sound clips and asked to identify the clip with the higher frequency or they are asked to estimate the frequency of the 10 clips. There are a total of 265 ordinal responses and 235 cardinal-converted-to-ordinal responses.\nIn our main experiment, we combine the data from all the experiments described above and test for statistically significant difference between the underlying distributions for ordinal responses and ordinal-converted-to-cardinal responses. We also test for difference in each individual experiment (which however have smaller sample sizes), the results are provided in Table\n2\n. We observe that the qualitatively more subjective experiments (photo age, search results, taglines) have a lower\np\np\n-value, which indicates that the ordinal responses are more different from cardinal-converted-to-ordinal responses in a more subjective setting.\nExperiment\nCombined\nAge\nSpellings\nDistances\nSearch results\nTaglines\nPiano\np\np\n-value\n0.003\n0.001\n0.657\n0.75\n0.187\n0.0829\n0.514\nTable 2\n:\np\np\n-value of two-sample test comparing the distribution of ordinal responses and the distribution of cardinal-converted-to-ordinal responses in the experiments described above\nEuropean football leagues\nIn this experiment, we obtain the match scores for four different European football leagues (English Premier League, Bundesliga, Ligue 1, La Liga) across two seasons (2016-2017, 2017-2018). There were 17 teams that played the two seasons in each of EPL, La Liga, Ligue 1 and 16 teams in Bundesliga. To test for statistically significant difference between the relative performance of the participating teams in the two consecutive seasons we combined the data from all four leagues. We also tested for difference in each individual league, the results are displayed in Table\n3\n. From the 2016-2017 season we have 202 pairwise-comparisons in EPL, 170 pairwise-comparisons in Bundesliga, 215 pairwise-comparisons in La Liga, and 201 pairwise-comparisons in Ligue 1. From the 2017-2018 season we have 214 pairwise-comparisons in EPL, 178 pairwise-comparisons in Bundesliga, 208 pairwise-comparisons in La Liga, and 201 pairwise-comparisons in Ligue 1. From the number of comparisons available our test does not detect any significant difference between the relative performance of teams in European football leagues over two consecutive seasons.\nLeague\nCombined\nEPL\nBundesliga\nLa Liga\nLigue 1\np\np\n-value\n0.971\n0.998\n0.691\n0.67\n0.787\nTable 3\n:\np\np\n-value of two-sample test comparing relative performance of teams in a football league over two consecutive seasons.\nReferences\nAilon,  (2012)\nAilon, N. (2012).\nAn active learning algorithm for ranking from pairwise preferences\nwith an almost optimal query complexity.\nJournal of Machine Learning Research\n, 13:137–164.\nAldous,  (2017)\nAldous, D. (2017).\nElo ratings and the sports model: A neglected topic in applied\nprobability?\nStatistical Science\n, 32(4):616–629.\nBalakrishnan and Wasserman,  (2018)\nBalakrishnan, S. and Wasserman, L. (2018).\nHypothesis testing for high-dimensional multinomials: A selective\nreview.\nAnn. Appl. Stat.\n, 12(2):727–749.\nBalakrishnan and Wasserman,  (2019)\nBalakrishnan, S. and Wasserman, L. (2019).\nHypothesis testing for densities and high-dimensional multinomials:\nSharp local minimax rates.\nAnn. Statist.\n, 47(4):1893–1927.\nBradley and Terry,  (1952)\nBradley, R. A. and Terry, M. E. (1952).\nRank analysis of incomplete block designs: I. The method of paired\ncomparisons.\nBiometrika\n, pages 324–345.\nBraverman and Mossel,  (2008)\nBraverman, M. and Mossel, E. (2008).\nNoisy sorting without resampling.\nIn\nProceedings of the nineteenth annual ACM-SIAM symposium on\nDiscrete algorithms\n, pages 268–276.\nCarpentier et al.,  (2018)\nCarpentier, A., Collier, O., Comminges, L., Tsybakov, A. B., and Wang, Y.\n(2018).\nMinimax rate of testing in sparse linear regression.\narXiv preprint arXiv:1804.06494\n.\nCattelan et al.,  (2013)\nCattelan, M., Varin, C., and Firth, D. (2013).\nDynamic Bradley–Terry modelling of sports tournaments.\nJournal of the Royal Statistical Society: Series C (Applied\nStatistics)\n, 62(1):135–150.\nCavagnaro and Davis-Stober,  (2014)\nCavagnaro, D. R. and Davis-Stober, C. P. (2014).\nTransitive in our preferences, but transitive in differen