s the cumulative distribution F σ 2 F_{\\sigma^{2}} would be enough to get an estimator of Γ ε ∗ \\Gamma^{*}_{\\varepsilon} . To build such predictor, we first introduce a learning sample 𝒟 n = { ( X i , Y i ) , i = 1 , … , n } \\mathcal{D}_{n}=\\{(X_{i},Y_{i}),\\;\\;i=1,\\ldots,n\\} which consists of n n independent copies of ( X , Y ) (X,Y) . This dataset helps us to construct estimators f ^ \\hat{f} and σ ^ 2 \\hat{\\sigma}^{2} of the regression function f ∗ f^{*} and the conditional variance function σ 2 \\sigma^{2} respectively. In this paper, we focus on estimator σ ^ 2 \\hat{\\sigma}^{2} which relies on the residual-based methods [ 11 ] . Based on 𝒟 n \\mathcal{D}_{n} , the estimator σ ^ 2 \\hat{\\sigma}^{2} is obtained by solving the regression problem of the output variable ( Y − f ^ ​ ( X ) ) 2 (Y-\\hat{f}(X))^{2} on the input variable X X . Estimating the last quantity F σ 2 F_{\\sigma^{2}} is rather simple by replacing cumulative distribution function by its empirical version. Since this term only depends on the marginal distribution ℙ X \\mathbb{P}_{X} , we estimate it using a second unlabeled dataset 𝒟 N = { X n + 1 , … , X n + N } \\mathcal{D}_{N}=\\{X_{n+1},\\ldots,X_{n+N}\\} composed of N N independent copies of X X . This is an important feature of our methodology since unlabeled data are usually easy to get. The dataset 𝒟 N \\mathcal{D}_{N} is assumed to be independent of 𝒟 n \\mathcal{D}_{n} . We set F ^ σ ^ 2 ( ⋅ ) = 1 N ∑ i = 1 N 𝟙 { σ ^ 2 ( X n + i ) ≤ ⋅ } , \\hat{F}_{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}}(\\cdot)=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\mathds{1}_{\\{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}(X_{n+i})\\leq\\cdot\\}}\\kern 5.0pt, as an estimator for F σ 2 F_{\\sigma^{2}} . With this notation, the plug-in ε \\varepsilon -predictor is the predictor with reject option defined for each x ∈ ℝ d x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d} as Γ ^ ε ​ ( x ) = { { f ^ ​ ( x ) } if ​ F ^ σ ^ 2 ​ ( σ ^ 2 ​ ( x ) ) ≤ 1 − ε ∅ otherwise . \\hat{\\Gamma}_{\\varepsilon}(x)=\\begin{cases}\\left\\{\\hat{f}(x)\\right\\}&amp;\\text{if}\\;\\;\\hat{F}_{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}}(\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}(x))\\leq 1-\\varepsilon\\\\ \\emptyset&amp;\\text{otherwise}\\kern 5.0pt.\\end{cases} (4) It is worth noting that the proposed methodology is flexible enough to rely upon any off-the-shelf estimators of the regression function f ∗ f^{*} and the conditional variance function σ 2 \\sigma^{2} . 3.2 Consistency of plug-in ε \\varepsilon -predictors In this part, we investigate the statistical properties of the plug-in ε \\varepsilon -predictors with reject option. This analysis requires an additional assumption on the following quantity F σ ^ 2 ( ⋅ ) = ℙ X ( σ ^ 2 ( X ) ≤ ⋅ | 𝒟 n ) . F_{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}}(\\cdot)=\\mathbb{P}_{X}\\left(\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}(X)\\leq\\cdot|\\mathcal{D}_{n}\\right)\\kern 5.0pt. Assumption 2 . The cumulative distribution function F σ ^ 2 F_{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}} of σ ^ 2 ​ ( X ) \\hat{\\sigma}^{2}(X) is continuous. This condition is analogous to Assumption 1 but deals with the estimator σ ^ 2 ​ ( X ) \\hat{\\sigma}^{2}(X) instead of the true conditional variance σ 2 ​ ( X ) \\sigma^{2}(X) . This difference makes Assumption 2 rather weak as the estimator σ ^ 2 ​ ( X ) \\hat{\\sigma}^{2}(X) is chosen by the practitioner. Moreover, we can make any estimator satisfy this condition by providing a smoothed version of it. We illustrate this strategy with k k NN algorithm in Section 4 . Next theorem is the main result of this section, it establishes the consistency of the predictor Γ ^ ε \\hat{\\Gamma}_{\\varepsilon} to the optimal one. Theorem 1 . Let ε ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) \\varepsilon\\in(0,1) . Assume that σ 2 \\sigma^{2} is bounded, f ^ \\hat{f} is a consistent estimator of f ∗ f^{*} w.r.t. the L 2 L_{2} risk, and σ ^ 2 \\hat{\\sigma}^{2} is a consistent estimator of σ 2 \\sigma^{2} w.r.t. the L 1 L_{1} risk. Under Assumptions 1 - 2 , the followings hold 𝔼 [ ℰ λ ε ( Γ ^ ε ) ] ⟶ n , N → + ∞ 0 , a n d 𝔼 [ | r ( Γ ^ ε ) − ε | ] ≤ C N − 1 / 2 , \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\mathcal{E}_{\\lambda_{\\varepsilon}}\\left(\\hat{\\Gamma}_{\\varepsilon}\\right)\\right]\\underset{n,N\\to+\\infty}{\\longrightarrow}0,\\quad{\\text{a}nd}\\quad\\mathbb{E}\\left[|r(\\hat{\\Gamma}_{\\varepsilon})-\\varepsilon|\\right]\\leq CN^{-1/2}\\kern 5.0pt, where C &gt; 0 C&gt;0 is an absolute constant. This theorem establishes the fact that the plug-in ε \\varepsilon -predictor behaves asymptotically as well as the optimal ε \\varepsilon -predictor both in terms of risk and rejection rate. The convergence of the rejection rate requires only Assumption 2 which is rather weak and can even be removed following the process detailed in Section 4.2 . In particular, the theorem shows that the rejection rate of the plug-in ε \\varepsilon -predictor is of level ε \\varepsilon up to a term of order O ( N − 1 / 2 ) O(N^{-1/2}) . This rate is similar to the one obtained in the classification setting [ 7 ] . It relies on the difference between the cumulative distribution F σ ^ 2 F_{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}} and its empirical counterpart F ^ σ ^ 2 \\hat{F}_{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}} that is controlled using Dvoretzky-Kiefer-Wolfowitz Inequality [ 17 ] . Interestingly, this result applies to any consistent estimators of f ∗ f^{*} and σ 2 \\sigma^{2} . The estimation of regression function f ∗ f^{*} is widely studied and suitable algorithm such as random forests, kernel procedures, or k k NN estimators can be used, see [ 3 , 10 , 20 , 22 , 23 ] . The estimation of the conditional variance function which relies on the residual-based methods has also been extensively studied based on kernel procedures, see for instance [ 9 , 11 , 12 , 14 , 21 ] . In the next section, we derive rates of convergence in the case where both estimators f ^ \\hat{f} and σ ^ 2 \\hat{\\sigma}^{2} rely on the k k NN algorithm. In particular, we establish rates of convergence for σ ^ 2 \\hat{\\sigma}^{2} in sup norm (see Proposition 6 in the supplementary material). 4 Application to k k NN algorithm: rates of convergence The plug-in ε \\varepsilon -predictor Γ ^ ε \\hat{\\Gamma}_{\\varepsilon} relies on estimators of the regression and the conditional variance functions. In this section, we consider the specific case of k k NN based estimations. We refer to the resulting predictor as k k NN predictor with reject option . Specifically, we establish rates of convergence for this procedure. In addition, since k k NN estimator of σ 2 \\sigma^{2} violates Assumption 2 , applying our methodology to k k NN has the benefit of illustrating the smoothing technique to make this condition be satisfied. 4.1 Assumptions To study the performance of the k k NN predictor with reject option in the finite sample regime, we assume that X X belongs to a regular compact set 𝒞 ⊂ ℝ d \\mathcal{C}\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{d} , see [ 1 ] . Besides, we make the following assumptions. Assumption 3 . The functions f ∗ f^{*} and σ 2 \\sigma^{2} are Lipschitz. Assumption 4 (Strong density assumption) . We assume that the marginal distribution ℙ X \\mathbb{P}_{X} admits a density μ \\mu w.r.t to the Lebesgue measure such that for all x ∈ 𝒞 x\\in\\mathcal{C} , we have 0 &lt; μ min ≤ μ ⁡ ( x ) ≤ μ max 0&lt;\\mu_{\\min}\\leq\\mu(x)\\leq\\mu_{\\max} . These two assumptions are rather classical when we deal with rate of convergence and we refer the reader to the baseline books [ 10 , 23 ] . In particular, we point out that the strong density assumption has been introduced in the context of binary classification for instance in [ 1 ] . The last assumption that we require highlights the behavior of σ 2 \\sigma^{2} around the threshold λ ε \\lambda_{\\varepsilon} . Assumption 5 ( α \\alpha -exponent assumption) . We say that σ 2 \\sigma^{2} has exponent α ≥ 0 \\alpha\\geq 0 (at level λ ε \\lambda_{\\varepsilon} ) with respect to ℙ X \\mathbb{P}_{X} if there exists c ∗ &gt; 0 c^{*}&gt;0 such that for all t &gt; 0 t&gt;0 ℙ X ​ ( 0 &lt; | σ 2 ​ ( X ) − λ ε | ≤ t ) ≤ c ∗ ​ t α . \\mathbb{P}_{X}\\left(0&lt;|\\sigma^{2}(X)-\\lambda_{\\varepsilon}|\\leq t\\right)\\leq c^{*}t^{\\alpha}\\kern 5.0pt. This assumption has been first introduced in [ 19 ] and is also referred as Margin assumption in the binary classification setting, see [ 16 ] . For α &gt; 0 \\alpha&gt;0 , Assumption 5 ensures that the random variable σ 2 ​ ( X ) \\sigma^{2}(X) can not concentrate too much around the threshold λ ε \\lambda_{\\varepsilon} . It allows to derive faster rates of convergence. Note that, if α = 0 \\alpha=0 there is no assumption. 4.2 k k NN predictor with reject option For any x ∈ ℝ d x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d} , we denote by ( X ( i , n ) ​ ( x ) , Y ( i , n ) ​ ( x ) ) , i = 1 , … ​ n (X_{(i,n)}(x),Y_{(i,n)}(x)),i=1,\\ldots n the reordered data according to the ℓ 2 \\ell_{2} distance in ℝ d \\mathbb{R}^{d} , meaning that ‖ X ( i , n ) ​ ( x ) − x ‖ &lt; ‖ X ( j , n ) ​ ( x ) − x ‖ \\|X_{(i,n)}(x)-x\\|&lt;\\|X_{(j,n)}(x)-x\\| for all i &lt; j i&lt;j in { 1 , … , n } \\{1,\\ldots,n\\} . Note that Assumption 4 ensures that ties occur with probability 0 0 (see [ 10 ] for more details). Let k = k n k=k_{n} be an integer. The k k NN estimator of f ∗ f^{*} and σ 2 \\sigma^{2} are then defined, for all x x , as follows f ^ ​ ( x ) = 1 k n ​ ∑ i = 1 k n Y ( i , n ) ​ ( x ) ​ and ​ σ ^ 2 ​ ( x ) = 1 k n ​ ∑ i = 1 k n ( Y ( i , n ) ​ ( x ) − f ^ ​ ( X ( i , n ) ​ ( x ) ) ) 2 . \\hat{f}(x)=\\frac{1}{k_{n}}\\sum_{i=1}^{k_{n}}Y_{(i,n)}(x)\\;\\;{\\rm and}\\;\\;\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{k_{n}}\\sum_{i=1}^{k_{n}}\\left(Y_{(i,n)}(x)-\\hat{f}(X_{(i,n)}(x))\\right)^{2}\\kern 5.0pt. Conditional on 𝒟 n \\mathcal{D}_{n} , the cumulative distribution function F σ ^ 2 F_{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}} is not continuous and then Assumption 2 does not hold. To avoid this issue, we introduce a random perturbation ζ \\zeta distributed according to the Uniform distribution on [ 0 , u ] [0,u] that is independent from every other random variable where u &gt; 0 u&gt;0 is a (small) fixed real number that will be specified later. Then, we define the random variable σ ¯ 2 ​ ( X , ζ ) := σ ^ 2 ​ ( X ) + ζ \\bar{\\sigma}^{2}(X,\\zeta):=\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}(X)+\\zeta . It is not difficult to see that, conditional on 𝒟 n \\mathcal{D}_{n} the cumulative distribution F σ ¯ 2 F_{\\bar{\\sigma}^{2}} of σ ¯ 2 ​ ( X , ζ ) \\bar{\\sigma}^{2}(X,\\zeta) is continuous. Furthermore, by the triangle inequality, the consistency of σ ^ 2 \\hat{\\sigma}^{2} implies the consistency of σ ¯ 2 \\bar{\\sigma}^{2} provided that u u tends to 0 0 . Therefore, we naturally define the k k NN predictor with reject option as follows. Let ( ζ 1 , … , ζ N ) (\\zeta_{1},\\ldots,\\zeta_{N}) be independent copies of ζ \\zeta and independent of every other random variable. We set F ^ σ ¯ 2 ( . ) = 1 N ∑ i = 1 N 𝟙 { σ ^ 2 ( X n + i ) + ζ i ≤ ⋅ } , \\hat{F}_{\\bar{\\sigma}^{2}}(.)=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\mathds{1}_{\\{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}(X_{n+i})+\\zeta_{i}\\leq\\cdot\\}}\\kern 5.0pt, and the k k NN ε \\varepsilon -predictor with reject option is then defined for all x x and ζ \\zeta as Γ ^ ε ​ ( x , ζ ) = { { f ^ ​ ( x ) } if ​ F ^ σ ¯ 2 ​ ( σ ¯ 2 ​ ( x , ζ ) ) ≤ 1 − ε ∅ otherwise . \\hat{\\Gamma}_{\\varepsilon}(x,\\zeta)=\\begin{cases}\\left\\{\\hat{f}(x)\\right\\}&amp;\\text{if}\\;\\;\\hat{F}_{\\bar{\\sigma}^{2}}(\\bar{\\sigma}^{2}(x,\\zeta))\\leq 1-\\varepsilon\\\\ \\emptyset&amp;\\text{otherwise}\\kern 5.0pt.\\end{cases} 4.3 Rates of convergence In this section, we derive the rates of convergence of the k k NN ε \\varepsilon -predictor in the following framework. We assume that Y Y is bounded or that Y Y satisfies Y = f ∗ ​ ( X ) + σ ⁡ ( X ) ​ ξ , Y=f^{*}(X)+\\sigma(X)\\xi\\kern 5.0pt, (5) where ξ \\xi is independent of X X and distributed according to a standard normal distribution. Note that these assumptions covers a broad class of applications. Under these assumptions, we can state the following result. Theorem 2 . Grant Assumptions 1 , 3 , 4 , and 5 . Let ε ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) \\varepsilon\\in(0,1) , if k n ∝ n 2 / ( d + 2 ) k_{n}\\propto n^{2/(d+2)} , and u ≤ n − 1 / (