{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2008.07386.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 36295, "equations": [], "text": "[2008.07386] Using Subjective Logic to Estimate Uncertainty in Multi-Armed Bandit Problems Using Subjective Logic to Estimate Uncertainty in Multi-Armed Bandit Problems Fabio Massimo Zennaro and Audun Jøsang OrcID: 0000-0003-0195-8301 OrcID: 0000-0001-6337-2264 Affiliation: Department of Informatics, University of Oslo, 0316 Oslo, Norway Affiliation: Department of Informatics, University of Oslo, 0316 Oslo, Norway E-mail fabiomz@ifi.uio.no,josang@mn.uio.no E-mail fabiomz@ifi.uio.no,josang@mn.uio.no Abstract The multi-armed bandit problem is a classical decision-making problem where an agent has to learn an optimal action balancing exploration and exploitation . Properly managing this trade-off requires a correct assessment of uncertainty; in multi-armed bandits, as in other machine learning applications, it is important to distinguish between stochasticity that is inherent to the system ( aleatoric uncertainty ) and stochasticity that derives from the limited knowledge of the agent ( epistemic uncertainty ). In this paper we consider the formalism of subjective logic , a concise and expressive framework to express Dirichlet-multinomial models as subjective opinions, and we apply it to the problem of multi-armed bandits. We propose new algorithms grounded in subjective logic to tackle the multi-armed bandit problem, we compare them against classical algorithms from the literature, and we analyze the insights they provide in evaluating the dynamics of uncertainty. Our preliminary results suggest that subjective logic quantities enable useful assessment of uncertainty that may be exploited by more refined agents. Keywords: Multi-armed bandits Subjective logic Uncertainty. 1 Introduction The multi-armed bandit problem is a basic instance of a reinforcement learning problem, in which an agent needs to deal with the uncertainty regarding the reward it may receive after taking an action [ 10 , 6 , 9 ] . Such an agent has to balance exploratory actions, aimed to reduce its overall uncertainty (but potentially detrimental with respect to its objective of accumulating as much reward as possible), and exploitative actions aimed at maximizing its rewards (but based on inherently uncertain and possibly incomplete knowledge of the value of all the actions). A multi-armed bandit problem captures the essential problem of taking actions in an uncertain context, and, as such, this model has been used to formalize challenges in many fields, from online advertisement to medical trials. Interest in assessing and quantifying uncertainty has increased in recent years, as a reliable estimation of uncertainty has been recognized as crucial for informed decision-making. Different frameworks to deal with uncertainty exist in the fields of logic, statistics and machine learning [ 2 ] . A central challenge concerns the ability to distinguish between two main forms of uncertainty: aleatoric uncertainty, related to the uncertainty deriving from the intrinsic stochasticity of an observed phenomenon; and epistemic uncertainty, related to the uncertainty deriving from the limits of the inference process itself [ 5 , 2 ] . This distinction has been studied using standard statistical models in machine learning [ 7 , 5 ] . A new and promising approach to the evaluation of uncertainties is based on the use of subjective logic [ 3 ] , a formalism that allows to express specific forms of probability distributions in a simple and interpretable way. This framework has been recently used to assess uncertainties in deep networks [ 8 , 4 ] . In this paper we consider the application of subjective logic to the problem of multi-armed bandits. We propose algorithms to solve the multi-armed bandit problem based primarily on subjective logic quantities, we experimentally assess their performance alongside other classical algorithms, and we analyze the insights we may get from the estimation of uncertainty that subjective logic allows. 2 Background This section presents the basic concepts related to multi-armed bandits, uncertainty and subjective logic. 2.1 Multi-armed Bandits The multi-armed bandits or k k -armed bandit problem [ 10 , 6 , 9 ] is a learning problem in which an agent is required to learn the best action a ∗ a^{*} in a set of actions 𝒜 \\mathcal{A} . Formally, an instance ℬ \\mathcal{B} of the multi-armed bandit problem is defined by a tuple: ℬ = ⟨ 𝒜 , ℛ ⟩ \\mathcal{B}=\\left\\langle\\mathcal{A},\\mathcal{R}\\right\\rangle where 𝒜 = { a 1 , a 2 , … , a k } \\mathcal{A}=\\left\\{a_{1},a_{2},...,a_{k}\\right\\} is a set of k k actions, and ℛ = { ρ 1 , ρ 2 , … , ρ k } \\mathcal{R}=\\left\\{\\rho_{1},\\rho_{2},...,\\rho_{k}\\right\\} is a set of k k probability distributions such that ρ i \\rho_{i} defines the probability distribution over the different rewards associated with taking action a i a_{i} . Whenever the agent takes action a i a_{i} , a reward r i r_{i} is sampled as a random variable R i R_{i} from ρ i \\rho_{i} . Few assumptions are made on the distributions ρ i \\rho_{i} : (i) rewards are finite (i.e., ρ i \\rho_{i} has finite mean and variance); (ii) rewards are independent; (iii) the distribution ρ i \\rho_{i} is stationary. This setup resembles playing with a slot machine equipped with k k levers (also known as a multi-armed bandit). We define the optimal action a ∗ a^{*} to be the action associated with the probability distribution ρ ∗ \\rho^{*} having the highest expected value, that is, ρ ∗ = ρ i ​ s . t . E ⁡ [ R i ] ≥ E ⁡ [ R j ] ​ ∀ j , 1 ≤ j ≤ k . \\rho^{*}=\\rho_{i}\\,\\,\\,s.t.\\,\\,\\,E\\left[R_{i}\\right]\\geq E\\left[R_{j}\\right]\\,\\,\\,\\forall j,1\\leq j\\leq k. Notice that the optimal action does not have to be unique. The true probability distributions ρ i \\rho_{i} and the expected rewards E ⁡ [ R i ] E\\left[R_{i}\\right] are unknown to the agent. The aim of the agent is to interact with the multi-armed bandit and learn the best action a ∗ a^{*} as quickly as possible. To formalize this, a temporal dimension is introduced. Let a ( t ) a^{(t)} and r ( t ) r^{(t)} denote the action and the reward at the time-step t t ; let also ρ ( t ) \\rho^{(t)} and R ( t ) R^{(t)} be the probability distribution and the random variable associated with action a ( t ) a^{(t)} . The objective of the agent is to maximize its expected rewards on a given time horizon T T , that is: max ∑ t = 0 T E [ R ( t ) ] \\max\\sum_{t=0}^{T}E\\left[R^{(t)}\\right] . The simple multi-armed bandit problem constitutes a non-trivial learning game in which the agent is required to carefully balance exploration (trying out actions in order to learn their true rewards) and exploitation (choosing the action estimated as the best in order to maximize the rewards). Classical approaches to solve this problem include randomized-exploration algorithms that relies on random actions to balance exploitation and exploration (e.g.: ϵ \\epsilon -greedy algorithm); statistical-exploration algorithms that estimates statistical measures to balance exploration and exploitation (e.g.: upper-confidence bound algorithm); or, preference-based algorithms that exploit gradient optimization to learn an optimal behavior (e.g.: gradient bandit algorithm). 2.2 Uncertainty Quantifying uncertainty is a crucial challenge in order to create reliable and interpretable statistical models. Let 𝒳 = { x ( 1 ) , x ( 2 ) , … , x ( N ) } \\mathcal{X}=\\left\\{x^{(1)},x^{(2)},...,x^{(N)}\\right\\} be a set of observations generated by an unknown stochastic model p ∗ ​ ( x ) p^{*}(x) . Let us define a parametric statistical model p ⁡ ( x | 𝜽 ) p(x|\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) to describe the observations 𝒳 \\mathcal{X} . In order to fit the model p ⁡ ( x | 𝜽 ) p(x|\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) we learn the values of the set of parameters 𝜽 \\boldsymbol{\\theta} from observations. Inevitably, this fitting procedure is affected by uncertainty, deriving both from the intrinsic randomness of the data generating process p ∗ ​ ( x ) p^{*}(x) and from the limited data to infer the parameters of the model p ⁡ ( x | 𝜽 ) p(x|\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) . We can then distinguish two main forms of uncertainty: • Aleatoric uncertainty represents the uncertainty that is inherent to the data and the process p ∗ ​ ( x ) p^{*}(x) that generated the data [ 5 ] . No matter how much data we provide, an optimally fitted parametric model p ⁡ ( x | 𝜽 ) p(x|\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) can not have less uncertainty than the true underlying generating stochastic model p ∗ ​ ( x ) p^{*}(x) ; in this sense, aleatoric uncertainty can not improve beyond its limit simply providing more data [ 5 , 2 ] . • Epistemic uncertainty represents the uncertainty that is due to finite sampling and the limits of inference in the model [ 5 ] . Interpolation in potentially under-sampled regions of the domain of 𝒳 \\mathcal{X} or extrapolation out-of-domain in regions uncovered by 𝒳 \\mathcal{X} is necessarily affected by uncertainty. Differently from aleatoric uncertainty, epistemic uncertainty may be improved by gathering more data, thus reducing uncertainty in-domain or out-domain. The present study focuses on these two forms of uncertainty 1 1 1 Notice that in the literature it is possible to identify additional types of uncertainties, and that the nomenclature is not universally agreed [ 2 , 7 ] . . Several measures may be adopted to estimate these forms of uncertainty [ 7 ] , and correctly evaluating these two forms of uncertainty would allow for a more insightful analysis of a model. For instance, high aleatoric uncertainty and low epistemic uncertainty may mean that we are dealing with an intrinsically random process that can not be characterized more precisely, even with more data; on the other hand, high epistemic uncertainty may suggest that our conclusions are based on a very limited set of observations, and that more data may be in order. 2.3 Subjective Logic Subjective logic [ 3 ] provides a concise formalism to represent Dirichlet-multinomial and Dirichlet-categorical models. Given a discrete domain 𝒜 = { a 1 , a 2 , … , a k } \\mathcal{A}=\\left\\{a_{1},a_{2},...,a_{k}\\right\\} with k k elements, a multinomial opinion over this domain is a tuple: ω = ( 𝒃 , u , 𝒄 ) , \\omega=\\left(\\boldsymbol{b},u,\\boldsymbol{c}\\right), where 𝒃 , 𝒄 ∈ ℝ ≥ 0 k \\boldsymbol{b},\\boldsymbol{c}\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0}^{k} , u ∈ ℝ ≥ 0 u\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0} , subject to the constraints: u + ∑ i = i k b i = 1 u+\\sum_{i=i}^{k}b_{i}=1 and ∑ i = 1 k c i = 1 \\sum_{i=1}^{k}c_{i}=1 . The vector 𝒃 \\boldsymbol{b} is interpreted as a belief vector , expressing the degree of certainty over the k k elements; u u is an uncertainty scalar , expressing the degree of certainty in modeling the belief; and 𝒄 \\boldsymbol{c} is interpreted as a base rate vector , expressing a prior distribution over the k k elements. Subjective modeling can also be seen as a form of hierarchical modelling, in which the belief vector captures first-order certainty about the distribution of beliefs over the domain, and the uncertainty scalar expresses a global second-order certainty about the modeling of beliefs. This formulation allows for an immediate distinction of the two forms of uncertainty we are concerned with: the belief vector with its distribution over the k k elements expresses aleatoric uncertainty, while the uncertainty scalar expresses epistemic uncertainty. In the subjective logic framework the probability of an element a i a_{i} in the domain of definition may be computed as: P ⁡ ( a i | ω ) = b i + u ​ c i . P(a_{i}|\\omega)=b_{i}+uc_{i}. (1) Moreover, there exist mappings between an opinion ω = ( 𝒃 , u , 𝒄 ) \\omega=\\left(\\boldsymbol{b},u,\\boldsymbol{c}\\right) , an evidential Dirichlet pdf s = 𝙳𝚒𝚛 𝚎 ​ ( 𝐞 ) s=\\mathtt{Dir_{e}}\\left(\\mathbf{e}\\right) with evidence parameter 𝐞 ∈ ℝ ≥ 0 k \\mathbf{e}\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0}^{k} , and a Dirichlet pdf q = 𝙳𝚒𝚛 ⁡ ( 𝜶 ) q=\\mathtt{Dir}\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\\right) with concentration parameter 𝜶 ∈ ℝ ≥ 0 k \\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0}^{k} : τ W ​ ( ω ) : { e i = W ​ b i u if ​ u ≠ 0 e i = ∞ otherwise \\tau_{W}\\left(\\omega\\right):\\begin{cases}e_{i}=\\frac{Wb_{i}}{u}&amp;\\textrm{if }u\\neq 0\\\\ e_{i}=\\infty&amp;\\textrm{otherwise}\\end{cases} (2) σ W ​ ( ω ) : { α i = W ⁡ ( b i u + c i ) if ​ u ≠ 0 α i = ∞ otherwise \\sigma_{W}\\left(\\omega\\right):\\begin{cases}\\alpha_{i}=W\\left(\\frac{b_{i}}{u}+c_{i}\\right)&amp;\\textrm{if }u\\neq 0\\\\ \\alpha_{i}=\\infty&amp;\\textrm{otherwise}\\end{cases} (3) where W W is a non-informative prior weight normally specified equal to 2 2 for consistency [ 3 ] . Since an opinion has more degrees of parametric freedom compared to an evidential Dirichlet or a Dirichlet distribution, there is no exact inverse, and mapping back a Dirichlet distribution onto a subjective opinion requires the choice of a base rate vector 𝒄 \\boldsymbol{c} : τ W , 𝒄 − 1 ​ ( 𝐞 ) : { b i = e i W + ∑ i = 1 k e i u = W W + ∑ i = 1 k e i \\tau^{-1}_{W,\\boldsymbol{c}}\\left(\\mathbf{e}\\right):\\begin{cases}b_{i}=\\frac{e_{i}}{W+\\sum_{i=1}^{k}e_{i}}\\\\ u=\\frac{W}{W+\\sum_{i=1}^{k}e_{i}}\\end{cases} (4) σ W , 𝒄 − 1 ​ ( 𝜶 ) : { b i = α i W − c i 1 + ∑ i = 1 k ( α i W − c i ) u = 1 1 + ∑ i = 1 k ( α i W − c i ) \\sigma^{-1}_{W,\\boldsymbol{c}}\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\\right):\\begin{cases}b_{i}=\\frac{\\frac{\\alpha_{i}}{W}-c_{i}}{1+\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\left(\\frac{\\alpha_{i}}{W}-c_{i}\\right)}\\\\ u=\\frac{1}{1+\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\left(\\frac{\\alpha_{i}}{W}-c_{i}\\right)}\\end{cases} (5) where, as before, we assume W = 2 W=2 as a non-informative prior weight. In conclusion, given an opinion ω = ( 𝒃 , u , 𝒄 ) \\omega=\\left(\\boldsymbol{b},u,\\boldsymbol{c}\\right) , it is possible to reconstruct a standard statistical Dirichlet-categorical model as: 𝜶 \\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{