δ h , h = 0 , … , J \\delta+\\delta_{h},h=0,\\ldots,J does not change ( 11 ). Therefore, without loss of generality, we can fix one α h \\alpha_{h} and one δ h \\delta_{h} ; here, somewhat arbitrarily, we decide to set α 0 = α J = 0 \\alpha_{0}=\\alpha_{J}=0 and δ J = − δ 0 = 1 \\delta_{J}=-\\delta_{0}=1 so, to define ( 11 ), J − 1 J-1 independent parameters are required. 7 7 7 For J J even (i.e., with odd number of categories), we need J 2 \\frac{J}{2} coefficients α h , h = 1 , … , J 2 \\alpha_{h},h=1,\\ldots,\\frac{J}{2} and J 2 − 1 \\frac{J}{2}-1 coefficients δ h , h = 1 , … , J 2 − 1 \\delta_{h},h=1,\\ldots,\\frac{J}{2}-1 , where δ J 2 = 0 \\delta_{\\frac{J}{2}}=0 due to ( 12 ). For J J odd, we must define α h , δ h , h = 0 , … , J − 1 2 \\alpha_{h},\\delta_{h},h=0,\\ldots,\\frac{J-1}{2} . While the choice of the model is arbitrary, 8 8 8 As we said in Sec. 1 , the CL model, ( Agresti 1992 , Sec. 2) , is the most popular choice in the sport rating literature, e.g., in Fahrmeir and Tutz 1994 or in Knorr-Held 2000 . the AC model defined by ( 11 ) has the advantage of being a generalization of the Bradley-Terry (if J = 1 J=1 ) and the Davidson (if J = 2 J=2 ) models which are known to underpin the Elo rating algorithm ( Szczecinski and Djebbi 2020 ) . 2.2 Rating algorithm Rating consists in infering the parameters 𝜽 t \\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{t} from the game outcomes y 1 , … , y t y_{1},\\ldots,y_{t} using the probabilistic model Pr ⁡ { Y t = y t | z t } \\Pr\\left\\{Y_{t}=y_{t}|z_{t}\\right\\} we have shown in the previous section. Following Szczecinski and Djebbi 2020 , we derive the rating algorithm in two steps: first, we define the objective function to be optimized; this is the log-likelihood of the observation y t y_{t} conditioned on the ratings 𝜽 t \\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{t} . Next, we apply the stochastic gradient (SG) optimization which yields an approximate maximum likelihood (ML) solution to the estimation problem. The log-likelihood of the result y t y