{z}{\\sigma}}}{\\sum_{l=0}^{J}10^{\\alpha_{l}+2\\tilde{\\delta}_{l}\\frac{z}{\\sigma}}}. (20) We note that we can treat y ~ t \\tilde{y}_{t} as a new result of the game which takes values in the set 𝒴 ~ = { 0 , δ ~ 1 , … , δ ~ J − 1 , 1 } \\tilde{\\mathcal{Y}}=\\{0,\\tilde{\\delta}_{1},\\ldots,\\tilde{\\delta}_{J-1},1\\} , where we used the obvious relationships δ ~ 0 = 0 \\tilde{\\delta}_{0}=0 and δ ~ J = 1 \\tilde{\\delta}_{J}=1 (implied by ( 19 ) and by δ h = − δ J − h \\delta_{h}=-\\delta_{J-h} ). The shifting and rescaling of the outcomes (i.e., the transformation from the integers y t y_{t} to fractions y ~ t \\tilde{y}_{t} defined in ( 19 )) allows us to treat y ~ t \\tilde{y}_{t} as a “score” of the game y ~ t ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] \\tilde{y}_{t}\\in[0,1] , exactly as it is the case in the Elo algorithm. From the antisymmetry δ h = − δ J − h \\delta_{h}=-\\delta_{J-h} and from Eq. ( 19 ) we obtain δ ~ J − h = 1 − δ ~ h , \\displaystyle\\tilde{\\delta}_{J-h}=1-\\tilde{\\delta}_{h}, (21) which also means that only in the binary and ternary games the score set is predefined, respectively, as 𝒴 ~ = { 0 , 1 } \\tilde{\\mathcal{Y}}=\\{0,1\\} and 𝒴 ~ = { 0 , 1 2 , 1 } \\tilde{\\mathcal{Y}}=\\{0,\\frac{1}{2},1\\} . On the other hand, for J &gt; 2 J&gt;2 we must first find (predefine or estimate) the coefficients δ h , h = 1 , … , J − 1 \\delta_{h},h=1,\\ldots,J-1 and they, in turn, define the scores 𝒴 ~ = { 0 , δ ~ 1 , … , δ ~ J − 1 , 1 } \\tilde{\\mathcal{Y}}=\\{0,\\tilde{\\delta}_{1},\\ldots,\\tilde{\\delta}_{J-1},1\\} . We show how to do it in Sec. 3 . We are now ready to find the skills’ update equations: from z t = θ t , i t − θ t , j t z_{t}=\\theta_{t,i_{t}}-\\theta_{t,j_{t}} we get d ​ L t ​ ( 𝜽 t ) d ​ θ t , i t = d ​ L t ​ ( 𝜽 t ) d ​ z t \\frac{\\,\\mathrm{d}L_{t}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{t})}{\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta_{t,i_{t}}}=\\frac{\\,\\mathrm{d}L_{t}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{t})}{\\,\\mathrm{d}z_{t}} and d ​ L t ​ ( 𝜽 t ) d ​ θ t , j t = − d ​ L t ​ ( 𝜽 t ) d ​ z t \