nd the value function at each of the N N time steps. The maximum drawdown constraint is a time-dependent constraint on the maximal proportion of wealth to invest (recall Lemma 3.3 ). In practice, it is a constraint on the sum of weights of each asset or equivalently on the output of A N ​ N A_{NN} . For that reason, we have implemented an appropriate penalty function that will reject undesirable values: G P ​ e ​ n ​ a ​ l ​ t ​ y ( A , r ) = K max ( | A | 1 ≤ 1 − q r , 0 ) , A ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] d , r ∈ [ q , 1 ] . \\displaystyle G_{Penalty}(A,r)=K{\\rm max}\\left(|A|_{{}_{1}}\\leq 1-\\frac{q}{r},0\\right),\\;\\;A\\in[0,1]^{d},\\;\\;r\\in[q,1]. This penalty function ensures that the strategy respects the maximum drawdown constraint at each time step, when the parameter K K is chosen sufficiently large. Input: the training distributions μ U ​ n ​ i ​ f \\mu_{Unif} and μ G ​ a ​ u ​ s ​ s k \\mu_{Gauss}^{k} ; ⊳ \\triangleright μ U ​ n ​ i ​ f = 𝒰 ⁡ ( q , 1 ) \\mu_{Unif}=\\mathcal{U}(q,1) ⊳ \\triangleright μ G ​ a ​ u ​ s ​ s k = 𝒩 ⁡ ( b 0 , Σ 0 − Σ k ) \\mu_{Gauss}^{k}=\\mathcal{N}\\left(b_{0},\\Sigma_{0}-\\Sigma_{k}\\right) Output: - estimate of the optimal strategy ( a ^ k ) k = 0 N − 1 \\left(\\hat{a}_{k}\\right)_{k=0}^{N-1} ; - estimate of the value function ( V ^ k ) k = 0 N − 1 \\left(\\hat{V}_{k}\\right)_{k=0}^{N-1} ; Set V ^ N = U \\hat{V}_{N}=U ; for k = N − 1 , … , 0 k=N-1,\\dots,0 do Compute: β ^ k ∈ argmin β ∈ ℝ 2 ​ d 2 + 56 ​ d + 283 ​ 𝔼 ​ [ G P ​ e ​ n ​ a ​ l ​ t ​ y ​ ( A N ​ N ​ ( ρ k , B ^ k , β ) , ρ k ) − V ^ k + 1 ​ ( ρ k + 1 β , B ^ k + 1 ) ] \\displaystyle\\hat{\\beta}_{k}\\in\\underset{\\beta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2d^{2}+56d+283}}{\\text{argmin}}\\mathbb{E}\\left[G_{Penalty}(A_{NN}(\\rho_{k},\\hat{B}_{k};\\beta),\\rho_{k})-\\hat{V}_{k+1}\\left(\\rho_{k+1}^{\\beta},\\hat{B}_{k+1}\\right)\\right] where ρ k ∼ μ U ​ n ​ i ​ f \\rho_{k}\\sim\\mu_{Unif} , B ^ k ∼ μ G ​ a ​ u ​ s ​ s k \\hat{B}_{k}\\sim\\mu_{Gauss}^{k} , B ^ k + 1 = H ~ k ​ ( B ^ k , ϵ ~ k + 1 ) \\hat{B}_{k+1}=\\tilde{H}_{k}(\\hat{B}_{k},\\tilde{\\epsilon}_{k+1}) and ρ k + 1 β = F ⁡ ( ρ k , B ^ k , A N ​ N ​ ( ρ k , B ^ k , β ) , ϵ ~ k + 1 ) \\rho_{k+1}^{\\beta}=F\\left(\\rho_{k},\\hat{B}_{k},A_{NN}\\left(\\rho_{k},\\hat{B}_{k};\\beta\\right),\\tilde{\\epsilon}_{k+1}\\right) ; ⊳ \\triangleright F ⁡ ( ρ , b , a , ϵ ) = min ⁡ ( 1 , ρ ⁡ ( 1 + ∑ i = 1 d a i ​ ( e b i + ϵ i − 1 ) ) ) F(\\rho,b,a,\\epsilon)={\\rm min}\\left(1,\\rho\\left(1+\\sum_{i=1}^{d}a^{i}\\left(e^{b^{i}+\\epsilon^{i}}-1\\right)\\right)\\right) ⊳ \\triangleright H ~ k ​ ( b , ϵ ) = b + Σ 0 ​ ( Γ + Σ 0 ​ k ) − 1 ​ ϵ \\tilde{H}_{k}(b,\\epsilon)=b+\\Sigma_{0}(\\Gamma+\\Sigma_{0}k)^{-1}\\epsilon Set a ^ k = A N ​ N ( . ; β ^ k ) ; \\hat{a}_{k}=A_{NN}\\left(.;\\hat{\\beta}_{k}\\right); ⊳ \\triangleright a ^ k \\hat{a}_{k} is the estimate of the optimal control at time k k . Compute: θ ^ k ∈ argmin θ ∈ ℝ 2 ​ d 2 + 54 ​ d + 261 ​ 𝔼 ​ [ ( V ^ k + 1 ​ ( ρ k + 1 β ^ k , B ^ k + 1 ) − V ​ F N ​ N ​ ( ρ k , B ^ k , θ ) ) 2 ] \\displaystyle\\hat{\\theta}_{k}\\in\\underset{\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2d^{2}+54d+261}}{\\text{argmin}}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left(\\hat{V}_{k+1}\\left(\\rho^{\\hat{\\beta}_{k}}_{k+1},\\hat{B}_{k+1}\\right)-VF_{NN}\\left(\\rho_{k},\\hat{B}_{k};\\theta\\right)\\right)^{2}\\right] Set V ^ k = V F N ​ N ( . , θ ^ k ) \\hat{V}_{k}=VF_{NN}\\left(.,\\hat{\\theta}_{k}\\right) ; ⊳ \\triangleright V ^ k \\hat{V}_{k} is the estimate of the value function at time k k . Algorithm 1 Hybrid-Now A major argument behind the choice of this algorithm is that, it is particularly relevant for problems in which the neural network approximation of the controls and value function at time k k , are close to the ones at time k + 1 k+1 . This is what we expect in our case. We can then take a small learning rate for the Adam optimizer which enforces the stability of the parameters’ update during the gradient-descent based learning procedure. 5.3 Numerical results In this section, we explain the setup of the simulation and exhibit the main results. We have used Tensorflow 2 and deep learning techniques for Python developped in Géron 2019 . We consider d = 3 d=3 risky assets and a riskless asset whose return is assumed to be 0 0 , on a 1-year investment horizon for the sake of simplicity. We consider 24 24 portfolio rebalancing during the 1-year period, i.e., one every two weeks. This means that we have N N = = 24 24 steps in the training of our neural networks. The parameters used in the simulation are detailed in Table 2 . Parameter Value Number of risky assets d d 3 3 Investment horizon in years T T 1 1 Number of steps/rebalancing N N 24 24 Number of simulations/trajectories N ~ \\tilde{N} 1000 1000 Degree of the CRRA utility function p p 0.8 0.8 Parameter of risk aversion q q 0.7 Annualized expectation of the drift B [ 0.05 0.025 0.12 ] \\begin{bmatrix}0.05&amp;0.025&amp;0.12\\end{bmatrix} Annualized covariance matrix of the drift B B [ 0.2 2 0 0 0 0.15 2 0 0 0 0.1 2 ] \\begin{bmatrix}0.2^{2}&amp;0&amp;0\\\\ 0&amp;0.15^{2}&amp;0\\\\ 0&amp;0&amp;0.1^{2}\\end{bmatrix} Annualized volatility of ϵ \\epsilon [ 0.08 0.04 0.22 ] \\begin{bmatrix}0.08&amp;0.04&amp;0.22\\end{bmatrix} Correlation matrix of ϵ \\epsilon [ 1 − 0.1 0.2 − 0.1 1 − 0.25 0.2 − 0.25 1 ] \\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;-0.1&amp;0.2\\\\ -0.1&amp;1&amp;-0.25\\\\ 0.2&amp;-0.25&amp;1\\end{bmatrix} Annualized covariance matrix of the noise ϵ \\epsilon [ 0.0064 − 0.00032 0.00352 − 0.00032 0.0016 − 0.0022 0.00352 − 0.0022 0.0484 ] \\begin{bmatrix}0.0064&amp;-0.00032&amp;0.00352\\\\ -0.00032&amp;0.0016&amp;-0.0022\\\\ 0.00352&amp;-0.0022&amp;0.0484\\end{bmatrix} Table 2: Values of the parameters used in the simulation. First, we show the numerical results for the learning and the non-learning strategies by presenting a performance and an allocation analysis in Subsection 5.3.1 . Then, we add the admissible constrained EW to the two previous ones and use this neutral strategy as a benchmark in Subsection 5.3.2 . Ultimately, in Subsection 5.3.3 , we illustrate numerically the convergence of the non-learning strategy to the constrained Merton problem when the loss aversion parameter q q vanishes. 5.3.1 Learning and non-learning strategies We simulate N ~ = 1000 \\tilde{N}=1000 trajectories for each strategy and exhibit the performance results with an initial wealth x 0 = 1 x_{0}=1 . Figures 4 illustrates the average historical level of the learning and non-learning strategies with a 95 % 95\\% confidence interval. Learning outperforms significantly Non-Learning with a narrower confidence interval revealing that less uncertainty surrounds Learning performance, thus yielding less risk. An interesting phenomenon, visible in Fig. 4 , is the nearly flat curve for Learning between time 0 0 and time 1 1 . Indeed, whereas Non-Learning starts investing immediately, Learning adopts a safer approach and needs a first time step before allocating a significant proportion of wealth. Given the level of uncertainty surrounding b 0 b_{0} , this first step allows Learning to fine-tune its allocation by updating the prior belief with the first return available at time 1 1 . On the contrary, Non-Learning, which cannot update its prior, starts investing at time 0 0 . Fig. 4 shows the ratio of Learning over Non-Learning. A ratio greater than one means that Learning outperforms Non-Learning and underperforms when less than one. It shows the significant outperformance of Learning over Non-Learning except during the first period where Learning was not significantly invested and Non-Learning had a positive return. Moreover, this graph reveals the typical increasing concave curve of the value of information described in Keppo et al. 2018 , in the context of investment decisions and costs of data analytics, and in De Franco et al. 2019a in the resolution of the Markowitz portfolio selection problem using a Bayesian learning approach. Figure 3: Historical Learning and Non-Learning levels with a 95% confidence interval. Figure 4: Historical ratio of Learning over Non-Learning levels. Table 3 gathers relevant statistics for both Learning and Non-Learning such as: average total performance, standard deviation of the terminal wealth X T X_{T} , Sharpe ratio computed as average total performance over standard deviation of terminal wealth. The maximum drawdown (MD) is examined through two statistics: noting M ​ D ℓ s ~ MD^{\\tilde{s}}_{\\ell} the maximum drawdown of the ℓ \\ell - t ​ h th trajectory of a strategy s ~ \\tilde{s} , the average MD is defined as, Avg ​ MD s ~ = 1 N ~ ​ ∑ ℓ = 1 N ~ MD ℓ s ~ , \\displaystyle{\\rm Avg\\;MD}^{\\tilde{s}}=\\frac{1}{\\tilde{N}}\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{\\tilde{N}}{\\rm MD}^{\\tilde{s}}_{\\ell}, for N ~ \\tilde{N} trajectories of the strategy s ~ \\tilde{s} , and the worst MD is defined as, Worst ​ MD s ~ = min ⁡ ( M ​ D 1 s ~ , … , M ​ D N ~ s ~ ) . \\displaystyle{\\rm Worst\\;MD}^{\\tilde{s}}={\\rm min}\\left(MD^{\\tilde{s}}_{1},\\dots,MD^{\\tilde{s}}_{\\tilde{N}}\\right). Finally, the Calmar ratio, computed as the ratio of the average total performance over the average maximum drawdown, is the last statistic exhibited. With the simulated dataset, Learning delivered, on average, a total performance of 9.34 % 9.34\\% while Non-Learning only 6.40 % 6.40\\% . Integrating the most recent information yielded a 2.94 % 2.94\\% excess return. Moreover, risk metrics are significantly better for Learning than for Non-Learning. Learning exhibits a lower standard deviation of terminal wealth than Non-Learning ( 11.88 % 11.88\\% versus 16.67 % 16.67\\% ), with a difference of 4.79 % 4.79\\% . More interestingly, the maximum drawdown is notably better controlled by Learning than by Non-Learning, on average ( − 1.53 % -1.53\\% versus − 6.54 % -6.54\\% ) and in the worst case ( − 11.74 % -11.74\\% versus − 27.18 % -27.18\\% ). This result suggests that learning from new observations, helps the strategy to better handle the dual objective of maximizing total wealth while controlling the maximum drawdown. We also note that learning improves the Sharpe ratio by 104.95 % 104.95\\% and the Calmar ratio by 525.26 % 525.26\\% . Statistic Learning Non-Learning Difference Avg total performance 9.34% 6.40% 2.94% Std dev. of X T X_{T} 11.88% 16.67% -4.79% Sharpe ratio 0.79 0.38 104.95% Avg MD -1.53% -6.54% 5.01% Worst MD -11.74% -27.18% 15.44% Calmar ratio 6.12 0.98 525.26% Table 3: Performance metrics: Learning and Non-Learning. The difference for ratios are computed as relative improvement. Fig. 5 and 6 focus more precisely on the portfolio allocation. The graphs of Fig. 5 show the historical average allocation for each of the three risky assets. First, none of the strategies invests in Asset 2 since it has the lowest expected return according to the prior, see Table 2 . Whereas Non-Learning focuses on Asset 3, the one with the highest expected return, Learning performs an optimal allocation between Asset 1 and Asset 3 since this strategy is not stuck with the initial estimate given by the prior. Therefore, Learning invests little at time 0, then balances nearly equally both Assets 1 and 3, and then invests only in Asset 3 after time step 12. Instead, Non-Learning is investing only in Asset 3, from time 0 0 until the end of the investment horizon. Figure 5: Historical Learning and Non-Learning asset allocations. The curves in Fig. 6 recall each asset’s optimal weight, but the main features are the colored areas that represent the average historical total percentage of wealth invested by each strategy. The dotted line represents the total allocation constraint they should satisfy to be admissible. To satisfy the maximum drawdown constraint, admissible strategies can only invest in risky assets the proportion of wealth that, in theory, could be totally lost. This explains why the non-learning strategy invests at full capacity on the asset that has the maximum expected return according to the prior distribution. We clearly see that both strategies satisfy their respective constraints. Indeed, looking at the left panel, Learning is far from saturating the constraint. It has invested, on average, roughly 10 % 10\\% of its wealth while its constraint was set around 30 % 30\\% . Non-learning invests at full capacity saturating its allocation constraint. Remark that this constraint is not a straight line since it depends on the value of the ratio: current wealth over current historical maximum, and evolves according to time. Figure 6: Historical Learning and Non-Learning total allocations. 5.3.2 Learning, non-learning and constrained equally-weighted strategies In this section, we add a simple constrained equally-weighted (EW) strategy to serve as a benchmark for both Learning and Non-Learning. At each time step, the constrained EW strategy invests, equally across the three assets, the proportion of wealth above the threshold q q . Fig. 7 shows the average historical levels of the three strategies: Learning, Non-Learning and constrained EW. We notice Non-Learning outperforms constrained EW and both have similar confidence intervals. It is not surprising to see that Non-Learning outperforms constrained EW since Non-Learning always bets on Asset 3, the most performing, while constrained EW diversifies the risks equally among the three assets. Figure 7: Historical Learning, Non-Learning and constrained EW (Const. EW) levels with a 95% confidence interval. Fig. 9 shows the ratio of Learning over constrained EW: it depicts the same concave shape as Fig. 4 . The outperformance of Non-Learning with respect to constrained EW is plot in Fig. 9 and confirms, on average, the similarity of the two strategies. Figure 8: Ratio Learning over constrained EW (Const. EW) according to time. Figure 9: Ratio Non-Learning over constrained EW (Const. EW) according to time. Table 4 collects relevant statistics for the three strategies. Learning clearly surpasses constrained EW: it outperforms by 5.49 % 5.49\\% while reducing uncertainty on terminal wealth by 1.92 % 1.92\\% resulting in an improvement of 182.08 % 182.08\\% of the Sharpe ratio. Moreover, it better handles maximum drawdown regarding both the average and the worst case, exhibiting an improvement of 3.17 % 3.17\\% and 10.09 % 10.09\\% respectively, enhancing the Calmar ratio by 647.56 % 647.56\\% . The Non-Learning and the constrained EW have similar profiles. Even if Non-Learning outperforms constrained EW by 2.5 % 2.5\\% , it has a higher uncertainty in terminal wealth ( + 2.87 % +2.87\\% ). This results in similar Sharpe ratios. Maximum drawdown, both on average and considering the worst case are better handled by constrained EW ( − 4.70 % -4.70\\% and − 21.83 % -21.83\\% respectively) than by Non-Learning ( − 6.54 % -6.54\\% and − 27.18 % -27.18\\% respectively) thanks to the diversification capacity of constrained EW. The better performance of Non-Learning compensates the better maximum drawdown handling of constrained EW, entailing a better Calmar ratio for Non-Learning 0.98 0.98 versus 0.82 0.82 for constrained EW. Statistic Const. EW L NL L - Const. EW NL - Const. EW Avg total performance 3.85% 9.34% 6.40% 5.49% 2.55% Std dev. of X T X_{T} 13.80% 11.88% 16.67% -1.92% 2.87% Sharpe ratio 0.28 0.79 0.38 182.08% 37.63% Avg MD -4.70% -1.53% -6.54% 3.17% -1.84% Worst MD -21.83% -11.74% -27.18% 10.09% -5.34% Calmar ratio 0.82 6.12 0.98 647.56% -19.56% Table 4: Performance metrics: Constrained EW (Const. EW) vs Learning (L) and Non-Learning (NL). The difference for ratios are computed as relative improvement. 5.3.3 Non-learning and Merton strategies We numerically analyze the impact of the drawdown parameter q q , and compare the non-learning strategies (assuming that the drift is equal to b 0 b_{0} ), with the constrained Merton strategy as described in Remark 4.2 . Fig. 10 confirms that when the loss aversion parameter q q goes to zero, the non-learning strategy approaches the Merton strategy. Figure 10: Wealth curves resulting from the Merton strategy and the non-learning strategy for different values of q q . In terms of assets’ allocation, the Merton strategy saturates the constraint only by investing in the asset with the highest expected return, Asset 3 3 , while the non-learning strategy adopts a similar approach and invests at full capacity in the same asset. To illustrate this point, we easily see that the areas at the top and bottom-left corner converge to the area at the bottom-right corner of Fig. 11 . Figure 11: Asset 3 3 average weights of the non-learning strategies with q ∈ { 0.7 , 0.4 , 0.1 } q\\in\\{0.7,0.4,0.1\\} and the Merton strategy. As q q vanishes, we observe evidence of the convergence of the Merton and the non-learning strategies, materialized by a converging allocation pattern and resulting wealth trajectories. It should not be surprising since both have in common not to learn from incoming information conveyed by the prices. 5.4 Sensitivities analysis In this subsection, we study the effect of changes in the uncertainty about the beliefs of B B . These beliefs take the form of an estimate b 0 b_{0} of B B , and a degree of uncertainty about this estimate, the covariance of Σ 0 \\Sigma_{0} of B B . For the sake of simplicity, we design Σ 0 \\Sigma_{0} as a diagonal matrix whose diagonal entries are variances representing the confidence the investor has in her beliefs about the drift. To easily model a change in Σ 0 \\Sigma_{0} , we define the modified covariance matrix Σ ~ \\tilde{\\Sigma} as Σ ~ u ​ n ​ c := u ​ n ​ c ∗ Σ 0 , \\displaystyle\\tilde{\\Sigma}_{unc}:=unc*\\Sigma_{0}, where u ​ n ​ c &gt; 0 unc&gt;0 . From now on, the prior of B B is 𝒩 ⁡ ( b 0 , Σ ~ u ​ n ​ c ) \\mathcal{N}(b_{0},\\tilde{\\Sigma}_{unc}) . A higher value of u ​ n ​ c unc means a higher uncertainty materialized by a lower confidence in the prior estimate of the expected return of B B , b 0 b_{0} . We consider learning strategies with values of u ​ n ​ c ∈ { 1 / 6 , 1 , 3 , 6 , 12 } unc\\in\\{1/6,\\;1,\\;3,\\;6,\\;12\\} . The value u ​ n ​ c = 1 unc=1 was used for Learning in Subsection 5.3 . Equation ( 2 ) implies that the returns’ probability distribution depends upon u ​ n ​ c unc . It implies that for each value of u ​ n ​ c unc , we need to compute both Learning and Non-Learning on the returns sample drawn from the same probability law to make relevant comparisons. Therefore, from a sample of a thousand returns paths’ draws, we plot in Fig. 12 the average curves of the excess return of Learning over its associated Non-Learning, for different values of the uncertainty parameter unc . Figure 12: Excess return of Learning over Non-Learning with a 95% confidence interval for different levels of uncertainty. Looking at Fig. 12 , we notice that when uncertainty about b 0 b_{0} is low, i.e. u ​ n ​ c = 1 / 6 unc=1/6 , Learning is close to Non-Learning and unsurprisingly the associated excess return is small. Then, as we increase the value of unc the curves steepen increasingly showing the effect of learning in generating excess return. Table 5 summarises key statistics for the ten strategies computed in this section. When ​ u ​ n ​ c = 1 / 6 \\emph{unc}=1/6 , Learning underperforms Non-Learning. This is explained by the fact that Non-Learning has no doubt about b 0 b_{0} and knows Asset 3 is the best performing asset acoording to its prior, whereas Learning, even with low uncertainty, needs to learn it generating a lag which explains the underperformance on average. For values of ​ u ​ n ​ c ≥ 1 \\emph{unc}\\geq 1 Learning outperforms Non-learning increasingly, as can be seen on Fig. 14 , at the cost of a growing standard deviation of terminal wealth. The Sharpe ratio of terminal wealth is higher for Learning than for Non-Learning for any value of ​ u ​ n ​ c \\emph{unc} . Nevertheless, an interesting fact is that the ratio rises from u ​ n ​ c = 1 / 6 unc=1/6 to u ​ n ​ c = 1 unc=1 , then reaches a level close to 0.8 0.8 for values of ​ u ​ n ​ c = 1 , 3 , 6 \\emph{unc}=1,3,6 then decreases when ​ u ​ n ​ c = 12 \\emph{unc}=12 . ​ u ​ n ​ c = 1 / 6 {\\emph{unc}=1/6} ​ u ​ n ​ c = 1 {\\emph{unc}=1} ​ u ​ n ​ c = 3 {\\emph{unc}=3} ​ u ​ n ​ c = 6 {\\emph{unc}=6} ​ u ​ n ​ c = 12 {\\emph{unc}=12} Statistic L NL L NL L NL L NL L NL Avg total performance 3.87% 4.35% 9.45% 6.00% 19.96% 10.25% 90.03% 16.22% 130.07% 30.44% Std dev. of X T X_{T} 5.81% 9.22% 12.10% 17.28% 25.01% 28.18 % 113.69% 41.24% 222.77% 70.84% Sharpe ratio 0.67 0.47 0.78 0.35 0.80 0.36 0.79 0.39 0.58 0.43 Avg MD -2.51% -5.21% -1.40% -6.78% -1.90% -8.40% -2.68% -10.14% -3.58% -11.35% Worst MD -7.64% -17.88% -5.46% -24.01% -7.99% -26.68% -15.62% -29.22% -16.98% -29.47% Calmar ratio 1.54 0.83 6.77 0.89 10.49 1.22 33.65 1.60 36.32 2.68 Table 5: Performance and risk metrics: Learning (L) vs Non-Learning (NL) for different values of uncertainty ​ u ​ n ​ c \\emph{unc} . This phenomenon is more visible on Fig. 14 that displays the Sharpe ratio of terminal wealth of Learning and Non-Learning according to the values of ​ u ​ n ​ c \\emph{unc} , and the associated relative improvement. Clearly, looking at Figures 14 and 14 , we remark that while increasing u ​ n ​ c unc gives more excess return, too high values of u ​ n ​ c unc in the model turn out to be a drag as far as Sharpe ratio improvement is concerned. Figure 13: Average total performance of Learning (L) and Non-Learning (NL), and excess return, for unc ∈ { 1 / 6 , 1 , 3 , 6 , 12 } \\in\\{1/6,1,3,6,12\\} . Figure 14: Sharpe ratio of terminal wealth of Learning (L) and Non-Learning (NL), and relative improvement, for unc ∈ { 1 / 6 , 1 , 3 , 6 , 12 } \\in\\{1/6,1,3,6,12\\} . For any value of ​ u ​ n ​ c \\emph{unc} , Learning handles maximum drawdown significantly better than Non-Learning whatever it is the average or the worst. This results in a better performance per unit of average maximum drawdown (Calmar ratio), for Learning. We also see that the maximum drawdown constraint is satisfied for every strategies of the sample and for any value of u ​ n ​ c unc since the worst maximum drawdown is always above − 30 % -30\\% , the lowest admissible value with a loss aversion parameter q q set at 0.7 0.7 . Figure 15: Average maximum drawdown of Learning (L) and Non-Learning (NL) and the gain from learning for unc ∈ { 1 / 6 , 1 , 3 , 6 , 12 } \\in\\{1/6,1,3,6,12\\} . Fig. 15 reveals how the average maximum drawdown behaves regarding the level of uncertainty. Non-Learning maximum drawdown behaves linearly with uncertainty: the wider the range of possible values of B B the higher the maximum drawdown is on average. It emphasizes its inability to adapt to an environment in which the returns have different behaviors compared to their expectations. Learning instead, manages to keep a low maximum drawdown for any value of ​ u ​ n ​ c \\emph{unc} . Given the previous remarks, it is obvious that the gain in maximum drawdown from learning grows with the level of uncertainty. Figures 16 - 20 represent portfolio allocations averaged over the simulations. They depict, for each value of the uncertainty parameter u ​ n ​ c unc , the average proportion of wealth invested, in each of the three assets, by Learning and Non-Learning. The purpose is not to compare the graphs with different values of u ​ n ​ c unc since the allocation is not performed on the same sample of returns. Rather, we can identify trends that are typically differentiating Learning from Non-Learning allocations. Since the maximum drawdown constraint is satisfied by the capped sum of total weights that can be invested, the allocations of both Learning and Non-Learning are mainly based on the expected returns of the assets. Non-Learning, by definition, does not depend on the value of the uncertainty parameter. Hence, no matter the value of u ​ n ​ c unc , its allocation is easy to characterize since it saturates its constraint investing in the asset that has the best expected return according to the prior. In our setup, Asset 3 3 has the highest expected return, so Non-Learning invests only in it and saturates its constraint of roughly 30 % 30\\% during all the investment period. The slight change of the average weight in Asset 3 3 comes from ρ \\rho , the ratio wealth over maximum wealth, changing over time. Figure 16: Learning and Non-Learning historical assets’ allocations with u ​ n ​ c = 1 / 6 unc=1/6 . Unlike Non-Learning, depending of the value of u ​ n ​ c unc , Learning can perform more sophisticated allocations because it can adjust the weights according to the incoming information. Nonetheless, in Fig. 16 , when u ​ n ​ c unc is low, Learning and Non-Learning look similar regarding their weights allocation since both strategies invest, as of time 0 0 , a significant proportion of their wealth only in Asset 3 3 . On the right panel of Fig. 16 , the progressive increase in the weight of Asset 3 3 illustrates the learning process. As time goes by, Learning progressively increases the weight in Asset 3 3 since it has the highest expected return. It also explains why Learning underperforms Non-Learning for low values of u ​ n ​ c unc ; contrary to Non-Learning which invests at full capacity in Asset 3 3 , Learning needs to learn that Asset 3 3 is the optimal choice. Figure 17: Learning and Non-Learning historical assets’ allocations with u ​ n ​ c = 1 unc=1 . Figure 18: Learning and Non-Learning historical assets’ allocations with u ​ n ​ c = 3 unc=3 . However, as uncertainty increases, Learning and Non-Learning strategies start differentiating. When u ​ n ​ c ≥ 1 unc\\geq 1 , Learning invests little, if any, at time 0 0 . In addition, an increase in u ​ n ​ c unc allows the inital drift to lie in a wider range and generates investment opportunities for Learning. This explains why Learning invests in Asset 1 1 when u ​ n ​ c = 1 , 3 , 6 , 12 unc={1,\\;3,\\;6,\\;12} although the estimate b 0 b_{0} for this asset is lower than for Asset 3 3 . In Fig. 19 , we see that Learning even invests in Asset 2 2 which has the lowest expected drift. Figure 19: Learning and Non-Learning historical assets’ allocations with u ​ n ​ c = 6 unc=6 . Figure 20: Learning and Non-Learning historical assets’ allocations with u ​ n ​ c = 12 unc=12 . Figures 21 - 25 illustrate the historical total percentage of wealth allocated for Learning and Non-Learning with different levels of uncertainty. As seen previously, Non-Learning has fully invested in Asset 3 3 for any value of u ​ n ​ c unc . Figure 21: Historical total allocations of Learning and Non-Learning with u ​ n ​ c = 1 / 6 unc=1/6 . Moreover, Learning has always less investment that Non-Learning for any level of uncertainty. It suggests that Learning yields a more cautious strategy than Non-Learning. This fact, in addition to its wait-and-see approach at time 0 0 and its ability to better handle maximum drawdown, makes Learning a safer and more conservative strategy than Non-Learning. This can be seen in Fig. 21 , where both Learning and Non-Learning have invested in Asset 3 3 , but not at the same pace. Non-Learning goes fully in Asset 3 3 at time 0 0 , whereas Learning increments slowly its weight in Asset 3 3 reaching 25 % 25\\% at the final step. When u ​ n ​ c unc is low, there is no value added to choose Learning over Non-Learning from a performance perspective. Nevertheless, Learning allows for a better management of risk as Table 5 exhibits. As u ​ n ​ c unc increases, in addition to being cautious, Learning mixes allocation in different assets, see Figures 22 - 25 , while Non-Learning is stuck with the highest expected return asset. Figure 22: Historical total allocations of Learning and Non-Learning with u ​ n ​ c = 1 unc=1 . Figure 23: Historical total allocations of Learning and Non-Learning with u ​ n ​ c = 3 unc=3 . Learning is able to be opportunistic and changes its allocation given the prices observed. For example in Fig. 22 , Learning starts investing in Asset 1 1 and 3 3 at time 1 1 and stops at time 12 12 to weigh Asset 1 1 while keeping Asset 3 3 . Similar remarks can be made for Fig. 23 , where Learning puts non negligeable weights in all three risky assets for u ​ n ​ c = 6 unc=6 in Fig. 24 . Figure 24: Historical total allocations of Learning and Non-Learning with u ​ n ​ c = 6 unc=6 . Figure 25: Historical total allocations of Learning and Non-Learning with u ​ n ​ c = 12 unc=12 . 6 Conclusion We have studied a discrete-time portfolio selection problem by taking into account both drift uncertainty and maximum drawdown constraint. The dynamic programming equation has been derived in the general case thanks to a specific change of measure. More explicit results have been provided in the Gaussian case using the Kalman filter. Moreover, a change of variable has reduced the dimensionality of the problem in the case of CRRA utility functions. Next, we have provided extensive numerical results in the Gaussian case with CRRA utility functions using recent deep neural network techniques. Our numerical analysis has clearly shown and quantified the better risk-return profile of the learning strategy versus the non-learning one. Indeed, besides outperforming the non-learning strategy, the learning one provides a significantly lower standard deviation of terminal wealth and a better controlled maximum drawdown. Confirming the results established in De Franco et al. 2019b , this study exhibits the benefits of learning in providing optimal portfolio allocations. Appendix 6.1 Proof of Proposition 3.1 For all k = 1 , … , N k=1,...,N , the law under ℙ ¯ \\overline{\\mathbb{P}} , of R k R_{k} given the filtration 𝒢 k − 1 \\mathcal{G}_{k-1} yields the unconditional law under ℙ \\mathbb{P} of ϵ k \\epsilon_{k} . Indeed, since ( Λ k ) k (\\Lambda_{k})_{k} is a ( ℙ , 𝔾 ) (\\mathbb{P},\\mathbb{G}) -martingale, we have from Bayes formula, for all Borelian F F ⊂ \\subset ℝ d \\mathbb{R}^{d} , ℙ ¯ ​ [ R k ∈ F | 𝒢 k − 1 ] \\displaystyle\\overline{\\mathbb{P}}[R_{