d Genetic Algorithms to create a\nrecommendation system for the team captain in choosing his                                Fig. 1. Workflow graph.\nplayers and their positions.                                            A. Dataset\n     Shimona et al. [13] used techniques from Data Mining                    The dataset used in our experiments is the open source\nto create a proper balance in the imbalanced dataset. They              ODIs (One Day Internationals) dataset. It consists of all the\ncould achieve 97% accuracy on the balanced dataset. The                 major international cricket matches from 1971 onwards. It\nperformance reduced on the imbalanced data.                             contains 1597 matches with 50 overs per innings. Each of\n     All the above papers tried to predict the results of the           the matches has ball-by-ball level statistics of both the\nmatch before the beginning of the match using the past                  innings of the match. The ball attributes and their\ninformation. However, very few works focus on modelling                 description are mentioned in Table I.\nthe overs or balls of the match. One such work is by Jhawar\net al. [14] which is the closest to our work. They attempt to                           Table I. Feature description\nmodel the ODI dataset. However, they train separate models             Attributes of ball    Description\nfor each over of the match which is very inefficient and               Innings number         First inning or Second inning\nunnecessary work. They first compare various machine\nlearning techniques like Decision Tree, SVM, KNN,                      Over and Ball          Number of the over and the ball\nGradient Boosting and Random Forest and find out that                  Batting Team Name      Team batting during that ball\n\n\n\n                                                                   2\n\fBatsman Name            Batsman batting at that ball                     the networks. Our variants are:\n                                                                              1) All overs input and single output model (model A):\nNon-striker Name        Non-strike batsman on the field                  The architecture of this model is depicted in Fig. 2a. As the\nBowler Name             Bowler of that ball                              name suggests, the LSTM cell has an input channel to all the\n                                                                         timestamps but the output channel is present only at the final\nRuns-off-bat            Runs scored in that ball                         cell. In this model, only one prediction is possible at the end\nExtras                  Extra runs during no or wide ball                of the match. One needs to train separate models for\n                                                                         predicting the match after every ball.\nWicket                  1 if there is any wicket in that ball                 Say we need to predict the match after the jth ball. Then\n                                                                         all the data fed to the network need to be up to the j th ball.\n     The dataset also has the following match level statistics:          The output node will be attached to the j th timestamp and\nteam, gender, season, date, series, match number, venue,\ncity, toss winner, toss decision, player of the match, umpire,\ntv umpire ,match referee, winning team and runs scored by\nthe winning team.\n\nB. Dataset Preprocessing and Encoding\n      For our LSTM model, we use the ball by ball level data\nwhile for the machine learning model, we use the match\nlevel statistics as explained later in this section. However,\nthe data needs to be preprocessed before feeding it into the\nneural network. The over and balls are given in the over.ball\nformat where ball is given from 1 to 6 (or 7 if no ball).\nIdeally, there must be 300 balls in one inning of the ODI.\nWe add any extra runs from no ball to its previous ball to\nform a vector of constant length of 300 balls. Next, for each\nof the balls, the continuous data is used as it is in the dataset.\nFor the categorical data such as names, we form a list of all\nthe team and player names and remove the outliers in the                 that gives the prediction of the model after the jth ball.\ndata such as a team with a single match. Then we form a\nOne-Hot encoding of the categorical data. For instance, the\nlist of 30 teams is converted into 30 vectors where the entry\nis 1 if the team is present and zero otherwise.\n\nC. Our Framework Overview\n     The architecture of our framework is given in Fig. 2. It\nshows the best variant of our model. The model consists of\nthree blocks: the Input Transformation (IT) block, the\nLSTM block which is the main building block and the\nOutput Transformation (OT) block.\n     The IT block takes a single ball’s feature vector as input\nand transforms it into a higher dimensional feature vector.\nThis block is shared by all the balls in that innings. The\noutput of this layer is fed to the LSTM cell where the\ncurrent cell’s weights are influenced by the corresponding\ninput features of the same timestamp and the output features\nof the previous timestamp. Next, the output of the LSTM\ncell is fed to the OT block which transforms the LSTM cell\noutput from a higher dimension to a two dimensional vector                    2) All overs input and multi output model (model B):\n(win or lose node). The softmax of this output layer gives               The architecture of this model is shown in Fig. 2b. It\nthe win probabilities of the match.                                      contains all the inputs and all the outputs to the LSTM cell.\n             Fig. 2a. Our all inputs single output model.                Hence, one can predict the match at every ball of the game\n              Fig. 2b. Our All inputs all outputs model.                 by looking at the output node of the corresponding\n                                                                         timestamp.\n     The final two node output is compared with the ground                    3) Randomly sampled input and single output model\ntruth of win or lose by the Binary Cross Entropy Loss. This                        (Model C):\nloss is aggregated for all the balls of the innings and the                   This model is similar in structure as that of Model A.\nmean loss is used to train the network.                                  Except that the input to this model would be a randomly\n                                                                         sampled sequence among all sequences available. To\nD. Network Variants: Four different types of models                      illustrate this model, consider the example below.\n     We propose four variants of our network and conduct                      Let’s say we have 1000 matches in the training data.\nrigorous experimentation to quantitatively compare between               We split the second innings ball by ball – leading us to a\n\n\n                                                                     3\n\fmaximum of 1000*300.. Then we set each of these records                      LGBM                 76.3%                61.3%\nto predict the match was won or not.\nM1B1 – W\n                                                                        C. Performance improvement\nM1B1,M1B2 – W\n                                                                              We conduct an ablation study of our model. In Table IV,\nM1B1,M1B2,M1B3. . . - W\n                                                                        first we show our models improvement by augmenting the\nM2B1 – L\n                                                                        pre-match results from the subsection B of this paper. We\nM2B1,M2B2 – L\n                              