{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2011.03645.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 79406, "equations": ["300 million, 827 mile fiber optic cable from New York City to Chicago. The cable reduced the round-trip latency to 13.1 milliseconds ( Adler 2012 ) by offering a more direct route, bypassing Philadelphia, than the previous 1000 mile cable took with round trip latency 14.5 milliseconds. We want to design systems that work well for society rather than promoting speed that is not needed but is merely a byproduct of the market design. Increasingly, businesses are seen not merely as profit-maximizing, but as responsible and responsive to their various stakeholders ( Table 2020 ) . Maximizing welfare rather than profit has various economic motivations as well. To gain market share and maximize long-term revenue, a company may also want to benefit other companies that it makes deals with. For example, many sponsored search auctions maximize welfare instead of revenue ( Nisan 2009 ) . Since profit is a lower bound of social welfare (when agents’ utility is non-negative), higher welfare potentially leads to high profit. Welfare-maximizing auctions and profit-maximizing auctions are shown to be very close in terms of both welfare and efficiency ( Aggarwal et al. 2009 ; Bulow and Klemperer 1994 ) . In our settings, though our mechanism maximizes social welfare, its profit is still high, illustrated in Figure 7 . Apart from social welfare concerns, another potential challenge with prediction markets is that agents may want to delay reporting their information to increase their rewards ( Azar et al. 2016 ; Chen and Waggoner 2016 ; Kong and Schoenebeck 2018 ) . We show that in the settings we study, this is still a problem for traditional prediction markets, and we resolve this problem in our mechanisms. 1.1. Our Contribution Motivated by the above concerns, we answer the following question: How to aggregate timely and truthful information to maximize social welfare? We formulate the process as a principal-agent problem. The principal first suggests a contract which maps agents’ ", "'s}}}\\right] . By the property of strictly proper scoring rule, his best strategy is to make p n = Pr [ Y | all signals ] p_{n}=\\Pr\\mathopen{}\\mathclose{{\\left[Y\\,\\middle|\\,\\text{all signals}}}\\right] for every possible p n − 1 p_{n-1} . Any deviation will lower his reward. This is achievable due to Proposition 2.3 . ∎ Proof of Lemma 4.4 . Since agents are symmetric to each other, we look for a symmetric equilibrium, where every agent i i invests the same amount of effort c i = c c_{i}=c . Let v k = 𝔼 [ S ( p k , Y ) − S ( p 0 , Y ) ] v_{k}=\\operatornamewithlimits{\\mathbb{E}}\\mathopen{}\\mathclose{{\\left[S(p_{k},Y)-S(p_{0},Y)}}\\right] , the expected increase of the score after k k updates. The expected social welfare is given by: W = 𝔼 [ S ( p k , Y ) ] − n c = 𝔼 [ v k ] + 𝔼 [ S ( p 0 , Y ) ] − n c . W=\\operatornamewithlimits{\\mathbb{E}}\\mathopen{}\\mathclose{{\\left[S(p_{k},Y)}}\\right]-nc=\\operatornamewithlimits{\\mathbb{E}}\\mathopen{}\\mathclose{{\\left[v_{k}}}\\right]+\\operatornamewithlimits{\\mathbb{E}}\\mathopen{}\\mathclose{{\\left[S(p_{0},Y)}}\\right]-nc. Setting the derivative to be zero, we have 0 \\displaystyle 0 = d W d c = d d c 𝔼 [ v k ] − n \\displaystyle=\\frac{\\differential W}{\\differential c}=\\frac{\\differential}{\\differential c}\\operatornamewithlimits{\\mathbb{E}}\\mathopen{}\\mathclose{{\\left[v_{k}}}\\right]-n = d F ⁡ ( c ) d c ​ d d F ⁡ ( c ) ​ ∑ k = 0 n OPEN ( n k CLOSE ) ​ F ​ ( c ) k ​ ( 1 − F ⁡ ( c ) ) n − k ​ v k − n \\displaystyle=\\frac{\\differential F(c)}{\\differential c}\\frac{\\differential}{\\differential F(c)}\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}F(c)^{k}(1-F(c))^{n-k}v_{k}-n = d F ⁡ ( c ) d c ​ ∑ k = 0 n OPEN ( n k CLOSE ) ⁡ ( k ​ F ​ ( c ) k − 1 ​ ( 1 − F ⁡ ( c ) ) n − k CLOSE \\displaystyle=\\frac{\\differential F(c)}{\\differential c}\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}\\Big(kF(c)^{k-1}(1-F(c))