∞ d F ⁡ ( c i ) / d c i = 0 \\lim_{c_{i}\\to\\infty}\\differential F(c_{i})/\\differential c_{i}=0 and thus lim c i → ∞ d u i / d c i = − 1 \\lim_{c_{i}\\to\\infty}\\differential u_{i}/\\differential c_{i}=-1 . Therefore, there is a unique c i c_{i} that maximizes the utility, which is either (a) c i = 0 c_{i}=0 or (b) the point with zero derivative. In case (b), we have (5) 0 = d u i d c i = d F ⁡ ( c i ) d c i ​ ∑ k = 0 n − 1 OPEN ( n − 1 k CLOSE ) ​ F ​ ( c ) k ​ ( 1 − F ⁡ ( c ) ) n − k − 1 ​ ( v k + 1 − v k ) − 1 . 0=\\frac{\\differential u_{i}}{\\differential c_{i}}=\\frac{\\differential F(c_{i})}{\\differential c_{i}}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\binom{n-1}{k}F(c)^{k}(1-F(c))^{n-k-1}(v_{k+1}-v_{k})-1. Equation 5 describes how an agent’s decision c i c_{i} should best response to those of others c c . In a symmetric equilibrium, c i = c c_{i}=c . Then, surprisingly, Equation 5 becomes equivalent to Equation 4 . In case (a), both individually optimal and socially optimal solutions are c i = 0 c_{i}=0 . In other words, the distributed maximization of each agent’s utility can result in the maximization of social welfare. ∎ Proof of Lemma 4.5 . Since signals are identically distributed and the effort function is the same for every agent, an agent is indistinguishable from another. The lemma simply follows. ∎ 5. Sequential Model In this section, we consider a setting that involves time. Its difference from the setting of the previous section is that signals are not received by agents immediately but will be eventually. For", "}} − ∑ k = 0 n − 1 OPEN ( n − 1 k CLOSE ) F c ( t ) k ( 1 − F c ( t ) ) n − k − 1 v k ) h ( t ) d t − n \\displaystyle\\hskip 42.67912pt-\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\binom{n-1}{k}F_{c}(t)^{k}(1-F_{c}(t))^{n-k-1}v_{k}\\Bigg)h(t)\\differential t-n (9) = n ​ ∫ t &gt; 0 d F c ​ ( t ) d c ​ ∑ k = 0 n − 1 OPEN ( n − 1 k CLOSE ) ​ F c ​ ( t ) k ​ ( 1 − F c ​ ( t ) ) n − k − 1 ⋅ ( v k + 1 − v k ) ​ h ​ ( t ) ​ d t − n . \\displaystyle\\begin{split}&amp;=n\\int_{t&gt;0}\\fr