We pause to make an important observation. From Equation 6 , the rating carries a rather intuitive interpretation: Gaussian factors in L L become L 2 L_{2} penalty terms, whereas logistic factors take on a more interesting form as L R L_{R} terms. From Figure 1 , we see that the L R L_{R} term behaves quadratically near the origin, but linearly at the extremities, effectively interpolating between L 2 L_{2} and L 1 L_{1} over a scale of magnitude β k \\beta_{k} It is well-known that minimizing a sum of L 2 L_{2} terms pushes the argument towards a weighted mean, while minimizing a sum of L 1 L_{1} terms pushes the argument towards a weighted median. With L R L_{R} terms, the net effect is that μ t \\mu_{t} acts like a robust average of the historical performances p k p_{k} . Specifically, one can check that μ t = ∑ k w k ​ p k ∑ k w k , where ​ w 0 := 1 β 0 2 ​ and \\mu_{t}=\\frac{\\sum_{k}w_{k}p_{k}}{\\sum_{k}w_{k}},\\text{ where }w_{0}:=\\frac{1}{\\beta_{0}^{2}}\\text{ and } (7) w k := π ( μ t − p k ) ​ β k ​ 3 ​ tanh ⁡ ( μ t − p k ) ​ π β k ​ 12 ​ for ​ k ∈ ℋ t . w_{k}:=\\frac{\\pi}{(\\mu_{t}-p_{k})\\beta_{k}\\sqrt{3}}\\tanh\\frac{(\\mu_{t}-p_{k})\\pi}{\\beta_{k}\\sqrt{12}}\\text{ for }k\\in{\\mathcal{H}}_{t}. w k w_{k} is close to 1 / β k 2 1/\\beta_{k}^{2} for typical performances, but can be up to π 2 / 6 \\pi^{2}/6 times more as | μ t − p k | → 0 |\\mu_{t}-p_{k}|\\rightarrow 0 , or vanish as | μ t − p k | → ∞ |\\mu_{t}-p_{k}|\\rightarrow\\infty . This feature is due to the thicker tails of the logistic distribution, as compared to the Gaussian, resulting in an algorithm that resists drastic rating changes in the presence of a few unusually good or bad performances. We’ll formally state this robustness property in Theorem 5.7 . Estimating skill uncertainty While there is no easy way to compute the variance of a posterior in the form of Equation 5 , it will be useful to have some estimate σ t 2 \\sigma_{t}^{2} of uncertainty. There is a simple formula in the ca