are Gaussian, then the posterior is also Gaussian. Adding an independent Gaussian diffusion to our posterior on S t − 1 S_{t-1} yields a Gaussian prior on S t S_{t} . By induction, the skill belief distribution forever remains Gaussian. This Gaussian specialization of the Elo-MMR framework lacks the R for robustness (see Theorem 5.7 ), so we call it Elo-MM χ \\chi . Logistic performance model After a player’s first contest round, the posterior in Equation 5 becomes non-Gaussian, rendering the integral in Equation 9 intractable. A very simple approach would be to replace the full posterior in Equation 5 by a Gaussian approximation with mean μ t \\mu_{t} (equal to the posterior MAP) and variance σ t 2 \\sigma_{t}^{2} (given by Equation 8 ). As in the previous case, applying diffusions in the Gaussian setting is a simple matter of adding means and variances. With this approximation, no memory is kept of the individual performances P t P_{t} . Priors are simply Gaussian, while posterior densities are the product of two factors: the Gaussian prior, and a logistic factor corresponding to the latest performance. To ensure robustness (see Section 5.2 ), μ t \\mu_{t} is computed as the argmax of this posterior before replacement by its Gaussian approximation. We call the rating system that takes this approach Elo-MMR( ∞ \\infty ). As the name implies, it turns out to be a special case of Elo-MMR( ρ \\rho ). In the general setting with ρ ∈ [ 0 , ∞ ) \\rho\\in[0,\\infty) , we keep the full posterior from Equation 5 . Since we cannot tractably compute the effect of a Gaussian diffusion, we seek a heuristic derivation of the next round’s prior, retaining a form similar to Equation 5 while satisfying many of the same properties as the intended diffusion. 4.1. Desirable properties of a “pseudodiffusion” We begin by listing some properties that our skill evolution algorithm, henceforth called a “pseudodiffusion”, should satisfy. The first two properties are natural: • Aligned incentives. First and foremost, the pseudodiffusion must not break the aligned incentives property of our rating system. That is, a rating-maximizing player should never be motivated to lose on purpose ( Theorem 5.5 ). • Rating preservation. The pseudodiffusion must not alter the arg ​ max \\argmax of the belief density. That is, the rating of a player should not change: μ t π = μ t − 1 \\mu^{\\pi}_{t}=\\mu_{t-1} . In addition, we borrow four properties of Gaussian diffusions: • Correct magnitude. Pseudodiffusion with parameter γ 2 \\gamma^{2} must increase the skill uncertainty, as measured by Equation 8 , by γ 2 \\gamma^{2} . • Composability. Two pseudodiffusions applied in sequence, first with parameter γ 1 2 \\gamma_{1}^{2} and then with γ 2 2 \\gamma_{2}^{2} , must have the same effect as a single pseudodiffusion with parameter γ 1 2 + γ 2 2 \\gamma_{1}^{2}+\\gamma_{2}^{2} . • Zero diffusion. In the limit as γ → 0 \\gamma\\rightarrow 0 , the effect of pseudodiffusion must vanish, i.e., not alter the belief distribution. • Zero uncertainty. In the limit as σ t − 1 → 0 \\sigma_{t-1}\\rightarrow 0 (i.e., when the previous rating μ t − 1 \\mu_{t-1} is a perfect estimate of S t − 1 S_{t-1} ), our belief on S t S_{t} must become Gaussian with mean μ t − 1 \\mu_{t-1} and variance γ 2 \\gamma^{2} . Finer-grained information regarding the prior history P ≤ t P_{\\leq t} must be erased. In particular, Elo-MMR( ∞ \\infty ) fails the zero diffusion property because it simplifies the belief distribution, even when γ = 0 \\gamma=0 . In the proof of Theorem 4.1 , we’ll see that Elo-MMR( 0 0 ) fails the zero uncertainty property. Thus, it is in fact necessary to have ρ \\rho strictly positive and finite. In Section 5.2 , we’ll come to interpret ρ \\rho as a kind of inverse momentum. 4.2. A heuristic pseudodiffusion algorithm Each factor in the posterior (see Equation 5 ) has a parameter β k \\beta_{k} . Define a factor’s weight to be w k := 1 / β k 2 w_{k}:=1/\\beta_{k}^{2} , which