tyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}$}}}}}}\nis a total preorder and\nK\n′\n¯\n\\textstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}\nhas the\nchain property by definition.\nSince\n⩽\nℬ\nK\n′\n{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K^{\\prime}}}\nalways has reflexivity and transitivity, we only need\nto show the totality property. Let\nb\n,\nb\n′\n∈\nB\nb,b^{\\prime}\\in B\nand suppose\nb\n⩽̸\nℬ\nK\n′\nb\n′\nb\\not\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K^{\\prime}}b^{\\prime}\n. We must show\nb\n′\n⩽\nℬ\nK\n′\nb\nb^{\\prime}\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K^{\\prime}}b\n, i.e.\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n′\n)\n⊇\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\n(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b^{\\prime})\\supseteq(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b)\n. To that end, let\na\n∈\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\na\\in(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b)\n.\nSince\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\n⊉\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n′\n)\n(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b)\\not\\supseteq(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b^{\\prime})\n, there is some\na\n^\n∈\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n′\n)\n\\hat{a}\\in(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b^{\\prime})\nwith\na\n^\n∉\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\n\\hat{a}\\notin(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b)\n. That is,\nb\n′\n∈\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\nb^{\\prime}\\in K^{\\prime}(\\hat{a})\nbut\nb\n∉\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\nb\\notin K^{\\prime}(\\hat{a})\n. Since\nb\n∈\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\nb\\in K^{\\prime}(a)\n, we have\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n⊈\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a)\\not\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(\\hat{a})\n. By the chain property for\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\n, we get\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\n⊂\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\nK^{\\prime}(\\hat{a})\\subset K^{\\prime}(a)\n. Finally, this means\nb\n′\n∈\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\n⊆\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\nb^{\\prime}\\in K^{\\prime}(\\hat{a})\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(a)\n, i.e\na\n∈\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n′\n)\na\\in(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b^{\\prime})\n. This shows\nb\n′\n⩽\nℬ\nK\n′\nb\nb^{\\prime}\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K^{\\prime}}b\nas required.\nIt remains to show that\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n¯\n,\nK\n′\n¯\n)\nd({{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}},{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}$}}}})\nis minimal. Since every\ntournament is the dual of its dual, any\nn\n×\nm\nn\\times m\nchain tournament is of\nthe form\nK\n′′\n¯\n\\textstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime\\prime}}\nfor an\nm\n×\nn\nm\\times n\ntournament\nK\n′′\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\n. The above\nargument shows that the chain property is preserved by taking the dual, so\nthat\nK\n′′\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\nhas the chain property also. Since\nK\n′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n, we have\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n≥\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\nd(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})\\geq d(K,K^{\\prime})\n. It is easily verified that the Hamming distance\nis also preserved under duals, so\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n¯\n,\nK\n′\n¯\n)\n=\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n≤\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n=\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n¯\n,\nK\n′′\n¯\n)\nd({{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}},{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime}}$}}}})=d(K,K^{\\prime})\\leq d(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})=d({{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}},{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K^{\\prime\\prime}}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime\\prime}}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime\\prime}}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K^{\\prime\\prime}}$}}}})\nWe have shown that\nK\n′\n¯\n\\textstyle\\o