K(a_{2})\nand\nK\n′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\nis some closest chain tournament with\nthe reverse inclusion\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n2\n)\n⊆\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a_{2})\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(a_{1})\n, then swapping\na\n1\na_{1}\nand\na\n2\na_{2}\nin\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nyields obtain another closest chain tournament\nK\n′′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n. Next, we show in\nLemma\nA.8\nthat\nby performing successive swaps in this way, we can find\nK\n′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\nsuch\nthat\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\n⊆\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n2\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a_{1})\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(a_{2})\nwhenever\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n1\n)\n⊂\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n2\n)\nK(a_{1})\\subset K(a_{2})\n(note the\nstrict inclusion). Finally, we modify this\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nin\nLemma\nA.9\nto additionally satisfy\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n2\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a_{1})=K^{\\prime}(a_{2})\nwhenever\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n1\n)\n=\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n2\n)\nK(a_{1})=K(a_{2})\n. This shows that there always exist an\nelement of\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\nextending the neighbourhood-subset relation\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}\n,\nand consequently it is possible to satisfy\nchain-min\nand\nmon\nsimultaneously.\nDefinition A.6.\nLet\nK\nK\nbe a tournament and\na\n1\n,\na\n2\n∈\nA\na_{1},a_{2}\\in A\n. We denote by\n𝗌𝗐𝖺𝗉\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\na\n1\n,\na\n2\n)\n\\mathsf{swap}({K};{a_{1}},{a_{2}})\nthe tournament obtained by swapping the\na\n1\na_{1}\nand\na\n2\na_{2}\n-th rows of\nK\nK\n, i.e.\n[\n𝗌𝗐𝖺𝗉\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\na\n1\n,\na\n2\n)\n]\na\n​\nb\n=\n{\nK\na\n1\n,\nb\n,\na\n=\na\n2\nK\na\n2\n,\nb\n,\na\n=\na\n1\nK\na\n,\nb\n,\na\n∉\n{\na\n1\n,\na\n2\n}\n[\\mathsf{swap}({K};{a_{1}},{a_{2}})]_{ab}=\\begin{cases}K_{a_{1},b},&a=a_{2}\\\\\nK_{a_{2},b},&a=a_{1}\\\\\nK_{a,b},&a\\notin\\{a_{1},a_{2}\\}\\end{cases}\nLemma A.7.\nSuppose\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n1\n)\n⊆\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n2\n)\nK(a_{1})\\subseteq K(a_{2})\nand\nK\n′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\nis such that\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n2\n)\n⊆\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a_{2})\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(a_{1})\n. Then\n𝗌𝗐𝖺𝗉\n⁡\n(\nK\n′\n,\na\n1\n,\na\n2\n)\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\mathsf{swap}({K^{\\prime}};{a_{1}},{a_{2}})\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n.\nX\nX\nY\nY\nX\n′\nX^{\\prime}\nY\n′\nY^{\\prime}\n(a)\nX\n△\n(\nX\n′\n∪\nY\n′\n)\nX\\mathrel{\\triangle}(X^{\\prime}\\cup Y^{\\prime})\nX\nX\nY\nY\nX\n′\nX^{\\prime}\nY\n′\nY^{\\prime}\n(b)\n(\nX\n∪\nY\n)\n△\nX\n′\n(X\\cup Y)\\mathrel{\\triangle}X^{\\prime}\nX\nX\nY\nY\nX\n′\nX^{\\prime}\nY\n′\nY^{\\prime}\n(c)\nX\n△\nX\n′\nX\\mathrel{\\triangle}X^{\\prime}\nX\nX\nY\nY\nX\n′\nX^{\\prime}\nY\n′\nY^{\\prime}\n(d)\n(\nX\n∪\nY\n)\n△\n(\nX\n′\n∪\nY\n′\n)\n(X\\cup Y)\\mathrel{\\triangle}(X^{\\prime}\\cup Y^{\\prime})\nFigure 2.\nDepictions of the sets in\nEquation\n7\nDepictions of the sets in \\Cref{eqn:swap_lemma_symdiffs}\nProof.\nWrite\nK\n′′\n=\n𝗌𝗐𝖺𝗉\n⁡\n(\nK\n′\n,\na\n1\n,\na\n2\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}=\\mathsf{swap}({K^{\\prime}};{a_{1}},{a_{2}})\n. It is clear that\nK\n′′\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\nhas the chain\nproperty since\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\ndoes. Since\nK\n′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n, we have\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n≥\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\nd(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})\\geq d(K,K^{\\prime})\n. We will show that\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n≤\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\nd(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})\\leq d(K,K^{\\prime})\nalso, which implies\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n=\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n=\nm\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nd(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})=d(K,K^{\\prime})=m({K})\nand thus\nK\n′′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n.\nTo that end, observe that for any tournament\nK\n^\n\\hat{K}\n,\nd\n(\nK\n,\nK\n^\n)\n=\n∑\na\n∈\nA\n|\nK\n(\na\n)\n△\nK\n^\n(\na\n)\n|\nd(K,\\hat{K})=\\sum_{a\\in A}{|K(a)\\mathrel{\\triangle}\\hat{K}(a)|}\nNoting that\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a)=K^{\\prime\\prime}(a)\nfor\na\n∉\n{\na\n1\n,\na\n2\n}\na\\notin\\{a_{1},a_{2}\\}\nand\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n2\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}(a_{1})=K^{\\prime}(a_{2})\n,\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n2\n)\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}(a_{2})=K^{\\prime}(a_{1})\n, we have\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n−\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n\\displaystyle d(K,K^{\\prime})-d(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})\n=\n∑\ni\n∈\n{\n1\n,\n2\n}\n(\n|\nK\n(\na\ni\n)\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\ni\n)\n|\n−\n|\nK\n(\na\ni\n)\n△\nK\n′′\n(\na\ni\n)\n|\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i\\in\\{1,2\\}}{\\left(|K(a_{i})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a_{i})|-|K(a_{i})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime\\prime}(a_{i})|\\right)}\n=\n|\nK\n(\na\n1\n)\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n1\n)\n|\n−\n|\nK\n(\na\n1\n)\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n2\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle=|K(a_{1})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a_{1})|-|K(a_{1})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a_{2})|\n+\n|\nK\n(\na\n2\n)\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n2\n)\n|\n−\n|\nK\n(\na\n2\n)\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n1\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle\\quad+|K(a_{2})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a_{2})|-|K(a_{2})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a_{1})|\nTo simplify notation, write\nX\n=\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n1\n)\nX=K(a_{1})\n,\nX\n′\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n2\n)\nX^{\\prime}=K^{\\prime}(a_{2})\n,\nY\n=\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n2\n)\n∖\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n1\n)\nY=K(a_{2})\\setminus K(a_{1})\nand\nY\n′\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\n∖\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n2\n)\nY^{\\prime}=K^{\\prime}(a_{1})\\setminus K^{\\prime}(a_{2})\nso that\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n1\n)\n=\nX\n;\n\\displaystyle K(a_{1})=X;\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n2\n)\n=\nX\n∪\nY\n\\displaystyle\\quad\\quad K(a_{2})=X\\cup Y\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\n=\nX\n′\n∪\nY\n′\n;\n\\displaystyle K^{\\prime}(a_{1})=X^{\\prime}\\cup Y^{\\prime};\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n2\n)\n=\nX\n′\n\\displaystyle\\quad\\quad K^{\\prime}(a_{2})=X^{\\prime}\nand\nX\n∩\nY\n=\nX\n′\n∩\nY\n′\n=\n∅\nX\\cap Y=X^{\\prime}\\cap Y^{\\prime}=\\emptyset\n. Rewriting the above we have\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n−\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n\\displaystyle d(K,K^{\\prime})-d(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})\n=\n|\nK\n(\na\n1\n)\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n1\n)\n|\n+\n|\nK\n(\na\n2\n)\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n2\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle=|K(a_{1})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a_{1})|+|K(a_{2})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a_{2})|\n−\n|\nK\n(\na\n1\n)\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n2\n)\n|\n−\n|\nK\n(\na\n2\n)\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n1\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle\\quad-|K(a_{1})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a_{2})|-|K(a_{2})\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a_{1})|\n=\n|\nX\n△\n(\nX\n′\n∪\nY\n′\n)\n|\n+\n|\n(\nX\n∪\nY\n)\n△\nX\n′\n|\n\\displaystyle=|X\\mathrel{\\triangle}(X^{\\prime}\\cup Y^{\\prime})|+|(X\\cup Y)\\mathrel{\\triangle}X^{\\prime}|\n(7)\n−\n|\nX\n△\nX\n′\n|\n−\n|\n(\nX\n∪\nY\n)\n△\n(\nX\n′\n∪\nY\n′\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle\\quad-|X\\mathrel{\\triangle}X^{\\prime}|-|(X\\cup Y)\\mathrel{\\triangle}(X^{\\prime}\\cup Y^{\\prime})|\nEach of the symmetric differences in\neq.\n7\nare\ndepicted in\nFigure\n2\n. Note that each of these sets can\nbe expressed as a union of the 8 disjoint subsets of\nX\n∪\nY\n∪\nX\n′\n∪\nY\n′\nX\\cup Y\\cup X^{\\prime}\\cup Y^{\\prime}\nshown in the figure. Expanding the symmetric differences in\neq.\n7\nand consulting\nFigure\n2\n,\nit can be seen that most terms cancel out, and in fact we are left with\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n−\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n=\n2\n​\n|\nY\n∩\nY\n′\n|\n≥\n0\nd(K,K^{\\prime})-d(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})=2|Y\\cap Y^{\\prime}|\\geq 0\nThis shows that\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n≤\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\nd(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})\\leq d(K,K^{\\prime})\n, and the proof is complete.\n∎\nNotation\n.\nFor a relation\nR\nR\non a set\nX\nX\nand\nx\n∈\nX\nx\\in X\n, write\nU\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nR\n)\n=\n{\ny\n∈\nX\n∣\nx\n𝑅\ny\n}\nU(x,R)=\\{y\\in X\\mid x\\mathrel{R}y\\}\nL\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nR\n)\n=\n{\ny\n∈\nX\n∣\ny\n𝑅\nx\n}\nL(x,R)=\\{y\\in X\\mid y\\mathrel{R}x\\}\nfor the upper- and lower-sets of\nx\nx\nrespectively.\nLemma A.8.\nFor any tournament\nK\nK\nthere is\nK\n′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\nsuch that for all\na\n∈\nA\na\\in A\n:\nU\n(\na\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\n⊆\nU\n(\na\n,\n⩽\nK\n′\n𝒜\n)\nU(a,{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})\\subseteq U(a,{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}})\nThat is,\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊂\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\nK(a)\\subset K(a^{\\prime})\nimplies\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a)\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(a^{\\prime})\nfor all\na\n,\na\n′\n∈\nA\na,a^{\\prime}\\in A\n.\nProof.\nWrite\nA\n=\n{\na\n1\n,\n…\n,\na\nm\n}\nA=\\{a_{1},\\ldots,a_{m}\\}\n, ordered such that\n|\nL\n(\na\n1\n,\n⩽\nK\n𝒜\n)\n|\n≤\n⋯\n≤\n|\nL\n(\na\nm\n,\n⩽\nK\n𝒜\n)\n|\n|L(a_{1},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})|\\leq\\cdots\\leq|L(a_{m},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})|\n. We will show by induction that for each\n0\n≤\ni\n≤\nm\n0\\leq i\\leq m\nthere is\nK\ni\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK_{i}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\nsuch that:\n1\n≤\nj\n≤\ni\n⟹\nU\n(\na\nj\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\n⊆\nU\n(\na\nj\n,\n⩽\nK\ni\n𝒜\n)\n1\\leq j\\leq i\\implies U(a_{j},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})\\subseteq U(a_{j},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K_{i}}})\nThe result follows by taking\nK\n′\n=\nK\nm\nK^{\\prime}=K_{m}\n.\nThe case\ni\n=\n0\ni=0\nis vacuously true, and we may take\nK\n0\nK_{0}\nto be an arbitrary\nmember of\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n. For the inductive step, suppose\nA.5\nholds for some\n0\n≤\ni\n<\nm\n0\\leq i<m\n. If\nU\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\n=\n∅\nU(a_{i+1},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})=\\emptyset\nthen we may set\nK\ni\n+\n1\n=\nK\ni\nK_{i+1}=K_{i}\n, so\nassume that\nU\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\nU(a_{i+1},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})\nis non-empty. Take some\na\n^\n∈\nmin\n(\nU\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\n,\n⩽\nK\ni\n𝒜\n)\n\\hat{a}\\in\\min(U(a_{i+1},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}}),{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K_{i}}})\n. Then\na\n^\n\\hat{a}\nhas (one of)\nthe smallest neighbourhoods in\nK\ni\nK_{i}\namongst those in\nA\nA\nwith a strictly\nlarger neighbourhood than\na\ni\n+\n1\na_{i+1}\nin\nK\nK\n.\nIf\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n⊆\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\nK_{i}(a_{i+1})\\subseteq K_{i}(\\hat{a})\nthen we claim\nA.5\nholds with\nK\ni\n+\n1\n=\nK\ni\nK_{i+1}=K_{i}\n. Indeed, for\nj\n<\ni\n+\n1\nj<i+1\nthe inclusion in\nA.5\nholds\nsince it does for\nK\ni\nK_{i}\n. For\nj\n=\ni\n+\n1\nj=i+1\n, let\na\n∈\nU\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\na\\in U(a_{i+1},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})\n.\nThe definition of\na\n^\n\\hat{a}\nimplies\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n)\n⊄\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\nK_{i}(a)\\not\\subset K_{i}(\\hat{a})\n;\nsince\nK\ni\nK_{i}\nhas the chain property this means\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\n⊆\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n)\nK_{i}(\\hat{a})\\subseteq K_{i}(a)\n. Consequently\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n⊆\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\n⊆\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n)\nK_{i}(a_{i+1})\\subseteq K_{i}(\\hat{a})\\subseteq K_{i}(a)\n, i.e.\na\n∈\nU\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\n⩽\nK\ni\n𝒜\n)\n=\nU\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\n⩽\nK\ni\n+\n1\n𝒜\n)\na\\in U(a_{i+1},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K_{i}}})=U(a_{i+1},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K_{i+1}}})\nas required.\nFor the remainder of the proof we therefore suppose\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n⊈\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\nK_{i}(a_{i+1})\\not\\subseteq K_{i}(\\hat{a})\n. The chain property for\nK\ni\nK_{i}\ngives\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\n⊂\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\nK_{i}(\\hat{a})\\subset K_{i}(a_{i+1})\n. Since\nK\ni\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK_{i}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\nand\nK\n⁡\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n⊂\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n^\n)\nK(a_{i+1})\\subset K(\\hat{a})\n, we may apply\nLemma\nA.7\n. Set\nK\ni\n+\n1\n=\n𝗌𝗐𝖺𝗉\n⁡\n(\nK\ni\n,\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\na\n^\n)\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK_{i+1}=\\mathsf{swap}({K_{i}};{a_{i+1}},{\\hat{a}})\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n. The inclusion in\nA.5\nis easy to show for\nj\n=\ni\n+\n1\nj=i+1\n: if\na\n∈\nU\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\na\\in U(a_{i+1},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})\nthen either\na\n=\na\n^\na=\\hat{a}\n– in which case\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n⊂\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\n)\nK_{i+1}(a_{i+1})\\subset K_{i+1}(a)\nby construction – or\na\n≠\na\n^\na\\neq\\hat{a}\nand\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n=\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\n⊆\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\n)\nK_{i+1}(a_{i+1})=K_{i}(\\hat{a})\\subseteq K_{i}(a)=K_{i+1}(a)\n. In\neither case\na\n∈\nU\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\n⩽\nK\ni\n+\n1\n𝒜\n)\na\\in U(a_{i+1},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K_{i+1}}})\nas required.\nNow suppose\n1\n≤\nj\n<\ni\n+\n1\n1\\leq j<i+1\n. First note that due to our assumption on the\nordering of\n{\na\n1\n,\n…\n,\na\nm\n}\n\\{a_{1},\\ldots,a_{m}\\}\n, we have\na\nj\n≠\na\n^\na_{j}\\neq\\hat{a}\n(indeed, if\na\nj\n=\na\n^\na_{j}=\\hat{a}\nthen\nK\n⁡\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n⊂\nK\n⁡\n(\na\nj\n)\nK(a_{i+1})\\subset K(a_{j})\nand\n|\nL\n(\na\nj\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\n|\n>\n|\nL\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\n|\n|L(a_{j},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})|>|L(a_{i+1},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})|\n). Since\na\nj\n≠\na\ni\n+\n1\na_{j}\\neq a_{i+1}\nalso,\na\nj\na_{j}\nwas not\ninvolved in the swapping in the construction of\nK\ni\n+\n1\nK_{i+1}\n, and consequently\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\nj\n)\n=\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\nj\n)\nK_{i+1}(a_{j})=K_{i}(a_{j})\n. Let\na\n∈\nU\n(\na\nj\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\na\\in U(a_{j},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})\n. We must show that\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\nj\n)\n⊆\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\n)\nK_{i+1}(a_{j})\\subseteq K_{i+1}(a)\n. We consider cases.\nCase 1:\na\n=\na\n^\na=\\hat{a}\n. Using the fact that\nA.5\nholds for\nK\ni\nK_{i}\nwe have\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\nj\n)\n=\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\nj\n)\n⊆\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\n⊂\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n=\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\nK_{i+1}(a_{j})=K_{i}(a_{j})\\subseteq K_{i}(\\hat{a})\\subset K_{i}(a_{i+1})=K_{i+1}(\\hat{a})\nCase 2:\na\n=\na\ni\n+\n1\na=a_{i+1}\n. Here\nK\n⁡\n(\na\nj\n)\n⊂\nK\n⁡\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n⊂\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n^\n)\nK(a_{j})\\subset K(a_{i+1})\\subset K(\\hat{a})\n, i.e.\na\n^\n∈\nU\n(\na\nj\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\n\\hat{a}\\in U(a_{j},{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})\n. Applying the inductive\nhypothesis again we have\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\nj\n)\n=\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\nj\n)\n⊆\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\n=\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\nK_{i+1}(a_{j})=K_{i}(a_{j})\\subseteq K_{i}(\\hat{a})=K_{i+1}(a_{i+1})\nCase 3:\na\n∉\n{\na\n^\n,\na\ni\n+\n1\n}\na\\notin\\{\\hat{a},a_{i+1}\\}\n. Here neither\na\nj\na_{j}\nnor\na\na\nwere involved in the swap, so\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\nj\n)\n=\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\nj\n)\n⊆\nK\ni\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\nK\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\na\n)\nK_{i+1}(a_{j})=K_{i}(a_{j})\\subseteq K_{i}(a)=K_{i+1}(a)\n.\nBy induction, the proof is complete.\n∎\nLemma A.9.\nLet\nK\nK\nbe a tournament and suppose\nK\n′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\nis such that\nU\n(\na\n,\n<\nK\n𝒜\n)\n⊆\nU\n(\na\n,\n⩽\nK\n′\n𝒜\n)\nU(a,{<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}})\\subseteq U(a,{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}})\nfor all\na\n∈\nA\na\\in A\n. Then there is\nK\n′′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\nsuch that\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n⊆\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n′′\n{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}}\\subseteq{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime\\prime}}}\n.\nProof.\nLet\nA\n1\n,\n…\n,\nA\nt\n⊆\nA\nA_{1},\\ldots,A_{t}\\subseteq A\nbe the equivalence classes of\n≈\n𝒜\nK\n{\\approx^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}}\n, the symmetric part of\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}}\n. Note that\na\n≈\n𝒜\nK\na\n′\na\\approx^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}a^{\\prime}\niff\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n=\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\nK(a)=K(a^{\\prime})\n, so we can associate each\nA\ni\nA_{i}\nwith a neighbourhood\nB\ni\n⊆\nB\nB_{i}\\subseteq B\nsuch that\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n=\nB\ni\nK(a)=B_{i}\nwhenever\na\n∈\nA\ni\na\\in A_{i}\n.\nOur aim is to select a single element from each equivalence class\nA\ni\nA_{i}\n,\nwhich we denote by\nf\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n)\nf(A_{i})\n, and modify\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nto set the neighbourhood of\neach\na\n∈\nA\ni\na\\in A_{i}\nto\nK\n′\n​\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n)\n)\nK^{\\prime}(f(A_{i}))\n. To that end, construct a function\nf\n:\n{\nA\n1\n,\n…\n,\nA\nt\n}\n→\nA\nf:\\{A_{1},\\ldots,A_{t}\\}\\to A\nsuch that\nf\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n)\n∈\narg\n​\nmin\na\n∈\nA\ni\n|\nB\ni\n△\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n|\n∈\nA\ni\nf(A_{i})\\in\\operatorname*{arg\\ min}_{a\\in A_{i}}{|B_{i}\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a)|}\\in A_{i}\nDefine\nK\n′′\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\nby\nK\na\n​\nb\n′′\n=\nK\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n]\n)\n,\nb\n′\nK^{\\prime\\prime}_{ab}=K^{\\prime}_{f([a]),b}\n, where\n[\na\n]\n[a]\ndenotes the\nequivalence class of\na\na\n. Then\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n]\n)\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}(a)=K^{\\prime}(f([a]))\nfor all\na\na\n.\nNext we show that\nK\n′′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n. Note that\nK\n′′\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\nhas the chain\nproperty, since\na\n1\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n′′\na\n2\na_{1}\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime\\prime}}a_{2}\niff\nf\n(\n[\na\n1\n]\n)\n⩽\nK\n′\n𝒜\nf\n(\n[\na\n2\n]\n)\nf([a_{1}])\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}f([a_{2}])\n,\nand\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n1\n]\n)\n,\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n2\n]\n)\nf([a_{1}]),f([a_{2}])\nare guaranteed to be comparable with respect to\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n′\n{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}}\nsince\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nhas the chain property. To show\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\nd(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})\nis\nminimal, observe that\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n\\displaystyle d(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})\n=\n∑\na\n∈\nA\n|\nK\n(\na\n)\n△\nK\n′′\n(\na\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{a\\in A}{|K(a)\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime\\prime}(a)|}\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nt\n∑\na\n∈\nA\ni\n|\nB\ni\n△\nK\n′\n(\nf\n(\nA\ni\n)\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{t}{\\sum_{a\\in A_{i}}{|B_{i}\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(f(A_{i}))|}}\nBy definition of\nf\nf\n, we have\n|\nB\ni\n△\nK\n′\n(\nf\n(\nA\ni\n)\n)\n|\n≤\n|\nB\ni\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n)\n|\n|B_{i}\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(f(A_{i}))|\\leq|B_{i}\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a)|\nfor all\na\n∈\nA\ni\na\\in A_{i}\n. Consequently\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′′\n)\n\\displaystyle d(K,K^{\\prime\\prime})\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nt\n∑\na\n∈\nA\ni\n|\nB\ni\n△\nK\n′\n(\na\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{i=1}^{t}{\\sum_{a\\in A_{i}}{|B_{i}\\mathrel{\\triangle}K^{\\prime}(a)|}}\n=\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=d(K,K^{\\prime})\n=\nm\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\displaystyle=m({K})\nwhich implies\nK\n′′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n.\nWe are now ready to prove the result. Suppose\na\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\na\n′\na\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}a^{\\prime}\ni.e.\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\nK(a)\\subseteq K(a^{\\prime})\n. If\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n=\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\nK(a)=K(a^{\\prime})\nthen\n[\na\n]\n=\n[\na\n′\n]\n[a]=[a^{\\prime}]\n, so\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n]\n)\n)\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n′\n]\n)\n)\n=\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}(a)=K^{\\prime}(f([a]))=K^{\\prime}(f([a^{\\prime}]))=K^{\\prime\\prime}(a^{\\prime})\nand in particular\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}(a)\\subseteq K^{\\prime\\prime}(a^{\\prime})\n. If instead\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊂\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\nK(a)\\subset K(a^{\\prime})\n, then\nK\n⁡\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n]\n)\n)\n=\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊂\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n=\nK\n⁡\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n′\n]\n)\n)\nK(f([a]))=K(a)\\subset K(a^{\\prime})=K(f([a^{\\prime}]))\n, i.e.\nf\n(\n[\na\n]\n)\n<\nK\n𝒜\nf\n(\n[\na\n′\n]\n)\nf([a])<^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}f([a^{\\prime}])\n. By the assumption on\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nin the statement of the lemma,\nthis means\nf\n(\n[\na\n]\n)\n⩽\nK\n′\n𝒜\nf\n(\n[\na\n′\n]\n)\nf([a])\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}f([a^{\\prime}])\n, and so\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n]\n)\n)\n⊆\nK\n′\n​\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\n[\na\n′\n]\n)\n)\n=\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}(a)=K^{\\prime}(f([a]))\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(f([a^{\\prime}]))=K^{\\prime\\prime}(a^{\\prime})\nIn either case\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n′′\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\nK^{\\prime\\prime}(a)\\subseteq K^{\\prime\\prime}(a^{\\prime})\n, i.e.\na\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n′′\na\n′\na\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime\\prime}}a^{\\prime}\n. Since\na\n,\na\n′\na,a^{\\prime}\nwere arbitrary, this shows that\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n⊆\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n′′\n{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}}\\subseteq{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime\\prime}}}\nas required.\n∎\nThe pieces are now in place to prove\nProposition\nA.4\nProof of\nProposition\nA.4\n.\nFor any tournament\nK\nK\n, write\nℳ\n𝗆𝗈𝗇\n(\nK\n)\n=\n{\nK\n′\n∈\nℳ\n(\nK\n)\n∣\n⩽\nK\n𝒜\n⊆\n⩽\nK\n′\n𝒜\n}\n\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}_{\\mathsf{mon}}\\left({K}\\right)=\\{K^{\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\\mid{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}}\\subseteq{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}}\\}\nBy\nLemma\nA.8\nand\nLemma\nA.9\n,\nℳ\n𝗆𝗈𝗇\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}_{\\mathsf{mon}}\\left({K}\\right)\nis non-empty. Let\n≪\n\\ll\nbe any total order on the set\n𝒦\n\\mathcal{K}\nof all tournaments.\nDefine a function\nα\n:\n𝒦\n→\n𝒦\n\\alpha:\\mathcal{K}\\to\\mathcal{K}\nby\nα\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n=\nmin\n⁡\n(\nℳ\n𝗆𝗈𝗇\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n,\n≪\n)\n∈\nℳ\n𝗆𝗈𝗇\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\alpha(K)=\\min(\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}_{\\mathsf{mon}}\\left({K}\\right),{\\ll})\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}_{\\mathsf{mon}}\\left({K}\\right)\nNote that the minimum is unique since\n≪\n{\\ll}\nis a total order. Defining an\noperator\nφ\n\\varphi\nby\nφ\n(\nK\n)\n=\n(\n⩽\nα\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n𝒜\n,\n⩽\nα\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nℬ\n)\n\\varphi(K)=({\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\alpha(K)}},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{\\alpha(K)}})\n,\nwe see that\nφ\n\\varphi\nsatisfies\nchain-min\nand\nmon\n, as\nrequired.\n∎\nA.6.\nProof of\nTheorem\n5.3\nThe following preliminary result is required.\nLemma A.10.\nLet\nk\nk\nand\nl\nl\nbe integers with\n1\n≤\nk\n≤\nl\n1\\leq k\\leq l\n. Then\n∑\ni\n=\nk\nl\n2\n−\ni\n<\n2\n−\n(\nk\n−\n1\n)\n\\sum_{i=k}^{l}{2^{-i}}<2^{-(k-1)}\nProof.\nThis follows from the formula for the sum of a finite geometric\nseries:\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nn\n−\n1\nr\ni\n=\n1\n−\nr\nn\n1\n−\nr\n\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}{r^{i}}=\\frac{1-r^{n}}{1-r}\nwhich holds for all\nr\n≠\n1\nr\\neq 1\n. In this case we have\n∑\ni\n=\nk\nl\n2\n−\ni\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=k}^{l}{2^{-i}}\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nl\n2\n−\ni\n−\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nk\n−\n1\n2\n−\ni\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=0}^{l}{2^{-i}}-\\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}{2^{-i}}\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nl\n(\n1\n2\n)\ni\n−\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nk\n−\n1\n(\n1\n2\n)\ni\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=0}^{l}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{i}}-\\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{i}}\n=\n1\n−\n(\n1\n2\n)\nl\n+\n1\n1\n−\n(\n1\n2\n)\n−\n1\n−\n(\n1\n2\n)\nk\n1\n−\n(\n1\n2\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{1-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{l+1}}{1-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}-\\frac{1-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{k}}{1-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}\n=\n2\n​\n(\n2\n−\nk\n−\n2\n−\n(\nl\n+\n1\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=2\\left(2^{-k}-2^{-(l+1)}\\right)\n=\n2\n−\n(\nk\n−\n1\n)\n−\n2\n−\nl\n⏟\n>\n0\n\\displaystyle=2^{-(k-1)}-\\underbrace{2^{-l}}_{>0}\n<\n2\n−\n(\nk\n−\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle<2^{-(k-1)}\nas required.\n∎\nProof of\nTheorem\n5.3\n.\nLet\n⊴\n\\trianglelefteq\nbe a total order on\nℕ\n×\nℕ\n\\mathbb{N}\\times\\mathbb{N}\nand let\nm\n,\nn\n∈\nℕ\nm,n\\in\\mathbb{N}\n. For\na\n∈\n[\nm\n]\na\\in[m]\nand\nb\n∈\n[\nn\n]\nb\\in[n]\n, write\np\n⁡\n(\na\n,\nb\n)\n=\n1\n+\n|\n{\n(\na\n′\n,\nb\n′\n)\n∈\n[\nm\n]\n×\n[\nn\n]\n:\n(\na\n′\n,\nb\n′\n)\n​\n⊲\n​\n(\na\n,\nb\n)\n}\n|\np(a,b)=1+|\\{(a^{\\prime},b^{\\prime})\\in[m]\\times[n]:(a^{\\prime},b^{\\prime})\\vartriangleleft(a,b)\\}|\nfor the ‘position’ of\n(\na\n,\nb\n)\n(a,b)\nin\n⊴\n↾\n(\n[\nm\n]\n×\n[\nn\n]\n)\n{\\trianglelefteq}\\upharpoonright([m]\\times[n])\n(where 1 corresponds to the minimal pair). Define\nw\nw\nby\nw\n⁡\n(\na\n,\nb\n)\n=\n1\n+\n2\n−\np\n⁡\n(\na\n,\nb\n)\nw(a,b)=1+2^{-p(a,b)}\nIf we abuse notation slightly and view\nw\nw\nas an\nm\n×\nn\nm\\times n\nmatrix, we\nhave, by construction,\nvec\n⊴\n⁡\n(\nw\n)\n=\n(\n1\n+\n2\n−\n1\n,\n…\n,\n1\n+\n2\n−\nm\n​\nn\n)\n\\operatorname{vec}_{\\trianglelefteq}(w)=(1+2^{-1},\\ldots,1+2^{-mn})\n. Noting that\n|\nK\na\n​\nb\n−\nK\na\n​\nb\n′\n|\n=\n[\nK\n⊕\nK\n′\n]\na\n​\nb\n|K_{ab}-K^{\\prime}_{ab}|=[K\\oplus K^{\\prime}]_{ab}\nfor any tournaments\nK\n,\nK\n′\nK,K^{\\prime}\n, and letting\n∙\n\\bullet\ndenote the\ndot product, it is easy to see that\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle d_{w}(K,K^{\\prime})\n=\nvec\n⊴\n⁡\n(\nw\n)\n∙\nvec\n⊴\n⁡\n(\nK\n⊕\nK\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\operatorname{vec}_{\\trianglelefteq}(w)\\bullet\\operatorname{vec}_{\\trianglelefteq}(K\\oplus K^{\\prime})\n=\n(\n1\n+\n2\n−\n1\n,\n…\n,\n1\n+\n2\n−\nm\n​\nn\n)\n∙\nvec\n⊴\n⁡\n(\nK\n⊕\nK\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=(1+2^{-1},\\ldots,1+2^{-mn})\\bullet\\operatorname{vec}_{\\trianglelefteq}(K\\oplus K^{\\prime})\n=\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n+\n𝒙\n∙\nvec\n⊴\n⁡\n(\nK\n⊕\nK\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=d(K,K^{\\prime})+\\bm{x}\\bullet\\operatorname{vec}_{\\trianglelefteq}(K\\oplus K^{\\prime})\nwhere\n𝒙\n=\n(\n2\n−\n1\n,\n…\n,\n2\n−\nm\n​\nn\n)\n\\bm{x}=(2^{-1},\\ldots,2^{-mn})\nand\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\nd(K,K^{\\prime})\nis the\nunweighted Hamming distance. In particular, since\n𝒙\n\\bm{x}\nand\nvec\n⊴\n⁡\n(\nK\n⊕\nK\n′\n)\n\\operatorname{vec}_{\\trianglelefteq}(K\\oplus K^{\\prime})\nare non-negative, we have\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n≥\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\nd_{w}(K,K^{\\prime})\\geq d(K,K^{\\prime})\n.\nNow, we will show that for any\nm\n×\nn\nm\\times n\ntournament\nK\nK\nand\nK\n′\n∈\n𝒞\nm\n,\nn\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{m,n}\nwith\nK\n′\n≠\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\neq\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K)\nwe have\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n)\n<\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\nd_{w}(K,\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K))<d_{w}(K,K^{\\prime})\n. Since\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n⊆\n𝒞\nm\n,\nn\n\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K)\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\\subseteq\\mathcal{C}_{m,n}\nby\ndefinition, this will show that\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K)\nis the\nunique minimum in\nEquation\n6\n, as required.\nSo, let\nK\nK\nbe an\nm\n×\nn\nm\\times n\ntournament and\nK\n′\n∈\n𝒞\nm\n,\nn\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{m,n}\n. To ease\nnotation, write\nv\n=\nvec\n⊴\n⁡\n(\nK\n⊕\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n)\nv=\\operatorname{vec}_{\\trianglelefteq}(K\\oplus\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K))\nand\nv\n′\n=\nvec\n⊴\n⁡\n(\nK\n⊕\nK\n′\n)\nv^{\\prime}=\\operatorname{vec}_{\\trianglelefteq}(K\\oplus K^{\\prime})\n.\nThere are two cases.\nCase 1:\nK\n′\n∉\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\not\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n. In this case we have\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n≥\nm\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n+\n1\nd(K,K^{\\prime})\\geq m({K})+1\n, and\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle d_{w}(K,\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K))\n=\nd\n​\n(\nK\n,\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n)\n⏟\n=\nm\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n+\n𝒙\n∙\nv\n\\displaystyle=\\underbrace{d(K,\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K))}_{=m({K})}+\\bm{x}\\bullet v\n=\nm\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n+\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n​\nn\n2\n−\ni\n⋅\nv\ni\n⏟\n≤\n1\n\\displaystyle=m({K})+\\sum_{i=1}^{mn}{2^{-i}\\cdot\\underbrace{v_{i}}_{\\leq 1}}\n≤\nm\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n+\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n​\nn\n2\n−\ni\n⏟\n<\n2\n−\n0\n=\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leq m({K})+\\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{mn}{2^{-i}}}_{<2^{-0}=1}\n<\nm\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n+\n1\n\\displaystyle<m({K})+1\n≤\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leq d(K,K^{\\prime})\n≤\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leq d_{w}(K,K^{\\prime})\nwhere\nLemma\nA.10\nwas applied in the 4th step. This shows\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n)\n<\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\nd_{w}(K,\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K))<d_{w}(K,K^{\\prime})\n, as required.\nCase 2:\nK\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n. In this case we have\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n)\n−\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle d(K,\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K))-d(K,K^{\\prime})\n=\n(\nm\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n+\n𝒙\n∙\nv\n)\n−\n(\nm\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n+\n𝒙\n∙\nv\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=(m({K})+\\bm{x}\\bullet v)-(m({K})+\\bm{x}\\bullet v^{\\prime})\n=\n𝒙\n∙\n(\nv\n−\nv\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\bm{x}\\bullet(v-v^{\\prime})\nNow, since\nK\n′\n∈\nℳ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\nK^{\\prime}\\in\\operatorname{\\mathcal{M}}\\left({K}\\right)\n,\nv\n′\nv^{\\prime}\nappears as one of the vectors over\nwhich the\narg\n​\nmin\n\\operatorname*{arg\\ min}\nis taken in\nEquation\n5\n. By definition of\nα\n⊴\n\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}\nwe therefore know that\nv\nv\nstrictly precedes\nv\n′\nv^{\\prime}\nwith respect to the lexicographic order on\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\nm\n​\nn\n\\{0,1\\}^{mn}\n.\nConsequently there is\nj\n≥\n1\nj\\geq 1\nsuch that\nv\ni\n=\nv\ni\n′\nv_{i}=v^{\\prime}_{i}\nfor\ni\n<\nj\ni<j\nand\nv\nj\n<\nv\nj\n′\nv_{j}<v^{\\prime}_{j}\n. That is,\nv\nj\n=\n0\nv_{j}=0\nand\nv\nj\n′\n=\n1\nv^{\\prime}_{j}=1\n. This means\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n)\n−\nd\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle d(K,\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K))-d(K,K^{\\prime})\n=\n𝒙\n∙\n(\nv\n−\nv\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\bm{x}\\bullet(v-v^{\\prime})\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n​\nn\n2\n−\ni\n​\n(\nv\ni\n−\nv\ni\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{mn}{2^{-i}(v_{i}-v^{\\prime}_{i})}\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nj\n−\n1\n2\n−\ni\n​\n(\nv\ni\n−\nv\ni\n′\n)\n⏟\n=\n0\n+\n∑\ni\n=\nj\nm\n​\nn\n2\n−\ni\n​\n(\nv\ni\n−\nv\ni\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{j-1}{2^{-i}\\underbrace{(v_{i}-v^{\\prime}_{i})}_{=0}}+\\sum_{i=j}^{mn}{2^{-i}(v_{i}-v^{\\prime}_{i})}\n=\n2\n−\nj\n​\n(\nv\nj\n−\nv\nj\n′\n)\n⏟\n=\n−\n1\n+\n∑\ni\n=\nj\n+\n1\nm\n​\nn\n2\n−\ni\n​\n(\nv\ni\n−\nv\ni\n′\n)\n⏟\n≤\n1\n\\displaystyle=2^{-j}\\underbrace{(v_{j}-v^{\\prime}_{j})}_{=-1}+\\sum_{i=j+1}^{mn}{2^{-i}\\underbrace{(v_{i}-v^{\\prime}_{i})}_{\\leq 1}}\n≤\n−\n2\n−\nj\n+\n∑\ni\n=\nj\n+\n1\nm\n​\nn\n2\n−\ni\n\\displaystyle\\leq-2^{-j}+\\sum_{i=j+1}^{mn}{2^{-i}}\n<\n−\n2\n−\nj\n+\n2\n−\nj\n\\displaystyle<-2^{-j}+2^{-j}\n=\n0\n\\displaystyle=0\nwhere\nLemma\nA.10\nwas applied in the second to last step.\nAgain, this shows\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nα\n⊴\n​\n(\nK\n)\n)\n<\nd\nw\n​\n(\nK\n,\nK\n′\n)\nd_{w}(K,\\alpha_{\\trianglelefteq}(K))<d_{w}(K,K^{\\prime})\n, and\nthe proof is complete.\n∎\nA.7.\nProof of\nTheorem\n6.1\nProof.\nFirst we set up some notation. For a total preorder\n⪯\n\\preceq\non a set\nZ\nZ\nand\nz\n∈\nZ\nz\\in Z\n, write\n[\nz\n]\n⪯\n[z]_{\\preceq}\nfor the rank of\n⪯\n{\\preceq}\ncontaining\nz\nz\n, i.e. the equivalence class of\nz\nz\nin the symmetric closure of\n⪯\n{\\preceq}\n:\n[\nz\n]\n⪯\n=\n{\nz\n′\n∈\nZ\n∣\nz\n⪯\nz\n′\n​\nand\n​\nz\n′\n⪯\nz\n}\n[z]_{\\preceq}=\\{z^{\\prime}\\in Z\\mid z\\preceq z^{\\prime}\\text{ and }z^{\\prime}\\preceq z\\}\nAlso note that\n⪯\n\\preceq\ncan be extended to a total order on the ranks by\nsetting\n[\nz\n]\n⪯\n≤\n[\nz\n′\n]\n⪯\n[z]_{\\preceq}\\leq[z^{\\prime}]_{\\preceq}\niff\nz\n⪯\nz\n′\nz\\preceq z^{\\prime}\n.\n(⇒) We start with the ‘only if’ statement of the theorem. Suppose\nφ\n\\varphi\nsatisfies\nchain-def\n, and let\nK\nK\nbe a tournament. We need\nto show that\n|\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n−\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n|\n≤\n1\n|\\mathsf{ranks}({\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}})-\\mathsf{ranks}({\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}})|\\leq 1\n.\nBy chain-definability, there is\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nwith the chain property such that\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\niff\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a)\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(a^{\\prime})\nand\nb\n⊑\nK\nφ\nb\n′\nb\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}b^{\\prime}\niff\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\n⊇\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n′\n)\n(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b)\\supseteq(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b^{\\prime})\n. Write\n𝒳\n=\n{\n[\na\n]\n⪯\nK\nφ\n∣\na\n∈\nA\n,\nK\n′\n(\na\n)\n≠\n∅\n}\n\\mathcal{X}=\\{[a]_{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\\mid a\\in A,K^{\\prime}(a)\\neq\\emptyset\\}\n𝒴\n=\n{\n[\nb\n]\n⊑\nK\nφ\n∣\nb\n∈\nB\n,\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n(\nb\n)\n≠\n∅\n}\n\\mathcal{Y}=\\{[b]_{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\\mid b\\in B,(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b)\\neq\\emptyset\\}\nfor the set of ranks in each of the two orders, excluding those who have\nempty neighbourhoods in\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\n. Note that\n[\na\n]\n⪯\nK\nφ\n=\n[\na\n′\n]\n⪯\nK\nφ\n[a]_{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}=[a^{\\prime}]_{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\nif and only if\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a)=K^{\\prime}(a^{\\prime})\n(and similar for\nB\nB\n).\nWe will show that\n|\n𝒳\n|\n=\n|\n𝒴\n|\n|\\mathcal{X}|=|\\mathcal{Y}|\n. Enumerate\n𝒳\n=\n{\nX\n1\n,\n…\n,\nX\ns\n}\n\\mathcal{X}=\\{X_{1},\\ldots,X_{s}\\}\nand\n𝒴\n=\n{\nY\n1\n,\n…\n,\nY\nt\n}\n\\mathcal{Y}=\\{Y_{1},\\ldots,Y_{t}\\}\n, ordered such\nthat\nX\n1\n<\n⋯\n<\nX\ns\nX_{1}<\\cdots<X_{s}\nand\nY\n1\n<\n⋯\n<\nY\nt\nY_{1}<\\cdots<Y_{t}\n. First we show\n|\n𝒳\n|\n≤\n|\n𝒴\n|\n|\\mathcal{X}|\\leq|\\mathcal{Y}|\n.\nFor each\n1\n≤\ni\n≤\ns\n1\\leq i\\leq s\n, the\na\ni\na_{i}\nbe an arbitrary element of\nX\ni\nX_{i}\n. Then\na\n1\n≺\nK\nφ\n⋯\n≺\nK\nφ\na\ns\na_{1}\\prec_{K}^{\\varphi}\\cdots\\prec_{K}^{\\varphi}a_{s}\n, so\n∅\n⊂\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\n⊂\n⋯\n⊂\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\ns\n)\n\\emptyset\\subset K^{\\prime}(a_{1})\\subset\\cdots\\subset K^{\\prime}(a_{s})\n. Since these inclusions are strict, we can\nchoose\nb\n1\n,\n…\n,\nb\ns\n∈\nB\nb_{1},\\ldots,b_{s}\\in B\nsuch that\nb\n1\n∈\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n1\n)\nb_{1}\\in K^{\\prime}(a_{1})\nand\nb\ni\n+\n1\n∈\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\ni\n+\n1\n)\n∖\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\ni\n)\nb_{i+1}\\in K^{\\prime}(a_{i+1})\\setminus K^{\\prime}(a_{i})\nfor\n1\n≤\ni\n<\ns\n1\\leq i<s\n.\nIt follows that\na\ni\n∈\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\ni\n)\n∖\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\ni\n+\n1\n)\na_{i}\\in(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b_{i})\\setminus(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b_{i+1})\n, and\nthus\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\ni\n)\n⊈\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\ni\n+\n1\n)\n(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b_{i})\\not\\subseteq(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b_{i+1})\n. Since\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nhas\nthe chain property, this means\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\ni\n+\n1\n)\n⊂\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\ni\n)\n(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b_{i+1})\\subset(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b_{i})\n, i.e.\nb\ni\n⊏\nK\nφ\nb\ni\n+\n1\nb_{i}\\sqsubset_{K}^{\\varphi}b_{i+1}\n.\nWe now have\nb\n1\n⊏\nK\nφ\n⋯\n⊏\nK\nφ\nb\ns\nb_{1}\\sqsubset_{K}^{\\varphi}\\cdots\\sqsubset_{K}^{\\varphi}b_{s}\n; a chain of\ns\ns\nstrict\ninequalities in\n⊑\nK\nφ\n\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}\n. The corresponding ranks\n[\nb\n1\n]\n,\n…\n,\n[\nb\ns\n]\n[b_{1}],\\ldots,[b_{s}]\nare all distinct and lie inside\n𝒴\n\\mathcal{Y}\n. But now we have found\ns\n=\n|\n𝒳\n|\ns=|\\mathcal{X}|\ndistinct elements of\n𝒴\n\\mathcal{Y}\n, so\n|\n𝒳\n|\n≤\n|\n𝒴\n|\n|\\mathcal{X}|\\leq|\\mathcal{Y}|\nas promised.\nRepeating this argument with the roles of\n𝒳\n\\mathcal{X}\nand\n𝒴\n\\mathcal{Y}\ninterchanged, we find that\n|\n𝒴\n|\n≤\n|\n𝒳\n|\n|\\mathcal{Y}|\\leq|\\mathcal{X}|\nalso, and\ntherefore\n|\n𝒳\n|\n=\n|\n𝒴\n|\n|\\mathcal{X}|=|\\mathcal{Y}|\n.\nTo conclude, note that\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n∈\n{\n|\n𝒳\n|\n,\n|\n𝒳\n|\n+\n1\n}\n\\mathsf{ranks}({\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}})\\in\\{|\\mathcal{X}|,|\\mathcal{X}|+1\\}\n, since there can exist at most one rank which was\nexcluded from\n𝒳\n\\mathcal{X}\n(namely, those\na\n∈\nA\na\\in A\nwith\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\n∅\nK^{\\prime}(a)=\\emptyset\n). For identical reasons,\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n∈\n{\n|\n𝒴\n|\n,\n|\n𝒴\n|\n+\n1\n}\n\\mathsf{ranks}({\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}})\\in\\{|\\mathcal{Y}|,|\\mathcal{Y}|+1\\}\n. Since\n|\n𝒳\n|\n=\n|\n𝒴\n|\n|\\mathcal{X}|=|\\mathcal{Y}|\n, it is clear that\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n\\mathsf{ranks}({\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}})\nand\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n\\mathsf{ranks}({\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}})\ncan differ by at most one, as required.\n(⇐) Now we prove the ‘if’ statement. Let\nK\nK\nbe a tournament. We have\n|\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n−\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n|\n≤\n1\n|\\mathsf{ranks}({\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}})-\\mathsf{ranks}({\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}})|\\leq 1\n, and must show there is\ntournament\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nwith the chain property such that\nφ\n(\nK\n)\n=\n(\n⩽\nK\n′\n𝒜\n,\n⩽\nK\n′\nℬ\n)\n\\varphi(K)=({\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K^{\\prime}}})\n.\nLet\nX\n1\n<\n⋯\n<\nX\ns\nX_{1}<\\cdots<X_{s}\nand\nY\n1\n<\n⋯\n<\nY\nt\nY_{1}<\\cdots<Y_{t}\nbe the ranks of\n⪯\nK\nφ\n\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}\nand\n⊑\nK\nφ\n\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}\nrespectively. By hypothesis\n|\ns\n−\nt\n|\n≤\n1\n|s-t|\\leq 1\n. Define\ng\n:\n{\n1\n,\n…\n,\ns\n}\n→\n{\n0\n,\n…\n,\nt\n}\ng:\\{1,\\ldots,s\\}\\to\\{0,\\ldots,t\\}\nby\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n=\n{\ni\n,\ns\n∈\n{\nt\n−\n1\n,\nt\n}\ni\n−\n1\n,\ns\n=\nt\n+\n1\ng(i)=\\begin{cases}i,&s\\in\\{t-1,t\\}\\\\\ni-1,&s=t+1\\end{cases}\nNot that the two cases above cover all possibilities, since\n|\ns\n−\nt\n|\n≤\n1\n|s-t|\\leq 1\n. For\ni\n∈\n[\ns\n]\ni\\in[s]\n, write\nN\ni\n=\n⋃\n0\n≤\nj\n≤\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\nY\nj\nN_{i}=\\bigcup_{0\\leq j\\leq g(i)}{Y_{j}}\nwhere\nY\n0\n:=\n∅\nY_{0}:=\\emptyset\n. Note that\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n+\n1\n)\n=\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n+\n1\ng(i+1)=g(i)+1\n, and consequently\nN\ni\n+\n1\n=\n⋃\nj\n≤\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n+\n1\nY\nj\n=\nN\ni\n∪\nY\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n+\n1\n=\nN\ni\n∪\nY\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n+\n1\n)\nN_{i+1}=\\bigcup_{j\\leq g(i)+1}{Y_{j}}=N_{i}\\cup Y_{g(i)+1}=N_{i}\\cup Y_{g(i+1)}\nSince\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n+\n1\n)\n>\n0\ng(i+1)>0\nwe have\nY\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n+\n1\n)\n≠\n∅\nY_{g(i+1)}\\neq\\emptyset\n, and thus\nN\ni\n+\n1\n⊃\nN\ni\nN_{i+1}\\supset N_{i}\nfor all\ni\n<\ns\ni<s\n.\nNow, for any\na\n∈\nA\na\\in A\n, let\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∈\n[\ns\n]\np(a)\\in[s]\nbe the unique integer such that\na\n∈\nX\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\na\\in X_{p(a)}\n; such\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\np(a)\nalways exists since\n{\nX\n1\n,\n…\n,\nX\ns\n}\n\\{X_{1},\\ldots,X_{s}\\}\nis a\npartition of\nA\nA\n. Note that due to the assumption on the ordering of the\nX\ni\nX_{i}\n, we have\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\nif and only if\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n≤\np\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\np(a)\\leq p(a^{\\prime})\n.\nLet\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nbe the unique tournament such that\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\nN\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a)=N_{p(a)}\nfor each\na\n∈\nA\na\\in A\n. Since\nN\n1\n⊂\n⋯\n⊂\nN\np\nN_{1}\\subset\\cdots\\subset N_{p}\n, we have\n(8)\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\n\\displaystyle a\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\n⇔\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n≤\np\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle\\iff p(a)\\leq p(a^{\\prime})\n⇔\nN\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nN\np\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle\\iff N_{p(a)}\\subseteq N_{p(a^{\\prime})}\n⇔\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle\\iff K^{\\prime}(a)\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(a^{\\prime})\n⇔\na\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n′\na\n′\n\\displaystyle\\iff a\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}a^{\\prime}\ni.e.\n⪯\nK\nφ\n=\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n′\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}={\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}}\n. Since\n⪯\nK\nφ\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\nis a total\npreorder, this shows that\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nhas the chain property.\nIt only remains to show that\n⊑\nK\nφ\n=\n⩽\nℬ\nK\n′\n{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}={\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K^{\\prime}}}\n. First note\nthat if\na\n∈\nX\ni\na\\in X_{i}\nand\nb\n∈\nY\nj\nb\\in Y_{j}\n, the fact that\n{\nY\n1\n,\n…\n,\nY\nt\n}\n\\{Y_{1},\\ldots,Y_{t}\\}\nare\ndisjoint implies\na\n∈\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\n\\displaystyle a\\in(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b)\n⇔\nb\n∈\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\nN\ni\n=\n⋃\n0\n≤\nk\n≤\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\nY\nk\n\\displaystyle\\iff b\\in K^{\\prime}(a)=N_{i}=\\bigcup_{0\\leq k\\leq g(i)}{Y_{k}}\n⇔\nj\n≤\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n\\displaystyle\\iff j\\leq g(i)\nHence\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\n(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b)\nonly depends on\nj\nj\n: every\nb\n∈\nY\nj\nb\\in Y_{j}\nshares the same\nneighbourhood\nM\nj\nM_{j}\n, given by\nM\nj\n=\n⋃\ni\n∈\n[\ns\n]\n:\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n≥\nj\nX\ni\nM_{j}=\\bigcup_{i\\in[s]:\\ g(i)\\geq j}{X_{i}}\nNote that if\n1\n≤\nj\n<\nt\n1\\leq j<t\n,\nM\nj\n\\displaystyle M_{j}\n=\n⋃\ni\n∈\n[\ns\n]\n:\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n≥\nj\nX\ni\n\\displaystyle=\\bigcup_{i\\in[s]:\\ g(i)\\geq j}{X_{i}}\n=\n(\n⋃\ni\n∈\n[\ns\n]\n:\ng\n⁡\n(\ni\n)\n≥\nj\n+\n1\nX\ni\n)\n∪\n(\n⋃\ni\n∈\ng\n−\n1\n​\n(\nj\n)\nX\ni\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\left(\\bigcup_{i\\in[s]:\\ g(i)\\geq j+1}{X_{i}}\\right)\\cup\\left(\\bigcup_{i\\in g^{-1}(j)}{X_{i}}\\right)\n=\nM\nj\n+\n1\n∪\n⋃\ni\n∈\ng\n−\n1\n​\n(\nj\n)\nX\ni\n\\displaystyle=M_{j+1}\\cup\\bigcup_{i\\in g^{-1}(j)}{X_{i}}\nSince\n1\n≤\nj\n<\nt\n1\\leq j<t\nwe have\ng\n−\n1\n​\n(\nj\n)\n=\n{\n{\nj\n}\n,\ns\n∈\n{\nt\n−\n1\n,\nt\n}\n{\nj\n+\n1\n}\n,\ns\n=\nt\n+\n1\ng^{-1}(j)=\\begin{cases}\\{j\\},&s\\in\\{t-1,t\\}\\\\\n\\{j+1\\},&s=t+1\\end{cases}\nIn particular\ng\n−\n1\n​\n(\nj\n)\n≠\n∅\ng^{-1}(j)\\neq\\emptyset\n, which means\n⋃\ni\n∈\ng\n−\n1\n​\n(\nj\n)\nX\ni\n≠\n∅\n\\bigcup_{i\\in g^{-1}(j)}{X_{i}}\\neq\\emptyset\nand thus\nM\nj\n⊃\nM\nj\n+\n1\nM_{j}\\supset M_{j+1}\nfor all\n1\n≤\nj\n<\nt\n1\\leq j<t\n.\nFinally, since\n(\nK\n′\n)\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\n=\nM\nj\n(K^{\\prime})^{-1}(b)=M_{j}\nfor\nb\n∈\nY\nj\nb\\in Y_{j}\nand\nM\n1\n⊃\n⋯\n⊃\nM\nt\nM_{1}\\supset\\cdots\\supset M_{t}\n, an argument almost identical to\n8\nshows that\n⊑\nK\nφ\n=\n⩽\nℬ\nK\n′\n{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}={\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K^{\\prime}}}\n.\nWe have shown that\nφ\n(\nK\n)\n=\n(\n⩽\nK\n′\n𝒜\n,\n⩽\nK\n′\nℬ\n)\n\\varphi(K)=({\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K^{\\prime}}})\nand that\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nhas the chain property, and the proof is therefore complete.\n∎\nA.8.\nProof that the interleaving procedure eventually terminates\nProposition A.11.\nLet\n(\nf\n,\ng\n)\n(f,g)\nbe selection functions. Fix a tournament\nK\nK\nand let\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\nA_{i},B_{i}\n(\ni\n≥\n0\ni\\geq 0\n) be as in\nDefinition\n6.3\n. Then there are\nj\n,\nj\n′\n≥\n1\nj,j^{\\prime}\\geq 1\nsuch that\nA\nj\n=\n∅\nA_{j}=\\emptyset\nand\nB\nj\n′\n=\n∅\nB_{j^{\\prime}}=\\emptyset\n. Moreover,\nthere is\nt\n≥\n1\nt\\geq 1\nsuch that both\nA\nt\n=\nB\nt\n=\n∅\nA_{t}=B_{t}=\\emptyset\n.\nProof.\nSuppose\ni\n≥\n0\ni\\geq 0\nand\nA\ni\n≠\n∅\nA_{i}\\neq\\emptyset\n. Then properties\n(i)\nand\n(ii)\nfor\nf\nf\nin\nDefinition\n6.2\nimply that\n∅\n⊂\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n⊆\nA\ni\n\\emptyset\\subset f(K,A_{i},B_{i})\\subseteq A_{i}\n, and consequently\nA\ni\n+\n1\n=\nA\ni\n∖\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n⊂\nA\ni\nA_{i+1}=A_{i}\\setminus f(K,A_{i},B_{i})\\subset A_{i}\n.\nSupposing that\nA\nj\n≠\n∅\nA_{j}\\neq\\emptyset\nfor all\nj\n≥\n0\nj\\geq 0\n, we would have\nA\n0\n⊃\nA\n1\n⊃\nA\n2\n⊃\n⋯\nA_{0}\\supset A_{1}\\supset A_{2}\\supset\\cdots\nwhich clearly cannot be the case\nsince each\nA\nj\nA_{j}\nlies inside\nA\nA\nwhich is a finite set. Hence there is\nj\n≥\n1\nj\\geq 1\nsuch that\nA\nj\n=\n∅\nA_{j}=\\emptyset\n. Moreover, since\nA\nj\n⊇\nA\nj\n+\n1\n⊇\nA\nj\n+\n2\n⊇\n⋯\nA_{j}\\supseteq A_{j+1}\\supseteq A_{j+2}\\supseteq\\cdots\n, we have\nA\nk\n=\n∅\nA_{k}=\\emptyset\nfor all\nk\n≥\nj\nk\\geq j\n.\nAn identical argument with\ng\ng\nshows that there is\nj\n′\n≥\n1\nj^{\\prime}\\geq 1\nsuch that\nB\nj\n′\n=\n∅\nB_{j^{\\prime}}=\\emptyset\nand\nB\nk\n=\n∅\nB_{k}=\\emptyset\nfor all\nk\n≥\nj\n′\nk\\geq j^{\\prime}\n.\nTaking\nt\n=\nmax\n⁡\n{\nj\n,\nj\n′\n}\nt=\\max\\{j,j^{\\prime}\\}\n, we have\nA\nt\n=\nB\nt\n=\n∅\nA_{t}=B_{t}=\\emptyset\nas required.\n∎\nA.9.\nProof of\nTheorem\n6.4\nProof.\nThroughout the proof we will refer to a pair of total preorders\n(\n⪯\n,\n⊑\n)\n({\\preceq},{\\sqsubseteq})\nas ‘chain-definable’ if there is a chain tournament\nK\nK\nsuch that\n⪯\n=\n⩽\n𝒜\nK\n{\\preceq}={\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K}}\nand\n⊑\n=\n⩽\nℬ\nK\n{\\sqsubseteq}={\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K}}\n.\n(⇐) First we prove the ‘if’ direction. Let\nφ\n=\nφ\nf\n,\ng\n𝗂𝗇𝗍\n\\varphi={\\varphi_{f,g}^{\\mathsf{int}}}\nbe an\ninterleaving operator with selection functions\n(\nf\n,\ng\n)\n(f,g)\n, and fix a\ntournament\nK\nK\n. We will show that\nφ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\varphi(K)\nis chain-definable.\nAs per\nProposition\nA.11\n, let\nj\n,\nj\n′\n≥\n1\nj,j^{\\prime}\\geq 1\nbe the\nminimal integers such that\nA\nj\n=\n∅\nA_{j}=\\emptyset\nand\nB\nj\n′\n=\n∅\nB_{j^{\\prime}}=\\emptyset\n. Then\nwe have\nA\n0\n⊃\n⋯\n⊃\nA\nj\n−\n1\n⊃\nA\nj\n=\n∅\nA_{0}\\supset\\cdots\\supset A_{j-1}\\supset A_{j}=\\emptyset\nand\nB\n0\n⊃\n⋯\n⊃\nB\nj\n′\n−\n1\n⊃\nB\nj\n′\n=\n∅\nB_{0}\\supset\\cdots\\supset B_{j^{\\prime}-1}\\supset B_{j^{\\prime}}=\\emptyset\n.\nRecall that, for\na\n∈\nA\na\\in A\n, we have by definition\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n=\nmax\n⁡\n{\ni\n∣\na\n∈\nA\ni\n}\nr(a)=\\max\\{i\\mid a\\in A_{i}\\}\n, which is the unique integer such that\na\n∈\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∖\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n+\n1\na\\in A_{r(a)}\\setminus A_{{r(a)}+1}\n. Since\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\niff\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n≥\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\nr(a)\\geq r(a^{\\prime})\n, it follows\nthat the non-empty sets\nA\n0\n∖\nA\n1\n,\n…\n,\nA\nj\n−\n1\n∖\nA\nj\nA_{0}\\setminus A_{1},\\ldots,A_{j-1}\\setminus A_{j}\nform the ranks of the total preorder\n⪯\nK\nφ\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\n(that is, the\nequivalence classes of the symmetric closure\n≈\nK\nφ\n{\\approx_{K}^{\\varphi}}\n). Thus,\n⪯\nK\nφ\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\nhas\nj\nj\nranks. An identical argument shows that\n⊑\nK\nφ\n{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\nhas\nj\n′\nj^{\\prime}\nranks.\nIt follows from\nTheorem\n6.1\nthat\nφ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\varphi(K)\nis chain-definable if and only if\n|\nj\n−\nj\n′\n|\n≤\n1\n|j-j^{\\prime}|\\leq 1\n. If\nj\n=\nj\n′\nj=j^{\\prime}\nthis is\nclear. Suppose\nj\n<\nj\n′\nj<j^{\\prime}\n. Then\nA\nj\n=\n∅\nA_{j}=\\emptyset\nand\nB\nj\n≠\n∅\nB_{j}\\neq\\emptyset\n.\nBy property\n(iii)\nfor\ng\ng\nin\nDefinition\n6.2\n, we have\ng\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\nj\n,\nB\nj\n)\n=\ng\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\n∅\n,\nB\nj\n)\n=\nB\nj\ng(K,A_{j},B_{j})=g(K,\\emptyset,B_{j})=B_{j}\n. But this means\nB\nj\n+\n1\n=\nB\nj\n∖\ng\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\nj\n,\nB\nj\n)\n=\nB\nj\n∖\nB\nj\n=\n∅\nB_{j+1}=B_{j}\\setminus g(K,A_{j},B_{j})=B_{j}\\setminus B_{j}=\\emptyset\n. Consequently\nj\n′\n=\nj\n+\n1\nj^{\\prime}=j+1\n, and\n|\nj\n−\nj\n′\n|\n=\n|\n−\n1\n|\n=\n1\n|j-j^{\\prime}|=|-1|=1\nIf instead\nj\n>\nj\n′\nj>j^{\\prime}\n, then a similar argument using property\n(iii)\nfor\nf\nf\nin\nDefinition\n6.2\nshows\nthat\nj\n=\nj\n′\n+\n1\nj=j^{\\prime}+1\n, and we have\n|\nj\n−\nj\n′\n|\n=\n|\n1\n|\n=\n1\n|j-j^{\\prime}|=|1|=1\n.\nHence\n|\nj\n−\nj\n′\n|\n≤\n1\n|j-j^{\\prime}|\\leq 1\nin all cases, and\nφ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\varphi(K)\nis chain-definable as\nrequired.\n(⇒) Now for the ‘only if’ direction. Suppose\nφ\n\\varphi\nsatisfies\nchain-def\n. We will define\nf\n,\ng\nf,g\nsuch that\nφ\n=\nφ\nf\n,\ng\n𝗂𝗇𝗍\n\\varphi={\\varphi_{f,g}^{\\mathsf{int}}}\n.\nThe idea behind the construction is straightforward: since\nf\nf\nand\ng\ng\npick\noff the next-top-ranked\nA\nA\ns and\nB\nB\ns at each iteration, simply define\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\nf(K,A_{i},B_{i})\nas the maximal elements of\nA\ni\nA_{i}\nwith respect to the\nexisting ordering\n⪯\nK\nφ\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\n(\ng\ng\nwill be defined similarly). The\ninterleaving algorithm will then select the ranks of\n⪯\nK\nφ\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\nand\n⊑\nK\nφ\n{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\none-by-one; the fact that\nφ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n\\varphi(K)\nis chain-definable\nensures that we select\nall\nthe ranks before the iterative procedure\nends. The formal details follow.\nFix a tournament\nK\nK\n. By\nTheorem\n6.1\n,\n|\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n−\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n|\n≤\n1\n|\\mathsf{ranks}({{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}})-\\mathsf{ranks}({{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}})|\\leq 1\n. Taking\nt\n=\nmax\n{\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n,\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n}\nt=\\max\\{\\mathsf{ranks}({{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}}),\\mathsf{ranks}({{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}})\\}\n, we can write\nX\n1\n,\n…\n,\nX\nt\n⊆\nA\nX_{1},\\ldots,X_{t}\\subseteq A\nand\nY\n1\n,\n…\n,\nY\nt\n⊆\nB\nY_{1},\\ldots,Y_{t}\\subseteq B\nfor the ranks\nof\n⪯\nK\nφ\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\nand\n⊑\nK\nφ\n{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\nrespectively, possibly with\nX\n1\n=\n∅\nX_{1}=\\emptyset\nif\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n=\n1\n+\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n\\mathsf{ranks}({{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}})=1+\\mathsf{ranks}({{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}})\nor\nY\n1\n=\n∅\nY_{1}=\\emptyset\nif\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n=\n1\n+\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n\\mathsf{ranks}({{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}})=1+\\mathsf{ranks}({{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}})\n. Note\nthat\nX\ni\n,\nY\ni\n≠\n∅\nX_{i},Y_{i}\\neq\\emptyset\nfor\ni\n>\n1\ni>1\n. Assume these sets are ordered\nsuch that\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\niff\ni\n≤\nj\ni\\leq j\nwhenever\na\n∈\nX\ni\na\\in X_{i}\nand\na\n′\n∈\nX\nj\na^{\\prime}\\in X_{j}\n(and similar for the\nY\ni\nY_{i}\n). Also note that the\nX\ni\n∩\nX\nj\n=\n∅\nX_{i}\\cap X_{j}=\\emptyset\nfor\ni\n≠\nj\ni\\neq j\n(and similar for the\nY\ni\nY_{i}\n).\nNow set\n22\n22\n22\nHere\nmax\n(\nZ\n,\n⪯\n)\n=\n{\nz\n∈\nZ\n∣\n∄\nz\n′\n∈\nZ\n:\nz\n≺\nz\n′\n}\n\\max(Z,{\\preceq})=\\{z\\in Z\\mid\\not\\exists z^{\\prime}\\in Z:z\\prec z^{\\prime}\\}\n, for any set\nZ\nZ\nand a total preorder\n⪯\n{\\preceq}\non\nZ\nZ\n(with strict part\n≺\n{\\prec}\n).\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\n=\n{\nmax\n(\nA\n′\n,\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n,\nB\n′\n≠\n∅\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n=\n∅\nf(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})=\\begin{cases}\\max(A^{\\prime},{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}),&B^{\\prime}\\neq\\emptyset\\\\\nA^{\\prime},&B^{\\prime}=\\emptyset\\end{cases}\ng\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\n=\n{\nmax\n(\nB\n′\n,\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n,\nA\n′\n≠\n∅\nB\n′\n,\nA\n′\n=\n∅\ng(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})=\\begin{cases}\\max(B^{\\prime},{\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}}),&A^{\\prime}\\neq\\emptyset\\\\\nB^{\\prime},&A^{\\prime}=\\emptyset\\end{cases}\nIt is not difficult to see that\nf\nf\nand\ng\ng\nsatisfy the conditions of\nDefinition\n6.2\nfor selection functions. We claim that for\nwith\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\nA_{i},B_{i}\ndenoting the interleaving sets for\nK\nK\nand\n(\nf\n,\ng\n)\n(f,g)\n, for\nall\n0\n≤\ni\n≤\nt\n0\\leq i\\leq t\nwe have\n(9)\nA\ni\n=\n⋃\nj\n=\n1\nt\n−\ni\nX\nj\n,\nB\ni\n=\n⋃\nj\n=\n1\nt\n−\ni\nY\nj\nA_{i}=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{t-i}{X_{j}},\\quad B_{i}=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{t-i}{Y_{j}}\\quad\\quad\nFor\ni\n=\n0\ni=0\nthis is clear: since\nX\n1\n,\n…\n,\nX\nt\nX_{1},\\ldots,X_{t}\ncontains all ranks of\n⪯\nK\nφ\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\nwe have\n⋃\nj\n=\n1\nt\n−\n0\n=\nX\n1\n∪\n⋯\n∪\nX\nt\n=\nA\n=\nA\n0\n\\bigcup_{j=1}^{t-0}=X_{1}\\cup\\cdots\\cup X_{t}=A=A_{0}\n(and similar for\nB\nB\n).\nNow suppose\n9\nholds for some\n0\n≤\ni\n<\nt\n0\\leq i<t\n. We\nwill show that\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n=\nX\nt\n−\ni\nf(K,A_{i},B_{i})=X_{t-i}\nby considering three possible\ncases, at least one of which must hold.\nCase 1:\n(\nA\ni\n≠\n∅\nA_{i}\\neq\\emptyset\n,\nB\ni\n≠\n∅\nB_{i}\\neq\\emptyset\n). Here we have\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n\\displaystyle f(K,A_{i},B_{i})\n=\nmax\n(\nA\ni\n,\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\max(A_{i},{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}})\n=\nmax\n(\nX\n1\n∪\n⋯\n∪\nX\nt\n−\ni\n,\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\max(X_{1}\\cup\\cdots\\cup X_{t-i},{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}})\n=\nX\nt\n−\ni\n\\displaystyle=X_{t-i}\nsince the\nX\nj\nX_{j}\nform (disjoint) ranks of\n⪯\nK\nφ\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\nwith\nX\nj\n≺\nX\nk\nX_{j}\\prec X_{k}\nfor\nj\n<\nk\nj<k\n.\nCase 2:\n(\nB\ni\n=\n∅\nB_{i}=\\emptyset\n). Here we have\n⋃\nj\n=\n1\nt\n−\ni\nY\nj\n=\n∅\n\\bigcup_{j=1}^{t-i}{Y_{j}}=\\emptyset\n. Since\nt\n−\ni\n≥\n1\nt-i\\geq 1\nand\nY\nj\n≠\n∅\nY_{j}\\neq\\emptyset\nfor\nj\n>\n1\nj>1\n, it must be the case that\nt\n−\ni\n=\n1\nt-i=1\nand\nB\ni\n=\nY\n1\n=\n∅\nB_{i}=Y_{1}=\\emptyset\n. Consequently by the induction hypothesis we have\nA\ni\n=\n⋃\nj\n=\n1\n1\nX\nj\n=\nX\n1\nA_{i}=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{1}{X_{j}}=X_{1}\n, and thus\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n\\displaystyle f(K,A_{i},B_{i})\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\n∅\n)\n\\displaystyle=f(K,A_{i},\\emptyset)\n=\nA\ni\n\\displaystyle=A_{i}\n=\nX\n1\n\\displaystyle=X_{1}\n=\nX\nt\n−\ni\n\\displaystyle=X_{t-i}\nCase 3:\n(\nA\ni\n=\n∅\nA_{i}=\\emptyset\n). By a similar argument as in case 2,\nwe must have\nt\n−\ni\n=\n1\nt-i=1\nand\nA\ni\n=\nX\n1\n=\n∅\nA_{i}=X_{1}=\\emptyset\n. Using the fact that\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n⊆\nA\ni\nf(K,A_{i},B_{i})\\subseteq A_{i}\nwe get\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n\\displaystyle f(K,A_{i},B_{i})\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\n∅\n,\nB\ni\n)\n⏟\n⊆\n∅\n\\displaystyle=\\underbrace{f(K,\\emptyset,B_{i})}_{\\subseteq\\emptyset}\n=\n∅\n\\displaystyle=\\emptyset\n=\nX\n1\n\\displaystyle=X_{1}\n=\nX\nt\n−\ni\n\\displaystyle=X_{t-i}\nWe have now covered all cases, and have shown that\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n=\nX\nt\n−\ni\nf(K,A_{i},B_{i})=X_{t-i}\nmust hold. Consequently, using again the fact that the\nX\nj\nX_{j}\nare\ndisjoint,\nA\ni\n+\n1\n\\displaystyle A_{i+1}\n=\nA\ni\n∖\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n\\displaystyle=A_{i}\\setminus f(K,A_{i},B_{i})\n=\n(\n⋃\nj\n=\n1\nt\n−\ni\nX\nj\n)\n∖\nX\nt\n−\ni\n\\displaystyle=\\left(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{t-i}{X_{j}}\\right)\\setminus X_{t-i}\n=\n⋃\nj\n=\n1\nt\n−\n(\ni\n+\n1\n)\nX\nj\n\\displaystyle=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{t-(i+1)}{X_{j}}\nas required. By almost identical arguments we can show that\ng\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n=\nY\nt\n−\ni\ng(K,A_{i},B_{i})=Y_{t-i}\n, and thus\nB\ni\n+\n1\n=\n⋃\nj\n=\n1\nt\n−\n(\ni\n+\n1\n)\nY\nj\nB_{i+1}=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{t-(i+1)}{Y_{j}}\nalso. By\ninduction,\n9\nholds for all\n0\n≤\ni\n≤\nt\n0\\leq i\\leq t\n.\nIt remains to show that\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\niff\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\nf\n,\ng\n𝗂𝗇𝗍\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{{\\varphi_{f,g}^{\\mathsf{int}}}}a^{\\prime}\nand that\nb\n⊑\nK\nφ\nb\n′\nb\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}b^{\\prime}\niff\nb\n⊑\nK\nφ\nf\n,\ng\n𝗂𝗇𝗍\nb\n′\nb\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{{\\varphi_{f,g}^{\\mathsf{int}}}}b^{\\prime}\n.\nFor\na\n∈\nA\na\\in A\n, let\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\np(a)\nbe the unique integer such that\na\n∈\nX\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\na\\in X_{p(a)}\n,\ni.e.\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\np(a)\nis the index of the rank of\na\na\nin the ordering\n⪯\nK\nφ\n{\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}}\n. Note that we have\na\n∈\nA\ni\n=\nX\n1\n∪\n⋯\n∪\nX\nt\n−\ni\n⇔\nt\n−\ni\n≥\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\na\\in A_{i}=X_{1}\\cup\\cdots\\cup X_{t-i}\\iff t-i\\geq p(a)\nand therefore\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n=\nmax\n⁡\n{\ni\n∣\na\n∈\nA\ni\n}\n=\nmax\n⁡\n{\ni\n∣\nt\n−\ni\n≥\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n}\n=\nt\n−\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\nr(a)=\\max\\{i\\mid a\\in A_{i}\\}=\\max\\{i\\mid t-i\\geq p(a)\\}=t-p(a)\nUsing the fact that\nX\ni\n≺\nX\nj\nX_{i}\\prec X_{j}\nfor\ni\n<\nj\ni<j\n, we get\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\nf\n,\ng\n𝗂𝗇𝗍\na\n′\n\\displaystyle a\\preceq_{K}^{{\\varphi_{f,g}^{\\mathsf{int}}}}a^{\\prime}\n⇔\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n≥\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle\\iff r(a)\\geq r(a^{\\prime})\n⇔\nt\n−\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n≥\nt\n−\np\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle\\iff t-p(a)\\geq t-p(a^{\\prime})\n⇔\np\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n≤\np\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle\\iff p(a)\\leq p(a^{\\prime})\n⇔\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\n\\displaystyle\\iff a\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\nA similar argument shows that\nb\n⊑\nK\nφ\nb\n′\nb\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}b^{\\prime}\niff\nb\n⊑\nK\nφ\nf\n,\ng\n𝗂𝗇𝗍\nb\n′\nb\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{{\\varphi_{f,g}^{\\mathsf{int}}}}b^{\\prime}\nfor any\nb\n,\nb\n′\n∈\nB\nb,b^{\\prime}\\in B\n. Since\nK\nK\nwas arbitrary, we\nhave shown that\nφ\n=\nφ\nf\n,\ng\n𝗂𝗇𝗍\n\\varphi={\\varphi_{f,g}^{\\mathsf{int}}}\nas required.\n∎\nA.10.\nProof of\nTheorem\n6.6\nProof.\nSince\nchain-min\nimplies\nchain-def\n,\nTheorem\n4.3\nimplies the existence of an\noperator with\nchain-def\nand\ndual\n, and an operator\nwith\nchain-def\nand\nmon\n. Moreover, the trivial\noperator which ranks all\nA\nA\ns and\nB\nB\ns equally satisfies\nanon\nand\nIIM\n. It only remains to show that there is an operator\nsatisfying both\nchain-def\nand\npos-resp\n.\nTo that end, for any tournament\nK\nK\n, define\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nby\nK\na\n​\nb\n′\n=\n{\n1\n,\nb\n≤\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n|\n0\n,\nb\n>\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n|\nK^{\\prime}_{ab}=\\begin{cases}1,&b\\leq|K(a)|\\\\\n0,&b>|K(a)|\\end{cases}\nNote that\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n=\n{\n1\n,\n…\n,\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n|\n}\nK^{\\prime}(a)=\\{1,\\ldots,|K(a)|\\}\nfor\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n|\n>\n0\n|K(a)|>0\n. Consequently\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\nK^{\\prime}(a)\\subseteq K^{\\prime}(a^{\\prime})\niff\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n|\n≤\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n|\n|K(a)|\\leq|K(a)|\n. We see that\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nhas the\nchain property, and the operator\nφ\n\\varphi\ndefined by\nφ\n(\nK\n)\n=\n(\n⩽\nK\n′\n𝒜\n,\n⩽\nK\n′\nℬ\n)\n\\varphi(K)=({\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{A}}_{K^{\\prime}}},{\\leqslant^{\\mathcal{B}}_{K^{\\prime}}})\nsatisfies\nchain-def\n. In\nparticular,\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\niff\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n|\n≤\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n|\n|K(a)|\\leq|K(a^{\\prime})|\n.\nTo show\npos-resp\n, suppose\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\nand\nK\na\n′\n,\nb\n=\n0\nK_{a^{\\prime},b}=0\nfor some\na\n,\na\n′\n∈\nA\na,a^{\\prime}\\in A\nand\nb\n∈\nB\nb\\in B\n. Write\nK\n^\n=\nK\n+\n𝟏\na\n′\n,\nb\n\\hat{K}=K+\\bm{1}_{a^{\\prime},b}\n.\nSince\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\nimplies\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n|\n≤\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n|\n|K(a)|\\leq|K(a^{\\prime})|\n, we have\n|\nK\n^\n​\n(\na\n′\n)\n|\n=\n1\n+\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n|\n>\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n|\n=\n|\nK\n^\n​\n(\na\n)\n|\n|\\hat{K}(a^{\\prime})|=1+|K(a^{\\prime})|>|K(a)|=|\\hat{K}(a)|\n, and therefore\na\n≺\nK\n^\nφ\na\n′\na\\prec_{\\hat{K}}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\nas required for\npos-resp\n.\n∎\nA.11.\nProof of\nTheorem\n6.7\nProof.\nFor contradiction, suppose there is an operator\nφ\n\\varphi\nsatisfying the\nstated axioms. Consider\nK\n=\n[\n0\n0\n0\n1\n1\n0\n1\n1\n]\nK=\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}0&0\\\\\n0&1\\\\\n1&0\\\\\n1&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]\nand two tournaments obtained by removing a single 1 entry:\nK\n1\n=\n[\n0\n0\n0\n𝟎\n1\n0\n1\n1\n]\n,\nK\n2\n=\n[\n0\n0\n0\n1\n1\n0\n1\n𝟎\n]\nK_{1}=\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}0&0\\\\\n0&\\bm{{\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}0}}\\\\\n1&0\\\\\n1&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right],\\quad K_{2}=\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}0&0\\\\\n0&1\\\\\n1&0\\\\\n1&\\bm{{\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}0}}\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]\nNow,\nanon\nin\nK\n1\nK_{1}\ngives\n1\n≈\nK\n1\nφ\n2\n1\\approx_{K_{1}}^{\\varphi}2\n(e.g. take\nσ\n=\n(\n1 2\n)\n\\sigma=(1\\ 2)\n,\nπ\n=\nid\nB\n\\pi=\\text{id}_{B}\n). In particular,\n1\n⪯\nK\n1\nφ\n2\n1\\preceq_{K_{1}}^{\\varphi}2\n, so\npos-resp\nimplies\n1\n≺\nK\nφ\n2\n1\\prec_{K}^{\\varphi}2\n. A similar argument\nwith\nK\n2\nK_{2}\nshows that\n3\n≈\nK\n2\nφ\n4\n3\\approx_{K_{2}}^{\\varphi}4\nand\n3\n≺\nK\nφ\n4\n3\\prec_{K}^{\\varphi}4\n.\nOn the other hand, applying\nanon\nto\nK\nK\ndirectly with\nσ\n=\n(\n2 3\n)\n\\sigma=(2\\ 3)\nand\nπ\n=\n(\n1 2\n)\n\\pi=(1\\ 2)\n, we see that\n2\n≈\nK\nφ\n3\n2\\approx_{K}^{\\varphi}3\n. The ranking of\nA\nA\nis thus fully determined as\n1\n≺\n2\n≈\n3\n≺\n4\n1\\prec 2\\approx 3\\prec 4\n. In particular,\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n=\n3\n\\mathsf{ranks}({\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}})=3\n.\nBut now considering the dual tournament\nK\n¯\n=\n[\n1\n1\n0\n0\n1\n0\n1\n0\n]\n{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}}=\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}1&1&0&0\\\\\n1&0&1&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]\nand\napplying permutations\nσ\n=\n(\n1 2\n)\n\\sigma=(1\\ 2)\nand\nπ\n=\n(\n2 3\n)\n\\pi=(2\\ 3)\n, we obtain\n1\n≈\nK\n¯\nφ\n2\n1\\approx_{{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}}}^{\\varphi}2\nby\nanon\n, i.e. the\nA\nA\nranking in\nK\n¯\n\\textstyle\\overline{K}\nis flat. By\ndual\nthis implies the\nB\nB\nranking in\nK\nK\nis flat, i.e.\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n=\n1\n\\mathsf{ranks}({\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}})=1\n. We see that\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⪯\nK\nφ\n)\n\\mathsf{ranks}({\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}})\nand\n𝗋𝖺𝗇𝗄𝗌\n(\n⊑\nK\nφ\n)\n\\mathsf{ranks}({\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}})\ndiffer by 2, contradicting\nchain-def\naccording to\nTheorem\n6.1\n.\n∎\nA.12.\nProof of\nTheorem\n6.8\nWe require a preliminary result providing sufficient conditions for an\ninterleaving operator\nφ\nf\n,\ng\n𝗂𝗇𝗍\n{\\varphi_{f,g}^{\\mathsf{int}}}\nto satisfy various axioms.\nLemma A.12.\nLet\nφ\n=\nφ\nf\n,\ng\nint\n\\varphi=\\varphi_{f,g}^{\\text{int}}\nbe an interleaving operator.\n(1)\nIf for any tournament\nK\nK\n,\nA\n′\n⊆\nA\nA^{\\prime}\\subseteq A\n,\nB\n′\n⊆\nB\nB^{\\prime}\\subseteq B\nand for\nany pair of permutations\nσ\n:\nA\n→\nA\n\\sigma:A\\to A\nand\nπ\n:\nB\n→\nB\n\\pi:B\\to B\nwe have\nf\n⁡\n(\nπ\n⁡\n(\nσ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n)\n,\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\n′\n)\n,\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\n′\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle f(\\pi(\\sigma(K)),\\sigma(A^{\\prime}),\\pi(B^{\\prime}))\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(f(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime}))\ng\n⁡\n(\nπ\n⁡\n(\nσ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n)\n,\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\n′\n)\n,\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\n′\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle g(\\pi(\\sigma(K)),\\sigma(A^{\\prime}),\\pi(B^{\\prime}))\n=\nπ\n⁡\n(\ng\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\pi(g(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime}))\nthen\nφ\n\\varphi\nsatisfies\nanon\n.\n(2)\nIf for any tournament\nK\nK\nand\nA\n′\n⊆\nA\nA^{\\prime}\\subseteq A\n,\nB\n⊆\nB\nB\\subseteq B\nwe have\ng\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n¯\n,\nB\n′\n,\nA\n′\n)\ng(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})=f({{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}},B^{\\prime},A^{\\prime})\nthen\nφ\n\\varphi\nsatisfies\ndual\n.\n(3)\nIf for any tournament\nK\nK\n,\nA\n′\n⊆\nA\nA^{\\prime}\\subseteq A\n,\nB\n′\n⊆\nB\nB^{\\prime}\\subseteq B\nand\na\n,\na\n′\n∈\nA\n′\na,a^{\\prime}\\in A^{\\prime}\nwe have\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n⟹\na\n∉\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\n​\nor\n​\na\n′\n∈\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\nK(a)\\subseteq K(a^{\\prime})\\implies a\\not\\in f(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})\\text{ or }a^{\\prime}\\in f(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})\nthen\nφ\n\\varphi\nsatisfies\nmon\n.\nProof.\nWe take each statement in turn.\n(1)\nLet\nK\nK\nbe a tournament. For brevity, write\nK\n′\n=\nπ\n⁡\n(\nσ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n)\nK^{\\prime}=\\pi(\\sigma(K))\n. Let us\nwrite\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\nA_{i},B_{i}\nand\nA\ni\n′\n,\nB\ni\n′\nA_{i}^{\\prime},B_{i}^{\\prime}\n(\ni\n≥\n0\n)\n(i\\geq 0)\nfor the sets defined in\nDefinition\n6.3\nfor\nK\nK\nand\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nrespectively. We claim that for\nall\ni\n≥\n0\ni\\geq 0\n:\n(10)\nA\ni\n′\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n)\n,\nB\ni\n′\n=\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\ni\n)\nA_{i}^{\\prime}=\\sigma(A_{i}),\\quad B_{i}^{\\prime}=\\pi(B_{i})\nFor\ni\n=\n0\ni=0\nthis is trivial since\nA\n0\n′\n=\nA\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\n)\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\n0\n)\nA_{0}^{\\prime}=A=\\sigma(A)=\\sigma(A_{0})\nsince\nσ\n\\sigma\nis a bijection. The fact that\nB\n0\n′\n=\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\n0\n)\nB_{0}^{\\prime}=\\pi(B_{0})\nis shown\nsimilarly.\nSuppose that\n10\nholds for some\ni\n≥\n0\ni\\geq 0\n. Then applying our assumption on\nf\nf\n:\nA\ni\n+\n1\n′\n\\displaystyle A_{i+1}^{\\prime}\n=\nA\ni\n′\n∖\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n′\n,\nA\ni\n′\n,\nB\ni\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=A_{i}^{\\prime}\\setminus f(K^{\\prime},A_{i}^{\\prime},B_{i}^{\\prime})\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n)\n∖\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n′\n,\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n)\n,\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\ni\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(A_{i})\\setminus f(K^{\\prime},\\sigma(A_{i}),\\pi(B_{i}))\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n)\n∖\nσ\n⁡\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(A_{i})\\setminus\\sigma(f(K,A_{i},B_{i}))\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n∖\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(A_{i}\\setminus f(K,A_{i},B_{i}))\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n+\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(A_{i+1})\n(note that\nσ\n⁡\n(\nX\n)\n∖\nσ\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nX\n∖\nY\n)\n\\sigma(X)\\setminus\\sigma(Y)=\\sigma(X\\setminus Y)\nholds\nfor any sets\nX\n,\nY\nX,Y\ndue to injectivity of\nσ\n\\sigma\n). Using the assumption\non\ng\ng\nwe can show that\nB\ni\n+\n1\n′\n=\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\ni\n+\n1\n)\nB_{i+1}^{\\prime}=\\pi(B_{i+1})\nin a similar manner.\nTherefore, by induction,\n10\nholds for\nall\ni\n≥\n0\ni\\geq 0\n. This means that for any\na\n∈\nA\na\\in A\nwe have\nσ\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∈\nA\ni\n′\n⇔\nσ\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∈\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\ni\n)\n⇔\na\n∈\nA\ni\n\\sigma(a)\\in A_{i}^{\\prime}\\iff\\sigma(a)\\in\\sigma(A_{i})\\iff a\\in A_{i}\nand therefore, with\nr\nK\nr_{K}\nand\nr\nK\n′\nr_{K^{\\prime}}\ndenoting the functions\nA\n→\nℕ\n0\nA\\to\\mathbb{N}_{0}\ndefined in\nDefinition\n6.3\nfor\nK\nK\nand\nK\n′\nK^{\\prime}\nrespectively,\nr\nK\n′\n​\n(\nσ\n​\n(\na\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle r_{K^{\\prime}}(\\sigma(a))\n=\nmax\n⁡\n{\ni\n∣\nσ\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∈\nA\ni\n′\n}\n\\displaystyle=\\max\\{i\\mid\\sigma(a)\\in A_{i}^{\\prime}\\}\n=\nmax\n⁡\n{\ni\n∣\na\n∈\nA\ni\n}\n\\displaystyle=\\max\\{i\\mid a\\in A_{i}\\}\n=\nr\nK\n​\n(\na\n)\n\\displaystyle=r_{K}(a)\nFrom this it easily follows that\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\niff\nσ\n(\na\n)\n⪯\nK\n′\nφ\nσ\n(\na\n′\n)\n\\sigma(a)\\preceq_{K^{\\prime}}^{\\varphi}\\sigma(a^{\\prime})\n, i.e.\nφ\n\\varphi\nsatisfies\nanon\n.\n(2)\nOnce again, fix a tournament\nK\nK\nand let\nA\ni\n,\nB\ni\nA_{i},B_{i}\nand\nA\ni\n′\n,\nB\ni\n′\nA_{i}^{\\prime},B_{i}^{\\prime}\ndenote\nthe sets from\nDefinition\n6.3\nfor\nK\nK\nand\nK\n¯\n\\textstyle\\overline{K}\nrespectively.\nIt is easy to show by induction that the assumption on\nf\nf\nand\ng\ng\nimplies\nA\ni\n′\n=\nB\ni\nA^{\\prime}_{i}=B_{i}\nand\nB\ni\n′\n=\nA\ni\nB_{i}^{\\prime}=A_{i}\nfor all\ni\n≥\n0\ni\\geq 0\n. This means that for any\nb\n∈\nB\nK\nb\\in B_{K}\n:\ns\nK\n​\n(\nb\n)\n\\displaystyle s_{K}(b)\n=\nmax\n⁡\n{\ni\n∣\nb\n∈\nB\ni\n}\n\\displaystyle=\\max\\{i\\mid b\\in B_{i}\\}\n=\nmax\n⁡\n{\ni\n∣\nb\n∈\nA\ni\n′\n}\n\\displaystyle=\\max\\{i\\mid b\\in A_{i}^{\\prime}\\}\n=\nr\nK\n¯\n​\n(\nb\n)\n\\displaystyle=r_{{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}}}(b)\nwhich implies\nb\n⊑\nK\nφ\nb\n′\nb\\sqsubseteq_{K}^{\\varphi}b^{\\prime}\niff\nb\n⪯\nK\n¯\nφ\nb\n′\nb\\preceq_{{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}}}^{\\varphi}b^{\\prime}\n, as\nrequired for\ndual\n.\n(3)\nLet\nK\nK\nbe a tournament and\na\n,\na\n′\n∈\nA\na,a^{\\prime}\\in A\nsuch that\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\nK(a)\\subseteq K(a^{\\prime})\n.\nWe must show that\na\n⪯\nK\nφ\na\n′\na\\preceq_{K}^{\\varphi}a^{\\prime}\n.\nSuppose otherwise, i.e.\na\n′\n≺\nK\nφ\na\na^{\\prime}\\prec_{K}^{\\varphi}a\n. Then\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n>\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\nr(a^{\\prime})>r(a)\n. Note that\nby definition of\nr\nr\n, we have\na\n∈\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∖\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n+\n1\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n,\nB\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n)\na\\in A_{r(a)}\\setminus A_{r(a)+1}=f(K,A_{r(a)},B_{r(a)})\n. Since\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n≥\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n+\n1\nr(a^{\\prime})\\geq r(a)+1\nand\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊇\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n+\n1\n⊇\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n+\n2\n⊇\n⋯\nA_{r(a)}\\supseteq A_{r(a)+1}\\supseteq A_{r(a)+2}\\supseteq\\cdots\n, we get\na\n′\n∈\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n+\n1\n⊆\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\na^{\\prime}\\in A_{r(a)+1}\\subseteq A_{r(a)}\n. In particular,\na\n′\n∉\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n,\nB\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n)\na^{\\prime}\\notin f(K,A_{r(a)},B_{r(a)})\n.\nPiecing this all together, we have\na\n,\na\n′\n∈\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\na,a^{\\prime}\\in A_{r(a)}\n,\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\nK(a)\\subseteq K(a^{\\prime})\n,\na\n∈\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n,\nB\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n)\na\\in f(K,A_{r(a)},B_{r(a)})\nand\na\n′\n∉\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n,\nB\nr\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n)\na^{\\prime}\\not\\in f(K,A_{r(a)},B_{r(a)})\n. But this directly contradicts our assumption on\nf\nf\n, so we are\ndone.\n∎\nProof of\nTheorem\n6.8\n.\nWe take each axiom in turn. Let\nf\nf\nand\ng\ng\nbe the selection functions\ncorresponding to\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}\nfrom\nExample\n6.5\n.\nchain-def.\nSince\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}\nis an interleaving operator,\nchain-def\nfollows from\nTheorem\n6.4\n.\nanon.\nLet\nK\nK\nbe a tournament and let\nσ\n:\nA\n→\nA\n\\sigma:A\\to A\nand\nπ\n:\nB\n→\nB\n\\pi:B\\to B\nbe bijective mappings. Write\nK\n′\n=\nπ\n⁡\n(\nσ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n)\nK^{\\prime}=\\pi(\\sigma(K))\n. We will show\nthat the conditions on\nf\nf\nand\ng\ng\nin\nLemma\nA.12\npart\n(\n1\n) are satisfied.\nLet\nA\n′\n⊆\nA\nA^{\\prime}\\subseteq A\nand\nB\n′\n⊆\nB\nB^{\\prime}\\subseteq B\n. We have\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n′\n,\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\n′\n)\n,\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\n′\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle f(K^{\\prime},\\sigma(A^{\\prime}),\\pi(B^{\\prime}))\n=\narg\n​\nmax\na\n^\n∈\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\n′\n)\n⁡\n|\nK\n′\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\n∩\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\n′\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle=\\operatorname*{arg\\ max}_{\\hat{a}\\in\\sigma(A^{\\prime})}{|K^{\\prime}(\\hat{a})\\cap\\pi(B^{\\prime})|}\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\narg\n​\nmax\na\n∈\nA\n′\n⁡\n|\nK\n′\n​\n(\nσ\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n)\n∩\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\n′\n)\n|\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(\\operatorname*{arg\\ max}_{a\\in A^{\\prime}}{|K^{\\prime}(\\sigma(a))\\cap\\pi(B^{\\prime})|})\nwhere we make the ‘substitution’\na\n=\nσ\n−\n1\n​\n(\na\n^\n)\na=\\sigma^{-1}(\\hat{a})\n. Using the\ndefintion of\nK\n′\n=\nπ\n⁡\n(\nσ\n⁡\n(\nK\n)\n)\nK^{\\prime}=\\pi(\\sigma(K))\nit is easily seen that\nK\n′\n​\n(\nσ\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n)\n=\nπ\n⁡\n(\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n)\nK^{\\prime}(\\sigma(a))=\\pi(K(a))\n. Also, since\nπ\n\\pi\nis a bijection we have\nπ\n⁡\n(\nX\n)\n∩\nπ\n⁡\n(\nY\n)\n=\nπ\n⁡\n(\nX\n∩\nY\n)\n\\pi(X)\\cap\\pi(Y)=\\pi(X\\cap Y)\nfor any sets\nX\nX\nand\nY\nY\n, and\n|\nπ\n⁡\n(\nX\n)\n|\n=\n|\nX\n|\n|\\pi(X)|=|X|\n. Thus\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n′\n,\nσ\n⁡\n(\nA\n′\n)\n,\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\n′\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle f(K^{\\prime},\\sigma(A^{\\prime}),\\pi(B^{\\prime}))\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\narg\n​\nmax\na\n∈\nA\n′\n⁡\n|\nK\n′\n​\n(\nσ\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n)\n∩\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\n′\n)\n|\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(\\operatorname*{arg\\ max}_{a\\in A^{\\prime}}{|K^{\\prime}(\\sigma(a))\\cap\\pi(B^{\\prime})|})\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\narg\n​\nmax\na\n∈\nA\n′\n⁡\n|\nπ\n⁡\n(\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n)\n∩\nπ\n⁡\n(\nB\n′\n)\n|\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(\\operatorname*{arg\\ max}_{a\\in A^{\\prime}}{|\\pi(K(a))\\cap\\pi(B^{\\prime})|})\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\narg\n​\nmax\na\n∈\nA\n′\n⁡\n|\nπ\n⁡\n(\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n)\n|\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(\\operatorname*{arg\\ max}_{a\\in A^{\\prime}}{|\\pi(K(a)\\cap B^{\\prime})|})\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\narg\n​\nmax\na\n∈\nA\n′\n⁡\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n|\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(\\operatorname*{arg\\ max}_{a\\in A^{\\prime}}{|K(a)\\cap B^{\\prime}|})\n=\nσ\n⁡\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma(f(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime}))\nas required. The result for\ng\ng\nfollows by a near-identical argument. Thus\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}\nsatisfies\nanon\nby\nLemma\nA.12\npart\n(\n1\n).\ndual.\nFix a tournament\nK\nK\nand let\nA\n′\n⊆\nA\nA^{\\prime}\\subseteq A\n,\nB\n′\n⊆\nB\nB^{\\prime}\\subseteq B\n. Note that for\nb\n∈\nB\n′\nb\\in B^{\\prime}\nwe have\n|\nK\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\n∩\nA\n′\n|\n\\displaystyle|K^{-1}(b)\\cap A^{\\prime}|\n=\n|\n(\nA\n∖\nK\n¯\n​\n(\nb\n)\n)\n∩\nA\n′\n|\n\\displaystyle=|(A\\setminus{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}}(b))\\cap A^{\\prime}|\n=\n|\nA\n′\n∖\nK\n¯\n​\n(\nb\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle=|A^{\\prime}\\setminus{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}}(b)|\n=\n|\nA\n′\n|\n−\n|\nK\n¯\n​\n(\nb\n)\n∩\nA\n′\n|\n\\displaystyle=|A^{\\prime}|-|{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}}(b)\\cap A^{\\prime}|\nConsequently\ng\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle g(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})\n=\narg\n​\nmin\nb\n∈\nB\n′\n⁡\n|\nK\n−\n1\n​\n(\nb\n)\n∩\nA\n′\n|\n\\displaystyle=\\operatorname*{arg\\ min}_{b\\in B^{\\prime}}{|K^{-1}(b)\\cap A^{\\prime}|}\n=\narg\n​\nmin\nb\n∈\nB\n′\n⁡\n(\n|\nA\n′\n|\n−\n|\nK\n¯\n​\n(\nb\n)\n∩\nA\n′\n|\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\operatorname*{arg\\ min}_{b\\in B^{\\prime}}{\\left(|A^{\\prime}|-|{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}}(b)\\cap A^{\\prime}|\\right)}\n=\narg\n​\nmax\nb\n∈\nB\n′\n⁡\n|\nK\n¯\n​\n(\nb\n)\n∩\nA\n′\n|\n\\displaystyle=\\operatorname*{arg\\ max}_{b\\in B^{\\prime}}{|{{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}}(b)\\cap A^{\\prime}|}\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n¯\n,\nB\n′\n,\nA\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle=f({{\\mathchoice{\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}{\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\overline{K}$}}}},B^{\\prime},A^{\\prime})\nand, by\nLemma\nA.12\npart\n(\n2\n),\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}\nsatisfies\ndual\n.\nmon.\nOnce again, we use\nLemma\nA.12\n. Let\nK\nK\nbe a tournament and\nA\n′\n⊆\nA\nA^{\\prime}\\subseteq A\n,\nB\n′\n⊆\nB\nB^{\\prime}\\subseteq B\n. Suppose\na\n,\na\n′\n∈\nA\n′\na,a^{\\prime}\\in A^{\\prime}\nwith\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n⊆\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\nK(a)\\subseteq K(a^{\\prime})\n. We need to show that either\na\n∉\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\na\\not\\in f(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})\nor\na\n′\n∈\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\na^{\\prime}\\in f(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})\nSuppose\na\n∈\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\na\\in f(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})\n. Then\na\n∈\narg\n​\nmax\na\n^\n∈\nA\n′\n⁡\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n^\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n|\na\\in\\operatorname*{arg\\ max}_{\\hat{a}\\in A^{\\prime}}{|K(\\hat{a})\\cap B^{\\prime}|}\n, so\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n|\n≥\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n|\n|K(a)\\cap B^{\\prime}|\\geq|K(a^{\\prime})\\cap B^{\\prime}|\n. On the\nother hand\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n⊆\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n∩\nB\n′\nK(a)\\cap B^{\\prime}\\subseteq K(a^{\\prime})\\cap B^{\\prime}\n, so\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n|\n≤\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n|\n|K(a)\\cap B^{\\prime}|\\leq|K(a^{\\prime})\\cap B^{\\prime}|\n. Consequently\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n|\n=\n|\nK\n⁡\n(\na\n′\n)\n∩\nB\n′\n|\n|K(a)\\cap B^{\\prime}|=|K(a^{\\prime})\\cap B^{\\prime}|\n, and so\na\n′\n∈\nf\n⁡\n(\nK\n,\nA\n′\n,\nB\n′\n)\na^{\\prime}\\in f(K,A^{\\prime},B^{\\prime})\n. This shows the property required by\nLemma\nA.12\npart\n(\n3\n) is satisfied, and thus\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}\nsatisfies\nmon\n.\npos-resp.\nWe have show that\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}\nsatisfies\nchain-def\n,\nanon\nand\ndual\n; due to\nimpossibility result of\nTheorem\n6.7\n,\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}\ncannot satisfy\npos-resp\n.\nIIM.\nWrite\nK\n1\n=\n[\n1\n0\n0\n0\n1\n0\n0\n1\n1\n]\n,\nK\n2\n=\n[\n1\n0\n0\n0\n1\n0\n1\n0\n1\n]\nK_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\\n0&1&0\\\\\n0&1&1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad K_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\\n0&1&0\\\\\n1&0&1\\end{bmatrix}\nNote that the first and second rows of each tournament are identical, so\nIIM\nwould imply\n1\n⪯\nK\n1\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n2\n1\\preceq_{K_{1}}^{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}2\niff\n1\n⪯\nK\n2\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n2\n1\\preceq_{K_{2}}^{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}2\n. However, it is easily verified that\n1\n≺\nK\n1\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n2\n1\\prec_{K_{1}}^{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}2\nwhereas\n2\n≺\nK\n2\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n1\n2\\prec_{K_{2}}^{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}1\n. Therefore\nφ\n𝖢𝖨\n{\\varphi_{\\mathsf{CI}}}\ndoes not satisfy\nIIM\n.\n∎\n◄\nFeeling\nlucky?\nConversion\nreport\nReport\nan issue\nView original\non arXiv\n►"}
{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2101.08175v1.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 65318, "equations": [], "text": "Bayesian GARCH Modeling of Functional Sports Data\nPatric Dolmeta\nAffiliation:\nDepartment of Decision Sciences, Università Bocconi, Milano, Italy\nRaffaele Argiento\nAffiliation:\nDepartment of Statistical Sciences, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Milano, Italy & Collegio Carlo Alberto, Torino, Italy\nSilvia Montagna\nThanks:\nsilvia.montagna@unito.it\nAffiliation:\nESOMAS Department, University di Torino, Torino, Italy & Collegio Carlo Alberto, Torino, Italy\nAbstract\nThe use of statistical methods in sport analytics has gained a rapidly growing interest over the last decade, and nowadays is common practice. In particular, the interest in understanding and predicting an athlete’s performance throughout his/her career is motivated by the need to evaluate the efficacy of training programs, anticipate fatigue to prevent injuries and detect unexpected of disproportionate increases in performance that might be indicative of doping. Moreover, fast evolving data gathering technologies require up to date modelling techniques that adapt to the distinctive features of sports data. In this work, we propose a hierarchical Bayesian model for describing and predicting the evolution of performance over time for shot put athletes. To account for seasonality and heterogeneity in recorded results, we rely both on a smooth functional contribution and on a linear mixed effect model with heteroskedastic errors to represent the athlete-specific trajectories. The resulting model provides an accurate description of the performance trajectories and helps specifying both the intra- and inter-seasonal variability of measurements. Further, the model allows for the prediction of athletes’ performance in future seasons. We apply our model to an extensive real world data set on performance data of professional shot put athletes recorded at elite competitions.\nKeywords:\nPerformance analysis Bayesian functional data analysis GARCH models Sport analytics Latent factor modelling\n1\nIntroduction\nShot put is a track and field event involving throwing (“putting”) the shot, a metal ball (7.26kg/16lb for men, 4kg/8.8lb for women), with one hand as far as possible from a seven-foot diameter (2.135m) circle. In order for each put to be considered valid, the shot must not drop below the line of the athlete’s shoulders and must land inside a designated 35-degree sector. Athletes commonly put four to six times per competition, and their best performance is recorded. Figure\n1\ndisplays the results of elite shot put competitions for four athletes with careers of different lengths.\nFigure 1:\nEach panel displays the performance results (points) of a professional shot put athlete throughout his/her career at elite competitions. Performance is measured in meters (length of the throw) and plotted against the days elapsed since January 1st of each athlete’s career starting year. The dark vertical lines represent seasons changing points, namely new year days.\nFurthermore, several athlete-specific variables (e.g., age, gender, doping history, nationality, etc.) that might be of interest in a sport analytics perspective are naturally gathered during elite competitions or can be easily retrieved retrospectively. Shot put competitions have been part of the modern Olympics since their revival, hence there exist complete and long-established data sets for shot put performances that can be exploited for performance analysis.\nSportive competitions might display some sort of seasonality in the results. We underline that here the term “seasonality” is not used to indicate a cyclical behaviour of the results over time as in the literature of time series but, rather, a time dependent gathering of observations. On one hand competitions are traditionally concentrated in some months of the year, and on the other hand weather and environmental conditions may affect the performances or even the practicability of the sport itself. Straightforward examples are sports leagues that run on a different schedule and host their season openers, playoffs and championships at different times of the year or, even more dramatically, winter sports as opposed to outdoor water sports. Shot put events range over the whole year, with indoor competitions held during Winter months and major tournaments, like the Olympics, Diamond League and World Championship organised during Summer. Therefore, it is reasonable to say that seasons coincides with calendar years. In Figure\n1\n, vertical lines represent new years’ days: the time point at which seasons change. To provide an accurate representation of the data, seasonality effects need to be taken into account. Note that, from a modelling perspective, the amplitude of seasons is arbitrary, and ideally any model should be easily adapted to any sport with any type of seasonality.\nThere exist a well established literature of both frequentist and Bayesian contributions to performance data analysis in various sports. We mention, among others,\nMalcata et al. 2014\nand\nMengersen et al. 2016\nin triathlon,\nCosta et al. 2010\nand\nCosta et al. 2013\nin swimming, and\nKoulis et al. 2014\nin cricket.\nAlbert 2016\ndescribe batting performance in baseball,\nWimmer et al. 2011\nperformance evolution in decathlon,\nYousefi and Swartz 2013\nin golf, and\nMontagna et al. 2020\nin tennis. Further,\nVaci et al. 2019\nrely on a Bayesian latent variable model to investigate age-related changes across the complete lifespan of basketball athletes on the basis of exponential functions.\nIn this work, we are interested in describing the evolution of performances of professional shot put athletes throughout their careers. To this end, we propose a Bayesian hierarchical model where results of each athlete are represented as error prone measurements of some underlying unknown function. The distinctive contribution of our approach is the form of such function, which has an additive structure with three components. First, we consider a smooth functional contribution for capturing the overall variability in athletes’ performances. For this component, we follow the approach in\nMontagna et al. 2012\n, who propose a Bayesian latent factor regression model for detecting the doping status of athletes given their shot put performance results and other covariates. However, the authors limit the analysis to data collected from 2012, whereas we aim at describing the trajectories in performance over the whole time span available for our data (1996 to 2016). For this time span, a global smoothness assumption for the trajectories could be too restrictive. Indeed, data may exhibit jumps localised at fixed and shared time points among all athletes. See, for example, athlete 303 in Figure\n1\n, whose results show a jump between the second and fourth season (calendar year) of his/her career. The presence of jumps between seasons is even more striking when yearly average performances are considered. We highlight this behaviour by showing yearly averages via thick horizontal lines in Figure\n1\n. Accordingly, the description of this yearly dependent contribution is delegated to a mixed effect model, that quantifies the seasonal mean for each athlete as a deviation from a grand mean. This component captures the inter-seasonal variability of the data set, whereas the smooth functional component describes the intra-seasonal evolution of performances. Finally, we complete our model specification accounting for the effect of a selection of time dependent covariates through a regressive component. We embed our model in a Bayesian framework by proposing suitable prior distributions for all parameters of interest. We believe the presented model represents a flexible tool to analyse evolution of performances in measurable sports, namely, all those disciplines for which results can be summarised by a unique measure (e.g., distance, time or weight).\nThe rest of the paper is organised as follows. In Section 2, we describe the motivating case study. In Section 3, we present the proposed model and briefly discuss possible alternative settings. Moreover, we elicit priors for our Bayesian approach. In Section 4, we outline the algorithm for posterior computation. In Section 5, we discuss posterior estimates, argue on the performance of the model and interpret the model’s parameters form a sports analytic perspective. Conclusions are presented in Section 6. Finally, the Appendix includes the complete description of the MCMC algorithm.\n2\nThe World Athletics shot put data set\nWorld Athletics (WA) is the world governing body for track and field athletic sports. It provides standardized rules, competition programs, regulated technical equipment, a list of official world records and verified measurements. The data at our disposal was obtained with permission from an open results database (\nwww.tilastopaja.eu\n) following institutional ethical approval (Prop_72_2017_18). The data set comprises 56,000 measurements of WA recognized elite shot put competitions for 1,115 athletes from 1976 to 2016. For each athlete, the data set reports the date of the event, the best result in meters, the finishing position, an indication of any doping violation during the athlete’s career as well as demographic information (athlete’s name, WA ID number, date of birth, sex and country of birth).\nIn this work, we restrict our analysis to results for athletes performing after 1996. Indeed, we pursue consistency of measurement accuracy, and 1996 represents a turning point in anti-doping regulation and fraud detection procedures. The resulting data set is still sufficiently broad for our purposes. It contains 41,033 observations for 653 athletes (309 males and 344 females). The outcome of interest is the shot distance, which ranges from a minimum if\n10.6\n10.6\nup to a maximum if\n22.56\n22.56\nmeters, with a mean of\n17.30\n17.30\nmeters.\nAs shown in Figure\n1\nfor a selection of athletes, data are collected over time. Hereafter we will denote as\nt\ni\n​\nj\nt_{ij}\nthe time at which the\nj\nj\n-th observation for athlete\ni\ni\nis recorded.\nt\ni\n​\nj\nt_{ij}\ncorresponds to the time elapsed from January 1st of each athlete’s career starting year to the date of the competition. Accordingly, equal time values for different athletes are likely to specify distinct years, but the same moment in those athletes’ careers. Moreover, different athletes will have observations ranging over a large time span, according to the length of their careers. Having described seasons as calendar years, athletes will also compete in a different number of seasons. Figure\n2\nshows the number of athletes per season as well as boxplots of the distribution of their mean performances across the various seasons. Athletes with the longest careers have been playing for 19 years. A general increasing trend in performance can be observed as a function of career length (right panel in Figure\n2\n) or, equivalently, the age of the athlete. In the following, we will discuss two different modelling choices for age, respectively, accounting for its time dependence and considering age as a fixed quantity, namely the age of the athlete at the beginning of his/her career.\nTable 1 reports descriptive statistics suggesting how sex, environment and doping have an effect on the average value of the result. We point out that in our dataset we only have 18 athletes who tested positive for doping at some point in their career. Information on the date the test was taken (or if multiple tests were taken) is not available in the data. As expected, performances for men are, on average, higher than for women. Similar effects, despite less evident in magnitude, also hold true for the variable environment, which takes values indoor and outdoor. Regarding environment, a further remark is in order. We already pointed out that major WA events take place outdoor (27,800 observations) during Summer months, whereas less competitive events are held inside between November and March (13,200 observations). Figure\n3\ndisplays results in gray when recorded outdoor, and in black otherwise. We can clearly see that field events gather in Summer months, whereas indoor events take place during Winter (solid lines).\nFigure 2:\nLeft: total number of athletes per season. Right: each boxplot shows the distribution of the athletes’ mean performances within each season.\nTable 1:\nPerformance results conditioned on covariates.\nMean\nSd\nMax\nMin\nTotal\n17.30\n1.78\n22.56\n10.6\nWomen\n16.09\n1.35\n21.70\n10.6\nMen\n18.55\n1.21\n22.56\n12.93\nNot Doped\n17.30\n1.79\n22.56\n10.6\nDoped\n17.77\n1.35\n20.88\n13.55\nIndoor\n17.17\n1.70\n22.23\n12.15\nOutdoor\n17.38\n1.81\n22.56\n10.6\nFigure 3:\nPerformance results displayed according to the variable environment, which takes values indoor (black) and outdoor (gray). Vertical lines corresponding to the label ticks represent season changes, whereas the two enclosing it indicate the boundaries of winter months.\n3\nThe model\nLet\nn\nn\ndenote the total number of athletes in the study. We assume that shot put performances for athlete\ni\ni\nare given by noisy measurements of an underlying function\ng\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\ng_{i}(t_{ij})\n:\ny\ni\n​\nj\n=\ng\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n+\nϵ\ni\n​\nj\ny_{ij}=g_{i}(t_{ij})+\\epsilon_{ij}\n(1)\nwith\nϵ\ni\n​\nj\n∼\ni\n​\ni\n​\nd\nN\n⁡\n(\n0\n,\nψ\n2\n)\n\\epsilon_{ij}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle iid}}{{\\sim}}N(0,\\psi^{2})\nindependent errors. Recall\nt\ni\n​\nj\nt_{ij}\nis the time at which the\nj\nj\n-th observation for athlete\ni\ni\nis collected, for\nj\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nn\ni\nj=1,\\ldots,n_{i}\n, where\nn\ni\nn_{i}\nis the total number of measurements available on athlete\ni\ni\n.\nWe further suggest an explicit functional form for\ng\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\ng_{i}(t_{ij})\n:\ng\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n=\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n+\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n+\n𝒙\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n​\n𝜷\ng_{i}(t_{ij})=f_{i}(t_{ij})+\\mu_{is}+\\boldsymbol{x}_{i}(t_{ij})\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\n(2)\nwhere\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\nf_{i}(t)\nis a smooth functional component for intra-seasonal variability,\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n\\mu_{is}\na season-specific intercept, and\n𝒙\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n​\n𝜷\n\\boldsymbol{x}_{i}(t)\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\nis an additional multiple regression component. Here\ns\n∈\n{\n1\n,\n2\n,\n…\n,\nS\ni\n}\ns\\in\\{1,2,\\ldots,S_{i}\\}\nindicates the season in which the shot was recorded. Specifically,\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n≡\nμ\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n=\n∑\ns\n=\n1\nS\ni\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n​\n𝕀\n(\nt\ni\ns\n,\nt\ni\ns\n+\n1\n)\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n\\mu_{is}\\equiv\\mu_{i}(t_{ij})=\\sum_{s=1}^{S_{i}}\\mu_{is}\\ \\mathbb{I}_{(t_{i}^{s},t_{i}^{s+1})}(t_{ij})\nis an athlete-specific step function taking value\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n\\mu_{is}\nfor all time points in season\ns\ns\n, delimited by\nt\ni\ns\nt_{i}^{s}\nand\nt\ni\ns\n+\n1\nt_{i}^{s+1}\n. For a complete treatment of the notation used insofar, please refer to Table\n2\n.\nWe will now discuss each of the three terms in Eq. (\n2\n) in more detail.\nTable 2:\nMathematical notation\nSymbol\nMeaning\ni\nIndex identifying the athlete\nj\nIndex identifying a specific observation\nn\nTotal number of athletes\nN\nTotal number of observations\nt\ni\n​\nj\nt_{ij}\nTime point at which the\nj\n’th observation of athlete\ni\nis recorded\nS\ni\nS_{i}\nTotal number of seasons for athlete\ni\ni\ns\nThe currently considered season\ng\ni\n​\ns\ng_{is}\nNumber of observations in season\ns\ns\nfor athlete\ni\ni\nr\nThe number of covariates to be considered\ny\ni\n​\nj\ny_{ij}\nResponse variable at time\nj\nj\nfor athlete\ni\ni\n3.1\nThe functional component\nThe functional component\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\nf_{i}(t)\nis meant to capture the subject-specific global evolution of the response variable. It explains the global dependence of the data from time. We require that these functions display a smooth behaviour: the latter is assured by assuming\n{\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n}\ni\n=\n1\nn\n\\{f_{i}(t)\\}_{i=1}^{n}\nare linear combinations of smooth basis functions,\n{\nb\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n}\nm\n=\n1\np\n\\{b_{m}(t)\\}_{m=1}^{p}\n. Note that, both the nature and the number\np\np\nof these bases are to be determined according to some properties we wish them to satisfy. In particular, we assume:\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n∑\nm\n=\n1\np\nθ\ni\n​\nm\n​\nb\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\nf_{i}(t)=\\sum_{{m}=1}^{p}\\theta_{im}b_{m}(t)\n(3)\nwhere\n{\nb\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n}\nm\n=\n1\np\n\\{b_{m}(t)\\}_{m=1}^{p}\nrepresent the B-spline basis\n(\nde Boor 1978\n)\nand\n{\nθ\ni\n​\nm\n}\nm\n=\n1\np\n\\{\\theta_{im}\\}_{m=1}^{p}\nare subject-specific coefficients.\nWe briefly recall that the B-spline basis of degree\nk\nk\non\n[\nL\n,\nU\n]\n[L,U]\nis a collection of\np\np\npolynomials defined recursively on a sequence of points, known as knots, and indicated with\nL\n≡\nt\n1\n≤\n…\n≤\nt\np\n+\nk\n+\n1\n≡\nU\nL\\equiv t_{1}\\leq\\ldots\\leq t_{p+k+1}\\equiv U\n.\nWe follow the common approach of choosing\nk\n=\n3\nk=3\n, leading to cubic splines\n(\nMarsden 1974\n, see, for instance,)\n. Moreover, we assume the knot sequence to be equispaced and\n(\nk\n+\n1\n)\n(k+1)\n-open. That is, the first and last\nk\n+\n1\nk+1\nknots are identified with the extremes of the definition interval, whereas the remaining\np\n−\nk\n−\n1\np-k-1\nknots divide said interval into sets of the same length. Under these assumptions, each basis function\nb\nj\n​\n(\nt\n)\nb_{j}(t)\nhas compact support over\nk\n+\n1\nk+1\nknots, precisely\n[\nt\nj\n,\nt\nj\n+\nk\n+\n1\n]\n[t_{j},t_{j+k+1}]\n. Moreover, together they span the space of piecewise polynomial functions of degree\nk\nk\non\n[\nL\n,\nU\n]\n[L,U]\nwith breakpoints\n{\nt\nn\n}\nn\n=\n1\np\n+\nK\n+\n1\n\\{t_{n}\\}_{n=1}^{p+K+1}\n. Finally, such functions are twice continuously differentiable at the breakpoints, de facto eliminating any visible type of discontinuity and providing a smooth result.\nThe number of basis functions is chosen to be large enough for sufficiently many basis functions to have a completely enclosed support in a given season. In particular, rescaling time to\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n[0,1]\n(for computational purposes and easier prior definition) seasons have amplitude\n0.054\n0.054\nand, if we want at least a local basis to be completely supported in one of them, we need\n4\n4\nknots to fall in it. Accordingly, we require about 75 internal knots and 80 degrees of freedom.\nWith the sake of tractability, a low dimensional representation of the individual curves is of interest. Following the approach by\nMontagna et al. 2012\n, we exploit a sparse latent factor model on the basis coefficients:\nθ\ni\n​\nm\n=\n∑\nl\n=\n1\nk\nλ\nm\n​\nl\n​\nη\ni\n​\nl\n+\nξ\ni\n​\nm\n\\theta_{im}=\\sum_{l=1}^{k}\\lambda_{ml}\\eta_{il}+\\xi_{im}\n(4)\nwhere\nλ\nm\n​\nl\n\\lambda_{ml}\nare the entries of a\n(\np\n×\nk\n)\n(p\\times k)\nfactor loading matrix\n𝚲\n\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}\n, and\n𝜼\n𝒊\n\\boldsymbol{\\eta_{i}}\nis a vector of\nk\nk\nlatent factors for subject\ni\ni\n. Finally,\n𝝃\n𝒊\n=\n(\nξ\ni\n​\n1\n,\n…\n,\nξ\ni\n​\np\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\xi_{i}}=(\\xi_{i1},\\ldots,\\xi_{ip})\nis a residual vector, independent of all other variables in the model. We assume:\n𝜼\n𝒊\n∼\ni\n​\ni\n​\nd\nN\nk\n​\n(\n𝟎\n,\n𝑰\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\eta_{i}}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle iid}}{{\\sim}}N_{k}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I})\n(5)\nand the error terms\n𝝃\n𝒊\n\\boldsymbol{\\xi_{i}}\nare assumed to have normal distribution with diagonal covariance matrix,\n𝝃\n𝒊\n∼\ni\n​\ni\n​\nd\nN\np\n​\n(\n𝟎\n,\nd\n​\ni\n​\na\n​\ng\n​\n(\nσ\n1\n−\n2\n,\n…\n,\nσ\np\n−\n2\n)\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\xi_{i}}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle iid}}{{\\sim}}N_{p}(\\boldsymbol{0},diag(\\sigma_{1}^{-2},\\ldots,\\sigma_{p}^{-2}))\n, with\nσ\nj\n−\n2\n∼\ni\n​\ni\n​\nd\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\na\nσ\n,\nb\nσ\n)\n\\sigma_{j}^{-2}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle iid}}{{\\sim}}Ga(a_{\\sigma},b_{\\sigma})\n.\nFor the modelling of the factor loading matrix\n𝚲\n\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}\n, we follow the approach in\nBhattacharya and Dunson 2011\n. Specifically, we adopt a multiplicative gamma process shrinkage prior which favours an unknown but small number of factors\nk\nk\n. The prior is specified as follows:\nλ\nm\n​\nl\n|\nϕ\nm\n​\nl\n−\n1\n,\nτ\nl\n−\n1\n∼\ni\n​\ni\n​\nd\nN\n⁡\n(\n0\n,\nϕ\nm\n​\nl\n−\n1\n​\nτ\nl\n−\n1\n)\nwith\n\\displaystyle\\lambda_{ml}|\\phi_{ml}^{-1},\\tau_{l}^{-1}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle iid}}{{\\sim}}N\\left(0,\\phi_{ml}^{-1}\\tau_{l}^{-1}\\right)\\qquad\\text{with}\n(6)\nϕ\nm\n​\nl\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\nν\nϕ\n2\n,\nν\nϕ\n2\n)\nτ\nl\n=\n∏\nv\n=\n1\nh\nϖ\nv\n\\displaystyle\\phi_{ml}\\sim Ga\\biggl(\\frac{\\nu_{\\phi}}{2},\\frac{\\nu_{\\phi}}{2}\\biggr)\\qquad\\tau_{l}=\\prod_{v=1}^{h}\\varpi_{v}\nϖ\n1\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\na\n1\n,\n1\n)\nϖ\nv\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\na\nv\n,\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\varpi_{1}\\sim Ga(a_{1},1)\\qquad\\varpi_{v}\\sim Ga(a_{v},1)\nHere\nτ\nl\n\\tau_{l}\nis a global stochastically increasing shrinkage parameter for the\nl\nl\n-th column which favors more shrinkage as the column index increases. Similarly,\nϕ\nm\n​\nl\n\\phi_{ml}\nare local shrinkage parameters for the elements in the\nl\nl\n-th column.\nBhattacharya and Dunson 2011\ndescribe a procedure to select the number of factors\nk\n≪\np\nk\\ll p\nadaptively. We follow their lead, thus\nk\nk\nis not set a priori but automatically tuned as the Gibbs sampler progresses. Refer to\nBhattacharya and Dunson 2011\nfor details. Note that, by combining Eq. (\n3\n) and Eq. (\n4\n) one gets:\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n∑\nl\n=\n1\nk\nη\ni\n​\nl\n​\nΦ\nl\n​\n(\nt\n)\n+\nr\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\nf_{i}(t)=\\sum_{l=1}^{k}\\eta_{il}\\Phi_{l}(t)+r_{i}(t)\nwhere\nΦ\nl\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n∑\nm\n=\n1\np\nλ\nm\n​\nl\n​\nb\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\Phi_{l}(t)=\\sum_{m=1}^{p}\\lambda_{ml}b_{m}(t)\nis a new, unknown non-local basis function learnt from the data and\nr\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\nr_{i}(t)\na functional error term. Although the number of pre-specified basis function\np\np\nis potentially large, the number of “operative” bases\nk\nk\nis\nk\n≪\np\nk\\ll p\nand learnt from the data.\n3.2\nThe seasonal component\nEarly graphical displays and straightforward exploratory analysis suggest a significant variability of the average response across seasons as displayed in Figure\n1\n. Namely, performances prove to be gathered over predetermined time intervals, the seasons (calendar years). However, it is reasonable to expect some degree of dependence for the average performance across seasons. To model such dependence, an autoregressive model for seasonal intercepts can be proposed. The idea behind this choice is to allow for borrowing of information across seasons, in the sense that the seasonal intercept\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n\\mu_{is}\nat season\ns\ns\nis influenced by the intercept at season\ns\n−\n1\ns-1\nthrough the autoregressive coefficient\nρ\ni\n\\rho_{i}\n. Namely,\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n|\nρ\ni\n,\nσ\nμ\n2\n∼\ni\n​\ni\n​\nd\nN\n⁡\n(\nρ\ni\n​\nμ\ni\n⁡\n(\ns\n−\n1\n)\n,\nσ\nμ\n2\n)\n\\mu_{is}\\ |\\ \\rho_{i},\\sigma_{\\mu}^{2}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle iid}}{{\\sim}}N\\left(\\rho_{i}\\mu_{i(s-1)},\\sigma_{\\mu}^{2}\\right)\n(7)\nHowever, when we first implemented this model, we noted how residuals presented a pattern which we would like to intercept with a finer model. Therefore, we consider a random intercept model with Normal Generalized Autoregressive Conditional Heteroskedastic (GARCH) errors\n(\nBollerslev 1986\n)\n. Specifically,\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n|\nm\n,\nh\ni\n​\ns\n=\nm\n+\nζ\ni\n​\ns\n∼\ni\n​\ni\n​\nd\nN\n⁡\n(\nm\n,\nh\ni\n​\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mu_{is}\\ |\\ m,h_{is}=m+\\zeta_{is}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle iid}}{{\\sim}}N(m,h_{is})\n(8)\nh\ni\n​\ns\n=\nα\n0\n+\nα\n1\n​\nζ\ni\n​\ns\n−\n1\n2\n+\nϖ\n​\nh\ni\n​\ns\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle h_{is}=\\alpha_{0}+\\alpha_{1}\\zeta_{is-1}^{2}+\\varpi h_{is-1}\n(9)\nwhere\nα\n0\n>\n0\n,\nα\n1\n≥\n0\n\\alpha_{0}>0,\\alpha_{1}\\geq 0\nand\nϖ\n≥\n0\n\\varpi\\geq 0\nto ensure a positive conditional variance and\nζ\ni\n​\ns\n=\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n−\nm\n\\zeta_{is}=\\mu_{is}-m\nwith\nh\ni\n​\n0\n=\nζ\ni\n​\n0\n:=\n0\nh_{i0}=\\zeta_{i0}:=0\nfor convenience. The additional assumption of wide-sense stationarity with\n𝔼\n⁡\n(\nζ\nt\n)\n=\n0\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}(\\zeta_{t})=0\n𝕍\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\n(\nζ\nt\n)\n=\nα\n0\n​\n(\n1\n−\nα\n1\n−\nϖ\n)\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{V}ar(\\zeta_{t})=\\alpha_{0}(1-\\alpha_{1}-\\varpi)^{-1}\nℂ\n​\no\n​\nv\n​\n(\nζ\nt\n,\nζ\ns\n)\n=\n0\n​\nfor\n​\nt\n≠\ns\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{C}ov(\\zeta_{t},\\zeta_{s})=0\\text{ for }t\\neq s\nis guaranteed by requiring\nα\n1\n+\nϖ\n<\n1\n\\alpha_{1}+\\varpi<1\n, as proven by\nBollerslev 1986\n.\nThree parameters of the seasonal component require prior specification: the overall mean\nm\nm\nand the conditional variance parameters,\nϖ\n\\varpi\nand\n𝜶\n=\n(\nα\n0\n,\nα\n1\n)\n⊤\n\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}=(\\alpha_{0},\\alpha_{1})^{\\top}\n. For the autoregressive and heteroskedastic parameters of the GARCH model, we propose non-informative priors satisfying the positivity constraint. For the overall mean parameter, we rely on a more informative Normal prior centered around the mean suggested by posterior analysis of preliminary versions of the model. In particular:\nm\n∼\nN\n⁡\n(\nμ\nm\n0\n,\nΣ\nm\n0\n)\n\\displaystyle m\\sim N(\\mu_{m_{0}},\\Sigma_{m_{0}})\n𝜶\n∼\nN\n2\n(\nμ\nα\n,\nΣ\nα\n)\n𝕀\n{\n𝜶\n>\n0\n}\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\\sim N_{2}(\\mu_{\\alpha},\\Sigma_{\\alpha})\\ \\mathbb{I}\\{\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}>0\\}\n(10)\nϖ\n∼\nN\n(\nμ\nϖ\n,\nΣ\nϖ\n)\n𝕀\n{\nϖ\n≥\n0\n}\n\\displaystyle\\varpi\\sim N(\\mu_{\\varpi},\\Sigma_{\\varpi})\\ \\mathbb{I}\\{\\varpi\\geq 0\\}\nwhere\n𝜶\n=\n(\nα\n0\n,\nα\n1\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}=(\\alpha_{0},\\alpha_{1})\nis a bidimensional vector. We complete the model specification assuming that the parameters are statistically independent and noticing that the hypothesis needed for wide-sense stationarity do not translate into actual prior conditions on the parameters. Hence, one of the objects of our analysis becomes to test whether the constraint\nα\n1\n+\nϖ\n<\n1\n\\alpha_{1}+\\varpi<1\nholds true.\n3.3\nCovariates\nWe consider the effect of three covariates, gender, age and environment, and assume conjugate prior choices for the covariates coefficients:\n𝜷\n∼\ni\n​\ni\n​\nd\nN\n⁡\n(\n𝜷\n𝟎\n,\nσ\nβ\n2\n​\n𝕀\n)\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle iid}}{{\\sim}}N(\\boldsymbol{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{0}},\\sigma_{\\beta}^{2}\\boldsymbol{\\mathbb{I}})\nσ\nβ\n−\n2\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\nν\nβ\n2\n,\nν\nβ\n​\nσ\nβ\n2\n2\n)\n\\displaystyle\\sigma_{\\beta}^{-2}\\sim Ga\\biggl(\\frac{\\nu_{\\beta}}{2},\\frac{\\nu_{\\beta}\\sigma_{\\beta}^{2}}{2}\\biggr)\n(11)\n4\nThe Bayesian update\nBecause of the additive nature of the overall sampling model (\n1\n) - (\n2\n), we are able to exploit a blocked Gibbs sampler grouping together the parameters of the three modelling components described in Section\n3\n.\nNote first that, because of the high dimensionality of the problem, it is computationally convenient to choose conditionally conjugate prior distributions for the parameters. Indeed, conjugacy guarantees analytical tractability of posterior distributions. In some cases, specifically for the conditional variances of GARCH errors, no conjugate model exists and updates rely on an adaptive version of the Metropolis Hastings algorithm for posterior sampling.\nAlgorithm\n1\noutlines our sampling scheme, while details are presented in Appendix\nA\n. As far as the parameters of the functional component\n𝜽\ni\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}\nare concerned, we follow\nMontagna et al. 2012\nby choosing conditionally conjugate prior distributions so that the update proceeds via simple Gibbs sampling steps. Analogously, the update of the regression coefficients\n𝜷\n\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\nand the error term\nψ\n\\psi\nproceeds straightforwardly by sampling from their full conditional posterior distributions. Conjugate priors for the GARCH parameters\nm\n,\nϖ\nm,\\varpi\nand\n𝜶\n\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\nare not available, therefore we resort to adaptive Metropolis schemes to draw values from their full conditionals. Specifically, we build an adaptive scale Metropolis such that the covariance matrix of the proposal density adapts at each iteration to achieve an\noptimal\nacceptance rate\n(\nHaario et al. 2001\n, see)\n.\nFurther details about the algorithm can be found in the Appendix\nA\n, whereas code is available at\nhttps://github.com/PatricDolmeta/Bayesian-GARCH-Modeling-of-Functional-Sports-Data.\nData:\ny\ni\n​\nj\n=\n(\ny\n11\n,\n…\n,\ny\nn\n​\nn\nn\n)\ny_{ij}=(y_{11},\\ldots,y_{nn_{n}})\nSet the required MCMC sample size\nG\nG\n, the burn-in period\ng\n0\ng_{0}\nand the thinning parameter\ng\ns\ng_{s}\n.\nInitialise\n𝜽\ni\n(\n0\n)\n,\n𝝁\ni\n(\n0\n)\n,\nm\n(\n0\n)\n,\nϖ\n(\n0\n)\n,\n𝜶\n(\n0\n)\n,\n𝜷\ni\n(\n0\n)\n,\nψ\ni\n(\n0\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}^{(0)},\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{i}^{(0)},m^{(0)},\\varpi^{(0)},\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}^{(0)},\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{i}^{(0)},\\psi_{i}^{(0)}\nFor\ng\n=\n0\n,\n…\n,\nG\ng=0,\\ldots,G\nUpdate functional component\nSet partial residuals\ny\ni\n​\nj\n(\n1\n)\n​\n(\ng\n)\n=\ny\ni\n​\nj\n−\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n(\ng\n)\n−\n𝒙\n𝒊\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n​\n𝜷\n(\ng\n)\ny_{ij}^{(1)(g)}=y_{ij}-\\mu_{i,s}^{(g)}-\\boldsymbol{x_{i}}(t_{ij})\\boldsymbol{\\beta}^{(g)}\nUpdate\n𝜽\ni\n(\ng\n+\n1\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}^{(g+1)}\non the base of Appendix A.1\nUpdate seasonal component\nSet partial residuals\ny\ni\n​\nj\n(\n2\n)\n​\n(\ng\n)\n=\ny\ni\n​\nj\n−\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n(\ng\n)\n−\n𝒙\n𝒊\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n​\n𝜷\n(\ng\n)\ny_{ij}^{(2)(g)}=y_{ij}-f_{i}(t_{ij})^{(g)}-\\boldsymbol{x_{i}}(t_{ij})\\boldsymbol{\\beta}^{(g)}\nUpdate\n𝝁\ni\n(\ng\n+\n1\n)\n,\nm\n(\ng\n+\n1\n)\n,\nϖ\n(\ng\n+\n1\n)\n,\n𝜶\n(\ng\n+\n1\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{i}^{(g+1)},m^{(g+1)},\\varpi^{(g+1)},\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}^{(g+1)}\non the base of Appendix A.2\nUpdate regressive component\nSet partial residuals\ny\ni\n​\nj\n(\n3\n)\n​\n(\ng\n)\n=\ny\ni\n​\nj\n−\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n(\ng\n)\n−\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n(\ng\n)\ny_{ij}^{(3)(g)}=y_{ij}-f_{i}(t_{ij})^{(g)}-\\mu_{i,s}^{(g)}\nUpdate\n𝜷\ni\n(\ng\n+\n1\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{i}^{(g+1)}\non the base of Appendix A.3\nUpdate error term\nSet partial residuals\nϵ\ni\n​\nj\n(\ng\n)\n=\ny\ni\n​\nj\n−\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n(\ng\n)\n−\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n(\ng\n)\n−\n𝒙\n𝒊\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n​\n𝜷\n(\ng\n)\n\\epsilon_{ij}^{(g)}=y_{ij}-f_{i}(t_{ij})^{(g)}-\\mu_{i,s}^{(g)}-\\boldsymbol{x_{i}}(t_{ij})\\boldsymbol{\\beta}^{(g)}\nUpdate\nψ\n(\ng\n+\n1\n)\n\\psi^{(g+1)}\non the base of Appendix A.4\nReturn\n𝜽\ni\n(\ng\n)\n,\n𝝁\ni\n(\ng\n)\n,\nm\n(\ng\n)\n,\nϖ\n(\ng\n)\n,\n𝜶\n(\ng\n)\n,\n𝜷\ni\n(\ng\n)\n,\nψ\ni\n(\ng\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}^{(g)},\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{i}^{(g)},m^{(g)},\\varpi^{(g)},\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}^{(g)},\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{i}^{(g)},\\psi_{i}^{(g)}\nfor\ng\n=\ng\n0\n,\ng\n0\n+\ng\ns\n,\ng\n0\n+\n2\n​\ng\ns\n,\n…\n,\nG\ng=g_{0},g_{0}+g_{s},g_{0}+2g_{s},\\ldots,G\nAlgorithm 1\nGibbs Sampler\n5\nPosterior analysis\nThe idea of estimating trajectories for athletes’ performances is a natural pursuit for the model specification we adopted. Indeed, describing observations as error prone measurements of an unknown underlying function suggests evaluating such function, once retrieved, on any number of points of interest. In practice, we will generate a fine grid of\nT\nT\nequispaced time points:\n{\nt\nk\n}\nk\n=\n1\nT\n\\{t_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{T}\nbetween\n0\n≡\nt\n1\n0\\equiv t_{1}\nand\n1\n≡\nt\nT\n1\\equiv t_{T}\nand evaluate the function on this grid.\nIn particular, we start by evaluating the athlete-specific functional component by exploiting the basis function representation. Being:\n𝚯\ni\n=\n[\nθ\ni\n​\n1\n(\n1\n)\nθ\ni\n​\n2\n(\n1\n)\n…\nθ\ni\n​\np\n(\n1\n)\nθ\ni\n​\n1\n(\n2\n)\nθ\ni\n​\n2\n(\n2\n)\n…\nθ\ni\n​\np\n(\n2\n)\n⋱\nθ\ni\n​\n1\n(\nG\n)\nθ\ni\n​\n2\n(\nG\n)\n…\nθ\ni\n​\np\n(\nG\n)\n]\n\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}_{i}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\theta_{i1}^{(1)}&\\theta_{i2}^{(1)}&\\ldots&\\theta_{ip}^{(1)}\\\\\n\\theta_{i1}^{(2)}&\\theta_{i2}^{(2)}&\\ldots&\\theta_{ip}^{(2)}\\\\\n\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\\n\\theta_{i1}^{(G)}&\\theta_{i2}^{(G)}&\\ldots&\\theta_{ip}^{(G)}\\end{bmatrix}\nthe matrix of individual-specific spline basis coefficients for all iterations\ng\n=\n1\n,\n…\n​\nG\ng=1,\\ldots G\nand\n𝒃\n⊤\n=\n[\nb\n1\n​\n(\nt\n1\n)\nb\n1\n​\n(\nt\n2\n)\n…\nb\n1\n​\n(\nt\nk\n)\n…\nb\n1\n​\n(\nt\nT\n)\nb\n2\n​\n(\nt\n1\n)\nb\n2\n​\n(\nt\n2\n)\n…\nb\n2\n​\n(\nt\nk\n)\n…\nb\n2\n​\n(\nt\nT\n)\n⋱\n⋱\nb\np\n​\n(\nt\n1\n)\nb\np\n​\n(\nt\n2\n)\n…\nb\np\n​\n(\nt\nk\n)\n…\nb\np\n​\n(\nt\nT\n)\n]\n\\boldsymbol{b}^{\\top}=\\begin{bmatrix}b_{1}(t_{1})&b_{1}(t_{2})&\\ldots&b_{1}(t_{k})&\\ldots&b_{1}(t_{T})\\\\\nb_{2}(t_{1})&b_{2}(t_{2})&\\ldots&b_{2}(t_{k})&\\ldots&b_{2}(t_{T})\\\\\n\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\\nb_{p}(t_{1})&b_{p}(t_{2})&\\ldots&b_{p}(t_{k})&\\ldots&b_{p}(t_{T})\\end{bmatrix}\nall values of a\np\np\n-dimensional, degree-\n3\n3\n, spline basis on a set of\nT\n+\n1\nT+1\nequispaced knots in the unit interval, the estimated contribution of the functional component to the overall trajectory is, at each iteration:\nf\ni\n(\ng\n)\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n∑\nm\n=\n1\np\nθ\ni\n​\nm\n(\ng\n)\n​\nb\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\nΘ\ni\n(\ng\n)\n​\n𝒃\nt\n⊤\nfor\n​\nt\n=\nt\n1\n,\n…\n,\nt\nT\n,\n{f}_{i}^{(g)}(t)=\\sum_{m=1}^{p}{\\theta}_{im}^{(g)}b_{m}(t)={\\Theta}_{i}^{(g)}\\boldsymbol{b}_{t}^{\\top}\\quad\\text{ for }t={t_{1},\\ldots,t_{T}},\nwhere\nΘ\ni\n(\ng\n)\n{\\Theta}_{i}^{(g)}\ncorresponds to the\ni\ni\n-th row of matrix\n𝚯\ni\n\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}_{i}\n.\nAs for the seasonal linear mixed effect, we modelled it as a piecewise continuous function taking individual- and season-specific values. Hence, when retrieving its estimated effect on any point in the time grid, we need to determine which season it belongs to. As discussed in Section 3, time is rescaled so that equal values across individuals indicate the same day of the year, possibly in different years. Therefore, season changes, that occur at new year’s days, can be easily computed by straightforward proportions. At this point, the season to which\nt\nk\nt_{k}\nbelongs to is obtained by comparison with the season thresholds. In the following Equation, the indicator variable\nχ\n(\nt\n∈\ns\n)\n\\chi_{(t\\in s)}\ndetermines to which season each time point belongs to. Accordingly, the estimated contribution of the seasonal component to the overall trajectory is, at each iteration:\nμ\ni\n(\ng\n)\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n∑\ns\n=\n1\nS\ni\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n(\ng\n)\n​\nχ\n(\nt\n∈\ns\n)\nfor\n​\nt\n=\nt\n1\n,\n…\n,\nt\nT\n.\n{\\mu}_{i}^{(g)}(t)=\\sum_{s=1}^{S_{i}}{\\mu}_{is}^{(g)}\\chi_{(t\\in s)}\\quad\\text{ for }t={t_{1},\\ldots,t_{T}}.\nLastly, the regressive component has to be taken into account. The estimated contribution of the regressive component to the overall trajectory is, at each iteration:\n∑\nl\n=\n1\nr\nx\ni\n​\nl\n​\n(\nt\n)\n​\nβ\nl\n(\ng\n)\n=\n𝒙\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n​\n𝜷\n(\ng\n)\nfor\n​\nt\n=\nt\n1\n,\n…\n,\nt\nT\n.\n\\sum_{l=1}^{r}x_{il}(t){\\beta}_{l}^{(g)}=\\boldsymbol{x}_{i}(t){\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}^{(g)}\\quad\\text{ for }t={t_{1},\\ldots,t_{T}}.\nGiven the three components, the overall estimate of the underlying function is obtained by adding these three components. In particular, the estimated mean trajectory can be written as:\ny\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n^\n=\n1\nG\n​\n∑\ng\n=\n1\nG\nf\ni\n(\ng\n)\n​\n(\nt\n)\n+\nμ\ni\n(\ng\n)\n​\n(\nt\n)\n+\n𝒙\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n​\n𝜷\n(\ng\n)\nfor\n​\nt\n=\nt\n1\n,\n…\n,\nt\nT\n\\widehat{y_{i}(t)}=\\frac{1}{G}\\sum_{g=1}^{G}{f}_{i}^{(g)}(t)+{\\mu}_{i}^{(g)}(t)+\\boldsymbol{x}_{i}(t){\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}^{(g)}\\quad\\text{ for }t={t_{1},\\ldots,t_{T}}\n(12)\nSimilarly,\n95\n%\n95\\%\ncredible intervals can be computed to quantify uncertainty around our point estimate.\n5.1\nModel application\nIn this Section, we fit different specifications of our model to the data described in Section\n2\n.\nIn general, we consider the additive structure of the sampling model illustrated in Equation\n2\n. Table\n3\nreports an overview on of the six models we compare. In Model\nM\n1\nM_{1}\n, the B-sline basis functions have 80 degrees of freedom, the seasonal component has GARCH errors and three regressors are taken into account:\nsex, age and environment\n.\nM\n2\nM_{2}\nrepresents a slight modification of\nM\n1\nM_{1}\ngiven by the fixed-age implementation. Here we consider the covariate\nage\nnot as a time dependent variable, but as a fixed value given by the age at the beginning of each athlete’s career. In model\nM\n3\nM_{3}\na simpler dependence structure among the seasonal effects is used. Namely, we assume an autoregressive model for\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n\\mu_{i}s\n(see Eq.\n7\n). For model\nM\n4\nM_{4}\n, we simply consider a larger number of basis functions, i.e. 120, accounting for up to three splines having support in a season and hence meant to better capture the intra-seasonal variability. Finally, models\nM\n5\nM_{5}\nand\nM\n6\nM_{6}\nallow for\ndoping\nas additional covariate, both in the case of the time-dependent and time-independent specification of\nage\n.\nPriors were chosen as discussed in Section\n3\n, and with hyperparameter choices summarized in Table\n4\n. To argue on the choice of the informative prior for the overall mean parameter\nm\nm\n, in Table\n5\nwe also report the results under a slight modification of model\nM\n1\nM_{1}\n, that we denote\nM\n1\n(\n2\n)\nM_{1}^{(2)}\n, yielding a vague prior for\nm\nm\n.\nTable 3:\nModels name and description.\nSymbol\nMeaning\nM\n1\nM_{1}\n80 df B-splines, GARCH, covariates: sex, age (time dependent), env.\nM\n2\nM_{2}\n80 df B-splines, GARCH, covariates: sex, age (time constant), env.\nM\n3\nM_{3}\n80 df B-splines, AR, covariates: sex, age (t. dep.), env.\nM\n4\nM_{4}\n120 df B-splines, GARCH, covariates: sex, age (t. dep.), env.\nM\n5\nM_{5}\n80 df B-splines, GARCH, covariates: sex, age (t. dep.), env., doping\nM\n6\nM_{6}\n80 df B-splines, GARCH, covariates: sex, age (t. const.), env., doping\nTable 4:\nHyperparameter choices. In the first column, we refer to the Equation where the hyperparameter first appears.\nRef.\nHyp.\nValue\nDescription\n(\n4\n)\na\nσ\na_{\\sigma}\n1.0\n1\ns\n​\nt\n1^{st}\nGamma coeff. of error term in the factor exp.\n(\n4\n)\nb\nσ\nb_{\\sigma}\n0.3\n2\nn\n​\nd\n2^{nd}\nGamma coeff. of error term in the factor exp.\n(\n6\n)\nν\nϕ\n\\nu_{\\phi}\n9\nGamma coeff.s of local shrink. param.\nϕ\nm\n​\nl\n\\phi_{ml}\n(\n6\n)\na\n1\na_{1}\n2.1\n1\ns\n​\nt\n1^{st}\nGamma coeff. of the\n1\ns\n​\nt\n1^{st}\nglobal shrink. factor\nδ\n1\n\\delta_{1}\n(\n6\n)\nb\n1\nb_{1}\n1.0\n2\nn\n​\nd\n2^{nd}\nGamma coeff. of the\n1\ns\n​\nt\n1^{st}\nglobal shrink. factor\nδ\n1\n\\delta_{1}\n(\n6\n)\na\nl\na_{l}\n2.1\n1\ns\n​\nt\n1^{st}\nGamma coeff. of the\nl\nl\n-th global shrink. factor\nδ\nl\n\\delta_{l}\n(\n6\n)\nb\nl\nb_{l}\n1.0\n2\nn\n​\nd\n2^{nd}\nGamma coeff. of the\nl\nl\n-th global shrink. factor\nδ\nl\n\\delta_{l}\n(\n10\n)\nμ\nm\n0\n\\mu_{m_{0}}\n-0.2\nMean of the overall mean\nm\nm\n(\n10\n)\nΣ\nm\n0\n\\Sigma_{m_{0}}\n0.0001\nVariance of the overall mean\nm\nm\n(\n10\n)\nμ\nα\n\\mu_{\\alpha}\n(0.0, 0.0)\nMean vector of the\n𝜶\n\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\nGARCH coeff.\n(\n10\n)\nΣ\nα\n\\Sigma_{\\alpha}\n𝕀\n2\n\\mathbb{I}_{2}\nCovariance matrix of the\n𝜶\n\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\nGARCH coeff.\n(\n10\n)\nμ\nϖ\n\\mu_{\\varpi}\n0.0\nMean of the\nϖ\n\\varpi\nGARCH coeff.\n(\n10\n)\nΣ\nϖ\n\\Sigma_{\\varpi}\n1\nVariance of the\nϖ\n\\varpi\nGARCH coeff.\n(\n11\n)\nν\nβ\n\\nu_{\\beta}\n0.5\n1\ns\n​\nt\n1^{st}\nGamma coeff.s regression param.\n(\n11\n)\nσ\nβ\n\\sigma_{\\beta}\n0.5\n2\nn\n​\nd\n2^{nd}\nGamma coeff.s of regression param.\n(\n1\n)\nμ\nψ\n\\mu_{\\psi}\n1.0\nMean of the error variance\nψ\n\\psi\n(\n1\n)\nσ\nψ\n\\sigma_{\\psi}\n1.0\nVariannce of the error variance\nψ\n\\psi\nFor all experiments, inference is obtained via posterior samples drawn by the Gibbs sampler introduced in Section\n4\n. In particular, we ran\n20,000\n20,000\niterations with a burn-in period of\n60\n%\n60\\%\nand a thinning of\n5\n5\n. Performances are compared by means of the logarithm of the pseudo marginal likelihood (LPML) index\n(\nGeisser and Eddy 1979\n)\n. This estimator for the log marginal likelihood is based on conditional predictive densities and provides an overall comparison of model fit, with higher values denoting better performing models.\nTable 5:\nModel and hyperparameter comparison for the models in Table\n3\n.\nModel\nLPML\nM\n1\nM_{1}\n−\n𝟒𝟓𝟗𝟒𝟑\n\\boldsymbol{-45943}\nM\n1\n(\n2\n)\nM_{1}^{(2)}\n-46573\nM\n2\nM_{2}\n−\n𝟒𝟓𝟒𝟕𝟐\n\\boldsymbol{-45472}\nM\n3\nM_{3}\n-46544\nM\n4\nM_{4}\n-46314\nM\n5\nM_{5}\n-48565\nM\n6\nM_{6}\n-48122\nPerformances for the different models are fairly similar: as a matter of fact, the model specifications do not differ in a significant way. Despite having a slightly lower LPML than the best performing model,\nM\n2\nM_{2}\n, we prefer looking at results for model\nM\n1\nM_{1}\nwith 80 degrees od freedom splines, GARCH errors and three regressors with time-dependent\nage\ndefinition because regression parameters prove to be significant in this setting. As far as the estimation of trajectories describing the evolution of athletes’ performances is concerned, we use the method discussed in Section\n5\n. Figure\n4\ndisplays the estimate (with\n95\n%\n95\\%\ncredible bounds) for a random selection of athletes (black) together with one-season-ahead performance prediction (grey). The results are graphically pleasing in terms of model fit, but some comments are of order. First, we acknowledge that the seasonal random intercept captures the majority of the variability in the data. Second, the functional component, which is meant to capture the overall variability in the data set, reduces to capture the intra-seasonal variability. Interestingly, the number on non-local bases selected by the adaptive procedure in\nBhattacharya and Dunson 2011\nis exactly equal to the number of seasons in the data set. This effect seems to be consistent with the choice of degrees of freedom, that limits the support of each spline to a unique season. Finally, the effect of covariates is very small in magnitude. Note that, because\nsex\nis time-constant, in the estimated trajectory we expect to recognise the contribution of\nage\nas a linear trend and the environmental effect as a diversification of summer and winter performances.\nFigure 4:\nPerformance trajectory estimates for a random selection of athletes. The\nx\nx\n-axis denotes the time measured in days from January 1st of the first season of career, whereas on the y-axis there is the length of throw in meters. Vertical lines represent calendar years (seasons in our notation). The final part of each trajectory (grey) for which no observations are available, represents one-season-ahead performance prediction.\nIn Figure\n5\nwe underline the effect of the three additive components of our functional model. The first panel (top-left) displays the observed data and the estimated trajectory for athlete 226. The top-right panel shows the seasonal contribution (i.e, an estimation of\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n;\ni\n=\n226\n;\ns\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nS\n226\n\\mu_{is};\\ i=226;\\ s=1,\\ldots,S_{226}\n). The third panel (bottom-left) reports the functional contribution (i.e, an estimate of\nf\n226\n​\n(\nt\n)\nf_{226}(t)\n). Finally, the bottom-right panel shows the effect of covariates. As anticipated, within the trajectory estimation of a unique athlete, we only recognise a performance drop predicted during winter months (negative effect of indoor environment on performances) in the bottom-right panel. The tight credible intervals for this component assures estimates to be significant, despite small, as we will discuss in further detail in the next Section.\nFigure 5:\nSingle contributions to the whole additive model as in Equation\n12\n. The first panel is the complete additive model, whereas the second (top-right) displays the estimate of the seasonal random intercept. The third panel (bottom-left) represents the functional contribution, while the bottom-right panel displays the regressive component.\n5.2\nParameters interpretation\nThe regression parameters can be easily interpreted from a sports’ analytics perspective. It is important to stress that, to improve convergence of the MCMC algorithm, we fitted our model centering athlete-specific data around their average. Accordingly, in our experiments the raw data\ny\ni\n​\nj\ny_{ij}\nwere substituted by the centered points:\ny\n~\ni\n​\nj\n=\ny\ni\n​\nj\n−\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nn\ni\ny\ni\n​\nj\nn\ni\n=\ny\ni\n​\nj\n−\ny\n¯\ni\nfor\n​\ni\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nn\n​\nand\n​\nj\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nn\ni\n\\tilde{y}_{ij}=y_{ij}-\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^{n_{i}}y_{ij}}{n_{i}}=y_{ij}-\\overline{y}_{i}\\quad\\text{for }i=1,\\ldots,n\\text{ and }\\ j=1,\\ldots,n_{i}\nas data-input for the model. We have to take into account this transformation when interpreting the regression\nparameters, especially when dealing with dummy variables.\nWe report the posterior mean estimate of the regression coefficients, their standard deviation, the effective sample size (ESS) and the\n95\n%\n95\\%\nposterior credible bounds for the most interesting models. Table\n6\ndisplays the results under model\nM\n1\nM_{1}\n, where covariates are\nsex\n(\nx\n1\nx_{1}\n),\nage\n(\nx\n2\nx_{2}\n) and\nenvironment\n(\nx\n3\nx_{3}\n).\nEven if the covariate effect is small in size, we observe that the\n95\n%\n95\\%\ncredible intervals do not contain zero,\nshowing a significant effect. In particular\nβ\n2\n\\beta_{2}\nis positive, suggesting that athletes, on average, increase their performances throughout their career. Further,\nβ\n3\n\\beta_{3}\nis positive, meaning that an athlete is likely to perform better outdoors than indoors. Finally,\nβ\n1\n\\beta_{1}\nis negative. Since\nsex\nis a time-constant dummy variable, its coefficient quantifies the difference in variability of the athlete’s performance around his/her average\ny\n¯\ni\n\\overline{y}_{i}\n. We conclude that\nfemale’s trajectories express less variability around their average than men’s. Parameter estimation under the other models considered in the paper are similar in sign with respect to the ones just discussed here. We report complete results in Appendix\nB\n.\nA final comment on results obtained using doping as additional regressor is required. We stressed that LPML performances for models\nM\n5\nM_{5}\nand\nM\n6\nM_{6}\nare quite low, however, this may be due to the fact that the data set is imbalanced, i.e., there are too few doped athletes (18 out of 653). In fact, the ESS of the parameter corresponding to\ndoping\nis very low. Nevertheless, it is interesting to observe that estimates are similar for all common parameters and that the coefficient of the doping regressor is\nnegative (even if the credible intervals contain zero). We conclude that the use of performance-enhancing drugs seems to have a negative effect on the variability of athletes’ performances.\nTable 6:\nPosterior mean estimate of the regression coefficients for model\nM\n1\nM_{1}\n(Table\n3\n), together with the standard deviation of their estimate, effective sample size (ESS) with respect to 1600 retained samples, and\n95\n%\n95\\%\nposterior credible bounds.\nCoeff\nMean\nSd\nESS\n2.5\n%\n2.5\\%\n97.5\n%\n97.5\\%\nβ\n1\n\\beta_{1}\n-0.120\n0.0270\n190\n-0.175\n-0.0675\nβ\n2\n\\beta_{2}\n6.22 e-03\n9.95 e-04\n170\n4.20 e-03\n8.20 e-03\nβ\n3\n\\beta_{3}\n0.0453\n9.55 e-03\n1600\n0.0269\n0.0643\n6\nDiscussion\nWe proposed an additive hierarchical Bayesian model for the analysis of athletes’ performances in a longitudinal context. Following\nMontagna and Hopker 2018\n, we proposed a smooth functional contribution for explaining the overall variability in the data set. The functions are represented by means of a high-dimensional set of pre-specified basis functions and a factor model on the basis coefficients ensures dimensionality reduction. We enriched the model by allowing for time-dependent covariates to affect estimates through a regressive component. Finally, we addressed the issue of seasonal gathering of sports data introducing a mixed effect model with GARCH errors which provides evolving random intercepts over different time intervals in the data set. While the motivation of our work comes from the analysis of shot put performance data, the methodology presented in this work is applicable to the analysis of performance data collected in all measurable sports.\nThe Bayesian latent factor methodology was originally developed for very sparse longitudinal\ndata, with the purpose of capturing a global trend in subject-specific trajectories. We balanced the model with the requirement of smoothness using a B-spline basis system and adding a seasonal random intercept. However, it is evident that the latter explains the majority of variability in the dataset. Therefore, it might be worth considering a functional basis that batter captures the intra-seasonal variability. Further, we observed that the contribution of the regressive component is consistent across various modelling choices.\nFinally, we looked at the effect of\ndoping\non results. Despite not being significant, the negative effect seems to suggest that performances of doped athletes are less variable. This is in line with previous literature suggesting that doping is more likely used to enhance performances in periods of decreasing fitness than to consolidate already good performances, generally exposed to strict controls. We think this aspect deserves further investigation, considering, for instance, more specific modeling techniques and a less imbalanced data set.\nIn conclusion, the attempt to extend exiting tools of functional statistics to the modelling of (shot put) performance data seems promising because of the adaptability of these methodologies to all sorts of performance longitudinal data in measurable sports.\nAcknowledgements\nWe would like to thank Prof. James Hopker (University of Kent, School of Sport and Exercise Sciences) for sharing the shot put data set, and for the helpful comments.\nConflict of interest\nThe authors declare that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest.\nAvailability of data and material\nThe raw data set is available at\nwww.tilastopaja.eu\n, whereas the data prepared for our analysis at\nhttps://github.com/PatricDolmeta/Bayesian-GARCH-Modeling-of-Functional-Sports-Data.\nCode availability\nThe code for the Bayesian analysis and the output production (numerical and graphical) is available at\nhttps://github.com/PatricDolmeta/Bayesian-GARCH-Modeling-of-Functional-Sports-Data.\nReferences\nAlbert (2016)\nAlbert J (2016) Improved component predictions of batting and pitching\nmeasures. Journal of Quantitative Analysis in Sports 12(2):73–85\nBhattacharya and Dunson (2011)\nBhattacharya A, Dunson D (2011) Sparse bayesian infinite factor models.\nBiometrika 98:291–306, DOI\n10.2307/23076151\nBollerslev (1986)\nBollerslev T (1986) Generalized autoregressive conditional heteroskedasticity.\nJournal of Econometrics 31(3):307 – 327\nde Boor (1978)\nde Boor C (1978) A Practical Guide to Splines. Springer-Verlag New York, New\nYork\nCosta et al. (2010)\nCosta M, Marinho D, Reis V, Silva A, Marques M, Bragada J, Barbosa T (2010)\nTracking the performance of world-ranked swimmers. Journal of sports science\n& medicine 9:411–7\nCosta et al. (2013)\nCosta M, Bragada J, Marinho D, Lopes V, Silva A, Barbosa T (2013) Longitudinal\nstudy in male swimmers: A hierachical modeling of energetics and\nbiomechanical contributions for performance. Journal of sports science &\nmedicine 12:614–22\nGeisser and Eddy (1979)\nGeisser S, Eddy WF (1979) A predictive approach to model selection. Journal of\nthe American Statistical Association 74(365):153–160\nHaario et al. (2001)\nHaario H, Saksman E, Tamminen J (2001) An adaptive metropolis algorithm.\nBernoulli 7:223–242\nKoulis et al. (2014)\nKoulis T, Muthukumarana S, Briercliffe C (2014) A bayesian stochastic model for\nbatting performance evaluation in one-day cricket. Journal of Quantitative\nAnalysis in Sports 10, DOI\n10.1515/jqas-2013-0057\nMalcata et al. (2014)\nMalcata R, Hopkins W, Pearson S (2014) Tracking career performance of\nsuccessful triathletes. Medicine and science in sports and exercise\n46:1227–34\nMarsden (1974)\nMarsden M (1974) Cubic spline interpolation of continuous functions. Journal of\nApproximation Theory 10(2):103 – 111\nMengersen et al. (2016)\nMengersen KL, Drovandi CC, Robert CP, Pyne DB, Gore CJ (2016) Bayesian\nestimation of small effects in exercise and sports science. PLOS ONE\n11(4):1–23\nMontagna and Hopker (2018)\nMontagna S, Hopker J (2018) A bayesian approach for the use of athlete\nperformance data within anti-doping. Frontiers in Physiology 9\nMontagna et al. (2012)\nMontagna S, Tokdar S, Neelon B, Dunson D (2012) Bayesian latent factor\nregression for functional and longitudinal data. Biometrics 68:1064–1073,\nDOI\n10.2307/41806025\nMontagna et al. (2020)\nMontagna S, Orani V, Argiento R (2020) Bayesian isotonic logistic regression\nvia constrained splines: an application to estimating the serve advantage in\nprofessional tennis. Statistical Methods and Applications\nDOI\n10.1007/s10260-020-00535-5\nVaci et al. (2019)\nVaci N, Cocić D, Gula B, Bilalic M (2019) Large data and bayesian\nmodeling—aging curves of nba players. Behavior Research Methods 51:1544 –\n1564\nWimmer et al. (2011)\nWimmer V, Fenske N, Pyrka P, Fahrmeir L (2011) Exploring competition\nperformance in decathlon using semi-parametric latent variable models.\nJournal of Quantitative Analysis in Sports 7:6–6\nYousefi and Swartz (2013)\nYousefi K, Swartz T (2013) Advanced putting metrics in golf. Journal of\nQuantitative Analysis in Sports 9:239–248\nAppendix A\nPosterior computations\nIn this Appendix, we discuss in greater detail the full conditional updates anticipated in Section\n4\nand referred to in Algorithm\n1\n.\nA.1\nFunctional model\nWith reference to the hierarchical model discussed in Section\n3.1\n, we get the following updates:\n1.\nRecalling that\n𝚲\n\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}\nis a\nq\n×\nk\nq\\times k\nmatrix, we update its\nj\nj\n-th row as:\nπ\n⁡\n(\n𝝀\n𝒋\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nN\nk\n​\n(\n(\n𝑫\n𝒋\n−\n𝟏\n+\nσ\nj\n−\n2\n​\n𝜼\n⊤\n​\n𝜼\n)\n−\n1\n​\n𝜼\n⊤\n​\nσ\nj\n−\n2\n​\n𝜽\n(\nj\n)\n,\n(\n𝑫\n𝒋\n−\n𝟏\n+\nσ\nj\n−\n2\n​\n𝜼\n⊤\n​\n𝜼\n)\n−\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\pi(\\boldsymbol{\\lambda_{j}}|\\ldots)\\sim N_{k}((\\boldsymbol{D^{-1}_{j}}+\\sigma_{j}^{-2}\\boldsymbol{\\eta^{\\top}}\\boldsymbol{\\eta})^{-1}\\boldsymbol{\\eta^{\\top}}\\sigma_{j}^{-2}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{(j)},(\\boldsymbol{D^{-1}_{j}}+\\sigma_{j}^{-2}\\boldsymbol{\\eta^{\\top}}\\boldsymbol{\\eta})^{-1})\nwhere\n𝑫\n𝒋\n−\n𝟏\n=\nd\n​\ni\n​\na\n​\ng\n​\n(\nϕ\nj\n​\n1\n−\n1\n​\nτ\n1\n−\n1\n,\n…\n,\nϕ\nj\n​\nk\n−\n1\n​\nτ\nk\n−\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{D^{-1}_{j}}=diag(\\phi_{j1}^{-1}\\tau_{1}^{-1},\\ldots,\\phi_{jk}^{-1}\\tau_{k}^{-1})\n𝜼\n⊤\n=\n(\nη\n1\n,\n…\n,\nη\nk\n)\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{\\eta^{\\top}}=(\\eta_{1},\\ldots,\\eta_{k})\n𝜽\n(\nj\n)\n=\n(\nθ\nj\n​\n1\n,\n…\n,\nθ\nj\n​\nn\n)\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{(j)}=(\\theta_{j1},\\ldots,\\theta_{jn})\n2.\nConcerning the local shrinkage parameters, we get:\nπ\n⁡\n(\nϕ\nj\n​\nh\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\nν\nϕ\n+\n1\n2\n,\nν\nϕ\n+\nτ\nh\n​\nλ\nj\n​\nh\n2\n2\n)\n\\pi(\\phi_{jh}|\\ldots)\\sim Ga\\biggl(\\frac{\\nu_{\\phi}+1}{2},\\frac{\\nu_{\\phi}+\\tau_{h}\\lambda_{jh}^{2}}{2}\\biggr)\n3.\nThe first global shrinkage parameter is updated as follows:\nπ\n⁡\n(\nδ\n1\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\na\n1\n+\nq\n​\nk\n2\n,\n1\n+\n1\n2\n​\n∑\nl\n=\n1\nk\nτ\nl\n(\n1\n)\n​\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nq\nϕ\nj\n​\nl\n​\nλ\nj\n​\nl\n2\n)\n\\pi(\\delta_{1}|\\ldots)\\sim Ga\\biggl(a_{1}+\\frac{qk}{2},1+\\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{l=1}^{k}\\tau_{l}^{(1)}\\sum_{j=1}^{q}\\phi_{jl}\\lambda_{jl}^{2}\\biggr)\n4.\nWhereas for the remaining ones:\nπ\n⁡\n(\nδ\nh\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\na\nh\n+\nq\n⁡\n(\nk\n−\nh\n−\n1\n)\n2\n,\n1\n+\n1\n2\n​\n∑\nl\n=\n1\nk\nτ\nl\n(\nh\n)\n​\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nq\nϕ\nj\n​\nl\n​\nλ\nj\n​\nl\n2\n)\n\\displaystyle\\pi(\\delta_{h}|\\ldots)\\sim Ga\\biggl(a_{h}+\\frac{q(k-h-1)}{2},1+\\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{l=1}^{k}\\tau_{l}^{(h)}\\sum_{j=1}^{q}\\phi_{jl}\\lambda_{jl}^{2}\\biggr)\nwith\nτ\nl\n(\nh\n)\n=\n∏\nt\n=\n1\n,\nt\n≠\nh\nl\nδ\nt\n\\displaystyle\\tau_{l}^{(h)}=\\prod_{t=1,t\\neq h}^{l}\\delta_{t}\n5.\nVariances of factor residuals can be sampled as:\nπ\n⁡\n(\nσ\nj\n−\n2\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\na\nσ\n+\nn\n2\n,\nb\nσ\n+\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\n(\n𝜽\n(\nj\n)\n−\n𝚲\n​\nη\ni\n)\n2\n2\n)\n\\pi(\\sigma_{j}^{-2}|\\ldots)\\sim Ga\\biggl(a_{\\sigma}+\\frac{n}{2},b_{\\sigma}+\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{(j)}-\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}\\eta_{i})^{2}}{2}\\biggr)\n6.\nLatent factors for a specific individual have conditional posterior distribution:\nπ\n⁡\n(\n𝜼\ni\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nN\n⁡\n(\n𝑨\n𝒊\n−\n1\n×\n𝑩\n𝒊\n,\n𝑨\n𝒊\n−\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\pi(\\boldsymbol{\\eta}_{i}|\\ldots)\\sim N(\\boldsymbol{A_{i}}^{-1}\\times\\boldsymbol{B_{i}},\\boldsymbol{A_{i}}^{-1})\nbeing\n𝑨\n𝒊\n=\n𝚲\n⊤\n​\n𝑩\n𝒊\n⊤\n​\n(\nψ\n2\n​\n𝑰\nn\ni\n+\n𝑩\n𝒊\n​\n𝚺\n​\n𝑩\n𝒊\n⊤\n)\n−\n1\n​\n𝑩\n𝒊\n​\n𝚲\n+\nI\nk\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{A_{i}}=\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}^{\\top}(\\psi^{2}\\boldsymbol{I}_{n_{i}}+\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}^{\\top})^{-1}\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}+I_{k}\n𝑩\n𝒊\n=\n𝚲\n⊤\n​\n𝑩\n𝒊\n⊤\n​\n(\nψ\n2\n​\n𝑰\nn\ni\n+\n𝑩\n𝒊\n​\n𝚺\n​\n𝑩\n𝒊\n⊤\n)\n−\n1\n​\ny\ni\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}=\\boldsymbol{\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}^{\\top}(\\psi^{2}\\boldsymbol{I}_{n_{i}}+\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}^{\\top})^{-1}y_{i}\n7.\nFinally, coefficients of the spline representation are sampled from:\nπ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\ni\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nN\n⁡\n(\n𝑪\n𝒊\n−\n1\n×\n𝑫\n𝒊\n,\n𝑪\n𝒊\n−\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\pi(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{i}|\\ldots)\\sim N(\\boldsymbol{C_{i}}^{-1}\\times\\boldsymbol{D_{i}},\\boldsymbol{C_{i}}^{-1})\nbeing\n𝑪\n𝒊\n=\nψ\n2\n​\n𝑩\n𝒊\n⊤\n​\n𝑩\n𝒊\n+\n𝚺\n−\n1\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{C_{i}}=\\psi^{2}\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}+\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}^{-1}\n𝑫\n𝒊\n=\nψ\n2\n​\n𝑩\n𝒊\n⊤\n​\ny\ni\n+\n𝚺\n−\n1\n​\n𝚲\n​\n𝜼\ni\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{D_{i}}=\\psi^{2}\\boldsymbol{B_{i}}^{\\top}y_{i}+\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma}^{-1}\\boldsymbol{\\Lambda}\\boldsymbol{\\eta}_{i}\nA.2\nSeasonal GARCH model\nConcerning the seasonal component, let us start noticing that the likelihood function of the GARCH model is:\nf\n⁡\n(\n{\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n}\ni\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nn\ns\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nS\ni\n|\nϖ\n,\nh\ni\n,\ns\n)\n=\n∏\ni\n=\n1\nn\n∏\ns\n=\n1\nS\ni\n1\n2\n​\nπ\n​\nh\ni\n,\ns\n​\ne\n​\nx\n​\np\n​\n{\n−\n(\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n−\nm\n)\n2\n⏞\nζ\ni\n,\ns\n2\n2\n​\nh\ni\n,\ns\n}\nf(\\ \\{\\mu_{i,s}\\}_{i=1,\\ldots,n}^{s=1,\\ldots,S_{i}}\\ |\\ \\varpi,h_{i,s})=\\prod_{i=1}^{n}\\prod_{s=1}^{S_{i}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi h_{i,s}}}exp\\left\\{-\\frac{\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\zeta_{i,s}^{2}}}{{\\overbrace{(\\mu_{i,s}-m)^{2}}}}}{2h_{i,s}}\\right\\}\n(13)\nwhereas the terms in the joint sampling distribution depending on\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n\\mu_{i,s}\nare:\n∏\ni\n=\n1\nn\n∏\ns\n=\n1\nS\ni\n∏\nj\n∈\ns\n1\n2\n​\nπ\n​\nψ\n2\n​\ne\n​\nx\n​\np\n​\n{\n−\n(\ny\ni\n,\nj\n(\n2\n)\n−\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\n2\n2\n​\nψ\n2\n}\n\\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^{n}\\prod_{s=1}^{S_{i}}\\prod_{j\\in s}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi\\psi^{2}}}exp\\left\\{-\\frac{(y_{i,j}^{(2)}-\\mu_{i,s})^{2}}{2\\psi^{2}}\\right\\}\nwhere\ny\ni\n,\nj\n(\n2\n)\n=\ny\ni\n,\nj\n−\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n,\nj\n)\n−\n𝒙\n⁡\n(\nt\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\n𝜷\n\\displaystyle y_{i,j}^{(2)}=y_{i,j}-f_{i}(t_{i,j})-\\boldsymbol{x}(t_{i,j})\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\nis a convenient writing for partial residuals, especially from the implementation point of view.\nConsequently,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n\\mu_{i,s}\ncan be updated taking samples from:\nq\nμ\n​\n(\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n|\nϖ\n,\ny\ni\n,\nj\n(\n2\n)\n,\nψ\n2\n)\n\\displaystyle q_{\\mu}(\\mu_{i,s}\\ |\\ \\varpi,y_{i,j}^{(2)},\\psi^{2})\n∝\ne\nx\np\n{\n−\n∑\nj\n∈\ns\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n2\n−\n2\n​\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n​\ny\ni\n,\nj\n(\n2\n)\n2\n​\nψ\n2\n}\n×\ne\nx\np\n{\n−\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n2\n−\n2\n​\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n​\nm\n2\n​\nh\ni\n,\ns\n}\n\\displaystyle\\propto exp\\left\\{-\\sum_{j\\in s}\\frac{\\mu_{i,s}^{2}-2\\mu_{i,s}y_{i,j}^{(2)}}{2\\psi^{2}}\\right\\}\\times exp\\left\\{-\\frac{\\mu_{i,s}^{2}-2\\mu_{i,s}m}{2h_{i,s}}\\right\\}\n∼\nN\n⁡\n(\nμ\n^\nμ\n,\nΣ\n^\nμ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\sim N(\\hat{\\mu}_{\\mu},\\hat{\\Sigma}_{\\mu})\nWhere\nΣ\n^\nμ\n=\n(\n∑\nj\n∈\ns\n\\displaystyle\\text{Where }\\hat{\\Sigma}_{\\mu}=\\biggl(\\sum_{j\\in s}\nOPEN\n1\nψ\n2\n+\n1\nh\ni\n,\ns\n)\n−\n1\n​\nand\n​\nμ\n^\nμ\n=\nΣ\n^\nμ\n−\n1\n​\n(\n∑\nj\n∈\ns\ny\ni\n,\nj\n(\n2\n)\nψ\n2\n+\nm\nh\ni\n,\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{\\psi^{2}}+\\frac{1}{h_{i,s}}\\biggr)^{-1}\\text{ and }\\ \\hat{\\mu}_{\\mu}=\\hat{\\Sigma}_{\\mu}^{-1}\\biggl(\\sum_{j\\in s}\\frac{y_{i,j}^{(2)}}{\\psi^{2}}+\\frac{m}{h_{i,s}}\\biggr)\nPosterior updates for other parameters are not straightforward in general because of the recursive definition of the conditional variance. As a consequence, we will rely on Metropolis Hastings steps within a Gibbs sampling algorithm. We iteratively generate:\nm\n[\nj\n]\n∼\np\n⁡\n(\nm\n|\nα\n[\nj\n−\n1\n]\n,\nϖ\n[\nj\n−\n1\n]\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle m^{[j]}\\sim p(m\\ |\\ \\alpha^{[j-1]},\\varpi^{[j-1]},\\mu_{i,s})\nα\n[\nj\n]\n∼\np\n⁡\n(\nα\n|\nm\n[\nj\n]\n,\nϖ\n[\nj\n−\n1\n]\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle\\alpha^{[j]}\\sim p(\\alpha\\ |\\ m^{[j]},\\varpi^{[j-1]},\\mu_{i,s})\nϖ\n[\nj\n]\n∼\np\n⁡\n(\nϖ\n|\nm\n[\nj\n]\n,\nα\n[\nj\n]\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle\\varpi^{[j]}\\sim p(\\varpi\\ |\\ m^{[j]},\\alpha^{[j]},\\mu_{i,s})\nA.2.1\nUpdate\nm\nm\nThe posterior distribution for\nm\nm\nis based on the GARCH model under the assumption that the conditional variances\n{\nh\ni\n,\ns\n}\ni\ns\n\\{h_{i,s}\\}_{i}^{s}\nare fixed, known and given by the writing in Eq. (\n9\n). In this case the likelihood function is:\nf\n⁡\n(\n{\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n}\ni\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nn\ns\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nS\ni\n|\n𝜶\n,\nϖ\n)\n=\n∏\ni\n=\n1\nn\n∏\ns\n=\n1\nS\ni\n1\n2\n​\nπ\n​\nh\ni\n,\ns\n​\ne\n​\nx\n​\np\n​\n{\n−\n(\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n−\nm\n)\n2\n2\n​\nh\ni\n,\ns\n}\nf(\\ \\{\\mu_{i,s}\\}_{i=1,\\ldots,n}^{s=1,\\ldots,S_{i}}\\ |\\ \\boldsymbol{\\alpha},\\varpi)=\\prod_{i=1}^{n}\\prod_{s=1}^{S_{i}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi h_{i,s}}}exp\\left\\{-\\frac{(\\mu_{i,s}-m)^{2}}{2h_{i,s}}\\right\\}\nAccordingly, the posterior samples for\nm\nm\nwill be drawn from:\nq\nm\n​\n(\nm\n|\n𝜶\n~\n,\nϖ\n~\n)\n\\displaystyle q_{m}(m\\ |\\ \\tilde{\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}},\\tilde{\\varpi})\n∝\ne\nx\np\n{\n−\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\n∑\ns\n=\n1\nS\ni\nm\n2\n−\n2\n​\nm\n​\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n2\n​\nh\ni\n,\ns\n}\n×\ne\nx\np\n{\n−\nm\n2\n−\n2\n​\nm\n​\nμ\nm\n0\n2\n​\nΣ\nm\n0\n}\n\\displaystyle\\propto exp\\left\\{-\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{s=1}^{S_{i}}\\frac{m^{2}-2m\\mu_{i,s}}{2h_{i,s}}\\right\\}\\times exp\\left\\{-\\frac{m^{2}-2m\\mu_{m_{0}}}{2\\Sigma_{m_{0}}}\\right\\}\n∼\nN\n⁡\n(\nμ\n^\nm\n,\nΣ\n^\nm\n)\n\\displaystyle\\sim N(\\hat{\\mu}_{m},\\hat{\\Sigma}_{m})\nWhere\n​\nΣ\n^\nm\n=\n(\nCLOSE\n\\displaystyle\\text{Where }\\hat{\\Sigma}_{m}=\\biggl(\nOPEN\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\n∑\ns\n=\n1\nS\ni\n1\nh\ni\n,\ns\n+\n1\nΣ\nm\n0\n)\n−\n1\n​\nand\n​\nμ\n^\nm\n=\nΣ\n^\nm\n−\n1\n​\n(\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\n∑\ns\n=\n1\nS\ni\nμ\ni\n,\ns\nh\ni\n,\ns\n+\nμ\nm\n0\nΣ\nm\n0\n)\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{s=1}^{S_{i}}\\frac{1}{h_{i,s}}+\\frac{1}{\\Sigma_{m_{0}}}\\biggr)^{-1}\\text{ and }\\ \\hat{\\mu}_{m}=\\hat{\\Sigma}_{m}^{-1}\\biggl(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{s=1}^{S_{i}}\\frac{\\mu_{i,s}}{h_{i,s}}+\\frac{\\mu_{m_{0}}}{\\Sigma_{m_{0}}}\\biggr)\nIt is here of capital importance that\nh\ni\n,\ns\nh_{i,s}\nare obtained by means of Eq. (\n9\n), using previous realisations the GARCH coefficients from the MH sampler.\nA.2.2\nUpdate\n𝜶\n\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\nTo generate samples from\n𝜶\n\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\nwe can not exploit conjugacy, therefore we would like to rely on a normal proposal distribution. Indeed, symmetry of proposal distributions significantly eases computations of acceptance probabilities in Metropolis algorithms. Unfortunately, we are given a non-negativity constraint for the parameter, which forces us to apply a bidimensional transformation from the positive quadrant into the real plane: let say\n(\nθ\n0\n,\nθ\n1\n)\n=\n(\nl\n​\no\n​\ng\n​\n(\nα\n0\n)\n,\nl\n​\no\n​\ng\n​\n(\nα\n1\n)\n)\n(\\theta_{0},\\theta_{1})=(log(\\alpha_{0}),log(\\alpha_{1}))\n. After transformation of the starting parameter, we propose a new sample employing a random walk sampler.\nThat is, a proposal of the form\n𝜽\n∗\n=\n𝜽\n+\n𝜻\n​\nϵ\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{\\ast}=\\boldsymbol{\\theta}+\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}\\boldsymbol{\\epsilon}\nwhere\nϵ\n\\boldsymbol{\\epsilon}\nis a multivariate standard normal random vector and\n𝜻\n\\boldsymbol{\\zeta}\na covariance matrix, defined according to an Adaptive scaling within the Adaptive Metropolis–Hastings algorithm (ASWAM) by\nHaario et al. 2001\n. In this approach, both the covariance matrix of the proposal is adapted to the covariance matrix of the target density and the scale parameter is updated to achieve an average acceptance rate of 0.234 (proven to be optimal in different scenarios).\nProvided it will be accepted, the actual parameter will be retrieved applying the inverse transform to\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\n.\nAs discussed above, the acceptance probability will not depend on the proposal distribution. Therefore:\nλ\nα\n=\nm\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n{\np\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n∗\n|\nϖ\n,\nm\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\np\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n~\n|\nϖ\n,\nm\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\n,\n1\n}\n\\lambda_{\\alpha}=min\\left\\{\\frac{p(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{\\ast}\\ |\\ \\varpi,\\ m,\\ \\mu_{i,s})}{p(\\tilde{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\\ |\\ \\varpi,\\ m,\\ \\mu_{i,s})},1\\right\\}\n(14)\nWith\np\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n|\nϖ\n,\nm\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\np(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ |\\ \\varpi,m,\\mu_{i,s})\nthe full conditional density of\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\ngiven the data and the rest of the parameters, which is clearly obtained from\np\n⁡\n(\n𝜶\n|\nϖ\n,\nm\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\np(\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}\\ |\\ \\varpi,m,\\mu_{i,s})\nby change of variables.\nA.2.3\nUpdate\nϖ\n\\varpi\nSimilar arguments apply for\nϖ\n\\varpi\n. Samples will be proposed according to a normal distribution centered around\nγ\n~\n=\nl\n​\no\n​\ng\n​\n(\nϖ\n~\n)\n\\tilde{\\gamma}=log(\\tilde{\\varpi})\n:\nγ\n∗\n∼\nN\n⁡\n(\nγ\n|\nγ\n~\n,\nΣ\nϖ\n)\n\\gamma^{\\ast}\\sim N(\\gamma|\\tilde{\\gamma},\\Sigma_{\\varpi})\nHere,\nΣ\nϖ\n\\Sigma_{\\varpi}\nis defined, for each iteration\ng\ng\nas:\nΣ\nϖ\n(\ng\n+\n1\n)\n=\nζ\n(\ng\n+\n1\n)\n=\nρ\n⁡\n(\nζ\n(\ng\n)\n∗\nw\n(\ng\n)\n​\n(\nλ\nθ\n(\ng\n)\n−\nλ\n¯\n)\n)\n\\sqrt{\\Sigma_{\\varpi}}^{(g+1)}=\\zeta^{(g+1)}=\\rho\\bigl(\\zeta^{(g)}*w^{(g)}\\bigl(\\lambda_{\\theta}^{(g)}-\\overline{\\lambda}\\bigr)\\bigr)\nto achieve an average optimal acceptance rate of\nλ\n¯\n=\n0.234\n\\overline{\\lambda}=0.234\nHaario et al. 2001\n.\nActual samples will be retrieved applying the inverse transform and accepted according to:\nλ\nϖ\n=\nm\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n{\np\n⁡\n(\nγ\n∗\n|\n𝜶\n,\nm\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\np\n⁡\n(\nγ\n~\n|\n𝜶\n,\nm\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\n,\n1\n}\n\\lambda_{\\varpi}=min\\left\\{\\frac{p(\\gamma^{\\ast}\\ |\\ \\boldsymbol{\\alpha},\\ m,\\ \\mu_{i,s})}{p(\\tilde{\\gamma}\\ |\\ \\boldsymbol{\\alpha},\\ m,\\ \\mu_{i,s})},1\\right\\}\n(15)\nwhere again\np\n⁡\n(\nγ\n|\n𝜶\n,\nm\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\np(\\gamma\\ |\\ \\boldsymbol{\\alpha},m,\\mu_{i,s})\nis the full conditional density of\nγ\n\\gamma\ngiven by change of variables from\np\n⁡\n(\nϖ\n|\n𝜶\n,\nm\n,\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n)\np(\\varpi\\ |\\ \\boldsymbol{\\alpha},m,\\mu_{i,s})\nA.3\nMultiple regression model\nOn the base of the prior settings and the overall sampling scheme\n1\nwe get:\n1.\nRegression coefficient, jointly, as:\nπ\n⁡\n(\n𝜷\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nN\n⁡\n(\nA\n−\n1\n×\nB\n,\nA\n−\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\pi(\\boldsymbol{\\beta}|\\ldots)\\sim N(A^{-1}\\times B,A^{-1})\nbeing\nA\n=\n𝒙\n⊤\n​\n𝒙\n/\nψ\n2\n+\nΣ\n𝜷\n0\n\\displaystyle A=\\boldsymbol{x^{\\top}}\\boldsymbol{x}/\\psi^{2}+\\Sigma_{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{0}}\nB\n=\nΣ\n𝜷\n0\n​\n𝜷\n0\n+\n𝒙\n⊤\n​\n𝒚\n(\n𝟑\n)\n/\nψ\n2\n\\displaystyle B=\\Sigma_{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{0}}\\boldsymbol{\\beta}_{0}+\\boldsymbol{x^{\\top}}\\boldsymbol{y^{(3)}}/\\psi^{2}\n2.\nThe variance hyperparameter instead is updated as:\nπ\n⁡\n(\nσ\nβ\n−\n2\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\nN\n+\nν\nβ\n2\n,\nν\nβ\n​\nσ\nβ\n2\n+\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nn\ni\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n−\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n​\nj\n)\n−\nμ\ni\n​\ns\n−\n𝒙\n​\n𝜷\n)\n2\n2\n)\n\\pi(\\sigma_{\\beta}^{-2}|\\ldots)\\sim Ga\\biggl(\\frac{N+\\nu_{\\beta}}{2},\\frac{\\nu_{\\beta}\\sigma_{\\beta}^{2}+\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j=1}^{n_{i}}(y_{ij}-f_{i}(t_{ij})-\\mu_{is}-\\boldsymbol{x}\\boldsymbol{\\beta})^{2}}{2}\\biggr)\nA.4\nError term\nFinally, the full conditional posterior distribution for the model variance is:\nπ\n⁡\n(\nψ\n−\n2\n|\n…\n)\n∼\nG\n​\na\n​\n(\nN\n+\nν\nψ\n2\n,\nν\nψ\n​\nσ\nβ\n2\n+\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\n∑\nj\n=\n1\nn\ni\nϵ\ni\n,\nj\n2\n2\n)\n\\displaystyle\\pi(\\psi^{-2}|\\ldots)\\sim Ga\\biggl(\\frac{N+\\nu_{\\psi}}{2},\\frac{\\nu_{\\psi}\\sigma_{\\beta}^{2}+\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{j=1}^{n_{i}}\\epsilon_{i,j}^{2}}{2}\\biggr)\nbeing\nϵ\ni\n,\nj\n=\ny\ni\n,\nj\n−\nf\ni\n​\n(\nt\ni\n,\nj\n)\n−\nμ\ni\n,\ns\n−\n𝒙\n​\n𝜷\n\\displaystyle\\epsilon_{i,j}=y_{i,j}-f_{i}(t_{i,j})-\\mu_{i,s}-\\boldsymbol{x}\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\nAppendix B\nRegression coefficients\nTable 7:\nPosterior mean estimate of the regression coefficients for model\nM\n2\nM_{2}\n(Table\n3\n), together with the standard deviation of their estimate, effective sample size (ESS) with respect to 1600 retained samples, and\n95\n%\n95\\%\nposterior credible bounds.\nCoeff\nMean\nSd\nESS\n2.5\n%\n2.5\\%\n97.5\n%\n97.5\\%\nβ\n1\n\\beta_{1}\n-5.58 e-03\n0.0263\n223\n-0.0585\n2.00 e-03\nβ\n2\n\\beta_{2}\n3.34 e-04\n1.16 e-03\n107\n-2.60 e-03\n2.00 e-03\nβ\n3\n\\beta_{3}\n0.0570\n9.09 e-03\n1600\n0.0395\n0.0743\nTable 8:\nPosterior mean estimate of the regression coefficients for model\nM\n5\nM_{5}\n(Table\n3\n), together with the standard deviation of their estimate, effective sample size (ESS) with respect to 600 retained samples, and\n95\n%\n95\\%\nposterior credible bounds.\nCoeff\nMean\nSd\nESS\n2.5\n%\n2.5\\%\n97.5\n%\n97.5\\%\nβ\n1\n\\beta_{1}\n-0.120\n0.0285\n77\n-0.178\n-0.0690\nβ\n2\n\\beta_{2}\n6.71 e-03\n1.11 e-03\n64\n4.40 e-3\n8.10 e-3\nβ\n3\n\\beta_{3}\n0.0373\n0.0108\n600\n0.0168\n0.0580\nβ\n4\n\\beta_{4}\n-0.116\n0.0761\n70\n-0.267\n-0.0365\nTable 9:\nPosterior mean estimate of the regression coefficients for model\nM\n6\nM_{6}\n(Table\n3\n), together with the standard deviation of their estimate, effective sample size (ESS), and\n95\n%\n95\\%\nposterior credible bounds.\nCoeff\nMean\nSd\nESS\n2.5\n%\n2.5\\%\n97.5\n%\n97.5\\%\nβ\n1\n\\beta_{1}\n-0.0104\n0.0250\n74\n-0.0617\n0.0362\nβ\n2\n\\beta_{2}\n-4.60 e-04\n1.14 e-03\n67\n2.80 e-03\n1.90 e-03\nβ\n3\n\\beta_{3}\n0.0578\n9.37 e-03\n600\n0.0408\n0.0756\nβ\n4\n\\beta_{4}\n-0.0103\n0.0638\n91\n-0.134\n0.115\n◄\nFeeling\nlucky?\nConversion\nreport\nReport\nan issue\nView original\non arXiv\n►"}
{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2101.09855.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 190004, "equations": ["\\delta\\to\\infty", "k=l\\in\\mathcal{I}_{S}", "\\forall l=1,\\ldots,K", "x,s\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K}", "q\\in[0,1]^{K}", "|\\mu|\\to\\infty", "\\mu\\to\\infty", "t\\to\\infty"], "text": "[2101.09855] Weak Signal Asymptotics for Sequentially Randomized Experiments Weak Signal Asymptotics for Sequentially Randomized Experiments Xu Kuang Email: kuangxu@stanford.edu Stefan Wager Email: swager@stanford.edu Stanford Graduate School of Business Abstract We use the lens of weak signal asymptotics to study a class of sequentially randomized experiments, including those that arise in solving multi-armed bandit problems. In an experiment with n n time steps, we let the mean reward gaps between actions scale to the order 1 / n 1/\\sqrt{n} so as to preserve the difficulty of the learning task as n n grows. In this regime, we show that the sample paths of a class of sequentially randomized experiments—adapted to this scaling regime and with arm selection probabilities that vary continuously with state—converge weakly to a diffusion limit, given as the solution to a stochastic differential equation. The diffusion limit enables us to derive refined, instance-specific characterization of stochastic dynamics, and to obtain several insights on the regret and belief evolution of a number of sequential experiments including Thompson sampling (but not UCB, which does not satisfy our continuity assumption). We show that all sequential experiments whose randomization probabilities have a Lipschitz-continuous dependence on the observed data suffer from sub-optimal regret performance when the reward gaps are relatively large. Conversely, we find that a version of Thompson sampling with an asymptotically uninformative prior variance achieves near-optimal instance-specific regret scaling, including with large reward gaps, but these good regret properties come at the cost of highly unstable posterior beliefs. Keywords : Diffusion approximation, Multi-armed bandit, Thompson sampling. 1 Introduction Sequential experiments, † † Version: June 2023. An earlier draft of this paper was circulated under the title Diffusion Asymptotics for Sequential Experiments . Xu Kuang published under a different full name in earlier versions of this manuscript. Please use X. Kuang and S. Wager when citing this paper. We are grateful to the referees and editors at Management Science for their detailed comments. We are also grateful for valuable feedback and suggestions from Susan Athey, Lin Fan, Peter Glynn, David Goldberg, Keisuke Hirano, Michael Harrison, David Hirshberg, Max Kasy, Emilie Kaufmann, Tor Lattimore, Neil Walton, Jack Porter, Daniel Russo, David Simchi-Levi, as well as seminar participants at a number of venues. pioneered by Wald 1947 and Robbins 1952 , involve collecting data over time using a design that adapts to past experience. Relative to classical randomized trials, they can effectively concentrate power on studying the most promising alternatives and save on costs by helping us avoid repeatedly taking sub-optimal actions. Such experiments have now become widely adopted for automated decision making; for example, Hill et al. 2017 show how sequential experiments can be used to optimize the layout and content of a website, while Ferreira et al. 2018 discuss applications to pricing and online revenue management. Most existing research in sequential experimentation has focused on proving robust, worst-case guarantees. This has been motivated in a large part by automated decision making applications, such as website optimization and dynamic pricing, where it is common to run a large number of experiments in parallel while searching over a decision space. In these settings, it is important to use robust methods that perform reasonably well across all problem instances, so that they do not require frequent human oversight; see Bubeck and Cesa-Bianchi 2012 for a review and discussion. More recently, however, there has been growing interest in settings where an agent performs a small number of high-stake sequential experiments: Athey et al. 2021 discuss the use of sequential experiments for learning better public health interventions, Caria et al. 2020 deploy them for targeting job search assistance for refugees, and Kasy and Teytelboym 2020 consider whom to test for an infectious disease in a setting where testing capacity is limited. In these applications where humans are likely to be closely monitoring and fine-tuning a small number of adaptive experiments, the relatively crude performance guarantees provided by worst-case analysis fall short in guiding the decision maker towards choosing the best policies. This gives rise to the need to develop a more refined, instance-specific understanding of the stochastic behavior of adaptive experiments. In particular, it would be of interest to understand: • How to quantify the performance of an experiment in an instance-specific manner, that is, as a function of features of the environment in which the experiment is being conducted, such as gaps in mean rewards among different actions. • Beyond just the mean, how to characterize the distributional properties of key performance measures of an adaptive experiment. • How the behavior of adaptive experiments evolves over time, and what sample paths of actions taken by adaptive experiments look like. Available worst-case-focused formal results, however, do not provide sharp answers to these questions that would apply broadly to popular algorithms for adaptive experimentation, including the ones used in the studies cited above. A central difficulty here is simply that sequential experiments induce intricate dependence patterns in the data that make sharp finite-sample analysis of their behavior exceedingly delicate. In this paper, we introduce a new approach to studying sequential experiments based on \\color black weak signal asymptotics. Specifically, we consider the behavior of adaptive experiments in a sequence of problems where, as the number of time steps n n grows to infinity, the gap between the mean rewards of different actions decays as 1 / n 1/\\sqrt{n} . \\color black We use this asymptotic regime to study the sequentially randomized Markov experiments, a general class of sequential experiments that includes several popular algorithms such as Thompson sampling ( Thompson 1933 ; Russo et al. 2018 ) . \\color black Our first result is that, under regularity conditions including continuity of how the arm selection probabilities vary with certain state variables, the sample paths of appropriately scaled sequentially randomized Markov experiments converge weakly to a diffusion limit characterized as the solution to a stochastic differential equation (SDE). We then show that this diffusion limit enables us to derive practical, instance-specific and distributional insights about the behavior of sequential experiments. The 1 / n 1/\\sqrt{n} arm gap scaling considered here is an important one. It can be thought of as a “moderate data” regime of sequential experimentation, where the problem’s inherent “signal strength,” expressed in terms of the mean reward gaps between the various actions, is not sufficiently stronge relative to sample size so as to make identifying the optimal action asymptotically trivial, but strong enough so that using a well designed policy is crucial as sub-optimal policies can lead to large regret. In using diffusion approximation to obtain insights about the behavior of large, dynamic processes, our approach builds on a long tradition in operations research, especially in the context of queuing networks ( Iglehart and Whitt 1970 ; Harrison and Reiman 1981 ; Reiman 1984 ; Harrison 1988 ; Glynn 1990 ; Kelly and Laws 1993 ; Gamarnik and Zeevi 2006 ) . A key insight from this line of work is that, by focusing on a heavy-traffic limit where server utilization approaches full capacity and wait times diverge, the behavior of a queuing network can be well approximated by a Brownian limit where, as argued by Kelly and Laws 1993 , “important features of good control policies are displayed in sharpest relief.” Likewise, in our setting, we find that focusing on a scaling regime with small gaps in the mean rewards between the arms and long time horizons enables us to capture key aspects of sequential experiments in terms of a tractable diffusion approximation. 1.1 Overview of Main Results Throughout this paper, we focus on the following K K -armed setting, also known as a stochastic K K -armed bandit. There is a sequence of decision points i = 1 , 2 , … , n i=1,\\,2,\\,\\ldots,\\,n at which an agent chooses which action A i ∈ { 1 , … , K } A_{i}\\in\\left\\{1,\\,\\ldots,\\,K\\right\\} to take and then observes a reward Y i ∈ ℝ Y_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R} . Here, Y i Y_{i} is assumed to be drawn from a distribution P A i P_{A_{i}} , where the action A i A_{i} may depend on past observations, and is conditionally independent from all other aspects of the system given the realization of A i A_{i} . Our goal is to use diffusion approximation to characterize the behavior of K K -armed bandits in terms of a number of metrics, including regret R n = n ​ sup 1 ≤ k ≤ K { μ k } − 𝔼 ⁡ [ ∑ i = 1 n μ A i ] , μ k = 𝔼 P k ​ [ Y ] , R^{n}=n\\sup_{1\\leq k\\leq K}\\left\\{\\mu_{k}\\right\\}-\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mu_{A_{i}}\\right],\\ \\ \\ \\ \\mu_{k}=\\mathbb{E}_{P_{k}}\\left[Y\\right], (1.1) i.e., the shortfall in rewards incurred by the bandit algorithm relative to always taking the best action. Unless otherwise specified, we will use “arm gap” as a short-hand for the gap between the mean rewards associated with the two best arms. The first part of our paper, Section 2 , \\color black introduces a weak signal scaling regime for the reward distributions, where arm gaps become small as the sample size of the experiment increases. We then establish that for a broad class of sequential experiments, which we refer to as sequentially randomized Markov experiments, the system dynamics converge to a diffusion limit under this weak signal asymptotic regime. This result applies to a wide variety of adaptive experimentation rules arising in statistics, machine learning and behavioral economics \\color black including Thompson sampling (see Examples 1 – 4 given below). We also find that this diffusion limit can be characterized using random time-change applied to a Brownian motion, which enables much of our subsequent theoretical analysis. \\color black Throughout our analysis, we assume that the arm selection probabilities in the sequentially randomized Markov experiments we consider vary continuously with relevant state variables. We provide convergence results for both when the limiting sampling function is Lipschitz continuous, and when it is continuous but non-Lipschitz; \\color black however, our results do not apply to bandit algorithms with a discontinuous deterministic component, such as UCB or ε \\varepsilon -greedy. In the second part of the paper, we use our diffusion limit to obtain insights on the regret performance of a host of practical adaptive experiments. First, we turn our attention to the class of sequentially randomized experiments that admit an asymptotically Lipschitz limiting sampling function, which includes among other examples the popular Gaussian Thompson sampling algorithm with an asymptotically proper prior. These experiments tend to have favorable stability properties, making them less likely to overreact to the observed data and their dynamics more amenable to analysis. Surprisingly, we prove in Section 4.1 that any experiment with an asymptotically Lipschitz sampling function necessarily has poor regret performance: in the diffusion limit, the scaled regret of any such experiment remains bounded from below as the arm gap gets large, whereas the corresponding minimax risk goes to zero. Our result thus shows that the benefits of stability of Lipschitz sampling come at the expense of regret performance, as the algorithm cannot not abandon a bad action quickly enough in the early stages of the experiment. As a counterpoint to the first finding, our next result demonstrates that, in a two-armed setting, we can achieve nearly instance-optimal regret by adopting a non-Lipschitz sampling function. We examine an “undersmoothed” version of the Thompson sampling algorithm where the agent uses an asymptotically uninformative prior distribution. We prove in Section 4.2 that the regret of undersmoothed Thompson sampling not only vanishes as the arm gap grows, but does so at a rate that is nearly optimal in comparison to known instance-specific lower bounds ( Mannor and Tsitsiklis 2004 ) , a sharp contrast to the negative result on Lipschitz sampling described earlier. This is, to the best of our knowledge, the first result to highlight good instance-dependent regret properties for Thompson sampling in the moderate signal-to-noise regime (i.e., with 1 / n 1/\\sqrt{n} -scale arm gaps). Finally, we leverage the diffusion limit to obtain insights on distributional properties of sample paths in an adaptive experiment. We prove in Section 5 that, for a family of one-armed problems, experiments with a “sensitive” sampling function—ones that respond quickly to observed data—will be highly unstable, where the sampling probability of choosing the optimal arm can swing wildly between very large to the very small, and that this can happen even with very large arm gaps. This result covers undersmoothed Thompson sampling, in which case our analysis implies large swings in its posterior beliefs. This finding complements our regret analysis to render a more complete and nuanced picture, suggesting that an agent may have to face a fundamental trade-off between achieving optimal regret and maintaining a relatively stable sample path. On the methodological front, our work introduces new tools to the study of adaptive experiments. We show weak convergence to the diffusion limit for sequentially randomized Markov experiments using the martingale framework developed by Stroock and Varadhan 2007 , which hinges on showing that an appropriately scaled generator of the discrete-time Markov process converges to the infinitesimal generator of the diffusion process. Our analysis of the regret profiles of Thompson sampling under the weak signal scaling relies on novel proof arguments that heavily exploit properties of Brownian motion, such as the Law of Iterated Logarithm. 1.2 Related Work The multi-armed bandit problem is a popular framework for studying sequential experimentation; see Bubeck and Cesa-Bianchi 2012 for a broad discussion focused on bounds on the regret ( 1.1 ). An early landmark result in this setting is due to Lai and Robbins 1985 , who showed that given any fixed set of arms { P k } k = 1 K \\left\\{P_{k}\\right\\}_{k=1}^{K} , a well-designed sequential algorithm can achieve regret that scales logarithmically with the number n n of time steps considered, i.e., R n = 𝒪 P ​ ( log ⁡ ( n ) ) R^{n}=\\mathcal{O}_{P}(\\log(n)) . Meanwhile, given any fixed time horizon n n , it is possible to choose probability distributions { P k } k = 1 K \\left\\{P_{k}\\right\\}_{k=1}^{K} such that the expected regret 𝔼 ⁡ [ R n ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[R^{n}\\right] of any sequential algorithm is lower-bounded to order K ​ n \\sqrt{Kn} ( Auer et al. 2002 ) . It is worth-noting that the problem instance that achieves the K ​ n \\sqrt{Kn} regret lower bound in Auer et al. 2002 involves the same mean reward scaling as we use for our diffusion limit, suggesting that the \\color black weak signal scaling proposed here captures the behavior of the most challenging (and thus potentially most interesting) sub-family of learning tasks. Thompson sampling ( Thompson 1933 ; Russo et al. 2018 ) has gained considerable popularity in recent years thanks to its simplicity and impressive empirical performance ( Chapelle and Li 2011 ) . Regret bounds for Thompson sampling have been established in the frequentist ( Agrawal and Goyal 2017 ; Kaufmann et al. 2012 ) and Bayesian ( Bubeck and Liu 2014 ; Lattimore and Szepesvári 2019 ; Russo and Van Roy 2016 ) settings; the setup here belongs to the first category. None of the existing instance-dependent regret bounds, however, appear to have sufficient precision to yield meaningful characterization in our regime. For example, the instance-dependent upper bound in Agrawal and Goyal 2017 contains a constant to the order of 1 / Δ 4 1/\\Delta^{4} , where Δ \\Delta is the gap in mean reward between optimal and sub-optimal arms. This would thus lead to a trivial bound of 𝒪 ⁡ ( n 2 ) \\mathcal{O}(n^{2}) in our regime, where mean rewards scale as 1 / n 1/\\sqrt{n} . Furthermore, many of the existing bounds also require delicate assumptions on the reward distributions (e.g., bounded support). In contrast, the \\color black weak signal asymptotics adopted in this paper are universal in the sense that they automatically allow us to obtain approximations for a much wider range of reward distributions, requiring only a bounded fourth moment. Our choice of the weak signal scaling and the ensuing diffusion limit are motivated by insights from both queueing theory and statistics. The scaling plays a prominent role in heavy-traffic diffusion approximation in queueing networks ( Gamarnik and Zeevi 2006 ; Harrison and Reiman 1981 ; Reiman 1984 ) ; in particular, Harrison 1988 uses diffusion approximation to study a dynamic control problem. Here, one considers a sequence of queueing systems in which the excessive service capacity, defined as the difference between arrival rate and service capacity, decays as 1 / T 1/\\sqrt{T} , where T T is the time horizon. Under this asymptotic regime, it is shown that suitably scaled queue-length and workload processes converge to reflected Brownian motion. Like in our problem, the diffusion regime here is helpful because it captures the most challenging problem instances, where the system is at once stable and exhibiting non-trivial performance variability. See also Glynn 1990 for an excellent survey for the use of diffusion approximation in operations research. The \\color black weak signal asymptotics are further inspired by a recurring insight from statistics that, in order for asymptotic analysis to yield a normal limit that can be used for finite-sample insight, we need to appropriately down-scale the arm gap as the sample size gets large. One concrete example of this phenomenon arises when we seek to learn optimal decision rules from (quasi-)experimental data. Here, in general, optimal behavior involves regret that decays as 1 / n 1/\\sqrt{n} with the sample size ( Athey and Wager 2021 ; Hirano and Porter 2009 ; Kitagawa and Tetenov 2018 ) ; however, this worst-case regret is only achieved if we let effect sizes decay as 1 / n 1/\\sqrt{n} . For any fixed sampling design, it’s possible to achieve faster than 1 / n 1/\\sqrt{n} rates asymptotically ( Luedtke and Chambaz 2020 ) . Our work is broadly related to work on adaptive learning and experimentation in the statistics and operations research literatures. The seminal work of Chernoff 1959 studies how to adaptively choose from a menu of available experiments for hypothesis testing, and the main result consists of a dynamic experiment that achieves the asymptotic optimal rate of convergence to the true hypothesis as the number of samples grows. A number of papers expand upon this framework, including Naghshvar and Javidi 2013 who drive a range of refinements and extensions on the minimax bounds, Russo 2020 who adapts the algorithm for best-arm identification, and Massoulié and Xu 2018 who consider simultaneous experiments run in a resource-limited setting. The asymptotic regime considered in this line of work is different from ours: They focus on the large-deviation regime where the problem instance stays fixed while the number of samples grows to infinity, whereas we consider the \\color black weak signal regime where the arm gap decreases along with the sample size. Another distinction is that this literature tends to treat what has become known as the post-experiment simple regret, where the agent’s cost is determined by any error incurred in declaring the incorrect hypothesis after the completion of the experiment, rather than the in-experiment regret accrued due to using sub-optimal arms during the experiment. There are some existing results where diffusion-based analysis has been used for action selection and optimal stopping in sequential experiments. Some of the earlier studies assume randomization is fixed over the horizon ( Siegmund 1985 ) . In contrast, in our multi-armed bandit setting the probabilities in the randomization depend on the history which creates a qualitatively different limit object. Chick and Gans 2009 consider optimal stopping in ranking and selection where the agent runs a sequence of experiments to determine which action carries the maximum expected payoff. They then apply diffusion approximation to the resulting Bellman equation and derive tractable structural results for simple cases. Araman and Caldentey 2021 study an adaptive optimal stopping problem with a binary hypothesis and use the diffusion limit to derive insights on how and when the agent should act, as well as the optimal choice of experiments. Wang and Zenios 2020 consider a model similar to Araman and Caldentey 2021 in the context of adaptive clinical trials, with the additional feature that the experiments can have different costs. Harrison and Sunar 2015 use diffusion approximation to study an optimal stopping problem with a binary hypothesis when a firm decides between making an investment versus taking further learning actions to gather more information. The approaches taken in these papers are however different from ours. First, they tend to focus on discrete (and often binary) hypothesis testing whereas we study multi-armed experiments with continuously-valued mean rewards. Second, the optimal stopping formulation is centered around the expected cost incurred by a one-shot, post-experiment action choice, in contrast to the in-experiment regret analyzed here. Third, most of these papers focus on solving exactly, or approximately, the Bayesian Bellman-optimal decision rule based on dynamic programming, whereas we are more interested in characterizing the dynamics of a range of heuristic policies, such as Thompson sampling, which are used in practice when the Bayesian priors of the model parameters are unknown or the agent operates under a frequentist, instance-specific performance mandate. Lastly, in this literature, diffusion approximation is sometimes evoked directly or introduced as a modeling assumption without a proof of convergence ( Chick and Gans 2009 ; Harrison and Sunar 2015 , e.g.,) . Finally, after we posted a first version of this paper, a number of authors have disseminated work that intersects with our main result. In the special case of Thompson sampling, Fan and Glynn 2021 derive the diffusion limit given in Theorems 1 and 3 using a different proof technique from us: As discussed further in Appendix A , we derive the diffusion limit by studying the asymptotic behavior of the Markov chain associated with our sequential experiments following Stroock and Varadhan 2007 , whereas Fan and Glynn 2021 develop a direct argument based on the continuous mapping theorem. Meanwhile, Hirano and Porter 2021 extend the “limits of experiments” analysis pioneered by Le Cam 1972 to discrete-time, batched sequential experiments. The limits of experiments paradigm also involves a 1 / n 1/\\sqrt{n} -scale local parametrization, and their results can be applied to sequentially randomized Markov experiments whose the randomization probabilities only change at a finite set of pre-specified times. Both Fan and Glynn 2021 and Hirano and Porter 2021 are based on research developed independently from ours. 2 Asymptotics for K-Armed Sequential Experiments As discussed above, the first goal of this paper is to establish a diffusion limit for a class of sequential experiments. To this end, we first introduce a broad class of sequential experimentation schemes in Section 2.1 , which we refer to as sequentially randomized Markov experiments. We describe a \\color black weak signal scaling for sequential experiments in Section 2.2 ; then, in Section 3 , we establish conditions under which—in this limit—sample paths of sequentially randomized Markov experiments converge weakly to the solution of a stochastic differential equation. Throughout our analysis, we work within the following K K -armed model. This model captures a number of interesting problems and is widely used in the literature ( Bubeck and Cesa-Bianchi 2012 ; Lai and Robbins 1985 , e.g.,) . However, we note that it does rule out some phenomena that may arise in applications; for example, we do not allow for distribution shift in the reward distribution of a given arm over time, and we do not allow for long-term consequences of actions, i.e., an action taken in period i i cannot affect arm-specific period- i ′ i^{\\prime} reward distributions for any i ′ &gt; i i^{\\prime}&gt;i . Extending our asymptotic analysis to allow for distribution shift or long-term effects would be of considerable interest, but we do not pursue this line of investigation in the present paper. Definition 1 (Stochastic K K -Armed Bandit) . A stochastic K K -armed bandit is characterized by time horizon n n and a set of K K reward distributions P k P_{k} for k = 1 , … , K k=1,\\,\\ldots,\\,K . At each decision points i = 1 , 2 , … , n i=1,\\,2,\\,\\ldots,\\,n , an agent chooses which action A i ∈ { 1 , … , K } A_{i}\\in\\left\\{1,\\,\\ldots,\\,K\\right\\} to take and then observes a reward Y i ∈ ℝ Y_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R} . The action A i A_{i} is a random variable that is measurable with respect to the observed history { A i ′ , Y i ′ } i ′ = 1 i − 1 \\left\\{A_{i^{\\prime}},\\,Y_{i^{\\prime}}\\right\\}_{i^{\\prime}=1}^{i-1} . Then, conditionally on the chosen action A i A_{i} , the reward Y i Y_{i} is drawn from the distribution P A i P_{A_{i}} , independently from the observed history. 2.1 Sequentially Randomized Markov Experiments In the interest of generality, we state our main results in the context of sequentially randomized experiments whose sampling probabilities depend on past observations only through the state variables Q k , i = ∑ j = 1 i 𝟏 ( { A j = k } ) , S k , i = ∑ j = 1 i 𝟏 ( { A j = k } ) Y j , Q_{k,i}=\\sum_{j=1}^{i}\\mathbf{1}\\left(\\left\\{A_{j}=k\\right\\}\\right),\\quad S_{k,i}=\\sum_{j=1}^{i}\\mathbf{1}\\left(\\left\\{A_{j}=k\\right\\}\\right)Y_{j}, (2.1) where Q k , i Q_{k,i} counts the cumulative number of times arm k k has been chosen by the time we collect the i i -th sample, and S k , i S_{k,i} measures its cumulative reward. When useful, we use the convention Q k , 0 = S k , 0 = 0 Q_{k,0}=S_{k,0}=0 . Working with this class of algorithms, which we refer to as sequentially randomized Markov experiments, enables us to state results that cover many popular ways of running sequential experiments without needing to derive specialized analyses for each of them. Definition 2 (Sequentially Randomized Markov Experiment) . A K K -armed sequentially randomized Markov experiment chooses the i i -th action A i A_{i} by taking a draw from a distribution A i | { A 1 , Y 1 , … , A i − 1 , Y i − 1 } ∼ Multinomial ⁡ ( π i ) , A_{i}\\,\\big|\\,\\left\\{A_{1},\\,Y_{1},\\,\\ldots,\\,A_{i-1},\\,Y_{i-1}\\right\\}\\sim\\operatorname{Multinomial}(\\pi_{i}), (2.2) where the sampling probabilities are computed using a measurable sampling function ψ \\psi , ψ : ℝ K × ℝ K → 𝚫 K , π i = ψ ⁡ ( Q i − 1 , S i − 1 ) , \\psi:\\mathbb{R}^{K}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{K}\\rightarrow\\mathbf{\\Delta}^{K},\\ \\ \\