bilities π i , k = ( S k , i ∨ α ) / ∑ l = 1 K ( S l , i ∨ α ) , \\pi_{i,k}=(S_{k,i}\\vee\\alpha)\\bigg/\\sum_{l=1}^{K}(S_{l,i}\\vee\\alpha), (2.5) where α ≥ 0 \\alpha\\geq 0 is a tuning parameter governing the amount of baseline exploration. More generally, the agent may ascribe to arm k k a weight f ⁡ ( S k , i ) f(S_{k,i}) , where f f is a non-negative potential function, and sample actions with probabilities proportional to the weights. The decision rule in ( 2.5 ) only depends on S S and thus satisfies ( 2.3 ). Example 3 . Thompson sampling is a popular Bayesian heuristic for running sequential experiments ( Thompson 1933 ) . In Thompson sampling an agent starts with a prior belief distribution G 0 G_{0} on the reward distributions { P k } k = 1 K \\left\\{P_{k}\\right\\}_{k=1}^{K} . Then, at each step i i , the agent samples a reward distribution from their posterior belief G i − 1 G_{i-1} , pulls the optimal arm that has the highest mean according to this sampled distribution, and uses the resulting realized reward to update the posterior G i G_{i} using Bayes’ rule. The motivation behind Thompson sampling is that it quickly converges to pulling the best arm, and thus achieves low regret ( Agrawal and Goyal 2017 ; Chapelle and Li 2011 ) . Thompson sampling does not always satisfy Definition 2 . However, widely used modeling choices involving exponential families for the { P k } k = 1 K \\left\\{P_{k}\\right\\}_{k=1}^{K} and conjugate priors for G 0 G_{0} result in these posterior probabilities ρ k , i \\rho_{k,i} satisfying the sufficiency condition ( 2.3 ) ( Russo et al. 2018 ) , in which case Thompson sampling yields a sequentially randomized Markov experiment in the sense of Definition 2 . In this paper, we will focus on one of the most widely used variants of this design, Gaussian Thompson sampling. In this setting the agent models P k n P_{k}^{n} as a Gaussian distribution with (unknown) mean μ k n \\mu_{k}^{n} and (known) variance σ k 2 \\sigma_{k}^{2} 