p of the baseline profit if outcome j j happens. Following e.g. [ GR07 ] , we give a geometric intuition for Proposition 3.1 to help explain why the properness of s s corresponds to the convexity of G G ; this intuition will be helpful for understanding Bregman divergence (below) and the proofs in Section 4 . Consider Figure 2 , which depicts some G G in the n = 2 n=2 outcome case, with the x x -axis corresponding to the probability of Outcome 1 (see Remark 3.5 for formal details). Suppose that the expert believes that the probability Outcome 1 is 0.7 0.7 . If the expert reports p = 0.7 p=0.7 , then their reward in the cases of Outcome 1 and Outcome 2 are the y y -values of the rightmost and leftmost points on the red line, respectively. Thus, in expectation, the expert’s reward is the y y -value of the red point. If instead the expert lies and reports p = 0.4 p=0.4 (say), then the y y -values of the rightmost and leftmost points on the blue line represent the expert’s rewards in the cases of Outcome 1 and Outcome 2, respectively. In this case, since Outcome 1 is still 70% likely, the expert’s expected reward is the y y -value of the blue point. Because G G is strictly convex , the blue point is strictly below the red point; that is, the expert is strictly better off reporting p = 0.7 p=0.7 . This argument holds in full generality: for any strictly convex function G G in any number of dimensions. Figure 2: A convex function G G , with tangent lines drawn at x = 0.7 x=0.7 and x = 0.4 x=0.4 . If an expert believes that the probability of an event is 0.7 0.7 , their expected score if they report p = 0.7 p=0.7 is the y y -value of the red point; if they instead report p = 0.4 p=0.4 , their expected score is the y y -value of the blue point. Because G G is convex, the red point is guaranteed to be above the blue point, so the expert is incentivized to be truthful. We will find Bregman divergence to be a useful concept for some of our proofs. Definition 3.2 (Bregman divergence) . Given a differentiable, strictly convex function G : S → ℝ G:S\\to\\mathbb{R} with gradient 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} , where S S is a convex subset of ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} , and given 𝐩 , 𝐪 ∈ S \\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in S , the Bregman divergence between 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} and 𝐪 \\mathbf{q} with respect to G G is D G ( 𝐩 ∥ 𝐪 ) := G ( 𝐩 ) − G ( 𝐪 ) − ⟨ 𝐠 ( 𝐪 ) , 𝐩 − 𝐪 ⟩ . D_{G}(\\mathbf{p}\\parallel\\mathbf{q}):=G(\\mathbf{p})-G(\\mathbf{q})-\\langle\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{q}),\\mathbf{p}-\\mathbf{q}\\rangle. (Note that Bregman divergence is not symmetric.) A geometric interpretation of D G ( 𝐩 ∥ 𝐪 ) D_{G}(\\mathbf{p}\\parallel\\mathbf{q}) is: if you draw the tangent plane to G G at 𝐪 \\mathbf{q} , how far below G ⁡ ( 𝐩 ) G(\\mathbf{p}) the value of that plane will be at 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} . For example, the distance between the red and blue points in Figure 2 is the Bregman divergence between the expert’s belief ( 0.7 , 0.3 ) (0.7,0.3) and their report ( 0.4 , 0.6 ) (0.4,0.6) . This gives the following interpretation of Bregman divergence in the context of proper scoring rules: Remark 3.3 . If G G is the expected reward function of a proper scoring rule, then D G ( 𝐩 ∥ 𝐪 ) D_{G}(\\mathbf{p}\\parallel\\mathbf{q}) is the expected reward lost by reporting 𝐪 \\mathbf{q} when your belief is 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} . Put otherwise, D G ( 𝐩 ∥ 𝐪 ) D_{G}(\\mathbf{p}\\parallel\\mathbf{q}) measures the “wrongness” of the report 𝐪 \\mathbf{q} relative to a correct answer of 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} . Proposition 3.4 (Well-known facts about Bregman divergence) . • D G ( 𝐩 ∥ 𝐪 ) ≥ 0 D_{G}(\\mathbf{p}\\parallel\\mathbf{q})\\geq 0 , with equality only when 𝐩 = 𝐪 \\mathbf{p}=\\mathbf{q} . • For any 𝐪 \\mathbf{q} , D G ( 𝐱 ∥ 𝐪 ) D_{G}(\\mathbf{x}\\parallel\\mathbf{q}) is a strictly convex function of 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} . Finally, we make a note about interpreting the n = 2 n=2 outcome case in one dimension. Remark 3.5 . Because Δ n \\Delta^{n} is ( n − 1 ) (n-1) -dimensional, we can think of the case of n = 2 n=2 outcomes 