 (convex exposure) . A proper scoring rule s s with forecast domain 𝒟 \\mathcal{D} has convex exposure if the range of its exposure function 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} on 𝒟 \\mathcal{D} is a convex set. The key fact about proper scoring rules with convex exposure is that for all 𝐩 1 , … , 𝐩 m \\mathbf{p}_{1},\\dots,\\mathbf{p}_{m} and w 1 , … , w m w_{1},\\dots,w_{m} , ∑ i w i ​ 𝐠 ​ ( 𝐩 i ) = 𝐠 ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) \\sum_{i}w_{i}\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}_{i})=\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{x}) for some 𝐱 ∈ 𝒟 \\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathcal{D} . This means that ∑ i w i ​ 𝐠 ​ ( 𝐩 i ) \\sum_{i}w_{i}\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}_{i}) is a subgradient of G G at 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} , so 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} is the weighted QA pool of the p i p_{i} ’s. In other words, we may write 𝐠 ⁡ ( 𝐩 ∗ ) = ∑ i w i ​ 𝐠 ​ ( 𝐩 i ) , \\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}^{*})=\\sum_{i}w_{i}\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}_{i}), (4) where 𝐩 ∗ \\mathbf{p}^{*} is the weighted QA pool of the given forecasts. The convex exposure property thus allows us to write down relations between exposures of forecasts that would otherwise not necessarily be true. The quadratic and logarithmic scoring rules, as well as all proper scoring rules for binary outcomes, have convex exposure. In Appendix D we explore in more depth the question of which commonly used proper scoring rules have convex exposure property. Remark 3.12 . Because the domain of G G is a subset of Δ n \\Delta^{n} (and thus lies in a plane that is orthogonal to the all-ones vector 𝟏 n \\mathbf{1}_{n} ), it makes the most sense to think of its gradient function 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} as taking on values in ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} modulo translation by the all-ones vector 𝟏 n \\mathbf{1}_{n} ; we will denote this space by ℝ n / T ⁡ ( 𝟏 n ) \\mathbb{R}^{n}/T(\\mathbf{1}_{n}) . Sometimes we find it convenient to treat G G as a function of n n variables rather than n − 1 n-1 variables out of convenience, thus artificially extending the domain of G G outside of the plane containing Δ n \\Delta^{n} . The component of the gradient of G G that is 