 j ∗ &gt; 0 p^{*}_{j}&gt;0 . This quantity is non-negative, and is positive unless all reports 𝐩 i \\mathbf{p}_{i} with positive weights are equal. One interpretation for this theorem statement is as follows. Consider an agent who is tasked with submitting a forecast, and who will be paid according to s s . The agent decides to sub-contract m m experts to get their opinions, paying expert i i the amount w i ​ s ​ ( 𝐩 i , j ) w_{i}s(\\mathbf{p}_{i};j) if the expert reports 𝐩 i \\mathbf{p}_{i} and outcome j j happens. (Perhaps experts whom the agent trusts more have higher w i w_{i} ’s.) Finally, the agent reports some (any) forecast 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} . Then u ⁡ ( 𝐩 , j ) u(\\mathbf{p};j) is precisely the agent’s profit (utility). The quantity min j ⁡ u ⁡ ( 𝐩 , j ) \\min_{j}u(\\mathbf{p};j) is the agent’s minimum possible profit over all outcomes. It is natural to ask which report 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} maximizes this quantity. Theorem 4.1 states that this maximum is achieved by the QA pool of the experts’ forecasts with respect to s s , and that this is the unique maximizer. A possible geometric intuition to keep in mind for the proof (below): for each expert i i , draw the plane tangent to G G at 𝐩 i \\mathbf{p}_{i} . For any j j , the value of this plane at δ j \\delta_{j} is s ⁡ ( 𝐩 i , j ) s(\\mathbf{p}_{i};j) . Now take the weighted average of all m m planes; this is a new plane whose intersection with any δ j \\delta_{j} is the total reward received by the experts if j j happens. Since G G is convex, this plane lies below G G . To figure out which point maximizes the agent’s guaranteed profit, push the plane upward until it hits G G . It will hit G G at 𝐩 ∗ \\mathbf{p}^{*} and the agent’s worst-case profit will be the vertical distance that the plane was pushed. Proof of Theorem 4.1 . By Equation 3 , computing the QA pool amounts to finding the minimizer 𝐩 ∗ \\mathbf{p}^{*} of the function G ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) − ⟨ 𝐱 , ∑ i = 1 m w i ​ 𝐠 ​ ( 𝐩 i ) ⟩ G(\\mathbf{x})-\\langle\\mathbf{x},\\