\to I satisfying the following natural properties: 17 17 17 Nagumo also provided a characterization, though with slightly different properties. (1) M ⁡ ( x 1 , … , x m ) M(x_{1},\\dots,x_{m}) is continuous and strictly increasing in each variable. (2) M M is symmetric in its arguments. (3) M ⁡ ( x , x , … , x ) = x M(x,x,\\dots,x)=x . (4) M ⁡ ( x 1 , … , x k , x k + 1 , … , x m ) M(x_{1},\\dots,x_{k},x_{k+1},\\dots,x_{m}) = M ⁡ ( y , … , y , x k + 1 , … , x m ) M(y,\\dots,y,x_{k+1},\\dots,x_{m}) , where y := M ⁡ ( x 1 , … , x k ) y:=M(x_{1},\\dots,x_{k}) appears k k times on the right-hand side. Informally, a subset of arguments to the mean function can be replaced with their mean. The four properties listed above can be viewed as an axiomatization of quasi-arithmetic means. Our notion of quasi-arithmetic pooling is exactly that of a quasi-arithmetic mean, except that it is more general in two ways. First, it allows for weights to accompany the arguments to the mean. Second, we are considering quasi-arithmetic means with respect to vector-valued functions 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} . In the n = 2 n=2 outcome case, 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} can be considered a scalar-valued function since it is defined on a one-dimensional space (see Remark 3.5 for details); but in general we cannot treat 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} as scalar-valued. 18 18 18 Another difference is that in the n = 2 n=2 case, we are restricting 𝒟 \\mathcal{D} to be an interval from 0 0 to 1 1 , though this is not a fundamental difference for the purposes of this section. Our goal is to extend the above axiomatization of quasi-arithmetic means in these two ways: first (in Section 6.1 ) to include weights as arguments, and second (in Section 6.2 ) to general n n (while still allowing arbitrary weights). 6.1 Generalizing to include weights as arguments The objects that we will be studying in this section are ones of the form ( p , w ) (p,w) , where w ≥ 0 w\\geq 0 and p ∈ 𝒟 p\\in\\mathcal{D} . In this subsection, 𝒟 \\mathcal{D} is a two-outcom