 that the axioms constitute a characterization of the class of QA pooling operators (Theorem C.7 ). On a high level, the proof is not dissimilar to that of Theorem 6.6 , though the details are fairly different and more technical. In conclusion, in Definition 6.5 we made a list of natural properties that a pooling operator may satisfy. Theorem 6.6 shows that the pooling operators satisfying these properties are exactly the QA pooling operators. In Appendix C.1 , we generalize this theorem to higher dimensions, thus fully axiomatizing QA pooling. This result gives us an additional important reason to believe that QA pooling with respect to a proper scoring rule is a fundamental notion. 7 Conclusions and future work While in this work we have focused on scoring rules for probability distributions over outcomes, scoring rules can be used to elicit various other properties, such as the expectation or the median of a random variable [ Sav71 ] [ GR07 ] [ LPS08 ] . The topic of property elicitation studies such scoring rules. The representation of a proper scoring rule in terms of its expected reward function ( [ GR07 , Theorem 2] , our Proposition 3.1 ) generalizes to arbitrary linear properties [ FK15 , Theorem 11] . That is, consider a random variable (or n n -tuple of random variables) X X and a convex function G G whose domain is (a superset of) the possible values of X X . If 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} is an expert’s forecast for 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[X\\right] and 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} is the realized outcome, then the scoring rule s ⁡ ( 𝐩 , 𝐱 ) := G ⁡ ( 𝐩 ) + ⟨ 𝐠 ⁡ ( 𝐩 ) , 𝐱 − 𝐩 ⟩ s(\\mathbf{p};\\mathbf{x}):=G(\\mathbf{p})+\\langle\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}),\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{p}\\rangle is proper. 24 24 24 In the setting of this paper, X X is the vector of random variables, where the j j -th variable is 1 1 if outcome j j happens and 0 0 otherwise; and 𝐱 = δ j \\mathbf{x}=\\delta_{j} where j j is the outcome that happens. Modulo a fairly straightforward generalization, all proper scoring rules for 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[X\\right] take this form. Our definition of QA pooling extends verbatim to the setting of eliciting linear properties. In this more general setting, for any proper scoring rule that has convex exposure (see Definition 3.11 ), it remains the case 𝐠 ⁡ ( 𝐩 ∗ ) = ∑ i w i ​ 𝐠 ​ ( 𝐩 i ) \\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}^{*})=\\sum_{i}w_{i}\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}_{i}) ; as a consequence, Theorems 4.1 , 5.1 , and 5.3 generalize. There are several other interesting directions for generalization and future work: • As mentioned in Section 2 , QA pooling can be interpreted in terms of cost function markets: if 𝐪 i \\mathbf{q}_{i} denotes the quantity of shares that each expert would buy in order to bring a market into line with the experts’ probabilities, then the QA pool is the probability corresponding to the quantity vector 𝐪 ∗ := ∑ i w i ​ 𝐪 i \\mathbf{q}^{*}:=\\sum_{i}w_{i}\\mathbf{q}_{i} . This follows from [ ACV13 , Eq. 22] ; the underlying reason is the convex duality between an expected reward function G G and the corresponding cost function C C . Exploring this connection further may yield insights into forecast aggregation through market mechanisms. • While idempotence is a fairly natural axiom, Bayesian approaches to aggregation often violate this axiom: for example, an aggregator who receives two independent estimates with the same probability should act differently than an aggregator who only received one of the estimates. It would be interesting to explore whether our results and techniques are applicable to notions of pooling that do not satisfy idempotence, or to other (perhaps Bayesian) settings. • As we discussed in Section 2 , there is a fair amount of work on aggregating forecasts with prediction markets — often ones that are based on proper scoring rules. Is there a natural trading-based interpretation of QA pooling? • Although our proof of Theorem 5.1 (that the QA pool of forecasts is concave in the experts’ weights) relies on the convex exposure property, we have not ruled out the possibility that the result holds even without this assumption. Is this the case? Even if not, are no-regret algorithms for learning weights still possible? • Our no-regret algorithm for learning weights relies on s s being bounded, because this allows us to place a concrete upper bound on ‖ ∇ L t ​ ( ⋅ ) ‖ 2 \\left\\lVert\\nabla L^{t}(\\cdot)\\right\\rVert_{2} . If s s is unbounded, in a fully adversarial setting it is impossible to achieve a no-regret guarantee. On the other hand, [ NR22 ] show that if experts are assumed to be calibrated, then learning the optimal weights for logarithmic pooling is possible when using the log scoring rule. Does this result generalize to learning optimal weights for QA pooling with respect to other unbounded proper scoring rules? • We have presented a list of axioms characterizing QA pooling operators. Is there an alternative axiomatization that uses equally natural but fewer axioms? • Do our results extend to probability distributions over infinitely many outcomes (e.g. for experts who wish to forecast a real number)? Acknowledgments This work was partially supported by the NSF under CCF-1813188 and DGE-2036197 and by the ARO under W911NF1910294. References [Abb09] Ali. Abbas “A Kullback-Leibler View of Linear and Log-Linear Pools” In Decision Analysis 6.1 , 2009, pp. 25–37 DOI: 10.1287/deca.1080.0133 [ABS18] Itai Arieli, Yakov Babichenko and Rann Smorodinsky “Robust forecast aggregation” In Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 115.52 , 2018, pp. E12135–E12143 DOI: 10.1073/pnas.1813934115 [ACR12] D Allard, A Comunian and Philippe Renard “Probability aggregation methods in geoscience” In Mathematical Geosciences 44.5 Springer, 2012, pp. 545–581 [ACV13] Jacob. Abernethy, Yiling Chen and Jennifer Vaughan “Efficient Market Making via Convex Optimization, and a Connection to Online Learning” In ACM Trans. Economics and Comput. 1.2 , 2013, pp. 12:1–12:39 DOI: 10