strength of evidence that E k E_{k} gives in favor of X X . We call this the “Bayesian evidence” in favor of X X given by E k E_{k} . The total Bayesian evidence that the expert has in favor of X X (i.e. the sum of these values over all E k E_{k} ) is the expert’s log odds of X X (i.e. log ⁡ Pr ​ [ X ] Pr ​ [ ¬ X ] \\log\\frac{\\text{Pr}\\left[X\\right]}{\\text{Pr}\\left[\\neg X\\right]} ). Logarithmic pooling takes the average of experts’ log odds of X X . As we have shown, if the experts are Bayesian, this amounts to taking the average of all experts’ amounts of Bayesian evidence in favor of X X . Appendix B Details omitted from Section 5 Proof of Theorem 5.1 . Let 𝐯 \\mathbf{v} and 𝐰 \\mathbf{w} be two weight vectors. We wish to show that for any c ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] c\\in[0,1] , we have WS j ​ ( c ​ 𝐯 + ( 1 − c ) ​ 𝐰 ) − c ​ WS j ​ ( 𝐯 ) − ( 1 − c ) ​ WS j ​ ( 𝐰 ) ≥ 0 . \\text{WS}_{j}(c\\mathbf{v}+(1-c)\\mathbf{w})-c\\text{WS}_{j}(\\mathbf{v})-(1-c)\\text{WS}_{j}(\\mathbf{w})\\geq 0. Recall the notation 𝐩 𝐰 ∗ \\mathbf{p}^{*}_{\\mathbf{w}} from Definition 3.6 . Note that 𝐠 ⁡ ( 𝐩 c ​ 𝐯 + ( 1 − c ) ​ 𝐰 ∗ ) = ∑ i = 1 m ( c ​ v i + ( 1 − c ) ​ w i ) ​ 𝐠 ​ ( 𝐩 i ) = c ​ 𝐠 ​ ( 𝐩 𝐯 ∗ ) + ( 1 − c ) ​ 𝐠 ​ ( 𝐩 𝐰 ∗ ) . \\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}^{*}_{c\\mathbf{v}+(1-c)\\mathbf{w}})=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(cv_{i}+(1-c)w_{i})\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}_{i})=c\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}^{*}_{\\mathbf{v}})+(1-c)\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p}^{*}_{\\mathbf{w}}). (5) We have WS j ​ ( c ​ 𝐯 + ( 1 − c ) ​ 𝐰 ) − c ​ WS j ​ ( 𝐯 ) − ( 1 − c ) ​ WS j ​ ( 𝐰 ) \\displaystyle\\quad\\text{WS}_{j}(c\\mathbf{v}+(1-c)\\mathbf{w})-c\\text{WS}_{j}(\\mathbf{v})-(1-c)\\text{WS}_{j}(\\mathbf{w}) = s ⁡ ( 𝐩 c ​ 𝐯 + ( 1 − c ) ​ 𝐰 ∗ , j ) − c ​ s ​ ( 𝐩 𝐯 ∗ , j ) − ( 1 − c ) ​ s ​ ( 𝐩 𝐰 ∗ , j ) \\displaystyle=s(\\mathbf{p}^{*}_{c\\mathbf{v}+(1-c)\\mathbf{w}};j)-cs(\\mathbf{p}^{*}_{\\mathbf{v}};j)-(1-c)s(\\mathbf{p}^{*}_{\\mathbf{w}};j) = G ⁡ ( 𝐩 c ​ 𝐯 + ( 1 − c ) ​ 𝐰 ∗ ) + ⟨ 𝐠 ⁡ ( 𝐩 c ​ 𝐯 + ( 1 − c ) ​ 𝐰 ∗ ) , δ j − 𝐩 c ​ 𝐯 + ( 1 − c 