eudo-barrier function if this condition is satisfied. Then s s has convex exposure. This is a statement of convex analysis — namely that if ‖ 𝐠 ‖ 2 \\left\\lVert\\mathbf{g}\\right\\rVert_{2} approaches ∞ \\infty on the boundary of a convex set, then the range of 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} is convex (assuming 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} is the gradient of a differentiable convex function). We refer the reader to [ Roc70a , Theorem 26.5] for the proof. In non-pathological cases, the basic intuition is that every 𝐯 ∈ { 𝐱 : ∑ i x i = 0 } \\mathbf{v}\\in\\{\\mathbf{x}:\\sum_{i}x_{i}=0\\} is the gradient of G G at some point. In these cases, ∇ G ​ ( 𝐱 ) = 𝐯 \\nabla G(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{v} where 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} minimizes G ⁡ ( 𝐯 ) − 𝐯 ⋅ 𝐱 G(\\mathbf{v})-\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{x} ; the lim k → ∞ ‖ g ⁡ ( 𝐱 k ) ‖ 2 = ∞ \\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\left\\lVert g(\\mathbf{x}_{k})\\right\\rVert_{2}=\\infty condition means that this minimum does not occur on the boundary of Δ n \\Delta^{n} . Corollary D.3 . The following scoring rules have convex exposure: • The logarithmic scoring rule. • The scoring rule given by G ( 𝐩 ) = − ∑ j p j γ G(\\mathbf{p})=-\\sum_{j}p_{j}^{\\gamma} for γ ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) \\gamma\\in(0,1) . • The scoring rule given by G ( 𝐩 ) = − ∑ j ln p j G(\\mathbf{p})=-\\sum_{j}\\ln p_{j} , which can be thought of as the limit of the G G in the previous bullet point as γ → 0 \\gamma\\to 0 . 34 34 34 This is a natural way to think of this scoring rule because ∇ G ​ ( 𝐩 ) = − ( p 1 − 1 , … , p n − 1 ) \\nabla G(\\mathbf{p})=-(p_{1}^{-1},\\dots,p_{n}^{-1}) . • The scoring rule h ​ s hs given by G h ​ s ( 𝐩 ) = − ∏ j p j 1 / n G_{hs}(\\mathbf{p})=-\\prod_{j}p_{j}^{1/n} . The h ​ s hs scoring rule The last of these scoring rules is a generalization of the scoring rule h ​ s ​ ( q ) = 1 − 1 − q q hs(q)=1-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-q}{q}} used in [ BB20 ] as part of proving their minimax theorem for randomized algorithms. 35 35 35 Here we are using the shorthand notation for the n = 2 n=2 outcome case discussed in Remark 3.5 of the main article. The authors used this scoring rule as a key ingredient in their minimax theorem for randomized algorithms. The key property of the scoring rule was a result about its amplification [ BB20 , Lemma 3.10] . The authors define a forecasting algorithm to be a generalization of a randomized algorithm that outputs an estimated probability that an output should be accepted. Then, roughly speaking, the authors show that given a forecasting algorithm R R , it is possible to create a forecasting algorithm R ′ R^{\\prime} that has a much larger expected score from the scoring rule h ​ s hs by combining running R R a small number of times and combining the outputs. This is an important new result in theoretical computer science and suggests that h ​ s hs deserves more attention. Since additive and multiplicative constants are irrelevant, we may treat h ​ s ​ ( q ) = − 1 2 ​ 1 − q q hs(q)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-q}{q}} . Observe that (in the case of two outcomes), the expected score G h ​ s G_{hs} on a report of q q is G h ​ s ​ ( q ) = q ⁡ ( − 1 2 ​ 1 − q q ) + ( 1 − q ) ​ ( − 1 2 ​ 1 − q q ) = − q ⁡ ( 1 − q ) . G_{hs}(q)=q\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-q}{q}}\\right)+(1-q)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-q}{q}}\\right)=-\\sqrt{q(1-q)}. That is, G h ​ s G_{hs} is precisely negative the geometric mean of q q and 1 − q 1-q . This motivates us to generalize h ​ s hs to a setting with n n outcomes by setting G h ​ s ( 𝐩 ) := − ∏ i = 1 n p i 1 / n . G_{hs}(\\mathbf{p}):=-\\prod_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}^{1/n}. It should not be obvious that this function is convex, but it turns out to be; this is the precise statement of an inequality known as Mahler’s inequality [ Wik18 ] . Next we note that the quadratic scoring rule has convex exposure, since its exposure function 𝐠 ⁡ ( 𝐩 ) = 2 ​ 𝐩 \\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p})=2\\mathbf{p} (modulo 𝟏 n \\mathbf{1}_{n} as discussed in Remark 3.12 of the main article) maps any convex set to a convex set. Proposition D.4 . The quadratic scoring rule has convex exposure. Spherical scoring rules — the third most studied proper scoring rules, after the quadratic and logarithmic rules — also have convex exposure. Definition D.5 (Spherical scoring rules) . [ GR07 , Example 2] For any α &gt; 1 \\alpha&gt;1 , define the spherical scoring rule with parameter α \\alpha to be the scoring rule given by G sph , α ​ ( 𝐩 ) := ( ∑ i = 1 n p i α ) 1 / α . G_{\\text{sph},\\alpha}(\\mathbf{p}):=\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}^{\\alpha}\\right)^{1/\\alpha}. If the “spherical scoring rule” is referenced with no parameter α \\alpha given, α \\alpha is presumed to equal 2 2 . Proposition D.6 . For any α &gt; 1 \\alpha&gt;1 , the spherical scoring rule with parameter α \\alpha has convex exposure. Proof. Fix α &gt; 1 \\alpha&gt;1 . We will write G G in place of G sph , α G_{\\text{sph},\\alpha} . We have 36 36 36 As discussed in Remark 3.12 of the main article, the range of 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} should be thought of as modulo T ⁡ ( 𝟏 n ) T(\\mathbf{1}_{n}) . However, we find it convenient for this proof to think of it as lying in ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} and project later. 𝐠 ⁡ ( 𝐩 ) = ( ∑ j = 1 n p j α ) ( 1 / α ) − 1 ​ ( p 1 α − 1 , … , p n α − 1 ) . \\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{p})=\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^{n}p_{j}^{\\alpha}\\right)^{(1/\\alpha)-1}(p_{1}^{\\alpha-1},\\dots,p_{n}^{\\alpha-1}). (8) Now, define the n n -dimensional unit β \\beta -sphere , i.e. { 𝐱 : ∑ j x j β = 1 } \\{\\mathbf{x}:\\sum_{j}x_{j}^{\\beta}=1\\} , and define the n n -dimensional unit β \\beta -ball correspondingly (i.e. with ≤ \\leq in place of = = ). The range of 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} is precisely the part of the n n -dimensional unit α α − 1 \\frac{\\alpha}{\\alpha-1} -sphere with all non-negative coordinates. Indeed, on the one hand, for any 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} we have ∑ j g j ​ ( 𝐩 ) α / ( α − 1 ) = ( ∑ j p j α ) − 1 ⋅ ∑ j p j α = 1 \\sum_{j}g_{j}(\\mathbf{p})^{\\alpha/(\\alpha-1)}=\\left(\\sum_{j}p_{j}^{\\alpha}\\right)^{-1}\\cdot\\sum_{j}p_{j}^{\\alpha}=1 (where g j ​