_{1} , and C C is a universal constant. If Assumptions 1 - 2 also hold, then for every η ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) \\eta\\in(0,1) , when we set t := 1 n ​ log ⁡ 2 η + l n ⋅ 4 ​ C ​ log ⁡ ( C ℋ ) ​ log ​ log ⁡ ( C ℋ ) ≤ α 4 t:=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}\\log\\frac{2}{\\eta}+\\frac{l}{n}\\cdot 4C\\log(C_{\\mathcal{H}})\\log\\log(C_{\\mathcal{H}})}\\leq\\frac{\\alpha}{4} , with probability at least 1 − η 1-\\eta , we have, for every optimal solution ( L ^ t ∗ , U ^ t ∗ ) (\\hat{L}_{t}^{*},\\hat{U}_{t}^{*}) of opt ^ ​ ( t ) \\widehat{\\mathrm{opt}}(t) , that ℙ π ​ ( Y ∈ [ L ^ t ∗ ​ ( X ) , U ^ t ∗ ​ ( X ) ] ) ≥ 1 − α \\mathbb{P}_{\\pi}(Y\\in[\\hat{L}_{t}^{*}(X),\\hat{U}_{t}^{*}(X)])\\geq 1-\\alpha and 𝔼 π X ​ [ U ^ t ∗ ​ ( X ) − L ^ t ∗ ​ ( X ) ] ≤ ℛ ∗ ​ ( ℋ ) \\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{X}}[\\hat{U}_{t}^{*}(X)-\\hat{L}_{t}^{*}(X)]\\leq\\mathcal{R}^{*}(\\mathcal{H}) (4.3) OPEN + 24 α ​ γ α 6 ​ 1 n ​ log ⁡ 2 η + l n ⋅ 4 ​ C ​ log ⁡ ( C ℋ ) ​ log ​ log ⁡ ( C ℋ CLOSE ) \\displaystyle+\\frac{24}{\\alpha\\gamma_{\\frac{\\alpha}{6}}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}\\log\\frac{2}{\\eta}+\\frac{l}{n}\\cdot 4C\\log(C_{\\mathcal{H}})\\log\\log(C_{\\mathcal{H}}}) + l n ⋅ 16 ​ C ​ max ⁡ { log ⁡ diam ⁡ ( Θ ) ​ ‖ ℒ ‖ 2 ‖ H ‖ 2 , 1 } ​ ‖ H ‖ ψ 2 2 ​ log ​ 4 η . \\displaystyle+\\sqrt{\\frac{l}{n}\\cdot 16C\\max\\big\\{\\log\\frac{\\mathrm{diam}\\left(\\Theta\\right)\\|\\mathcal{L}\\|_{2}}{\\|H\\|_{2}},1\\big\\}\\|H\\|_{\\psi_{2}}^{2}\\log\\frac{4}{\\eta}}. The Lipschitz class is beyond the scope of Rosenfeld et al. 2018 . Theorem 4.4 states that the required sample size for this class to achieve a certain learning accuracy is of order Ω ⁡ ( l ​ log ⁡ ( ‖ ℒ ‖ 1 ) ) \\Omega(l\\log(\\|\\mathcal{L}\\|_{1})) , which depends on the dimension of the parameter space and the Lipschitz coefficient. Correspondingly, the optimality gap on the interval width is O ⁡ ( l ​ log ⁡ ( ‖ ℒ ‖ 2 ) / n ) O(\\sqrt{l\\log(\\|\\mathcal{L}\\|_{2})/n}) . Here the logarithmic factor associated with the Lipschitz coefficient is significant because ‖ ℒ ‖ 2 \\|\\mathcal{L}\\|_{2} can be exponential in the number of layers for deep neural networks (see more details below). Note that our Lipschitzness is in the class parameter θ \\theta rather than the input x x . The latter has recently been used to regularize neural networks to improve generalization gaps (e.g., Bartlett et al. 2017 ; Yoshida and Miyato 2017 ; Gouk et al. 2018 ) and robustness against adversarial attacks (e.g., Cisse et al. 2017 ; Hein and Andriushchenko 2017 ; Tsuzuku et al. 2018 ), but can potentially lead to a loss in expressiveness of the network ( Huster et al. 2018 ; Anil et al. 2019 ) because of the size restriction on network weights. Our result does not restrict the sensitivity of the network in the inputs, and in turn its expressiveness. To further distinguish the technical novelty of Theorem 4.3 and Theorem 4.4 , note that established results on uniform convergence bounds (UCBs) assuming VC or Lipschitzness on the hypothesis class ℋ \\mathcal{H} can only handle the objective (width) on ℋ \\mathcal{H} , but not the constraint (coverage) on a different hypothesis class of indicator functions on the joint ( x , y ) (x,y) space. Our analysis explicitly controls this constraint complexity to achieve joint optimality-feasibility guarantees. The proof of Theorem 4.3 involves bounding the VC-dimension of the indicator class through intersections of VC set classes, while that of Theorem 4.4 requires directly bounding the bracketing number and evaluating the entropy integral. Moreover, even for the objective, it appears that the explicit UCBs with potentially unbounded VC or Lipschitz classes considered here are new to the best of our knowledge. We showcase the application of Theorems 4.3 and 4.4 in two important classes: Regression trees and neural networks. Appendix C presents an additional class of linear hypothesis. Regression Tree. Suppose we build two binary trees to construct L L , U U respectively. The tree has at most S + 1 S+1 terminal nodes, and each non-terminal node is split according to x i ∗ ≤ q x_{i^{*}}\\leq q or x i ∗ &gt; q x_{i^{*}}&gt;q for some i ∗ ∈ { 1 , … , d } i^{*}\\in\\{1,\\ldots,d\\} and q ∈ ℝ q\\in\\mathbb{R} . In other words, at most S S splits are allowed. A regression tree constructed this way is therefore a piece-wise constant function: h ⁡ ( x ) = ∑ s = 1 S + 1 c s ​ I x ∈ R s h(x)=\\sum_{s=1}^{S+1}c_{s}I_{x\\in R_{s}} , where each c s ≥ 0 c_{s}\\geq 0 , ∪ s = 1 S + 1 R s = 𝒳 \\cup_{s=1}^{S+1}R_{s}=\\mathcal{X} , and each R s R_{s} is a hyper-rectangle in ℝ d \\mathbb{R}^{d} that takes the form R s = { x ∈ 𝒳 : x i k , 1 ≤ q i k , 1 ​ for ​ k = 1 , … , S 1 x i k , 2 &gt; q i k , 2 ​ for ​ k = 1 , … , S 2 each ​ q i k , 1 , q i k , 2 ∈ [ − ∞ , + ∞ ] 0 ≤ S 1 + S 2 ≤ S } . R_{s}=\\left\\{x\\in\\mathcal{X}:\\begin{array}[]{l}x_{i_{k,1}}\\leq q_{i_{k,1}}\\;\\text{for}\\;k=1,\\ldots,S_{1}\\\\ x_{i_{k,2}}&gt;q_{i_{k,2}}\\;\\text{for}\\;k=1,\\ldots,S_{2}\\\\ \\text{each }q_{i_{k,1}},q_{i_{k,2}}\\in[-\\infty,+\\infty]\\\\ 0\\leq S_{1}+S_{2}\\leq S\\end{array}\\right\\}. Let hypothesis class ℋ \\mathcal{H} be the collection of all such regression trees. We use Theorem 4.3 . Note that the augmented class ℋ + = ℋ \\mathcal{H}_{+}=\\mathcal{H} . Since the regression tree takes constant values on each rectangle, its subgraph in the space 𝒳 × ℝ \\mathcal{X}\\times\\mathbb{R} is a union of hyper-rectangles, i.e., { ( x , z ) ∈ 𝒳 × ℝ : h ( x ) &gt; z } = ∪ s = 1 S + 1 ( R s ∪ ( − ∞ , c s ) ) \\{(x,z)\\in\\mathcal{X}\\times\\mathbb{R}:h(x)&gt;z\\}=\\cup_{s=1}^{S+1}\\big(R_{s}\\cup(-\\infty,c_{s})\\big) . Note that each R s ∪ ( − ∞ , c s ) R_{s}\\cup(-\\infty,c_{s}) is an intersection of at most S + 1 S+1 axis-parallel cuts in ℝ d + 1 \\mathbb{R}^{d+1} , and the set of all axis-parallel cuts is shown to have a VC dimension O ⁡ ( log ⁡ ( d ) ) O(\\log(d)) ( Gey 2018 ) . vc ⁡ ( ℋ ) \\mathrm{vc}\\left(\\mathcal{H}\\right) can therefore be obtained by applying VC bounds for unions and int