 assumed, the conclusion of Theorem A.1 holds for strong π X \\pi_{X} -GC ℋ \\mathcal{H} . Appendix B Further Discussion of Theorem 4.3 We provide further discussion on Theorem 4.3 regarding ℋ + \\mathcal{H}_{+} versus ℋ \\mathcal{H} in the bound. Note that, since ℋ ⊂ ℋ + \\mathcal{H}\\subset\\mathcal{H}_{+} , the augmented class ℋ + \\mathcal{H}_{+} being VC-subgraph is a stronger condition than ℋ \\mathcal{H} being VC-subgraph. Nonetheless, we comment that this is a technical assumption used to accommodate potentially unbounded outcomes or PIs (e.g., Assumption 1 implies unboundedness of functions in ℋ \\mathcal{H} ). When Y Y is uniformly bounded, say within [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] , it suffices to consider bounded L , U L,U only in PI construction. In that case, ℋ \\mathcal{H} being VC-subgraph already suffices to ensure similar finite-sample bounds. Appendix C Linear Hypothesis Class We present a joint coverage-width guarantee for PIs constructed from a linear hypothesis class. Consider the linear hypothesis ℋ = { a T x + b : ∥ a ∥ 1 ≤ B , b ∈ ℝ } \\mathcal{H}=\\{a^{T}x+b:\\|a\\|_{1}\\leq B,b\\in\\mathbb{R}\\} for some B &gt; 0 B&gt;0 . The l 1 l_{1} -norm of the coefficient is set bounded to control model complexity. We demonstrate how Theorem 4.3 is applied to this class. First note that the augmented class ℋ + = ℋ \\mathcal{H}_{+}=\\mathcal{H} is the same class of the linear function class { a T x + b : ∥ a ∥ 1 ≤ B , b ∈ ℝ } \\{a^{T}x+b:\\|a\\|_{1}\\leq B,b\\in\\mathbb{R}\\} , which is VC-subgraph of dimension at most d + 2 d+2 (see, e.g., Theorem 2.6.7 in Van der Vaart and Wellner 1996 ), and hence vc ⁡ ( ℋ ) = vc ⁡ ( ℋ + ) ≤ d + 2 \\mathrm{vc}\\left(\\mathcal{H}\\right)=\\mathrm{vc}\\left(\\mathcal{H}_{+}\\right)\\leq d+2 . Therefore ϕ 1 ​ ( n , ϵ , ℋ ) ≤ 2 ​ exp ⁡ ( − n ​ ϵ 2 C ​ ‖ H ‖ ψ 2 2 ​ vc ​ ( ℋ + ) ) \\phi_{1}(n,\\epsilon,\\mathcal{H})\\leq 2\\exp\\Big(-\\frac{n\\epsilon^{2}}{C\\|H\\|_{\\psi_{2}}^{2}\\mathrm{vc}\\left(\\mathcal{H}_{+}\\right)}\\Big) (C.1)