thbb{E}_{\\pi_{X}}[h(X)]\\rvert\\leq B\\|\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_{i}-\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{X}}[X]\\|_{\\infty} , and applying the sub-Gaussian concentration inequality to the supremum norm gives rise to the following: Theorem C.1 (Linear hypothesis class) . For the linear class ℋ \\mathcal{H} defined as above we have ϕ 1 ​ ( n , ϵ , ℋ ) ≤ 2 ​ exp ⁡ ( − ϵ 2 ​ n C ​ B 2 ​ ‖ ‖ X − 𝔼 π X ​ [ X ] ‖ ∞ ‖ ψ 2 2 ​ log ⁡ d ) \\phi_{1}(n,\\epsilon,\\mathcal{H})\\leq 2\\exp\\Big(-\\frac{\\epsilon^{2}n}{CB^{2}\\|\\|X-\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{X}}[X]\\|_{\\infty}\\|_{\\psi_{2}}^{2}\\log d}\\Big) where C C is a universal constant. Appendix D Alternate Analysis of Neural Networks using VC Dimension In Section 4 we have established the joint coverage-width guarantee for PIs constructed from neural networks using our Lipschitz class results (Theorem 4.4 ). Here we provide an alternate approach to analyze neural networks via our VC class results (Theorem 4.3 ). We consider the VC dimension of a real-valued neural network as a VC-subgraph class, which is also known as Pollard’s pseudo-dimension Pollard 2012 . Bounds for pseudo-dimension are relatively well-established for neural networks with sigmoid or piece-wise polynomial activation. For example, when all activation functions are sigmoid, the class is VC-subgraph with vc ⁡ ( ℋ ) = O ⁡ ( W 2 ​ U 2 ) \\mathrm{vc}\\left(\\mathcal{H}\\right)=O(W^{2}U^{2}) (Theorem 14.2 in Anthony and Bartlett 2009 ). Alternatively, if all the activation functions are piece-wise polynomials with a bounded number of pieces and of bounded degrees, e.g., rectified linear unit (ReLU, see LeCun et al. 2015 ; Goodfellow et al. 2016 ) or linear activation, then we have vc ⁡ ( ℋ ) = O ⁡ ( W ​ U ) \\mathrm{vc}\\left(\\mathcal{H}\\right)=O(WU) (Theorem 8 in Bartlett et al. 2019 ) and simultaneously that vc ⁡ ( ℋ ) = O ⁡ ( W ​ S 2 + W ​ S ​ log ⁡ ( W ) ) \\mathrm{vc}\\left(\\mathcal{H}\\right)=O(WS^{2}+WS\\log(W)) (Theorem 8.8 in Anthony and Bartlett 2009 , Theorem 6 in Bartlett et al. 2019 , and Theorem 1 in Bartlett et al. 1999 ). Similar bounds are also available (e.g., Theorem 8.14 in Anthony and Bartlett 2009 ) when the network involves both sigmoid and piece-wise polynomial activation functions. On the other hand, the augmented class ℋ + \\mathcal{H}_{+} is a subclass of an augmented neural network where the output unit of ℋ \\mathcal{H} serves as the last hidden layer (with a single neuron) followed by a new output unit with linear activation, i.e., the class { a h + b : h ∈ ℋ , a ∈ ℝ , b ∈ ℝ } \\{ah+b:h\\in\\mathcal{H},a\\in\\mathbb{R},b\\in\\mathbb{R}\\} , and thus its VC-subgraph property can be propagated to this augmented class. Appendix E More Discussions of Finite-Sample Guarantees for the Coverage Calibration Procedure We provide further interpretations on the margin q 1 − β ​ σ ^ j / n v q_{1-\\beta}\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}/\\sqrt{n_{v}} in Algorithm 1 , and the error terms of ( 5.1 ) in Theorem 5.1 . The margin q 1 − β ​ σ ^ j / n v q_{1-\\beta}\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}/\\sqrt{n_{v}} in Algorithm 1 is reasoned from the CLT that n v ​ ( CR ^ ​ ( PI 1 ) − CR ⁡ ( PI 1 ) , … , CR ^ ​ ( PI m ) − CR ⁡ ( PI m ) ) ​ → 𝑑 ​ N ​ ( 0 , Σ ) \\sqrt{n_{v}}\\big(\\hat{\\mathrm{CR}}(\\mathrm{PI}_{1})-\\mathrm{CR}(\\mathrm{PI}_{1}),\\ldots,\\hat{\\mathrm{CR}}(\\mathrm{PI}_{m})-\\mathrm{CR}(\\mathrm{PI}_{m})\\big)\\overset{d}{\\to}N(0,\\Sigma) where Σ \\Sigma is the covariance matrix with Σ j 1 , j 2 = Cov π ​ ( I Y ∈ PI j 1 ​ ( X ) , I Y ∈ PI j 2 ​ ( X ) ) \\Sigma_{j_{1},j_{2}}=\\mathrm{Cov}_{\\pi}(I_{Y\\in\\mathrm{PI}_{j_{1}}(X)},I_{Y\\in\\mathrm{PI}_{j_{2}}(X)}) . Approximating Σ \\Sigma with the sample covariance Σ ^ \\hat{\\Sigma} from Step 1 of Algorithm 1 and applying the continuous mapping theorem, we have n v ​ max j ⁡ ( CR ^ ​ ( PI j ) − CR ⁡ ( PI j ) ) / σ ^ j ​ → 𝑑 ​ max j ​ Z j / σ ^ j \\sqrt{n_{v}}\\max_{j}(\\hat{\\mathrm{CR}}(\\mathrm{PI}_{j})-\\mathrm{CR}(\\mathrm{PI}_{j}))/\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}\\overset{d}{\\to}\\max_{j}Z_{j}/\\hat{\\sigma}_{j} where ( Z j ) j = 1 , … , m (Z_{j})_{j=1,\\ldots,m} follows N ⁡ ( 0 , Σ ) N(0,\\Sigma) . Therefore, using the 1 − β 1-\\beta quantile of max j ⁡ Z j / σ ^ j \\max_{j}Z_{j}/\\hat{\\sigma}_{j} in the margin leads to a uniform control of the statistical errors in CR ^ ​ ( PI j ) \\hat{\\mathrm{CR}}(\\mathrm{PI}_{j}) ’s with probability approximately 1 − β 1-\\beta . Theorem 5.1 states this approximation concretely. The polynomial term in ( 5.1 ) corresponds to the error of the joint central limit convergence, and the exponential error term quantifies the probability of the undesirable event that none of the candidate PIs satisfies the penalized constraint in Step 3. In practice, one usually targets at relatively high coverage rates, say at least 50 % 50\\% , and would train the candidate PIs in such a way that the true coverage rates of some of the PIs sufficiently exceed the highest target level, e.g., by heavily penalizing the coverage error. In that case, α min = α ~ \\alpha_{\\min}=\\tilde{\\alpha} , and α ¯ &lt; α min \\underline{\\alpha}&lt;\\alpha_{\\min} with a sufficient gap, therefore using a sample size n v n_{v} of order Ω ⁡ ( log 7 ⁡ ( m ) / α min ) \\Omega(\\log^{7}(m)/\\alpha_{\\min}) is enough for ensuring ϵ &gt; 0 \\epsilon&gt;0 and the probability ( 5.1 ) close to 1 − β 1-\\beta so that correct coverage rates are guaranteed with high confidence. This logarithmic dependence on m m allows us to advantageously use lots of candidate models in the calibration step. Another notable feature of the finite-sample error is its independence of K K , the number of target rates. This independence arises from the choice of the margin based on the Gaussian supremum that leads to a uniform control of the statistical errors in the empirical coverage rates. This provides the flexibility of constructing PIs for arbitrarily many target levels simultaneously. Besides coverage attainment, our calibration procedure also possesses guaranteed performance regarding the other side of the feasibility-optimality tradeoff, provided that only the calibration data are used to assess the width in Step 3 of Algorithm 1 . This is detailed in the following result: Theorem E.1 . Assume all the candidate PIs in Algorithm 1 are selected from a hypothesis class ℋ \\mathcal{H} whose envelope H ⁡ ( x ) := sup h ∈ ℋ | h ⁡ ( x ) − 𝔼 π X ​ [ h ⁡ ( X ) ] | H(x):=\\sup_{h\\in\\mathcal{H}}\\lvert h(x)-\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{X}}[h(X)]\\rvert has a finite sub-Gaussian norm ‖ H ‖ ψ 2 &lt; ∞ \\|H\\|_{\\psi_{2}}&lt;\\infty . If in Step 3 of Algorithm 1 each j 1 − α k ∗ j^{*}_{1-\\alpha_{k}} is selected according to j 1 − α k ∗ = arg ⁡ min 1 ≤ j ≤ m ​ { 1 n v ​ ∑ i = 1 n v | PI j ​ ( X i ′ ) | : CR ^ ​ ( PI j ) ≥ 1 − α k + q 1 − β ​ σ ^ j n v } j^{*}_{1-\\alpha_{k}}=\\underset{1\\leq j\\leq m}{\\operatorname{arg}\\,\\operatorname{min}}\\;\\Big\\{\\frac{1}{n_{v}}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_{v}}\\lvert\\mathrm{PI}_{j}(X^{\\prime}_{i})\\rvert:\\hat{\\mathrm{CR}}(\\mathrm{PI}_{j})\\geq 1-\\alpha_{k}+\\frac{q_{1-\\beta}\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}}{\\sqrt{n_{v}}}\\Big\\} and all other steps are kept the same, then for every ϵ &gt; 0 \\epsilon&gt;0 we have ℙ 𝒟 v ( 𝔼 π X [ U j 1 − α k ∗ ( X ) − L j 1 − α k ∗ ( X ) ] ≤ min j : CR ⁡ ( PI j ) ≥ 1 − α k + ϵ 𝔼 π X [ U j ( X ) − L j ( X ) ] + 2 C ϵ ∥ H ∥ ψ 2 for all k = 1 , … , K ) \\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}_{\\mathcal{D}_{v}}\\Big(\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{X}}[U_{j^{*}_{1-\\alpha_{k}}}(X)-L_{j^{*}_{1-\\alpha_{k}}}(X)]\\leq\\min_{j:\\mathrm{CR(\\mathrm{PI}_{j})\\geq 1-\\alpha_{k}+\\epsilon}}\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{X}}[U_{j}(X)-L_{j}(X)]+2C\\epsilon\\|H\\|_{\\psi_{2}}\\text{ for all }k=1,\\ldots,K\\Big) ≥ \\displaystyle\\geq 1 − 8 ​ m ​ exp ⁡ ( − 1 4 ​ max ⁡ { ϵ − C ​ log ⁡ ( m / β ) n v , 0 } 2 ​ n v ) \\displaystyle 1-8m\\exp\\Big(-\\frac{1}{4}\\max\\big\\{\\epsilon-C\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(m/\\beta)}{n_{v}}},0\\big\\}^{2}n_{v}\\Big) for some universal constant C C . Appendix F Alternate Calibration Scheme We present an alternate coverage calibration scheme than Algorithm 1 that switches the Gaussian vector used in the margin from “normalized” to “unnormalized”. To explain, the margin q 1 − β ​ σ ^ j / n v q_{1-\\beta}\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}/\\sqrt{n_{v}} used in Algorithm 1 is set proportional to the standard deviation of the empirical coverage rate for each individual PI. An alternative is to set q 1 − β ′ q^{\\prime}_{1-\\beta} , the 1 − β 1-\\beta quantile of max ⁡ { Z j : 1 ≤ j ≤ m } \\max\\{Z_{j}:1\\leq j\\leq m\\} , as a uniform margin for all the PIs, which also captures the uniform error in coverage rates due to the convergence n v ​ max j ⁡ ( CR ^ ​ ( PI j ) − CR ⁡ ( PI j ) ) ​ → 𝑑 ​ max j ​ Z j \\sqrt{n_{v}}\\max_{j}(\\hat{\\mathrm{CR}}(\\mathrm{PI}_{j})-\\mathrm{CR}(\\mathrm{PI}_{j}))\\overset{d}{\\to}\\max_{j}Z_{j} . This alternative scheme is depicted in Algorithm 2 . Input : Same as in Algorithm 1 . Procedure: 1. Same as in Algorithm 1 . 2. Compute q 1 − β ′ q^{\\prime}_{1-\\beta} , the ( 1 − β ) (1-\\beta) -quantile of max ⁡ { Z j : 1 ≤ j ≤ m } \\max\\{Z_{j}:1\\leq j\\leq m\\} where ( Z 1 , … , Z m ) (Z_{1},\\ldots,Z_{m}) is a multivariate Gaussian with mean zero and covariance Σ ^ \\hat{\\Sigma} . 3. For each coverage rate k = 1 , … , K k=1,\\ldots,K compute j 1 − α k ∗ = arg ⁡ min 1 ≤ j ≤ m { 1 n + n v ( ∑ i = 1 n | PI j ( X i ) | + ∑ i = 1 n v | PI j ( X i ′ ) | ) : CR ^ ( PI j ) ≥ 1 − α k + q 1 − β ′ n v } j^{*}_{1-\\alpha_{k}}=\\underset{1\\leq j\\leq m}{\\operatorname{arg}\\,\\operatorname{min}}\\;\\Big\\{\\frac{1}{n+n_{v}}\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\lvert\\mathrm{PI}_{j}(X_{i})\\rvert+\\sum_{i=1}^{n_{v}}\\lvert\\mathrm{PI}_{j}(X^{\\prime}_{i})|\\Big):\\hat{\\mathrm{CR}}(\\mathrm{PI}_{j})\\geq 1-\\alpha_{k}+\\frac{q^{\\prime}_{1-\\beta}}{\\sqrt{n_{v}}}\\Big\\} where { X i } i = 1 n \\{X_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n} is the training data set. Output : PI j 1 − α k ∗ \\mathrm{PI}_{j^{*}_{1-\\alpha_{k}}} for k = 1 , … , K k=1,\\ldots,K . Algorithm 2 Unnormalized PI Calibration Algorithm 2 enjoys a similar finite-sample performance guarantee: Theorem F.1 . Under the same setting of Theorem 5.1 , the finite sample error ( 5.1 ) continues to hold for the PIs output by Algorithm 2 , but with ϵ = max ⁡ { α min − α ¯ − C 1 ​ log ⁡ ( m / β ) / n v , 0 } \\epsilon=\\max\\{\\alpha_{\\min}-\\underline{\\alpha}-C_{1}\\sqrt{\\log(m/\\beta)/n_{v}},0\\} . We compare Algorithms 1 and 2 in terms of statistical efficiency. Like for Algorithm 1 , if the target coverage rates are above 50 % 50\\% and the maximal achieved coverage rate of the candidate PIs sufficiently exceeds the highest target level, then α min = α ~ \\alpha_{\\min}=\\tilde{\\alpha} , and α ¯ &lt; α min \\underline{\\alpha}&lt;\\alpha_{\\min} with a sufficient gap. The new expression for ϵ \\epsilon in Theorem F.1 now implies a sample size n v n_{v} of order Ω ⁡ ( log ⁡ m α min 2 + log 7 ⁡ m α min ) \\Omega\\big(\\frac{\\log m}{\\alpha_{\\min}^{2}}+\\frac{\\log^{7}m}{\\alpha_{\\min}}\\big) for Algorithm 2 to guarantee correct coverage rates with high confidence. Note that the dependence on α min \\alpha_{\\min} grows from a linear one in Algorithm 1 to quadratic, suggesting that Algorithm 1 is more powerful in the case of high target coverage levels. However, when the target coverage levels are moderate (around 50 % 50\\% ), Algorithm 2 is more efficient instead, due to a smaller margin than the one in Algorithm 1 for PIs with moderate coverage rates. To explain, denote by PI j ¯ \\mathrm{PI}_{\\bar{j}} the PI with the maximal sample standard deviation (coverage closest to 50 % 50\\% ), i.e., σ ^ j ¯ = max 1 ≤ j ≤ m ⁡ σ ^ j \\hat{\\sigma}_{\\bar{j}}=\\max_{1\\leq j\\leq m}\\hat{\\sigma}_{j} , then the margin used for PI j ¯ \\mathrm{PI}_{\\bar{j}} in Algorithm 1 satisfies q 1 − β ​ σ ^ j ¯ \\displaystyle q_{1-\\beta}\\hat{\\sigma}_{\\bar{j}} = \\displaystyle= σ ^ j ¯ ⋅ 1 − β ​ quantile of ​ max 1 ≤ j ≤ m ​ Z j / σ ^ j \\displaystyle\\hat{\\sigma}_{\\bar{j}}\\cdot 1-\\beta\\text{ quantile of }\\max_{1\\leq j\\leq m}Z_{j}/\\hat{\\sigma}_{j} = \\displaystyle= 1 − β ​ quantile of ​ max 1 ≤ j ≤ m ​ σ ^ j ¯ ​ Z j / σ ^ j \\displaystyle 1-\\beta\\text{ quantile of }\\max_{1\\leq j\\leq m}\\hat{\\sigma}_{\\bar{j}}Z_{j}/\\hat{\\sigma}_{j} &gt; \\displaystyle&gt; 1 − β ​ quantile of ​ max 1 ≤ j ≤ m ​ Z j ​ if not all σ ^ j ’s are equal \\displaystyle 1-\\beta\\text{ quantile of }\\max_{1\\leq j\\leq m}Z_{j}\\text{\\ \\ \\ \\ if not all $\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}$'s are equal} = \\displaystyle= q 1 − β ′ \\displaystyle q^{\\prime}_{1-\\beta} which is the margin used by Algorithm 2 . Appendix G A Lagrangian Formulation for Training Neural-Network-Based Prediction Intervals We discuss a Lagrangian formulation of ( 3.2 ) for training neural networks to construct PIs. This formulation has the dual multiplier set as the tunable parameter to balance the tradeoff between the objective and the constraint in ( 3.2 ). Specifically, we use L ⁡ ( δ , λ ) = 𝔼 π ^ X ​ [ U ⁡ ( X ) − L ⁡ ( X ) ] + λ ⁡ ( 1 − α + t − ℙ π ^ ​ ( Y ∈ [ L ⁡ ( X ) , U ⁡ ( X ) ] ) ) L(\\delta;\\lambda)=\\mathbb{E}_{\\hat{\\pi}_{X}}[U(X)-L(X)]+\\lambda(1-\\alpha+t-\\mathbb{P}_{\\hat{\\pi}}(Y\\in[L(X),U(X)])) or L ⁡ ( δ , λ ) = 1 n ​ ∑ i = 1 n ( U ⁡ ( x i ) − L ⁡ ( x i ) ) + λ n ​ ∑ i = 1 n I y i ∉ [ L ⁡ ( x i ) , U ⁡ ( x i ) ] + constant L(\\delta;\\lambda)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(U(x_{i})-L(x_{i}))+\\frac{\\lambda}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}I_{y_{i}\\notin[L(x_{i}),U(x_{i})]}+\\text{constant} where λ \\lambda is the multiplier. In practice, we use a “soft” version of the Lagrangian function for gradient descent. The “soft” loss we adopt is introduced in Section 6 . We can build multiple PI models by using different parameters λ &gt; 0 \\lambda&gt;0 . Then, these models are calibrated using Algorithm 1 or 2 so that the coverage constraint in ( 3.1 ) is satisfied. Intuitively, if λ \\lambda is large, ∑ i = 1 m ( U ⁡ ( X i ) − L ⁡ ( X i ) ) \\sum_{i=1}^{m}(U(X_{i})-L(X_{i})) contributes less to the overall loss function, and hence the resulting interval tends to be wide but have a high coverage rate. On the contrary, a small λ \\lambda entails a short interval with a low coverage rate. Hence, a neural network is a reasonable approach to solve ( 3.2 ) since a neural network with the above loss can directly address the tradeoff between the interval width and the coverage rate. Appendix H Empirical Process Background For a class 𝒢 \\mathcal{G} of measurable functions from 𝒳 \\mathcal{X} to ℝ \\mathbb{R} such that 𝔼 π X ​ [ | g ⁡ ( X ) | ] &lt; ∞ \\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{X}}[\\lvert g(X)\\rvert]&lt;\\infty for every g ∈ 𝒢 g\\in\\mathcal{G} , we say it is weak (resp. strong) π X \\pi_{X} -Glivenko–Cantelli (GC) if sup g ∈ 𝒢 | 1 n ​ ∑ i = 1 n g ⁡ ( X i ) − 𝔼 π X ​ [ g ⁡ ( X ) ] | → 0 \\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal{G}}\\big\\lvert\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}g(X_{i})-\\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_{X}}[g(X)]\\big\\rvert\\to 0 in probability (resp. almost surely) as n → ∞ n\\to\\infty . For a class 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} of measurable subsets of 𝒳 \\mathcal{X} , i.e., S ⊂ 𝒳 S\\subset\\mathcal{X} for every S ∈ 𝒮 S\\in\\mathcal{S} , we say it’s weak (resp. strong) π X \\pi_{X} -GC if the corresponding indicator class { I ⋅ ∈ S : S ∈ 𝒮 } \\{I_{\\cdot\\in S}:S\\in\\mathcal{S}\\} is weak (resp. strong) π X \\pi_{X} -GC. When no ambiguity arises, we sometimes suppress the underlying distribution π X \\pi_{X} . A collection of k k points { x 1 , … , x k } ⊂ 𝒳 \\{x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}\\}\\subset\\mathcal{X} is said to be shattered by a class 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} of subsets of 𝒳 \\mathcal{X} if card ⁡ ( { { x 1 , … , x k } ∩ S : S ⊂ 𝒮 } ) = 2 k \\mathrm{card}\\left(\\{\\{x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}\\}\\cap S:S\\subset\\mathcal{S}\\}\\right)=2^{k} , where card ⁡ ( ⋅ ) \\mathrm{card}\\left(\\cdot\\right) denotes the cardinality of a set. The VC dimension of the class 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} is defined as vc ⁡ ( 𝒮 ) := max ⁡ { k : ∃ { x 1 , … , x k } ⊂ 𝒳 ​ shattered by ​ 𝒮 } \\mathrm{vc}\\left(\\mathcal{S}\\right):=\\max\\{k:\\exists\\;\\{x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}\\}\\subset\\mathcal{X}\\text{ shattered by 