nd argue that it suffices to prove the result in general. Given k + ℓ k+\\ell quantile levels τ 1 &lt; ⋯ &lt; τ k + ℓ \\tau_{1}&lt;\\cdots&lt;\\tau_{k+\\ell} , we abbreviate q i = g ^ ​ ( x , τ i ) q_{i}=\\hat{g}(x;\\tau_{i}) for i = 1 , … , k + ℓ i=1,\\ldots,k+\\ell . We suppose, in what follows, that q 1 = ⋯ = q k q_{1}=\\cdots=q_{k} and q k + 1 = ⋯ = q k + ℓ q_{k+1}=\\cdots=q_{k+\\ell} . We then show that averaging can only improve the pinball loss, where by averaging, we mean replacing the original vector ( q 1 , … , q k + ℓ ) (q_{1},\\ldots,q_{k+\\ell}) by ( q ¯ , … , q ¯ ) (\\bar{q},\\ldots,\\bar{q}) , with q ¯ = 1 k + ℓ ​ ∑ i = 1 k + ℓ q i \\bar{q}=\\frac{1}{k+\\ell}\\sum_{i=1}^{k+\\ell}q_{i} . This suffices to prove the desired result, because isotonic projection can be solved using PAVA, which carries out a sequence of steps (whenever there is a violation of the monotonicity condition) that are precisely of the form we study here. Assume q k &gt; q k + 1 q_{k}&gt;q_{k+1} , otherwise there is nothing to prove. We break the argument down into four cases. In each case, as in the proof of part (i), when we speak of the loss difference below, we mean the pre-sort loss minus the post-sort loss. Case 1: y ≥ q k y\\geq q_{k} . We have L ⁡ ( ( q 1 , … , q k + ℓ ) , y ) \\displaystyle L((q_{1},\\ldots,q_{k+\\ell}),y) = ∑ i = 1 k τ i ​ ( y − q k ) + ∑ i = k + 1 k + ℓ τ i ​ ( y − q k + 1 ) , \\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\tau_{i}(y-q_{k})+\\sum_{i=k+1}^{k+\\ell}\\tau_{i}(y-q_{k+1}), L ⁡ ( ( q ¯ , … , q ¯ ) , y ) \\displaystyle L((\\bar{q},\\ldots,\\bar{q}),y) = ∑ i = 1 k + ℓ τ i ​ ( y − q ¯ ) , \\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{k+\\ell}\\tau_{i}(y-\\bar{q}), so the loss difference is ∑ i = 1 k τ i ​ ( q ¯ − q k ) + ∑ i = k + 1 k + ℓ τ i ​ ( q ¯ − q k + 1 ) = k ​ ℓ k + ℓ ​ ( 1 ℓ ​ ∑ i = k + 1 k + ℓ τ i − 1 k ​ ∑ i = 1 k τ i ) ​ ( q k − q k + 1 ) ≥ 0 . \\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\tau_{i}(\\bar{q}-q_{k})+\\sum_{i=k+1}^{k+\\ell}\\tau_{i}(\\bar{q}-q_{k+1})=\\frac{k\\ell}{k+\\ell}\\bigg(\\f