_{i=k}^{k+\\ell}(1-\\tau_{i})(q_{k+1}-\\bar{q})\\\\ =\\ell(y-q_{k+1})+\\frac{k\\ell}{k+\\ell}\\bigg(\\frac{1}{k}\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(1-\\tau_{i})-\\frac{1}{\\ell}\\sum_{i={k+1}}^{k+\\ell}(1-\\tau_{i})\\bigg)(q_{k}-q_{k+1})\\geq 0. Case 4: y &lt; q k y&lt;q_{k} . Then L ⁡ ( ( q 1 , … , q k + ℓ ) , y ) \\displaystyle L((q_{1},\\ldots,q_{k+\\ell}),y) = ∑ i = 1 k ( 1 − τ i ) ​ ( q k − y ) + ∑ i = k + 1 k + ℓ ( 1 − τ i ) ​ ( q k + 1 − y ) , \\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(1-\\tau_{i})(q_{k}-y)+\\sum_{i=k+1}^{k+\\ell}(1-\\tau_{i})(q_{k+1}-y), L ⁡ ( ( q ¯ , … , q ¯ ) , y ) \\displaystyle L((\\bar{q},\\ldots,\\bar{q}),y) = ∑ i = 1 k + ℓ ( 1 − τ i ) ​ ( q ¯ − y ) , \\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{k+\\ell}(1-\\tau_{i})(\\bar{q}-y), so the loss difference is ∑ i = 1 k ( 1 − τ i ) ​ ( q k − q ¯ ) + ∑ i = k k + ℓ ( 1 − τ i ) ​ ( q k + 1 − q ¯ ) = k ​ ℓ k + ℓ ​ ( 1 k ​ ∑ i = 1 k ( 1 − τ i ) − 1 ℓ ​ ∑ i = k + 1 k + ℓ ( 1 − τ i ) ) ​ ( q k − q k + 1 ) ≥ 0 . \\sum_{i=1}^{k}(1-\\tau_{i})(q_{k}-\\bar{q})+\\sum_{i=k}^{k+\\ell}(1-\\tau_{i})(q_{k+1}-\\bar{q})=\\frac{k\\ell}{k+\\ell}\\bigg(\\frac{1}{k}\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(1-\\tau_{i})-\\frac{1}{\\ell}\\sum_{i={k+1}}^{k+\\ell}(1-\\tau_{i})\\bigg)(q_{k}-q_{k+1})\\geq 0. This completes the proof. A.4 Proof of Proposition 5 A.4.1 Proof of part (i) This is immediate from the fact that f ⁡ ( v ) = w 1 ​ f 1 ​ ( v ) + w 2 ​ f 2 ​ ( v ) f(v)=w_{1}f_{1}(v)+w_{2}f_{2}(v) and f 2 ​ ( v ) / f 1 ​ ( v ) → 0 f_{2}(v)/f_{1}(v)\\to 0 . A.4.2 Proof of part (ii) This part is more subtle. Using ( 14 ), note that we may write 1 f ¯ ​ ( Q ¯ ​ ( u ) ) = w 1 f 1 ​ ( Q 1 ​ ( u ) ) + w 2 f 2 ​ ( Q 2 ​ ( u ) ) . \\frac{1}{\\bar{f}(\\bar{Q}(u))}=\\frac{w_{1}}{f_{1}(Q_{1}(u))}+\\frac{w_{2}}{f_{2}(Q_{2}(u))}. For v = Q ¯ ​ ( u ) v=\\bar{Q}(u) , consider f 1 ​ ( v ) f ¯ ​ ( v ) = w 1 ​ f 1 ​ ( Q ¯ ​ ( u ) ) f 1 ​ ( Q 1 ​ ( u ) ) + w 2 ​ f 1 ​ ( Q ¯ ​ ( u ) ) f 2 ​ ( Q 2 ​ ( u ) ) . \\frac{f_{1}(v)}{\\bar{f}(v)}=\\frac{w_{1}f_{1}(\\bar{Q}(u))}{f_{1}(Q_{1}(u))}+\\frac{w_{2}f_{1}(\\bar