egment B ​ C BC , together with all four endpoints. We can now see that F F is not dense in ∂ + Conv ⁡ F \\partial^{+}\\operatorname{Conv}F , because F F has a gap consisting of the interior of the line segment B ​ C BC , while Conv ⁡ F \\operatorname{Conv}F has positive-facing boundary there. Finally, we give a family of examples of proper scoring rules that satisfy (b)(i) and (b)(ii) but are not continuous. The family will even include some scores that are finite everywhere on Ω \\Omega . Let ℛ \\mathcal{R} be the set of probabilities that are regular in the Bayesian sense, i.e., probability functions p p such that p ⁡ ( { ω } ) &gt; 0 p(\\{\\omega\\})&gt;0 for all ω \\omega . Note that if p p is regular and s s is strictly proper, then we must have E p ​ ( s ⁡ ( p ) ) &gt; − ∞ E_{p}(s(p))&gt;-\\infty or else we couldn’t have E p ​ ( s ⁡ ( q ) ) &lt; E p ​ ( s ⁡ ( p ) ) E_{p}(s(q))&lt;E_{p}(s(p)) for a different probability q q . Then by regularity of p p it follows that s ⁡ ( p ) s(p) is finite. Let s s be any continuous strictly proper everywhere-finite accuracy scoring rule. Choose any α ∈ [ − ∞ , M ] \\alpha\\in[-\\infty,M] such that α ≤ s ​ ( p ) ​ ( ω ) \\alpha\\leq s(p)(\\omega) for all p ∈ 𝒫 p\\in\\mathcal{P} and ω ∈ Ω \\omega\\in\\Omega . Define a scoring rule s α s_{\\alpha} as follows. If either p ∉ 𝒫 p\\notin\\mathcal{P} or p ∈ ℛ p\\in\\mathcal{R} , let s α ​ ( p ) = s ​ ( p ) s_{\\alpha}(p)=s(p) . If p ∈ 𝒫 \\ ℛ p\\in\\mathcal{P}\\backslash\\mathcal{R} , then let s α ​ ( p ) ​ ( ω ) = s ⁡ ( p ) ​ ( ω ) s_{\\alpha}(p)(\\omega)=s(p)(\\omega) if p ⁡ ( { ω } ) &gt; 0 p(\\{\\omega\\})&gt;0 , and s α ​ ( p ) ​ ( ω ) = α s_{\\alpha}(p)(\\omega)=\\alpha if p ⁡ ( { ω } ) = 0 p(\\{\\omega\\})=0 . In other words, we have tweaked s s so that in the case where the forecast assigns zero probability to some outcome ω \\omega , we assign α \\alpha there. It is easy to see that s α s_{\\alpha} is strictly proper because E p ​ s α ​ ( p ) = E p ​ s ​ ( p ) E_{p}s_{\\alpha}(p)=E_{p}s(p) and E p ​ s α ​ ( q ) ≤ E q ​ s ​ ( q ) E_{p}s_{\\alpha}(q)\\leq E_{q}s(q) for all p , q ∈ 𝒫 p,q\\in\\mathcal{P} . For a fixed ω \\omega , the set of possible values of s ​ ( p ) ​ ( ω ) s(p)(\\omega) is contained in a finite interval, since a continuous function on a compact set takes on a compact set of values, and 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} is compact. Thus we can choose α \\alpha so that α &lt; s ​ ( c ) ​ ( ω ) \\alpha&lt;s(c)(\\omega) for all ω \\omega , letting α \\alpha be − ∞ -\\infty or a finite value as we wish. Then s α s_{\\alpha} will be discontinuous everywhere on 𝒫 \\ ℛ \\mathcal{P}\\backslash\\mathcal{R} , since s α s_{\\alpha} is discontinuous wherever it differs from s s . And if α \\alpha is finite, then s α s_{\\alpha} will be everywhere finite as well. We now show that s α s_{\\alpha} satisfies the conditions of Theorem 1 . First, s s satisfies condition (b) by Proposition 1 , and hence it satisfies condition (a) by the Theorem. Now let s α ′ s_{\\alpha}^{\\prime} be a quasi-strictly proper extension of s α s_{\\alpha} to 𝒞 \\mathcal{C} . Define s ′ ​ ( c ) = s ​ ( c ) s^{\\prime}(c)=s(c) for c ∈ 𝒫 c\\in\\mathcal{P} and s ′ ​ ( c ) = s α ′ ​ ( c ) s^{\\prime}(c)=s_{\\alpha}^{\\prime}(c) for c ∉ 𝒫 c\\notin\\mathcal{P} . Then s ′ s^{\\prime} is quasi-strictly proper, because for any c ∉ 𝒫 c\\notin\\mathcal{P} and p ∈ 𝒫 p\\in\\mathcal{P} we have E p ​ s ′ ​ ( c ) = E p ​ s α ′ ​ ( c ) &lt; E p ​ s α ​ ( p ) = E p ​ s ​ ( p ) = E p ​ s ′ ​ ( p ) , E_{p}s^{\\prime}(c)=E_{p}s_{\\alpha}^{\\prime}(c)&lt;E_{p}s_{\\alpha}(p)=E_{p}s(p)=E_{p}s^{\\prime}(p), where the first inequality follows from the quasi-strict propriety of s α ′ s_{\\alpha}^{\\prime} . Thus, because s s satisfies condition (a), for any c ∈ 𝒫 c\\in\\mathcal{P} there is a p ∈ 𝒫 p\\in\\mathcal{P} such that s ′ ​ ( c ) s^{\\prime}(c) is strictly dominated by s ′ ​ ( p ) s^{\\prime}(p) . Since s ′ s^{\\prime} is continuous on 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} and ℛ \\mathcal{R} is dense in 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} 