o account, possibly after some rescaling to the Seeger score. Newcomer Progression For the ELO numbers in Chess it is well-established fact that younger, or newer players get a larger K K , value to be able to grow in their experience and climb up the ranking more quickly. With a larger number of games and ELO value, the factor then decreases (from say K = 40 K=40 to K = 10 K=10 ). This also compensates for an wrong assumption on the playing strength in the initial ELO. Experiments The ELO ranking routine is embedded in our Skat AI [ 12 , 13 , 14 ] written in C++, compiled with gcc version 4.9.2 (optimization level -O2 ). As the formulas are straight-forward, we strongly believe that a Skat web application with a world-wide ranking list similar to 2700chess.com can be derived easily. For the time being our linux executable program runs on 1 core of an Intel Xeon Gold 6140 CPU @ 2.30GHz. ELO Ranking of Human Players We implemented the proposed ELO System for the game of Skat. As the input we selected a database of 83844 83844 expert games, played on a server in 2329 2329 series (tables) of 36 36 games each. The data we process has anonymous (alias hashed, blind) player names and table indices, to avoid options for giving advantage to known Skat players. For this series of human games, we derived a proper ELO ranking, according to the above formulas and different parameterization for K K , the hand strength function or the inclusion of reducing the effect of card luck. On our computer this analysis takes about 4 ​ s 4s mainly due to computing the winning probabilities. The outcome is a ELO highscore table. Tables IV and V show the derived list. No P-No Player Name (hashed) Elo 1 P-2 550d2996ded442172116845433d09570 856.77 2 P-25 bb01edd8abe48241a43b81af3895c925 854.80 3 P-12 542e18999d44ec8427481bf9b75c4bc6 853.42 4 P-27 693730a9db9143b7fc0a37d948b6fb76 853.32 5 P-31 e243ef566bac2607033c320e56ca3c 853.06 ⋮ ⋮ ⋮ TABLE IV: ELO ranking for Skat on sample with K = 0.02 K=0