n}\\quad x=0,\\\\ \\log(\\frac{p_{1}}{p_{2}})&amp;\\quad\\text{when}\\quad x=1.\\end{cases} (8) In principle, if the expected value of Δ \\Delta is positive we tend to prefer the first forecast, vice versa if it is negative. Considering the observation as a Bernoulli random variable X ∼ Ber ​ ( p ∗ ) X\\sim\\text{Ber}(p^{*}) , the difference Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) \\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X) is also a binary random variable, assuming the values Δ 0 = Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , 0 ) \\Delta_{0}=\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},0) , Δ 1 = Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , 1 ) \\Delta_{1}=\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},1) with probabilities 1 − p ∗ 1-p^{*} and p ∗ p^{*} . The distribution of Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) \\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X) is therefore completely determined by the distribution of X X : Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) = X ​ Δ 1 + ( 1 − X ) ​ Δ 0 = Δ 0 + X ⁡ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) . \\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X)=X\\Delta_{1}+(1-X)\\Delta_{0}=\\Delta_{0}+X(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0}). (9) It follows that the expected value and variance of Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) \\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X) are determined by the properties of X X : 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) ] \\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X)] = Δ 0 + 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ] ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) = Δ 0 + p ∗ ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) \\displaystyle=\\Delta_{0}+\\mathbb{E}[X](\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0})=\\Delta_{0}+p^{*}(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0}) (10) 𝕍 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) ] \\displaystyle\\mathbb{V}[\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X)] = 𝕍 ⁡ [ X ] ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) 2 = p ∗ ​ ( 1 − p ∗ ) ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) 2 \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{V}[X](\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0})^{2}=p^{*}(1-p^{*})(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0})^{2} (11) We can give an alternative definition of the properness based on the random variable Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) \\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X) . In fact, a scoring rule S S is said to be proper if 𝔼 X ​ [ Δ ⁡ ( p , p ∗ , X ) ] ≤ 0 \\mathbb{E}_{X}[\\Delta(p,p^{*},X)]\\leq 0 when p ≠ p ∗ p\\neq p^{*} , no forecast have an expected score higher than the data generating model p ∗ p^{*} . However, they can achieve the same score. S S is strictly proper if 𝔼 X ​ [ Δ ] = 0 \\mathbb{E}_{X}[\\Delta]=0 if and only if p = p ∗ p=p^{*} , the highest score, on average, is achieved only by the data generating model. The definition implies, also, that proper scoring rules are invariant under linear transformations, in the sense that, a linear transformation of a proper score yields another proper score and the operation does not change the ranking. Figure 1 reports the expected score difference between a candidate forecast p p and the true value p ∗ = 0.001 p^{*}=0.001 using the Brier and the log score. The value p ∗ = 0.001 p^{*}=0.001 was chosen to be comparable to the estimated probability of having an event with magnitude greater than 5.5 calculated the days before the L’Aquila earthquake in the neighbourhood of where it struck [Fig. 4 in Marzocchi and Lombardi 2009 ]. To enable a visual comparison, the expected Brier score values have been normalized to match the curvature of the log score when p = p ∗ p=p^{*} . This is done by multiplying the expected Brier score values by the ratio of the second derivatives of the two expected scores calculated at p = p ∗ p=p^{*} . The proper score scale invariance ensures that the ranking obtained using the original and normalized version of the Brier score is unchanged. Both expected score differences are uniquely maximized at p = p ∗ p=p^{*} which means that the forecast matching the true probability has the highest expected score. This is an easy way to assess if a scoring rule for binary outcomes is proper or not. Furthermore, Figure 1 offers an example of how different scores penalize differently the same forecasts. The log score is asymmetric and takes into account the relative differences between the forecasts (equation 8 ), and if p ∗ ≠ 0 p^{*}\\neq 0 the expected score for p = { 0 , 1 } p=\\{0,1\\} is − ∞ -\\infty . This means that the log score heavily penalizes overconfidence in the forecast. The Brier score, instead, considers the absolute difference between forecasts (equation 7 ) resulting in a symmetric distribution. For example, using the Brier score, a forecast p = 0 p=0 will be preferred to any forecast in ( 2 ​ p ∗ , 1 ) (2p^{*},1) , for any p ∗ &lt; 1 / 2 p^{*}&lt;1/2 . The choice of score, and consequently the style of penalty, should reflect the task at hand. Predicting p = 0 , 1 p=0,1 means that we are absolutely certain about the outcome of X X . If the forecasts under evaluation are planned to be used in an alarm based system, for which an alarm is broadcasted if the probability is above or below a certain threshold, being overconfident may put lives at risk and perhaps the log score would be the right choice in this situation. On the other hand, the Brier score may be suitable when being overconfident is less dangerous. This example illustrates the flexibility of proper scores and how important it is to choose the right one depending on the purposes of the forecast under evaluation. Figure 1: Differences in the scores expected value for a generic value of the forecast p p and the optimal forecast p = p ∗ p=p^{*} , namely 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ] = S E ​ ( p | p ∗ ) − S E ​ ( p ∗ | p ∗ ) \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta]=S^{E}(p|p^{*})-S^{E}(p^{*}|p^{*}) , in the case p ∗ = 0.001 p^{*}=0.001 . Panel (a) p ∈ ( 0 , 0.006 ] p\\in(0,0.006] , Panel (b) p ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) p\\in(0,1) . The expected Brier score have been normalised to match the curvature of the log score when p = p ∗ p=p^{*} . 3 Improper scores Scores which are not proper are called improper. Being improper means that a model may exist with expected score greater than the data generating model. In the specific case of probabilistic forecasts for binary events, a score is improper if it is biased towards models which systematically under/overestimate the true probability p ∗ p^{*} . In the context of earthquake forecasting experiments we do not know the true value of p ∗ p^{*} . Therefore, it is crucial to use proper scoring rules for which we are sure that, at least on average, they will prefer the closest model to the data generating one. Improper scoring rules do not have this property, which implies that the smallest or the largest (or any other) forecast, on average, could achieve the highest score. This is in clear contrast with the aim of any forecasting experiment. Below, we demonstrate that the parimutuel gambling score ( Zhuang 2010 ; Zechar and Zhuang 2014 ) is an example of a scoring rule which is proper only in a specific situation and not in general. 3.1 Definition of the parimutuel gambling score The parimutuel gambling score was designed to rank forecasting models for binary events and was applied to rank earthquake forecasting models in CSEP experiments ( Taroni et al. 2018 ; Zechar and Zhuang 2010 ) . Initially, it was used to compare models against a reference model ( Zhuang 2010 ) , which is improper. Later, it was generalized to compare models against each other simultaneously ( Zechar and Zhuang 2014 ) , the case with only two players is the special case for which the score is proper, all the others are not. The score is based on a gambling scheme in which the forecasting models play the role of the gamblers and, for each observation, they obtain a reward proportional to the probability assigned by the gambler to the event occurring. In particular, it is a zero-sum game, in the sense that bids and rewards in each bin sum to zero, which makes the parimutuel gambling score relative to one forecast dependent on the other forecasts. In contract to the Brier and log scores, it is not possible to define the parimutuel gambling score using the form S ⁡ ( p | x ) S(p|x) because it needs at least two forecasts to be evaluated and is a function of them all. Given a set of k k forecasts 𝐩 = ( p 1 , … , p k ) \\mathbf{p}=(p_{1},...,p_{k}) , we define S G ​ ( 𝐩 | x ) S_{G}(\\mathbf{p}|x) as the vector such that the i i -th component, S G , i ​ ( 𝐩 | x ) S_{G,i}(\\mathbf{p}|x) , is given by the parimutuel gambling score of the i i -th forecast, given x x has been observed. In the case of the Brier and log score the components of the vector S ⁡ ( 𝐩 | x ) S(\\mathbf{p}|x) are defined independently, in the case of the parimutuel gambling score they have to be defined jointly. Let p ¯ \\bar{p} be the average probability involved in the gambling scheme, namely p ¯ = ∑ i = 1 k p i / k \\bar{p}=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}p_{i}/k . The parimutuel gambling score relative to the i i -th forecast is defined as S G , i ​ ( 𝐩 | x ) = { p i p ¯ − 1 , x = 1 , 1 − p i 1 − p ¯ − 1 , x = 0 . S_{G,i}(\\mathbf{p}|x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{p_{i}}{\\overline{p}}-1,\\quad&amp;x=1,\\\\ \\frac{1-p_{i}}{1-\\overline{p}}-1,\\quad&amp;x=0.\\end{cases} The above expression is a zero-sum game, meaning that ∑ i S G , i ​ ( 𝐩 | x ) = 0 \\sum_{i}S_{G,i}(\\mathbf{p}|x)=0 , therefore the rewards may be positive or negative. Each gambler obtains a positive reward if and only if they assign a greater probability to the observed event than the average gambler involved in the game. Vice versa, the reward is negative if the probability is smaller. The expected value with respect the true probability p ∗ p^{*} is given by S G , i E ​ ( 𝐩 | p ∗ ) \\displaystyle S_{G,i}^{E}(\\mathbf{p}|p^{*}) = p ∗ ​ ( p i p ¯ − 1 ) + ( 1 − p ∗ ) ​ ( 1 − p i 1 − p ¯ − 1 ) , \\displaystyle=p^{*}\\left(\\frac{p_{i}}{\\bar{p}}-1\\right)+(1-p^{*})\\left(\\frac{1-p_{i}}{1-\\bar{p}}-1\\right), = p ∗ ​ p i p ¯ + ( 1 − p ∗ ) ​ ( 1 − p i ) 1 − p ¯ − 1 , \\displaystyle=\\frac{p^{*}p_{i}}{\\bar{p}}+\\frac{(1-p^{*})(1-p_{i})}{1-\\bar{p}}-1, = ( p i − p ¯ ) ​ ( p ∗ − p ¯ ) p ¯ ​ ( 1 − p ¯ ) . \\displaystyle=\\frac{(p_{i}-\\bar{p})\\left(p^{*}-\\bar{p}\\right)}{\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}. (12) The denominator involves all the probabilities in the game which demonstrates the interdependence with all other forecasts and complicates the study of the derivatives. However, it is still possible to prove that the gambling score is strictly proper when k = 2 k=2 . In this case, 𝐩 = ( p 1 , p