 = p ∗ p_{1}=p^{*} does not maximize S G , i E ​ ( 𝐩 | p ∗ ) S_{G,i}^{E}(\\mathbf{p}|p^{*}) as shown in Figure 2 . This means that the score becomes biased when we rank forecasts based on the score difference against a reference model. Figure 2: Expected value of the parimutuel gambling score (k = 2), S G , 1 E ​ ( 𝐩 | p ∗ ) S^{E}_{G,1}(\\mathbf{p}|p^{*}) , varying p 1 ∈ ( 0 , 0.004 ) p_{1}\\in(0,0.004) , p 2 = { p ∗ , 2 p ∗ , 4 p ∗ p_{2}=\\{p^{*},2p^{*},4p^{*} } (a) and p 2 = { p ∗ , p ∗ / 2 , p ∗ / 4 } p_{2}=\\{p^{*},p^{*}/2,p^{*}/4\\} (b). The solid vertical line represents the true probability p ∗ = 0.001 p^{*}=0.001 . The expected scores have been normalized so that their minimum is equal to -1. Formally, we are considering pairwise vectors 𝐩 1 = ( p 1 , p 0 ) \\mathbf{p}_{1}=(p_{1},p_{0}) , 𝐩 2 = ( p 2 , p 0 ) \\mathbf{p}_{2}=(p_{2},p_{0}) , etc., where p 0 p_{0} is the reference model. For each of these we can estimate pairwise comparison score vectors S G ​ ( 𝐩 1 | x ) S_{G}(\\mathbf{p}_{1}|x) , S G ​ ( 𝐩 2 | x ) S_{G}(\\mathbf{p}_{2}|x) , and so on. The first component of each vector, namely S G , 1 ​ ( 𝐩 1 | x ) S_{G,1}(\\mathbf{p}_{1}|x) , S G , 1 ​ ( 𝐩 2 | x ) S_{G,1}(\\mathbf{p}_{2}|x) , etc, represents the score of p 1 p_{1} and, respectively, p 2 p_{2} against the reference model p 0 p_{0} . At this point, one would be tempted to rank the models based on S G , 1 ​ ( 𝐩 1 | x ) S_{G,1}(\\mathbf{p}_{1}|x) and S G , 1 ​ ( 𝐩 2 | x ) S_{G,1}(\\mathbf{p}_{2}|x) , and this is the approach taken in Taroni et al. 2014 in which the official national time-independent model ( Gruppo di Lavoro 2004 ) is used as reference model. If the parimutuel gambling score is used to rank forecasts based on the score difference relative to a reference model, it will not reliably favour the model closest to the true one, and the size of the bias will depend on the choice of the reference model. For example, in Figure 2 a, for p 2 = 0.004 p_{2}=0.004 , the gambling score is maximized at p 1 = 0 p_{1}=0 . This means that if the reference model is p 0 = 0.004 p_{0}=0.004 , the overconfident forecast p 1 = 0 p_{1}=0 would be favoured by the ranking even if another forecast is perfect, e.g. p 3 = p ∗ p_{3}=p^{*} . This problem can be particularly relevant in operational seismology where it is common for candidate forecasts to be compared against a reference model which is known to be based on simplistic assumptions (for example a homogeneous Poisson process). Hereon, the term pairwise gambling score refers to the comparison against a reference model as described in this section, while the term full gambling score will refer to the case where the forecasts compete directly against each other as we describe in the next section. Using this terminology, the full gambling score with k = 2 k=2 is the only proper score. 3.3 Improperness of the multi-forecast gambling score for 𝒌 ≥ 𝟑 \\boldsymbol{k\\geq 3} The generalized version of the full parimutuel gambling score, as presented in Zechar and Zhuang 2014 , for k ≥ 3 k\\geq 3 is improper. For example, when k = 3 k=3 and p 2 = p ∗ p_{2}=p^{*} , following equation 12 the difference between the expected score for p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} is given by 3 ​ p ¯ ​ ( 1 − p ¯ ) ​ [ S G , 1 E ​ ( p 1 , p ∗ , p 3 | p ∗ ) − S G , 2 E ​ ( p 1 , p ∗ , p 3 | p ∗ ) ] \\displaystyle 3\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})[S^{E}_{G,1}(p_{1},p^{*},p_{3}|p^{*})-S^{E}_{G,2}(p_{1},p^{*},p_{3}|p^{*})] = 3 ​ ( p 1 − p ∗ ) ​ ( p ∗ − p ¯ ) \\displaystyle=3(p_{1}-p^{*})(p^{*}-\\bar{p}) = ( p 1 − p ∗ ) ​ ( 2 ​ p ∗ − p 1 − p 3 ) , \\displaystyle=(p_{1}-p^{*})(2p^{*}-p_{1}-p_{3}), with both sides scaled by the common factor 3 ​ p ¯ ​ ( 1 − p ¯ ) 3\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p}) . This means that when 2 ​ p ∗ − p 3 ≥ p 1 2p^{*}-p_{3}\\geq p_{1} , the forecast p 1 p_{1} will have a positive score for any p 1 ≥ p ∗ p_{1}\\geq p^{*} . Any value of p 1 ∈ [ p ∗ , 2 ​ p ∗ − p 3 ] p_{1}\\in[p^{*},2p^{*}-p_{3}] will be preferred to p 2 p_{2} that is equal to p ∗ p^{*} . When 2 ​ p ∗ − p 3 ≤ p 1 2p^{*}-p_{3}\\leq p_{1} , with the same reasoning, p 1 p_{1} is preferred over p 2 = p ∗ p_{2}=p^{*} in the interval [ 2 ​ p ∗ − p 3 , p ∗ ] [2p^{*}-p_{3},p^{*}] . In Figure 3 we consider k = 3 k=3 , p ∗ = p 2 = 0.001 p^{*}=p_{2}=0.001 and report the difference between the expected scores of p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} , namely S G , 1 E ​ ( 𝐩 | p ∗ ) − S G , 2 E ​ ( 𝐩 | p ∗ ) S^{E}_{G,1}(\\mathbf{p}|p^{*})-S^{E}_{G,2}(\\mathbf{p}|p^{*}) , for different values of p 3 p_{3} . The expected score difference is not maximize at p 1 = p ∗ p_{1}=p^{*} , which means that the score is biased, and the \"direction\" of the bias depends on p 3 p_{3} being greater than or equal to p ∗ p^{*} . Figure 3: Expected gambling score differences (k = 3) between p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} , S G , 1 E ​ ( 𝐩 | p ∗ ) − S G , 2 E ​ ( 𝐩 | p ∗ ) S^{E}_{G,1}(\\mathbf{p}|p^{*})-S^{E}_{G,2}(\\mathbf{p}|p^{*}) , as a function of p 1 ∈ ( 0 , 0.004 ) p_{1}\\in(0,0.004) , p 2 = p ∗ = 0.001 p_{2}=p^{*}=0.001 (vertical line), and p 3 ∈ { p ∗ , p ∗ / 2 , p ∗ / 3 } p_{3}\\in\\{p^{*},p^{*}/2,p^{*}/3\\} (a) and p 3 ∈ { p ∗ , 2 ​ p ∗ , 3 ​ p ∗ } p_{3}\\in\\{p^{*},2p^{*},3p^{*}\\} (b) . Consider k &gt; 3 k&gt;3 gamblers who propose probabilities 𝐩 = { p 1 , … , p k } \\mathbf{p}=\\{p_{1},...,p_{k}\\} . It is helpful to consider the vector of probabilities that excludes the first component; we name this 𝐩 − 1 = 𝐩 / { p 1 } \\mathbf{p}_{-1}=\\mathbf{p}/\\{p_{1}\\} and its mean p ¯ − 1 \\bar{p}_{-1} . Assuming p 2 = p ∗ p_{2}=p^{*} , thus 𝐩 = ( p 1 , p ∗ , … , p k ) \\mathbf{p}=(p_{1},p^{*},...,p_{k}) , we have that k ​ p ¯ ​ ( 1 − p ¯ ) ​ [ S G , 1 E ​ ( 𝐩 | p ∗ ) − S G , 2 E ​ ( 𝐩 | p ∗ ) ] \\displaystyle k\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})[S^{E}_{G,1}(\\mathbf{p}|p^{*})-S^{E}_{G,2}(\\mathbf{p}|p^{*})] = k ⁡ ( p 1 − p ∗ ) ​ ( p ∗ − p ¯ ) , \\displaystyle=k(p_{1}-p^{*})(p^{*}-\\bar{p}), = ( p 1 − p ∗ ) ​ [ k ​ p ∗ − p 1 − ( k − 1 ) ​ p ¯ − 1 ] , \\displaystyle=(p_{1}-p^{*})[kp^{*}-p_{1}-(k-1)\\bar{p}_{-1}], from which we conclude that the expected score difference is positive when p 1 ∈ [ k ​ p ∗ − ( k − 1 ) ​ p ¯ − 1 , p ∗ ] p_{1}\\in[kp^{*}-(k-1)\\bar{p}_{-1},p^{*}] or p 1 ∈ [ p ∗ , k ​ p ∗ − ( k − 1 ) ​ p ¯ − 1 ] p_{1}\\in[p^{*},kp^{*}-(k-1)\\bar{p}_{-1}] , depending on if p ∗ ≶ p ¯ − 1 p^{*}\\lessgtr\\bar{p}_{-1} . Specifically, when p ∗ &gt; p ¯ − 1 p^{*}&gt;\\bar{p}_{-1} (Figure 3 a) the first gambler is encouraged to bet on a p 1 &gt; p ∗ p_{1}&gt;p^{*} and vice versa when p ∗ &lt; p ¯ − 1 p^{*}&lt;\\bar{p}_{-1} (Figure 3 b). Furthermore, p ∗ p^{*} is always an extreme of the interval where the expected score difference is positive. Considering p ¯ − 1 \\bar{p}_{-1} as fixed, the length of the interval is an increasing function of the number of gamblers k k (Figure 4 ) which means that the size of the set of forecasts capable of obtaining a score value higher than the data generating model is an increasing function of the number of forecasts involved in the comparison. Figure 4: Expected gambling score differences ( k ∈ { 3 , 5 , 10 , 20 } k\\in\\{3,5,10,20\\} ) between p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} as a function of p 1 p_{1} considering p 2 = p ∗ = 0.001 p_{2}=p^{*}=0.001 (vertical line). The averageof the forecast probabilities (excluding the first forecast) is the constant p ¯ − 1 = p ∗ / 2 \\bar{p}_{-1}=p^{*}/2 (a) and p ¯ − 1 = 2 ​ p ∗ \\bar{p}_{-1}=2p^{*} (b). It is clear that the multi-forecast parimutuel gambling score favours models that are contrary to the average of the other forecasts. This could be particularly dangerous when evaluating the performance of earthquake forecasting models. For example, the trigger for an alarm being broadcast (or not) is often defined when the probability of having an earthquake above a certain magnitude exceeds a specified threshold. Using a model chosen looking at the full parimutuel gambling score could therefore lead to broadcasting alarms when they are not needed ( p ≫ p ∗ p\\gg p^{*} , ’crying wolf’, Figure 4 a) or not broadcasting an alarm when needed ( p ≪ p ∗ p\\ll p^{*} , providing ’false reassurance’, Figure 4 b). The root of the problems with this score is that the score, relative to a candidate forecast, explicitly depends on the other forecasts. This design brings two problems: (i) the score, even in the special case when it is proper, can be used improperly and (ii) the score is never proper when considering more than two models. The Brier and log scores do not suffer from the same problem since the score of a forecast depends only on the forecast and the observation. Furthermore, the improperness demonstrated here can be expressed in terms that show that the gambling metaphor is part of the problem: If the outcome x = 0 x=0 is likely (e.g. p ∗ = 0.001 p^{*}=0.001 ) and the majority of the forecasts have too large probabilities, then the expected gain is higher for an overconfident forecast, p ≪ p ∗ p\\ll p^{*} , since that will give the forecaster a larger share of the total payout. 4 Forecasting across multiple bins Until now, we have analysed the expected score for a single bin, here we analyse the ability to express a preference between two forecasts using the average score across multiple bins. We assume to have access only to one observation x i ∈ { 0 , 1 } x_{i}\\in\\{0,1\\} per bin . We analyse extensively the case in which the probability of observing x i = 1 x_{i}=1 , is the same for each bin, p i ∗ = p ∗ p^{*}_{i}=p^{*} for any i = 1 , … , N i=1,...,N . We refer to this as the Multiple Bins Single Probability case; the only quantity of interest is p ∗ p^{*} and a forecast is represented by a single value p p . Even though, the Multiple Bins Single Probability case is clearly unrealistic in practice, it builds the basic concepts we will then use to explore the Multiple Bins Multiple Probabilities case where the probability of observing x i = 1 x_{i}=1 is potentially different for each bin. Considering multiple bins, we observe a realization of the random variable X i ∼ Ber ​ ( p i ∗ ) X_{i}\\sim\\text{Ber}(p^{*}_{i}) for i = 1 , … , N i=1,...,N . A forecast is given by the vector 𝐩 = ( p 1 , … , p N ) \\mathbf{p}=(p_{1},...,p_{N}) specifying the probability of X i = 1 X_{i}=1 for each bin. Following the terminology in the literature regarding Bernoulli random variables, the event X i = 1 X_{i}=1 is referred to as a success . The quantity X S = ∑ i X i X_{S}=\\sum_{i}X_{i} is therefore referred as the sum of the observations or the number of successes or the number of active bins. Given an arbitrary scoring rule S ⁡ ( p | X ) S(p|X) , the average score associated with the forecast 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} is given by: S ⁡ ( 𝐩 | 𝐗 ) = 1 N ​ ∑ i = 1 N S ⁡ ( p i | X i ) . S(\\mathbf{p}|\\mathbf{X})=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}S(p_{i}|X_{i}). The quantity S ⁡ ( 𝐩 | 𝐗 ) S(\\mathbf{p}|\\mathbf{X}) is a random variable itself, because it is a function of random variables 𝐗 \\mathbf{X} . To compare two forecasts 𝐩 1 \\mathbf{p}_{1} and 𝐩 2 \\mathbf{p}_{2} , we study their score difference: Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐗 ) \\displaystyle\\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{X}) = 1 N ​ ( ∑ i = 1 N S ⁡ ( p 1 ​ i | X i ) − ∑ i = 1 N S ⁡ ( p 2 ​ i | X i ) ) , \\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{N}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{N}S(p_{1i}|X_{i})-\\sum_{i=1}^{N}S(p_{2i}|X_{i})\\right), = 1 N ​ ∑ i = 1 N Δ ⁡ ( p 1 ​ i , p 2 ​ i , X i ) . \\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\Delta(p_{1i},p_{2i},X_{i}). The quantity Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐗 ) \\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{X}) is also a random variable as it too depends on the vector of random variables 𝐗 \\mathbf{X} . If S ⁡ ( p | X ) S(p|X) is a proper scoring rule, and if the expected value of the score difference is positive, namely 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐗 ) ] &gt; 0 \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{X})]&gt;0 , the forecast 𝐩 1 \\mathbf{p}_{1} is \"closer\" to the true 𝐩 ∗ \\mathbf{p}^{*} than the alternative forecast 𝐩 2 \\mathbf{p}_{2} . The expected value should be considered with respect the distribution of the observations 𝐗 \\mathbf{X} . However, we do not observe the full distribution - we only observe a sample (i.e. we observe the quantity Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐱 ) \\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{x}) which is a realization of the random variable Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐗 ) \\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{X}) ). Even if the expected score difference 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐗 ) ] \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{X})] is positive, which means that we should express a preference for the first forecast, the observed score difference Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐱 ) \\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{x}) may be negative and lead to the opposite conclusion. To avoid this problem we need to account for the uncertainty around the observed Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐱 ) \\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{x}) which is the point estimate of the expected score difference 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐗 ) ] \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{X})] . 4.1 The distribution of score differences - Multiple Bins Single Probability For the Multiple Bins Single Probability case, the observation in each bin is a binary random variable X i ∼ Ber ​ ( p ∗ ) X_{i}\\sim\\text{Ber}(p^{*}) , i = 1 , … , N i=1,...,N . Given an arbitrary scoring rule S ⁡ ( p | X ) S(p|X) and two candidate forecasts p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} , the score difference for the i i -th bin is a discrete random variable with distribution: Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X i ) = { Δ 0 = S ⁡ ( p 1 | 0 ) − S ⁡ ( p 2 | 0 ) with probability 1 − p ∗ , Δ 1 = S ⁡ ( p 1 | 1 ) − S ⁡ ( p 2 | 1 ) with probability p ∗ . \\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X_{i})=\\begin{cases}\\Delta_{0}=S(p_{1}|0)-S(p_{2}|0)\\quad\\quad\\text{with probability}\\quad 1-p^{*},\\\\ \\Delta_{1}=S(p_{1}|1)-S(p_{2}|1)\\quad\\quad\\text{with probability}\\quad p^{*}.\\end{cases} The forecasts are ranked based on the average score difference across all bins: Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐗 ) \\displaystyle\\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{X}) = 1 N ​ ∑ i = 1 N Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X i ) , \\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X_{i}), = 1 N ​ ∑ i = 1 N ( Δ 0 + X i ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) ) , \\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\left(\\Delta_{0}+X_{i}(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0})\\right), = Δ 0 + X S N ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) , \\displaystyle=\\Delta_{0}+\\frac{X_{S}}{N}(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0}), where, X S = ∑ i X i X_{S}=\\sum_{i}X_{i} is the sum of all observations or, equivalently, the total number of successes. By definition, X S X_{S} is the sum of N N (assumed to be) independent and identically distributed Bernoulli trials X i X_{i} . Therefore, X S X_{S} has a Binomial distribution with size parameter N N , the number of bins, and probability parameter p ∗ p^{*} . When we observe a sample x 1 , … , x N x_{1},...,x_{N} , the observed score difference is given by: Δ ⁡ ( 𝐩 1 , 𝐩 2 , 𝐱 ) = Δ 0 + x S N ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) , \\Delta(\\mathbf{p}_{1},\\mathbf{p}_{2},\\mathbf{x})=\\Delta_{0}+\\frac{\\text{x}_{S}}{N}(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0}), where x S \\text{x}_{S} is a realization of the random variable X S X_{S} . The observed score difference depends on the observations only through the quantity x S / N \\text{x}_{S}/N . Thus, it is enough to study the quantity x S / N \\text{x}_{S}/N to make inference about the expected value of the score difference. The quantity x S / N \\text{x}_{S}/N it is said to be sufficient ( Fisher 1922 ) with respect to the expected score difference because it contains all the information provided by the observations x 1 , … , x N x_{1},...,x_{N} on the parameter of interest (in this case, the expected score difference 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) ] \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X)] ). For an introduction to statistical inference and the theory behind we refer to ( Schervish 2012 ; Hastie et al. 2009 ) . 4.2 Confidence Intervals for the Expected Score Difference A way to account for the uncertainty around the observed score difference is to consider an interval estimate of the expected value of the score difference. Once a sample 𝐱 = x 1 , … , x N \\mathbf{x}=x_{1},...,x_{N} has been observed and the confidence interval calculated, if the entire interval lies above zero we express a preference towards p 1 p_{1} , alternatively if it lies below zero we express a preference towards p 2 p_{2} . If the interval contains the value zero we conclude that the observed sample does not contain enough information to express a preference. It is important to consider the latter case as a possible outcome because it is an indication that we need to collect more data or that the forecasts perform similarly (as measured by the score) and provide an additional information than the pure rankings. We are considering the confidence interval for the expected value of the score difference: 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , 𝐗 ) ] \\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},\\mathbf{X})] = Δ 0 + 𝔼 ⁡ [ X S ] N ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) , \\displaystyle=\\Delta_{0}+\\frac{\\mathbb{E}[X_{S}]}{N}(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0}), = Δ 0 + p ∗ ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) . \\displaystyle=\\Delta_{0}+p^{*}(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0}). (13) Having an observation 𝐱 = x 1 , … , x N \\mathbf{x}=x_{1},...,x_{N} per bin, the point estimate of 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) ] \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X)] is the observed score difference: Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , 𝐱 ) = Δ 0 + p ^ ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) , \\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},\\mathbf{x})=\\Delta_{0}+\\hat{p}(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0}), (14) where p ^ = x S / N \\hat{p}=\\text{x}_{S}/N is the observed probability of success. Comparing the equations 13 and 14 , the point estimate of the score difference is retrieved plugging in the point estimate of the probability of success p ^ \\hat{p} in place of p ∗ p^{*} . In the same way, to retrieve an interval estimate of the expected score difference is sufficient to retrieve an interval estimate of the probability of success p ∗ p^{*} . Therefore, we need the confidence interval of level α \\alpha for the true probability p ∗ p^{*} given observations x 1 , … , x N x_{1},...,x_{N} from a Ber ( p ∗ ) (p^{*}) , namely C ​ I p ∗ ​ ( α ) = ( p ^ L , p ^ U ) CI_{p^{*}}(\\alpha)=(\\hat{p}_{L},\\hat{p}_{U}) , and plug those values into expression 14 to obtain a confidence interval for 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , X ) ] \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},X)] , namely C ​ I Δ ​ ( α ) = ( Δ L , Δ U ) CI_{\\Delta}(\\alpha)=(\\Delta_{L},\\Delta_{U}) . Various methods have been found to estimate p ^ L \\hat{p}_{L} and p ^ U \\hat{p}_{U} , most of them relying on a Gaussian approximation. However, this approximation is not reliable for small sample sizes (number of bins N N ) and for values of p ∗ p^{*} close to zero or one, as in our case ( Wallis 2013 ) . Hereafter, we use the Clopper-Pearson confidence interval ( Clopper and Pearson 1934 ) . This method is referred to as exact because it relies on cumulative binomial probabilities rather than an approximation and is therefore more efficient and accurate than simulation based methods. The confidence interval with level α \\alpha for p ^ \\hat{p} is given by: p L ​ ( α ) \\displaystyle p_{L}(\\alpha) = BetaQ ​ ( α 2 , x S , N − x S + 1 ) , \\displaystyle=\\text{BetaQ}(\\frac{\\alpha}{2};\\text{x}_{S},N-\\text{x}_{S}+1), p U ​ ( α ) \\displaystyle p_{U}(\\alpha) = BetaQ ​ ( 1 − α 2 , x S + 1 , N − x S ) , \\displaystyle=\\text{BetaQ}(1-\\frac{\\alpha}{2};\\text{x}_{S}+1,N-\\text{x}_{S}), where the function BetaQ ​ ( q , a , b ) \\text{BetaQ}(q;a,b) is the q q -th quantile of a Beta distribution with parameters a a and b b . We can construct confidence intervals for 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , 𝐗 ) ] \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},\\mathbf{X})] as follows: Δ L \\displaystyle\\Delta_{L} = Δ 0 + p ^ L ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) , \\displaystyle=\\Delta_{0}+\\hat{p}_{L}(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0}), Δ U \\displaystyle\\Delta_{U} = Δ 0 + p ^ U ​ ( Δ 1 − Δ 0 ) . \\displaystyle=\\Delta_{0}+\\hat{p}_{U}(\\Delta_{1}-\\Delta_{0}). The obtained confidence interval for p ∗ p^{*} depends on the data only through the sum of the observations x S \\text{x}_{S} , which is a sufficient statistic for the problem. Similarly, the confidence interval for 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ⁡ ( p 1 , p 2 , 𝐗 ) ] \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta(p_{1},p_{2},\\mathbf{X})] depends on the data through the value of the sufficient statistic, x S \\text{x}_{S} . Figure 5 shows the confidence interval for the score difference as a function of the sum of observations x S x_{S} considering two competing forecasts p 1 = 0.001 p_{1}=0.001 , p 2 = p 1 / 3 p_{2}=p_{1}/3 , a reference model for the pairwise gambling score p 0 = 5 ​ p 1 p_{0}=5p_{1} and N = 10,000 N=10,000 bins. Here, we do not need to choose a value for p ∗ p^{*} . Indeed, the confidence interval is determined solely by the forecast and observation. The Brier, log and full gambling score(Figure 5 a, 5 c, 5 d) all express a preference for p 1 p_{1} if we observe x S &gt; 12 \\text{x}_{S}&gt;12 , while they express a preference for p 2 p_{2} when x S &lt; 2 \\text{x}_{S}&lt;2 . This result is expected because p 1 &gt; p 2 p_{1}&gt;p_{2} , which means that x S &gt; 12 \\text{x}_{S}&gt;12 is much more probable under p 1 p_{1} than p 2 p_{2} . In fact, the average number of successes using p 1 p_{1} is N ​ p 1 = 10 Np_{1}=10 while N ​ p 2 = 3.34 Np_{2}=3.34 . The same reasoning applies when we express a preference for p 2 p_{2} ( x S &lt; 2 \\text{x}_{S}&lt;2 ). The pairwise gambling score (Figure 5 (b)), instead, requires x S &gt; 24 \\text{x}_{S}&gt;24 to express a preference for p 1 p_{1} and x S &lt; 9 \\text{x}_{S}&lt;9 to express a preference for p 2 p_{2} . It is heavily biased toward the forecast closer to zero. In fact, when p 1 p_{1} is the true probability, the probability of observing x S &gt; 24 \\text{x}_{S}&gt;24 is less than 0.0001 and the probability of observing x S &lt; 9 \\text{x}_{S}&lt;9 is 0.33. Therefore, we are more likely to express a preference for p 2 p_{2} than for p 1 p_{1} , even when p 1 = p ∗ p_{1}=p^{*} . This reinforces the problems with employ improper scores introduced in Section 3 . Figure 5: Confidence interval (shaded area) and point estimate (black solid line) for 𝔼 ⁡ [ Δ ] \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta] as a function of the number of observed successes x S \\text{x}_{S} considering p 1 = 0.001 p_{1}=0.001 , p 2 = p 1 / 3 p_{2}=p_{1}/3 , p 0 = 5 ​ p 1 p_{0}=5p_{1} and N = 10000. In each plot shows a different score: (a) Brier score; (b) pairwise gambling score; (c) logarithmic score; (d) full gambling score. Black solid line represents the observed score difference while the orange area represents the confidence interval. The black vertical dashed lines represent the interval of values of x S \\text{x}_{S} for which we do not express a preference 4.3 Preference Probabilities The confidence interval for the expected score difference, C ​ I Δ ​ ( α ) CI_{\\Delta}(\\alpha) , is a function of the competing forecasts, the scoring rule and depends on the data only through the sum of the observations x S = ∑ i x i \\text{x}_{S}=\\sum_{i}x_{i} . In particular, there are a range of values (between the dashed lines in Figure 5 ) of x S \\text{x}_{S} for which we are not able to express a preference. We refer to this interval as ( x m ​ i ​ n , x m ​ a ​ x ) (\\text{x}_{min},\\text{x}_{max}) . With respect to the sum of the observations x S x_{S} there are only three possible outcomes: x S &lt; x m ​ i ​ n \\displaystyle\\text{x}_{S}&lt;\\text{x}_{min} ⟶ preference for ​ p 2 , \\displaystyle\\longrightarrow\\text{preference for }p_{2}, x m ​ a ​ x ≤ x S ≤ x m ​ a ​ x \\displaystyle\\text{x}_{max}\\leq\\text{x}_{S}\\leq\\text{x}_{max} ⟶ no preference , \\displaystyle\\longrightarrow\\text{no preference}, x S &gt; x m ​ a ​ x \\displaystyle\\text{x}_{S}&gt;\\text{x}_{max} ⟶ preference for ​ p 1 . \\displaystyle\\longrightarrow\\text{preference for }p_{1}. The values x m ​ i ​ n \\text{x}_{min} and x m ​ a ​ x \\text{x}_{max} are determined solely by p 1 p_{1} , p 2 p_{2} , the number of bins N N and the scoring rule. Table 1 reports the values of x m ​ i ​ n \\text{x}_{min} and x m ​ a ​ x \\text{x}_{max} for the scoring rules depicted in Figure 5 . These values can be used to compute the preference probabilities once a value for p ∗ p^{*} is assumed. Indeed, in the Multiple Bins Single Probability case, the distribution of X S X_{S} is a Binomial distribution, X S ∼ Bin ​ ( N , p ∗ ) X_{S}\\sim\\text{Bin}(N,p^{*}) . Table 2 reports the probabilities of i) no preference; ii) Preference for p 1 p_{1} ; iii) Preference for p 2 p_{2} . The probabilities are calculated considering alternatively p ∗ p^{*} equal to p 1 p_{1} (first half of the table) or p 2 p_{2} (second half of the table). Table 1: Multiple Bins Single Probability case: table reporting the values x m ​ i ​ n \\text{x}_{min} and x m ​ a ​ x \\text{x}_{max} for the Brier, log, pairwise gambling (PG) and full gambling (FG) score. The reported values refers to the case where N = 10,000 N=10,000 , p 1 = 0.001 p_{1}=0.001 , p 2 = p 1 / 3 p_{2}=p_{1}/3 , and do not depend on p ∗ p^{*} . Score x m ​ i ​ n \\text{x}_{min} x m ​ a ​ x \\text{x}_{max} Brier 2 12 Log 2 11 PG 9 24 FG 2 12 The similarity among the values x m ​ i ​ n \\text{x}_{min} and x m ​ a ​ x \\text{x}_{max} for the proper scores lead to similar preference probabilities. The proper scores always assign the greatest probability to the case in which we are not able to express a preference, however, when p ∗ = p 1 p^{*}=p_{1} it is unlikely to express a preference for p 2 p_{2} . Vice versa when p ∗ = p 2 p^{*}=p_{2} . There is a slightly difference between the Brier and the log score coming from the different penalty applied to forecasts close to zero. The log score penalises more heavily forecasts close to zero and, in fact, when p 1 = p ∗ p_{1}=p^{*} chances to express a preference for p 1 p_{1} are higher than using the Brier score. The full gambling score for p 1 p_{1} against p 2 p_{2} is proportional to the Brier score difference between p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} (see Section 3.2) and thus, their preference probabilities coincide. Considering the pairwise gambling score the probability of expressing a preference for p 1 p_{1} is always very close to zero, even when p ∗ = p 1 p^{*}=p_{1} . This shows again that it is possible to find a combination of p 1 p_{1} , p 2 p_{2} and p 0 p_{0} such that the model providing the smallest forecast obtains the highest reward with probability over 0.9 0.9 , even when the other forecast is equal to the true p ∗ p^{*} . Table 2: Multiple Bins Single Probability case: table reporting for each score (row) the probabilities of expressing (or not) a preference using either the Brier, log-, pairwise gambling (PG) or full gambling (FG) score. The probabilities are calculated considering N = 10,000 N=10,000 , p 1 = 0.001 p_{1}=0.001 , p 2 = p 1 / 3 p_{2}=p_{1}/3 and considering two cases: p ∗ = p 1 p^{*}=p_{1} and p ∗ = p 2 p^{*}=p_{2} . Score No pref p 1 p_{1} p 2 p_{2} p ∗ = p 1 p^{*}=p_{1} Brier 0.7912 0.2083 0.0005 Log 0.6963 0.3032 0.0005 PG 0.6672 0.0000 0.3327 FG 0.7912 0.2083 0.0005 p ∗ = p 2 p^{*}=p_{2} Brier 0.8454 0.0000 0.1545 Log 0.8453 0.0000 0.1545 PG 0.0073 0.2083 0.9927 FG 0.8454 0.0000 0.1545 Figure 6 shows the preference probabilities as a function of p ∗ ∈ ( 10 − 6 , 10 − 2 ) p^{*}\\in(10^{-6},10^{-2}) which is the range of values of the 5-year adaptively-smoothed forecast for Italy (aggregating over the magnitude bins) used later to illustrate the Multiple Bins Multiple Probabilities case. The Brier score behaves as expected. The probability of expressing a preference for p 2 p_{2} increases as p ∗ p^{*} goes to zero, which is what we expect given p 2 &lt; p 1 p_{2}&lt;p_{1} . On the other hand, the probability of preferring p 1 p_{1} increases when p ∗ p^{*} increases, because p 1 &gt; p 2 p_{1}&gt;p_{2} . Finally, the probability of not being able to express a preference is higher when p 2 &lt; p ∗ &lt; p 1 p_{2}&lt;p^{*}&lt;p_{1} (Figure 6 a). The pairwise gambling score, instead, does not behave as expected. The probability of preferring p 1 p_{1} is almost zero in the range of values of p ∗ p^{*} considered in the example. The two most probable outcomes are: expressing a preference for p 2 p_{2} or not expressing a preference at all. Figure 6: Multiple Bins Single Probability case: each plot shows the probability of each possible outcome (solid lines no preference, dotted lines preference for p 1 p_{1} , dashed lines preference for p 2 p_{2} ) as a function of p ∗ p^{*} using the Brier score (a) and the pairwise gambling score (b) considering p 1 = 0.001 p_{1}=0.001 and p 2 = p 1 / 3 p_{2}=p_{1}/3 (vertical lines), p 0 = 5 ​ p 1 p_{0}=5p_{1} and N = 10,000 N=10,000 . The true probability p ∗ p^{*} varies in ( 10 − 6 , 2 ⋅ 10 − 2 ) (10^{-6},2\\cdot 10^{-2}) which is a realistic range of values in Italy. 4.4 Probability of expressing a preference It is interesting to study how the probability for each case changes as a function of p ∗ p^{*} for different numbers of bins N N and different ratios between p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} . To do that it is useful to focus only on two possible outcomes: expressing a preference and not expressing a preference. The probability of expressing a preference is given by the probability of observing a sample such that the sum of the observations x S \\text{x}_{S} is greater than x m ​ a ​ x \\text{x}_{max} or smaller than x m ​ i ​ n \\text{x}_{min} . We refer to this probability as β \\beta which is given by β = 1 − Pr [ x m ​ i ​ n ≤ X S ≤ x m ​ a ​ x ] . \\beta=1-\\Pr[\\text{x}_{min}\\leq X_{S}\\leq\\text{x}_{max}]. This probability depends on the scoring rule, the forecasts p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} ,the number of bins N N and the true probability p ∗ p^{*} . We study β \\beta as a function of p ∗ p^{*} for different numbers of bins. In this artificial case, to increase the number of bins we are considering additional bins with the same probability, we are explicitly not splitting any bin; this is analogous to increase the data at hand applying the model to a larger spatio-temporal region. Figure 7 a considers only the Brier score. The region of p ∗ p^{*} presenting low values for β \\beta shrinks when the number of bins increase which simply means that the more data we have, the more chances of expressing a preference. Moreover, β \\beta is at the minimum when p ∗ ∈ ( p 2 , p 1 ) p^{*}\\in(p_{2},p_{1}) , which is reasonable because if the distances | p ∗ − p 1 | |p^{*}-p_{1}| and | p ∗ − p 2 | |p^{*}-p_{2}| are similar the probability of no preference should be high. The N = 2000 N=2000 can be explained considering p 1 = p ∗ p_{1}=p^{*} . In this case, the expected sum of observations is N ​ p 1 = 2 Np_{1}=2 and it is more probable to observe X S &lt; 2 X_{S}&lt;2 than X S &gt; 2 X_{S}&gt;2 . Given that N ​ p 2 &lt; N ​ p 1 Np_{2}&lt;Np_{1} , the probability of not expressing a preference is high. Figure 7 presents the probability β \\beta as function of p ∗ p^{*} for different scores with a fixed number of bins N = 5000 N=5000 (b) and N = 20000 N=20000 (c). For N = 5000 N=5000 , the proper scores (Brier, log and full gambling score) present the same values of β \\beta for any value of p ∗ p^{*} . For N = 20000 N=20000 , the proper scores start to behave differently. The Brier and full gambling score still coincide, while the log score is slightly different. Specifically, the log score presents higher β \\beta values when p ∗ = p 1 p^{*}=p_{1} , and lower when p ∗ = p 2 p^{*}=p_{2} . This depends on the different penalties applied to forecasts close to zero. The log score presents greater chances of expressing a preference for p 1 p_{1} when p ∗ = p 1 p^{*}=p_{1} because the other forecast p 2 p_{2} is smaller than p 1 p_{1} and, therefore, penalized. On the other hand, when p ∗ = p 2 p^{*}=p_{2} the log score presents smaller β \\beta values than the Brier score. In contrast to the proper scores, the pairwise gambling score reaches its minimum β \\beta value for p ∗ &gt; p 1 p^{*}&gt;p_{1} . Here, the pairwise gambling score tends to express a preference for the smaller forecast even when the other one is closer to p ∗ p^{*} . This leads to higher values of β \\beta when p ∗ ∈ ( p 2 , p 1 ) p^{*}\\in(p_{2},p_{1}) because the pairwise gambling score will likely express a preference for p 2 p_{2} . Only when p ∗ &gt; p 1 p^{*}&gt;p_{1} the probability of no preference grows and the value of β \\beta decreases accordingly. Figure 7: Multiple Bins Single Probability case: (a) Brier score preference probability as a function of p ∗ p^{*} for different numbers of bins N ∈ { 2000 , 5000 , 10000 , 20000 } N\\in\\{2000,5000,10000,20000\\} . (b-c) Probability of expressing a preference as a function of p ∗ p^{*} . Colors represent the different scores: Brier, log , pairwise gambling (PG), and full gambling (FG) score. The Brier and FG scores coincide. The number of bins is fixed to N = 5000 (b) and N = 20000 (c). We set p 1 = 0.001 p_{1}=0.001 , p 2 = p 1 / 3 p_{2}=p_{1}/3 (vertical lines), p 0 = 5 ​ p 1 p_{0}=5p_{1} , and p ∗ ∈ ( 10 − 6 , 2 − 3 ) p^{*}\\in(10^{-6},2^{-3}) which is a realistic range of values in Italy. Given that p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} are scalars, we can consider β \\beta as a function of the ratio between p 1 p_{1} and p 2 p_{2} , ω = p 2 / p 1 \\omega=p_{2}/p_{1} , for a fixed p ∗ p^{*} . In principle, we expect that β \\beta is an increasing function of ω \\omega . We assume that the first forecast and the true probability are identical p 1 = p ∗ = 0.001 p_{1}=p^{*}=0.001 . The reference model for the pairwise gambling score is p 0 = 5 ​ p 1 p_{0}=5p_{1} and we consider different numbers of bins N ∈ { 2000 , 5000 , 10000 , 20000 } N\\in\\{2000,5000,10000,20000\\} . The ratio ω = p 2 / p 1 \\omega=p_{2}/p_{1} varies in the interval ( 0.1 , 4 ) (0.1,4) . We expect low β \\beta values when ω \\omega is around one (similar forecast) and high β \\beta values otherwise. Figure 8 a shows that, as expected, for N &gt; 2000 N&gt;2000 , β \\beta has its minimum when ω = 1 \\omega=1 . Considering ω \\omega as fixed, β \\beta is an increasing function of the number of bins. Figure 8 b-c compares the β \\beta values relative to different scores for a fixed number of bins, N = 5000 N=5000 (b) and N = 20000 N=20000 (c). The Brier and full gambling score coincide, whilst the log score presents slightly different β \\beta values. As before, this is due to the different penalties applied to forecasts close to zero. The pairwise gambling score is not consistent with the trends in the proper scores. Using this score and considering N = 20000 N=20000 (Figure 8 c), the probability β \\beta is consistently greater than 0.5 0.5 for the considered values of ω \\omega . Considering that p 1 = p ∗ p_{1}=p^{*} , the quantity ω \\omega is also the ratio between p 2 p_{2} and p ∗ p^{*} . This implies that regardless of ω \\omega , we will erroneously express a preference for p 2 p_{2} with a probability above 0.5 0.5 . Importantly, these sanity checks of a proposed scoring procedure can be done before looking at the observations. It is possible to check if forecasts can, in principle, be distinguished in light of the amount of expected data. We recommend the use of such exploitative figures when introducing a new scoring rule whose performance have not been tested. If the proposed scoring rule does not behave acceptably in this simple scenario, it is unlikely that it would behave acceptably in a real application. Figure 8: Multiple Bins Single Probability case: (a) Probability of expressing a preference using the Brier score as a function of ω = p 2 / p 1 ∈ ( 0.1 , 4 ) \\omega=p_{2}/p_{1}\\in(0.1,4) for different numbers of bins N ∈ { 2000 , 5000 , 10000 , 20000 } N\\in\\{2000,5000,10000,20000\\} . We set p 1 = p ∗ = 0.001 p_{1}=p^{*}=0.001 , and the reference model is p 0 = 5 ​ p 1 p_{0}=5p_{1} . (Bottom) Probability of expressing a preference as a function of ω \\omega . Colors represent the different scores: Brier, log, pairwise gambling (PG), and full gambling (FG) score. The number of bins is fixed to N = 5000 (b) and N = 20000 (c). 4.5 Score difference distribution - Multiple Bins Multiple Probabilities The Multiple Bins Multiple Probabilities case generalizes the Multiple Bins Single Probability case, and is much more similar to a real earthquake forecasting experiment. For example, the forecasts involved in the first CSEP experiments ( Field 2007 ; Schorlemmer and Gerstenberger 2007b ; Zechar et al. 2013 ; Michael and Werner 2018 ) were mostly grid-based forecasts providing for each space-time-magnitude bin, the expected number of earthquakes. Then, the number of events in each bin is modelled using a Poisson distribution with intensity equal to the number of events provided by the forecasts and the probability of observing at least one event is calculated accordingly. In this scenario, we do not have analytical results for the score difference distribution and we need to recur to simulations. We now want to specify a true model which has more realistic probabilities. Since we do not actually know these in reality, we choose to work with one of the CSEP models that was submitted to the 2010 Italy experiment ( Taroni et al. 2018 ) . We choose to simulate synthetic data from the 5-year adaptively-smoothed forecast for Italy ( Werner et al. 2010 ) and explore the ability of the scoring rules to discriminate between linearly scaled versions of this true model. This means that we are considering only one time bin of size 5 years, while the space-magnitude domain is divided in multiple regular bins. The spatial domain is represented by the coloured area in Figure 9 and it is divided in 0.1 × 0.1 0.1\\times 0.1 longitude-latitude bins. The magnitude domain ranges from 4.95 4.95 to 9.05 9.05 magnitude units and is divided in bins of length 0.1 0.1 . The forecast 