com/2280965/2020/12/31/analytics-review-giroud-lloris-rice-leeds/ Appendix A Appendix on Reproducibility In this appendix we give further details on our models and techniques used for reproducibility purposes. A.1. Data Conversion For this project, we used StatsBomb’s event-based data for the 2017/2018 and 2018/2019 English Premier League seasons (total of 760 games). Due to the inconsistency of events in terms of number of features, we used a data abstraction package called SPADL (Soccer Player Action Description Language). As mentioned in ( Decroos et al. 2019 ) , SPADL transforms the event stream data of JSON format into “a common vocabulary that enables subsequent data analysis.\" This library was created to convert different data providers’ event data into a consistent format. Therefore, our model is reproducible with any data. Table 12 shows some features of the first 5 instances in the event-stream data. Table 12. StatBomb’s Premier League data converted to SPADL Game ID Time in seconds Team ID Player ID Start x Start y … Result ID Type name Player name Country name 14562 1 58 9923 52.0588 34.4304 … 1 pass Bobby Reid England 14562 2 58 9917 41.4706 34.4304 … 1 dribble Joe Ralls England 14562 2 58 9917 41.4706 34.4304 … 1 pass Joe Ralls England 14562 4 58 9924 25.5882 19.7975 … 1 dribble Sean Morrison England 14562 6 58 9924 26.4706 18.9367 … 0 pass Sean Morrison England We then calculated the xT of every moving successful action in the dataset. Figure 7 illustrates the frequency of each action type. Figure 7. Frequency of each action type in StatBomb’s data After preparing the data, we extracted all 3 consecutive, moving, and successful actions for our first database. Each extraction was 1 instance where 2 actions were considered as input (xT and coordinates) and the third one as output (xT only). This amounted to 802,046 instances. The next step was extracting similar sequences (2 actions) that happened before a failed event due to a defensive action by the opposition. We separated interceptions from tackles which resulted in 75,691 interceptions to value and 17,423 tackles. Before training, we applied Scikit-Learn’s MinMaxScaler 12 12 12 https://scikit-learn.org/ on our dataset for normalization. A.2. Model Setup For our model, we used the implementation of a Multi Layer Perceptron from Keras’ library. The network was trained using a Mean Absolute Error loss function and an Exponential Linear Unit activation function. The deep network was composed of 3 dense hidden layers with 10 neurons each, and ran using Adadelta optimizer for 50 epochs. For the experiments in the paper, we used the scipy.stats 13 13 13 https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/stats.html library for the statistical outcomes and the matplotlib 14 14 14 https://matplotlib.org/ library for all graphs. ◄ Feeling lucky? Conversion report Report an issue View&nbsp;original on&nbsp;arXiv ► Copyright Privacy Policy Generated by L a T e XML oxide"}
{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2106.03589v3.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 188910, "equations": [], "text": "Nonparametric adaptive control and prediction: theory and randomized algorithms\nNicholas M. Boffi\nThanks:\nAuthors contributed equally.\nAffiliation:\nCourant Institute of Mathematical Sciences, New York University\nStephen Tu\n∗\nAffiliation:\nGoogle Brain Robotics\nJean-Jacques Slotine\nAffiliation:\nGoogle Brain Robotics\nAffiliation:\nNonlinear Systems Laboratory, Massachusetts Institute of Technology\nAbstract\nA key assumption in the theory of nonlinear adaptive control\nis that the uncertainty of the system can be expressed in the linear span\nof a set of known basis functions.\nWhile this assumption leads to efficient algorithms, it limits applications to very specific classes of systems.\nWe introduce a novel\nnonparametric\nadaptive algorithm that estimates an infinite-dimensional density over parameters online to learn an unknown dynamics in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space. Surprisingly, the resulting control input admits an analytical expression that enables its implementation despite its underlying infinite-dimensional structure.\nWhile this adaptive input is rich and expressive – subsuming, for example, traditional linear parameterizations – its computational complexity grows linearly with time, making it comparatively more expensive than its parametric counterparts.\nLeveraging the theory of random Fourier features, we provide an efficient randomized implementation that recovers the complexity of classical parametric methods while provably retaining the expressivity of the nonparametric input.\nIn particular, our explicit bounds only depend\npolynomially\non the underlying parameters of the system, allowing our proposed algorithms to efficiently scale\nto high-dimensional systems.\nAs an illustration of the method, we demonstrate the ability of the randomized approximation algorithm to learn a predictive model of a 60-dimensional system consisting of ten point masses interacting through Newtonian gravitation.\nBy reinterpretation as a gradient flow on a specific loss, we conclude with a natural extension of our kernel-based adaptive algorithms to deep neural networks. We show empirically that the extra expressivity afforded by deep representations can lead to improved performance at the expense of closed-loop stability that is both rigorously guaranteed and consistently observed for kernel machines.\n1\nIntroduction\nOne of the fundamental assumptions of nonlinear adaptive systems theory is\nthat the uncertainty of the system can be written as a linear expansion in a set of known basis functions that are nonlinear in the system state.\nWhile such linear parameterizations enable the derivation of efficient algorithms with provable guarantees, results outside of this restrictive regime are scarce.\nNotable examples typically leverage notions of monotonicity\n(\nAstolfi and Ortega 2003\n;\nTyukin et al. 2007\n)\nor convexity\n(\nAnnaswamy et al. 1998\n;\nFradkov et al. 1999\n)\nto make the underlying learning problem tractable.\nHere we broaden the applicability of adaptive control by relaxing this classical assumption. In statistical learning, nonlinear function approximation is handled through the use of reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces (RKHSs)\n(\nCucker and Smale 2002\n)\n, which are infinite-dimensional function spaces that admit tractable algorithms reminiscent of finite-dimensional linear regression. Inspired by this approach, we develop an adaptive input that learns directly over an RKHS without reference to a finite-dimensional vector of parameters.\nOne significant drawback of RKHSs is their computational cost. While the representer theorem ensures that estimation in an RKHS can always be cast as a finite expansion over the dataset, the number of parameters grows with its size, which makes learning on large datasets computationally demanding. A key breakthrough in overcoming this difficulty was the theory of random Fourier features, which shows that\nelements in many RKHSs can be approximated in the linear span of a finite set of\nrandom\nbasis functions with high probability. Remarkably, the number of\nrandom basis functions needed can be shown to scale polynomially\n(\nRahimi and Recht 2008b\n)\nin the\nfunction norm and the ambient dimension, which enables efficient computation even in\nhigh-dimensional spaces.\nIn the dynamical systems setting considered in this work, the system trajectory plays the role of the dataset, and the horizon plays the role of its size. Paralleling the statistical learning setting, the complexity of the nonparametric adaptive input that we introduce grows with this horizon. To overcome this complication, we leverage the theory of random features to provide high-probability guarantees on the possibility of uniformly approximating the nonparametric input via a finite-dimensional expansion in random basis functions. Importantly, this approach leads to efficient update laws that match the computational complexity of parametric methods while retaining the expressivity of the RKHS.\nWe focus on two primary problem settings. The first setting is the classical problem of adaptive control with matched uncertainty, where the uncertainty is assumed to live in the span of the control matrix. Our second application is in adaptive state estimation, where we seek to learn a model of an unknown dynamics governing the evolution of a particular state variable. As a byproduct of our analysis, we exhibit a duality between these two problems reminiscent of the duality between LQR and Kalman filtering in linear control theory. In both settings, we assume that the unmodelled component can be written as the sum of a term that can be linearly parameterized with known physically-motivated basis functions and a term assumed to live in an RKHS. This setup captures the practically relevant setting where a learner can leverage some available physical knowledge of the system but also must perform estimation in a purely unstructured fashion to achieve ideal performance.\nThe paper is organized as follows. In Section\n2\n, we review related work and summarize our contribution. In Section\n3\n, we formulate the adaptive control and prediction problems. In Section\n4\n, we develop a theory of nonparametric adaptive control, building upon a simple observation reminiscent of the “kernel trick” in machine learning. In Section\n5\n, we review the theory of random Fourier features, which we subsequently apply in Section\n6\nto design practical adaptive algorithms that asymptotically drive the control or prediction error to a ball around zero. The radius of the ball scales with the approximation error of the random feature expansion, and we give an explicit bound on the number of features needed to ensure that the tracking or prediction error falls below a tolerance threshold\nε\n\\varepsilon\nwith high probability.\nIn Section\n7\n, we first study the performance of the nonparametric method in comparison to its randomized approximations on a synthetic adaptive control problem. We subsequently illustrate the effectiveness of its randomized approximations in very high dimension\nby constructing an adaptive predictor for a\n60\n60\n-dimensional\nHamiltonian dynamical system describing the motion of a collection of particles interacting through a\n1\n/\nr\n2\n1/r^{2}\npotential.\n2\nRelated Work and Summary of Contributions\nUniform approximation for adaptive control\nMost related to the present contribution is a line of work initiated by\nKurdila and Lei 2013\nand followed by\nBobade et al. 2019\n, who study adaptive control and estimation in RKHSs. In these works, a nonparametric input is treated as an ideal, non-implementable abstraction, and this abstract input is approximated via orthogonal projections or a fixed grid of radial basis functions. Asymptotic convergence results are shown for the approximations, but no finite-sample theory is given, and the grid of centers is chosen in an\nad-hoc\nfashion. By gridding the space, these past approaches essentially reduce to a classic line of work by\nSanner and Slotine 1992\n, who approximate an unknown dynamics uniformly with a sum of radial basis functions. These basis functions are spaced on a regular grid, and the grid resolution is chosen based on considerations from sampling theory to ensure a sufficient degree of uniform approximation for the control application. Importantly, while these gridding-based approaches are suitable and highly efficient for low-dimensional systems, they become intractable for higher-dimensional systems. From the perspective of constructing a regular grid, “low-dimensional” is often as restrictive as four-dimensional, which is easily surpassed by modern control applications.\nAnother closely related line of work is\nChowdhary et al. 2012\n;\nChowdhary et al. 2015\n, who propose to use Gaussian Process (GP) regression for model reference adaptive control.\nThe primary difference with our work is that we derive a control\nlaw (cf.\nSection\n4\n) that operates purely in continuous-time, which obviates the need to take a time derivative of the error signal as supervision. This is important in practice, since it is well-known that computing the time derivative of a signal (e.g. with finite difference approximations) can amplify measurement noise.\nAn additional difference is that our theory quantifies the relation between\nthe number of random features in the function approximation (which governs its quality) and the size of the ball around the desired trajectory to which the system will converge.\nFinally, our work relies on random feature approximations\n(\nRahimi and Recht 2007\n)\nfor tractability,\nwhich is simpler to implement in practice than approaches based on sparse GPs.\nRandomization and dimensionality-dependence\nWe show that a nonparametric controller can be implemented as the action of a certain kernel integral operator against a known signal over the system trajectory, and we provide an intuitive derivation via the celebrated “kernel trick”. This result naturally leads to the randomized approximation methods developed here, which can be seen as a stochastic alternative to a fixed grid of basis functions. The main advantage of randomization is computational: due to concentration of measure, the number of basis functions needed for our construction grows polynomially in the state and input dimension of the underlying control problem. This permits our method to scale to much higher-dimensional systems than prior methods based on gridding, which require a number of basis functions that grows\nexponentially\nin dimension. Moreover, our work provides a natural path towards developing a theory of adaptive control with more expressive function classes such as single-layer neural networks\n(\nBach 2017\n;\nBengio et al. 2006\n)\n, as well as alternative approximation schemes such as the Nyström method\n(\nLu et al. 2016\n)\n.\nRandom feature approximations\nOur randomized algorithm is based on random Fourier features\n(\nRahimi and Recht 2007\n;\nRahimi and Recht 2008b\n;\nRahimi and Recht 2008a\n)\nand their extension to vector-valued functions\n(\nBrault et al. 2016\n;\nMinh 2016\n)\n. We build heavily on the results of\nRahimi and Recht 2008b\n, who prove that the\nL\n∞\nL_{\\infty}\napproximation error\nover a compact set for a function\nf\nf\nin an RKHS\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\ndecays as\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nK\n)\nO(1/\\sqrt{K})\n, where\nK\nK\nis the number of features drawn\nfrom a particular distribution induced by\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\n. This rate matches that due to\nBarron 1993\nfor approximation of functions whose gradients have absolutely integrable Fourier transforms via sums of sigmoidal basis functions.\nControl and robotic learning\nIn control and robotics applications, several authors have utilized\nrandom features for function approximation in learning\nstable vector fields\n(\nSindhwani et al. 2018\n)\n,\ncontrol contraction metrics\n(\nSingh et al. 2020\n)\n,\nLyapunov functions\n(\nBoffi et al. 2020\n)\n,\nand in velocity gradient-based adaptation\n(\nBoffi et al. 2021\n)\n.\nHowever, these works do not analyze the effect of the\napproximation error introduced by random features\non the control performance, nor do they provide any bounds\non the number of random features needed to achieve a\nspecified level of uniform approximation. Adaptive control laws have also been developed for robotic manipulators by exploiting the structure of the governing Euler-Lagrange equations\n(\nSlotine and Li 1987\n)\n; it is straightforward to extend our results to this setting, or to augment existing robotic adaptive control laws with a nonparametric component to improve robustness to unmodeled disturbances.\nGenerality of results\nWhile the focus of this work is on nonparametric adaptive control and randomized approximation schemes, we have written our results generally to capture a variety of different\nsettings in adaptive control, including Lyapunov-based adaptive control\n(\nKrstić et al. 1995\n)\n, speed/velocity gradient methods\n(\nFradkov et al. 1999\n;\nKrstić et al. 1995\n)\n,\nmirror descent\n(\nBoffi and Slotine 2021\n)\n, and contraction metrics\n(\nLopez and Slotine 2021\n)\n. We believe that this unification of results represents one of the most general treatments of nonlinear adaptive control available in the literature, and see it to be of independent interest.\n3\nProblem Formulation\nAdaptive control\nWe study nonlinear dynamical systems in\nmatched uncertainty form\nx\n˙\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\np\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n)\n,\n\\dot{x}=f(x,t)+g(x,t)\\left(u(x,t)-Y(x,t)\\alpha_{p}-h(x)\\right),\n(3.1)\nwhere\nf\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nn\nf:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n}\nis the “nominal dynamics” representing the behavior of the system in the absence of any inputs,\ng\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nn\n×\nd\ng:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d}\nis the control matrix describing how an input enters the system,\nu\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nd\nu:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nis the control input chosen by the learner,\nY\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nd\n×\np\nY:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times p}\nis a matrix of basis functions describing the system’s physical structure,\nα\np\n∈\nℝ\np\n\\alpha_{p}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p}\nis a corresponding vector of physical parameters,\nand\nh\n∈\nℋ\nh\\in\\mathcal{H}\nis an unknown dynamics in an operator-valued RKHS\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\nof functions mapping\nℝ\nn\n↦\nℝ\nd\n\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\mapsto\\mathbb{R}^{d}\n(\nCarmeli et al. 2010\n)\n1\n1\n1\nA formal definition of an operator-valued RKHS will be provided in Section\n4\n.\n. Both\nh\nh\nand\nα\np\n\\alpha_{p}\nare unknown, and the goal is to drive\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nto a bounded desired trajectory\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\nx_{d}(t)\nby learning a suitable input\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\nu(x,t)\nonline. As a supervisory signal, the learner observes an error\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∈\nℝ\ns\ne(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{s}\nat each\nt\nt\nwith dynamics\ne\n˙\n=\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\np\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\dot{e}=f_{e}(e,t)+g_{e}(x,t)(u(x,t)-Y(x,t)\\alpha_{p}-h(x)),\n(3.2)\nwhere\nf\ne\n:\nℝ\ns\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\ns\nf_{e}:\\mathbb{R}^{s}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{s}\nand\ng\ne\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\ns\n×\nd\ng_{e}:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{s\\times d}\n.\nWhile the most natural\nerror signal is the trajectory tracking\nerror\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)=x(t)-x_{d}(t)\n,\nwe formulate the error signal\nmore abstractly to allow for controllers that\nonly actuate higher order derivatives of the state.\nThis is discussed more in\n3.6\n.\nRemark 3.1\n.\nOur formulation with\nh\nh\nautonomous can be relaxed by considering an RKHS of functions mapping\nℝ\nn\n+\n1\n↦\nℝ\nd\n\\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\\mapsto\\mathbb{R}^{d}\n, i.e., by treating time explicitly as an input variable.\nAdaptive prediction\nWe study nonlinear dynamical systems that can be additively decomposed\nx\n˙\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\np\n+\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n,\n\\dot{x}=f(x,t)=Y(x,t)\\alpha_{p}+h(x),\nwhere\nf\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nn\nf:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n}\nis an unknown dynamics\ncomposed of terms that have a similar interpretation to the control setting. The goal is to learn an approximation\nf\n^\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nn\n\\hat{f}:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n}\nof the true dynamics\nf\nf\nby designing an estimator\nx\n^\n˙\n=\nf\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n+\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n\\dot{\\hat{x}}=\\hat{f}(\\hat{x},t)+k(\\hat{x},x(t))\n(3.3)\nthat will ensure\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\hat{x}(t)\nasymptotically approaches\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\n. In (\n3.3\n),\nk\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\nn\n→\nℝ\nn\nk:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n}\nis a feedback term satisfying\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nx\n)\n=\n0\nk(x,x)=0\nfor all\nx\nx\nthat is used to ensure\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\hat{x}(t)\nremains close to\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nduring learning. In this setting, the error signal can be taken as the prediction error\ne\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\n​\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)=\\hat{x}(t)-x(t)\n. Moreover, the estimator state\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\hat{x}(t)\nplays the role of\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nfrom the control setting, while\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nplays the role of the desired trajectory\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\nx_{d}(t)\n. When measurements are no longer available, the open-loop system\nx\n^\n˙\n=\nf\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n\\dot{\\hat{x}}=\\hat{f}(\\hat{x},t)\nmay be used to extrapolate the state to make predictions into the future. If measurements are only available at some discrete sampling frequency, the dynamics\nf\n^\n\\hat{f}\ncan be used to interpolate the value of the state between sampling points. This discrete setting is expanded upon in\nAppendix\nA\n.\n3.1\nNotation\nWe consider algorithms that update estimates of the physical parameters\nα\n^\np\n∈\nO\np\n⊆\nℝ\np\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}\\in O_{p}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{p}\nand model parameters (when applicable)\nα\n^\nm\n∈\nO\nm\n⊆\nℝ\nm\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}\\in O_{m}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{m}\nonline, where\nO\np\nO_{p}\nand\nO\nm\nO_{m}\nare open convex subsets.\nWe fix twice differentiable mirror maps\n2\n2\n2\nSee e.g.\n(\nBubeck 2015\n, Section 4.1)\nfor a definition.\n(potential functions)\nψ\np\n:\nO\np\n→\nℝ\n\\psi_{p}:O_{p}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\n(resp.\nψ\nm\n:\nO\nm\n→\nℝ\n\\psi_{m}:O_{m}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\n)\nthat are strongly convex with respect to a norm\n∥\n⋅\n∥\n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert\non\nO\np\nO_{p}\n(resp.\n∥\n⋅\n∥\n′\n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert^{\\prime}\non\nO\nm\nO_{m}\n)\nand have locally Lipschitz Hessians.\nFor a potential\nψ\n\\psi\n, we let\nd\nψ\n(\nα\n∥\nα\n^\n)\n=\nψ\n(\nα\n)\n−\nψ\n(\nα\n^\n)\n−\n∇\nψ\n(\nα\n^\n)\n𝖳\n(\nα\n^\n−\nα\n)\nd_{\\psi}\\left(\\alpha\\|\\hat{\\alpha}\\right)=\\psi(\\alpha)-\\psi(\\hat{\\alpha})-\\nabla\\psi(\\hat{\\alpha})^{\\mathsf{T}}(\\hat{\\alpha}-\\alpha)\ndenote the Bregman divergence associated with\nψ\n\\psi\n. We use\n∥\n⋅\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_{2}\nto denote the\nℓ\n2\n\\ell_{2}\nnorm,\n∥\n⋅\n∥\nop\n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\nto denote the\nℓ\n2\n→\nℓ\n2\n\\ell_{2}\\rightarrow\\ell_{2}\noperator norm of a matrix,\nB\n2\nn\n​\n(\nR\n)\nB_{2}^{n}(R)\nto denote the closed\nℓ\n2\n\\ell_{2}\nball of radius\nR\nR\nin\nℝ\nn\n\\mathbb{R}^{n}\n,\n𝕊\nn\n−\n1\n\\mathbb{S}^{n-1}\nto denote the unit sphere in\nℝ\nn\n\\mathbb{R}^{n}\n,\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\nto denote the non-negative reals, and\n𝖲𝗒𝗆\n⩾\n0\nn\n×\nn\n\\mathsf{Sym}_{\\geqslant 0}^{n\\times n}\nto denote the set of symmetric positive semidefinite\nn\n×\nn\nn\\times n\nmatrices.\nMore generally, for a normed vector space\nE\nE\n,\n∥\n⋅\n∥\nE\n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_{E}\ndenotes\nits norm, and\nB\nE\n​\n(\nR\n)\nB_{E}(R)\ndenotes a closed ball in\nE\nE\nof radius\nR\nR\n.\nFor a measure\nν\n\\nu\n, measurable space\nΘ\n\\Theta\n, and positive integer\nq\nq\n,\nthe space\nL\n2\nq\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\nL_{2}^{q}(\\Theta,\\nu)\ndenotes the real Hilbert space of\nsquare integrable measurable functions\nf\n:\nΘ\n→\nℝ\nq\nf:\\Theta\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{q}\nwith norm\n∥\nf\n∥\nL\n2\nq\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n=\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n∥\n2\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\lVert f\\rVert_{L_{2}^{q}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}=\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert f(\\theta)\\rVert_{2}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\n.\nWe will often drop the dependence on\nq\nq\nwhen it is clear from the context. Finally, for a positive definite metric\nM\n:\nℝ\nn\n→\n𝖲𝗒𝗆\n⩾\n0\nn\n×\nn\nM:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathsf{Sym}_{\\geqslant 0}^{n\\times n}\n,\nthe Riemannian energy\nE\nM\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\nn\n→\nℝ\n⩾\n0\nE_{M}:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\nis defined as:\nE\nM\n​\n(\nx\n,\ny\n)\n:=\ninf\nγ\n∫\n0\n1\nγ\ns\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\n​\n(\nγ\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n)\n​\nγ\ns\n​\n(\ns\n)\n​\n𝑑\ns\n,\nγ\ns\n​\n(\ns\n)\n=\nd\n​\nγ\nd\n​\ns\n​\n(\ns\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle E_{M}(x,y):=\\inf_{\\gamma}\\int_{0}^{1}\\gamma_{s}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M(\\gamma(s))\\gamma_{s}(s)ds,\\>\\>\\gamma_{s}(s)=\\frac{d\\gamma}{ds}(s),\nwhere the infimum ranges over smooth curves\nγ\n\\gamma\nsatisfying\nγ\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n=\nx\n\\gamma(0)=x\nand\nγ\n⁡\n(\n1\n)\n=\ny\n\\gamma(1)=y\n.\n3.2\nAssumptions\nTo make the above learning problems tractable and to simplify our presentation of results, we require some standard definitions and assumptions. The first requirement is regularity of the nominal dynamics, control matrix, and basis functions.\nDefinition 3.2\n.\nLet\nE\n1\nE_{1}\nand\nE\n2\nE_{2}\nbe normed vector spaces.\nA function\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\nf(x,t)\nmapping\nE\n1\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n↦\nE\n2\nE_{1}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\mapsto E_{2}\nis said to be locally Lipschitz in\nx\nx\nif for every finite\nT\n>\n0\nT>0\nand\nR\n>\n0\nR>0\n,\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\nE\n1\n⩽\nR\n,\n∥\ny\n∥\nE\n1\n⩽\nR\n,\nx\n≠\ny\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nE\n2\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\nE\n1\n<\n∞\n.\n\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\sup_{\\begin{subarray}{c}\\lVert x\\rVert_{E_{1}}\\leqslant R,\\\\\n\\lVert y\\rVert_{E_{1}}\\leqslant R,\\\\\nx\\neq y\\end{subarray}}\\frac{\\lVert f(x,t)-f(y,t)\\rVert_{E_{2}}}{\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{E_{1}}}<\\infty.\nFurthermore,\nf\nf\nis said to be locally bounded in\nx\nx\nuniformly in\nt\nt\nif for every finite\nR\n>\n0\nR>0\n,\nsup\nt\n∈\nℝ\n⩾\n0\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\nE\n1\n⩽\nR\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nE\n2\n<\n∞\n.\n\\sup_{t\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}}\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{E_{1}}\\leqslant R}\\lVert f(x,t)\\rVert_{E_{2}}<\\infty.\nAssumption 3.3\n(Dynamics regularity)\n.\nThe functions\nf\nf\n,\ng\ng\n, and\nY\nY\nare known to the learner. Moreover,\nf\nf\n,\ng\ng\n,\nY\nY\n, and\nh\nh\nare locally Lipschitz in\nx\nx\nand locally bounded in\nx\nx\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n.\nOur second requirement is a set of reasonable conditions on the error to ensure it provides a suitable signal for learning.\nAssumption 3.4\n(Error regularity)\n.\nf\ne\nf_{e}\nand\ng\ne\ng_{e}\nare locally Lipschitz in their first argument and locally bounded in their first argument uniformly in\nt\nt\n. Moreover, the following three conditions hold:\n(i)\nIn the absence of the unknown dynamics and any input, zero error is a fixed point,\nf\ne\n​\n(\n0\n,\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle f_{e}(0,t)\n=\n0\n​\nfor all\n​\nt\n⩾\n0\n.\n\\displaystyle=0\\>\\>\\text{for all}\\>\\>t\\geqslant 0.\n(3.4)\n(ii)\nBounded error implies a bounded deviation from the desired trajectory,\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n<\n∞\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}<\\infty\nimplies\n​\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n<\n∞\n​\nfor all\n​\nT\n>\n0\n.\n\\displaystyle\\text{ implies }\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\lVert x(t)-x_{d}(t)\\rVert_{2}<\\infty\\>\\>\\text{for all}\\>\\>T>0.\n(3.5)\n(iii)\nA convergent error signal implies a convergent trajectory\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n=\n0\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}=0\nimplies\n​\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n=\n0\n.\n\\displaystyle\\text{ implies }\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert x(t)-x_{d}(t)\\rVert_{2}=0.\n(3.6)\nTo demonstrate that such error signals can be constructed in practice, we provide a few simple illustrative examples.\nExample 3.5\n(Systems with regularity)\n.\nConsider a system satisfying Assumption\n3.3\n. Then\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)=x(t)-x_{d}(t)\nsatisfies the requirements in Assumption\n3.4\n.\nExample 3.6\n(Controllable linear time-invariant systems)\n.\nConsider the linear time-invariant system\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\nA\n​\nx\nf(x,t)=Ax\nand\ng\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\nB\ng(x,t)=B\nwith the pair\n(\nA\n,\nB\n)\n(A,B)\ncontrollable. Let\nz\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∈\nℝ\nn\nz(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\ndenote the state of the system expressed in control canonical form, and let\nz\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∈\nℝ\nn\nz_{d}(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\ndenote the corresponding desired trajectory. Define\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\nH\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n​\n(\nz\n1\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nz\nd\n,\n1\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\ne(t)=H(s)\\left(z_{1}(t)-z_{d,1}(t)\\right)\nwhere\nH\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\nH(s)\nis a stable transfer function with at most\nn\n−\n1\nn-1\npoles and\nz\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\nz_{i}(t)\ndenotes the\ni\nth\ni^{\\text{th}}\ncomponent of\nz\nz\n. Then\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)\nsatisfies the requirements of Assumption\n3.4\n.\nThe following stability assumption on the error model is key to our analysis. This assumption is equivalent to requiring that in the absence of the unknown dynamics and adaptive input, the system will nominally tend to the desired trajectory.\nAssumption 3.7\n(Lyapunov stability of the error)\n.\nThe error system (\n3.2\n) admits a continuously differentiable Lyapunov function\nQ\n:\nℝ\ns\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nQ:\\mathbb{R}^{s}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\nsatisfying for every\ne\n∈\nℝ\ns\ne\\in\\mathbb{R}^{s}\nand\nt\n⩾\n0\nt\\geqslant 0\n,\n(i)\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n\\nabla Q(e,t)\nand\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}(e,t)\nare\nlocally bounded in\ne\ne\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n,\n(ii)\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n\\nabla Q(e,t)\nis locally Lipschitz in\ne\ne\n,\n(iii)\n⟨\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⟩\n+\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⩽\n−\nρ\n⁡\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n\\langle\\nabla Q(e,t),f_{e}(e,t)\\rangle+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}(e,t)\\leqslant-\\rho(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})\n, and\n(iv)\nμ\n1\n​\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n⩽\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⩽\nμ\n2\n​\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n\\mu_{1}(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})\\leqslant Q(e,t)\\leqslant\\mu_{2}(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})\n,\nwhere\nρ\n,\nμ\n1\n,\n\\rho,\\mu_{1},\nand\nμ\n2\n\\mu_{2}\ndenote class-\n𝒦\n∞\n\\mathcal{K}_{\\infty}\nfunctions.\nWhile we focus on Lyapunov stability of the error dynamics, our results encompass incremental forms of stability such as contraction\n(\nLohmiller and Slotine 1998\n)\n.\nRemark 3.8\n(Contraction)\n.\nWe say that the error system is contracting in a metric\nM\n:\nℝ\ns\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\n𝖲𝗒𝗆\n⩾\n0\ns\n×\ns\nM:\\mathbb{R}^{s}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathsf{Sym}^{s\\times s}_{\\geqslant 0}\nif for some\nλ\n>\n0\n\\lambda>0\n,\n∂\nf\ne\n∂\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n+\nM\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n​\n∂\nf\ne\n∂\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n+\nM\n˙\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n≼\n−\n2\n​\nλ\n​\nM\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\n∀\ne\n∈\nℝ\ns\n,\nt\n∈\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n.\n\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial f_{e}}{\\partial e}(e,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}M(e,t)+M(e,t)\\frac{\\partial f_{e}}{\\partial e}(e,t)+\\dot{M}(e,t)\\preccurlyeq-2\\lambda M(e,t),\\>\\>\\forall e\\in\\mathbb{R}^{s},t\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}.\n(3.7)\nTaking the first variation of the Riemannian energy between the error\ne\ne\nand the zero trajectory\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n=\nE\nM\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\ne\n,\n0\n)\nQ(e,t)=E_{M(\\cdot,t)}(e,0)\nshows that\n⟨\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⟩\n+\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⩽\n−\n2\n​\nλ\n​\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n\\langle\\nabla Q(e,t),f_{e}(e,t)\\rangle+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}(e,t)\\leqslant-2\\lambda Q(e,t)\n, so that the energy serves as an exponentially stable Lyapunov function. This correspondence will be used in the prediction setting with\ne\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\n​\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)=\\hat{x}(t)-x(t)\n.\n4\nNonparametric adaptive control and prediction\nIn this section, we present our primary result in the nonparametric setting. Given a Lyapunov function for the error dynamics as stated in Assumption\n3.7\n, the standard procedure in adaptive nonlinear control is to approximate the unknown dynamics\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\nh(x)\nappearing in (\n3.1\n) & (\n3.2\n) by an expansion in known basis functions\nΦ\n:\nℝ\nn\n→\nℝ\nd\n×\np\n\\Phi:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times p}\n(\nSanner and Slotine 1992\n)\nh\n^\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\nΦ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n​\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\n\\hat{h}(x,t)=\\Phi(x)\\hat{\\alpha}(t),\n(4.1)\nand to update the parameter estimates\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∈\nℝ\np\n\\hat{\\alpha}(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p}\naccording to a Lyapunov-based update law\nα\n^\n˙\n(\nt\n)\n=\n−\nγ\nΦ\n(\nx\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\n\\dot{\\hat{\\alpha}}(t)=-\\gamma\\Phi(x)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e,t),\n(4.2)\nfor\nγ\n>\n0\n\\gamma>0\na learning rate.\n4.1\nNonparametric form\nWe start with the following simple observation about the construction in (\n4.1\n) & (\n4.2\n), which is analogous to the “kernel trick” in machine learning.\nObservation 4.1\n(Kernel trick)\n.\nAssume\nα\n^\n​\n(\n0\n)\n=\n0\n\\hat{\\alpha}(0)=0\n3\n3\n3\nNote that this is without loss of generality, since any non-zero\nα\n^\n​\n(\n0\n)\n\\hat{\\alpha}(0)\nresults in a non-zero\nh\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\n0\n)\n\\hat{h}(\\cdot,0)\nwhich can simply be absorbed into\nh\nh\n.\n. Then the adaptive approximation (\n4.1\n) with parameters updated according to the algorithm (\n4.2\n) is equivalent to the nonparametric approximation\nh\n^\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\n∫\n0\nt\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nτ\n)\n)\n​\nc\n​\n(\nτ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nτ\n,\n\\hat{h}(x,t)=\\int_{0}^{t}\\mathsf{K}(x,x(\\tau))c(\\tau)d\\tau,\n(4.3)\nwhere we have defined the kernel function\n𝖪\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\nn\n→\nℝ\nd\n×\nd\n\\mathsf{K}:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d}\nand coefficients\nc\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∈\nℝ\nd\nc(t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nas:\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\ny\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathsf{K}(x,y)\n=\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n​\nΦ\n​\n(\ny\n)\n𝖳\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\Phi(x)\\Phi(y)^{\\mathsf{T}},\nc\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle c(t)\n=\n−\nγ\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=-\\gamma g_{e}(x(t),t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e(t),t).\nThe proof is simple and proceeds by formally writing the solution of (\n4.2\n) as an integral over time. Observation\n4.1\ndemonstrates that the function estimates formed by classical adaptive control algorithms only depend on inner products between the basis functions and do not, in principle, require any reference to a vector of parameter estimates. This implies that the basis functions need not be finite-dimensional so long as they admit a computationally inexpensive procedure for computing their inner products, which is precisely the case for an RKHS.\nData-adapted centers\nRestricting to the case where\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\n⋅\n)\n\\mathsf{K}(\\cdot,\\cdot)\nis the Gaussian kernel, (\n4.3\n) can be seen as leaving a “trail” of Gaussians along the system trajectory\nx\n⁡\n(\nτ\n)\nx(\\tau)\nfor\nτ\n<\nt\n\\tau<t\n. In this sense, similar to kernel machines in statistical learning, (\n4.3\n) automatically constructs data-adapted centers at which to place spatially-localized basis functions.\nComplexity\nThe price paid for the expressivity in the representation (\n4.3\n) is that\nh\n^\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n\\hat{h}(x,t)\nnow obeys a partial differential equation that must be solved over a horizon of length\nt\nt\nat each\nx\n∈\nℝ\nn\nx\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\n,\n∂\nh\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n​\nc\n​\n(\nt\n)\n.\n\\frac{\\partial\\hat{h}}{\\partial t}(x,t)=\\mathsf{K}(x,x(t))c(t).\n(4.4)\nWhile (\n4.4\n) is decoupled in space so that a global solve is not required, past work from time\nτ\n<\nt\n\\tau<t\ncannot be re-used at time\nt\nt\n. Hence, unlike standard parametric methods that incur an\n𝒪\n⁡\n(\n1\n)\n\\mathcal{O}(1)\ncost at each timestep, solving (\n4.4\n) for the value of\nh\n^\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n\\hat{h}(x,t)\nat a given spatial location\nx\nx\nincurs an\n𝒪\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n\\mathcal{O}(t)\ncost at each time\nt\nt\n. For most applications, this is prohibitively expensive, and we now turn to efficient approximation schemes that circumvent this difficulty.\n4.2\nRandom feature space\nObservation\n4.1\nmotivates us to work with function classes\ndescribed by kernels. The following definition\nintroduces the notion of an operator-valued kernel.\nDefinition 4.2\n(Operator-valued reproducing kernel, see e.g.,\nCarmeli et al. 2010\n)\n.\nA kernel\n𝖪\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\nn\n→\nℝ\nd\n×\nd\n\\mathsf{K}:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d}\nis said to be an operator-valued reproducing\nkernel for an RKHS\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\nif\n(i)\nFor every\n{\nx\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nN\n⊆\nℝ\nn\n\\{x_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{n}\nand\n{\nw\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nN\n⊆\nℝ\nd\n\\{w_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{d}\n, it holds\nthat\n∑\ni\n,\nj\n=\n1\nN\n⟨\nw\ni\n,\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\ni\n,\nx\nj\n)\n​\nw\nj\n⟩\n⩾\n0\n\\sum_{i,j=1}^{N}\\langle w_{i},\\mathsf{K}(x_{i},x_{j})w_{j}\\rangle\\geqslant 0\n.\n(ii)\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nx\n)\n​\nw\n∈\nℋ\n\\mathsf{K}(\\cdot,x)w\\in\\mathcal{H}\nfor every\nx\n∈\nℝ\nn\nx\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\nand\nw\n∈\nℝ\nd\nw\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\n.\n(iii)\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\ncan be written\nℋ\n=\n𝖼𝗅\n{\nf\n|\n∃\n{\nx\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nn\n,\n{\nw\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nn\ns\n.\nt\n.\nf\n(\n⋅\n)\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\n𝖪\n(\n⋅\n,\nx\ni\n)\nω\ni\n}\n\\mathcal{H}=\\mathsf{cl}\\left\\{f\\,\\Bigg|\\,\\exists\\>\\{x_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n},\\{w_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\>s.t.\\>f(\\cdot)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mathsf{K}(\\cdot,x_{i})\\omega_{i}\\right\\}\n.\nThe adaptive algorithms we formulate will be valid for any RKHS\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\nwith a known operator-valued kernel\n𝖪\n\\mathsf{K}\n. However, we focus on RKHSs with specific structure that will enable the design of efficient randomized approximations. These function spaces are described by the following assumption.\nAssumption 4.3\n(The function class\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\n, see e.g.,\nBach 2017\n)\n.\nThe unknown dynamics\nh\nh\nlies in an RKHS\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\nwith known operator-valued kernel\n𝖪\n\\mathsf{K}\n. Moreover,\n𝖪\n\\mathsf{K}\nmay be written in terms of a feature map\nΦ\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nΘ\n→\nℝ\nd\n×\nd\n1\n\\Phi:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\Theta\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d_{1}}\nas\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\ny\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nΦ\n​\n(\ny\n,\nθ\n)\n𝖳\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mathsf{K}(x,y)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\Phi(y,\\theta)^{\\mathsf{T}}d\\nu(\\theta),\n(4.5)\nwith\nd\n1\n⩽\nd\nd_{1}\\leqslant d\nand where\nν\n\\nu\nis a known probability measure on a measurable space\nΘ\n\\Theta\n.\nIn Assumption\n4.3\n, we have overloaded the definition of the feature map\nΦ\n\\Phi\nas a generalization of the structure of\n𝖪\n\\mathsf{K}\nseen in Observation\n4.1\n. Assumption\n4.3\nis not very restrictive, as many rich kernels applied in practice – such as the Gaussian and Laplace kernels – can readily written in this form. In particular, the operator-valued generalization of Bochner’s theorem\n(\nBrault et al. 2016\n)\nstates that any translation-invariant kernel can be written in the form (\n4.5\n) with a feature map\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n=\nB\n⁡\n(\nw\n)\n​\ncos\n⁡\n(\nw\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\n)\n,\n\\Phi(x,\\theta)=B(w)\\cos(w^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b),\n(4.6)\nwhere\nΘ\n⊆\nℝ\nn\n+\n1\n\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\n,\nθ\n=\n(\nw\n,\nb\n)\n\\theta=(w,b)\n,\nw\n∈\nℝ\nn\nw\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\n,\nb\n∈\nℝ\nb\\in\\mathbb{R}\n, and for suitable choices of\nν\n\\nu\nand\nB\n:\nℝ\nn\n→\nℝ\nd\n×\nd\n1\nB:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d_{1}}\n.\nUnder Assumption\n4.3\n, it is well-known (c.f.\nBach 2017\n, Appendix A) that\nh\n∈\nℋ\nh\\in\\mathcal{H}\ncan be written, for some square-integrable signed density\nα\n:\nΘ\n→\nℝ\nd\n1\n\\alpha:\\Theta\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\nwith respect to the base measure\nν\n\\nu\n, as the integral\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n,\n∥\nh\n∥\nℋ\n2\n=\n∥\nα\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n.\nh(\\cdot)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta)\\alpha(\\theta)d\\nu(\\theta),\\>\\>\\lVert h\\rVert^{2}_{\\mathcal{H}}=\\lVert\\alpha\\rVert^{2}_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}.\n(4.7)\nThe corresponding Hilbert space is referred to as\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\n(\nBach 2017\n;\nBengio et al. 2006\n)\n.\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\nis related to the Banach space of single-layer neural networks\nℱ\n1\n\\mathcal{F}_{1}\n, which may be obtained by taking the union over all possible base measures for\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\n. The space\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\nis convenient for our purposes because it allows us to treat the infinite-dimensional density over parameters\nα\n\\alpha\nsimilar to a standard finite-dimensional vector of parameters. To do so, we introduce a second moment regularity condition that will ensure the nonparametric input leads to a stable and convergent feedback system.\nAssumption 4.4\n(Second moment regularity of\nΦ\n\\Phi\n)\n.\nFor every\nx\n∈\nℝ\nn\nx\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\n, the second moment of the feature matrix is finite, i.e.,\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n<\n∞\n\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)<\\infty\n.\nFurthermore, for every\nR\n>\n0\nR>0\n,\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n,\n∥\ny\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n,\nx\n≠\ny\n(\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n−\nΦ\n⁡\n(\ny\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\n2\n<\n∞\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{\\begin{subarray}{c}\\lVert x\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R,\\lVert y\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R,\\\\\nx\\neq y\\end{subarray}}\\frac{\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)-\\Phi(y,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}}{\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{2}}<\\infty.\nTo obtain accurate parametric approximations, we may sample points\nθ\ni\n∈\nΘ\n\\theta_{i}\\in\\Theta\ni.i.d. from the base measure\nν\n\\nu\n. This has the effect of discretizing the density\nα\n\\alpha\ninto a vector of parameters that can be learned using standard adaptive methods.\n4.3\nMain results\nFor simplicity of exposition, we restrict to the case where\nα\np\n=\n0\n\\alpha_{p}=0\nin (\n3.1\n) to focus on convergence of the nonparametric input; the randomized approximations in Section\n6\nwill adapt over both physical and mathematical parameter estimates simultaneously. Moreover, we focus here on the setting of adaptive control. Later, the proof of Theorem\n6.7\nwill highlight a duality between adaptive control and adaptive prediction that immediately implies an analogous result for prediction.\nThe following theorem demonstrates that the nonparametric adaptation algorithm leads to a stable and convergent trajectory.\nTheorem 4.5\n(Convergence)\n.\nConsider system (\n3.1\n) under Assumptions\n3.7\n,\n4.3\n, and\n4.4\n. Fix\nα\np\n=\n0\n\\alpha_{p}=0\nand let\nγ\n>\n0\n\\gamma>0\n.\nThen the adaptive control input\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle u(x,t)\n=\n−\nγ\n∫\n0\nt\n𝖪\n(\nx\n,\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n)\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nτ\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nτ\n)\nd\nτ\n\\displaystyle=-\\gamma\\int_{0}^{t}\\mathsf{K}(x,x(\\tau))g_{e}(x(\\tau),\\tau)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e(\\tau),\\tau)d\\tau\nensures that both\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nand\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)\nexist and are uniformly bounded for all\nt\n⩾\n0\nt\\geqslant 0\n. Moreover,\nu\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∈\nℋ\nu(\\cdot,t)\\in\\mathcal{H}\nfor all\nt\n⩾\n0\nt\\geqslant 0\nand\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n=\n0\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert x(t)-x_{d}(t)\\rVert_{2}=0\n.\nNext, we study the interpolation properties of the input\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\nu(x,t)\nalong the desired trajectory. To this end, we\nstrengthen Definition\n3.2\nto be uniform in\nt\nt\n.\nDefinition 4.6\n.\nLet\nE\n1\nE_{1}\nand\nE\n2\nE_{2}\nbe normed vector spaces.\nA function\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\nf(x,t)\nmapping\nE\n1\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n↦\nE\n2\nE_{1}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\mapsto E_{2}\nis said to be locally Lipschitz in\nt\nt\nuniformly in\nx\nx\nif the following\ntwo conditions hold for every\nR\n>\n0\nR>0\n:\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\nE\n1\n⩽\nR\nsup\nt\n1\n,\nt\n2\n∈\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n,\nt\n1\n≠\nt\n2\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n1\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n2\n)\n∥\nE\n2\n|\nt\n1\n−\nt\n2\n|\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{E_{1}}\\leqslant R}\\sup_{\\begin{subarray}{c}t_{1},t_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0},\\\\\nt_{1}\\neq t_{2}\\end{subarray}}\\frac{\\lVert f(x,t_{1})-f(x,t_{2})\\rVert_{E_{2}}}{|t_{1}-t_{2}|}\n<\n∞\n,\n\\displaystyle<\\infty,\nsup\nt\n∈\nℝ\n⩾\n0\nsup\n∥\nx\n1\n∥\nE\n1\n⩽\nR\n,\n∥\nx\n2\n∥\nE\n1\n⩽\nR\n,\nx\n1\n≠\nx\n2\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n1\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n2\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nE\n2\n∥\nx\n1\n−\nx\n2\n∥\nE\n1\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}}\\sup_{\\begin{subarray}{c}\\lVert x_{1}\\rVert_{E_{1}}\\leqslant R,\\\\\n\\lVert x_{2}\\rVert_{E_{1}}\\leqslant R,\\\\\nx_{1}\\neq x_{2}\\end{subarray}}\\frac{\\lVert f(x_{1},t)-f(x_{2},t)\\rVert_{E_{2}}}{\\lVert x_{1}-x_{2}\\rVert_{E_{1}}}\n<\n∞\n.\n\\displaystyle<\\infty.\nWith Definition\n4.6\nin hand, we may state the following theorem.\nTheorem 4.7\n(Interpolation)\n.\nConsider the setting of Theorem\n4.5\n. Suppose furthermore that both\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\nf_{e}(e,t)\nand\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\ng_{e}(x,t)\nare locally Lipschitz in their first argument\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n.\nFinally, suppose that for every\nR\n>\n0\nR>0\n,\n∫\nΘ\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n<\n∞\n.\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\Theta}\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)<\\infty.\nThen the nonparametric input asymptotically interpolates the unknown in the span of the control matrix,\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n)\n∥\n2\n=\n0\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert g_{e}(x(t),t)(u(x(t),t)-h(x(t)))\\rVert_{2}=0\n.\nMirroring the finite-dimensional setting considered by\nBoffi and Slotine 2021\n, we now demonstrate that the adaptive input in Theorem\n4.5\nconverges to the minimum RKHS-norm interpolating solution.\nTheorem 4.8\n(Implicit regularization)\n.\nConsider the setting of Theorem\n4.5\n. Define the interpolating set over the trajectory\n𝒜\n:=\n{\nh\n¯\n∈\nℋ\n:\nh\n¯\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n)\n=\nh\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n)\n,\n∀\nt\n⩾\n0\n}\n,\n\\mathcal{A}:=\\left\\{\\bar{h}\\in\\mathcal{H}:\\bar{h}(x(t))=h(x(t)),\\ \\forall t\\geqslant 0\\right\\},\nand assume that\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\nu\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∈\n𝒜\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}u(\\cdot,t)\\in\\mathcal{A}\n. Then,\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\nu\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∈\narg\n​\nmin\nh\n¯\n∈\n𝒜\n⁡\n∥\nh\n¯\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n∥\nℋ\n.\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}u(\\cdot,t)\\in\\argmin_{\\bar{h}\\in\\mathcal{A}}\\>\\lVert\\bar{h}(\\cdot)\\rVert_{\\mathcal{H}}.\n(4.8)\nGiven these results for the computationally expensive nonparametric input, we now turn to develop a theory of efficient randomized approximation schemes.\n5\nRandom feature approximation\n5.1\nApproximation theory\nWe now demonstrate how the function space\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\nleads to efficient randomized approximation algorithms. These randomized algorithms will enable us to restore the computational advantages of classical finite-dimensional parametric approximations while retaining the expressiveness of the RKHS\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\nwith high probability. Roughly speaking, the approach will be to apply the law of large numbers to the expectation (\n4.7\n), which leads to a finite-dimensional approximation\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n≈\n1\nK\n​\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\ni\n,\nh(\\cdot)\\approx\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta_{i})\\alpha_{i},\nwhere the\nθ\ni\n∼\nν\n\\theta_{i}\\sim\\nu\nare drawn i.i.d. from the base measure\nν\n\\nu\nand the\nα\ni\n=\nα\n⁡\n(\nθ\ni\n)\n∈\nℝ\nd\n1\n\\alpha_{i}=\\alpha(\\theta_{i})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\nare treated as parameters to be learned.\nK\nK\ndenotes the number of sampling points and will tune the accuracy of the approximation. We provide a bound on the number of random features\nK\nK\nneeded to ensure that there exists a set of weights\n{\nα\ni\n}\n\\{\\alpha_{i}\\}\ncapable of\nε\n\\varepsilon\n-uniformly approximating\nh\nh\non a fixed compact set\nX\n⊂\nℝ\nn\nX\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{n}\n. To begin, let\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nδ\n)\nB_{\\Phi}(\\delta)\nbe any function that satisfies,\nfor any\nδ\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\delta\\in(0,1)\n,\nℙ\nθ\n∼\nν\n​\n(\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n>\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nδ\n)\n)\n⩽\nδ\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}_{\\theta\\sim\\nu}\\left(\\sup_{x\\in X}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}>B_{\\Phi}(\\delta)\\right)\\leqslant\\delta.\nThen, for any\nη\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\eta\\in(0,1)\n, define a truncated version of\nΦ\n\\Phi\nas\nΦ\nη\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n:=\nΦ\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n𝟏\n{\n∥\nΦ\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n⩽\nB\nΦ\n(\nη\n)\n}\n.\n\\displaystyle\\Phi_{\\eta}(x,\\theta):=\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\mathbf{1}\\left\\{\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\leqslant B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\right\\}.\nWe will be interested in approximating functions over the subset\nℱ\n2\n​\n(\nB\n)\n=\n{\nf\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n|\ness\n​\nsup\nθ\n∈\nΘ\n⁡\n∥\nα\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\n}\n⊂\nℱ\n2\n,\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}(B)=\\left\\{f(\\cdot)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta)\\alpha(\\theta)d\\nu(\\theta)\\,\\Bigg|\\,\\esssup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}\\lVert\\alpha(\\theta)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B\\right\\}\\subset\\mathcal{F}_{2},\nwhich is dense in\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\nas\nB\n→\n∞\nB\\rightarrow\\infty\n(\nRahimi and Recht 2008b\n)\n; this bound on the density\nα\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n\\alpha(\\theta)\nis needed to obtain a uniform approximation result. With this notation in hand, we may extend the approximation theory of\nRahimi and Recht 2008b\nto vector-valued functions.\nProposition 5.1\n(Approximation error)\n.\nLet\nX\n⊂\nℝ\nn\nX\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{n}\nbe compact. Fix\nδ\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\delta\\in(0,1)\n,\nB\nh\n>\n0\nB_{h}>0\n,\nh\n∈\nℱ\n2\n​\n(\nB\nh\n)\nh\\in\\mathcal{F}_{2}(B_{h})\n, and a positive integer\nK\nK\n.\nLet\nθ\n1\n,\n…\n,\nθ\nK\n\\theta_{1},...,\\theta_{K}\nbe i.i.d. draws from\nν\n\\nu\n.\nPut\nη\n=\nδ\n2\n​\nK\n\\eta=\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\n.\nWith probability at least\n1\n−\nδ\n1-\\delta\n, there exist\nweights\n{\nα\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n⊂\nℝ\nd\n1\n\\{\\alpha_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K}\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\nsuch that\n∥\nα\ni\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nh\n\\lVert\\alpha_{i}\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{h}\nfor\ni\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nK\ni=1,...,K\n, and\n‖\n1\nK\n​\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\ni\n−\nh\n‖\n∞\n\\displaystyle\\left\\lVert\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta_{i})\\alpha_{i}-h\\right\\rVert_{\\infty}\n⩽\n2\nK\n​\n𝔼\n​\n‖\n∑\nk\n=\n1\nK\nε\ni\n​\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\n​\n(\nθ\ni\n)\n‖\n∞\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\frac{2}{K}\\mathbb{E}\\left\\lVert\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\varepsilon_{i}\\Phi_{\\eta}(\\cdot,\\theta_{i})\\alpha(\\theta_{i})\\right\\rVert_{\\infty}\n+\n2\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\n​\nB\nh\n​\nlog\n⁡\n(\n2\n/\nδ\n)\nK\n+\nB\nh\n​\nδ\n​\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n𝔼\n​\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n2\n​\nK\n.\n\\displaystyle\\qquad+\\sqrt{2}B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)B_{h}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{K}}+B_{h}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\delta\\sup_{x\\in X}\\mathbb{E}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}}{2K}}.\nAbove, each\nε\ni\n\\varepsilon_{i}\nis an i.i.d. Rademacher random variable\n4\n4\n4\nThat is,\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nε\ni\n=\n1\n)\n=\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nε\ni\n=\n−\n1\n)\n=\n1\n/\n2\n\\mathbb{P}(\\varepsilon_{i}=1)=\\mathbb{P}(\\varepsilon_{i}=-1)=1/2\n.\nand\n∥\nf\n∥\n∞\n:=\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert f\\rVert_{\\infty}:=\\sup_{x\\in X}\\lVert f(x)\\rVert_{2}\n.\nIn order to bound the Rademacher complexity term\nappearing in Proposition\n5.1\n,\nwe now make a few more assumptions on the structure of\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n\\Phi(x,\\theta)\n. These assumptions are motivated by the operator-valued Bochner’s theorem\n(\nBrault et al. 2016\n)\n.\nAssumption 5.2\n.\nThe feature space\nΘ\n\\Theta\nis a subset of\nℝ\nn\n+\n1\n\\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\n, so that\nθ\n∈\nΘ\n\\theta\\in\\Theta\nmay be written as\nθ\n=\n(\nw\n,\nb\n)\n\\theta=(w,b)\nwith\nw\n∈\nℝ\nn\nw\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\nand\nb\n∈\nℝ\nb\\in\\mathbb{R}\n. Moreover, the feature map can be factorized as\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n=\nϕ\n⁡\n(\nw\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\n)\n​\nM\n​\n(\nw\n)\n\\Phi(x,\\theta)=\\phi(w^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b)M(w)\nfor\nM\n:\nℝ\nn\n→\nℝ\nd\n×\nd\n1\nM:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d_{1}}\nand a\n1\n1\n-Lipschitz scalar function\nϕ\n:\nℝ\n→\n[\n−\n1\n,\n1\n]\n\\phi:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow[-1,1]\n.\nBecause\n|\nϕ\n|\n⩽\n1\n|\\phi|\\leqslant 1\n, we may take\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nδ\n)\nB_{\\Phi}(\\delta)\nto be any function that satisfies\nℙ\n⁡\n(\n∥\nM\n⁡\n(\nw\n)\n∥\nop\n>\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nδ\n)\n)\n⩽\nδ\n\\mathbb{P}(\\lVert M(w)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}>B_{\\Phi}(\\delta))\\leqslant\\delta\n.\nAccordingly, we have\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n=\nM\nη\n​\n(\nw\n)\n​\nϕ\n​\n(\nw\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\n)\n\\Phi_{\\eta}(x,\\theta)=M_{\\eta}(w)\\phi(w^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b)\nwith\nM\nη\n​\n(\nw\n)\nM_{\\eta}(w)\ndefined as\nM\nη\n(\nw\n)\n:=\nM\n(\nw\n)\n𝟏\n{\n∥\nM\n(\nw\n)\n∥\nop\n⩽\nB\nΦ\n(\nη\n)\n}\nM_{\\eta}(w):=M(w)\\mathbf{1}\\{\\lVert M(w)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\leqslant B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\}\n. With these extra assumptions in place, we can bound the Rademacher complexity term as follows.\nProposition 5.3\n(Rademacher complexity bound)\n.\nLet Assumption\n5.2\nhold, and denote\nB\nX\n:=\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\nB_{X}:=\\sup_{x\\in X}\\lVert x\\rVert_{2}\n. Then for any\nη\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\eta\\in(0,1)\n,\n2\nK\n​\n𝔼\n​\n‖\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nε\ni\n​\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\n​\n(\nθ\ni\n)\n‖\n∞\n\\displaystyle\\frac{2}{K}\\mathbb{E}\\left\\lVert\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\varepsilon_{i}\\Phi_{\\eta}(\\cdot;\\theta_{i})\\alpha(\\theta_{i})\\right\\rVert_{\\infty}\n⩽\n4\n​\nB\nh\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\nK\n​\n[\nB\nX\n​\n𝔼\n​\n∥\nw\n1\n∥\n2\n2\n+\nd\n1\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\frac{4B_{h}B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)}{\\sqrt{K}}\\left[B_{X}\\sqrt{\\mathbb{E}\\lVert w_{1}\\rVert_{2}^{2}}+\\sqrt{d_{1}}\\right].\nCombining Proposition\n5.1\nand Proposition\n5.3\n, we have that\nwith probability\n1\n−\nδ\n1-\\delta\n,\ninf\n{\nα\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n⊆\nℝ\nd\n1\n:\n∥\nα\ni\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nh\n‖\n1\nK\n∑\nk\n=\n1\nK\nΦ\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\ni\n)\nα\ni\n−\nh\n‖\n∞\n\\displaystyle\\inf_{\\{\\alpha_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}:\\lVert\\alpha_{i}\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{h}}\\left\\lVert\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta_{i})\\alpha_{i}-h\\right\\rVert_{\\infty}\n⩽\nB\nh\nK\n​\n[\n2\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nδ\n2\n​\nK\n)\n​\n(\n2\n​\nB\nX\n​\n𝔼\n​\n∥\nw\n1\n∥\n2\n2\n+\n2\n​\nd\n1\n+\nlog\n⁡\n(\n2\n/\nδ\n)\n)\n+\nδ\n2\n​\n𝔼\n​\n∥\nM\n⁡\n(\nw\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\frac{B_{h}}{\\sqrt{K}}\\bigg[2B_{\\Phi}\\left(\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)\\left(2B_{X}\\sqrt{\\mathbb{E}\\lVert w_{1}\\rVert_{2}^{2}}+2\\sqrt{d_{1}}+\\sqrt{\\log(2/\\delta)}\\right)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\delta}{2}\\mathbb{E}\\lVert M(w)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}}\\bigg].\n(5.1)\nTo simplify this expression, we now look at some particular choices of kernels.\n5.2\nExamples of Reproducing Kernels\nIn what follows, we consider a few examples of vector-valued kernels.\n5.2.1\nShift-invariant kernels\nFirst, we consider shift-invariant kernels from\nBrault et al. 2016\nand\nMinh 2016\n.\nLet\n𝗄\n⁡\n(\nx\n−\nz\n)\n\\mathsf{k}(x-z)\nbe an arbitrary scalar shift-invariant kernel\nand denote by\nμ\n\\mu\nthe normalized inverse Fourier transform of\n𝗄\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\mathsf{k}(\\cdot)\n.\nWe will assume generically that\n𝔼\nw\n∼\nμ\n​\n∥\nw\n∥\n2\n2\n≍\nn\n\\mathbb{E}_{w\\sim\\mu}\\lVert w\\rVert_{2}^{2}\\asymp n\nwhere\nμ\n\\mu\ndenotes the marginal of\nν\n\\nu\nover\nb\nb\n.\nDecomposable kernels\nLet\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nz\n)\n=\nA\n​\n𝗄\n​\n(\nx\n−\nz\n)\n\\mathsf{K}(x,z)=A\\mathsf{k}(x-z)\nfor any positive semidefinite\nA\n=\nB\n​\nB\n𝖳\nA=BB^{\\mathsf{T}}\n.\nThen\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n=\nB\n​\ncos\n⁡\n(\nw\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\n)\n\\Phi(x,\\theta)=B\\cos(w^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b)\nand\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nδ\n)\n=\n∥\nB\n∥\nop\nB_{\\Phi}(\\delta)=\\lVert B\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\n.\nHere, the approximation error bound (\n5.1\n)\nscales as\nB\nh\n​\n∥\nB\n∥\nop\nK\n​\n(\nB\nX\n​\nn\n+\nd\n1\n)\n\\frac{B_{h}\\lVert B\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}}{\\sqrt{K}}\\left(B_{X}\\sqrt{n}+\\sqrt{d_{1}}\\right)\n.\nCurl-free kernel\nLet\nn\n=\nd\nn=d\nand set\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nz\n)\n=\n−\n∇\n2\n𝗄\n​\n(\nx\n−\nz\n)\n\\mathsf{K}(x,z)=-\\nabla^{2}\\mathsf{k}(x-z)\n.\nThen\nA\n⁡\n(\nw\n)\n=\nw\n​\nw\n𝖳\nA(w)=ww^{\\mathsf{T}}\nand\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n=\nw\n​\ncos\n⁡\n(\nw\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\n)\n\\Phi(x,\\theta)=w\\cos(w^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b)\n. If\nμ\n∼\nN\n⁡\n(\n0\n,\nσ\n2\n​\nI\n)\n\\mu\\sim N(0,\\sigma^{2}I)\n, then\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nδ\n)\n=\nn\n+\n2\n​\nσ\n​\nlog\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nδ\n)\nB_{\\Phi}(\\delta)=\\sqrt{n}+2\\sigma\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\delta)}\nby standard Gaussian concentration results.\nThe approximation error bound (\n5.1\n) then scales as\nB\nh\n​\n(\nB\nx\n∨\n1\n)\nK\n​\n(\nn\n+\nlog\n⁡\nK\n)\n\\frac{B_{h}(B_{x}\\vee 1)}{\\sqrt{K}}(n+\\log{K})\n.\nDivergence-free kernel\nAgain let\nn\n=\nd\nn=d\n. Set\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nz\n)\n=\n(\n∇\n2\n−\nI\n​\nΔ\n)\n​\n𝗄\n​\n(\nx\n−\nz\n)\n\\mathsf{K}(x,z)=(\\nabla^{2}-I\\Delta)\\mathsf{k}(x-z)\n,\nwhere\nΔ\n\\Delta\nis the Laplacian and\nI\nI\nis the identity matrix.\nThen\nA\n⁡\n(\nw\n)\n=\n∥\nw\n∥\n2\n2\n​\nP\nw\n⟂\nA(w)=\\lVert w\\rVert_{2}^{2}P^{\\perp}_{w}\n,\nwhere\nP\nM\nP_{M}\ndenotes the orthogonal projection onto the range of\nM\nM\nand\nP\nM\n⟂\n=\nI\n−\nP\nM\nP^{\\perp}_{M}=I-P_{M}\n. Hence,\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n=\n∥\nw\n∥\n2\n​\nP\nw\n⟂\n​\ncos\n⁡\n(\nw\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\n)\n\\Phi(x,\\theta)=\\lVert w\\rVert_{2}P^{\\perp}_{w}\\cos(w^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b)\n.\nIf\nν\n∼\nN\n⁡\n(\n0\n,\nσ\n2\n​\nI\n)\n\\nu\\sim N(0,\\sigma^{2}I)\n, then\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nδ\n)\n=\nn\n+\n2\n​\nσ\n​\nlog\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nδ\n)\nB_{\\Phi}(\\delta)=\\sqrt{n}+2\\sigma\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\delta)}\n.\nThe approximation error bound (\n4.5\n)\nalso scales as\nB\nh\n​\n(\nB\nx\n∨\n1\n)\nK\n​\n(\nn\n+\nlog\n⁡\nK\n)\n\\frac{B_{h}(B_{x}\\vee 1)}{\\sqrt{K}}(n+\\log{K})\n.\n5.2.2\nOther kernels\nWe now consider some other possible choices of kernels.\nKernels leveraging prior physical information\nAny known physical structure can easily be combined with\nreproducing kernels. As a concrete example,\nsuppose the state\nx\nx\ndecomposes as\nx\n=\n(\nx\n1\n,\nx\n2\n)\n∈\nℝ\nn\n1\n+\nn\n2\nx=(x_{1},x_{2})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}+n_{2}}\n, and\nthat the unknown dynamics factorizes as\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n=\nh\n1\n​\n(\nx\n1\n)\n​\nh\n2\n​\n(\nx\n2\n)\nh(x)=h_{1}(x_{1})h_{2}(x_{2})\n, where\nh\n1\n:\nℝ\nn\n1\n→\nℝ\nd\nh_{1}:\\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nis a known vector-valued function and\nh\n2\n:\nℝ\nn\n2\n→\nℝ\nh_{2}:\\mathbb{R}^{n_{2}}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\nis an unknown function in an RKHS with scalar kernel\n𝗄\n\\mathsf{k}\n.\nThen we can set\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\n(\nx\n1\n,\nx\n2\n)\n,\n(\nz\n1\n,\nz\n2\n)\n)\n=\nh\n1\n​\n(\nx\n1\n)\n​\nh\n1\n​\n(\nz\n1\n)\n𝖳\n​\n𝗄\n​\n(\nx\n2\n,\nz\n2\n)\n\\mathsf{K}((x_{1},x_{2}),(z_{1},z_{2}))=h_{1}(x_{1})h_{1}(z_{1})^{\\mathsf{T}}\\mathsf{k}(x_{2},z_{2})\n.\nThis type of structural simplification is common\nin, e.g., robotic applications\n(\nSanner and Slotine 1995\n)\n.\nThe neural tangent kernel\nThe neural tangent kernel\n(\nJacot et al. 2018\n)\nwas recently developed as an approximation to infinitely wide deep neural networks. Consider a network\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\nh(x,\\theta)\n, where\nx\nx\ndenotes the network input\nand\nθ\n\\theta\ndenotes the network parameters.\nThe NTK is defined as the following kernel:\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nz\n)\n=\n𝔼\nθ\n∼\n𝒟\n​\n[\n∂\nh\n∂\nθ\n​\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n𝖳\n​\n∂\nh\n∂\nθ\n​\n(\nz\n,\nθ\n)\n]\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mathsf{K}(x,z)=\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta\\sim\\mathcal{D}}\\left[\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial\\theta}(x,\\theta)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial\\theta}(z,\\theta)\\right],\nwhere\n𝒟\n\\mathcal{D}\nis the distribution used to initialize the\nweights of the network.\nExpressions of the NTK for various common architectures are available in closed form\n(\nArora et al. 2019\n)\n.\n6\nRandomized adaptive control and prediction\nWe now demonstrate how the nonparametric input in Theorem\n4.5\ncan be approximated using the uniform approximation theory of Section\n5\nto obtain adaptive control and prediction algorithms with high-probability guarantees of convergence. We state completely general results under the assumption that the unknown dynamics\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\nh(\\cdot)\ncan be uniformly approximated to a desired degree of accuracy, similar to the classical results of\nSanner and Slotine 1992\nbut in a generalized context. Taking\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\nh(\\cdot)\nto lie in the function space\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\nand applying the results of Section\n5\nimmediately gives a sufficient bound on the number of random features needed to track the desired trajectory to a given tolerance.\n6.1\nDeadzones\nBefore we present our main approximate algorithms, we first introduce the notion of a deadzone. Since any finite-dimensional\napproximation to\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\nh(\\cdot)\nwill have some non-zero approximation error,\nany adaptive algorithm cannot learn below this noise floor;\na deadzone allows us to disable adaptation when the only residual error\nremaining is due to approximation error.\nDefinition 6.1\n.\nLet\nΔ\n>\n0\n\\Delta>0\n.\nA continuously differentiable function\nσ\nΔ\n:\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\n\\sigma_{\\Delta}:\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\nis called\nΔ\n\\Delta\n-admissible deadzone if:\n(i)\n0\n⩽\nσ\nΔ\n0\\leqslant\\sigma_{\\Delta}\nand\nσ\nΔ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n=\n0\n\\sigma_{\\Delta}(x)=0\nfor all\nx\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nΔ\n]\nx\\in[0,\\Delta]\n,\n(ii)\n0\n⩽\nσ\nΔ\n′\n0\\leqslant\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}\nand\nσ\nΔ\n′\n​\n(\nx\n)\n=\n0\n\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(x)=0\nfor all\nx\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nΔ\n]\nx\\in[0,\\Delta]\n,\n(iii)\nσ\nΔ\n′\n\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}\nis locally Lipschitz.\nThe function\nσ\nΔ\n\\sigma_{\\Delta}\nis called a\n(\nΔ\n,\nL\n,\nB\n)\n(\\Delta,L,B)\n-admissible deadzone if\ncondition (\niii\n) is replaced with the condition that\nσ\nΔ\n′\n\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}\nis\nL\nL\n-Lipschitz and\nB\nB\n-bounded.\nWe now give some examples of\nΔ\n\\Delta\n-admissible deadzones.\nThe first example is a direct extension of the deadzone used\nin\nSanner and Slotine 1992\n.\nExample 6.2\n.\nFix a scalar\nδ\n>\n0\n\\delta>0\n. Let\ns\nδ\n:\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\n⩾\n0\ns_{\\delta}:\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\nbe defined as\ns\nδ\n(\nx\n)\n:=\n(\nx\n−\nδ\n)\n𝟏\n{\nx\n>\nδ\n}\ns_{\\delta}(x):=(x-\\delta)\\mathbf{1}\\{x>\\delta\\}\n.\nFor any\nΔ\n>\n0\n\\Delta>0\n, the function\nx\n↦\ns\nΔ\n2\n​\n(\nx\n)\nx\\mapsto s_{\\sqrt{\\Delta}}^{2}(\\sqrt{x})\nis a\n(\nΔ\n,\n1\n/\n(\n2\n​\nΔ\n)\n,\n1\n)\n(\\Delta,1/(2\\Delta),1)\n-admissible deadzone.\nAn issue with a deadzone based on\ns\nδ\ns_{\\delta}\nis that the Lipschitz\nconstant of the derivative diverges with vanishing\nΔ\n\\Delta\n. This makes it challenging to prove sharp “approximate interpolation” results similar to Theorem\n4.7\n.\nTo remedy this issue, we construct a deadzone\nwhere the Lipschitz constant of the derivative is\ndecoupled from\nΔ\n\\Delta\n. The following construction\nis directly inspired by smooth approximations to the hinge loss\nfor support vector machines (see e.g.\nChapelle 2007\n).\nExample 6.3\n.\nFix\nδ\n>\n0\n\\delta>0\nand\nγ\n>\n0\n\\gamma>0\n.\nDefine\ns\nδ\n,\nγ\ns_{\\delta,\\gamma}\nas:\ns\nδ\n,\nγ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n:=\n{\n0\nif\n​\nx\n⩽\nδ\n,\n(\nx\n−\nδ\n)\n2\n4\n​\nγ\nif\n​\nx\n∈\n(\nδ\n,\nδ\n+\n2\n​\nγ\n)\n,\nx\n−\n(\nδ\n+\nγ\n)\nif\n​\nx\n⩾\nδ\n+\n2\n​\nγ\n.\n\\displaystyle s_{\\delta,\\gamma}(x):=\\begin{cases}0&\\text{if }x\\leqslant\\delta,\\\\\n\\frac{(x-\\delta)^{2}}{4\\gamma}&\\text{if }x\\in(\\delta,\\delta+2\\gamma),\\\\\nx-(\\delta+\\gamma)&\\text{if }x\\geqslant\\delta+2\\gamma.\\end{cases}\nFor any\nΔ\n>\n0\n\\Delta>0\nand\nγ\n>\n0\n\\gamma>0\n, the function\ns\nΔ\n,\nγ\ns_{\\Delta,\\gamma}\nis a\n(\nΔ\n,\n1\n/\n(\n2\n​\nγ\n)\n,\n1\n)\n(\\Delta,1/(2\\gamma),1)\n-admissible deadzone.\nWorked details of Examples\n6.2\nand\n6.3\nmay be found in Appendix\nE\n. Our results to come will be stated in terms of an arbitrary deadzone according to Definition\n6.1\n, but concrete instantiations can be found via these prescriptions.\n6.2\nAdaptive control\nWe are now ready to present our main result\nin the setting of approximate control. The following is a general result about adaptive control with uniform approximation that can be applied with an arbitrary choice of basis.\nTheorem 6.4\n(Adaptive control with finite-dimensional approximation)\n.\nSuppose that Assumption\n3.7\nholds.\nLet\nα\nℓ\n,\n0\n:=\narg\n⁡\nmin\nα\n∈\nO\nℓ\n​\nψ\nℓ\n​\n(\nα\n)\n\\alpha_{\\ell,0}:=\\arg\\min_{\\alpha\\in O_{\\ell}}\\psi_{\\ell}(\\alpha)\nfor\nℓ\n∈\n{\np\n,\nm\n}\n\\ell\\in\\{p,m\\}\n.\nFix\nB\nα\np\n>\n0\nB_{\\alpha_{p}}>0\nsatisfying\nd\nψ\np\n(\nα\np\n∥\nα\np\n,\n0\n)\n⩽\nB\nα\np\nd_{\\psi_{p}}\\left(\\alpha_{p}\\|\\alpha_{p,0}\\right)\\leqslant B_{\\alpha_{p}}\n,\nB\nα\nm\n>\n0\nB_{\\alpha_{m}}>0\n, and\nR\nR\nsatisfying\nR\n>\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n,\n0\n)\n+\nB\nα\np\n+\nB\nα\nm\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle R>\\mu_{1}^{-1}\\left(Q(e(0),0)+B_{\\alpha_{p}}+B_{\\alpha_{m}}\\right).\nSuppose there exists a finite\nC\ne\nC_{e}\nsuch that\nfor every\nT\n>\n0\nT>0\n:\nmax\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\n⁡\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n​\nimplies\n​\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nT\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\ne\n​\nR\n.\n\\displaystyle\\max_{t\\in[0,T]}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R\\text{ implies }\\lVert x(T)-x_{d}(T)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant C_{e}R.\n(6.1)\nLet\nΨ\n:\nℝ\nn\n→\nℝ\nd\n×\nm\n\\Psi:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times m}\nbe a locally Lipschitz feature map.\nDefine the constants\nB\nd\n\\displaystyle B_{d}\n:\n=\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\n∥\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle:=\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\lVert x_{d}(t)\\rVert_{2},\nB\nx\n\\displaystyle B_{x}\n:\n=\nC\ne\n​\nR\n+\nB\nd\n,\n\\displaystyle:=C_{e}R+B_{d},\nB\ng\ne\n\\displaystyle B_{g_{e}}\n:\n=\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nx\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nop\n,\n\\displaystyle:=\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{x}}\\lVert g_{e}(x,t)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}},\nB\n∇\nQ\n\\displaystyle B_{\\nabla Q}\n:\n=\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\nsup\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n∥\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle:=\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\sup_{\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R}\\lVert\\nabla Q(e,t)\\rVert_{2},\nB\napprox\n\\displaystyle B_{\\mathrm{approx}}\n:\n=\ninf\nd\nψ\nm\n(\nα\nm\n∥\nα\nm\n,\n0\n)\n⩽\nB\nα\nm\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nx\n∥\nΨ\n(\nx\n)\nα\nm\n−\nh\n(\nx\n)\n∥\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle:=\\inf_{d_{\\psi_{m}}\\left(\\alpha_{m}\\|\\alpha_{m,0}\\right)\\leqslant B_{\\alpha_{m}}}\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{x}}\\lVert\\Psi(x)\\alpha_{m}-h(x)\\rVert_{2}.\nLet\nΔ\n\\Delta\nbe any positive constant satisfying\nΔ\n⩾\nμ\n2\n​\n(\nρ\n−\n1\n​\n(\n2\n​\nB\ng\ne\n​\nB\n∇\nQ\n​\nB\napprox\n)\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\Delta\\geqslant\\mu_{2}(\\rho^{-1}(2B_{g_{e}}B_{\\nabla Q}B_{\\mathrm{approx}})),\nand let\nσ\nΔ\n\\sigma_{\\Delta}\nbe a\nΔ\n\\Delta\n-admissible deadzone.\nThen the dynamical system\nx\n˙\n\\displaystyle\\dot{x}\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\np\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=f(x,t)+g(x,t)(u(x,t)-Y(x,t)\\alpha_{p}-h(x)),\ne\n˙\n\\displaystyle\\dot{e}\n=\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\np\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=f_{e}(e,t)+g_{e}(x,t)(u(x,t)-Y(x,t)\\alpha_{p}-h(x)),\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle u(x,t)\n=\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\n^\np\n+\nΨ\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n​\nα\n^\nm\n,\n\\displaystyle=Y(x,t)\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}+\\Psi(x)\\hat{\\alpha}_{m},\nd\nd\n​\nt\n∇\nψ\np\n(\nα\n^\np\n)\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}\\nabla\\psi_{p}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{p})\n=\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n)\nY\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e,t))Y(x,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(e,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e,t),\nd\nd\n​\nt\n∇\nψ\nm\n(\nα\n^\nm\n)\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}\\nabla\\psi_{m}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{m})\n=\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n)\nΨ\n(\nx\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e,t))\\Psi(x)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(e,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e,t),\nwith initial conditions\nx\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n=\nx\n0\nx(0)=x_{0}\n,\ne\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n=\nm\n⁡\n(\nx\n0\n,\n0\n)\ne(0)=m(x_{0},0)\n,\nα\n^\np\n​\n(\n0\n)\n=\nα\np\n,\n0\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}(0)=\\alpha_{p,0}\n,\nand\nα\n^\nm\n​\n(\n0\n)\n=\nα\nm\n,\n0\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}(0)=\\alpha_{m,0}\nhas a\nsolution\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\np\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\n(x(t),e(t),\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}(t),\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}(t))\nthat exists for all\nt\n⩾\n0\nt\\geqslant 0\n.\nFurthermore,\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nΔ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\mu_{1}^{-1}(\\Delta).\nTheorem\n6.4\ncan be used in conjunction with the results of Section\n5\nto obtain a high-probability guarantee for control, as illustrated by the following example.\nExample 6.5\n(Adaptive control with random features)\n.\nSuppose for simplicity that\nd\nψ\nm\n(\nx\n∥\ny\n)\n=\n1\n2\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\n2\n2\nd_{\\psi_{m}}\\left(x\\|y\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{2}^{2}\nis the Euclidean distance.\nFix a positive integer\nK\nK\n,\nand let\nδ\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n1\n)\n\\delta\\in(0,1)\n.\nAssume that\nh\n∈\nℱ\n2\n​\n(\nB\nh\n)\nh\\in\\mathcal{F}_{2}(B_{h})\nunder\nAssumption\n5.2\n,\nand again for simplicity assume that\nthe kernel is decomposable as in Section\n5.2\n.\nSet\nB\nα\nm\n=\nB\nh\n2\n/\n2\nB_{\\alpha_{m}}=B_{h}^{2}/2\n. Let\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K}\nbe i.i.d. draws from\nν\n\\nu\n. Then, by Equation\n5.1\n,\nwith probability at least\n1\n−\nδ\n1-\\delta\nthere exists\nα\nm\n=\n(\nα\nm\n,\n1\n,\n…\n,\nα\nm\n,\nK\n)\n∈\nℝ\nK\n​\nd\n1\n\\alpha_{m}=(\\alpha_{m,1},...,\\alpha_{m,K})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{Kd_{1}}\nsatisfying\n∥\nα\nm\n,\ni\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nh\n/\nK\n\\lVert\\alpha_{m,i}\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{h}/K\nfor\ni\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nK\ni=1,...,K\nand\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nx\n∥\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n−\nΨ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n)\n​\nα\nm\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\n⁡\n(\nh\n,\nδ\n)\n​\n(\nB\nx\n​\nn\n+\nd\n1\n)\nK\n,\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{x}}\\lVert h(x)-\\Psi\\left(x;\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K}\\right)\\alpha_{m}\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\frac{C(h,\\delta)(B_{x}\\sqrt{n}+\\sqrt{d_{1}})}{\\sqrt{K}},\nwith\nΨ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n)\n=\n[\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n1\n)\n,\n…\n,\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\nK\n)\n]\n∈\nℝ\nd\n×\nK\n​\nd\n1\n\\Psi(x;\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\Phi(x,\\theta_{1}),...,\\Phi(x,\\theta_{K})\\end{bmatrix}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times Kd_{1}}\n.\nHere,\nC\n⁡\n(\nh\n,\nδ\n)\n>\n0\nC(h,\\delta)>0\nis a constant that depends only on\nh\nh\nand\nδ\n\\delta\n.\nNote that\nd\nψ\nm\n(\nα\nm\n∥\n0\n)\n=\n1\n2\n∥\nα\nm\n∥\n2\n2\n=\n1\n2\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\n∥\nα\nm\n,\ni\n∥\n2\n2\n⩽\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nB\nh\n2\n2\n​\nK\n2\n=\nB\nh\n2\n2\n​\nK\n⩽\nB\nα\nm\n,\n\\displaystyle d_{\\psi_{m}}\\left(\\alpha_{m}\\|0\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\alpha_{m}\\rVert_{2}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\lVert\\alpha_{m,i}\\rVert_{2}^{2}\\leqslant\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\frac{B_{h}^{2}}{2K^{2}}=\\frac{B_{h}^{2}}{2K}\\leqslant B_{\\alpha_{m}},\nso that\nB\napprox\n⩽\nC\n⁡\n(\nh\n,\nδ\n)\n​\n(\nB\nx\n​\nn\n+\nd\n1\n)\nK\nB_{\\mathrm{approx}}\\leqslant\\frac{C(h,\\delta)(B_{x}\\sqrt{n}+\\sqrt{d_{1}})}{\\sqrt{K}}\n.\nHence, to ensure\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nε\n\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\varepsilon\nfor some\nε\n>\n0\n\\varepsilon>0\n, it suffices to take\nK\nK\nsatisfying\nK\n⩾\n4\n​\nB\ng\ne\n2\n​\nB\n∇\nQ\n2\n​\nC\n​\n(\nh\n,\nδ\n)\n2\n​\n(\nB\nx\n​\nn\n+\nd\n1\n)\n2\nρ\n2\n​\n(\nμ\n2\n−\n1\n​\n(\nμ\n1\n​\n(\nε\n)\n)\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle K\\geqslant\\frac{4B_{g_{e}}^{2}B_{\\nabla Q}^{2}C(h,\\delta)^{2}(B_{x}\\sqrt{n}+\\sqrt{d_{1}})^{2}}{\\rho^{2}(\\mu_{2}^{-1}(\\mu_{1}(\\varepsilon)))}.\nSuppose that\nμ\n1\n​\n(\nx\n)\n=\nμ\n​\nx\n\\mu_{1}(x)=\\mu x\n,\nμ\n2\n​\n(\nx\n)\n=\nL\n​\nx\n\\mu_{2}(x)=Lx\n, and\nρ\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n=\nβ\n​\nx\n\\rho(x)=\\beta x\n5\n5\n5\nFor\nV\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nV(t)\na quadratic Lyapunov function certifying exponential stability, it is a simple calculation to show that one can take\nQ\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\nV\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nQ(t)=\\sqrt{V(t)}\nto obtain such linear functions for\nμ\n1\n,\nμ\n2\n\\mu_{1},\\mu_{2}\nand\nρ\n\\rho\n.\n. Then this bound simplifies to\nK\n⩾\n4\nβ\n2\n​\nε\n2\n​\n(\nL\nμ\n)\n2\n​\nB\ng\ne\n2\n​\nB\n∇\nQ\n2\n​\nC\n​\n(\nh\n,\nδ\n)\n2\n​\n(\nB\nx\n​\nn\n+\nd\n1\n)\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle K\\geqslant\\frac{4}{\\beta^{2}\\varepsilon^{2}}\\left(\\frac{L}{\\mu}\\right)^{2}B_{g_{e}}^{2}B_{\\nabla Q}^{2}C(h,\\delta)^{2}(B_{x}\\sqrt{n}+\\sqrt{d_{1}})^{2}.\nApproximation region\nFor simplicity of presentation, we have chosen the approximation region in Theorem\n6.4\nlarge enough to cover the variation of the error signal throughout\nadaptation. Alternatively, the approximation region can be specified\na-priori\n, and sliding mode control can be used to force the system to stay inside the approximation region. Such a formulation requires additional technical assumptions on the error dynamics.\nContraction\nAssume that the error dynamics is contracting. Then we may take\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\nQ(e,t)\nto be the Riemannian energy as in Remark\n3.8\nand set\nψ\nℓ\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n=\n1\n2\n​\n∥\n⋅\n∥\n2\n2\n\\psi_{\\ell}(\\cdot)=\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_{2}^{2}\nfor\nℓ\n∈\n{\np\n,\nm\n}\n\\ell\\in\\{p,m\\}\nto recover the contraction metric-based adaptation law due to\nLopez and Slotine 2021\nα\n^\n˙\nm\n=\n−\nΨ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n𝖳\n​\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n​\nγ\ns\n​\n(\ne\n,\n0\n,\nt\n)\n.\n\\dot{\\hat{\\alpha}}_{m}=-\\Psi(x)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}M(e,t)\\gamma_{s}(e,0,t).\nHere,\nγ\ns\n​\n(\ne\n,\n0\n,\nt\n)\n\\gamma_{s}(e,0,t)\ndenotes the tangent vector to a geodesic in the metric\nM\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\nM(e,t)\nbetween\ne\ne\nand the origin at the endpoint\ne\ne\n(a similar metric-based update also holds for\nα\n^\np\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}\n).\nMirror descent\nBy analogy to mirror descent, the choice of potential functions\nψ\np\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\psi_{p}(\\cdot)\nand\nψ\nm\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\psi_{m}(\\cdot)\ncan be used to regularize the learned physical and random feature models, or can be used to improve convergence when adapted to the problem geometry\n(\nBoffi and Slotine 2021\n)\n. The random sinusoidal features considered in Section\n5\nare uniformly bounded in\nℓ\n∞\n\\ell_{\\infty}\nnorm independent of the number of parameters. This observation suggests that, for a large number of features, a potential function strongly convex with respect to the\nℓ\n1\n\\ell_{1}\nnorm such as the hypentropy potential due to\nGhai et al. 2020\nmay lead to improved performance.\nInterpolation\nWe conclude our treatment of adaptive control by presenting an approximate version of Theorem\n4.7\n, which demonstrates\nhow the approximation error from finite-dimensional truncation\ntranslates into an interpolation error for the learned dynamics approximation.\nSpecifically, if Theorem\n6.4\nis invoked with a\n(\nΔ\n,\nL\n,\nB\n)\n(\\Delta,L,B)\n-admissible deadzone,\nthen the following result shows that the interpolation error is bounded by\nO\n⁡\n(\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nΔ\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\nL\n)\n)\nO\\left(\\sqrt{\\mu_{1}^{-1}(\\Delta)(1+L)}\\right)\n. This motivates the construction in Example\n6.3\n.\nTheorem 6.6\n(Approximate interpolation)\n.\nSuppose the hypotheses of Theorem\n6.4\nhold.\nLet\nσ\nΔ\n\\sigma_{\\Delta}\ndenote a\n(\nΔ\n,\nL\n,\nB\n)\n(\\Delta,L,B)\n-admissible deadzone,\nand assume that\nf\ne\nf_{e}\n,\ng\ne\ng_{e}\n, and\nY\nY\nare locally Lipschitz in\ntheir first arguments uniformly in\nt\nt\n.\nThen there exist constants\nC\n1\n>\n0\nC_{1}>0\nand\nC\n2\n>\n0\nC_{2}>0\nnot depending on\nΔ\n\\Delta\nsuch that\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\np\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\n1\n​\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nΔ\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\nL\n)\n+\nC\n2\n​\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nΔ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert g_{e}(x(t),t)(u(x(t),t)-Y(x(t),t)\\alpha_{p}-h(x(t)))\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant C_{1}\\sqrt{\\mu_{1}^{-1}(\\Delta)(1+L)}+C_{2}\\mu_{1}^{-1}(\\Delta).\n6.3\nAdaptive prediction\nSimilar to Theorem\n6.4\n, the following theorem designs a predictor by leveraging the ability to uniformly approximate the unknown dynamics to a suitable degree of accuracy.\nTheorem 6.7\n(Adaptive prediction with uniform approximation)\n.\nSuppose that the trajectory\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nof the system\nx\n˙\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n\\dot{x}=f(x,t)\nis uniformly bounded.\nChoose a continuous and locally Lipschitz\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n)\nk(\\hat{x},x)\nsuch that\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n+\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\nf(\\hat{x},t)+k(\\hat{x},x(t))\nis\ncontracting in a metric\nM\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\n𝖲𝗒𝗆\n⩾\n0\nn\n×\nn\nM:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathsf{Sym}^{n\\times n}_{\\geqslant 0}\nwith rate\nλ\n>\n0\n\\lambda>0\n,\nand suppose that the metric\nM\nM\nsatisfies\nμ\n​\nI\n≼\nM\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n≼\nL\n​\nI\n\\mu I\\preccurlyeq M(\\hat{x},t)\\preccurlyeq LI\nfor all\nx\n^\n\\hat{x}\nand\nt\nt\n.\nLet\nγ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nx\n^\n,\nx\n,\nt\n)\n:\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n→\nℝ\nn\n\\gamma(\\cdot;\\hat{x},x,t):[0,1]\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n}\ndenote a geodesic between\nx\n^\n\\hat{x}\nand\nx\nx\nin the metric\nM\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\nM(\\hat{x},t)\n, and let\nγ\ns\n​\n(\ns\n,\nx\n^\n,\nx\n,\nt\n)\n\\gamma_{s}(s;\\hat{x},x,t)\ndenote the derivative of\ns\n↦\nγ\n⁡\n(\ns\n,\nx\n^\n,\nx\n,\nt\n)\ns\\mapsto\\gamma(s;\\hat{x},x,t)\n.\nSuppose that the map\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n↦\n∥\nγ\ns\n​\n(\n0\n,\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n(\\hat{x},t)\\mapsto\\lVert\\gamma_{s}(0;\\hat{x},x(t),t)\\rVert_{2}\nis locally bounded in\nx\n^\n\\hat{x}\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n.\nFix any\nB\nα\np\n>\n0\nB_{\\alpha_{p}}>0\nsatisfying\nd\nψ\np\n(\nα\np\n∥\nα\np\n,\n0\n)\n⩽\nB\nα\np\nd_{\\psi_{p}}\\left(\\alpha_{p}\\|\\alpha_{p,0}\\right)\\leqslant B_{\\alpha_{p}}\n,\nany\nB\nα\nm\n>\n0\nB_{\\alpha_{m}}>0\n, and\nany\nR\nR\nsatisfying\nR\n>\nQ\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n​\n(\n0\n)\n,\n0\n)\n+\nB\nα\np\n+\nB\nα\nm\nμ\n,\nQ\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n:=\nE\nM\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle R>\\sqrt{\\frac{Q(\\hat{x}(0),0)+B_{\\alpha_{p}}+B_{\\alpha_{m}}}{\\mu}},\\>\\>Q(\\hat{x},t):=E_{M(\\cdot,t)}(\\hat{x},x(t)).\nLet\nΨ\n:\nℝ\nn\n→\nℝ\nd\n×\nm\n\\Psi:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times m}\nbe a locally Lipschitz feature map.\nDefine the following constants\nB\nx\n\\displaystyle B_{x}\n:\n=\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle:=\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\lVert x(t)\\rVert_{2},\nB\nx\n^\n\\displaystyle B_{\\hat{x}}\n:\n=\nR\n+\nB\nx\n,\n\\displaystyle:=R+B_{x},\nB\nγ\n\\displaystyle B_{\\gamma}\n:\n=\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\nsup\n∥\nx\n^\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nx\n^\n∥\nγ\ns\n​\n(\n0\n,\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle:=\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\sup_{\\lVert\\hat{x}\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{\\hat{x}}}\\lVert\\gamma_{s}(0;\\hat{x},x(t),t)\\rVert_{2},\nB\napprox\n\\displaystyle B_{\\mathrm{approx}}\n:\n=\ninf\nd\nψ\nm\n(\nα\nm\n∥\nα\nm\n,\n0\n)\n⩽\nB\nα\nm\nsup\n∥\nx\n^\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nx\n^\n∥\nΨ\n(\nx\n^\n)\nα\nm\n−\nh\n(\nx\n^\n)\n∥\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle:=\\inf_{d_{\\psi_{m}}\\left(\\alpha_{m}\\|\\alpha_{m,0}\\right)\\leqslant B_{\\alpha_{m}}}\\sup_{\\lVert\\hat{x}\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{\\hat{x}}}\\lVert\\Psi(\\hat{x})\\alpha_{m}-h(\\hat{x})\\rVert_{2}.\nChoose any\nΔ\n\\Delta\nsatisfying\nΔ\n⩾\nL\n2\n​\nB\nγ\n​\nB\napprox\nλ\n​\nμ\n\\Delta\\geqslant\\frac{L^{2}B_{\\gamma}B_{\\mathrm{approx}}}{\\lambda\\mu}\n,\nand let\nσ\nΔ\n\\sigma_{\\Delta}\nbe a\nΔ\n\\Delta\n-admissible deadzone.\nThen the dynamical system\nx\n^\n˙\n\\displaystyle\\dot{\\hat{x}}\n=\nf\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nα\n^\np\n,\nα\n^\nm\n,\nt\n)\n+\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\hat{f}(\\hat{x},\\hat{\\alpha}_{p},\\hat{\\alpha}_{m},t)+k(\\hat{x},x(t)),\nf\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nα\n^\np\n,\nα\n^\nm\n,\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle\\hat{f}(\\hat{x},\\hat{\\alpha}_{p},\\hat{\\alpha}_{m},t)\n=\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\n^\np\n+\nΨ\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n)\n​\nα\n^\nm\n,\n\\displaystyle=Y(\\hat{x},t)\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}+\\Psi(\\hat{x})\\hat{\\alpha}_{m},\nd\nd\n​\nt\n∇\nψ\np\n(\nα\n^\np\n)\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}\\nabla\\psi_{p}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{p})\n=\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n)\nY\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(\\hat{x},t))Y(\\hat{x},t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(\\hat{x},t),\nd\nd\n​\nt\n∇\nψ\nm\n(\nα\n^\nm\n)\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}\\nabla\\psi_{m}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{m})\n=\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n)\nΨ\n(\nx\n^\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(\\hat{x},t))\\Psi(\\hat{x})^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(\\hat{x},t),\nwith initial conditions\nx\n^\n​\n(\n0\n)\n=\nx\n^\n0\n\\hat{x}(0)=\\hat{x}_{0}\n,\nα\n^\np\n​\n(\n0\n)\n=\nα\np\n,\n0\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}(0)=\\alpha_{p,0}\n, and\nα\n^\nm\n​\n(\n0\n)\n=\nα\nm\n,\n0\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}(0)=\\alpha_{m,0}\nhas a solution that exists for all\nt\n⩾\n0\nt\\geqslant 0\n. Furthermore,\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nΔ\nμ\n.\n\\displaystyle\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert\\hat{x}(t)-x(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\sqrt{\\frac{\\Delta}{\\mu}}.\nConstructing a metric\n6.7\nrequires a metric\nM\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\nM(\\hat{x},t)\nsuch that\nf\n¯\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n:=\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n+\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n\\bar{f}(\\hat{x},t):=f(\\hat{x},t)+k(\\hat{x},x(t))\nis contracting. One such metric can always be obtained by taking\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n)\n=\n−\nζ\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n−\nx\n)\nk(\\hat{x},x)=-\\zeta(\\hat{x}-x)\n, in which case\n∂\nf\n¯\n∂\nx\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n=\n∂\nf\n∂\nx\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n−\nζ\n​\nI\n\\frac{\\partial\\bar{f}}{\\partial\\hat{x}}(\\hat{x},t)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial\\hat{x}}(\\hat{x},t)-\\zeta I\n. If we further assume that\n∂\nf\n∂\nx\n^\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial\\hat{x}}\nis locally bounded in\nx\n^\n\\hat{x}\nuniformly in\nt\nt\nand that\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nis uniformly bounded, then there exists a finite\nζ\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n∞\n)\n\\zeta\\in(0,\\infty)\nsuch that\nf\n¯\n\\bar{f}\nis contracting in the identity metric\nM\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n=\nI\nM(\\hat{x},t)=I\n.\nIn the case where\nf\nf\nis known,\nk\nk\ncan be tailored to the system physics to obtain improved convergence\n(\nChung and Slotine 2009\n)\n.\nDuality\nThe proof of Theorem\n6.7\nhighlights a duality between the nonlinear adaptive control and nonlinear adaptive prediction problems reminiscent of the duality between LQR and Kalman filtering in linear control theory. Intuitively, any model capable of predicting the time evolution of a system could be used to control the system. Conversely, a model that can be used to control a system could instead be used to predict its evolution.\nInterpolation\nTheorem\n6.7\nassumes that the true system state\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nis measured continuously and concludes that the learned prediction\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\hat{x}(t)\nwill asymptotically become consistent with the observed measurements up to a level specified by the accuracy of the uniform approximation. Applying duality, the interpolation result in Theorem\n6.6\nshows that the learned model\nf\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nα\n^\np\n,\nα\n^\nm\n,\nt\n)\n\\hat{f}(\\hat{x},\\hat{\\alpha}_{p},\\hat{\\alpha}_{m},t)\nbecomes approximately consistent with the true model along the trajectory\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\n.\nDiscrete sampling\nIn practical applications, measurements of the true system state are obtained at discrete instants, and an open-loop predictor with fixed parameters is used to extrapolate beyond them. The parameters are then updated according to a discretized adaptation law when measurements are received. In\nAppendix\nA\n, we demonstrate how the nominal contraction properties required by Theorem\n6.7\ncan be preserved with discrete measurements by taking the feedback term\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n)\nk(\\hat{x},x)\nto have a sufficiently high contraction rate in comparison to the spacing between measurements\nΔ\n​\nt\n\\Delta t\n.\n7\nSimulations\nWe now study the empirical performance of the nonparametric method and its randomized approximation. In the control setting we directly compare the kernel and approximate inputs. In prediction we illustrate the ability of the random feature approximation to scale to high-dimensional systems. In addition, we study the convergence of the prediction and interpolation errors as a function of\nK\nK\n.\n7.1\nAdaptive control\nHere we consider a synthetic example in adaptive control to compare the nonparametric adaptive input to its randomized approximation.\nSystem dynamics\nWe study the stable linear time-invariant system\nx\n˙\n=\nA\n⁡\n(\nx\n−\n3\n2\n​\n𝟏\n)\n+\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n,\nx\n∈\nℝ\n5\n,\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n=\nsin\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n​\nerf\n​\n(\nx\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\dot{x}=A\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{1}\\right)+u(x,t)-h(x),\\>\\>x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{5},\\>\\>h(x)=\\sin(x)\\mathrm{erf}(x),\n(7.1)\nwhere\nA\nA\nis a known matrix\nwith eigenvalues lying entirely in the left half-plane and\n𝟏\n\\mathbf{1}\ndenotes the vector of ones. The operations defining\nh\nh\nare applied elementwise to each coordinate.\nThe error signal is set to\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\n3\n2\n​\n𝟏\ne(t)=x(t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{1}\n, and the desired trajectory is constant at the nominal equilibrium point\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n3\n2\n​\n𝟏\nx_{d}(t)=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{1}\n. This system admits a Lyapunov function\nQ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\n1\n2\n​\n(\nx\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\n𝖳\n​\nP\n​\n(\nx\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\nQ(x,t)=\\frac{1}{2}(x-x_{d}(t))^{\\mathsf{T}}P(x-x_{d}(t))\n,\nwhere\nP\nP\nis the unique positive definite solution to the Lyapunov matrix equation\nA\n𝖳\n​\nP\n+\nP\n​\nA\n=\n−\nI\nA^{\\mathsf{T}}P+PA=-I\n.\nImplementation\nWe apply a nonparametric input generated by the Gaussian kernel\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\ny\n)\n=\nexp\n⁡\n(\n−\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\n2\n2\n2\n​\nσ\n2\n)\n​\nI\n,\nσ\n=\n0.1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathsf{K}(x,y)=\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{2}^{2}}{2\\sigma^{2}}\\right)I,\\>\\>\\sigma=0.1.\nFor its randomized approximation, we use the\nrandom Fourier features described in Section\n5.2\n. Both the randomized and nonparametric adaptive laws are obtained by forward Euler integration with a fixed timestep\nΔ\n​\nt\n=\n0.001\n\\Delta t=0.001\n. At each time, the kernel input (\n4.3\n)\nis evaluated via a Riemann sum approximation at the same resolution,\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\n∫\n0\nt\nK\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nτ\n)\n)\n​\nc\n​\n(\nτ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nτ\n≈\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nn\nt\nK\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\ni\n)\n)\n​\nc\n​\n(\nt\ni\n)\n​\nΔ\n​\nt\n\\displaystyle u(x,t)=\\int_{0}^{t}K(x,x(\\tau))c(\\tau)d\\tau\\approx\\sum_{i=0}^{n_{t}}K(x,x(t_{i}))c(t_{i})\\Delta t\nwith\nn\nt\n=\nt\n/\nΔ\n​\nt\nn_{t}=t/\\Delta t\n. This corresponds to solving the pointwise-decoupled partial differential equation\n∂\nu\n∂\nt\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\nK\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n​\nc\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}(x,t)=K(x,x(t))c(t)\nagain via forward Euler integration with a timestep\nΔ\n​\nt\n\\Delta t\n.\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/control/kernel_input_control_long.pdf]\\put(5.0,60.0){{A}}\n\\end{overpic}\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/control/kernel_input_input_magnitude_long.pdf]\\put(5.0,60.0){{B}}\n\\end{overpic}\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/control/interpolation.pdf]\\put(5.0,60.0){{C}}\n\\end{overpic}\nFigure 1\n:\nAdaptive control.\n(A) Tracking error as a function of time. Error bars display the\n20\n%\n/\n80\n%\n20\\%/80\\%\nquantiles over\n20\n20\ntrials (draws of the\nθ\ni\n\\theta_{i}\n) for each choice of\nK\nK\n. Solid lines display the median. The tracking error decreases monotonically with the number of features, and the kernel input obtains the best performance by several orders of magnitude. (B) Magnitude of the adaptive input over time. The kernel input obtains the best performance despite using the lowest input magnitude. (C) Interpolation error as a function of time. Similar to the tracking error, the interpolation error decreases monotonically with increasing\nK\nK\n, and the kernel input obtains the best performance by several orders of magnitude.\nResults (Figure\n1\n)\nError bars around the random feature curves display the\n20\n%\n20\\%\nand\n80\n%\n80\\%\nquantiles, while the solid central curves display the corresponding median. In comparison to each value of\nK\nK\n, the kernel input obtains the best tracking performance both transiently and asymptotically by several orders of magnitude. The tracking error at each fixed time decreases monotonically as a function of\nK\nK\n(Figure\n1\nA).\nThe overall magnitude of the adaptive control input\n∥\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert u(x,t)\\rVert_{2}\ndecreases monotonically as a function of\nK\nK\n, and the kernel input consistently applies the lowest magnitude input despite obtaining the best performance (Figure\n1\nB).\nSimilar to the tracking error, the kernel input obtains the best dynamics approximation by several orders of magnitude, and the dynamics interpolation error decreases monotonically as a function of\nK\nK\nfor each fixed time (Figure\n1\nC).\n7.2\nAdaptive prediction\nThe infinite-dimensional input considered in Theorem\n4.5\nenjoys guarantees that are independent of the system dimension. As shown by (\n5.1\n), the accuracy of the random feature approximation only depends polynomially on the system dimension. These observations suggest that the nonparametric input and its randomized approximations should scale well to high-dimensional systems.\nFailures of uniform gridding\nAny gridding-based approach must depend\nexponentially\non the system dimension, and as a consequence suffers from the curse of dimensionality.\nModern robotic systems, for instance, often have state dimension in the twenties or thirties, which renders such approaches inapplicable for robotic control.\nFor illustration, consider a uniform gridding method as suggested by the calculations in\nSanner and Slotine 1992\n. For\na nine-dimensional system, placing only ten basis functions in each direction would require one billion total basis functions, a computationally and statistically intractable number.\nHere, we study the efficiency of our randomized method in forming a predictive model of a sixty-dimensional system, and find that our randomized approach leads to good accuracy.\nm\nm\n-body system\nConsider a system of\nm\nm\npoint masses interacting via Newtonian gravitation in\nd\nd\ndimensions, and denote by\nq\ni\n∈\nℝ\nd\nq_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nthe position of mass\ni\ni\nand\np\ni\n∈\nℝ\nd\np_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nthe momentum of mass\ni\ni\n. Assuming equal masses, such a system admits a Hamiltonian in non-dimensionalized units\nH\n⁡\n(\n{\np\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nm\n,\n{\nq\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nm\n)\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n∥\np\ni\n∥\n2\n2\n2\n−\n∑\ni\n<\nj\nm\n1\n∥\nq\ni\n−\nq\nj\n∥\n2\n,\nH\\left(\\left\\{p_{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{m},\\left\\{q_{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\right)=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\frac{\\lVert p_{i}\\rVert_{2}^{2}}{2}-\\sum_{i<j}^{m}\\frac{1}{\\lVert q_{i}-q_{j}\\rVert_{2}},\nand a corresponding symplectic dynamics\nq\n˙\ni\n=\n∂\nH\n∂\np\ni\n\\dot{q}_{i}=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_{i}}\n,\np\n˙\ni\n=\n−\n∂\nH\n∂\nq\ni\n\\dot{p}_{i}=-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q_{i}}\n. We denote by\nx\n=\n(\nq\n𝖳\n,\np\n𝖳\n)\n𝖳\n∈\nℝ\n2\n​\nm\n​\nd\nx=(q^{\\mathsf{T}},p^{\\mathsf{T}})^{\\mathsf{T}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2md}\nwith\nq\n∈\nℝ\nm\n​\nd\nq\\in\\mathbb{R}^{md}\nand\np\n∈\nℝ\nm\n​\nd\np\\in\\mathbb{R}^{md}\nvectors containing the stacked\nq\ni\nq_{i}\nand\np\ni\np_{i}\nover\ni\ni\n.\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/prediction/err_norm.pdf]\\put(5.0,55.0){{A}}\n\\end{overpic}\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/prediction/interpolation.pdf]\\put(5.0,55.0){{B}}\n\\end{overpic}\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/prediction/adaptive_particle_traj.pdf]\\put(5.0,75.0){{C}}\n\\end{overpic}\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/prediction/non_adaptive_particle_traj.pdf]\\put(5.0,75.0){{D}}\n\\end{overpic}\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/prediction/vary_features_wide.pdf]\\put(5.0,60.0){{E}}\n\\end{overpic}\nFigure 2\n:\nAdaptive prediction.\nAll adaptive trajectories use\nK\n=\n2500\nK=2500\nunless otherwise stated.\n(A/B) Prediction error (A) and dynamics interpolation error (B) over time. Both errors smoothly decreases to a ball around zero.\n(C/D) Example position prediction trajectory for the particles with learning (C) and without learning (D). Low-opacity fade denotes particle trajectories in time. The learned system accurately predicts the ground truth, while the system without learning fails to accurately capture the particle motion.\n(E) Prediction and interpolation errors at\nt\n=\n100\nt=100\nas a function of the number of features\nK\nK\n(solid: median, error bars: 20% / 80% quantiles over\n10\n10\ntrials per\nK\nK\nvalue). As\nK\nK\nincreases, the asymptotic prediction error decreases as a power law\n∼\nK\n−\nξ\n\\sim K^{-\\xi}\n, and the interpolation error decreases as a distinct power law\n∼\nK\n−\nζ\n\\sim K^{-\\zeta}\n. Best-fit power laws obtained via nonlinear least squares are shown in dashed with\nξ\n≈\n1.28\n±\n0.03\n\\xi\\approx 1.28\\pm 0.03\nand\nζ\n≈\n0.77\n±\n0.03\n\\zeta\\approx 0.77\\pm 0.03\n.\nHamiltonian estimation\nLet\nq\n^\ni\n∈\nℝ\nd\n\\hat{q}_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nand\np\n^\ni\n∈\nℝ\nd\n\\hat{p}_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\ndenote estimates of the coordinates and momenta of the masses. Similar to the original offline method developed in\nChen et al. 2019\nand the online approach due to\nBoffi and Slotine 2021\n, consider learning a model of the Hamiltonian\nH\n^\n​\n(\n{\np\n^\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nm\n,\n{\nq\n^\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nm\n,\nt\n)\n\\widehat{H}\\left(\\left\\{\\hat{p}_{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{m},\\left\\{\\hat{q}_{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{m},t\\right)\nby evolving the state estimates according to\nq\n^\n˙\ni\n\\displaystyle\\dot{\\hat{q}}_{i}\n=\n∂\nH\n^\n∂\np\n^\ni\n​\n(\n{\np\n^\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nm\n,\n{\nq\n^\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nm\n,\nt\n)\n+\nk\n⋅\n(\nq\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nq\n^\ni\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\partial\\widehat{H}}{\\partial\\hat{p}_{i}}\\left(\\left\\{\\hat{p}_{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{m},\\left\\{\\hat{q}_{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{m},t\\right)+k\\cdot\\left(q_{i}(t)-\\hat{q}_{i}\\right),\np\n^\n˙\ni\n\\displaystyle\\dot{\\hat{p}}_{i}\n=\n−\n∂\nH\n^\n∂\nq\n^\ni\n​\n(\n{\np\n^\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nm\n,\n{\nq\n^\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nm\n,\nt\n)\n+\nk\n⋅\n(\np\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\np\n^\ni\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=-\\frac{\\partial\\widehat{H}}{\\partial\\hat{q}_{i}}\\left(\\left\\{\\hat{p}_{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{m},\\left\\{\\hat{q}_{i}\\right\\}_{i=1}^{m},t\\right)+k\\cdot\\left(p_{i}(t)-\\hat{p}_{i}\\right),\nwhere\nk\n>\n0\nk>0\ndenotes a measurement gain and where\nq\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\nq_{i}(t)\nand\np\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\np_{i}(t)\ndenote measurements of the true system state. The error signals\nq\n~\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\nq\n^\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nq\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\tilde{q}(t)=\\hat{q}_{i}(t)-q_{i}(t)\nand\np\n~\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\np\n^\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\np\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\tilde{p}(t)=\\hat{p}_{i}(t)-p_{i}(t)\ncan be used to update the Hamiltonian estimate\nH\n^\n\\widehat{H}\nuntil\nq\n^\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\hat{q}_{i}(t)\nand\np\n^\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\hat{p}_{i}(t)\nbecome consistent with\nq\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\nq_{i}(t)\nand\np\ni\n​\n(\nt\n)\np_{i}(t)\n.\nSymplectic kernel\nDefine the symplectic matrix\nJ\n=\n(\n0\nI\n−\nI\n0\n)\n,\nso that\n​\nx\n˙\n=\nJ\n​\n∇\nx\nH\n​\n(\nx\n)\nJ=\\begin{pmatrix}0&I\\\\\n-I&0\\end{pmatrix},\\>\\>\\>\\text{so that}\\>\\>\\>\\dot{x}=J\\nabla_{x}H(x)\nand let\nx\n^\n=\n(\nq\n^\n𝖳\n,\np\n^\n𝖳\n)\n𝖳\n\\hat{x}=(\\hat{q}^{\\mathsf{T}},\\hat{p}^{\\mathsf{T}})^{\\mathsf{T}}\nwith\nq\n^\n∈\nℝ\nm\n​\nd\n\\hat{q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{md}\nand\np\n^\n∈\nℝ\nm\n​\nd\n\\hat{p}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{md}\nthe stacked vectors of\nq\n^\ni\n\\hat{q}_{i}\nand\np\n^\ni\n\\hat{p}_{i}\nover\ni\ni\n. We search for the Hamiltonian estimate\nH\n^\n\\widehat{H}\nover an RKHS\nℋ\n𝗄\n\\mathcal{H}_{\\mathsf{k}}\ncorresponding to a scalar-valued translation-invariant kernel\n𝗄\n:\nℝ\n→\nℝ\n\\mathsf{k}:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\n. Similar to the curl-free kernel seen in Section\n5.2\n, we define the\nsymplectic kernel\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\ny\n)\n=\n−\nJ\n​\n∇\n2\n𝗄\n​\n(\nx\n−\ny\n)\n​\nJ\n𝖳\n,\n\\mathsf{K}(x,y)=-J\\nabla^{2}\\mathsf{k}(x-y)J^{\\mathsf{T}},\n(7.2)\nwhich describes the RKHS corresponding to the dynamics\nJ\n​\n∇\nx\n^\n​\nH\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n)\nJ\\nabla_{\\hat{x}}\\widehat{H}(\\hat{x})\nfor\nH\n^\n∈\nℋ\n𝗄\n\\widehat{H}\\in\\mathcal{H}_{\\mathsf{k}}\n. Taking\n𝗄\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\mathsf{k}(\\cdot)\nto be the Gaussian kernel, we may write (\n7.2\n) as\n𝖪\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\ny\n)\n=\n−\nJ\n​\n𝔼\n​\n[\nw\n​\nw\n𝖳\n​\ncos\n⁡\n(\nw\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\n)\n​\ncos\n⁡\n(\nw\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\n)\n]\n​\nJ\n𝖳\n\\mathsf{K}(x,y)=-J\\mathbb{E}[ww^{\\mathsf{T}}\\cos(w^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b)\\cos(w^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b)]J^{\\mathsf{T}}\nwith the expectation taken over\nw\n∼\n𝖭\n⁡\n(\n0\n,\nσ\nw\n2\n​\nI\n)\nw\\sim\\mathsf{N}(0,\\sigma_{w}^{2}I)\nand\nb\n∼\n𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿\n⁡\n(\n0\n,\n2\n​\nπ\n)\nb\\sim\\mathsf{Unif}(0,2\\pi)\n. Let\nΨ\n:\nℝ\n2\n​\nm\n​\nd\n→\nℝ\nK\n\\Psi:\\mathbb{R}^{2md}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{K}\ndenote a vector of random features. We may take each component\nΨ\ni\n​\n(\nx\n^\n)\n=\ncos\n⁡\n(\nw\ni\n𝖳\n​\nx\n^\n+\nb\ni\n)\n\\Psi_{i}(\\hat{x})=\\cos(w_{i}^{\\mathsf{T}}\\hat{x}+b_{i})\nwith the\n(\nw\ni\n,\nb\ni\n)\n(w_{i},b_{i})\ni.i.d. samples and write, for\nγ\n>\n0\n\\gamma>0\na learning rate,\nH\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle\\widehat{H}(\\hat{x},t)\n=\nΨ\n​\n(\nx\n^\n)\n𝖳\n​\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\Psi(\\hat{x})^{\\mathsf{T}}\\hat{\\alpha}(t),\nα\n^\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle\\dot{\\hat{\\alpha}}(t)\n=\n−\nγ\n⁡\n(\n[\n∇\np\n^\nΨ\n​\n(\nx\n^\n)\n]\n𝖳\n​\nq\n~\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\n[\n∇\nq\n^\nΨ\n​\n(\nx\n^\n)\n]\n𝖳\n​\np\n~\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=-\\gamma\\left(\\left[\\nabla_{\\hat{p}}\\Psi(\\hat{x})\\right]^{\\mathsf{T}}\\tilde{q}(t)-\\left[\\nabla_{\\hat{q}}\\Psi(\\hat{x})\\right]^{\\mathsf{T}}\\tilde{p}(t)\\right).\nResults (Figure\n2\n)\nWe consider the sixty-dimensional ten body problem (\nm\n=\n10\nm=10\n) in three dimensions (\nd\n=\n3\nd=3\n).\nWith\nK\n=\n2500\nK=2500\nfeatures, the prediction and interpolation errors for the positions\nq\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nq\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\hat{q}(t)-q(t)\n, momenta\np\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\np\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\hat{p}(t)-p(t)\n, and corresponding dynamics\n∇\np\n^\nH\n^\n\\nabla_{\\hat{p}}\\widehat{H}\nand\n−\n∇\nq\n^\nH\n^\n-\\nabla_{\\hat{q}}\\widehat{H}\nare driven to a small ball around zero (Figure\n2\nA/B).\nIn the early stages of learning (\nt\n≲\n5\nt\\lesssim 5\n), the trajectory prediction oscillates around the target trajectory. As learning proceeds, the prediction becomes smoother and accurately tracks the true system trajectory (Figure\n2\nC).\nAs the number of random features\nK\nK\nincreases, the sizes of the asymptotic balls in both the prediction and interpolation errors decrease as a power law in\nK\nK\n(Figure\n2\nD).\nPower law exponents\nLet\nξ\n\\xi\ndenote the exponent\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n∼\nK\n−\nξ\n\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert\\hat{x}(t)-x(t)\\rVert_{2}\\sim K^{-\\xi}\nin the power law for the prediction error, and let\nζ\n\\zeta\ndenote an analogous quantity for the interpolation error.\nNonlinear least-squares fits lead to estimates (\n±\n\\pm\ndenotes 95% confidence intervals)\nξ\n≈\n1.28\n±\n0.03\n\\xi\\approx 1.28\\pm 0.03\nand\nζ\n≈\n0.77\n±\n0.03\n\\zeta\\approx 0.77\\pm 0.03\n.\nFor the adaptive predictor considered in this section, a Lyapunov function for the nominal error dynamics is the quadratic\nV\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n2\nV(t)=\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}^{2}\n.\nMoreover, due to the feedback term\nk\n⋅\n(\nx\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\nk\\cdot\\left(x(t)-\\hat{x}(t)\\right)\n, the nominal dynamics is exponentially stable with rate\nk\nk\n, and we may take\nρ\n⁡\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n∝\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n2\n\\rho(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})\\propto\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}^{2}\n.\nThis setting was considered in Example\n6.5\nand leads to the analytical predictions\nξ\n=\n1\n/\n2\n\\xi=1/2\nand\nζ\n=\n1\n/\n4\n\\zeta=1/4\n, where\nζ\n=\nξ\n/\n2\n\\zeta=\\xi/2\nfollows after an application of Theorem\n6.6\n.\nThe rates we obtain empirically are\nfaster than the\n𝒪\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nK\n)\n\\mathcal{O}\\left(1/\\sqrt{K}\\right)\nMonte-Carlo rate for random feature approximations\npredicted by our theory.\nOne plausible explanation for this observation is that more features are required to see the\n𝒪\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nK\n)\n\\mathcal{O}\\left(1/\\sqrt{K}\\right)\ntail behavior, as suggested by the flattening of the curve observed near\nK\n≈\n20,000\nK\\approx 20,000\n.\n7.3\nAdaptive control with deep neural networks\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/network/network_error.pdf]\\put(5.0,55.0){{A}}\n\\end{overpic}\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/network/network_input.pdf]\\put(0.0,55.0){{B}}\n\\end{overpic}\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/network/params_linear_w32.pdf]\\put(5.0,60.0){{C}}\n\\end{overpic}\n\\begin{overpic}[figs/network/params_hidden_w32.pdf]\\put(0.0,60.0){{D}}\n\\end{overpic}\nFigure 3\n:\nAdaptive control with multilayer networks.\n(A) Tracking error\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert x(t)-x_{d}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nfor the system without adaptation, the nominal dynamics, an adaptive system with a neural network approximation, and an adaptive system with a random feature approximation. The system without adaptation is driven unstable, while the adaptive systems with neural network and random feature approximations both regulate the actual trajectory to a ball around the desired trajectory. (B) Input norm\n∥\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert u(x(t),t)\\rVert_{2}\n. The neural network system undergoes an initial transient with large input. (C/D) Linear (C) and hidden layer (D) weights over time for the multilayer representation with width of\n32\n32\nneurons. The hidden layer weights change significantly from their initialization, indicating that the network is operating outside of the kernel regime.\nThe preceding sections established the adaptive controllability of high-dimensional systems via randomized approximations of kernel machines.\nThese random feature methods can be viewed as linearizations of neural networks\n(\nGhorbani et al. 2020a\n;\nJacot et al. 2018\n)\n, and have been shown to suffer from the curse of dimensionality when the target function only depends on a few relevant directions in the input space\n(\nBach 2017\n;\nGhorbani et al. 2020b\n)\n.\nNeural networks with a single hidden layer do not exhibit the same difficulties, which raises the question if it is possible to use deep neural networks for adaptive control.\nA gradient flow\nThe Lyapunov-based adaptive law (\n4.2\n) that forms the basis for the learning rules in Theorems\n6.4\n&\n6.7\nmay also be written as the gradient flow\n(\nFradkov et al. 1999\n)\nα\n^\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n−\nγ\n​\n∇\nα\n^\nQ\n˙\n​\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n.\n\\dot{\\hat{\\alpha}}(t)=-\\gamma\\nabla_{\\hat{\\alpha}}\\dot{Q}(e(t),\\hat{\\alpha}(t),t).\n(7.3)\nIn (\n7.3\n),\nQ\n˙\n​\n(\ne\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\n,\nt\n)\n\\dot{Q}(e(t),\\hat{\\alpha},t)\ndenotes the time derivative of the Lyapunov function\nQ\nQ\nfor the nominal error dynamics along the actual error trajectory\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)\n.\nThis formulation of the method shows that the parameters are updated to promote stability by enforcing negativity of\nQ\n˙\n​\n(\ne\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n\\dot{Q}(e(t),\\hat{\\alpha}(t),t)\n.\nImportantly, (\n7.3\n) leads to a simple algorithm for non-linearly parameterized function approximators such as neural networks.\nChoosing as adaptive input\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\nφ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n,\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\nu(x,t)=\\varphi(x,t,\\hat{\\alpha}(t))\na neural network and omitting time arguments for brevity, (\n7.3\n) becomes\nα\n^\n˙\n=\n−\nγ\n(\n∇\nα\n^\nφ\n(\nx\n,\nt\n,\nα\n^\n)\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n.\n\\dot{\\hat{\\alpha}}=-\\gamma\\left(\\nabla_{\\hat{\\alpha}}\\varphi(x,t,\\hat{\\alpha})\\right)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e,t).\n(7.4)\nIn (\n7.4\n), the linear basis functions\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\nY(x,t)\nfrom (\n4.2\n) have been replaced by the Jacobian of the neural network evaluated at the current parameter estimates.\nIn general, it is challenging to obtain practical theoretical guarantees for (\n7.4\n) due to the nonconvexity, time-dependence, and feedback properties of the resulting online optimization problem.\nNevertheless, for expressive classes of functions that empirically exhibit benign optimization landscapes such as neural networks, it is plausible that there exists a target set of parameters\nα\n\\alpha\nthat can render\nQ\n˙\n​\n(\ne\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n,\nt\n)\n\\dot{Q}(e(t),\\alpha,t)\nnegative definite and that these parameters can be found via gradient-based optimization.\nAn unstable system\nTo test the adaptive law (\n7.4\n), we study a more difficult variant of (\n7.1\n) with a time-varying desired trajectory and an unknown dynamics that renders the system unstable in the absence of adaptation,\nx\n˙\n\\displaystyle\\dot{x}\n=\nA\n(\nx\n−\nx\nd\n(\nt\n)\n)\n+\nx\n˙\nd\n(\nt\n)\n+\nu\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n(\nx\n)\n,\nx\n∈\nℝ\n5\n,\n\\displaystyle=A(x-x_{d}(t))+\\dot{x}_{d}(t)+u(x,t)-h(x),\\>\\>\\>x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{5},\n(7.5)\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle x_{d}(t)\n=\nsin\n⁡\n(\n2\n​\nπ\n​\nt\n+\ncos\n⁡\n(\n2\n​\nπ\n​\nt\n)\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\sin\\left(2\\pi t+\\cos\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\pi t\\right)\\right),\nh\ni\n​\n(\nx\n)\n\\displaystyle h_{i}(x)\n=\n1\n4\n​\nx\ni\n4\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{4}x_{i}^{4},\nwhere\nA\nA\nis a known stable matrix and\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\nx_{d}(t)\ndenotes the desired trajectory. We take\nQ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\n1\n2\n​\n(\nx\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\n𝖳\n​\nP\n​\n(\nx\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\nQ(x,t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-x_{d}(t)\\right)^{\\mathsf{T}}P\\left(x-x_{d}(t)\\right)\nwith\nA\n𝖳\n​\nP\n+\nP\n​\nA\n=\n−\nI\nA^{\\mathsf{T}}P+PA=-I\nas in Section\n7.1\n. We consider single hidden-layer neural networks with width of\n32\n32\nor\n64\n64\nneurons and the\nswish\nactivation function. For comparison, we use the same random Fourier feature approximation of the Gaussian kernel as in Section\n7.1\n. We set\nγ\n=\n20\n\\gamma=20\nfor the random feature adaptation law and\nγ\n=\n10\n\\gamma=10\nfor the neural network.\nResults (\nFigure\n3\n)\nBoth the random feature and neural network representations effectively stabilize the system and regulate the actual trajectory to a ball around the desired trajectory (Figure\n3\nA).\nThe neural network obtains slightly improved performance over the random feature method for both choices of the width despite having similar or fewer parameters.\nThis can be traced to learning of the hidden-layer weights, which indicates that the network is operating outside of the kernel regime (Figure\n3\nC/D).\nIn exchange for this improved performance, we find that the neural network adaptation law (\n7.4\n) is significantly more brittle to choice of hyperparameters: while the random feature approximation is provably stable for any choice of learning rate, the neural network adaptation law empirically renders the system unstable for many choices of learning rate.\nMoreover, we find that the stable range of learning rates depends on the network architecture. Increasing the network depth without careful tuning of the learning rate often leads to instability.\nSignatures of this phenomenon can be seen in the input norm\n∥\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert u(x,t)\\rVert_{2}\neven for trajectories that remain stable (Figure\n3\nB), where the magnitude of the neural network input is seen to exceed that of the random feature input by one or two orders of magnitude during an initial transient.\nDiscussion\nThese observations are consistent with both the theory presented in this work and the approximation properties of neural networks.\nNeural networks, in principle, can perform better than kernel methods due to greater expressivity.\nNevertheless, due to the worst-case difficulty of the corresponding online nonconvex optimization, a stability proof as provided in this work for kernel methods is likely out of reach.\nFor adaptive control systems where stability of the closed-loop dynamics is necessary, these considerations may render kernel methods a more desirable choice than deeper architectures.\nNevertheless, understanding if the adaptive law (\n7.4\n) can be modified to ensure stability of the closed-loop dynamics – or, to the same end, if a neural network-based adaptive system can be augmented with a kernel-based approach – are interesting directions of future research.\n8\nConclusions and future directions\nIn this work, we introduced a novel nonparametric method for adaptive control and prediction that estimates the unknown dynamics over a reproducing kernel Hilbert space. By restricting to the space\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\n, we analyzed efficient finite-dimensional randomized approximations that scale well to high dimension.\nA promising future direction of work is to study the Banach space\nℱ\n1\n\\mathcal{F}_{1}\nof single-layer neural networks of the form\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nμ\n​\n(\n𝑑\nθ\n)\nh(\\cdot)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta)\\mu(d\\theta)\nfor a signed Radon measure\nμ\n\\mu\n(\nBach 2017\n;\nBengio et al. 2006\n)\n.\nThe space\nℱ\n1\n\\mathcal{F}_{1}\nadmits convergence analyses for gradient-based optimization via the theory of Wasserstein gradient flows, as well as efficient approximation via particle methods\n(\nMei et al. 2018\n;\nRotskoff and Vanden-Eijnden 2019\n)\n. Such approaches could in principle be generalized to the adaptive control setting considered here via the gradient flow algorithm (\n7.4\n).\n9\nAcknowledgments\nNMB thanks Eric Vanden-Eijnden and Joan Bruna for many instructive discussions on the function spaces\nℱ\n1\n\\mathcal{F}_{1}\nand\nℱ\n2\n\\mathcal{F}_{2}\n. All authors thank Pannag Sanketi and Vikas Sindhwani for helpful feedback.\nReferences\nAnnaswamy et al. (1998)\nAnuradha M. Annaswamy, Fredrik P. Skantze, and Ai-Poh Loh.\nAdaptive control of continuous time systems with convex/concave\nparametrization.\nAutomatica\n, 34(1):33–49, 1998.\nArora et al. (2019)\nSanjeev Arora, Simon S Du, Wei Hu, Zhiyuan Li, Russ R Salakhutdinov, and\nRuosong Wang.\nOn exact computation with an infinitely wide neural net.\nIn\nNeural Information Processing Systems\n, 2019.\nAstolfi and Ortega (2003)\nAlessandro Astolfi and Romeo Ortega.\nImmersion and invariance: a new tool for stabilization and adaptive\ncontrol of nonlinear systems.\nIEEE Transactions on Automatic Control\n, 48(4):590–606, 2003.\nBach (2017)\nFrancis Bach.\nBreaking the curse of dimensionality with convex neural networks.\nJournal of Machine Learning Research\n, 18(1):629–681, 2017.\nBarron (1993)\nAndrew R. Barron.\nUniversal approximation bounds for superpositions of a sigmoidal\nfunction.\nIEEE Transactions on Information Theory\n, 39(3):930–945, 1993.\nBengio et al. (2006)\nYoshua Bengio, Nicolas Roux, Pascal Vincent, Olivier Delalleau, and Patrice\nMarcotte.\nConvex neural networks.\nIn\nNeural Information Processing Systems\n, 2006.\nBobade et al. (2019)\nParag Bobade, Suprotim Majumdar, Savio Pereira, Andrew J. Kurdila, and John B.\nFerris.\nAdaptive estimation for nonlinear systems using reproducing kernel\nhilbert spaces.\nAdvances in Computational Mathematics\n, 45:869–896,\n2019.\nBoffi and Slotine (2021)\nNicholas M. Boff