current synchronization of\nlagrangian systems.\nIEEE Transactions on Robotics\n, 25(3):686–700, 2009.\nCucker and Smale (2002)\nFelipe Cucker and Steve Smale.\nOn the mathematical foundations of learning.\nBulletin of the American Mathematical Society\n, 39(1):1–49, 2002.\nDriver (2004)\nBruce K. Driver.\nAnalysis Tools with Examples\n.\n2004.\nFradkov et al. (1999)\nAlexander L. Fradkov, Iliya V. Miroshnik, and Vladimir O. Nikiforov.\nNonlinear and Adaptive Control of Complex Systems\n.\nSpringer, 1999.\nGhai et al. (2020)\nUdaya Ghai, Elad Hazan, and Yoram Singer.\nExponentiated gradient meets gradient descent.\nIn\nProceedings of the 31st International Conference on\nAlgorithmic Learning Theory\n, 2020.\nGhorbani et al. (2020a)\nBehrooz Ghorbani, Song Mei, Theodor Misiakiewicz, and Andrea Montanari.\nLinearized two-layers neural networks in high dimension.\narXiv:1904.12191\n, February 2020a.\nGhorbani et al. (2020b)\nBehrooz Ghorbani, Song Mei, Theodor Misiakiewicz, and Andrea Montanari.\nWhen Do Neural Networks Outperform Kernel Methods?\narXiv:2006.13409\n, June 2020b.\nJacot et al. (2018)\nArthur Jacot, Franck Gabriel, and Clément Hongler.\nNeural tangent kernel: Convergence and generalization in neural\nnetworks.\nIn\nNeural Information Processing Systems\n, 2018.\nKrstić et al. (1995)\nMiroslav Krstić, Ioannis Kanellakopoulos, and Petar Kokotović.\nNonlinear and Adaptive Control Design\n.\nWiley, 1995.\nKurdila and Lei (2013)\nAndrew Kurdila and Yu Lei.\nAdaptive control via embedding in reproducing kernel hilbert spaces.\nIn\n2013 American Control Conference\n, 2013.\nLohmiller and Slotine (1998)\nWinfried Lohmiller and Jean-Jacques E. Slotine.\nOn contraction analysis for non-linear systems.\nAutomatica\n, 34(6):683–696, 1998.\nLohmiller and Slotine (2000)\nWinfried Lohmiller and Jean-Jacques E. Slotine.\nNonlinear process control using contraction theory.\nAIChE Journal\n, 46(3):588–596, 2000.\nLopez and Slotine (2021)\nBrett T. Lopez and Jean-Jacques E. Slotine.\nAdaptive nonlinear control with contraction metrics.\nIEEE Control Systems Letters\n, 5(1):205–210, 2021.\nLu et al. (2016)\nJing Lu, Steven C.H. Hoi, Jialei Wang, Peilin Zhao, and Zhi-Yong Liu.\nLarge scale online kernel learning.\nJournal of Machine Learning Research\n, 17(47):1–43, 2016.\nMaurer (2016)\nAndreas Maurer.\nA vector-contraction inequality for Rademacher complexities.\narXiv preprint arXiv:1605.00251\n, 2016.\nMei et al. (2018)\nSong Mei, Andrea Montanari, and Phan-Minh Nguyen.\nA mean field view of the landscape of two-layer neural networks.\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\n, 115(33), 2018.\nMinh (2016)\nHá Quang Minh.\nOperator-valued bochner theorem, fourier feature maps for\noperator-valued kernels, and vector-valued learning.\narXiv preprint arXiv:1608.05639\n, 2016.\nPham (2008)\nQuang-Cuong Pham.\nAnalysis of discrete and hybrid stochastic systems by nonlinear\ncontraction theory.\nIn\n2008 10th International Conference on Control, Automation,\nRobotics and Vision\n, 2008.\nRahimi and Recht (2007)\nAli Rahimi and Benjamin Recht.\nRandom features for large-scale kernel machines.\nIn\nNeural Information Processing Systems\n, 2007.\nRahimi and Recht (2008a)\nAli Rahimi and Benjamin Recht.\nWeighted sums of random kitchen sinks: Replacing minimization with\nrandomization in learning.\nIn\nNeural Information Processing Systems\n, 2008a.\nRahimi and Recht (2008b)\nAli Rahimi and Benjamin Recht.\nUniform approximation of functions with random bases.\nIn\n2008 46th Annual Allerton Conference on Communication,\nControl, and Computing\n, 2008b.\nRotskoff and Vanden-Eijnden (2019)\nGrant M. Rotskoff and Eric Vanden-Eijnden.\nTrainability and accuracy of neural networks: An interacting particle\nsystem approach.\narXiv preprint arXiv:1805.00915\n, 2019.\nSanner and Slotine (1992)\nRobert M. Sanner and Jean-Jacques E. Slotine.\nGaussian networks for direct adaptive control.\nIEEE Transactions on Neural Networks\n, 3(6):837–863, 1992.\nSanner and Slotine (1995)\nRobert M. Sanner and Jean-Jacques E. Slotine.\nStable adaptive control of robot manipulators using ”neural”\nnetworks.\nNeural Computation\n, 7:753–790, 1995.\nSindhwani et al. (2018)\nVikas Sindhwani, Stephen Tu, and Seyed Mohammad Khansari-Zadeh.\nLearning contracting vector fields for stable imitation learning.\narXiv preprint arXiv:1804.04878\n, 2018.\nSingh et al. (2020)\nSumeet Singh, Spencer M. Richards, Vikas Sindhwani, Jean-Jacques E. Slotine,\nand Marco Pavone.\nLearning stabilizable nonlinear dynamics with contraction-based\nregularization.\nThe International Journal of Robotics Research\n, 40(10–11):1123–1150, 2020.\nSlotine and Li (1987)\nJean-Jacques E. Slotine and Weiping Li.\nOn the adaptive control of robot manipulators.\nThe International Journal of Robotics Research\n, 6(3), 1987.\nTyukin et al. (2007)\nIvan Y. Tyukin, Danil V. Prokhorov, and Cees van Leeuwen.\nAdaptation and parameter estimation in systems with unstable target\ndynamics and nonlinear parametrization.\nIEEE Transactions on Automatic Control\n, 52(9):1543–1559, 2007.\nAppendix A\nDiscrete sampling\nAssume that measurements of the true system state\n{\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\ni\n)\n}\ni\n=\n0\n∞\n\\left\\{x(t_{i})\\right\\}_{i=0}^{\\infty}\nare received at potentially non-uniformly spaced intervals\nt\ni\n=\nt\n0\n+\n∑\ni\n′\n=\n0\ni\n−\n1\nΔ\n​\nt\ni\n′\nt_{i}=t_{0}+\\sum_{i^{\\prime}=0}^{i-1}\\Delta t_{i^{\\prime}}\n. Denote\nx\n^\ni\n=\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\ni\n)\n\\hat{x}_{i}=\\hat{x}(t_{i})\nand let\nϕ\nt\ni\n+\nΔ\n​\nt\ni\n​\n(\nx\n^\ni\n)\n\\phi_{t_{i}+\\Delta t_{i}}(\\hat{x}_{i})\ndenote the flow from time\nt\ni\nt_{i}\nto time\nt\ni\n+\n1\n=\nt\ni\n+\nΔ\n​\nt\ni\nt_{i+1}=t_{i}+\\Delta t_{i}\nof the\nsystem\nx\n^\n˙\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n\\dot{\\hat{x}}=f(\\hat{x},t)\nstarting at\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\ni\n)\n=\nx\n^\ni\n\\hat{x}(t_{i})=\\hat{x}_{i}\n. We are interested in the contraction properties of the hybrid system\nx\n^\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n\\displaystyle\\hat{x}_{i+1/2}\n=\nϕ\nt\ni\n+\nΔ\n​\nt\ni\n​\n(\nx\n^\ni\n)\n,\nx\n^\ni\n+\n1\n=\nk\ni\n​\n(\nx\n^\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n,\nx\ni\n+\n1\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\phi_{t_{i}+\\Delta t_{i}}(\\hat{x}_{i}),\\>\\>\\hat{x}_{i+1}=k_{i}(\\hat{x}_{i+1/2},x_{i+1}),\nwhere\nx\ni\n+\n1\nx_{i+1}\ndenotes the measurement\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\ni\n+\n1\n)\nx(t_{i+1})\n. The following result is similar to\nLohmiller and Slotine 2000\n.\nProposition A.1\n.\nSuppose that there exists some\nΘ\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nn\n×\nn\n\\Theta:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}\nand\n0\n<\nβ\n<\n1\n0<\\beta<1\nsuch that\ny\ni\n+\n1\n=\nk\ni\n​\n(\ny\ni\n,\nx\n)\ny_{i+1}=k_{i}(y_{i},x)\nis contracting as a discrete-time dynamical system with rate\nβ\n\\beta\nfor any\nx\nx\n, i.e.,\nF\ni\n:=\nΘ\n⁡\n(\ny\ni\n+\n1\n,\nt\ni\n+\n1\n)\n​\n∂\nk\ni\n∂\ny\n​\n(\ny\ni\n,\nx\n)\n​\nΘ\n​\n(\ny\ni\n,\nt\ni\n)\n−\n1\n,\nF\ni\n𝖳\n​\nF\ni\n≼\nβ\n​\nI\n.\nF_{i}:=\\Theta(y_{i+1},t_{i+1})\\frac{\\partial k_{i}}{\\partial y}(y_{i},x)\\Theta(y_{i},t_{i})^{-1},\\>\\>F_{i}^{\\mathsf{T}}F_{i}\\preccurlyeq\\beta I.\nAssume that\nk\ni\n​\n(\nx\n,\nx\n)\n=\nx\nk_{i}(x,x)=x\nfor all\nx\n∈\nℝ\nn\nx\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\n, and denote by\nλ\n¯\ni\n\\displaystyle\\bar{\\lambda}_{i}\n=\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\nt\ni\n,\nt\ni\n+\n1\n]\nλ\nmax\n​\n{\n𝖲𝗒𝗆\n⁡\n(\nΘ\n˙\n​\n(\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n+\nΘ\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\n∂\nf\n^\n∂\nx\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\nΘ\n​\n(\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\n1\n)\n}\n\\displaystyle=\\sup_{t\\in[t_{i},t_{i+1}]}\\lambda_{\\max}\\Bigg\\{\\mathsf{Sym}\\left(\\dot{\\Theta}(\\hat{x}(t),t)+\\Theta(\\hat{x}(t),t)\\frac{\\partial\\hat{f}}{\\partial\\hat{x}}(\\hat{x}(t),t)\\Theta(\\hat{x}(t),t)^{-1}\\right)\\Bigg\\}\nthe maximum expansion rate of the open loop dynamics between\nt\ni\nt_{i}\nand\nt\ni\n+\n1\nt_{i+1}\nin the metric\nM\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n=\nΘ\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n​\nΘ\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\nM(\\hat{x},t)=\\Theta(\\hat{x},t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\Theta(\\hat{x},t)\n. Then the Riemannian energy in the metric\nM\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\nM(\\hat{x},t)\nobeys\nE\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\ni\n+\n1\n,\nx\ni\n+\n1\n)\n⩽\nβ\n​\ne\nλ\n¯\ni\n​\nΔ\n​\nt\ni\n​\nE\n​\n(\nx\n^\ni\n,\nx\ni\n)\n.\nE(\\hat{x}_{i+1},x_{i+1})\\leqslant\\beta e^{\\bar{\\lambda}_{i}\\Delta t_{i}}E(\\hat{x}_{i},x_{i}).\nProof.\nLet\nM\ni\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n=\nM\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\ni\n)\nM_{i}(\\cdot)=M(\\cdot,t_{i})\n.\nLet\nt\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n=\nt\ni\n+\nΔ\n​\nt\ni\n−\nt_{i+1/2}=t_{i}+\\Delta t_{i}^{-}\ndenote the instant before the measurement. Let\nγ\ni\n+\n1\n:\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n→\nℝ\nn\n\\gamma^{i+1}:[0,1]\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n}\ndenote a geodesic in the metric\nM\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\nM_{i+1}(\\cdot)\nbetween\nx\n^\ni\n+\n1\n\\hat{x}_{i+1}\nand\nx\ni\n+\n1\nx_{i+1}\n,\nand let\nγ\ns\ni\n+\n1\n=\nd\nd\n​\ns\n​\nγ\ni\n+\n1\n\\gamma^{i+1}_{s}=\\frac{d}{ds}\\gamma^{i+1}\n. The Riemannian energy under the metric\nM\ni\n+\n1\nM_{i+1}\nis then\nE\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\ni\n+\n1\n,\nx\ni\n+\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle E(\\hat{x}_{i+1},x_{i+1})\n=\n∫\n0\n1\nγ\ns\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\ns\n)\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\ns\n)\n​\n𝑑\ns\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\int_{0}^{1}\\gamma_{s}^{i+1}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{i+1}\\left(\\gamma^{i+1}(s)\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i+1}(s)ds.\nNow let\nγ\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n:\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n→\nℝ\nn\n\\gamma^{i+1/2}:[0,1]\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n}\ndenote a geodesic in the metric\nM\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\nM_{i+1/2}(\\cdot)\nbetween\nx\n^\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n\\hat{x}_{i+1/2}\nand\nx\ni\n+\n1\nx_{i+1}\n. Observe that because\nk\ni\n​\n(\nx\n,\nx\n)\n=\nx\nk_{i}(x,x)=x\nfor all\nx\nx\n,\nx\n^\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n=\nx\ni\n+\n1\n\\hat{x}_{i+1/2}=x_{i+1}\nis a fixed point. Then, by contraction of\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n)\nk(\\hat{x},x)\nin\nx\n^\n\\hat{x}\nwith rate\n0\n<\nβ\n<\n1\n0<\\beta<1\n(cf.\nLohmiller and Slotine 1998\n,\nPham 2008\n):\n∫\n0\n1\nγ\ns\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\ns\n)\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n+\n1\n​\n(\ns\n)\n​\n𝑑\ns\n⩽\nβ\n​\n∫\n0\n1\nγ\ns\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n​\n(\ns\n)\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n​\n(\ns\n)\n​\n𝑑\ns\n.\n\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1}\\gamma_{s}^{i+1}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{i+1}\\left(\\gamma^{i+1}(s)\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i+1}(s)\\>ds\\leqslant\\beta\\int_{0}^{1}\\gamma_{s}^{i+1/2}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{i+1/2}\\left(\\gamma^{i+1/2}(s)\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i+1/2}(s)\\>ds.\nLet\nγ\ni\n:\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n→\nℝ\nn\n\\gamma^{i}:[0,1]\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n}\ndenote a geodesic in the metric\nM\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\nM(\\hat{x},t)\nbetween\nx\n^\ni\n\\hat{x}_{i}\nand\nx\ni\nx_{i}\n, let\nψ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n=\nΘ\n⁡\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n\\psi_{t}(s)=\\Theta\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s),t\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s)\n, and define\nJ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle J_{t}(s)\n=\nΘ\n˙\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n+\nΘ\n⁡\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\n∂\nf\n∂\nx\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\nΘ\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\dot{\\Theta}\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s),t\\right)+\\Theta\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s),t\\right)\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s),t\\right)\\Theta\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s),t\\right)^{-1}.\nObserve that\nd\nd\n​\nt\n​\nψ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n=\nJ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n​\nψ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\psi_{t}(s)=J_{t}(s)\\psi_{t}(s)\n. Hence,\nd\nd\n​\nt\n​\n[\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}\\left[\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s),t\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s)\\right]\n=\n2\n​\nψ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nJ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n​\nψ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle=2\\psi_{t}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}J_{t}(s)\\psi_{t}(s)\n⩽\nλ\n¯\n​\nψ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nψ\nt\n​\n(\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\bar{\\lambda}\\psi_{t}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\psi_{t}(s)\n=\nλ\n¯\ni\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\bar{\\lambda}_{i}\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s),t\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s).\nThen, by the comparison lemma,\nγ\ns\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n​\n(\ns\n)\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n+\n1\n/\n2\n​\n(\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle\\gamma_{s}^{i+1/2}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{i+1/2}\\left(\\gamma^{i+1/2}(s)\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i+1/2}(s)\n⩽\ne\nλ\n¯\ni\n​\nΔ\n​\nt\ni\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\ni\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant e^{\\bar{\\lambda}_{i}\\Delta t_{i}}\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{i}\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s)\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s).\nPlugging this in to our previous bound,\nE\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\ni\n+\n1\n,\nx\ni\n+\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle E(\\hat{x}_{i+1},x_{i+1})\n⩽\nβ\n​\ne\nλ\n¯\ni\n​\nΔ\n​\nt\ni\n​\n∫\n0\n1\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\ni\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n​\n𝑑\ns\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\beta e^{\\bar{\\lambda}_{i}\\Delta t_{i}}\\int_{0}^{1}\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{i}\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s)\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s)\\>ds.\nObserving that\n∫\n0\n1\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\n​\n(\nγ\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\nγ\ns\ni\n​\n(\ns\n)\n​\n𝑑\ns\n=\nE\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\ni\n,\nx\ni\n)\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s)^{\\mathsf{T}}M\\left(\\gamma^{i}(s),t\\right)\\gamma_{s}^{i}(s)ds=E(\\hat{x}_{i},x_{i})\ncompletes the proof.\n∎\nAppendix B\nPreliminary results\nLet\nE\nE\nand\nE\n′\nE^{\\prime}\nbe normed vector spaces.\nDenote by\nℒ\n⁡\n(\nE\n,\nE\n′\n)\n\\mathcal{L}(E,E^{\\prime})\nthe space\nof linear operators from\nE\nE\nto\nE\n′\nE^{\\prime}\nequipped with the operator norm.\nA function\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\nf(x,t)\nmapping\nE\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n↦\nF\nE\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\mapsto F\nwith\nE\nE\nand\nF\nF\nnormed vector spaces is said to be\nlocally bounded in\nx\nx\nif for every\nR\n>\n0\nR>0\nand\nT\n>\n0\nT>0\n,\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\nE\n⩽\nR\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n<\n∞\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{E}\\leqslant R}\\lVert f(x,t)\\rVert_{F}<\\infty.\nProposition B.1\n.\nLet\n{\nE\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\n2\n\\{E_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{2}\n,\n{\nF\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\n2\n\\{F_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{2}\nbe normed vector spaces and let\nf\ni\n:\nE\ni\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nF\ni\nf_{i}:E_{i}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow F_{i}\nfor\ni\n∈\n{\n1\n,\n2\n}\ni\\in\\{1,2\\}\nbe locally\nLipschitz. Then the following hold\n(i)\nIf\nE\n1\n=\nE\n2\nE_{1}=E_{2}\nand\nF\n1\n=\nF\n2\nF_{1}=F_{2}\n, then the sum\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n+\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n(x,t)\\mapsto f_{1}(x,t)+f_{2}(x,t)\nis locally Lipschitz.\n(ii)\nIf\nE\n1\n=\nE\n2\nE_{1}=E_{2}\n,\nF\n1\n=\nℒ\n⁡\n(\nF\n2\n,\nF\n3\n)\nF_{1}=\\mathcal{L}(F_{2},F_{3})\n,\nand both\nf\n1\nf_{1}\nand\nf\n2\nf_{2}\nare locally bounded,\nthen the\nproduct\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n(x,t)\\mapsto f_{1}(x,t)f_{2}(x,t)\nis locally\nLipschitz and locally bounded.\n(iii)\nIf\nF\n1\n=\nE\n2\nF_{1}=E_{2}\nand both\nf\n1\nf_{1}\nand\nf\n2\nf_{2}\nare locally bounded, then the composition\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nf\n2\n​\n(\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n(x,t)\\mapsto f_{2}(f_{1}(x,t),t)\nis locally Lipschitz and locally bounded.\nProof.\nLet\nR\nR\nand\nT\nT\nbe arbitrary positive constants.\nLet\nC\n=\nC\n⁡\n(\nR\n,\nT\n)\n>\n0\nC=C(R,T)>0\nbe a finite positive constant such that:\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\n∥\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n1\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n1\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\lVert f_{1}(x,t)-f_{1}(y,t)\\rVert_{F_{1}}\n⩽\nC\n​\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\nE\n1\n​\n∀\nx\n,\ny\n∈\nB\nE\n1\n​\n(\nR\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant C\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{E_{1}}\\>\\>\\forall x,y\\in B_{E_{1}}(R),\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\n∥\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n2\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n2\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\lVert f_{2}(x,t)-f_{2}(y,t)\\rVert_{F_{2}}\n⩽\nC\n​\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\nE\n2\n​\n∀\nx\n,\ny\n∈\nB\nE\n2\n​\n(\nR\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant C\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{E_{2}}\\>\\>\\forall x,y\\in B_{E_{2}}(R).\nNow, let\nx\n,\ny\n∈\nB\nE\n1\n​\n(\nR\n)\nx,y\\in B_{E_{1}}(R)\nand\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\nt\\in[0,T]\nbe arbitrary.\nThe sum\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n+\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n(x,t)\\mapsto f_{1}(x,t)+f_{2}(x,t)\n.\nObserve that\n∥\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\n(\nf\n2\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\n2\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n)\n∥\nF\n1\n\\displaystyle\\lVert f_{1}(x,t)+g_{1}(x,t)-(f_{2}(y,t)+g_{2}(y,t))\\rVert_{F_{1}}\n⩽\n∥\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n1\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n1\n+\n∥\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n2\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n1\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\lVert f_{1}(x,t)-f_{1}(y,t)\\rVert_{F_{1}}+\\lVert f_{2}(x,t)-f_{2}(y,t)\\rVert_{F_{1}}\n⩽\n2\n​\nC\n​\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\nE\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant 2C\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{E_{1}}.\nThe product\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n(x,t)\\mapsto f_{1}(x,t)f_{2}(x,t)\n.\nLet\nC\n′\n=\nC\n′\n​\n(\nR\n,\nT\n)\n>\n0\nC^{\\prime}=C^{\\prime}(R,T)>0\nbe a finite positive constant such that:\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\nE\n1\n⩽\nR\nmax\n⁡\n{\n∥\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nℒ\n⁡\n(\nF\n2\n,\nF\n3\n)\n,\n∥\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n2\n}\n⩽\nC\n′\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{E_{1}}\\leqslant R}\\max\\{\\lVert f_{1}(x,t)\\rVert_{\\mathcal{L}(F_{2},F_{3})},\\lVert f_{2}(x,t)\\rVert_{F_{2}}\\}\\leqslant C^{\\prime}.\nNow observe that\n∥\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n1\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n​\nf\n2\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n3\n\\displaystyle\\lVert f_{1}(x,t)f_{2}(x,t)-f_{1}(y,t)f_{2}(y,t)\\rVert_{F_{3}}\n⩽\n∥\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n1\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nℒ\n⁡\n(\nF\n2\n,\nF\n3\n)\n​\n∥\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\lVert f_{1}(x,t)-f_{1}(y,t)\\rVert_{\\mathcal{L}(F_{2},F_{3})}\\lVert f_{2}(x,t)\\rVert_{F_{2}}\n+\n∥\nf\n1\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nℒ\n⁡\n(\nF\n2\n,\nF\n3\n)\n​\n∥\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n2\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n2\n\\displaystyle\\qquad+\\lVert f_{1}(y,t)\\rVert_{\\mathcal{L}(F_{2},F_{3})}\\lVert f_{2}(x,t)-f_{2}(y,t)\\rVert_{F_{2}}\n⩽\n2\n​\nC\n​\nC\n′\n​\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\nE\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant 2CC^{\\prime}\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{E_{1}}.\nThe fact that the composition is locally bounded is immediate.\nThe composition\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nf\n2\n​\n(\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n(x,t)\\mapsto f_{2}(f_{1}(x,t),t)\n.\nFirst, let\nC\n′\n=\nC\n′\n​\n(\nR\n,\nT\n)\nC^{\\prime}=C^{\\prime}(R,T)\nbe such that:\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\nE\n1\n⩽\nR\n∥\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n1\n⩽\nC\n′\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{E_{1}}\\leqslant R}\\lVert f_{1}(x,t)\\rVert_{F_{1}}\\leqslant C^{\\prime}.\nNext, let\nC\n′′\n=\nC\n′′\n​\n(\nR\n,\nT\n)\nC^{\\prime\\prime}=C^{\\prime\\prime}(R,T)\nbe such that:\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\n∥\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n2\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n2\n⩽\nC\n′′\n​\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\nE\n2\n​\n∀\nx\n,\ny\n∈\nB\nE\n2\n​\n(\nC\n′\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\lVert f_{2}(x,t)-f_{2}(y,t)\\rVert_{F_{2}}\\leqslant C^{\\prime\\prime}\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{E_{2}}\\>\\>\\forall x,y\\in B_{E_{2}}(C^{\\prime}).\nThen we have:\n∥\nf\n2\n​\n(\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n2\n​\n(\nf\n1\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n2\n⩽\nC\n′′\n​\n∥\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n1\n​\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nF\n1\n⩽\nC\n​\nC\n′′\n​\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\nE\n1\n\\displaystyle\\lVert f_{2}(f_{1}(x,t),t)-f_{2}(f_{1}(y,t),t)\\rVert_{F_{2}}\\leqslant C^{\\prime\\prime}\\lVert f_{1}(x,t)-f_{1}(y,t)\\rVert_{F_{1}}\\leqslant CC^{\\prime\\prime}\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{E_{1}}\nThis shows that the composition is locally Lipschitz.\nThe fact that the composition is locally bounded is immediate.\n∎\nNow, let\nE\nE\nand\nF\nF\nbe normed vector spaces and\nlet\nU\n⊆\nE\nU\\subseteq E\n.\nA function\nf\n:\nU\n→\nF\nf:U\\rightarrow F\nis said to be globally\nLipschitz (uniformly Lipschitz) if\nsup\nx\n,\ny\n∈\nU\n,\nx\n≠\ny\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\ny\n)\n∥\nF\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\nE\n<\n∞\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{x,y\\in U,x\\neq y}\\frac{\\lVert f(x)-f(y)\\rVert_{F}}{\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{E}}<\\infty.\nA function\nf\n:\nU\n→\nF\nf:U\\rightarrow F\nis said to be globally bounded\n(uniformly bounded) if:\nsup\nx\n∈\nU\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n∥\nF\n<\n∞\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{x\\in U}\\lVert f(x)\\rVert_{F}<\\infty.\nProposition B.2\n.\nLet\n{\nE\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\n2\n\\{E_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{2}\nand\n{\nF\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\n2\n\\{F_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{2}\nbe collections of normed vector spaces.\nLet\nf\ni\n:\nU\ni\n→\nF\ni\nf_{i}:U_{i}\\rightarrow F_{i}\nwith\nU\ni\n⊆\nE\ni\nU_{i}\\subseteq E_{i}\nfor\ni\n∈\n{\n1\n,\n2\n}\ni\\in\\{1,2\\}\nbe globally Lipschitz. Then the following hold\n1.\nIf\nE\n1\n=\nE\n2\nE_{1}=E_{2}\nand\nF\n1\n=\nF\n2\nF_{1}=F_{2}\n, then the sum\nx\n↦\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n)\n+\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n)\nx\\mapsto f_{1}(x)+f_{2}(x)\nis globally Lipschitz.\n2.\nIf\nE\n1\n=\nE\n2\nE_{1}=E_{2}\n,\nF\n1\n=\nℒ\n⁡\n(\nF\n2\n,\nF\n3\n)\nF_{1}=\\mathcal{L}(F_{2},F_{3})\n, and both\nf\n1\nf_{1}\nand\nf\n2\nf_{2}\nare globally bounded, then the product\nx\n↦\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n)\n​\nf\n2\n​\n(\nx\n)\nx\\mapsto f_{1}(x)f_{2}(x)\nis globally Lipschitz and globally bounded.\n3.\nIf\nF\n1\n=\nE\n2\nF_{1}=E_{2}\nand both\nf\n1\nf_{1}\nand\nf\n2\nf_{2}\nare globally bounded, then the composition\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nf\n2\n​\n(\nf\n1\n​\n(\nx\n)\n)\n(x,t)\\mapsto f_{2}(f_{1}(x))\nis globally Lipschitz and globally bounded.\nProof.\nNearly identical proof as Proposition\nB.1\n∎\nThe following result generalizes Barbalat’s lemma to the case when the limiting\nvalue of a function\nf\nf\nonly converges to a ball. We first state Barbalat’s lemma, and then state our generalization.\nProposition B.3\n(Barbalat’s lemma)\n.\nLet\nf\n∈\nC\n1\n​\n(\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n,\nℝ\n)\nf\\in C^{1}(\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0},\\mathbb{R})\nsatisfy\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n<\n∞\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}f(t)<\\infty\n. Further assume that\nf\n′\nf^{\\prime}\nis uniformly continuous. Then\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n0\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}f^{\\prime}(t)=0\n.\nProposition B.4\n(Generalized Barbalat’s lemma)\n.\nLet\nf\n∈\nC\n1\n​\n(\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n,\nℝ\n)\nf\\in C^{1}(\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0},\\mathbb{R})\nsatisfy\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n|\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nα\n|\n⩽\nε\n\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}|f(t)-\\alpha|\\leqslant\\varepsilon\nfor some\nα\n∈\nℝ\n\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{R}\nand\nε\n⩾\n0\n\\varepsilon\\geqslant 0\n. Further assume that\nf\n′\nf^{\\prime}\nis\nL\nL\n-Lipschitz.\nThen,\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n|\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n|\n⩽\n2\n​\nε\n​\nL\n.\n\\displaystyle\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}|f^{\\prime}(t)|\\leqslant 2\\sqrt{\\varepsilon L}.\nProof.\nSuppose for a contradiction that\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n|\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n|\n>\n2\n​\nε\n​\nL\n\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}|f^{\\prime}(t)|>2\\sqrt{\\varepsilon L}\n.\nThen there exists an increasing sequence\n{\nt\nn\n}\nn\n⩾\n1\n\\{t_{n}\\}_{n\\geqslant 1}\nwith\nt\nn\n→\n∞\nt_{n}\\rightarrow\\infty\nsuch that\n|\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\nn\n)\n|\n>\n2\n​\nε\n​\nL\n|f^{\\prime}(t_{n})|>2\\sqrt{\\varepsilon L}\nfor all\nn\n⩾\n1\nn\\geqslant 1\n.\nDefine\nδ\n:=\n2\n​\nε\n​\nL\n/\nL\n\\delta:=2\\sqrt{\\varepsilon L}/L\n. Then for any\nn\n⩾\n1\nn\\geqslant 1\n, we have\n|\n∫\nt\nn\nt\nn\n+\nδ\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n​\n𝑑\nt\n|\n\\displaystyle\\left|\\int_{t_{n}}^{t_{n}+\\delta}f^{\\prime}(t)dt\\right|\n=\n|\nδ\n​\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\nn\n)\n+\n∫\nt\nn\nt\nn\n+\nδ\n(\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\nn\n)\n)\n​\n𝑑\nt\n|\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\left|\\delta f^{\\prime}(t_{n})+\\int_{t_{n}}^{t_{n}+\\delta}(f^{\\prime}(t)-f^{\\prime}(t_{n}))dt\\right|,\n⩾\nδ\n​\n|\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\nn\n)\n|\n−\n∫\nt\nn\nt\nn\n+\nδ\n|\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\nn\n)\n|\n​\n𝑑\nt\n,\n\\displaystyle\\geqslant\\delta|f^{\\prime}(t_{n})|-\\int_{t_{n}}^{t_{n}+\\delta}|f^{\\prime}(t)-f^{\\prime}(t_{n})|dt,\n>\nδ\n​\n2\n​\nε\n​\nL\n−\nL\n​\n∫\nt\nn\nt\nn\n+\nδ\n|\nt\nn\n−\nt\n|\n​\n𝑑\nt\n,\n\\displaystyle>\\delta 2\\sqrt{\\varepsilon L}-L\\int_{t_{n}}^{t_{n}+\\delta}|t_{n}-t|dt,\n=\nδ\n​\n2\n​\nε\n​\nL\n−\nL\n2\n​\nδ\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\delta 2\\sqrt{\\varepsilon L}-\\frac{L}{2}\\delta^{2},\n=\n2\n​\nε\n.\n\\displaystyle=2\\varepsilon.\nThis lower bound implies that for any\nn\n⩾\n1\nn\\geqslant 1\n|\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\nn\n+\nδ\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\nn\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle|f(t_{n}+\\delta)-f(t_{n})|\n=\n|\n∫\nt\nn\nt\nn\n+\nδ\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n​\n𝑑\nt\n|\n>\n2\n​\nε\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\left|\\int_{t_{n}}^{t_{n}+\\delta}f^{\\prime}(t)dt\\right|>2\\varepsilon.\nThis bound implies\n2\n​\nε\n\\displaystyle 2\\varepsilon\n<\nlim sup\nn\n→\n∞\n|\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\nn\n+\nδ\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\nn\n)\n|\n,\n\\displaystyle<\\limsup_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}|f(t_{n}+\\delta)-f(t_{n})|,\n⩽\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n|\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\n+\nδ\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n|\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}|f(t+\\delta)-f(t)|,\n⩽\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n|\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\n+\nδ\n)\n−\nα\n|\n+\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n|\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nα\n|\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}|f(t+\\delta)-\\alpha|+\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}|f(t)-\\alpha|,\n⩽\n2\n​\nε\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant 2\\varepsilon,\nwhich yields a contradiction.\n∎\nIn adaptive control, a typical use of Barbalat’s lemma\nis to conclude (via deadzones) that the error signal tends to a small value. In the sequel, we will use\nBarbalat’s lemma in conjunction with the\ngeneralized Barbalat’s lemma (Proposition\nB.4\n)\nto argue that both the error signal and the\ntime derivative\nof the error\nsignal are small. The time derivative of the error\nsignal can be written as a nominal term plus the\nerror of the adaptive signal. By controlling\nthis quantity, we will be able to show that the\nerror of the adaptive signal is small as well,\nallowing us to prove approximate interpolation type results (Theorem\n6.6\n).\nSharpness of the bound\nProposition\nB.4\nis sharp in the following sense.\nFix any\nε\n>\n0\n\\varepsilon>0\nand\nω\n∈\nℝ\n\\omega\\in\\mathbb{R}\n,\nand define\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n:=\nε\n​\nsin\n⁡\n(\nω\nε\n​\nt\n)\nf(t):=\\varepsilon\\sin\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\omega}{\\varepsilon}}t\\right)\n.\nClearly\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n|\nf\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n|\n=\nε\n\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}|f(t)|=\\varepsilon\n, and furthermore\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\nε\n​\nω\n​\ncos\n⁡\n(\nω\nε\n​\nt\n)\n,\nf\n′′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n−\nω\n​\nsin\n⁡\n(\nω\nε\n​\nt\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle f^{\\prime}(t)=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\omega}\\cos\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\omega}{\\varepsilon}}t\\right),\\>\\>f^{\\prime\\prime}(t)=-\\omega\\sin\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\omega}{\\varepsilon}}t\\right).\nThis shows that the smallest valid global\nLipschitz constant for\nf\n′\nf^{\\prime}\nis\nω\n\\omega\n.\nFurthermore,\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n|\nf\n′\n​\n(\nt\n)\n|\n=\nε\n​\nω\n.\n\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}|f^{\\prime}(t)|=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\omega}.\nAppendix C\nOmitted proofs for Section\n4\nC.1\nProof of Theorem\n4.5\nWe first state the following technical lemma.\nLemma C.1\n.\nLet\nE\nE\ndenote the Banach space\nE\n:=\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\ns\n×\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\nE:=\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{s}\\times L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)\nequipped with the norm\n∥\n(\nx\n,\ne\n,\nα\n^\n)\n∥\nE\n:=\nmax\n⁡\n{\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\n,\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n,\n∥\nα\n^\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n}\n\\lVert(x,e,\\hat{\\alpha})\\rVert_{E}:=\\max\\{\\lVert x\\rVert_{2},\\lVert e\\rVert_{2},\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}\\}\n. Write\nz\n=\n(\nx\n,\ne\n,\nα\n^\n)\nz=(x,e,\\hat{\\alpha})\nfor\nz\n∈\nE\nz\\in E\nand define the function\nF\n:\nE\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nE\nF:E\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow E\nas:\nF\n⁡\n(\nz\n,\nt\n)\n:=\n[\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n^\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\nd\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n)\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n^\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\nd\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n)\n−\nγ\nΦ\n(\nx\n,\n⋅\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle F(z,t):=\\begin{bmatrix}f(x,t)+g(x,t)\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\hat{\\alpha}(\\theta)d\\nu(\\theta)-h(x)\\right)\\\\\nf_{e}(e,t)+g_{e}(x,t)\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\hat{\\alpha}(\\theta)d\\nu(\\theta)-h(x)\\right)\\\\\n-\\gamma\\Phi(x,\\cdot)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e,t)\\end{bmatrix}.\nThen, under Assumption\n4.4\n,\nF\n⁡\n(\nz\n,\nt\n)\nF(z,t)\nis locally Lipschitz in\nz\nz\nwith respect to\n∥\n⋅\n∥\nE\n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_{E}\n. That is, for each\nR\n>\n0\nR>0\nand\nT\n>\n0\nT>0\n,\nletting\nB\nE\n​\n(\nR\n)\n:=\n{\nz\n∈\nE\n:\n∥\nz\n∥\nE\n⩽\nR\n}\nB_{E}(R):=\\{z\\in E:\\lVert z\\rVert_{E}\\leqslant R\\}\n,\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\nsup\nz\n1\n,\nz\n2\n∈\nB\nE\n​\n(\nR\n)\n∥\nF\n⁡\n(\nz\n1\n,\nt\n)\n−\nF\n⁡\n(\nz\n2\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nE\n∥\nz\n1\n−\nz\n2\n∥\nE\n<\n∞\n.\n\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}\\sup_{z_{1},z_{2}\\in B_{E}(R)}\\frac{\\lVert F(z_{1},t)-F(z_{2},t)\\rVert_{E}}{\\lVert z_{1}-z_{2}\\rVert_{E}}<\\infty.\nProof.\nBy the composition rules for locally Lipschitz functions (cf. Proposition\nB.1\n),\nit suffices to show that\nthe functions\nψ\n1\n:\nE\n→\nℝ\nd\n\\psi_{1}:E\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d}\nand\nψ\n2\n:\nE\n→\nℒ\n⁡\n(\nℝ\nd\n1\n,\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n)\n\\psi_{2}:E\\rightarrow\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}},L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu))\ndefined by\nψ\n1\n​\n(\n(\n,\n,\n,\n,\n,\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle\\psi_{1}((x,e,\\hat{\\alpha}))\n:\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n^\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle:=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\hat{\\alpha}(\\theta)d\\nu(\\theta),\nψ\n2\n​\n(\n(\n,\n,\n,\n,\n,\n)\n)\n​\n(\nq\n)\n\\displaystyle\\psi_{2}((x,e,\\hat{\\alpha}))(q)\n:\n=\nΦ\n​\n(\nx\n,\n⋅\n)\n𝖳\n​\nq\n​\n∀\nq\n∈\nℝ\nd\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle:=\\Phi(x,\\cdot)^{\\mathsf{T}}q\\>\\>\\forall q\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}.\nare locally Lipschitz and locally bounded. We view both\nψ\n1\n\\psi_{1}\nand\nψ\n2\n\\psi_{2}\nas functions defined on\nE\nE\n, consistent with their appearance in the definition of\nF\n⁡\n(\nz\n,\nt\n)\nF(z,t)\n; however, clearly\nψ\n1\n\\psi_{1}\nis independent of\ne\ne\nand\nψ\n2\n\\psi_{2}\nis independent of both\ne\ne\nand\nα\n^\n\\hat{\\alpha}\n.\nBecause\nψ\n1\n\\psi_{1}\nand\nψ\n2\n\\psi_{2}\ndo not depend on time\nt\nt\n,\nlocally Lipschitz implies locally bounded.\nWe first show that\nψ\n1\n\\psi_{1}\nis locally Lipschitz.\nFix an\nR\n>\n0\nR>0\nand let\nz\n1\n=\n(\nx\n1\n,\ne\n1\n,\nα\n^\n1\n)\nz_{1}=(x_{1},e_{1},\\hat{\\alpha}_{1})\n,\nz\n2\n=\n(\nx\n2\n,\ne\n2\n,\nα\n^\n2\n)\nz_{2}=(x_{2},e_{2},\\hat{\\alpha}_{2})\nbe contained in\nB\nE\n​\n(\nR\n)\nB_{E}(R)\n.\nBy Assumption\n4.4\n,\nthere exists a\nC\n=\nC\n⁡\n(\nR\n)\n>\n0\nC=C(R)>0\nsuch that the following conditions hold:\nsup\nx\n∈\nB\n2\nn\n​\n(\nR\n)\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{x\\in B_{2}^{n}(R)}\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\n⩽\nC\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant C^{2},\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n1\n,\nθ\n)\n−\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n2\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x_{1},\\theta)-\\Phi(x_{2},\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\n⩽\nC\n2\n​\n∥\nx\n1\n−\nx\n2\n∥\n2\n2\n​\n∀\nx\n1\n,\nx\n2\n∈\nB\n2\nn\n​\n(\nR\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant C^{2}\\lVert x_{1}-x_{2}\\rVert_{2}^{2}\\>\\>\\forall x_{1},x_{2}\\in B_{2}^{n}(R).\nBy the triangle inequality and Cauchy-Schwarz,\n∥\nψ\n1\n​\n(\nz\n1\n)\n−\nψ\n1\n​\n(\nz\n2\n)\n∥\n2\n\\displaystyle\\lVert\\psi_{1}(z_{1})-\\psi_{1}(z_{2})\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n1\n,\nθ\n)\n−\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n2\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nα\n^\n1\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n∥\n2\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\sqrt{\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x_{1},\\theta)-\\Phi(x_{2},\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)}\\sqrt{\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{1}(\\theta)\\rVert_{2}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)}\n+\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n1\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nα\n^\n1\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n−\nα\n^\n2\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n∥\n2\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\qquad+\\sqrt{\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x_{1},\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)}\\sqrt{\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{1}(\\theta)-\\hat{\\alpha}_{2}(\\theta)\\rVert_{2}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)}\n⩽\nC\n​\nR\n​\n∥\nx\n1\n−\nx\n2\n∥\n2\n+\nC\n​\n∥\nα\n1\n−\nα\n2\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant CR\\lVert x_{1}-x_{2}\\rVert_{2}+C\\lVert\\alpha_{1}-\\alpha_{2}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}\n⩽\nC\n⁡\n(\n1\n+\nR\n)\n​\n∥\nz\n1\n−\nz\n2\n∥\nE\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant C(1+R)\\lVert z_{1}-z_{2}\\rVert_{E}.\nThis shows that\nψ\n1\n\\psi_{1}\nis locally Lipschitz.\nTo show that\nψ\n2\n\\psi_{2}\nis locally Lipschitz, by Cauchy-Schwarz,\n∥\nψ\n2\n​\n(\nz\n1\n)\n−\nψ\n2\n​\n(\nz\n2\n)\n∥\nℒ\n⁡\n(\nℝ\nd\n1\n,\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle\\lVert\\psi_{2}(z_{1})-\\psi_{2}(z_{2})\\rVert_{\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}},L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu))}\n=\nsup\n∥\nq\n∥\n2\n=\n1\n∥\n(\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n1\n,\n⋅\n)\n−\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n2\n,\n⋅\n)\n)\n𝖳\n​\nq\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sup_{\\lVert q\\rVert_{2}=1}\\lVert(\\Phi(x_{1},\\cdot)-\\Phi(x_{2},\\cdot))^{\\mathsf{T}}q\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}\n=\nsup\n∥\nq\n∥\n2\n=\n1\n(\n∫\nΘ\n∥\n(\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n1\n,\nθ\n)\n−\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n2\n,\nθ\n)\n)\n𝖳\n​\nq\n∥\n2\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\sup_{\\lVert q\\rVert_{2}=1}\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert(\\Phi(x_{1},\\theta)-\\Phi(x_{2},\\theta))^{\\mathsf{T}}q\\rVert_{2}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}\n⩽\n(\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n1\n,\nθ\n)\n−\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n2\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x_{1},\\theta)-\\Phi(x_{2},\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}\n⩽\nC\n​\n∥\nx\n1\n−\nx\n2\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\n​\n∥\nz\n1\n−\nz\n2\n∥\nE\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant C\\lVert x_{1}-x_{2}\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant C\\lVert z_{1}-z_{2}\\rVert_{E}.\n∎\nWe now require the following result concerned with existence and uniqueness of solutions to ordinary differential equations defined on Banach spaces. This result will be used in conjunction with Lemma\nC.1\nto assert the existence of our nonparametric input over an interval of time. Via a Lyapunov argument, we can then extend the interval to infinity.\nProposition C.2\n(Existence of a maximal solution (see e.g., Proposition 11.8 of\nDriver 2004\n))\n.\nLet\nE\nE\nbe a Banach space,\nU\nU\nbe an open subset of\nE\nE\n,\nT\n⊆\nℝ\nT\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}\nbe an interval of time containing\n0\n0\n, and\nF\n:\nU\n×\nT\n→\nE\nF:U\\times T\\rightarrow E\nbe a continuous vector field on\nE\nE\n. Assume that\nF\nF\nis locally Lipschitz in the following sense.\nFor every\nx\n0\n∈\nU\nx_{0}\\in U\nand compact\nI\n⊆\nT\nI\\subseteq T\n,\nthere exists finite positive\nL\n=\nL\n⁡\n(\nx\n0\n,\nI\n)\nL=L(x_{0},I)\nand\nR\n=\nR\n⁡\n(\nx\n0\n,\nI\n)\nR=R(x_{0},I)\nsuch that:\nsup\nt\n∈\nI\n∥\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\ny\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nE\n⩽\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\nE\n​\n∀\nx\n,\ny\n∈\nB\nE\n​\n(\nx\n0\n,\nR\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in I}\\lVert f(x,t)-f(y,t)\\rVert_{E}\\leqslant\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{E}\\>\\>\\forall x,y\\in B_{E}(x_{0},R).\nThen for each\nx\n0\n∈\nU\nx_{0}\\in U\n, there exists a maximal interval\nI\n⁡\n(\nx\n0\n)\n=\n(\na\n⁡\n(\nx\n0\n)\n,\nb\n⁡\n(\nx\n0\n)\n)\n⊆\nT\nI(x_{0})=(a(x_{0}),b(x_{0}))\\subseteq T\nwith\na\n(\nx\n0\n)\n∈\n[\n−\n∞\n,\n0\n)\na(x_{0})\\in[-\\infty,0)\nand\nb\n(\nx\n0\n)\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n+\n∞\n]\nb(x_{0})\\in(0,+\\infty]\nsuch that the ordinary differential equation\nx\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\nF\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n=\nx\n0\n\\dot{x}(t)=F(x(t),t),\\>\\>\\>x(0)=x_{0}\nhas a unique continuously differentiable solution\nx\n:\nI\n⁡\n(\nx\n0\n)\n→\nU\nx:I(x_{0})\\rightarrow U\n.\nWe may now state our proof of the main nonparametric theorem.\nSee\n4.5\nProof.\nBy Assumption\n4.3\n, there exists a signed density\nα\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n∈\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n\\alpha(\\theta)\\in L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)\nsuch that\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n,\n∥\nh\n∥\nℋ\n2\n=\n∥\nα\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n.\nh(\\cdot)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta)\\alpha(\\theta)d\\nu(\\theta),\\>\\>\\lVert h\\rVert_{\\mathcal{H}}^{2}=\\lVert\\alpha\\rVert^{2}_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}.\nDefine the signed density\nα\n^\n:\nΘ\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nd\n1\n\\hat{\\alpha}:\\Theta\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\nby\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\n0\n)\n=\n0\n\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,0)=0\nand the pointwise update for\nθ\n∈\nΘ\n\\theta\\in\\Theta\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n=\n−\nγ\nΦ\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n.\n\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\theta,t)=-\\gamma\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x(t),t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e(t),t).\nObserve that by Lemma\nC.1\nand Proposition\nC.2\n, there exists some maximal\nT\nmax\n∈\n(\n0\n,\n∞\n]\nT_{\\max}\\in(0,\\infty]\nsuch that the curve\nt\n↦\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\nt\\mapsto(x(t),e(t),\\hat{\\alpha}(t))\nexists, is unique, and is continuously differentiable. Moreover, we may write the input as\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n^\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle u(x,t)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\hat{\\alpha}(\\theta,t)d\\nu(\\theta).\nBy means of contradiction, let us suppose that\nT\nmax\n<\n∞\nT_{\\max}<\\infty\n. For\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\nt\\in[0,T_{\\max})\n, define\nα\n~\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n:=\nα\n^\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n−\nα\n⁡\n(\nθ\n)\n\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\theta,t):=\\hat{\\alpha}(\\theta,t)-\\alpha(\\theta)\nso that\nu\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n~\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n.\nu(\\cdot,t)-h(\\cdot)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta)\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\theta,t)d\\nu(\\theta).\nNow consider the Lyapunov-like function\nV\n:\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n→\nℝ\nV:[0,T_{\\max})\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\n,\nV\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n+\n1\n2\n​\nγ\n​\n∥\nα\n~\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n.\nV(t)=Q(e(t),t)+\\frac{1}{2\\gamma}\\lVert\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}.\nWe note that because\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\nL_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)\nis a real Hilbert space,\nthe map\nu\n↦\n∥\nu\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\nu\\mapsto\\lVert u\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}\nis (Fréchet) differentiable with derivative\nh\n↦\n2\n​\n⟨\nu\n,\nh\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\nh\\mapsto 2\\langle u,h\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}\nTherefore, by the differentiability of the curve\nt\n↦\nα\n~\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)\nand the chain rule, we have:\nd\nd\n​\nt\n​\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nα\n~\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n=\n2\n​\n⟨\nα\n~\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\theta,t)\\rVert^{2}_{2}d\\nu(\\theta)=2\\left\\langle\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t),\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\cdot,t)\\right\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}.\nComputing the time derivative, for any\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\nt\\in[0,T_{\\max})\n,\nV\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle\\dot{V}(t)\n=\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n+\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n(\nf\ne\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n(\nu\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n)\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}(e(t),t)+\\nabla Q(e(t),t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\left(f_{e}(e(t),t)+g_{e}(x(t),t)\\left(u(x(t),t)-h(x(t))\\right)\\right)\n+\n1\nγ\n​\n⟨\nα\n~\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\qquad+\\frac{1}{\\gamma}\\left\\langle\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t),\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\cdot,t)\\right\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)},\n⩽\n−\nρ\n(\n∥\ne\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n)\n+\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n(\nu\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant-\\rho\\left(\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\right)+\\nabla Q(e(t),t)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(e(t),t)\\left(u(x(t),t)-h(x(t))\\right)\n+\n1\nγ\n​\n⟨\nα\n~\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\qquad+\\frac{1}{\\gamma}\\left\\langle\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t),\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\cdot,t)\\right\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)},\nwhere we have applied Assumption\n3.7\n. Now, observe that\n⟨\nα\n~\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n=\n−\nγ\n∫\nΘ\n⟨\nα\n~\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n,\nΦ\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n⟩\nd\nν\n(\nθ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\left\\langle\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t),\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\cdot,t)\\right\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}=-\\gamma\\int_{\\Theta}\\langle\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\theta,t),\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x(t),t)\\nabla Q(e(t),t)\\rangle d\\nu(\\theta)\nso that the last two terms in\nV\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\dot{V}(t)\ncancel, and hence:\nV\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n⩽\n−\nρ\n⁡\n(\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n)\n.\n\\dot{V}(t)\\leqslant-\\rho(\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}).\nNow, because\nV\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n⩽\n0\n\\dot{V}(t)\\leqslant 0\nfor all\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\nt\\in[0,T_{\\max})\n,\nV\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n⩽\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\nV(t)\\leqslant V(0)\n.\nTherefore, since\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n⩾\nμ\n1\n​\n(\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n)\nQ(e(t),t)\\geqslant\\mu_{1}(\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2})\nand\nμ\n1\n\\mu_{1}\nis a class\n𝒦\n∞\n\\mathcal{K}_{\\infty}\nfunction,\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n<\n∞\n,\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n∥\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n<\n∞\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in[0,T_{\\max})}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}<\\infty,\\>\\>\\sup_{t\\in[0,T_{\\max})}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}<\\infty.\nFurthermore,\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n<\n∞\n\\sup_{t\\in[0,T_{\\max})}\\lVert x(t)-x_{d}(t)\\rVert_{2}<\\infty\nby requirement (\n3.5\n) on the error signal,\nand since\nx\nd\nx_{d}\nis uniformly bounded, we also have that\nsup\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n<\n∞\n\\sup_{t\\in[0,T_{\\max})}\\lVert x(t)\\rVert_{2}<\\infty\n.\nThis contradicts that\nT\nmax\nT_{\\max}\nis finite, so we conclude that\nT\nmax\n=\n∞\nT_{\\max}=\\infty\n. This implies that\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\nmax\n⁡\n{\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n,\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n,\n∥\nα\n~\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n}\n<\n∞\n,\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\max\\{\\lVert x(t)\\rVert_{2},\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2},\\lVert\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}\\}<\\infty,\nso that\nu\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∈\nℋ\nu(\\cdot,t)\\in\\mathcal{H}\nfor all\nt\n⩾\n0\nt\\geqslant 0\n. This proves the first two claims. Now, integrating both sides of\nV\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\dot{V}(t)\n,\n∫\n0\n∞\nρ\n⁡\n(\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n)\n​\n𝑑\nt\n⩽\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n.\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\rho\\left(\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\right)dt\\leqslant V(0).\nTo complete the proof, we now need to show that\nt\n↦\nρ\n⁡\n(\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n)\nt\\mapsto\\rho(\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2})\nis uniformly\ncontinuous on\n[\n0\n,\n∞\n)\n[0,\\infty)\nand apply Barbalat’s lemma.\nWe first show that\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz in\nt\nt\n.\nTo do so, we bound\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\n∥\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\lVert\\dot{e}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nand apply\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n1\n)\n−\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n2\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\n∥\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n​\n|\nt\n1\n−\nt\n2\n|\n\\lVert e(t_{1})-e(t_{2})\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\lVert\\dot{e}(t)\\rVert_{2}|t_{1}-t_{2}|\n.\nLet\nC\nΦ\n:=\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\n(\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\n2\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\nC_{\\Phi}:=\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)\\rVert_{2}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}\n.\nBecause\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nis uniformly bounded,\nC\nΦ\nC_{\\Phi}\nis finite by Assumption\n4.4\n.\nNext,\n∥\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\n(\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n~\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n⩽\nC\nΦ\n​\n2\n​\nγ\n​\nV\n​\n(\n0\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\lVert u(x(t),t)-h(x(t))\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}\\lVert\\tilde{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}\\leqslant C_{\\Phi}\\sqrt{2\\gamma V(0)}.\nNow, observe that\n∥\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\displaystyle\\lVert\\dot{e}(t)\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\n∥\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n+\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nop\n​\n∥\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n∥\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\lVert f_{e}(e(t),t)\\rVert_{2}+\\lVert g_{e}(x(t),t)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\lVert u(x(t),t)-h(x(t))\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\n∥\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n+\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nop\n​\nC\nΦ\n​\n2\n​\nγ\n​\nV\n​\n(\n0\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\lVert f_{e}(e(t),t)\\rVert_{2}+\\lVert g_{e}(x(t),t)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}C_{\\Phi}\\sqrt{2\\gamma V(0)}.\nBecause both\nf\ne\nf_{e}\nand\ng\ne\ng_{e}\nare locally bounded in\nx\nx\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n,\n∥\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\dot{e}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nis uniformly bounded in\nt\nt\n.\nTherefore,\nt\n↦\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\nt\\mapsto\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\nis uniformly Lipschitz and\nt\n↦\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\nt\\mapsto\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\nis uniformly continuous.\nNow, because\nρ\n\\rho\nis continuous, it is uniformly continuous on\nthe range of\nt\n↦\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\nt\\mapsto\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\n. Since the composition of two uniformly continuous functions remains uniformly continuous,\nt\n↦\nρ\n⁡\n(\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n)\nt\\mapsto\\rho(\\lVert e(t)\\rVert)\nis uniformly continuous.\nBy Barbalat’s lemma, this implies that\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\nρ\n⁡\n(\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n)\n=\n0\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\rho(\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2})=0\n. By continuity of\nρ\n\\rho\nand the fact that\nρ\n⁡\n(\na\n)\n=\n0\n\\rho(a)=0\nif and only if\na\n=\n0\na=0\n, we conclude that\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n=\n0\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}=0\n.\nFrom the requirement (\n3.6\n)\non the error signal, we conclude that\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert x(t)-x_{d}(t)\\rVert_{2}\n.\n∎\nC.2\nProof of Theorem\n4.7\nSee\n4.7\nProof.\nRecall that the error dynamics satisfy:\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\dot{e}(t)=f_{e}(e(t),t)+g_{e}(x(t),t)(u(x(t),t)-h(x(t))).\nFrom the proof of Theorem\n4.5\n,\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\n0\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}e(t)=0\n.\nIf we show in addition that\nt\n↦\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto\\dot{e}(t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz,\nthen by Barbalat’s lemma (applied to each coordinate),\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n0\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\dot{e}(t)=0\n.\nSince\nf\ne\n​\n(\n0\n,\nt\n)\n=\n0\nf_{e}(0,t)=0\nand\nf\ne\nf_{e}\nis locally Lipschitz in\ne\ne\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n,\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n0\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\dot{e}(t)=0\nimplies\nthat\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n)\n∥\n2\n=\n0\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert g_{e}(x(t),t)(u(x(t),t)-h(x(t)))\\rVert_{2}=0\n.\nIt remains to show that\nt\n↦\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto\\dot{e}(t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz.\nBy the composition rule (cf. Proposition\nB.2\n),\nit suffices to show that the functions:\nt\n↦\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n,\nt\n↦\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n,\nt\n↦\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n,\nt\n↦\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle t\\mapsto f_{e}(e(t),t),\\>\\>t\\mapsto g_{e}(x(t),t),\\>\\>t\\mapsto u(x(t),t),\\>\\>t\\mapsto h(x(t)),\nare all uniformly Lipschitz and bounded.\nFrom the proof of Theorem\n4.5\n,\nboth\nt\n↦\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto e(t)\nand\nt\n↦\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto x(t)\nare uniformly bounded, and\nt\n↦\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto e(t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz. A nearly identical argument shows that\nt\n↦\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto x(t)\nis also uniformly Lipschitz.\nTherefore, since\nf\ne\nf_{e}\n,\ng\ne\ng_{e}\n, and\nh\nh\nare all locally Lipschitz and locally bounded uniformly in\nt\nt\n,\nit is clear that\nt\n↦\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto f_{e}(e(t),t)\n,\nt\n↦\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto g_{e}(x(t),t)\n, and\nt\n↦\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\nt\\mapsto h(x(t))\nare all uniformly Lipschitz and uniformly bounded.\nTo see that\nt\n↦\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto u(x(t),t)\nis also uniformly Lipschitz, we first\nchoose a finite constant\nC\n>\n0\nC>0\nsuch that\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\nmax\n⁡\n{\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n,\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nop\n,\n∥\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n}\n⩽\nC\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\max\\{\\lVert x(t)\\rVert_{2},\\lVert g_{e}(x(t),t)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}},\\lVert\\nabla Q(e(t),t)\\rVert_{2}\\}\\leqslant C.\nNow observe that\nfor every\nθ\n\\theta\nand\nt\nt\n,\n‖\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n‖\n2\n\\displaystyle\\left\\lVert\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\theta,t)\\right\\rVert_{2}\n=\nγ\n‖\nΦ\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n‖\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\gamma\\left\\lVert\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x(t),t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e(t),t)\\right\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\nγ\n​\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n​\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nop\n​\n∥\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\gamma\\lVert\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\lVert g_{e}(x(t),t)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\lVert\\nabla Q(e(t),t)\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\nγ\n​\nC\n2\n​\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\gamma C^{2}\\lVert\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}.\nPut\nC\nΦ\n:=\n(\n∫\nΘ\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\nC_{\\Phi}:=\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant C}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}\n, which is finite by assumption.\nFix\nt\n1\n,\nt\n2\nt_{1},t_{2}\n, and for\ni\n∈\n{\n1\n,\n2\n}\ni\\in\\{1,2\\}\ndefine:\nu\ni\n:=\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\ni\n)\n,\nt\ni\n)\n,\nΦ\ni\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n:=\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\ni\n)\n,\n⋅\n)\n,\nα\n^\ni\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n:=\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\ni\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle u_{i}:=u(x(t_{i}),t_{i}),\\>\\>\\Phi_{i}(\\cdot):=\\Phi(x(t_{i}),\\cdot),\\>\\>\\hat{\\alpha}_{i}(\\cdot):=\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t_{i}).\nWe have:\n∥\nα\n^\n1\n−\nα\n^\n2\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n\\displaystyle\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{1}-\\hat{\\alpha}_{2}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}\n=\n(\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nα\n^\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n1\n)\n−\nα\n^\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n2\n)\n∥\n2\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}(\\theta,t_{1})-\\hat{\\alpha}(\\theta,t_{2})\\rVert_{2}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}\n⩽\n(\n∫\nΘ\n‖\n∫\nt\n1\nt\n2\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n​\n𝑑\nt\n‖\n2\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\left\\lVert\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\theta,t)dt\\right\\rVert^{2}_{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}\n⩽\n(\n∫\nΘ\n(\n∫\nt\n1\nt\n2\n‖\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n‖\n2\n​\n𝑑\nt\n)\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\left(\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\left\\lVert\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\theta,t)\\right\\rVert_{2}dt\\right)^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}\n⩽\nγ\n​\nC\n2\n​\n(\n∫\nΘ\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n​\n|\nt\n1\n−\nt\n2\n|\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\gamma C^{2}\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant C}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}|t_{1}-t_{2}|\n⩽\nγ\n​\nC\n2\n​\nC\nΦ\n​\n|\nt\n1\n−\nt\n2\n|\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\gamma C^{2}C_{\\Phi}|t_{1}-t_{2}|.\nNext, let\nC\nΦ\n′\nC^{\\prime}_{\\Phi}\nbe a finite constant such that\n(\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n−\nΦ\n⁡\n(\ny\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n⩽\nC\nΦ\n′\n​\n∥\nx\n−\ny\n∥\n2\n​\n∀\nx\n,\ny\n∈\nB\n2\nn\n​\n(\nC\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)-\\Phi(y,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}\\leqslant C^{\\prime}_{\\Phi}\\lVert x-y\\rVert_{2}\\>\\>\\forall x,y\\in B_{2}^{n}(C).\nThen,\n∥\nu\n1\n−\nu\n2\n∥\n2\n\\displaystyle\\lVert u_{1}-u_{2}\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n1\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n^\n1\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n−\nΦ\n2\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n^\n2\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n∥\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi_{1}(\\theta)\\hat{\\alpha}_{1}(\\theta)-\\Phi_{2}(\\theta)\\hat{\\alpha}_{2}(\\theta)\\rVert_{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\n⩽\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n1\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n−\nΦ\n2\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n1\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n∥\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n+\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n2\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n1\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n−\nα\n^\n2\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n∥\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi_{1}(\\theta)-\\Phi_{2}(\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{1}(\\theta)\\rVert_{2}d\\nu(\\theta)+\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi_{2}(\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{1}(\\theta)-\\hat{\\alpha}_{2}(\\theta)\\rVert_{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\n⩽\nC\n​\n(\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n1\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n−\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n2\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n)\n1\n/\n2\n+\nC\nΦ\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n1\n−\nα\n^\n2\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant C\\left(\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x(t_{1}),\\theta)-\\Phi(x(t_{2}),\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\\right)^{1/2}+C_{\\Phi}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{1}-\\hat{\\alpha}_{2}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}\n⩽\nC\n​\nC\nΦ\n′\n​\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n1\n)\n−\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n2\n)\n∥\n2\n+\nγ\n​\nC\n2\n​\nC\nΦ\n2\n​\n|\nt\n1\n−\nt\n2\n|\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant CC^{\\prime}_{\\Phi}\\lVert x(t_{1})-x(t_{2})\\rVert_{2}+\\gamma C^{2}C^{2}_{\\Phi}|t_{1}-t_{2}|\n⩽\n(\nC\n2\n​\nC\nΦ\n′\n+\nγ\n​\nC\n2\n​\nC\nΦ\n2\n)\n​\n|\nt\n1\n−\nt\n2\n|\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant(C^{2}C^{\\prime}_{\\Phi}+\\gamma C^{2}C^{2}_{\\Phi})|t_{1}-t_{2}|.\nThis shows that\nt\n↦\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto u(x(t),t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz.\nTo conclude, we argue that\nt\n↦\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto u(x(t),t)\nis uniformly bounded:\n∥\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\displaystyle\\lVert u(x(t),t)\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}(t)\\rVert_{2}d\\nu(\\theta)\n⩽\n∫\nΘ\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\sqrt{\\int_{\\Theta}\\lVert\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}d\\nu(\\theta)}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}(t)\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\nC\nΦ\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant C_{\\Phi}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}(t)\\rVert_{2}.\nThe proof of Theorem\n4.5\nshows that\n∥\nα\n~\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\tilde{\\alpha}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nis uniformly bounded,\nand therefore so is\n∥\nα\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nby the triangle inequality.\n∎\nC.3\nProof of Theorem\n4.8\nSee\n4.8\nProof.\nFrom Theorem\n4.5\n,\nu\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∈\nℋ\nu(\\cdot,t)\\in\\mathcal{H}\nfor all\nt\n⩾\n0\nt\\geqslant 0\n.\nLet\nh\n¯\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n∈\nℋ\n\\bar{h}(\\cdot)\\in\\mathcal{H}\nbe arbitrary. Then by\nAssumption\n4.3\nthere exists\nα\n¯\n∈\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n\\bar{\\alpha}\\in L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)\nsuch that\nh\n¯\n​\n(\nx\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n¯\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n.\n\\bar{h}(x)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\bar{\\alpha}(\\theta)d\\nu(\\theta).\nConsider the Lyapunov-like function\nV\n:\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nV:\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\n,\nV\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\n1\n2\n​\n∥\nu\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n−\nh\n¯\n∥\nℋ\n2\n=\n1\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n−\nα\n¯\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n,\nV(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert u(\\cdot,t)-\\bar{h}\\rVert_{\\mathcal{H}}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)-\\bar{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2},\nwhere\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n∈\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)\\in L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)\nwas defined in the proof of Theorem\n4.5\nby the partial differential equation\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n(\nθ\n,\nt\n)\n=\n−\nγ\nΦ\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\n(\nθ\n,\n0\n)\n=\n0\n.\n\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\theta,t)=-\\gamma\\Phi(x(t),\\theta)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x(t),t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e(t),t),\\>\\>\\hat{\\alpha}(\\theta,0)=0.\nComputing the time derivative of\nV\nV\n,\nV\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n=\n⟨\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n−\nα\n¯\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\dot{V}(t)=\\left\\langle\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)-\\bar{\\alpha},\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\cdot,t)\\right\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}.\nIntegrating both sides of the above from\n0\n0\nto\nt\nt\n,\n1\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n−\nα\n¯\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n=\n1\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n¯\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n+\n∫\n0\nt\n⟨\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nτ\n)\n−\nα\n¯\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nτ\n)\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n​\n𝑑\nτ\n,\n\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)-\\bar{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\bar{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}+\\int_{0}^{t}\\left\\langle\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,\\tau)-\\bar{\\alpha},\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\cdot,\\tau)\\right\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}d\\tau,\nDefine\nα\n^\n∞\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\infty}(\\theta)\nto be the density such that\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\nu\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n^\n∞\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}u(\\cdot,t)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta)\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\infty}(\\theta)d\\nu(\\theta)\n.\nTaking the limit as\nt\n→\n∞\nt\\rightarrow\\infty\nof both sides,\n1\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n∞\n−\nα\n¯\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\infty}-\\bar{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}\n=\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\n1\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n−\nα\n¯\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n\\displaystyle=\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,t)-\\bar{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}\n=\n1\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n¯\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n+\n∫\n0\n∞\n⟨\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nτ\n)\n−\nα\n¯\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nτ\n)\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n​\n𝑑\nτ\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\bar{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}+\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\left\\langle\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,\\tau)-\\bar{\\alpha},\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\cdot,\\tau)\\right\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}d\\tau.\n(C.1)\nNow take\nh\n¯\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n∈\n𝒜\n\\bar{h}(\\cdot)\\in\\mathcal{A}\n. Observe that, by definition of\n𝒜\n\\mathcal{A}\n,\nfor any\nτ\n⩾\n0\n\\tau\\geqslant 0\n,\n⟨\nα\n¯\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nτ\n)\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n\\displaystyle\\left\\langle\\bar{\\alpha},\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\cdot,\\tau)\\right\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}\n=\n−\nγ\n∫\nΘ\nα\n¯\n(\nθ\n)\n𝖳\nΦ\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nθ\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nτ\n)\n\\displaystyle=-\\gamma\\int_{\\Theta}\\bar{\\alpha}(\\theta)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\Phi(x(\\tau),\\theta)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x(\\tau),t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e(\\tau),\\tau)\n=\n−\nγ\nh\n¯\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nτ\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nτ\n)\n\\displaystyle=-\\gamma\\bar{h}(x(\\tau))^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x(\\tau),\\tau)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e(\\tau),\\tau)\n=\n−\nγ\nh\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nτ\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nτ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=-\\gamma h(x(\\tau))^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(x(\\tau),\\tau)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e(\\tau),\\tau).\nHence, (\nC.1\n) may be re-written,\n1\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n^\n∞\n−\nα\n¯\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\infty}-\\bar{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}\n=\n1\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n¯\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n2\n+\n∫\n0\n∞\n⟨\nα\n^\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nτ\n)\n,\n∂\nα\n^\n∂\nt\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nτ\n)\n⟩\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n​\n𝑑\nτ\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2}\\lVert\\bar{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}^{2}+\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\left\\langle\\hat{\\alpha}(\\cdot,\\tau),\\frac{\\partial\\hat{\\alpha}}{\\partial t}(\\cdot,\\tau)\\right\\rangle_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}d\\tau\n+\nγ\n∫\n0\n∞\nh\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n)\n𝖳\ng\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nτ\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\nx\n(\nτ\n)\n,\nτ\n)\nd\nτ\n,\n\\displaystyle\\qquad+\\gamma\\int_{0}^{\\infty}h(x(\\tau))^{\\mathsf{T}}g(x(\\tau),\\tau)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(x(\\tau),\\tau)d\\tau,\nwhich has eliminated the dependence of the right-hand side on\nα\n¯\n\\bar{\\alpha}\nexcept for in the first term.\nLet\n𝒜\n¯\n:=\n{\nα\n¯\n∈\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n:\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n¯\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n∈\n𝒜\n}\n\\bar{\\mathcal{A}}:=\\{\\bar{\\alpha}\\in L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu):h(\\cdot)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta)\\bar{\\alpha}(\\theta)d\\nu\\in\\mathcal{A}\\}\n.\nSince\nα\n^\n∞\n∈\n𝒜\n¯\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\infty}\\in\\bar{\\mathcal{A}}\nby assumption, taking\nthe arg min over both sides of the above equation,\nα\n^\n∞\n∈\narg\n​\nmin\nα\n¯\n∈\n𝒜\n¯\n⁡\n∥\nα\n¯\n∥\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\infty}\\in\\argmin_{\\bar{\\alpha}\\in\\bar{\\mathcal{A}}}\\lVert\\bar{\\alpha}\\rVert_{L_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)}.\nThe claim now follows by the correspondence\nbetween\nL\n2\n​\n(\nΘ\n,\nν\n)\nL_{2}(\\Theta,\\nu)\nand\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\n.\n∎\nAppendix D\nOmitted proofs for Section\n5\nD.1\nProof of Proposition\n5.1\nSee\n5.1\nProof.\nWe first define a truncated target function\nh\nη\n​\n(\nx\n)\nh_{\\eta}(x)\nand its truncated approximation\nh\n^\nη\n​\n(\nx\n,\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n)\n\\hat{h}_{\\eta}(x;\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K})\nh\nη\n​\n(\nx\n)\n\\displaystyle h_{\\eta}(x)\n:\n=\n∫\nΘ\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n​\nα\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n​\n𝑑\nν\n​\n(\nθ\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle:=\\int_{\\Theta}\\Phi_{\\eta}(x,\\theta)\\alpha(\\theta)\\>d\\nu(\\theta),\nh\n^\nη\n​\n(\nx\n,\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n)\n\\displaystyle\\hat{h}_{\\eta}(x;\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K})\n:\n=\n1\nK\n​\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\nx\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\n​\n(\nθ\ni\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle:=\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\Phi_{\\eta}(x,\\theta_{i})\\alpha(\\theta_{i}).\nClearly, for each\nx\n∈\nℝ\nn\nx\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\n,\n𝔼\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n​\nh\n^\nη\n​\n(\nx\n,\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K}}\\hat{h}_{\\eta}(x;\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K})\n=\nh\nη\n​\n(\nx\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=h_{\\eta}(x).\nNow, consider two sets\n{\nθ\ni\n}\n⊆\nΘ\n\\{\\theta_{i}\\}\\subseteq\\Theta\nand\n{\nθ\n~\ni\n}\n⊆\nΘ\n\\{\\tilde{\\theta}_{i}\\}\\subseteq\\Theta\nthat differ in only one index\ni\ni\n. Observe that\n∥\nh\n^\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n)\n−\nh\n^\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\n{\nθ\n~\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n)\n∥\n∞\n⩽\n2\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\n​\nB\nh\nK\n.\n\\displaystyle\\lVert\\hat{h}_{\\eta}(\\cdot;\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K})-\\hat{h}_{\\eta}(\\cdot;\\{\\tilde{\\theta}_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K})\\rVert_{\\infty}\\leqslant\\frac{2B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)B_{h}}{K}.\nHence, by McDiarmid’s inequality, with probability at least\n1\n−\nδ\n/\n2\n1-\\delta/2\n,\n∥\nh\n^\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n)\n−\nh\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n∥\n∞\n\\displaystyle\\lVert\\hat{h}_{\\eta}(\\cdot;\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K})-h_{\\eta}(\\cdot)\\rVert_{\\infty}\n⩽\n𝔼\n​\n∥\nh\n^\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\n{\nθ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n)\n−\nh\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n∥\n∞\n+\n2\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\n​\nB\nh\n​\nlog\n⁡\n(\n2\n/\nδ\n)\nK\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\mathbb{E}\\lVert\\hat{h}_{\\eta}(\\cdot;\\{\\theta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K})-h_{\\eta}(\\cdot)\\rVert_{\\infty}+\\sqrt{2}B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)B_{h}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{K}}\n⩽\n2\nK\n​\n𝔼\n​\n‖\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nε\ni\n​\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\ni\n‖\n∞\n+\n2\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\n​\nB\nh\n​\nlog\n⁡\n(\n2\n/\nδ\n)\nK\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\frac{2}{K}\\mathbb{E}\\left\\lVert\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\varepsilon_{i}\\Phi_{\\eta}(\\cdot;\\theta_{i})\\alpha_{i}\\right\\rVert_{\\infty}+\\sqrt{2}B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)B_{h}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{K}},\nwhere the last inequality follows by a standard symmetrization argument.\nDefine the event\nℰ\n\\mathcal{E}\nas\nℰ\n:=\n{\nmax\ni\n=\n1\n,\n…\n,\nK\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n∥\nΦ\n(\nx\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n∥\nop\n⩽\nB\nΦ\n(\nη\n)\n}\n.\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{E}:=\\left\\{\\max_{i=1,...,K}\\sup_{x\\in X}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta_{i})\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\leqslant B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\right\\}.\nBy our assumption on\nB\nΦ\nB_{\\Phi}\nand a union bound, we have that\nℙ\n⁡\n(\nℰ\nc\n)\n⩽\nδ\n/\n2\n\\mathbb{P}(\\mathcal{E}^{c})\\leqslant\\delta/2\n.\nFurthermore,\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\n⋅\n)\n\\Phi(\\cdot,\\cdot)\nand\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\n⋅\n)\n\\Phi_{\\eta}(\\cdot,\\cdot)\nagree on\nℰ\n\\mathcal{E}\nby definition, so that\n𝟏\n​\n{\nℰ\n}\n​\n‖\n1\nK\n​\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nΦ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\ni\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n‖\n∞\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf{1}\\{\\mathcal{E}\\}\\left\\lVert\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\Phi(\\cdot,\\theta_{i})\\alpha_{i}-h(\\cdot)\\right\\rVert_{\\infty}\n=\n𝟏\n​\n{\nℰ\n}\n​\n‖\n1\nK\n​\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\ni\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n‖\n∞\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{1}\\{\\mathcal{E}\\}\\left\\lVert\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\Phi_{\\eta}(\\cdot,\\theta_{i})\\alpha_{i}-h(\\cdot)\\right\\rVert_{\\infty}\n⩽\n‖\n1\nK\n​\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\ni\n−\nh\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n‖\n∞\n+\n∥\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n−\nh\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n∥\n∞\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\left\\lVert\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\Phi_{\\eta}(\\cdot,\\theta_{i})\\alpha_{i}-h_{\\eta}(\\cdot)\\right\\rVert_{\\infty}+\\lVert h(\\cdot)-h_{\\eta}(\\cdot)\\rVert_{\\infty}.\nWe now focus on bounding the term on the right-hand side.\nWe write\nh\n(\nx\n)\n−\nh\nη\n(\nx\n)\n=\n∫\nΘ\n𝟏\n{\n∥\nΦ\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n>\nB\nΦ\n(\nη\n)\n}\nΦ\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\nα\n(\nθ\n)\nd\nν\n(\nθ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle h(x)-h_{\\eta}(x)=\\int_{\\Theta}\\mathbf{1}\\{\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}>B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\}\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\alpha(\\theta)d\\nu(\\theta).\nThis implies the estimate\n∥\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n−\nh\nη\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n∥\n∞\n\\displaystyle\\lVert h(\\cdot)-h_{\\eta}(\\cdot)\\rVert_{\\infty}\n⩽\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\nB\nh\n𝔼\nθ\n∼\nν\n𝟏\n{\n∥\nΦ\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n>\nB\nΦ\n(\nη\n)\n}\n∥\nΦ\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\sup_{x\\in X}B_{h}\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta\\sim\\nu}\\mathbf{1}\\{\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}>B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\n⩽\nB\nh\n​\nη\n​\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n𝔼\n​\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant B_{h}\\sqrt{\\eta}\\sqrt{\\sup_{x\\in X}\\mathbb{E}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}}\n=\nB\nh\n​\nδ\n​\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n𝔼\n​\n∥\nΦ\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nθ\n)\n∥\nop\n2\n2\n​\nK\n.\n\\displaystyle=B_{h}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\delta\\sup_{x\\in X}\\mathbb{E}\\lVert\\Phi(x,\\theta)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}^{2}}{2K}}.\nThe claim now follows by a union bound.\n∎\nD.2\nProof of Proposition\n5.3\nTo state the proof of the proposition, we will require the following useful result.\nLemma D.1\n(\nMaurer 2016\n, Corollary 4)\n.\nLet\n𝒳\n\\mathcal{X}\nbe any set, let\n(\nx\n1\n,\n…\n,\nx\nn\n)\n∈\n𝒳\nn\n(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{n})\\in\\mathcal{X}^{n}\n, let\nℱ\n\\mathcal{F}\nbe a class of functions\nf\n:\n𝒳\n→\nℓ\n2\nf:\\mathcal{X}\\rightarrow\\ell_{2}\n, and let\nh\ni\n:\nℓ\n2\n→\nℝ\nh_{i}:\\ell_{2}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\nhave Lipschitz constant\nL\nL\n. Then,\n𝔼\n​\nsup\nf\n∈\nℱ\n∑\ni\nε\ni\n​\nh\ni\n​\n(\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\ni\n)\n)\n⩽\n2\n​\nL\n​\n𝔼\n​\nsup\nf\n∈\nℱ\n∑\ni\n,\nk\nε\ni\n,\nk\n​\nf\nk\n​\n(\nx\ni\n)\n\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal{F}}\\sum_{i}\\varepsilon_{i}h_{i}(f(x_{i}))\\leqslant\\sqrt{2}L\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal{F}}\\sum_{i,k}\\varepsilon_{i,k}f_{k}(x_{i})\nwhere the\nε\ni\n​\nk\n\\varepsilon_{ik}\nare i.i.d. Rademacher random variables and\nf\nk\n​\n(\nx\ni\n)\nf_{k}(x_{i})\nis the\nk\nth\nk^{\\text{th}}\ncomponent of\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\ni\n)\nf(x_{i})\n.\nWe may now proceed with the proof.\nSee\n5.3\nProof.\nPut\nα\ni\n=\nα\n⁡\n(\nθ\ni\n)\n\\alpha_{i}=\\alpha(\\theta_{i})\nand\nM\nη\n,\ni\n:=\nM\nη\n​\n(\nw\ni\n)\nM_{\\eta,i}:=M_{\\eta}(w_{i})\n. We write, by definition of the\n∥\n⋅\n∥\n∞\n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_{\\infty}\n-norm and duality,\n𝔼\n​\n‖\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nε\ni\n​\nΦ\nη\n​\n(\nx\n,\nθ\ni\n)\n​\nα\ni\n‖\n∞\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left\\lVert\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\varepsilon_{i}\\Phi_{\\eta}(x;\\theta_{i})\\alpha_{i}\\right\\rVert_{\\infty}\n=\n𝔼\n​\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\nsup\nψ\n∈\n𝕊\nd\n1\n−\n1\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nε\ni\n​\nψ\n𝖳\n​\nM\nη\n,\ni\n​\nα\ni\n​\nϕ\n​\n(\nw\ni\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\ni\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{x\\in X}\\sup_{\\psi\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d_{1}-1}}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\varepsilon_{i}\\psi^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{\\eta,i}\\alpha_{i}\\phi(w_{i}^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b_{i}).\nTowards applying Lemma\nD.1\n, for a tuple\n(\nx\n,\nψ\n)\n∈\nX\n×\n𝕊\nd\n1\n−\n1\n(x,\\psi)\\in X\\times\\mathbb{S}^{d_{1}-1}\n,\ndefine\nf\nx\n,\nψ\n​\n(\nw\n,\nb\n)\n:=\n(\nw\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\nψ\n)\n.\nf_{x,\\psi}(w,b):=\\begin{pmatrix}w^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b\\\\\n\\psi\\end{pmatrix}.\nNext, define\nh\ni\n:\nℝ\n×\n𝕊\nd\n1\n−\n1\n→\nℝ\nh_{i}:\\mathbb{R}\\times\\mathbb{S}^{d_{1}-1}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\nas\nh\ni\n​\n(\nv\n1\n,\nv\n2\n)\n:=\nv\n2\n𝖳\n​\nM\nη\n,\ni\n​\nα\ni\n​\nϕ\n​\n(\nv\n1\n)\n.\nh_{i}(v_{1},v_{2}):=v_{2}^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{\\eta,i}\\alpha_{i}\\phi(v_{1}).\nWe need to show that\nh\ni\nh_{i}\nis Lipschitz continuous. Let\nv\n=\n(\nv\n1\n,\nv\n2\n)\nv=(v_{1},v_{2})\n,\nw\n=\n(\nw\n1\n,\nw\n2\n)\nw=(w_{1},w_{2})\n, and observe that\n|\nh\ni\n​\n(\nv\n1\n,\nv\n2\n)\n−\nh\ni\n​\n(\nw\n1\n,\nw\n2\n)\n|\n⩽\n2\n​\nB\nh\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\n​\n∥\nv\n−\nw\n∥\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle|h_{i}(v_{1},v_{2})-h_{i}(w_{1},w_{2})|\\leqslant\\sqrt{2}B_{h}B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\lVert v-w\\rVert_{2},\nwhere we have applied the triangle inequality. Now, let\n{\nξ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n⊆\n{\n±\n1\n}\n\\{\\xi_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K}\\subseteq\\{\\pm 1\\}\nand\n{\nζ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nK\n⊆\n{\n±\n1\n}\nd\n1\n\\{\\zeta_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K}\\subseteq\\{\\pm 1\\}^{d_{1}}\nbe independent random vectors with i.i.d. Rademacher random variables as entries. Then, by Lemma\nD.1\n,\n𝔼\n​\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\nsup\nψ\n∈\n𝕊\nd\n1\n−\n1\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nε\ni\n​\nψ\n𝖳\n​\nM\nη\n,\ni\n​\nα\ni\n​\nϕ\n​\n(\nw\ni\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\ni\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{x\\in X}\\sup_{\\psi\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d_{1}-1}}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\varepsilon_{i}\\psi^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{\\eta,i}\\alpha_{i}\\phi(w_{i}^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b_{i})\n=\n𝔼\n​\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n,\nψ\n∈\n𝕊\nd\n1\n−\n1\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nε\ni\n​\nh\ni\n​\n(\nF\nx\n,\nψ\n​\n(\nw\ni\n,\nb\ni\n)\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{x\\in X,\\psi\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d_{1}-1}}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\varepsilon_{i}h_{i}(F_{x,\\psi}(w_{i},b_{i})),\n⩽\n2\n​\nB\nh\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\n​\n𝔼\n​\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n,\nψ\n∈\n𝕊\nd\n1\n−\n1\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\n⟨\n(\nξ\ni\nζ\ni\n)\n,\n(\nw\ni\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\nb\ni\nψ\n)\n⟩\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant 2B_{h}B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{x\\in X,\\psi\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d_{1}-1}}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\left\\langle\\begin{pmatrix}\\xi_{i}\\\\\n\\zeta_{i}\\end{pmatrix},\\begin{pmatrix}w_{i}^{\\mathsf{T}}x+b_{i}\\\\\n\\psi\\end{pmatrix}\\right\\rangle,\n=\n2\n​\nB\nh\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\n​\n[\n𝔼\n​\nsup\nx\n∈\nX\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nξ\ni\n​\nw\ni\n𝖳\n​\nx\n+\n𝔼\n​\nsup\nψ\n∈\n𝕊\nq\n−\n1\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nζ\ni\n𝖳\n​\nψ\n]\n,\n\\displaystyle=2B_{h}B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\left[\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{x\\in X}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\xi_{i}w_{i}^{\\mathsf{T}}x+\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{\\psi\\in\\mathbb{S}^{q-1}}\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\zeta_{i}^{\\mathsf{T}}\\psi\\right],\n⩽\n2\n​\nB\nh\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\n​\n[\nB\nX\n​\n𝔼\n​\n‖\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nK\nξ\ni\n​\nw\ni\n‖\n2\n+\n𝔼\n​\n‖\n∑\nk\n=\n1\nK\nζ\ni\n‖\n2\n]\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant 2B_{h}B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\left[B_{X}\\mathbb{E}\\left\\lVert\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\xi_{i}w_{i}\\right\\rVert_{2}+\\mathbb{E}\\left\\lVert\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\zeta_{i}\\right\\rVert_{2}\\right],\n⩽\n2\n​\nK\n​\nB\nh\n​\nB\nΦ\n​\n(\nη\n)\n​\n[\nB\nX\n​\n𝔼\n​\n∥\nw\n1\n∥\n2\n2\n+\nd\n1\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant 2\\sqrt{K}B_{h}B_{\\Phi}(\\eta)\\left[B_{X}\\sqrt{\\mathbb{E}\\lVert w_{1}\\rVert_{2}^{2}}+\\sqrt{d_{1}}\\right].\nThis completes the proof.\n∎\nAppendix E\nOmitted proofs for Section\n6\nE.1\nDetails of Example\n6.2\nFirst, for any\nδ\n>\n0\n\\delta>0\n, we define the function\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n:=\ns\nδ\n​\n(\nx\n)\nx\n.\n\\displaystyle\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(x):=\\frac{s_{\\delta}(x)}{x}.\nLemma E.1\n.\nFor any\nδ\n>\n0\n\\delta>0\n, the function\nx\n↦\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\nx\n)\nx\\mapsto\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{x})\nis\n1\n2\n​\nδ\n2\n\\frac{1}{2\\delta^{2}}\n-Lipschitz on\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\n.\nProof.\nFix\nx\n,\ny\n∈\nℝ\n⩾\n0\nx,y\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\n.\nWithout loss of generality, suppose that\nx\n⩽\ny\nx\\leqslant y\n(otherwise, we may flip the roles of\nx\nx\nand\ny\ny\n).\nIf\ny\n⩽\nδ\n2\ny\\leqslant\\delta^{2}\n, then\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n=\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\ny\n)\n=\n0\n\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{x})=\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{y})=0\n,\nin which case the claim is trivial.\nNow suppose that\nx\n⩾\nδ\n2\nx\\geqslant\\delta^{2}\n.\nOn\n[\nδ\n,\n∞\n)\n[\\delta,\\infty)\n,\nwe have that\ns\n¯\nδ\n\\bar{s}_{\\delta}\ncoincides with\nx\n↦\n1\n−\nδ\n/\nx\nx\\mapsto 1-\\delta/x\n, and hence\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n−\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\ny\n)\n\\displaystyle\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{x})-\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{y})\n=\n1\n−\nδ\nx\n−\n(\n1\n−\nδ\ny\n)\n=\nδ\n⁡\n(\n1\ny\n−\n1\nx\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=1-\\frac{\\delta}{\\sqrt{x}}-\\left(1-\\frac{\\delta}{\\sqrt{y}}\\right)=\\delta\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).\nThe function\nx\n↦\n1\n/\nx\nx\\mapsto 1/\\sqrt{x}\nis\n1\n2\n​\nδ\n3\n\\frac{1}{2\\delta^{3}}\n-Lipschitz on\n[\nδ\n2\n,\n∞\n)\n[\\delta^{2},\\infty)\n, and hence\n|\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n−\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\ny\n)\n|\n⩽\n1\n2\n​\nδ\n2\n​\n|\nx\n−\ny\n|\n.\n\\displaystyle|\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{x})-\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{y})|\\leqslant\\frac{1}{2\\delta^{2}}|x-y|.\nFinally, we suppose that\nx\n⩽\nδ\n2\n⩽\ny\nx\\leqslant\\delta^{2}\\leqslant y\n.\nBy concavity of the square root on\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\n,\ny\n⩽\nδ\n+\n1\n2\n​\nδ\n​\n(\ny\n−\nδ\n2\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sqrt{y}\\leqslant\\delta+\\frac{1}{2\\delta}(y-\\delta^{2}).\nTherefore,\n|\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n−\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\ny\n)\n|\n=\n|\ns\n¯\nδ\n​\n(\ny\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle|\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{x})-\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{y})|=|\\bar{s}_{\\delta}(\\sqrt{y})|\n=\n1\n−\nδ\ny\n=\ny\n−\nδ\ny\n⩽\ny\n−\nδ\nδ\n⩽\ny\n−\nδ\n2\n2\n​\nδ\n2\n⩽\ny\n−\nx\n2\n​\nδ\n2\n=\n|\ny\n−\nx\n|\n2\n​\nδ\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle=1-\\frac{\\delta}{\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}-\\delta}{\\sqrt{y}}\\leqslant\\frac{\\sqrt{y}-\\delta}{\\delta}\\leqslant\\frac{y-\\delta^{2}}{2\\delta^{2}}\\leqslant\\frac{y-x}{2\\delta^{2}}=\\frac{|y-x|}{2\\delta^{2}}.\n∎\nSee\n6.2\nProof.\nIt is straightforward to check that\nd\nd\n​\nx\n​\ns\nΔ\n2\n​\n(\nx\n)\n=\ns\n¯\nΔ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n\\frac{d}{dx}s^{2}_{\\sqrt{\\Delta}}(\\sqrt{x})=\\bar{s}_{\\sqrt{\\Delta}}(\\sqrt{x})\n.\nConditions (i) and (ii) are immediately satisfied. To check condition (iii), observe that by Lemma\nE.1\n,\ns\n¯\nΔ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n\\bar{s}_{\\sqrt{\\Delta}}(\\sqrt{x})\nis\n1\n/\n(\n2\n​\nΔ\n)\n1/(2\\Delta)\n-Lipschitz.\nFinally,\ns\n¯\nΔ\n​\n(\nx\n)\n⩽\n1\n\\bar{s}_{\\sqrt{\\Delta}}(\\sqrt{x})\\leqslant 1\nfor all\nx\n⩾\n0\nx\\geqslant 0\n.\n∎\nE.2\nDetails of Example\n6.3\nSee\n6.3\nProof.\nIt is easy to check that the derivative\ns\nΔ\n,\nγ\n′\ns^{\\prime}_{\\Delta,\\gamma}\nexists, is continuous,\nand is given by:\ns\nΔ\n,\nγ\n′\n​\n(\nx\n)\n=\n{\n0\nif\n​\nx\n⩽\nΔ\n,\nx\n−\nΔ\n2\n​\nγ\nif\n​\nx\n∈\n(\nΔ\n,\nΔ\n+\n2\n​\nγ\n)\n,\n1\nif\n​\nx\n⩾\nΔ\n+\n2\n​\nγ\n.\n\\displaystyle s^{\\prime}_{\\Delta,\\gamma}(x)=\\begin{cases}0&\\text{if }x\\leqslant\\Delta,\\\\\n\\frac{x-\\Delta}{2\\gamma}&\\text{if }x\\in(\\Delta,\\Delta+2\\gamma),\\\\\n1&\\text{if }x\\geqslant\\Delta+2\\gamma.\\end{cases}\nIt is also easy to check that this derivative is\n1\n2\n​\nγ\n\\frac{1}{2\\gamma}\n-Lipschitz\nand bounded by\n1\n1\n.\n∎\nE.3\nProof of Theorem\n6.4\nProposition E.2\n.\nFix any\nΔ\n>\n0\n\\Delta>0\n.\nLet\nσ\nΔ\n\\sigma_{\\Delta}\nbe\nΔ\n\\Delta\n-admissible.\nDefine\nF\n:\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\ns\n×\nO\np\n×\nO\nm\n×\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n→\nℝ\nn\n×\nℝ\ns\n×\nℝ\np\n×\nℝ\nm\nF:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{s}\\times O_{p}\\times O_{m}\\times\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{s}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{p}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{m}\nas:\nF\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\ne\n,\nα\n^\np\n,\nα\n^\nm\n,\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle F(x,e,\\hat{\\alpha}_{p},\\hat{\\alpha}_{m},t)\n:\n=\n[\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\n~\np\n+\nΨ\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n​\nα\n^\nm\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n)\n,\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n+\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\n~\np\n+\nΨ\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n​\nα\n^\nm\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n)\n,\n−\nσ\n′\nΔ\n(\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n)\n[\n∇\n2\nψ\np\n(\nα\n^\np\n)\n]\n−\n1\nY\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\n−\nσ\n′\nΔ\n(\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n)\n[\n∇\n2\nψ\nm\n(\nα\n^\nm\n)\n]\n−\n1\nΨ\n(\nx\n)\n𝖳\ng\ne\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n.\n]\n.\n\\displaystyle:=\\begin{bmatrix}f(x,t)+g(x,t)(Y(x,t)\\tilde{\\alpha}_{p}+\\Psi(x)\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}-h(x)),\\\\\nf_{e}(e,t)+g_{e}(x,t)(Y(x,t)\\tilde{\\alpha}_{p}+\\Psi(x)\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}-h(x)),\\\\\n-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e,t))[\\nabla^{2}\\psi_{p}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{p})]^{-1}Y(x,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(e,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e,t),\\\\\n-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e,t))[\\nabla^{2}\\psi_{m}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{m})]^{-1}\\Psi(x)^{\\mathsf{T}}g_{e}(e,t)^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q(e,t).\\end{bmatrix}.\nThe function\nF\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\ne\n,\nα\n^\np\n,\nα\n^\nm\n,\nt\n)\nF(x,e,\\hat{\\alpha}_{p},\\hat{\\alpha}_{m},t)\nis locally\nLipschitz in\n(\nx\n,\ne\n,\nα\n^\np\n,\nα\n^\nm\n)\n(x,e,\\hat{\\alpha}_{p},\\hat{\\alpha}_{m})\n.\nProof.\nThe functions\nf\n,\nf\ne\n,\ng\n,\ng\ne\n,\nh\n,\nY\n,\nΨ\n,\n∇\nQ\n,\nB\nh\nf,f_{e},g,g_{e},h,Y,\\Psi,\\nabla Q,B_{h}\n, and\nσ\nΔ\n′\n\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}\nare all\nlocally Lipschitz and locally bounded\nby assumption.\nAs long as we can check that both\nζ\n1\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n:=\nσ\nΔ\n′\n​\n(\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n)\n\\zeta_{1}(e,t):=\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e,t))\nand\nζ\n2\n,\nℓ\n​\n(\nα\n^\n)\n:=\n[\n∇\n2\nψ\nℓ\n​\n(\nα\n^\n)\n]\n−\n1\n\\zeta_{2,\\ell}(\\hat{\\alpha}):=[\\nabla^{2}\\psi_{\\ell}(\\hat{\\alpha})]^{-1}\nfor\nℓ\n∈\n{\np\n,\nm\n}\n\\ell\\in\\{p,m\\}\nare locally Lipschitz and locally bounded, then\nthe result follows via repeated applications\nof the sum and product composition rules (Proposition\nB.1\n).\nWe now verify that\nζ\n1\n\\zeta_{1}\nis locally Lipschitz\nand locally bounded.\nSince\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n\\nabla Q(e,t)\nis locally bounded, this means that\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\nQ(e,t)\nis locally Lipschitz.\nFurthermore, since\n0\n⩽\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⩽\nμ\n2\n​\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n0\\leqslant Q(e,t)\\leqslant\\mu_{2}(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})\n,\nit is clear that\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\nQ(e,t)\nis locally bounded.\nNext,\nσ\nΔ\n′\n\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}\nis locally Lipschitz by admissibility.\nSince\nσ\nΔ\n′\n\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}\ndoes not depend on time, then it is\nalso locally bounded.\nThis shows that\nζ\n1\n\\zeta_{1}\nis locally Lipschitz and bounded,\nsince it is the composition of two locally Lipschitz and bounded functions.\nFor\nζ\n2\n,\nℓ\n\\zeta_{2,\\ell}\n, we first observe that,\nsince\nψ\nℓ\n\\psi_{\\ell}\nis strongly convex with respect to a norm\n∥\n⋅\n∥\n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert\non\nO\nℓ\nO_{\\ell}\n, there exists a\nc\n>\n0\nc>0\nsuch that\n∇\n2\nψ\nℓ\n​\n(\nα\n^\n)\n≽\nc\n​\nI\n\\nabla^{2}\\psi_{\\ell}(\\hat{\\alpha})\\succcurlyeq cI\nfor all\nα\n^\n∈\nO\nℓ\n\\hat{\\alpha}\\in O_{\\ell}\n.\nNext, for any invertible square matrices\nA\n,\nB\nA,B\n, we have the algebraic identity\nA\n−\n1\n−\nB\n−\n1\n=\nA\n−\n1\n​\n(\nB\n−\nA\n)\n​\nB\n−\n1\nA^{-1}-B^{-1}=A^{-1}(B-A)B^{-1}\n.\nTherefore, for any two\nα\n^\n1\n,\nα\n^\n2\n\\hat{\\alpha}_{1},\\hat{\\alpha}_{2}\n,\n∥\n[\n∇\n2\nψ\nℓ\n​\n(\nα\n^\n1\n)\n]\n−\n1\n−\n[\n∇\n2\nψ\nℓ\n​\n(\nα\n^\n2\n)\n]\n−\n1\n∥\nop\n⩽\nc\n−\n2\n​\n∥\n∇\n2\nψ\n​\n(\nα\n^\n1\n)\n−\n∇\n2\nψ\n​\n(\nα\n^\n2\n)\n∥\nop\n.\n\\displaystyle\\lVert[\\nabla^{2}\\psi_{\\ell}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{1})]^{-1}-[\\nabla^{2}\\psi_{\\ell}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{2})]^{-1}\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\leqslant c^{-2}\\lVert\\nabla^{2}\\psi(\\hat{\\alpha}_{1})-\\nabla^{2}\\psi(\\hat{\\alpha}_{2})\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}.\nBecause the potential\nψ\nℓ\n\\psi_{\\ell}\nhas locally Lipschitz Hessians, this shows that\nζ\n2\n,\nℓ\n\\zeta_{2,\\ell}\nis locally Lipschitz. Since\nζ\n2\n,\nℓ\n\\zeta_{2,\\ell}\ndoes not depend on time, it is also\nlocally bounded.\n∎\nProposition E.3\n.\nLet\nO\n⊆\nℝ\nℓ\nO\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell}\nbe an open convex set, and\nlet\nψ\n:\nO\n→\nℝ\n\\psi:O\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\nbe a strongly convex potential with respect to some norm\n∥\n⋅\n∥\n\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert\non\nO\nO\n.\nThen we have that\ninf\nα\n,\nα\n^\n∈\nO\n,\nα\n≠\nα\n^\nd\nψ\n(\nα\n∥\nα\n^\n)\n∥\nα\n−\nα\n^\n∥\n2\n2\n>\n0\n.\n\\displaystyle\\inf_{\\begin{subarray}{c}\\alpha,\\hat{\\alpha}\\in O,\\\\\n\\alpha\\neq\\hat{\\alpha}\\end{subarray}}\\frac{d_{\\psi}\\left(\\alpha\\|\\hat{\\alpha}\\right)}{\\lVert\\alpha-\\hat{\\alpha}\\rVert_{2}^{2}}>0.\nProof.\nBecause all norms on\nℝ\nℓ\n\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell}\nare equivalent, strong convexity\non\nO\nO\nthere exists a\nc\n>\n0\nc>0\nsuch that\n∇\n2\nψ\n​\n(\na\n)\n≽\nc\n​\nI\n\\nabla^{2}\\psi(a)\\succcurlyeq cI\nfor all\na\n∈\nO\na\\in O\n.\nBy Taylor’s theorem, for any arbitrary\nα\n,\nα\n^\n\\alpha,\\hat{\\alpha}\n,\nd\nψ\n(\nα\n∥\nα\n^\n)\n\\displaystyle d_{\\psi}\\left(\\alpha\\|\\hat{\\alpha}\\right)\n=\n1\n2\n​\n(\nα\n−\nα\n^\n)\n𝖳\n​\n[\n∫\n0\n1\n∇\n2\nψ\n​\n(\n(\n1\n−\nt\n)\n​\nα\n^\n+\nα\n​\nt\n)\n​\n𝑑\nt\n]\n​\n(\nα\n−\nα\n^\n)\n⩾\nc\n2\n​\n∥\nα\n−\nα\n^\n∥\n2\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2}(\\alpha-\\hat{\\alpha})^{\\mathsf{T}}\\left[\\int_{0}^{1}\\nabla^{2}\\psi((1-t)\\hat{\\alpha}+\\alpha t)dt\\right](\\alpha-\\hat{\\alpha})\\geqslant\\frac{c}{2}\\lVert\\alpha-\\hat{\\alpha}\\rVert^{2}_{2}.\nThe claim now follows.\n∎\nSee\n6.4\nProof.\nBy Proposition\nE.2\n,\nthe right-hand side of the dynamical system on\n(\nx\n,\ne\n,\nα\n^\np\n,\nα\n^\nm\n)\n(x,e,\\hat{\\alpha}_{p},\\hat{\\alpha}_{m})\nis locally Lipschitz, and\ntherefore there exists a maximal time\nT\nmax\n>\n0\nT_{\\max}>0\nsuch that there exists a unique\nC\n1\nC^{1}\ncurve\nt\n↦\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\np\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\nt\\mapsto(x(t),e(t),\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}(t),\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}(t))\nthat satisfies the dynamics on\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n[0,T_{\\max})\n.\nWe now define the candidate Lyapunov function\nV\n:\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n→\nℝ\n⩾\n0\nV:[0,T_{\\max})\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}\nas\nV\n(\nt\n)\n=\nσ\nΔ\n(\nQ\n(\ne\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n)\n+\nd\nψ\np\n(\nα\np\n∥\nα\n^\np\n)\n+\nd\nψ\nm\n(\nα\nm\n∥\nα\n^\nm\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle V(t)=\\sigma_{\\Delta}(Q(e(t),t))+d_{\\psi_{p}}\\left(\\alpha_{p}\\|\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}\\right)+d_{\\psi_{m}}\\left(\\alpha_{m}\\|\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}\\right).\nwhere\nα\nm\n\\alpha_{m}\nis the minimizing\nα\nm\n\\alpha_{m}\nin the definition\nB\napprox\nB_{\\text{approx}}\n6\n6\n6\nSuch a minimizing\nα\nm\n\\alpha_{m}\nexists since the function\nα\nm\n↦\nsup\n∥\nx\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nx\n∥\nΨ\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n​\nα\nm\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n∥\n2\n\\alpha_{m}\\mapsto\\sup_{\\lVert x\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{x}}\\lVert\\Psi(x)\\alpha_{m}-h(x)\\rVert_{2}\nis continuous and the set\n{\nd\nψ\nm\n(\nα\nm\n∥\nα\nm\n,\n0\n)\n⩽\nB\nα\nm\n}\n\\{d_{\\psi_{m}}\\left(\\alpha_{m}\\|\\alpha_{m,0}\\right)\\leqslant B_{\\alpha_{m}}\\}\nis closed.\n.\nTaking the time derivative of\nV\nV\nand suppressing dependence on time,\nd\nd\n​\nt\n​\nV\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}V(t)\n=\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\n(\n⟨\n∇\nQ\n,\nf\ne\n+\ng\ne\n(\nY\nα\n~\np\n+\nΨ\nα\nm\n−\nh\n)\n⟩\n+\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n)\n+\n⟨\nd\nd\n​\nt\n∇\nψ\np\n(\nα\n^\np\n)\n,\nα\n~\np\n⟩\n+\n⟨\nd\nd\n​\nt\n∇\nψ\nm\n(\nα\n^\nm\n)\n,\nα\n~\nm\n⟩\n\\displaystyle=\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\left(\\langle\\nabla Q,f_{e}+g_{e}(Y\\tilde{\\alpha}_{p}+\\Psi\\alpha_{m}-h)\\rangle+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}\\right)+\\left\\langle\\frac{d}{dt}\\nabla\\psi_{p}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}),\\tilde{\\alpha}_{p}\\right\\rangle+\\left\\langle\\frac{d}{dt}\\nabla\\psi_{m}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}),\\tilde{\\alpha}_{m}\\right\\rangle\n⩽\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\n(\n−\nρ\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n+\n⟨\n∇\nQ\n,\ng\ne\n(\nY\nα\n~\np\n+\nΨ\nα\nm\n−\nh\n)\n⟩\n)\n+\n⟨\nd\nd\n​\nt\n∇\nψ\np\n(\nα\n^\np\n)\n,\nα\n~\np\n⟩\n+\n⟨\nd\nd\n​\nt\n∇\nψ\nm\n(\nα\n^\nm\n)\n,\nα\n~\nm\n⟩\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\left(-\\rho(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})+\\langle\\nabla Q,g_{e}(Y\\tilde{\\alpha}_{p}+\\Psi\\alpha_{m}-h)\\rangle\\right)+\\left\\langle\\frac{d}{dt}\\nabla\\psi_{p}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}),\\tilde{\\alpha}_{p}\\right\\rangle+\\left\\langle\\frac{d}{dt}\\nabla\\psi_{m}(\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}),\\tilde{\\alpha}_{m}\\right\\rangle\n=\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\nρ\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n+\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\n⟨\ng\ne\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n,\nΨ\nα\n^\nm\n−\nh\n⟩\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\n⟨\ng\ne\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n,\nΨ\nα\n~\nm\n⟩\n\\displaystyle=-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\rho(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})+\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\left\\langle g_{e}^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q,\\Psi\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}-h\\right\\rangle-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\left\\langle g_{e}^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q,\\Psi\\tilde{\\alpha}_{m}\\right\\rangle\n=\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\nρ\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n+\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\n⟨\ng\ne\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n,\nΨ\nα\nm\n−\nh\n⟩\n\\displaystyle=-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\rho(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})+\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\left\\langle g_{e}^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q,\\Psi\\alpha_{m}-h\\right\\rangle\n⩽\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\nρ\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n+\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\n∥\ng\ne\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n∥\n2\n∥\nΨ\nα\nm\n−\nh\n∥\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\rho(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})+\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\lVert g_{e}^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q\\rVert_{2}\\lVert\\Psi\\alpha_{m}-h\\rVert_{2}.\nBecause\nσ\nΔ\n\\sigma_{\\Delta}\nis a\nΔ\n\\Delta\n-admissible deadzone,\nσ\nΔ\n′\n>\n0\n\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}>0\nonly when\nQ\n>\nΔ\nQ>\\Delta\n.\nBut since\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⩽\nμ\n2\n​\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\nQ(e,t)\\leqslant\\mu_{2}(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2})\n,\nwe have\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n>\nμ\n2\n−\n1\n​\n(\nΔ\n)\n\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}>\\mu_{2}^{-1}(\\Delta)\n.\nTherefore,\nd\nd\n​\nt\n​\nV\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}V(t)\n⩽\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\nρ\n(\nμ\n2\n−\n1\n(\nΔ\n)\n)\n+\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\n∥\ng\ne\n𝖳\n∇\nQ\n∥\n2\n∥\nΨ\nα\nm\n−\nh\n∥\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\rho(\\mu_{2}^{-1}(\\Delta))+\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\lVert g_{e}^{\\mathsf{T}}\\nabla Q\\rVert_{2}\\lVert\\Psi\\alpha_{m}-h\\rVert_{2}.\n(E.1)\nLet\nT\n0\nT_{0}\nbe defined as\nT\n0\n:=\nsup\n{\nT\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n∣\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n​\n∀\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n]\n}\n.\n\\displaystyle T_{0}:=\\sup\\{T\\in[0,T_{\\max})\\mid\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R\\>\\>\\forall t\\in[0,T]\\}.\nNote that since\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n)\n⩽\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n,\n0\n)\n+\nB\nα\np\n+\nB\nα\nm\n)\n<\nR\n,\n\\displaystyle\\lVert e(0)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\mu_{1}^{-1}(V(0))\\leqslant\\mu_{1}^{-1}\\left(Q(e(0),0)+B_{\\alpha_{p}}+B_{\\alpha_{m}}\\right)<R,\nT\n0\nT_{0}\nis well-defined.\nNow, by means of contradiction,\nsuppose\nT\n0\n<\nT\nmax\nT_{0}<T_{\\max}\n.\nFor every\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n0\n]\nt\\in[0,T_{0}]\n, by (\n6.1\n), we have that\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\nd\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\ne\n​\nR\n\\lVert x(t)-x_{d}(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant C_{e}R\n,\nand hence\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\ne\n​\nR\n+\nB\nd\n=\nB\nx\n\\lVert x(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant C_{e}R+B_{d}=B_{x}\n.\nHence, by the definition of\nB\ng\ne\nB_{g_{e}}\nand\nB\n∇\nQ\nB_{\\nabla Q}\n,\nfrom (\nE.1\n)\nand the requirement that\nΔ\n⩾\nμ\n2\n​\n(\nρ\n−\n1\n​\n(\n2\n​\nB\ng\ne\n​\nB\n∇\nQ\n​\nB\napprox\n)\n)\n\\Delta\\geqslant\\mu_{2}(\\rho^{-1}(2B_{g_{e}}B_{\\nabla Q}B_{\\mathrm{approx}}))\n, for every\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n0\n]\nt\\in[0,T_{0}]\n,\nd\nd\n​\nt\n​\nV\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}V(t)\n⩽\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n​\n(\nQ\n)\n​\nρ\n​\n(\nμ\n2\n−\n1\n​\n(\nΔ\n)\n)\n+\nσ\nΔ\n′\n​\n(\nQ\n)\n​\nB\ng\ne\n​\nB\n∇\nQ\n​\nB\napprox\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\rho(\\mu_{2}^{-1}(\\Delta))+\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)B_{g_{e}}B_{\\nabla Q}B_{\\mathrm{approx}}\n⩽\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\nρ\n(\nμ\n2\n−\n1\n(\nΔ\n)\n)\n/\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\rho(\\mu_{2}^{-1}(\\Delta))/2.\nHence,\nV\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n⩽\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\nV(t)\\leqslant V(0)\nfor all\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n0\n]\nt\\in[0,T_{0}]\n.\nOn the other hand, since\nT\n0\nT_{0}\nis maximal,\nwe must have that\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nT\n0\n)\n∥\n2\n=\nR\n\\lVert e(T_{0})\\rVert_{2}=R\n,\notherwise, if\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nT\n0\n)\n∥\n2\n<\nR\n\\lVert e(T_{0})\\rVert_{2}<R\n,\nby continuity of the solution\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)\non\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n[0,T_{\\max})\n,\nthere would exist a\nδ\n>\n0\n\\delta>0\nsuch that for all\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\n0\n+\nδ\n]\nt\\in[0,T_{0}+\\delta]\n,\nwe have\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R\n.\nThis means then that,\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n⩾\nV\n⁡\n(\nT\n0\n)\n⩾\nμ\n1\n​\n(\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\n∥\n2\n)\n=\nμ\n1\n​\n(\nR\n)\n>\nμ\n1\n​\n(\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n)\n)\n=\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle V(0)\\geqslant V(T_{0})\\geqslant\\mu_{1}(\\lVert e(T)\\rVert_{2})=\\mu_{1}(R)>\\mu_{1}(\\mu_{1}^{-1}(V(0)))=V(0),\na contradiction. Hence\nT\n0\n=\nT\nmax\nT_{0}=T_{\\max}\n.\nNow we argue that\nT\nmax\nT_{\\max}\ncannot be finite.\nSuppose towards a contradiction that\nT\nmax\nT_{\\max}\nis finite.\nWe already have\nmax\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\n⁡\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n\\max_{t\\in[0,T_{\\max})}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R\n.\nThis implies that\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\ne\n​\nR\n+\nB\nd\n=\nB\nx\n\\lVert x(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant C_{e}R+B_{d}=B_{x}\nfor\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\nt\\in[0,T_{\\max})\n.\nFinally,\nsince\nV\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n⩽\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\nV(t)\\leqslant V(0)\non all\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\nt\\in[0,T_{\\max})\n,\nthis shows that both\n∥\nα\n^\np\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nand\n∥\nα\n^\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nare uniformly bounded\nfor all\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\nT\nmax\n)\nt\\in[0,T_{\\max})\nvia Proposition\nE.3\n.\nThis contradicts the maximality of\nT\nmax\nT_{\\max}\n, showing\nthat\nT\nmax\n=\n∞\nT_{\\max}=\\infty\n.\nTo continue the proof,\nwe integrate the inequality\nd\nd\n​\nt\nV\n(\nt\n)\n⩽\n−\nσ\nΔ\n′\n(\nQ\n)\nρ\n(\nμ\n2\n−\n1\n(\nΔ\n)\n)\n/\n2\n\\frac{d}{dt}V(t)\\leqslant-\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q)\\rho(\\mu_{2}^{-1}(\\Delta))/2\nto conclude that\n∫\n0\n∞\nσ\nΔ\n′\n​\n(\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n)\n​\n𝑑\nt\n⩽\n2\n​\nV\n​\n(\n0\n)\nρ\n⁡\n(\nμ\n2\n−\n1\n​\n(\nΔ\n)\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e(t),t))dt\\leqslant\\frac{2V(0)}{\\rho(\\mu_{2}^{-1}(\\Delta))}.\nWe now argue that the integrand\nt\n↦\nσ\nΔ\n′\n​\n(\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n)\nt\\mapsto\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e(t),t))\nis uniformly continuous.\nTo do this, we\nwill argue that (a)\nt\n↦\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto Q(e(t),t)\nis uniformly bounded,\n(b)\nt\n↦\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto e(t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz,\nand (c)\nt\n↦\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto Q(e(t),t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz.\nTo see (a), we note that\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n⩽\nV\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n⩽\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\nQ(e(t),t)\\leqslant V(t)\\leqslant V(0)\n.\nTo see (b), we note that:\n∥\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\n∥\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n+\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nop\n​\n(\n∥\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\nop\n​\n∥\nα\n~\np\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n+\n∥\nΨ\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n∥\nop\n​\n∥\nα\n^\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n+\n∥\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n∥\n2\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\lVert\\dot{e}(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\lVert f_{e}(e(t),t)\\rVert_{2}+\\lVert g_{e}(x(t),t)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}(\\lVert Y(x(t),t)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\lVert\\tilde{\\alpha}_{p}(t)\\rVert_{2}+\\lVert\\Psi(x(t))\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}(t)\\rVert_{2}+\\lVert h(x(t))\\rVert_{2}).\nSince\nf\ne\nf_{e}\n,\ng\ne\ng_{e}\n,\nY\nY\n,\nΨ\n\\Psi\n, and\nh\nh\nare locally bounded\nin the first argument uniformly in\nt\nt\n,\nand since\n∥\nα\n^\np\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nand\n∥\nα\n^\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nare uniformly bounded,\nthis shows that\n∥\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\dot{e}(t)\\rVert_{2}\nis uniformly bounded,\nand hence\nt\n↦\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto e(t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz.\nTo see (c), we observe that:\n|\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n,\ns\n)\n−\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle|Q(e(s),s)-Q(e(t),t)|\n⩽\n|\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n,\ns\n)\n−\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n|\n+\n|\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n|\n\\displaystyle\\qquad\\qquad\\leqslant|Q(e(s),s)-Q(e(s),t)|+|Q(e(s),t)-Q(e(t),t)|\n⩽\n[\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\nsup\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n|\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n|\n]\n​\n|\ns\n−\nt\n|\n+\n[\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\nsup\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n∥\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n]\n​\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n−\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\qquad\\qquad\\leqslant\\left[\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\sup_{\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R}\\left|\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}(e,t)\\right|\\right]|s-t|+\\left[\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\sup_{\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R}\\lVert\\nabla Q(e,t)\\rVert_{2}\\right]\\lVert e(s)-e(t)\\rVert_{2}.\nSince\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}\nand\n∇\nQ\n\\nabla Q\nare both locally bounded in\ne\ne\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n, and since\nt\n↦\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto e(t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz,\nwe see that\nt\n↦\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nt\\mapsto Q(e(t),t)\nis also uniformly Lipschitz.\nWe now argue that\nt\n↦\nσ\nΔ\n′\n​\n(\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n)\nt\\mapsto\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e(t),t))\nis uniformly continuous.\nSince\nσ\nΔ\n′\n\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}\nis locally Lipschitz,\nit is uniformly Lipschitz on\n[\n0\n,\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\n]\n[0,V(0)]\n.\nTherefore,\nt\n↦\nσ\nΔ\n′\n​\n(\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n)\nt\\mapsto\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e(t),t))\nis the composition of two Lipschitz functions, and is hence Lipschitz\n(and therefore uniformly continuous).\nFrom this, we apply Barbalat’s lemma to conclude that:\nlim\nt\n→\n∞\nσ\nΔ\n′\n​\n(\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n)\n=\n0\n.\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}(Q(e(t),t))=0.\nSince\nσ\nΔ\n\\sigma_{\\Delta}\nis a\nΔ\n\\Delta\n-admissible deadzone, this implies that\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nΔ\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\mu_{1}^{-1}(\\Delta).\n∎\nE.4\nProof of Theorem\n6.7\nSee\n6.7\nProof.\nWe proceed by reduction to Theorem\n6.4\n. Observe that we may write the predictor (\n3.3\n) in the matched uncertainty form (\n3.1\n) with\ng\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n=\nI\ng(\\hat{x},t)=I\nx\n^\n˙\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n+\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n+\n(\nf\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nα\n^\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n)\n.\n\\dot{\\hat{x}}=f(\\hat{x},t)+k(\\hat{x},x(t))+\\left(\\hat{f}(\\hat{x},\\hat{\\alpha},t)-f(\\hat{x},t)\\right).\nThis is an adaptive control problem with input\nf\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nα\n^\n,\nt\n)\n\\hat{f}(\\hat{x},\\hat{\\alpha},t)\nand desired trajectory\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\n. The “nominal dynamics”\nf\n¯\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n:=\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n+\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n\\bar{f}(\\hat{x},t):=f(\\hat{x},t)+k(\\hat{x},x(t))\nis contracting\nat rate\nλ\n\\lambda\nin the metric\nM\nM\nby assumption, meaning that\n∂\nf\n¯\n∂\nx\n^\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\nM\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n+\nM\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n∂\nf\n¯\n∂\nx\n^\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n+\nM\n˙\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n≼\n−\n2\nλ\nM\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n∀\nx\n^\n∈\nℝ\nn\n,\nt\n∈\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n.\n\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial\\bar{f}}{\\partial\\hat{x}}(\\hat{x},t)^{\\mathsf{T}}M(\\hat{x},t)+M(\\hat{x},t)\\frac{\\partial\\bar{f}}{\\partial\\hat{x}}(\\hat{x},t)+\\dot{M}(\\hat{x},t)\\preccurlyeq-2\\lambda M(\\hat{x},t)\\>\\>\\forall\\hat{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n},t\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}.\nLet the error signal\ne\n​\n(\nt\n)\n:=\nx\n^\n​\n(\nt\n)\n−\nx\n​\n(\nt\n)\ne(t):=\\hat{x}(t)-x(t)\n.\nThe error dynamics are\ne\n˙\n\\displaystyle\\dot{e}\n=\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n+\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n+\n(\nf\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nα\n^\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=f(\\hat{x},t)-f(x(t),t)+k(\\hat{x},x(t))+\\left(\\hat{f}(\\hat{x},\\hat{\\alpha},t)-f(\\hat{x},t)\\right)\n=\nf\n¯\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n+\n(\nf\n^\n​\n(\nx\n^\n,\nα\n^\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\bar{f}(\\hat{x},t)-f(x(t),t)+\\left(\\hat{f}(\\hat{x},\\hat{\\alpha},t)-f(\\hat{x},t)\\right).\nHence we can define\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n:=\nf\n¯\n​\n(\ne\n+\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n,\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\n:=\nI\n.\n\\displaystyle f_{e}(e,t):=\\bar{f}(e+x(t),t)-f(x(t),t),\\>\\>g_{e}(x,t):=I.\nWe first check that\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\nf_{e}(e,t)\nis locally Lipschitz and locally bounded uniformly in\nt\nt\nvia Proposition\nB.1\n.\nFirst we consider\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nf\n⁡\n(\ne\n+\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n(e,t)\\mapsto f(e+x(t),t)\n.\nWrite this map as the composition\nf\n⁡\n(\nϕ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nf(\\phi(e,t),t)\nwith\nϕ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n:=\ne\n+\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n\\phi(e,t):=e+x(t)\n. Since the signal\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nis uniformly bounded,\nit is clear that\nϕ\n\\phi\nis both locally Lipschitz and locally bounded\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n. Since the outer function\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nt\n)\nf(x,t)\nis also locally\nLipschitz and locally bounded uniformly in\nt\nt\n, the composition remains locally Lipschitz and locally bounded uniformly in\nt\nt\n.\nNext, since\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n)\nk(\\hat{x},x)\nis locally Lipschitz in\nx\n^\n\\hat{x}\nand continuous,\nand since\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nis uniformly bounded, the function\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n(\\hat{x},t)\\mapsto k(\\hat{x},x(t))\nis locally Lipschitz\nand locally bounded uniformly in\nt\nt\n.\nBy an identical composition argument, so is\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nk\n⁡\n(\ne\n+\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n(e,t)\\mapsto k(e+x(t),x(t))\n. Finally, the function\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n↦\nf\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n(e,t)\\mapsto f(x(t),t)\nis trivially locally Lipschitz and locally bounded uniformly in\nt\nt\nsince\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\nis bounded. Therefore,\nf\ne\nf_{e}\nis locally Lipschitz and locally bounded uniformly in\nt\nt\n.\nThe Jacobian\n∂\nf\ne\n∂\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n=\n∂\nf\n¯\n∂\nx\n^\n​\n(\ne\n+\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n\\frac{\\partial f_{e}}{\\partial e}(e,t)=\\frac{\\partial\\bar{f}}{\\partial\\hat{x}}(e+x(t),t)\n,\nwhich shows that\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\nf_{e}(e,t)\nis contracting at rate\nλ\n\\lambda\nin the\nmetric\nM\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n:=\nM\n⁡\n(\ne\n+\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\nM_{e}(e,t):=M(e+x(t),t)\n.\nFurthermore, it is easy to check that\ne\n=\n0\ne=0\nis a particular solution to\ne\n˙\n=\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n\\dot{e}=f_{e}(e,t)\n,\nas\nk\n⁡\n(\nx\n,\nx\n)\n=\n0\nk(x,x)=0\nfor all\nx\nx\n.\nTherefore,\nf\ne\nf_{e}\nadmits an exponentially stable Lyapunov function\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n=\nE\nM\ne\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\ne\n,\n0\n)\nQ(e,t)=E_{M_{e}(\\cdot,t)}(e,0)\nthat satisfies:\n⟨\n∇\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\nf\ne\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⟩\n+\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⩽\n−\n2\nλ\nQ\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n∀\ne\n∈\nℝ\nn\n,\nt\n∈\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n.\n\\displaystyle\\langle\\nabla Q(e,t),f_{e}(e,t)\\rangle+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}(e,t)\\leqslant-2\\lambda Q(e,t)\\>\\>\\forall e\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n},t\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}.\nMoreover, because\nμ\n​\nI\n≼\nM\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n≼\nL\n​\nI\n\\mu I\\preccurlyeq M_{e}(e,t)\\preccurlyeq LI\n,\nμ\n​\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n2\n⩽\nQ\n⁡\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n⩽\nL\n​\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n2\n​\n∀\ne\n∈\nℝ\nn\n,\nt\n∈\nℝ\n⩾\n0\n.\n\\mu\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}^{2}\\leqslant Q(e,t)\\leqslant L\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}^{2}\\>\\>\\forall e\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n},t\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\geqslant 0}.\nNow, observe that\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n=\nM\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n​\nγ\ns\n​\n(\n0\n,\ne\n+\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n,\n\\nabla Q(e,t)=M_{e}(e,t)\\gamma_{s}(0;e+x(t),x(t),t),\nso that\nB\n∇\nQ\n=\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\nsup\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n∥\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nL\n​\nB\nγ\nB_{\\nabla Q}=\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\sup_{\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R}\\lVert\\nabla Q(e,t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant LB_{\\gamma}\n.\nFurthermore, by the boundedness of\nM\nM\nand\nthe assumption that\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n↦\n∥\nγ\ns\n​\n(\n0\n,\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n(\\hat{x},t)\\mapsto\\lVert\\gamma_{s}(0;\\hat{x},x(t),t)\\rVert_{2}\nis\nlocally bounded in\nx\n^\n\\hat{x}\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n,\nwe have that\n∇\nQ\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n\\nabla Q(e,t)\nis locally bounded in\ne\ne\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n.\nSimilarly, since\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n=\n−\nγ\ns\n​\n(\n1\n,\ne\n+\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n𝖳\n​\nM\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n​\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}(e,t)=-\\gamma_{s}(1;e+x(t),x(t),t)^{\\mathsf{T}}M_{e}(e,t)f_{e}(e,t),\nby the boundedness of\nM\nM\n, the assumption that\n(\nx\n^\n,\nt\n)\n↦\n∥\nγ\ns\n​\n(\n1\n,\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n(\\hat{x},t)\\mapsto\\lVert\\gamma_{s}(1;\\hat{x},x(t),t)\\rVert_{2}\nis locally bounded\nin\nx\n^\n\\hat{x}\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n(since geodesics have constant speed,\nwe have\n∥\nγ\ns\n​\n(\n1\n,\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n=\n∥\nγ\ns\n​\n(\n0\n,\nx\n^\n,\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\lVert\\gamma_{s}(1;\\hat{x},x(t),t)\\rVert_{2}=\\lVert\\gamma_{s}(0;\\hat{x},x(t),t)\\rVert_{2}\n),\nwe have that\n∂\nQ\n∂\nt\n​\n(\ne\n,\nt\n)\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial t}(e,t)\nis locally bounded in\nx\n^\n\\hat{x}\nuniformly in\nt\nt\n. Hence, we can invoke Theorem\n6.4\nwith\nC\ne\n=\n1\n,\nB\nd\n=\nB\nx\n,\nB\nx\n=\nB\nx\n^\n,\nB\ng\ne\n=\n1\n,\nB\n∇\nQ\n=\nL\n​\nB\nγ\n,\n\\displaystyle C_{e}=1,\\>\\>B_{d}=B_{x},\\>\\>B_{x}=B_{\\hat{x}},\\>\\>B_{g_{e}}=1,\\>\\>B_{\\nabla Q}=LB_{\\gamma},\nρ\n⁡\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n=\n2\n​\nλ\n​\nμ\n​\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n2\n,\nμ\n1\n​\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n=\nμ\n​\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n2\n,\nμ\n2\n​\n(\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n)\n=\nL\n​\n∥\ne\n∥\n2\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\rho\\left(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}\\right)=2\\lambda\\mu\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}^{2},\\>\\>\\mu_{1}\\left(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}\\right)=\\mu\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}^{2},\\>\\>\\mu_{2}\\left(\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}\\right)=L\\lVert e\\rVert_{2}^{2}.\nThe result now follows.\n∎\nE.5\nProof of Theorem\n6.6\nSee\n6.6\nProof.\nFrom the proof of Theorem\n6.4\n,\nthe solution\nt\n↦\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\np\n​\n(\nt\n)\n,\nα\n^\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n)\nt\\mapsto(x(t),e(t),\\hat{\\alpha}_{p}(t),\\hat{\\alpha}_{m}(t))\nexists for\nt\n⩾\n0\nt\\geqslant 0\n, is unique, and is continuously differentiable. Furthermore,\nby Proposition\nE.3\nwe have the following\nuniform estimates\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nR\n,\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\n∥\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nx\n,\nsup\nt\n⩾\n0\n∥\nα\n^\nℓ\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nB\nα\nℓ\nc\nℓ\n+\n∥\nα\nℓ\n,\n0\n∥\n2\n+\nV\n⁡\n(\n0\n)\nc\nℓ\n,\nℓ\n∈\n{\np\n,\nm\n}\n.\n\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant R,\\>\\>\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\lVert x(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant B_{x},\\>\\>\\sup_{t\\geqslant 0}\\lVert\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\ell}(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant\\sqrt{\\frac{B_{\\alpha_{\\ell}}}{c_{\\ell}}}+\\lVert\\alpha_{\\ell,0}\\rVert_{2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{V(0)}{c_{\\ell}}},\\>\\>\\ell\\in\\{p,m\\}.\nHere,\nc\np\nc_{p}\n(resp.\nc\nm\nc_{m}\n) is a constant depending only on the ambient dimension\np\np\nand\nψ\np\n\\psi_{p}\n(resp.\nm\nm\nand\nψ\nm\n\\psi_{m}\n).\nNow, applying that\nf\n,\ng\n,\nf\ne\n,\ng\ne\n,\nY\n,\nΨ\nf,g,f_{e},g_{e},Y,\\Psi\n, and\nh\nh\nare all locally bounded in their first arguments uniformly in\nt\nt\n,\nthat\n∇\n2\nψ\nℓ\n\\nabla^{2}\\psi_{\\ell}\nis uniformly\nbounded from below for\nℓ\n∈\n{\np\n,\nm\n}\n\\ell\\in\\{p,m\\}\n,\nand the assumption that\nσ\nΔ\n′\n\\sigma^{\\prime}_{\\Delta}\nis\nB\nB\n-bounded, we conclude that\nx\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\dot{x}(t)\n,\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\dot{e}(t)\n,\nα\n˙\np\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\dot{\\alpha}_{p}(t)\n,\nand\nα\n˙\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\dot{\\alpha}_{m}(t)\nare all uniformly bounded. Hence\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\nx(t)\n,\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\ne(t)\n,\nα\np\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\alpha_{p}(t)\n, and\nα\nm\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\alpha_{m}(t)\nare uniformly Lipschitz\nwith Lipschitz constants that do not depend on\nΔ\n\\Delta\nand\nL\nL\n.\nNext, the fact that\nf\ne\nf_{e}\n,\ng\ne\ng_{e}\n,\nY\nY\n,\nΨ\n\\Psi\n, and\nh\nh\nare locally Lipschitz and locally bounded in their first arguments\nuniformly in\nt\nt\nimplies that\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n\\dot{e}(t)\nis uniformly Lipschitz,\nwith a Lipschitz constant that depends affinely on\nL\nL\n.\nTherefore, by Proposition\nB.4\n,\nwe have that:\nlim sup\nt\n→\n∞\n∥\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n⩽\nC\n1\n​\nμ\n1\n−\n1\n​\n(\nΔ\n)\n​\n(\n1\n+\nL\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\limsup_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\lVert\\dot{e}(t)\\rVert_{2}\\leqslant C_{1}\\sqrt{\\mu_{1}^{-1}(\\Delta)(1+L)},\nfor a constant\nC\n1\nC_{1}\nthat does not depend on\nΔ\n\\Delta\nand\nL\nL\n.\nNow for any\nt\nt\n,\n∥\ng\ne\n​\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\n(\nu\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n−\nY\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n​\nα\np\n−\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n)\n)\n∥\n2\n\\displaystyle\\lVert g_{e}(x(t),t)(u(x(t),t)-Y(x(t),t)\\alpha_{p}-h(x(t)))\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\n∥\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n+\n∥\nf\ne\n​\n(\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n,\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\lVert\\dot{e}(t)\\rVert_{2}+\\lVert f_{e}(e(t),t)\\rVert_{2}\n⩽\n∥\ne\n˙\n​\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n+\nC\n2\n​\n∥\ne\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n∥\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle\\leqslant\\lVert\\dot{e}(t)\\rVert_{2}+C_{2}\\lVert e(t)\\rVert_{2},\nwhere\nC\n2\nC_{2}\ndoes not depend on\nΔ\n\\Delta\nand\nL\nL\n. Taking the\nlim sup\n\\limsup\non both sides yields the claim.\n∎\n◄\nFeeling\nlucky?\nConversion\nreport\nReport\nan issue\nView original\non arXiv\n►"}
{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2106.04028.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 204285, "equations": ["R^{2}&gt;c_{\\text{crit}}", "d=1,...,D", "i=1,..,H", "i=1,...,H", "l=1,...,L", "i=1,...,D/H"], "text": "[2106.04028] Deep Learning Statistical Arbitrage Deep Learning Statistical Arbitrage Thanks: We thank Robert Anderson, Jose Blanchet, Marcelo Fernandes, Kay Giesecke, Lisa Goldberg, Amit Goyal (discussant), Bryan Kelly, Robert Korajczyk, Martin Lettau, Sophia Li, Marcelo Medeiros, Scott Murray, George Papanicolaou, Guofu Zhou (discussant) and seminar and conference participants at Stanford, UC Berkeley, Rutgers University, GSU CEAR Finance Conference, NBER-NSF Time-Series Conference, AI and Big Data in Finance Research Seminar, AI in Fintech Forum, World Congress of the Bachelier Finance Society, Society of Financial Econometrics Annual Conference, the Econometric Research in Finance Workshop, the Meeting of the Brazilian Finance Society, World Online Seminars on Machine Learning in Finance, the Machine Learning and Quantitative Finance Workshop at Oxford, Annual Bloomberg-Columbia Machine Learning in Finance Workshop, NVIDIA AI Webinar, Vanguard Academic Seminar, INFORMS and the Western Conference on Mathematical Finance for helpful comments. We thank MSCI for generous research support. Jorge Guijarro-Ordonez Thanks: Stanford University, Department of Mathematics, Email: jguiord@stanford.edu. Markus Pelger Thanks: Stanford University, Department of Management Science &amp; Engineering, Email: mpelger@stanford.edu. Greg Zanotti Thanks: Stanford University, Department of Management Science &amp; Engineering, Email: gzanotti@stanford.edu. This draft: September 25, 2022 First draft: March 15, 2019 Statistical arbitrage exploits temporal price differences between similar assets. We develop a unifying conceptual framework for statistical arbitrage and a novel data driven solution. First, we construct arbitrage portfolios of similar assets as residual portfolios from conditional latent asset pricing factors. Second, we extract their time series signals with a powerful machine-learning time-series solution, a convolutional transformer. Lastly, we use these signals to form an optimal trading policy, that maximizes risk-adjusted returns under constraints. Our comprehensive empirical study on daily US equities shows a high compensation for arbitrageurs to enforce the law of one price. Our arbitrage strategies obtain consistently high out-of-sample mean returns and Sharpe ratios, and substantially outperform all benchmark approaches. Keywords: statistical arbitrage, pairs trading, machine learning, deep learning, big data, stock returns, convolutional neural network, transformer, attention, factor model, market efficiency, investment. JEL classification: C14, C38, C55, G12 I Introduction Statistical arbitrage is one of the pillars of quantitative trading, and has long been used by hedge funds and investment banks. The term statistical arbitrage encompasses a wide variety of investment strategies, which identify and exploit temporal price differences between similar assets using statistical methods. Conceptually, they are based on relative trades between a stock and a mimicking portfolio. The mimicking portfolio is constructed to be “similar” to the target stock, usually based on historical co-movements in the price time-series or similar firm characteristics. When the spread between the prices of the two comparison assets widens, the arbitrageur sells the winner and buys the loser. If their prices move back together, the arbitrageur will profit. While Wall Street has developed a plethora of proprietary tools for sophisticated arbitrage trading, there is still a lack of understanding of how much arbitrage opportunity is actually left in financial markets. In this paper we answer the two key questions around statistical arbitrage: What are the important elements of a successful arbitrage strategy and how much realistic arbitrage is in financial markets? Every statistical arbitrage strategy needs to solve the following three fundamental problems: Given a large universe of assets, what are long-short portfolios of similar assets? Given these portfolios, what are time series signals that indicate the presence of temporary price deviations? Last, but not least, given these signals, how should an arbitrageur trade them to optimize a trading objective while taking into account possible constraints and market frictions? Each of these three questions poses substantial challenges, that prior work has only partly addressed. First, it is a hard problem to find long-short portfolios for all stocks as it is a priori unknown what constitutes “similarity”. This problem requires considering all the big data available for a large number of assets and times, including not just conventional return data but also exogenous information like asset characteristics. Second, extracting the right signals requires detecting flexibly all the relevant patterns in the noisy, complex, low-sample-size time series of the portfolio prices. Last but not least, optimal trading rules on a multitude of signals and assets are complicated and depend on the trading objective. All of these challenges fundamentally require flexible estimation tools that can deal with many variables. It is a natural idea to use machine learning techniques like deep neural networks to deal with the high dimensionality and complex functional dependencies of the problem. However, our problem is different from the usual prediction task, where machine learning tools excel. We show how to optimally design a machine learning solution to our problem that leverages the economic structure and objective. In this paper, we propose a unifying conceptual framework that generalizes common approaches to statistical arbitrage. Statistical arbitrage can be decomposed into three fundamental elements: (1) arbitrage portfolio generation, (2) arbitrage signal extraction and (3) the arbitrage allocation decision given the signal. By decomposing different methods into their arbitrage portfolio, signal and allocation element, we can compare different methods and study which components are the most relevant for successful trading. For each step we develop a novel machine learning implementation, which we compare with conventional methods. As a result, we construct a new deep learning statistical arbitrage approach. Our new approach constructs arbitrage portfolios with a conditional latent factor model, extracts the signals with the currently most successful machine learning time-series method and maps them into a trading allocation with a flexible neural network. These components are integrated and optimized over a global economic objective, which maximizes the risk-adjusted return under constraints. Empirically, our general model outperforms out-of-sample the leading benchmark approaches and provides a clear insight into the structure of statistical arbitrage. To construct arbitrage portfolios, we introduce the economically motivated asset pricing perspective to create them as residuals relative to asset pricing models. This perspective allows us to take advantage of the recent developments in asset pricing and to also include a large set of firm characteristics in the construction of the arbitrage portfolios. We use fundamental risk factors and conditional and unconditional statistical factors for our asset pricing models. Similarity between assets is captured by similar exposure to those factors. Hence, arbitrage portfolios are trades relative to mimicking stock portfolios, which are well-diversified portfolios with the same exposure to risk factors. In the case of statistical principal component factors, the mimicking portfolios correspond to assets with the highest correlation with the target stocks. Arbitrage Pricing Theory provides a justification for mean reversion patterns. It implies that, with an appropriate model, the corresponding factor portfolios represent the “fair price” of each of the assets. Therefore, the residual portfolios relative to the asset pricing factors capture the temporary deviations from the fair price of each of the assets and should only temporally deviate from their long-term mean. Importantly, the residuals are tradeable portfolios, which are only weakly cross-sectionally correlated, and close to orthogonal to firm characteristics and systematic factors. These properties allow us to extract a stationary time-series model for the signal. To detect time series patterns and signals in the residual portfolios, we introduce a filter perspective and estimate them with a flexible data-driven filter based on convolutional networks combined with transformers. In this way, we do not prescribe a potentially misspecified function to extract the time series structure, for example, by estimating the parameters of a given parametric time-series model, or the coefficients of a decomposition into given basis functions, as in conventional methods. Instead, we directly learn in a data-driven way what the optimal pattern extraction function is for our trading objective. The convolutional transformer is the ideal method for this purpose. Convolutional neural networks are the state-of-the-art AI method for pattern recognition, in particular in computer vision. In our case they identify the local patterns in the data and may be thought as a nonlinear and learnable generalization of conventional kernel-based data filters. Transformer networks are the most successful AI model for time series in natural language processing. In our model, they combine the local patterns to global time-series patterns. Their combination results in a data-driven flexible time-series filter that can essentially extract any complex time-series signal, while providing an interpretable model. To find the optimal trading allocation, we propose neural networks to map the arbitrage signals into a complex trading allocation. This generalizes conventional parametric rules, for example fixed rules based on thresholds, which are only valid under strong model assumptions and a small signal dimension. Importantly, these components are integrated and optimized over a global economic objective, which maximizes the risk-adjusted return under constraints. This allows our model to learn the optimal signals and allocation for the actual trading objective, which is different from a prediction objective. The trading objective can maximize the Sharpe ratio or expected return subject to a risk penalty, while taking into account constraints important to real investment managers, such as restricting turnover, leverage, or proportion of short trades. Our comprehensive empirical out-of-sample analysis is based on the daily returns of roughly the 550 largest and most liquid stocks in the U.S. from 1998 to 2016. We estimate the out-of-sample residuals on a rolling window relative to the empirically most important factor models. These are observed fundamental factors, for example the Fama-French 5 factors and price trend factors, locally estimated latent factors based on principal component analysis (PCA) or locally estimated conditional latent factors that include the information in 46 firm-specific characteristics and are based on the Instrumented PCA (IPCA) of Kelly et al. 2019 . We extract the trading signal with one of the most successful parametric models, based on the mean-reverting Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process, a frequency decomposition of the time-series with a Fourier transformation and our novel convolutional network with transformer. Finally, we compare the trading allocations based on parametric or nonparametric rules estimated with different risk-adjusted trading objectives. Our empirical main findings are five-fold. First, our deep learning statistical arbitrage model substantially outperforms all benchmark approaches out-of-sample. In fact, our model can achieve an impressive annual Sharpe ratio larger than four. While respecting short-selling constraints we can obtain annual out-of-sample mean returns of 20%. This performance is four times better than one of the best parametric arbitrage models, and twice as good as an alternative deep learning model without the convolutional transformer filter. These results are particularly impressive as we only trade the largest and most liquid stocks. Hence, our model establishes a new standard for arbitrage trading. Second, the performance of our deep learning model suggests that there is a substantial amount of short-term arbitrage in financial markets. The profitability of our strategies is orthogonal to market movements and conventional risk factors including momentum and reversal factors and does not constitute a risk-premium. Our strategy performs consistently well over the full time horizon. The model is extremely robust to the choice of tuning parameters, and the period when it is estimated. Importantly, our arbitrage strategy remains profitable in the presence of realistic transaction and holdings costs. Arbitrage signals are persistent over short time horizons. While arbitrageurs correct most mispricing over the horizon of a month, around half of the Sharpe ratio can persist for a holding period of one week. Assessing the amount of arbitrage in financial markets with unconditional pricing errors relative to factor models or with parametric statistical arbitrage models, severely underestimates the amount of statistical arbitrage. Third, the trading signal extraction is the most challenging and separating element among different arbitrage models. Surprisingly, the choice of asset pricing factors has only a minor effect on the overall performance. Residuals relative to the five Fama-French factors and five locally estimated principal component factors perform very well with out-of-sample Sharpe ratios above 3.2 for our deep learning model. Five conditional IPCA factors increase the out-of-sample Sharpe ratio to 4.2, which suggests that asset characteristics provide additional useful information. Increasing the number of risk factors beyond five has only a marginal effect. Similarly, the other benchmark models are robust to the choice of factor model as long as it contains sufficiently many factors. The distinguishing element is the time-series model to extract the arbitrage signal. The convolutional transformer doubles the performance relative to an identical deep learning model with a pre-specified frequency filter. Importantly, we highlight that time-series modeling requires a time-series machine learning approach, which takes temporal dependency into account. An off-the-shelf nonparametric machine learning method like conventional neural networks, that estimates an arbitrage allocation directly from residuals, performs substantially worse. Fourth, successful arbitrage trading is based on local asymmetric trend and reversion patterns. Our convolutional transformer framework provides an interpretable representation of the underlying patterns, based on local basic patterns and global “dependency factors”. The building blocks of arbitrage trading are smooth trend and reversion patterns. The arbitrage trading is short-term and the last 30 trading days seem to capture the relevant information. Interestingly, the direction of policies is asymmetric. The model reacts quickly on downturn movements, but more cautiously on uptrends. More specifically, the “dependency factors” which are the most active in downturn movements focus only on the most recent 10 days, while those for upward movements focus on the first 20 days in a 30-day window. Fifth, time-series-based trading patterns should be extracted from residuals and not directly from returns. For an appropriate factor model, the residuals are only weakly correlated and close to stationary in both, the time and cross-sectional dimension. Hence, it is meaningful to extract a uniform trading pattern, that is based only on the past time-series information, from the residuals. In contrast stock returns are dominated by a few factors, which severely limits the actual independent time-series information, and are strongly heterogenous due to their variation in firm characteristics. While the level of stock returns is extremely hard to predict, even with flexible machine learning methods, residuals capture relative movements and remove the level component. These properties make residuals analyzable from a purely time-series based perspective and, unlike the existing literature, they allow us to incorporate alternative data into the portfolio construction process. This also highlights a fundamental difference with most of the existing financial machine learning literature: We do not use characteristics to get features for prediction, but rather to obtain the data orthogonal to these features. Related Literature Our paper builds on the classical statistical arbitrage literature, in which the three main problems of portfolio generation, pattern extraction, and allocation decision have traditionally been considered independently. Classical statistical methods of generating arbitrage portfolios have mostly focused on obtaining multiple pairs or small portfolios of assets, using techniques like the distance method of Gatev et al. 2006 , the cointegration approach of Vidyamurthy 2004 , or copulas as in Rad et al. 2016 . In contrast, more general methods that exploit large panels of stock returns include the use of PCA factor models, as in Avellaneda and Lee 2010 and its extension in Yeo and Papanicolaou 2017 , and the maximization of mean-reversion and sparsity statistics as in d’Aspremont 2011 . We include the model of Yeo and Papanicolaou 2017 as the parametric benchmark model in our study as it has one of the best empirical performances among the class of parametric models. Our paper paper contributes to this literature by introducing a general asset pricing perspective to obtain the arbitrage portfolios as residuals. This allows us to take advantage of conditional asset pricing models, that include time-varying firm characteristics in addition to the return time-series, and provides a more disciplined, economically motivated approach. The signal extraction step for these models assumes parametric time series models for the arbitrage portfolios, whereas the allocations are often decided from the estimated parameters by using stochastic control methods or given threshold rules and one-period optimizations. Some representative papers of the first approach include Jurek and Yang 2007 , Mudchanatongsuk et al. 2008 , Cartea and Jaimungal 2016 , Lintilhac and Tourin 2016 and Leung and Li 2015 , whereas the second one is illustrated by Elliott et al. 2005 and Yeo and Papanicolaou 2017 . Both approaches are special cases of our more general framework. Mulvey et al. 2020 and Kim and Kim 2019 are examples of including machine learning elements within the parametric statistical arbitrage framework, by either solving a stochastic control problem with neural networks or estimating a time-varying threshold rule with reinforcement learning. Our paper is complementary to the emerging literature that uses machine learning methods for asset pricing. While the asset pricing literature aims to explain the risk premia of assets, our focus is on the residual component which is not explained by the asset pricing models. Chen et al. 2022 , Bryzgalova et al. 2019 and Kozak et al. 2020 estimate the stochastic discount factor (SDF), which explains the risk premia of assets, with deep neural networks, decision trees or elastic net regularization. These papers employ advanced statistical methods to solve a conditional method of moment problem in the presence of many variables. The workhorse models in equity asset pricing are based on linear factor models exemplified by Fama and French 1993 ; Fama and French 2015 . Recently, new methods have been developed to extract statistical asset pricing factors from large panels with various versions of principal component analysis (PCA). The Risk-Premium PCA in Lettau and Pelger 2020a ; Lettau and Pelger 2020b includes a pricing error penalty to detect weak factors that explain the cross-section of returns. The high-frequency PCA in Pelger 2020 uses high-frequency data to estimate local time-varying latent risk factors and the Instrumented PCA (IPCA) of Kelly et al. 2019 estimates conditional latent factors by allowing the loadings to be functions of time-varying asset characteristics. Gu et al. 2021 generalize IPCA to allow the loadings to be nonlinear functions of characteristics. Giglio and Xiu 2021 use PCA factors to account for missing priced factors in risk premia estimation. He et al. 2022 highlight the important insight that the time-series of residuals can be informative for trading. They test asset pricing models by studying the profitability of trading residuals based on high-minus-low sorting strategies of prior residual returns. Our paper is related to the growing literature on return prediction with machine learning methods, which has shown the benefits of regularized flexible methods. In their pioneering work Gu et al. 2020 conduct a comparison of machine learning methods for predicting the panel of individual U.S. stock returns based on the asset-specific characteristics and economic conditions in the previous period. Freyberger et al. 2020 use different methods for predicting stock returns. In a similar spirit, Bianchi et al. 2019 predict bond returns, Li and Rossi (2020) predict mutual fund returns, and Bali et al. 2022 predict option returns. A related stream extends cross-sectional machine learning prediction to higher moments and residuals, which are used for investment decisions. Li and Tang 2022 use machine learning to predict risk measures and estimate conditional volatilities. Kaniel et al. 2022 and DeMiguel et al. 2022 predict the skill of mutual fund managers based on residuals from benchmark asset pricing factors. The return prediction literature is fundamentally estimating the risk premia of assets, while our focus is on understanding and exploiting the temporal deviations thereof. This different goal is reflected in the different methods that are needed. These return predictions estimate a nonparametric cross-sectional model between current returns and large set of covariates from the last period, but do not estimate a time-series model. In contrast, the important challenge that we solve is to extract a complex time-series pattern. A related stream of the return prediction literature forecasts returns using past returns, generally followed by some long-short investment policy based on the prediction. For example, Krauss et al. 2017 use various machine learning methods for this type of prediction. 1 1 1 Similar studies include Fischer et al. 2019 , Chen et al. 2018 , Huck 2009 , and Dunis et al. 2006 . However, they use general nonparametric function estimates, which are not specifically designed for time-series data. Lim and Zohren 2020 show that it is important for machine learning solutions to explicitly account for temporal dependence when they are applied to time-series data. Murray et al. 2021 and Jiang et al. 2022 build on this insight and use machine learning to learn price trends for return forecasting. Forecasting returns and building a long-short portfolio based on the prediction is different from statistical arbitrage trading as it combines a risk premium and potential arbitrage component. It is not based on temporary price differences and also in general not orthogonal to common risk factors and market movements. In this paper we highlight the challenge of inferring complex time-series information and argue that using returns directly as an input to a time-series machine learning method, can be suboptimal as returns are dominated by a few factor time-series and are heterogeneous due to cross-sectionally and time-varying characteristics. In contrast, appropriate residuals are close to uncorrelated and locally cross-sectionally stationary. Hence, appropriate residuals allow the extraction of a complex time-series pattern. Naturally, our work overlaps with the literature on using machine learning tools for investment. The SDF estimated by asset pricing models, like in Chen et al. 2022 and Bryzgalova et al. 2019 , directly maps into a conditionally mean-variance efficient portfolio and hence an attractive investment opportunity. However, by construction this investment portfolio is not orthogonal but fully exposed to systematic risk, which is exactly the opposite for an arbitrage portfolio. Prediction approaches also imply investment strategies, typically long-short portfolios based on the prediction signal. However, estimating a signal with a prediction objective, is not necessarily providing an optimal signal for investment. Bryzgalova et al. 2019 and Chen et al. 2022 illustrate that machine learning models that use a trading objective can result in a substantially more profitable investment than models that estimate a signal with a prediction objective, while using the same information as input and having the same flexibility. This is also confirmed in Cong et al. 2020 , who use an investment objective and reinforcement learning to construct machine learning investment portfolios. Our paper contributes to this literature by estimating investment strategies, that are orthogonal to systematic risk and are based on a trading objective with constraints. Finally, our approach is also informed by the recent deep learning for time series literature. The transformer method was first introduced in the groundbreaking paper by Vaswani et al. 2017 . We are the first to bring this idea into the context of statistical arbitrage and adopt it to the economic problem. II Model The fundamental problem of statistical arbitrage consists of three elements: (1) The identification of similar assets to generate arbitrage portfolios, (2) the extraction of time-series signals for the temporary deviations of the similarity between assets and (3) a trading policy in the arbitrage portfolios based on the time-series signals. We discuss each element separately. A Arbitrage portfolios We consider a panel of excess returns R n , t R_{n,t} , that is the return minus risk free rate of stock n = 1 , … , N t n=1,...,N_{t} at time t = 1 , … , T t=1,...,T . The number of available assets at time t t can be time-varying. The excess return vector of all assets at time t t is denoted as R t = ( R 1 , t ⋯ R N t , t ) ⊤ R_{t}=\\begin{pmatrix}R_{1,t}&amp;\\cdots&amp;R_{N_{t},t}\\end{pmatrix}^{\\top} . We use a general asset pricing model to identify similar assets. In this context, similarity is defined as the same exposure to systematic risk, which implies that assets with the same risk exposure should have the same fundamental value. We assume that asset returns can be modeled by a conditional factor model: R n , t = β n , t − 1 ⊤ ​ F t + ϵ n , t . \\displaystyle R_{n,t}=\\beta_{n,t-1}^{\\top}F_{t}+\\epsilon_{n,t}. The K K factors F ∈ ℝ T × K F\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{T\\times K} capture the systematic risk, while the risk loadings β t − 1 ∈ ℝ N t × K \\beta_{t-1}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{N_{t}\\times K} can be general functions of the information set at time t − 1 t-1 and hence can be time-varying. This general formulation includes the empirically most successful factor models. In our empirical analysis we will include observed traded factors, e.g. the Fama-French 5 factor model, latent factors based on the principal components analysis (PCA) of stock returns and conditional latent factors estimated with Instrumented Principal Component Analysis (IPCA). Without loss of generality, we can treat the factors as excess returns of traded assets. Either the factors are traded, for example a market factor, in which case we include them in the returns R t R_{t} . Otherwise, we can generate factor mimicking portfolios by projecting them on the asset space, as for example with latent factors: F t = w t − 1 F ⊤ ​ R t . \\displaystyle F_{t}={w_{t-1}^{F}}^{\\top}R_{t}. We define arbitrage portfolios as residual portfolios ϵ n , t = R n , t − β n , t − 1 ⊤ ​ F t \\epsilon_{n,t}=R_{n,t}-\\beta_{n,t-1}^{\\top}F_{t} . As factors are traded assets, the arbitrage portfolios are themselves traded portfolios. Hence, the vector of residual portfolios equals ϵ t \\displaystyle\\epsilon_{t} = R t − β t − 1 ​ w t − 1 F ⊤ ​ R t = ( I N t − β t − 1 ​ w t − 1 F ⊤ ) ⏟ Φ t − 1 ​ R t = Φ t − 1 ​ R t . \\displaystyle=R_{t}-\\beta_{t-1}{w_{t-1}^{F}}^{\\top}R_{t}=\\underbrace{\\left(I_{N_{t}}-\\beta_{t-1}{w_{t-1}^{F}}^{\\top}\\right)}_{\\Phi_{t-1}}R_{t}=\\Phi_{t-1}R_{t}. (1) Arbitrage portfolios are trades relative to mimicking stock portfolios. The factor component in the residuals corresponds to a well-diversified mimicking portfolio, that is is ‘‘close’’ to the specific stock in the relative trade. More specifically, in the case of PCA factors, we construct well-diversified portfolios of stocks that have the highest correlation with each target stock in the relative trades. This is conceptually the same idea as using some form of clustering algorithm to construct a portfolio of highly correlated stocks. 2 2 2 In fact, under appropriate assumptions a clustering problem can be solved by a latent factor estimation. Hence, residuals of PCA factors construct relative trades based on correlations in past return time-series. In the case of IPCA factors, we intuitively construct for each stock a well-diversified mimicking portfolio that is as similar as possible in terms of the underlying firm characteristics. Hence, the mimicking portfolio represents an asset with very similar firm fundamentals. Lastly, for Fama-French factors, we construct mimicking portfolios with the same exposure to those fundamental factors. In all cases, the choice of factor model implies a notation of similarity. By construction the mimicking portfolio and the target stock have identical loadings to the selected factors. Arbitrage trading relative to mimicking portfolios takes advantage of all assets in the market. In practice and modern statistical arbitrage theory, it has replaced the more restrictive idea of pairs trading, which considers relative trades between only two stocks. On an intuitive level, the mimicking portfolio represents the reference asset in the relative trade. It can be constructed even if there does not exist an individual stock that is highly correlated with a target stock. Our arbitrage portfolios based on residuals also allow us to benefit from an asset pricing perspective. Arbitrage portfolios are projections on the return space that annihilate systematic asset risk. For an appropriate asset pricing model, the residual portfolios should not earn a risk premium. This is the fundamental assumption behind any arbitrage argument. As deviations from a mean of zero have to be temporary, arbitrage trading bets on the mean revision of the residuals. In particular, for an appropriate factor model the residuals are expected to have the following properties: First, the unconditional mean of the arbitrage portfolios is zero, 𝔼 ⁡ [ ϵ n , t ] = 0 \\mathbbm{E}[\\epsilon_{n,t}]=0 . Second, the arbitrage portfolios are only weakly cross-sectionally dependent. We confirm empirically that once we use sufficiently many statistical or fundamental factors the residuals are indeed close to uncorrelated and have an average mean close to zero. However, while the asset pricing perspective provides a theoretical argument for mean reversion in residuals, our approach is still valid even if we omit some relevant risk factors. The relative trades would then only exploit the temporal time-series deviations from the risk factors captured by the mimicking portfolios. We denote by ℱ t − 1 \\mathcal{F}_{t-1} the filtration generated by the returns R t − 1 R_{t-1} , which include the factors, and the information set that captures the risk exposure β t − 1 \\beta_{t-1} , which is typically based on asset specific characteristics or past returns. B Arbitrage signal The arbitrage signal is extracted from the time-series of the arbitrage portfolios. These time-series signals are the input for a trading policy. An example for an arbitrage signal would be a parametric model for mean reversion that is estimated for each arbitrage portfolio. The trading strategy for each arbitrage portfolio would depend on its speed of mean reversion and its deviation from the long run mean. More generally, the arbitrage signal is the estimation of a time-series model, which can be parametric or nonparametric. An important class of models are filtering approaches. Conceptually, time-series models are multivariate functional mappings between sequences, which take into account the temporal order of the elements and potentially complex dependencies between the elements of the input sequence. We apply the signal extraction to the time-series of the last L L lagged residuals, which we denote in vector notation as ϵ n , t − 1 L := ( ϵ n , t − L ⋯ ϵ n , t − 1 ) . \\displaystyle\\epsilon_{n,t-1}^{L}:=\\begin{pmatrix}\\epsilon_{n,t-L}&amp;\\cdots&amp;\\epsilon_{n,t-1}\\end{pmatrix}. The arbitrage signal function is a mapping 𝜽 ∈ 𝚯 \\bm{\\theta}\\in\\bm{\\Theta} from ℝ L \\mathbbm{R}^{L} to ℝ p \\mathbbm{R}^{p} , where 𝚯 \\bm{\\Theta} defines an appropriate function space: 𝜽 ⁡ ( ⋅ ) : ϵ n , t − 1 L → θ n , t − 1 . \\displaystyle\\bm{\\theta}(\\cdot):\\epsilon_{n,t-1}^{L}\\rightarrow\\theta_{n,t-1}. The signals θ n , t − 1 ∈ ℝ p \\theta_{n,t-1}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{p} for the arbitrage portfolio n n at time t t only depend on the time-series of lagged returns ϵ n , t − 1 L \\epsilon_{n,t-1}^{L} . Note that the dimensionality of the signal can be the same as for the input sequence. Formally, the function 𝜽 \\bm{\\theta} is a mapping from the filtration ℱ n , t − 1 ϵ , L \\mathcal{F}_{n,t-1}^{\\epsilon,L} generated by ϵ n , t − 1 L \\epsilon_{n,t-1}^{L} into the filtration ℱ n , t − 1 θ \\mathcal{F}_{n,t-1}^{\\theta} generated by θ n , t − 1 \\theta_{n,t-1} and ℱ n , t − 1 θ ⊆ ℱ n , t − 1 ϵ , L \\mathcal{F}_{n,t-1}^{\\theta}\\subseteq\\mathcal{F}_{n,t-1}^{\\epsilon,L} . We use the notation of evaluating functions elementwise, that is 𝜽 ⁡ ( ϵ t − 1 L ) = ( θ 1 , t − 1 ⋯ θ N t , t − 1 ) = θ t − 1 ∈ ℝ N t \\bm{\\theta}(\\epsilon_{t-1}^{L})=\\begin{pmatrix}\\theta_{1,t-1}&amp;\\cdots&amp;\\theta_{N_{t},t-1}\\end{pmatrix}=\\theta_{t-1}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{N_{t}} with ϵ t − 1 L = ( ϵ 1 , t − 1 ⋯ ϵ N t , t − 1 ) \\epsilon_{t-1}^{L}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\epsilon_{1,t-1}&amp;\\cdots&amp;\\epsilon_{N_{t},t-1}\\end{pmatrix} . The arbitrage signal θ n , t − 1 \\theta_{n,t-1} is a sufficient statistic for the trading policy; that is, all relevant information for trading decisions is summarized in it. This also implies that two arbitrage portfolios with the same signal get the same weight in the trading strategy. More formally, this means that the arbitrage signal defines equivalence classes for the arbitrage portfolios. The most relevant signals summarize reversal patterns and their direction with a small number of parameters. A potential trading policy could be to hold long positions in residuals with a predicted upward movement and go short in residuals that are in a downward cycle. This problem formulation makes two implicit assumptions. First, the residual time-series follow a stationary distribution conditioned on its lagged returns. This is a very general framework that includes the most important models for financial time-series. Second, the first L L lagged returns are a sufficient statistic to obtain the arbitrage signal θ n , t − 1 \\theta_{n,t-1} . This reflects the motivation that arbitrage is a temporary deviation of the fair price. The lookback window can be chosen to be arbitrarily large, but in practice it is limited by the availability of lagged returns. C Arbitrage trading The trading policy assigns an investment weight to each arbitrage portfolio based on its signal. The allocation weight is the solution to an optimization problem, which models a general risk-return tradeoff and can also include trading frictions and constraints. An important case are mean-variance efficient portfolios with transaction costs and short sale constraints. An arbitrage allocation is a mapping from ℝ p \\mathbbm{R}^{p} to ℝ \\mathbbm{R} in a function class 𝑾 \\bm{W} , that assigns a weight w n , t − 1 ϵ w^{\\epsilon}_{n,t-1} for the arbitrage portfolio ϵ n , t − 1 \\epsilon_{n,t-1} in the investment strategy using only the arbitrage signal θ n , t \\theta_{n,t} : 𝒘 ϵ : θ n , t − 1 → w n , t − 1 ϵ . \\displaystyle\\bm{w^{\\epsilon}}:\\theta_{n,t-1}\\rightarrow w^{\\epsilon}_{n,t-1}. Given a concave utility function U ⁡ ( ⋅ ) U(\\cdot) , the allocation function is the solution to max 𝒘 ϵ ∈ 𝑾 , 𝜽 ∈ 𝚯 ⁡ 𝔼 t − 1 ​ [ U ⁡ ( w t − 1 R ​ R t ) ] \\displaystyle\\max_{\\bm{w^{\\epsilon}}\\in\\bm{W},\\bm{\\theta}\\in\\bm{\\Theta}}\\mathbbm{E}_{t-1}\\left[U\\left(w^{R}_{t-1}R_{t}\\right)\\right] (2) s.t. w t − 1 R = w t − 1 ϵ ⊤ ​ Φ t − 1 ‖ w t − 1 ϵ ⊤ ​ Φ t − 1 ‖ 1 and w t − 1 ϵ = 𝒘 ϵ ​ ( 𝜽 ⁡ ( ϵ t − 1 L ) ) . \\displaystyle\\text{s.t. }\\qquad w^{R}_{t-1}=\\frac{{w^{\\epsilon}_{t-1}}^{\\top}\\Phi_{t-1}}{\\|{w^{\\epsilon}_{t-1}}^{\\top}\\Phi_{t-1}\\|_{1}}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad w^{\\epsilon}_{t-1}=\\bm{w^{\\epsilon}}(\\bm{\\theta}(\\epsilon_{t-1}^{L})). (3) In the presence of trading costs, we calculate the expected utility of the portfolio net return by subtracting from the portfolio return w t − 1 R ​ R t w^{R}_{t-1}R_{t} the trading costs that are associated with the stock allocation w t − 1 R w^{R}_{t-1} . The trading costs can capture the transaction costs from frequent rebalancing and the higher costs of short selling compared to long positions. The stock weights w t − 1 R w^{R}_{t-1} are normalized to add up to one in absolute value, which implicitly imposes a leverage constraint. The conditional expectation uses the general filtration ℱ t − 1 \\mathcal{F}_{t-1} . This is a combined optimization problem, which simultaneously solves for the optimal allocation function and arbitrage signal function. As the weight is a composition of the two functions, i.e. w t − 1 ϵ = 𝒘 ϵ ​ ( 𝜽 ⁡ ( ϵ t − 1 L ) ) w^{\\epsilon}_{t-1}=\\bm{w^{\\epsilon}}(\\bm{\\theta}(\\epsilon_{t-1}^{L})) , the decomposition into a signal and allocation function is in general not uniquely identified. This means there can be multiple representations of 𝜽 \\bm{\\theta} and 𝒘 ϵ \\bm{w^{\\epsilon}} , that will result in the same trading policy. We use a decomposition that allows us to compare the problem to classical arbitrage approaches, for which this separation is uniquely identified. The key feature of the signal function 𝜽 \\bm{\\theta} is that it models a time-series, that means it is a mapping that explicitly models the temporal order and the dependency between the elements of ϵ t − 1 L \\epsilon_{t-1}^{L} . The allocation function 𝒘 ϵ \\bm{w^{\\epsilon}} can be a complex nonlinear function, but does not explicitly model time-series behavior. This means that 𝒘 ϵ \\bm{w^{\\epsilon}} is implicitly limited in the dependency patterns of its input elements that it can capture. We will consider arbitrage trading that maximizes the Sharpe ratio or achieves the highest average return for a given level of variance risk. More specifically we will solve for max 𝒘 ϵ ∈ 𝑾 , 𝜽 ∈ 𝚯 ⁡ 𝔼 ⁡ [ w t − 1 R ⊤ ​ R t ] Var ​ ( w t − 1 R ⊤ ​ R t ) or max 𝒘 ϵ ∈ 𝑾 , 𝜽 ∈ 𝚯 ⁡ 𝔼 ⁡ [ w t − 1 R ⊤ ​ R t ] − γ ​ Var ​ ( w t − 1 R ⊤ ​ R t ) \\displaystyle\\max_{\\bm{w^{\\epsilon}}\\in\\bm{W},\\bm{\\theta}\\in\\bm{\\Theta}}\\frac{\\mathbbm{E}\\left[{w_{t-1}^{R}}^{\\top}R_{t}\\right]}{\\sqrt{\\text{Var}({w_{t-1}^{R}}^{\\top}R_{t})}}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\max_{\\bm{w^{\\epsilon}}\\in\\bm{W},\\bm{\\theta}\\in\\bm{\\Theta}}\\mathbbm{E}[{w_{t-1}^{R}}^{\\top}R_{t}]-\\gamma\\text{Var}({w_{t-1}^{R}}^{\\top}R_{t}) (4) s.t. w t − 1 R = w t − 1 ϵ ⊤ ​ Φ t − 1 ‖ w t − 1 ϵ ⊤ ​ Φ t − 1 ‖ 1 and w t − 1 ϵ = 𝒘 ϵ ​ ( 𝜽 ⁡ ( ϵ t − 1 L ) ) . \\displaystyle\\text{s.t. }\\qquad w^{R}_{t-1}=\\frac{{w^{\\epsilon}_{t-1}}^{\\top}\\Phi_{t-1}}{\\|{w^{\\epsilon}_{t-1}}^{\\top}\\Phi_{t-1}\\|_{1}}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\qquad w^{\\epsilon}_{t-1}=\\bm{w^{\\epsilon}}(\\bm{\\theta}(\\epsilon_{t-1}^{L})). (5) for some risk aversion parameter γ \\gamma . We will consider this formulation with and without trading costs. 3 3 3 While the conventional mean-variance optimization applies a penalty to the variance, we found that it is numerically beneficial to use the standard deviation instead. Both formulations describe the same fundamental trade-off. Many relevant models estimate the signal and allocation function separately. The arbitrage signals can be estimated as the parameters of a parametric time-series model, the serial moments for a given stationary distribution or a time-series filter. In these cases, the signal estimation solves a separate optimization problem as part of the estimation. Given the signals, the allocation function is the solution of max 𝒘 ϵ ∈ 𝑾 ⁡ 𝔼 t − 1 ​ [ U ⁡ ( w t − 1 R ​ R t ) ] s.t. w t − 1 R = w ϵ ​ ( θ t − 1 ) ⊤ ​ Φ t − 1 ‖ w ϵ ​ ( θ t − 1 ) ⊤ ​ Φ t − 1 ‖ 1 . \\displaystyle\\max_{\\bm{w^{\\epsilon}}\\in\\bm{W}}\\mathbbm{E}_{t-1}\\left[U\\left(w^{R}_{t-1}R_{t}\\right)\\right]\\qquad\\text{s.t.}\\qquad w^{R}_{t-1}=\\frac{w^{\\epsilon}(\\theta_{t-1})^{\\top}\\Phi_{t-1}}{\\|w^{\\epsilon}(\\theta_{t-1})^{\\top}\\Phi_{t-1}\\|_{1}}. (6) We provide an extensive study of the importance of the different elements in statistical arbitrage trading. We find that the most important driver for profitable portfolios is the arbitrage signal function; that is, a good model to extract time-series behavior and to time the predictable mean reversion patterns is essential. The arbitrage portfolios of asset pricing models that are sufficiently rich result in roughly the same performance. Once an informative signal is extracted, parametric and nonparametric allocation functions can take advantage of it. We find that the key element is to consider a sufficiently general class of functions 𝚯 \\bm{\\Theta} for the arbitrage signal and to estimate the signal that is the most relevant for trading. In other words, the largest gains in statistical arbitrage come from flexible time-series signals 𝜽 \\bm{\\theta} and a joint optimization problem. D Models for Arbitrage Signal and Allocation Functions In this section we introduce different functional models for the signal and allocation functions. They range from the most restrictive assumptions for simple parametric models to the most flexible model, which is our sophisticated deep neural network architecture. The general problem is the estimation of a signal and allocation function given the residual time-series. Here, we take the residual returns as given, i.e. we have selected an asset pricing model. In order to illustrate the key elements of the allocation functions, we consider trading the residuals directly. Projecting the residuals back into the original return space is identical for the different methods and discussed in the empirical part. The conceptual steps are illustrated in Figure 1 . Figure 1 : Conceptual Arbitrage Model This figure illustrates the conceptual structure of our statistical arbitrage framework. The model takes as input the last L L cumulative returns of a residual portfolio on a lookback window at a given time and outputs the predicted optimal allocation weight for that residual for the next time. The model is composed of a signal extraction function and an allocation function. The input to the signal extraction functions are the last L L cumulative residuals. We simplify the notation by dropping the time index t − 1 t-1 and the asset index n n and define the generic input vector x := Int ​ ( ϵ n , t − 1 L ) = ( ϵ n , t − L ∑ l = 1 2 ϵ n , t − L − 1 + l ⋯ ∑ l = 1 L ϵ n , t − L − 1 + l ) . \\displaystyle x:=\\text{\\bf Int}\\left(\\epsilon_{n,t-1}^{L}\\right)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\epsilon_{n,t-L}&amp;\\sum_{l=1}^{2}\\epsilon_{n,t-L-1+l}&amp;\\cdots&amp;\\sum_{l=1}^{L}\\epsilon_{n,t-L-1+l}\\end{pmatrix}. Here the operation Int ​ ( ⋅ ) \\text{\\bf Int}(\\cdot) simply integrates a discrete time-series. We can view the cumulative residuals as the residual “price” process. We discuss three different classes of models for the signal function 𝜽 \\bm{\\theta} that vary in the degree of flexibility of the type of patterns that they can capture. Similarly, we consider different classes of models for the allocation function 𝒘 ϵ \\bm{w^{\\epsilon}} . D.1 Parametric Models Our first benchmark method is a parametric model and corresponds to classical mean-reversion trading. In this framework, the cumulative residuals x x are assumed to be the discrete realizations of continuous time model: x = ( X 1 ⋯ X L ) . \\displaystyle x=\\begin{pmatrix}X_{1}&amp;\\cdots&amp;X_{L}\\end{pmatrix}. Following the influential papers by Avellaneda and Lee 2010 and Yeo and Papanicolaou 2017 we model X t X_{t} as an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process d ​ X t = κ ⁡ ( μ − X t ) ​ d ​ t + σ ​ d ​ B t \\displaystyle dX_{t}=\\kappa\\left(\\mu-X_{t}\\right)dt+\\sigma dB_{t} for a Brownian motion B t B_{t} . These are the standard models for mean-reversion trading and Avellaneda and Lee 2010 among others have shown their good empirical performance. The parameters of this model are estimated from the moments of the discretized time-series, as described in detail along with the other implementation details in Appendix B.B . The parameters for each residual process, the last cumulative sum and a goodness of fit measure form the signals for the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck model: θ OU = ( κ ^ μ ^ σ ^ X L R 2 ) . \\displaystyle\\theta^{\\text{OU}}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\hat{\\kappa}&amp;\\hat{\\mu}&amp;\\hat{\\sigma}&amp;X_{L}&amp;R^{2}\\end{pmatrix}. Following Yeo and Papanicolaou 2017 we also include the goodness of fit parameter R 2 R^{2} as part of the signal. R 2 R^{2} has the conventional definition of the ratio of squared values explained by the model normalized by total squared values. If the R 2 R^{2} value is too low, the predictions of the model seem to be unreliable, which can be taken into account in a trading policy. Hence, for each cumulative residual vector ϵ n , t − 1 L \\epsilon_{n,t-1}^{L} we obtain the signal θ n , t − 1 OU = ( κ ^ n , t − 1 μ ^ n , t − 1 σ ^ n , t − 1 ∑ l = 1 L ϵ n , t − 1 + l R n , t − 1 2 ) . \\displaystyle\\theta^{\\text{OU}}_{n,t-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\hat{\\kappa}_{n,t-1}&amp;\\hat{\\mu}_{n,t-1}&amp;\\hat{\\sigma}_{n,t-1}&amp;\\sum_{l=1}^{L}\\epsilon_{n,t-1+l}&amp;R_{n,t-1}^{2}\\end{pmatrix}. Avellaneda and Lee 2010 and Yeo and Papanicolaou 2017 advocate a classical mean-reversion thresholding rule, which implies the following allocation function 4 4 4 The allocation function is derived by maximizing an expected trading profit. This deviates slightly from our objective of either maximizing the Sharpe ratio or the expected return subject to a variance penalty. As this is the most common arbitrage trading rule, we include it as a natural benchmark. : 𝒘 ϵ | OU ​ ( θ OU ) = { − 1 if ​ X L − μ σ / 2 ​ κ &gt; c thresh ​ and R 2 &gt; c crit 1 if ​ X L − μ σ / 2 ​ κ &lt; − c thresh ​ and R 2 &gt; c crit 0 otherwise \\displaystyle{\\bm{w}}^{\\epsilon|\\text{OU}}\\left(\\theta^{\\text{OU}}\\right)=\\left\\{\\begin{array}[]{ll}-1&amp;\\text{if }\\frac{X_{L}-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{2\\kappa}}&gt;c_{\\text{thresh}}\\text{ and $R^{2}&gt;c_{\\text{crit}}$}\\\\ 1&amp;\\text{if }\\frac{X_{L}-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{2\\kappa}}&lt;-c_{\\text{thresh}}\\text{ and $R^{2}&gt;c_{\\text{crit}}$}\\\\ 0&amp;\\text{otherwise }\\end{array}\\right. The threshold parameters c thresh c_{\\text{thresh}} and c crit c_{\\text{crit}} are tuning parameters. The strategy suggests to buy or sell residuals based on the ratio X L − μ σ / 2 ​ κ \\frac{X_{L}-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{2\\kappa}} . If this ratio exceeds a threshold, it is likely that the process reverts back to its long term mean, which starts the trading. If the R 2 R^{2} value is too low, the predictions of the model seem to be unreliable, which stops the trading. This will be our parametric benchmark model. It has a parametric model for both the signal and allocation function. Figure A.2 in the Appendix illustrates this model with an empirical example. In this figure we show the allocation weights and signals of the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck with threshold model as well as the more flexible models that we are going to discuss next. The models are estimated on the empirical data, and the residual is a representative empirical example. In more detail, we consider the residuals from five IPCA factors and estimate the benchmark models as explained in the empirical Section III.N . The left subplots display the cumulative residual process along with the out-of-sample allocation weights w l ϵ w_{l}^{\\epsilon} that each model assigns to this specific residual. The evaluation of this illustrative example is a simplification of the general model that we use in our empirical main analysis. In this example, we consider trading only this specific residual and hence normalize the weights to { − 1 , 0 , 1 } \\{-1,0,1\\} . In our empirical analysis we trade all residuals and map them back into the original stock returns. The middle column shows the time-series of estimated out-of-sample signals for each model, by applying the θ l \\theta_{l} arbitrage signal function to the previous L = 30 L=30 cumulative returns of the residual. The right column displays the out-of-sample cumulative returns of trading this particular residual based on the corresponding allocation weights. The last row in Figure A.2 shows the results for the OU+Threshold model. The cumulative return of trading this residual is negative, suggesting that the parametric model fails. The residual time-series with the corresponding allocation weights in subplot (g) explain why. The trading allocation does not assign a positive weight during the uptrend and wrongly assigns a constant negative weight, when the residual price process follows a mean-reversion pattern with positive and negative returns. A parametric model can break down if it is misspecified. This is not only the case for trend patterns, but also if there are multiple mean reversion patterns of different frequencies. Subplot (h) shows the signal. 5 5 5 For better readability we have scaled the parameters of the OU process by a factor of five, but this still represents the same model as the scaling cancels out in the allocation function. As a minor modification, we use the ratio σ / 2 ​ κ \\sigma/\\sqrt{2\\kappa} as a signal instead of two individual parameters, as the conventional regression estimator of the OU process directly provides the ratio, but requires additional moments for the individual parameters. However, this results in an equivalent presentation of the model as only the ratio enters the allocation function. We see that changes in the allocation function are related to sharp changes in at least one of the signals, but overall, the signal does not seem to represent the complex price patterns of the residual. A natural generalization is to allow for a more flexible allocation function given the same time-series signals. We will consider for all our models also a general feedforward neural network (FFN) to map the signal into an allocation weight. FFNs are nonparametric estimators that can capture very general functional relationships. 6 6 6 Appendix B.A provides the details for estimating a FFN as a functional mapping 𝒈 FFN : ℝ p → ℝ \\bm{g^{\\text{FFN}}}:\\mathbbm{R}^{p}\\rightarrow\\mathbbm{R} . Hence, we also consider the additional model that restricts the signal function, but allows for a flexible allocation function: 𝒘 ϵ | OU-FFN ​ ( θ OU ) = 𝒈 FFN ​ ( θ OU ) . \\displaystyle{\\bm{w}}^{\\epsilon|\\text{OU-FFN}}\\left(\\theta^{\\text{OU}}\\right)=\\bm{g^{\\text{FFN}}}\\left(\\theta^{\\text{OU}}\\right). We will show empirically that the gains of a flexible allocation function are minor relative to the very simple parametric model. D.2 Pre-Specified Filters As a generalization of the restrictive parametric model of the last subsection, we consider more general time-series models. Many relevant time-series models can be formulated as filtering problems. Filters are transformations of time-series that provide an alternative representation of the original time-series which emphasizes certain dynamic patterns. A time-invariant linear filter can be formulated as θ l = ∑ j = 1 L W j filter ​ x j , \\displaystyle\\theta_{l}=\\sum_{j=1}^{L}W_{j}^{\\text{filter}}x_{j}, which is a linear mapping from ℝ L \\mathbbm{R}^{L} into ℝ L \\mathbbm{R}^{L} with the matrix W filter ∈ ℝ L × L W^{\\text{filter}}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L\\times L} . The estimation of causal ARMA processes is an example for such filters. A spectral decomposition based on a frequency filter is the most relevant filter for our problem of finding mean reversion patterns. A Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) provides a frequency decomposition of the original time-series and separates the movements into mean reverting processes of different frequencies. FFT applies the filter W j FFT = e 2 ​ π ​ i L ​ j W_{j}^{\\text{FFT}}=e^{\\frac{2\\pi i}{L}j} in the complex plane, but for real-valued time-series it is equivalent to fitting the following model: x l = a 0 + ∑ j = 1 L / 2 − 1 ( a j ⋅ cos ​ ( 2 ​ π ​ j L ​ l ) + b j ⋅ sin ​ ( 2 ​ π ​ j L ​ l ) ) + a L / 2 ​ cos ​ ( π ​ l ) . \\displaystyle x_{l}=a_{0}+\\sum_{j=1}^{L/2-1}\\left(a_{j}\\cdot\\text{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi j}{L}l\\right)+b_{j}\\cdot\\text{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi j}{L}l\\right)\\right)+a_{L/2}\\text{cos}\\left(\\pi l\\right). The FFT representation is given by coefficients of the trigonometric representation θ FFT = ( a 0 ⋯ a L / 2 b 1 ⋯ b L / 2 − 1 ) ∈ ℝ L . \\displaystyle\\theta^{\\text{FFT}}=\\begin{pmatrix}a_{0}&amp;\\cdots&amp;a_{L/2}&amp;b_{1}&amp;\\cdots&amp;b_{L/2-1}\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L}. The coefficients a l a_{l} and b l b_{l} can be interpreted as “loadings” or exposure to long or short-term reversal patterns. Note that the FFT is an invertible transformation. Hence, it simply represents the original time-series in a different form without losing any information. It is based on the insight that not the magnitude of the original data but the relative relationship in a time-series matters. We use a flexible feedforward neural network for the allocation function 𝒘 ϵ | FFT ​ ( θ FFT ) = 𝒈 FFN ​ ( θ FFT ) . \\displaystyle{\\bm{w}}^{\\epsilon|\\text{FFT}}\\left(\\theta^{\\text{FFT}}\\right)=\\bm{g^{\\text{FFN}}}\\left(\\theta^{\\text{FFT}}\\right). The usual intuition behind filtering is to use the frequency representation to cut off frequencies that have low coefficients and therefore remove noise in the representation. The FFN is essentially implementing this filtering step of removing less important frequencies. We illustrate the model within our running example in Figure A.2 . The middle row shows the results for the FFT+FFN model. The cumulative residual in subplot (d) seems to be a combination of low and high-frequency movements with an initial trend component. The signal in subplot (e) suggests that the FFT filter seems to capture the low frequency reversal pattern. However, it misses the high-frequency components as indicated by the simplistic allocation function. The trading strategy takes a long position for the first half and a short position for the second part. While this simple allocation results in a positive cumulative return, in this example it neglects the more complex local reversal patterns. While the FFT framework is an improvement over the simple OU model as it can deal with multiple combined mean-reversion patterns of different frequencies, it fails if the data follows a pattern that cannot be well approximated by a small number of the pre-specified basis functions. For completeness, our empirical analysis will also report the case of a trivial filter, which simply takes the residuals as signals, and combines them with a general allocation function: 𝜽 ident ​ ( x ) \\displaystyle\\bm{\\theta}^{\\text{ident}}(x) = x = θ ident \\displaystyle=x=\\theta^{\\text{ident}} 𝒘 ϵ | FFN ​ ( θ ident ) \\displaystyle{\\bm{w}}^{\\epsilon|\\text{FFN}}\\left(\\theta^{\\text{ident}}\\right) = 𝒈 FFN ​ ( x ) . \\displaystyle=\\bm{g^{\\text{FFN}}}\\left(x\\right). This is a good example to emphasize the importance of a time-series model. While FFNs are flexible in learning low dimensional functional relationships, they are limited in learning a complex dependency model. For example, the FFN architecture we consider is not sufficiently flexible to learn the FFT transformation and hence has a worse performance on the original time-series compared to frequency-transformed time-series. While Xiaohong Chen and White 1999 have shown that FFNs are “universal approximators” of low-dimensional functional relationships, they also show that FFN can suffer from a curse of dimensionality when capturing complex dependencies between the input. Although the time domain and frequency domain representations of the input are equivalent under the Fourier transform, clearly the time-series model implied by the frequency domain representation allows for a more effective learning of an arbitrage policy. However, the choice of the pre-specified filter limits the time-series patterns that can be exploited. The solution is our data driven filter presented in the next section. D.3 Convolutional Neural Network with Transformer Our benchmark machine learning model is a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) combined with a Transformer. It uses the most advanced state-of-the art machine learning tools tailored to our problem. Convolutional networks are in fact the most successful networks for computer vision, i.e. for pattern detection. Transformers have rapidly become the model of choice for sequence modeling such as Natural Language Processing (NLP) problems, replacing older recurrent neural network models such as the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network. The CNN and transformer framework has two key elements: (1) Local filters and (2) the temporal combination of these local filters. The CNN can be interpreted as a set of data driven flexible local filters. A transformer can be viewed as a data driven flexible time-series model to capture complex dependencies between local patterns. We use the CNN+Transformer to generate the time-series signal. The allocation function is then modeled as a flexible data driven allocation with an FFN. The CNN estimates D D local filters of size D size D_{\\text{size}} : y l ( 0 ) = ∑ m = 1 D size W m ( 0 ) ​ x l − m + 1 \\displaystyle y_{l}^{(0)}=\\sum_{m=1}^{D_{\\text{size}}}W_{m}^{(0)}x_{l-m+1} for a matrix W ( 0 ) ∈ ℝ D size × D W^{(0)}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{D_{\\text{size}}\\times D} . The local filters are a mapping from x ∈ ℝ L x\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L} to y ( 0 ) ∈ ℝ L × D y^{(0)}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L\\times D} given by the convolution y ( 0 ) = W ( 0 ) ∗ x y^{(0)}=W^{(0)}*x . Figure 2 shows examples of these local filters for D size = 3 D_{\\text{size}}=3 . The values of y ( 0 ) y^{(0)} can be interpreted as the “loadings” or exposure to local basis patterns. For example, if x x represents a global upward trend, its filtered representation should have mainly large values for the local upward trend filter. Figure 2 : Examples of Local Filters (a) Upward trend (b) Downward trend (c) Up reversal (d) Down reversal These figures show the most important local filters estimated for the benchmark model in our empirical analysis. These are projections of our higher dimensional nonlinear filter from a 2-layer CNN into two-dimensional linear filters. The convolutional mapping can be repeated in multiple layers to obtain a multi-layer CNN. First, the output of the first layer of the CNN is transformed nonlinearly by applying the ReLU ​ ( ⋅ ) \\text{ReLU}(\\cdot) function: x l , d ( 1 ) = ReLU ​ ( y l , d ( 0 ) ) := max ⁡ ( y l , d ( 0 ) , 0 ) . \\displaystyle x_{l,d}^{(1)}=\\text{ReLU}\\left(y_{l,d}^{(0)}\\right):=\\max(y_{l,d}^{(0)},0). The second layer is given by a higher dimensional filtering projection: y l , d ( 2 ) \\displaystyle y_{l,d}^{(2)} = ∑ m = 1 D size ∑ j = 1 D W d , j , m ( 1 ) ​ x l − m + 1 , j ( 1 ) , \\displaystyle=\\sum_{m=1}^{D_{\\text{size}}}\\sum_{j=1}^{D}W_{d,j,m}^{(1)}x_{l-m+1,j}^{(1)}, x l , d ( 2 ) \\displaystyle x_{l,d}^{(2)} = ReLU ​ ( y l , d ( 1 ) ) . \\displaystyle=\\text{ReLU}\\left(y_{l,d}^{(1)}\\right). The final output of the CNN is x ~ ∈ ℝ L × D \\tilde{x}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L\\times D} . Our benchmark model is a 2-layered convolutional neural network. The number of layers is a hyperparameter selected on the validation data. Figure 3 illustrates the structure of the 2-layer CNN. While this description captures all the conceptual elements, the actual implementation includes additional details, such as bias terms, instance normalization and residual connection to improve the implementation as explained in Appendix B.C . For a 1-layer CNN without the final nonlinear transformation, i.e. for a simple local linear filter, the patterns can be visualized by the vectors W m ( 0 ) W_{m}^{(0)} . In our case of a 2-layer CNN the local filter can capture more complex patterns as it applies a 3-dimensional weighting scheme in the array W ( 1 ) W^{(1)} and nonlinear transformations. In order to visualize the type of patterns, we project the local filter into a simple local linear filter. We want to find the basic patterns that activate only one of the D D filters, but none of the others, i.e. we are looking for an orthogonal representation of the projection. 7 7 7 A local filter can be formalized as a mapping from the local D size D_{\\text{size}} points of a sequence to the activation of the D D filters: ϕ : ℝ D size → ℝ D \\phi:\\mathbbm{R}^{D_{\\text{size}}}\\rightarrow\\mathbbm{R}^{D} . Denote by e d ∈ ℝ D e_{d}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{D} a vector that is 0 everywhere except for the value 1 at position d d , i.e. e d = ( 0 ⋯ 1 0 ⋯ 0 ) e_{d}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;\\cdots&amp;1&amp;0&amp;\\cdots 0\\end{pmatrix} . Fundamentally, we want to invert the local filter to obtain ϕ − 1 ​ ( e d ) \\phi^{-1}(e_{d}) to find the local sequences that only activates filter d d . In general, the inverse is a set and not unique. Our example basic patterns in Figure 2 solve argmin x loc , d ∈ ℝ D size ​ ‖ ϕ ⁡ ( x loc , d ) − e d ‖ 2 for d = 1 , … , D . \\displaystyle\\text{argmin}_{x_{\\text{loc},d}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{D_{\\text{size}}}}\\|\\phi(x_{\\text{loc},d})-e_{d}\\|_{2}\\qquad\\text{for $d=1,...,D$.} The example plots for local filters in Figure 2 are projections of our higher dimensional nonlinear filter into two-dimensional linear filters. The examples show some of the most important local filters for our empirical benchmark model. While these projections are of course not complete representations of the nonlinear filters of the CNN, they provide an intuition for the type of patterns which are activated by specific filters. Our 2-layer CNN network has a local window size of D size = 2 D_{\\text{size}}=2 , but because of the 2-layer structure it captures information from two neighboring points. Hence, the projection on a one-dimensional linear filter has a local window size of three as depicted in Figure 2 . Figure 3 : Convolutional Network Architecture This figure shows the structure of our convolutional network. The network takes as input a window of L L consecutive daily cumulative returns a residual, and outputs D D features for each block of D size D_{\\text{size}} days. Each of the features is a nonlinear function of the observations in the block, and captures a common pattern. The output of the CNN x ~ ∈ ℝ L × D \\tilde{x}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L\\times D} is used as an input to the transformer. The CNN projection provides a more informative representation of the dynamics than the original time-series as it captures the relative local dependencies between data points. However, by construction the CNN is only a local representation, and we need the transformer network to detect the global patterns. A transformer network is a model of temporal dependencies between local filters. Given the local structure x ~ \\tilde{x} the transformer estimates the temporal interactions between the L L different blocks by computing a “global pattern projection”: Assume there are H H different global patterns. The transformer will calculate projections on these H H patterns with the “attention weights”. We first introduce a simplified linear projection model before extending it to the actual transformer. For each of the i = 1 , … , H i=1,...,H patterns we have projections defined by α i ∈ ℝ L × L \\alpha_{i}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L\\times L} : h i simple = ∑ j = 1 L α i , j ​ x ~ j for i = 1 , . . , H . \\displaystyle h^{\\text{simple}}_{i}=\\sum_{j=1}^{L}\\alpha_{i,j}\\tilde{x}_{j}\\qquad\\text{ for $i=1,..,H$}. The “attention function” 𝜶 i ( . , . ) ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] {\\bm{\\alpha}_{i}}(.,.)\\in[0,1] captures dependencies between the last local patterns x ~ L \\tilde{x}_{L} and the prior local patterns x ~ j \\tilde{x}_{j} : α i , j = 𝜶 i ​ ( x ~ L , x ~ j ) for i = 1 , … , H . \\displaystyle\\alpha_{i,j}={\\bm{\\alpha}_{i}}\\left(\\tilde{x}_{L},\\tilde{x}_{j}\\right)\\qquad\\text{for $i=1,...,H$}. Each projection h i simple h^{\\text{simple}}_{i} is called an “attention head”. These attention heads h i simple h^{\\text{simple}}_{i} could be interpreted as “loadings” or “exposure” for a specific “pattern factor” α i \\alpha_{i} . For example, a global upward trend can be captured by an attention function that puts weight on subsequent local upward trends. Another example would be sinusoidal mean reversion patterns which would put weights on alternating “curved” local basis patterns. The projection on these weights captures how much a specific time-series x ~ \\tilde{x} is exposed to this global pattern. Hence, h i simple h^{\\text{simple}}_{i} measures the exposure to the global pattern i i of the time-series x ~ \\tilde{x} . Each attention head can focus on a specific global pattern, which we then combine to obtain our signal. The fundamental challenge is to learn attention functions that can model complex dependencies. The crucial innovation in transformers is their modeling of the attention functions 𝜶 i {\\bm{\\alpha}_{i}} and attention heads h i h_{i} . In order to deal with the high dimensionality of the problem, transformers consider lower dimensional projections of x ~ \\tilde{x} into ℝ D / H \\mathbbm{R}^{D/H} and use the lower-dimensional scaled dot product attention mechanism for 𝜶 i {\\bm{\\alpha}_{i}} as explained in Appendix B.C . More specifically, each attention head h i ∈ ℝ L × D / H h_{i}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L\\times D/H} is based on 8 8 8 The actual implementation also includes bias terms which we neglect here for simplicity. The Appendix provides the implementation details. the projected input x ~ ​ W i V \\tilde{x}W_{i}^{V} with W i V ∈ ℝ D × D / H W_{i}^{V}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{D\\times D/H} and α i ∈ ℝ L × L \\alpha_{i}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L\\times L} : h i = α i ​ x ~ ​ W i V for i = 1 , . . , H . \\displaystyle h_{i}=\\alpha_{i}{\\tilde{x}}W_{i}^{V}\\qquad\\text{ for $i=1,..,H$}. The projection on all global basis patterns h ∈ ℝ L × D / H h\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L\\times D/H} is given by a weighted linear combination of the different attention heads h proj = ( h 1 ⋯ h H ) ​ W O \\displaystyle h^{\\text{proj}}=\\begin{pmatrix}h_{1}&amp;\\cdots&amp;h_{H}\\end{pmatrix}W^{O} with W O ∈ ℝ D × D W^{O}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{D\\times D} . This final projection can, for example, model a combination of a global trend and mean reversion patterns. In conclusion, h proj h^{\\text{proj}} represents the time-series in terms of the H H global patterns. This is analogous to a Fourier filter, but without pre-specifying the global patterns a priori. All parts of the CNN+Transformer network, i.e. the local patterns, the attention functions and the projections on global patterns, are estimated from the data. Figure 4 : Transformer Network Architecture This figure shows the structure of our transformer network. The model takes as input the matrix x ~ ∈ ℝ L × D \\tilde{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times D} that we obtain as output of the convolutional network depicted in Figure 3 , which contains D D features for each of the L L blocks of the original time series. These features are projected onto H H attention heads, which capture the global temporal dependency patterns. The projections on these attention heads represent our arbitrage signals, which are the input to the feedforward network that estimates the allocation weight for the residual on the next day. The trading signal θ CNN+Trans \\theta^{\\text{CNN+Trans}} equals the global pattern projection for the final cumulative return projection 9 9 9 In principle, we can use the complete matrix h ∈ ℝ L × D h\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{L\\times D} as the signal. However, conceptually the global pattern at the end of the time period should be the most relevant for the next realization of the process. We have also implemented a transformer that uses the full matrix, with similar results and the variable importance rankings suggest that only h L h_{L} is selected in the allocation function. h L proj h^{\\text{proj}}_{L} : θ CNN+Trans = h L proj ∈ ℝ H , \\displaystyle\\theta^{\\text{CNN+Trans}}=h^{\\text{proj}}_{L}\\in\\mathbbm{R}^{H}, which is then used as input to a time-wise feedforward network allocation function 𝒘 ϵ | CNN+Trans ​ ( θ CNN+Trans ) = 𝒈 FFN ​ ( θ CNN+Trans ) . \\displaystyle{\\bm{w}}^{\\epsilon|\\text{CNN+Trans}}\\left(\\theta^{\\text{CNN+Trans}}\\right)=\\bm{g^{\\text{FFN}}}\\left(\\theta^{\\text{CNN+Trans}}\\right). The separation between signal and allocation is not uniquely identified as we use a joint optimization problem. We have chosen a separation that maps naturally into the classical examples considered in the previous subsections. Figure 4 illustrates the transformer network architecture. We have presented a 1-layer transformer network, which is our benchmark model. The transformed data can be used as input in more iterations of the transformer to obtain a multi-layer transformer. We illustrate the CNN+Transformer model in the first row of Figure A.2 in the Appendix for an empirical residual example. First, it is apparent that the cumulative returns of the strategy in subplot (c) outperforms the previous two models. This is because the allocation weights in subplot (a) capture not only the low frequency reversal patterns, but also the high-frequency cycles and trend components. This also implies that the allocation weights change more frequently to capture the higher frequency components. This more sophisticated allocation function requires a more complex signal as illustrated in subplot (b). Each change in the allocation can be traced back to changes in at least one of the signals. While the signals themselves are hard to interpret, we will leverage the transformer structure to extract interpretable “global dependency factors” in our main analysis. Figure A.3 in the Appendix provides another example to illustrate the differences between the three models. This example has a strong negative trend component with a superimposed mean-reversion. Only the CNN+Transformer captures both type of patterns. III Empirical Analysis A Data We collect daily equity return data for the securities on CRSP from January 1978 through December 2016. We use the first part of the sample to estimate the various factor models, which gives us the residuals for the time period from January 1998 to December 2016 for the arbitrage trading. The arbitrage strategies trade on a daily frequency at the close of each day. We use the daily adjusted returns to account for dividends and splits and the one-month Treasury bill rates from the Kenneth French Data Library as the risk-free rate. In addition, we complement the stock returns with the 46 firm-specific characteristics from Chen et al. 2022 , which are listed in Table A.I . All these variables are constructed either from accounting variables from the CRSP/Compustat database or from past returns from CRSP. The full details on the construction of these variables are in the Internet Appendix of Chen et al. 2022 . Our analysis uses only the most liquid stocks in order to avoid trading and market friction issues. More specifically, we consider only the stocks whose market capitalization at the previous month was larger than 0.01% of the total market capitalization at that previous month, which is the same selection criterion as in Kozak et al. 2020 . On average this leaves us with approximately the largest 550 stocks, which correspond roughly to the S&amp;P 500 index. This is an unbalanced dataset, as the stocks that we consider each month need not be the same as in the next month, but it is essentially balanced on a daily frequency in rolling windows of up to one year in our trading period from 1998 through 2016. For each stock we have its cross-sectionally centered and rank-transformed characteristics of the previous month. This is a standard transformation to deal with the different scales which is robust to outliers and time-variation, and has also been used in Chen et al. 2022 , Kozak et al. 2020 , Kelly et al. 2019 , and Freyberger et al. 2020 . Our daily residual time-series start in 1998 as we have a large number of missing values in daily individual stock returns prior to this date, but almost no missing daily values in our sample. 10 10 10 Of all the stocks that have daily returns observed in a the local lockback window of L = 30 L=30 days, only 0.1% have a missing return the next day for the out-of-sample trading, in which case we do not trade this stock. Hence, our data set of stocks with market capitalization higher than 0.01% of the total market capitalization, has essentially no missing daily values on a local window for the time period after 1998. We want to point out that the time period after 1998 also seems to be more challenging for arbitrage trading or factor trading, and hence our results can be viewed as conservative lower bounds. B Factor model estimation As discussed in Section II.A , we construct the statistical arbitrage portfolios by using the residuals of a general factor model for the daily excess returns of a collection of stocks. In particular, we consider the three empirically most successful families of factor models in our implementation. For each family, we conduct a rolling window estimation to obtain daily residuals out of sample from 1998 through 2016. This means that the residual composition matrix Φ t − 1 \\Phi_{t-1} of equation ( 1 ) depends only on the information up to time t − 1 t-1 , and hence there is no look-ahead bias in trading the residuals. The rolling window estimation is necessary because of the time-variation in risk exposure of individual stocks and the unbalanced nature of a panel of individuals stock returns. The three classes of factor models consists of pre-specified factors, latent unconditional factors and latent conditional factors: 1. Fama-French factors: We consider 1, 3, 5 and 8 factors based on various versions and extensions of the Fama-French factor models and downloaded from the Kenneth French Data Library. We consider them as tradeable assets in our universe. Each model includes the previous one and adds additional characteristic-based risk factors: (a) K = 1 K=1 : CAPM model with the excess return of a market factor (b) K = 3 K=3 : Fama-French 3 factor model includes a market, size and value factor (c) K = 5 K=5 : Fama-French 3 factor model + investment and profitability factors (d) K = 8 K=8 : Fama-French 5 factor model + momentum, short-term reversal and long-term reversal factors. We estimate the loadings of the individual stock returns daily with a linear regression on the factors with a rolling window on the previous 60 days and compute the residual for the current day out-of-sample. This is the same procedure as in Carhart 1997 . At each day we only consider the stocks with no missing observations in the daily returns within the rolling window, which in any window removes at most 2% of the stocks given our market capitalization filter. 2. PCA factors: We consider 1, 3, 5, 8, 10, and 15 latent factors, which are estimated daily on a rolling window. At each time t − 1 t-1 , we use the last 252 days, or roughly one trading year, to estimate the correlation matrix from which we extract the PCA factors. 11 11 11 This is the same procedure as in Avellaneda and Lee 2010 . Then, we use the last 60 days to estimate the loadings on the latent factors using linear regressions, and compute residuals for the current day out-of-sample. At each day we only consider the stocks with no missing observations in the daily returns during the rolling window, which in any window removes at most 2% of the stocks given our market capitalization filter. 3. IPCA factors: We consider 1, 3, 5, 8, 10, and 15 factors in the Instrumented PCA (IPCA) model of Kelly et al. 2019 . This is a conditional latent factor model, in which the loadings β t − 1 \\beta_{t-1} are a linear function of the asset characteristics at time t − 1 t-1 . As the characteristics change at most each month, we reestimate the IPCA model on rolling window every year using the monthly returns and characteristics of the last 240 months. The IPCA provides the factor weights and loadings for each stock as a function of the stock characteristics. Hence, we do not need to estimate the loadings for individual stocks with an additional time-series regression, but use the loading function and the characteristics at time t − 1 t-1 to obtain the out-of-sample residuals at time t t . The other details of the estimation process are carried out in the way outlined in Kelly et al. 2019 . In addition to the factor models above, we also include the excess returns of the individual stocks without projecting out the factors. This “zero-factor model” simply consists of the original excess returns of stocks in our universe and is denoted as K = 0 K=0 . For each factor model, in our empirical analysis we observe that the cumulative residuals exhibit consistent and relatively regular mean-reverting behavior. After taking out sufficiently many factors, the residuals of different stocks are only weakly correlated. C Implementation Given the daily out-of-sample residuals from 1998 through 2016 we estimate the trading signal and policy on a rolling window to obtain the out-of-sample returns of the strategy. For each strategy we calculate the annualized sample mean μ \\mu , annualized volatility σ \\sigma and annualized Sharpe ratio 12 12 12 We obtain the annualized metrics from the daily returns using the standard calculations μ = 252 T ​ ∑ t = 1 T R t \\mu=\\frac{252}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}R_{t} and σ = 252 T ​ ∑ t = 1 T ( R t − μ ) 2 \\sigma=\\sqrt{\\frac{252}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}(R_{t}-\\mu)^{2}} . SR = μ σ \\text{SR}=\\frac{\\mu}{\\sigma} . The Sharpe ratio represents a risk-adjusted average return. Our main models estimate arbitrage strategies to maximize the Sharpe ratio without transaction costs. In Section III.E we also consider a mean-variance objective and in Section III.J we include transaction costs in the estimation and evaluation. Our strategies trade the residuals of all stocks, which are mapped back into positions of the original stocks. We use the standard normalization that the absolute values of the individual stock portfolio weights sums up to one, i.e. we use the normalization ‖ ω t − 1 R ‖ 1 = 1 \\|\\omega^{R}_{t-1}\\|_{1}=1 . This normalization implies a leverage constraint as short positions are bounded by one. The trading signal is based on a local lookback window of L = 30 L=30 days. We show in Section III.I , that the results are robust to this choice and are very comparable for a lookback window of L = 60 L=60 days. Our main results use a rolling window of 1,000 days to estimate the deep learning models. For computational reasons we re-estimate the network only every 125 days using the previous 1,000 days. Section III.I shows that our results are robust to this choice. Our main results show the out-of-sample trading performance from January 2002 to December 2016 as we use the first four years to estimate the signal and allocation function. The hyperparameters for the deep learning models are based on the validation results summarized in Appendix C.A . Our benchmark model is a 2-layer CNN with D = 8 D=8 local convolutional filters and local window size of D s ​ i ​ z ​ e = 2 D_{size}=2 days. The transformer has H = 4 H=4 attention heads, which can be interpreted as capturing four different global patterns. The results are extremely robust to the choice of hyperparameters. Appendix B.D includes all the technical details for implementing the deep learning models. D Main Results Table I displays the main results for various arbitrage models. It reports the annualized Sharpe ratio, mean return and volatility for our principal deep trading strategy CNN+Transformer and the two benchmark models, Fourier+FFN and OU+Threshold, for every factor model described in Section III.B . The CNN+Transformer model and Fourier+FFN model are estimated with a Sharpe ratio objective. We obtain the daily out-of-sample residuals for different number of factors K K for the time period January 1998 to December 2016. The daily returns of the out-of-sample arbitrage trading is then evaluated from January 2002 to December 2016, as we use a rolling window of four years to estimate the deep learning models. Table I: OOS Annualized Performance Based on Sharpe Ratio Objective 