arameters, here θ = (η1 ,η 2 , β ) is based on\n\nMaximum Likelihood procedures by conditioning on observed values of the ∆rm ~ iid N ( 0, γ ) ’s random\n\nvariables (Pawitan, 2013, page 278). However, as ∆rm pops into both H and A parameters terms, one may\n\nexpect some correlation between H and A goals scored in a match, here negative due to opposite signs. The\n\nquestion arises on whether this correlation generated by sampling the y m , k ' s in a population of matches can\n\nbe expressed in a closed form.\n\nTo do that, we will rely on basic results on the exponential transformation Y = exp ( X ) of a normal\n\ndistribution X ~ N ( µ X , σ X2 ) . These are:\n\n\n               E (Y ) = µY = exp ( µ x ) exp (σ X2 / 2 )  ;\n\n\n               Var (Y ) = σ Y2 = µY2 exp (σ X2 ) − 1 ; Cov (Y1 , Y2 ) = σ Y1Y2 = µY1 µY2 exp σ X1 X 2 − 1 .\n                                                                                                   (       )\n\nNow, the law of total variance says upon conditioning and deconditioning for an auxiliary variable:\n\nVar (Y ) = E Z  Var ( Y | Z = z )  + VarZ  E ( Y | Z = z )  . In our case, Y being Poisson and Z , its parameter\n\nλ , one has E (Y | λ ) =Var (Y | λ ) = λ , and E ( λ ) as Var ( λ ) follow from log(λk ) ~ N (ηk , γ ) so that:\n\n\n                                   {\n               Var ( ym,k ) = µk 1 + µk exp ( β 2γ ) − 1 .}\nSimilarly, assuming conditional independence between ym ,1 ,and y m ,2 one gets for the covariance:\n\n\n               Cov ( ym,1 , ym,2 ) = µ1µ2 exp ( − β 2γ ) − 1 ,\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                           18\n\fwhere µk = exp (ηk ) exp β 2γ / 2  , k = 1,2 ], and the correlation ρ12 between ym ,1 and ym ,2 is :\n                           (        )\n\n                                    µ1µ2  exp ( − β 2γ ) − 1\n              ρ12 =                                                             .\n                      1 + µ1 exp ( β 2γ ) − 1 1 + µ2 exp ( β 2γ ) − 1\n\n\nIn this model β 2γ ≥ 0 and ρ12 is always negative for β ≠ 0 . Using the values of parameters estimated on\nthe 2014-2019 sample (6x124=744 matches) i.e., η1 = 0.1197 , η 2 = 0.1197 + 0.3293 = 0.4490 , β = 0.3014\n\nand γ = 2 (due to standardization of team rates rk ), one obtains ρ12 = −0.1894 .\n\nThis is in good agreement with the observed value of this correlation coefficient ρ120 = −235 and 95% CI (-\n\n0.076,0314). Values of this correlation (free of any model) show the same constant negative tendency over\n\nperiods in the range of -0.101 (2017) to -0.298 (2014).\n\nTable B-2: Estimation of the correlation coefficient between Home and Away goals scored in a match and\n\nits corresponding 95% confidence interval (Low, Up)\n\n          season         2014           2015      2016             2017     2018     2019       2014-19*\n\n           Low          -0.450      -0.375        -0.313       -0.272      -0.528    -0.406       -0.394\n\n            Est         -0.298      -0.213        -0.145       -0.101      -0.389    -0.248       -0.235\n\n            Up          -0.128      -0.039        0.031            0.076   -0.229    -0.075       -0.062\n\n*Pooled within-season\n\nSimilar results were observed using different transformation of H and A goals e.g., Ascombe, Tukey and\n\nlog as well as non-parametric estimators of correlation: Spearman and Kendall.\n\n\nReferences\nAldous, D. 2017. Elo Ratings and the sport model: a neglected topic in applied probability. Statistical\n   Science 32: 616-629\nBernardo, J.M. 1979. “Expected information as expected utility.” The Annals of Statistics 7: 686-690.\nBradley, A. A., T. Hashino, and S. S. Schwartz. 2003. “Distributions oriented verification of probability\n   forecasts for small data samples.” Weather Forecasting 18: 903–917.\nBrier, G. 1950. “Verification of forecasts expressed in terms of probability.” Monthly Weather Review 78:\n   1-3\n\n\n                                                                                                           19\n\fBröcker, J. 2012. “Probability forecasts”. In Forecast Verification: A Practitioner’s Guide in Atmospheric\n    Science, Second Edition. Edited by l. T. Jolliffe, and D.B. Stephenson. 2012 John Wiley & Sons.\nBröcker, J. 2015. “Resolution and discrimination–two sides of the same coin.” Quarterly Journal of the\n    Royal Meteorological Society 141: 1277–1282.\nCox, D.R.1958. “Two Further Applications of a Model for Binary Regression.” Biometrika 45:562-565.\nDagaev, D., and V.Y. Rudyak. 2019. “Seeding the UEFA Champions League participants: evaluation of the\n    reforms,” Journal of Quantitative Analysis in Sports 15: 129-140.\nDawid, A. P. 1986. “Probability Forecasting.” Encyclopedia of Statistical Science 7: 210-218.\nDimitriadis, T., T. Gneiting, and A.I. Jordan. 2021. “Stable reliability diagrams for probabilistic classifiers.”\n    PNAS 118, (8): e2016191118.doi: 10.1073/pnas.2016191118.\nDixon, M. J., and S. G. Coles. 1997. “Modelling Association Football Scores and Inefficiencies in the\n    Football Betting Market.” Journal of the Royal Statistical Society: Series C (Applied Statistics) 46: 265–\n    280.\nForrest, D., and R. Simmons. 2000. “Forecasting Sport: The Behaviour and Performance of Football\n    Tipsters.” International Journal of Forecasting 16: 317-331.\nGneiting, T., and A.E. Raftery. 2007. “Strictly Proper Scoring Rules, Prediction, and Estimation.” Journal\n    of the American Statistical Association 102: 359–378.\nGroll, A., T. Kneib, A. Mayr, and G. Schauberger. 2018. “On the dependency of soccer scores – a sparse\n    bivariate Poisson model for the UEFA European football championship 2016.” Journal of Quantitative\n    Analysis of Sports 14: 65–79\nGuo, C., G. Pleiss, and K.Q. Weinberger. 2017. “On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks.” arXiv:\n    1706.04599v2\nGweon, H., and H. Yu. 2019. “How reliable is your reliability diagram? “Pattern Recognition Letters 125:\n    687-693.\nHvattum, L.M., and H. Arntzen. 2009. “Using ELO ratings for match result prediction in association\n    football”. International Journal of Forecasting 26: 460-470\nJolliffe, I., and D. Stephenson. 2003. “Forecast Verification: A Practitioner’s Guide in Atmospheric\n    Science.” John Wiley-Blackwell\nKahneman D. 2011. “Thinking, fast and slow.” Farrar, Straus and Giroux.\nKarlis, D., and I. Ntzoufras. 2003. “Analysis of Sports Data by Using Bivariate Poisson Models.” The\n  Statistician 52: 381–393.\nMcHale, I., and P. Scarf. 2011. “Modelling the dependence of goals scored by opposing teams in\n  international soccer matches” Statistical Modelling 11: 219-236\nMitchell K. 2018. “Score Decompositions in Forecast Verification.” PhD diss., University of Exeter, UK\nMurphy A.H. 1973. “A new vector partition of the probability score.” Journal of Applied Meteorology 12:\n    595–600.\nMurphy, A. H., and R.L., Winkler. 1987. “A general framework for forecast verification”. Monthly Weather\n    Review 155: 1330-1338.\nPawitan, Y. 2001. “In all likelihood: statistical modelling and inference using likelihood.” Oxford:\n    Clarendon Press.\nPinheiro, J. C. 2002. “Model building using covariates in nonlinear mixed-effects models.” Journal de la\n    Société Française de Statistique 143 : 79-101.\nReade, J. J., C. Singleton, and A. Brown. A. 2021. “Evaluating Strange Forecasts: The Curious Case of\n    Football Match Scorelines.” Scottish Journal of Political Economy 68: 261-285.\n\n\n\n                                                                                                              20\n\fScarf, P., and J. Selliti Rangel Jr. 2016. “Models for outcomes of soccer matches.” In Handbook of\n   Statistical Methods and Analyses in Sports, Eds by J. Albert, M.E., Glickman, T. B, Swartz ,and R.H.\n   Koning, 341-354. CRC Press, Chapman\nSeillier-Moiseiwitsch, F., and Dawid, A.P. (1993), “On Testing the Validity of Sequential Probability\n   Forecasts” Journal of the American Statistical Association 88: 55-359.\nSelliti Rangel Jr, J. 2018. “Estimation and Forecasting Team Strength Dynamics in Football: Investigation\n   into Structural Breaks” PhD diss., University of Sallford, UK.\nSiegert, S. 2017. “Simplifying and generalising Murphy’s Brier score decomposition.” Quarterly Journal\n   of the Royal Meteorological Society 143: 1178–1183.\nSpiegelhalter, D. J. 1986. “Probabilistic prediction in patient management and clinical trials”, Statistics in\n   Medicine 5: 421–433.\nWheatcroft, E. 2019. “Interpreting the skill score form of forecast performance metrics.” International\n   Journal of Forecasting, 35, 573-579.\nWilks, D. S. (2011), “Statistical Methods in the Atmospheric Sciences.”, 3rd Edition Academic Press,\n   Oxford.\nWinkler, R.L., and A.H. Murphy. 1979. “The Use of Probabilities in Forecasts of Maximum and Minimum\n   Temperatures.” The Meteorological Magazine 108: 317-329.\nYates, J.F.1982. “External correspondence: Decompositions of the mean probability score.” Organizational\n   Behavior and Human Performance 30: 132–156.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                           21\n\f22\n\f   1. Home Win, Draw and Away Win for the Poisson Model (POI)\n\n\n\n\n   2. Home Win, Draw and Away Win for the Bookmakers Odds Probabilities (ODD)\n\nFigure 2: Box-plots of the conditional distributions of probabilistic forecasts of binary events (HWIN,\n\n   DRAW and AWIN) given the observed outcomes (X=0) and (X=1) and according to the forecasting\n\n   procedures: 1) Poisson loglinear regression (POI) and 2) Bookmakers Odds Probabilities (ODD)\n\n                                                                                                    23\n\f"}
{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2107.01854v1.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 194445, "equations": [], "text": "Poisoning Attack against Estimating from Pairwise Comparisons\nThanks:\nK. Ma is with the School of Computer Science and Technology, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China, and with the Artificial Intelligence Research Center, Peng Cheng Laboratory, Shenzhen 518055, China. E-mail: make@ucas.ac.cn\nQ. Xu is with the Key Laboratory of Intelligent Information Processing, Institute of Computing Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100190, China. E-mail: qianqian.xu@vipl.ict.ac.cn, xuqianqian@ict.ac.cn.\nJ. Zeng is with the School of Computer and Information Engineering, Jiangxi Normal University, Nanchang, Jiangxi, 330022, China. E-mail: jinshanzeng@jxnu.edu.cn, jsh.zeng@gmail.com\nX. Cao is with the State Key Laboratory of Information Security (SKLOIS), Institute of Information Engineering, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100093, China, and with the School of Cyber Security, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China. E-mail: caoxiaochun@iie.ac.cn\nQ. Huang is with the Key Laboratory of Intelligent Information Processing, Institute of Computing Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100190, China, and with the School of Computer Science and Technology, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China, and with the Key Laboratory of Big Data Mining and Knowledge Management, the School of Economics and Management, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China, and with the Artificial Intelligence Research Center, Peng Cheng Laboratory, Shenzhen 518055, China. E-mail: qmhuang@ucas.ac.cn.\nThanks:\nKe Ma\nQianqian Xu\nJinshan Zeng\nAffiliation:\nXiaochun Cao, \nand Qingming Huang,\nAbstract\nAs pairwise ranking becomes broadly employed for elections, sports competitions, recommendation, information retrieval and so on, attackers have strong motivation and incentives to manipulate or disrupt the ranking list. They could inject malicious comparisons into the training data to fool the target ranking algorithm. Such a technique is called “\npoisoning attack\n” in regression and classification tasks. In this paper, to the best of our knowledge, we initiate the first systematic investigation of data poisoning attack on the pairwise ranking algorithms, which can be generally formalized as the dynamic and static games between the ranker and the attacker, and can be modeled as certain kinds of integer programming problems mathematically. To break the computational hurdle of the underlying integer programming problems, we reformulate them into the distributionally robust optimization (DRO) problems, which are computational tractable. Based on such DRO formulations, we propose two efficient poisoning attack algorithms and establish the associated theoretical guarantees including the existence of Nash equilibrium and the generalization ability bounds. The effectiveness of the suggested poisoning attack strategies is demonstrated by a series of toy simulations and several real data experiments. These experimental results show that the proposed methods can significantly reduce the performance of the ranker in the sense that the correlation between the true ranking list and the aggregated results with toxic data can be decreased dramatically.\nIndex Terms:\nAdversarial Learning, Poisoning Attack, Pairwise Comparison, Rank Aggregation, Robust Game, Distributionally Robust Optimization.\nI\nIntroduction\nRank aggregation, in particular estimating a ranking based on comparisons between pairs of objects, arises in a variety of disciplines, including the social choice theory\n[\n3\n]\n, psychology\n[\n16\n]\n, statistics\n[\n34\n]\n, machine learning\n[\n39\n]\n, bioinformatics\n[\n37\n]\nand others. The convenience of these rank aggregation methods relies on their utilization of the ordinal data. Without features, the comparisons only contain the partial ranking lists generated by human beings. For instance, the voters who participated in an election choose one over the other candidates, which generate pairwise comparisons between the candidates. As another example workers in a crowdsourcing platform are often asked to identify the better advertisement of two possible visualization modes. Competitive sports such as tennis or chess also involve a serious of competitions between two players. From a modeling perspective, the rank aggregation approach treats pairwise comparisons as an access to estimate the underlying “scores” or “qualities” of the items being compared (\ne.g.\n, preference of candidates, skill levels of tennis players, and advertisement performance). A vast body of prior work has made the significant progress in studying both statistical and computational aspects\n[\n61\n,\n64\n,\n63\n,\n58\n,\n59\n,\n76\n,\n65\n,\n57\n]\n.\nHowever, the existing work ignores the security issue. Beyond statistical property and computational complexity, situations become complicated when the pairwise ranking algorithms are utilized in\nhigh-stakes\napplications,\ne.g.\nelections, sports competitions, and recommendation. In pursuit of huge economic benefits, the potential attackers have strong motivations and incentives to manipulate or disrupt the aggregated results. When the victims are ranking algorithms, a profit-oriented adversary could try his/her best to manipulate or disrupt the ranking list which will favor his/her demands-say, the attacker could place the special object at the top of the recommendation list, help the particular candidate to win an election or just defeat the candidate who should have won the election. If the attackers compromise the integrity of ranking results, the fairness and rationality will be lost in these high-stakes applications. Unfortunately, the security risk and serious threat of pairwise ranking problem have not been comprehensively examined yet. Can rank aggregation algorithms with pairwise comparisons be easily manipulated or disrupted? How reliable are their results in the high-stakes applications?\nTo the best of our knowledge, the adversarial arsenal for pairwise ranking methods has never been serious studied. On one hand, the pairwise comparisons are the most simple data in the literature as just binary variables can represent them. Due to the absence of features, modifying these binary data is an easy job. On the other hand, any single comparison does not dictate the aggregated result. Even manipulating a small quantity of binary data could not affect the final global ranking. Such a contradiction inspires us to initiate an adversarial investigation of pairwise ranking problem.\nTo execute the attack strategy in the scenario, the adversary must analyze the characteristics of pairwise ranking problems. Unlike the supervised learning tasks (\ne.g.\nregression, classification, multi-arm bandit and reinforcement learning), the rank aggregation does not need the\ntest protocol\n. This means that the\nevasion attacks\n(\na.k.a\nadversarial examples\n[\n24\n]\n) are not realistic. Evasion attack causes the fixed model to misbehave by well-crafted test data. But there is no test phrase to implement such a kind of attack. To archive his/her goal, the adversary needs to inject the manipulated data into the training data. Thus, rank aggregation in an adversarial setting is inherently related to the challenging\npoisoning attacks\n[\n10\n,\n31\n]\n. Next, the adversary should consider the discrete property of the pairwise comparisons. Unlike the data consisting of features in continuous space, the input of pairwise ranking only consists of binary data. The adversary could only add, delete or flip pairwise comparisons to execute the poisoning attacks. Such limitations make the substantial attack operations on pairwise ranking even harder. How to design efficient algorithms that are able to inject toxic data in a discrete domain? It is the distinguishable characteristic of our work which is different with the existing poisoning attack approaches\n[\n47\n,\n43\n,\n42\n,\n49\n,\n48\n,\n33\n,\n31\n,\n79\n,\n15\n]\n.\nGiven these challenges, we propose a principle framework for adversarial perturbations of pairwise comparisons that aims to break the integrity of rank aggregation result. In particular, we focus on the parametric model solved by maximum likelihood estimation\n[\n34\n]\n. We make the following contributions:\n•\nWe propose two game-theoretic frameworks specifically designed for adversary with the full or limited knowledge of the victim algorithm. By introducing the uncertainty set around the original data, the adversary aims to find a toxic distribution which will maximize the risk of estimating the ranking parameters. The dynamic threat model assumes that the adversary is aware of the original pairwise comparisons, the ranking algorithm and the ranking parameter learned from the original data. This model relates to a dynamic distributionally robust game. Besides, we propose a weaker threat model which assumes that the adversary only predominates the original data and the ranking algorithm. It induces a static distributionally robust game where the adversary can only execute the attacks in the “black-box” attack style.\n•\nDifferent statistical attacks corresponding to the dynamic and static threat models are formulated into the bi-level optimization problem and distributionally robust optimization problem. In the bi-level optimization problem, we adopt\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence to describe the uncertainty set around the original data. The optimal attack strategy can be obtained by the projection onto a simplex. In the distributionally robust optimization problem, the uncertainty set is a Wasserstein ball. Based on the strong duality, the optimal attack behavior is obtained by a least square problem with a special regularization.\n•\nWe prove the existence of robust optimization equilibrium and establish a minimax framework for pairwise ranking under adversarial setting.\nTo the best of our knowledge, this is the first systematic study of attacking rank aggregation under different adversarial models. The extensively evaluations are conducted on several datasets from different high-stake domains, including election, crowdsourcing, and recommendation. Our experiments demonstrate that the proposed poisoning attack could significantly decrease the correlation between the true ranking list and the aggregated result.\nNotations\nLet\n𝑽\n\\boldsymbol{V}\nbe a finite set. We will adopt the following notation from combinatorics:\n(\n𝑽\nk\n)\n:=\nset of all\n​\nk\n​\nelement subset of\n​\n𝑽\n.\n\\binom{\\boldsymbol{V}}{k}:=\\text{set of all}\\ k\\ \\text{element subset of}\\ \\boldsymbol{V}.\nIn particular\n(\n𝑽\n2\n)\n\\binom{\\boldsymbol{V}}{2}\nwould be the set of all unordered pairs of elements of\n𝑽\n\\boldsymbol{V}\n. The sets of ordered pair will be denoted\n𝑽\n×\n𝑽\n\\boldsymbol{V}\\times\\boldsymbol{V}\n. Ordered and unordered pairs will be delimited by parentheses\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n(i,\\ j)\nand braces\n[\ni\n,\nj\n]\n[i,\\ j]\nrespectively. We will use positive integers to indicate alternatives and voters. Henceforth,\n𝑽\n\\boldsymbol{V}\nwill always be the set\n[\nn\n]\n=\n{\n1\n,\n…\n,\nn\n}\n[n]=\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\nand will denote a set of alternatives to be ranked.\n𝒰\n=\n{\n1\n,\n…\n,\nm\n}\n\\mathcal{U}=\\{1,\\dots,m\\}\nwill denote a set of voters. For\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n𝑽\ni,\\ j\\in\\boldsymbol{V}\n, we write\ni\n≻\nj\ni\\succ j\nto mean that alternative\ni\ni\nis preferred over alternative\nj\nj\n. If we wish to emphasize the preference judgment of a particular voter\nu\n∈\n𝒰\nu\\in\\mathcal{U}\n, we will write\ni\n≻\nu\nj\ni\\succ_{u}j\n. Suppose that\n𝛀\n⊂\nℝ\nn\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{n}\nis the data space, we denote\n(\n𝛀\n,\nd\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\n⋅\n)\n)\n(\\boldsymbol{\\Omega},d(\\cdot,\\ \\cdot))\nas a metric space equipped with some metric\nd\n:\n𝛀\n×\n𝛀\n→\nℝ\nd:\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\\times\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\n.\nII\nRanking with Pairwise Comparisons\nGiven a collection\n𝑽\n\\boldsymbol{V}\nof\nn\nn\nalternatives, we suppose that each\ni\n∈\n𝑽\ni\\in\\boldsymbol{V}\nhas a certain numeric quality score\nθ\ni\n∗\n\\theta^{*}_{i}\n. We represent the quality scores of\n𝑽\n\\boldsymbol{V}\nas a vector\n𝜽\n∗\n∈\nℝ\nn\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}\n. Suppose that a comparison of any pair\n[\ni\n,\nj\n]\n∈\n(\n𝑽\nk\n)\n[i,\\ j]\\in\\binom{\\boldsymbol{V}}{k}\nis generated via the comparison of the corresponding scores\nθ\ni\n∗\n,\nθ\nj\n∗\n\\theta^{*}_{i},\\ \\theta^{*}_{j}\nin the presence of noise. Let\ny\ni\n​\nj\n∗\ny^{*}_{ij}\nbe the true direction of a pair\n[\ni\n,\nj\n]\n[i,\\ j]\nas\ny\ni\n​\nj\n∗\n=\n{\n1\n,\nθ\ni\n∗\n>\nθ\nj\n∗\n,\n−\n1\n,\nθ\ni\n∗\n<\nθ\nj\n∗\n.\ny^{*}_{ij}=\\begin{cases}\\ \\ \\ 1,&\\theta^{*}_{i}>\\theta^{*}_{j},\\\\\n-1,&\\theta^{*}_{i}<\\theta^{*}_{j}.\\end{cases}\n(1)\nLet\n𝒞\n\\mathcal{C}\nbe a collection of\nN\nN\npairwise comparisons\n𝒞\n=\n{\n𝒄\n=\n[\ni\n,\nj\n]\n|\ny\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n,\ni\n,\nj\n∈\n𝑽\n,\ni\n≠\nj\n}\n,\n\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\boldsymbol{c}=[i,\\ j]\\ |\\ y_{ij}=1,\\ i,\\ j\\in\\boldsymbol{V},\\ i\\neq j\\},\n(2)\nand\ny\ni\n​\nj\ny_{ij}\nis the label of pair\n[\ni\n,\nj\n]\n[i,\\ j]\nwhich could not be consist with\ny\ni\n​\nj\n∗\ny^{*}_{ij}\n. It is worth noting that\n𝒞\n\\mathcal{C}\nis always a multi-set. For any pair\n[\ni\n,\nj\n]\n[i,\\ j]\n, it could be labeled by multiple users. Given a set of voter\n𝒰\n=\n{\nu\n1\n,\n…\n,\nu\nm\n}\n\\mathcal{U}=\\{u_{1},\\dots,u_{m}\\}\n, let\ny\ni\n​\nj\nu\ny^{u}_{ij}\nbe the judgment of pair\n[\ni\n,\nj\n]\n[i,\\ j]\ngiven by voter\nu\n∈\n𝒰\nu\\in\\mathcal{U}\n. We can aggregate\ny\ni\n​\nj\nu\n1\n,\n…\n,\ny\ni\n​\nj\nu\nm\ny^{u_{1}}_{ij},\\dots,y^{u_{m}}_{ij}\ninto a weight\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\nw^{0}_{ij}\n. Define\nw\n⁡\n(\ni\n,\nj\n,\nu\n)\nw(i,\\ j,\\ u)\nas the indicator of\ny\ni\n​\nj\nu\ny^{u}_{ij}\n:\nw\n⁡\n(\ni\n,\nj\n,\nu\n)\n=\n{\n1\n,\nif\n​\ny\ni\n​\nj\nu\n=\n1\n,\nu\n∈\n𝒰\n0\n,\notherwise\nw(i,\\ j,\\ u)=\\begin{cases}\\ 1,&\\text{if}\\ y^{u}_{ij}=1,\\ u\\in\\mathcal{U}\\\\\n\\ 0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n(3)\nand the weight\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\nw^{0}_{ij}\nof\ny\ni\n​\nj\ny_{ij}\nis\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\n=\n∑\nu\n∈\n𝒰\n​\nw\n​\n(\ni\n,\nj\n,\nu\n)\n.\nw^{0}_{ij}=\\underset{u\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{U}}{\\sum}\\ w(i,\\ j,\\ u).\n(4)\nMoreover, we introduce the comparison matrix\n𝑨\n\\boldsymbol{A}\n. If there exists a comparison\n𝒄\n∈\n𝒞\n\\boldsymbol{c}\\in\\mathcal{C}\n, it can be described by its label\ny\ni\n​\nj\ny_{ij}\nand a row of\n𝑨\n\\boldsymbol{A}\nas\n𝒂\n𝒄\n=\n{\na\n1\n𝒄\n,\n…\n,\na\n|\n𝒞\n|\n𝒄\n}\n\\boldsymbol{a}^{\\boldsymbol{c}}=\\{a^{\\boldsymbol{c}}_{1},\\dots,a^{\\boldsymbol{c}}_{|\\mathcal{C}|}\\}\n:\na\nk\n𝒄\n=\n{\n1\n,\nk\n=\ni\n,\n−\n1\n,\nk\n=\nj\n,\n0\n,\notherwise.\na^{\\boldsymbol{c}}_{k}=\\begin{cases}\\ \\ \\ 1,&k=i,\\\\\n-1,&k=j,\\\\\n\\ \\ \\ 0,&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\n(5)\nThen the data of pairwise ranking problem can be represented by\n𝒞\n𝒰\n=\n{\n𝑨\n,\n𝒚\n,\n𝒘\n0\n}\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}=\\{\\boldsymbol{A},\\ \\boldsymbol{y},\\ \\boldsymbol{w}_{0}\\}\nwhere\n𝒘\n0\n=\n{\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\n}\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{0}=\\{w^{0}_{ij}\\}\n,\n𝒚\n=\n{\ny\ni\n​\nj\n}\n\\boldsymbol{y}=\\{y_{ij}\\}\nis a\nn\n⁡\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n/\n2\nn(n-1)/2\n-d\nbinary\nvector.\nIn statistical ranking or estimation from pairwise comparison, our goal is to obtain a score vector\n𝜽\n^\n\\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}}\nto minimize a loss of a global ranking on the given data\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\n.\n𝜽\n^\n∈\narg min\n𝜽\n∈\nℝ\nn\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒞\n𝒰\n)\n.\n\\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}}\\in\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\mathbb{R}^{n}}{\\textbf{{arg min}}}\\ \\ \\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ \\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}).\n(6)\nIn particular, let the estimation of\ny\ni\n​\nj\ny_{ij}\nbe\ny\n^\ni\n​\nj\n=\nsgn\n​\n(\n⟨\n𝒂\n𝒄\n,\n𝜽\n⟩\n+\nε\n𝒄\n)\n,\n∀\n𝒄\n∈\n𝒞\n,\n\\hat{y}_{ij}=\\textbf{{sgn}}\\Big(\\langle\\boldsymbol{a}^{\\boldsymbol{c}},\\ \\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\rangle+\\varepsilon_{\\boldsymbol{c}}\\Big),\\ \\forall\\ \\boldsymbol{c}\\in\\mathcal{C},\n(7)\nwhere\nsgn\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\textbf{{sgn}}(\\cdot)\nis the sign function,\nε\nc\n\\varepsilon_{c}\nis the independent and identically distributed (\ni.i.d\n) noise variable and has a cumulative distribution function (\nc.d.f\n)\nF\nF\n. Actually, (\n6\n) minimizes the derivation between the observed label\n𝒚\n\\boldsymbol{y}\nand its estimation\n𝒚\n^\n=\n{\ny\n^\ni\n​\nj\n}\n\\hat{\\boldsymbol{y}}=\\{\\hat{y}_{ij}\\}\nbased on the observing data\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\n. In addition, the random variable\nε\nc\n\\varepsilon_{c}\nplays the role of a noise parameter, with a higher magnitude of\nε\nc\n\\varepsilon_{c}\nleading to more uncertainty in the comparisons and the higher probability of sign inconsistency occurred between\ny\ni\n​\nj\ny_{ij}\nand\nθ\ni\n−\nθ\nj\n\\theta_{i}-\\theta_{j}\n. The event that object\ni\ni\ndominating object\nj\nj\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\ny_{ij}=1\n) is generally independent of the order of the two items being compared, thus, the following holds:\nPr\n⁡\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n=\n1\n)\n=\n1\n−\nPr\n⁡\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n=\n−\n1\n)\n\\Pr(y_{ij}=1)=1-\\Pr(y_{ij}=-1)\n(8)\nand\nF\nF\nis a symmetric\nc.d.f\nwhose continuous inverse is well-defined. Some typical examples of (\n7\n) are the uniform model\n[\n63\n]\n, the Bradley-Terry- Luce (BTL) model\n[\n14\n,\n46\n]\n, and the Thurstone model with Gaussian noise (Case V)\n[\n67\n]\n, which have been extensively studied in literature (\ne.g.\n,\n[\n17\n,\n78\n]\n). In this paper, we focus on the\nUniform Model\n: one can adopt the symmetric\nc.d.f\nF\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\nt\n+\n1\n2\nF(t)=\\frac{t+1}{2}\n, and the general set-up (\n7\n) turns to be a linear model. Furthermore, the loss function in (\n6\n) can be specialized as the weighted sum-of-squares function:\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒞\n𝒰\n)\n\\displaystyle\\ell\\ (\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ \\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}})\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n1\n2\n​\n|\n𝒞\n𝒰\n|\n​\n‖\n𝒚\n−\n𝑨\n​\n𝜽\n‖\n2\n,\n𝒘\n0\n2\n\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{2|\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}|}\\ \\|\\ \\boldsymbol{y}-\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\|^{2}_{2,\\ \\boldsymbol{w}_{0}}\n(9)\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n1\n2\n​\n|\n𝒞\n𝒰\n|\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n−\nθ\ni\n+\nθ\nj\n)\n2\n.\n\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{2|\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}|}\\ \\underset{(i,\\ j)}{\\sum}w^{0}_{ij}\\ (\\ y_{ij}-\\theta_{i}+\\theta_{j}\\ )^{2}.\nIII\nMethodology\nIn this section, we systematically introduce the\nmethodology for poisoning attacks on pairwise ranking. Specifically, we first start by introducing two game-theoretic threat models including the full knowledge and the limited knowledge adversaries. Then we present the corresponding algorithms to generate the optimal strategies of these threat models at different uncertainty budgets. Finally, the existence of equilibrium and the results of generalization analysis are discussed in the end of this section.\nIII-A\nPoisoning Attack on Pairwise Ranking\nWe provide here a detailed adversarial framework for poisoning attacks against pairwise ranking algorithms. The framework consists of defining the adversary’s goal, knowledge of the attacked method, and capability of manipulating the pairwise data, to eventually define the optimal poisoning attack strategies.\nThe Goal of Adversary.\nIf an adversary executes the poisoning attack, he/she will provide the ranker with the toxic data. This action will mislead its opponent into picking parameters to generate a different ranking result from\n𝜽\n^\n\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\nobtained by the original data\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\nin (\n6\n). Let\n𝜽\n¯\n\\bar{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\nbe the solution of (\n6\n) with the toxic data, it satisfies\nd\n⁡\n(\nπ\n𝜽\n∗\n,\nπ\n𝜽\n^\n)\n≤\nd\n⁡\n(\nπ\n𝜽\n∗\n,\nπ\n𝜽\n¯\n)\n,\nd(\\pi_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}},\\ \\pi_{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}})\\leq d(\\pi_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}},\\ \\pi_{\\bar{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}}),\n(10)\nwhere\n𝜽\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}\nis the true quality scores of the objects,\nπ\n𝜽\n\\pi_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\nis the ranking list decided by\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\nand\nd\n⁡\n(\nπ\n1\n,\nπ\n2\n)\nd(\\pi_{1},\\ \\pi_{2})\nmeasures the similarity of two ordered lists\nπ\n1\n\\pi_{1}\nand\nπ\n2\n\\pi_{2}\n.\nThe Knowledge of Adversary.\nWe assume two distinct attack scenarios which are distinguished by the knowledge of adversary, referred to as\ndynamic\nand\nstatic\nattacks in the following. The adversaries in the two scenarios have different knowledge of the victims.\n•\nIn\ndynamic\nattacks, the attacker is assumed to know the observed data\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\n, the ranking algorithm, and even the ranking parameters\n𝜽\n^\n\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\nobtained by the original data\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\nin (\n6\n). If a dictator wants to sabotage the election which will subvert his/her predominant, he/she would not need to manipulate the results of the election. Making the most competitive opponent lose the advantage in the key districts will achieve the purpose. The dictator could execute the dynamic strategies as the aggregation process is a “white-box” to him/her. This adversarial mechanism can be implemented by establishing the\nhierarchical\nrelationship between the ranker and the attacker. The attacker is assumed to anticipate the reactions of the ranker; this allows him/her to choose the best—or optimal—strategy accordingly. Such a\nhierarchical\ninteraction results in the fact that the mathematical program related to the ranking process is part of the adversary’s constraints. It is also known as the\ndynamic\nor Stackelberg (leader-follower) game\n[\n7\n]\nin the literature: the two agents take their actions in a sequential (or repeated) manner. Moreover, the\nhierarchical\nrelationship is the major feature of bi-level optimization. The bi-level program includes two mathematical programs within a single instance, one of these problems being part of the constraints of the other one.\n•\nIn\nstatic\nattacks, the attacker could not grasp\n𝜽\n^\n\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\nbut is still aware of the observed data\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\nand the ranking algorithm. This scenario comes from the fact that the ranking aggregation problem does not need the\ntest protocol\n. Once the adversary provides the modified data, the victim would generate the ranking list immediately. There is no chance to monitor the ranker’s behavior. In most cases, the adversary can not obtain\n𝜽\n^\n\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\n. There is no feedback for the adversary to update his/her strategies. A competitor of the e-commerce platform, who wants to disrupt the recommendation results and destroy the user experience, would execute the static strategies. Promoting the rank of specific goods is challenging. Disrupting the normal ranking result is sufficient to archive his/her purpose. The competitor could only execute the static strategies as the aggregation process is a “gray-box”. The leading e-commerce platform is the only one who could access the ranking parameters. The objective function and the pairwise comparisons for recommendation can be perceivable to the adversary. This adversarial mechanism should be modeled as a\nstatic\ngame. A static game is one in which a single decision is made by each player, and each player has no knowledge of the decision made by the other players before making their own decision. In other words, decisions or actions are made simultaneously (or the order is irrelevant).\nThe Capability of Adversary.\nTo modify the original data\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\nin poisoning attacks, the adversary will inject an arbitrary pair\n[\ni\n,\nj\n]\n∈\n(\n𝑽\nk\n)\n[i,\\ j]\\in\\binom{\\boldsymbol{V}}{k}\nwith any directions into\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\n, delete the existing comparison\n𝒄\n=\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n\\boldsymbol{c}=(i,\\ j)\nin\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\nor just flip the label of\n𝒄\n\\boldsymbol{c}\n. The three kinds of operations require some new representations of the observed set. We augment the observed data\n𝒞\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathcal{U}}\nwith the comparisons which are not labeled by users in\n𝒰\n\\mathcal{U}\n. Let\n𝒟\n=\n𝑽\n×\n𝑽\n\\mathcal{D}=\\boldsymbol{V}\\times\\boldsymbol{V}\nbe the set of all ordered pairs, and\n|\n𝒟\n|\n=\nN\n=\nn\n⁡\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n|\\mathcal{D}|=N=n(n-1)\n. The weights of all possible comparisons are\n𝒘\n0\n′\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\nand there exist\n0\n0\nentries in\n𝒘\n0\n′\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\n.\nAs\n𝒟\n\\mathcal{D}\nis the complete comparison set, the comparison matrix\n𝑩\n\\boldsymbol{B}\nwill be fixed and we can adopt a\nn\n⁡\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\nn(n-1)\n-d\nsingle-value\nvector to represent the labels, saying that\n𝒚\n′\n\\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\nis a vector with all entries are\n1\n1\n. Now all attack operations (adding, deleting and flipping) can be executed by increasing or decreasing the corresponding weight\n𝒘\n0\n′\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\n.\n\\displaystyle\n𝑩\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{B}\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n[\n𝒃\n1\n,\n2\n𝒃\n1\n,\n3\n𝒃\nn\n,\nn\n−\n2\n𝒃\nn\n,\nn\n−\n1\n]\n⊂\n{\n−\n1\n,\n0\n,\n1\n}\nN\n×\nn\n,\n\\displaystyle\\begin{bmatrix}\\boldsymbol{b}_{1,2}\\\\\n\\boldsymbol{b}_{1,3}\\\\\n\\vdots\\\\\n\\ \\ \\boldsymbol{b}_{n,n-2}\\ \\ \\\\\n\\ \\ \\boldsymbol{b}_{n,n-1}\\ \\ \\end{bmatrix}\\subset\\big\\{-1,\\ 0,\\ 1\\big\\}^{N\\times n},\n(11)\n𝒚\n′\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n{\ny\n1\n,\n2\n,\ny\n2\n,\n1\n,\n…\n,\ny\nn\n−\n1\n,\nn\n,\ny\nn\n,\nn\n−\n1\n}\n⊤\n\\displaystyle\\big\\{\\ y_{1,2},\\ y_{2,1},\\ \\dots,\\ y_{n-1,n},\\ y_{n,n-1}\\ \\big\\}^{\\top}\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n{\n1\n,\n1\n,\n…\n,\n1\n,\n1\n}\n⊤\n.\n\\displaystyle\\big\\{\\ 1,\\ 1,\\ \\dots,\\ 1,\\ 1\\ \\big\\}^{\\top}.\nBesides injecting the toxic data, the attacker also needs to disguise himself/herself. It means that the adversary needs to coordinate a poisoned\n𝒘\n=\n{\nw\ni\n​\nj\n}\n\\boldsymbol{w}=\\{w_{ij}\\}\nassociated with\n𝒘\n0\n′\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\n. Intuitively, the adversary could not obtain\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\nthrough the drastic changes, neither on each\nw\ni\n​\nj\nw_{ij}\nnor\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\nw\ni\n​\nj\n\\sum_{(i,j)}w_{ij}\n. Such limitations lead to the following constraints for the adversary’s action. First, the total difference between\n𝒘\n0\n′\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\nand\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\nwould be smaller than\nb\nb\n, namely,\n‖\n𝒘\n−\n𝒘\n0\n′\n‖\n1\n≤\nb\n,\nb\n∈\nℕ\n+\n.\n\\|\\ \\boldsymbol{w}\\ -\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\\ \\|_{1}\\ \\leq\\ b,\\ \\ b\\ \\in\\ \\mathbb{N}_{+}.\n(12)\nHere the positive integer\nb\nb\nbounds the total number of malicious samples thereby limiting the capabilities of the attacker. Furthermore, the adversary could not alter the number of votes on each pairwise comparison\n𝒄\n∈\n𝒟\n\\boldsymbol{c}\\in\\mathcal{D}\nobviously. This constraint on the adversary leads to the following condition:\n‖\n𝒘\n−\n𝒘\n0\n′\n‖\n∞\n≤\nl\n,\nl\n∈\nℕ\n+\n,\nl\n≤\nmin\n​\n{\nmax\n​\n(\n𝒘\n0\n′\n)\n,\nb\n}\n.\n\\|\\ \\boldsymbol{w}\\ -\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\\ \\|_{\\infty}\\leq\\ l,\\ l\\in\\mathbb{N}_{+},\\ l\\leq\\textbf{{min}}\\{\\textbf{{max}}(\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}),\\ b\\}.\n(13)\nThe positive integer\nl\nl\nleads the conservative perturbations on the observed samples. To summarize, the adversary‘s action set\n𝛀\n1\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{1}\nis\n𝛀\n1\n=\n{\n𝒘\n|\n𝒘\n∈\nℕ\nN\n,\nl\n,\nb\n∈\nℕ\n,\n‖\n𝒘\n−\n𝒘\n0\n′\n‖\n1\n≤\nb\n,\n‖\n𝒘\n−\n𝒘\n0\n′\n‖\n∞\n≤\nl\n,\nl\n≤\nmin\n​\n{\nmax\n​\n(\n𝒘\n0\n′\n)\n,\nb\n}\n}\n.\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{1}\\ \\ =\\ \\ \\left\\{\\ \\ \\boldsymbol{w}\\ \\ \\left|\\ \\begin{matrix}\\boldsymbol{w}\\ \\in\\ \\mathbb{N}^{N},\\ \\ l,\\ b\\ \\in\\ \\mathbb{N},\\\\\n\\ \\|\\ \\boldsymbol{w}\\ -\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\\ \\|_{1}\\ \\leq\\ b,\\\\\n\\ \\|\\ \\boldsymbol{w}\\ -\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\\ \\|_{\\infty}\\leq\\ l,\\\\\n\\ \\ l\\ \\leq\\ \\textbf{{min}}\\{\\textbf{{max}}(\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}),\\ b\\}\\ \\ \\end{matrix}\\right.\\right\\}.\n(14)\nFurthermore, the attacker must pay for his/her malicious behaviors. Let\ns\n:\nℕ\nN\n×\nℕ\nN\n→\nℝ\ns:\\mathbb{N}^{N}\\times\\mathbb{N}^{N}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\nis a “cost” function measured the overhead of the perturbation as changing\n𝒘\n0\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{0}\ninto\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\n. The attacker hopes that the toxic weight\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\nwill represent the lowest cost option. Let\n𝛀\n2\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{2}\nbe the budget set of the adversary\n𝛀\n2\n=\n{\n𝒘\n|\n𝒘\n∈\narg min\ns\n(\n𝒘\n,\n𝒘\n0\n′\n)\n}\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{2}\\ \\ =\\ \\ \\left\\{\\boldsymbol{w}\\ \\Big|\\ \\boldsymbol{w}\\in\\underset{}{\\textbf{{arg min}}}\\ \\ s(\\boldsymbol{w},\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0})\\right\\}\n(15)\nFinally, the action set is\n𝛀\n0\n=\n𝛀\n1\n∩\n𝛀\n1\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{0}=\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{1}\\cap\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{1}\nwhich figures out the capability of the adversary.\nPoisoning Attack Strategies.\nHere we specify the different poisoning strategies for the two attack scenarios.\n•\nDynamic attack strategy.\nConsider the goal and knowledge of attacker, we formulate the interaction between ranker and the adversary with full knowledge as a dynamic game. In this game, information is assumed to be complete (\ni.e.\n, the players’ payoff functions, as well as the constraint set\n𝛀\n0\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{0}\nand the flexible set of ranking parameter\n𝚯\n\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}\n, are common knowledge) and perfect (\ni.e.\n, the attacker knows the ranker’s decision). Having received the ranker’s decision\n𝜽\n^\n\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\n, the attacker chooses a feasible decision\n𝒘\n∈\n𝛀\n0\n\\boldsymbol{w}\\in\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{0}\nthat maximizes the ranker’s loss function to increase the risk of the ranker’s estimation based on\n{\n𝒘\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n}\n\\{\\boldsymbol{w},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\\}\n. Such a dynamic game can be formulated into the following bi-level optimization problem:\nmax\n𝒘\n∈\n𝛀\n0\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\boldsymbol{w}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{0}}{\\textbf{{max}}}\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝒘\n,\n𝜽\n^\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\ \\ \\ell(\\boldsymbol{w};\\ \\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}),\n(16a)\nsubject to\n𝜽\n^\n∈\narg min\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒘\n0\n′\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\ \\ \\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}}\\in\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\textbf{{arg min}}}\\ \\ \\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}).\\\n(16b)\nThe upper level optimization (\n16a\n) amounts to selecting the toxic data\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\nto maximize the loss function of the ranker, while the lower level optimization (\n16b\n) corresponds to calculate the ranking parameter\n𝜽\n^\n\\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}}\nwith original data\n{\n𝒘\n0\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n}\n\\{\\boldsymbol{w}_{0},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\\}\n. Once the adversary generates\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\n, he/she will deliver the toxic data to the ranker. Then the poisoned parameter\n𝜽\n¯\n\\bar{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\nwill be obtained by\n𝜽\n¯\n=\narg min\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒘\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n)\n.\n\\boldsymbol{\\bar{\\theta}}=\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\textbf{{arg min}}}\\ \\ \\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ \\boldsymbol{w},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}).\n(17)\n•\nStatic attack strategy.\nThis strategy is represented such a type of adversary whose ability is to inflict the highest possible risk of the ranker when no information about his/her interests is available. It means that the two players make decisions simultaneously, and the attacker does not knows the ranker’s decision. Such a static game can be formulated into the following min-max optimization problem:\nmin\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\textbf{{min}}}\nmax\n𝒘\n∈\n𝛀\n0\n​\nℓ\n​\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒘\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\boldsymbol{w}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{0}}{\\textbf{{max}}}\\ \\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta},\\ \\boldsymbol{w};\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}).\n(18)\nThe poisoned parameter\n𝜽\n¯\n\\bar{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\nwill be solved by (\n17\n).\nHowever, solving the dynamic and static attack strategies from (\n16\n) and (\n18\n) are challenging. On one hand, the bi-level optimization (\n16\n) and the min-max problem (\n18\n)are both mixed-integer programming problem as the variable\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\nis restricted to be positive integers. On the other hand, the feasible set\n𝛀\n0\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{0}\ncorresponds to a non-linear constraint as it requires to find the perturbation in the neighborhood of\n𝒘\n0\n′\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\nwith the lowest cost. It is well-known that linear integer programmings are NP-complete problems\n[\n36\n]\n. Such a non-linear constraint makes these problems even more complex. Obviously, adopting the heuristic methods to solve the optimal attack strategies (\n16\n) and (\n18\n) is sub-optimal. In this part, we will develop the other model based on ideas from distributionally robust optimization\n[\n54\n,\n12\n,\n22\n]\nthat provides the tractable convex formulations for solving the optimal strategies in the dynamic and static scenarios.\nIII-B\nDistributional Perspective and Robust Game\nIn the above formulations (\n16\n) and (\n18\n), the attacker modifies the number of votes on each pairwise comparison with constraints\n𝛀\n0\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{0}\n. This formulation leads to the mixed-integer programming problem. Here we introduce a distributional perspective to establish the tractable optimization problem. Generally speaking, the attacker and the ranker both access the original data\n𝒟\n\\mathcal{D}\nto play the dynamic or static game. The non-toxic pairwise comparisons\n𝒟\n=\n{\n𝒘\n0\n′\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n}\n\\mathcal{D}=\\{\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\\}\nare actually drawn from an empirical distribution\nℙ\nN\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}\nℙ\nN\n=\n1\nN\n​\n∑\n𝒄\n∈\n𝒞\n​\nδ\n​\n(\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\n′\n,\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n,\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\n,\n𝒄\n=\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n,\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}\\ =\\ \\frac{1}{N}\\ \\underset{\\boldsymbol{c}\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{C}}{\\sum}\\ \\delta({w^{0}_{ij}}^{\\prime},\\ \\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j},\\ {y_{ij}}^{\\prime}),\\ \\ \\boldsymbol{c}=(i,\\ j),\nwhere\nδ\n⁡\n(\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\n′\n,\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n,\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\n\\delta({w^{0}_{ij}}^{\\prime},\\ \\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j},\\ {y_{ij}}^{\\prime})\nis the Dirac probability measure on\n(\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\n′\n,\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n,\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\n({w^{0}_{ij}}^{\\prime},\\ \\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j},\\ {y_{ij}}^{\\prime})\n. With\n𝑩\n\\boldsymbol{B}\nand\n𝒚\n′\n\\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\nas (\n11\n), the marginal distribution of\n𝒘\n0\n′\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\nplays a vital role in the sequel. With some abuse of symbol, we treat the marginal distribution of\n𝒘\n0\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{0}\nas the distribution of the original data and\nℙ\nN\n=\n1\nN\n​\n∑\n𝒄\n∈\n𝒞\n​\nδ\n​\n(\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\n′\n)\n,\n𝒄\n=\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n.\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}=\\frac{1}{N}\\ \\underset{\\boldsymbol{c}\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{C}}{\\sum}\\ \\delta({w^{0}_{ij}}^{\\prime}),\\ \\ \\boldsymbol{c}=(i,\\ j).\nThe attacker chooses a perturbation function\nψ\n:\nℕ\nN\n→\nℕ\nN\n\\psi:\\mathbb{N}^{N}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{N}^{N}\nthat changes the weight\n𝒘\n0\n′\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\nto\n𝒘\n∈\n𝛀\n0\n\\boldsymbol{w}\\in\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{0}\n. Such a perturbation\nψ\n\\psi\ninduces a transition from the empirical distribution\nℙ\nN\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}\nto a poisoned distribution\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\n. If the attacker selects\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\nin a sufficiently small\nneighborhood\nof\nℙ\nN\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}\n, namely, the “distance” between the poisoned distribution\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\nand the empirical distribution\nP\nP\nwould be sufficiently small, the attacker could obtain a “local” solution and\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\nis a “good” approximation of\nℙ\nN\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}\nin the sense of such a “distance”. Therefore, the poisoned sample\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\nwould satisfy the constraints (\n12\n) and (\n13\n). Here we directly work with the empirical distribution\nℙ\nN\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}\n(or other nominal distribution) and consider\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\nis close to the nominal distribution in terms of a certain statistical distance.\nThere exists some popular choices of the statistical distance, such as\nϕ\n\\phi\n-divergences\n[\n9\n,\n32\n,\n8\n,\n75\n,\n53\n,\n54\n,\n18\n]\n, Prokhorov metric\n[\n21\n]\n, Wasserstein distances\n[\n77\n,\n11\n,\n23\n,\n51\n,\n40\n]\nand maximum mean discrepancy\n[\n66\n]\n.\nFor dynamic attack strategy (\n16\n), we adopt the\nϕ\n\\phi\n-divergence\n[\n41\n]\nas the discrepancy measure between the empirical distribution\nℙ\nn\n\\mathbb{P}_{n}\nand the toxic distribution\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\n.\nDefinition 1\n(\nϕ\n\\phi\n-divergence and\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\n-divergence)\n.\nLet\nϕ\n:\nℝ\n+\n→\nℝ\n\\phi:\\mathbb{R}_{+}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\nbe a convex function with\nϕ\n⁡\n(\n1\n)\n=\n0\n\\phi(1)=0\n. Then the\nϕ\n\\phi\n-divergence between distributions\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\nand\nℙ\n\\mathbb{P}\ndefined on a measurable space\n𝒳\n\\mathcal{X}\nis\nd\nϕ\n(\nℚ\n|\n|\nℙ\n)\n=\n∫\nϕ\n(\nd\n​\nℚ\nd\n​\nℙ\n)\nd\nℙ\n=\n∫\n𝒳\nϕ\n(\nq\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\np\n⁡\n(\nx\n)\n)\np\n(\nx\n)\nd\nμ\n(\nx\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle d_{\\phi}(\\mathbb{Q}\\ ||\\ \\mathbb{P})=\\int\\phi\\left(\\frac{d\\mathbb{Q}}{d\\mathbb{P}}\\right)\\mathrm{d}\\mathbb{P}=\\int_{\\mathcal{X}}\\phi\\left(\\frac{q(x)}{p(x)}\\right)p(x)\\mathrm{d}\\mu(x),\nwhere\nμ\n\\mu\nis a\nσ\n\\sigma\n-finite measure on\n𝒳\n\\mathcal{X}\nsatisfying\nℚ\n,\nℙ\n\\mathbb{Q},\\mathbb{P}\nare absolutely continuous with respect to\nμ\n\\mu\n, and\nq\n=\nd\n​\nℚ\nd\n​\nμ\nq=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\mathbb{Q}}{\\mathrm{d}\\mu}\n,\np\n=\nd\n​\nℙ\nd\n​\nμ\np=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\mathbb{P}}{\\mathrm{d}\\mu}\nare the Radon–Nikodym derivative with respect to\nμ\n\\mu\n. If\nϕ\n\\phi\nis adopted as\nϕ\n⁡\n(\nt\n)\n=\n1\n2\n​\n(\nt\n−\n1\n)\n2\n\\phi(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(t-1)^{2}\n, it is known as the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\n-divergence.\nSuppose that\n𝖃\n⁡\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{X}}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\nis a set of probability distributions from the empirical distribution with\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\n-divergence. This\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\nball with radius\nα\n\\alpha\nis given by\n𝖃\nα\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n=\n{\nℚ\n∈\n𝒫\n(\n𝛀\n1\n)\n|\nd\nχ\n2\n(\nℚ\n|\n|\nℙ\nN\n)\n≤\nα\n}\n,\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{X}}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})=\\left\\{\\ \\mathbb{Q}\\in\\mathcal{P}(\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{1})\\ \\Big|\\ d_{\\chi^{2}}(\\mathbb{Q}\\ ||\\ \\mathbb{P}_{N})\\leq\\alpha\\ \\right\\},\n(19)\nwhere\n𝒫\n⁡\n(\n𝛀\n1\n)\n\\mathcal{P}(\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{1})\ndenotes the set of all Borel probability measures on\n𝛀\n1\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{1}\n. With carefully chosen\nα\n\\alpha\n, the adversary chooses\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\nfrom the toxic distribution\nℚ\n∈\n𝖃\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\mathbb{Q}\\in\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{X}}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\n.\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\ncould satisfy the neighborhood constraints as (\n12\n) and (\n13\n). Replacing the minimal ‘cost’ constraint (\n15\n) by the neighborhood constraint defined with the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\nball, we formulate the following bi-level optimization to obtain the dynamic attack strategy\nmax\nℚ\n∈\n𝖃\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{X}}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}{\\textbf{{max}}}\n𝔼\n𝒘\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nℓ\n​\n(\n𝒘\n,\n𝜽\n^\n)\n]\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{w}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\big[\\ell(\\boldsymbol{w};\\ \\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}})\\big],\n(20)\nsubject to\n\\displaystyle\\textbf{{subject to}}\n𝜽\n^\n=\narg min\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒘\n0\n′\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}}=\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\textbf{{arg min}}}\\ \\ \\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}).\nThe\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\n-divergence and the “local” neighborhood constraint\nℚ\n∈\n𝖃\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\mathbb{Q}\\in\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{X}}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\nwill help us to develop a tractable algorithm for the dynamic attack strategy.\nDifferent with the dynamic attack strategy, the ranking parameter\n𝜽\n^\n\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\nwould be unknown for the adversary in the static attack strategy. The\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\ndivergence will not help to simplify the min-max problem (\n18\n). To sum up, we adopt the\np\np\n-Wasserstein distance\n[\n22\n]\nas the discrepancy measure between the empirical distribution\nℙ\nn\n\\mathbb{P}_{n}\nand the toxic distribution\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\nfor the static attack strategy. The\np\np\n-Wasserstein distance will help us to reformulate the min-max problem (\n18\n) into a single regularized problem.\nDefinition 2\n(\np\np\n-Wasserstein distance)\n.\nLet\np\n∈\n[\n1\n,\n∞\n]\np\\in[1,\\infty]\n. The\np\np\n-Wasserstein distance between distributions\nℙ\n,\nℚ\n∈\n𝒫\n⁡\n(\n𝛀\n)\n\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q}\\in\\mathcal{P}(\\boldsymbol{\\Omega})\nis defined as\n•\n1\n≤\np\n<\n∞\n1\\leq p<\\infty\n𝒲\np\n​\n(\nℙ\n,\nℚ\n)\n=\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{W}_{p}\\ (\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q})\\ \\ \\ \\ \\ =\n(21)\n(\nmin\nγ\n∈\nΓ\n⁡\n(\nℙ\n,\nℚ\n)\n​\n{\n∫\n𝛀\n×\n𝛀\n[\nd\n⁡\n(\n𝒘\n,\n𝒘\n′\n)\n]\np\n​\nγ\n​\n(\n𝑑\n𝒘\n,\nd\n​\n𝒘\n′\n)\n}\n)\n1\np\n\\displaystyle\\left(\\underset{\\gamma\\in\\Gamma(\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q})}{\\textbf{{min}}}\\left\\{{\\int}_{\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\\times\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}}\\Big[d\\Big(\\boldsymbol{w},\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}\\Big)\\Big]^{p}\\ \\gamma\\Big(\\mathrm{d}\\boldsymbol{w},\\ \\mathrm{d}\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}\\Big)\\right\\}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{p}}\n•\np\n=\n∞\np=\\infty\n𝒲\np\n​\n(\nℙ\n,\nℚ\n)\n=\ninf\nγ\n∈\nΓ\n⁡\n(\nℙ\n,\nℚ\n)\n​\nγ\n​\n-\ness sup\n𝛀\n×\n𝛀\n​\nd\n​\n(\n𝒘\n,\n𝒘\n′\n)\n\\mathcal{W}_{p}\\ (\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q})\\ \\ \\ =\\underset{\\gamma\\in\\Gamma(\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q})}{\\textbf{{inf}}}\\ \\underset{\\vphantom{\\gamma\\in\\Gamma(\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q})}\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\\times\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}}{\\gamma\\textnormal{-}\\textbf{{ess sup}}}\\ d\\Big(\\boldsymbol{w},\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}\\Big)\n(22)\nwhere\nΓ\n⁡\n(\nℙ\n,\nℚ\n)\n\\Gamma(\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q})\ndenotes the set of all Borel probability distributions on\n𝛀\n×\n𝛀\n\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\\times\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\nwith marginal distributions\nℙ\n\\mathbb{P}\nand\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\n,\nd\n:\n𝛀\n×\n𝛀\n→\nℝ\n+\nd:\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\\times\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}_{+}\nis a nonnegative function, and\nγ\n​\n-\ness sup\n\\gamma\\textnormal{-}\\textbf{{ess sup}}\nexpresses the essential supremum of\nd\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\n⋅\n)\nd(\\cdot,\\ \\cdot)\nwith respect to the measure\nγ\n\\gamma\n.\nThe Wasserstein distance (\n21\n) and (\n22\n) arise in the problem of optimal transport\n[\n52\n,\n72\n]\n: for any coupling\nγ\n∈\nΓ\n⁡\n(\nℙ\n,\nℚ\n)\n\\gamma\\in\\Gamma(\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q})\n, the conditional distribution\nγ\n𝒘\n|\n𝒘\n′\n\\gamma_{\\boldsymbol{w}|\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}}\ncan be viewed as a randomized overhead for ‘transporting’ a unit quantity of some material from a random location\n𝒘\n∼\nℙ\n\\boldsymbol{w}\\sim\\mathbb{P}\nto another location\n𝒘\n′\n∼\nℚ\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}\n. If the cost of transportation from\n𝒘\n∈\n𝛀\n\\boldsymbol{w}\\in\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\nto\n𝒘\n′\n∈\n𝛀\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}\\in\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}\nis given by\n[\nd\n⁡\n(\n𝒘\n,\n𝒘\n′\n)\n]\np\n[d(\\boldsymbol{w},\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime})]^{p}\n,\n𝒲\np\n​\n(\nℙ\n,\nℚ\n)\n\\mathcal{W}_{p}\\ (\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q})\nwill be the minimum expected transport cost\n[\n60\n]\n.\nSuppose that\n𝖂\np\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}_{p}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\nis a set of probability distributions constructed from the empirical distribution\nℙ\nN\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}\nwith\np\np\n-Wasserstein distance. This Wasserstein ball of radius\nα\n\\alpha\nis given by\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n=\n{\nℚ\n∈\n𝒫\n⁡\n(\n𝛀\n1\n)\n|\n𝒲\np\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n,\nℚ\n)\n≤\nα\n}\n.\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}_{p}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})=\\left\\{\\ \\mathbb{Q}\\in\\mathcal{P}(\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}_{1})\\ \\Big|\\ \\mathcal{W}_{p}\\ (\\mathbb{P}_{N},\\ \\mathbb{Q})\\leq\\alpha\\ \\right\\}.\n(23)\nWith local uncertainty set\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{p}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\n, the min-max optimization (\n18\n) could be expressed as the following distributionally robust optimization (DRO) problem:\nmin\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\vphantom{\\mathbb{Q}^{\\top}}\\textbf{{min}}}\nsup\nℚ\n∈\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n𝔼\n𝒘\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒘\n)\n]\n,\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{p}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}{\\textbf{{sup}}}\\ \\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{w}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\Big[\\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta},\\ \\boldsymbol{w})\\Big],\n(24)\nwhere the supremum operation w.r.t.\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\nmeans that all players’ optimal decision is based on the worst expected value of\nℓ\n\\ell\nfrom the set of distributions\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{p}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\n. Here we replace the minimal ‘cost’ constraint in (\n18\n) by the neighborhood constraint on the worst-case expectation. With the local constraint\nℚ\n∈\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\mathbb{Q}\\in\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{p}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\n, the Wasserstein distance between the empirical distribution\nℙ\nN\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}\nand the perturbed distribution\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\nmust be smaller than a given budget\nα\n\\alpha\nas\n𝒲\np\n​\n(\nℙ\n,\nℚ\n)\n≤\nα\n\\mathcal{W}_{p}\\ (\\mathbb{P},\\ \\mathbb{Q})\\leq\\alpha\n. It means that the attacker has a budget\nα\n\\alpha\nto implement his/her perturbation on the original data for ranking aggregation. The robust game formulation (\n24\n) would relax the coarse-grid constraint as (\n14\n), and the analysis in the sequel reveals the central role played by this relaxation.\nActually, the bi-level problem (\n20\n) and the DRO problem (\n24\n) relate to a general robust game\n[\n1\n,\n44\n,\n45\n]\nbetween the attacker and the ranker as\nmin\n𝒙\nr\n∈\n𝒳\nr\n​\nsup\nℚ\n∈\n𝖀\n𝔼\n𝝃\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nf\nr\n​\n(\n𝒙\nr\n,\n𝒙\n−\nr\n,\n𝝃\n)\n]\n,\nr\n=\n1\n,\n2\n\\underset{\\boldsymbol{x}_{r}\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{X}_{r}}{\\textbf{{min}}}\\ \\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{U}}}{\\textbf{{sup}}}\\ \\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\Big[f_{r}(\\boldsymbol{x}_{r},\\ \\boldsymbol{x}_{-r},\\ \\boldsymbol{\\xi})\\Big],\\ \\ r=1,\\ 2\\\n(25)\nwhere\nr\nr\nindicates the role of the agent in the robust game,\n𝒙\nr\n\\boldsymbol{x}_{r}\nis the decision variable of the special player\nr\nr\n, and\n𝒙\n−\nr\n\\boldsymbol{x}_{-r}\ndenotes the decision variables of its rivals, and\n𝒳\nr\n\\mathcal{X}_{r}\nis the action set of player\nr\nr\n. The random variable\n𝝃\n\\boldsymbol{\\xi}\nillustrates the uncertainty or inaccuracy of distributional information to the players, and\n𝖀\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{U}}\nis the uncertainty set of distribution of random variable\n𝝃\n\\boldsymbol{\\xi}\nfor all players (\ni.e.\n,\n𝖃\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{X}}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\nand\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}_{p}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\n). The pay-off function\nf\nr\nf_{r}\ncould be different for each player and the corresponding game is a non-zero sum game. Comparing the general case (\n25\n) with (\n20\n) and (\n24\n), all players in (\n24\n) focus on the same pay-off function as\nf\n1\n=\nf\n2\n=\nℓ\nf_{1}=f_{2}=\\ell\n. Moreover, the decision variable of the ranker\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\nequals to\n𝒙\n1\n\\boldsymbol{x}_{1}\n. The random variable\n𝝃\n\\boldsymbol{\\xi}\nrepresents the distribution of pairwise comparison as\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\n. So the decision variable of the attacker\n𝒙\n2\n\\boldsymbol{x}_{2}\nwill be the constant (its role has been replaced by\n𝝃\n\\boldsymbol{\\xi}\n). The robust game problem is first proposed by Bertsimas and Aghassi in\n[\n1\n]\n. It expands the boundaries of research of the classical Nash game\n[\n73\n,\n55\n,\n56\n]\nand the Bayesian game\n[\n26\n,\n27\n,\n28\n]\n. Different form the Nash and the Bayesian game\n[\n1\n]\n, the only common knowledge of all participants in robust game is that all players being aware about an uncertainty set like\n𝔛\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\mathfrak{X}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\nand\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}_{p}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\n. All possible parameters of payoff function are related to this set. Here we investigate the existence of the equilibrium for distributionally robust Nash equilibrium of the proposed model (\n25\n). First, we give the definition of the distributionally robust Nash equilibrium.\nDefinition 3\n.\nA pair of different players’ action\n{\n𝐱\n1\n∗\n,\n𝐱\n2\n∗\n}\n\\{\\boldsymbol{x}^{*}_{1},\\ \\boldsymbol{x}^{*}_{2}\\}\nis called a distributionally robust Nash equilibrium (DRNE) of (\n25\n) if they satisfy the following\n𝒙\nr\n∗\n∈\narg min\n𝒙\nr\n∈\n𝒳\nr\n​\nsup\nℚ\n∈\n𝖀\n𝔼\n𝝃\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nf\nr\n​\n(\n𝒙\nr\n,\n𝒙\n−\nr\n,\n𝝃\n)\n]\n,\nr\n=\n1\n,\n2\n.\n\\boldsymbol{x}^{*}_{r}\\ \\in\\ \\underset{\\boldsymbol{x}_{r}\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{X}_{r}}{\\vphantom{\\mathbb{Q}\\in\\mathcal{Q}^{\\top}_{r}}\\textbf{{arg min}}}\\ \\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{U}}}{\\textbf{{sup}}}\\ \\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\Big[f_{r}(\\boldsymbol{x}_{r},\\ \\boldsymbol{x}_{-r},\\ \\boldsymbol{\\xi})\\Big],\\ r=1,2.\n(26)\nNext, we can prove the existence of DRNE for the general robust game (\n25\n).\nTheorem 1\n.\nLet the pay-off function\nf\nr\n,\nr\n=\n1\n,\n2\nf_{r},\\ r=1,2\nbe the weighted sum-of-squared loss\nℓ\n\\ell\n(\n9\n) in (\n25\n). If the uncertainty set is\n𝖃\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{X}}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\nor\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}_{p}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\n, the general robust game (\n25\n) has a DRNE.\nTo prove this existence result, we reformulate the problem (\n25\n) into a single optimization problem and show that the single problem has an optimal solution. The detailed proof is provided in the Appendix\nA\n.\nIII-C\nOptimization\nIn this part we show our algorithms for computing the adversarial strategies. Suppose the total number of pairwise comparison without perturbation is\nM\n0\nM^{0}\n, and the frequencies of each type of the observed comparisons are\n𝒑\n=\n1\nM\n0\n⋅\n𝒘\n0\n′\n,\nM\n0\n=\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\nw\ni\n​\nj\n0\n′\n.\n\\boldsymbol{p}=\\frac{1}{M^{0}}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0},\\ \\ M^{0}=\\sum_{(i,j)}\\ {w^{0}_{ij}}^{\\prime}.\n(27)\nLet the maximum toxic dosage be\nκ\n\\kappa\n. It suggests that the number of toxic pairwise comparisons\nM\nM\nsatisfies\nM\n=\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\nw\ni\n​\nj\n≤\n(\n1\n+\nκ\n)\n⋅\nM\n0\n.\nM\\ =\\ \\underset{(i,j)}{\\sum}\\ w_{ij}\\ \\leq\\ (1+\\kappa)\\cdot M^{0}.\n(28)\nWe replace the toxic weight\n𝒘\n\\boldsymbol{w}\nwith its frequency\n𝒒\n=\n{\nq\ni\n​\nj\n}\n∈\nℝ\n+\nN\n\\boldsymbol{q}=\\{q_{ij}\\}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N}_{+}\nwhen analyzing the equilibrium, studying the statistical nature of the worst-case estimator and solving the corresponding optimization problem. We relax the integer programming problem into a general optimization by such a variable substitution. Thus, the pay-off function (\n9\n) turns to be\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒒\n)\n=\n1\n2\n​\nN\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n−\nθ\ni\n+\nθ\nj\n)\n2\n,\n\\displaystyle\\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta},\\ \\boldsymbol{q})=\\frac{1}{2N}\\ \\underset{(i,j)}{\\sum}\\ q_{ij}\\big(y_{ij}-\\theta_{i}+\\theta_{j}\\big)^{2},\n(29)\nand we still adopt\nℙ\nN\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}\nand\nℚ\n\\mathbb{Q}\nas the distribution of the empirical data and the toxic data. Furthermore, we can implement the integer attack with the optimal\n𝒒\n\\boldsymbol{q}\nand\nM\nM\n. Now we come to solve the bi-level optimization (\n20\n) and the distributionally robust optimization problem (\n24\n) with the variable substitution:\nmax\nℚ\n∈\n𝖃\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{X}}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}{\\textbf{{max}}}\n𝔼\n𝒒\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nℓ\n​\n(\n𝒒\n,\n𝜽\n^\n)\n]\n,\n\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{q}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\big[\\ell(\\boldsymbol{q};\\ \\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}})\\big],\n(30)\nsubject to\n\\displaystyle\\textbf{{subject to}}\n𝜽\n^\n=\narg min\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒘\n0\n′\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}}=\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\textbf{{arg min}}}\\ \\ \\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ \\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}),\nand\nmin\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\vphantom{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{p}\\ (\\mathbb{P}_{N})}\\textbf{{min}}}\nsup\nℚ\n∈\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n𝔼\n𝒒\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒒\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle\\ \\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{p}\\ (\\mathbb{P}_{N})}{\\textbf{{sup}}}\\ \\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{q}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\ \\Big[\\ \\ell\\big(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ \\boldsymbol{q}\\big)\\ \\Big]\n(31)\nFor the dynamic attack strategy (\n30\n), a similar formulation has been studied for archiving a better variance-bias trade-off in maximum likelihood estimation\n[\n54\n]\n. Based on the\nχ\n2\n\\chi^{2}\n-divergence, the bi-level problem (\n30\n) turns to be a convex problem. We provide a detailed process of solving (\n30\n) in the supplementary material.\nThe distributionally robust optimization formulation (\n31\n) involves optimizing over the uncertainty set\n𝖂\n2\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n\\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})\n, which contains countless probability measures. However, recent strong duality results of distributionally robust optimization involving Wasserstein uncertainty set\n[\n23\n, Theorem 1]\nand\n[\n12\n, Theorem 1]\n) ensure that the inner supremum in (\n31\n) admits an equivalent reformulation which would be a tractable, univariate optimization problem. In the adversarial scenario of pairwise ranking, we have the following result. The DRO problem (\n31\n) could be reformulated as a regularized regression problem.\nTheorem 2\n.\nLet\n𝒵\n=\n{\n𝐩\n,\n𝐁\n,\n𝐲\n′\n}\n\\mathcal{Z}=\\big\\{\\boldsymbol{p},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\\big\\}\nbe the observed data set, where\n𝐁\n\\boldsymbol{B}\nand\n𝐲\n′\n\\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\nare defined as (\n11\n),\n𝐩\n\\boldsymbol{p}\nis the frequency of each type of pairwise comparison as (\n27\n). Consider the loss function of\n𝐳\n\\boldsymbol{z}\n, and the distance function between\n𝐳\n𝐜\n\\boldsymbol{z}_{\\boldsymbol{c}}\n,\n𝐳\n𝐜\n′\n\\boldsymbol{z}^{\\prime}_{\\boldsymbol{c}}\nare based on the\nℓ\n2\n\\ell_{2}\n-norm. In other words, we take\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝛉\n,\n𝐳\n)\n\\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta},\\ \\boldsymbol{z})\nas (\n29\n) and\nd\n⁡\n(\n𝒛\n𝒄\n,\n𝒛\n𝒄\n′\n)\n\\displaystyle d(\\boldsymbol{z}_{\\boldsymbol{c}},\\ \\boldsymbol{z}^{\\prime}_{\\boldsymbol{c}})\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n‖\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n,\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n,\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\n−\n(\nq\ni\n​\nj\n,\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n,\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n)\n‖\n2\n\\displaystyle\\big\\|\\big(p_{ij},\\ \\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j},\\ y^{\\prime}_{ij}\\big)-\\big(q_{ij},\\ \\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j},\\ y^{\\prime}_{ij}\\big)\\big\\|_{2}\n(32)\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n|\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n|\n.\n\\displaystyle\\big|\\ p_{ij}-q_{ij}\\ \\big|.\nThen, the DRO problem (\n31\n) has an equivalent form:\nmin\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\nsup\nℚ\n∈\n𝖂\n2\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n𝔼\n𝒒\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒒\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\vphantom{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{p}\\ (\\mathbb{P}_{N})}\\textbf{{min}}}\\ \\ \\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}{\\textbf{{sup}}}\\ \\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{q}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\Big[\\ \\ell\\big(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ \\boldsymbol{q}\\big)\\ \\Big]\n(33)\n=\n\\displaystyle=\nmin\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\nℒ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\n+\nℛ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\n,\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\vphantom{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\mathfrak{W}^{\\alpha}_{p}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}\\textbf{{min}}}\\ \\ \\mathcal{L}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\ +\\ \\mathcal{R}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}),\nwhere\nℒ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\n=\n1\n2\n​\nN\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\np\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n−\n𝜽\n⊤\n​\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n)\n2\n,\n\\mathcal{L}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})=\\frac{1}{2N}\\underset{(i,\\ j)}{\\sum}\\ p_{ij}(y^{\\prime}_{ij}-\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j})^{2},\n(34)\nand\nℛ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n)\n=\nα\n4\n​\nN\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n−\n𝜽\n⊤\n​\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n)\n2\n.\n\\mathcal{R}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\alpha}{4N}\\underset{(i,\\ j)}{\\sum}(y^{\\prime}_{ij}-\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j})^{2}}.\n(35)\nWe provide a detailed proof in the Appendix\nB\n. The example of linear regression with Wasserstein distance based uncertainty sets has been considered in\n[\n11\n]\n. The representation for regularized linear regression in Theorem\n2\ncan be seen as an extension of\n[\n11\n]\n. We adopt the weighted sum-of-squared loss and the “regularization” (\n35\n) here is not the\nℓ\n2\n\\ell_{2}\n-norm of\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\n. (\n35\n) can be treated as a “regularization” which is the square root of the residual between\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\ny^{\\prime}_{ij}\nand its estimation. It represents a ‘worst’ case in pairwise ranking: all possible comparisons appear and they have the same number of votes. In this case, the pairwise ranking algorithm could not generate a reasonable ranking result. The uncertainty budget\nα\n\\alpha\nplay the role as the regularization parameter. As\nα\n\\alpha\nincrease, the ranking scores\n𝜽\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\nobtained by (\n33\n) would come closer to the solution of (\n35\n). The validity of the analysis above will be illustrated in the empirical studies.\nWith Theorem\n2\n, we will have the following corollary which gives a tractable method to obtain the worst-case distribution\n𝒒\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{q}^{*}_{\\alpha}\n. If we have the worst-case solution, we can solve the corresponding dual variable from the optimal vale of the original DRO problem.\nCorollary 1\n.\nFor\nλ\n≥\n0\n\\lambda\\geq 0\nand the weighted least square loss (\n29\n), we define\nψ\n:\nℝ\nN\n→\nℝ\n\\psi:\\mathbb{R}^{N}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}\nψ\nλ\n,\nℓ\n​\n(\n𝒑\n,\n𝜽\n)\n\\displaystyle\\psi_{\\lambda,\\ \\ell}(\\boldsymbol{p};\\ \\boldsymbol{\\theta})\n(36)\n=\n\\displaystyle=\nsup\n𝒛\n′\n∈\nℝ\nn\n+\n2\n​\n1\nN\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\n{\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n−\nλ\n​\n(\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n2\n}\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\boldsymbol{z}^{\\prime}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n+2}}{\\textbf{{sup}}}\\ \\frac{1}{N}\\underset{(i,j)}{\\sum}\\Big\\{\\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ q_{ij})-\\lambda(p_{ij}-q_{ij})^{2}\\Big\\}\nwhere\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\nq\ni\n​\nj\n)\n=\nq\ni\n​\nj\n2\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n−\nθ\ni\n+\nθ\nj\n)\n2\n.\n\\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ q_{ij})=\\frac{q_{ij}}{2}\\big(y^{\\prime}_{ij}-\\theta_{i}+\\theta_{j}\\big)^{2}.\n(37)\nLet\nI\nprimal\n=\nsup\nℚ\n∈\n𝖂\n2\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n​\n𝔼\n𝒛\n′\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝜽\n,\n𝒛\n′\n)\n]\n,\nI_{\\textit{primal}}=\\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{2}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}{\\textbf{{sup}}}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{z}^{\\prime}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\ \\Big[\\ell\\big(\\boldsymbol{\\theta};\\ \\boldsymbol{z}^{\\prime}\\big)\\Big],\n(38)\nwe have\nI\nprimal\n=\ninf\nλ\n≥\n0\n​\n{\nλ\n​\nα\n+\n𝔼\n𝒛\n′\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nψ\nλ\n,\nℓ\n​\n(\n𝒑\n,\n𝜽\n)\n]\n}\n.\nI_{\\textit{primal}}=\\underset{\\lambda\\geq 0}{\\textbf{{inf}}}\\ \\Bigg\\{\\ \\lambda\\alpha+\\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{z}^{\\prime}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\Big[\\psi_{\\lambda,\\ \\ell}(\\boldsymbol{p};\\ \\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\Big]\\Bigg\\}.\n(39)\nMoreover, let\n𝛉\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nbe the optimal solution of the right hand side of (\n33\n) and the dual variable of\n𝛉\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nis\nλ\nα\n∗\n\\lambda^{*}_{\\alpha}\nwill be a solution of (\n39\n):\nλ\nα\n∗\n=\n1\n16\n​\nN\n​\nα\n⋅\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n−\n(\n𝜽\nα\n∗\n)\n⊤\n​\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n)\n2\n.\n\\lambda^{*}_{\\alpha}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16N\\alpha}\\cdot\\underset{(i,j)}{\\sum}\\big(y^{\\prime}_{ij}-(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha})^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j}\\big)^{2}}.\n(40)\nThe optimal static attack strategy\n𝐪\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{q}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nis a solution of (\n36\n) corresponding to\n𝛉\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nand\nλ\nα\n∗\n\\lambda^{*}_{\\alpha}\n:\n𝒒\nα\n∗\n=\narg max\n𝒒\n∈\nℝ\n+\nN\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\n{\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n−\n(\n𝜽\nα\n∗\n)\n⊤\n​\n𝒂\n𝒄\n)\n2\n−\nλ\nα\n∗\n​\n|\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n|\n2\n}\n.\n\\boldsymbol{q}^{*}_{\\alpha}=\\underset{\\boldsymbol{q}\\ \\in\\ \\mathbb{R}^{N}_{+}}{\\textbf{{arg max}}}\\ \\underset{(i,j)}{\\sum}\\Bigg\\{q_{ij}\\Big(y^{\\prime}_{ij}-(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha})^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{a}_{\\boldsymbol{c}}\\Big)^{2}-\\lambda^{*}_{\\alpha}|p_{ij}-q_{ij}|^{2}\\Bigg\\}.\nInput :\nthe original data\n{\n𝒘\n0\n′\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n}\n\\{\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\\}\n, the maximum toxic dosage\nκ\n\\kappa\n, the uncertainty budget\nα\n\\alpha\n.\n1\nInitialize the frequency of weights\n𝒑\n\\boldsymbol{p}\nby (\n27\n)\n2\nObtain the worst-case ranking scoring\n𝜽\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nunder the uncertainty budget\nα\n\\alpha\n𝜽\nα\n∗\n∈\narg min\n𝜽\n∈\n𝚯\n​\n(\nα\n4\n​\nN\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n−\n𝜽\n⊤\n​\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n)\n2\n+\n1\n2\n​\nN\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\np\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n−\n𝜽\n⊤\n​\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n)\n2\n)\n.\n\\scriptstyle\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha}\\ \\in\\ \\underset{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\in\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}}{\\vphantom{\\mathbb{Q}\\in\\mathfrak{W}^{\\alpha}_{p}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}\\textbf{{arg min}}}\\ \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\alpha}{4N}\\underset{(i,j)}{\\sum}(y^{\\prime}_{ij}-\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j})^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2N}\\underset{(i,j)}{\\sum}p_{ij}(y^{\\prime}_{ij}-\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j})^{2}\\right).\n3\nCalculate the optimal dual variable through (\n40\n)\nλ\nα\n∗\n=\n1\n16\n​\nN\n​\nα\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n−\n(\n𝜽\nα\n∗\n)\n⊤\n​\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n)\n2\n.\n\\scriptstyle\\lambda^{*}_{\\alpha}\\ =\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16N\\alpha}\\underset{(i,j)}{\\sum}\\big(y^{\\prime}_{ij}-(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha})^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j}\\big)^{2}}.\n4\nObtain the toxic distribution\n𝒒\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{q}^{*}_{\\alpha}\ncorresponding to\n𝜽\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nand\nλ\nα\n∗\n\\lambda^{*}_{\\alpha}\n𝒒\nα\n∗\n∈\narg max\n𝒒\n∈\nℝ\n+\nN\n​\n∑\n(\ni\n,\nj\n)\n​\n{\nq\ni\n​\nj\n​\n(\ny\ni\n​\nj\n′\n−\n(\n𝜽\nα\n∗\n)\n⊤\n​\n𝒃\ni\n,\nj\n)\n2\n−\nλ\nα\n∗\n​\n|\np\ni\n​\nj\n−\nq\ni\n​\nj\n|\n2\n}\n.\n\\scriptstyle\\boldsymbol{q}^{*}_{\\alpha}\\ \\in\\ \\underset{\\boldsymbol{q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N}_{+}}{\\textbf{{arg max}}}\\ \\underset{(i,j)}{\\sum}\\ \\Big\\{q_{ij}(y^{\\prime}_{ij}-(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha})^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{b}_{i,j})^{2}-\\lambda^{*}_{\\alpha}|p_{ij}-q_{ij}|^{2}\\Big\\}.\n5\nAssign the toxic weights with\n𝒒\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{q}^{*}_{\\alpha}\n𝒘\nα\n=\nM\n0\n​\n(\n1\n+\nκ\n)\n⋅\n𝒒\nα\n∗\n.\n\\scriptstyle\\boldsymbol{w}_{\\alpha}\\ =\\ M_{0}(1+\\kappa)\\cdot\\boldsymbol{q}^{*}_{\\alpha}.\n6\nRound\n𝒘\nα\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{\\alpha}\nto obtain the\n𝒘\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nas integer vector\n𝒘\nα\n∗\n=\nrounding\n​\n(\n𝒘\nα\n)\n.\n\\scriptstyle\\boldsymbol{w}^{*}_{\\alpha}\\ =\\ \\textbf{{rounding}}(\\boldsymbol{w}_{\\alpha}).\n7\nOutput :\nthe poisoned data\n{\n𝒘\nα\n∗\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n}\n\\{\\boldsymbol{w}^{*}_{\\alpha},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\\}\n.\nAlgorithm 1\nStatic Poisoning Attack on Pairwise Ranking.\nFinally, we describe the whole optimization of the static poisoning attack on pairwise ranking with\nAlgorithm\n1\n. First, the adversary changes the original weight\n𝒘\n0\n′\n\\boldsymbol{w}^{\\prime}_{0}\ninto the frequency\n𝒑\n\\boldsymbol{p}\nas the initialization (line 1). By\nTheorem\n2\n, the attacker could obtain the worst-case estimation\n𝜽\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nthrough (\n33\n) (line 2). But the attacker cannot adopt\n𝜽\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nas the attack operation. Here we solve the dual variable\nλ\nα\n∗\n\\lambda^{*}_{\\alpha}\n(line 4) to find the toxic distribution. Then the toxic distribution\n𝒒\nα\n∗\n\\boldsymbol{q}^{*}_{\\alpha}\nwith uncertainty budget\nα\n\\alpha\nis obtained by\nCorollary\n1\n(line 4). With some rounding operation (line 5 & 6), the adversary prepares the poisoned data\n{\n𝒘\nα\n∗\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n}\n\\{\\boldsymbol{w}^{*}_{\\alpha},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\\}\n. Then the poisoned data is provided to the ranker who solved the ranking parameter by (\n17\n). Then the whole poisoning process will be completed.\nIII-D\nTheoretical Analysis\nIn this section, we come back to (\n31\n) and give a couple of inequalities relating the local worst-case (or local minimax) risks and the usual statistical risks of the pairwise ranking under adversarial conditions. In the traditional paradigm of statistical learning\n[\n71\n]\n, we have a class of probability measures\n𝒫\n\\mathcal{P}\non a measurable instance space\n𝒵\n\\mathcal{Z}\nand a class\nℱ\n\\mathcal{F}\nof measurable functions\nℓ\n:\n𝒵\n→\nℝ\n+\n\\ell:\\mathcal{Z}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}_{+}\n. Each\nℓ\n∈\nℱ\n\\ell\\in\\mathcal{F}\nquantifies the loss of a certain decision rule or a hypothesis. With a slight abuse of terminology, we will refer to\nℱ\n\\mathcal{F}\nas the hypothesis space. The (expected) risk of a hypothesis\nℓ\n\\ell\non instances generated according to\nℙ\n∈\n𝒫\n⁡\n(\n𝒵\n)\n\\mathbb{P}\\in\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{Z})\nis given by\nR\nℙ\n​\n(\nℓ\n)\n:=\n𝔼\n𝒛\n∼\nℙ\n​\n[\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝒛\n)\n]\n=\n∫\n𝒵\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝒛\n)\n​\nℙ\n​\n(\n𝑑\n𝒛\n)\n.\nR_{\\mathbb{P}}(\\ell):=\\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathbb{P}}\\big[\\ell(\\boldsymbol{z})\\big]={\\int}_{\\mathcal{Z}}\\ \\ell(\\boldsymbol{z})\\ \\mathbb{P}(\\mathrm{d}\\boldsymbol{z}).\n(41)\nGiven an\nN\nN\n-tuple\n{\n𝒛\n1\n,\n…\n,\n𝒛\nN\n}\n\\{\\boldsymbol{z}_{1},\\dots,\\boldsymbol{z}_{N}\\}\nof\ni.i.d.\ntraining examples drawn from an unknown distribution\nℙ\n∈\n𝒫\n\\mathbb{P}\\in\\mathcal{P}\n, the objective is to find a hypothesis\nf\n∈\nℱ\nf\\in\\mathcal{F}\nwhose risk\nR\n⁡\n(\nℙ\n,\nℓ\n)\nR(\\mathbb{P},\\ \\ell)\nis close to the minimum risk\nR\nℙ\n∗\n​\n(\nℱ\n)\n:=\ninf\nℓ\n∈\nℱ\n​\nR\nℙ\n​\n(\nℓ\n)\nR^{*}_{\\mathbb{P}}(\\mathcal{F}):=\\underset{\\ell\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{F}}{\\textbf{{inf}}}\\ R_{\\mathbb{P}}(\\ell)\n(42)\nwith high probability. Under some suitable regularity assumptions, this objective can be accomplished via Empirical Risk Minimization (ERM):\nR\nℙ\nN\n​\n(\nℓ\n)\n:=\n1\nN\n​\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nN\nℓ\n⁡\n(\n𝒛\nc\n)\nR_{\\mathbb{P}_{N}}(\\ell):=\\frac{1}{N}\\ \\sum^{N}_{i=1}\\ \\ell(\\boldsymbol{z}_{c})\n(43)\nand the minimum empirical risk is\nR\nℙ\nN\n∗\n​\n(\nℱ\n)\n:=\nmin\nℓ\n∈\nℱ\n​\nR\nℙ\nN\n​\n(\nℓ\n)\n,\nR^{*}_{\\mathbb{P}_{N}}(\\mathcal{F}):=\\underset{\\ell\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{F}}{\\textbf{{min}}}\\ R_{\\mathbb{P}_{N}}(\\ell),\n(44)\nwhere\nℙ\nN\n=\n1\nN\n​\n∑\nc\n=\n1\nN\nδ\n𝒛\nc\n\\mathbb{P}_{N}=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum^{N}_{c=1}\\delta_{\\boldsymbol{z}_{c}}\nis the empirical distribution of the training examples. Meanwhile, the minimax risk\n[\n40\n]\ncan be defined as\nR\n^\nℙ\nN\n​\n(\nℱ\n)\n:=\nmin\nℓ\n∈\nℱ\nsup\nℚ\n∈\n𝖂\n⁡\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n​\nR\nℚ\n​\n(\nℓ\n)\n\\hat{R}_{\\mathbb{P}_{N}}(\\mathcal{F}):=\\ \\underset{\\ell\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{F}\\vphantom{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\mathfrak{W}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}}{\\textbf{{min}}\\vphantom{\\textbf{{sup}}}}\\ \\ \\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}{\\textbf{{sup}}}\\ R_{\\mathbb{Q}}(\\ell)\n(45)\nWe assume that the instance space\n𝒵\n\\mathcal{Z}\nis a Polish space (\ni.e.\n, a complete separable metric space) with metric\nd\n𝒵\nd_{\\mathcal{Z}}\n. We denote by\n𝒫\n⁡\n(\n𝒵\n)\n\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{Z})\nthe space of all Borel probability measures on\n𝒵\n\\mathcal{Z}\n, and by\n𝒫\nm\n​\n(\n𝒵\n)\n\\mathcal{P}_{m}(\\mathcal{Z})\nwith\nm\n≥\n1\nm\\geq 1\nthe space of all\nℙ\n∈\n𝒫\n⁡\n(\n𝒵\n)\n\\mathbb{P}\\in\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{Z})\nwith finite\nm\n​\n-\nth\nm\\text{-{th}}\nmoments. The metric structure of\n𝒵\n\\mathcal{Z}\ncan be used to define a family of metrics on the spaces\n𝒫\nm\n​\n(\n𝒵\n)\n\\mathcal{P}_{m}(\\mathcal{Z})\n. We then define the local worst-case risk of\nℓ\n\\ell\nat\n𝒫\n\\mathcal{P}\n,\nR\nℙ\n,\nα\n,\np\n​\n(\nℓ\n)\n:=\nsup\nℚ\n∈\n𝖂\np\nα\n​\n(\nℙ\nN\n)\n​\nR\nℚ\n​\n(\nℓ\n)\nR_{\\mathbb{P},\\alpha,p}(\\ell):=\\underset{\\mathbb{Q}\\ \\in\\ \\boldsymbol{\\mathfrak{W}}^{\\alpha}_{p}(\\mathbb{P}_{N})}{\\textbf{{sup}}}\\ R_{\\mathbb{Q}}(\\ell)\n(46)\nand the local minimax risk of\n𝒫\n\\mathcal{P}\n,\nR\nℙ\n,\nα\n,\np\n∗\n​\n(\nℱ\n)\n:=\ninf\nℓ\n∈\nℱ\n​\nR\nℙ\n,\nα\n,\np\n​\n(\nℓ\n)\n.\nR^{*}_{\\mathbb{P},\\alpha,p}(\\mathcal{F}):=\\underset{\\ell\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{F}}{\\textbf{{inf}}}\\ R_{\\mathbb{P},\\alpha,p}(\\ell).\n(47)\nNext, we analyze the performance of the local minimax ERM procedure of the pairwise ranking, namely,\nℓ\n^\n∈\narg min\nℓ\n∈\nℱ\n​\nR\nℙ\nN\n,\nα\n,\n2\n​\n(\nℓ\n)\n.\n\\hat{\\ell}\\in\\underset{\\ell\\ \\in\\ \\mathcal{F}}{\\textbf{{arg min}}}\\ R_{\\mathbb{P}_{N},\\alpha,2}(\\ell).\n(48)\nTheorem 3\n.\nConsider the setting of pairwise ranking problem with the sum-of-squared loss, for any\nt\n>\n0\nt>0\n, it holds\nPr\n(\n∃\nℓ\n∈\nℱ\n:\nR\nℙ\n,\nα\n,\n2\n(\nℓ\n)\n>\nς\n1\n)\n≤\ne\n−\n2\n​\nt\n2\n\\Pr\\Big(\\ \\exists\\ \\ell\\in\\mathcal{F}:R_{\\mathbb{P},\\alpha,2}(\\ell)>\\varsigma_{1}\\ \\Big)\\ \\leq\\ e^{-2t^{2}}\n(49)\nand\nPr\n(\n∃\nℓ\n∈\nℱ\n:\nR\nℙ\nN\n,\nα\n,\n2\n(\nℓ\n)\n>\nς\n2\n)\n≤\n2\ne\n−\n2\n​\nt\n2\n\\ \\ \\Pr\\Big(\\exists\\ \\ell\\in\\mathcal{F}:R_{\\mathbb{P}_{N},\\alpha,2}(\\ell)>\\varsigma_{2}\\Big)\\ \\leq\\ 2e^{-2t^{2}}\n(50)\nwhere\nς\n1\n=\nmin\nλ\n≥\n0\n{\nλ\n​\nα\n2\n+\n𝔼\n𝒛\n∼\nℚ\n​\n[\nψ\nλ\n,\nℓ\n​\n(\n𝒛\n)\n]\n}\n+\n24\n​\n𝒥\n​\n(\nℱ\n)\n+\nt\nN\n.\n\\displaystyle\\varsigma_{1}=\\underset{\\lambda\\geq 0}{\\textbf{{min}}}\\ \\ \\Bigg\\{\\ \\lambda\\alpha^{2}\\ +\\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\ \\big[\\ \\psi_{\\lambda,\\ell}\\ (\\boldsymbol{z})\\ \\big]\\Bigg\\}+\\frac{24\\mathcal{J}(\\mathcal{F})+t}{\\sqrt{N\\ }}.\n(51)\nand\nς\n2\n=\nmin\nλ\n≥\n0\n{\n(\nλ\n+\n1\n)\nα\n2\n+\n𝔼\n𝒛\n∼\nℚ\n[\nψ\nλ\n,\nℓ\n(\n𝒛\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle\\varsigma_{2}=\\underset{\\lambda\\geq 0}{\\textbf{{min}}}\\ \\ \\Bigg\\{\\ (\\lambda+1)\\alpha^{2}\\ +\\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathbb{Q}}\\ \\big[\\ \\psi_{\\lambda,\\ell}\\ (\\boldsymbol{z})\\ \\big]\n(52)\nmin\nλ\n≥\n0\n{\n+\nlog\n​\n(\nλ\n+\n1\n)\nN\n}\n+\n24\n​\n𝒥\n​\n(\nℱ\n)\n+\nt\nN\n,\n\\displaystyle\\color[rgb]{1,1,1}{\\displaystyle\\underset{\\lambda\\geq 0}{\\textbf{{min}}}\\ \\ \\Bigg\\{}\\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ +\\frac{\\sqrt{\\textbf{{log}}(\\lambda+1)}}{\\sqrt{N\\ }}\\ \\Bigg\\}+\\frac{24\\mathcal{J}(\\mathcal{F})+t}{\\sqrt{N\\ }},\nwhere\n𝒥\n⁡\n(\nℱ\n)\n\\mathcal{J}(\\mathcal{F})\nis the Dudley’s entropy integral\n[\n20\n]\n, which is served as the complexity measure of the hypothesis class\nℱ\n\\mathcal{F}\n.\nTheorem\n3\nis a type of data-dependent generalization bounds which is proposed for margin cost function class\n[\n38\n,\n40\n]\n. By the strong duality results, we can establish this result from the dual representation of the Wasserstein DRO problem. The detailed proof is provided in the Appendix\nD\n. Here we note that the hypothesis selected by the minimax ERM procedure (\n48\n) are uniform smoothness with respect to the underlying metric\nd\n𝒵\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\n⋅\n)\nd_{\\mathcal{Z}}(\\cdot,\\cdot)\n. Further, we have the following result. Proofs are relegated to the Appendices\nE\n.\nTheorem 4\n.\nConsider the setting of pairwise ranking problem with the sum-of-squared loss, the following holds with probability as least\n1\n−\nη\n1-\\eta\nR\nℙ\n,\nα\n,\n2\n​\n(\nℓ\n^\n)\n−\nR\nℙ\n,\nα\n,\n2\n∗\n​\n(\nℱ\n)\n\\displaystyle R_{\\mathbb{P},\\alpha,2}(\\hat{\\ell})-R^{*}_{\\mathbb{P},\\alpha,2}(\\mathcal{F})\n(53)\n≤\n\\displaystyle\\leq\n48\n​\n𝒥\n​\n(\nℱ\n)\nN\n+\n48\n​\nL\n​\n[\ndiam\n​\n(\n𝒵\n)\n]\n2\nα\n​\nN\n+\n3\n​\nlog\n​\n(\n2\nη\n)\n2\n​\nN\n,\n\\displaystyle\\frac{48\\mathcal{J}(\\mathcal{F})}{\\sqrt{N}}+\\frac{48L\\big[\\text{diam}(\\mathcal{Z})\\big]^{2}}{\\alpha\\sqrt{N}}+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\textbf{{log}}(\\frac{2}{\\eta})}{2N}},\nwhere\ndiam\n​\n(\n𝒵\n)\n\\text{diam}(\\mathcal{Z})\nis the diameter of\n𝑂𝑃𝐸𝑁\n𝒵\n)\n\\mathcal{Z})\ndiam\n​\n(\n𝒵\n)\n=\nsup\n𝒛\n,\n𝒛\n′\n∈\n𝒵\n​\nd\n𝒵\n​\n(\n𝒛\n,\n𝒛\n′\n)\n.\n\\text{diam}(\\mathcal{Z})=\\underset{\\boldsymbol{z},\\boldsymbol{z}^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{Z}}{\\textbf{{sup}}}d_{\\mathcal{Z}}(\\boldsymbol{z},\\ \\boldsymbol{z}^{\\prime}).\n(54)\nIV\nExperiments\nIn this section, four examples are exhibited with both simulated and real-world data to illustrate the validity of the proposed poisoning attack on pairwise ranking. The first example is with simulated data while the latter three exploit real-world datasets involved crowdsourcing, election and recommendation.\nIV-A\nSimulated Study\nSettings.\nWe first validate our poisoning attack framework on simulated data. We create a random total ordering on set\n𝑽\n\\boldsymbol{V}\nwith\nn\nn\ncandidates as the ground-truth ranking and generate the comparison matrix\n𝑩\n\\boldsymbol{B}\nand the labels\n𝒚\n′\n\\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\nas (\n11\n). Next, we generate the ground-truth weight of each comparisons\n𝒘\n0\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{0}\n. Notice that the original data\n{\n𝒘\n0\n,\n𝑩\n,\n𝒚\n′\n}\n\\{\\boldsymbol{w}_{0},\\ \\boldsymbol{B},\\ \\boldsymbol{y}^{\\prime}\\}\nconsists of some noisy comparisons. In the simulation study, we can specify the percentage of noisy comparisons, denoted as\nϱ\n\\varrho\n. We validate the proposed attack framework when\nn\nn\n,\n𝒘\n0\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{0}\nand\nϱ\n\\varrho\nvary. Moreover, the maximum toxic dosage\nκ\n\\kappa\nand the uncertainty budget\nα\n\\alpha\nare the hyper-parameters of the Algorithm\n1\n. Since the annotations of pairwise data are usually collected via crowdsourcing platforms where the attacker could produce hundreds of zombie accounts easily to inject the poisoned pairwise comparisons, we also vary\nκ\n\\kappa\nand\nα\n\\alpha\nin our experiments. At last, there exists a rounding operator in the Algorithm\n1\nand we explore the results of different rounding functions,\ne.g.\nceiling, floor, and the nearest integer of each element in\n𝒘\nα\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{\\alpha}\n.\nCompetitors.\nTo the best of our knowledge, the proposed method is the first poisoning attack on pairwise ranking. To see whether our proposed method could provide efficient perturbation data for misleading the pairwise ranking algorithm, we implement the random perturbation attack (referred to as ‘Random’) and the Stackelberg or dynamic game attack (referred to as ‘Dynamic’) as the competitors.\n•\nThe\nrandom perturbation attack\nmodifies\n𝒘\n0\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{0}\nas\n𝒘\nrandom\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{\\text{random}}\nto manipulate the ranking result. The random perturbation attack generates\n𝒘\nrandom\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{\\text{random}}\nand obeys the constraints (\n12\n) and (\n13\n) to hide his/her behaviors. We vary\nb\nb\nand\nl\nl\nto explore the ability of random attack. The random perturbation data is noted as\n𝒵\nrandom\n=\n{\n𝑨\n,\n𝒚\n,\n𝒘\nrandom\n}\n\\mathcal{Z}_{\\text{random}}=\\{\\boldsymbol{A},\\ \\boldsymbol{y},\\ \\boldsymbol{w}_{\\text{random}}\\}\n. We assume this attacker is also lack of prior knowledge on the true ranking. So the random perturbation attack also adopts the fixed label set\n𝒚\n\\boldsymbol{y}\n.\n•\nThe\nStackelberg (dynamic) game attack\ncomes from (\n16\n). To execute this type of poisoning attack, the adversary would have the full knowledge of original training data\n𝒘\n0\n\\boldsymbol{w}_{0}\nand the corresponding relative ranking score\n𝜽\noriginal\n\\boldsymbol{\\theta}_{\\text{original}}\n. With these advantages, the adversary can adjust his/her strategies to provide the optimal malicious action with the bi-level optimization like (\n16\n). Without a doubt, the adversary endues with the privilege by such a hierarchical relation. For the fair competition, we only perform one round of the leader-follower game as the other competitors. Notice that this kind to attack is also proposed by this paper. Due to the length limitation, we provide the details of this attack in the supplementary materials.\nIt is worth noting that the poisoning attack with dynamic game is not a practical attack method. (\n16\n) is a bi-level optimization and the maximization process needs the solution of the minimization problem. In other words, the attacker must obtain the relative ranking score\n𝜽\n^\n\\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\theta}}\nestimated from the original training data without perturbation. This operation is much harder than injecting some modified training samples into the victim’s training set. Only the so-called “\nwhite-box\n” setting would satisfy its necessary requirements. As the ‘Dynamic’ method needs more exorbitant conditions, the ‘Dynamic’ method only reflects the vulnerability of ranking aggregation algorithms but can not show the superiority of the ‘Static’ method.\nEvaluation Metrics.\nWe adopt the following measures for evaluating the ranking results aggregated by the different sets of pairwise comparisons.\n•\nKendall\nτ\n\\tau\nDistance (Kendall-\nτ\n\\tau\n).\nThe Kendall rank correlation coefficient evaluates the degree of similarity between two sets of ranks given the same objects. This coefficient depends upon the number of inversions of pairs of objects which would be needed to transform one rank order into the other. Let\nV\n=\n[\nn\n]\nV=[n]\nbe a set of\nn\nn\ncandidates and\nπ\n1\n,\nπ\n2\n\\pi_{1},\\ \\pi_{2}\nare two total orders or permutations on\nV\nV\n, the Kendall\nτ\n\\tau\ndistance is defined to be\nd\nK\n​\n(\nπ\n1\n,\nπ\n2\n)\n=\n2\nn\n⁡\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n⋅\nϑ\n,\nd_{K}(\\pi_{1},\\ \\pi_{2})=\\frac{2}{n(n-1)}\\cdot\\vartheta,\n(55)\nwhere\nϑ\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nn\n−\n1\n∑\nj\n=\ni\n+\n1\nn\nϑ\n⁡\n(\nπ\n1\n​\n(\ni\n)\n,\nπ\n1\n​\n(\nj\n)\n,\nπ\n2\n​\n(\ni\n)\n,\nπ\n2\n​\n(\nj\n)\n)\n\\vartheta=\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\sum_{j=i+1}^{n}\\vartheta(\\pi_{1}(i),\\ \\pi_{1}(j),\\ \\pi_{2}(i),\\ \\pi_{2}(j))\n(56)\nis the number of different pairs between these two ordered sets\nπ\n1\n,\nπ\n2\n\\pi_{1},\\pi_{2}\nas\nϑ\n⁡\n(\nπ\n1\n​\n(\ni\n)\n,\nπ\n1\n​\n(\nj\n)\n,\nπ\n2\n​\n(\ni\n)\n,\nπ\n2\n​\n(\nj\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle\\vartheta(\\pi_{1}(i),\\ \\pi_{1}(j),\\ \\pi_{2}(i),\\ \\pi_{2}(j))\n(57)\n=\n\\displaystyle=\n{\n1\n,\nif\n​\n(\nπ\n1\n​\n(\ni\n)\n−\nπ\n1\n​\n(\nj\n)\n)\n​\n(\nπ\n2\n​\n(\ni\n)\n−\nπ\n2\n​\n(\nj\n)\n)\n>\n0\n,\n−\n1\n,\nif\n​\n(\nπ\n1\n​\n(\ni\n)\n−\nπ\n1\n​\n(\nj\n)\n)\n​\n(\nπ\n2\n​\n(\ni\n)\n−\nπ\n2\n​\n(\nj\n)\n)\n<\n0\n,\n0\n,\notherwise,\n\\displaystyle\\left\\{\\begin{matrix}\\ \\ 1,&\\text{if\\ }(\\pi_{1}(i)-\\pi_{1}(j))(\\pi_{2}(i)-\\pi_{2}(j))>0,\\\\\n-1,&\\text{if\\ }(\\pi_{1}(i)-\\pi_{1}(j))(\\pi_{2}(i)-\\pi_{2}(j))<0,\\\\\n\\ \\ 0,&\\text{otherwise, }\\end{matrix}\\right.\nand\nπ\n1\n​\n(\ni\n)\n\\pi_{1}(i)\nrepresents the ranking score of the\ni\nt\n​\nh\ni^{th}\nobject in ranking list\nπ\n1\n\\pi_{1}\n. Kendall\nτ\n\\tau\ndistance counts the number of pairwise mismatches between two rank orders. Then this metric considers all candidates of\nV\nV\n. However, Kendall-\nτ\n\\tau\nignores the importance of the top objects in a ranking list.\n•\nReciprocal Rank (R-Rank).\nThe reciprocal rank is a statistic measure for evaluating any process that produces an order list of possible responses to a sample of queries, ordered by the probability of correctness or the ranking scores. The reciprocal rank of a rank order is the multiplicative inverse of the rank of the first correct object:\nR\n​\nR\n=\n1\nrank\ni\n,\nRR=\\frac{1}{\\text{rank}_{i}},\n(58)\nwhere\nrank\ni\n{\\text{rank}_{i}}\nrefers to the rank position of the first candidates of the ground-truth ranking in the other list.\n•\nPrecision at\nK\nK\n(P\n@\n​\nK\n@K\n).\nPrecision at\nK\nK\nis the proportion of the top-\nK\nK\nobjects in the other rank order that are consistent with the true ranking. In this case, the precision and recall will be the same. So we do not report the recall and F score for our poisoning attack method.\n•\nAverage Precision at\nK\nK\n(AP\n@\n​\nK\n@K\n).\nAverage precision at K is a weighted average of the precision. If the top objects in the new ranking list are consistent with the true ranking, they will contribute more than the tail objects in this metric.\n•\nNormalized Discounted Cumulative Gain at K (NDCG\n@\n​\nK\n@K\n).\nUsing a graded relevance scale of objects in ranking result, discounted cumulative gain (DCG) measures the usefulness, or gain, of the objects based on its position in the order list when recovering to the true ranking. The gain is accumulated from the top to the bottom, with the gain of each result discounted at lower ranks. Compared to DCG, NDCG will be normalized by the ideal DCG.\nTABLE I\n:\nComparative results of different attack methods on simulated data.\nMethod\nBudget\nKendall-\nτ\n\\tau\nTendency (ideal)\nR-Rank\nP\n@\n​\n3\n@3\nAP\n@\n​\n3\n@3\nNDCG\n@\n​\n3\n@3\nOriginal\n-\n1.0000\n-\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\nRandom\n0.05/0.05\n0.9556\n-\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\nStatic\n10\n−\n6\n10^{-6}\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n10\n−\n5\n10^{-5}\n1.0000 (\n−\n-\n)\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n10\n−\n4\n10^{-4}\n1.0000 (\n−\n-\n)\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n10\n−\n3\n10^{-3}\n-0.6889 (\n↓\n\\downarrow\n)\n0.1111\n0.0000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n10\n−\n2\n10^{-2}\n-1.0000 (\n↓\n\\downarrow\n)\n0.1000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n10\n−\n1\n10^{-1}\n-0.8222\n0.1250\n0.0000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n1\n1\n-0.9111\n0.1111\n0.0000\n0.0000\n0.0000\nDynamic\n10\n−\n6\n10^{-6}\n-0.7333\n0.1429\n0.0000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n10\n−\n5\n10^{-5}\n-0.7333 (\n−\n-\n)\n0.1429\n0.0000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n10\n−\n4\n10^{-4}\n-0.7333 (\n−\n-\n)\n0.1429\n0.0000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n10\n−\n3\n10^{-3}\n-0.7333 (\n−\n-\n)\n0.1429\n0.0000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n10\n−\n2\n10^{-2}\n0.5111 (\n↑\n\\uparrow\n)\n1.0000\n0.3333\n0.3333\n0.4040\n10\n−\n1\n10^{-1}\n0.3778 (\n↓\n\\downarrow\n)\n0.5000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n0.0000\n1\n1\n-0.4222 (\n↓\n\\downarrow\n)\n1.0000\n0.3333\n0.3333\n0.4040\nMethod\nBudget\nKendall-\nτ\n\\tau\nTendency (ideal)\nR-Rank\nP\n@\n​\n5\n@5\nAP\n@\n​\n5\n@5\nNDCG\n@\n​\n5\n@5\nOriginal\n-\n1.0000\n-\n1.0000\n1.0000\n1.0000\n