arm with the largest empirical reward till round t t , and m 2 ​ ( t ) m_{2}(t) is the arm with the largest UCB index U k ​ ( n k , δ K ) U_{k}\\left(n_{k},\\frac{\\delta}{K}\\right) among the rest. The LUCB algorithm stops if the lower confidence bound of the first arm m 1 ​ ( t ) m_{1}(t) is larger than the upper confidence index of all other arms. 2 2 2 Equivalently, one can eliminate an arm k k from 𝒜 t \\mathcal{A}_{t} at the end of each round if the upper confidence index of arm k k is smaller than the lower confidence index of some other arm, and stop the algorithm when the set of active arms | 𝒜 t | = 1 |\\mathcal{A}_{t}|=1 . This implementation of the LUCB algorithm has the same guarantees as the one proposed in kalyanakrishnan2012pac ; jamieson2014best while obtaining similar empirical performance. Subsequently, another algorithm LUCB++ simchowitz2017simulator ; tanczos2017kl was designed that operates in a similar manner to LUCB but constructs the upper confidence and lower confidence indices with different confidence parameters for m 1 ​ ( t ) , m 2 ​ ( t ) m_{1}(t),m_{2}(t) . The details of the upper confidence and lower confidence indices for each of these algorithms are presented in Table 3 . Note that our metric for comparison is the total number of samples collectively drawn from the arms. As LUCB algorithms sample two arms at each round, the total number of samples drawn from the LUCB algorithms is two times the number of rounds t t . By comparing the total number of samples and not the number of rounds t t , we draw a fair comparison between the performance of LUCB and lil’UCB algorithm. All the approaches described above work well for the case where rewards are known to be either sub-Gaussian or bounded. Furthermore, if the class of distribution is known (e.g., it is known that rewards are Gaussian with known σ \\sigma and unknown μ \\mu ), then there are two more approaches known in the literature, namely Top Two Thompson Sampling (TTTS) shang2020fixed and Tracking garivier2016optimal . In TTTS, the player computes a posterior distribution on the mean reward of each arm and then applies Thompson sampling on the posterior to obtain two samples. It stops when the posterior probability of an arm k k being optimal exceeds a certain threshold τ k ​ ( n k , δ ) \\tau_{k}(n_{k},\\delta) . The TTTS algorithm can be computationally intensive as it involves the computation of posterior probability in each round of their algorithm. In garivier2016optimal , authors evaluate a lower bound for the Multi-Armed bandit problem in the form of an optimization problem. They propose a tracking based approach, that solves the optimization problem at each round to obtain an estimated rate at which each arm should be sampled at round t t and sample arms in proportion to that rate. More recently, degenne2019non proposed alternative approaches to the track-and-stop algorithm that do not require solving an optimization problem at each round. Instead, they view the optimization problem as an unknown game and have sampling rules based on iterative saddle point strategies. All of the approaches listed above require knowing the class of reward distribution. Since we only assume that the rewards are bounded and not the class of distribution, we do not focus on extending TTTS or Tracking based approaches to the correlated bandit setting in this paper. Algorithm Confidence Bound B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) B(n_{k},\\delta) Type Samples Drawn Succ Elimination even2002pac log ⁡ ( π 2 ​ n k 2 3 ​ δ ) 2 ​ n k \\sqrt{\\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{2}n_{k}^{2}}{3\\delta}\\right)}{2n_{k}}} Racing 577209.4 lil Succ Elimination jamieson2014best 0.85 ​ log ⁡ ( log ⁡ ( 0.2585 ​ n k ) ) + 0.96 ​ log ⁡ ( 67.59 / δ ) n k 0.85\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(\\log(0.2585n_{k}))+0.96\\log(67.59/\\delta)}{n_{k}}} Racing 120498.5 KL-Racing kaufmann2013information d ⁡ ( B ) = 2 ​ log ⁡ ( 11.1 ​ t 1.1 δ ) d(B)=2\\log\\left(\\frac{11.1t^{1.1}}{\\delta}\\right) * Racing 147780.4 Racin