nd suggested in howard2018timeuniform , and hence we use that for all our implementations of Racing, LUCB, LUCB++ and our proposed algorithm in the rest of the paper. The reported sample complexity is for the task of identifying best movie genre from the set of 18 movie genres in the Movielens dataset. Experimental setup is described in detail in Section 7 . 3.2 Developments in Confidence sequence B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) B(n_{k},\\delta) It is important to note that the performance of the algorithms described above depends critically on the tightness of the confidence bound B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) B(n_{k},\\delta) . For instance, initially the LUCB algorithm was proposed with the confidence interval B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) = log ⁡ ( 405 ​ n k 1.1 δ ​ log ⁡ ( 405 ​ n k 1.1 δ ) ) 2 ​ n k B(n_{k},\\delta)=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{405n_{k}^{1.1}}{\\delta}\\log\\left(\\frac{405n_{k}^{1.1}}{\\delta}\\right)\\right)}{2n_{k}}} (See kalyanakrishnan2012pac ) for [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] bounded random variables. Subsequently tighter bounds as in jamieson2014best , kaufmann2013information were developed, which led to performance improvements in the LUCB algorithm. See Table 4 for a comparison different confidence bound developed over time and how they affect the empirical performance of the best-arm identification algorithms 3 3 3 The bound proposed in kaufmann2013information ; tanczos2017kl are KL based bounds that evaluate the indices U k ​ ( n k , δ ) , L k ​ ( n k , δ ) U_{k}(n_{k},\\delta),L_{k}(n_{k},\\delta) as inf { j &gt; μ ^ k : n k ​ ( t ) ​ d k ​ l ​ ( μ ^ k , j ) &lt; d ⁡ ( B ) } \\inf\\{j&gt;\\hat{\\mu}_{k}:n_{k}(t)d_{kl}(\\hat{\\mu}_{k},j)&lt;d(B)\\} and sup { j &lt; μ ^ k : n k ( t ) d k ​ l ( μ ^ k , j ) &lt; d ( B ) \\sup\\{j&lt;\\hat{\\mu}_{k}:n_{k}(t)d_{kl}(\\hat{\\mu}_{k},j)&lt;d(B) . The distance d k ​ l ​ ( x , y ) d_{kl}(x,y) is evaluated as x ​ log ⁡ ( x / y ) + ( 1 − x ) ​ log ⁡ ( ( 1 − x ) / ( 1 − y ) ) x\\log(x/y)+(1-x)\\log((1-x)/(1-y)) . For a more detailed comparison of different confidence bounds B k ​ ( n k , δ ) B_{k}(n_{k},\\delta) , we refer the reader to Table 2 of howard2018timeuniform . To the best of our knowledge, the tightest 1 − δ 1-\\delta anytime confidence interval for bounded and sub-Gaussian random variables is proposed in howard2018timeuniform , which constructs B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) = 0.85 ​ log ⁡ ( log ⁡ ( 0.5 ​ n k ) ) + 0.72 ​ log ⁡ ( 5.2 / δ ) n k . B(n_{k},\\delta)=0.85\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(\\log(0.5n_{k}))+0.72\\log(5.2/\\delta)}{n_{k}}}. (5) Due to this observation, which is also supported by empirical evidence in Table 4 , we use the bound suggested by howard2018timeuniform in all implementations of Successive Elimination, LUCB and our proposed algorithm. However, our algorithm and analysis extend to arbitrary 1 − δ 1-\\delta anytime confidence interval B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) B(n_{k},\\delta) . We would also like to highlight the fact that lil’UCB is known to have the best known theoretical sample complexity (in terms of its dependency on the number of arms K K ). The LUCB algorithm stops with probability 1 − δ 1-\\delta after obtaining at most ∑ k ∈ 𝒦 2 ​ ζ Δ k 2 ​ ( log ⁡ ( K ​ log ⁡ ( 1 Δ k 2 ) δ ) ) \\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{K}}\\frac{2\\zeta}{\\Delta_{k}^{2}}\\left(\\log\\left(\\frac{K\\log\\left(\\frac{1}{\\Delta_{k}^{2}}\\right)}{\\delta}\\right)\\right) samples, where Δ k = μ k ∗ − μ k \\Delta_{k}=\\mu_{k^{*}}-\\mu_{k} , the difference in mean reward of optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} and mean reward of arm k k . And Δ k ∗ = min k ≠ k ∗ ⁡ Δ k \\Delta_{k^{*}}=\\min_{k\\neq k^{*}}\\Delta_{k} , the gap between best and second best arm. It is known that lil’UCB algorithm has a sample complexity O ⁡ ( ∑ k ∈ 𝒦 1 Δ k 2 ​ log ⁡ ( log ⁡ ( 1 Δ k 2 ) δ ) ) \\mathrm{O}\\left(\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{K}}\\frac{1}{\\Delta_{k}^{2}}\\log\\left(\\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{1}{\\Delta_{k}^{2}}\\right)}{\\delta}\\right)\\right) i.e., it avoids the log ⁡ ( K ) \\log(K) term in the numerator, and hence has the best known theoretical sample complexity. However, it has been observed (both in jamieson2014best and our experiments) that its empirical performance is inferior to that of the LUCB algorithm. Due to this reason, we focus on proposing an algorithm C-LUCB that extends the LUCB approach to the correlated bandit setting. We have included the performance of lil’UCB in all our experiments. 3.3 Algorithms outside the classical setting Unlike the regret-minimization problem, the best-arm identification problem is relatively unexplored outside of the classical multi-armed bandit setting. A rare exception is the structured bandit setting, where mean rewards corresponding to different arms are related to one another through a hidden parameter θ \\theta . The underlying value of θ \\theta is fixed and unknown, but the mean reward mappings θ → μ k ​ ( θ ) \\theta\\rightarrow\\mu_{k}(\\theta) are known. The linear bandit setting is a special case of structured bandits, where mean reward mappings are of the form x k ⊺ ​ θ x_{k}^{\\intercal}\\theta with x k x_{k} known to the player. The best-arm identification problem has been studied in soare2014best ; tao2018best for linear bandits and in huang2017structured for the general structured bandit setting. Other special cases of structured bandits include global bandits ata2015global , regional bandits wang2018regional and the generalized linear bandits shen2018generalized ; to the best of our knowledge the best arm identification problem has not been addressed in these special cases. Note that in the full generality, the structured bandit framework is simply a bandit problem with constraints on the joint probability distribution van2020optimal , but that setting has only been studied for the objective of regret minimization and not best-arm identification. To the best of our knowledge, the structured bandits work studying best-arm identification soare2014best ; tao2018best ; huang2017structured assume the presence of a hidden parameter θ \\theta through which mean rewards of different arms are related to one another. Our correlated bandit framework focuses on structured bandit settings by modeling the correlations explicitly through the knowledge of pseudo-rewards. Recently, best-arm identification was studied under the spectral bandit framework kocak2020best , which assumes that the arms are the nodes of known a weighted graph, with w a , b w_{a,b} denoting the weight between arms a a and arms b b . The spectral bandit framework poses a restriction on the relationship between mean rewards of individual arms by assuming that ∑ a , b ∈ 𝒦 w a , b ​ ( μ a − μ b ) 2 2 ≤ R \\sum_{a,b\\in\\mathcal{K}}w_{a,b}\\frac{(\\mu_{a}-\\mu_{b})^{2}}{2}\\leq R , where R R is known to the player. The correlated bandit model considered in this paper is fundamentally different from the structured bandit framework as detailed below. 1. The model studied here explicitly models the correlations in the rewards of different arms at any given round t t . In structured bandits, the mean rewards are related to each other, but the reward realizations at a given round are not necessarily correlated. Similar to structured bandits, the work on spectral bandits kocak2020best considers a setup with constrains between mean rewards of different arms, but does not capture the correlations explicitly in their framework. 2. It is also possible to use the structured bandit framework for the objective of identify best global recommendation in an ad-campaign. However, there are two major challenges i) In deciding upon the hidden parameter θ \\theta that we need to use, through which the mean rewards are related to one another. ii) Secondly, in the structured bandits framework, the reward mappings from θ \\theta to μ k ​ ( θ ) \\mu_{k}(\\theta) need to be exact . If they happen to be incorrect, then the algorithms for structured bandit cannot be used as they rely on the correctness of μ k ​ ( θ ) \\mu_{k}(\\theta) to construct confidence intervals on the unknown parameter θ \\theta . In contrast, the model studied here only relies on the pseudo-rewards being upper bounds on the conditional expectations 𝔼 ⁡ [ R ℓ | R k = r ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[{R_{\\ell}|R_{k}=r}\\right] . Our proposed algorithm works even when these bounds are not tight. The lack of hidden parameter θ \\theta and pseudo-rewards being upper bounds on conditional expectations make the model studied in this paper more suitable for practical scenarios where the goal is to identify the best global recommendation. 4 Proposed Correlated-LUCB Best-arm Identification Algorithm In the correlated MAB framework, the rewards observed from one arm can help estimate the rewards from other arms. Our key idea is to use this information to reduce the number of samples taken before stopping. We do so by maintaining the empirical pseudo-rewards of all pairs of distinct arms at each round t t . 4.1 Empirical Pseudo-Rewards and New UCB indices In our correlated MAB framework, pseudo-reward of arm ℓ \\ell with respect to arm k k provides us an estimate on the reward of arm ℓ \\ell through the reward sample obtained from arm k k . We now define the notion of empirical pseudo-reward which can be used to obtain an optimistic estimate of μ ℓ \\mu_{\\ell} through just reward samples of arm k k . Definition 2 (Empirical and Expected Pseudo-Reward) . After t t rounds, arm k k is sampled n k ​ ( t ) n_{k}(t) times. Using these n k ​ ( t ) n_{k}(t) reward realizations, we can construct the empirical pseudo-reward ϕ ^ ℓ , k ​ ( t ) \\hat{\\phi}_{\\ell,k}(t) for each arm ℓ \\ell with respect to arm k k as follows. ϕ ^ ℓ , k ​ ( t ) ≜ ∑ τ = 1 t 𝟙 k τ = k ​ s ℓ , k ​ ( r k τ ) n k ​ ( t ) , ℓ ∈ { 1 , … , K } ∖ { k } . \\displaystyle\\hat{\\phi}_{\\ell,k}(t)\\triangleq\\frac{\\sum_{\\tau=1}^{t}\\mathbbm{1}_{k_{\\tau}=k}\\ s_{\\ell,k}(r_{k_{\\tau}})}{n_{k}(t)},\\qquad\\ell\\in\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}\\setminus\\{k\\}. (6) The expected pseudo-reward of arm ℓ \\ell with respect to arm k k is defined as ϕ ℓ , k ≜ 𝔼 ⁡ [ s ℓ , k ​ ( R k ) ] . \\displaystyle\\phi_{\\ell,k}\\triangleq\\mathbb{E}\\left[{s_{\\ell,k}(R_{k})}\\right]. (7) For convenience, we set ϕ ^ k , k ​ ( t ) = μ ^ k ​ ( t ) \\hat{\\phi}_{k,k}(t)=\\hat{\\mu}_{k}(t) and ϕ k , k = μ k \\phi_{k,k}=\\mu_{k} . Note that the empirical pseudo-reward ϕ ^ ℓ , k ​ ( t ) \\hat{\\phi}_{\\ell,k}(t) is defined with respect to arm k k and it is only a function of the rewards observed by sampling arm k k . Observe that 𝔼 ⁡ [ s ℓ , k ​ ( R k ) ] ≥ 𝔼 ⁡ [ 𝔼 ⁡ [ R ℓ | R k = r ] ] = μ ℓ \\mathbb{E}\\left[{s_{\\ell,k}(R_{k})}\\right]\\geq\\mathbb{E}\\left[{\\mathbb{E}\\left[{R_{\\ell}|R_{k}=r}\\right]}\\right]=\\mu_{\\ell} . Due to this, empirical pseudo-reward ϕ ^ ℓ , k ​ ( t ) \\hat{\\phi}_{\\ell,k}(t) can serve as an estimated upper bound on μ ℓ \\mu_{\\ell} . Using the definitions of empirical pseudo-reward, we now define auxiliary UCB indices, namely crossUCB and pseudoUCB indices, which are used in the selection and elimination strategy of the C-LUCB algorithm. Definition 3 (CrossUCB Index U ~ ℓ , k ​ ( t , δ ) \\tilde{U}_{\\ell,k}(t,\\delta) ) . At the end of round t t , we have n k ​ ( t ) n_{k}(t) samples of arm k k . Using these, we define the CrossUCB Index of arm ℓ \\ell with respect to arm k k as U ~ ℓ , k ​ ( t , δ ) ≜ ϕ ^ ℓ , k ​ ( t ) + B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) . \\tilde{U}_{\\ell,k}(t,\\delta)\\triangleq\\hat{\\phi}_{\\ell,k}(t)+B(n_{k},\\delta). (8) Furthermore, we define U ~ ℓ ​ ( t , δ ) = min k ⁡ U ~ ℓ , k ​ ( t , δ ) , \\tilde{U}_{\\ell}(t,\\delta)=\\min_{k}\\tilde{U}_{\\ell,k}(t,\\delta), i.e., the tightest of the K K upper bounds, U ~ ℓ , k ​ ( t , δ ) \\tilde{U}_{\\ell,k}(t,\\delta) , for arm ℓ \\ell . Note that the CrossUCB index for arm ℓ \\ell with respect to arm k k , U ~ ℓ , k ​ ( t , δ ) \\tilde{U}_{\\ell,k}(t,\\delta) is constructed only through the samples obtained from arm k k . Furthermore, we have U ~ k , k ​ ( t , δ ) = μ ^ k ​ ( t ) + B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) \\tilde{U}_{k,k}(t,\\delta)=\\hat{\\mu}_{k}(t)+B(n_{k},\\delta) , which coincides with the standard upper confidence index used in the best-arm identification literature. We use the confidence bound suggested by howard2018timeuniform (see Section 3 ) for the construction of B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) B(n_{k},\\delta) for [ 0 , b ] [0,b] bounded random variables, i.e., B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) = 1.7 ​ b 2 ​ log ⁡ ( log ⁡ ( b 2 ​ n k 2 ) ) + 0.72 ​ log ⁡ ( 5.2 / δ ) n k . B(n_{k},\\delta)=\\frac{1.7b}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log\\left(\\log\\left(\\frac{b^{2}n_{k}}{2}\\right)\\right)+0.72\\log(5.2/\\delta)}{n_{k}}}. (9) As pseudo-rewards are upper bounds on conditional expected reward, they can only be used to construct alternative upper bounds on the mean reward of other arms and not alternative lower bounds. Due to this reason, we keep the definition of lower confidence index L k ​ ( t , δ ) L_{k}(t,\\delta) the same as that in the classical multi-armed bandit setting, i.e., L k ​ ( t , δ ) = μ ^ k ​ ( t ) − B ⁡ ( n k , δ ) L_{k}(t,\\delta)=\\hat{\\mu}_{k}(t)-B(n_{k},\\delta) . In addition to the CrossUCB and the LCB index for each arm, we now define the PseudoUCB index of arm ℓ \\ell with respect to arm k k . The PseudoUCB indices prove useful for the design and analysis of our proposed algorithm. Definition 4 (PseudoUCB Index I ℓ , k ​ ( t ) I_{\\ell,k}(t) ) . We define the PseudoUCB Index of arm ℓ \\ell with respect to arm k k as follows. I ℓ , k ​ ( t ) ≜ ϕ ^ ℓ , k ​ ( t ) + b ​ 2 ​ log ⁡ t n k ​ ( t ) I_{\\ell,k}(t)\\triangleq\\hat{\\phi}_{\\ell,k}(t)+b\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\log t}{n_{k}(t)}} (10) Furthermore, we define I ℓ ​ ( t ) = min k ⁡ I ℓ , k ​ ( t ) I_{\\ell}(t)=\\min_{k}I_{\\ell,k}(t) , the tightest of the K K upper bounds for arm ℓ \\ell . Note that the PseudoUCB Index uses a confidence bound, b ​ 2 ​ log ⁡ t n k ​ ( t ) b\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\log t}{n_{k}(t)}} , which is typically used in the UCB1 algorithm ( auer2002finite ) for the objective of cumulative reward maximization. It has the property that Pr ⁡ ( I ℓ ​ ( t ) &lt; μ ℓ ) ≤ K ​ t − 3 \\Pr(I_{\\ell}(t)&lt;\\mu_{\\ell})\\leq Kt^{-3} [See 3 ], i.e., the probability of mean lying outside the pseudoUCB index I ℓ ​ ( t ) I_{\\ell}(t) at round t t decays exponentially with the number of rounds t t . This property allows us to show desirable sample complexity results for our proposed algorithm in Section 5 . We now present the C-LUCB algorithm, that makes use of the PseudoUCB, CrossUCB and LCB indices in its strategy for sampling arms, eliminating arms and stopping the algorithm. 4.2 C-LUCB Algorithm The C-LUCB algorithm maintains a set of active arms 𝒜 t \\mathcal{A}_{t} , which is initialized to the set of all arms 𝒦 = { 1 , … , K } \\mathcal{K}=\\{1,\\dots,K\\} . At each round t t , it samples arms, eliminates arms and then decides whether to stop as described below. 1. Sampling Strategy: At each round t t , the C-LUCB algorithm samples two arms m 1 ​ ( t ) m_{1}(t) and m 2 ​ ( t ) m_{2}(t) , where m 1 ​ ( t ) = arg ​ max k ∈ 𝒜 t ⁡ I k ​ ( t ) , m 2 ​ ( t ) = arg ​ max k ∈ 𝒜 t ∖ { m 1 ​ ( t ) } ​ min ​ ( U ~ k , k ​ ( t , δ 2 ​ K ) , I k ​ ( t ) ) . m_{1}(t)=\\argmax_{k\\in\\mathcal{A}_{t}}I_{k}(t),\\quad m_{2}(t)=\\argmax_{k\\in\\mathcal{A}_{t}\\setminus\\{m_{1}(t)\\}}\\min\\left(\\tilde{U}_{k,k}\\left(t,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right),I_{k}(t)\\right). 2. Elimination Criteria: The C-LUCB algorithm removes an arm k k from the set 𝒜 t \\mathcal{A}_{t} , if the CrossUCB index of arm k k is smaller than the LCB index of some other arm in 𝒜 t \\mathcal{A}_{t} , i.e., if U ~ k ​ ( t , δ 2 ​ K ) &lt; max ℓ ∈ 𝒜 t ⁡ L ℓ ​ ( t , δ 2 ​ K ) . \\tilde{U}_{k}\\left(t,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)&lt;\\max_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal{A}_{t}}L_{\\ell}\\left(t,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right). Here, U ~ ℓ ​ ( t , δ 2 ​ K ) = min k ⁡ U ~ ℓ , k ​ ( t , δ 2 ​ K ) . \\tilde{U}_{\\ell}\\left(t,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)=\\min_{k}\\tilde{U}_{\\ell,k}\\left(t,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right). 3. Stopping Criteria: If | 𝒜 t | = 1 |\\mathcal{A}_{t}|=1 , stop the algorithm and declare the arm in 𝒜 t