arly. For the sample complexity analysis, we assume that the rewards are bounded between [ 0 , 1 ] ​ ∀ k ∈ 𝒦 [0,1]\\forall{k\\in\\mathcal{K}} . Note that the algorithms do not require this condition and the analysis can also be generalized to any bounded rewards. 5.1 Competitive and Non-competitive arms We now define the notion of competitive and non-competitive arms, which are important to interpret our sample complexity results for the C-LUCB algorithm. Let k ∗ k^{*} denote the arm with the largest mean and k ( 2 ) k^{(2)} denote the arm with the second largest mean. Definition 5 (Non-Competitive and Competitive arms) . An arm ℓ \\ell is said to be non-competitive if the expected reward of the second best arm k ( 2 ) k^{(2)} is strictly larger than the expected pseudo-reward of arm ℓ \\ell with respect to the optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} , i.e, Δ ~ ℓ ≜ ( μ k ( 2 ) − ϕ ℓ , k ∗ ) &gt; 0 \\tilde{\\Delta}_{\\ell}\\triangleq(\\mu_{k^{(2)}}-\\phi_{\\ell,k^{*}})&gt;0 . Similarly, an arm ℓ \\ell is said to be competitive if Δ ~ ℓ = ( μ k ( 2 ) − ϕ ℓ , k ∗ ) ≤ 0 \\tilde{\\Delta}_{\\ell}=(\\mu_{k^{(2)}}-\\phi_{\\ell,k^{*}})\\leq 0 . We refer to Δ ~ ℓ \\tilde{\\Delta}_{\\ell} as the pseudo-gap of arm ℓ \\ell in the rest of the paper. We denote the set of the competitive arms as 𝒞 \\mathcal{C} and the total number of competitive arms as C C in this paper. The best arm k ∗ k^{*} and second best arm k ( 2 ) k^{(2)} have pseudo-gaps Δ ~ k ∗ = ( μ k ( 2 ) − ϕ k ∗ , k ∗ ) &lt; 0 \\tilde{\\Delta}_{k^{*}}=(\\mu_{k^{(2)}}-\\phi_{k^{*},k^{*}})&lt;0 and Δ ~ k ( 2 ) = ( μ k ( 2 ) − ϕ k ( 2 ) , k ∗ ) ≤ 0 \\tilde{\\Delta}_{k^{(2)}}=(\\mu_{k^{(2)}}-\\phi_{k^{(2)},k^{*}})\\leq 0 respectively, and hence are counted in the set of competitive arms. As ϕ ℓ , k ∗ ≥ μ ℓ \\phi_{\\ell,k^{*}}\\geq\\mu_{\\ell} , the pseudo-gap Δ ~ ℓ ≤ Δ ℓ \\tilde{\\Delta}_{\\ell}\\leq\\Delta_{\\ell} . Due to this, we have 2 ≤ C ≤ K 2\\leq C\\leq K . The central idea behind our C-LUCB approach is that after sampling the optimal arm k ∗ k^{*} sufficiently large number of times, the non-competitive (and thus sub-optimal) arms will not be selected as m 1 ​ ( t ) m_{1}(t) or m 2 ​ ( t ) m_{2}(t) by the C-LUCB algorithm, and thus will not be explored explicitly. Furthermore, the non-competitive arms can be eliminated from the information obtained through arm k ∗ k^{*} . As a result, the non-competitive arms contribute only an O ⁡ ( 1 ) \\mathrm{O}(1) term in the sample complexity, i.e., the contribution is independent of the confidence parameter δ \\delta . However, the competitive arms cannot be discerned as sub-optimal by just using the rewards observed from the optimal arm, and have to be explored O ⁡ ( log ⁡ ( 1 δ ) ) \\mathrm{O}\\left(\\log\\left(\\frac{1}{\\delta}\\right)\\right) times each. Thus, we are able to reduce a K K -armed bandit to a C C -armed bandit problem, where C C is the number of competitive arms. 4 4 4 Observe that k ∗ k^{*} and subsequently C C are both unknown to the algorithm. Before the start of the algorithm, it is not known which arm is optimal/competitive/non-competitive. 5.2 Analysis of C-LUCB We start by first proving the ( 1 − δ ) (1-\\delta) -correctness of C-LUCB algorithm and then analyzing its sample complexity in terms of the number of samples obtained until the stopping criterion is satisfied. Theorem 1 ( ( 1 − δ ) (1-\\delta) correctness of C-LUCB) . Upon stopping, the C-LUCB algorithm declares arm k ∗ k^{*} as the best arm with probability 1 − δ 1-\\delta . Proof Sketch. To prove theorem 1, we define three events ℰ 1 , ℰ 2 \\mathcal{E}_{1},\\mathcal{E}_{2} and ℰ 3 \\mathcal{E}_{3} below. Let ℰ 1 \\mathcal{E}_{1} be the event that empirical mean of all arm lie within their confidence intervals uniformly for all t ≥ 1 t\\geq 1 ℰ 1 = { ∀ t ≥ 1 , ∀ k ∈ 𝒦 , μ ^ k ( t ) − B ( n k ( t ) , δ 2 ​ K ) ≤ μ k ≤ μ ^ k + B ( n k ( t ) , δ 2 ​ K ) } \\mathcal{E}_{1}=\\Bigg\\{\\forall{t\\geq 1},\\forall{k\\in\\mathcal{K}},~~~\\hat{\\mu}_{k}(t)-B\\left(n_{k}(t),\\fr