ithin their confidence intervals uniformly for all t ≥ 1 t\\geq 1 ℰ 1 = { ∀ t ≥ 1 , ∀ k ∈ 𝒦 , μ ^ k ( t ) − B ( n k ( t ) , δ 2 ​ K ) ≤ μ k ≤ μ ^ k + B ( n k ( t ) , δ 2 ​ K ) } \\mathcal{E}_{1}=\\Bigg\\{\\forall{t\\geq 1},\\forall{k\\in\\mathcal{K}},~~~\\hat{\\mu}_{k}(t)-B\\left(n_{k}(t),\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)\\leq\\mu_{k}\\leq\\hat{\\mu}_{k}+B\\left(n_{k}(t),\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)\\Bigg\\} (87) Define ℰ 2 \\mathcal{E}_{2} to be the event that empirical pseudo-reward of optimal arm with respect to all other arms lie within their crossUCB indices uniformly for all t ≥ 1 t\\geq 1 , i.e., ℰ 2 = { ∀ t ≥ 1 , ∀ ℓ ∈ 𝒦 , ϕ k ∗ , ℓ ≤ ϕ ^ k ∗ , ℓ ( t ) + B ( n ℓ ( t ) , δ 2 ​ K ) } \\mathcal{E}_{2}=\\Bigg\\{\\forall{t\\geq 1},\\forall{\\ell\\in\\mathcal{K}},~~~\\phi_{k^{*},\\ell}\\leq\\hat{\\phi}_{k^{*},\\ell}(t)+B\\left(n_{\\ell}(t),\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)\\Bigg\\} (88) Similarly define ℰ 3 \\mathcal{E}_{3} to be the event that the empirical pseudo-reward of the sub-optimal arms with respect to the optimal arm lies within their crossUCB indices uniformly for all t ≥ 1 t\\geq 1 , i.e., ℰ 3 = { ∀ t ≥ 1 , ∀ ℓ ∈ 𝒦 , ϕ ℓ , k ∗ ≤ ϕ ^ ℓ , k ∗ ( t ) + B ( n k ∗ ( t ) , δ 2 ​ K ) } \\mathcal{E}_{3}=\\Bigg\\{\\forall{t\\geq 1},\\forall{\\ell\\in\\mathcal{K}},~~~\\phi_{\\ell,k^{*}}\\leq\\hat{\\phi}_{\\ell,k^{*}}(t)+B\\left(n_{k^{*}}(t),\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)\\Bigg\\} (89) Furthermore, we define ℰ \\mathcal{E} to be the intersection of the three events, i.e., ℰ = ℰ 1 ∩ ℰ 2 ∩ ℰ 3 . \\mathcal{E}=\\mathcal{E}_{1}\\cap\\mathcal{E}_{2}\\cap\\mathcal{E}_{3}. (90) Due to the nature of anytime confidence intervals (See Equation 4 ) and union bound over the set of arms, we have Pr ⁡ ( ℰ 1 c ) ≤ δ 2 \\Pr(\\mathcal{E}^{c}_{1})\\leq\\frac{\\delta}{2} , Pr ⁡ ( ℰ 2 c ) ≤ δ 4 \\Pr(\\mathcal{E}_{2}^{c})\\leq\\frac{\\delta}{4} and Pr ⁡ ( ℰ 3 c ) ≤ δ 4 \\Pr(\\mathcal{E}^{c}_{3})\\leq\\frac{\\delta}{4} giving us Pr ⁡ ( ℰ c ) ≤ δ \\Pr(\\mathcal{E}^{c})\\leq\\delta . We now show that under the event ℰ \\mathcal{E} , the C-LUCB algorithm cannot stop with an arm k ≠ k ∗ k\\neq k^{*} . We do that through a proof by contradiction. Suppose, the algorithm stops with arm k ≠ k ∗ k\\neq k^{*} , i.e., arm k k is the only arm in set 𝒜 t \\mathcal{A}_{t} . In such a scenario, ∃ τ , k ≠ k ∗ : U ~ k ∗ ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) &lt; L k ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) \\exists\\tau,k\\neq k^{*}:\\tilde{U}_{k^{*}}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)&lt;L_{k}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right) . This can only occur if one of the following events occur, 1. OPEN U ~ k ∗ , k ∗ ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) &lt; L k ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) ) \\tilde{U}_{k^{*},k^{*}}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)&lt;L_{k}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)) 2. U ~ k ∗ , ℓ ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) &lt; L k ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) ℓ ≠ k ∗ \\tilde{U}_{k^{*},\\ell}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)&lt;L_{k}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)\\quad\\ell\\neq k^{*} See that under the event ℰ \\mathcal{E} , U ~ k ∗ , ℓ ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) &gt; μ k ∗ \\tilde{U}_{k^{*},\\ell}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)&gt;\\mu_{k^{*}} and L k ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) &lt; μ k ∀ τ , k L_{k}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)&lt;\\mu_{k}\\quad\\forall{\\tau,k} . This shows that under the event ℰ \\mathcal{E} , U ~ k ∗ ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) &gt; L k ​ ( τ , δ 2 ​ K ) ∀ k , τ \\tilde{U}_{k^{*}}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)&gt;L_{k}\\left(\\tau,\\frac{\\delta}{2K}\\right)\\quad\\forall{k,\\tau} as μ k ∗ &gt; μ k ∀ k ≠ k ∗ \\mu_{k^{*}}&gt;\\mu_{k}\\quad\\forall{k\\neq k^{*}} . This implies that the algorithm returns the best arm with probability at least 1 − δ 1-\\delta as Pr ⁡ ( ℰ c ) ≤ δ \\Pr(\\mathcal{E}^{c})\\leq\\delta . ∎ Appendix G 1 − δ 1-\\delta Correctness of C-LUCB++ We now show that the C-LUCB++ algorithm declares the arm k ∗ k^{*} as the best arm with probability at least 1 − δ 1-\\delta . Proof. To prove the correctness of C-LUCB++, we use similar arguments as done in the proof of Theorem 1 for the C-LUCB