p;P500 fund, one fifth in the bond fund, and the remaining three fifths held in cash, accruing interest payment at the risk-free rate. Alternately, a leverage vector of ( 0.2 , 1.8 ) (0.2,1.8) would indicate that we would invest one fifth of initial capital in the S&amp;P500 fund, borrow an amount equal to the initial capital, and invest the four fifths remaining initial capital, along with the borrowed sum, in the bond fund. 2.1 Objective There are many possible investment objectives. For example, one could attempt to minimize the probability of ultimately losing all their capital, attempt to maximize the expected return relative to some benchmark, maximize some utility function of wealth, etc. In this paper we aim for good long-term growth profiles for our capital { A t } \\{A_{t}\\} . To state this more precisely, we will be particularly interested in the expected log-return per unit time L = 𝐄 ⁡ [ log ⁡ ( A t + δ / A t ) ] / δ L={{\\bf E}\\left[\\log(A_{t+\\delta}/A_{t})\\right]}/\\delta (11) and log-return variance per unit time V = Var ​ ( log ⁡ ( A t + δ / A t ) ) / δ . V=\\mbox{\\bf Var}(\\log(A_{t+\\delta}/A_{t}))/\\delta. (12) As we will see below, this approach leads us to a convenient stochastic calculus-based derivation of Kelly’s formula, and yields additional useful insights. Direct maximization of L L (with no regard for V V ) yields the so-called growth-optimal portfolio , also referred to as the Kelly portfolio . In the long-run, the growth-optimal portfolio almost-surely leads to more wealth than any competing portfolio. 2 2 2 More formally, if { A t } \\{A_{t}\\} represents the capital associated with the growth-optimal portfolio, and { A t ′ } \\{A^{\\prime}_{t}\\} represents the capital from another portfolio in the same instruments, chosen in a different manner, then under a modest set of regularity conditions, we can show that ( A t ′ / A t ) → a . s . 0 (A^{\\prime}_{t}/A_{t})\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle a.s.}}{{\\rightarrow}}0 as t → ∞ . t\\rightarrow\\infty. The price to pay for this growth dominance however, is relatively large up and down-swings in { A t } \\{A_{t}\\} . Fractional Kelly schemes , which simply apply a fractional multiplier to the growth-optimal portfolio leverage vector, provide one way to address this problem. 2.2 Mathematical Analysis We are now in a position to analyze the impact of leverage choice. For the sake of exposition, we address the univariate case before developing the multivariate case. 2.2.1 Univariate Case In the univariate ( m = 1 m=1 ) case, we can write down a formal stochastic differential equation to describe the price { P t } \\{P_{t}\\} of an instrument over time t ∈ ℝ , t ≥ 0 , t\\in{\\mathbb{R}},t\\geq 0, as d ​ P t \\displaystyle dP_{t} = \\displaystyle= μ ​ P t ​ d ​ t + σ ​ P t ​ d ​ W t , \\displaystyle\\mu P_{t}dt+\\sigma P_{t}dW_{t}, (13) P 0 \\displaystyle P_{0} = \\displaystyle= 1 , \\displaystyle 1, (14) where { W t } \\{W_{t}\\} is a standard Brownian motion. The solution to ( 13 , 14 ) is a geometric Brownian motion, for which d ​ log ⁡ ( P t ) = ( μ − σ 2 / 2 ) ​ d ​ t + σ ​ d ​ W t . d\\log(P_{t})=(\\mu-\\sigma^{2}/2)dt+\\sigma dW_{t}. (15) (This follows directly from an application of Itô’s formula to ( 13 , 14 ).) The process has several important properties, which we will simply state here without proof. 1. It provides a realistic description of many real-life instruments that can be bought as investments. The parameters μ \\mu and σ \\sigma vary, however, over different investments and arguably also over time for a particular investment. 2. P t &gt; 0 . P_{t}&gt;0. 3. The log-return of the price satisfies log ⁡ ( P t + δ / P t ) ∼ N ​ ( μ ​ δ − σ 2 ​ δ / 2 , σ 2 ​ δ ) . \\log(P_{t+\\delta}/P_{t})\\sim\\mbox{N}(\\mu\\delta-\\sigma^{2}\\delta/2,\\sigma^{2}\\delta). (16) Now let us consider the behavior of total capital { A t , t ≥ 0 } \\{A_{t},~t\\geq 0\\} over time when we invest a fraction k k (leverage) in the instrument whose price P t P_{t} is given by ( 13 , 14 ). It follows directly from ( 10 ) that d ​ A t \\displaystyle dA_{t} = \\displaystyle= ( 1 − k ) ​ r ​ A t ​ d ​ t + k ​ A t ​ d ​ P t / P t \\displaystyle(1-k)rA_{t}dt+kA_{t}dP_{t}/P_{t} (17) = \\displaystyle= [ ( 1 − k ) ​ r + k ​ μ ] ​ A t ​ d ​ t + k ​ A t ​ σ ​ d ​ W t . \\displaystyle[(1-k)r+k\\mu]A_{t}dt+kA_{t}\\sigma dW_{t}. (18) In other words, like the underlying instrument price, our amount of capital A t A_{t} also follows a geometric Brownian motion process, but with different parameters. Consequently, d ​ log ⁡ ( A t ) = [ ( 1 − k ) ​ r + k ​ μ − k 2 ​ σ 2 / 2 ] ​ d ​ t + k ​ σ ​ d ​ W t . d\\log(A_{t})=[(1-k)r+k\\mu-k^{2}\\sigma^{2}/2]dt+k\\sigma dW_{t}. (19) It is very important to note 3 3 3 It is a common mistake to presume, for example, that a triple-leverage investment yields triple the log-returns over time. This is not the case. that d ​ log ⁡ ( A t ) ≠ k ⋅ d ​ log ⁡ ( P t ) . d\\log(A_{t})\\neq k\\cdot d\\log(P_{t}). (20) It follows directly from ( 19 ) that 1. A t &gt; 0 . A_{t}&gt;0. This is our guarantee that we do not lose more than our initial capital. However, note that it relies on the unrealistic assumption that we are able to carry out continuous reinvestment of profits/losses. 2. The log of total amount of capital (including reinvested profits/losses) satisfies log ⁡ ( A t + δ / A t ) ∼ N ​ ( r ​ δ + k ⁡ ( μ − r ) ​ δ − k 2 ​ σ 2 ​ δ / 2 , k 2 ​ σ 2 ​ δ ) . \\log(A_{t+\\delta}/A_{t})\\sim\\mbox{N}(r\\delta+k(\\mu-r)\\delta-k^{2}\\sigma^{2}\\delta/2,k^{2}\\sigma^{2}\\delta). (21) At any time t t , equation ( 21 ) provides a predictive distribution for our amount of capital at a time point ( t + δ ) (t+\\delta) in the future. Specifically, it is log-normal. Following on from ( 21 ), we see that the expected log-return per unit time of our capital is L ⁡ ( k ) = 𝐄 ⁡ [ log ⁡ ( A t + δ / A t ) ] / δ = r + k ⁡ ( μ − r ) − k 2 ​ σ 2 / 2 . L(k)={{\\bf E}\\left[\\log(A_{t+\\delta}/A_{t})\\right]}/\\delta=r+k(\\mu-r)-k^{