f e}_{m}, where 𝐞 m = ( 1 , 1 , … , 1 ) T ∈ ℝ m . {\\bf e}_{m}=(1,1,\\ldots,1)^{T}\\in{\\mathbb{R}}^{m}. Applying ( 10 ), it is straightforward to show that there is another standard Brownian motion { W t } \\{W_{t}\\} such that the total capital { A t } \\{A_{t}\\} satisfies d ​ A t \\displaystyle dA_{t} = \\displaystyle= ( ( 1 − 𝐤 ⋅ 𝐞 m ) ​ r + 𝐤 ⋅ μ ) ​ A t ​ d ​ t + ( 𝐤 T ​ Σ ​ 𝐤 ) 1 / 2 ​ A t ​ d ​ W t \\displaystyle((1-{\\bf k}\\cdot{\\bf e}_{m})r+{\\bf k}\\cdot{\\bf\\mu})A_{t}dt+({\\bf k}^{T}\\Sigma{\\bf k})^{1/2}A_{t}dW_{t} (28) = \\displaystyle= ( r − 𝐤 ⋅ 𝐫 + 𝐤 ⋅ μ ) ​ A t ​ d ​ t + ( 𝐤 T ​ Σ ​ 𝐤 ) 1 / 2 ​ A t ​ d ​ W t , \\displaystyle(r-{\\bf k}\\cdot{\\bf r}+{\\bf k}\\cdot{\\bf\\mu})A_{t}dt+({\\bf k}^{T}\\Sigma{\\bf k})^{1/2}A_{t}dW_{t}, (29) and it follows directly from application of Itô’s formula that d ​ log ⁡ ( A t ) = ( r + 𝐤 ⋅ ( μ − 𝐫 ) − 𝐤 T ​ Σ ​ 𝐤 / 2 ) ​ d ​ t + ( 𝐤 T ​ Σ ​ 𝐤 ) 1 / 2 ​ d ​ W t , d\\log(A_{t})=(r+{\\bf k}\\cdot({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r})-{\\bf k}^{T}\\Sigma{\\bf k}/2)dt+({\\bf k}^{T}\\Sigma{\\bf k})^{1/2}dW_{t}, (30) so the expected log-return per unit time is given by L ⁡ ( 𝐤 ) = 𝐄 ⁡ [ log ⁡ ( A t + δ / A t ) ] / δ = r + 𝐤 ⋅ ( μ − 𝐫 ) − 𝐤 T ​ Σ ​ 𝐤 / 2 , L({\\bf k})={{\\bf E}\\left[\\log(A_{t+\\delta}/A_{t})\\right]}/\\delta=r+{\\bf k}\\cdot({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r})-{\\bf k}^{T}\\Sigma{\\bf k}/2, (31) Consequently Kelly’s formula becomes 𝐤 ∗ = Σ − 1 ​ ( μ − 𝐫 ) , {\\bf k}^{*}=\\Sigma^{-1}({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r}), (32) with maximum expected log-return per unit time L ⁡ ( 𝐤 ∗ ) = r + 1 2 ​ ( μ − 𝐫 ) T ​ Σ − 1 ​ ( μ − 𝐫 ) . L({\\bf k}^{*})=r+\\frac{1}{2}({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r})^{T}\\Sigma^{-1}({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r}). (33) We will also be interested in the variance of the log-return per unit time. From ( 30 ), this is easily seen to be V ⁡ ( 𝐤 ∗ ) = Var ​ ( log ⁡ ( A t + δ / A t ) ) / δ = 𝐤 T ​ Σ ​ 𝐤 . V({\\bf k}^{*})=\\mbox{\\bf Var}(\\log(A_{t+\\delta}/A_{t}))/\\delta={\\bf k}^{T}\\Sigma{\\bf k}. (34) Readers may note that ( 32 ) is recognizable as the solution of a standard Markowitz mean-variance portfolio weight selection problem ( Markowitz 1952 , see) . 2.3 Sharpe Ratios and Fractional Kelly Investment In the finance industry, in the univariate case, it is common practice to refer to the quantity ( μ − r ) / σ (\\mu-r)/\\sigma , as the Sharpe ratio of the investment. 4 4 4 It is important to differentiate between this quantity, and estimators thereof. Unfortunately, people often use the same term to refer to both the quantity and its estimator(s). More generally, we will define the Sharpe ratio of a single investment or a set of investments as S = [ ( μ − 𝐫 ) T ​ Σ − 1 ​ ( μ − 𝐫 ) ] 1 / 2 , S=[({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r})^{T}\\Sigma^{-1}({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r})]^{1/2}, (35) where μ {\\bf\\mu} and Σ \\Sigma are the parameters defining the growth and volatility of the component investments, and 𝐫 {\\bf r} is the vector whose elements are all equal to the risk-free rate. (Note that the one-dimensional multivariate expression is consistent with the univariate definition.) Sharpe ratios are clearly important because the maximum growth rates in ( 24 , 33 ) increase quadratically with respect to the Sharpe ratio. We have seen that Kelly’s formula prescribes the leverage that maximizes expected log-return per unit time L L of an investment. However, to limit up/down-swings in capital, it is desirable to operate with a lower total level of risk. To achieve this, we can apply a multiplier to ( 32 ), 𝐤 α = α ​ Σ − 1 ​ ( μ − 𝐫 ) , α ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] . {\\bf k}_{\\alpha}=\\alpha\\Sigma^{-1}({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r}),\\quad\\alpha\\in[0,1]. (36) This quantity is often referred to as fractional Kelly leverage. In this light, fractional Kelly investment can be viewed as a particular means of leverage selection, with α \\alpha specifying the level of risk relative to that required for the growth-optimal portfolio. The following result makes a formal connection between leverage, log-returns and Sharpe ratios. Theorem 2.1 (Sharpe-Leverage Performance Profile) Suppose that we apply fractional Kelly leverage ( 36 ) with multiplier α ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] \\alpha\\in[0,1] to the portfolio with prices governed by ( 25 ), earning/paying the risk-free interest rate r r on the cash/debt component. Then the resulting capital process { A t } \\{A_{t}\\} is a geometric Brownian motion satisfying d ​ log ⁡ ( A t ) = [ r + ( α − α 2 / 2 ) ​ S 2 ] ​ d ​ t + α ​ S ​ d ​ W t , d\\log(A_{t})=[r+(\\alpha-\\alpha^{2}/2)S^{2}]dt+\\alpha SdW_{t}, (37) where S = [ ( μ − 𝐫 ) T ​ Σ − 1 ​ ( μ − 𝐫 ) ] 1 / 2 S=[({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r})^{T}\\Sigma^{-1}({\\bf\\mu}-{\\bf r})]^{1/2} denotes the Sharpe ratio of the portfolio. Consequently, { A t } \\{A_{t}\\} has expected log-return per unit time L ⁡ ( 𝐤 α ) = 𝐄 ⁡ [ log ⁡ ( A t + δ / A t ) ] / δ = r + ( α − α 2 / 2 ) ​ S 2 L({\\bf k}_{\\alpha})={{\\bf E}\\left[\\log(A_{t+\\delta}/A_{t})\\right]}/\\delta=r+(\\alpha-\\alpha^{2}/2)S^{2} (38) and log-return variance per unit time V ⁡ ( 𝐤 α ) = Var ​ ( log ⁡ ( A t + δ / A t ) ) / δ = α 2 ​ S 2 . V({\\bf k}_{\\alpha})=\\mbox{\\bf Var}(\\log(A_{t+\\delta}/A_{t}))/\\delta=\\alpha^{2}S^{2}. (39) Proof: The derivation of ( 30 ) above establishes that { A t } \\{A_{t}\\} is a geometric Brownian motion. Substituting ( 36 ) into ( 30 ) and making use of the definition ( 35 ) yields ( 37 ). The remaining results then follow directly. □ \\Box When the conditions of Theorem 2.1 are met and α = 1 , \\alpha=1, we obtain the growth-optimal portfolio. More generally, equations ( 38 , 39 ) show the trade-off we obtain with different values of α . \\alpha. Values closer to one yield higher expected log-return per unit time on capital, but values closer to zero give better (reduced) variance of the log-return around the mean. 3 Examples and Applications We now consider some practical implications of the theory outlined above, beginning with analysis that would be of interest to a typical individual with some savings to invest. 3.1 Equity and Bonds Mix Let us consider a portfolio consisting of only two instruments: { P t , 1 } \\{P_{t,1}\\} will represent the price of Vanguard 500 Mutual Fund Index shares (VFIAX), and { P t , 2 } \\{P_{t,2}\\} will represent the price of Vanguard Long-Term Treasury Fund shares (VUSUX). Figure 1: Equity (VFIAX) and Bond (VUSUX) performance from Feb. 12th, 2001 to Aug. 24th, 2021. Data source: Yahoo Finance. Figure 1 shows differences of logarithms of adjusted daily closing prices for these two instruments from 2001 to 2021. This time period covers the dot-com bubble of 2002 and the sub-prime crisis of 2008, as well as more recent market turbulence at the onset of the coronavirus pandemic in early 2020. In what follows, we will assume that the risk-free rate r r is equal to zero. This is a fairly reasonable approximation for the post-sub-prime period from 2008 to 2021, although results here can be easily adapted if r r is assumed to be non-zero. 3.1.1 Parameter Estimation From the raw data shown in the plots in Figure 1 , we can easily compute estimators of μ = ( μ 1 , μ 2 ) {\\bf\\mu}=(\\mu_{1},\\mu_{2}) , σ = ( σ 1 , σ 2 ) {\\bf\\sigma}=(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2}) and R R . Let the unit of time be one year, assume there are 260 trading days each year, and define the daily log-returns (shown in the left part of Figure 1 ) as D t , j = log ( P ( t + 1 ) / 260 , j ) − log ( P t / 260 , j ) , t = 1 , 2 , … , n − 1 , D_{t,j}=\\log(P_{(t+1)/260,j})-\\log(P_{t/260,j}),\\quad t=1,2,\\ldots,n-1, (40) where n n is the total number of trading days of data. We know from ( 16 ) that D t , j ∼ N ​ ( ( μ j − σ j 2 / 2 ) / 260 , σ 2 / 260 ) . D_{t,j}\\sim\\mbox{N}((\\mu_{j}-\\sigma^{2}_{j}/2)/260,\\sigma^{2}/260). (41) Furthermore, Corr ​ ( D t , 1 , D t , 2 ) = R 12 . \\mbox{Corr}(D_{t,1},D_{t,2})=R_{12}. (42) We can therefore take sample mean and variance from our two daily price series, compute sample correlation, and use them to construct method-of-moments estimators σ ^ j 2 \\displaystyle\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}_{j} = \\displaystyle= 260 n − 2 ​ ∑ t = 1 n − 1 ( D t , j − D ¯ j ) 2 , D ¯ j = 1 n − 1 ​ ∑ t = 1 n − 1 D t , j , \\displaystyle{260\\over n-2}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-1}(D_{t,j}-\\bar{D}_{j})^{2},\\quad\\bar{D}_{j}={1\\over n-1}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-1}D_{t,j}, (43) σ ^ j \\displaystyle\\hat{\\sigma}_{j} = \\displaystyle= σ ^ j 2 , \\displaystyle\\sqrt{\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}_{j}}, (44) μ ^ j \\displaystyle\\hat{\\mu}_{j} = \\displaystyle= 260 ​ D ¯ j + σ ^ j 2 / 2 , \\displaystyle 260\\bar{D}_{j}+\\hat{\\sigma}^{2}_{j}/2, (45) R ^ j ​ k \\displaystyle\\hat{R}_{jk} = \\displaystyle= 260 σ ^ j ​ σ ^ k ​ ( n − 2 ) ​ ∑ t = 1 n − 1 ( D t , j − D ¯ j ) ​ ( D t , k − D ¯ k ) \\displaystyle{260\\over\\hat{\\sigma}_{j}\\hat{\\sigma}_{k}(n-2)}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-1}(D_{t,j}-\\bar{D}_{j})(D_{t,k}-\\bar{D}_{k}) (46) With the raw data described above, we obtain μ ^ = ( 0.099 , 0.051 ) T , σ ^ = ( 0.199 , 0.123 ) T , R ^ 12 = − 0.377 . \\hat{{\\bf\\mu}}=(0.099,0.051)^{T},\\quad\\hat{{\\bf\\sigma}}=(0.199,0.123)^{T},\\quad\\hat{R}_{12}=-0.377. (47) From a practical perspective, we also want to consider the different impact of taxation on these two instruments. In the United States, tax on a mutual fund that tracks the S&amp;P500 is typically close to the long-term capital gain rate of 20%, while tax on bond funds, although more complex to compute, is typically closer to standard income tax, which is often around 40%. To approximate the impact of taxation, we will therefore adjust the estimates ( 47 ) above by multiplying components of μ \\mu by one minus the corresponding tax rate, giving us final estimates μ ^ = ( 0.079 , 0.031 ) , Σ ^ = [ 0.0396 − 0.0093 − 0.0093 0.0152 ] . \\hat{{\\bf\\mu}}=(0.079,0.031),\\quad\\hat{\\Sigma}=\\left[\\begin{array}[]{cc}\\hphantom{-}0.0396&amp;-0.0093\\\\ -0.0093&amp;\\hphantom{-}0.0152\\end{array}\\right]. (48) From these estimates, in turn, we can determine the Kelly leverage for the growth-optimal portfolio by applying ( 32 ), obtaining 𝐤 ∗ = Σ ^ − 1 ​ μ ^ ≃ ( 2.89 , 3.78 ) T . {\\bf k}^{*}=\\hat{\\Sigma}^{-1}\\hat{{\\bf\\mu}}\\simeq(2.89,3.78)^{T}. (49) If μ {\\bf\\mu} and Σ \\Sigma were indeed the same as these estimates, and if we could borrow money to provide leverage, this says we could maximize growth over time of capital by borrowing 5.67 5.67 times our initial capital (assuming a no-interest loan) to obtain total leverage of 2.89 + 3.78 = 6.67 2.89+3.78=6.67 . We would then invest the original capital along with the borrowed capital into the two components of the portfolio, rebalancing to hold the fraction of capital in the two components constant. The resulting expected annual log-return on initial (non-borrowed) capital would be 𝐤 ∗ ⋅ μ ^ − 𝐤 ∗ T ​ Σ ^ ​ 𝐤 ∗ / 2 ≃ 0.172 {\\bf k}^{*}\\cdot\\hat{{\\bf\\mu}}-{{\\bf k}^{*}}^{T}\\hat{\\Sigma}{\\bf k}^{*}/2\\simeq 0.172 (50) In this argument we have pretended that there is no estimation error. In fact, that source of noise can have a significant impact, but that analysis is beyond the scope of this paper. 3.1.2 Leverage Value Configurations We now consider a range of different investment possibilities. Some of these will be applied to only one of our two instruments, and for these cases we will use m = 1 m=1 along with the corresponding (marginal) components of μ ^ \\hat{{\\bf\\mu}} , Σ ^ \\hat{\\Sigma} and the corresponding estimate S ^ \\hat{S} of Sharpe ratio. In the more interesting cases we will examine portfolios consisting of both instruments. We consider the following cases. Leverage Drift/Diffusion Kelly-Fraction Name k k μ ^ \\hat{\\mu} Σ ^ \\hat{\\Sigma} S ^ \\hat{S} α ^ \\hat{\\alpha} Single-instrument ( m = 1 m=1 ) portfolios Non-Leveraged Equity 1.00 0.079 0.199 2 0.199^{2} 0.398 0.502 Double-Equity 2.00 0.079 0.199 2 0.199^{2} 0.398 1.003 Triple-Equity 3.00 0.079 0.199 2 0.199^{2} 0.398 1.505 Non-Leveraged Bonds 1.00 0.031 0.123 2 0.123^{2} 0.252 0.488 Double-Bonds 2.00 0.031 0.123 2 0.123^{2} 0.252 0.976 Two-instrument ( m = 2 m=2 ) portfolios Fractional Kelly(0.30) (0.87,1.13) (0.079,0.031) Σ ^ \\hat{\\Sigma} (eqn 48 ) 0.588 0.30 Constr. Kelly(2.0) (1.33,0.67) (0.079,0.031) Σ ^ \\hat{\\Sigma} (eqn 48 ) 0.588 NA Full-Kelly (2.89,3.78) (0.079,0.031) Σ ^ \\hat{\\Sigma} (eqn 48 ) 0.588 1.0 Table 1: Example portfolios. Cases are constructed as follows. • Non-leveraged/Double/Triple Equities/Bonds: The non-leveraged approach invests all capital directly into the equities/bonds instrument. Double and triple versions invest all capital in the same instrument but with leverage 2 2 or 3 3 , respectively. • Fractional Kelly (0.30): This applies leverage α = 0.30 \\alpha=0.30 times the full-Kelly leverage. In this example, it results in total leverage κ \\kappa approximately equal to 2.0 . 2.0. • Constrained Kelly (2.0): Here we choose 𝐤 {\\bf k} so as to maximize expected growth L L subject to the constraint that κ = 2 . \\kappa=2. This can be done using Lagrange multipliers, as described in Appendix A.1 . • Full-Kelly: This choice, given by ( 49 ) leads to maximum growth rate over time, at the cost of high variance and draw-downs. Table 1 also shows a term α ^ = 𝐤 / ( Σ ^ − 1 ​ μ ^ ) , \\hat{\\alpha}={\\bf k}/(\\hat{\\Sigma}^{-1}\\hat{{\\bf\\mu}}), (51) which is only defined when the leverage vector 𝐤 {\\bf k} is a multiple of Σ ^ − 1 ​ μ ^ \\hat{\\Sigma}^{-1}\\hat{{\\bf\\mu}} , and in that case represents an estimate of the ratio of portfolio leverage to growth-optimal leverage. Values less than 1.0 1.0 are desirable in this context, since it is obvious from ( 38 ) and ( 39 ) that α &gt; 1 \\alpha&gt;1 implies we are taking unnecessary extra risk for a return that can also be achieved with α &lt; 1 . \\alpha&lt;1. 3.1.3 Simulation Results It is straightforward to simulate hypothetical capital invested in the portfolios corresponding to the test cases. Using data ranging from Feb 13th, 2001 to March 18th, 2021, we simply compute the daily portfolio returns using the recorded data. In order to approximate the tax-effect on the two components, we also apply a negative drift by subtracting the tax adjustment used on μ {\\bf\\mu} in ( 48 ) on a daily basis from the log-returns of the data. Figure 2: Simulated post-tax capital of hypothetical portfolios built of VFIAX and VUSUX, assuming starting capital of $100000, for a range of different leverage vectors. Figure 2 shows cumulative capital { A t } \\{A_{t}\\} over time for a range of different leverage settings. The full-Kelly leverage case is omitted from the plot since it distorts the scale, but the full-Kelly case is included in Figure 3 , which shows the log of cumulative capital. Figure 3: Simulated post-tax log-return of hypothetical portfolios built of VFIAX and VUSUX, for a range of different leverage vectors. The full-Kelly case clearly dominates the others in terms of growth, but it also loses 87.7% of its capital value from Dec. 5th, 2007 to Mar. 6th, 2009 during the sub-prime crisis. The simple triple-leverage equity investment also stands out with its low return and 96.3% loss from Feb 14, 2001 to Mar 6, 2009. Growth Draw-down Name L ^ \\hat{L} V ^ 1 / 2 {\\hat{V}}^{1/2} Max. Start End Final Value Single-instrument ( m = 1 m=1 ) portfolios Non-Leveraged Equity 0.060 0.200 56.4% Oct 8 2007 Mar 6 2009 $328,143 Double-Equity 0.081 0.400 84.6% Oct 8 2007 Mar 6 2009 $486,418 Triple-Equity 0.060 0.603 96.4% Feb 14 2001 Mar 6 2009 $319,574 Non-Leveraged Bonds 0.023 0.123 28.3% Mar 6 2020 Mar 30 2021 $158,109 Double-Bonds 0.031 0.247 50.7% Mar 6 2020 Mar 30 2021 $184,841 Two-instrument ( m = 2 m=2 ) portfolios Fractional Kelly(0.30) 0.089 0.176 41.9% Dec 5 2007 Mar 6 2009 $576,464 Constr. Kelly(2.0) 0.097 0.240 62.7% Oct 8 2007 Mar 6 2009 $675,775 Full-Kelly 0.172 0.594 89.8% Dec 5 2007 Mar 6 2009 $3,002,829 Table 2: Summary of simulated performance of example portfolios derived from the VFIAX and VUSUX instruments, simulated from Feb. 12th, 2001 to Aug. 24th, 2021, with initial capital $ 100,000 . \\$100,000. Risk-free interest rate is assumed to be zero. L ^ \\hat{L} denotes the sample average of the annualized log-return, V ^ 1 / 2 {\\hat{V}}^{1/2} denotes the annualized sample standard deviation of those log-returns, and the maximum draw-down is defined as the maximum drop from peak to trough with trough occurring after the peak. Table 2 shows corresponding summary statistics, including the sample (annualized) growth rate L ^ \\hat{L} , the corresponding sample variance V ^ \\hat{V} , as well as the final value and maximum draw-down over the time period, which is defined as the maximum peak-to-trough drop such that the trough occurs after the peak. From a psychological perspective, investors pay significant attention to difference between the historical maximum and the current value of an investment. Large values of this “draw-down” typically induce great anxiety. Several important observations can be made from the results. First, the full-Kelly case and the triple-equity case both exhibit very large maximum draw-downs over this time period - 89.8% and 96.4%, respectively. Few investors would hold onto an investment after losses that large. Both the fractional Kelly and the constrained Kelly options appear to give a fairly good compromise between greater growth and higher variance/draw-downs. Of course different investors may different preferences, but it is notable that of the choices in Table 2 , apart from the non-leveraged bond case, the fractional Kelly option has the smallest value of V ^ \\hat{V} and still yields a respectable growth rate L ^ . \\hat{L}. 3.2 Application to Fund Evaluation Hedge fund managers generally release very little information about the nature of their trading operations. However, they often release yearly or quarterly statments of actual returns on their hedge funds. We have already seen that Theorem 2.1 assists in choosing a desirable leverage vector. Here we see how it leads to a simple method-of-moments approach to estimate portfolio Sharpe ratio and risk deployment of a fund, using only such publicly available return data. The approach relies on an implicit assumption that the fund is applying a fractional Kelly approach, or something similar. In light of the previous observation at the end of Subsection 2.2.2 that fractional Kelly leverage is equivalent to industry-standard Markowitz mean-variance optimization, this is not an unrealistic assumption. This “reverse-engineering” allows us to evaluate funds in a more nuanced way than simple inspection of past returns. For example, we can easily differentiate between funds that obtain high returns by taking excessive risk, and those that obtain high returns by deployment of high-Sharpe ratio portfolios. Consider, for example, the annual returns of the well-known (and very strongly performing) Renaissance Medallion fund. These returns are publicly available in Appendix 1 of Zuckerman 2019 , and can easily be translated into log-returns and viewed in light of equations ( 38 ) and ( 39 ). Without re-printing the full table itself, we give summary statistics of their resulting (before-fee) log-returns in Table 3 below. Date Range 1988-2018 inclusive # Data Points 31 Average Log-Return 0.490 Std. Dev. (Log-Return) 0.187 Table 3: Summary statistics of Renaissance Medallion Fund returns as given in Zuckerman 2019 . Re-arranging the pair of equations ( 38 , 39 ), we see that for a fractional Kelly investor, α \\displaystyle\\alpha = \\displaystyle= 2 ​ V ​ ( 2 ​ L + V ) − 1 \\displaystyle 2V(2L+V)^{-1} (52) S 2 \\displaystyle S^{2} = \\displaystyle= α − 1 ​ ( L + V / 2 ) . \\displaystyle\\alpha^{-1}(L+V/2). (53) By simply matching L = 0.490 L=0.490 and V = 0.187 2 V=0.187^{2} from the table above, we see that the Medallion returns are consistent with deployment of a portfolio with Sharpe ratio S ≃ 2.72 S\\simeq 2.72 at approximately α = 0.068 \\alpha=0.068 times the Kelly-optimal leverage. The (estimated) value of 0.068 0.068 places them comfortably below the obvious danger point. A value equal to 1.0 1.0 would indicate deployment of risk at the growth-optimal level, with its extreme volatility. Such a value is not palatable to a typical investor. Any value larger than 1.0 1.0 would indicate a sub-optimal deployment of risk. A value larger than 2.0 2.0 would indicate that the fund will likely eventually collapse in the sense that its value will converge in probability to zero. 4 Concluding Remarks Given a portfolio of instruments with risk, some capital A 0 , A_{0}, and a risk-free interest rate r r , we have seen that the Kelly portfolio maximizes long-term growth of capital, but with distressingly large draw-downs along the way. This undesirable quality can be mitigated by the use of fractional Kelly portfolios, at the cost of a reduction in long-term growth rate. In Theorem 2.1 , we have directly quantified this distributional relationship, and given explicit expressions that help us to find a desirable balance between growth and variance of returns. We have also seen how, under a set of reasonable assumptions, fractional Kelly investment is the same as Markowitz mean-variance optimization. The stochastic differential equation methodology used in this paper has been used by others in the same context, notably Browne 1997 , but appears not to be widely appreciated. Within this framework, one could potentially generalize the geometric Brownian motion price models ( 13 , 25 ) to jump-diffusion models, thereby allowing for the more realistic case of skewed and heavy-tailed log-returns. Using multivariate stochastic calculus, it would also possible to carry out analysis of situations where drift and/or diffusion coefficients μ {\\bf\\mu} and Σ \\Sigma are not known and must be estimated. On the practical side, the relationships given in Theorem 2.1 have broad applicability. They apply equally well to simple investment of an individual’s personal funds as to the more sophisticated operations of a quantitative trading organization or other fund. For example, individual investors can use the implied return distributions to assess the quality of leveraged mutual funds under various assumptions. At the more sophisticated end of the spectrum, a quantitative trading operation with a porfolio of known Sharpe ratio could use the result to determine how much capital/leverage to deploy, while remaining within distributional constraints imposed by their investors. More generally, the stochastic differential equation framework provides a solid foundation with which to address important yet-unsolved practical investment problems. 5 Acknowledgements The author is grateful to Peter Brockwell, Jonathan Baxter, and Peter Dodds for their valuable comments and suggestions. This work has benefited substantially from their feedback. Appendix A Supporting Results A.1 Constrained Maximization of Expected Log-Return per Unit Time Suppose we wish to choose the leverage vector k ∈ ℝ m k\\in{\\mathbb{R}}^{m} to maximize expected log-return per unit time (restating equation ( 31 )) L ⁡ ( 𝐤 ) = 𝐤 ⋅ μ − 1 2 ​ 𝐤 T ​ Σ ​ 𝐤 , L({\\bf k})={\\bf k}\\cdot{\\bf\\mu}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\bf k}^{T}\\Sigma{\\bf k}, (54) subject to the constraint that total-leverage is κ = ∑ j = 1 m k j = 𝐤 ⋅ 𝐞 m = κ 0 , \\kappa=\\sum_{j=1}^{m}k_{j}={\\bf k}\\cdot{\\bf e}_{m}=\\kappa_{0}, (55) where 𝐞 m = ( 1 , … , 1 ) T ∈ ℝ m . {\\bf e}_{m}=(1,\\ldots,1)^{T}\\in{\\mathbb{R}}^{m}. We can express the constraint as ( 𝐤 ⋅ 𝐞 m − κ 0 ) = 0 . ({\\bf k}\\cdot{\\bf e}_{m}-\\kappa_{0})=0. (56) Thus the Lagrangian is ℒ ⁡ ( 𝐤 ) = 𝐤 ⋅ μ − 1 2 ​ 𝐤 T ​ Σ ​ 𝐤 − λ ⁡ ( 𝐤 ⋅ 𝐞 m − κ 0 ) , {\\cal L}({\\bf k})={\\bf k}\\cdot{\\bf\\mu}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\bf k}^{T}\\Sigma{\\bf k}-\\lambda({\\bf k}\\cdot{\\bf e}_{m}-\\kappa_{0}), (57) Differentiating with respect to the vector 𝐤 {\\bf k} , and setting to zero, we find that the value maximizing ( 54 ) takes the form 𝐤 = Σ − 1 ​ ( μ − λ ​ 𝐞 m ) . {\\bf k}=\\Sigma^{-1}({\\bf\\mu}-\\lambda{\\bf e}_{m}). (58) Substituting ( 58 ) into the constraint ( 56 ), we can solve for λ \\lambda , obtaining λ = ( 𝐞 m T ​ Σ − 1 ​ μ − κ 0 ) ​ ( 𝐞 m T ​ Σ − 1 ​ 𝐞 m ) − 1 . \\lambda=({\\bf e}_{m}^{T}\\Sigma^{-1}{\\bf\\mu}-\\kappa_{0})({\\bf e}_{m}^{T}\\Sigma^{-1}{\\bf e}_{m})^{-1}. (59) Equations ( 58 ) and ( 59 ) together specify the leverage vector maximizing L L subject to the required constraint. References Breiman (1961) Leo Breiman. Optimal gambling systems for favorable games. Fourth Berkeley Symposium on Probability and Statistics , 1:65–78, 1961. Browne (1997) Sid Browne. Survival and growth with a liability: Optimal portfolio strategies in continuous time. Mathematics of Operations Research , 22(2):468–498, 1997. Hakansson (1971) Nils Hakansson. Capital growth and the mean-variance approach to portfolio selection. Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis , 26:857–884, 1971. Karatzas and Shreve (1991) I. Karatzas and S. Shreve. Brownian Motion and Stochastic Calculus . Springer, 1991. Kelly (1956) J. L. Kelly. A new interpretation of information rate. Bell System Technical Journal , 35(4):917–926, 1956. MacLean et al. (2004) L.C. MacLean, R. Sanegre, Y. Zhao, and W.T. Ziemba. Capital growth with security. Journal of Economic Dynamics and Control , 28:937–954, 2004. MacLean et al. (2012) L.C. MacLean, E.O. Thorp, and W.T. Ziemba, editors. The Kelly Capital Growth Investment Criterion , volume 3 of World Scientific Handbook in Financial Economic Series . World Scientific, 2012. Markowitz (1952) H. Markowitz. Portfolio selection. Journal of Finance , 7(1):77–91, 1952. Øksendal (2003) B. Øksendal. Stochastic Differential Equations . Springer, 6th edition, 2003. Thorpe (1975) E.O. Thorpe. Portfolio choice and the Kelly criterion. In W.T. Ziemba and T.G. Vickson, editors, Stochastic Optimization Models in Finance , pages 599–619. Academic Press, 1975. Zuckerman (2019) Gregory Zuckerman. The Man Who Solved the Market: How Jim Simons Launched the Quant Revolution . Penguin Random House LLC, 2019. ◄ Feeling lucky? Conversion 