ehat{T}(F,r) the pushforward distribution of F F under T ^ ​ ( ⋅ , r ) : W ∪ → ℝ \\widehat{T}(\\cdot,r):W^{\\cup}\\to\\mathbbm{R} . Consider a scoring rule S S on ℝ \\mathbbm{R} that is proper relative to T ^ ​ ( 𝒫 ) := { T ^ ​ ( F , r ) , F ∈ 𝒫 , r ∈ ℛ } \\widehat{T}(\\mathcal{P}):=\\{\\widehat{T}(F,r)\\,,\\,F\\in\\mathcal{P},r\\in\\mathcal{R}\\} . Then, the scoring rule S T ^ ​ ( 𝐲 , F ) := S ⁡ ( T ^ ​ ( 𝐲 , r ) , T ^ ​ ( F , r ) ) S_{\\widehat{T}}(\\mathbf{y},F):=S(\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{y},r),\\widehat{T}(F,r)) is proper relative to 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} . Note that S T ^ S_{\\widehat{T}} is usually not strictly proper, even if S S is, since we might have T ^ ​ ( F , r ) = T ^ ​ ( G , r ) \\widehat{T}(F,r)=\\widehat{T}(G,r) for distributions F ≠ G F\\neq G . The key to rendering this result useful is the choice of the proper scoring rule S S on the real line. Note that we recover Proposition 3.1 if T ^ \\widehat{T} is unbiased and we choose S S to be the SE in ( 3 ), S ⁡ ( y , F ) = ( y − 𝔼 F ​ [ X ] ) 2 . S(y,F)=(y-\\mathbbm{E}_{F}[X])^{2}. However, a preferable choice is the CRPS in ( 4 ) as it is strictly proper with respect to all distributions with finite first moment ( Gneiting and Raftery 2007 ) . Choosing the CRPS allows to calculate S T ^ S_{\\widehat{T}} by Monte-Carlo approximation, without requiring knowledge of the pushforward measure T ^ ​ ( F ) . \\widehat{T}(F). When applying Proposition 3.5 with the CRPS formula in ( 4 ), we obtain by the change-of-variables formula, supressing r r for brevity, S T ^ ​ ( 𝐲 , F ) \\displaystyle S_{\\widehat{T}}(\\mathbf{y},F) = 𝔼 T ^ ​ ( F ) ​ [ | T ^ ​ ( 𝐲 ) − X | ] − 1 2 ​ 𝔼 T ^ ​ ( F ) ​ [ | X ′ − X | ] \\displaystyle=\\mathbbm{E}_{\\widehat{T}(F)}[|\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{y})-X|]-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbbm{E}_{\\widehat{T}(F)}[|X^{\\prime}-X|] = 𝔼 F ​ [ | T ^ ​ ( 𝐲 ) − T ^ ​ ( 𝐗 ) | ] − 1 2 ​ 𝔼 F ​ [ | T ^ ​ ( 𝐗 ′ ) − T ^ ​ ( 𝐗 ) | ] . \\displaystyle=\\mathbbm{E}_{F}[|\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{y})-\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{X})|]-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbbm{E}_{F}[|\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{X}^{\\prime})-\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{X})|]. Here, X , X ′ X,X^{\\prime} are independent random variables with distribution T ^ ​ ( F ) \\widehat{T}(F) , and 𝐗 ′ \\mathbf{X}^{\\prime} and 𝐗 \\mathbf{X} are independent point processes with distribution F F . The latter expression can be approximated by Monte-Carlo sampling from the point process distribution F F . Another, somewhat surprising, advantage of this approach is that considering T ^ ​ ( F ) \\widehat{T}(F) rather than T ⁡ ( F ) T(F) can increase the sensitivity of the score, in the following sense. It frequently happens that competing models have identical (theoretical) summary statistic T ⁡ ( F ) T(F) : For example, homogeneous models with the same expected number of points all share the same intensity, and all Poisson models have identical K K -functions. However, the estimated summary statistics T ^ ​ ( F ) \\widehat{T}(F) are generally differently distributed for different models. Since the strictly proper CRPS is applied to T ^ ​ ( F ) \\widehat{T}(F) , misspecified models can be detected even if their theoretical summary statistics T ⁡ ( F ) T(F) fits the observation perfectly. For example, we show in the next section that the score based on estimators of the K K -function assigns the lowest mean score to the correct model when several Poisson models are compared. The main result of this section is the following corollary, applying Propositions 3.3 and 3.5 to the CRPS (which is non-negative). Corollary 3.6 (Summary statistic score) . Consider a non-negative weighting function w : ℛ → ℝ w:\\mathcal{R}\\to\\mathbbm{R} and an estimator for a summary statistic T ^ \\widehat{T} that is integrable with respect to F ⊗ w ⁡ ( r ) ​ d ​ r F\\otimes w(r)dr for all F F in 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} . The scoring rule defined by S T ^ ​ ( 𝐲 , F ) \\displaystyle S_{\\widehat{T}}(\\mathbf{y},F) : = 𝔼 F ​ [ ∫ ℛ | T ^ ​ ( 𝐲 , r ) − T ^ ​ ( 𝐗 , r ) | ​ w ​ ( r ) ​ 𝑑 r ] − 1 2 ​ 𝔼 F ​ [ ∫ ℛ | T ^ ​ ( 𝐗 ′ , r ) − T ^ ​ ( 𝐗 , r ) | ​ w ​ ( r ) ​ 𝑑 r ] \\displaystyle:=\\mathbbm{E}_{F}\\bigg[\\int_{\\mathcal{R}}|\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{y},r)-\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{X},r)|w(r)\\,dr\\bigg]-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbbm{E}_{F}\\bigg[\\int_{\\mathcal{R}}|\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{X}^{\\prime},r)-\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{X},r)|w(r)\\,dr\\bigg] is proper relative to 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} . Note that both expectations can be calculated from random draws of the predictive point process distribution F F , by Monte-Carlo approximation. An advantage of these scores are the very weak assumptions of this corollary which are satisfied for almost all point process models and summary statistic estimates. The weighting function w ⁡ ( r ) w(r) can be used to focus the attention of the score to certain areas, for example clustering at short range, when the K K -function is considered, or the spatial distribution of points in a certain subregion of W W , when the intensity is considered. For assessing the overall fit we will set w = 1 w=1 throughout the studies in this paper. In this construction, T ^ \\widehat{T} can be any real- or function-valued mapping satisfying the condition of Corollary 3.6 . In particular, no connection of the summary statistic estimator to the underlying summary statistic is required. The estimator does not need to be unbiased. Below we list two examples which will be used to show the efficacy of our approach for point process model evaluation. Example 3.7 (Intensity score) . The intensity function λ \\lambda of a point process is typically estimated by kernel estimators. These estimators are generally biased, making it impossible to apply Proposition 3.1 . For a kernel k k (i.e. a density on W W ) and a bandwidth σ &gt; 0 \\sigma&gt;0 , the kernel intensity estimator is based on the rescaled kernel k σ ​ ( w ) := k ⁡ ( w / σ ) k_{\\sigma}(w):=k(w/\\sigma) . It is defined as λ ^ σ ​ ( 𝐲 , w ) = ∑ y ∈ 𝐲 k σ ​ ( w − y ) / c W , σ ​ ( y ) , \\widehat{\\lambda}_{\\sigma}(\\mathbf{y},w)=\\sum_{y\\in\\mathbf{y}}k_{\\sigma}(w-y)/c_{W,\\sigma}(y), (6) where c W , σ c_{W,\\sigma} are edge correction factors defined as c W , σ ​ ( y ) = ∫ W k σ ​ ( w − y ) ​ 𝑑 w c_{W,\\sigma}(y)=\\int_{W}k_{\\sigma}(w-y)\\,dw . By Corollary 3.6 the intensity score, defined as S λ ^ σ ​ ( 𝐲 , F ) := \\displaystyle S_{\\widehat{\\lambda}_{\\sigma}}(\\mathbf{y},F):= 𝔼 F ​ [ ∫ W | λ ^ σ ​ ( 𝐲 , w ) − λ ^ σ ​ ( 𝐗 , w ) | ​ 𝑑 w ] − 1 2 ​ 𝔼 F ​ [ ∫ W | λ ^ σ ​ ( 𝐗 ′ , w ) − λ ^ σ ​ ( 𝐗 , w ) | ​ 𝑑 w ] , \\displaystyle\\mathbbm{E}_{F}\\bigg[\\int_{W}|\\widehat{\\lambda}_{\\sigma}(\\mathbf{y},w)-\\widehat{\\lambda}_{\\sigma}(\\mathbf{X},w)|\\,dw\\bigg]-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbbm{E}_{F}\\bigg[\\int_{W}|\\widehat{\\lambda}_{\\sigma}(\\mathbf{X}^{\\prime},w)-\\widehat{\\lambda}_{\\sigma}(\\mathbf{X},w)|dw\\bigg], (7) constitutes a proper scoring rule. Unless stated differently, we choose k k to be an isotropic Gaussian kernel with standard deviation 1, such that k σ k_{\\sigma} has standard deviation σ \\sigma . Since this score targets the intensity function, it assesses, roughly speaking, whether the predictive distribution has the correct spatial distribution and number of points, but neglects point interactions. On the other hand, if we are more interested in whether a predictive model reflects point interaction correctly, we can consider an estimator for the K K -function. Example 3.8 ( K K -function score) . The standard estimator for Ripley’s K K -function is defined as K ^ ​ ( 𝐲 , r ) := ∑ y 1 ≠ y 2 ∈ 𝐲 𝟙 { | y 1 − y 2 | &lt; r } λ ^ ​ ( y 1 ) ​ λ ^ ​ ( y 2 ) ​ | W ∩ W y 1 − y 2 | , \\widehat{K}(\\mathbf{y},r):=\\sum_{y_{1}\\neq y_{2}\\in\\mathbf{y}}\\frac{\\mathbb{1}\\{|y_{1}-y_{2}|&lt;r\\}}{\\widehat{\\lambda}(y_{1})\\widehat{\\lambda}(y_{2})|W\\cap W_{y_{1}-y_{2}}|}, (8) where W y 1 − y 2 W_{y_{1}-y_{2}} denotes the shifted set W + y 1 − y 2 W+y_{1}-y_{2} , and λ ^ \\widehat{\\lambda} is a kernel estimator for the intensity. Thus, we obtain the proper K K -function score S K ^ ​ ( 𝐲 , F ) := \\displaystyle S_{\\widehat{K}}(\\mathbf{y},F):= ∫ 0 R 𝔼 F ​ [ | K ^ ​ ( 𝐲 , r ) − K ^ ​ ( 𝐗 , r ) | ] ​ 𝑑 r − 1 2 ​ ∫ 0 R 𝔼 F ​ [ | K ^ ​ ( 𝐗 ′ , r ) − K ^ ​ ( 𝐗 , r ) | ] ​ 𝑑 r , \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{R}\\mathbbm{E}_{F}[|\\widehat{K}(\\mathbf{y},r)-\\widehat{K}(\\mathbf{X},r)|]\\,dr-\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{R}\\mathbbm{E}_{F}[|\\widehat{K}(\\mathbf{X}^{\\prime},r)-\\widehat{K}(\\mathbf{X},r)|]\\,dr, (9) where R R is an upper limit that should be chosen small relative to the diameter of W W . As K ^ \\widehat{K} is sensitive to point interaction, this scoring rule specifically targets correct representation of point interaction in the predictive model. On the other hand, it will be relatively insensitive to misspecification of the intensity function, and for example, struggle to differentiate between different Poisson processes, which have the same K K -function, see the example is Section 4 . 3.3 Other scoring rules Depending on the set of forecast models that are compared, other proper scoring rules might be available: The logarithmic score can be evaluated when all predictions have known densities, in particular when all competing models are Poisson. This score is strictly proper and tests for significance of score differences tend to have higher power than for other scores, see Lerch et al. 2017 and Section 5 . Therefore, the logarithmic score should be employed when available. Moreover, Brehmer et al. 2021 introduce several new scoring rules for point processes. However, as the authors remark, most of these also require knowledge of the density or other mathematical properties of the process. For example, they show that the scoring rule S λ ( F , 𝐲 ) := − ∑ y i ∈ 𝐲 log ( λ F ( y i ) ) + n ( 𝐲 ) | Λ F | + c ( | Λ F | − n ( 𝐲 ) ) 2 \\displaystyle S_{\\lambda}(F,\\mathbf{y}):=-\\sum_{y_{i}\\in\\mathbf{y}}\\log(\\lambda_{F}(y_{i}))+n(\\mathbf{y})|\\Lambda_{F}|+c(|\\Lambda_{F}|-n(\\mathbf{y}))^{2} (10) is proper for any c &gt; 0 c&gt;0 , where | Λ F | = ∫ W λ F ​ ( w ) ​ 𝑑 w |\\Lambda_{F}|=\\int_{W}\\lambda_{F}(w)dw , and n ⁡ ( 𝐲 ) n(\\mathbf{y}) denotes the number of points in the point pattern 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} . Since this score evaluates the fit of the intensity of the predictive model, it provides an alternative to the intensity estimator score in ( 3.7 ). Evaluating this score requires knowledge of the intensity for all considered prediction models. Intensities are known for most double stochastic Poisson processes, also known as Cox processes, but only for a few models of the Markov type ( Møller and Waagepetersen 2003 ) . Another option to create scoring rules sensitive to the intensity is by dividing the observation space W W into a large number N N of spatial bins. This reduces observed point patterns 𝐲 ∈ W ∪ \\mathbf{y}\\in W^{\\cup} to counts of points per bin, and proper scoring rules can be applied to the counts, see Brehmer et al. 2021 for a discussion. This method is frequently applied in the evaluation of earthquake rate predictions ( Schorlemmer et al. 2018 ) . It can be viewed as an application of Proposition 3.5 , since the binning constitutes a mapping from the space of point patterns into a simpler space where scoring rules are available. 3.4 Computational aspects In practice, for evaluating the scores proposed in Corollary 3.6 , n n realizations T 1 , … , T n T_{1},...,T_{n} of T ^ ​ ( 𝐗 ) , 𝐗 ∼ F \\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{X}),\\mathbf{X}\\sim F need to be generated in order to approximate the score by 1 n ​ ∑ i = 1 n [ ∫ ℛ | T ^ ​ ( 𝐲 , r ) − T i ​ ( r ) | ​ w ​ ( r ) ​ 𝑑 r ] − 1 2 ​ n ​ ( n − 1 ) ​ ∑ i ≠ j [ ∫ ℛ | T i ​ ( r ) − T j ​ ( r ) | ​ w ​ ( r ) ​ 𝑑 r ] , \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigg[\\int_{\\mathcal{R}}|\\widehat{T}(\\mathbf{y},r)-T_{i}(r)|w(r)\\,dr\\bigg]-\\frac{1}{2n(n-1)}\\sum_{i\\neq j}\\bigg[\\int_{\\mathcal{R}}|T_{i}(r)-T_{j}(r)|w(r)\\,dr\\bigg], where the integrals are calculated numerically. This generates a trade-off in that increasing n n reduces the uncertainty of the Monte-Carlo approximation but increases computation costs for the score evaluation. As a reference, the simulation study presented in Section 5 uses n = 300 n=300 and requires the computation of 9000 scores in total. These calculations take approximately 15 minutes, running on a laptop with 32 GB RAM, using a single core of a quadcore processor with 2.9 GHz. In many point process applications, the number of available observations is limited, diminishing the drawback from using scoring rules that are computationally costly. In long-range earthquake forecasting, for example, evaluation periods typically include five years or more of earthquakes, and new observations therefore take a long time to materialize. In ecology, collecting observations usually requires field studies and work-intensive data labeling, similarly resulting in a small number of available observations. In such cases, it may be particularly important to aim to minimize the uncertainty of the Monte-Carlo approximation. 4 Simulation study 1: Detecting differences in intensity and interaction In this simulation study, we analyze some properties of the proposed intensity- and the K K -function score. 4.1 Competing models We consider the five different point process models listed in Figure 1 . For study area, we consider the spatial window W = [ 0 , 10 ] × [ 0 , 10 ] . W=[0,10]\\times[0,10]. The first two models (hP and hP+) are homogeneous Poisson processes with 50 and 60 expected points, respectively. The third model (ihP) is an inhomogeneous Poisson process with 50 expected points, and with an intensity that increases linearly in the distance from the lower left corner of the window. The fourth model (Str) is a homogeneous Strauss process, in which the points repel each other, and the typical point pattern is more regular than for the homogeneous Poisson process. The Strauss process is defined by its density f ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) = c ​ β n ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) ​ γ s R ​ ( 𝐱 ) , f(\\mathbf{x})=c\\beta^{n(\\mathbf{x})}\\gamma^{s_{R}(\\mathbf{x})}, where c c is an untractable normalizing constant, β &gt; 0 , R &gt; 0 \\beta&gt;0,R&gt;0 , and γ ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) \\gamma\\in(0,1) are parameters, n ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) n(\\mathbf{x}) denotes the number of points in 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} and s R ​ ( 𝐱 ) s_{R}(\\mathbf{x}) is the number of pairs of points in 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} with distance less than R R . The value R R is the range of interaction between points, and γ \\gamma determines the strength of the interaction, with smaller γ \\gamma leading to stronger inhibition between close points. We set γ = 0.5 \\gamma=0.5 , R = 1 R=1 and β = 1.15 \\beta=1.15 , resulting in an expected number of points of approximately 50, the same as for the models hP and ihP. Name Model Intensity Point interaction 𝔼 ⁡ [ n ⁡ ( 𝐗 ) ] \\mathbbm{E}[n(\\mathbf{X})] hP homogeneous Poisson 1 / 2 1/2 none 50 hP+ homogeneous Poisson 3 / 5 3/5 none 60 ihP inhomogeneous Poisson ∼ x 2 + y 2 \\sim\\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}\\ none 50 Str homogeneous Strauss ≈ 1 / 2 \\approx 1/2 inhibition ≈ 50 \\approx 50 ihT inhomogeneous Thomas ∼ x 2 + y 2 \\sim\\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}} clustering ≈ 50 \\approx 50 Figure 1 : Example plots (top row) and characteristics of the five models considered in the first simulation study. The spatial window is [ 0 , 10 ] × [ 0 , 10 ] [0,10]\\times[0,10] , i.e. for the inhomogeneous processes, the intensity increases with distance from the lower left corner. The fifth model is an inhomogeneous Thomas process (ihT), which is constructed by generating an (invisible) Poisson process of parent points, where each parent then generates a random number of offsprings that are spatially distributed according to a Gaussian kernel centered at the parent point. Here, we choose an inhomogeneous parent process with intensity equal to half of that of model ihP, the number of offsprings per parent is Poisson distributed with mean 2, and the standard deviation of the (homogeneous) Gaussian dispersion kernel is set to 0.5. By these choices, the Thomas process has an intensity similar to model ihP, and the number of expected points within the observation window is again approximately 50. As seen in the examples in Figure 1 , simulations from these models may be difficult to tell apart by eye and the models are chosen to challenge the scoring functions. 4.2 Evaluation We consider each of the five models both as true distribution G G and as predictive distribution F F , for a total of 25 combinations. For each combination we estimate 𝔼 G ​ [ S T ^ ​ ( 𝐘 , F ) ] \\mathbbm{E}_{G}[S_{\\widehat{T}}(\\mathbf{Y},F)] by simulating 100 independent copies of 𝐘 ∼ G \\mathbf{Y}\\sim G and averaging S T ^ ​ ( 𝐘 , F ) . S_{\\widehat{T}}(\\mathbf{Y},F). For the computation of S T ^ ​ ( 𝐘 , F ) S_{\\widehat{T}}(\\mathbf{Y},F) the expectations are approximated by Monte-Carlo approximation based on 100 independent draws from F F . As summary statistic estimator T ^ \\widehat{T} we consider both the kernel estimator λ ^ \\widehat{\\lambda} in ( 6 ) and the K-function estimator K ^ \\widehat{K} in ( 8 ). All computations in this paper are carried out using the R -package spatstat ( Baddeley et al. 2015 ) . For the kernel estimator we use bandwidth σ \\sigma =1.25 which is the default value (for the considered spatial window) of the spatstat functions density.ppp and Kinhom.ppp that are employed to calculate the estimators. Figure 2 : The mean scores 𝔼 G ​ [ S λ ^ ​ ( 𝐘 , F ) ] \\mathbbm{E}_{G}[S_{\\widehat{\\lambda}}(\\mathbf{Y},F)] (left hand side) and 𝔼 G ​ [ S K ^ ​ ( 𝐘 , F ) ] \\mathbbm{E}_{G}[S_{\\widehat{K}}(\\mathbf{Y},F)] (right hand side) for each combination of the five competing models. The x x -axis shows the true distribution G G of the data, and the score for the correct distribution is additionally marked by a plus. The results are presented in Figure 2 . For both scoring rules, the expected score is minimized for the true model under all distributions. The results highlight the sensitivity of the score to the underlying summary statistic. For example, under the intensity score, the mean scores for the models hP and Str are very similar, since these models have nearly identical intensities. On the other hand, the intensity score very clearly separates the homogeneous from the inhomogeneous models. Unlike for the intensity score, the K K -function score identifies a clear difference between the models hP and Str. However, the differences in the K K -function scores are particularly small when selecting between the two homogeneous Poisson models hP and hP+. 4.3 Significance of the results The significance of the mean score differences in Figure 2 can be assessed by permutation tests as e.g. described in Möller et al. 2013 and Good 2013 . Table 1 shows the p p -values of permutation tests for all combinations of observed and predicted model. The results underline that the scores are targeting different properties: While the score differences for S λ ^ S_{\\widehat{\\lambda}} are always significant at a 5 % 5\\% level, when observed and predicted model have different intensity, the score differences under S K ^ S_{\\widehat{K}} are significant whenever they have different point interaction. In practice, it is therefore advisable to apply scoring rules sensitive to properties that are most important for the study at hand, and, in case of doubt, compare competing models based on a variety of attributes. Note that the differences between the mean K K -function scores of the different Poisson models are not significant at the 5% level. As these models have identical theoretical K K -function, this is to be expected. It is therefore rather remarkable that the mean score gets minimized in the correct model for all model combinations. This follows from the estimated K K -functions K ^ \\widehat{K} having different distributions under the different Poisson models, and these differences being identified by the strictly proper CRPS. A similar effect is e.g. reported in Thorarinsdottir et al. 2016 in the context of assessing the multivariate structure of high-dimensional real-valued predictions. S λ ^ ​ ( Y , F ) S_{\\widehat{\\lambda}}(Y,F) F : F: hP hP+ ihP Str ihT G G : hP - &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 0 8.0 &lt; &lt; 0.1 hP+ &lt; &lt; 0.1 - &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 ihP &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 - &lt; &lt; 0.1 0 8.4 Str 15.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 - &lt; &lt; 0.1 ihT &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 0 4.4 &lt; &lt; 0.1 - S K ^ ​ ( Y , F ) S_{\\widehat{K}}(Y,F) F : F: hP hP+ ihP Str ihT G G : hP - 38.3 17.6 &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 hP+ 38.9 - 16.7 &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 ihP 0 8.2 0 6.2 - &lt; &lt; 0.1 0 0.5 Str &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 - &lt; &lt; 0.1 ihT &lt; &lt; 0.1 &lt; &lt; 0.1 0 0.7 &lt; &lt; 0.1 - Table 1 : p p -values (in % \\% ) of permutation tests assessing the significance of the difference between the score of predictive distribution F F and the score of the true distribution G G . Values above 5 5 % (in bold) indicate nonsignificance, and the score cannot reliably distinguish between F F and G G in the setting of simulation study 1 and a sample size of 100 point patterns. 5 Simulation study 2: The role of the bandwidth for the intensity score In a second simulation study, we analyze the effect of the bandwidth parameter σ \\sigma in the intensity score, and compare its performance to the logarithmic score. Specifically, we assess how reliably the logarithmic score and the intensity score identify the correct model when the mean score is calculated from samples (or observations) of varying sizes. This study uses an observation window of the same size as before but with approximately twice as many expected points. We consider a range of bandwidth parameters σ \\sigma to analyze how the bandwidth affects the ability of the score to identify the correct model. 5.1 Competing models Here, we focus on models where the density is available in closed form for a straight-forward evaluation of the logarithmic score. As observation-generating model we consider a Poisson model with a standard Gaussian kernel (multiplied by a scalar) as intensity function. The competing predictive models are various Poisson processes based on Gaussian kernels, which allows us to look at the effect of a variety of different misspecifications in the prediction: Local shift (wrong mean μ \\mu of the kernel), misspecified spread (wrong standard deviation η \\eta ), misspecified shape (wrong correlation ρ \\rho ), as well as misspecification in the expected number of points, see Figure 3 . We denote the models by F 1 , … , F 6 F_{1},...,F_{6} , with F 1 F_{1} being the observation model. Again, the model parameters are chosen such that it is challenging to tell the models apart by eye. obs. model μ = ( 0.1 , 0 ) \\mu=(0.1,0) η = ( 0.9 , 0.9 ) \\eta=(0.9,0.9) η = ( 1.1 , 1.1 ) \\eta=(1.1,1.1) ρ = 0.1 \\rho=0.1 𝔼 ⁡ [ n ⁡ ( 𝐗 ) ] = 105 \\mathbbm{E}[n(\\mathbf{X})]=105 Figure 3 : The different Poisson models considered in the second simulation study. The spatial window is W = [ − 5 , 5 ] × [ − 5 , 5 ] W=[-5,5]\\times[-5,5] . From left to right: The observations are distributed according to F 1 F_{1} , a Poisson model with the intensity equal a standard Gaussian kernel, multiplied by 100. Model F 2 F_{2} has an intensity with misspecified mean μ \\mu . Models F 3 F_{3} and F 4 F_{4} have mean zero and a misspecified standard deviation η = 0.9 \\eta=0.9 and η = 1.1 , \\eta=1.1, respectively (both in x x - and y y -component). In model F 5 F_{5} , a mean-zero unit-variance Gaussian kernel with correlation of 0.1 between x x - and y y -component is used. The intensity of model F 6 F_{6} is again standard Gaussian, but multiplied by 105, leading to a slightly larger number of expected points. 5.2 Evaluation We generate 300 random samples { 𝐘 j } j = 1 , … , 300 \\{\\mathbf{Y}_{j}\\}_{j=1,...,300} from the observation model F 1 F_{1} . Then, for each of the predictive models F 1 , … , F 6 F_{1},...,F_{6} we compute the 300 score evaluations S ⁡ ( 𝐘 j , F i ) S(\\mathbf{Y}_{j},F_{i}) , for the logarithmic score and the intensity score with varying bandwidth parameters σ \\sigma . The latter requires Monte-Carlo approximation of the two expectations in ( 7 ), which are each based on 300 i.i.d. realizations from the predictive models. We then subsample from the collection of scores { S ⁡ ( 𝐘 j , F i ) } j = 1 , … , 300 ; i = 1 , … , 6 \\{S(\\mathbf{Y}_{j},F_{i})\\}_{j=1,...,300;\\,i=1,...,6} in order to assess the performance of the mean score under differently sized observation sets. Figure 4 : Mean scores under true distribution F 1 F_{1} . The logarithmic score is compared with the intensity score for a range of different bandwidths σ \\sigma . The boxes indicate variability in the mean scores based on 10 (left) or 100 (right) observations. Figure 4 shows sample distributions (as boxplots) of the mean scores when 10 (resp. 100) observations are available. For ease of comparison, we apply affine positive linear functions to all scores to fit them to the same scale which does not affect the ordering of competing models. When the number of available observations is N = 10 N=10 , the uncertainty in the estimated mean scores is much larger than the score differences between different predictive models. For N = 100 N=100 , the differences in mean score increase relative to the spread, indicating that the scores can clearer identify the correct predictive distribution. The figure indicates quite good performance of the intensity score with bandwidths σ = 0.2 , 0.4 , 0.8 \\sigma=0.2,\\,0.4,\\,0.8 . 5.3 Significance of the results As competing models should be evaluated on the same set of observations ( Gneiting and Raftery 2007 ) , there is potential correlation in the score values for competing models. Figure 5 shows how the correlation in score values for models F 1 F_{1} and F 5 F_{5} varies between the scores: the correlation is stronger for the logarithmic score than the intensity score, and, for the latter, the correlation changes with the bandwith. A stronger correlation can counteract small differences between marginal distributions of score values, which renders the results in Figure 4 inconclusive regarding which score is most likely to prefer the correct model. To account for the joint distribution of score values, Figure 6 shows (approximated) p p -values for permutation tests comparing the correct model to each of the competing models. Figure 5 : Scatter plot of mean scores for the two models F 1 F_{1} and F 5 F_{5} , evaluated on 10 random observations distributed according to F 1 F_{1} . For each dot the coordinates correspond to the two mean scores evaluated on the same (synthetic) observation. Figure 6 : Estimated p p -values of a permutation test comparing the 5 incorrect models to the correct model, as a function of the number of available observations. Each model’s deviation from the correct model is indicated in the panel above the plot. The dashed horizontal line highlights the classical 5 % 5\\% significance level. For each curve, the leftmost point corresponds to the p p -value for a single observation, N = 1 N=1 . To imitate common applications, we focus on 1 to 15 available observations. Specifically, we sample 100 mean scores for each of the models F 1 , . . , F 6 F_{1},..,F_{6} for N = 1 , … , 15 N=1,...,15 observed point patterns using the same sets of observations for all competing models. Each result for a wrong model is then compared to the corresponding result for the true model by a permutation test with 100 permutation resamples, yielding a total of 45,000 permutation tests (6 scoring rules, 5 competing models, 1 to 15 observations, 100 repetitions for each configuration). The average p p -value for each configuration is reported in Figure 6 . The logarithmic score almost always has the highest probability of correctly identifying the true model, in line with intuition gathered from the Neyman-Pearson lemma suggesting that log-likelihood based inference tends to be most powerful, see e.g. the discussion in Lerch et al. 2017 . Note, however, that the Neyman-Pearson lemma considers log-ratio tests and does not apply to permutation tests. The intensity scores with bandwidths σ = 0.2 , 0.4 , 0.8 \\sigma=0.2,0.4,0.8 lead to very similar p p -values, slightly higher but comparable to the log-score p p -values. Overall, the scores are quite good at identifying the correct model for 10 or more observations, except for the case where the model misspecification is only given by approximately 5% more points. The overall high probabilities of all considered scores to identify the correct model under such small sample sizes is particularly promising considering the likeness of the competing models in this study. 5.4 Bandwidth selection for the intensity score Generally, the choice of bandwidth for kernel estimators introduces a bias-variance trade-off; small bandwidths reduce the bias of the estimator but increase its variance ( Abramson 1982 ) . Our findings here highlight this effect, since the scores with bandwidths σ = 0.1 \\sigma=0.1 and σ = 1.6 \\sigma=1.6 tend to have the lowest discriminability. The optimal bias-variance trade-off depends on mathematical properties of the underlying point process model ( Diggle and Marron 1988 ) , and is therefore unknown in practical applications. The bandwidth parameter constitutes a penalty on spatial displacement of points: For larger bandwidths, the intensity estimator uses a wider smoothing kernel and the score becomes increasingly forgiving towards spatial displacements of points. This is highlighted in Figure 6 by the lack of sensitivity towards the misspecification ρ = 0.1 \\rho=0.1 for σ = 1.6 \\sigma=1.6 . For the misspecification μ x = 0.1 \\mu_{x}=0.1 , the whole point pattern and thus the entire intensity estimate is shifted. This is detected by the score independent of bandwidth with a wider bandwidth having a variance-stabilizing effect, increasing the discriminability. A small value of σ \\sigma , on the other hand, increases the variance of the intensity estimate and thus decreases the sensitivity of the score. In conclusion, the performance of the intensity score appears not overly sensitive to the choice of bandwidth. However, smaller bandwidths increase the sensitivity of the score to spatial displacements in the predictive model at the cost of higher sampling uncertainty in the score computation. We recommend choosing a small bandwidth, subject to it being substantially larger than the typical distance between nearest points. While the intensity score is almost as sensitive to misspecifications in the prediction as the logarithmic score, the logarithmic score may be preferred in situations where it can be applied to all competing models to avoid unnecessary computational costs and score uncertainty. 6 Earthquake rate prediction In this section we apply the intensity estimator score to evaluate earthquake rate predictions for California. Specifically, we focus on long-term predictions that quantify the expected rate of earthquakes over longer periods, e.g. five years. Such forecasts are, for example, used in urban planning ( Jena et al. 2020 ) ; for an overview, see Schorlemmer et al. 2018 and references therein. 6.1 Data Our data set is a catalog of all earthquakes of magnitude 3 or higher on the Richter scale in northern California–a window defined by 117.5 ∘ − 126 ∘ 117.5^{\\circ}-126^{\\circ} W and 34.5 ∘ − 42 ∘ 34.5^{\\circ}-42^{\\circ} N–that occurred between January 1, 1968 and December 31, 2019. The catalog contains a total of 17 799 earthquakes and is publicly available at http://www.ncedc.org . The observation window and a subset of the data are shown in Figure 7 . Figure 7 : Locations of earthquakes in northern California between 2011 and 2019. The point size indicates the magnitude of the earthquake on the Richter scale. 6.2 Competing models We follow Veen and Schoenberg 2006 and model the spatial distribution of earthquakes using a semi-parametric Poisson model with an intensity given by a mixture of an inhomogeneous intensity μ η ​ ( x , y ) \\mu_{\\eta}(x,y) and a homogeneous intensity ν \\nu , λ α , η ​ ( x , y ) = α ​ μ η ​ ( x , y ) + ( 1 − α ) ​ ν , \\lambda_{\\alpha,\\eta}(x,y)=\\alpha\\mu_{\\eta}(x,y)+(1-\\alpha)\\nu, where α ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] \\alpha\\in[0,1] and η &gt; 0 \\eta&gt;0 are model parameters. The inhomogeneous part μ η ​ ( x , y ) \\mu_{\\eta}(x,y) accounts for the fact that earthquake locations are generally clustered around seismic faults; it is estimated by kernel smoothing of all earthquakes in the training period with magnitude 3.5 or larger using a Gaussian kernel with standard deviation η \\eta in both x x - and y y -coordinate and correlation estimated from the data. The homogeneous part ν \\nu accounts for earthquakes potentially occurring far away from previously recorded earthquakes; it is estimated as N / A N/A where N N is the total number of observed earthquakes with magnitude 3 or larger, and A A is the area of the observation window. The model assumes stationarity in time and the intensity is scaled by the length of the considered time period. The aim of the analysis is to find good values for the model parameters α \\alpha and η \\eta in a prediction context. In a cross-validation study, we compare spatial predictions for a range of values for α \\alpha and η \\eta under the intensity score and the logarithmic score. Veen and Schoenberg 2006 previously performed an in-sample assessment of these models for earthquakes in southern California using a visual comparison of scaled K K -functions for fixed η = 8 \\eta=8 km and a range of values for α \\alpha . 6.3 Evaluation Assuming stationarity in time, we treat our data set as cyclic, i.e. we assume that the year 2019 is followed by the year 1968. A training set consists of 21 consecutive years, to match the set-up of Veen and Schoenberg 2006 . The remaining years (with a one year break before and after the training set to reduce correlation due to aftershocks) are used for evaluation, resulting in a validation set with 29 years of data. This procedure is repeated 52 times, so that each year of data is the first year in the training set exactly once. We compute mean scores for the parameter choices α ∈ { 0.6 , 0.65 , … , 1 } \\alpha\\in\\{0.6,0.65,...,1\\} and η ∈ { 2 k : k = 1 , … , 5 } \\eta\\in\\{2^{k}\\,:\\,k=1,...,5\\} , where the unit of η \\eta is kilometer. We employ the intensity score with bandwidths σ = 4 \\sigma=4 km and σ = 8 \\sigma=8 km in addition to the logarithmic score. The data contains on average 122 earthquakes within a 4 km radius of an earthquake location, and 277 earthquakes within an 8 km radius of an earthquake location, rendering these values substantially larger than the typcial distance between nearest points. Figure 8 : Mean scores for earthquake rate prediction models based on a 52-fold cross-validation study, for the logarithmic score (left) and the intensity score with σ = 4 \\sigma=4 km (middle) and σ = 8 \\sigma=8 km (right). For ease of comparison, the scores have been rescaled to fit the same range. The resulting scores are presented in Figure 8 . Overall, the model rankings resulting from the three scores show many similarities. In particular, all the scores agree that the best choices for η \\eta are 4 4 km or 8 8 km, and the best choice of α \\alpha is between 0.65 0.65 and 0.85 0.85 . Specifially, the optimum under the logarithmic score is ( α , η ) = ( 0.75 , 4 ) (\\alpha,\\eta)=(0.75,4) , it is ( α , η ) = ( 0.65 , 4 ) (\\alpha,\\eta)=(0.65,4) for the intensity score with σ = 4 \\sigma=4 km and ( α , η ) = ( 0.85 , 8 ) (\\alpha,\\eta)=(0.85,8) for the intensity score with σ = 8 \\sigma=8 km. The results indicate that the optimal value of α \\alpha is increasing in η \\eta , indictating that the weight of the homogeneous component ν \\nu increases with a narrower kernel in the inhomogeneous component μ η ​ ( x , y ) \\mu_{\\eta}(x,y) . Similarly, it can be observed that the intensity score with bandwidth σ = 8 \\sigma=8 km prefers both larger η \\eta and larger α \\alpha than the other two scores, in line with the intuition discussed in the previous section that increasing bandwidth corresponds to a lower penalty for spatial displacements of points. 6.4 Discussion In their analysis, Veen and Schoenberg 2006 set η = 8 \\eta=8 km and concluded, by visual inspection of inhomogeneous L L -functions, that α ≈ 0.7 \\alpha\\approx 0.7 provided the best fit to their data. This is in good agreement with our out-of-sample findings, even if the current data set covers a longer time period and we consider an overlapping but not identical region. We assume that the earthquake point process is stationary in time. However, since the data was collected over a timespan of more than 50 years, it is to be expected that the quality of the measurements varies over time. Indeed, the number of observations per year is more variable earlier in the time period, and exhibits a slight downward trend over time. While our study design partly accounts for this by merging early and late years, such features should be accounted for in operational prediction settings. 7 Predicting spatial distribution of trees In this application, we apply the K K -function score to evaluate models that describe the spatial distribution of trees. The use of spatial point process models has substantially increased in ecology and forestry over the last decades, as they allow to address many key questions such as local dominance of species and species-area relationships, see Velázquez et al. 2016 and Wiegand et al. 2017 for overviews. 7.1 Data We study location data of Abies Amabilis (Pacific silver fir) at eight disjoint 25 × 25 25\\times 25 m plots at Findley Lake Reserve in Washington State, USA. A description of the site conditions is given in Grier et al. 1981 . Figure 9 shows the location of trees at two of the plots for three different time points over 31 years. The area was clear-cut in 1957; the trees in our dataset were present as seedlings before the clear-cut and there appears to have been no reproduction in the stand since then. The first observation was made in 1978, 21 21 years after the area was clear-cut. On average, roughly 80 % 80\\% of the original trees were still present in the second observation in 1990 and approximately 25 % 25\\% of them were present in the third observation in 2009. Figure 9 : Abies amabilis (Pacific silver fir) at two disjoint 25 × 25 25\\times 25 m plots at Findley Lake Reserve in Washington State, USA. The area was clear-cut in 1957; the first column shows trees present in 1978, 21 years after the clear-cut; the second column shows the trees still present in 1990, 33 years after the clear-cut; the third column shows the remaining trees in 2009, 52 years after the clear-cut. 7.2 Competing models Since the data do not show any signs of anisotrophy, we only consider spatially homogeneous models. Our emphasis lies in analyzing which model best captures the spatial clustering present in the data, particularly for the years 1978 and 1990 (see Figure 9 ). For this, we compare five competing models, all of which have been introduced in the context of modeling spatial distribution of plants, see the references given in Table 2 . All five models belong to the class of doubly stochastic Poisson processes, or Cox processes, obtained by considering the intensity function itself a stochastic process. Four models are of the shot-noise type, where the random intensity is obtained by a sum of positive kernel functions k ( ⋅ − x i ) k(\\cdot-x_{i}) centered around points x 1 , x 2 , … x_{1},x_{2},\\ldots belonging to a homogeneous Poisson process with intensity κ &gt; 0 \\kappa&gt;0 . The models we consider are the Matérn, the Thomas, the variance Gamma and the Cauchy model. In addition, we consider the log Gaussian Cox process (LGCP). Table 2 gives a short characterization of each model, as well as references for more details. There is a long-standing tradition in the literature of modelling spatial distribution of trees with the Matérn and Thomas processes, cf. Stoyan et al. 2000 . More recently, the variance Gamma, the Cauchy and the LGCP models have become popular in this context, see e.g. Jalilian et al. 2020 , Waagepetersen et al. 2016 and Zhang et al. 2019 . Table 2 : Specification of the five models used in the Pacific silver fir application. The first four models are shot-noise Cox processes, described in terms of a positive kernel k k . They have an additional parameter κ \\kappa , the intensity of the underlying homogeneous Poisson process. For the log Gaussian Cox process (LGCP), the random intensity is the exponential of a stationary Gaussian random field Z Z . Here, b ⁡ ( 0 , σ ) b(0,\\sigma) refers to the ball of radius σ \\sigma around 0, ϕ σ \\phi_{\\sigma} is the isotropic mean-zero bivariate Gaussian kernel with standard deviation σ ​ I \\sigma I , and K ν K_{\\nu} denotes the modified Bessel function of the second kind. Model Kernel function/intensity Parameters Reference Matérn k ( w ) = ξ 𝟙 { w ∈ b ( 0 , σ ) } k(w)=\\xi\\mathbb{1}\\{w\\in b(0,\\sigma)\\} κ , ξ , σ \\kappa,\\xi,\\sigma Matérn 1960 Thomas k ⁡ ( w ) = ξ ​ ϕ σ ​ ( w ) k(w)=\\xi\\phi_{\\sigma}(w) κ , ξ , σ \\kappa,\\xi,\\sigma Thomas 1949 varGam k ⁡ ( w ) = ξ ​ 1 π ​ 2 ν + 1 ​ σ 2 ​ Γ ​ ( ν + 1 ) ​ ( ‖ w ‖ / σ ) ν ​ K ν ​ ( ‖ u ‖ / σ ) k(w)=\\xi\\frac{1}{\\pi 2^{\\nu+1}\\sigma^{2}\\Gamma(\\nu+1)}(\\|w\\|/\\sigma)^{\\nu}K_{\\nu}(\\|u\\|/\\sigma) κ , ξ , σ , ν \\kappa,\\xi,\\sigma,\\nu Jalilian et al. 2013 Cauchy k ( w ) = 1 2 ​ π ​ σ 2 ( 1 + ‖ w ‖ 2 σ 2 ) − 3 / 2 k(w)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sigma^{2}}\\bigg(1+\\frac{\\|w\\|^{2}}{\\sigma^{2}}\\bigg)^{-3/2} κ , ξ , σ \\kappa,\\xi,\\sigma Ghorbani 2013 LGCP λ ⁡ ( w ) = exp ⁡ ( Z ⁡ ( w ) ) , \\lambda(w)=\\exp(Z(w)), 𝔼 ⁡ [ Z ⁡ ( w ) ] = μ , \\mathbbm{E}[Z(w)]=\\mu, μ , τ , σ \\mu,\\tau,\\sigma Møller et al. 1998 Cov ( Z ( w 1 ) , Z ( w 2 ) ) = τ 2 exp ( − ∥ w 1 − w 2 ∥ / σ ) \\text{Cov}(Z(w_{1}),Z(w_{2}))=\\tau^{2}\\exp(-\\|w_{1}-w_{2}\\|/\\sigma) All five models are fitted in the same manner by minimum contrast, see Diggle and Gratton 1984 and Waagepetersen 2007 . First, the empirical K K -function is estimated from the training set by averaging the estimator in ( 8 ) across all plots in the training set. Second, the parameters Θ \\Theta of the model are fitted to minimize the integrated distance Θ ^ = arg ​ min Θ ⁡ { ∫ 0 r max ( K ^ ​ ( r ) 1 / 4 − K ​ ( r , Θ ) 1 / 4 ) 2 ​ dr } , \\displaystyle\\widehat{\\Theta}=\\argmin_{\\Theta}\\bigg\\{\\int_{0}^{r_{\\max}}(\\widehat{K}(r)^{1/4}-K(r;\\Theta)^{1/4})^{2}\\,dr\\bigg\\}, (11) where K ⁡ ( r , Θ ) K(r;\\Theta) is the theoretical K K -function for the model with parameter Θ . \\Theta. The upper limit r max r_{\\max} should be chosen small relative to the plot dimensions ( Diggle 2013 ) , but the choice of r max r_{\\max} introduces a certain level of arbitrariness in the estimation procedure. We choose r max = 6.25 , r_{\\max}=6.25, one fourth of the plot length, the default value of spatstat . 7.3 Evaluation The observations for each year are analyzed separately such that in each analysis, eight independent realizations of the underlying process are available. We consider a prediction scenario where four of the observations are used to fit a predictive distribution, which is then verified against the other four observations. This is repeated for all possible combinations, leading to a total of 280 score evaluations per year. Some of the scores are correlated, namely if they are based on the same observation (but use potentially different training sets), or if their training sets overlap. Figure 10 : Mean K K -function scores for predicting the spatial distribution of Pacific silver fir for the three years available in the dataset. The models are specified in Table 2 and a homogeneous Poisson model is shown for reference. The boxplots indicate bootstrapped distributions of the mean K K -function score. With a focus on the appropriate modelling of point interaction, we employ the K K -function score for evaluating predictive performance. The results are shown in Figure 10 . For all three time points, the LGCP, variance Gamma and Cauchy model clearly outperform the Matérn and Thomas model. The overall best score is achieved by the Cauchy model for the years 1978 and 1990, and by the LGCP for the year 2009. For the data from 1978 and 1990, permutation tests confirm the superior performance of the Cauchy model against all competing models ( p p -values below 0.01 for all models). For these years, the LGCP mean score is slightly lower than that for the variance Gamma process. These differences, however, are not significant ( p p -value of 0.4 0.4 for 1978 and of 0.46 0.46 for 1990). For the 2009 data, the LGCP model does not significantly outperform the Cauchy model ( p p -value of 0.15), but it is significantly better than the variance Gamma model ( p p -value of 0.04). The lower significance and higher variability of scores for 2009 is caused by the smaller number of observed trees per plot. A reduced number of points increases the variance in the K K -function estimator, increasing the score uncertainty. 7.4 Discussion Previous studies have compared the fit of several of these models to related data sets. Ghorbani 2013 analyzes long-leaf pine location data and shows that the Cauchy model provides a better fit than the Thomas model. Zhang et al. 2019 fit both the Matérn and the variance Gamma model to location data of black locust trees and conclude that the variance Gamma model provides a better fit. In Jalilian et al. 2013 , the authors fit the Thomas, the variance Gamma and the Cauchy model to data sets of three species of rainforest trees. The variance Gamma model provides the best fit for two of the three considered species, and the Cauchy model for the third. Both the Cauchy and the variance Gamma model consistently outperform the Thomas model for all three species. In these studies, the model assessment is performed in-sample. Our out-of-sample study mostly leads to similar conclusions, except that the variance Gamma model gets outperformed by the Cauchy and LGCP models. A possible reason for this is that the variance Gamma model has an additional parameter compared to the other models. This additional flexibility gives an advantage in in-sample studies where the model fit is assessed. In a prediction setting, on the other hand, selecting models with a high number of parameters can increase the risk of overfitting, resulting in reduced predictive performance. 8 Conclusions and discussion This paper introduces a new class of proper scoring rules that combines estimators for summary statistics with the continuous ranked probability score (CRPS). The proposed scoring rules can be computed from random draws of the predictive models. Therefore they can be applied to a wider range of predictive distributions than the commonly used logarithmic score, which requires the density of the predictive model to be known. Moreover, the K K -function score is, to the best of our knowledge, the first proper score for point processes focusing on correct representation of point interactions. Generally, our scores do not require knowledge of mathematical properties of the predictive models, just the ability to generate random draws. This is a particular advantage in the context of point processes where mathematical properties are frequently untractable. The R package spatstat Baddeley et al. 2015 provides a diverse set of tools for simulating a large set of spatial point process models and provides implementations of all common summary statistic estimators. spatstat therefore provides a platform that makes the introduced scoring rules easily applicable in a variety of situations. The code for all studies presented in this paper is publicly available at github.com/ClaudioHeinrich/PointProcessSRs . Our approach is based on the intuitive principle that, when the observation space is complex, the observations and predictions can be mapped into a simpler space for validation. This approach is not restricted to point processes, and opens a fruitful new perspective on the validation of forecasts that live on complex spaces. Indeed, when the observation space is involved, finding proper scoring rules can be difficult, even more so when they ought to be sensitive to certain high level properties of the observation-generating process. The mapping approach replaces this challenge by the much easier task of finding real-(or function-)valued mappings sensitive to these properties. Potential other applications include high-dimensional forecasts and forecasts of spatial fields, as well as function-valued forecasts. In the context of point processes, it is natural to use estimators of summary statistics in the mapping principle, as they are designed to be sensitive to high-level properties of the point process such as clustering or inhomogeneity. The variogram score from Scheuerer and Hamill 2015 follows a similar principle in a different setting, utilizing the variogram estimator of random vectors. An additional advantage of this approach is that there is a wide range of literature analyzing summary statistics and their estimators, and practicioners are familiar using them, which makes scoring rules based on summary statistics easier to interpret. Besides the subjective selection of a mapping, the mapping approach requires the selection of a proper scoring rule on the codomain. We opted for the CRPS since it is strictly proper and can be efficiently approximated by Monte-Carlo methods. When computational time is highly important, an attractive alternative might be the mean square error (MSE), S ⁡ ( 𝐲 , F ) := ( 𝐲 − 𝔼 F ​ [ 𝐗 ] ) 2 S(\\mathbf{y},F):=(\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbbm{E}_{F}[\\mathbf{X}])^{2} . The expectation of the pushforward measure T ^ ​ ( F ) \\widehat{T}(F) can be Monte-Carlo approximated, and the computational costs are lower, since for the CRPS the term 𝔼 F ​ [ | 𝐲 − 𝐗 | ] \\mathbbm{E}_{F}[|\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{X}|] needs to be approximated for each observation 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} separately. However, since the MSE is not strictly proper the resulting score may be less sensitive to miscalibrations. There are several avenues for potential future research. Generally, assessing the usefulness of this mapping approach in other contexts as mentioned above should be investigated. An important application for point-process-valued forecasts is earthquake rate forecasting, and a variety of methods for forecast validation has been developed in this community. However, prediction so far mostly focused exclusively on the intensity, and moving to probabilistic predictions is an ongoing endeavor, see Schorlemmer et al. 2018 . This requires the introduction of new evaluation metrics, and the scores presented in this paper could provide an entry point. References Abramson (1982) Abramson IS. On bandwidth variation in kernel estimates-a square root law. Ann Stat 1982;p. 1217–1223. Baddeley and Silverman (1984) Baddeley AJ, Silverman BW. A cautionary example on the use of second-order methods for analyzing point patterns. Biometrics 1984;40(4):1089–1093. Baddeley et al. (2000) Baddeley AJ, Møller J, Waagepetersen R. Non- and semi-parametric estimation of interaction in inhomogeneous point patterns. Stat Neerl 2000;54(3):329–350. Baddeley et al. (2005) Baddeley A, Turner R, Møller J, Hazelton M. Residual analysis for spatial point processes. J Roy Stat Soc B 2005;67:617–666. Baddeley et al. (2011) Baddeley A, Rubak E, Møller J. Score, pseudo-score and residual diagnostics for spatial point process models. Stat Sci 2011;26(4):613–646. Baddeley et al. (2015) Baddeley A, Rubak E, Turner R. Spatial Point Patterns: Methodology and Application