e above setup: Theorem 2.1 ( [ 3 ] ) . Let 𝒳 \\mathcal{X} , 𝒜 \\mathcal{A} , and L L be as above. Further, denote with H H , I I , and d d the associated (generalized) scoring rule, entropy, and divergence. Then 1. the scoring rule ( 3 ) is proper , that is μ ↦ 𝔼 X ∼ ν ​ [ s ⁡ ( X , μ ) ] \\displaystyle\\mu\\mapsto\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\nu}[s(X,\\mu)] (7) is minimized at μ = ν \\mu=\\nu . 2. μ ↦ H ⁡ ( μ ) \\mu\\mapsto H(\\mu) is concave, 3. ( ν , μ ) ↦ 𝔼 X ∼ ν ​ [ s ⁡ ( X , μ ) ] (\\nu,\\mu)\\mapsto\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\nu}[s(X,\\mu)] is affine in ν \\nu for every μ \\mu , 4. d ⁡ ( μ , ν ) ⩾ 0 d(\\mu,\\nu)\\geqslant 0 with equality for μ = ν \\mu=\\nu , 5. I ⁡ ( μ , ν ) ≥ 0 I(\\mu,\\nu)\\geq 0 with equality for μ ⟂ ⟂ ν \\mu\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\nu . Different applications areas demand different scoring rules. Classical choices for the Euclidean case 𝒳 = ℝ n \\mathcal{X}=\\mathbb{R}^{n} are besides the already mentioned log score, the Brier score, the Tsallis score, the Bregman score, the Hyvärinen score, etc.; see [ 3 ] for details. The aim of the remainder of this article is to study the case when 𝒳 \\mathcal{X} is a space of paths or a space of equivalence classes of paths (under reparametrisation). A Toy Example: From Feature Maps to Bayes Actions. To motivate our scoring rule for paths let us first revisit the vector-valued case, 𝒳 = ℝ n \\mathcal{X}=\\mathbb{R}^{n} . To arrive at a proper scoring rule, the space of Bayes actions 𝒜 \\mathcal{A} should be large enough to characterize any (sufficiently nice) probability measures on 𝒳 = ℝ n \\mathcal{X}=\\mathbb{R}^{n} . A classic way to characterize a probability measure, is to consider the sequence of moments, ( 1 , 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ] , 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ⊗ 2 ] , 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ⊗ 3 ] , … ) \\displaystyle(1,\\mathbb{E}[X],\\mathbb{E}[X^{\\otimes 2}],\\mathbb{E}[X^{\\otimes 3}],\\ldots) (8) that is, 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ] \\mathbb{E}[X] is the mean vector, 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ⊗ 2 ] \\mathbb{E}[X^{\\otimes 2}] is the covariance matrix, etc. (We tacitly assume that the sequence of moments is well-defined and decays quickly enough so that it characterizes the measure, see Remark 2.2 ). The sequence ( 8 ) is an element of the set ℋ ≔ ∏ m ≥ 0 ( ℝ n ) ⊗ m \\displaystyle\\mathcal{H}\\coloneqq\\prod_{m\\geq 0}(\\mathbb{R}^{n})^{\\otimes m} (9) of sequences of tensor of increasing degree m m . In fact, this set ℋ \\mathcal{H} forms a vector space by element-wise addition of tensors of the same degree. If we define the “feature map” φ : ℝ n → ℋ , x ↦ ( 1 , x , x ⊗ 2 , … ) . \\displaystyle\\varphi:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\to\\mathcal{H},\\quad x\\mapsto(1,x,x^{\\otimes 2},\\ldots). (10) With the above notation, the moment sequence ( 8 ) is simply the mean of φ ⁡ ( X ) \\varphi(X) , 𝔼 ⁡ [ φ ⁡ ( X ) ] = ( 1 , 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ] , 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ⊗ 2 ] , 𝔼 ⁡ [ X ⊗ 3 ] , … ) ∈ ℋ \\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi(X)]=(1,\\mathbb{E}[X],\\mathbb{E}[X^{\\otimes 2}],\\mathbb{E}[X^{\\otimes 3}],\\ldots)\\in\\mathcal{H} (11) Using a well-known characterization of the mean as minimizer of a quadratic we can introduce the loss function ℒ ⁡ ( x , a ) ≔ ‖ φ ⁡ ( X ) − a ‖ 2 \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}(x,a)\\coloneqq\\|\\varphi(X)-a\\|^{2} (12) which associates with a probability measure μ \\mu on ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} the Bayes action a μ ≡ 𝔼 ⁡ [ φ ⁡ ( X ) ] ≡ argmin m ∈ ℋ ​ 𝔼 ​ [ ℒ ⁡ ( X , a ) ] . \\displaystyle a_{\\mu}\\equiv\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi(X)]\\equiv\\underset{m\\in\\mathcal{H}}{\\operatorname{argmin}}\\mathbb{E}[\\mathcal{L}(X,a)]. (13) It follows from general principles that the resulting scoring rule on the state 𝒳 = ℝ n \\mathcal{X}=\\mathbb{R}^{n} and action space 𝒜 = ℋ \\mathcal{A}=\\mathcal{H} , s ⁡ ( x , μ ) = ℒ ⁡ ( x , a μ ) \\displaystyle s(x,\\mu)=\\mathcal{L}(x,a_{\\mu}) (14) is proper. Despite the elementary nature of this example it gives us a simple way to associate with any “feature map” a Bayes action and a scoring rule and already simple variations lead to interesting questions: for example, if the quadratic loss function is replaced by the absolute value, one ends with medians of moment as Bayes action and many other choices are possible. Such questions fall under the framework of “elicitation” of properties of probability measures with scoring rules which is an active research area, already in the classical vector-valued (even scalar) case; see [ 41 ] and [ 42 , 43 , 44 ] for some of the recent advances. Remark 2.2 . The question which probability measures on ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} are characterized by the moment sequence ( 8 ) is classical but quite subtle in general. But for compactly supported measures this trivially holds. Our focus will soon shift to probability measures on pathspace but since spaces of paths are generically not even locally compact, compactness is a too strong assumption; in fact, important examples of measures on pathspace such as geometric Brownian motion are not characterized by their “signature moments” that we will use in Section 3 and Section 4 . However, one can replace the moment sequence ( 8 ) by a normalized moment sequence that characterizes any probability measure on ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} and this extends to path space and signature moments, see [ 17 ] for details. Hence, for simplicity, we assume throughout that the probability measures are characterized by their expected feature map (since this is possible by a slight modification of the feature map). 3 Structure of the Space of (Unparametrized) Paths We review classic mathematical results about spaces of paths going back to seminal work of Chen [ 4 ] . The main result is the existence of a “feature map” Φ : Tracks → ℋ , 𝐱 ↦ Φ ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) \\displaystyle\\Phi:\\operatorname{Tracks}\\to\\mathcal{H},\\,\\quad\\mathbf{x}\\mapsto\\Phi(\\mathbf{x}) (15) that has as domain the set Tracks \\operatorname{Tracks} that consist of equivalence classes of paths that evolve in ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} , and as co-domain the linear space ℋ \\mathcal{H} . We already encountered ℋ \\mathcal{H} in Section 2 where it arose as the vector space of sequences of tensors 𝐭 m ∈ ( ℝ n ) ⊗ m \\mathbf{t}_{m}\\in(\\mathbb{R}^{n})^{\\otimes m} of increasing degree m m , see ( 9 ). However, ℋ \\mathcal{H} is not only a vector space but a so-called Hopf algebra: we can multiply elements of ℋ \\mathcal{H} and take the “inverse” of elements of ℋ \\mathcal{H} . One of the well-known and attractive properties of the map ( 15 ) is that these two algebraic operations (multiplication and inversion) capture the natural operations on Tracks \\operatorname{Tracks} (conatentation and reversal). Exploiting this correspondence will be essential for our main results in Section 4 . The Domain of Paths. A bounded variation path 1 1 1 For simplicity we focus on (equivalence classes of) bounded variation paths but all the results immediately extend to paths with much less regularity such as trajectories of stochastic differential equations or (fractional) Brownian motion by replacing the iterated Riemann–Stieltjes integrals by stochastic integrals or rough path integrals [ 13 ] 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} in ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} is a continuous map 𝐱 : [ 0 , T ] → ℝ n such that ∥ 𝐱 ∥ ≔ sup ( t 1 , … , t L ) : 0 ≤ t 1 &lt; … &lt; t L &lt; T ∑ ∥ 𝐱 ( t i + 1 ) − 𝐱 ( t i ) ∥ &lt; ∞ . \\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}:[0,T]\\to\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\,\\text{ such that }\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|\\coloneqq\\sup_{(t_{1},\\ldots,t_{L}):0\\leq t_{1}&lt;\\ldots&lt;t_{L}&lt;T}\\sum\\|\\mathbf{x}(t_{i+1})-\\mathbf{x}(t_{i})\\|&lt;\\infty. (16) For a , b ∈ ℝ n a,b\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n} we denote with Paths ⁡ ( a , b ) \\operatorname{Paths}({{a},{b}}) the set of all continuous bounded variation paths that start at a a and end in b b , Paths ( a , b ) ≔ { 𝐱 | 𝐱 : [ 0 , T ] → ℝ n is of bounded variation, T &gt; 0 , 𝐱 ( 0 ) = a , 𝐱 ( T ) = b } . \\displaystyle\\operatorname{Paths}({{a},{b}})\\coloneqq\\{\\mathbf{x}|\\mathbf{x}:[0,T]\\to\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\text{ is of bounded variation, }T&gt;0,\\mathbf{x}(0)=a,\\mathbf{x}(T)=b\\}. (17) and by Paths ≔ ⋃ a , b ∈ ℝ n Paths ⁡ ( a , b ) \\displaystyle\\operatorname{P