s of high-frequency limits when the grid gets finer, that is sup | t i + 1 m − t i m | → 0 \\sup|t_{i+1}^{m}-t_{i}^{m}|\\to 0 as m → ∞ m\\to\\infty for a sequence ( t i m ) i = 1 , … , L m (t_{i}^{m})_{i=1,\\ldots,L_{m}} . Finally, many popular models are naturally formulated in continuous time rather than discrete time. From Tracks to Paths. Our guiding philosophy is that the fundamental object is the set Tracks \\operatorname{Tracks} rather than the set Paths \\operatorname{Paths} since the former allows to ignore the time parametrization; note that the set of time parametrisations is infinite-dimensional since every continuous function τ : [ 0 , T ] → [ 0 , T ′ ] \\tau:[0,T]\\to[0,T^{\\prime}] can be used to reparametrize a path t ↦ 𝐱 ⁡ ( t ) t\\mapsto\\mathbf{x}(t) to t ↦ 𝐱 ⁡ ( τ ⁡ ( t ) ) t\\mapsto\\mathbf{x}(\\tau(t)) , hence working with Tracks \\operatorname{Tracks} factors out an infinite-dimensional class of invariances. Nevertheless, for certain applications the parametrization matters – at least to a certain degree. However, this can be easily addressed by adding time as an additional coordinate: to emphasize the dimension n n of the state space ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} in which the paths evolve we write Paths n \\operatorname{Paths}_{n} (instead of just Paths \\operatorname{Paths} that we used until now); similarly Tracks n \\operatorname{Tracks}_{n} for the set of equivalence classes of Paths n \\operatorname{Paths}_{n} . Given 𝐱 ∈ Paths n \\mathbf{x}\\in\\operatorname{Paths}_{n} we embed Paths n ↪ Paths n + 1 , 𝐱 ↦ ( t ↦ ( t , 𝐱 ⁡ ( t ) ) ) . \\displaystyle\\operatorname{Paths}_{n}\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{Paths}_{n+1},\\quad\\mathbf{x}\\mapsto\\left(t\\mapsto(t,\\mathbf{x}(t))\\right). (46) That is a path evolving in ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} is turned into a path in ℝ n + 1 \\mathbb{R}^{n+1} by simply adding an additional coordinate that is time itself. This makes the parametrization part of the “shape” of the trajectory which in turn is exactly the information that distinguishes tracks, hence Paths n ↪ Tracks n + 1 . \\displaystyle\\operatorname{Paths}_{n}\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{Tracks}_{n+1}. (47) This injection shows that any scoring rule for tracks induces a scoring rule for paths. 4 Scoring Rules For Tracks and Paths Motivated by the toy example in Section 2 with the moment feature map φ \\varphi for data in ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} , we now follow the analogous reasoning on the non-Euclidean space of tracks by using the feature map Φ \\Phi instead of φ \\varphi , note that neither the domain nor the image of Φ \\Phi is a linear space as its image is the group G G that is embedded into the linear space ℋ \\mathcal{H} . Recall that in Section 3 we have seen that it is exactly the group structure that captures the structure of space of tracks of concatenation and reversal. This motivates us to replace the additive inverse in ​ φ ​ ( X ) − m ​ by the group inverse to get ​ Φ ​ ( 𝐗 ) ​ m − 1 . \\displaystyle\\text{replace the additive inverse in }\\varphi(X)-m\\text{ by the group inverse to get }\\Phi(\\mathbf{X})m^{-1}. (48) Our first main result Theorem 2.1 shows that this indeed leads to a proper scoring rule on the space of tracks and operations on Tracks \\operatorname{Tracks} turn into algebraic operations in decision space. Consquences of this result are Proposition 4.3 and Corollary 4.4 which show how the associated entropy on the space of tracks is invariant to time-reversal and behaves under conditioning on the past. A Scoring Rule for Tracks. We need to introduce an additional space ℋ ⋆ \\mathcal{H}^{\\star} wedged between ℋ \\mathcal{H} and G G defined as the space of all elements 𝐭 ∈ ℋ \\mathbf{t}\\in\\mathcal{H} starting with a one, formally ℋ ⋆ = { 𝐭 ∈ ℋ : 𝐭 0 = 1 } . \\displaystyle\\mathcal{H}^{\\star}=\\{\\mathbf{t}\\in\\mathcal{H}:\\mathbf{t}_{0}=1\\}. (49) Unlike ℋ \\mathcal{H} , ℋ ⋆ \\mathcal{H}^{\\star} is not a vector space or a Hopf algebra, but it is a group like G G while also being conve