tisfies L ⁡ ( 𝐭 ) = L ⁡ ( α ⁡ ( 𝐭 ) ) L(\\mathbf{t})=L(\\alpha(\\mathbf{t})) , then a μ ← right ≔ \\displaystyle a^{\\text{right}}_{\\overleftarrow{\\mu}}\\coloneqq argmin m ∈ T ⁡ ( ℝ n ) ​ 𝔼 𝐗 ∼ μ ← ​ L ​ ( Φ ⁡ ( 𝐗 ) ​ m − 1 ) = \\displaystyle\\underset{m\\in\\mathrm{T}({\\mathbb{R}^{n}})}{\\operatorname{argmin}}\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbf{X}\\sim\\overleftarrow{\\mu}}L(\\Phi(\\mathbf{X})m^{-1})= (70) argmin m ∈ T ⁡ ( ℝ n ) ​ 𝔼 𝐗 ∼ μ ​ L ​ ( α ⁡ ( Φ ⁡ ( 𝐗 ) ) ​ m − 1 ) = \\displaystyle\\underset{m\\in\\mathrm{T}({\\mathbb{R}^{n}})}{\\operatorname{argmin}}\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbf{X}\\sim\\mu}L(\\alpha(\\Phi(\\mathbf{X}))m^{-1})= (71) argmin m ∈ T ⁡ ( ℝ n ) ​ 𝔼 𝐗 ∼ μ ​ L ​ ( α ⁡ ( m − 1 ) ​ Φ ​ ( 𝐗 ) ) ≔ α ⁡ ( a μ left ) . \\displaystyle\\underset{m\\in\\mathrm{T}({\\mathbb{R}^{n}})}{\\operatorname{argmin}}\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbf{X}\\sim\\mu}L(\\alpha(m^{-1})\\Phi(\\mathbf{X}))\\coloneqq\\alpha(a^{\\text{left}}_{\\mu}). (72) ∎ Entropy, Divergence, and Mutual Information on the Space of Tracks. We now focus on the (generalized) entropy, divergence and mutual information for probability measures on tracks that results from Definition 4.1 . Proposition 4.3 . For any two probability measures μ \\mu and ν \\nu on Tracks \\operatorname{Tracks} it holds that 1. H left ​ ( μ ⋆ ν | μ ) = H left ​ ( ν | μ ) H^{\\text{left}}(\\mu\\star\\nu|\\mu)=H^{\\text{left}}(\\nu|\\mu) and H right ​ ( μ ⋆ ν | ν ) = H right ​ ( μ | ν ) H^{\\text{right}}(\\mu\\star\\nu|\\nu)=H^{\\text{right}}(\\mu|\\nu) 2. If L L satisfies L ⁡ ( 𝐭 ) = L ⁡ ( α ​ 𝐭 ) L(\\mathbf{t})=L(\\alpha\\mathbf{t}) , then H right ​ ( μ ) \\displaystyle H^{\\text{right}}(\\mu) = H left ​ ( μ ← ) , \\displaystyle=H^{\\text{left}}(\\overleftarrow{\\mu}), (73) d right ​ ( ν , μ ) \\displaystyle d^{\\text{right}}(\\nu,\\mu) = d left ​ ( ν ← , μ ← ) , \\displaystyle=d^{\\text{left}}(\\overleftarrow{\\nu},\\overleftarrow{\\mu}), (74) I right ​ ( μ , ν ) \\displaystyle I^{\\text{right}}(\\mu,\\nu) = I left ​ ( μ ← , ν ) \\displaystyle=I^{\\text{left}}(\\overleftarrow{\\mu},\\nu) (75) Proof. ( 1 ) H left ​ ( μ ⋆ ν | μ ) \\displaystyle H^{\\text{left}}(\\mu\\star\\nu|\\mu) = 𝔼 X ∼ μ ⋆ ν | μ ​ L ​ ( ( a μ ⋆ ν | μ left ) − 1 ​ Φ ​ ( X ) ) \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\mu\\star\\nu|\\mu}L((a^{\\text{left}}_{\\mu\\star\\nu|\\mu})^{-1}\\Phi(X)) (76) = 𝔼 X ∼ ν | μ ​ L ​ ( ( a ν | μ left ) − 1 ​ ν ​ ( Φ ) − 1 ​ ν ​ ( Φ ) ​ Φ ​ ( X ) ) = H left ​ ( ν | μ ) . \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\nu|\\mu}L((a^{\\text{left}}_{\\nu|\\mu})^{-1}\\nu(\\Phi)^{-1}\\nu(\\Phi)\\Phi(X))=H^{\\text{left}}(\\nu|\\mu). (77) ( 2 ) 𝔼 X ∼ ν ​ L ​ ( Φ ⁡ ( X ) ​ ( a μ right ) − 1 ) = 𝔼 X ∼ ν ​ L ​ ( α ​ ( a μ right ) − 1 ​ α ​ Φ ​ ( X ) ) = 𝔼 X ∼ ν ← ​ L ​ ( ( a μ ← left ) − 1 ​ α ​ Φ ​ ( X ) ) . \\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\nu}L(\\Phi(X)(a^{\\text{right}}_{\\mu})^{-1})=\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\nu}L(\\alpha(a^{\\text{right}}_{\\mu})^{-1}\\alpha\\Phi(X))=\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\overleftarrow{\\nu}}L((a^{\\text{left}}_{\\overleftarrow{\\mu}})^{-1}\\alpha\\Phi(X)). (78) The other equalities follow. ∎ Corollary 4.4 . If L L satisfies L ⁡ ( 𝐭 ) = L ⁡ ( α ⁡ ( 𝐭 ) ) L(\\mathbf{t})=L(\\alpha(\\mathbf{t})) and a measure μ \\mu is reversible , that is, μ \\mu and μ ← \\overleftarrow{\\mu} are equal up to their starting distribution, then H right ​ ( μ ) = H left ​ ( μ ) . \\displaystyle H^{\\text{right}}(\\mu)=H^{\\text{left}}(\\mu). (79) In the experiments we will simulate sample paths from Brownian motion and since this is a reversible process it will not matter if we use the left- or right entropy. Remark 4.5 . An alternative to using the group structure in Definition 4.1 of the Bayes act is to use that the group G G is embedded into the linear space ℋ \\mathcal{H} and use this linear structure. That is, we define a Bayes act as a μ ≔ argmin m ∈ T ⁡ ( ℝ n ) ⁡ 𝔼 𝐗 ∼ μ ​ [ L ​ ( Φ ⁡ ( 𝐗 ) − m ) 2 ] a_{\\mu}\\coloneqq\\operatorname{argmin}_{m\\in\\mathrm{T}({\\mathbb{R}^{n}})}\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbf{X}\\sim\\mu}[L(\\Phi(\\mathbf{X})-m)^{2}] . It is easy to show that this gives a proper scoring rule and that a μ = 𝔼 ⁡ [ Φ ⁡ ( 𝐗 ) ] a_{\\mu}=\\mathbb{E}[\\Phi(\\mathbf{X})] . However, this scoring rule relies on the embedding of the group into its ambient vector space and does not account for or respect the group structure. Moreover, the resulting divergence and entropy reduce to just the Euclidean distance and usual variance. The same remark extends to (signature) kernel based scoring, where linear methods are used in an RKHS; see the discussion about non-kernel based scoring in the introduction. Remark 4.6 . We identify a stochastic process as a path- or sequence-valued random variable, possibly even ignoring its parametrization. However, for some applications the filtration of a stochastic process matters and one could ask to extend the scoring rule framework to this. A kernel that captures the filtration was introduced in [ 50 ] and a kernel algorithm and new applications given in [ 19 ] . To get a non-kernel scoring one could try to replace Φ \\Phi in Definition ( 4.1 ) by the higher-rank signature from [ 50 ] . 5 Gradient Descent on the Space of Tracks Given a smooth function f : ℝ n → ℝ f:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\to\\mathbb{R} , the simplest update rule for gradient descent is x i + 1 = x i − η ∇ f ( x i ) \\displaystyle x_{i+1}=x_{i}-\\eta\\nabla f(x_{i}) (80) and under additional regularity of f f , the resulting sequence ( x i ) i ⊂ ℝ n (x_{i})_{i}\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{n} converges to a minimizer of f f . Our interest lies minimizing functions f : Tracks → ℝ f:\\operatorname{Tracks}\\to\\mathbb{R} . In accordance with our guiding theme we do not identify these domains as linear spaces where classical gradient descent can be applied. However, we have seen that Φ \\Phi provides an isomorphism between the space of tracks and the free group G G (up to forgetting the starting point of the track) Tracks ≃ G \\displaystyle\\operatorname{Tracks}\\simeq G (81) Hence, the minimization problem of a function f f of tracks can be re-formulated as a minimization problem of a function F = F ⁡ ( g ) ≔ f ∘ Φ − 1 ​ ( g ) F=F(g)\\coloneqq f\\circ\\Phi^{-1}(g) on the free group G G . That is, the general problem we try to solve is to find argmin g ∈ G ⁡ F ⁡ ( g ) ​ for ​ F : G → ℝ \\displaystyle\\operatorname{argmin}_{g\\in G}F(g)\\text{ for }F:G\\to\\mathbb{R} (82) for any F F in class of sufficiently “smooth” real-valued functions on G G . There have been many attempts to generalize gradient descent to non-linear domains. Arguably the the case of Riemannian manifolds [ 51 ] is the most well-developed among these. However, the group G G does not come with a Riemannian structure (to wit, only a Sub-Riemannian structure [ 52 ] ). We follow here a somewhat different approach inspired by work of Pierre Pansu [ 53 ] that directly uses the group structure to define gradients. We show that this gradient in turn allows us to give a straightforward generalization of the gradient update rule ( 80 ) from ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} to G G , so that the resulting sequence ( g i ) i ⊂ G (g_{i})_{i}\\subset G converges to the minimizer. Pansu Derivatives. The derivative D ​ f ​ ( x ) ​ ( ⋅ ) Df(x)(\\cdot) of a function f : ℝ n → ℝ \\displaystyle f:\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\to\\mathbb{R} (83) at a point x x is a linear functional of ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} that can be defined as the limit D ​ f ​ ( x ) ​ ( h ) ≔ lim h → 0 f ⁡ ( x + λ ​ h ) − f ⁡ ( x ) λ . \\displaystyle Df(x)(h)\\coloneqq\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+\\lambda h)-f(x)}{\\lambda}. (84) Identifying ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} as a the additive group ( ℝ n , + ) (\\mathbb{R}^{n},+) one can regard the difference quotient that appears in the limit as applying to the group operation to x x and λ ​ h \\lambda h . Hence, if we have also have a generalization of the multiplication with the scalar λ \\lambda , then the above difference quotient makes sense for other groups than the additive group ( ℝ n , + ) (\\mathbb{R}^{n},+) . To formalize scalar multiplication, it turns out that the right notion is that of a Carnot group: a Carnot group is a Lie group C C that carries a left-invariant geodesic distance dist : C × C → [ 0 , ∞ ) \\operatorname{dist}:C\\times C\\to[0,\\infty) and for each λ &gt; 0 \\lambda&gt;0 a bijection δ λ : C → C ​ such that ​ dist ⁡ ( δ λ ​ ( g ) , δ λ ​ ( h ) ) = λ ​ dist ⁡ ( g , h ) . \\displaystyle\\delta_{\\lambda}:C\\to C\\text{ such that }\\operatorname{dist}\\left(\\delta_{\\lambda}(g),\\delta_{\\lambda}(h)\\right)=\\lambda\\operatorname{dist}\\left(g,h\\right). (85) However, our focus is on the free group G G and here the scaling δ λ \\delta_{\\lambda} by a scalar λ &gt; 0 \\lambda&gt;0 has an explicit form Proposition 5.1 . The group G ⊂ ℋ G\\subset\\mathcal{H} equipped with the geodesic distance and δ : λ × G → G , δ λ ​ g = δ λ ​ ( 1 , g 1 , g 2 , g 3 , … ) = ( 1 , λ ​ g 1 , λ 2 ​ g 2 , λ 3 ​ g 3 , … ) . \\displaystyle\\delta:\\lambda\\times G\\to G,\\quad\\delta_{\\lambda}g=\\delta_{\\lambda}(1,g_{1},g_{2},g_{3},\\ldots)=(1,\\lambda g_{1},\\lambda^{2}g_{2},\\lambda^{3}g_{3},\\ldots). (86) forms a limit of Carnot groups. We now have all we need to define the (Pansu) derivative. We denote by G ⋆ G^{\\star} the topological dual of G G . Definition 5.2 . Let f : G → ℝ f:G\\to\\mathbb{R} . We define D ​ f : G → G ⋆ Df:G\\to G^{\\star} as D ​ f ​ ( g ) ​ h = lim λ ↓ 0 f ⁡ ( g ​ δ λ ​ h ) − f ⁡ ( g ) λ \\displaystyle Df(g)h=\\lim_{\\lambda\\downarrow 0}\\frac{f(g\\delta_{\\lambda}h)-f(g)}{\\lambda} (87) whenever this limit exists and call D ​ f ​ ( g ) ​ h Df(g)h the Pansu derivative of f f at g g in direction h h . If f f has a Pansu derivative for all g ∈ G g\\in G then we say that f f is Pansu differentiable. Analogous we define the spaces C k ​ ( G , ℝ ) C^{k}(G,\\mathbb{R}) of k k -times Pansu differentiable functions. The Pansu derivative behaves very similar to the classic linear gradient. For example, for the proof of convergence of gradient descent on G G we make use of the following “Taylor expansion”. Lemma 5.3 . If f ∈ C 3 ​ ( G , ℝ ) f\\in C^{3}(G,\\mathbb{R}) , then f ⁡ ( g ​ h ) = f ⁡ ( g ) + D ​ f ​ ( g ) ​ h 1 + 1 2 ​ D 2 ​ f ​ ( g ) ​ h 2 + O ⁡ ( ∥ h 3 ∥ ) \\displaystyle f(gh)=f(g)+Df(g)h_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}D^{2}f(g)h_{2}+O(\\lVert h_{3}\\rVert) (88) Proof. Consider the function A : ℝ → ℝ A:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R} A ⁡ ( λ ) = f ⁡ ( g ​ δ λ ​ h ) \\displaystyle A(\\lambda)=f(g\\delta_{\\lambda}h) (89) then A A is C 3 C^{3} and by a (classical linear) Taylor expansion we may write A ⁡ ( 1 ) = A ⁡ ( 0 ) + A ˙ ​ ( 0 ) + 1 2 ​ A ¨ ​ ( 0 ) + O ⁡ ( ∥ A ( 3 ) ​ ( 0 ) ∥ ) \\displaystyle A(1)=A(0)+\\dot{A}(0)+\\frac{1}{2}\\ddot{A}(0)+O(\\lVert A^{(3)}(0)\\rVert) (90) which translates into the asserted equation since h ⊗ n h^{\\otimes n} is contained in h n h_{n} . ∎ Remark 5.4 . A popular approach to differentiating functions of paths is to use a Fréchet derivative as in Malliavin calculus, i.e. one identifies the space of paths as a linear space, see [ 54 ] . However, the above Pansu derivative is of a very different nature and – by construction – respects the non-Euclidean structure of paths resp. tracks. Gradient Descent on G G . The idea of gradient descent to take a step in a direction that minimises f f in a neighbourhood B h B_{h} . In our (Lie) group G G , a natural choice is to choose some vector v ∈ ℝ n v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n} and use an exponential neighbourhood g ​ e η ​ v ge^{\\eta v} of g ∈ G g\\in G where exp \\exp denotes the exponential from Lie algebra to Lie group. Hence, the question becomes how choose v v to minimise f ⁡ ( g ​ e η ​ v ) f(ge^{\\eta v}) . To do so, note that f ⁡ ( g ​ e η ​ v ) = f ⁡ ( g ​ δ η ​ e v ) \\displaystyle f(ge^{\\eta v})=f(g\\delta_{\\eta}e^{v}) (91) whenever v ∈ ℝ n v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n} . Now using Lemma 5.3 shows f ⁡ ( g ​ δ η ​ e v ) = f ⁡ ( g ) + D ​ f ​ ( g ) ​ e 1 η ​ v + η 2 = f ⁡ ( g ) + η ​ D ​ f ​ ( g ) ⋅ v + η 2 . \\displaystyle f(g\\delta_{\\eta}e^{v})=f(g)+Df(g)e^{\\eta v}_{1}+\\eta^{2}=f(g)+\\eta Df(g)\\cdot v+\\eta^{2}. (92) This suggests that the direction of steepest descent in the exponential neighbourhood is indeed given by the Pansu derivative (henceforth, and with slight abuse of notation, we identify the resulting element of G ⋆ G^{\\star} as an element of the Hilbert space ℋ \\mathcal{H} since G G and G ∗ G^{*} both embed into ℋ \\mathcal{H} ), v = − D ​ f ​ ( g ) . \\displaystyle v=-Df(g). (93) This leads to the following geometric descent rule g i + 1 = g i ​ e − η ​ D ​ f ​ ( g i ) . \\displaystyle g_{i+1}=g_{i}e^{-\\eta Df(g_{i})}. (94) As for classic gradient descent, we need a notion of convexity to guarantee convergence to a minimum. Definition 5.5 . We say that a function f : G → ℝ f:G\\to\\mathbb{R} is geometrically convex if f ⁡ ( g ​ δ λ ​ ( g − 1 ​ h ) ) ⩽ ( 1 − λ ) ​ f ​ ( g ) + λ ​ f ​ ( h ) \\displaystyle f(g\\delta_{\\lambda}(g^{-1}h))\\leqslant(1-\\lambda)f(g)+\\lambda f(h) (95) for any 0 ⩽ λ ⩽ 1 0\\leqslant\\lambda\\leqslant 1 . As in the linear case, one can show that if f : G → ℝ f:G\\to\\mathbb{R} is geometrically convex and C 2 C^{2} , then D 2 ​ f ​ ( g ) D^{2}f(g) is positive definite everywhere. However, D 2 ​ f D^{2}f is not symmetric in general unlike in the linear case. Putting everything together allows us to mimic the convergence proof of gradient descent in linear spaces which ultimately justifies the above informal derivation of the update rule. Theorem 5.6 . 1. The geometric update rule is transitive, that is, one may go from any point in the group to any other point using updates of the form ( 94 ). 2. If f f is geometrically convex and bounded from below with bounded second Pansu derivatives, then for η \\eta sufficiently small the sequence in Equation ( 94 ) converges to a minimum Proof. Item 1 follows from Chow’s theorem [ 55 ] which states that G G is generated by simple exponentials. For Item 2 let L L be a bound on the derivatives of f f . We may write f ⁡ ( g ​ h ) ⩽ f ⁡ ( g ) + D ​ f ​ ( g ) ​ h 1 + 1 2 ​ L ​ ∥ h 2 ∥ . \\displaystyle f(gh)\\leqslant f(g)+Df(g)h_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}L\\lVert h_{2}\\rVert. (96) Hence, if g n + 1 = g n ​ e − η ​ D ​ f ​ ( g n ) g_{n+1}=g_{n}e^{-\\eta Df(g_{n})} , then f ⁡ ( g n + 1 ) \\displaystyle f(g_{n+1}) ⩽ f ⁡ ( g n ) − η ​ ∥ D ​ f ​ ( g n ) ∥ 2 + 1 2 ​ η 2 ​ L ​ ∥ D ​ f ​ ( g n ) ∥ 2 = f ⁡ ( g n ) + ( L 2 ​ η 2 − η ) ​ ∥ D ​ f ​ ( g n ) ∥ 2 \\displaystyle\\leqslant f(g_{n})-\\eta\\lVert Df(g_{n})\\rVert^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\eta^{2}L\\lVert Df(g_{n})\\rVert^{2}=f(g_{n})+(\\frac{L}{2}\\eta^{2}-\\eta)\\lVert Df(g_{n})\\rVert^{2} (97) which is smaller than f ⁡ ( g n ) f(g_{n}) for η \\eta small enough whenever D ​ f ​ ( g n ) ≠ 0 Df(g_{n})\\not=0 . Hence this is a strictly decreasing sequence and converges to a minimum. ∎ Theorem 5.6 gives the analogous convergence guarantees as regular gradient descent and is simple to implement. 6 Experiments In all our experiments we take as loss function L L the squared norm, L ⁡ ( 𝐭 ) = ∥ 𝐭 ∥ 2 = ∑ m = 1 M ∥ 𝐭 m ∥ 2 . \\displaystyle L(\\mathbf{t})=\\lVert\\mathbf{t}\\rVert^{2}=\\sum_{m=1}^{M}\\lVert\\mathbf{t}_{m}\\rVert^{2}. (98) which is symmetric by Lemma 3.6 . Note that the function L L is smooth as a sum of squares, and since Φ \\Phi is also smooth, since it is polynomial in the increments of its input when computed up to some fixed degree M M , we can easily compute d , H , d,H, and I I by automatic differentiation. For the computation of Φ \\Phi we use the signatory [ 56 ] package which allows for fast and easy computations. Remark 6.1 . In the experiments we will simulate sample paths from Brownian motion. As this is a reversible process it does not matter if entropy is computed from the left or the right by Corollary 4.4 , and we will normally compute it from the right. 6.1 Comparing a warped time-series to itself One natural question to ask is how well the regularised versions of DTW that allow for differentiation are able to incorporate the parametrisation invariance. One drawback of these regularisation of DTW is that it typically leads to a trade-off between the smoothness and parametrization invariance. Given two TS 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} and 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} we then use sDTW d sDTW ​ ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) d_{\\text{sDTW}}(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}) which is the regularised version of DTW introduced in [ 8 ] which is differentiable and to a certain degree parametrisation invariant quantity. The scoring rule framework that we presented in the previous sections provides divergence not only between TS but probability measures on TS, d ⁡ ( ν , μ ) \\displaystyle d(\\nu,\\mu) ≡ 𝔼 X ∼ ν ​ [ S ⁡ ( X , μ ) ] − H ⁡ ( ν ) , \\displaystyle\\equiv\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\nu}[S(X,\\mu)]-H(\\nu), (99) but as a special case, we can restrict this to point measures to get a “divergence” between two TS akin to sDTW which reduces to the formula ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) ↦ d ⁡ ( δ 𝐱 , δ 𝐲 ) = L ⁡ ( Φ ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) ​ Φ ​ ( 𝐲 ) − 1 ) . \\displaystyle(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})\\mapsto d(\\delta_{\\mathbf{x}},\\delta_{\\mathbf{y}})=L(\\Phi(\\mathbf{x})\\Phi(\\mathbf{y})^{-1}). (100) To study the different behaviour between these two “divergences” – sDTW and ( 100 ) – we generated samples paths from a Brownian motion to get 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} and warped it a time change φ : [ 0 , T ] → [ 0 , T ] , φ ⁡ ( t ) = T ​ ( t / T ) p \\varphi:[0,T]\\to[0,T],\\varphi(t)=T(t/T)^{p} to get 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} where p ∈ [ 1 , ∞ ) p\\in[1,\\infty) is a parameter that determines the severity of the warping. Finally, we sampled on a discrete time grid with resolution 10 − 2 10^{-2} . The results are shown in Figure 1 . The geometric score stays close to 0 0 regardless of the value of p p but the sDTW divergence will increase with p p and the rate of increase depends on the γ \\gamma parameter. As γ \\gamma tends to 0 0 the sDTW score will tend to the geometric score, but it will lose its smoothness while doing so. It is worth noting that γ = 1 \\gamma=1 is considered a default value. To achieve the same level of invariance enjoyed by the the divergence ( 100 ) in a DTW paradigm on can use the classical non-smooth DTW algorithms which cannot be updated using gradient descent. In contrast, the signature divergence ( 100 ) is always differentiable and parametrization invariant. Further, the signature divergence is more general in the sense that it is not just a divergence between TS but between probability measures on TS which allows in principle for many other applications such as variational inference. Figure 1 : Geometric scoring compared to sDTW for different values of γ \\gamma plotted against the logarithm of p p for p p between 1 1 and 25 25 . The implementation of sDTW is taken from the excellent Python package from [ 8 ] . 6.2 Mutual information Recall that the mutual information between two probability measures μ \\mu and ν \\nu is defined as I ⁡ ( μ , ν ) \\displaystyle I(\\mu,\\nu) ≔ H ⁡ ( μ ) − 𝔼 U ∼ ν ​ [ H ⁡ ( μ | U ) ] . \\displaystyle\\coloneqq H(\\mu)-\\mathbb{E}_{U\\sim\\nu}[H(\\mu|U)]. (101) and provides a dependency measure between μ \\mu and ν \\nu . Closest related to independence on paths are the signature cumulants [ 57 ] which have been proven to be useful in applications [ 58 , 59 ] . However, signature cumulants only compare probability measures on paths [tracks] with other probability measures on paths [tracks]. In contrast, the mutual information ( 101 ) allows to measures dependency between a probability measure μ \\mu on paths [tracks] and a probability measure ν \\nu on an arbitrary measurable space; in particular, this allows to measure dependence relations between TS and scalar-valued random variables. Figure 2 : The left hand side shows sample trajectories from 𝐘 \\mathbf{Y} for different correlation values ρ \\rho . The right plot shows the mutual information between 𝐘 \\mathbf{Y} and the rotational speed ω \\omega as a function of ρ \\rho . To demonstrate this, we consider two examples Mutual information between a stochastic process and a scalar. We consider the stochastic process 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} defined as 𝐲 t = ρ ​ t ​ ( cos ⁡ ω ​ t sin ⁡ ω ​ t ) + 1 − ρ 2 ​ 𝐱 t \\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}_{t}=\\rho t{\\cos\\omega t\\choose\\sin\\omega t}+\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}\\mathbf{x}_{t} (102) where ω \\omega is sampled from the uniform distribution on [ 0 , 8 ​ π ) [0,8\\pi) and 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} is a standard 2 2 -dimensional Brownian motion independent from ω \\omega . 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} and 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} are generated on the interval [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] on a grid with resolution 10 − 2 10^{-2} . The right plot in Figure 2 shows some sample trajectories of the process 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} . The left plot in Figure 2 shows the mutual information between (the law of) ω \\omega and 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} for various values of ρ \\rho . As expected, the mutual information increases monotone with ρ \\rho and is bounded by the entropy. Mutual Information between two unparametrized stochastic processes. We consider two independent a Brownian motions 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} and 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} as before, and a random parametrisation ϕ ⁡ ( t ) = T ​ ( t / T ) p \\phi(t)=T(t/T)^{p} where p ∈ [ 1 , 10 ] p\\in[1,10] is uniformly distributed independent of 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} . The stochastic process 𝐳 \\mathbf{z} is defined as 𝐳 t = ρ ​ 𝐱 ϕ ⁡ ( t ) + 1 − ρ 2 ​ 𝐲 t . \\displaystyle\\mathbf{z}_{t}=\\rho\\mathbf{x}_{\\phi(t)}+\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}\\mathbf{y}_{t}. (103) We then compute the mutual information between 𝐳 \\mathbf{z} and 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} as a function of ρ \\rho . The results are shown in Figure 3 . Figure 3 : The left plot shows one-dimensional projections of sample paths of 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} for different warping parameters p p . The right plot shows the mutual information between 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} and 𝐳 \\mathbf{z} as a function of ρ \\rho . Acknowledgements PB is supported by the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council [EP/R513295/1]. HO is supported by the EPSRC grant “DATASIG”, the Turing Institute, and the Oxford-Man Institute of Quantitative Finance. Appendix A Tree-like equivalence of paths Informally, a path x : [ 0 , T ] → ℝ n x:[0,T]\\to\\mathbb{R}^{n} is tree-like if the set it traces out in ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} looks like a tree. The formal definition is Definition A.1 ( [ 60 ] ) . A continuous path x : [ 0 , T ] → E x:[0,T]\\to E is said to be tree-like if there exists an ℝ \\mathbb{R} -tree τ \\tau , a continuous map φ : [ 0 , T ] → τ \\varphi:[0,T]\\to\\tau and a map ψ : τ → E \\psi:\\tau\\to E such that φ ⁡ ( 0 ) = φ ⁡ ( T ) \\varphi(0)=\\varphi(T) and x = ψ ∘ φ x=\\psi\\circ\\varphi . Two paths 𝐱 , 𝐲 \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} are tree-like equivalent if 𝐱 ⋆ 𝐲 − 1 \\mathbf{x}\\star\\mathbf{y}^{-1} is tree-like and we denote this relation with 𝐱 ∼ 𝐲 \\mathbf{x}\\sim\\mathbf{y} . That is, if 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} and 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} follow the same trajectory in ℝ n \\mathbb{R}^{n} up to tree-like excursions then 𝐱 ∼ 𝐲 \\mathbf{x}\\sim\\mathbf{y} . In particular, if 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} is just b ​ x bx under time-reparametrization, 𝐲 ⁡ ( t ) = 𝐱 ⁡ ( τ ⁡ ( t ) ) \\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(\\tau(t)) for an increasing τ \\tau , then 𝐱 ∼ 𝐲 \\mathbf{x}\\sim\\mathbf{y} . See also [ 57 , Appendix B] for more details. It is easy to check that ∼ \\sim is an equivalence relation, hence we can define Tracks ( a , b ) ≔ Paths ( a , b ) / ∼ , and Tracks ≔ Paths / ∼ , \\displaystyle\\operatorname{Tracks}({{a},{b}})\\coloneqq\\operatorname{Paths}({{a},{b}})/{\\sim}\\text{, and }\\operatorname{Tracks}\\coloneqq\\operatorname{Paths}/{\\sim}, (104) References [1] Glenn W Brier et al. Verification of forecasts expressed in terms of probability. Monthly weather review , 78(1):1–3, 1950. [2] Tilmann Gneiting and Adrian E Raftery. Strictly proper scoring rules, prediction, and estimation. Journal of the American Statistical Association , 102(477):359–378, 2007. [3] Philip Dawid. The geometry of proper scoring rules. Annals of the Institute of Statistical Mathematics , 59(1):77–93, 2007. [4] Kuo-Tsai Chen. Integration of paths—a faithful representation of paths by non-commutative formal power serie