{2}~;~\\alpha,x)\\Big]~\\Bigg]\\leq 2(\\alpha+\\beta) Corollary A.4 tells us that the agreement between any two selective ensembles is at least 1 − 2 ​ ( α + β ) 1-2(\\alpha+\\beta) . For a fixed n n , decreasing α \\alpha will lead to a higher abstention rate. Thus in order for α \\alpha and β \\beta to both be small, as would be necessary for a high fraction of consistently-predicted points, we may require a large number of constituent models, n n . Figure 2 illustrates the trade-off between α \\alpha , β \\beta , and n n , depending on the base level of agreement of the constituent models. In Section 5 , we show empirically that even with small values of n n , abstention rates of selective ensembles are reasonably low in practice. Figure 2: The left two plots show abstention rates as a function of the underlying probability of agreement among models over 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} , i.e., the probability that any given model will return the mode prediction, with plots denoting varying numbers of constituent models. The right two graphs demonstrate the relationship between consistency of the ensemble models as given by Corollary A.4 . In summary, selective ensembles accomplish three primary things: (1) they identify points on which the mode prediction cannot be determined, (2) they bound the fraction of points that can be inconsistently predicted, and (3) they provide a means of reliably inferring the mode prediction when the abstention rate can be kept sufficiently low. 5 Evaluation mean accuracy ± \\pm standard deviation Randomness Ger. Credit Adult Seizure Warfarin Tai. Credit FMNIST Colon RS .730 ± .020 .730\\pm.020 .842 ± 1 ​ e − 3 .842\\pm 1e-3 .973 ± 2 ​ e − 3 .973\\pm 2e-3 .686 ± 3 ​ e − 3 .686\\pm 3e-3 .820 ± 1 ​ e − 3 .820\\pm 1e-3 .916 ± 3 ​ e − 3 .916\\pm 3e-3 .927 ± 2 ​ e − 3 .927\\pm 2e-3 LOO .729 ± .012 .729\\pm.012 .843 ± 7 ​ e − 4 .843\\pm 7e-4 .976 ± 2 ​ e − 3 .976\\pm 2e-3 .686 ± 2 ​ e − 3 .686\\pm 2e-3 .820 ± 1 ​ e − 3 .820\\pm 1e-3 .917 ± 8 ​ e − 4 .917\\