ctive. Adversarial training of generative networks is however unstable and difficult. A well-known consequence of unstable adversarial training is mode collapse ( Goodfellow 2016 ; Isola et al. 2017 ; Arora et al. 2017 ; Bellemare et al. 2017 ; Arora et al. 2018 ; Richardson and Weiss 2018 ) , in which the generative distribution underestimates uncertainty and, in extreme cases, can collapse to a single point. Mode collapse has been related to the approximations involved in adversarial training: Arora et al. 2017 showed that mode collapse can arise due to finite capacity of the critic c ψ c_{\\psi} , while Bellemare et al. 2017 and Bińkowski et al. 2018 respectively linked it to using finite data and a finite number of steps in optimizing the c ψ c_{\\psi} network and subsequently using it to obtain gradient estimates for ϕ \\phi , which are thus biased. To avoid adversarial training altogether and bypass the above issues, Moment Matching Networks ( Li et al. 2015 ; Dziugaite et al. 2015 ) are trained by considering D D to be the squared Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) induced by a positive definite kernel k k D k ( P ⋆ | | P ϕ ) := 𝔼 [ k ( 𝐗 , 𝐗 ′ ) − 2 k ( 𝐗 , 𝐘 ) + k ( 𝐘 , 𝐘 ′ ) ] , 𝐗 , 𝐗 ′ ∼ P ϕ , 𝐘 , 𝐘 ′ ∼ P ⋆ D_{k}\\left({P}^{\\star}||{P}^{\\phi}\\right):=\\mathbb{E}\\left[k\\left(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X}^{\\prime}\\right)-2k(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{Y})+k\\left(\\mathbf{Y},\\mathbf{Y}^{\\prime}\\right)\\right],\\quad\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X}^{\\prime}\\sim P^{\\phi},\\quad\\mathbf{Y},\\mathbf{Y}^{\\prime}\\sim P^{\\star} (4) From Eq. ( 4 ), we can obtain an empirical unbiased estimate of D k D_{k} and its gradients without introducing a critic network. However, using a fixed kernel on raw data can yield small discriminative power (as in the case of images, where numerical values have little meaning), leading to a poor fit of P ϕ P^{\\phi} to P ⋆ P^{\\star} . Hence, Li et al. 2017 suggested applying a learnable transformation before computing the kernel, with paramete