coring rules A Scoring Rule (SR) S S is a function of a distribution and an observation; see Gneiting and Raftery 2007 ; Dawid and Musio 2014 for an overview of their properties and usage. Generally, S ⁡ ( P ϕ , 𝐲 ) S(P^{\\phi},\\mathbf{y}) represents a penalty assigned to the distribution P ϕ P^{\\phi} when 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} is observed. If 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} is the realization of a random variable 𝐘 ∼ P ⋆ \\mathbf{Y}\\sim P^{\\star} , the expected SR is S ⁡ ( P ϕ , P ⋆ ) := 𝔼 𝐘 ∼ P ⋆ ​ S ​ ( P ϕ , 𝐘 ) . S(P^{\\phi},P^{\\star}):=\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbf{Y}\\sim P^{\\star}}S(P^{\\phi},\\mathbf{Y}). S S is said to be proper relative to a set of distributions 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} if the expected Scoring Rule is minimized in P ϕ P^{\\phi} when P ϕ = P ⋆ P^{\\phi}=P^{\\star} S ⁡ ( P ⋆ , P ⋆ ) ≤ S ⁡ ( P ϕ , P ⋆ ) ​ ∀ P ϕ , P ⋆ ∈ 𝒫 . S(P^{\\star},P^{\\star})\\leq S(P^{\\phi},P^{\\star})\\ \\forall\\ P^{\\phi},P^{\\star}\\in\\mathcal{P}. Moreover, S S is strictly proper relative to 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} if P ϕ = P ⋆ P^{\\phi}=P^{\\star} is the unique minimum. In practice, assuming that ∃ ϕ ⋆ : P ϕ ⋆ = P ⋆ \\exists\\ \\phi^{\\star}:P^{\\phi^{\\star}}=P^{\\star} , P ϕ ≠ P ⋆ P^{\\phi}\\neq P^{\\star} can still minimize an expected proper SR S ⁡ ( P ϕ , P ⋆ ) S(P^{\\phi},P^{\\star}) , which in turn implies there may be multiple minima (still, the different minima can be thought of as more “similar” to P ⋆ P^{\\star} than other distributions, in some way); instead, if S S is strictly proper, P ⋆ P^{\\star} and P ϕ P^{\\phi} coincide if and only if P ϕ P^{\\phi} is the (unique) minimum of the expected SR. In case where ∄ ϕ : P ϕ = P ⋆ \\nexists\\ \\phi:P^{\\phi}=P^{\\star} , then the expected proper SR decreases as P ϕ P^{\\phi} becomes more similar to the data distribution P ⋆ P^{\\star} ; however, nothing can be said on the number of minima without more information on 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} , even if S S is strictly proper. For a strictly proper SR S S , the quantity D ( P ⋆ | | P ϕ ) := S ( P ϕ , P ⋆ ) − S (