f{y}\\|_{2}^{\\beta}-\\mathbb{E}\\|\\mathbf{X}-\\mathbf{X}^{\\prime}\\|_{2}^{\\beta}. (79) The probabilistic forecasting literature ( Gneiting and Raftery 2007 ) use a different convention of the energy score and the subsequent kernel score, which amounts to multiplying our definitions by 1 / 2 1/2 . We follow here the convention used in the statistical inference literature ( Rizzo and Székely 2016 ; Chérief-Abdellatif and Alquier 2020 ; Nguyen et al. 2020 ) The Energy Score is strictly proper for the class of probability measures P ϕ P^{\\phi} such that 𝔼 𝐗 ∼ P ϕ ​ ‖ 𝐗 ‖ β &lt; ∞ \\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbf{X}\\sim P^{\\phi}}\\|\\mathbf{X}\\|^{\\beta}&lt;\\infty ( Gneiting and Raftery 2007 ) . The Energy Score is related to the Energy distance (Eq. ( 77 )), which is a metric between probability distributions ( Rizzo and Székely 2016 ) . We will fix β = 1 \\beta=1 in the rest of this work. Additionally, for a univariate distribution and β = 1 \\beta=1 , the Energy Score recovers the Continuous Ranked Probability Score (CRPS), widely used in meteorology (e.g, see Hersbach 2000 ). B.2.2 Kernel Score For a positive definite kernel k ⁡ ( ⋅ , ⋅ ) k(\\cdot,\\cdot) , the kernel Scoring Rule can be defined as ( Gneiting and Raftery 2007 ) S k ​ ( P ϕ , 𝐲 ) = 𝔼 ⁡ [ k ⁡ ( 𝐗 , 𝐗 ′ ) ] − 2 ⋅ 𝔼 ⁡ [ k ⁡ ( 𝐗 , 𝐲 ) ] . S_{k}(P^{\\phi},\\mathbf{y})=\\mathbb{E}[k(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X}^{\\prime})]-2\\cdot\\mathbb{E}[k(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{y})]. (80) The Kernel Score is connected to the squared Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD, Gretton et al. 2012 ) relative to the kernel k k , see Eq. ( 4 ) in the main text. S k S_{k} is proper for the class of probability distributions for which 𝔼 ⁡ [ k ⁡ ( 𝐗 , 𝐗 ′ ) ] \\mathbb{E}[k(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X}^{\\prime})] is finite (by Theorem 4 in Gneiting and Raftery 2007 ). Additionally, it is strictly proper under conditions on k k ensuring that the MMD is a metric for probability distributions on 𝒴 \\mathcal{Y} Gretton et al. 2012 . These conditions are satisfied, among others, by the Gaussian kernel (which we will use in this work) k ⁡ ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) = exp ⁡ ( − ‖ 𝐱 − 𝐲 ‖ 2 2 2 ​ γ 2 ) , k(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})=\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\|\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y}\\|_{2}^{2}}{2\\gamma^{2}}\\right), (81) in which γ \\gamma is a scalar bandwidth. B.2.3 Patched Score For the Patched Score, we consider different overlapping patches of the input data; denote as 𝒫 \\mathcal{P} the set of patches and as p ∈ 𝒫 p\\in\\mathcal{P} an individual patch; the patches are of a given size and spaced by a given spacing. Then, we compute a SR S S for multivariate distributions on each patch separately, and then add the results S p ​ ( P ϕ , 𝐲 ) = ∑ p ∈ 𝒫 S ⁡ ( P ϕ | p , 𝐲 | p ) , S_{p}(P^{\\phi},\\mathbf{y})=\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal{P}}S(P^{\\phi}|_{p},\\mathbf{y}|_{p}), (82) where 𝐲 | p \\mathbf{y}|_{p} denotes the components of 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} in the patch p p and P ϕ | p P^{\\phi}|_{p} denotes the marginal distribution induced by P ϕ P^{\\phi} for components in the patch p p . See Figure 4 for a representation. As mentioned in the main body (Sec. 3.2 in the main text), the resulting SR is not strictly proper, as far away correlations are discarded. Notice how the topology of data for our global weather data set is periodic along the longitudinal direction (i.e., horizontally in Figure 4 ). The patches we define follow this. Figure 4 : Patched SR: a SR for multivariate data is computed on localized patches, and the resulting values are summed. Appendix C Stochastic Gradient Descent for generative-SR networks We discuss here how we can get unbiased gradient estimates for the prequential SR in Eq. ( 10 ) in the main text with respect to the parameters of the generative network ϕ \\phi . In order to do that, we first discuss how to obtain unbiased estimates of the SRs we use across this work. Then, we show how those allow to obtain unbiased gradient estimates. C.1 Unbiased scoring rule estimates Consider we have draws 𝐱 j ∼ P , j = 1 , … , m \\mathb