{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2201.03387.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 52785, "equations": [], "text": "[2201.03387] Adaptive Strategies in Kelly’s Horse Races Model May 2022 Adaptive Strategies in Kelly’s Horse Races Model Armand Despons Luca Peliti Affiliation: SMRI, Santa Marinella (RM), 00058 Italy David Lacoste Email: armand.despons@espci.fr Abstract We formulate an adaptive version of Kelly’s horse model in which the gambler learns from past race results using Bayesian inference. We characterize the cost of this gambling strategy and we analyze the asymptotic scaling of the difference between the growth rate of the gambler and the optimal growth rate, known as the gambler’s regret. We also explain how this adaptive strategy relates to the universal portfolio strategy, and we build improved adaptive strategies in which the gambler exploits the information contained in the bookmaker odds distribution. Contents 1 Introduction 2 Definition of Kelly’s model 3 Adaptive strategy 3.1 Optimality of the strategy 3.2 The cost of learning 3.3 Extension to correlated races 4 Laplace’s rule of succession with prior information 4.1 The modified Laplace’s rule with prior information 4.2 Limitations of the modified Laplace’s strategy 5 Conclusion References 1 Introduction In 1956, R. Kelly [ 1 ] extended the work of C. Shannon on communication to gambling models. This now classic piece of information theory [ 2 ] turned out to have fundamental implications for investment strategies in finance and beyond. In the context of biology, Kelly’s paper lead to a clarification of the notion of value of information [ 3 ] . This value of information quantifies the quality of the strategies adopted by individuals in a population when faced with unpredictably varying environments [ 4 ] . In these conditions, it is sometimes advantageous for individuals to accept a reduction of their short-term reproductive success, in exchange for longer-term risk reduction, a strategy known as bet-hedging [ 5 ] . Such a strategy is employed for instance by cells to cope with antibiotics or by plants to cope with a fluctuating climate [ 6 ] . Kelly’s strategy is known to be risky, and for this reason most gamblers use fractional Kelly’s strategies, with reduced risk and growth rate [ 7 ] . This observation hints at a trade-off between the risk the gambler is ready to take and the average long-term growth rate of his capital. In a recent work, we have studied the properties of this trade-off inspired by ideas in Stochastic Thermodynamics [ 8 ] . We then studied another manifestation of this trade-off this time in the context of a biological population with phenotypic switching in a fluctuating environment [ 9 ] . In that work, the phenotypic switching rate of individuals in the population is not affected by the state of the environment, which means that no sensing mechanism is present. Naturally, real biological systems are able to sense their environment, extract relevant information from noisy measurements, and adjust their phenotypic response accordingly [ 10 ] . An open question in this field is thus the precise quantification of the cost that biological systems must pay for this sensing. This question motivates the present paper. To evaluate this cost, we go back to Kelly’s model, and we modify it to add some adaptation mechanisms based on Bayesian inference [ 11 ] . The adaptive strategy we build in that way has similarities with the one used in universal portfolios [ 12 ] and is therefore optimal among all the strategies of this type. We quantify the cost of our adaptive strategy, which in the context of Kelly’s horse races takes the form of a capital loss of the gambler with respect to his/her optimal capital. We derive an asymptotic form for this cost both for uncorrelated and correlated races. We then investigate improved strategies in which the gambler leverages the information contained in the odd distribution chosen by the bookmaker. 2 Definition of Kelly’s model Let us recall the basic elements of Kelly’s horse race model [ 1 ] . A race involves M M horses, and is described by a normalized vector of winning probabilities 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} , an inverse-odds vector 𝐫 \\mathbf{r} and a gambler strategy 𝐛 \\mathbf{b} . The latter corresponds to a specific allocation of the gambler’s capital on the M M horses: if we denote by C t C_{t} the gambler’s capital at time t t , the amount of capital invested on horse x x reads b x ​ C t \\mathrm{b}_{x}C_{t} . We further assume that, after each race, the gambler invests his whole capital, i.e., ∑ x = 1 M b x = 1 \\sum_{x=1}^{M}\\mathrm{b}_{x}=1 , always betting a non-zero amount on all horses, i.e., ∀ x ∈ [ 1 , M ] : b x ≠ 0 \\forall x\\in[1,M]:\\ \\mathrm{b}_{x}\\neq 0 . The inverse-odds vector 𝐫 \\mathbf{r} is set by the bookmaker and is not necessarily normalized, in fact the value of ∑ x r x \\sum_{x}\\mathrm{r}_{x} controls whether or not the bookmaker is extracting/injecting additional capital: if the sum of the components of the inverse-odds is strictly bigger (resp. strictly lower) than 1 the bookmaker extracts (resp. injects) additional capital. In the following, we focus on the neutral case where the bookmaker neither extracts nor injects capital, which means that the inverse-odds vector is normalized: ∑ x r x = 1 \\sum_{x}\\mathrm{r}_{x}=1 . This case is usually referred to as gambling without track-take in the horse-racing literature [ 1 , 2 ] . Thus, the evolution of the gambler’s capital after one race reads: C t + 1 = b x r x ​ C t , with a probability ​ p x , C_{t+1}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{b}_{x}}{\\mathrm{r}_{x}}C_{t},\\quad\\text{with a probability }\\mathrm{p}_{x}, (1) which implies that the log of the capital, log ⁡ - ​ cap ​ ( t ) ≡ log ⁡ C t \\log\\!\\text{-}\\mathrm{cap}(t)\\equiv\\log C_{t} , evolves additively: log ⁡ - ​ cap ​ ( t ) = ∑ τ = 1 t log ⁡ ( b x τ r x τ ) , \\log\\!\\text{-}\\mathrm{cap}(t)=\\sum_{\\tau=1}^{t}\\log\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{b}_{x_{\\tau}}}{\\mathrm{r}_{x_{\\tau}}}\\right), (2) where x τ x_{\\tau} denotes the index of the winner of the τ \\tau -th race and we assumed log ⁡ - ​ cap ​ ( 0 ) = 0 \\log\\!\\text{-}\\mathrm{cap}(0)=0 (i.e. C 0 = 1 C_{0}=1 ). Since the races are assumed to be independent, the terms log ⁡ ( b x τ / r x τ ) \\log(\\mathrm{b}_{x_{\\tau}}/\\mathrm{r}_{x_{\\tau}}) in ( 2 ) are independent and identically distributed, and we can use the weak law of the large numbers : log ⁡ - ​ cap ​ ( t ) t → t → ∞ 𝔼 ⁡ [ log ⁡ ( b x r x ) ] \\displaystyle\\dfrac{\\log\\!\\text{-}\\mathrm{cap}(t)}{t}\\xrightarrow[t\\to\\infty]{}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\log\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{b}_{x}}{\\mathrm{r}_{x}}\\right)\\right] (3) in probability. Then 𝔼 [ log ( b x r x ) ] ≡ ∑ x p x log ( b x r x ) = D K ​ L ( 𝕡 | | 𝕣 ) − D K ​ L ( 𝕡 | | 𝕓 ) , \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\log\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{b}_{x}}{\\mathrm{r}_{x}}\\right)\\right]\\equiv\\sum_{x}\\mathrm{p}_{x}\\log\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{b}_{x}}{\\mathrm{r}_{x}}\\right)=D_{KL}\\left(\\mathbb{p}||\\mathbb{r}\\right)-D_{KL}\\left(\\mathbb{p}||\\mathbb{b}\\right), (4) where D KL D_{\\text{KL}} stands for the Kullback-Leibler divergence [ 2 , sec. 2.3] . From an information theoretic point of view, ( 3 ) and ( 4 ) imply that the capital of the gambler increases in the long term only if the gambler has a better knowledge of 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} than the bookmaker, otherwise it decreases. It also follows from this analysis that the optimal strategy 𝐛 KELLY = 𝐩 \\mathbf{b}^{\\text{\\tiny{KELLY}}}=\\mathbf{p} , called Kelly’s strategy [ 1 ] , overtakes any other strategies in the long-term. Its optimum growth rate is the positive quantity D KL ( 𝐩 ∥ 𝐫 ) D_{\\text{KL}}\\left(\\mathbf{p}\\|\\mathbf{r}\\right) . Note that thanks to the assumption that the inverse-odds vector is normalized, another strategy is possible, namely 𝐛 NULL = 𝐫 \\mathbf{b}^{\\text{\\tiny NULL}}=\\mathbf{r} . We have called this strategy the null strategy [ 8 ] , because it yields a constant capital as can be seen from Eqs. ( 3 )-( 4 ). Indeed, in this null strategy, the gambler has exactly the same knowledge as the bookmaker, hence he/her neither looses nor gains capital. 3 Adaptive strategy Kelly’s strategy assumes that the probability vector p ¯ \\underline{p} is known, but this, in practice, is never the case. Hence, it is of great interest to build strategies to learn 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} , in order to play the optimal betting strategy. In information theory, these strategies are called universal precisely because they do not require any knowledge of 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} . From a theoretical point of view, universal strategies are related to lossless source coding [ 15 ] . While general theories of history-dependent gambling are available [ 16 ] , here we want to construct an adaptive strategy specifically for Kelly’s problem. We use an estimator based on Laplace’s rule of succession [ 17 ] : b x LAPL , t + 1 ≡ n x t + 1 t + M , \\mathrm{b}_{x}^{\\text{\\tiny{LAPL}},~t+1}\\equiv\\dfrac{n_{x}^{t}+1}{t+M}, (5) where the vector 𝐧 t \\mathbf{n}^{t} contains the history of all race results up to time t t : n ¯ t ≡ [ n 1 t , … , n M t ] T \\underline{n}^{t}\\equiv\\left[n_{1}^{t},\\ldots,n_{M}^{t}\\right]^{\\mathrm{T}} . In this notation, n x t n_{x}^{t} represents the number of times the horse x x has won among the t t previous races, and obviously ∑ x = 1 M n x t = t \\sum_{x=1}^{M}n_{x}^{t}=t . (a) (b) Figure 1 : (a) Time evolution of the log-capital of Kelly’s strategy (red solid curve), of Laplace’s strategy ( 5 ) (orange solid curve) and of the modified Laplace’s strategy using the odds distribution as prior ( 25 ) (blue solid curve). Both adaptive strategies undergo a learning phase in which the log-capital decreases during a time t ⋆ t^{\\star} , and in the long run they perform as well as Kelly’s strategy. Note that the modified Laplace’s strategy reduces the initial loss of capital associated to the learning phase. The average log-capital associated with Laplace’s strategy ( 13 ) is the black dotted curve. In this figure, the log-capital is averaged over 3000 realizations with M = 10 M=10 and ϵ = 0.1 \\epsilon=0.1 . (b) Linear dependence of the rescaled t ⋆ t^{\\star} as function of M M , the number of horses. The color dots represent numerical estimates with error bars. The black crosses and the dashed curve represent the analytical prediction of ( 14 ). For the each colored dot, we have chosen 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} and 𝐫 \\mathbf{r} randomly for a fixed M M and ϵ = 0.1 \\epsilon=0.1 and we have averaged over 50 realizations of t ⋆ t^{\\star} , itself computed from the average over 30 realizations of the log-capital. This result ( 5 ) follows from a standard application of Bayesian inference : one starts from the likelihood of 𝐧 t \\mathbf{n}^{t} , which is the multinominal distribution: ℙ [ 𝐧 t | b ¯ ] = t ! n 1 t ! ⋯ n M t ! b 1 n 1 t ⋯ b M n M t . \\mathbb{P}\\left[\\left.\\mathbf{n}^{t}\\right|\\underline{b}\\right]=\\dfrac{t!}{n_{1}^{t}!\\cdots n_{M}^{t}!}\\mathrm{b}_{1}^{n_{1}^{t}}\\cdots\\mathrm{b}_{M}^{n_{M}^{t}}. (6) The posterior distribution is then given by Bayes’ rule : ℙ [ b ¯ | 𝐧 t ] = ℙ [ 𝐧 t | b ¯ ] ℙ [ b ¯ ] ℙ ⁡ [ 𝐧 t ] \\mathbb{P}\\left[\\underline{b}\\left|\\mathbf{n}^{t}\\right.\\right]=\\dfrac{\\mathbb{P}\\left[\\left.\\mathbf{n}^{t}\\right|\\underline{b}\\right]\\mathbb{P}\\left[\\underline{b}\\right]}{\\mathbb{P}\\left[\\mathbf{n}^{t}\\right]} . Assuming a uniform prior ℙ ⁡ [ 𝐛 ] ∝ δ ⁡ ( ∑ x b x − 1 ) \\mathbb{P}\\left[\\mathbf{b}\\right]\\propto\\delta\\left(\\sum_{x}\\mathrm{b}_{x}-1\\right) , the posterior distribution reads: ℙ [ 𝐛 | 𝐧 t ] = ( t + M − 1 ) ! n 1 t ! ⋯ n M t ! b 1 n 1 t ⋯ b M n M t δ ( ∑ x b x − 1 ) . \\mathbb{P}\\left[\\mathbf{b}\\left|\\mathbf{n}^{t}\\right.\\right]=\\dfrac{(t+M-1)!}{n_{1}^{t}!\\cdots n_{M}^{t}!}~\\mathrm{b}_{1}^{n_{1}^{t}}\\cdots\\mathrm{b}_{M}^{n_{M}^{t}}~\\delta\\left(\\sum_{x}\\mathrm{b}_{x}-1\\right). (7) Integrating ( 7 ) with respect to all