\\tiny{LAPL}},~t}(1-\\mathrm{b}_{x}^{\\text{\\tiny{LAPL}},~t})}{t+M+1}}. (10) Here we assumed that the distribution 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} is constant in time. Laplace’s strategy would still perform well if 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} was a slowly varying function of times, i.e. if that distribution was varying on a time scale much longer than that of 𝐛 LAPL \\mathbf{b}^{\\text{\\tiny LAPL}} , which by ( 9 ) is given by ( M − 1 ) / 2 (M-1)/2 . 3.1 Optimality of the strategy As mentioned in the introduction, Laplace’s rule qualifies as universal because no foreknowledge of the distribution of the races p ¯ \\underline{p} is needed, instead the distribution is learned over time. In the literature on portfolios, a strategy of this type is known under the name of universal portfolio . This strategy is important in practice because it performs asymptotically as well as the best constant balanced portfolios which would have a foreknowledge of the stock prices [ 12 ] . Here, we explain precisely in what sense these two strategies are related. In the portfolio case, 𝐛 \\mathbf{b} denotes the proportion of wealth invested on a certain day in an ensemble of stocks. The total capital is multiplied after each day by the product of 𝐛 \\mathbf{b} with a matrix which describes the increase in the price of all the stocks from one day to the next. Thus, in the portfolio case, the gain of the capital is distributed on all the selected stocks, in contrast to Kelly’s model in which a single horse is selected at each race. To connect this strategy to Kelly’s horse race, let us consider this portfolio in the case where only a single stock contributes each day. Let us then denote the index of that stock up to time a t t in a vector 𝐱 t ≡ [ x 1 , ⋯ , x t ] T \\mathbf{x}^{t}\\equiv\\left[x_{1},\\cdots,x_{t}\\right]^{\\text{T}} , and let C [ 𝐛 , t | 𝐱 t ] C\\left[\\mathbf{b},t|\\mathbf{x}^{t}\\right] be the total capital a portfolio 𝐛 \\mathbf{b} would have gained given the history 𝐱 t \\mathbf{x}^{t} up to time t t . According to Ref. [ 12 ] , the bet component b x \\mathrm{b}_{x} in the next race is given by b x PORTF , t + 1 ≡ ∫ ℝ + M d 𝐛 δ ( ∑ x ~ b x ~ − 1 ) b x C [ 𝐛 , t | 𝐱 t ] ∫ ℝ + M d 𝐛 δ ( ∑ x ~ b x ~ − 1 ) C [ 𝐛 , t | 𝐱 t ] . \\mathrm{b}_{x}^{\\text{\\tiny PORTF},~t+1}\\equiv\\dfrac{\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^{M}_{+}}\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{b}~\\delta\\left(\\sum_{\\tilde{x}}\\mathrm{b}_{\\tilde{x}}-1\\right)~\\mathrm{b}_{x}~C\\left[\\mathbf{b},t|\\mathbf{x}^{t}\\right]}{\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^{M}_{+}}\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{b}~\\delta\\left(\\sum_{\\tilde{x}}\\mathrm{b}_{\\tilde{x}}-1\\right)~C\\left[\\mathbf{b},t|\\mathbf{x}^{t}\\right]}. (11) In the case of Kelly’s model, where C [ 𝐛 , t | 𝐱 t ] = ∏ i = 1 t b x i o x i C\\left[\\mathbf{b},t|\\mathbf{x}^{t}\\right]=\\prod_{i=1}^{t}\\mathrm{b}_{x_{i}}\\mathrm{o}_{x_{i}} , the integrals above can be performed explicitly and one recovers exactly ( 5 ). Therefore, the adaptive strategy based on Laplace’s rule of succession outlined above and the universal portfolio strategy are equivalent when considering a single stock. Since the universal portfolio is known to outperform any other strategy asymptotically, it follows that the adaptive strategy based on Laplace’s rule is also optimal among all strategies given the assumptions of the model. Naturally, the universal portfolio has a wider applicability and is more suited to the stock market, because it accounts for distributed gains among many stocks. In this case of distributed gains, interesting and far reaching connections between Kelly’s horse race and optimality in game theory have been studied [ 18 ] . 3.2 The cost of learning A typical evolution of the log-capital in the adaptive strategy is shown in Figure 1(a) (orange curve), together with the optimal strategy based on Kelly’s criterion (red solid curve). The figure shows that the gambler loses capital at short times because of his/her poor knowledge of the distribution p ¯ \\underline{p} , but he/she catches up on long times once the distribution has been learned. The duration of the burn-in phase is an interesting quantity, which we call t ∗ t^{*} and which is shown in Figure 1(a) . This time is a proxy for the actual loss of capital during the burn-in phase, and for this reason we may regard it as the cost of learning. Clearly, this time depends on the knowledge of the bookmaker : if the bookmaker has a perfect knowledge of the races, he can set the odds according to ( r ¯ = p ¯ \\underline{r}=\\underline{p} ), in which case the burn-in time is very large and the log-capital will only decrease. On the contrary, if the bookmaker has a poor knowledge of the system, the inverse odds are far from p ¯ \\underline{p} , and in that case, the burn-in time tends to be very short. In practice, we introduce a parameter ϵ \\epsilon and a random vector 𝜼 \\boldsymbol{\\eta} uniformly distributed in the simplex with 𝐫 = 𝐩 + ϵ ⁡ ( 𝜼 − 𝐩 ) \\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+\\epsilon~(\\boldsymbol{\\eta}-\\mathbf{p}) , such that for ϵ = 0 \\epsilon=0 one has 𝐫 = 𝐩 \\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p} and for ϵ = 1 \\epsilon=1 , 𝐫 \\mathbf{r} is independent of 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} . To quantify the effect more precisely, it is convenient to define t ⋆ t^{\\star} as the minimum of the log-capital in the adaptive strategy, and to focus on the difference of log-capital between Kelly’s strategy and Laplace’s strategy: Δ ⁡ ( t ) ≡ log ⁡ - ​ cap KELLY ​ ( t ) − log ⁡ - ​ cap ​ ( t ) \\Delta(t)\\equiv\\log\\!\\text{-}\\mathrm{cap}^{\\text{KELLY}}(t)-\\log\\!\\text{-}\\mathrm{cap}(t) . The quantity Δ ⁡ ( t ) \\Delta(t) has been considered before in the multi-armed bandit problem [ 19 ] or in the portfolio theory [ 12 , 20 ] , it is usually called the regret associated with the adaptive strategy. The important point here is that the regret is odd-independent: Δ ( t ) = ∑ i = 1 t [ log p x i − log b x i LAPL , i ] \\Delta(t)=\\left.\\left.\\sum_{i=1}^{t}\\right[\\log\\mathrm{p}_{x_{i}}-\\log\\mathrm{b}_{x_{i}}^{\\text{LAPL},~i}\\right] . Averaging the regret associated with Laplace’s strategy on the race realizations yields (see Appendix A for details on the derivation): ⟨ Δ ⁡ ( t ) ⟩ = ⟨ Δ ⁡ ( t 0 ) ⟩ + M − 1 2 ​ log ⁡ t t 0 + 1 , \\left\\langle\\Delta(t)\\right\\rangle=\\left\\langle\\Delta(t_{0})\\right\\rangle+\\dfrac{M-1}{2}\\log\\dfrac{t}{t_{0}+1}, (12) for t 0 &gt; max 𝑥 ​ p x − 1 t_{0}&gt;\\underset{x}{\\max}~\\mathrm{p}_{x}^{-1} . From ( 12 ) one sees that ⟨ Δ ⁡ ( t ) ⟩ ∼ log ⁡ t \\left\\langle\\Delta(t)\\right\\rangle\\sim\\log t , a feature already observed for the optimal adaptive strategy in the multi-armed bandit problem [ 19 ] . Since we know the long-term behavior of Kelly’s strategy from ( 3 ), we deduce an expression for the average log-capital of our adaptive strategy: ⟨ log - cap ( t ) ⟩ = D KL ( p ¯ ∥ r ¯ ) t − M − 1 2 log t t 0 + 1 − ⟨ Δ ( t 0 ) ⟩ . \\left\\langle\\log\\!\\text{-}\\mathrm{cap}(t)\\right\\rangle=D_{\\text{KL}}\\left(\\underline{p}\\|\\underline{r}\\right)t-\\dfrac{M-1}{2}\\log\\dfrac{t}{t_{0}+1}-\\left\\langle\\Delta(t_{0})\\right\\rangle. (13) This theoretical prediction is shown in Figure 1(a) (black dotted curve) for a time t 0 t_{0} respecting the condition for ( 12 ). By taking the derivative of ( 13 ) with respect to time, one obtains the following estimate for t ⋆ t^{\\star} : t ⋆ = M − 1 2 ​ 1 D KL ( p ¯ ∥ r ¯ ) . t^{\\star}=\\dfrac{M-1}{2}\\dfrac{1}{D_{\\text{KL}}\\left(\\underline{p}\\|\\underline{r}\\right)}. (14) As shown in Figure 1(b) , this time t ⋆ t^{\\star} indeed increases linearly with M M and is inversely proportional to the KL-divergence between p ¯ \\underline{p} and r ¯ \\underline{r} , which quantifies the knowledge of the races by the bookmaker. Since ( 14 ) is based on an asymptotic result, namely ( 13 ), this expression over-estimates the real t ⋆ t^{\\star} but becomes accurate in the limit when t ⋆ t^{\\star} is larger than the time t 0 t_{0} . At this point, it is important to appreciate that t ⋆ t^{\\star} in the above formula still depends on 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} , which is the very quantity we are trying to learn in the adaptive strategy, therefore ( 14 ) could appear useless. In fact, this is not so, because the gambler can still use 𝐛 LAPL , t \\mathbf{b}^{\\text{LAPL},~t} in ( 14 ) to obtain an estimate for the time t ⋆ t^{\\star} . In practice, if the gambler is allowed not to play initially, he/she should wait this time t ⋆ t^{\\star} before starting to bet. 3.3 Extension to correlated races It is straightforward to extend the above strategy to the case of correlated races, provided they are Markovian, in other words, when the results of each race only depends on the result of the previous race. In such an extension, the quantity to be learned is no longer a vector of M M components, but an M × M M\\times M matrix { p x , y } \\left\\{\\mathrm{p}_{x,y}\\right\\} whose columns 𝐩 ⊙ | y \\mathbf{p}_{\\odot|y} are the conditional probability of winning given that horse y y has won in the previous race. Similarly, the odds and the bets are also matrices of size M × M M\\times M . Let us assume that the associated Markov chain is irreducible, and thus admits a unique stationary distribution p ¯ s \\underline{p}^{\\text{s}} . The equation governing the evolution of the log-capital is log ⁡ - ​ cap ​ ( t ) ​ ∼ t → ∞ ​ ( ∑ x , y = 1 M p x , y ​ log ⁡ b x | y r x | y ) ​ t , \\log\\!\\text{-}\\mathrm{cap}(t)\\underset{t\\to\\infty}{\\sim}\\left(\\sum_{x,y=1}^{M}\\mathrm{p}_{x,y}\\log\\dfrac{\\mathrm{b}_{x|y}}{\\mathrm{r}_{x|y}}\\right)t, (15) where p x , y ≡ p x | y ⋅ p y s \\mathrm{p}_{x,y}\\equiv\\mathrm{p}_{x|y}\\cdot\\mathrm{p}^{\\text{s}}_{y} and where r x | y \\mathrm{r}_{x|y} are the inverse odds conditioned on the last race’s result. Kelly’s strategy is again the one that maximizes the long-term growth rate, given the normalization of the conditional bets b x , y KELLY = p x , y \\mathrm{b}_{x,y}^{\\text{KELLY}}=\\mathrm{p}_{x,y} [ 2 ] . We now adapt Laplace rule’s of succession to take into account correlations: b x | y LAPL , t + 1 = n x | y t + 1 τ y t + M , \\mathrm{b}_{x|y}^{\\text{LAPL},~t+1}=\\dfrac{n_{x|y}^{t}+1}{\\tau_{y}^{t}+M}, (16) where n x | y t n_{x|y}^{t} and τ y t \\tau_{y}^{t} are respectively the number of times horse x x wins immediately following a victory of horse y y , and the number of times horse y y has won irrespective of the previous events. Qualitatively the behavior for correlated or uncorrelated races are similar, except that the data to be learned in the correlated case lies in a higher dimensional space, and as a result, the learning phase lasts longer and the losses in terms of log-capital are heavier. Indeed, the regret now scales as M 2 M^{2} while still being logarithmic in time (see Appendix C): ⟨ Δ ⁡ ( t ) ⟩ = ⟨ Δ ⁡ ( t 0 ) ⟩ + M ⁡ ( M − 1 ) 2 ​ log ⁡ t t 0 + 1 . \\left\\langle\\Delta(t)\\right\\rangle=\\left\\langle\\Delta(t_{0})\\right\\rangle+\\dfrac{M(M-1)}{2}\\log\\dfrac{t}{t_{0}+1}. (17) In the end, one obtains the following estimate of the learning time : t ⋆ = M ⁡ ( M − 1 ) 2 ​ ⟨ log ⁡ p x | y r x | y ⟩ . t^{\\star}=\\dfrac{M(M-1)}{2\\left\\langle\\log\\dfrac{\\mathrm{p}_{x|y}}{\\mathrm{r}_{x|y}}\\right\\rangle}. (18) In fact one could have predicted the behavior in M ⁡ ( M − 1 ) M(M-1) because in the learning of the M × M M\\times M matrix, the last row is determined by the constraint on the normalization. The extension to Markov races of higher order n n is straightforward. Now, the relevant distribution is p x | 𝐘 \\mathrm{p}_{x|\\mathbf{Y}} where 𝐘 \\mathbf{Y} store the n n previous winners. Thus, the matrix of the conditional probability becomes a M × M n M\\times M^{n} matrix. As before, the last row is determined by the normalization of each conditional probability, hence the effective number of degrees of freedom is M n ​ ( M − 1 ) M^{n}(M-1) . Therefore, one obtains: ⟨ Δ ⁡ ( t ) ⟩ = ⟨ Δ ⁡ ( t 0 ) ⟩ + M n ​ ( M − 1 ) 2 ​ log ⁡ t t 0 + 1 , \\left\\langle\\Delta(t)\\right\\rangle=\\left\\langle\\Delta(t_{0})\\right\\rangle+\\dfrac{M^{n}(M-1)}{2}\\log\\dfrac{t}{t_{0}+1}, (19) and: t ⋆ = M n ​ ( M − 1 ) 2 ​ ⟨ log ⁡ p x | 𝐘 r x | 𝐘 ⟩ . t^{\\star}=\\dfrac{M^{n}(M-1)}{2\\left\\langle\\log\\dfrac{\\mathrm{p}_{x|\\mathbf{Y}}}{\\mathrm{r}_{x|\\mathbf{Y}}}\\right\\rangle}. (20) 4 Laplace’s rule of succession with prior information 4.1 The modified Laplace’s rule with prior information In order to decrease the duration of the learning phase which is responsible for the initial loss in the log capital, a possibility is to use a non-uniform prior in the derivation of Laplace rule. Before starting to bet, the gambler could reasonably assume that the odds chosen by the bookmaker are probably not random, but contain some useful information which can be exploited in a non-uniform prior. To do so, we use a conjugate prior which, in the case of Kelly’s horse races, is the multinomial distribution: ℙ [ 𝐛 ] = τ ! a 1 τ ! ⋯ a M τ ! b 1 a 1 τ ⋯ b M a M τ δ ( ∑ x b x − 1 ) , \\mathbb{P}\\left[\\mathbf{b}\\right]=\\dfrac{\\tau!}{a_{1}^{\\tau}!\\cdots a_{M}^{\\tau}!}\\mathrm{b}_{1}^{a_{1}^{\\tau}}\\cdots\\mathrm{b}_{M}^{a_{M}^{\\tau}}~\\delta\\left(\\sum_{x}\\mathrm{b}_{x}-1\\right), (21) where the a x τ a_{x}^{\\tau} ’s are free parameters and τ ≡ ∑ x a x τ \\tau\\equiv\\sum_{x}a_{x}^{\\tau} . As both the prior and the likelihood are multinomial the two merge into a multinomial posterior: ℙ [ 𝐛 | 𝐧 t ] ∝ b 1 n 1 t + a 1 τ ⋯ b M n M t + a M τ δ ( ∑ x b x − 1 ) . \\mathbb{P}\\left[\\mathbf{b}\\left|\\mathbf{n}^{t}\\right.\\right]\\propto\\mathrm{b}_{1}^{n_{1}^{t}+a_{1}^{\\tau}}\\cdots\\mathrm{b}_{M}^{n_{M}^{t}+a_{M}^{\\tau}}~\\delta\\left(\\sum_{x}\\mathrm{b}_{x}-1\\right). (22) Taking the expectation of the x x -th component b x \\mathrm{b}_{x} with the measure defined by ( 22 ) gives a modified version of Laplace’s rule of succession for a non-uniform prior : b x md-LAPL , t + 1 = n ~ x t ~ + 1 t ~ + M , \\mathrm{b}_{x}^{\\text{\\tiny md-LAPL},~t+1}=\\dfrac{\\tilde{n}^{\\tilde{t}}_{x}+1}{\\tilde{t}+M}, (23) where t ~ ≡ t + τ \\tilde{t}\\equiv t+\\tau and n ~ x t ~ ≡ n x t + a x τ \\tilde{n}^{\\tilde{t}}_{x}\\equiv n^{t}_{x}+a^{\\tau}_{x} . The interpretation of ( 23 ) is that this prior is equivalent to taking into account τ ∈ ℝ \\tau\\in\\mathbb{R} fictional races before the first one, with a x τ a_{x}^{\\tau} being the equivalent of n x t n_{x}^{t} during these τ \\tau fictive races. Following the same route as with the uniform prior, one finds this reduced posterior ( 21 ): ℙ [ b x | 𝐧 t ] = ( t ~ + M − 1 ) ! n ~ x t ~ ! ​ ( t ~ − n ~ x t ~ + M − 2 ) ! b x n ~ x t ~ ( 1 − b x ) t ~ − n ~ x t ~ + M − 2 . \\mathbb{P}\\left[\\mathrm{b}_{x}\\left|\\mathbf{n}^{t}\\right.\\right]=\\dfrac{(\\tilde{t}+M-1)!}{\\tilde{n}^{\\tilde{t}}_{x}!~(\\tilde{t}-\\tilde{n}^{\\tilde{t}}_{x}+M-2)!}\\mathrm{b}_{x}^{\\tilde{n}^{\\tilde{t}}_{x}}(1-\\mathrm{b}_{x})^{\\tilde{t}-\\tilde{n}^{\\tilde{t}}_{x}+M-2}. (24) At this point, the parameters of the prior (the a i τ a_{i}^{\\tau} ’s) need to be specified. Assuming the bookmaker is well informed of the real 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} , the gambler should favor an initial bet distribution closer to 𝐫 \\mathbf{r} over a random distribution. This can be done by imposing: b x md-LAPL , t = 1 = a x τ + 1 t ~ + M ≡ r x , \\mathrm{b}_{x}^{\\text{\\tiny md-LAPL},~t=1}=\\dfrac{a_{x}^{\\tau}+1}{\\tilde{t}+M}\\equiv\\mathrm{r}_{x}, (25) which leads to: a x τ = r x ⋅ ( τ + M ) − 1 . a_{x}^{\\tau}=\\mathrm{r}_{x}\\cdot(\\tau+M)-1. (26) In order for the information in possession of the bookmaker to be exploitable, the prior should remain bounded. Thus, for all components x x , the parameters of the reduced prior ( 24 ) should be strictly positive. This means : ∀ x , { a x τ &gt; 0 τ − a x τ + M − 2 &gt; 0 . \\forall x,~\\begin{cases}a_{x}^{\\tau}&gt;0\\\\ \\tau-a_{x}^{\\tau}+M-2&gt;0\\end{cases}. (27) Replacing a x τ a_{x}^{\\tau} by its expression given by ( 26 ) one finds that the first condition reduces to τ &gt; 1 min 𝑥 ​ r x − M \\tau&gt;\\frac{1}{\\underset{x}{\\min}~\\mathrm{r}_{x}}-M , while the second implies τ &gt; 1 1 − max 𝑥 ​ r x − M \\tau&gt;\\frac{1}{1-\\underset{x}{\\max}~\\mathrm{r}_{x}}-M . Finally : τ &gt; max ⁡ [ 1 min 𝑥 ​ r x , 1 1 − max 𝑥 ​ r x ] − M ≥ 0 . \\tau&gt;\\max\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\underset{x}{\\min}~\\mathrm{r}_{x}},~\\dfrac{1}{1-\\underset{x}{\\max}~\\mathrm{r}_{x}}\\right]-M\\geq 0. (28) Since ( 26 ) already enforces ∑ x a x τ = τ \\sum_{x}a_{x}^{\\tau}=\\tau , τ \\tau becomes the only free parameter of our problem. Hence choosing any value of τ \\tau fulfilling ( 28 ) induces a set of a x τ a_{x}^{\\tau} fulfilling ( 25 ). Note that a similar strategy could be built with any other form of prior information. For instance, when this information is contained in a distribution 𝝅 \\boldsymbol{\\pi} , the gambler should bet according to : b x md-LAPL , t = 1 = a x τ + 1 τ + M = π x . \\mathrm{b}_{x}^{\\text{\\tiny md-LAPL},~t=1}=\\dfrac{a_{x}^{\\tau}+1}{\\tau+M}=\\pi_{x}. (29) 4.2 Limitations of the modified Laplace’s strategy It is important to highlight that introducing a non-uniform prior only benefits the short term behavior of the log-capital, because one can show that the asymptotic regret of the modified Laplace’s strategy is unchanged by the new choice of the prior: ⟨ Δ ⁡ ( t ) ⟩ ∼ M − 1 2 ​ log ⁡ t \\left\\langle\\Delta(t)\\right\\rangle\\sim\\frac{M-1}{2}\\log t (see. Appendix B for the proof). This also implies that the learning time t ∗ t^{*} is unchanged in the modified Laplace’s strategy. Further, it is expected that using the inverse-odds as prior information is advantageous only if the latter are more informative than using the uniform bet 1 / M 1/M initially in the original Laplace’s rule. In other words, there is the prerequisite that this inverse-odds distribution is closer to 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} than a uniform distribution would be. From Eq. 4 , one can see that this means that the original strategy should start with a learning phase with a negative growth rate. If the original strategy has already a positive growth rate initially, then the bets are already better than what the odds distribution could provide, and in that case, the modified Laplace’s strategy should not be used because it will not bring a benefit over the original Laplace’s strategy. Indeed, using the inverse-odds as prior information results in a strategy between Laplace’s original strategy and the null strategy 𝐛 NULL = 𝐫 \\mathbf{b}^{\\text{\\tiny NULL}}=\\mathbf{r} , and the null strategy, as we mentioned earlier, has a vanishing capital growth rate. Figure 1(a) summarizes what we discussed, showing alongside, Laplace’s strategy (orange solid curve) and the modified Laplace’s based on the inverse-odds (blue solid curve) when the bookmaker has an accurate knowledge of 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} . We also tested that when the original Laplace strategy has a positive slope initially, the modified Laplace’s strategy can also start with a positive slope, albeit with a smaller value, and therefore the modified Laplace strategy in this case does not bring any benefit. Finally, we note that the idea of using non-uniform priors has also been used in the litterature on portfolios. The latter is referred as μ \\mu -weighted universal portfolios [ 20 ] . The basic idea is to replace the uniform measure, which was only enforcing the normalization of the bets in ( 11 ) by a different arbitrary measure μ \\mu , which plays the role of a prior. The authors of this reference used a Dirichlet distribution for μ \\mu , which is a continuous measure, analogous to the multinomial prior we used. 5 Conclusion In this work, we build an adaptive version of Kelly’s horse race model based on Bayesian inference, and we clarify its connection to the theory of universal portfolios. In practice, this strategy suffers a significant drop in the capital of the gambler up to a certain time. We recover the known logarithmic dependence on time for this gambler’s regret, for both correlated and uncorrelated races. We then build an improved adaptive strategy, in which the gambler exploits the information contained in the distribution of the odds of the bookmaker. When this distribution is informative, the cost of the learning phase can be significantly reduced, without affecting the long term growth rate. An interesting question in this context would be to study the limit of the performance of our adaptive strategy using the notion of directed information [ 21 , 22 ] . As mentioned in the introduction, one motivation for this work was to understand the process of adaptation in biological evolution and in particular its cost. In this correspondance between gambling and biology, the environment plays a similar role as the race results, phenotypic decisions taken by biological systems are the equivalent of the bets, the multiplicative rate of the biological population is the equivalent of the odds, and the population growth rate is the equivalent of the long term capital growth rate. The adaptive strategy which we have described in this paper is a form of learning, by which individuals leverage the information acquired from observing past environmental states. One benefit of our work is the characterization of this initial learning phase with its specific time scale. Naturally, the biological problem is vastly more complex than the model we looked at: the information acquired by biological systems from the environment is often partial, corrupted and distorded [ 23 ] . The benefit of sensing is significant when the environment is time-dependent and individuals have memory [ 3 ] . Further, the growth of a biological population can also act back on the environment. Some of these effects could be taken into account in our model by making odds time-dependent or dependent on the bets [ 24 ] , extensions which could also be interesting in the context of investment strategies. While adaptation in biology comes with complex costs associated with the making and the maintenance of the sensing/learning mechanisms, the approach of this paper provides a simple and direct way to model adaptation and its cost, which we hope will be useful for future endeavours on this topic. Acknowledgements We acknowledge fruitful discussions with R. Pugatch. DL acknowledges support from (ANR-11-LABX-0038, ANR-10-IDEX-0001-02). Appendix A Average log-capital behavior for uncorrelated races Each component of 𝐧 t \\mathbf{n}^{t} is given by the sum of independent R.V.: n x t = ∑ i = 1 t ( n x i − n x i − 1 ) ≡ ∑ i = 1 t w x i , n_{x}^{t}=\\sum_{i=1}^{t}\\left(n_{x}^{i}-n_{x}^{i-1}\\right)\\equiv\\\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{t}w_{x}^{i}, (30) where w x i = 1 w_{x}^{i}=1 if x x wins the race i i (with probability p x \\mathrm{p}_{x} ), 0 0 if it looses (with probability ( 1 − p x ) (1-\\mathrm{p}_{x}) ). One immediately sees that: 𝔼 W ​ [ w x ] = p x \\mathbb{E}_{W}\\left[w_{x}\\right]=\\mathrm{p}_{x} and σ x 2 ≡ 𝔼 W ​ [ ( w x − 𝔼 W ​ [ w x ] ) 2 ] = p x ​ ( 1 − p x ) \\sigma_{x}^{2}\\equiv\\mathbb{E}_{W}\\left[\\left(w_{x}-\\mathbb{E}_{W}\\left[w_{x}\\right]\\right)^{2}\\right]=\\mathrm{p}_{x}(1-\\mathrm{p}_{x}) . Hence the application of the central limit theorem (CLT) gives: n x t ​ ≈ t → ∞ ​ p x ​ t + t ​ σ x ⋅ z t , n_{x}^{t}\\underset{t\\to\\infty}{\\approx}\\mathrm{p}_{x}t+\\sqrt{t}\\sigma_{x}\\cdot z_{t}, (31) where z t z_{t} is the realization at time t t of Z Z , a standard Gaussian R.V. of mean 0 and variance 1. Note that the leading order of ( 31 ) ensures that Laplace’s rule converges to p x \\mathrm{p}_{x} when the number of race increases. Plugging the expression of n x t n^{t}_{x} obtained with the CLT in log ⁡ b x t + 1 LAPL , t + 1 \\log\\mathrm{b}_{x_{t+1}}^{\\text{\\tiny LAPL},~t+1} reads: log ⁡ b x LAPL , t + 1 ≈ log ⁡ p x + 1 t ​ σ x p x ​ z t + 1 − 1 2 ​ ( σ x p x ​ z t + 1 ) 2 + 1 p x ​ t − M t . \\log\\mathrm{b}_{x}^{\\text{\\tiny LAPL},~t+1}\\approx\\log\\mathrm{p}_{x}+\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\dfrac{\\sigma_{x}}{\\mathrm{p}_{x}}z_{t+1}\\\\ -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(