EEE Transactions on Neural Networks\n, 22(9):1368–1380, 2011.\nScarselli et al. (2005)\nScarselli, F., Yong, S. L., Gori, M., Hagenbuchner, M., Tsoi, A. C., and\nMaggini, M.\nGraph neural networks for ranking web pages.\nIn\nThe 2005 IEEE/WIC/ACM International Conference on Web\nIntelligence (WI’05)\n. IEEE, 2005.\nSchütze et al. (2008)\nSchütze, H., Manning, C. D., and Raghavan, P.\nIntroduction to information retrieval\n, volume 39.\nCambridge University Press Cambridge, 2008.\nSinger (2011)\nSinger, A.\nAngular synchronization by eigenvectors and semidefinite programming.\nApplied and Computational Harmonic Analysis\n, 30(1):20–36, 2011.\nTalbi (2013)\nTalbi, E.-G.\nA taxonomy of metaheuristics for bi-level optimization.\nIn\nMetaheuristics for bi-level optimization\n, pp.  1–39.\nSpringer, 2013.\nTong et al. (2020)\nTong, Z., Liang, Y., Sun, C., Li, X., Rosenblum, D., and Lim, A.\nDigraph inception convolutional networks.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n,\n33:17907–17918, 2020.\nTsokos et al. (2019)\nTsokos, A., Narayanan, S., Kosmidis, I., Baio, G., Cucuringu, M., Whitaker, G.,\nand Király, F.\nModeling outcomes of soccer matches.\nMachine Learning\n, 108(1):77–95, 2019.\nVon Luxburg (2007)\nVon Luxburg, U.\nA tutorial on spectral clustering.\nStatistics and computing\n, 17(4):395–416,\n2007.\nVu et al. (2019)\nVu, T., Raich, R., and Fu, X.\nOn convergence of projected gradient descent for minimizing a\nlarge-scale quadratic over the unit sphere.\nIn\n2019 IEEE 29th International Workshop on Machine Learning\nfor Signal Processing (MLSP)\n, pp.  1–6. IEEE, 2019.\nWang et al. (2021)\nWang, J., Xie, H., Wang, F. L., Lee, L.-K., and Au, O. T. S.\nTop-n personalized recommendation with graph neural networks in\nmoocs.\nComputers and Education: Artificial Intelligence\n, 2:100010, 2021.\nWu et al. (2020)\nWu, Z., Pan, S., Chen, F., Long, G., Zhang, C., and Philip, S. Y.\nA comprehensive survey on graph neural networks.\nIEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Systems\n,\n32(1):4–24, 2020.\nYang et al. (2021)\nYang, Y., Liu, T., Wang, Y., Zhou, J., Gan, Q., Wei, Z., Zhang, Z., Huang, Z.,\nand Wipf, D.\nGraph neural networks inspired by classical iterative algorithms.\nIn\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning\n. PMLR, 2021.\nZhang et al. (2021)\nZhang, X., He, Y., Brugnone, N., Perlmutter, M., and Hirn, M.\nMagnet: A neural network for directed graphs.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n, 34, 2021.\nZhou et al. (2020)\nZhou, J., Cui, G., Hu, S., Zhang, Z., Yang, C., Liu, Z., Wang, L., Li, C., and\nSun, M.\nGraph neural networks: A review of methods and applications.\nAI Open\n, 1:57–81, 2020.\nAppendix A\nImplementation Details\nA.1\nSetup\nWe use all data for training for at most 1000 epochs, and stop early if the loss does not decrease for 200 epochs. For proximal variants, we use 50 epochs for pretraining. We use Adam\n(\nKingma & Ba 2014\n)\nand Stochastic Gradient Descend (SGD) as the optimizers and\nℓ\n2\n\\ell_{2}\nregularization with weight decay\n5\n⋅\n10\n−\n4\n5\\cdot 10^{-4}\nto avoid overfitting. We use as learning rate 0.01 throughout for GNNRank-N methods as well as pretraining with Adam, and 10 times that of pretraining learning rate for GNNRank-P methods with SGD. We run a grid search on hyperparameters.\nFor real-world data sets, we conduct 10 repeated runs, while for synthetic data, we generate 5 synthetic networks under the same setting, each with 2 repeated runs.\nNote that we do not evaluate our method by the loss which is used to devise the method, as that would not be fair; instead we employ\nℒ\nu\n​\np\n​\ns\n​\ne\n​\nt\n,\nn\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nv\n​\ne\n\\mathcal{L}_{upset,naive}\nor\nℒ\nu\n​\np\n​\ns\n​\ne\n​\nt\n,\ns\n​\ni\n​\nm\n​\np\n​\nl\n​\ne\n\\mathcal{L}_{upset,simple}\nwhich are never used in training. Thus, the comparison is fair. When ground truths are given, we follow\n(\nd’Aspremont et al. 2021\n)\nto use Kendall Tau values for comparison.\nA.2\nCodes, Data and Hardware\nTo fully reproduce our results, codes and preprocessed data are available at\nhttps://github.com/SherylHYX/GNNRank\n. Experiments were conducted on a compute node with 8 Nvidia Tesla T4, 96 Intel Xeon Platinum 8259CL CPUs @ 2.50GHz and\n378\n378\nGB RAM. Most experiments can be completed within a week, including all variants, hyperparameter searches and ablation studies, except for those on MVR.\nThe data sets considered here are relatively small and the same applies to GNNRank’s competitive papers. However, even a network with\n100\n100\nnodes has more than\n10\n157\n10^{157}\npossible rankings; this large scale task needs efficient methods. Although each individual task does not require much resource (often\n<\n<\n5min/run),\nfor the paper we have 78 real-world and 18 synthetic data sets,\neach requires 10 runs for each of the 36 (proximal innerproduct, proximal dist) + 126 (proximal baseline) + 12 (non-proximal) = 174 variants, thus 167,040 runs, plus an extra ablation study.\nAppendix B\nDetails on Finding\n𝐐\n\\mathbf{Q}\nfor Proximal Gradient Steps\nThere are infinitely many choices of valid\n𝐐\n\\mathbf{Q}\n;\nhere we construct one of the special\n𝐐\n\\mathbf{Q}\n’s for which\nwe can compute\n𝐐𝐋\n\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{L}\nefficiently.\nSince\n𝐐\n⊤\n​\n𝐞\n1\n=\n𝟏\n/\nn\n\\mathbf{Q}^{\\top}\\mathbf{e}_{1}=\\mathbf{1}/\\sqrt{n}\n, we can construct\n𝐑\n=\n𝐐\n⊤\n\\mathbf{R}=\\mathbf{Q}^{\\top}\nas a series of matrix multiplication of\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n(n-1)\nrotation matrices on two adjacent axes\n𝐑\n=\n𝐑\nn\n−\n1\n𝐑\nn\n−\n2\n⋯\n𝐑\n2\n𝐑\n1\n\\mathbf{R}=\\mathbf{R}_{n-1}\\mathbf{R}_{n-2}\\cdots\\mathbf{R}_{2}\\mathbf{R}_{1}\nwhere\n𝐑\nk\n\\mathbf{R}_{k}\nis defined by:\n[\n𝐑\nk\n]\ni\n​\nj\n=\n{\n1\nn\n−\nk\ni\n=\nk\n,\nj\n=\nk\n​\nor\n​\ni\n=\nk\n+\n1\n,\nj\n=\nk\n+\n1\n−\nn\n−\nk\n−\n1\nn\n−\nk\ni\n=\nk\n,\nj\n=\nk\n+\n1\nn\n−\nk\n−\n1\nn\n−\nk\ni\n=\nk\n+\n1\n,\nj\n=\nk\n1\ni\n=\nj\n​\n, and\n​\ni\n,\nj\n≠\nk\n​\nor\n​\nk\n+\n1\n0\notherwise.\n\\left[\\mathbf{R}_{k}\\right]_{ij}=\\begin{cases}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n-k}}&i=k,j=k\\text{ or }i=k+1,j=k+1\\\\\n-\\sqrt{\\frac{n-k-1}{n-k}}&i=k,j=k+1\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{n-k-1}{n-k}}&i=k+1,j=k\\\\\n1&i=j\\text{, and }i,j\\neq k\\text{ or }k+1\\\\\n0&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\nTo explain this construction, starting from\n𝐞\n1\n=\n[\n1\n0\n⋯\n0\n]\n⊤\n\\mathbf{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&\\cdots&0\\end{bmatrix}^{\\top}\n,\nwe carry out a rotation on the first and second axis to make the first element\n1\n/\nn\n\\sqrt{1/n}\n. The rotation matrix is\n𝐑\n1\n=\n[\n1\nn\n−\nn\n−\n1\nn\n⋯\nn\n−\n1\nn\n1\nn\n⋯\n1\n⋯\n1\n⋯\n⋱\n1\n]\n\\mathbf{R}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&-\\sqrt{\\frac{n-1}{n}}&&&\\cdots&\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{n-1}{n}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&&&\\cdots&\\\\\n&&1&&\\cdots&\\\\\n&&&1&\\cdots&\\\\\n\\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\\n&&&&&1\\end{bmatrix}\nWe observe that\n𝐑\n1\n​\n𝐞\n1\n=\n[\n1\nn\nn\n−\n1\nn\n0\n0\n⋯\n0\n]\n⊤\n.\n\\mathbf{R}_{1}\\mathbf{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{n-1}{n}}&0&0&\\cdots&0\\end{bmatrix}^{\\top}.\nThe second rotation\noccurs on the second and third axis to render\n𝐑\n2\n​\n𝐑\n1\n​\n𝐞\n1\n=\n[\n1\nn\n1\nn\nn\n−\n2\nn\n0\n⋯\n0\n]\n⊤\n\\mathbf{R}_{2}\\mathbf{R}_{1}\\mathbf{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{n-2}{n}}&0&\\cdots&0\\end{bmatrix}^{\\top}\n; this\n𝐑\n2\n\\mathbf{R}_{2}\nis\n𝐑\n2\n=\n[\n1\n⋯\n1\nn\n−\n1\n−\nn\n−\n2\nn\n−\n1\n⋯\nn\n−\n2\nn\n−\n1\n1\nn\n−\n1\n⋯\n1\n⋯\n⋱\n1\n]\n\\mathbf{R}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1&&&&\\cdots&\\\\\n&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n-1}}&-\\sqrt{\\frac{n-2}{n-1}}&&\\cdots&\\\\\n&\\sqrt{\\frac{n-2}{n-1}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n-1}}&&\\cdots&\\\\\n&&&1&\\cdots&\\\\\n\\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\\n&&&&&1\\end{bmatrix}\nThe general matrices\nR\nk\nR_{k}\nare obtained by continuing this construction.\nThe matrix\n𝐑\n\\mathbf{R}\nis\n[\n1\nn\n−\nn\n−\n1\nn\n⋯\n1\nn\n1\nn\n⁡\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n−\nn\n−\n2\nn\n−\n1\n⋯\n1\nn\n1\nn\n⁡\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n1\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nn\n−\n2\n)\n−\nn\n−\n3\nn\n−\n2\n⋯\n1\nn\n1\nn\n⁡\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n1\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nn\n−\n2\n)\n1\n(\nn\n−\n2\n)\n​\n(\nn\n−\n3\n)\n⋯\n⋱\n1\nn\n1\nn\n⁡\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n1\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n​\n(\nn\n−\n2\n)\n1\n(\nn\n−\n2\n)\n​\n(\nn\n−\n3\n)\n⋯\n1\n2\n]\n\\scriptsize\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&-\\sqrt{\\frac{n-1}{n}}&&&\\cdots&\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}}&-\\sqrt{\\frac{n-2}{n-1}}&&\\cdots&\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(n-1)(n-2)}}&-\\sqrt{\\frac{n-3}{n-2}}&\\cdots&\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(n-1)(n-2)}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(n-2)(n-3)}}&\\cdots&\\\\\n\\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(n-1)(n-2)}}&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(n-2)(n-3)}}&\\cdots&\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\nWe then put\n𝐐\n=\n𝐑\n⊤\n\\mathbf{Q}=\\mathbf{R}^{\\top}\n.\nThe resulting\n𝐐\n\\mathbf{Q}\nis\nthe following upper Hessenberg matrix, which is independent of model parameters and can be efficiently precomputed\n𝐐\ni\n​\nj\n=\n{\n1\nn\ni\n=\n1\n−\nn\n−\ni\n+\n1\nn\n−\ni\n+\n2\ni\n≥\n2\n,\nj\n=\ni\n−\n1\n1\n(\nn\n−\ni\n+\n1\n)\n​\n(\nn\n−\ni\n+\n2\n)\ni\n≥\n2\n,\nj\n≥\ni\n0\notherwise.\n\\mathbf{Q}_{ij}=\\begin{cases}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}}&i=1\\\\\n-\\sqrt{\\frac{n-i+1}{n-i+2}}&i\\geq 2,j=i-1\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(n-i+1)(n-i+2)}}&i\\geq 2,j\\geq i\\\\\n0&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\nThe computation of\n𝐐𝐋\n\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{L}\ntakes\nO\n⁡\n(\nn\n2\n)\nO(n^{2})\ntime since\n𝐐\n\\mathbf{Q}\nis a summation of (1) an upper-triangular matrix\n𝐔\n\\mathbf{U}\nwith the same non-zero value on the same row, and (2) a shift matrix\n𝐕\n\\mathbf{V}\n(with negative subdiagonal). Then\n𝐔𝐋\n\\mathbf{U}\\mathbf{L}\nis essentially a cumulative sum from the bottom row to the top row, followed by a row-wise multiplication of the diagonal of\n𝐔\n\\mathbf{U}\n, and\n𝐕𝐋\n\\mathbf{V}\\mathbf{L}\nis essentially a combination of row-wise multiplication and indexing.\nAppendix C\nTheoretical Analysis and Practical Considerations on Convergence of the Proximal Gradient Steps\nFirst we prove a theorem useful for the main result.\nTheorem C.1\n.\nLet\n{\nα\nγ\n>\n0\n}\nγ\n=\n1\nΓ\n\\{\\alpha_{\\gamma}>0\\}_{\\gamma=1}^{\\Gamma}\nin Algo.\n1\nbe fixed and equal to\nα\n\\alpha\n. Let\nρ\n\\rho\nbe the Fiedler eigenvalue of\n𝐒\n\\mathbf{S}\n, i.e., the second smallest eigenvalue of\n𝐋\n\\mathbf{L}\n. Let\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\nbe a Fiedler eigenvector corresponding to\nρ\n,\n\\rho,\nlet\n𝐲\n∗\n=\n[\n𝐐𝐫\n∗\n]\n2\n:\nn\n\\mathbf{y}^{*}=\\left[\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{r}^{*}\\right]_{2:n}\nand\nL\n~\n=\n[\n𝐐𝐋𝐐\n⊤\n]\n2\n:\nn\n,\n2\n:\nn\n.\n\\widetilde{L}=\\left[\\mathbf{QLQ}^{\\top}\\right]_{2:n,2:n}.\nLet\nλ\n1\n≥\nλ\n2\n⋯\n≥\nλ\nn\n−\n1\n=\n0\n\\lambda_{1}\\geq\\lambda_{2}\\cdots\\geq\\lambda_{n-1}=0\nbe the eigenvalues of\n𝐏\n​\n𝐋\n~\n​\n𝐏\n,\n\\mathbf{P}\\widetilde{\\mathbf{L}}\\mathbf{P},\nwhere\n𝐏\n=\n𝐈\n−\n𝐲\n∗\n​\n𝐲\n∗\n⁣\n⊤\n\\mathbf{P}=\\mathbf{I}-\\mathbf{y}^{*}\\mathbf{y}^{*\\top}\n.\nLet\nρ\nα\n=\nmax\n1\n≤\ni\n≤\nn\n−\n2\n⁡\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\ni\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nρ\n\\rho_{\\alpha}=\\max_{1\\leq i\\leq n-2}\\frac{|1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{i}|}{1-2\\alpha\\rho}\n. If\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\nis a strict local minimum of problem (\n5\n), and if\nρ\nα\n<\n1\n\\rho_{\\alpha}<1\nand\nα\n⁡\n(\nλ\n1\n+\nρ\n)\n<\n1\n,\n\\alpha(\\lambda_{1}+\\rho)<1,\nthen Algo.\n1\nconverges locally uniformly to\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\n.\nProof.\nOur problem (\n7\n) is a special case of the problem (2) in\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\n(henceforth (\n8\n) further below, of minimizing a quadratic over a sphere), where our fixed point\n𝐲\n∗\n\\mathbf{y}^{*}\nis determined by\n𝐟\nα\n​\n(\n𝐱\n¯\n)\n=\n𝒫\n𝒮\nn\n−\n2\n​\n(\n−\nα\n⁡\n(\n2\n​\n𝐋\n~\n​\n𝐱\n¯\n)\n)\n=\nx\n¯\n\\mathbf{f}_{\\alpha}(\\mathbf{\\overline{x}})=\\mathcal{P}_{\\mathcal{S}^{n-2}}(-\\alpha(2\\widetilde{\\mathbf{L}}\\mathbf{\\overline{x}}))=\\mathbf{\\mathbf{}}{\\overline{x}}\n. Recall that the spherical projection operator\n𝒫\n𝒮\nn\n−\n2\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n:\nℝ\nn\n−\n1\n→\nℝ\nn\n−\n1\n\\mathcal{P}_{\\mathcal{S}^{n-2}}(\\cdot):\\mathbb{R}^{n-1}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{n-1}\nis defined to be\n𝒫\n𝒮\nn\n−\n2\n​\n(\n𝐱\n)\n=\n𝐱\n‖\n𝐱\n‖\n2\n\\mathcal{P}_{\\mathcal{S}^{n-2}}(\\mathbf{x})=\\frac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\left\\lVert\\mathbf{x}\\right\\rVert_{2}}\nif\n𝐱\n≠\n𝟎\n\\mathbf{x}\\neq\\mathbf{0}\n, and\n𝒫\n𝒮\nn\n−\n2\n​\n(\n𝟎\n)\n=\n[\n1\n,\n0\n,\n…\n,\n0\n]\n⊤\n,\n\\mathcal{P}_{\\mathcal{S}^{n-2}}(\\mathbf{0})=[1,0,\\dots,0]^{\\top},\nwhere\n𝟎\n=\n[\n0\n,\n…\n,\n0\n]\n⊤\n.\n\\mathbf{0}=[0,\\dots,0]^{\\top}.\nIt is shown in Lemma 1 of\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\n(henceforth Lemma\nC.3\nfurther below) that\n𝐲\n∗\n\\mathbf{y}^{*}\nwith\n‖\n𝐲\n∗\n‖\n=\n1\n||\\mathbf{y}^{*}||=1\nbeing a solution of (\n7\n) is equivalent to the existence of a constant\nρ\n⁡\n(\n𝐱\n¯\n)\n\\rho(\\mathbf{\\overline{x}})\nsuch that\n𝐋\n~\n​\n𝐲\n∗\n=\nρ\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n∗\n)\n⋅\n𝐲\n∗\n,\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{L}}\\mathbf{y}^{*}=\\rho(\\mathbf{y}^{*})\\cdot\\mathbf{y}^{*},\nrendering\nρ\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n∗\n)\n\\rho(\\mathbf{y}^{*})\nto be an eigenvalue of\n𝐋\n~\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{L}}\n. Theorem 1 in\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\n(henceforth Theorem\nC.5\nbelow) states that with an initial step size\nα\n>\n0\n\\alpha>0\n, such that\n2\n​\nα\n​\n(\nρ\n⁡\n(\n𝐲\n∗\n)\n+\nλ\n1\n)\n<\n2\n2\\alpha(\\rho(\\mathbf{y}^{*})+\\lambda_{1})<2\n, as long as\nOPEN\nρ\nα\n=\nmax\n1\n≤\ni\n≤\nn\n−\n2\n⁡\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\ni\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nγ\n​\n(\n𝐲\n∗\nCLOSE\n)\n<\n1\n,\n\\rho_{\\alpha}=\\max_{1\\leq i\\leq n-2}\\frac{|1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{i}|}{1-2\\alpha\\gamma(\\mathbf{y}^{*}})<1,\nthe proximal gradients steps will converge to a local minimum.\nNote that problem (\n5\n) and problem (\n7\n) are equivalent, and there is a one-to-one correspondence between\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\nand\n𝐲\n∗\n,\n\\mathbf{y}^{*},\nnamely,\n𝐲\n∗\n=\n[\n𝐐𝐫\n∗\n]\n2\n:\nn\n∈\nℝ\nn\n−\n1\n,\n\\mathbf{y}^{*}=\\left[\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{r}^{*}\\right]_{2:n}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n-1},\nand\n𝐫\n∗\n=\nCONCAT\n​\n(\n0\n,\n[\n𝐐\n⊤\n​\n𝐫\n∗\n]\n)\n∈\nℝ\nn\n.\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}=\\text{CONCAT}(0,\\left[\\mathbf{Q}^{\\top}\\mathbf{r}^{*}\\right])\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}.\nHence,\nsince\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\nis a strict local minimum of problem (\n5\n), we have that\n𝐲\n∗\n\\mathbf{y}^{*}\nis a strict local minimum of problem (\n7\n).\nBy\nLemma\nC.3\nfrom\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\n, we have\nρ\n<\nλ\nn\n−\n2\n\\rho<\\lambda_{n-2}\n, since\n𝐲\n∗\n\\mathbf{y}^{*}\nis a strict local minimum. In particular,\nby\nTheorem\nC.5\nin\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\n, when\n𝐲\n∗\n\\mathbf{y}^{*}\nis a strict local minimum to problem (\n8\n),\nif\n2\n​\nα\n​\n(\nρ\n+\nλ\n1\n)\n<\n2\n2\\alpha(\\rho+\\lambda_{1})<2\n, i.e.,\nα\n⁡\n(\nρ\n+\nλ\n1\n)\n<\n1\n,\n\\alpha(\\rho+\\lambda_{1})<1,\nthere is a constant M such that\n‖\n𝐲\nγ\n−\n𝐲\n∗\n‖\n≤\nM\n​\n‖\n𝐲\n0\n−\n𝐲\n∗\n‖\n​\n(\nρ\nα\n+\no\n⁡\n(\n1\n)\n)\nγ\n,\n||\\mathbf{y}^{\\gamma}-\\mathbf{y}^{*}||\\leq M||\\mathbf{y}^{0}-\\mathbf{y}^{*}||(\\rho_{\\alpha}+o(1))^{\\gamma},\nwhere\n𝐲\n0\n\\mathbf{y}^{0}\nis the initial guess at proximal gradient step 0, and\nγ\n\\gamma\ndenotes the step number. As\nρ\nα\n<\n1\n\\rho_{\\alpha}<1\nis assumed, convergence follows.\n∎\nFor convergence, it suffices to ensure that\nρ\nα\n<\n1\n,\n\\rho_{\\alpha}<1,\ni.e. to have\n2\n​\nα\n​\nγ\n​\n(\n𝐱\n¯\n)\n<\n1\n2\\alpha\\gamma(\\mathbf{\\overline{x}})<1\n,\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\n1\n|\n<\n1\n−\nα\n​\nγ\n​\n(\n𝐱\n¯\n)\n|1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{1}|<1-\\alpha\\gamma(\\mathbf{\\overline{x}})\nand\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\nn\n−\n2\n|\n<\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nγ\n​\n(\n𝐱\n¯\n)\n|1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{n-2}|<1-2\\alpha\\gamma(\\mathbf{\\overline{x}})\n. In particular, in our problem we take\nρ\n⁡\n(\n𝐱\n¯\n)\n=\nρ\n\\rho(\\mathbf{\\overline{x}})=\\rho\n, which is also the smallest eigenvalue of\n𝐋\n~\n,\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{L}},\nso\nρ\n<\nλ\nn\n−\n2\n≤\nλ\n1\n.\n\\rho<\\lambda_{n-2}\\leq\\lambda_{1}.\nTo apply Theorem\nC.1\nin order to obtain a convergence guarantee in Algo.\n1\n, as\nλ\nn\n−\n2\n>\nρ\n,\n\\lambda_{n-2}>\\rho,\n(which is equivalent to\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\nbeing a strict local minimum) it remains to use an\nα\n\\alpha\nvalue such that\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\n1\n<\n1\n.\n2\\alpha\\lambda_{1}<1.\nLemma\nC.4\nof\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\nalso suggests that if\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\n1\n<\n1\n2\\alpha\\lambda_{1}<1\n, then\nwe indeed have\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\nas a global minimizer to problem (\n5\n).\nFinally we have the ingredients to prove the main result.\nTheorem C.2\n.\nLet\n{\nα\nγ\n>\n0\n}\nγ\n=\n1\nΓ\n\\{\\alpha_{\\gamma}>0\\}_{\\gamma=1}^{\\Gamma}\nin Algo.\n1\nbe fixed (equal to\nα\n\\alpha\n) and let\nρ\n\\rho\nbe the Fiedler eigenvalue of\n𝐒\n\\mathbf{S}\n. Denote a Fiedler eigenvector by\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\n. Assume that\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\nis a strict local minimizer of problem (\n5\n).\nIf\n0\n<\nα\n<\n1\n4\n​\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n,\n0<\\alpha<\\frac{1}{4(n-1)},\nthen with our definition of the similarity matrix, Algo.\n1\nconverges locally uniformly to\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\n.\nProof.\nVia our similarity matrix construction process, entries in\n𝐒\n\\mathbf{S}\nare upper-bounded by 1, so the degree matrix\n𝐃\n\\mathbf{D}\nhas entries bounded above by\nn\n,\nn,\nthe number of nodes, and lower bounded by 0, and that\n𝐋\n\\mathbf{L}\nis positive semi-definite. We thus have that the eigenvalues of\n𝐋\n\\mathbf{L}\nare in\n[\n0\n,\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n+\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n×\n1\n]\n[0,(n-1)+(n-1)\\times 1]\nby the Gershgorin disc theorem. Since\n𝐐\n\\mathbf{Q}\nis orthogonal, we have that eigenvalues of\n𝐋\n~\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{L}}\nare also within the range\n[\n0\n,\n2\n​\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n]\n.\n[0,2(n-1)].\nTo see this, suppose\n𝐱\n\\mathbf{x}\nis an eigenvector of\n𝐋\n\\mathbf{L}\nwith eigenvalue\nλ\n,\n\\lambda,\ni.e.,\n𝐋𝐱\n=\nλ\n​\n𝐱\n\\mathbf{Lx}=\\lambda\\mathbf{x}\n, so\n𝐐𝐋𝐐\n⊤\n(\n𝐐𝐱\n)\n=\n𝐐𝐋\n⁡\n(\n𝐐\n⊤\n𝐐\n)\n​\n𝐱\n=\n𝐐𝐋𝐱\n=\n𝐐\n⁡\n(\n𝐋𝐱\n)\n=\n𝐐\n​\nλ\n​\n𝐱\n=\nλ\n⁡\n(\n𝐐𝐱\n)\n.\n\\mathbf{QLQ}\\top(\\mathbf{Qx})=\\mathbf{QL}(\\mathbf{Q}\\top\\mathbf{Q})\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{QL}\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{Lx})=\\mathbf{Q}\\lambda\\mathbf{x}=\\lambda(\\mathbf{Qx}).\nTherefore, the values\n{\nλ\ni\n}\ni\n=\n1\nn\n−\n2\n\\{\\lambda_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n-2}\nin Theorem\nC.1\nare bounded above by\n2\n​\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n.\n2(n-1).\nThus, we have that\nρ\n+\nλ\n1\n≤\n2\n​\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n+\n2\n​\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n=\n4\n​\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n.\n\\rho+\\lambda_{1}\\leq 2(n-1)+2(n-1)=4(n-1).\nFor convergence, it suffices to ensure that\nρ\nα\n=\nmax\n1\n≤\ni\n≤\nn\n−\n2\n⁡\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\ni\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nρ\n<\n1\n,\n\\rho_{\\alpha}=\\max_{1\\leq i\\leq n-2}\\frac{|1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{i}|}{1-2\\alpha\\rho}<1,\ni.e. to have\n2\n​\nα\n​\nρ\n<\n1\n2\\alpha\\rho<1\n,\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\n1\n|\n<\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nρ\n|1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{1}|<1-2\\alpha\\rho\n, and\n|\n1\n−\nα\n​\nλ\nn\n−\n2\n|\n<\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nρ\n|1-\\alpha\\lambda_{n-2}|<1-2\\alpha\\rho\n.\nThus, since we have\nλ\nn\n−\n2\n>\nρ\n\\lambda_{n-2}>\\rho\nby\nLemma\nC.4\nfrom\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\nand the proof of Theorem\nC.1\n, it remains to use an\nα\n\\alpha\nvalue such that\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\n1\n<\n1\n,\n2\\alpha\\lambda_{1}<1,\nthen\n2\n​\nα\n​\nρ\n<\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\n1\n<\n1\n2\\alpha\\rho<2\\alpha\\lambda_{1}<1\n, and\n0\n<\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\n1\n=\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\n1\n|\n≤\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\nn\n−\n2\n=\n|\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\nn\n−\n2\n|\n<\n1\n−\n2\n​\nα\n​\nρ\n.\n0<1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{1}=|1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{1}|\\leq 1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{n-2}=|1-2\\alpha\\lambda_{n-2}|<1-2\\alpha\\rho.\nWith\nα\n<\n1\n4\n​\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n,\n\\alpha<\\frac{1}{4(n-1)},\nwe indeed have\n2\n​\nα\n​\nλ\n1\n<\n2\n×\n1\n4\n​\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n×\n2\n​\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n=\n1\n.\n2\\alpha\\lambda_{1}<2\\times\\frac{1}{4(n-1)}\\times 2(n-1)=1.\n∎\nOur current setting is to initialize all\nα\n\\alpha\nvalues to be\n1\nn\n−\n1\n\\frac{1}{n-1}\n.\nNote that within Algo.\n1\n, the\nα\n\\alpha\nvalues are fixed. Although not guaranteed to converge with the initial\nα\n\\alpha\n, they could be adapted by our outer training loop, i.e. by optimization over the ranking loss function.\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\nalso suggests the value of the optimal step size to be\n2\nλ\n1\n+\nλ\nn\n−\n2\n,\n\\frac{2}{\\lambda_{1}+\\lambda_{n-2}},\nwhich during training our method could in principle reproduce if the optimal step size is actually important for making accurate final rankings.\nWe remark that real-world weighted networks have the corresponding Fiedler eigenvalue of multiplicity 1; this multiplicity is larger than 1, only for\nspecial classes of graphs that exhibit certain symmetries. Our pragmatic assumption that the Fiedler eigenvalue has multiplicity 1, which thus means\n𝐫\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}^{*}\nbeing a strict local minimum of problem\n5\n, is not a very restrictive one.\nFor completeness, the remainder of this section recalls the setup and main results from\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\n. The problem considered therein is that of minimizing a quadratic form over the sphere\nmin\n𝐱\n⁡\n𝐱\n⊤\n​\n𝐁𝐱\n−\n𝐛\n⊤\n​\n𝐱\ns.t.\n\\displaystyle\\min_{\\mathbf{x}}\\mathbf{x}^{\\top}\\mathbf{Bx}-\\mathbf{b}^{\\top}\\mathbf{x}\\quad\\text{ s.t.}\n‖\n𝐱\n‖\n2\n2\n=\n1\n\\displaystyle\\quad\\left\\lVert\\mathbf{x}\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}=1\n(8)\nwith the matrix\n𝐁\n∈\nℝ\nm\n×\nm\n\\mathbf{B}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times m}\nassumed symmetric but not positive semi-definite, hence a non-convex objective function. Upon considering the corresponding Lagrangian function, with\nν\n\\nu\ndenoting the Lagrange multiplier, the authors prove the following results, which also constitute building blocks in our analysis. The eigenvalues of\n𝐀\n\\mathbf{A}\nare given by\nλ\nm\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\n𝐁\n)\n≤\nλ\nm\n−\n1\n≤\n…\n≤\nλ\n1\n≤\nλ\nm\n​\na\n​\nx\n​\n(\n𝐁\n)\n\\lambda_{min}(\\mathbf{B})\\leq\\lambda_{m-1}\\leq\\ldots\\leq\\lambda_{1}\\leq\\lambda_{max}(\\mathbf{B})\n.\nLemma C.3\n(Lemma 1 from\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\n)\n.\n(Stationary conditions). The vector\n𝐱\n∗\n\\mathbf{x}_{*}\nis a stationary point of problem (\n8\n) if and only if\n𝐱\n∗\n∈\n𝒮\nm\n−\n1\n=\n{\n𝐱\n∈\nℝ\nm\n:\n∥\n𝐱\n∥\n=\n1\n}\n\\mathbf{x}_{*}\\in\\mathcal{S}^{m-1}=\\{\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}:\\lVert\\mathbf{x}\\rVert=1\\}\nand there exists a constant\nν\n⁡\n(\n𝐱\n∗\n)\n\\nu(\\mathbf{x}_{*})\nsuch that\n𝐫\n∗\n=\nA\n​\n𝐱\n∗\n−\nb\n=\nν\n⁡\n(\n𝐱\n∗\n)\n⋅\n𝐱\n∗\n\\mathbf{r}_{*}=A\\mathbf{x}_{*}-b=\\nu(\\mathbf{x}_{*})\\cdot\\mathbf{x}_{*}\n.\nLemma C.4\n(Lemma 2 from\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\n)\n.\nA stationary point\n𝐱\n∗\n\\mathbf{x}_{*}\nof problem (\n8\n) is a\nstrict\nlocal minimum if and only if\nν\n⁡\n(\n𝐱\n∗\n)\n<\nλ\nm\n−\n1\n​\n(\n𝐱\n∗\n)\n\\nu(\\mathbf{x}_{*})<\\lambda_{m-1}(\\mathbf{x}_{*})\n. Furthermore,\n𝐱\n∗\n\\mathbf{x}_{*}\nis a\nglobal minimizer\nof problem (\n8\n) if and only if\nν\n⁡\n(\n𝐱\n∗\n)\n≤\nλ\nm\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\n𝐁\n)\n.\n\\nu(\\mathbf{x}_{*})\\leq\\lambda_{min}(\\mathbf{B}).\nThe following result establishes the result that\nProjected Gradient Descent (PGD) converges to a local minimum at an asymptotic linear rate\nρ\nβ\n\\rho_{\\beta}\n.\nTheorem C.5\n(Theorem 1 from\n(\nVu et al. 2019\n)\n))\n.\nThe vector\n𝐱\n∗\n\\mathbf{x}_{*}\nis a strict local minimum of problem (\n8\n), i.e.\nν\n<\nλ\nm\n−\n1\n\\nu<\\lambda_{m-1}\n, if and only if there exists\nβ\n>\n0\n\\beta>0\nsuch that Algorithm 1 [Projected Gradient Descent (PGD)] with step size\nα\n\\alpha\nconverges locally uniformly to\n𝐱\n∗\n\\mathbf{x}_{*}\n. Furthermore, for any step size\nβ\n>\n0\n\\beta>0\nsuch that\nβ\n⁡\n(\nλ\n1\n+\nν\n)\n<\n2\n\\beta(\\lambda_{1}+\\nu)<2\n, the sequence\n{\n𝐱\n(\nt\n)\n}\n\\{\\mathbf{x}^{(t)}\\}\nsatisfies\n∥\n𝐱\n(\nt\n)\n−\n𝐱\n∗\n∥\n≤\nM\n⁡\n∥\n𝐱\n(\n0\n)\n−\n𝐱\n∗\n∥\n​\n(\nρ\nα\n+\no\n⁡\n(\n1\n)\n)\nt\n,\n\\lVert\\mathbf{x}^{(t)}-\\mathbf{x}_{*}\\rVert\\leq M\\lVert\\mathbf{x}^{(0)}-\\mathbf{x}_{*}\\rVert\\left(\\rho_{\\alpha}+o(1)\\right)^{t},\nfor some constant\nM\n>\n0\nM>0\nand\nρ\nβ\n=\nmax\n1\n≤\ni\n≤\nm\n−\n1\n⁡\n|\n1\n−\nβ\n​\nλ\ni\n|\n1\n−\nβ\n​\nν\n.\n\\rho_{\\beta}=\\max_{1\\leq i\\leq m-1}\\frac{|1-\\beta\\lambda_{i}|}{1-\\beta\\nu}.\nAppendix D\nDetailed Summary Statistics\nTable\n5\ngives the number of nodes (\nn\nn\n), the number of directed edges (\n|\nℰ\n|\n|\\mathcal{E}|\n), the number of reciprocal edges (\n|\nℰ\nr\n|\n|\\mathcal{E}^{r}|\n) (self-loops are counted once and for\nu\n≠\nv\nu\\neq v\n, a reciprocal edge\nu\n→\nv\n,\nv\n→\nu\nu\\rightarrow v,v\\rightarrow u\nis counted twice) as well as their percentage among all edges, for the real-world networks, illustrating the variability in network size and density (defined as\n|\nℰ\n|\n/\n[\nn\n⁡\n(\nn\n−\n1\n)\n]\n{|\\mathcal{E}|}/[{n(n-1)}]\n).\nAs we do not have input features available, we use the eigengap of the Hermitian matrix\n(\n𝐀\n−\n𝐀\n⊤\n)\n⋅\ni\n,\n(\\mathbf{A}-\\mathbf{A}^{\\top})\\cdot i,\nwhere\n𝐀\n\\mathbf{A}\nis the adjacency matrix and\ni\ni\nthe imaginary unit, introduced in\n(\nCucuringu et al. 2020\n)\n, to determine the value\nK\n,\nK,\nwhich is assumed to be the number of clusters if we are solving a clustering problem.\nWe then stack the real and imaginary parts of the top\nK\nK\neigenvectors of\n(\n𝐀\n−\n𝐀\n⊤\n)\n⋅\ni\n(\\mathbf{A}-\\mathbf{A}^{\\top})\\cdot i\ninto\n2\n​\nK\n2K\n-dimensional input features for GNNs. We also report the embedding dimension\nd\nd\nused in the experiments, for each of the data sets.\nTable 5:\nSummary statistics\nfor the real-world networks.\nData\nn\nn\n|\nℰ\n|\n|\\mathcal{E}|\ndensity\n|\nℰ\nr\n|\n|\\mathcal{E}^{r}|\n|\nℰ\nr\n|\n|\nℰ\n|\n\\frac{|\\mathcal{E}^{r}|}{|\\mathcal{E}|}\n(%)\nK\nK\nd\nd\nHeadToHead\n602\n5010\n1.38e-02\n464\n9.26\n48\n32\nBasketball (1985)\n282\n2904\n3.66e-02\n998\n34.37\n20\n16\nBasketball finer (1985)\n282\n4814\n6.08e-02\n4814\n100.00\n20\n16\nBasketball (1986)\n283\n293