{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2203.07029v2.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 62126, "equations": [], "text": "SuperCone:\nUnified User Segmentation over Heterogeneous Experts via Concept Meta-learning\nKeqian Li\n†\n, Yifan Hu\n†\nYahoo Research\n†\nAbstract.\nWe study the problem of user segmentation: given a set of users and one or more predefined groups or segments, assign users to their corresponding segments.\nAs an example, for a segment indicating particular interest in a certain area of sports or entertainment,\nthe task will be to predict whether each single user will belong to the segment.\nHowever, there may exist numerous long tail prediction tasks that suffer from data availability and may be of heterogeneous nature,\nwhich make it hard to capture using single off the shelf model architectures.\nIn this work, we present\nSuperCone\n, our unified predicative segments system that addresses the above challenges.\nIt builds on top of a flat concept representation\n(\nli2021hadoop\n;\nli2022metacon\n)\nthat summarizes each user’s heterogeneous digital footprints,\nand uniformly models each of the prediction task using an approach called \"super learning \",\nthat is, combining prediction models with diverse architectures or learning method that are not compatible with each other.\nFollowing this, we provide an end to end approach that learns to flexibly attend to best suited heterogeneous experts adaptively,\nwhile at the same time incorporating deep representations of the input concepts that augments the above experts.\nExperiments show that\nSuperCone\nsignificantly outperform state-of-the-art recommendation and ranking algorithms on a wide range of predicative segment tasks and public structured data learning benchmarks.\nFigure 1\n.\nIllustration of\nSuperCone\nuse cases.\nFine-grained segments are provided to users for site customization and to\nonline advertisers for delivering effective content.\n1.\nIntroduction\nWe study the following problem:\ngiven a set of users and one or more predefined groups or segments, assign users to their corresponding segments based on the users’ available online activities and existing membership between users and segments.\nAs a long-standing problem that has been in online information exchange with significant social and economic impact\n(\nli2022\n)\n, it has played a pivotal role in supporting the continued growth of the platform that allow users to customize their content and for\nthe online advertisers to effectively budget their campaign.\n(\ncahill1997target\n)\n1\n1\n1\nhttps://www.facebook.com/business/ads/ad-targeting\n2\n2\n2\nhttps://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/2497941?hl=en\n.\nTypical user segmentation systems are constructed as follows. As illustrated in\nFigure 1\n, consumer and advertiser may log on to the system and indicate their preference of content, either in an explicit or implicit fashion. Large scale ranking and recommendation model\n(\nzhao2019recommending\n;\ntang2020progressive\n)\nmay follow that are designed for particular prediction tasks. However, existing approaches are not suitable for applying directly for the vast amount of possible prediction tasks because of the reasons:\n(1)\nTask Heterogeneity\n:\nDigital footprints such as users’ online activities may be logged and integrated from a wide range of contexts and physical machine types\nwith large variations in terms of modality and schema, making it hard for learning system to adapt.\n(2)\nLong-tailness\n: The fine granularity of segments that benefit information customization also results in\nextremely large number of prediction tasks,\nmany of which belongs to the\nlong tail of the distribution with\ninsufficient signal or observations.\n(3)\nData Availability\n:\nA large majority of the of available learning signals are\nimplicit feedback\n(\nhu2008collaborative\n)\nin nature and may suffer\nmissing at random\n(MAR) effect.\nIn addition, the increasing awareness of user privacy\nand compliance to regulation such as GDPR and platform constraints\nsuch as\nChromageddon\n(\nchromium\n)\n,\n(\nvoigt2017eu\n)\n(\niqbal2021towards\n)\nApp Tracking Transparency\n3\n3\n3\nhttps://developer.apple.com/documentation/apptrackingtransparency\nand\nIntelligent Tracking Prevention\nall contribute to the availability of data.\nAlgorithm that relies on rich and complete features will suffer from the decrease in efficiency or effectiveness.\nFigure 2\n.\nOverview of\nSuperCone\nunified user segmentation system.\nOnline activities are integrated from heterogeneous sources where unfolded representation for each prediction tasks are constructed.\nA unified learning paradigm is then applied across different types of tasks by training heterogeneous experts and learning to combine them adaptively.\nTo address the above challenges,\nwe present\nSuperCone\n,\nour unified approach for user segmentation that is able to apply to all prediction tasks in a consistent and reliable manner.\nIt builds the distributed concept representation\n(\nli2021automl\n;\nlake2015human\n;\nli2022metacon\n)\nin order to obtain reliable representation of signal from heterogeneous signals for each prediction task,\nand model each of the tasks\nby combining heterogeneous prediction models that varies in architectures or even learning method following the super learning paradigm\n(\nnaimi2018stacked\n)\n,\nwhile at the same time flexibly incorporating adaptive expert combination module\nand deep representations learning module from original input to augmenting the heterogeneous experts.\nWe then provide an end-to-end approach\nfor jointly learning the heterogeneous experts,\nthe expert combination module, and the representations learning module under a principled meta learning framework.\nOur contribution can be summarized as follows.\n•\nWe propose a unified solution to the critical problem of user segmentation,\nSuperCone\n, as an end-to-end solution that efficiently learns and combines arbitrary heterogeneous prediction models that are trained under a principled meta learning framework with provable performance advantages over other possible learning systems.\n•\nWe conduct extensive evaluations of\nSuperCone\nover numerous user segmentation task to demonstrate its substantial performance gain over the state-of-the-art recommendation and ranking approaches, the previous production system.\n•\nWe apply\nSuperCone\nto structured data learning problem and reports it superior performance on several public benchmark data-sets to further demonstrates the generalization of our approach.\n2.\nRelated Work\nIn this section,\nwe discuss key related work in relations to\nSuperCone\nfor the four following categories:\nindustrial ranking and recommendation system,\nconcept learning,\n, meta learning\nand super learning.\n2.1.\nIndustrial Ranking and Recommendation System\nIndustrial ranking and recommendation system are key to many aspects of internet business in areas such as including advertising\n(\nmcmahan2013ad\n)\nand content serving\n(\ncovington2016deep\n)\n,\nwith the majority of the approaches following the point wise learning-to-rank paradigm\n(\ncovington2016deep\n)\n.\nFor example, a ranking model may serve the resulted recommendation according to the predicted likelihood of engagement\n(\ntang2020progressive\n)\nsuch as satisfaction or end conversion.\nOur work differs from the above by following a novel super learning architecture to incorporate heterogeneous experts\nand apply it in the user segmentation scenario with low data availability and long tail tasks.\n2.2.\nConcept Learning\nThe research on concept learning\n(\nlake2015human\n)\nfocuses on mining concepts\nfrom heterogeneous sources such as\nrelational data and semi-structured data lake\n(\nwang2015concept\n)\nor\nfrom unstructured data such as text documents\n(\nli2019mining\n)\n.\nInstead of unsupervised mining,\n(\nli2018unsupervised\n;\nli2014social\n;\nli2018concept\n;\nli2019hiercon\n;\nli2017discovering\n)\n,\nmore recent work focuses specifically on obtaining reliable flat concept representation\nfrom heterogeneous information such as user’s implicit feedback\n(\nli2021automl\n)\nfor large scale distributed learning scenarios.\nOur approach builds on top of previous work and\nfurther learns the interplay across downstream learners following the super learning framework.\n2.3.\nEnsemble Learning\nThe research on ensemble learning\n(\ndong2020survey\n)\nfocuses on leveraging multiple machine learning models, commonly referred to as \"experts\".\nA particular branch known as \"super learning\" in statistics\n(\nnaimi2018stacked\n)\nfocuses on training machine learning model such as a regression model for combining the predictions of individual \"experts\" into the final prediction.\nOur method bring a couple of theoretical and practical advancement in this area,\nincluding a much more generalized scheme for combining the individual models with provable optimality guarantee and a novel architecture for deep representation learning over \"experts\".\n2.4.\nMeta Learning\nThe research of meta learning, also known as \"learning to learning\", focuses on\nthe learning mechanism that gains experience and improves its performance over multiple learning episodes\n(\nthrun1998learning\n)\n.\nOne of the most general class of all is architectural search,\nwhere multiple instantiation of the model are learned jointly, with most performing ones being kept\nand unfitted ones being discarded\n(\nliu2018darts\n;\nreal2019regularized\n)\n.\nOur work apply meta learning in the context of optimizing the learning based on heterogeneous experts in building unified user segmentation systems.\n3.\nProblem Overview\nAs illustrated in\nFigure 2\n,\nour unified system of user segmentation will ingest items of interests from a variety of domains such as Hosted Content, Mobile, Advertisement and Finance\nwith a diverse range of knowledge enrichment, resulting in a heterogeneous information network of users and events, and existing segments.\neach with their schema, modality and patterns of interconnection.\nThe first step of our pipeline is to perform data integration to construct a set of\nunfolded concept\nwhere\nwhere different types of interconnections\nbetween entities in the information network\nare serialized as an atomic concept\n(\nli2021automl\n)\n, following the\nHadoop-MTA\n(\nli2021automl\n)\napproach.\nFormally, in order to predict a particular segment, let\n𝒮\n\\mathcal{S}\nbe the set of users (i.e. entity in\n(\nli2021automl\n)\n) that we predict the segment for\nand\n𝒴\n\\mathcal{Y}\nbe the set of possible labels.\nWe represent the resulting\nunfolded concepts\nas a real-valued concept vector\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\n(\\vec{c_{s}})\nfor each user\ns\ns\n,\nwith index being the list of concept vocabulary\n𝒞\n\\mathcal{C}\nand value being the intensity of its association to corresponding concepts.\nFor clarity, we first describe the scenario for learning with homogeneous expert.\nSpecifically, we assume a particular expert\nh\nj\nh_{j}\nassociated with a hypothesis space\nH\nj\n⊆\nℝ\n𝒞\n→\n𝒴\nH_{j}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{C}}\\to\\mathcal{Y}\n.\nWe abstract the algorithm for training the expert and assume an efficient oracle\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n𝒟\n)\n\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega;\\mathcal{D})\nfor obtaining the trained experts\nbased on a given dataset\n𝒟\n\\mathcal{D}\nand meta-parameter\nω\n∈\nΩ\n\\omega\\in\\Omega\nthat controls how the models are learned, such as model hyperparameters\n(\nfranceschi2018bilevel\n)\n.\n:\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n𝒟\n)\n≜\narg\n⁡\nmin\nθ\nj\n∈\nΘ\nj\n​\nR\nj\n𝒟\n​\n(\nh\nj\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\nj\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega;\\mathcal{D})\\triangleq\\arg\\min_{\\theta_{j}\\in\\Theta_{j}}R^{\\mathcal{D}}_{j}(h_{j}(\\cdot;\\theta_{j}))\n(1)\n=\n∑\ns\n∈\n𝒟\n𝐋\nj\n​\n(\nh\nj\n​\n(\nc\ns\n→\n,\nθ\nj\n)\n,\n𝐲\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{D}}\\mathbf{L}_{j}(h_{j}(\\vec{c_{s}};\\theta_{j}),\\mathbf{y}(s))\nHere\nθ\nj\n\\theta_{j}\nis the set of learn-able parameters contained in the parameter space\nΘ\nj\n\\Theta_{j}\n,\nand\n𝐋\nj\n\\mathbf{L}_{j}\nis the loss used for training\nh\nj\nh_{j}\n, e.g. the loss function used for back-propagation.\nThe task of unfolded concept learning task\nwith\nhomogeneous\nexpert will then utilize one such oracle.\nIt can be stated as follows\nDefinition 1 (Unfolded Concept Learning With Homogeneous Expert).\nAssuming the label function of interest\n𝐲\n:\n𝒮\n→\n𝒴\n\\mathbf{y}:\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{Y}\nmapping each user to a label in\n𝒴\n\\mathcal{Y}\n, a probability density of the entity\n𝐪\n:\n𝒮\n→\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n\\mathbf{q}:\\mathcal{S}\\to[0,1]\n, and a sampled dataset\n𝒟\n\\mathcal{D}\n,\nthe task is to learn a model\n𝐡\nj\n∈\nH\nj\n\\mathbf{h}_{j}\\in H_{j}\n,\nthat minimize the expected risk\naccording to a given criterion\n𝐋\n\\mathbf{L}\nminimize\n𝜔\n​\nR\n​\n(\nh\nj\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\n⋅\n,\nω\n)\n)\n≜\n𝔼\n𝐪\n​\n[\n𝐋\n⁡\n(\nh\nj\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n𝒟\n)\n)\n,\n𝐲\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n)\n]\n\\displaystyle\\underset{\\omega}{\\text{minimize }}R(h_{j}(\\cdot;\\cdot,\\omega))\\triangleq\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbf{q}}[\\mathbf{L}(h_{j}(\\cdot;\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega;\\mathcal{D})),\\mathbf{y}(s))]\n=\n∫\n𝒮\n𝐋\n⁡\n(\nh\nj\n​\n(\nc\ns\n→\n,\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n𝒟\n)\n)\n,\n𝐲\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n)\n​\n𝐪\n​\n(\ns\n)\n​\n𝑑\ns\n\\displaystyle=\\int_{\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbf{L}(h_{j}(\\vec{c_{s}};\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega;\\mathcal{D})),\\mathbf{y}(s))\\mathbf{q}(s)ds\nwhere\nθ\ni\n∈\nΘ\ni\n\\theta_{i}\\in\\Theta_{i}\ndenotes the task specific parameter\nand\nω\n∈\nΩ\n\\omega\\in\\Omega\ndenotes the meta-parameter.\nFigure 3\n.\nIllustration of architecture of the meta-module of\nSuperCone\n.\nHeterogeneous experts are interconnected and reapplied recursively, where all intermediate outputs are allowed to participate in the final prediction, which is gated by the expert combination network.\nA neural network for learning the complementary expert are learned in parallel to the heterogeneous experts to allow sharing and complementing the expertise.\nWe start the formalization of the user segmentation problem by considering the meta-learning problem in a more general setting.\nAssuming a distribution over tasks\n𝐩\n𝕋\n:\n𝕋\n→\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n\\mathbf{p}_{\\mathbb{T}}:\\mathbb{T}\\to[0,1]\n,\nwe first assume a source (i.e. meta training) dataset of\nM\nM\ntasks sampled from\n𝕋\n\\mathbb{T}\n,\neach containing a training set\n(i.e. support set in meta learning literature\n(\nbechtle2021meta\n)\n) and\nvalidation set (query set in meta learning literature\n(\nbechtle2021meta\n)\n) with\nnon-overlapping i.i.d. samples\ndrawn from instances distribution\n𝐪\nj\n\\mathbf{q}_{j}\nof task\n𝐓\nj\n\\mathbf{T}_{j}\n, as\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\n≜\n{\n(\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nj\n)\n,\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nv\n​\na\n​\nl\n​\n(\nj\n)\n}\nj\n=\n1\nM\n\\mathscr{D}_{source}\\triangleq\\{(\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{source},\\mathcal{D}^{val~(j)}_{source}\\}_{j=1}^{M}\n.\nLikewise, we assume a target dataset (i.e. meta test) of\nQ\nQ\ntasks sampled from\n𝕋\n\\mathbb{T}\n,\neach containing a training set\n(i.e. support set) and\ntest set (query set) with\nnon-overlapping i.i.d. samples\ndrawn from instances distribution\n𝐪\nj\n\\mathbf{q}_{j}\nof task\n𝐓\nj\n\\mathbf{T}_{j}\n, as\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\n≜\n{\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nj\n)\n,\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\nt\n​\ne\n​\ns\n​\nt\n​\n(\nj\n)\n}\nj\n=\n1\nQ\n\\mathscr{D}_{target}\\triangleq\\{\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{target},\\mathcal{D}^{test~(j)}_{target}\\}_{j=1}^{Q}\n.\nThe goal is to obtain the “meta knowledge” in the form of\nω\n\\omega\nfrom\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\n\\mathscr{D}_{source}\nwhich will then be applied to improve downstream performance in\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\n\\mathscr{D}_{target}\n,\nby fine-tuning on each individual training set at meta-test time.\nFor the task of learning with heterogeneous experts, however, we focus on the scenario where source and target set are not separate.\nSpecifically,\nwe only require one dataset\n𝒟\n\\mathcal{D}\nto serve as the source dataset, for meta-training,\nand one target dataset, for meta-test.\nWe assume each of task\nj\nj\n,\nj\n=\n1\n​\n…\n​\nJ\nj=1\\ldots J\n, where\nthe only difference between tasks is the\nparticular expert\nh\nj\nh_{j}\n,\neach associated with a hypothesis space\nH\nj\n⊆\nℝ\n𝒞\n→\n𝒴\nH_{j}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{C}}\\to\\mathcal{Y}\n, a set of learn-able parameter\nθ\nj\n∈\nΘ\nj\n\\theta_{j}\\in\\Theta_{j}\n, and a training oracle\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n𝒟\n)\n\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega;\\mathcal{D})\nsatisfying\nEquation 1\n. The end goal of meta training, then, is to obtain optimal generalization error on the single test target set.\nFormally, we assume\nall the available instances will be used for both the source and target set.\nGiven a sample of data\n𝒟\n≜\n{\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n,\n𝒟\nt\n​\ne\n​\ns\n​\nt\n}\n\\mathscr{D}\\triangleq\\{\\mathcal{D}^{train},\\mathcal{D}^{test}\\}\ndrawn i.i.d from the instance distribution\n𝐪\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n\\mathbf{q}(s)\n.\nWe will use some or all of the instances from\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n\\mathcal{D}^{train}\nfor training the individual experts\nh\nj\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\nj\n,\nω\n)\nh_{j}(\\cdot;\\theta_{j},\\omega)\n, i.e.\n(\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nj\n)\n⊆\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n,\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nv\n​\na\n​\nl\n​\n(\nj\n)\n⊆\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\nCLOSE\n(\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{source}\\subseteq\\mathcal{D}^{train},\\mathcal{D}^{val~(j)}_{source}\\subseteq\\mathcal{D}^{train}\n,\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nj\n)\n∩\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nv\n​\na\n​\nl\n​\n(\nj\n)\n=\n∅\n\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{source}\\cap\\mathcal{D}^{val~(j)}_{source}=\\emptyset\n.\nLikewise, the dataset used for meta-test consume some or all of the training instances, i.e.,\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nj\n)\n⊆\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n,\nj\n=\n1\n​\n…\n​\nJ\n\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{target}\\subseteq\\mathcal{D}^{train},j=1\\ldots J\n.\nThe final goal is to learn a joint model based on the adapted experts on the target training set,\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n;\n(\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nj\n)\n)\nCLOSE\n\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega;(\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{target})\nfor\nj\n=\n1\n​\n…\n​\nJ\nj=1\\ldots J\n, denoted as\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nω\n,\n{\nh\nj\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nj\n)\n)\n)\n}\n)\nh(\\cdot;\\omega,\\{h_{j}(\\cdot;\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega;\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{target}))\\})\n,\nthat achieves the best generalization error.\nDefinition 2 (Unfolded Concept Learning With Heterogeneous Experts).\nAssuming the label function of interest\n𝐲\n:\n𝒮\n→\n𝒴\n\\mathbf{y}:\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{Y}\n, a\na sampled dataset\n𝒟\n\\mathscr{D}\n, a set of heterogeneous experts\nh\nj\nh_{j}\nwith inner training oracle\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n𝒟\n)\n\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega,\\mathcal{D})\nfor\nj\n=\n1\n​\n…\n​\nJ\nj=1\\ldots J\n,\nthe task is to learn a combined model\nh\nh\nthat minimize a given loss criteria\n𝐋\n:\n𝒴\n×\n𝒴\n→\nℝ\n\\mathbf{L}:\\mathcal{Y}\\times\\mathcal{Y}\\to\\mathbb{R}\nminimize\n​\nR\n{\nH\nj\n}\nj\n=\n1\nJ\n,\nΩ\n𝒟\nt\n​\ne\n​\ns\n​\nt\n≜\n\\displaystyle\\underset{}{\\text{minimize }}R^{\\mathcal{D}^{test}}_{\\{H_{j}\\}^{J}_{j=1},\\Omega}\\triangleq\n=\nR\n𝒟\nt\n​\ne\n​\ns\n​\nt\n​\n(\nh\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n,\nω\n∗\n,\n{\nh\nj\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n∗\n,\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nj\n)\n)\n)\n}\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=R^{\\mathcal{D}^{test}}(h(\\cdot;\\omega^{*},\\{h_{j}(\\cdot;\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega^{*};\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{target}))\\}))\n(2)\n=\n∑\ns\n∈\n𝒟\nt\n​\ne\n​\ns\n​\nt\n𝐋\n⁡\n(\n(\n,\n,\n,\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{D}^{test}}\\mathbf{L}((h(\\vec{c_{s}};\\omega^{*},\\{h_{j}(\\cdot;\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega^{*};\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{target}))\\}),\\mathbf{y}(s)))\n(3)\ns.t.\nω\n∗\n=\narg\n⁡\nmin\nω\n​\n𝐋\nm\n​\ne\n​\nt\n​\na\n​\n(\n{\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n⋅\n)\n|\nj\n=\n1\n​\n…\n​\nJ\n}\n,\nω\n,\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n)\n\\displaystyle\\omega^{*}=\\arg\\min_{\\omega}\\mathbf{L}^{meta}(\\{\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega,\\cdot)|j=1\\ldots J\\},\\omega,\\mathcal{D}^{train})\nwhere\n𝐋\nm\n​\ne\n​\nt\n​\na\n\\mathbf{L}^{meta}\nis a meta loss to be specified by the meta-training procedure, such as the cross entropy error of temporal difference error\n(\nfinn2017model\n)\n.\nThe problem of unfolded concept learning with heterogeneous experts\nextends unfolded concept learning for efficient and scalable distributed AutoML\nand retains the representation power. By considering its relation to unfolded concept learning problem\n(\nli2021automl\n)\n, we have the following problem complexity results\nTheorem 1.\nThe above problem of Unfolded Concept Learning With Heterogeneous Experts is no less difficult than\nthe\nLearning In Heterogeneous Data Problem (Definition 1 in\n(\nli2021automl\n)\n),\nLearning In Relational Database (Definition 2 in\n(\nli2021automl\n)\n),\nHeterogeneous Graph Learning (Definition 3 in\n(\nli2021automl\n)\n), and\nFirst order Logic Graph Learning (Definition 4 in\n(\nli2021automl\n)\n).\nIn fact, there exists efficient linear time reduction from\nLearning In Heterogeneous Data Problem,\nLearning In Relational Database,\nHeterogeneous Graph Learning, and\nFirst order Logic Graph Learning to Unfolded Concept Learning With Heterogeneous Experts problem.\nFigure 4\n.\nIllustration of meta optimization procedure\n4.\nChoice of\nΩ\n\\Omega\nWe divide our discussion of our approach into two parts, the representation of the meta module and the optimization procedure.\nIn this section, we focus on the first part of the meta parameter space\nΩ\n\\Omega\n, for any given choices of\nΘ\nj\n\\Theta_{j}\nof each individual experts\nH\nj\nH_{j}\n.\nThe solution space induced by meta parameter\nω\n\\omega\nbrings inductive bias to the downstream tasks and affect the efficiency of learning procedure of each task.\nThere are several key challenges for the task of model building in critical user segmentation use cases\n•\nTask Agnostic Expertise Modeling\nThe choice of\nΩ\n\\Omega\nshould allow flexibly modeling over a large variety of tasks types\nand best utilizing the power of experts from\nℋ\n=\n{\nH\nj\n|\nj\n=\n1\n​\n…\n​\nJ\n}\n\\mathcal{H}=\\{H_{j}|j=1\\ldots J\\}\nin an adaptive without task-specific engineering.\n•\nRepresentation Power\nThe choice of\nΩ\n\\Omega\nshould contain enough representation capacity for inducing deep representation of data and not\nnot limit itself to specific features or classes of functions\n.\n•\nFirst Order Influence\nThe influence of meta parameter\nω\n\\omega\nover the learning mechanism should allow for efficient meta-optimization for performance critical application, and\nnot incurring to higher order gradient computation\n(\nfinn2017model\n)\nduring the learning of\nω\n\\omega\n.\nPrevious approaches mostly fall into the following categories:\ntraditional super learning and ensemble learning approaches\n(\npolley2010super\n)\nare heuristic in nature and fail to meet the second criteria;\ntraditional deep learning approaches\n(\nzhao2019recommending\n)\nand fails the first one by not incorporating the power of heterogeneous experts;\nthe majority of the existing meta-learning approaches relies on higher order and bi-level optimization\n(\nfinn2017model\n)\n(\nfranceschi2018bilevel\n)\nand are disqualified by the third criteria.\nTo address this, we present the\nSuperAug\nmeta-learning architecture that constructs a large portfolio of augmented experts and learns deep representation for both direct prediction from unfolded concepts and indirect combination of heterogeneous experts,\nwhile at the same time respecting their individual prediction power and expertise interpret-ability. It consists of the following 3 main components.\nHeterogeneous Experts Sluice Network\nUpon a given set of heterogeneous experts, we aim to construct a augmented set of experts\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\nby\nby enumerating nested combinations\namong the experts.\nSpecifically, the space of experts\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\nfollows the given rules:\n(1)\nAny expert model with hypothesis space\nH\nH\nbelonging to initial experts\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\nwill also belong to\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\n(2)\nAny arithmetic combination between an arbitrary number of experts in\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\nwill also belong to\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\n(3)\nAny recursive application of an expert with hypothesis\nH\nH\nbelong to\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\nover an arbitrary number of outputs from models from\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\nwill also belong to\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\nThe Expert Expansion is implemented in a heterogeneous expert network in\nSuperAug\nfollowing the sluice network architecture\n(\nruder2019latent\n)\n, with an additional layer by layer skip connections.\nAs shown in\nFigure 3\n, the output at each level of densely connected experts\nσ\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\sigma(\\cdot)\nwill be fed to both the immediately next level as input as well as future levels, and the subsequent connected layers henceforth.\nComplementary Expert\nTo further augment the model capacity and obtain deep representation of the data,\nwe incorporate a complementary expert module with hypothesis\nH\nC\n​\no\n​\nm\n​\np\nH_{Comp}\nthat allows flexible modulation of information flow while respecting the simplicity of network design.\nTo that end,\nwe follow the neural multi-mixture of experts architecture that learns an ensemble of individual experts in an end to end fashion\n(\njacobs1991adaptive\n;\nma2018modeling\n)\n.\nSpecifically, we divide the neural net into the following:\nthe end output module\n𝐓𝐨𝐰𝐞𝐫\n\\mathbf{Tower}\nfor producing the output for the particular task;\nthe inner expert neural submodules\n𝐈𝐧𝐧𝐞𝐫𝐄𝐱𝐩𝐞𝐫𝐭\nt\n\\mathbf{InnerExpert}_{t}\n,\n1\n≤\nt\n≤\nE\n1\\leq t\\leq E\n;\nand the gating network\n𝐆𝐚𝐭𝐞\ni\n\\mathbf{Gate}_{i}\nthat projects the input into\nℝ\nE\n\\mathbb{R}^{E}\ndirectly from the original data representation\nc\ns\n→\n\\vec{c_{s}}\n.\nThe prediction of the final complementary expert that map concept vector representation\nc\ns\n→\n\\vec{c_{s}}\ninto label space\n𝒴\n\\mathcal{Y}\n,\nH\nC\n​\no\n​\nm\n​\np\n​\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\nH_{Comp}(\\vec{c_{s}})\n,\ncan then be expressed as\n(4)\nh\na\n​\nl\n​\nt\n​\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\n\\displaystyle h_{alt}(\\vec{c_{s}})\n=\n𝐓𝐨𝐰𝐞𝐫\n⁡\n(\nv\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{Tower}(v_{s})\n(5)\nv\ns\n\\displaystyle v_{s}\n=\n∑\nt\nE\n(\nsoftmax\n​\n(\n𝐆𝐚𝐭𝐞\n⁡\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\n)\n(\nt\n)\n⋅\n𝐈𝐧𝐧𝐞𝐫𝐄𝐱𝐩𝐞𝐫𝐭\nt\n​\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{t}^{E}\\Big(\\textrm{softmax}(\\mathbf{Gate}(\\vec{c_{s}}))_{(t)}\\cdot\\mathbf{InnerExpert}_{t}(\\vec{c_{s}})\\Big)\nHere the intermediate representation\nv\ns\nv_{s}\nis a weighted sum by a shallow network\n𝐆𝐚𝐭𝐞\ni\n​\n(\nc\ns\nm\n​\ne\n​\nt\n​\na\n→\n)\n\\mathbf{Gate}_{i}(\\vec{c_{s}^{meta}})\nafter normalizing into unit simplex via\nsoftmax\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\textrm{softmax}(\\cdot)\n.\nEach\n𝐈𝐧𝐧𝐞𝐫𝐄𝐱𝐩𝐞𝐫𝐭\nt\n\\mathbf{InnerExpert}_{t}\n, will, in turn, be an ensemble of submodules mapping\nc\ns\n→\n\\vec{c_{s}}\nto a fixed-length vector.\n(6)\n𝐈𝐧𝐧𝐞𝐫𝐄𝐱𝐩𝐞𝐫𝐭\nt\n​\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf{InnerExpert}_{t}(\\vec{c_{s}})\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n0\nL\n𝐃𝐞𝐩𝐭𝐡\nt\n,\ni\n​\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=0}^{L}\\mathbf{Depth}_{t,i}(\\vec{c_{s}})\n(7)\n𝐃𝐞𝐩𝐭𝐡\nt\n,\ni\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf{Depth}_{t,i}\n=\n𝐏𝐫𝐨𝐣\nt\n,\ni\n​\n(\n𝐏𝐫𝐨𝐣\nt\n,\ni\n−\n1\n​\n(\n…\n​\n(\n𝐄𝐦𝐛𝐞𝐝\n⁡\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\n​\n…\n)\n)\nCLOSE\n\\displaystyle=\\mathbf{Proj}_{t,i}\\Big(\\mathbf{Proj}_{t,i-1}\\Big(\\ldots(\\mathbf{Embed}\\Big(\\vec{c_{s}}\\Big)\\ldots\\Big)\\Big)\nHere\n𝐃𝐞𝐩𝐭𝐡\nt\n,\ni\n\\mathbf{Depth}_{t,i}\ndenotes an intermediate output for the inner expert\nt\nt\nat depth\ni\ni\n, consisting of projection in the form of\n𝐏𝐫𝐨𝐣\nt\n,\ni\n\\mathbf{Proj}_{t,i}\n, implemented as a linear layer followed by a\nr\n​\ne\n​\nl\n​\nu\nrelu\nactivation.\nAs illustrated in the alternative expert component in\nFigure 3\n, an ensemble of neural experts will first be combined to form a deep representation from the concept vector, and further be combined with the rest of heterogeneous experts.\nExpert Combination\nOne distinctive advantage of\nSuperAug\nover traditional ensemble approaches is the ability to adaptively weigh-in different predictions across experts adaptively.\nTo that end, we follow the DARTS meta learning\n(\nliu2018darts\n)\ndesign for building the expert combination module.\nHere assuming the experts from\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\nare arranged as a array of mappings\n{\nh\n1\n,\nh\n2\n,\n…\n,\nh\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n}\n\\{h_{1},h_{2},\\ldots,h_{\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}}\\}\n,\nthe combination network component\n𝐂𝐨𝐦𝐛\n⁡\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\mathbf{Comb}(\\cdot)\n,\nwill map the concept vector\nc\ns\n→\n\\vec{c_{s}}\ninto a\n|\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n|\n+\n1\n|\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}|+1\ndimension vector.\nThe final model prediction,\nh\n⁡\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\nh(\\vec{c_{s}})\n, is then produced using another layer of weighted sums over all possible experts\n(8)\nh\n⁡\n(\nc\n→\ns\n)\n=\n∑\nt\n∈\n{\n1\n,\n2\n,\n…\n,\nT\n}\n​\n⋃\n{\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n}\n(\nsoftmax\n​\n(\n𝐂𝐨𝐦𝐛\n⁡\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\n)\n(\nt\n)\n⋅\nh\nt\n​\n(\nc\ns\n→\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle h(\\vec{c}_{s})=\\sum_{t\\in\\{1,2,\\ldots,T\\}\\bigcup\\{Aug\\}}\\Big(\\textrm{softmax}(\\mathbf{Comb}(\\vec{c_{s}}))_{(t)}\\cdot h_{t}(\\vec{c_{s}})\\Big)\n5.\nmeta optimization\nIn this section, we describe the approach for optimizing the meta-parameters\nω\n\\omega\n, that are agnostic to the heterogeneous experts in\nℋ\n\\mathcal{H}\n.\nNaive approach that directly treats the original input dataset\n𝒟\n\\mathscr{D}\nto compute the meta loss\n𝐋\nm\n​\ne\n​\nt\n​\na\n\\mathbf{L}^{meta}\nor using it as the support set\n(\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nj\n)\nCLOSE\n(\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{source}\nmight lead to \"meta-overfit\" where the combination network and the added experts from\nℋ\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n\\mathcal{H}_{Aug}\nfalsely rely on overfitted experts.\nIn contrast, we propose a principled framework to construct a meta-training set that eliminates the phenomenon and achieves generalization with provable guarantee.\nThe high level intuition is to\nextract non-overlapping subset of the data as the support and query set as the source data meta-training to minimize the discrepancy between meta-training and deployment.\nOur optimization method makes no assumption about the heterogeneous experts, including the existence of gradients in its learning process.\nThe optimization is shown in\nFigure 4\n, where each level of heterogeneous experts is trained recursively on previous levels with its own meta-training set based on the cross-validation split,\nwith the final level corresponding to the\nSuperAug\narchitecture.\nSpecifically, we can index heterogeneous experts by the depth it depends on other experts, with\nh\nj\n(\nk\n)\nh^{(k)}_{j}\ndenoting the\nj\nj\nth expert at\nk\nk\nth layer,\nk\n=\n1\n,\n2\n,\n…\n,\nK\nk=1,2,\\ldots,K\n.\nAt each depth, we have a cross validation scheme,\nV\n(\nk\n)\nV^{(k)}\nmapping instance\ns\ns\nfrom\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n\\mathcal{D}^{train}\nto a fold among\n1\n,\n2\n,\n…\n,\nV\n1,2,\\ldots,V\n, the learning proceed by creating higher-order meta training dataset at each\nk\nk\nth layer,\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n)\n\\mathcal{D}^{train~(k)}\nas\n(9)\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{D}^{train~(k)}\n≜\n{\n(\nx\ns\n(\nk\n)\n→\n,\nz\ns\n(\nk\n)\n→\n|\nx\ns\n→\n∈\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n)\n}\n\\displaystyle\\triangleq\\{(\\vec{x^{(k)}_{s}},\\vec{z^{(k)}_{s}}|\\vec{x_{s}}\\in\\mathcal{D}^{train~(k)}\\}\n(10)\nz\ns\n(\nk\n)\n→\n(\nj\n)\n\\displaystyle\\vec{z^{(k)}_{s}}_{(j)}\n≜\nh\nj\n(\nk\n)\n​\n(\nx\ns\n→\n,\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n(\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n−\n1\n)\n)\n∼\ns\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle\\triangleq h^{(k)}_{j}(\\vec{x_{s}};\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega,(\\mathcal{D}^{train~(k-1)})^{\\sim s}))\nwith\n(\nD\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n)\n)\n∼\ns\n({D}^{train~(k)})^{\\sim s}\ndenoting the subset of\n(\nD\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n)\n)\n({D}^{train~(k)})\nnot in the same fold as instance\ni\ni\n, formally\n(11)\n(\nD\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n)\n)\n∼\ns\n≜\n{\nV\n(\nk\n)\n​\n(\ns\n)\n≠\nV\n(\nk\n)\n​\n(\ns\n′\n)\n|\nx\ns\n′\n→\n∈\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n)\n}\n\\displaystyle({D}^{train~(k)})^{\\sim s}\\triangleq\\{V^{(k)}(s)\\neq V^{(k)}(s^{\\prime})|\\vec{x_{s^{\\prime}}}\\in\\mathcal{D}^{train~(k)}\\}\nAnd the meta-parameter\nω\n\\omega\nis trained using the last layer of the constructed meta-training dataset\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n≜\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nK\n)\n\\mathcal{D}^{train}_{source}\\triangleq\\mathcal{D}^{train~(K)}\n,\nwith respect to the meta loss defined as follows\n(12)\n𝐋\nm\n​\ne\n​\nt\n​\na\n​\n(\n{\nθ\nj\n∗\n​\n(\nω\n,\n⋅\n)\n|\nj\n=\n1\n​\n…\n​\nJ\n}\n,\nω\n,\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n)\n≜\n∑\nx\ns\n→\n∈\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n𝐋\n⁡\n(\nh\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nx\ns\n→\n)\n,\n𝐲\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf{L}^{meta}(\\{\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega,\\cdot)|j=1\\ldots J\\},\\omega,\\mathcal{D}^{train})\\triangleq\\sum_{\\vec{x_{s}}\\in\\mathcal{D}^{train}_{source}}\\mathbf{L}\\Big(h^{train}(\\vec{x_{s}}),\\mathbf{y}(s)\\Big)\nwith the meta-training time model\nh\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nx\ns\n→\n)\nh^{train}(\\vec{x_{s}})\ndefined by replacing the output of all heterogeneous experts directly by taking all but the first\n|\n𝒞\n|\n|\\mathcal{C}|\nelements from the input,\nx\ns\n→\n[\n:\n|\n𝒞\n|\n]\n\\vec{x_{s}}_{[:|\\mathcal{C}|]}\nand feeding the alternative expert and the combination network with the original feature,\nx\ns\n→\n[\n:\n|\n𝒞\n|\n]\n\\vec{x_{s}}_{[:|\\mathcal{C}|]}\n. Formally,\nh\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nx\n→\n)\n\\displaystyle h^{train}(\\vec{x})\n≜\n∑\nt\n∈\n{\n1\n,\n2\n,\n…\n,\nT\n}\n​\n⋃\n{\nA\n​\nu\n​\ng\n}\nv\ns\nt\n\\displaystyle\\triangleq\\sum_{t\\in\\{1,2,\\ldots,T\\}\\bigcup\\{Aug\\}}v_{s}^{t}\nv\ns\nt\n\\displaystyle v_{s}^{t}\n≜\n(\nsoftmax\n(\n𝐂𝐨𝐦𝐛\n(\nx\ns\n→\n[\n:\n|\n𝒞\n|\n]\n)\n)\n(\nt\n)\n⋅\n(\nh\na\n​\nl\n​\nt\n(\nx\ns\n→\n[\n:\n|\n𝒞\n|\n]\n)\n,\nx\ns\n→\n[\n|\n𝒞\n|\n:\n]\n)\n(\nt\n)\n\\displaystyle\\triangleq\\Big(\\textrm{softmax}(\\mathbf{Comb}(\\vec{x_{s}}_{[:|\\mathcal{C}|]})\\Big)_{(t)}\\cdot\\Big(h_{alt}(\\vec{x_{s}}_{[:|\\mathcal{C}|]}),\\vec{x_{s}}_{[|\\mathcal{C}|:]}\\Big)_{(t)}\nThe learning of the network parameter thus become an end-to-end optimization problem which can be solved using efficient gradient based methods\n(\nliu2018darts\n)\n.\nFinally, at meta-test time, the source set for each of the heterogeneous experts\nh\nj\n(\nk\n)\nh^{(k)}_{j}\n,\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n,\nj\n)\n\\mathcal{D}^{train~(k,j)}_{target}\nis defined as the\nk\nk\n-th high order meta training dataset, i.e.\n𝒟\nt\n​\na\n​\nr\n​\ng\n​\ne\n​\nt\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n,\nj\n)\n≜\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n​\n(\nk\n)\n\\mathcal{D}^{train~(k,j)}_{target}\\triangleq\\mathcal{D}^{train~(k)}\n.\nWe also have the following results regarding the model’s asymptotic and finite sample generalization error over arbitrary heterogeneous expert or the meta learning architecture.\nTheorem 2.\nAssume\n𝒴\n\\mathcal{Y}\nwith bounded cardinality, for any prediction model\n𝐲\n′\n\\mathbf{y}^{\\prime}\n, there exists an parameter space of SuperAug\nΩ\n\\Omega\nwith the same or less generalization error on instance distribution\n𝐪\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n\\mathbf{q}(s)\nfor every instantiation of the data\n𝒟\n\\mathscr{D}\nin an asymptotic sense.\nMoreover, for float-point based implementation of meta-parameter\nω\n\\omega\n, and\nK\n=\n1\nK=1\n, then its generalization error will converge to 0 or to the best prediction model under a\nO\n⁡\n(\nl\n​\no\n​\ng\n​\nn\nn\n)\nO(\\frac{logn}{n})\nrate.\nProof\nWe start with the case of asymptotic generalization error.\nConsider an arbitrary prediction model\nh\n1\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\nh_{1}(\\cdot)\nwith a learning oracle\nθ\n1\n∗\n​\n(\n𝒟\n)\n\\theta^{*}_{1}(\\mathcal{D})\n, we construct the following\nSuperAug\narchitecture with a series of heterogeneous experts including\nh\n1\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\nh_{1}(\\cdot)\n,\nW.L.O.G. we assume it is the first expert with index 1, since the\nSuperAug\narchitecture will further optimize the training time error compared to its input, with probability at least\n1\n−\nδ\n1-\\delta\nwe have\n∫\ns\n∈\n𝒮\n𝐋\n⁡\n(\n(\n,\n,\n,\n)\n)\n​\n𝐪\n​\n(\ns\n)\n\\displaystyle\\int_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbf{L}((h(\\vec{c_{s}};\\omega^{*},\\{h_{j}(\\cdot;\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega^{*};\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{target}))\\}),\\mathbf{y}(s)))\\mathbf{q}(s)\n≤\n\\displaystyle\\leq\n∑\ns\n∈\n𝒟\ns\n​\no\n​\nu\n​\nr\n​\nc\n​\ne\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n𝐋\n⁡\n(\n(\n,\n,\n,\n)\n)\n+\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{D}^{train}_{source}}\\mathbf{L}((h(\\vec{c_{s}};\\omega^{*},\\{h_{j}(\\cdot;\\theta^{*}_{j}(\\omega^{*};\\mathcal{D}^{train~(j)}_{target}))\\}),\\mathbf{y}(s)))+\n𝒪\n⁡\n(\nOPEN\nC\n1\n​\nl\n​\no\n​\ng\n​\n|\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n|\n+\nC\n2\n+\nl\n​\no\n​\ng\n​\n1\n/\nδ\n|\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n|\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{O}\\bigg(\\sqrt{\\frac{C_{1}log|\\mathcal{D}^{train}|+C_{2}+log1/\\delta}{|\\mathcal{D}^{train}|})}\\bigg)\n≤\n\\displaystyle\\leq\n∫\ns\n∈\n𝒮\n𝐋\n⁡\n(\nh\n1\n​\n(\n⋅\n,\nθ\n1\n∗\n​\n(\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n)\n)\n,\n𝐲\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n)\n​\n𝐪\n​\n(\ns\n)\n+\n\\displaystyle\\int_{s\\in\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbf{L}(h_{1}(\\cdot;\\theta^{*}_{1}(\\mathcal{D}^{train})),\\mathbf{y}(s))\\mathbf{q}(s)+\n𝒪\n⁡\n(\nOPEN\nC\n1\n​\nl\n​\no\n​\ng\n​\n|\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n|\n+\nC\n2\n+\nl\n​\no\n​\ng\n​\n1\n/\nδ\n|\n𝒟\nt\n​\nr\n​\na\n​\ni\n​\nn\n|\n)\n)\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{O}\\bigg(\\sqrt{\\frac{C_{1}log|\\mathcal{D}^{train}|+C_{2}+log1/\\delta}{|\\mathcal{D}^{train}|})}\\bigg)\nwhere the first and second in-equality is established with\n(\nvapnik1999nature\n)\nand\nC\n1\nC_{1}\n,\nC\n2\nC_{2}\nare fixed constant.\nFor the second part of the theorem,\nagain consider an arbitrary prediction model\nh\n1\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\nh_{1}(\\cdot)\n, we construct 1 level\nSuperAug\narchitecture with a series of heterogeneous experts including\nh\n1\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\nh_{1}(\\cdot)\nas the first expert with index 1, along with a series of experts that output the original feature\nx\ns\n→\n\\vec{x_{s}}\ninto the expert combination\nω\n\\omega\n. If we denote\nh\n∗\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\nh^{*}(\\cdot)\nas the expected risk minimizer and\nd\n⁡\n(\nh\n,\nh\n∗\n)\n≜\nE\ns\n∼\n𝐪\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n​\n(\n𝐋\n⁡\n(\nh\n⁡\n(\nx\ns\n→\n)\n,\n𝐲\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n)\n−\n𝐋\n⁡\n(\nh\n∗\n​\n(\nx\ns\n→\n)\n,\n𝐲\n⁡\n(\ns\n)\n)\n)\nd(h,h^{*})\\triangleq E_{s\\sim\\mathbf{q}(s)}(\\mathbf{L}(h(\\vec{x_{s}}),\\mathbf{y}(s))-\\mathbf{L}(h^{*}(\\vec{x_{s}}),\\mathbf{y}(s)))\nbe the expected performance of a model\nh\nh\n,\nby leveraging\nthe\nresults in Equation 2 in\n(\nvan2007super\n)\n,\nfrom which the convergence results will follow from the fact that\nfor every\nδ\n>\n0\n\\delta>0\nthere exists a constant\nC\nC\nthat\n1\nV\n​\n∑\nv\n=\n1\nV\nE\n​\nd\n​\n(\nh\n⁡\n(\nc\ns\