{"id": "arxiv-sweep/fulltext/2204.13087v2.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "fulltext", "chars": 100157, "equations": ["M_{i}", "N^{t}_{i}=0", "y_{t+1}=0", "{}^{\\text{p}}", "u^{\\star}", "v^{\\star}", "{}^{\\star}", "i\\in[m]", "\\left\\lvert p-\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\mathbf{1}\\{p_{t}=M_{i}\\}y_{t}/N_{i}^{T}\\right\\rvert\\leq\\epsilon/2", "mK_{k}\\leq t\\leq T_{k}", "\\max_{i\\in[m]}\\left\\lvert p_{i}^{t}-p\\right\\rvert\\leq\\epsilon/\\log T_{k}\\leq\\epsilon"], "text": "Faster online calibration without randomization:\ninterval forecasts and the power of two choices\nChirag Gupta\nAaditya Ramdas\nAffiliation:\nchiragg@cmu.edu, aramdas@cmu.edu\nAffiliation:\nCarnegie Mellon University\nAugust 7, 2026\nAbstract\nWe study the problem of making calibrated probabilistic forecasts for a binary sequence generated by an adversarial nature. Following the seminal paper of Foster and Vohra (1998), nature is often modeled as an adaptive adversary who sees all activity of the forecaster except the randomization that the forecaster may deploy. A number of papers have proposed randomized forecasting strategies that achieve an\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration error rate of\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/\\sqrt{T})\n, which we prove is tight in general. On the other hand, it is well known that it is not possible to be calibrated without randomization, or if nature also sees the forecaster’s randomization; in both cases the calibration error could be\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n)\n\\Omega(1)\n. Inspired by the equally seminal works on the power of two choices and imprecise probability theory, we study a small variant of the standard online calibration problem. The adversary gives the forecaster the option of making\ntwo nearby probabilistic forecasts\n, or equivalently an interval forecast of small width, and the endpoint closest to the revealed outcome is used to judge calibration. This power of two choices, or imprecise forecast, accords the forecaster with significant power—we show that a faster\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration rate of\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/T)\ncan be achieved even without deploying any randomization.\n1\nIntroduction\nA number of machine learning and statistics applications rely on probabilistic predictions. In economics, the influential discrete choice framework uses probabilistic modeling at its core\n(\nMcFadden 1974\n)\n.\nSpiegelhalter 1986\nargued that when predictive models are used in medicine for detecting disease, categorizing patient risk, and clinical trials, it is imperative that they provide accurate probabilities, in order to appropriately guide downstream decisions. Weather forecasters (and their audiences) would like to know the probability of precipitation on a given day\n(\nBrier 1950\n)\n.\nWe study the problem of producing probabilistic forecasts for binary events, that are calibrated without any assumptions on the data-generating process. Informally, a forecaster is calibrated if, on all the days that the forecaster produces a forecast\np\nt\np_{t}\nthat is approximately equal to\np\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\np\\in[0,1]\n, the empirical average of the observations\ny\nt\n∈\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\ny_{t}\\in\\{0,1\\}\nis also approximately equal to\np\np\n, and this is true for every\np\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\np\\in[0,1]\nthat is frequently close to a forecast\n(\nDawid 1982\n)\n. We formalize this next.\n1.1\nCalibration games and\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration\nCalibration-Game-I (classical)\n(nature is an adaptive adversary)\nAt time\nt\n=\n1\n,\n2\n,\n…\n,\nt=1,2,\\ldots,\n•\nForecaster plays\nu\nt\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n)\nu_{t}\\in\\Delta([0,1])\n.\n•\nNature plays\nv\nt\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\n)\nv_{t}\\in\\Delta(\\{0,1\\})\n.\n•\nForecaster predicts\np\nt\n∼\nu\nt\np_{t}\\sim u_{t}\n.\n•\nNature reveals\ny\nt\n∼\nv\nt\ny_{t}\\sim v_{t}\n.\nCalibration-Game-II (POTC)\n(nature is an adaptive adversary,\nforecaster has two nearby choices)\nFix\nϵ\n>\n0\n\\epsilon>0\n. At time\nt\n=\n1\n,\n2\n,\n…\n,\nt=1,2,\\ldots,\n•\nForecaster plays\np\nt\n​\n0\n,\np\nt\n​\n1\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\np_{t0},p_{t1}\\in[0,1]\n, such that\np\nt\n​\n0\n≤\np\nt\n​\n1\np_{t0}\\leq p_{t1}\nand\n|\np\nt\n​\n1\n−\np\nt\n​\n0\n|\n≤\n2\n​\nϵ\n\\left\\lvert p_{t1}-p_{t0}\\right\\rvert\\leq 2\\epsilon\n.\n•\nNature reveals\ny\nt\n∈\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\ny_{t}\\in\\{0,1\\}\n.\n•\nIf\ny\nt\n=\n1\ny_{t}=1\n, set\np\nt\n=\np\nt\n​\n1\np_{t}=p_{t1}\n;\nelse set\np\nt\n=\np\nt\n​\n0\np_{t}=p_{t0}\n.\nCalibration-Game-I models the problem as a game between a forecaster and nature. The forecaster produces a\nrandomized\nforecast\nu\nt\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n)\nu_{t}\\in\\Delta([0,1])\n, which is a distribution over the space of forecasts\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n[0,1]\n.\nΔ\n⁡\n(\nS\n)\n\\Delta(S)\ndenotes the set of probability distributions over the set\nS\nS\n(in every case,\nS\nS\nis a standard set like\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n[0,1]\nwith a canonical\nσ\n\\sigma\n-algebra). Nature observes\nu\nt\nu_{t}\nand responds with a Bernoulli distribution for the outcome\nv\nt\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\n)\n=\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nv_{t}\\in\\Delta(\\{0,1\\})=[0,1]\n. We abuse notation slightly and use\nv\nt\nv_{t}\nto denote both the Bernoulli distribution and its parameter in\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n[0,1]\n. Then forecaster and nature draw their actual actions, the forecast\np\nt\n∼\nu\nt\np_{t}\\sim u_{t}\nand the outcome\ny\nt\n∼\nv\nt\ny_{t}\\sim v_{t}\n, simultaneously. At time\nT\n>\n1\nT>1\n, the prior activities\n(\nu\nt\n,\nv\nt\n,\np\nt\n,\ny\nt\n)\nt\n=\n1\nT\n−\n1\n(u_{t},v_{t},p_{t},y_{t})_{t=1}^{T-1}\nare known to both players. The goal of the forecaster is to appear calibrated, defined shortly. Nature wishes to prevent the forecaster from appearing calibrated. Such a nature is typically referred to as an adaptive adversary.\nEven before defining calibration formally, we can see that randomization is essential for the forecaster to demonstrate any semblance of being calibrated. If the forecaster is forced to put all his mass on a single\np\nt\np_{t}\nat each time (or equivalently if nature is an adaptive\noffline\nadversary), nature can play\nv\nt\n=\ny\nt\n=\n𝟏\n{\np\nt\n≤\n0.5\n}\nv_{t}=y_{t}=\\mathbf{1}\\{p_{t}\\leq 0.5\\}\nto render the forecaster highly miscalibrated\n(\nOakes 1985\n;\nDawid 1985\n)\n.\nIn anticipation of a forthcoming definition of\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration error (equation (\n1\n)), we note that it will suffice for forecasters to only make discrete forecasts. Let\nϵ\n>\n0\n\\epsilon>0\nbe a discretization or tolerance level, which is a small constant such as\n0.1\n0.1\nor\n0.01\n0.01\ndepending on the application. For technical simplicity, we assume that\nϵ\n=\n1\n/\n2\n​\nm\n\\epsilon=1/2m\nfor some integer\nm\n≥\n2\nm\\geq 2\n. Consider the\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-cover of\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n[0,1]\ngiven by the\nm\nm\nintervals\nI\n1\n=\n[\n0\n,\n1\n/\nm\n)\n,\nI\n2\n=\n[\n1\n/\nm\n,\n2\n/\nm\n)\n,\n…\n,\nI\nm\n=\n[\n1\n−\n1\n/\nm\n,\n1\n]\nI_{1}=[0,1/m),I_{2}=[1/m,2/m),\\ldots,I_{m}=[1-1/m,1]\n. At time\nt\nt\n, the forecaster makes a forecast on the ‘\n2\n​\nϵ\n2\\epsilon\n-grid’ of the mid-points of these intervals:\np\nt\n∈\n{\nM\n1\n:=\n1\n/\n2\nm\n=\nϵ\n,\nM\n2\n:=\n3\n/\n2\nm\n=\n3\nϵ\n,\n…\n,\nM\nm\n:=\n1\n−\n1\n/\n2\nm\n=\n1\n−\nϵ\n}\n.\np_{t}\\in\\{M_{1}:=1/2m=\\epsilon,M_{2}:=3/2m=3\\epsilon,\\ldots,M_{m}:=1-1/2m=1-\\epsilon\\}.\nDenote the total number of times the forecast is\np\nt\n=\nM\ni\np_{t}=M_{i}\nuntil time\nT\n≥\n1\nT\\geq 1\nas\nN\ni\nT\n:=\n|\n{\nt\n≤\nT\n:\np\nt\n=\nM\ni\n}\n|\n,\nN_{i}^{T}:=\\left\\lvert\\{t\\leq T:p_{t}=M_{i}\\}\\right\\rvert,\nand the observed average of the\ny\nt\ny_{t}\n’s when\np\nt\n=\nM\ni\np_{t}=M_{i}\nas\np\nT\ni\n:=\n{\n1\nN\ni\nT\n∑\nt\n≤\nT\n:\np\nt\n=\nM\ni\ny\nt\nif\nN\nT\ni\n>\n0\n,\nM\ni\n​\notherwise\n.\n\\ p^{T}_{i}:=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{N^{T}_{i}}{\\sum_{t\\leq T:p_{t}=M_{i}}y_{t}}~~~\\hfill\\text{ if }N^{T}_{i}>0,\\\\\nM_{i}\\hfill\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}\nFollowing\nFoster 1999\n, the (\nℓ\n1\n\\ell_{1}\n-)calibration error at time\nT\nT\n,\nCE\nT\n\\text{CE}_{T}\n, is defined as the weighted sum of the prediction errors for each possible forecast:\nCE\nT\n:=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\nN\ni\nT\nT\n⋅\n|\nM\ni\n−\np\ni\nT\n|\n, or equivalently\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n|\n1\nT\n∑\nt\n=\n1\nT\n𝟏\n{\np\nt\n=\nM\ni\n}\n(\nM\ni\n−\ny\nt\n)\n|\n.\n\\text{CE}_{T}:=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\frac{N_{i}^{T}}{T}\\cdot\\left\\lvert M_{i}-p_{i}^{T}\\right\\rvert\\text{, or equivalently }\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\left\\lvert\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\mathbf{1}\\{p_{t}=M_{i}\\}(M_{i}-y_{t})\\right\\rvert.\nFinally, we define the\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration error\n(\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n)\n(\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T})\nas the calibration error with a slack of\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n:\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n:=\nmax\n⁡\n(\nCE\nT\n−\nϵ\n,\n0\n)\n.\n\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}:=\\max(\\text{CE}_{T}-\\epsilon,0).\n(1)\nIn Calibration-Game-I, the forecaster and nature are allowed to randomize, thus\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\nis a random quantity. A commonly studied object is its expected value. A forecaster is said to be\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibrated if, for\nany\nstrategy of nature, the forecaster satisfies\nlim\nT\n→\n∞\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n]\n=\n0\n,\nor equivalently\n​\n𝔼\n​\n[\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n]\n=\no\nT\n​\n(\n1\n)\n⏟\nf\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\n.\n\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\right]=0,\\text{ or equivalently }\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\right]=\\underbrace{o_{T}(1)}_{f(T)}.\n(2)\nWe are interested in the worst case value of\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n]\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\right]\nagainst an adversarial nature, denoted as\nf\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\nf(T)\n, henceforth called the\nϵ\n​\n-calibration rate\n\\epsilon\\text{-calibration rate}\nor simply calibration rate. We show results about the asymptotic dependence of\nf\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\nf(T)\nas\nT\n→\n∞\nT\\to\\infty\n, holding\nϵ\n\\epsilon\nas a fixed problem parameter on which\nf\nf\nmay depend arbitrarily.\n1.2\nRelated work and our contributions\nA number of papers have proposed\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibrated forecasting algorithms which guarantee\nf\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\n=\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nf(T)=O(1/\\sqrt{T})\n—the first was the seminal work of\nFoster and Vohra 1998\n, followed by a number of alternative proofs and generalizations of their result (\nFoster 1999\n,\nFudenberg and Levine 1999\n,\nVovk et al. 2005\n,\nMannor and Stoltz 2010\n,\nAbernethy et al. 2011\n,\nPerchet 2015\n).\nIn Theorem\n3\n, we show that the\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/\\sqrt{T})\nrate achieved by these algorithms is tight. There is a strategy for nature that ensures\nf\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\n=\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nf(T)=\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\n. Our proof uses a non-constructive lower bound for Blackwell approachability games\n(\nMannor and Perchet 2013\n)\n.\nQiao and Valiant 2021\nrecently showed that the worst-case calibration error without the\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-slack,\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nCE\nT\n]\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{CE}_{T}\\right]\n, is\nΩ\n⁡\n(\nT\n−\n0.472\n)\n\\Omega(T^{-0.472})\n. In contrast, we treat\nϵ\n\\epsilon\nas a small constant fixed ahead of time, and consider lower bounds on\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n]\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\right]\n. Neither goal subsumes the other, so our lower bound complements theirs. In particular, observe that\n𝔼\n[\nCE\nT\n]\n=\nΩ\n(\nT\n−\n1\n/\n2\n)\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{CE}_{T}\\right]=\\Omega(T^{-1/2})\ncan be forced by nature by playing a non-adaptive Bernoulli strategy, drawing independently each\ny\nt\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\np\n)\ny_{t}\\sim\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\nfor some\np\np\n. This strategy seems insufficient for deriving a useful lower bound on\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n]\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\right]\n.\nThese comparisons are further discussed in Section\n4.6\n.\nFoster 1999\nshowed that calibration is a Blackwell approachability instance (see Section\n4.1\n), and while the rate\nf\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\n=\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nf(T)=\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\nhas not been formally established earlier (to the best of our knowledge), it is the rate one expects from a general Blackwell approachability instance\n(\nCesa-Bianchi and Lugosi 2006\n, Remark 7.7)\n. Instead, the community has looked to establish positive results for alternative notions: calibration with more stringent tests than\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration\n(\nPerchet 2015\n;\nRakhlin et al. 2011\n)\n, calibration where the output space takes more than two values\n(\nMannor and Stoltz 2010\n)\n, calibration with checking rules\n(\nLehrer 2001\n;\nSandroni et al. 2003\n;\nVovk et al. 2005\n)\n, weak calibration\n(\nKakade and Foster 2004\n)\n, and smooth calibration\n(\nFoster and Hart 2018\n)\n. In particular, while no deterministic forecaster playing Calibration-Game-I can be\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibrated, there exist deterministic forecasters who are weakly/smoothly calibrated\n(\nFoster and Hart 2018\n)\n.\nIn our work, we take a slightly different approach from these papers. We retain the classical definition of\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration but change the calibration game. In Calibration-Game-II, also called the Power-Of-Two-Choices (POTC) game, the forecaster reveals two forecasts\np\nt\n​\n0\n,\np\nt\n​\n1\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\np_{t0},p_{t1}\\in[0,1]\n, such that\np\nt\n​\n0\n≤\np\nt\n​\n1\np_{t0}\\leq p_{t1}\nand\n|\np\nt\n​\n1\n−\np\nt\n​\n0\n|\n≤\n2\n​\nϵ\n\\left\\lvert p_{t1}-p_{t0}\\right\\rvert\\leq 2\\epsilon\n. Since the earlier binning scheme used a\n2\n​\nϵ\n2\\epsilon\n-grid, that is\n1\n/\nm\n=\n2\n​\nϵ\n1/m=2\\epsilon\n, this effectively allows the forecaster to choose a full bin as their forecast (rather than its midpoint), or equivalently to choose two consecutive bin midpoints. Thus there is no randomization, and nature knows the two forecasts. If nature chooses to play\ny\nt\n=\n0\ny_{t}=0\n,\np\nt\n=\np\nt\n​\n0\np_{t}=p_{t0}\nis used to judge the calibration of the forecaster, and if nature chooses to play\ny\nt\n=\n1\ny_{t}=1\n,\np\nt\n=\np\nt\n​\n1\np_{t}=p_{t1}\nis used. (One could say that the forecast closer to reality is used for measuring calibration, or that the forecaster decides which one of the two forecasts to use; these are all equivalent.) Obviously, without the restriction of\np\nt\n​\n0\n,\np\nt\n​\n1\np_{t0},p_{t1}\nbeing\n2\n​\nϵ\n2\\epsilon\n-close, the problem is trivial: the forecaster would predict\np\nt\n​\n0\n=\n0\np_{t0}=0\nand\np\nt\n​\n1\n=\n1\np_{t1}=1\nin each round, and achieve zero error in every round. Requiring\n|\np\nt\n​\n1\n−\np\nt\n​\n0\n|\n≤\n2\n​\nϵ\n\\left\\lvert p_{t1}-p_{t0}\\right\\rvert\\leq 2\\epsilon\nmakes the problem interesting. The POTC setup may appear surprising to some and we devote Section\n2\nto motivating it.\nThe summary of our main result (Theorem\n1\n) is as follows. In the POTC game, the forecaster can ensure—deterministically—that\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n=\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n.\n\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}=O(1/T).\n(3)\nCompared to (\n2\n), there is no expectation operator anymore since the forecaster is deterministic and nature being fully adaptively adversarial does not benefit from randomizing.\nOur forecaster is a variant of\nFoster 1999\n. While Foster’s forecaster randomizes over two nearby forecasts (making it\nalmost deterministic\nin the sense of\nFoster and Hart 2021\n), our forecaster predicts both these values and is judged with respect to the better one (and is actually, not almost, deterministic).\nRemark 1\n(Generalization from binary to bounded outputs)\n.\nThe POTC game can be modified for bounded, instead of binary, outputs. That is, nature can play\nv\nt\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nv_{t}\\in[0,1]\nand calibration would be judged with respect to the average of the\nv\nt\nv_{t}\n’s on the instances when\np\nt\n=\nM\ni\np_{t}=M_{i}\n. Note that this is not the same as nature playing\ny\nt\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\nv\nt\n)\ny_{t}\\sim\\text{Bernoulli}(v_{t})\n, since the calibration loss (left-hand-side of (\n3\n)) is not linear in\ny\nt\ny_{t}\n. With bounded outputs, the same\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/T)\ncalibration rate can be achieved by a minor modification to our proposed forecasting strategy; see Appendix\nC\nfor more details. A similar remark holds for Calibration-Game-I and the corresponding lower bound of\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\n. This latter fact is evident without further details since the lower bound can only increase if nature is given more flexibility.\nOrganization.\nSection\n2\nprovides further context and motivation for the POTC game. Section\n3\npresents our algorithm for the POTC game and proves the fast calibration rate of\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/T)\nfor it (Theorem\n1\n). Section\n4\nreviews the well-known equivalence between calibration and Blackwell’s approachability theorem\n(\nBlackwell 1956\n)\n, using which we prove the slow calibration rate of\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\nfor Calibration-Game-I (Theorem\n3\n). Most proofs are presented alongside the results. Section\n5\nconcludes with a discussion.\n2\nMotivation for the POTC calibration game\nCalibration-Game-II or the POTC game is motivated by two rich fields of literature: imprecise probability and the power of two choices.\n2.1\nA practical perspective via imprecise probability\nThe reader may wonder what the practical usefulness of the POTC game is. Why would we judge the forecaster in such a manner? The answer is that our earlier problem was phrased in a fashion that makes the connection to the power of two choices transparent. But one can also re-cast the problem in the language of\nimprecise probability\n. In this area, one is typically not restricted to work with single, unique probability measures, but instead the axioms of probability are relaxed, and added flexibility is provided in order to work with\nupper\nand\nlower\nprobability measures\n(\nWalley and Fine 1982\n)\n.\nIn the context of our problem, instead of saying that the probability of rain is\n0.3\n0.3\n, a forecaster is allowed to say\n0.3\n±\nϵ\n0.3\\pm\\epsilon\n. One may just say that the forecaster is slightly uncertain and does not wish to commit to a point forecast, and indeed we may not force a forecaster to announce a point forecast against their will. From a Bayesian or game-theoretic perspective, we may say that the forecaster allows bets against their forecast, represented as a contract which pays off\ny\nt\ny_{t}\n, but the forecaster’s prices for buying and selling such a contract are slightly different. From a practical perspective, this type of\ninterval\nforecast arguably has almost the same utility and interpretability to a layman as the corresponding point forecast. The use of upper and lower forecasts (translated to prices or betting odds) is standard in game-theoretic probability\n(\nShafer and Vovk 2019\n)\n. Separately, the recent work of\nde Cooman and De Bock 2022\nestablishes that\nrandomness is inherently imprecise\nin a formal sense, and provides a different justification for the use of interval forecasts for binary sequences.\nRemarkably, this small and seemingly insignificant change in reporting leads to a huge change in our ability to achieve calibration. (This gain can be rather puzzling: we were binning/gridding anyway, so why not report a full bin rather than its midpoint? How could that possibly improve our calibration error?!) Of course, we must figure out how to judge the quality of such an interval forecast: we must swap out\n𝟏\n{\np\nt\n=\nM\ni\n}\n(\nM\ni\n−\ny\nt\n)\n\\mathbf{1}\\{p_{t}=M_{i}\\}(M_{i}-y_{t})\nin\nCE\nT\n\\text{CE}_{T}\nfor a generalized notion of error that dictates how far\ny\nt\ny_{t}\nwas from the forecasted interval\nA\nt\n:=\n[\np\nt\n​\n0\n,\np\nt\n​\n1\n]\nA_{t}:=[p_{t0},p_{t1}]\n. To do this, we use the distance from a point to a convex set: we replace\nM\ni\nM_{i}\nwith the projection of\ny\nt\ny_{t}\nonto\nA\nt\nA_{t}\n, denoted\nproj\n​\n(\ny\nt\n,\nA\nt\n)\n\\text{proj}(y_{t},A_{t})\n. This is exactly what our POTC version does, just expressed differently.\nWhen we generalize the definition of calibration, it is notationally simpler to restrict the forecasted interval endpoints to be the same\nm\nm\ngridpoints, meaning that\nA\nt\n=\n[\nM\ni\n,\nM\ni\n+\n1\n]\nA_{t}=[M_{i},M_{i+1}]\nfor some\ni\n≤\nm\n−\n1\ni\\leq m-1\n(rather than\nA\nt\n=\nI\ni\nA_{t}=I_{i}\n, the intervals whose midpoints are\nM\ni\nM_{i}\n). In this case, we call a method that produces interval forecasts\n(\nI\nt\n)\nt\n≥\n1\n(I_{t})_{t\\geq 1}\nas being\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibrated if:\nmax\n(\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n|\n1\nT\n∑\nt\n=\n1\nT\n𝟏\n{\nproj\n(\ny\nt\n,\nA\nt\n)\n=\nM\ni\n}\ndist\n(\ny\nt\n,\nA\nt\n)\n|\n−\nϵ\n,\n0\n)\n=\no\n(\n1\n)\n,\n\\max\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\left\\lvert\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\mathbf{1}\\{\\text{proj}(y_{t},A_{t})=M_{i}\\}\\text{dist}(y_{t},A_{t})\\right\\rvert-\\epsilon,0\\right)=o(1),\n(4)\nwhere\ndist\n​\n(\ny\n,\nA\n)\n:=\n|\ny\n−\nproj\n​\n(\ny\n,\nA\n)\n|\n\\text{dist}(y,A):=|y-\\text{proj}(y,A)|\nis the distance of\ny\ny\nto interval\nA\nA\n. (When the interval is a single point, we recover the original definition of calibration, but in this case we know that randomization is necessary for\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration.)\nImprecise probability has also made an intriguing appearance in the simpler setting commonly considered in machine learning, of achieving calibration in offline binary classification in the presence of i.i.d. data. This is a problem where theoretical progress has been made on designing\ndistribution-free\nalgorithms that have calibration guarantees by just assuming that the covariate-label pairs of data are i.i.d., while also performing well on real data\n(\nGupta et al. 2020\n;\nGupta and Ramdas 2021\n;\nGupta and Ramdas 2022\n)\n. Venn predictors are a class of distribution-free algorithms that produce imprecise probability forecasts\n(\nVovk et al. 2003\n;\nVovk and Petej 2014\n)\n. On observing the covariates of a new point, Venn predictors output a particular interval of probabilities\n[\np\n0\n,\np\n1\n]\n[p_{0},p_{1}]\nfor the unknown binary label. A strong, but slightly odd, calibration property holds: the authors prove that\np\nY\np_{Y}\n(a random and unknown prediction, since\nY\nY\nis unknown and random) achieves\nexact\ncalibration in finite samples.\nOne can, in some sense, view our work as extending the use of such imprecise interval forecasts to the online calibration setting with adversarial data.\n2.2\nThe varied applications of the power of two choices\nThe power of two choices (POTC) refers to a remarkable result by\nAzar et al. 1994\nfor the problem of load balancing. Suppose\nn\nn\nballs are placed independently and uniformly at random into\nn\nn\nbins. It can be shown that with high probability, the maximum number of balls in a bin (the maximum\nload\n) will be\nΘ\n~\n​\n(\nlog\n⁡\nn\n)\n\\widetilde{\\Theta}(\\log n)\n. Consider a different setup where the balls are placed sequentially, and for each ball, two bin indices are drawn uniformly at random and offered to a\nload-balancer\nwho gets to decide which of the two bins to place the ball in. The load-balancer attempts to reduce the maximum load by following a natural strategy: at each step, place the ball in the bin with lesser load. It turns out that with this strategy the maximum load drops exponentially to\nΘ\n~\n​\n(\nlog\n⁡\nlog\n⁡\nn\n)\n\\widetilde{\\Theta}(\\log\\log n)\n.\nThe POTC result has led a number of applications. In a network where one of many servers can fulfil a request, it is exponentially better to choose two servers (instead of one) at random and allocate the server with fewer existing requests\n(\nAzar et al. 1994\n)\n. Using two hashes instead of one significantly reduces the load of a single hash bucket\n(\nBroder and Mitzenmacher 2001\n)\n. In circuit routing, selecting one of two possible circuits provably leads to decongestion\n(\nCole et al. 1998\n)\n. When allocating a task to one of many resources where an intensive query needs to be made about the resource capacity, querying two resources is often better than querying all resources, or querying a single resource\n(\nAzar et al. 1994\n)\n. Recently,\nDwivedi et al. 2019\nused the POTC to develop an online thinning algorithm that produces\nlow-discrepancy sequences\non hypercubes, with applications to quasi Monte Carlo integration. For further applications and a survey of mathematical techniques, we refer the reader to the thesis of\nMitzenmacher 1996\n, or the survey by\nMitzenmacher et al. 2001\n.\nIn this paper, we find yet another intriguing phenomenon involving the POTC, this time in the context of calibration. We modify the classical setup of calibration (Calibration-Game-I) to the POTC setup (Calibration-Game-II), by offering the forecaster two nearby choices. We show that this change accords the forecaster with significant power, enabling faster calibration, even without randomization.\n3\nMain results: algorithm and analysis\nConsider the POTC game (Calibration-Game-II). Recall that the forecaster’s probabilities correspond to the mid-points of the intervals\nI\n1\n=\n[\n0\n,\n1\n/\nm\n)\n,\n…\n,\nI\nm\n=\n[\n1\n−\n1\n/\nm\n,\n1\n]\nI_{1}=[0,1/m),\\ldots,I_{m}=[1-1/m,1]\n, given by\nM\n1\n=\n1\n/\n2\n​\nm\n,\n…\n,\nM\nm\n=\n1\n−\n1\n/\n2\n​\nm\nM_{1}=1/2m,\\ldots,M_{m}=1-1/2m\n. The forecaster can play either\n(\np\nt\n​\n0\n,\np\nt\n​\n1\n)\n=\n(\nM\ni\n,\nM\ni\n)\n(p_{t0},p_{t1})=(M_{i},M_{i})\nor\n(\np\nt\n​\n0\n,\np\nt\n​\n1\n)\n=\n(\nM\ni\n,\nM\ni\n+\n1\n)\n(p_{t0},p_{t1})=(M_{i},M_{i+1})\nfor some\ni\ni\n. We can also say that the forecaster predicts one of the two intervals\n{\nM\ni\n}\n\\{M_{i}\\}\nor\n[\nM\ni\n,\nM\ni\n+\n1\n]\n[M_{i},M_{i+1}]\nrespectively.\nWe introduce some notation to describe the algorithm. For\ni\n∈\n[\nm\n]\n:=\n{\n1\n,\n2\n,\n…\n,\nm\n}\ni\\in[m]:=\\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}\nand\nt\n≥\n1\nt\\geq 1\n, define:\n(left endpoint of interval\ni\ni\n)\nl\ni\n=\n(\ni\n−\n1\n)\n/\nm\n,\n\\displaystyle l_{i}=(i-1)/m,\n(right endpoint of interval\ni\ni\n)\nr\ni\n=\ni\n/\nm\n,\n\\displaystyle r_{i}=i/m,\n(frequency of interval\ni\ni\n)\nN\ni\nt\n=\n|\n{\n𝟏\n{\np\ns\n=\nM\ni\n}\n:\ns\n≤\nt\n}\n|\n,\n\\displaystyle N^{t}_{i}=\\left\\lvert\\{\\mathbf{1}\\{p_{s}=M_{i}\\}:s\\leq t\\}\\right\\rvert,\n(observed average when\nM\ni\nM_{i}\nwas forecasted)\np\ni\nt\n=\n{\n∑\ns\n=\n1\nt\ny\ns\n𝟏\n{\np\ns\n=\nM\ni\n}\n/\nN\ni\nt\nif\nN\nt\ni\n>\n0\nM\ni\nif\nN\ni\nt\n=\n0\n,\n\\displaystyle p^{t}_{i}=\\begin{cases}\\sum_{s=1}^{t}y_{s}\\mathbf{1}\\{p_{s}=M_{i}\\}/N_{i}^{t}~~\\text{ if }N^{t}_{i}>0\\\\\n\\text{$M_{i}$~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~if $N^{t}_{i}=0$},\\end{cases}\n(deficit)\nd\ni\nt\n=\nl\ni\n−\np\ni\nt\n,\n\\displaystyle d_{i}^{t}=l_{i}-p_{i}^{t},\n(excess)\ne\ni\nt\n=\np\ni\nt\n−\nr\ni\n.\n\\displaystyle e_{i}^{t}=p_{i}^{t}-r_{i}.\nThe terminology ‘deficit’ alludes to the fact that if\np\ni\nt\np_{i}^{t}\nis smaller than desired (to the left of\nl\ni\nl_{i}\n), then\nd\ni\nt\n>\n0\nd_{i}^{t}>0\n(\np\ni\nt\np_{i}^{t}\nis ‘in deficit’). ‘Excess’ has the opposite interpretation.\nPOTC-Cal: Algorithm for forecaster in Calibration-Game-II\n(for notation, see Section\n3\nbelow)\n•\nAt time\nt\n=\n1\nt=1\n, play\n(\np\n10\n,\np\n11\n)\n=\n(\nM\n1\n,\nM\n1\n)\n(p_{10},p_{11})=(M_{1},M_{1})\n. Thus\np\n1\n=\nM\n1\np_{1}=M_{1}\n.\n•\nAt time\nt\n+\n1\nt+1\n(\nOPEN\nt\n≥\n1\n)\nt\\geq 1)\n, if\ncondition A: there exists an\ni\n∈\n[\nm\n]\ni\\in[m]\nsuch that\nd\ni\nt\n≤\n0\nd_{i}^{t}\\leq 0\nand\ne\ni\nt\n≤\n0\ne_{i}^{t}\\leq 0\n,\nis satisfied, play\n(\nM\ni\n,\nM\ni\n)\n(M_{i},M_{i})\nfor any\ni\ni\nthat verifies condition A (that is,\np\nt\n+\n1\n=\nM\ni\np_{t+1}=M_{i}\n). Otherwise,\ncondition B: there exists an\ni\n∈\n[\nm\n−\n1\n]\ni\\in[m-1]\nsuch that\ne\ni\nt\n>\n0\ne_{i}^{t}>0\nand\nd\ni\n+\n1\nt\n>\n0\nd_{i+1}^{t}>0\n,\nmust be satisfied (see Lemma\n5\n). Play\n(\nM\ni\n,\nM\ni\n+\n1\n)\n(M_{i},M_{i+1})\nfor any index\ni\ni\nthat verifies condition B (that is,\np\nt\n+\n1\n=\nM\ni\np_{t+1}=M_{i}\nif\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n0\ny_{t+1}=0\nand\np\nt\n+\n1\n=\nM\ni\n+\n1\np_{t+1}=M_{i+1}\nif\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n1\ny_{t+1}=1\n).\n3.1\nForecasting algorithm\nThe algorithm, presented on top of this page, is a variant of the one proposed by\nFoster 1999\n. Foster’s forecaster isolates two relevant\nM\ni\nM_{i}\n’s and randomizes over them; we use the same\nM\ni\nM_{i}\n’s to form the reported interval. At time\nt\n+\n1\nt+1\n, if there is a forecast\nM\ni\nM_{i}\nthat is already ‘good’ in the sense that\np\ni\nt\n∈\n[\nl\ni\n,\nr\ni\n]\np_{i}^{t}\\in[l_{i},r_{i}]\n, the forecaster predicts\nM\ni\nM_{i}\n. Otherwise, the forecaster finds two consecutive values\n(\nM\ni\n,\nM\ni\n+\n1\n)\n(M_{i},M_{i+1})\nsuch that\np\ni\nt\np_{i}^{t}\nis in excess and\np\ni\n+\n1\nt\np_{i+1}^{t}\nis in deficit (such an\ni\ni\nexists by Lemma\n5\n, Appendix\nA\n). The forecaster plays\n(\nM\ni\n,\nM\ni\n+\n1\n)\n(M_{i},M_{i+1})\n. If nature reveals\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n0\ny_{t+1}=0\n, then\np\nt\n+\n1\n=\nM\ni\np_{t+1}=M_{i}\n, and the excess of\np\ni\nt\np_{i}^{t}\ndecreases. If nature reveals\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n1\ny_{t+1}=1\n, then\np\nt\n+\n1\n=\nM\ni\n+\n1\np_{t+1}=M_{i+1}\n, and the deficit of\np\ni\n+\n1\nt\np_{i+1}^{t}\ndecreases.\n3.2\nAnalysis of POTC-Cal\nWe now present our main result along with a short proof.\nTheorem 1\n.\nPOTC-Cal satisfies, at any time\nT\n≥\n1\nT\\geq 1\n, for any strategy of nature,\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n≤\nm\n/\nT\n.\n\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\leq m/T.\n(5)\nProof.\nConsider any\nt\n≥\n1\nt\\geq 1\n. We write each of the\nm\nm\nterms in the calibration error at time\nt\nt\n,\nCE\nt\n\\text{CE}_{t}\n, as follows:\n|\n1\nt\n∑\ns\n=\n1\nt\n𝟏\n{\np\ns\n=\nM\ni\n}\n(\nM\ni\n−\ny\ns\n)\n|\n=\nN\ni\nt\n​\n|\nM\ni\n−\np\ni\nt\n|\nt\n=\nN\ni\nt\n​\n(\nϵ\n+\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n,\ne\ni\nt\n)\n)\nt\n.\n\\displaystyle\\left\\lvert\\frac{1}{t}\\sum_{s=1}^{t}\\mathbf{1}\\{p_{s}=M_{i}\\}(M_{i}-y_{s})\\right\\rvert=\\frac{N_{i}^{t}\\left\\lvert M_{i}-p_{i}^{t}\\right\\rvert}{t}=\\frac{N_{i}^{t}(\\epsilon+\\max(d_{i}^{t},e_{i}^{t}))}{t}.\nDefine\nE\nt\n(\ni\n)\n:=\nN\ni\nt\n​\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n,\ne\ni\nt\n)\nE_{t}^{(i)}:=N_{i}^{t}\\max(d_{i}^{t},e_{i}^{t})\n, and observe that\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n=\nmax\n⁡\n(\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\nN\ni\nT\n​\nϵ\n+\nE\nT\n(\ni\n)\nT\n−\nϵ\n,\n0\n)\n=\nmax\n⁡\n(\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\nE\nT\n(\ni\n)\nT\n,\n0\n)\n.\n\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}=\\max\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\frac{N_{i}^{T}\\epsilon+E_{T}^{(i)}}{T}-\\epsilon,0\\right)=\\max\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\frac{E_{T}^{(i)}}{T},0\\right).\nWe will show that for every\ni\n∈\n[\nm\n]\ni\\in[m]\n,\nE\nT\n(\ni\n)\n≤\n1\nE_{T}^{(i)}\\leq 1\n, proving the theorem.\nConsider some specific\ni\n∈\n[\nm\n]\ni\\in[m]\n. If action\ni\ni\nis never played, then\nN\ni\nT\n=\n0\nN_{i}^{T}=0\n, and\nE\nT\n(\ni\n)\n=\n0\nE_{T}^{(i)}=0\n. Suppose an action\ni\ni\nhas\nN\ni\nT\n>\n0\nN_{i}^{T}>0\n. For each\n1\n≤\nt\n<\nT\n1\\leq t<T\n, if\na\nt\n+\n1\n≠\ni\na_{t+1}\\neq i\n, then\nE\nt\n+\n1\n(\ni\n)\n=\nE\nt\n(\ni\n)\nE_{t+1}^{(i)}=E_{t}^{(i)}\n. If\na\nt\n+\n1\n=\ni\na_{t+1}=i\n, then by Lemmas\n1\nand\n2\n(stated and proved below),\nE\nt\n+\n1\n(\ni\n)\n≤\nmax\n⁡\n(\nE\nt\n(\ni\n)\n,\n1\n)\nE_{t+1}^{(i)}\\leq\\max(E_{t}^{(i)},1)\n. In other words, at all\nt\nt\n, the value of\nE\nt\n+\n1\n(\ni\n)\nE_{t+1}^{(i)}\neither stays bounded by\n1\n1\n, or decreases compared to the previous value\nE\nt\n(\ni\n)\nE_{t}^{(i)}\n. A trivial inductive argument thus implies\nE\nT\n(\ni\n)\n≤\n1\nE_{T}^{(i)}\\leq 1\n. For completeness, we verify the base case: since\np\n1\n=\nM\n1\np_{1}=M_{1}\n,\nE\n1\n(\ni\n≠\n1\n)\n=\n0\nE_{1}^{(i\\neq 1)}=0\nand\nE\n1\n(\n1\n)\n≤\n1\nE_{1}^{(1)}\\leq 1\n(as\nd\n1\n1\n≤\n0\nd_{1}^{1}\\leq 0\nand\ne\n1\n1\n≤\n1\ne_{1}^{1}\\leq 1\n). ∎\nLemma 1\n.\nSuppose condition A was satisfied at time\nt\n+\n1\nt+1\nand the forecast was\np\nt\n+\n1\n=\nM\ni\np_{t+1}=M_{i}\n. Then,\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\n,\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n)\n≤\n1\nN_{i}^{t+1}\\max(d_{i}^{t+1},e_{i}^{t+1})\\leq 1\n.\nProof.\nSince\np\nt\n+\n1\n=\nM\ni\np_{t+1}=M_{i}\n,\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n=\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\nN_{i}^{t+1}=N_{i}^{t}+1\n, and\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\np\ni\nt\n+\n1\n=\nN\ni\nt\n​\np\ni\nt\n+\ny\nt\n+\n1\nN_{i}^{t+1}p_{i}^{t+1}=N_{i}^{t}p_{i}^{t}+y_{t+1}\n. Then,\n|\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\n−\nd\ni\nt\n|\n=\n|\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n−\ne\ni\nt\n|\n=\n|\np\ni\nt\n+\n1\n−\np\ni\nt\n|\n\\displaystyle\\left\\lvert d_{i}^{t+1}-d_{i}^{t}\\right\\rvert=\\left\\lvert e_{i}^{t+1}-e_{i}^{t}\\right\\rvert=\\left\\lvert p_{i}^{t+1}-p_{i}^{t}\\right\\rvert\n=\n|\nN\ni\nt\n​\np\ni\nt\n+\ny\nt\n+\n1\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n−\nN\ni\nt\n​\np\ni\nt\n+\np\ni\nt\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n|\n\\displaystyle=\\left\\lvert\\frac{N_{i}^{t}p_{i}^{t}+y_{t+1}}{N_{i}^{t}+1}-\\frac{N_{i}^{t}p_{i}^{t}+p_{i}^{t}}{N_{i}^{t}+1}\\right\\rvert\n(6)\n=\n|\ny\nt\n+\n1\n−\np\ni\nt\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n|\n≤\n1\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n=\n1\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\left\\lvert\\frac{y_{t+1}-p_{i}^{t}}{N_{i}^{t}+1}\\right\\rvert\\leq\\frac{1}{N_{i}^{t}+1}=\\frac{1}{N_{i}^{t+1}}.\nSince by condition A,\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n,\ne\ni\nt\n)\n≤\n0\n\\max(d_{i}^{t},e_{i}^{t})\\leq 0\n, we obtain\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\n,\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n)\n≤\n1\n/\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n\\max(d_{i}^{t+1},e_{i}^{t+1})\\leq 1/N_{i}^{t+1}\n.\n∎\nLemma 2\n.\nSuppose condition A was not satisfied at time\nt\n+\n1\nt+1\nand the forecast was\np\nt\n+\n1\n=\nM\ni\np_{t+1}=M_{i}\n, following condition B. Then\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\n,\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n)\n≤\nmax\n⁡\n(\nN\ni\nt\n​\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n,\ne\ni\nt\n)\n,\n1\n)\nN_{i}^{t+1}\\max(d_{i}^{t+1},e_{i}^{t+1})\\leq\\max(N_{i}^{t}\\max(d_{i}^{t},e_{i}^{t}),1)\n.\nProof.\nSuppose\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n0\ny_{t+1}=0\n. Since we are playing as per condition B,\ne\ni\nt\n>\n0\ne_{i}^{t}>0\n. Since\nd\ni\nt\n+\ne\ni\nt\n=\nl\ni\n−\nr\ni\n=\n−\n1\n/\nm\nd_{i}^{t}+e_{i}^{t}=l_{i}-r_{i}=-1/m\n, we have that\nd\ni\nt\n<\n0\nd_{i}^{t}<0\n. Thus,\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n0\n⟹\ne\ni\nt\n>\n0\n​\nand\n​\nd\ni\nt\n<\n0\n.\ny_{t+1}=0\\implies\\ e_{i}^{t}>0\\text{ and }d_{i}^{t}<0.\nSimilarly, it can be verified that\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n1\n⟹\ne\ni\nt\n<\n0\n​\nand\n​\nd\ni\nt\n>\n0\ny_{t+1}=1\\implies e_{i}^{t}<0\\text{ and }d_{i}^{t}>0\n. Below we assume without loss of generality that\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n0\ny_{t+1}=0\n. (A similar argument goes through for the case\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n1\ny_{t+1}=1\n.)\nWe derive how\nN\ni\nt\n​\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n,\ne\ni\nt\n)\nN_{i}^{t}\\max(d_{i}^{t},e_{i}^{t})\nchanges when going from\nt\nt\nto\nt\n+\n1\nt+1\n. There are two cases:\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n≥\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\ne_{i}^{t+1}\\geq d_{i}^{t+1}\nor\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n<\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\ne_{i}^{t+1}<d_{i}^{t+1}\n. If\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n≥\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\ne_{i}^{t+1}\\geq d_{i}^{t+1}\n, then\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\n,\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n)\n=\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n\\displaystyle N_{i}^{t+1}\\max(d_{i}^{t+1},e_{i}^{t+1})=N_{i}^{t+1}e_{i}^{t+1}\n=\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\np\ni\nt\n+\n1\n−\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\nr\ni\n\\displaystyle=N_{i}^{t+1}p_{i}^{t+1}-N_{i}^{t+1}r_{i}\n=\nN\ni\nt\np\ni\nt\n−\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\nr\ni\n(since\ny\nt\n+\n1\n=\n0\n)\n\\displaystyle=N_{i}^{t}p_{i}^{t}-N_{i}^{t+1}r_{i}\\quad\\text{(since $y_{t+1}=0$)}\n=\nN\ni\nt\n​\ne\ni\nt\n−\nr\ni\n\\displaystyle=N_{i}^{t}e_{i}^{t}-r_{i}\n=\nN\ni\nt\n​\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n,\ne\ni\nt\n)\n−\nr\ni\n≤\nN\ni\nt\n​\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n,\ne\ni\nt\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=N_{i}^{t}\\max(d_{i}^{t},e_{i}^{t})-r_{i}\\leq N_{i}^{t}\\max(d_{i}^{t},e_{i}^{t}).\nOn the other hand if\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n<\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\ne_{i}^{t+1}<d_{i}^{t+1}\n, then,\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\nmax\n⁡\n(\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\n,\ne\ni\nt\n+\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle N_{i}^{t+1}\\max(d_{i}^{t+1},e_{i}^{t+1})\n=\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\n\\displaystyle=N_{i}^{t+1}d_{i}^{t+1}\n≤\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\n(\nd\ni\nt\n+\n|\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\n−\nd\ni\nt\n|\n)\n\\displaystyle\\leq N_{i}^{t+1}(d_{i}^{t}+\\left\\lvert d_{i}^{t+1}-d_{i}^{t}\\right\\rvert)\n<\n(\n∗\n)\n​\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n​\n(\n0\n+\n1\n/\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n)\n=\n1\n.\n\\displaystyle\\overset{(*)}{<}N_{i}^{t+1}(0+1/N_{i}^{t+1})=1.\nInequality\n(\n∗\n)\n(*)\nholds since\nd\ni\nt\n<\n0\nd_{i}^{t}<0\nand\n|\nd\ni\nt\n+\n1\n−\nd\ni\nt\n|\n≤\n1\n/\nN\ni\nt\n+\n1\n\\left\\lvert d_{i}^{t+1}-d_{i}^{t}\\right\\rvert\\leq 1/N_{i}^{t+1}\n(see set of equations (\n6\n)).\n∎\n4\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\nlower bound for the classical calibration game\nCalibration-Game-I can be viewed as a repeated game with vector-valued payoffs/rewards. Such games were studied by\nBlackwell 1956\n, and are now commonly referred to as Blackwell approachability games. We review the reduction from calibration to Blackwell approachability and use it to prove the lower bound. Throughout this section, we denote the action space of the forecaster as\n𝒳\n=\n{\nM\n1\n,\nM\n2\n,\n…\n,\nM\nm\n}\n\\mathcal{X}=\\{M_{1},M_{2},\\ldots,M_{m}\\}\nand that of nature as\n𝒴\n=\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\n\\mathcal{Y}=\\{0,1\\}\n. The random plays of the forecaster lie in\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\n\\Delta(\\mathcal{X})\nwhich is a probability simplex in\nm\nm\ndimensions. We embed\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\n\\Delta(\\mathcal{X})\nin\nℝ\nm\n\\mathbb{R}^{m}\nto simplify discussion.\n4.1\nCalibration as an instance of Blackwell approachability\nThe fact that calibration can be modelled as a Blackwell approachability instance is well-known (since\nFoster 1999\n;\nHart and Mas-Colell 2000\n). Suppose the actions of the forecaster and nature give a reward\nr\n:\n𝒳\n×\n𝒴\n→\nℝ\nm\nr:\\mathcal{X}\\times\\mathcal{Y}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{m}\ndefined as follows: the\ni\ni\n-th component of the reward vector\na\n=\nr\n⁡\n(\np\n∈\n𝒳\n,\ny\n∈\n𝒴\n)\n∈\nℝ\nm\na=r(p\\in\\mathcal{X},y\\in\\mathcal{Y})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\nis given by\na\ni\n=\n𝟏\n{\np\n=\nM\ni\n}\n⋅\n(\nM\ni\n−\ny\n)\n.\na_{i}=\\mathbf{1}\\{p=M_{i}\\}\\cdot(M_{i}-y).\n(7)\nLet\na\n¯\nT\n:=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nT\nr\n⁡\n(\np\nt\n,\ny\nt\n)\n/\nT\n\\bar{a}^{T}:=\\sum_{i=1}^{T}r(p_{t},y_{t})/T\nbe the average reward vector given component-wise by\na\n¯\ni\nT\n=\n∑\nt\n=\n1\nT\n𝟏\n{\np\nt\n=\nM\ni\n}\n(\nM\ni\n−\ny\nt\n)\n/\nT\n\\bar{a}^{T}_{i}=\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\mathbf{1}\\{p_{t}=M_{i}\\}(M_{i}-y_{t})/T\n. Let\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nbe the\nℓ\n1\n\\ell_{1}\n-ball with radius\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n, and\ndist\nthe\nℓ\n1\n\\ell_{1}\n-distance function. Note that\ndist\n(\na\n¯\nT\n,\nB\nϵ\n)\n=\nmax\n(\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n|\n1\nT\n∑\nt\n=\n1\nT\n𝟏\n{\np\nt\n=\nM\ni\n}\n(\nM\ni\n−\ny\nt\n)\n|\n−\nϵ\n,\n0\n)\n=\nϵ\n-CE\nT\n.\n\\text{dist}(\\bar{a}^{T},B_{\\epsilon})=\\max\\left({\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\left\\lvert\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\mathbf{1}\\{p_{t}=M_{i}\\}(M_{i}-y_{t})\\right\\rvert}-\\epsilon,0\\right)=\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}.\nThus, the\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration condition (\n2\n) is equivalent to\nlim\nT\n→\n∞\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\ndist\n​\n(\na\n¯\nT\n,\nB\nϵ\n)\n]\n=\n0\n\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{dist}(\\bar{a}^{T},B_{\\epsilon})\\right]=0\n. If this condition is satisfied, we say that\na\n¯\nT\n\\bar{a}^{T}\napproaches\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nin the limit.\nBlackwell 1956\nestablished necessary and sufficient conditions for approachability.\nTheorem 2\n(Corollary to Theorem 3 by\nBlackwell 1956\n)\n.\nAssume the same setup as Calibration-Game-I, but the players receive a vector-valued reward\nr\nr\n, as defined component-wise in (\n7\n). The forecaster can ensure that\nlim\nT\n→\n∞\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\ndist\n​\n(\na\n¯\nT\n,\nB\nϵ\n)\n]\n=\n0\n\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{dist}(\\bar{a}^{T},B_{\\epsilon})\\right]=0\nif and only if for every\nv\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒴\n)\n=\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nv\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{Y})=[0,1]\n, there exists\nu\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\nu\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{X})\nsuch that the expected reward belongs to\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\n:\n∀\nv\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒴\n)\n,\n∃\nu\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\n:\n𝔼\np\n∼\nu\n,\ny\n∼\nv\n​\n[\nr\n⁡\n(\np\n,\ny\n)\n]\n=\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\nu\ni\n​\n(\n(\n1\n−\nv\n)\n⋅\nr\n⁡\n(\nM\ni\n,\n0\n)\n+\nv\n⋅\nr\n⁡\n(\nM\ni\n,\n1\n)\n)\n∈\nB\nϵ\n.\n\\forall v\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{Y}),\\ \\exists u\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{X}):\\mathbb{E}_{p\\sim u,y\\sim v}\\left[r(p,y)\\right]=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}u_{i}((1-v)\\cdot r(M_{i},0)+v\\cdot r(M_{i},1))\\in B_{\\epsilon}.\n(8)\nThe hypothetical situation considered in the theorem is akin to a one-shot game but with the order of the players reversed: nature plays\nv\nv\nfirst and the forecaster responds with\nu\nu\n. If the forecaster can\nrespond\nto every play by nature and ensure that the expected reward lies in\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\n, then the forecaster can ensure that\na\n¯\nT\n\\bar{a}^{T}\napproaches\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nin the sequential game (where nature goes second each time).\nAbernethy et al. 2011\ncall this\nresponse-satisfiability\n; in their words, Theorem\n2\nis interepreted as response-satisfiability\n⇔\n\\iff\napproachability.\nProposition 1\n.\nThe forecaster can exhibit response satisfiability (\n8\n). Thus the forecaster playing Calibration-Game-I can ensure\nlim\nT\n→\n∞\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\ndist\n​\n(\na\n¯\nT\n,\nB\nϵ\n)\n]\n=\n0\n\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{dist}(\\bar{a}^{T},B_{\\epsilon})\\right]=0\nand be\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibrated (\n2\n).\nThe proof of this well-known result is in Appendix\nA.3\n. A second question is of the rate at which the expected reward vector approaches the desired set. As reviewed in the introduction, a number of papers have shown that the rate of approachability for the\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration game is\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/\\sqrt{T})\n. We show that this rate cannot be improved.\n4.2\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\nlower bound for the\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration error rate\nTheorem 3\n.\nA forecaster playing Calibration-Game-I against an adversarial nature cannot achieve\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration at a rate faster than\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/\\sqrt{T})\n. That is, for every strategy of the forecaster, there is a strategy of nature that ensures\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n]\n=\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n.\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\right]=\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T}).\n(9)\nMannor and Perchet 2013\nanalyzed the convergence rate in approachability games and characterized conditions, which if satisfied by a target set\n𝒞\n\\mathcal{C}\n, entail that nature can ensure\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\ndist\n​\n(\na\n¯\nT\n,\n𝒞\n)\n]\n=\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{dist}(\\bar{a}^{T},\\mathcal{C})\\right]=\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\n. In particular, if these conditions are satisfied\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\n, Theorem\n3\nfollows immediately since\ndist\n​\n(\na\n¯\nT\n,\n𝒞\n)\n=\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n\\text{dist}(\\bar{a}^{T},\\mathcal{C})=\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\n.\nTheorem 4\n(Theorem 6.ii by\nMannor and Perchet 2013\n)\n.\nLet\n𝒞\n\\mathcal{C}\nbe a closed convex set that is (i) minimal approachable, and (ii) mixed approachable. Then\n𝒞\n\\mathcal{C}\ncannot be approached at a rate faster than\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/\\sqrt{T})\n, or in other words,\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\ndist\n​\n(\na\n¯\nT\n,\n𝒞\n)\n]\n=\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{dist}(\\bar{a}^{T},\\mathcal{C})\\right]=\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\n, where\na\n¯\nT\n\\bar{a}^{T}\nis the average reward vector.\nIn what follows, we introduce the conditions (i) minimal approachability and (ii) mixed approachability in the context of our calibration game, and show that they are satisfied by\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\n(Lemma\n3\nand Lemma\n4\nrespectively).\n4.3\nMinimal approachability\nFor a point\nu\n∈\nℝ\nm\nu\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\nand a convex set\nK\n⊆\nℝ\nm\nK\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^{m}\n, define the distance of\nu\nu\nfrom\nK\nK\nas\nd\nK\n​\n(\nu\n)\n=\ninf\nu\n′\n∈\nK\n‖\nu\n−\nu\n′\n‖\n2\nd_{K}(u)=\\inf_{u^{\\prime}\\in K}\\|u-u^{\\prime}\\|_{2}\n. For any\nλ\n>\n0\n\\lambda>0\n, a convex set\nK\n′\n⊆\nK\nK^{\\prime}\\subseteq K\nis said to be a\nλ\n\\lambda\n-shrinkage of\nK\nK\nif\n{\nu\n:\nd\nK\n′\n​\n(\nu\n)\n≤\nλ\n}\n⊆\nK\n\\{u:d_{K^{\\prime}}(u)\\leq\\lambda\\}\\subseteq K\n. In the following definition of minimal approachability, we implicitly assume that the set of action sets of the players and the corresponding rewards have been fixed, and the goal is to characterize which convex sets are approachable and which are not.\nDefinition 1\n.\nA set\nK\nK\nis\nminimal\napproachable if\nK\nK\nis approachable, but no\nλ\n\\lambda\n-shrinkage of\nK\nK\nis approachable.\nWe now show the first condition required by Theorem\n4\n.\nLemma 3\n.\nThe set\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nis minimal approachable.\nProof.\nProposition\n1\nshows that\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nis approachable so it remains to show that the minimality condition holds. Let\nK\nK\nbe a\nλ\n\\lambda\n-shrinkage of\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nfor some\nλ\n>\n0\n\\lambda>0\n. We first argue that\nK\n⊆\nB\nϵ\n−\nλ\nK\\subseteq B_{\\epsilon-\\lambda}\n. Suppose this were not the case, that is, there exists\nu\n∈\nK\nu\\in K\nsuch that\nu\n∉\nB\nϵ\n−\nλ\nu\\notin B_{\\epsilon-\\lambda}\n. By definition of the\nℓ\n1\n\\ell_{1}\n-ball\nB\nϵ\n−\nλ\nB_{\\epsilon-\\lambda}\n, this means that\n‖\nu\n‖\n1\n>\nϵ\n−\nλ\n\\|u\\|_{1}>\\epsilon-\\lambda\n. We will show that such a\nu\nu\ncannot belong to any\nλ\n\\lambda\n-shrinkage of\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\n, in particular it cannot belong to\nK\nK\n, leading to a contradiction.\nConsider the point\nu\n′\n=\nu\n+\n(\nλ\n/\n‖\nu\n‖\n2\n)\n​\nu\nu^{\\prime}=u+(\\lambda/\\|u\\|_{2})u\n. Note that\nd\nK\n​\n(\nu\n′\n)\n≤\n‖\nu\n−\nu\n′\n‖\n2\n=\nλ\nd_{K}(u^{\\prime})\\leq\\|u-u^{\\prime}\\|_{2}=\\lambda\n. Since\nK\nK\nis a\nλ\n\\lambda\n-shrinkage of\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\n, this implies that\nu\n′\n∈\nB\nϵ\nu^{\\prime}\\in B_{\\epsilon}\n, or\n‖\nu\n′\n‖\n1\n≤\nϵ\n\\|u^{\\prime}\\|_{1}\\leq\\epsilon\n. On the other hand, we have,\n(\nϵ\n≥\n)\n​\n‖\nu\n′\n‖\n1\n\\displaystyle(\\epsilon\\geq)\\ \\|u^{\\prime}\\|_{1}\n=\n‖\nu\n‖\n1\n​\n(\n1\n+\nλ\n/\n‖\nu\n‖\n2\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\|u\\|_{1}(1+\\lambda/\\|u\\|_{2})\n≥\n‖\nu\n‖\n1\n​\n(\n1\n+\nλ\n/\n‖\nu\n‖\n1\n)\n\\displaystyle\\geq\\|u\\|_{1}(1+\\lambda/\\|u\\|_{1})\n(for any vector\nv\n∈\nℝ\nm\nv\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\n,\n‖\nv\n‖\n1\n≥\n‖\nv\n‖\n2\n\\|v\\|_{1}\\geq\\|v\\|_{2}\n)\n=\n‖\nu\n‖\n1\n+\nλ\n>\n(\nϵ\n−\nλ\n)\n+\nλ\n=\nϵ\n,\n\\displaystyle=\\|u\\|_{1}+\\lambda>(\\epsilon-\\lambda)+\\lambda=\\epsilon,\nwhich is a contradiction. Thus\nK\n⊆\nB\nϵ\n−\nλ\nK\\subseteq B_{\\epsilon-\\lambda}\n, as claimed.\nIt follows that\nK\nK\nis approachable only if\nB\nϵ\n−\nλ\nB_{\\epsilon-\\lambda}\nis approachable. We now show that for every\nλ\n>\n0\n\\lambda>0\n,\nB\nϵ\n−\nλ\nB_{\\epsilon-\\lambda}\nis not approachable.\nAs in the proof of Proposition\n1\n, the\ni\ni\n-th component of the reward vector is given by\nu\ni\n​\n(\nM\ni\n−\nv\n)\nu_{i}(M_{i}-v)\n. Suppose\nv\n=\n1\n/\nm\nv=1/m\n. Then for every\nM\ni\nM_{i}\n,\n|\nM\ni\n−\nv\n|\n≥\n1\n/\n2\n​\nm\n=\nϵ\n\\left\\lvert M_{i}-v\\right\\rvert\\geq 1/2m=\\epsilon\n, by definition of\nm\nm\n. Thus\n|\nu\ni\n​\n(\nM\ni\n−\nv\n)\n|\n≥\nu\ni\n​\nϵ\n\\left\\lvert u_{i}(M_{i}-v)\\right\\rvert\\geq u_{i}\\epsilon\n, and\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n|\nu\ni\n​\n(\nM\ni\n−\nv\n)\n|\n≥\n∑\nu\ni\n​\nϵ\n=\nϵ\n\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\left\\lvert u_{i}(M_{i}-v)\\right\\rvert\\geq\\sum u_{i}\\epsilon=\\epsilon\n. Equivalently,\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nr\n⁡\n(\np\n,\ny\n)\n]\n∉\nB\nϵ\n−\nλ\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[r(p,y)\\right]\\notin B_{\\epsilon-\\lambda}\n. By Theorem\n2\n,\nB\nϵ\n−\nλ\nB_{\\epsilon-\\lambda}\nis not approachable.\n∎\nIn order to describe the second condition required by Theorem\n4\nand show that it holds for\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\n, we need additional technical setup. The following subsection serves this purpose.\n4.4\nReducing approachability to scalar-valued games\nThe vector-valued approachability game induces a number of scalar-valued min-max games, one for each direction in\nℝ\nm\n\\mathbb{R}^{m}\n. The value of these scalar games is closely connected to the question of approachability.\nConsider the approachability of\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nwith respect to individual directions, represented by arbitrary vectors\nq\n∈\nℝ\nm\nq\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\n(for intuition, one may equivalently think of\nq\nq\nbeing direction vectors, those with\nℓ\n2\n\\ell_{2}\n-norm equal to one, but this restriction is technically unnecessary; we stick to\nq\n∈\nℝ\nm\nq\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\n). Let\nc\n∈\nB\nϵ\nc\\in B_{\\epsilon}\nbe such that\nq\nq\nbelongs to the\nnormal cone\nof\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nat\nc\nc\n, that is,\n⟨\nc\n,\nq\n⟩\n=\nsup\nc\n′\n∈\nB\nϵ\n⟨\nc\n′\n,\nq\n⟩\n=\nϵ\n​\n‖\nq\n‖\n∞\n\\left\\langle c,q\\right\\rangle=\\sup_{c^{\\prime}\\in B_{\\epsilon}}\\left\\langle c^{\\prime},q\\right\\rangle=\\epsilon\\|q\\|_{\\infty}\n. We call such a pair\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n(c,q)\nas\nadmissible\n.\nConsider the following one-shot min-max game defined for every admissible\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n(c,q)\n:\nVal\n​\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n\\displaystyle\\text{Val}(c,q)\n=\nmin\nu\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\n⁡\nmax\nv\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n​\n⟨\n𝔼\np\n∼\nu\n,\ny\n∼\nv\n​\n[\nr\n⁡\n(\np\n,\ny\n)\n]\n−\nc\n,\nq\n⟩\n\\displaystyle=\\min_{u\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{X})}\\max_{v\\in[0,1]}\\left\\langle\\mathbb{E}_{p\\sim u,y\\sim v}\\left[r(p,y)\\right]-c,q\\right\\rangle\n=\nmin\nu\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\n⁡\nmax\nv\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n⁡\n(\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\nu\ni\n​\nq\ni\n​\n(\n(\n1\n−\nv\n)\n​\nM\ni\n+\nv\n⁡\n(\nM\ni\n−\n1\n)\n)\n−\n⟨\nc\n,\nq\n⟩\n)\n\\displaystyle=\\min_{u\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{X})}\\max_{v\\in[0,1]}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{m}u_{i}q_{i}((1-v)M_{i}+v(M_{i}-1))-\\left\\langle c,q\\right\\rangle\\right)\n=\nmin\nu\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\n⁡\nmax\nv\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n⁡\n(\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\nu\ni\n​\nq\ni\n​\n(\nM\ni\n−\nv\n)\n−\nϵ\n​\n‖\nq\n‖\n∞\n)\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\min_{u\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{X})}\\max_{v\\in[0,1]}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{m}u_{i}q_{i}(M_{i}-v)-\\epsilon\\|q\\|_{\\infty}\\right).\nTo appreciate the relationship between the\nVal\n​\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n\\text{Val}(c,q)\ngames and the\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\n-approachability game, consider the following. Suppose the forecaster can guarantee\none-shot approachability\n, that is, there exists a fixed\nu\n⋆\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\nu^{\\star}\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{X})\nsuch that for every\nv\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nv\\in[0,1]\n,\n𝔼\np\n∼\nu\n⋆\n,\ny\n∼\nv\n​\n[\nr\n⁡\n(\np\n,\ny\n)\n]\n∈\nB\nϵ\n\\mathbb{E}_{p\\sim u^{\\star},y\\sim v}\\left[r(p,y)\\right]\\in B_{\\epsilon}\n. By definition of the normal cone, for every admissible\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n(c,q)\n, and any\nc\n′\n∈\nB\nϵ\nc^{\\prime}\\in B_{\\epsilon}\n, it holds that\n⟨\nc\n′\n−\nc\n,\nq\n⟩\n≤\n0\n\\left\\langle c^{\\prime}-c,q\\right\\rangle\\leq 0\n. In particular,\n𝔼\np\n∼\nu\n⋆\n,\ny\n∼\nv\n​\n[\nr\n⁡\n(\np\n,\ny\n)\n]\n∈\nB\nϵ\n\\mathbb{E}_{p\\sim u^{\\star},y\\sim v}\\left[r(p,y)\\right]\\in B_{\\epsilon}\nis such a\nc\n′\nc^{\\prime}\n. It follows that for every admissible\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n(c,q)\n,\nVal(c, q)\n≤\n0\n\\text{Val(c, q)}\\leq 0\n.\nThis observation does not hold in the reverse direction: even if\nVal\n​\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n≤\n0\n\\text{Val}(c,q)\\leq 0\nfor every admissible\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n(c,q)\n, one-shot approachability need not hold. The intuition is that the optimal\nu\nu\nfor different\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n(c,q)\ncan be different, and it is unclear how to merge them to achieve one-shot forecasting for the approachability game. In a remarkable result,\nBlackwell 1956\nshowed that the result does hold in the reverse direction for the\nrepeated\napproachability game (as opposed to the one-shot approachability game).\nTheorem 5\n(Theorem 1 by\nBlackwell 1956\n)\n.\nA convex set\nK\nK\nis approachable if and only if for every admissible\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n(c,q)\n,\nVal\n​\n(\nc\n,\nq\n)\n≤\n0\n\\text{Val}(c,q)\\leq 0\n.\nThis condition has also been termed as halfspace-satisfiability by\nAbernethy et al. 2011\n. The result was stated in the language of convex cones by\nMannor and Perchet 2013\n. Notice that for our problem, the min-max game does not depend on\nc\nc\n, once we replace\n⟨\nc\n,\nq\n⟩\n\\left\\langle c,q\\right\\rangle\nwith\nϵ\n​\n‖\nq\n‖\n∞\n\\epsilon\\|q\\|_{\\infty}\n. We thus simplify notation and index our games only by\nq\n∈\nℝ\nm\nq\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\n:\nVal\n​\n(\nq\n)\n:=\nmin\nu\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\n⁡\nmax\nv\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n⁡\n(\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\nu\ni\n​\nq\ni\n​\n(\nM\ni\n−\nv\n)\n−\nϵ\n​\n‖\nq\n‖\n∞\n)\n.\n\\text{Val}(q):=\\min_{u\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{X})}\\max_{v\\in[0,1]}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{m}u_{i}q_{i}(M_{i}-v)-\\epsilon\\|q\\|_{\\infty}\\right).\n(10)\nWe know that\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nis approachable (Proposition\n1\n) and hence by Blackwell’s result, halfspace-satisfiability must hold. That is, for every\nq\n∈\nℝ\nm\nq\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\n,\nVal\n​\n(\nq\n)\n≤\n0\n\\text{Val}(q)\\leq 0\n. For completeness, we verify this in Proposition\n2\n(Appendix\nA\n).\nHaving defined the\nVal\n​\n(\nq\n)\n\\text{Val}(q)\ngames, we are now ready to define mixed approachability.\n4.5\nPure\n⋆\ngame and mixed approachability\nIn order to achieve a small value in the\nVal\n​\n(\nq\n)\n\\text{Val}(q)\ngame, the forecaster may play a randomized strategy, that is,\nu\n⋆\n≠\ne\ni\nu^{\\star}\\neq e_{i}\n, where\ne\ni\ne_{i}\nis one of the canonical basis vectors of\nℝ\nm\n\\mathbb{R}^{m}\n. On the other hand, since nature goes second, she has no incentive to randomize: there will exist an optimal strategy\nv\n⋆\n∈\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\nv^{\\star}\\in\\{0,1\\}\n. In the following ‘pure’ game, the forecaster is also not allowed to randomize over his actions.\nVal\np\n​\n(\nq\n)\n:=\nmin\np\n∈\n𝒳\n⁡\nmax\ny\n∈\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\n​\n⟨\nr\n⁡\n(\np\n,\ny\n)\n,\nq\n⟩\n−\nϵ\n​\n‖\nq\n‖\n∞\n.\n\\displaystyle\\text{Val${}^{\\text{p}}$}(q):=\\min_{p\\in\\mathcal{X}}\\max_{y\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\left\\langle r(p,y),q\\right\\rangle-\\epsilon\\|q\\|_{\\infty}.\n(11)\nThe superscript ‘p’ in\nVal\np\n​\n(\n⋅\n)\n\\text{Val${}^{\\text{p}}$}(\\cdot)\nrefers to the min-max game being over pure actions\np\n∈\n𝒳\np\\in\\mathcal{X}\nand\ny\n∈\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\ny\\in\\{0,1\\}\n. Let us refer to this as the pure game, and the game (\n10\n) as the mixed game.\nSuppose the approaching player (forecaster in our case) can ensure halfspace-satisfiability using only pure actions:\n∀\nq\n,\nVal\np\n​\n(\nq\n)\n≤\n0\n\\forall q,\\ \\text{Val${}^{\\text{p}}$}(q)\\leq 0\n.\nMannor and Perchet 2013\nshowed that if this is true then then the approaching player can ensure approachability at a fast rate of\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nn\n)\nO(1/n)\n. However, it is possible to achieve the fast rate even if the above condition is not true. Characterizing a situation where the fast rate is unachievable requires considering another game, whose value lies between the pure and mixed games. Define\n𝒳\n⋆\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{X}^{\\star}\n=\n{\np\n∈\n𝒳\n:\np\n∈\nsupport\n​\n(\nu\n⋆\n)\n​\n, where\nu\n⋆\nis some optimal mixed strategy for the forecaster\n}\n;\n\\displaystyle=\\{p\\in\\mathcal{X}:p\\in\\text{support}(u^{\\star})\\text{, where $u^{\\star}$ is some optimal mixed strategy for the forecaster}\\};\n𝒴\n⋆\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{Y}^{\\star}\n=\n{\ny\n∈\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\n:\ny\n∈\nsupport\n​\n(\nv\n⋆\n)\n​\n, where\nv\n⋆\nis some optimal mixed strategy for nature\n}\n.\n\\displaystyle=\\{y\\in\\{0,1\\}:y\\in\\text{support}(v^{\\star})\\text{, where $v^{\\star}$ is some optimal mixed strategy for nature}\\}.\nThen define the pure\n⋆\ngame and its value as follows,\nVal\n⋆\n​\n(\nq\n)\n:=\nmin\np\n∈\n𝒳\n⋆\n⁡\nmax\ny\n∈\n𝒴\n⋆\n​\n⟨\nr\n⁡\n(\np\n,\ny\n)\n,\nq\n⟩\n−\nϵ\n​\n‖\nq\n‖\n∞\n.\n\\text{Val${}^{\\star}$}(q):=\\min_{p\\in\\mathcal{X}^{\\star}}\\max_{y\\in\\mathcal{Y}^{\\star}}\\left\\langle r(p,y),q\\right\\rangle-\\epsilon\\|q\\|_{\\infty}.\n(12)\nIt can be shown that for any\nq\nq\n,\nVal\n​\n(\nq\n)\n≤\nVal\n⋆\n​\n(\nq\n)\n≤\nVal\np\n​\n(\nq\n)\n\\text{Val}(q)\\leq\\text{Val${}^{\\star}$}(q)\\leq\\text{Val${}^{\\text{p}}$}(q)\n(\nMannor and Perchet 2013\n)\n. We now define mixed approachability.\nDefinition 2\n.\nAn approachable set is said to be mixed approachable if there exists a\nq\n∈\nℝ\nm\nq\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\nsuch that while\nVal\n​\n(\nq\n)\n=\n0\n\\text{Val}(q)=0\n,\nVal\n⋆\n​\n(\nq\n)\n>\n0\n\\text{Val${}^{\\star}$}(q)>0\n.\nThe following lemma witnesses a\nq\nq\nthat shows that the mixed approachability condition required by Theorem\n4\nis satisfied. The witnessed\nq\nq\nin the proof is identified based on case (d) in the proof of Proposition\n2\n.\nLemma 4\n.\nThere exists a\nq\n∈\nℝ\nm\nq\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\nsuch that\nVal\n⋆\n​\n(\nq\n)\n>\nVal\n​\n(\nq\n)\n=\n0\n\\text{Val${}^{\\star}$}(q)>\\text{Val}(q)=0\n. Thus\nB\nϵ\nB_{\\epsilon}\nis mixed approachable.\nProof.\nSet\nq\n1\n:\nm\n−\n1\n=\n−\n1\nq_{1:m-1}=-1\n(i.e.\nq\ni\n=\n−\n1\nq_{i}=-1\nfor all\ni\n∈\n[\nm\n−\n1\n]\ni\\in[m-1]\n) and\nq\nm\n=\n1\nq_{m}=1\n. Let us compute\nVal\n​\n(\nq\n)\n\\text{Val}(q)\n. The game for nature reduces to maximizing\n(\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n−\n1\nu\ni\n−\nu\nm\n)\n​\nv\n(\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}u_{i}-u_{m})v\nwhich can be done by playing\nv\n=\n𝟏\n{\nu\nm\n≤\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n−\n1\nu\ni\n}\nv=\\mathbf{1}\\{u_{m}\\leq\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}u_{i}\\}\n. We perform case work to identify the optimal play for the forecaster.\n•\nIf\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n−\n1\nu\ni\n≤\nu\nm\n\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}u_{i}\\leq u_{m}\n,\nv\n⁡\n(\nu\nm\n−\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n−\n1\nu\ni\n)\n=\n0\nv(u_{m}-\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}u_{i})=0\n. The objective for the forecaster reduces to:\nmin\nu\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\n,\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n−\n1\nu\ni\n≤\nu\nm\n⁡\nu\nm\n​\nM\nm\n−\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n−\n1\nu\ni\n​\nM\ni\n−\nϵ\n.\n\\min_{u\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{X}),\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}u_{i}\\leq u_{m}}u_{m}M_{m}-\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}u_{i}M_{i}-\\epsilon.\nNote that\n0\n<\nM\n1\n<\nM\n2\n<\n…\n<\nM\nm\n0<M_{1}<M_{2}<\\ldots<M_{m}\n. Thus the forecaster would set the minimum possible value of\nu\nm\nu_{m}\n, which under the constraints is equal to\n0.5\n0.5\n. For the second term, the largest coefficient of a\nu\ni\nu_{i}\nin the summation is\nM\nm\n−\n1\nM_{m-1}\n. Thus in order to minimize, the forecaster would set the maximum possible value of\nu\nm\n−\n1\nu_{m-1}\n, which under the constraints is\n1\n−\nu\nm\n=\n0.5\n1-u_{m}=0.5\n. We conclude that the minimum occurs at\nu\nm\n=\nu\nm\n−\n1\n=\n0.5\nu_{m}=u_{m-1}=0.5\n,\nu\ni\n∉\n{\nm\n−\n1\n,\nm\n}\n=\n0\nu_{i\\notin\\{m-1,m\\}}=0\n.\nThe objective value is equal to\n0.5\n​\n(\nM\nm\n−\nM\nm\n−\n1\n)\n−\nϵ\n=\n0\n0.5(M_{m}-M_{m-1})-\\epsilon=0\n.\n•\nIf\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n−\n1\nu\ni\n≥\nu\nm\n\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}u_{i}\\geq u_{m}\n,\nv\n=\n1\nv=1\nis an optimal play for nature. The objective for the forecaster reduces to:\nmin\nu\n∈\nΔ\n⁡\n(\n𝒳\n)\n,\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n−\n1\nu\ni\n≥\nu\nm\n\\displaystyle\\min_{u\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{X}),\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}u_{i}\\geq u_{m}}\nu\nm\n​\n(\nM\nm\n−\n1\n)\n−\n∑\ni\n=\n1\nm\n−\n1\nu\ni\n​\n(\nM\ni\n−\n1\n)\n−\nϵ\n.\n\\displaystyle u_{m}(M_{m}-1)-\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}u_{i}(M_{i}-1)-\\epsilon.\nAs in the other case, this game is solved by observing that since\nM\n1\n<\nM\n2\n<\n…\n<\nM\nm\n<\n1\nM_{1}<M_{2}<\\ldots<M_{m}<1\n, the forecaster would want to put the maximum possible value on\nu\nm\nu_{m}\nwhich is\n0.5\n0.5\nunder the constraints. Among\nu\n1\n:\nm\n−\n1\nu_{1:m-1}\n, the multiplier of\n(\nM\nm\n−\n1\n−\n1\n)\n(M_{m-1}-1)\nhurts the least. Thus the forecaster sets\nu\nm\n−\n1\n=\n0.5\nu_{m-1}=0.5\nand\nu\ni\n∉\n{\nm\n−\n1\n,\nm\n}\nu_{i\\notin\\{m-1,m\\}}\n. The objective value at the minimum is equal to\n0\n0\n.\nIn each case, the forecaster’s optimal play is\nu\nm\n=\nu\nm\n−\n1\n=\n0.5\nu_{m}=u_{m-1}=0.5\n,\nu\ni\n∉\n{\nm\n−\n1\n,\nm\n}\n=\n0\nu_{i\\notin\\{m-1,m\\}}=0\n. This essentially makes nature’s action irrelevant; nature’s optimal response is any\nv\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\nv\\in[0,1]\n. Thus we have shown that\nVal\n​\n(\nq\n)\n=\n0\n\\text{Val}(q)=0\n,\n𝒳\n⋆\n=\n{\nM\nm\n−\n1\n,\nM\nm\n}\n\\mathcal{X}^{\\star}=\\{M_{m-1},M_{m}\\}\n, and\n𝒴\n⋆\n=\n{\n0\n,\n1\n}\n\\mathcal{Y}^{\\star}=\\{0,1\\}\n. Let us now compute\nVal\n⋆\n(\nq\n)\n=\nmin\np\n∈\n𝒳\n⋆\nmax\ny\n∈\n𝒴\n⋆\n(\n𝟏\n{\np\n=\nM\nm\n}\n(\nM\nm\n−\ny\n)\n−\n𝟏\n{\np\n=\nM\nm\n−\n1\n}\n(\nM\nm\n−\n1\n−\ny\n)\n−\nϵ\n)\n.\n\\text{Val${}^{\\star}$}(q)=\\min_{p\\in\\mathcal{X}^{\\star}}\\max_{y\\in\\mathcal{Y}^{\\star}}\\left(\\mathbf{1}\\{p=M_{m}\\}(M_{m}-y)-\\mathbf{1}\\{p=M_{m-1}\\}(M_{m-1}-y)-\\epsilon\\right).\nIf the forecast is\np\n=\nM\nm\np=M_{m}\n, nature can respond\ny\n=\n0\ny=0\nto achieve an overall objective of\n1\n−\n2\n​\nϵ\n>\n0\n1-2\\epsilon>0\n. (We have assumed\nm\n≥\n2\nm\\geq 2\nso that\nϵ\n<\n0.5\n\\epsilon<0.5\n.) If the forecast is\np\n=\nM\nm\n=\n1\np=M_{m=1}\n, nature can respond\ny\n=\n1\ny=1\nto achieve an overall objective of\n2\n​\nϵ\n>\n0\n2\\epsilon>0\n. Thus,\nVal\n⋆\n​\n(\nq\n)\n>\n0\n\\text{Val${}^{\\star}$}(q)>0\n.\n∎\n4.6\nRelationship to previous lower bounds for calibration\nQiao and Valiant 2021\nrecently constructed a strategy of nature that ensures that the calibration error of any forecaster playing Calibration-Game-I satisfies\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nCE\nT\n]\n=\nΩ\n⁡\n(\nT\n−\n0.472\n)\n.\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{CE}_{T}\\right]=\\Omega(T^{-0.472}).\nThis bound is interesting on its own and neither weaker nor stronger than the bound we show in Theorem\n3\n. By studying\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n]\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\right]\n, we allow the forecaster a slack of\nϵ\n\\epsilon\nin his calibration error, which is standard in several earlier cited works (see Section\n1.2\n), and may be sufficient in practice given that the forecasts are themselves on a\n2\n​\nϵ\n2\\epsilon\n-grid.\nQiao and Valiant 2021\nalso noted that\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nCE\nT\n]\n=\nΩ\n⁡\n(\nT\n−\n0.5\n)\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{CE}_{T}\\right]=\\Omega(T^{-0.5})\ncan be forced using a\nBernoulli strategy\n: at each time step, nature plays\ny\nt\n∼\nBernoulli\n​\n(\np\n)\ny_{t}\\sim\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\nfor some fixed\np\n∈\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\np\\in[0,1]\nunknown to the forecaster. However, in Appendix\nB\n, we provide initial (but not conclusive) evidence that the Bernoulli strategy seems insufficient to guarantee\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n]\n=\nΩ\n⁡\n(\nT\n−\n0.5\n)\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\right]=\\Omega(T^{-0.5})\n. We construct an\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibrated forecaster that satisfies\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nCE\nT\n−\nϵ\n]\n≤\nO\n⁡\n(\npoly\n​\n(\nlog\n⁡\nT\n)\n/\nT\n)\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\text{CE}_{T}-\\epsilon\\right]\\leq O(\\text{poly}(\\log T)/T)\nfor the Bernoulli strategy (\npoly\n​\n(\nlog\n⁡\nT\n)\n\\text{poly}(\\log T)\ncorresponds to polynomial terms in\nlog\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\n\\log(T)\n). We conjecture that the stronger statement\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nϵ\n​\n-CE\nT\n]\n=\n𝔼\n⁡\n[\nmax\n⁡\n(\nCE\nT\n−\nϵ\n,\n0\n)\n]\n≤\nO\n⁡\n(\npoly\n​\n(\nlog\n⁡\nT\n)\n/\nT\n)\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\epsilon\\text{-CE}_{T}\\right]=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\max(\\text{CE}_{T}-\\epsilon,0)\\right]\\leq O(\\text{poly}(\\log T)/T)\nalso holds, which would mean that the Bernoulli strategy is insufficient to derive a\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\nbound on the\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration rate (as shown by Theorem\n3\n).\n5\nSummary\nThis paper connects three rich areas of the literature in a natural way: online calibration, the power of two choices, and imprecise probability. In summary, we show that by allowing the forecaster to output a deterministic short interval of probabilities (of length at most\n2\n​\nϵ\n2\\epsilon\n), we can achieve a faster rate of\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/T)\nfor\nϵ\n\\epsilon\n-calibration against a fully adaptive adversarial reality who presents the binary outcome after observing the interval forecast. This should be compared to the\nΘ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n\\Theta(1/\\sqrt{T})\nrate achievable with randomized point forecasts (the upper bound is a seminal result by\nFoster and Vohra 1998\n, the lower bound is ours), or the\nΘ\n⁡\n(\n1\n)\n\\Theta(1)\nfor deterministic point forecasts.\nArguably, such narrow intervals are as practically interpretable as point forecasts, and since some sort of binning anyway underlies most calibration algorithms, it also feels natural to allow the forecaster to express their uncertainty in this fashion, especially since it avoids randomization and improves calibration. Thus, we view our work as a theoretical contribution with clear practical implications.\nSeveral open questions remain, since we open a rather new line of investigation. We mention two: (a) lower bounds for our setting are unknown, and (b) we don’t know if models providing more than two choices could possibly improve the rate further. We suspect that\n1\n/\nT\n1/T\nis the optimal rate since it corresponds to constant cumulative calibration error (without normalization by\nT\nT\n), which is incurred at\nt\n=\n1\nt=1\nitself and seems unavoidable. Finally, it would be interesting to (c) figure out multidimensional analogs of our paper. We leave these for future work.\nWe also note some POTC-style results in online learning.\nNeu and Zhivotovskiy 2020\nshow that for expert-based classification, providing the learner the choice to abstain from making a prediction improves the regret from\nΩ\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\n\\Omega(1/\\sqrt{T})\nto\nO\n⁡\n(\n1\n/\nT\n)\nO(1/T)\n, similar to what we obtain in Theorem\n1\n. In zero-order convex optimization or bandit convex optimization, allowing the learner two function evaluations enables the unknown gradient to be estimated using finite difference, leading to significantly improved rates (\nAgarwal et al. 2010\nand follow-up work). For example, in the strongly convex case the rate improves from\nΩ\n⁡\n(\nT\n)\n\\Omega(\\sqrt{T})\nto\nO\n⁡\n(\nlog\n⁡\nT\n)\nO(\\log T)\n. Such improvements also hold for non-smooth functions\n(\nShamir 2017\n)\n. It would be interesting to consider POTC setups for multi-armed bandits (two arm-pulls instead of one) or expert-based online learning (choosing two experts instead of randomizing or choosing one expert).\nAcknowledgements\nCG would like to thank the Bloomberg Data Science Ph.D. Fellowship for their generous support. We are grateful to Yusha Liu, Mingda Qiao, and the anonymous COLT reviewers, for feedback that helped improve a previous version of the paper.\nReferences\nAbernethy et al. [2011]\nJacob Abernethy, Peter L Bartlett, and Elad Hazan.\nBlackwell approachability and no-regret learning are equivalent.\nIn\nConference on Learning Theory\n, 2011.\nAgarwal et al. [2010]\nAlekh Agarwal, Ofer Dekel, and Lin Xiao.\nOptimal algorithms for online convex optimization with multi-point\nbandit feedback.\nIn\nConference on Learning Theory\n, 2010.\nAzar et al. [1994]\nYossi Azar, Andrei Z Broder, Anna R Karlin, and Eli Upfal.\nBalanced allocations.\nIn\nSymposium on Theory of Computing\n, 1994.\nBlackwell [1956]\nDavid Blackwell.\nAn analog of the minimax theorem for vector payoffs.\nPacific Journal of Mathematics\n, 6(1):1–8,\n1956.\nBrier [1950]\nGlenn W Brier.\nVerification of forecasts expressed in terms of probability.\nMonthly Weather Review\n, 78(1):1–3, 1950.\nBroder and Mitzenmacher [2001]\nAndrei Broder and Michael Mitzenmacher.\nUsing multiple hash functions to improve IP lookups.\nIn\nIEEE International Conference on Computer Communications\n,\n2001.\nCesa-Bianchi and Lugosi [2006]\nNicolo Cesa-Bianchi and Gábor Lugosi.\nPrediction, learning, and games\n.\nCambridge University Press, 2006.\nCole et al. [1998]\nRichard Cole, Bruce M Maggs, Friedhelm Meyer auf der Heide, Michael\nMitzenmacher, Andréa W Richa, Klaus Schröder, Ramesh K Sitaraman, and\nBerthold Vöcking.\nRandomized protocols for low-congestion circuit routing in multistage\ninterconnection networks.\nIn\nSymposium on Theory of Computing\n, 1998.\nDawid [1982]\nA Philip Dawid.\nThe well-calibrated Bayesian.\nJournal of the American Statistical Association\n, 77(379):605–610, 1982.\nDawid [1985]\nA Philip Dawid.\nComment: The impossibility of inductive inference.\nJournal of the American Statistical Association\n, 80(390):340–341, 1985.\nde Cooman and De Bock [2022]\nGert de Cooman and Jasper De Bock.\nRandomness is inherently imprecise.\nInternational Journal of Approximate Reasoning\n, 141:28–68, 2022.\nDwivedi et al. [2019]\nRaaz Dwivedi, Ohad N Feldheim