ors; ( θ 1 , … , θ I ) ∈ Θ I (\\theta_{1},\\ldots,\\theta_{I})\\in\\Theta_{I} and ( π 1 , j , … , π I , j ) ∈ Θ I (\\pi_{1,j},\\ldots,\\pi_{I,j})\\in\\Theta_{I} are the associated subjective beliefs as we defined in previous sections. Under the above setting, the trader’s decision problem 𝒫 j ​ ( 𝐱 j ) \\mathcal{P}_{j}(\\mathbf{x}_{j}) generally lacks a closed-form solution. Nonetheless, when all participants have identical risk parameters, the trader’s decision can be solved as follows. Proposition 5.1. In the CRRA uitlity-based market definied by ( 17 ) with γ = γ m \\gamma=\\gamma_{m} , given { 𝐱 j , t } | j = 1 J \\{\\mathbf{x}_{j,t}\\}|_{j=1}^{J} and 𝐲 t \\mathbf{y}_{t} at time t t , the solution of the j j -th trader’s decision problem 𝒫 j ​ ( 𝐱 j , t ) \\mathcal{P}_{j}{(\\mathbf{x}_{j,t})} is z i ∗ = ( y i , t ⋅ κ i , j − x i , j , t ⋅ λ j ∗ ) / ( λ j ∗ + κ i , j ) z^{*}_{i}=(y_{i,t}\\cdot\\kappa_{i,j}-x_{i,j,t}\\cdot\\lambda^{*}_{j})/(\\lambda^{*}_{j}+\\kappa_{i,j}) for i = 1 , … , I i=1,\\ldots,I , and the market states are updated to (18) y i , t + 1 = λ j ∗ κ i , j + λ j ∗ ​ ( x i , j , t + y i , t ) , x i , j , t + 1 = κ i , j κ i , j + λ j ∗ ​ ( x i , j , t + y i , t ) , \\displaystyle y_{i,t+1}=\\frac{\\lambda_{j}^{*}}{\\kappa_{i,j}+\\lambda_{j}^{*}}\\big(x_{i,j,t}+y_{i,t}\\big),~~x_{i,j,t+1}=\\frac{\\kappa_{i,j}}{\\kappa_{i,j}+\\lambda_{j}^{*}}\\big(x_{i,j,t}+y_{i,t}\\big), where κ i , j ≜ ( π i , j / θ i ) 1 1 − γ \\kappa_{i,j}\\triangleq\\big(\\pi_{i,j}\\big/\\theta_{i}\\big)^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma}} , for i = 1 , … , I i=1,\\ldots,I and j = 1 , … , J j=1,\\ldots,J , and λ j ∗ &gt; 0 \\lambda_{j}^{*}&gt;0 is the solution to the following equation, (19) ∑ i = 1 I ( λ j ∗ λ j ∗ + κ i , j ​ ( x i , j , t + y i , t ) ) γ ​ θ i = W 0 γ . \\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\left(\\frac{\\lambda_{j}^{*}}{\\lambda_{j}^{*}+\\kappa_{i,j}}(x_{i,j,t}+y_{i,t})\\right)^{\\gamma}\\theta_{i}=W_{0}^{\\gamma}. This proposition shows that in a CRRA-utility-based market, the initial wealth of each trader is recursively adapted into the wealth vector 𝐲 t \\mathbf{y}_{t} of the market maker, hence the limiting 𝐲 ∗ \\mathbf{y}^{*} may be affected by the initial wealth allocation, as opposed to the risk measure-based markets. The following example demonstrates how the initial wealth, risk parameters, and trading sequence could affect limiting price. Example 5.2. We consider a CRRA utility-based market with three assets ( I = 3 I=3 ) and two traders ( J = 2 J=2 ) whose belief are 𝝅 1 = ( 0.2 , 0.2 , 0.6 ) \\boldsymbol{\\pi}_{1}=(0.2,0.2,0.6) and 𝝅 2 = ( 0.6 , 0.1 , 0.3 ) \\boldsymbol{\\pi}_{2}=(0.6,0.1,0.3) , respectively. The market maker’s parameters are θ 1 = θ 2 = θ 3 = 1 / 3 \\theta_{1}=\\theta_{2}=\\theta_{3}=1/3 , W 0 = 5 W_{0}=5 and γ m = γ \\gamma_{m}=\\gamma . The two traders’ initial wealth are w 1 , 0 = w 1 w_{1,0}=w^{1} and w 2 , 0 = w 2 w_{2,0}=w^{2} where we vary the parameters ( w 1 , w 2 ) (w^{1},w^{2}) to examine their impact on the limiting prices. In Trading Process 1 , we consider two trading sequences: 𝒮 1 = { 1 , 2 , 1 , 2 , … } \\mathcal{S}_{1}=\\{1,2,1,2,\\ldots\\} and 𝒮 2 = { 2 , 1 , 2 , 1 , … } \\mathcal{S}_{2}=\\{2,1,2,1,\\ldots\\} . 14 14 14 Note that if the same trader arrives twice in a row, the second trade is vacuous because the first one is already utility maximizing. Figure 1(a) depicts the utility value trajectories generated by these trading processes for different ( w 1 , w 2 ) (w^{1},w^{2}) when γ = 0.5 \\gamma=0.5 . The Pareto efficient frontier (represented by the solid line) is obtained by solving problem 𝒫 po ​ ( 𝝂 ) \\mathcal{P}_{\\textrm{po}}(\\boldsymbol{\\nu}) for different 𝝂 \\boldsymbol{\\nu} . This figure illustrates that, for a given wealth distribution and fixed trading sequence (either 𝒮 1 \\mathcal{S}_{1} or 𝒮 2 \\mathcal{S}_{2} ), the utility value trajectories indeed converge to points on the Pareto efficient frontier, confirming the result in Theorem 3.2 . However, even for identical initial wealth, different trading sequences may generate different utility paths (and hence different limiting prices). Figure 1(b) demonstrates the impact of the risk parameter. It shows the price trajectories of p 1 , t p_{1,t} and p 3 , t p_{3,t} when w 1 = w 2 = 10 w^{1}=w^{2}=10 for the trading sequence S 1 S_{1} . This illustration makes it clear that a change in the risk aversion parameter, e.g., from γ = 0.5 \\gamma=0.5 to γ = 0.7 \\gamma=0.7 , has an effect on the limiting prices. Table 1 provides details of the limiting prices for different ( w 1 , w 2 ) (w^{1},w^{2}) and γ \\gamma . A closer examination reveals that even with fixed initial wealth, the limiting price may vary under different trading sequences, although both 𝒮 1 \\mathcal{S}_{1} and 𝒮 2 \\mathcal{S}_{2} satisfy the IP property. 15 15 15 It is worth noting that when there are only two traders, 𝒮 1 \\mathcal{S}_{1} and 𝒮 2 \\mathcal{S}_{2} are effectively the only possible sequences that satisfy the IP property. The impact of the trading sequence is also discussed in ( Sethi and Vaughan 2016 ) . However, among these factors, the trading sequence has less impact compared to the initial wealth and the risk parameter. Figure 1 . The utility and price trajectories in Example 5.2 (a) Trajectory of the utility (b) Trajectory of the price Table 1. Limiting wealth allocation and price in Example 5.2 Wealth Risk Limiting Price w 1 w^{1} w 2 w^{2} γ \\gamma p 1 ∗ p_{1}^{*} p 2 ∗ p_{2}^{*} p 3 ∗ p_{3}^{*} 10 10 0.5 (0.7) 𝒮 1 : \\mathcal{S}_{1}: 0.4044 (0.3959) 0.1839 (0.1849) 0.4117 (0.4192) 𝒮 2 : \\mathcal{S}_{2}: 0.4045 (0.4004) 0.1838 (0.1841) 0.4117 (0.4155) 8 12 0.5 (0.7) 𝒮 1 : \\mathcal{S}_{1}: 0.4436 (0.4449) 0.1745 (0.1726) 0.3819 (0.3825) 𝒮 2 : \\mathcal{S}_{2}: 0.4440 (0.4800) 0.1743 (0.1741) 0.3817 (0.3459) 12 8 0.5 (0.7) 𝒮 1 : \\mathcal{S}_{1}: 0.3624 (0.3463) 0.1933 (0.1959) 0.4443 (0.4578) 𝒮 2 : \\mathcal{S}_{2}: 0.3621 (0.3776) 0.1933 (0.2044) 0.4446 (0.4180) As demonstrated in Example 5.2 , the trading sequence can impact the limiting price. A key observation for understanding this phenomenon is that 𝒮 1 \\mathcal{S}_{1} can be equivalently viewed as 𝒮 2 \\mathcal{S}_{2} preceded by an extra Trader 1 action. Such an action results in an initial market state different from that of the original 𝒮 2 \\mathcal{S}_{2} . As Proposition 5.1 suggests, different initial wealth results in a different limiting price; thus the limiting prices for the two sequences 𝒮 1 \\mathcal{S}_{1} and 𝒮 2 \\mathcal{S}_{2} are different. To illustrate, we consider Example 5.2 with w 1 = w 2 = 10 w^{1}=w^{2}=10 . The extra action of 𝒮 1 \\mathcal{S}_{1} (the first trade) yields market states 𝐱 1 = ( 7.49 , 7.49.13.50 ) \\mathbf{x}_{1}=(7.49,7.49.13.50) , 𝐱 2 = ( 10 , 10 , 10 ) \\mathbf{x}_{2}=(10,10,10) , and 𝐲 = ( 7.51 , 7.51 , 1.50 ) \\mathbf{y}=(7.51,7.51,1.50) . After the first trade, sequence 𝒮 1 \\mathcal{S}_{1} becomes identical to 𝒮 2 \\mathcal{S}_{2} . However, for 𝒮 2 \\mathcal{S}_{2} , the initial condition is 𝐱 1 = ( 10 , 10 , 10 ) \\mathbf{x}_{1}=(10,10,10) , 𝐱 2 = ( 10 , 10 , 10 ) \\mathbf{x}_{2}=(10,10,10) , and 𝐲 = ( 5 , 5 , 5 ) \\mathbf{y}=(5,5,5) , which is different from the states yield from 𝒮 1 \\mathcal{S}_{1} after the first trading, thus the two sequences result in different limiting prices. Practically, traders interact with the market maker randomly, leading to a random trading path. Consequently, the limiting price becomes a trading-sequence-dependent random variable. However, our next result characterizes the limiting price for a given trading sequence and demonstrates that price invariance can be achieved when the trader population is large. Theorem 5.3. For the CRRA-utility-based market, we have the following results: (i) Given a trading sequence 𝒮 ∈ Φ \\mathcal{S}\\in\\Phi , the Trading Process 1 converges to the limiting wealth allocation { 𝐱 j ∗ } \\{\\mathbf{x}_{j}^{*}\\} characterized by problem 𝒫 po ​ ( 𝛎 ∗ ) \\mathcal{P}_{\\textrm{po}}(\\boldsymbol{\\nu}^{*}) with some weights 𝛎 ∗ = ( ν 1 ∗ , … , ν J ∗ ) ∈ ℝ + J ∖ { 𝟎 } \\boldsymbol{\\nu}^{*}=(\\nu_{1}^{*},\\ldots,\\nu_{J}^{*})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{J}_{+}\\setminus\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} ; and the limiting price is given by (20) p i ∗ = ξ ∗ i / ∑ k = 1 I ξ k ∗ , i = 1 , … , I , \\displaystyle p_{i}^{*}=\\xi^{*}_{i}\\Big/\\sum_{k=1}^{I}\\xi_{k}^{*},~~~i=1,\\ldots,I, where 𝛏 ∗ = ( ξ 1 ∗ , … , ξ I ∗ ) \\boldsymbol{\\xi}^{*}=(\\xi_{1}^{*},\\ldots,\\xi_{I}^{*}) is the unique solution of the following equations: (21) ξ i ∗ \\displaystyle\\xi_{i}^{*} = ( ∑ j = 1 J ( ν j ∗ ) 1 1 − γ ⋅ π i , j 1 1 − γ + ( ξ i ∗ ) γ − γ m ( 1 − γ ) ​ ( 1 − γ m ) ⋅ ℒ ⁡ ( 𝝃 ∗ , 𝜽 ) ⋅ W 0 ⋅ θ i 1 1 − γ m w 0 all ) 1 − γ , i = 1 , … , I \\displaystyle=\\Bigg(\\frac{\\sum\\limits^{J}_{j=1}(\\nu_{j}^{*})^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma}}\\cdot\\pi_{i,j}^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma}}+(\\xi_{i}^{*})^{\\frac{\\gamma-\\gamma_{m}}{(1-\\gamma)(1-\\gamma_{m})}}\\cdot\\mathcal{L}(\\boldsymbol{\\xi}^{*},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\cdot W_{0}\\cdot\\theta_{i}^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma_{m}}}}{w_{0}^{\\textrm{all}}}\\Bigg)^{1-\\gamma},~~i=1,\\ldots,I where ℒ ⁡ ( 𝛏 , 𝛉 ) ≜ ( ∑ k = 1 I θ k ​ ( θ k / ξ k ) γ m 1 − γ m ) − 1 γ m \\mathcal{L}(\\boldsymbol{\\xi},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\triangleq\\left(\\sum\\limits_{k=1}^{I}\\theta_{k}(\\theta_{k}/\\xi_{k})^{\\frac{\\gamma_{m}}{1-\\gamma_{m}}}\\right)^{-\\frac{1}{\\gamma_{m}}} . (ii) In Trading Process 1 , if traders’ beliefs 𝛑 j \\boldsymbol{\\pi}_{j} and initial wealth w j , 0 w_{j,0} are independently and identically distributed random variables for j = 1 , … , J j=1,\\ldots,J ; with each trader having an equal probability of trading with the market maker at each trading instance, then the solution { ξ i ∗ } | i = 1 I \\{\\xi_{i}^{*}\\}|_{i=1}^{I} defined in Eq. ( 21 ) and the associated limiting price { p i ∗ } | i = 1 I \\{p_{i}^{*}\\}|_{i=1}^{I} generated by each trading sample path converge almost surely to a pair of constant vectors as the trader population J → ∞ J\\rightarrow\\infty . The first result in Theorem 5.3 provides an explicit method for computing the limiting price 𝐩 ∗ \\mathbf{p}^{*} for a sepecified trading sequence with the associated weighting parameters 𝝂 ∗ \\boldsymbol{\\nu}^{*} . It is worth noting that the functional form of the limiting price ( 20 ) and ( 21 ) remain consistent, as the influence of the trading sequence is solely reflected through the weights 𝝂 ∗ \\boldsymbol{\\nu}^{*} . The second result in Theorem 5.3 is an interesting product of the price formula. One can consider it a prediction market analogue of the “Law of Large Numbers” . It implies that in a randomly trading market with a large trader population, the variation of limiting price induced by different trading sequences becomes negligible. This underscores the significance of trader participation in prediction markets, as it not only enhances liquidity but also increases the reliability of limiting prices. Imagine a prediction market that yields predictions (limiting price) that are heavily dependent on the trading sequence. In such a case, one cannot know whether its prediction truly represents the overall beliefs or only happens to be a biased result of a specific trading sequence. Such uncertainty will undermine the interpretability of prices and jeopardize the prediction accuracy. Theorem 5.3 also provides several potentially useful insights for market maker designing. Combining Eqs. ( 20 ) and ( 21 ), the limiting price has the following decomposition: (22) p i ∗ = ( ∑ j = 1 J ( ν j ∑ k = 1 I ξ k ∗ ) 1 ( 1 − γ ) w 0 all ( π i , j ) 1 1 − γ + ( p i ∗ ) γ − γ m ( 1 − γ ) ​ ( 1 − γ m ) ⋅ ℒ ( 𝐩 ∗ , 𝜽 ) W 0 w 0 all θ i 1 1 − γ m ) 1 − γ , i = 1 , … , I . \\displaystyle p_{i}^{*}=\\Bigg(\\sum_{j=1}^{J}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\nu_{j}}{\\sum_{k=1}^{I}\\xi_{k}^{*}}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{(1-\\gamma)}}}{w_{0}^{\\textrm{all}}}\\left(\\pi_{i,j}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma}}+(p_{i}^{*})^{\\frac{\\gamma-\\gamma_{m}}{(1-\\gamma)(1-\\gamma_{m})}}\\cdot\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{p}^{*},\\bm{\\theta})\\frac{W_{0}}{w_{0}^{\\textrm{all}}}\\theta_{i}^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma_{m}}}\\Bigg)^{1-\\gamma},\\quad i=1,\\ldots,I. This decomposition can be explained as follows. First, a smaller initial wealth W 0 W_{0} can reduce the market maker’s impact on the limiting price. Second, a market maker with higher risk aversion (smaller γ m \\gamma_{m} ) also diminishes its influence on the limiting price because ( p i ∗ ) γ − γ m ( 1 − γ ) ​ ( 1 − γ m ) (p_{i}^{*})^{\\frac{\\gamma-\\gamma_{m}}{(1-\\gamma)(1-\\gamma_{m})}} is a decreasing function of γ m \\gamma_{m} for p i ∗ ∈ ( 0 , 1 ) p_{i}^{*}\\in(0,1) . Third, the function ℒ ⁡ ( 𝐩 ∗ , 𝜽 ) \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{p}^{*},\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) exhibits an intriguing suppressive effect designed to mitigate the price distortion caused by the market maker’ externality. To explain further, ℒ ⁡ ( 𝐩 ∗ , 𝜽 ) \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{p}^{*},\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) reaches its maximum value of 1 1 when 𝐩 ∗ = 𝜽 \\mathbf{p}^{*}=\\boldsymbol{\\theta} . When the market consensus 𝐩 ∗ \\mathbf{p}^{*} significantly deviates from the market maker’s belief 𝜽 \\boldsymbol{\\theta} , the term ℒ ⁡ ( 𝐩 ∗ , 𝜽 ) \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{p}^{*},\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) lessens its impact, effectively reducing the market maker’s influence on the limiting price. It is worth noting that such a suppressive effect stems from the utility preserving condition ( 8 ), underscoring the additional benefits of the MU-based mechanism. With the above results, in the next section, we investigate how to approximate the limiting price under a finite trader scenario through choosing an appropriate weight 𝝂 ∗ \\boldsymbol{\\nu}^{*} . 5.2. Heuristic Weighting Parameters and Price Formula The previous section has emphasized the vital role of determining the weighting parameters, 𝝂 ∗ \\boldsymbol{\\nu}^{*} , in solving problem 𝒫 po ​ ( 𝝂 ∗ ) \\mathcal{P}_{\\textrm{po}}(\\boldsymbol{\\nu}^{*}) to establish the limiting price. Given the practical difficulty of obtaining the “true” weighting parameters 𝝂 ∗ \\boldsymbol{\\nu}^{*} before trading converges, we propose an approximation using deterministic weights. Drawing inspiration from previous analysis, which highlights Pareto optimality of the limiting wealth allocation and underscores the significance of both the initial wealth distribution w j , 0 w_{j,0} and the risk parameter γ \\gamma in shaping the limiting price (e.g., see Table 1 ), we introduce the concept of Pareto Optimal Induced (POI) weights, denoted as 𝝂 ^ = ( ν ^ 1 , … , ν ^ J ) \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\nu}}=(\\hat{\\nu}_{1},\\ldots,\\hat{\\nu}_{J}) defined as: (23) ν ^ j = ( w j , 0 ) 1 − γ , j = 1 , … , J . \\displaystyle\\hat{\\nu}_{j}=\\big(w_{j,0}\\big)^{1-\\gamma},~~j=1,\\ldots,J. Applying Theorem 5.3 , we replace 𝝂 ∗ \\boldsymbol{\\nu}^{*} with the POI weights 𝝂 ^ \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\nu}} in Eq. ( 21 ) to calculate the associated POI limiting price, denoted as 𝐩 ^ = ( p ^ 1 , … , p ^ I ) \\hat{\\mathbf{p}}=(\\hat{p}_{1},\\ldots,\\hat{p}_{I}) , with its components given as follows: (24) p ^ i = ξ ^ i / ∑ k = 1 I ξ ^ k , i = 1 , … , I , \\displaystyle\\hat{p}_{i}=\\hat{\\xi}_{i}\\Big/\\sum_{k=1}^{I}\\hat{\\xi}_{k},~~~i=1,\\ldots,I,\\ where 𝝃 ^ = ( ξ ^ 1 , … , ξ ^ I ) \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}}=(\\hat{\\xi}_{1},\\ldots,\\hat{\\xi}_{I}) is the unique solution of the following equation, (25) ξ ^ i = ( ∑ j = 1 J w j , 0 ⋅ π i , j 1 1 − γ + ξ ^ i γ − γ m ( 1 − γ ) ​ ( 1 − γ m ) ⋅ ℒ ⁡ ( 𝝃 ^ , 𝜽 ) ⋅ W 0 ⋅ θ i 1 1 − γ m w 0 all ) 1 − γ , i = 1 , … , I . \\displaystyle\\hat{\\xi}_{i}=\\Bigg(\\frac{\\sum\\limits^{J}_{j=1}w_{j,0}\\cdot\\pi_{i,j}^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma}}+\\hat{\\xi}_{i}^{\\frac{\\gamma-\\gamma_{m}}{(1-\\gamma)(1-\\gamma_{m})}}\\cdot\\mathcal{L}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\cdot W_{0}\\cdot\\theta_{i}^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma_{m}}}}{w_{0}^{\\textrm{all}}}\\Bigg)^{1-\\gamma},~~i=1,\\ldots,I. Now we provide some interpretation for the POI limiting price. Eq ( 25 ) implies that the price p ^ i \\hat{p}_{i} is directly linked to a weighted average that considers all participants’ wealth and risk-adjusted beliefs regarding the i i -th asset. More specifically, the term ∑ j = 1 J w j , 0 ⋅ π i , j 1 1 − γ \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^{J}w_{j,0}\\cdot\\pi_{i,j}^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma}} represents the avarage risk-adjusted belief contributed from all the traders weighted by their own wealth. In contrast, the term ξ ^ i γ − γ m ( 1 − γ ) ​ ( 1 − γ m ) ⋅ ℒ ⁡ ( 𝝃 ^ , 𝜽 ) ⋅ W 0 ⋅ θ i 1 1 − γ m \\hat{\\xi}_{i}^{\\frac{\\gamma-\\gamma_{m}}{(1-\\gamma)(1-\\gamma_{m})}}\\cdot\\mathcal{L}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\cdot W_{0}\\cdot\\theta_{i}^{\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma_{m}}} which combines the risk difference and belief divergence, controls the contribution of market maker’s risk-adjusted belief in such a price. The POI price formula ( 24 ) and ( 25 ) encompass specific market scenarios as special cases. When both market maker and traders adopt the logarithmic utilities, i.e., γ = γ m = 0 \\gamma=\\gamma_{m}=0 in ( 17 ), Eq. ( 25 ) will simplify to: (26) ξ ^ i = ∑ j = 1 J w j , 0 ⋅ π i , j + ℒ 0 ​ ( 𝝃 ^ , 𝜽 ) ⋅ W 0 ⋅ θ i w 0 all , i = 1 , … , I , \\displaystyle\\hat{\\xi}_{i}=\\frac{\\sum\\limits^{J}_{j=1}w_{j,0}\\cdot\\pi_{i,j}+\\mathcal{L}_{0}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\cdot W_{0}\\cdot\\theta_{i}}{w_{0}^{\\textrm{all}}},~~~i=1,\\ldots,I, where ℒ 0 ( 𝝃 ^ , 𝜽 ) = exp ( − ∑ k = 1 I θ k log ( θ k / ξ ^ k ) ) \\mathcal{L}_{0}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})=\\exp(-\\sum\\limits_{k=1}^{I}\\theta_{k}\\log(\\theta_{k}/\\hat{\\xi}_{k})) representing the exponential Kullback-Leibler Divergence (KLD) between 𝜽 \\boldsymbol{\\theta} and 𝝃 ^ \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}} . If we further set ℒ 0 ​ ( 𝝃 ^ , 𝜽 ) ≡ 1 \\mathcal{L}_{0}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}},\\boldsymbol{\\theta})\\equiv 1 then the price formula ( 24 ) becomes the wealth-weighted average of market beilefs discussed in ( Sethi and Vaughan 2016 ) . We would like to highlight that the solution methodology for deriving the CRRA-utility based limiting price, e.g., Eq. ( 21 ) and the POI weights ( 23 ) remain applicable even when market participant’s MU functions extend beyond the CRRA family of utilities. One example is the hybrid market model where the market maker adopts exponential utility, as given by Eq. ( 10 ), while the traders adopt logarithmic utility. In this scenario, the POI limiting price remains the same as ( 24 ), but the equations for 𝝃 ^ \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\xi}} become: (27) ξ ^ i = ∑ j = 1 J w j , 0 ⋅ π i , j + 1 β ​ ξ ^ i ​ log ⁡ ( θ i / ξ ^ i ∑ k = 1 I ξ ^ k ) ∑ j = 1 J w j , 0 , i = 1 , 2 , … , I . \\displaystyle\\hat{\\xi}_{i}=\\frac{\\sum\\limits_{j=1}^{J}w_{j,0}\\cdot\\pi_{i,j}+\\frac{1}{\\beta}\\hat{\\xi}_{i}\\log\\left(\\theta_{i}\\Big/\\frac{\\hat{\\xi}_{i}}{\\sum_{k=1}^{I}\\hat{\\xi}_{k}}\\right)}{\\sum\\limits_{j=1}^{J}w_{j,0}},\\quad i=1,2,...,I. We provide more details of Eq. ( 27 ) in Appendix A.5 . Our numerical experiments (given in Appendix A.5 ) demonstrate that the price formula ( 27 ) outperforms the conventional wealth-weighted average price reported in ( Sethi and Vaughan 2016 ) . The CRRA-utility-based pricing formula can also be extended to a more comprehensive market model where all participants adopt utilities with hyperbolic absolute risk aversion (HARA utility), which finds wide applications in economic modeling. A brief discussion of this model is presented in Appendix A.6 . 5.3. Evaluation of Approximation Scheme In this section, we first employ simulation to validate the accuracy of the POI weights ( 23 ) and the price formula ( 24 ) in estimating the true weighting coefficients and the true limiting price, respectively. Following this validation, we proceed to examine how the risk parameters and initial wealth impact the accuracy of our approximation. We consider a CRRA-utility-based market with I = 3 I=3 , W 0 = 10 W_{0}=10 , and θ 1 = θ 2 = θ 3 = 1 / 3 \\theta_{1}=\\theta_{2}=\\theta_{3}=1/3 . Traders are generated randomly as follows. The j j -th trader’s belief is sampled from 𝝅 j = α ⋅ 𝝅 ¯ + ( 1 − α ) ⋅ ϵ / | ϵ | \\boldsymbol{\\pi}_{j}=\\alpha\\cdot\\bar{\\boldsymbol{\\pi}}+(1-\\alpha)\\cdot\\boldsymbol{\\epsilon}/|\\boldsymbol{\\epsilon}| , where 𝝅 ¯ \\bar{\\boldsymbol{\\pi}} is the baseline belief evenly sampled from ( 0.6 , 0.2 , 0.2 ) (0.6,0.2,0.2) and ( 0.2 , 0.2 , 0.6 ) (0.2,0.2,0.6) ; ϵ \\boldsymbol{\\epsilon} is uniformly distributed random noise on [ 0 , 1 ] 3 [0,1]^{3} , and α = 0.5 \\alpha=0.5 controls the market belief structure. We conduct simulations according to Trading Process 1 , with each trader randomly selected with equal probability to interact with the market maker at each time period. For each set of parameters, we perform N = 5 × J N=5\\times J rounds of simulation, and we denote the k k -th sample of the limiting wealth allocation as { 𝐱 1 sim ​ ( k ) , … , 𝐱 J sim ​ ( k ) } | k = 1 N \\{\\mathbf{x}_{1}^{\\textrm{sim}}(k),\\ldots,\\mathbf{x}_{J}^{\\textrm{sim}}(k)\\}|_{k=1}^{N} . • Evaluation by limiting wealth: To verify the accuracy of the POI weights ( 23 ), we first solve the problem 𝒫 po ​ ( 𝝂 ^ ) \\mathcal{P}_{\\textrm{po}}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\nu}}) whose solution is denoted as { 𝐱 ^ 1 , … , 𝐱 ^ J } \\{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{1},\\ldots,\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{J}\\} . If the heuristic weight 𝝂 ^ \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\nu}} provides an accurate approximation for the “true” weighting parameter, then the wealth allocation { 𝐱 ^ 1 , … , 𝐱 ^ J } \\{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{1},\\ldots,\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{J}\\} should be close to the sample limiting wealth allocation { 𝐱 1 sim ​ ( k ) , … , 𝐱 J sim ​ ( k ) } | k = 1 N \\{\\mathbf{x}_{1}^{\\textrm{sim}}(k),\\ldots,\\mathbf{x}_{J}^{\\textrm{sim}}(k)\\}|_{k=1}^{N} generated by the simulation. We introduce the following quantity to measure the difference for each sample, δ 𝐱 ​ ( k ) ≜ ∑ j = 1 J ‖ 𝐱 j sim ​ ( k ) − 𝐱 ^ j ‖ / ∑ j = 1 J ‖ 𝐱 j sim ​ ( k ) ‖ , \\displaystyle\\delta_{\\ma