e risk neutral. Using the similar notations in Section 4.1 , in t t -th round of trading, the j j -th trader interacts with the market. The market states are 𝐲 t = ( y 1 , t , … , y I , t ) \\mathbf{y}_{t}=(y_{1,t},\\ldots,y_{I,t}) and j j -the trader’s wealth is 𝐱 j , t = ( x 1 , j , t , … , x I , j , t ) \\mathbf{x}_{j,t}=(x_{1,j,t},\\ldots,x_{I,j,t}) . The j j -th trader’s decision problem is as follows, max 𝐳 \\displaystyle\\max_{\\mathbf{z}}~~ ∑ i = 1 I π i , j ​ ( x i , j , t + z i ) \\displaystyle~\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\pi_{i,j}(x_{i,j,t}+z_{i}) (79) Subject to ∑ i = 1 I θ i ​ ( 1 − e − β ⁡ ( y i − z i ) ) ≥ 1 − e − β ​ W 0 . \\displaystyle~~~\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\theta_{i}(1-e^{-\\beta(y_{i}-z_{i})})\\geq 1-e^{-\\beta W_{0}}. Checking the optimality condition, we get the optimal solution, z i ∗ = y i − 1 β ln ( λ ​ β ​ θ i π i , j ) , i = 1 , … , I , \\displaystyle z_{i}^{*}=y_{i}-\\frac{1}{\\beta}\\ln\\left(\\frac{\\lambda\\beta\\theta_{i}}{\\pi_{i,j}}\\right),~~i=1,\\ldots,I, where λ ≥ 0 \\lambda\\geq 0 is the Lagrange multiplier. It can be verified that λ ≠ 0 \\lambda\\not=0 . Thus the constraint ( 79 ) is binding for z i ∗ z_{i}^{*} , which gives the solution of λ ∗ = 1 β ​ e β ​ W 0 \\lambda^{*}=\\frac{1}{\\beta}e^{\\beta W_{0}} . After the trading, the market state is updated to y i , t + 1 = y i , t − z i ∗ = 1 β ​ ln ⁡ ( λ ∗ ​ β ​ θ i π i , j ) y_{i,t+1}=y_{i,t}-z^{*}_{i}=\\frac{1}{\\beta}\\ln\\left(\\frac{\\lambda^{*}\\beta\\theta_{i}}{\\pi_{i,j}}\\right) for i = 1 , … , I i=1,\\ldots,I . The price is updated to p i , t + 1 = θ i ​ e − β ⁡ ( y i − z i ∗ ) ∑ k = 1 I θ k ​ e − β ⁡ ( y k − z k ∗ ) = π i , j , \\displaystyle p_{i,t+1}=\\frac{\\theta_{i}e^{-\\beta(y_{i}-z_{i}^{*})}}{\\sum\\limits_{k=1}^{I}\\theta_{k}e^{-\\beta(y_{k}-z_{k}^{*})}}=\\pi_{i,j}, for i = 1 , … , I i=1,\\ldots,I . That is to say, after the j j -th trader interacts with the market, the price is modified exactly according to the j j -th trader’s belief. Appendix B Properties of MU functions and Examples B.1. Examples of MU Functions One prominent feature of our framework is that it provides more freedom in designing multivariate utility function. The following example shows how various desirable objectives can be made explicit and combined with each other using our MU framework. We then provide some examples of MU functions which go beyond the conventional framework of Expected Utility or the Risk measure -based MU function. Let I I represent the number of securities, ( θ 1 , … , θ I ) ∈ Θ (\\theta_{1},\\ldots,\\theta_{I})\\in\\Theta denote the market maker’s belief, and 𝐲 = ( y 1 , … , y I ) \\mathbf{y}=(y_{1},\\ldots,y_{I}) the wealth vector. • We may consider the following MU function, (80) U ⁡ ( 𝐲 ) \\displaystyle U(\\mathbf{y}) = − 1 β ​ ln ⁡ ( ∑ i = 1 I θ i ​ e − β ​ y i ) + η ​ ∑ i = 1 I θ i ​ ln ⁡ ( y i + B ) , \\displaystyle=-\\frac{1}{\\beta}\\ln\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\theta_{i}e^{-\\beta y_{i}}\\right)+\\eta\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\theta_{i}\\ln\\big(y_{i}+B\\big), where η ≥ 0 \\eta\\geq 0 is the parameter. The first term of U ⁡ ( 𝐲 ) U(\\mathbf{y}) is the so called Entropic Risk Measure [ Föllmer and Schied 2011 ] and the second term represents the classical expected utility. It’s worth noting that the second term establishes an explicit lower bound of − B -B for the market maker’s terminal wealth. In fact, if an explicit worst-case loss is desired, one can always add a penalty term 1 γ ​ ∑ i = 1 I θ i ​ ( y i + B ) γ \\frac{1}{\\gamma}\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\theta_{i}(y_{i}+B)^{\\gamma} with γ ≤ 0 \\gamma\\leq 0 to the original multivariate utility function U ⁡ ( 𝐲 ) U(\\mathbf{y}) . • We can construct the following MU function, (81) U ⁡ ( 𝐲 ) = min i = 1 , … , I ⁡ { y i } + η γ ​ ( ∑ i = 1 I θ i ​ y i + B ) γ , \\displaystyle U(\\mathbf{y})=\\min_{i=1,\\ldots,I}~\\{y_{i}\\}+\\frac{\\eta}{\\gamma}\\big(\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\theta_{i}y_{i}+B\\big)^{\\gamma}, where η ≥ 0 \\eta\\geq 0 and γ ≤ 1 \\gamma\\leq 1 are the paramter. In this MU function, the first term represents the worst-case terminal wealth for the market maker, and it is a concave function because it is the point-wise minimum of 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} . The second term establishes a specific lower bound of − B -B for the expectation, ∑ i = 1 I θ i ​ y i \\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\theta_{i}y_{i} . It’s important to note that as the parameter γ \\gamma approaches 1 1 , the second term approximates the expectation of the wealth (with a shift of B B ). Consequently, a market maker employing this MU function ( 81 ) may seek to strike a balance between minimizing worst-case losses and maximizing expectations. • Using the MU framework, we can not only design the functional form of the MU function but also customize the domain of the MU function. Thus, it is possible to include some “hard constraint” on the market state in MU function. Specifically, consider the function U ⁡ ( 𝐲 ) = ∑ i = 1 I θ i ​ u ​ ( y i ) U(\\mathbf{y})=\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\theta_{i}u(y_{i}) where u ⁡ ( ⋅ ) u(\\cdot) is an conventional univariate utility function. We then define the domain of this MU function as dom ​ ( U ) ≜ { 𝐲 : 𝐲 ≥ 𝐋 } ∩ { 𝐲 : ρ ⁡ ( 𝐲 ) ≤ ρ 0 } ∩ { 𝐲 : ∑ i = 1 I θ i ​ y i ≥ − B } , \\displaystyle\\textbf{{dom}}(U)\\triangleq\\{\\mathbf{y}:~\\mathbf{y}\\geq\\mathbf{L}\\}\\cap\\{\\mathbf{y}:~\\rho(\\mathbf{y})\\leq\\rho_{0}\\}\\cap\\{\\mathbf{y}:~\\sum_{i=1}^{I}\\theta_{i}y_{i}\\geq-B\\}, where ρ 0 ​ ( ⋅ ) \\rho_{0}(\\cdot) can be viewed as some risk measure. As for the domain of the MU function, the first term bounds the worst-case loss, the second term bounds the risk, and the last term bounds the expectation of the wealth. B.2. Equivalence Between Various Mechanisms In this section, we explicitly assume the domain of U U is polyhedral: dom ( U ) ≜ { 𝐲 : 𝐚 k ⊤ 𝐲 ≥ b k , 𝐚 k &gt; 𝟎 , k ∈ 𝒦 } \\textbf{{dom}}(U)\\triangleq\\{\\mathbf{y}:~\\mathbf{a}_{k}^{\\top}\\mathbf{y}\\geq b_{k},~\\mathbf{a}_{k}&gt;\\mathbf{0},~k\\in\\mathcal{K}\\} where 𝒦 \\mathcal{K} is some index set. The proof for the case dom ​ ( U ) = ℝ I \\textbf{{dom}}(U)=\\mathbb{R}^{I} is similar. A scoring rule 𝐒 \\mathbf{S} is a reward mechanism that prompts participants to report their subjective probability belief and pays them according to their predicting accuracy. Specifically, if someone reports 𝐩 ∈ Θ I \\mathbf{p}\\in\\Theta_{I} , then he/she gets paid 𝐒 ​ ( 𝐩 ) i \\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{p})_{i} if outcome i i happens. A scoring rule is proper if truthfully reporting belief is a utility maximizing strategy for a myopic, risk-neutral participant. Additionally, it is strictly proper if truthful reporting is the only utility-maximizing strategy. [ Hanson 2003 ] invented a market implementation for scoring rules by transforming the belief reporting process into a sequential procedure. Following a similar rationale with the Market Equivalence Theorem given by [ Chen and Pennock 2007 ] , the following proposition shows how to retrieve a proper scoring rule from a multivariate utility function. We first introduce the following definition: Definition B.1. Consider a mapping 𝐒 ⁡ ( ⋅ ) \\mathbf{S}(\\cdot) from a point to a set. If for any 𝐩 n → 𝐩 ∗ \\mathbf{p}_{n}\\to\\mathbf{p}^{*} , 𝐬 n ∈ 𝐒 ⁡ ( 𝐩 n ) \\mathbf{s}_{n}\\in\\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{p}_{n}) , it has lim n → ∞ 𝐬 n ∈ 𝐒 ⁡ ( 𝐩 ∗ ) \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\mathbf{s}_{n}\\in\\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{p}^{*}) , then we say 𝐒 ⁡ ( ⋅ ) \\mathbf{S}(\\cdot) is upper-hemicontinuous. Proposition B.2. If U ⁡ ( ⋅ ) : ℝ I → ℝ U(\\cdot):\\mathbb{R}^{I}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R} is a concave function and U U -based pricing rule has bounded loss, for 𝐩 ∈ Θ I \\mathbf{p}\\in\\Theta_{I} , we introduce 𝐒 \\mathbf{S} as, (82) 𝐒 ⁡ ( 𝐩 ) ≜ − arg ⁡ min \\displaystyle\\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{p})\\triangleq-{\\arg\\min} { 𝐩 ⊤ 𝐲 | U ( 𝐲 ) ≥ U ( W 0 ⋅ 𝐞 ) , 𝐲 ∈ dom ( U ) } , \\displaystyle~~\\Big\\{\\mathbf{p}^{\\top}\\mathbf{y}~|~U(\\mathbf{y})\\geq U(W_{0}\\cdot\\mathbf{e}),~\\mathbf{y}\\in\\textbf{{dom}}(U)\\Big\\}, where dom ( U ) = { 𝐲 : 𝐚 k ⊤ 𝐲 ≥ b k , 𝐚 k &gt; 𝟎 , k ∈ 𝒦 } \\textbf