or the order k ⋅ 𝐞 k\\cdot\\mathbf{e} . Otherwise, the previous trading may not achieve its optimal price. □ \\square • Bounded Worst-case Loss : Although a market maker is designed to provide liquidity, it is certainly not designed to be infinitely exploited. To avoid such catastrophe, one might wish to put a cap on the worst-case loss. Obviously MU function based market maker suffers bounded worst-case loss if and only if the utility level set ℒ ≜ { 𝐲 ∈ ℝ I | U ⁡ ( 𝐲 ) ≥ U ⁡ ( W 0 ⋅ 𝐞 ) } \\mathcal{L}\\triangleq\\{\\mathbf{y}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{I}~|~U(\\mathbf{y})\\geq U(W_{0}\\cdot\\mathbf{e})\\} is lower bounded by some vector 𝐲 ¯ ∈ ℝ I \\underline{\\mathbf{y}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{I} , i.e., it has 𝐲 ¯ ≤ 𝐲 \\underline{\\mathbf{y}}\\leq\\mathbf{y} for all 𝐲 ∈ ℒ \\mathbf{y}\\in\\mathcal{L} . The utility level set ℒ \\mathcal{L} actually describes all the acceptable terminal wealth 𝐲 \\mathbf{y} of the market maker. Indeed, each element of 𝐲 ¯ = ( y ¯ 1 , y ¯ 2 , … , y ¯ n ) \\underline{\\mathbf{y}}=\\big(\\underline{y}_{1},\\underline{y}_{2},\\ldots,\\underline{y}_{n}\\big) can be characterized as follows, (88) 𝒫 i \\displaystyle\\mathcal{P}_{i}~ min 𝐲 { y i | 𝐲 = ( y 1 , ⋯ , y I ) , U ( 𝐲 ) ≥ U ( W 0 ⋅ 𝐞 ) } . \\displaystyle~\\min_{\\mathbf{y}}\\{y_{i}~|~\\mathbf{y}=(y_{1},\\cdots,y_{I}),~U(\\mathbf{y})\\geq U(W_{0}\\cdot\\mathbf{e})\\}. Worst-case loss bound can be found by solving subproblems 𝒫 i \\mathcal{P}_{i} for all i = 1 , ⋯ , I i=1,\\cdots,I . Obviously, the worst-case loss has a natural bound when the domain of utility U U itself is bounded from below, e.g., U ⁡ ( 𝐲 ) = ∑ i π i ​ ( y i + B i ) γ , γ &lt; 1 U(\\mathbf{y})=\\sum_{i}\\pi_{i}(y_{i}+B_{i})^{\\gamma},~\\gamma&lt;1 . If such a domain bound design is not desired, then we need to find to other means to bound the loss. One viable way is to resort to transaction restrictions. Specifically, in real world traders are usually constrained to have non-negative wealth position when true state is revealed (It i