bm{1}_{T^{d}}\\bm{\\psi}{{}^{\\top}},\\bm{\\Pi}\\right&gt;+\\left&lt;\\bm{\\varphi}\\bm{1}_{N}{{}^{\\top}},\\bm{\\Pi}\\right&gt;+\\left&lt;\\bm{\\beta}\\bm{X}{{}^{\\top}},\\bm{\\Pi}\\right&gt; = ⟨ 𝒜 ⁡ ( 𝝍 , 𝝋 , 𝜷 ) , 𝚷 ⟩ 𝒲 . \\displaystyle=\\left&lt;\\mathcal{A}(\\bm{\\psi},\\bm{\\varphi},\\bm{\\beta}),\\bm{\\Pi}\\right&gt;_{\\mathcal{W}}. Thus, 𝒜 ( 𝝍 , 𝝋 , 𝜷 ) = 𝟏 T d 𝝍 + ⊤ 𝝋 𝟏 N + ⊤ 𝜷 𝑿 ⊤ \\mathcal{A}(\\bm{\\psi},\\bm{\\varphi},\\bm{\\beta})=\\bm{1}_{T^{d}}\\bm{\\psi}{{}^{\\top}}+\\bm{\\varphi}\\bm{1}_{N}{{}^{\\top}}+\\bm{\\beta}\\bm{X}{{}^{\\top}} . We then define the functions f ∗ ​ ( 𝒜 ∗ ​ 𝚷 ) \\displaystyle f^{*}(\\mathcal{A}^{*}\\bm{\\Pi}) = i ( 𝝂 , 𝝁 , 𝑿 ¯ ) ​ ( 𝒜 ∗ ​ 𝚷 ) \\displaystyle=i_{(\\bm{\\nu},\\bm{\\mu},\\bar{\\bm{X}})}\\left(\\mathcal{A}^{*}\\bm{\\Pi}\\right) g ∗ ​ ( 𝚷 ) \\displaystyle g^{*}(\\bm{\\Pi}) = ⟨ 𝚷 , − 𝑺 ⟩ + ε ⁡ ⟨ 𝚷 , log ⁡ 𝚷 ⟩ , \\displaystyle=\\left&lt;\\bm{\\Pi},-\\bm{S}\\right&gt;+\\varepsilon\\left&lt;\\bm{\\Pi},\\log\\bm{\\Pi}\\right&gt;, and their corresponding convex conjugates are therefore given by, f ⁡ ( 𝝍 , 𝝋 , 𝜷 ) \\displaystyle f(\\bm{\\psi},\\bm{\\varphi},\\bm{\\beta}) = ⟨ ( 𝝍 , 𝝋 , 𝜷 ) , ( 𝝂 , 𝝁 , 𝑿 ¯ ) ⟩ 𝒱 = ⟨ 𝝍 , 𝝂 ⟩ + ⟨ 𝝋 , 𝝁 ⟩ + ⟨ 𝜷 , 𝑿 ¯ ⟩ \\displaystyle=\\left&lt;(\\bm{\\psi},\\bm{\\varphi},\\bm{\\beta}),(\\bm{\\nu},\\bm{\\mu},\\bar{\\bm{X}})\\right&gt;_{\\mathcal{V}}=\\left&lt;\\bm{\\psi},\\bm{\\nu}\\right&gt;+\\left&lt;\\bm{\\varphi},\\bm{\\mu}\\right&gt;+\\left&lt;\\bm{\\beta},\\bar{\\bm{X}}\\right&gt; g ⁡ ( 𝑾 ) \\displaystyle g(\\bm{W}) = ε ​ ∑ i ​ j exp ⁡ ( W i ​ j + S i ​ j − 1 ε ) . \\displaystyle=\\varepsilon\\sum_{ij}\\mathrm{exp}\\left({\\frac{W_{ij}+S_{ij}-1}{\\varepsilon}}\\right). Using f ∗ f^{*} and g ∗ g^{*} , we can write ( 17 ) as min 𝑾 ⁡ { f ∗ ​ ( 𝒜 ∗ ​ 𝑾 ) + g ∗ ​ ( 𝑾 ) } , \\min_{\\bm{W}}\\left\\{f^{*}(\\mathcal{A}^{*}\\bm{W})+g^{*}(\\bm{W})\\right\\}, then by the Fenchel-Rockafeller duality theorem, we get the equivalent dual form max 𝑽 ⁡ { − f ⁡ ( − 𝑽 ) − g ⁡ ( 𝒜 ​ 𝑽 ) } . \\max_{\\bm{V}}\\left\\{-f(-\\bm{V})-g(\\mathcal{A}\\bm{V})\\right\\}. Substituting 𝑽 = ( − 𝝍 , − 𝝋 , − 𝜷 ) \\bm{V}=\\left(-\\bm{\\psi},-\\bm{\\varphi},-\\bm{\\beta}\\right) (the dual variables of eq. 16 ), converting to a minimization problem, and omitting constant factors in the objective, we get min 𝝍 , 𝝋 , 𝜷 𝝍 𝝂 ⊤ + 𝝋 𝝁 ⊤ + tr ( 𝜷 𝑿 ¯ ⊤ ) + ε ∑ i = 1 T d ∑ j = 1 N exp ( 1 ε ( S i ​ j − ψ j − φ i − 𝜷 i 𝒙 j ⊤ ) ) . \\min_{\\bm{\\psi},\\bm{\\varphi},\\bm{\\beta}}\\bm{\\psi}{{}^{\\top}}\\bm{\\nu}+\\bm{\\varphi}{{}^{\\top}}\\bm{\\mu}+\\mathrm{tr}\\left(\\bm{\\beta}{{}^{\\top}}\\bar{\\bm{X}}\\right)+\\varepsilon\\sum_{i=1}^{T^{d}}\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\mathrm{exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon}\\left(S_{ij}-\\psi_{j}-\\varphi_{i}-\\bm{\\beta}_{i}{{}^{\\top}}\\bm{x}_{j}\\right)\\right). (18) Now we write 𝝋 \\bm{\\varphi} in terms of 𝝍 , 𝜷 \\bm{\\psi},\\bm{\\beta} by using a first-order optimality condition of the above problem. Taking the derivative w.r.t. φ i \\varphi_{i} and setting to zero, we have: 0 \\displaystyle 0 = μ i − exp ( − φ i ε ) ∑ j = 1 N exp ( 1 ε ( S i ​ j − ψ j − 𝜷 i 𝒙 j ⊤ ) ) \\displaystyle=\\mu_{i}-\\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\frac{\\varphi_{i}}{\\varepsilon}\\right)\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\mathrm{exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon}\\left({S_{ij}-\\psi_{j}-\\bm{\\beta}_{i}{{}^{\\top}}\\bm{x}_{j}}\\right)\\right) exp ⁡ ( − φ i ε ) \\displaystyle\\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\frac{\\varphi_{i}}{\\varepsilon}\\right) = μ i ∑ j exp ( 1 ε ( S i ​ j − ψ j − 𝜷 i ⊤ 𝒙 j ) ) \\displaystyle=\\frac{\\mu_{i}}{\\sum_{j}\\mathrm{exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon}\\left({S_{ij}-\\psi_{j}-\\bm{\\beta}_{i}{{}^{\\top}}\\bm{x}_{j}}\\right)\\right)} (19) φ i \\displaystyle\\varphi_{i} = ε log ( 1 μ i ∑ j exp ( 1 ε ( S i ​ j − ψ j − 𝜷 i 𝒙 j ⊤ ) ) ) \\displaystyle=\\varepsilon\\log\\left(\\frac{1}{\\mu_{i}}\\sum_{j}\\mathrm{exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon}\\left({S_{ij}-\\psi_{j}-\\bm{\\beta}_{i}{{}^{\\top}}\\bm{x}_{j}}\\right)\\right)\\right) (20) Finally, substituting eqs. 19 and 20 into eq. 18 and omitting constant terms yields, min 𝝍 , 𝜷 𝝍 𝝂 ⊤ + tr ( 𝜷 𝑿 ¯ ⊤ ) + ε ∑ i = 1 T d μ i log ( ∑ j = 1 N exp ( 1 ε ( S i ​ j − 𝜷 i 𝒙 j ⊤ − ψ j ) ) ) , \\min_{\\bm{\\psi},\\bm{\\beta}}~\\bm{\\psi}{{}^{\\top}}\\bm{\\nu}+\\mathrm{tr}\\left(\\bm{\\beta}{{}^{\\top}}\\bar{\\bm{X}}\\right)+\\varepsilon\\sum_{i=1}^{T^{d}}\\mu_{i}\\log\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\mathrm{exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon}\\left(S_{ij}-\\bm{\\beta}_{i}{{}^{\\top}}\\bm{x}_{j}-\\psi_{j}\\right)\\right)\\right), (21) where S i ​ j = 𝒖 i ⊤ 𝒚 j S_{ij}=\\bm{u}_{i}{{}^{\\top}}\\bm{y}_{j} . Thus, we obtain that eq. 21 is equal to eq. 7 . In summary, we have shown that the relaxed dual formulation of the VQR problem that we solve ( 7 ), is equivalent to an entropic-regularized version of the OT-based primal formulation eq. 5 . A.6 Extracting the Vector Quantile Regression Coefficients The dual variables obtained from the OT formulation ( eq. 4 ) are 𝝋 ∈ ℝ T d \\bm{\\varphi}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{T^{d}} and 𝜷 ∈ ℝ T d × k \\bm{\\beta}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{T^{d}\\times k} . In the case of scalar quantiles ( d = 1 d=1 ) we can obtain the conditional quantile function from the dual variables by applying the T × T T\\times T finite differences matrix 𝑫 ⊤ \\bm{D}{{}^{\\top}} defined in section A.1 : Q ^ Y | 𝐗 ( u ; 𝒙 ) = [ 𝑫 ( 𝜷 𝒙 + 𝝋 ) ⊤ ] u . \\widehat{Q}_{\\mathrm{Y}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(u;\\bm{x})=\\left[\\bm{D}{{}^{\\top}}\\left(\\bm{\\beta}\\bm{x}+\\bm{\\varphi}\\right)\\right]_{u}. This is effectively taking the u u -th component the first-order discrete derivative where u u is one of the T T discrete quantile levels. In the vector case, we have T d T^{d} discrete d d -dimensional quantile levels. Equivalently, the relation between the dual variables and the quantile function is then Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 ′ , 𝒙 ) = [ ∇ 𝒖 { 𝜷 ​ 𝒙 + 𝝋 } ] 𝒖 ′ . \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u}^{\\prime};\\bm{x})=\\left[\\nabla_{\\bm{u}}\\left\\{\\bm{\\beta}\\bm{x}+\\bm{\\varphi}\\right\\}\\right]_{\\bm{u}^{\\prime}}. Here 𝜷 ​ 𝒙 + 𝝋 \\bm{\\beta}\\bm{x}+\\bm{\\varphi} is in ℝ T d \\mathbb{R}^{T^{d}} and its gradient with respect to 𝒖 \\bm{u} , ∇ 𝒖 { 𝜷 ​ 𝒙 + 𝝋 } \\nabla_{\\bm{u}}\\left\\{\\bm{\\beta}\\bm{x}+\\bm{\\varphi}\\right\\} , is in ℝ T d × d \\mathbb{R}^{T^{d}\\times d} . We then evaluate its gradient at o ne of the discrete levels 𝒖 ′ \\bm{u}^{\\prime} to obtain the d d -dimensional quantile. As explained in section 3 , the expression 𝜷 ​ 𝒙 + 𝝋 \\bm{\\beta}\\bm{x}+\\bm{\\varphi} is convex in u u , and thus the co-monotonicity of the estimated CVQF is obtained by virtue of it being the gradient of a convex function. Appendix B Convergence of VQR Below we mention a few comments regarding the optimization and approximation error of VQR. Linear VQR. The relaxed dual formulation of VQR, presented in eq. 6 , is an unconstrained smooth convex minimization problem. Thus, in this case, gradient descent is guaranteed to converge to the optimal solution up to any desired precision. Moreover, given fixed quantile levels, our objective can be written as the minimization of an expectation under the data distribution P ( 𝐗 , 𝐘 ) P_{(\\bm{\\mathrm{X}},\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}})} . Under these criteria, performing SGD by sampling i.i.d. from the data distribution, is known to converge to the global minimum ( Hazan 2019 , Chapter 3). We refer to Section 5.4 in ( Peyré &amp; Cuturi 2019 ) (and references therein) for further analysis and details regarding the convergence rates of SGD and other stochastic optimization methods applied to this problem. Specifically, stochastic averaged gradient (SAG) was shown to have improved convergence rates compared to SGD Genevay et al. 2016 . However, in practice we find that SGD converges well for this problem (see Figure 2 , Appendix fig. 10(a) ), and we use it here for simplicity. Nonlinear VQR. In the case of non-linear VQR, as formulated in eq. 8 , the optimization is over a non-convex objective, and thus no convergence to a global minimum is guaranteed. Convergence analyses for non convex objectives only provide guarantees for weak forms of convergence. For e.g., the analysis methodology introduced by Ghadimi &amp; Lan 2013 can be used to show that under the assumption of uniformly bounded gradient estimates, the norm of the gradient of the loss function decreases on average as 𝒪 ( t − 1 / 2 ) \\mathcal{O}(t^{-1/2}) , when t → ∞ t\\to\\infty , where t t is the iteration. This can be viewed as a weak form of convergence that does not guarantee convergence to a fixed point, even to a local minimum. Approximation error as ε → 0 \\varepsilon\\to 0 . The nonlinear VQR formulation ( 8 ) produces an estimate Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ε \\widehat{Q}^{\\varepsilon}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}} . By decreasing ε \\varepsilon , one can make the problem more exact, as we have shown in practice ( fig. 10(b) ). Following the approach of Proposition A.1 in Genevay et al. 2016 , it can be shown that this estimate approaches the true CVQF as ε → 0 \\varepsilon\\to 0 . Appendix C Conditional Vector Quantile Functions C.1 Intuitions About Vector Quantiles To interpret the meaning of CVQFs, let us consider the 2-dimensional case, where we assume 𝐘 = ( Y 1 , Y 2 ) ⊤ \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}=(\\mathrm{Y}_{1},\\mathrm{Y}_{2}){{}^{\\top}} . Given a specific covariates vector 𝒙 = ( x 1 , … , x k ) ⊤ \\bm{x}=(x_{1},\\dots,x_{k}){{}^{\\top}} , and level 𝒖 = ( u 1 , u 2 ) ⊤ \\bm{u}=(u_{1},u_{2}){{}^{\\top}} we may write the components of the conditional vector quantile function as, Q 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) = [ Q 1 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) Q 2 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) ] = [ Q Y 1 | Y 2 , 𝐗 ​ ( u 1 , Q Y 2 | 𝐗 ​ ( u 2 , 𝒙 ) , 𝒙 ) Q Y 2 | Y 1 , 𝐗 ​ ( u 2 , Q Y 1 | 𝐗 ​ ( u 1 , 𝒙 ) , 𝒙 ) ] , \\displaystyle Q_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x})=\\begin{bmatrix}Q_{1}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x})\\\\ Q_{2}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}Q_{\\mathrm{Y}_{1}|\\mathrm{Y}_{2},\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}\\left(u_{1};Q_{\\mathrm{Y}_{2}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}\\left(u_{2};\\bm{x}\\right),\\bm{x}\\right)\\\\ Q_{\\mathrm{Y}_{2}|\\mathrm{Y}_{1},\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}\\left(u_{2};Q_{\\mathrm{Y}_{1}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}\\left(u_{1};\\bm{x}\\right),\\bm{x}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}, where Q Y i | Y j , 𝐗 ​ ( u , y , 𝒙 ) Q_{\\mathrm{Y}_{i}|\\mathrm{Y}_{j},\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(u;y,\\bm{x}) denotes the scalar quantile function of the random variable Y i \\mathrm{Y}_{i} at level u u , given Y j = y , 𝐗 = 𝒙 \\mathrm{Y}_{j}=y,\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x} . Thus, for example, the first component Q 1 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) Q_{1}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}) is a 2D surface where moving along u 1 u_{1} for a fixed u 2 u_{2} yields a scalar, non-decreasing function representing the quantiles of Y 1 \\mathrm{Y}_{1} when Y 2 \\mathrm{Y}_{2} is at a value corresponding to level u 2 u_{2} (see Figure 6(b) ). In addition, the vector quantile function is co-monotonic with 𝒖 \\bm{u} in the sense defined by eq. 1 . For higher dimensions, it becomes more involved as the conditioning in each component is on the vector quantile of all the remaining components of the target 𝐘 \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}} ( Figure 6(c) ). (a) (b) (c) Figure A6: Visualization (a) 1d, (b) 2d, and (c) 3d vector quantile functions estimated on the MVN dataset. In each plot, Q i ​ ( 𝒖 ) Q_{i}(\\bm{u}) is the i i th component of the vector quantile 𝑸 𝐘 ​ ( 𝒖 ) \\bm{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}}(\\bm{u}) , plotted over all quantile levels 𝒖 \\bm{u} . The number of quantile levels calculated was 50, 25 and 10 for 1d, 2d and 3d quantiles respectively. For 2d quantiles (b), the top plot shows multiple monotonic quantile curves of one variable while keeping the other at a fixed level. C.2 Conditional Quantile Contours Figure A7: Visualization of α \\alpha -quantile contours constructed using separable quantiles and vector quantiles . All the contours presented contain ∼ 90 % \\sim 90\\% of the points (i.e., α = 0.025 \\alpha=0.025 ). Left (top to bottom): samples drawn from bivariate normal, heart-shaped, and star-shaped densities. Middle &amp; Right: black triangles overlaid on the density constitute the α − \\alpha- quantile contour estimated using separable quantiles and vector quantiles, respectively. The bivariate normal density has zero mean, unit variance and correlation of ρ = − 0.7 \\rho=-0.7 . Vector quantile contours accurately capture the shape of distribution whereas the separable quantile contours are always box-shaped due to the assumption of independence. A useful property of CVQFs is that they allow a natural extension of α \\alpha -confidence intervals to high dimensional distributions, which we denote as α \\alpha -contours. Vector quantile contours. Formally, we define the conditional contour of 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x} at confidence level α \\alpha as 𝒬 𝐘 | 𝐗 α ​ ( 𝒙 ) \\mathcal{Q}^{\\alpha}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{x}) , given by 𝒬 𝐘 | 𝐗 α ​ ( 𝒙 ) \\displaystyle\\mathcal{Q}^{\\alpha}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{x}) = { Q 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) | 𝒖 ∈ 𝒰 α } , \\displaystyle=\\left\\{Q_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x})~|~\\bm{u}\\in\\mathcal{U}_{\\alpha}\\right\\}, (22) 𝒰 α \\displaystyle\\mathcal{U}_{\\alpha} = ⋃ i = 1 d 𝒰 α i , \\displaystyle=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{d}\\mathcal{U}_{\\alpha}^{i}, 𝒰 α i \\displaystyle\\mathcal{U}_{\\alpha}^{i} = { ( u 1 , … , u d ) | ⊤ u i ∈ [ α , 1 − α ] , u − i ∈ { α , 1 − α } } \\displaystyle=\\left\\{(u_{1},\\dots,u_{d}){{}^{\\top}}~|~u_{i}\\in[\\alpha,1-\\alpha],~u_{-i}\\in\\left\\{\\alpha,1-\\alpha\\right\\}\\right\\} where u − i u_{-i} denotes any component of 𝒖 = ( u 1 , … , u d ) ⊤ \\bm{u}=(u_{1},\\dots,u_{d}){{}^{\\top}} except for u i u_{i} . Note that the contour can be calculated not only using the true CVQF, Q 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) Q_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}) , as above, but also using an estimated CVQF, Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}) . For simplicity consider the 2d case. By fixing e.g. u 1 u_{1} to be one of { α , 1 − α } \\left\\{\\alpha,1-\\alpha\\right\\} and then sweeping over u 2 u_{2} (and vice versa), we obtain a set of vector quantile levels 𝒰 α \\mathcal{U}_{\\alpha} corresponding to the confidence level α \\alpha (see fig. 1(a) , left and right). Mapping the set 𝒰 α \\mathcal{U}_{\\alpha} back to the domain of 𝐘 \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}} by using the values of the CVQF Q 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) Q_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}) along it, we obtain a contour of arbitrary shape, which contains 100 ⋅ ( 1 − 2 ​ α ) d 100\\cdot(1-2\\alpha)^{d} percent of the d d -dimensional distribution (see fig. 1(a) , middle and fig. A7 , right). Separable quantile contours. In contrast to vector quantile contours obtained by VQR, which can accurately model distributions with arbitrary shapes, using separable quantiles produces trivial box-shaped contours. Consider a CVQF estimated with separable quantiles as presented in eq. 10 . Due to the dependence of each component of Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 Sep ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) \\widehat{Q}^{\\text{Sep}}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}) only on the corresponding component of 𝒖 \\bm{u} , the shape of the resulting quantile contour will always be box-shaped (see fig. A7 , middle). Such trivial contours result in inferior performance for applications of uncertainty estimation, where a confidence region with a the smallest possible area for a given confidence level is desired. Appendix D Datasets D.1 Synthetic Datasets Below we describe the four synthetic datasets which were used in the experiments. MVN. Data was generated from a linear model 𝒚 = 𝑨 ​ 𝒙 + 𝜼 \\bm{y}=\\bm{A}\\bm{x}+\\bm{\\eta} , where 𝒙 ∼ 𝕌 ​ [ 0 , 1 ] k \\bm{x}\\sim\\mathbb{U}[0,1]^{k} , 𝑨 ∈ ℝ d × k \\bm{A}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times k} is a random projection matrix and 𝜼 \\bm{\\eta} is multivariate Gaussian noise with a random covariance matrix. Conditional-banana. This dataset was introduced by Feldman et al. 2021 , and inspired by a similar dataset in Carlier et al. 2017 . The target variable 𝐘 ∈ ℝ 2 \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2} has a banana-shaped conditional distribution, and its shape changes non-trivially when conditioned on a continuous-valued covariate X ∈ ℝ \\mathrm{X}\\in\\mathbb{R} (see the left-most column of the panel presented in Figure A13 ). The data generating process is defined as follows X ∼ 𝕌 ⁡ [ 0.8 , 3.2 ] , z ∼ 𝕌 ⁡ [ − π , π ] , ϕ ∼ 𝕌 ⁡ [ 0 , 2 ​ π ] , r ∼ 𝕌 ⁡ [ − 0.1 , 0.1 ] \\displaystyle\\mathrm{X}\\sim\\mathbb{U}[0.8,3.2],~~\\mathrm{z}\\sim\\mathbb{U}[-\\pi,\\pi],~~\\mathrm{\\phi}\\sim\\mathbb{U}[0,2\\pi],~~\\mathrm{r}\\sim\\mathbb{U}[-0.1,0.1] β ^ ∼ 𝕌 ​ [ 0 , 1 ] k , β = β ^ ‖ β ^ ‖ 1 \\displaystyle\\mathrm{\\hat{\\beta}}\\sim\\mathbb{U}[0,1]^{k},~~\\mathrm{\\beta}=\\frac{\\mathrm{\\hat{\\beta}}}{||\\mathrm{\\hat{\\beta}}||_{1}} Y 0 = 1 2 ​ ( − cos ⁡ ( 𝐙 ) + 1 ) + r ​ sin ⁡ ( ϕ ) + sin ⁡ ( X ) , Y 1 = Z β ​ X + r ​ cos ⁡ ( ϕ ) , \\displaystyle\\mathrm{Y}_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-\\cos{(\\bm{\\mathrm{Z}})}+1\\right)+r\\sin{(\\phi)}+\\sin{\\left(\\mathrm{X}\\right)},~~\\mathrm{Y}_{1}=\\frac{\\mathrm{Z}}{{\\beta}\\mathrm{X}}+\\mathrm{r}\\cos{(\\phi)}, and then 𝐘 = [ Y 0 , Y 1 ] ⊤ \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}=\\left[\\mathrm{Y}_{0},\\mathrm{Y}_{1}\\right]{{}^{\\top}} . Synthetic glasses. This dataset was introduced by Brando et al. 2022 . The target variable Y ∈ ℝ \\mathrm{Y}\\in\\mathbb{R} has a bimodal conditional distribution. The mode locations shift periodically when conditioned on a continuous-valued covariate X ∼ 𝕌 ⁡ [ 0 , 1 ] \\mathrm{X}\\sim\\mathbb{U}[0,1] ( fig. 12(a) ). The data generating process is defined as z 1 \\displaystyle\\mathrm{z}_{1} = 3 ​ π ​ X \\displaystyle=3\\pi\\mathrm{X}~ z 2 \\displaystyle\\mathrm{z}_{2} = π ⁡ ( 1 + 3 ​ X ) \\displaystyle=\\pi(1+3\\mathrm{X}) ϵ \\displaystyle\\mathrm{\\epsilon} ∼ Beta ⁡ ( α = 0.5 , β = 1 ) \\displaystyle\\sim\\mathrm{Beta}\\left(\\alpha=0.5,\\beta=1\\right)~ γ \\displaystyle\\mathrm{\\gamma} ∼ Categorical ⁡ ( 0 , 1 ) \\displaystyle\\sim\\mathrm{Categorical}(0,1) Y 1 \\displaystyle\\mathrm{Y}_{1} = 5 ​ sin ⁡ ( z 1 ) + 2.5 + ϵ \\displaystyle=5\\sin(\\mathrm{z}_{1})+2.5+\\mathrm{\\epsilon}~ Y 2 \\displaystyle\\mathrm{Y}_{2} = 5 ​ sin ⁡ ( z 2 ) + 2.5 − ϵ \\displaystyle=5\\sin(\\mathrm{z}_{2})+2.5-\\mathrm{\\epsilon} Y \\displaystyle\\mathrm{Y} = γ ​ Y 1 + ( 1 − γ ) ​ Y 2 . \\displaystyle=\\mathrm{\\gamma}\\mathrm{Y}_{1}+(1-\\mathrm{\\gamma})\\mathrm{Y}_{2}. Rotating star. In this dataset, the target variable of 𝐘 ∈ ℝ 2 \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2} has a star-shaped conditional distribution, and its shape is rotated by X ∈ ℝ \\mathrm{X}\\in\\mathbb{R} degrees when conditioned on a discrete-valued X \\mathrm{X} , taking values in [ 0 , 10 , 20 , … , 60 ] \\left[0,10,20,\\ldots,60\\right] . Data is generated based on a 600 × 600 600\\times 600 binary image of a star. See the first column in Figure A14 to visualize conditional distributions as a function of X \\mathrm{X} . Since conditional distributions differ only by a rotation, 𝔼 ⁡ [ 𝐘 | X ] \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\mathrm{X}\\right] remains the same for all X \\mathrm{X} . However, the shape of the distribution changes substantially with X \\mathrm{X} , especially in tails. Thus, this dataset is a challenging candidate for estimating vector quantiles which must also represent these tails in order to properly recover the shape of the conditional distribution. D.2 Real Datasets We perform real data experiments on the blog_data , bio , house , meps_20 datasets obtained from ( Feldman et al. 2021 ) . The original real datasets contained one-dimensional targets. Feldman et al. 2021 constructed an additional target variable by selecting a feature that has high correlation with first target variable and small correlation to the other input features, so that it is hard to predict. Summary of these datasets is presented in table A1 . Dataset Name N N k k d {d} Additional Response blog_data 52397 279 2 Time between the blog post publication and base-time bio 45730 8 2 F7 - Euclidean distance house 21613 17 2 Latitude of a house meps_20 17541 138 2 Overall rating of feelings Table A1: Information about the real data sets. Appendix E Evaluation Metrics E.1 Metrics for Synthetic Data The quality of an estimated CVQF can be measured by how well it upholds strong representation and co-monotonicity ( eqs. 1 and 2 ). However, measuring strong representation requires knowledge of the ground-truth joint distribution P ( 𝐗 , 𝐘 ) P_{(\\bm{\\mathrm{X}},\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}})} or its conditional quantile function. With synthetic data, adherence to strong representation can be measured via several proxy metrics. The second key property, violations of co-monotonicity, can be measured directly. Below we describe the metrics we use to evaluate these properties on estimated conditional quantile functions. Entropy of inverse CVQF. If strong representation holds, the inverted CVQF, Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 − 1 ​ ( 𝐘 | 𝐗 ) \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}^{-1}(\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}) , must result in a uniform distribution, when evaluated on samples drawn from the true conditional distribution. As a measure of uniformity, we calculate a normalized entropy of the inverted CVQF. The inversion procedure is done as follows. 1. Sample L L evaluation covariate vectors, { 𝒙 l } l = 1 L \\left\\{\\bm{x}_{l}\\right\\}_{l=1}^{L} values at random from the ground truth distribution P 𝐗 P_{\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}} . 2. For each 𝒙 l \\bm{x}_{l} , (a) Sample M M points { 𝒚 m , l } m = 1 M \\left\\{\\bm{y}_{m,l}\\right\\}_{m=1}^{M} from 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l} . (b) For each 𝒚 m , l \\bm{y}_{m,l} , find the level of the closest vector quantile w.r.t. the Euclidean distance, i.e. 𝒖 m , l = arg ⁡ min 𝒖 ′ ⁡ ‖ 𝒚 m − Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 ′ , 𝒙 l ) ‖ 2 . \\bm{u}_{m,l}=\\arg\\min_{\\bm{u}^{\\prime}}\\left\\lVert\\bm{y}_{m}-\\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u}^{\\prime};\\bm{x}_{l})\\right\\rVert_{2}. (c) For i ∈ { 1 , … , T d } i\\in\\left\\{1,\\dots,T^{d}\\right\\} denote c i c_{i} as the number of times that the quantile level i i is found in { 𝒖 m , l } m = 1 M \\left\\{\\bm{u}_{m,l}\\right\\}_{m=1}^{M} , and calculate p i = c i / M p_{i}=c_{i}/M . (d) Calculate the entropy h l = − ∑ i p i log p i h_{l}=-\\sum_{i}p_{i}\\log p_{i} and the normalized entropy h ~ l = ( exp ⁡ ( h l ) − 1 ) / ( T d − 1 ) \\widetilde{h}_{l}=\\left(\\mathrm{exp}\\left(h_{l}\\right)-1\\right)/(T^{d}-1) . 3. Report the CVQF inverse entropy as 1 L ​ ∑ l h ~ l \\frac{1}{L}\\sum_{l}{\\widetilde{h}_{l}} . Note that, 1. The entropy metric is normalized such that its values are in [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] where 1 1 corresponds to a uniform distribution, and 0 0 corresponds to a delta distribution. 2. Some non-uniformity arises due to the quantization of the quantile level grid into T T discrete levels per dimension. We report a reference entropy, calculated on a uniform sample of M M quantile-level grid points. 3. We used L = 20 L=20 and M = 4000 M=4000 . Distribution distance (KDE-L1). Since the CVQF fully represents the conditional distribution P ( 𝐘 | 𝐗 ) P_{(\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}})} it can serve as a generative model for it. We used inverse-transform sampling to generate data from the estimated conditional distribution though the fitted VQR model, Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}) , as follows. 1. Sample L L evaluation covariate vectors, { 𝒙 l } l = 1 L \\left\\{\\bm{x}_{l}\\right\\}_{l=1}^{L} values at random from the ground truth distribution P 𝐗 P_{\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}} . 2. For each 𝒙 l \\bm{x}_{l} , (a) Sample M M quantile levels { 𝒖 m , l } m = 1 M \\left\\{\\bm{u}_{m,l}\\right\\}_{m=1}^{M} uniformly. (b) Generate M M samples from the estimated distribution of 𝐘 | 𝐗 \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}} using the estimated CVQF: { 𝒚 ^ m , l = Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ( 𝒖 m , l ; 𝒙 l ) } m = 1 M \\left\\{\\hat{\\bm{y}}_{m,l}=\\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u}_{m,l};\\bm{x}_{l})\\right\\}_{m=1}^{M} . (c) Sample an additional M M points { 𝒚 m , l ∗ } m = 1 M \\left\\{\\bm{y}^{*}_{m,l}\\right\\}_{m=1}^{M} from the ground truth conditional distribution P 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l P_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}} . (d) Calculate a Kernel Density Estimate (KDE) f ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l \\widehat{f}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}} from the VQR samples { 𝒚 ^ m , l } \\left\\{\\hat{\\bm{y}}_{m,l}\\right\\} . (e) Calculate the KDE f 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l ∗ f^{*}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}} from the ground truth samples { 𝒚 m , l ∗ } \\left\\{\\bm{y}^{*}_{m,l}\\right\\} . 3. Calculate the KDE-L1 metric as 1 L ​ ∑ l = 1 L ‖ f 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l ∗ − f ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l ‖ 1 . \\frac{1}{L}\\sum_{l=1}^{L}\\left\\lVert f^{*}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}}-\\widehat{f}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}}\\right\\rVert_{1}. The KDEs are calculated with 100 100 bins per dimension. An isotropic Gauassian kernel was used, with σ = 0.1 \\sigma=0.1 for the conditional-banana dataset and σ = 0.035 \\sigma=0.035 for the star dataset. We used pykeops ( Charlier et al. 2021 ) for a fast implementation of high-dimensional KDEs. We used L = 20 L=20 and M = 4000 M=4000 . Quantile function distance (QFD). This metric measures the distance between a true CVQF, Q 𝐘 | 𝐗 ∗ Q^{*}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}} , and an estimate for it obtained by VQR, Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}} . 1. Sample L L evaluation covariate vectors, { 𝒙 l } l = 1 L \\left\\{\\bm{x}_{l}\\right\\}_{l=1}^{L} values at random from the ground truth distribution P 𝐗 P_{\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}} . 2. For each 𝒙 l \\bm{x}_{l} , (a) Sample M M points { 𝒚 m , l } m = 1 M \\left\\{\\bm{y}_{m,l}\\right\\}_{m=1}^{M} from the ground truth conditional distribution P 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l P_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}} . (b) Estimate an unconditional vector quantile function on { 𝒚 m , l } m = 1 M \\left\\{\\bm{y}_{m,l}\\right\\}_{m=1}^{M} , i.e. perform vector quantile estimation, not regression. Denote the estimated unconditional vector quantile function as Q 𝐘 | 𝐗 ∗ Q^{*}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}} . This serves as a proxy for the ground-truth conditional quantile function. (c) Denote the estimated conditional quantile function evaluated at 𝒙 l \\bm{x}_{l} : Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}} . (d) Compute the normalized difference between them elementwise over each of the T d T^{d} discrete quantile levels, i.e., d l = ‖ Q 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l ∗ − Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l ‖ 2 / ‖ Q 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 l ∗ ‖ 2 . d_{l}=\\left\\lVert Q^{*}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}}-\\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}}\\right\\rVert_{2}/\\left\\lVert Q^{*}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x}_{l}}\\right\\rVert_{2}. 3. Calculate the QFD metric as 1 L ​ ∑ l = 1 L d l \\frac{1}{L}\\sum_{l=1}^{L}d_{l} . We used L = 20 L=20 and M = 4000 M=4000 . Percentage of co-monotonicity violations (MV). This value can be measured directly. Given an estimated vector quantile function Q ^ ​ ( 𝒖 ) \\widehat{Q}(\\bm{u}) , with T T levels per dimension, there are in total T 2 ​ d T^{2d} quantile level pairs . Thus, we measure 1 T 2 ​ d ∑ i , j T d 𝕀 { ( 𝒖 i − 𝒖 j ) ( Q ^ ( 𝒖 i ) − Q ^ ( 𝒖 j ) ) ⊤ &lt; 0 } . \\frac{1}{T^{2d}}\\sum_{i,j}^{T^{d}}\\mathbb{I}\\left\\{(\\bm{u}_{i}-\\bm{u}_{j}){{}^{\\top}}(\\widehat{Q}(\\bm{u}_{i})-\\widehat{Q}(\\bm{u}_{j}))&lt;0\\right\\}. E.2 Metrics for Real Data With real data, only finite samples from the ground truth distribution are available. In particular, since our real datasets have a continuous 𝐗 \\bm{\\mathrm{X}} , this means that we never have more than one sample from each conditional distribution 𝐘 | 𝐗 = 𝒙 \\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}=\\bm{x} . Strong representation can therefore not be quantified directly as with the above metrics. Instead, we opt for metrics which can be evaluated on finite samples and are specifically suitable for our chosen application of distribution-free uncertainty estimation ( section 6.3 ). Size of a conditional α \\alpha -confidence set (AC). This metric approximates | 𝒞 α ​ ( 𝒙 ) | \\left|\\mathcal{C}_{\\alpha}(\\bm{x})\\right| i.e. the size of an α \\alpha -confidence set constructed for a specific covariate 𝒙 \\bm{x} . Lower values of this metric are better, as they indicate that the confidence set is a better fit for the shape of the data distribution because, intuitively, the implication is that the same proportion of the data distribution can be represented by a smaller region. The metric is computed as follows. 1. Estimate a CVQF Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}) , e.g. via VQR ( 7 ), NL-VQR ( 8 ), or separable quantiles ( 10 ). 2. Choose a test covariate, denoted by 𝒙 T \\bm{x}^{T} . 3. Construct the corresponding conditional α \\alpha -contour, 𝒬 𝐘 | 𝐗 α ​ ( 𝒙 T ) \\mathcal{Q}^{\\alpha}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{x}^{T}) , as defined by eq. 22 , using the estimate Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 T ) \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}^{T}) . 4. Construct the corresponding conditional α \\alpha -confidence set 𝒞 α ​ ( 𝒙 T ) \\mathcal{C}_{\\alpha}(\\bm{x}^{T}) from the contour 𝒬 𝐘 | 𝐗 α ​ ( 𝒙 T ) \\mathcal{Q}^{\\alpha}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{x}^{T}) by calculating a convex hull of the points within the contour. 5. Calculate the value of the metric as the volume of the resulting convex hull (area for d = 2 d=2 ). We note that using a convex hull is only a linear approximation of the true 𝒞 α ​ ( 𝒙 ) \\mathcal{C}_{\\alpha}(\\bm{x}) defined by the points in the contour. For reasonable values of T T , we find it to be a good approximation, and it is an upper-bound on the area/volume of the true 𝒞 α ​ ( 𝒙 ) \\mathcal{C}_{\\alpha}(\\bm{x}) . In practice we use scipy with qhull ( Virtanen et al. 2020 ; Barber et al. 1996 ) to construct d d -dimensional convex hulls and measure their volume. Marginal Coverage (MC). This metric measures the proportion of unseen data points that are contained within the the conditional α \\alpha -confidence sets, 𝒞 α ​ ( 𝒙 ) \\mathcal{C}_{\\alpha}(\\bm{x}) , that was obtained from a given estimated CVQF. It is computed as follows. 1. Estimate a CVQF Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}) using one of the aforementioned methods on a training set sampled from P 𝐘 | 𝐗 P_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}} . 2. Denote a disjoint held-out test set { 𝒙 j T , 𝒚 j T } j = 1 N T ∼ P 𝐘 | 𝐗 . \\left\\{\\bm{x}^{T}_{j},\\bm{y}^{T}_{j}\\right\\}_{j=1}^{N_{T}}\\sim P_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}. 3. Measure the marginal coverage as 1 N T ∑ j N T 𝕀 { 𝒚 j T ∈ 𝒞 α ( 𝒙 j T ) } , \\frac{1}{N_{T}}\\sum_{j}^{N_{T}}\\mathbb{I}\\left\\{\\bm{y}^{T}_{j}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{\\alpha}(\\bm{x}^{T}_{j})\\right\\}, where 𝒞 α ​ ( 𝒙 j T ) \\mathcal{C}_{\\alpha}(\\bm{x}^{T}_{j}) is constructed as a convex hull of the conditional α \\alpha -contour 𝒬 𝐘 | 𝐗 α ​ ( 𝒙 j T ) \\mathcal{Q}^{\\alpha}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{x}^{T}_{j}) constructed using the estimated Q ^ 𝐘 | 𝐗 ​ ( 𝒖 , 𝒙 ) \\widehat{Q}_{\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}}|\\bm{\\mathrm{X}}}(\\bm{u};\\bm{x}) ( eq. 22 ). Appendix F Additional Experiments F.1 Scale Scale with respect to d d and k k . Figure A8 demonstrates the runtime of our solver with respect to the number of target dimensions d d and covariate dimensions k k . Figure A8: Effect of d d and k k on runtime. VQR solver runtime is shown as a function of number of target dimensions d d (left) and covariate dimensions k k (right). Calculated on the MVN dataset with N = 10 ​ k N=10k ; for d d experiment T = 10 T=10 ; for k k experiment T = 50 T=50 and d = 2 d=2 . Runtime scales exponentially with d d as expected, due to having T d T^{d} quantile levels. For k k , we can see that runtime remains relatively constant even for hundreds of dimensions, then increses due to memory constraints. Comparison to general-purpose solver. To showcase the scalability of our solver, we compare its runtime against an off-the-shelf linear program solver, ECOS, available within the CVXPY package ( Diamond &amp; Boyd 2016 ) . These results are shown in Figure A9 . As expected, our custom VQR solver approaches the performance of the general purpose solver which achieves slightly more accurate solutions due to solving an exact problem. However, the results demonstrate that with a linear program solver, the runtime quickly becomes prohibitive even for small problems. Crucially, it is important to note that linear program solvers only allow solving the linear VQR problem, while our solver supports nonlinear VQR. Figure A9: Our proposed solver is orders of magnitude faster than general-purpose linear program solver, and approaches the exact solution as the problem size increases. VQR and CVX (using ECOS LP solver) solver runtimes (solid lines) are shown as a function of number of samples N N (left) and quantile levels per dimension T T (right). Dashed lines indicate KDE-L1 as measure of solution quality. CVX obtains the ideal solution due to solving an exact problem. Calculated on the MVN dataset d = 2 d=2 , k = 10 k=10 . Left: T = 30 T=30 ; Right: N = 2000 N=2000 . F.2 Optimization To further evaluate our proposed VQR solver, we experimented with various values of ε \\varepsilon and batch sizes of samples and quantile levels, denoted as B N B_{N} and B T B_{T} respectively. Figure 10(a) shows the effect of the mini-batch size in both N N and T T on the accuracy of the solution. We measured the QFD metric, averaged over 100 evaluation 𝒙 \\bm{x} values. These results are presented in Figure 10(b) . As expected we observe improved accuracy when increasing batch sizes (both B N B_{N} and B T B_{T} ) and when decreasing ε \\varepsilon . (a) Effect of batch sizes B N B_{N} and B T B_{T} on optimization. Computed on MVN dataset with d = 2 d=2 and k = 1 k=1 . The vertical axis is the QFD metric defined in section 6 . Left: Sweeping over B N B_{N} ; T = 25 T=25 . Right: Sweep over B T B_{T} ; T = 50 T=50 produces 50 2 50^{2} total levels. (b) Decreasing ε \\varepsilon in the relaxed dual improves strong representation. Y-axis: QFD metric. Computed on MVN dataset with N = 20 ​ k N=20k , k = 1 k=1 . Right: T = 50 T=50 , d = 2 d=2 ; Left: T = 100 T=100 , d = 1 d=1 . Figure A10: Optimization experiments showing the effect of ε \\varepsilon and both batch sizes on convergence. F.3 VMR Figure 4 shows the effectiveness of our VMR procedure. We can see that when applying VMR, there are no monotonicity violations, even for low values of ε \\varepsilon , and, moreover, QFD metric improves. Figures 12(b) and 12(c) demonstrate how VMR can remove monotonicity violations, even when the distribution cannot be estimated correctly (in this case due to linear VQR). Finally, fig. A11 visualizes the co-monotonicity violations in 2d. Figure A11: Visualization of monotonicity violations in 2d. Left: co-monotonicity matrix, showing the value of ( 𝒖 i − 𝒖 j ) ( Q ( 𝒖 i ) − Q ( 𝒖 j ) ) ⊤ (\\bm{u}_{i}-\\bm{u}_{j}){{}^{\\top}}(Q(\\bm{u}_{i})-Q(\\bm{u}_{j})) for all i , j i,j , after fitting VQR without VMR with d = 2 d=2 , T = 10 T=10 on the MVN dataset. Right: quantile-level pairs i , j i,j where co-monotonicity is violated. VMR resolves all these violations. F.4 Nonlinear VQR Here we include additional results comparing linear and nonlinear VQR for various datasets. Figure 12(c) compares linear and nonlinear VQR on the synthetic glasses dataset. Figures A13 and A14 and table A3 present the results of linear and nonlinear VQR on the conditional-banana and rotating star datasets for many values of 𝒙 \\bm{x} . (a) Ground truth joint distribution (b) VQR (c) VQR + VMR (d) NL-VQR (e) NL-VQR + VMR Figure A12: VQR and NL-VQR can be used for Simultaneous Quantile Regression (SQR), and VMR eliminates quantile crossings. SQR on the synthetic glasses dataset (a; gray points show ground-truth distribution). Linear VQR fails to correctly model the conditional distribution (b, c; gray points sampled from linear VQR), while nonlinear VQR reconstructs the shape exactly (d, e; gray points sampled from nonlinear VQR). In both cases case, VMR successfully eliminates any quantile crossings (c, e). Figure A13: Qualitative evaluation of VQR, CVAE and NL-VQR on the conditional-banana dataset. Depicted are the kernel-density estimates of samples drawn from each of these methods. Models were trained with N = 20 ​ k N=20k with T = 50 T=50 quantile levels per dimension. Numbers depict the KDE-L1 metric. Better viewed as GIF provided in the supplementary material. Figure A14: Qualitative evaluation of linear VQR, CVAE and nonlinear VQR on the rotating stars dataset. Depicted are the kernel-density estimates of samples drawn from each of these methods. Models were trained with N = 20 ​ k N=20k with T = 50 T=50 quantile levels per dimension. Numbers depict the KDE-L1 metric. Better viewed as GIF provided in the supplementary material. Table A2: Quantitative evaluation of linear VQR, CVAE and nonlinear VQR on the conditional-banana dataset. Evaluated on N = 4000 N=4000 samples with T = 50 T=50 levels per dimension. The KDE-L1, QFD and Entropy metrics are defined in section 6 . Arrows ↑ \\uparrow / ↓ \\downarrow indicate when higher/lower is better; Numbers are mean ± \\pm std calculated over the 20 values of 𝒙 \\bm{x} . Reference entropy is obtained by uniformly sampling the 2d quantile grid. CVAE VQR NL-VQR KDE-L1 ↓ \\downarrow 0.175 ± 0.021 0.175\\pm 0.021 0.873 ± 0.175 0.873\\pm 0.175 0.135 ± 0.024 \\mathbf{0.135}\\pm 0.024 Quantile Function Distance (QFD) ↓ \\downarrow - 0.179 ± 0.034 0.179\\pm 0.034 0.055 ± 0.023 \\mathbf{0.055}\\pm 0.023 Inverse CVQF Entropy (Ref: 0.70 0.70 ) ↑ \\uparrow - 0.309 ± 0.077 0.309\\pm 0.077 0.560 ± 0.014 \\mathbf{0.560}\\pm 0.014 Train time (min) ↓ \\downarrow 35 35 3.5 \\mathbf{3.5} 4 4 Table A3: Quantitative evaluation of linear VQR, CVAE, and nonlinear VQR on the rotating stars dataset. Evaluated on N = 4000 N=4000 samples with T = 50 T=50 levels per dimension. The KDE-L1, QFD and Entropy metrics are defined in section 6 . Arrows ↑ \\uparrow / ↓ \\downarrow indicate when higher/lower is better; Numbers are mean ± \\pm std calculated over the 20 values of 𝒙 \\bm{x} . Reference entropy is obtained by uniformly sampling the 2d quantile grid. CVAE VQR NL-VQR KDE-L1 ↓ \\downarrow 0.95 ± 0.04 0.95\\pm 0.04 0.35 ± 0.06 0.35\\pm 0.06 0.20 ± 0.01 \\mathbf{0.20}\\pm 0.01 Quantile Function Distance (QFD) ↓ \\downarrow – 0.18 ± 0.04 0.18\\pm 0.04 0.08 ± 0.01 \\mathbf{0.08}\\pm 0.01 Inverse CVQF Entropy (Ref: 0.69 0.69 ) ↑ \\uparrow – 0.51 ± 0.02 0.51\\pm 0.02 0.59 ± 0.02 \\mathbf{0.59}\\pm 0.02 Train time (min) ↓ \\downarrow 35 35 3.5 \\mathbf{3.5} 4 4 Figure A15: Nonlinear VQR produces substantially smaller α \\alpha -confidence sets in real data experiments. Comparison of nonlinear VQR (NL-VQR), separable nonlinear QR (Sep-NLQR), linear VQR (VQR), and linear separable QR approaches (Sep-QR) on the four real datasets presented in table A1 . The confidence sets are constructed for each method in such a way to produce same desired level of marginal coverage (blue dashed line). Error bars generated from 10 10 trials, each with a different randomly-sampled train/test split. Figure A16: Nonlinear VQR produces α \\alpha -quantile contours which model the shape of the conditional distribution and have significantly smaller area compared to other methods . Each column depicts α \\alpha -quantile contours for two test covariates (top, bottom) sampled from bio (left), house prices (middle), and blog_data (right) datasets. Separable QR approaches produce box-shaped confidence regions due to the assumption of statistical independence, whereas VQR and nonlinear VQR produce contours with non-trivial shapes that adapt to the test covariate at hand (each column, top vs bottom). The areas of the α \\alpha -confidence sets are reported relative to NL-VQR in the legend. Appendix G Implementation details Solver implementation details. The log-sum-exp term in eq. 7 becomes numerically unstable as ε \\varepsilon decreases, which can be mitigated by implementing it as logsumexp ε ​ ( 𝒙 ) = logsumexp ε ​ ( 𝒙 − max k ⁡ { x k } ) + max k ⁡ { x k } . \\mathrm{logsumexp}_{\\varepsilon}(\\bm{x})=\\mathrm{logsumexp}_{\\varepsilon}\\left(\\bm{x}-\\max_{k}\\{x_{k}\\}\\right)+\\max_{k}\\{x_{k}\\}. Scale and Optimization experiment. For both scale and optimization experiments, we run VQR for 10 ​ k 10k iterations and use a learning rate scheduler that decays the learning rate by a factor of 0.9 0.9 every 500 500 iterations if the error does not drop by 0.5 % 0.5\\% . Synthetic glasses experiment. We set N = 10 ​ k N=10k , T = 100 T=100 , and ε = 0.001 \\varepsilon=0.001 . We optimized both VQR and NL-VQR for 40 ​ k 40k iterations and use a learning rate scheduler that decays the learning rate by a factor of 0.9 0.9 every 500 500 iterations if the error does not drop by 0.5 % 0.5\\% . In NL-VQR, as g θ g_{\\theta} , we used a 3-layer fully-connected network with each hidden layer of size 1000 1000 . We used skip-connections, batch-norm, and ReLU nonlinearities between the hidden layers. For NL-VQR and VQR, we set the initial learning rate to be 0.4 0.4 and 1 1 , respectively. Conditional Banana and Rotating Star experiments. We draw N = 20 ​ k N=20k samples from P ( X , 𝐘 ) P_{(\\mathrm{X},\\bm{\\mathrm{Y}})} and fit T = 50 T=50 quantile levels per dimension ( d = 2 d=2 ) for linear and nonlinear VQR (NL-VQR). Evaluation is performed by measuring all aforementioned metrics on 4000 4000 evaluation samples from 20 20 true conditional distributions, conditioned on x = [ 1.1 , 1.2 , … , 3.0 ] x=[1.1,1.2,\\dots,3.0] . For NL-VQR, as g 𝜽 g_{\\bm{\\theta}} we use a small MLP with three hidden layers of size ( 2 , 10 , 20 ) (2,10,20) and a ReLU non-linearity. Thus g 𝜽 g_{\\bm{\\theta}} lifts X \\mathrm{X} into 20 20 dimensions on which VQR is performed. We set ε = 0.005 \\varepsilon=0.005 and optimized both VQR and NL-VQR for 20 ​ k 20k iterations. We used the same learning rate and schedulers as in the synthetic glasses experiment. Real data experiments. In all the real data experiments, we randomly split the data into 80 % 80\\% training set and 20 % 20\\% hold-out test set. Fitting is performed on the train split, and evaluation metrics, marginal coverage and quantile contour area (calculated as reported in section E.2 ), are measured on the test split. We repeat this procedure 10 10 times with different random splits. The baselines we evaluate are NL-VQR, VQR, separable nonlinear QR (Sep-NLQR), and separable linear QR (Sep-QR). In the separable baselines, two QR models are fit separately, one for each target variable. For both nonlinear separable QR and NL-VQR baselines, we choose g θ g_{\\theta} to be an MLP with three hidden layers. In the case of NL-VQR, hidden layers sizes are set to ( 100 , 60 , 20 ) (100,60,20) and for nonlinear separable QR, they are set to ( 50 , 30 , 10 ) (50,30,10) . All methods were run for 40 ​ k 40k iterations, with learning rate set to 0.3 0.3 and ε = 0.01 \\varepsilon=0.01 . We set T = 50 T=50 for NL-VQR and VQR baselines, and T = 100 T=100 for separable linear and nonlinear QR baselines. The α \\alpha parameter that determines the α \\alpha -quantile contour construction is calibrated separately for each method and dataset. The goal of the calibration procedure is to achieve consistent coverage across baselines in order to allow for the comparison of the α \\alpha -quantile contour areas. Table A4 presents the calibrated α \\alpha ’s that were used. Dataset NL-VQR VQR Sep-NLQR Sep-QR α \\alpha Cov. Area α \\alpha Cov. Area α \\alpha Cov. Area α \\alpha Cov. Area bio 0.05 0.05 85.00 85.00 1.57 1.57 0.03 0.03 82.87 82.87 2.49 2.49 0.01 0.01 85.13 85.13 3.18 3.18 0.02 0.02 85.76 85.76 3.59 3.59 blog_data 0.05 0.05 84.17 84.17 1.19 1.19 0.05 0.05 84.50 84.50 3.41 3.41 0.025 0.025 84.60 84.60 3.02 3.02 0.025 0.025 71.10 71.10 4.73 4.73 house 0.05 0.05 82.29 82.29 1.69 1.69 0.06 0.06 80.75 80.75 3.39 3.39 0.025 0.025 82.51 82.51 2.53 2.53 0.03 0.03 82.48 82.48 4.76 4.76 meps_20 0.02 0.02 74.13 74.13 1.81 1.81 0.06 0.06 74.82 74.82 4.04 4.04 0.01 0.01 74.73 74.73 2.78 2.78 0.01 0.01 75.77 75.77 9.70 9.70 Table A4: Choice of α \\alpha for different real datasets. Nominal (requested) coverage for bio and blog_data was 85 % 85\\% . For house and meps_20 datasets, it was 82 % 82\\% and 75 % 75\\% , respectively. Machine configuration. All experiments were run on a machine with an Intel Xeon E5 CPU, 256GB of RAM and an Nvidia Titan 2080Ti GPU with 11GB dedicated graphics memory. Appendix H Software We provide our VQR solvers as part of a comprehensive open-source python package vqr which can be installed with pip install vqr . The source code is available at https://github.com/vistalab-technion/vqr and it contains several jupyter notebooks which demonstrate the use of our package. ⬇ import numpy as np from vqr import VectorQuantileRegressor from vqr . solvers . regularized_lse import RegularizedDualVQRSolver # Create the VQR solver and regressor. vqr_solver = RegularizedDualVQRSolver ( verbose = True , T =50, epsilon =1 e -2, num_epochs =1000, lr =0.9 ) vqr = VectorQuantileRegressor ( solver = vqr_solver ) # Fit the model on some training data. vqr . fit ( X_train , Y_train ) # Sample from the fitted conditional distribution, given a specific x. Y_sampled = vqr . sample ( n =100, x = X_test [0]) # Calculate coverage on the samples. cov_sampled = vqr . coverage ( Y_sampled , x = X_test [0], alpha = alpha ) Listing 1: Minimal example of using the vqr library. Demonstrates instantiation of solver and regressor, fitting VQR, sampling from the fitted CVQF and coverage calculation. We note that although a reference Matlab implementation of linear VQR by the original authors exists , this implementation relies on solving the exact formulation ( 5 ) using a general-purpose linear program solver. We found it to be prohibitively slow to use general-purpose linear program solvers for VQR ( fig. A9 ). For example, we noted a runtime of 4.5 hours for N = 7 ​ k , d = 2 , k = 10 , T = 30 N=7k,d=2,k=10,T=30 , and for N = 10 ​ k N=10k the solver did not converge even after 8 hours. Thus, this approach is unsuitable even for modestly-sized problems. Moreover, when using such solvers, only linear VQR can be performed. For comparison, our proposed solver converges to a solution of the same quality on problems of these sizes in less than one minute and supports nonlinear VQR. ◄ Feeling lucky? Conversion report Report an issue View&nbsp;original on&nbsp;arXiv ► Copyright Privacy Policy Generated by L a T e XML oxide"}
